/* 內建閱讀測驗文章庫（a14 起）：B1、B2、應用文，以及 A2 入門（a201–a220）、C1 挑戰（a221–a240）、C2 頂尖（a241–a260），和第二批 B1（a261–a280）、C1（a281–a290）、C2（a291–a300）。每篇 5 題、中文解析與逐段中文翻譯，由第二位老師盲解審核後加入。 */
window.READING_LIBRARY = [{"id": "a14", "title": "Why We Forget, and How to Remember Longer", "title_zh": "我們為什麼會遺忘，又該如何記得更久", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "Science"], "passage": "Have you ever studied hard for a vocabulary quiz, done well, and then found a week later that you could hardly remember any of the words? If so, you are not alone. Forgetting is a normal part of how the brain works, and scientists have been studying it for more than a hundred years.\n\nOne of the first people to study memory carefully was Hermann Ebbinghaus, a German psychologist. In the 1880s, he made up more than two thousand nonsense syllables, such as \"WID\" and \"ZOF,\" which had no meaning at all. He used them so that what he already knew about real words would not affect the results. He learned lists of these syllables and then tested himself after twenty minutes, an hour, a day, and even a month. When he put his results on a graph, he found what is now called the \"forgetting curve.\" It showed that most forgetting happens very quickly. Within the first day, much of the new information was already gone. After that, forgetting slowed down.\n\nThe good news is that Ebbinghaus also found a way to fight the curve. Each time he reviewed a list, he forgot it more slowly than before. In other words, every review makes a memory stronger. This idea led to a study method called spaced repetition. Instead of reviewing something many times in one night, you review it several times with longer and longer breaks in between.\n\nHow can students use this idea? Suppose you learn twenty new English words on Monday. Review them on Tuesday, again on Thursday, then the next Monday, and once more two weeks later. Each review may take only ten minutes, because you are not starting from zero. Many students write the review dates on their word cards or use flashcard apps that plan the schedule for them.\n\nSpaced repetition still takes effort. However, it is far more efficient than cramming the night before a test. Cramming may help you pass tomorrow's quiz, but it rarely helps you remember anything for a big exam months later. If you want knowledge to stay with you, the secret is not studying longer but reviewing at the right time.", "passage_zh": "你是否曾經為了單字小考努力念書、考得不錯，結果一個星期後卻發現幾乎一個字都想不起來？如果有，你並不孤單。遺忘是大腦運作的正常現象，而科學家研究這件事已經超過一百年了。\n\n最早仔細研究記憶的人之一，是德國心理學家赫爾曼‧艾賓浩斯。在 1880 年代，他自創了兩千多個毫無意義的音節，例如「WID」和「ZOF」。他使用這些音節，是為了不讓自己對真實單字原有的認識影響實驗結果。他背下一串串這樣的音節，然後在二十分鐘、一小時、一天，甚至一個月後再測驗自己。當他把結果畫成圖表時，他發現了現在所謂的「遺忘曲線」。曲線顯示，大部分的遺忘發生得非常快。在第一天之內，許多新資訊就已經忘掉了；在那之後，遺忘的速度才變慢。\n\n好消息是，艾賓浩斯也找到了對抗這條曲線的方法。他每複習一次清單，遺忘的速度就比之前更慢。換句話說，每一次複習都會讓記憶更牢固。這個概念發展出一種叫做「間隔重複」的讀書方法：不要在一個晚上把同樣的內容複習很多次，而是分成好幾次複習，而且每次之間的間隔越拉越長。\n\n學生可以怎麼運用這個概念呢？假設你在星期一學了二十個英文新單字。星期二複習一次，星期四再複習一次，接著下個星期一，兩個星期後再複習最後一次。每次複習可能只要十分鐘，因為你並不是從零開始。許多學生會在單字卡上寫下複習日期，或使用能幫他們安排複習時間表的單字卡 app。\n\n間隔重複仍然需要付出努力。不過，它比考前一晚臨時抱佛腳有效率得多。臨時抱佛腳也許能幫你通過明天的小考，卻很少能幫你在幾個月後的大考中記住任何東西。如果你希望知識留在腦中，祕訣不在於念得更久，而在於在對的時間複習。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The life story of a famous German psychologist", "Why we forget new information and how spaced review helps us remember it", "Why nonsense syllables are easier to remember than real words", "How to get a high score on a quiz by studying all night"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先說明遺忘曲線（人學新東西後很快就會忘記），再介紹「間隔重複」的複習方法與實際做法。(A) 文中只提到艾賓浩斯的研究，沒有介紹他的生平；(C) 第二段說無意義音節是為了避免影響實驗結果，並沒有說它比較好記；(D) 最後一段指出熬夜臨時抱佛腳很難讓人長期記住，與文意相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did Ebbinghaus use nonsense syllables in his experiments?", "options": ["They were shorter and easier to say than German words.", "He wanted to create a new language for students.", "He did not want what he already knew about real words to affect the results.", "Real words were too difficult for him to remember."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：He used them so that what he already knew about real words would not affect the results. 他用無意義音節，是為了不讓自己原本對真實單字的認識影響結果。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the forgetting curve, when does most forgetting happen?", "options": ["Very soon after learning, especially within the first day", "About one month after learning", "Only when people learn words with no meaning", "Only on the night before a test"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段末：most forgetting happens very quickly. Within the first day, much of the new information was already gone. After that, forgetting slowed down. 大部分遺忘發生在剛學完不久、尤其是第一天之內。(B) 一個月後遺忘已經變慢；(C) 文中沒有說遺忘只發生在無意義的字上；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following study plans best follows the idea of spaced repetition?", "options": ["Reviewing a word list five times on Sunday night", "Learning new words every day without looking at the old ones again", "Reading a word list once and testing yourself a month later", "Reviewing a word list the next day, a few days later, and again after a week or two"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說明間隔重複是 review it several times with longer and longer breaks in between（分次複習，間隔越拉越長），第四段的例子也是隔天、隔幾天、隔一週、隔兩週複習。(A) 是在一個晚上重複很多次，正是文中說不要做的方式；(B) 完全不複習舊單字；(C) 只讀一次，沒有分次複習。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"cramming\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["studying a lot in a very short time just before a test", "going to bed early before an important exam", "asking a teacher for help after class", "reviewing slowly over many weeks"], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段 cramming the night before a test 指「考前一晚塞進大量內容」，也就是臨時抱佛腳，並說它 may help you pass tomorrow's quiz（也許能應付明天的小考）。(B)(C) 與文意無關；(D) 是間隔重複的做法，與 cramming 正好相反。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a15", "title": "Twenty-Five Minutes at a Time: The Pomodoro Technique", "title_zh": "一次二十五分鐘：番茄鐘工作法", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "Life"], "passage": "Wei-ting, a second-year student at a vocational high school in Kaohsiung, used to sit at her desk for three hours every evening. However, when she looked back at what she had actually done, the answer was often \"not much.\" She checked her phone, went to get snacks, and drifted into daydreams. Her desk time was long, but her real study time was short.\n\nThen her English teacher introduced the class to the Pomodoro Technique. It was created in the late 1980s by Francesco Cirillo, an Italian university student who was having trouble focusing on his own studies. He used a kitchen timer shaped like a tomato to time himself. Pomodoro is the Italian word for tomato, and that is how the method got its name.\n\nThe rules are simple. First, choose one task, such as finishing a page of math problems. Next, set a timer for 25 minutes and work only on that task until the timer rings. Then take a five-minute break: stand up, stretch, or drink some water. Each 25-minute period is called one \"pomodoro.\" After four pomodoros, take a longer break of 15 to 30 minutes.\n\nWei-ting found the method helpful for two reasons. First, 25 minutes did not feel scary, so it was easier to get started. Even on days when she did not feel like studying, she told herself, \"Just one pomodoro,\" and she usually kept going after that. Second, knowing that a break was coming made it easier to ignore her phone. If she suddenly remembered a message she wanted to send, she wrote it on a piece of paper and sent it during the break.\n\nOf course, the technique does not suit everyone perfectly. Some students find that 25 minutes is too short when they are in the middle of a hard problem, so they change it to 40 or 50 minutes. That is fine, because the main idea is to work in short, focused periods with planned rest. Now Wei-ting studies for only two hours a night, but she gets more done than she ever did in three.", "passage_zh": "瑋婷是高雄一所高職的二年級學生，她以前每天晚上都會在書桌前坐三個小時。然而，當她回頭檢視自己實際完成了什麼，答案常常是「沒做多少」。她會滑手機、起身拿零食，還會不知不覺地發起白日夢。她坐在書桌前的時間很長，真正讀書的時間卻很短。\n\n後來，她的英文老師向全班介紹了番茄鐘工作法。這個方法是在 1980 年代後期由法蘭西斯科‧西里洛所創，他當時是一名義大利大學生，自己念書時也很難專心。他用一個番茄造型的廚房計時器來替自己計時。Pomodoro 是義大利文的「番茄」，這個方法就是因此而得名。\n\n規則很簡單。首先，選定一項任務，例如寫完一頁數學題。接著，把計時器設定為 25 分鐘，在計時器響起之前只做這一件事。然後休息五分鐘：站起來、伸展一下，或喝點水。每一段 25 分鐘稱為一個「番茄鐘」。完成四個番茄鐘之後，再休息比較長的 15 到 30 分鐘。\n\n瑋婷發現這個方法有幫助，原因有兩個。第一，25 分鐘聽起來並不可怕，所以比較容易開始。即使在不想讀書的日子，她也會告訴自己「只做一個番茄鐘就好」，而且通常做完之後就會繼續下去。第二，知道休息時間快到了，讓她比較容易不去理會手機。如果她突然想起要傳某則訊息，就先寫在紙上，等休息時再傳。\n\n當然，這個方法並不完全適合每個人。有些學生發現，正在解一道難題時，25 分鐘太短了，所以會把時間改成 40 或 50 分鐘。這樣也沒關係，因為這個方法的主要精神，就是用短時間專心做事，並搭配事先安排好的休息。現在瑋婷每晚只讀兩個小時，完成的進度卻比以前讀三個小時還要多。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a student used a time-management method to study more effectively", "Why many Italian university students enjoy cooking", "How to stop using cell phones forever", "Why students should study for at least three hours every night"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以瑋婷為例，介紹番茄鐘工作法的由來、規則，以及它如何幫助她更有效率地讀書。(B) 番茄造型計時器只是名稱由來，與烹飪無關；(C) 文中只說休息時再處理手機訊息，沒有說要永遠不用手機；(D) 最後一段說她現在只讀兩小時卻完成更多，與此相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Where does the name \"Pomodoro\" come from?", "options": ["The name of Francesco Cirillo's teacher", "A city in Italy where the method was first used", "A kitchen timer shaped like a tomato", "A kind of Italian snack that students like"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：He used a kitchen timer shaped like a tomato... Pomodoro is the Italian word for tomato, and that is how the method got its name. 名稱來自西里洛用的番茄造型廚房計時器。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the rules, what should a student do after finishing four pomodoros?", "options": ["Take a five-minute break", "Take a break of 15 to 30 minutes", "Start the fifth pomodoro right away", "Stop studying for the rest of the day"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段最後一句：After four pomodoros, take a longer break of 15 to 30 minutes. 完成四個番茄鐘後應休息 15 到 30 分鐘。(A) 五分鐘是每個番茄鐘之間的短休息；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did Wei-ting write messages on a piece of paper?", "options": ["Her phone was broken, so she could not send them.", "Her teacher asked her to send letters instead of messages.", "She wanted to practice her handwriting.", "She wanted to keep focusing and send them during her break."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：knowing that a break was coming made it easier to ignore her phone. If she suddenly remembered a message... she wrote it on a piece of paper and sent it during the break. 她先把要傳的訊息記下來，是為了讀書時不被手機打斷，休息時再處理。(A)(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"drifted\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["ran quickly", "moved slowly without a plan or control", "argued loudly", "fell down suddenly"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段 She checked her phone, went to get snacks, and drifted into daydreams. 列舉她讀書時分心的情形，drifted into daydreams 指「不知不覺、不受控制地陷入白日夢」，drift 的意思是漂流、緩慢而無目的地移動。(A)(C)(D) 都不符合「分心發呆」的語境。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a16", "title": "Write Away Your Worries: Dealing with Test Anxiety", "title_zh": "把擔心寫下來：如何面對考試焦慮", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "Health"], "passage": "Your hands are cold, your heart is beating fast, and your mind suddenly goes blank, even though you have studied for weeks. This feeling is called test anxiety, and many students experience it before big exams. A little stress can keep you alert and ready to act, but too much can make it hard to show what you really know.\n\nWhy does this happen? Scientists believe that worry uses up part of our working memory. Working memory is like the brain's notepad: it holds information for a short time while we think. When a student keeps thinking \"What if I fail?\", there is less room left on the notepad for the math problem in front of him or her.\n\nIn 2011, researchers at the University of Chicago tested a surprisingly simple idea. Just before an important final exam, they asked one group of high school students to spend about ten minutes writing freely about their thoughts and worries about the test. Another group did not write about their worries. Among students who usually felt very nervous before tests, those who wrote about their worries did better on the exam than those who did not. The researchers believe that putting worries on paper helped to \"empty\" them from the students' minds, so the students had more brain power left for the test.\n\nYou can try this yourself. On the morning of a test, or the night before, take a piece of paper and write down everything that is worrying you. Be honest, because no one else needs to read it. Then take a few slow, deep breaths. Breathing slowly tells your body that it is safe and helps your heart calm down.\n\nFinally, remember that anxiety is not a sign of weakness. It often means that you care about the result. Instead of telling yourself, \"I'm so nervous,\" try saying, \"I'm excited and ready.\" Some studies suggest that seeing your nervous feelings as energy can actually help you perform better. If your worries ever feel too heavy to carry alone, talk to a teacher, a school counselor, or your family. With the right tools, you can make stress work for you, not against you.", "passage_zh": "你的雙手冰冷、心跳加速，腦袋突然一片空白，儘管你已經準備了好幾個星期。這種感覺叫做考試焦慮，許多學生在大考前都會有這種經驗。一點點壓力能讓你保持警覺、隨時準備好行動，但壓力太大就會讓你很難展現真正的實力。\n\n為什麼會這樣呢？科學家認為，擔憂會占用我們一部分的工作記憶。工作記憶就像大腦的記事本：在我們思考的時候，它會把資訊短暫地記下來。當一個學生腦中一直想著「萬一我考不好怎麼辦？」，記事本上能留給眼前那道數學題的空間就變少了。\n\n2011 年，芝加哥大學的研究人員測試了一個簡單得出人意料的方法。在一場重要的期末考開始前，他們請一組高中生花大約十分鐘，自由地寫下自己對這次考試的想法與擔憂；另一組學生則沒有寫下擔憂。結果，在平常考前就會非常緊張的學生當中，寫下擔憂的人考得比沒有寫的人好。研究人員認為，把擔憂寫在紙上有助於把它們從學生的腦中「清空」，讓學生留下更多腦力來應付考試。\n\n你也可以自己試試看。在考試當天早上或前一天晚上，拿出一張紙，把所有讓你擔心的事情都寫下來。要誠實地寫，因為沒有其他人需要看到它。接著，慢慢地做幾次深呼吸。緩慢的呼吸會告訴身體它是安全的，並幫助心跳平穩下來。\n\n最後，請記得焦慮並不是軟弱的表現，它往往代表你在乎結果。與其對自己說「我好緊張」，不如試著說「我很興奮，也準備好了」。有些研究指出，把緊張的感覺看成能量，其實能幫助你表現得更好。如果你覺得擔憂沉重到無法獨自承受，可以找老師、學校的輔導老師或家人談一談。只要用對方法，你就能讓壓力幫助你，而不是和你作對。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To tell students that exams are not important at all", "To introduce the history of the University of Chicago", "To prove that nervous students always fail their exams", "To explain test anxiety and suggest ways to deal with it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一、二段說明什麼是考試焦慮及其成因，第三至五段介紹研究結果並提出寫下擔憂、深呼吸、把緊張視為能量等方法。(A) 文中沒有說考試不重要；(B) 芝加哥大學只是做研究的單位；(C) 第三段指出容易緊張的學生寫下擔憂後反而考得更好，與此相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why can worrying make it hard to solve problems on a test?", "options": ["It takes up space in working memory that is needed for thinking.", "It makes students sleep badly the night before.", "It makes students forget the date of the test.", "It causes students to write much more slowly."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：worry uses up part of our working memory... there is less room left on the notepad for the math problem. 擔憂占用了思考時需要的工作記憶空間。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In the 2011 study, what did one group of students do just before the exam?", "options": ["They took a short deep-breathing class.", "They studied the test material for ten more minutes.", "They wrote about their thoughts and worries for about ten minutes.", "They talked with their teachers about the questions on the test."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：they asked one group of high school students to spend about ten minutes writing freely about their thoughts and worries about the test. 這組學生在考前花約十分鐘寫下想法與擔憂。(A) 深呼吸是第四段作者給讀者的建議，不是研究內容；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"alert\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["tired and sleepy", "angry and upset", "lonely and sad", "awake and paying attention"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段 A little stress can keep you alert and ready to act，與後面的 ready to act（準備好行動）並列，可知 alert 是「警覺的、注意力集中的」。(A) 想睡與「準備好行動」相反；(B)(C) 與文意無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "A classmate says, \"I'm so nervous about tomorrow's test.\" Based on the passage, which reply would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["\"Being nervous means you didn't study enough.\"", "\"Then you should stay up all night and study more.\"", "\"Feeling nervous shows you care. Try to see it as energy.\"", "\"Keep your worries to yourself and don't write anything down.\""], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一段說焦慮 often means that you care about the result，並建議 seeing your nervous feelings as energy（把緊張看成能量）。(A) 與「焦慮不是軟弱的表現」不符；(B) 文中未建議熬夜；(D) 與第三、四段「把擔憂寫下來」的建議相反。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a17", "title": "One Page, Three Parts: The Cornell Note-Taking System", "title_zh": "一頁分三區：康乃爾筆記法", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education"], "passage": "Many students take notes in class by writing down everything the teacher says, as fast as they can. Later, when they open their notebooks to review, they find pages full of messy sentences and no clear idea of what is important. If this sounds familiar, the Cornell note-taking system may help.\n\nThe system was developed in the 1950s by Walter Pauk, a professor at Cornell University in the United States. He wanted to help college students study more effectively. His method is still popular today, partly because it needs nothing more than a pen and a sheet of paper.\n\nTo use it, divide a page into three parts. First, draw a line across the page about five centimeters from the bottom. Then draw a line from the top of the page down to that line, about six centimeters from the left edge. The large space on the right is for the notes you take during class. Write short phrases instead of full sentences, and use abbreviations, such as \"e.g.\" for \"for example\" or \"info\" for \"information.\"\n\nThe narrow column on the left is called the cue column. After class, read through your notes and write key words or questions in this column. For example, next to notes about the causes of a war, you might write, \"Why did the war start?\" The space at the bottom of the page is for a summary. In two or three sentences, write the main ideas of the whole page in your own words.\n\nThe real power of the system appears when you review. Cover the right side of the page with a piece of paper, so that only the questions on the left can be seen. Try to answer them out loud, and then uncover your notes to check. This turns your notes into a self-test, which is much more useful than simply reading them again. Students who review this way often find that they remember more and feel calmer before exams.", "passage_zh": "許多學生上課做筆記的方式，是盡可能快速地把老師說的每一句話都寫下來。之後打開筆記本複習時，才發現整頁都是凌亂的句子，根本看不出什麼才是重點。如果這聽起來很熟悉，康乃爾筆記法或許能幫上忙。\n\n這套方法是在 1950 年代由美國康乃爾大學的教授華特‧鮑克所發展出來的。他希望幫助大學生更有效率地讀書。這個方法到今天仍然很受歡迎，部分原因是它只需要一支筆和一張紙。\n\n使用方法是把一頁分成三個區塊。首先，在距離頁面底部大約五公分的地方畫一條橫線。接著，在距離左邊大約六公分的地方，從頁面頂端往下畫一條直線，畫到剛才那條橫線為止。右邊的大空間用來寫上課時的筆記。要寫簡短的片語而不是完整的句子，並使用縮寫，例如用「e.g.」代表「for example（例如）」，用「info」代表「information（資訊）」。\n\n左邊那一欄窄窄的區塊叫做「提示欄」。下課後，把筆記從頭讀一遍，並在這一欄寫下關鍵字或問題。例如，在關於某場戰爭起因的筆記旁邊，你可以寫「這場戰爭為什麼會爆發？」頁面底部的空間則用來寫摘要：用兩、三句話，以自己的話寫出整頁的重點。\n\n這套方法真正的威力在複習時才會展現出來。用一張紙蓋住頁面右邊，只露出左邊的問題。試著把答案大聲說出來，然後再掀開筆記核對。這樣就把筆記變成了自我測驗，比單純再讀一遍有用得多。用這種方式複習的學生，常常發現自己記得更多，考前也比較不緊張。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history of Cornell University in the United States", "Why students should write down every word the teacher says", "A way to organize a page for taking and reviewing notes", "How to draw straight lines neatly in a notebook"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹康乃爾筆記法：如何把一頁分成三區（第三段）、各區寫什麼（第三、四段），以及如何用它複習（第五段）。(A) 康乃爾大學只是發明者任教的學校；(B) 第一段指出把每句話都寫下來正是問題所在；(D) 畫線只是步驟之一，並非主旨。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what should be written in the cue column?", "options": ["Everything the teacher says in class", "Key words or questions about the notes", "A summary of the page in full sentences", "The date and the name of the teacher"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：The narrow column on the left is called the cue column... write key words or questions in this column. 提示欄寫的是關鍵字或問題。(A) 課堂筆記寫在右邊大空間，且不是每句都抄；(C) 摘要寫在頁面底部；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "When should a student fill in the cue column?", "options": ["After class, while reading through the notes", "Before the class begins", "While the teacher is speaking in class", "Only on the night before the exam"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：After class, read through your notes and write key words or questions in this column. 提示欄是下課後重讀筆記時填寫的。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 上課時寫的是右邊的筆記區；(D) 文中沒有說只在考前一晚寫。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer suggest covering the right side of the page when reviewing?", "options": ["To keep the notes clean and neat", "To save paper for the next class", "To stop classmates from copying the notes", "To test how much you can remember without looking"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：蓋住右邊、只看左邊的問題並試著回答，This turns your notes into a self-test（把筆記變成自我測驗）。目的在檢查自己不看筆記能記得多少。(A)(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"abbreviations\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["short forms of words or phrases", "small pictures and drawings", "long and complete sentences", "words from foreign languages"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 use abbreviations, such as \"e.g.\" for \"for example\" or \"info\" for \"information\"，從例子可知 abbreviations 是把字詞寫成較短的形式，也就是「縮寫」。(B) 圖畫與例子不符；(C) 同段前面才說要寫短的片語而非完整句子；(D) 例子並非外語。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a18", "title": "Hao-yu's Notebook of Mistakes", "title_zh": "浩宇的錯題本", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "Life"], "passage": "When Hao-yu got his first mock exam back in his third year of senior high, he felt terrible. He had scored only 42 in math. What made it worse was that many of the questions looked familiar. He had seen similar ones in class, and he had even gotten some of them wrong before. \"Why do I keep making the same mistakes?\" he asked himself.\n\nHis math teacher, Ms. Lin, gave him some advice. \"Don't just look at your score and throw the test into your bag,\" she said. \"Every wrong answer is trying to tell you something. Start a notebook of mistakes.\"\n\nThat evening, Hao-yu bought a new notebook. For each question he had gotten wrong, he followed three steps. First, he copied the question onto the left page. Second, on the right page, he wrote down why he had made the mistake. Was it because he did not understand an idea, because he did not read the question carefully, or simply because he made a careless calculation? Third, below that, he wrote out the correct solution step by step, trying not to look at the answer key.\n\nAt first, the work was slow and boring. However, after a few weeks, Hao-yu noticed something interesting. Most of his mistakes fell into just a few groups. He often mixed up two similar formulas, and he often forgot to check whether his answer should be positive or negative. Once he knew his weak points, he could practice them on purpose instead of doing exercises at random.\n\nEvery Sunday, he covered the solutions and tried the questions in his notebook again. If he got a question right two times in a row, he marked it with a star and stopped reviewing it. Four months later, his math score on the mock exam rose to 71. More importantly, he had learned that mistakes are not something to hide. They are a map that shows you exactly where to go next.", "passage_zh": "浩宇在高三拿回第一次模擬考的考卷時，心情糟透了。他的數學只考了 42 分。更讓他難過的是，很多題目看起來都很眼熟。他在課堂上看過類似的題目，有些甚至以前就答錯過。「為什麼我總是犯同樣的錯？」他問自己。\n\n他的數學老師林老師給了他一些建議。「不要只看分數，然後就把考卷丟進書包裡，」她說，「每一個錯誤的答案都在試著告訴你一些事。開始寫一本錯題本吧。」\n\n當天晚上，浩宇買了一本新的筆記本。每一道他答錯的題目，他都按照三個步驟處理。第一，把題目抄在左邊那一頁。第二，在右邊那一頁寫下自己為什麼會錯：是因為不懂某個觀念、沒有仔細讀題目，還是只是計算時粗心？第三，在下面一步一步寫出正確的解法，並盡量不看解答。\n\n一開始，這項工作又慢又無聊。然而，幾個星期後，浩宇注意到一件有趣的事：他大部分的錯誤其實只分成少數幾類。他常常把兩個相似的公式搞混，也常常忘記檢查答案應該是正數還是負數。一旦知道自己的弱點在哪裡，他就能刻意加強練習，而不是隨便亂做題目。\n\n每個星期天，他會把解法遮起來，重新做一次錯題本裡的題目。如果某一題連續答對兩次，他就在旁邊畫一顆星，之後不再複習它。四個月後，他模擬考的數學成績進步到 71 分。更重要的是，他學到了錯誤並不是需要隱藏的東西，而是一張地圖，清楚地告訴你下一步該往哪裡走。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main lesson of the story?", "options": ["Math is the most difficult subject for most students.", "Studying your mistakes carefully can help you improve.", "Students should always look at the answer key first.", "Mock exams are a waste of time for third-year students."], "answer": 1, "explain": "故事描述浩宇透過錯題本分析錯誤原因、找出弱點並定期重做，數學成績從 42 分進步到 71 分，最後一段也點出錯誤能指引下一步。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 第三段說他寫解法時盡量不看解答，與此相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What did Hao-yu write on the right page of his notebook first?", "options": ["The question he had gotten wrong", "His score on the mock exam", "The reason why he had made the mistake", "Ms. Lin's comments on his test"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：Second, on the right page, he wrote down why he had made the mistake. 右頁先寫下犯錯的原因，接著才在下方寫正確解法。(A) 題目抄在左頁；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 4?", "options": ["Hao-yu's mistakes were completely different from one another.", "Hao-yu stopped doing math exercises after a few weeks.", "Hao-yu found a mistake in one of the formulas in his textbook.", "Knowing his common mistakes helped Hao-yu practice in a more focused way."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說他的錯誤大多只分成幾類，Once he knew his weak points, he could practice them on purpose instead of doing exercises at random，可推知了解常犯的錯誤讓他能更有針對性地練習。(A) 與 fell into just a few groups 相反；(B) 他是改成針對弱點練習，並非停止練習；(C) 他是自己把公式搞混，不是課本有錯。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "When did Hao-yu stop reviewing a question in his notebook?", "options": ["After he answered it correctly two times in a row", "After he copied it into the notebook", "After one month had passed", "After Ms. Lin checked his solution"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：If he got a question right two times in a row, he marked it with a star and stopped reviewing it. 某題連續答對兩次後就畫星號、不再複習。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by calling mistakes \"a map\" in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Students should spend more time studying geography.", "Mistakes are embarrassing and should be kept secret.", "Mistakes show students which areas they need to work on.", "Maps are very useful for solving math problems."], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一句 They are a map that shows you exactly where to go next.，They 指 mistakes，意思是錯誤就像地圖，能告訴你接下來該加強哪些地方。(A)(D) 是把「地圖」照字面解釋；(B) 與前一句 mistakes are not something to hide 相反。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a19", "title": "Behind the Kitchen Door: Training for a Cooking Certificate", "title_zh": "廚房門後：為廚師證照苦練", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "Career"], "passage": "At six o'clock on a Tuesday evening, most students at a vocational high school in Tainan have already gone home. But in the training kitchen on the third floor, Yi-chen is still standing at a cutting board, slicing carrots into thin, even pieces. She is a second-year student in the school's food and beverage program, and in two months she will take the test for a Class C certificate in Chinese cooking.\n\nIn Taiwan, many vocational high school students take skills tests to earn certificates in their fields. A certificate shows that a person has the basic skills needed for a certain job. For the cooking certificate, students must pass two parts: a written test about food safety, nutrition and kitchen work, and a practical test, in which they have to prepare and cook several dishes within a strict time limit.\n\nFor Yi-chen, the hardest part is not the cooking but the cutting. Every vegetable must be cut into the size and shape that the rules require. \"At first, I needed almost twice the time allowed,\" she says. \"My teacher told me that speed comes only with practice, so I take vegetables home and cut them almost every day.\" Her family has eaten a lot of carrot soup this year.\n\nAnother challenge is hygiene, which means keeping yourself, your tools and the food clean and safe. During the test, examiners watch every move. Candidates can lose points for mistakes that may seem small, such as touching their hair and then handling food without washing their hands, or putting cooked and raw food on the same plate. \"Delicious food is not enough,\" her teacher, Mr. Kuo, often reminds the class. \"In a real restaurant, one mistake with hygiene can make many customers sick.\"\n\nYi-chen admits that the training is tiring, and sometimes she wishes she could go home early like her friends. But she has a clear goal. She hopes to study culinary arts at a university of technology and someday open a small restaurant serving the dishes her grandmother taught her. \"The certificate is just the first step,\" she says with a smile. \"But every step counts.\"", "passage_zh": "星期二晚上六點，台南一所高職的大多數學生都已經回家了。但在三樓的實習廚房裡，宜蓁仍站在砧板前，把紅蘿蔔切成又薄又均勻的片。她是學校餐飲管理科的二年級學生，兩個月後，她就要參加中餐烹調丙級證照的檢定考試。\n\n在台灣，許多高職學生會參加技能檢定，取得自己所學領域的證照。證照證明一個人具備從事某項工作所需的基本技能。要取得這張廚師證照，學生必須通過兩個部分：一是關於食品安全、營養與廚房工作的學科筆試，二是術科實作測驗，必須在嚴格的時間限制內準備並烹調好幾道菜。\n\n對宜蓁來說，最難的部分不是烹調，而是刀工。每一種蔬菜都必須切成規定的大小和形狀。「一開始，我需要的時間幾乎是規定的兩倍，」她說，「老師告訴我，速度只能靠練習累積，所以我幾乎每天都把蔬菜帶回家切。」她的家人今年喝了很多紅蘿蔔湯。\n\n另一個挑戰是衛生，也就是讓自己、器具和食物都保持乾淨與安全。測驗時，監評人員會仔細盯著每一個動作。應檢人可能因為一些看似微小的疏失被扣分，例如摸了頭髮之後沒有洗手就去碰食物，或是把熟食和生食放在同一個盤子裡。「光是菜好吃還不夠，」她的老師郭老師常常提醒全班，「在真正的餐廳裡，一個衛生上的疏失就可能讓許多客人生病。」\n\n宜蓁承認訓練很累，有時候她也希望能像朋友們一樣早點回家。但她有明確的目標：她希望進入科技大學就讀廚藝相關科系，將來開一家小餐廳，賣奶奶教她做的那些菜。「證照只是第一步，」她笑著說，「但每一步都很重要。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to make carrot soup at home", "A student's hard work to prepare for a cooking skills test", "Why restaurants in Tainan are so popular", "The rules for opening a small restaurant in Taiwan"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文描述餐飲科學生宜蓁為了中餐烹調丙級證照，課後留校練刀工、注意衛生規定，並說明她的目標。(A) 紅蘿蔔湯只是她練刀工的附帶結果；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what do students have to do in the practical test?", "options": ["Answer questions about food safety and nutrition", "Serve customers in a real restaurant", "Write a report about their grandmother's dishes", "Prepare and cook several dishes within a time limit"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：a practical test, in which they have to prepare and cook several dishes within a strict time limit. 術科測驗要在嚴格時限內準備並烹調幾道菜。(A) 是學科筆試的內容；(B)(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why has Yi-chen's family eaten a lot of carrot soup this year?", "options": ["Carrot soup is her grandmother's favorite dish.", "The test requires students to make carrot soup.", "Yi-chen practices cutting vegetables at home almost every day.", "Carrots are the cheapest vegetable in the market."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：so I take vegetables home and cut them almost every day（她幾乎每天把蔬菜帶回家切），緊接著說家人今年喝了很多紅蘿蔔湯，可推知是她在家練刀工切的紅蘿蔔被拿來煮湯。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following could make a candidate lose points in the test?", "options": ["Putting cooked food and raw food on the same plate", "Washing hands before handling the food", "Cutting carrots into thin, even pieces", "Finishing all the dishes before the time limit"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段列出會被扣分的例子：摸頭髮後沒洗手就碰食物，or putting cooked and raw food on the same plate（把熟食和生食放在同一個盤子）。(B)(C)(D) 都是正確或被要求的做法，不會被扣分。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"candidates\" in paragraph 4 refers to ______.", "options": ["the examiners who watch the test", "the people who are taking the test", "the customers in a real restaurant", "the teachers who train the students"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段前一句說 examiners watch every move（監評人員盯著每個動作），接著說 Candidates can lose points（會被扣分），會被扣分的是參加考試的人，所以 candidates 指應檢人。(A) 監評人員是打分數的人；(C)(D) 不參加考試。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a20", "title": "Pen or Keyboard? Taking Notes in Class", "title_zh": "用筆還是用鍵盤？課堂筆記的學問", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "Technology"], "passage": "In more and more classrooms, students take notes on laptops or tablets instead of in paper notebooks. Typing has clear advantages. Most people can type much faster than they can write by hand, and typed notes are neat, easy to search, and hard to lose if they are saved online. So it seems natural to think that typing is the better choice. However, some research suggests that the old-fashioned pen may still have something to offer.\n\nIn 2014, two American psychologists, Pam Mueller and Daniel Oppenheimer, did a well-known experiment. They asked college students to watch short talks and take notes, either on a laptop or by hand. Afterward, the students answered questions about the talks. Some questions tested facts, such as dates or numbers. Others tested whether the students really understood the ideas in the talks.\n\nThe results were interesting. Students who typed wrote down many more words, and they often copied what the speaker said word for word. On the fact questions, the two groups did about equally well. On the questions about ideas, however, the students who wrote by hand did better. The researchers explained that because handwriting is slower, students cannot write everything down. They have to listen, decide what is important, and put it in their own words. This extra thinking helps them understand and remember.\n\nLater studies have not always found such a large difference, and some researchers now believe the effect may be smaller than it first seemed. Still, the main lesson is useful: the tool matters less than how you use it. Typing every word like a recording machine is not the same as learning.\n\nSo what should students do? If you type your notes, try not to copy everything. Stop now and then and sum up the teacher's points in a few words of your own. Also, turn off notifications, since games and messages are only one click away on a laptop. If you write by hand, do not worry about being slower. Your brain may be doing exactly the work it needs to do.", "passage_zh": "在越來越多的教室裡，學生用筆電或平板而不是紙本筆記本來做筆記。打字有明顯的優點：大多數人打字比手寫快得多，而且打出來的筆記整齊、容易搜尋，存在網路上也不容易弄丟。因此，人們很自然地會認為打字是比較好的選擇。然而，有些研究顯示，老派的紙筆或許仍有它的長處。\n\n2014 年，兩位美國心理學家潘‧穆勒與丹尼爾‧歐本海默做了一項著名的實驗。他們請大學生觀看幾段簡短的演講並做筆記，有的用筆電，有的用手寫。之後，學生要回答關於演講內容的問題。有些問題測驗的是事實，例如日期或數字；另一些則測驗學生是否真正理解演講中的觀念。\n\n結果很有意思。用打字的學生記下的字數多得多，而且常常把講者說的話一字不漏地照抄下來。在事實題上，兩組的表現差不多；但在觀念題上，手寫的學生表現比較好。研究人員解釋，因為手寫速度比較慢，學生無法把每句話都寫下來。他們必須一邊聽、一邊判斷什麼是重點，再用自己的話寫出來。這些額外的思考有助於他們理解和記憶。\n\n後來的研究並不一定都發現這麼大的差異，有些研究人員現在認為，這個效果可能比一開始看起來的要小。儘管如此，這個研究的主要啟示仍然很有用：工具本身不如使用工具的方式重要。像錄音機一樣把每個字都打下來，並不等於學習。\n\n那麼，學生該怎麼做呢？如果你用打字做筆記，盡量不要什麼都照抄。偶爾停下來，用幾句自己的話總結老師的重點。另外，要關掉通知，因為在筆電上，遊戲和訊息都只有一鍵之遙。如果你用手寫，也不必擔心速度比較慢。你的大腦也許正好在做它需要做的工作。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To tell students never to use laptops in class", "To describe the lives of two American psychologists", "To persuade schools to buy tablets for every student", "To compare two ways of taking notes and give students advice"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文比較打字與手寫做筆記的優缺點，介紹相關研究，最後一段給學生建議。(A) 最後一段也教學生如何用筆電做好筆記，並非禁止使用；(B) 兩位心理學家只是研究者，文中沒有介紹生平；(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In the 2014 experiment, what did the students who typed often do?", "options": ["They wrote down fewer words than the other group.", "They drew pictures instead of writing words.", "They copied the speaker's words exactly.", "They stopped taking notes halfway through the talks."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：Students who typed wrote down many more words, and they often copied what the speaker said word for word. 打字組常一字不漏地照抄講者的話。(A) 打字組寫得更多，不是更少；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the experiment, on which kind of questions did the handwriting group do better?", "options": ["Questions that tested understanding of ideas", "Questions about dates", "Questions about numbers", "All kinds of questions in the test"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：On the fact questions, the two groups did about equally well. On the questions about ideas, however, the students who wrote by hand did better. 手寫組只在觀念理解題表現較好。(B)(C) 日期、數字屬於事實題，兩組差不多；(D) 並非所有題型都較好。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the researchers, why does writing by hand help students understand?", "options": ["Because pens are cheaper and easier to carry than laptops", "Because students must choose the key points and put them in their own words", "Because handwritten notes are neater than typed notes", "Because teachers usually prefer to read handwritten notes"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：because handwriting is slower... They have to listen, decide what is important, and put it in their own words. This extra thinking helps them understand and remember. 手寫較慢，學生必須篩選重點並用自己的話寫下，這些額外的思考幫助理解。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 第一段說整齊是打字筆記的優點。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward typing notes in class?", "options": ["Strongly against it, because laptops are always harmful", "Not interested in it at all", "It can work well if students sum up ideas instead of copying everything", "It is always better than writing by hand"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說 the tool matters less than how you use it（工具不如使用方式重要），第五段建議打字的人不要全部照抄，要用自己的話總結並關掉通知，可見作者認為打字只要用對方法也可以。(A) 作者並未全盤反對；(B) 作者花了整篇討論，並非不感興趣；(D) 研究顯示手寫在觀念題較好，作者並不認為打字一定比較好。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a21", "title": "Brooms Before Books: When Students Clean Their Own School", "title_zh": "先拿掃把再拿課本：學生自己打掃校園", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "Culture"], "passage": "On his first morning at a senior high school in Taichung, Lukas, an exchange student from Germany, was handed a broom. \"It's cleaning time,\" said his classmate Jia-hui. For the next fifteen minutes, everyone in the class was busy. Some students swept the floor, some wiped the windows, and two boys carried the trash bags downstairs. Lukas was surprised. At his school in Germany, professional cleaners came after school, and students only had to clean the board now and then.\n\nIn Taiwan, however, this is a normal part of school life. In most schools, each class takes care of its own classroom and also a public area, such as a hallway, a staircase, or part of the sports field. Students take turns doing different jobs, and a class officer in charge of cleaning checks that every job is done. Some schools even give prizes to the cleanest classes. Japan has a similar tradition, and students there also clean their schools every day.\n\nWhy do schools ask students to do this work? Teachers say it teaches responsibility. When you have spent time sweeping a floor, you think twice before you drop paper on it. It also teaches teamwork, because a large area can only be cleaned quickly if everyone does his or her part. Some people also believe it helps students respect the workers who keep public places clean every day.\n\nNot everyone loves cleaning time, of course. Some students complain that it takes time away from studying, especially in the months before big exams. Others say the system is unfair when one or two classmates always disappear just before the work begins. Still, many graduates look back on it with warm memories. For them, chatting with friends while sweeping up fallen leaves is part of what makes school life special.\n\nBy the end of his year in Taiwan, Lukas had become an expert at cleaning windows. \"At first I thought it was strange,\" he wrote in a blog post. \"Now I think it's a good idea. I've learned to take care of the place where I spend so much of my time. I might even suggest it to my school when I go back to Germany.\"", "passage_zh": "路卡斯是來自德國的交換學生。他在台中一所高中的第一天早上，就被塞了一支掃把。「現在是打掃時間，」他的同學佳慧說。接下來的十五分鐘，全班每個人都忙個不停：有人掃地，有人擦窗戶，還有兩個男生把垃圾袋提到樓下。路卡斯很驚訝。在他德國的學校，放學後會有專業的清潔人員來打掃，學生只需要偶爾擦擦黑板而已。\n\n然而在台灣，這是學校生活中很平常的一部分。在大部分的學校裡，每一班都要負責打掃自己的教室，以及一塊公共區域，例如走廊、樓梯或運動場的一部分。學生輪流做不同的工作，並由負責清潔的班級幹部檢查每項工作是否完成。有些學校甚至會頒獎給最乾淨的班級。日本也有類似的傳統，那裡的學生也每天打掃自己的學校。\n\n學校為什麼要學生做這些工作呢？老師們說，這能培養責任感。當你花時間掃過一塊地，要在上面丟紙屑之前就會三思。它也能培養團隊合作，因為一大塊區域只有在每個人都做好自己的部分時，才能很快打掃乾淨。也有人認為，這能幫助學生尊重每天維持公共場所整潔的清潔人員。\n\n當然，不是每個人都喜歡打掃時間。有些學生抱怨這會占用讀書的時間，尤其是在大考前的那幾個月。也有人說，如果總有一兩個同學在工作開始前就消失不見，這種制度就很不公平。儘管如此，許多畢業生回想起來，仍覺得那是溫暖的回憶。對他們來說，一邊和朋友聊天、一邊掃落葉，正是學校生活特別的地方。\n\n在台灣的這一年結束時，路卡斯已經成了擦窗戶的高手。「一開始我覺得這很奇怪，」他在一篇部落格文章中寫道，「現在我覺得這是個好主意。我學會了好好照顧自己花這麼多時間待著的地方。等我回到德國，說不定還會向我的學校提出這個建議。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why schools in Germany are cleaner than schools in Taiwan", "How to clean windows quickly and properly", "The custom of students cleaning their own schools in Taiwan", "Why Lukas decided to become an exchange student"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文從德國交換學生路卡斯的經驗出發，介紹台灣學生自己打掃校園的做法、學校的理由、學生的不同看法，以及路卡斯想法的轉變。(A) 文中沒有比較哪國學校比較乾淨；(B) 擦窗戶只是細節；(D) 文中未提及他為何當交換學生。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 1, what was cleaning like at Lukas's school in Germany?", "options": ["Professional cleaners did most of the work after school.", "Students cleaned the whole school every morning.", "Teachers cleaned their own classrooms at noon.", "Classrooms were cleaned only once a year."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段最後一句：At his school in Germany, professional cleaners came after school, and students only had to clean the board now and then. 德國學校是放學後由專業清潔人員打掃，學生只偶爾擦黑板。(B) 是台灣的情形；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason for asking students to clean?", "options": ["It teaches students to be responsible.", "It teaches students to work as a team.", "It helps students respect cleaning workers.", "It helps schools save money on hiring workers."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段提到的理由有三：培養責任感 (teaches responsibility)、團隊合作 (teamwork)、尊重清潔人員 (respect the workers)，分別對應 (A)(B)(C)。文中完全沒有提到節省聘請清潔人員的費用。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Lukas's feelings about cleaning time?", "options": ["He still thought it was a strange and useless custom.", "His feelings changed from surprise to approval.", "He was angry because his classmates did not help him.", "He wanted to stop cleaning because it took time from studying."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說他一開始 was surprised，最後一段他寫道 At first I thought it was strange... Now I think it's a good idea，甚至想向德國的學校提議，可知他從驚訝轉為認同。(A) 與 Now I think it's a good idea 相反；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 是第四段部分台灣學生的抱怨，不是路卡斯的想法。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"think twice\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["think carefully before doing something", "forget something very quickly", "repeat the same words two times", "change your mind without any reason"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 When you have spent time sweeping a floor, you think twice before you drop paper on it. 意思是自己辛苦掃過地，要丟紙屑之前就會「三思、仔細考慮」，所以 think twice 指做某事前先仔細想想。(B)(C)(D) 都不符合上下文。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a22", "title": "The Fifth Taste: How Umami Was Discovered", "title_zh": "第五種味道：鮮味的發現", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Food", "Science"], "passage": "For a long time, people in the West believed that the tongue could sense only four basic tastes: sweet, sour, salty and bitter. But think about a bowl of beef noodle soup, a ripe tomato or a piece of cheese. They all have a rich, satisfying flavor that does not belong to any of those groups. Today, scientists call this flavor umami, and it is known as the fifth basic taste.\n\nThe story of umami began in Japan in 1908. A chemistry professor named Kikunae Ikeda noticed that dashi, a Japanese soup stock made with kombu (a kind of dried seaweed), had a special flavor. After many experiments, he discovered that the flavor came from a substance called glutamate. He named the taste \"umami,\" from a Japanese word meaning \"delicious.\" A year later, a company began selling glutamate as a seasoning powder, now known as MSG.\n\nHowever, many scientists did not accept umami as a real taste for nearly a hundred years. Some believed it was only a mix of other tastes, such as salty and sweet. Things changed in the early 2000s, when researchers found receptors on the tongue that react to glutamate. In other words, the tongue has its own \"sensors\" for umami, just as it does for sweetness and bitterness. This finding finally gave umami its place beside the other four tastes.\n\nUmami is found naturally in many foods. Tomatoes, mushrooms, soy sauce, seaweed, dried fish and cheese all contain substances that create this taste. The amount often increases as food ripens, dries or ages. That is why a red, ripe tomato tastes richer than a green one, and why cooks add dried mushrooms or dried fish to soups. Even human breast milk is rich in glutamate, so most of us experience umami from our very first meals.\n\nWhy do humans enjoy this taste so much? Many scientists believe that umami tells us a food contains protein, which the body needs to build and repair muscles. Just as sweetness signals energy and bitterness often warns us of poison, umami helps guide us toward food that is good for us. So the next time you enjoy a bowl of hot soup, remember that your tongue is doing some clever chemistry.", "passage_zh": "長久以來，西方人認為舌頭只能感受到四種基本味道：甜、酸、鹹、苦。但想想一碗牛肉麵、一顆成熟的番茄或一片起司，它們都有一種濃郁又令人滿足的味道，並不屬於這幾種中的任何一種。今天，科學家稱這種味道為「鮮味」（umami），它被認為是第五種基本味道。\n\n鮮味的故事始於1908年的日本。一位名叫池田菊苗的化學教授注意到，日式高湯（dashi）——一種用昆布（一種乾燥的海帶）熬煮的湯底——有一種特別的味道。經過許多實驗後，他發現這種味道來自一種叫做麩胺酸（glutamate）的物質。他將這種味道命名為「umami」，取自日文中意思是「美味」的字。一年後，一家公司開始把麩胺酸做成調味粉販售，也就是現在所知道的味精（MSG）。\n\n然而，將近一百年的時間裡，許多科學家都不承認鮮味是一種真正的味道。有些人認為它只是其他味道（例如鹹和甜）的混合。到了2000年代初期，情況有了改變：研究人員在舌頭上發現了會對麩胺酸產生反應的受體。換句話說，舌頭有專門感應鮮味的「感應器」，就像它有感應甜味和苦味的感應器一樣。這項發現終於讓鮮味和其他四種味道並列。\n\n許多食物天然就含有鮮味。番茄、菇類、醬油、海藻、魚乾和起司都含有能產生這種味道的物質。食物在成熟、乾燥或熟成的過程中，這些物質的含量常常會增加。這就是為什麼紅熟的番茄吃起來比青番茄更濃郁，也是廚師會在湯裡加入乾香菇或魚乾的原因。就連人類的母乳也富含麩胺酸，所以大多數人從生命中最初的幾餐就開始體驗鮮味了。\n\n為什麼人類這麼喜歡這種味道呢？許多科學家相信，鮮味告訴我們這種食物含有蛋白質，而身體需要蛋白質來建造和修復肌肉。就像甜味代表能量、苦味常常警告我們有毒一樣，鮮味能引導我們找到對身體有益的食物。所以下次你享用一碗熱湯時，別忘了你的舌頭正在進行一場巧妙的化學作用。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How MSG is made in factories today", "The discovery of umami and why it is a basic taste", "Why Japanese food is healthier than Western food", "How to make soup stock with dried seaweed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從西方傳統的四種味道談起，介紹池田菊苗發現鮮味的經過（第二段）、科學界後來如何證實鮮味是第五種基本味道（第三段），以及人類喜愛鮮味的原因（第五段）。(A) 味精只在第二段順帶提到，文中沒有說明工廠如何製造；(C) 文中沒有比較日式與西式飲食哪個較健康；(D) 高湯只是池田發現鮮味的起點，並非主旨。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Where did Kikunae Ikeda first notice the special flavor that he later called umami?", "options": ["In a piece of aged cheese", "In a ripe red tomato", "In a soup stock made with dried seaweed", "In human breast milk"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Kikunae Ikeda noticed that dashi, a Japanese soup stock made with kombu (a kind of dried seaweed), had a special flavor. 池田是從用昆布熬煮的日式高湯中注意到這種味道。(A)(B)(D) 起司、番茄、母乳都是第四段提到含有鮮味的食物，但不是池田最初注意到鮮味的地方。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did many scientists refuse to accept umami as a real taste for a long time?", "options": ["They thought it was only a mix of other tastes.", "They believed MSG was bad for people's health.", "Ikeda did not explain how he had found glutamate.", "No one outside Japan had ever tasted dashi."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Some believed it was only a mix of other tastes, such as salty and sweet. 有些科學家認為鮮味只是鹹、甜等味道的混合。(B) 文中沒有提到味精有害健康；(C) 文中說池田經過許多實驗找出麩胺酸，沒有說他不肯說明；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"ripens\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["is cut into small pieces", "is cooked in boiling water", "is kept in a cold refrigerator", "becomes fully grown and ready to eat"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The amount often increases as food ripens, dries or ages. 後面接著說 a red, ripe tomato tastes richer than a green one，可知 ripen 是指蔬果「成熟」，從青綠變成可以吃的狀態。(A)(B)(C) 切塊、煮、冷藏都和文中「紅熟的番茄比青番茄濃郁」的說明無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the last paragraph?", "options": ["Bitter foods are always safe to eat.", "Umami gives the body more energy than sugar does.", "Babies are not able to taste umami.", "People may enjoy umami because it points to food with protein."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段說鮮味告訴我們 a food contains protein，並且 helps guide us toward food that is good for us，可推論人類喜愛鮮味，是因為它代表食物含有身體需要的蛋白質。(A) 文中說苦味常警告有毒，與「總是安全」相反；(B) 文中說代表能量的是甜味；(C) 第四段說人從最初幾餐（母乳）就體驗鮮味，與此相反。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a23", "title": "How Sweet Is Your Bubble Tea?", "title_zh": "你的珍珠奶茶有多甜？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Food"], "passage": "When Pei-shan's health teacher asked the class to keep a \"drink diary\" for one month, she thought it would be easy. She never drank soda, and she seldom ate candy. The only sweet thing in her day, she believed, was the large cup of brown sugar bubble milk tea she bought at the shop near her school almost every afternoon.\n\nThe diary told a different story. In class, the teacher explained that a large cup of full-sugar bubble tea may contain 50 grams of sugar or more. The chewy pearls add extra calories, too, because they are made from starch and are often soaked in sugar syrup. The World Health Organization suggests that people get less than 10 percent of their daily energy from added sugars. For an average adult, that is about 50 grams a day, and less than 25 grams is even better. \"So one drink can use up my sugar limit for the whole day,\" Pei-shan realized.\n\nShe did not want to give up her favorite drink, so she decided to make small changes instead. In Taiwan, most drink shops let customers choose how sweet their drinks are: full sugar, less sugar (about 70 percent), half sugar, light sugar (about 30 percent) or no sugar. In the second week, Pei-shan switched to half sugar. In the third week, she tried light sugar and ordered a medium cup instead of a large one. She also cut down from five drinks a week to three.\n\nAt first, the tea tasted strange, as if something was missing. But near the end of the month, something surprising happened. When a friend let her try a sip of a full-sugar drink, it tasted far too sweet. Her teacher explained that our sense of taste can slowly change. When we eat less sugar for a while, we often become more sensitive to sweetness, so a smaller amount feels like enough.\n\nIn her final report, Pei-shan wrote, \"I didn't have to quit bubble tea. I just had to pay attention to it.\" Her classmates were inspired, too. Several of them now check the sugar level before they order, and the whole class has agreed to try \"No-Sugar Fridays\" until the end of the semester.", "passage_zh": "健康教育老師要全班寫一個月的「飲料日記」時，佩珊以為這很簡單。她從來不喝汽水，也很少吃糖果。她認為自己一天當中唯一的甜食，就是幾乎每天下午在學校附近的店買的那一大杯黑糖珍珠奶茶。\n\n但日記呈現的卻是另一回事。老師在課堂上說明，一大杯全糖的珍珠奶茶可能含有50公克甚至更多的糖。有嚼勁的珍珠也會增加額外的熱量，因為它們是用澱粉做的，而且常常泡在糖漿裡。世界衛生組織建議，每天從添加糖攝取的熱量應少於總熱量的百分之十。對一般成人來說，大約是每天50公克，而少於25公克更好。「所以一杯飲料就可能把我一整天的糖分額度用光了。」佩珊恍然大悟。\n\n她不想放棄最愛的飲料，所以決定改做一些小小的改變。在台灣，大多數飲料店都讓顧客選擇飲料的甜度：全糖、少糖（約七分）、半糖、微糖（約三分）或無糖。第二週，佩珊改喝半糖。第三週，她試著喝微糖，並把大杯改成中杯。她也從每週喝五杯減少到三杯。\n\n一開始，茶喝起來怪怪的，好像少了什麼。但快到月底時，發生了一件令人驚訝的事。朋友讓她喝一口全糖飲料時，她覺得實在太甜了。老師解釋說，我們的味覺會慢慢改變。當我們有一段時間少吃糖，常會對甜味變得更敏感，所以比較少的糖就讓人覺得夠甜了。\n\n佩珊在期末報告中寫道：「我不必戒掉珍珠奶茶，只需要多留意它。」她的同學也受到啟發。現在有好幾位同學點飲料前會先確認甜度，全班也同意在這學期結束前試行「無糖星期五」。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How bubble tea was first invented in Taiwan", "Why students should never drink bubble tea", "How to make brown sugar pearls at home", "How a student cut down on sugar without giving up her favorite drink"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文敘述佩珊透過飲料日記發現珍奶含糖量很高，於是降低甜度、改喝中杯並減少杯數（第三段），最後在報告中寫下「不必戒掉，只需要多留意」（第五段）。(A)(C) 文中沒有提到珍奶的起源或珍珠的做法；(B) 佩珊並沒有戒掉珍奶，第五段 I didn't have to quit bubble tea 與「絕對不要喝」不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the World Health Organization, about how much added sugar should an average adult have in one day at most?", "options": ["10 grams", "25 grams", "50 grams", "70 grams"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：For an average adult, that is about 50 grams a day, and less than 25 grams is even better. 世界衛生組織建議的上限約為每天 50 公克。(B) 25 公克是「更理想」的目標，不是上限；(A) 文中的 10 是百分比（百分之十），不是公克數；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "If Pei-shan orders a drink with \"light sugar,\" how sweet will it be?", "options": ["About 30 percent of the full sugar level", "About 50 percent of the full sugar level", "About 70 percent of the full sugar level", "It will have no sugar at all."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：light sugar (about 30 percent)，微糖約為全糖的三成。(B) 是 half sugar（半糖）；(C) 是 less sugar（少糖）；(D) 是 no sugar（無糖）。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the full-sugar drink taste too sweet to Pei-shan near the end of the month?", "options": ["Her friend's drink shop used a different kind of sugar.", "Her sense of taste had changed after she ate less sugar.", "She had a cold, so she could not taste things well.", "The drink had twice as many pearls as usual."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：When we eat less sugar for a while, we often become more sensitive to sweetness. 佩珊減糖一段時間後，味覺對甜味變得更敏感，因此覺得全糖飲料太甜。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到糖的種類、感冒或珍珠的數量。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"sensitive\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["bored with something", "able to notice something easily", "afraid of something", "careless about something"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說少吃糖一段時間後會 become more sensitive to sweetness, so a smaller amount feels like enough，也就是一點點甜就能察覺，sensitive 意思是「敏感的、容易察覺的」。(A) 厭倦的、(C) 害怕的、(D) 不在乎的，都無法解釋為什麼少量的糖就讓人覺得夠甜。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a24", "title": "Why Chili Peppers Set Your Mouth on Fire", "title_zh": "辣椒為什麼讓嘴巴像著火？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Food"], "passage": "Bite into a hot chili pepper, and your mouth may feel as if it is on fire. Your face turns red, your eyes water and you may even start to sweat. Yet the pepper is not actually hot at all; it may have come straight out of the refrigerator. So what is really happening?\n\nThe answer is a chemical called capsaicin, found mainly in the white inner part of the pepper that holds the seeds. Capsaicin is not picked up by your taste buds. Instead, it attaches to pain sensors in your mouth, the same sensors that warn you when something is dangerously hot, like a cup of boiling tea. When capsaicin touches them, they send the message \"Burning!\" to your brain. Your brain believes it and tries to cool your body down, which is why you sweat. In other words, spiciness is not a taste like sweet or salty but a feeling of pain.\n\nIn 1912, an American pharmacist named Wilbur Scoville created a way to measure how hot peppers are. In his test, a pepper extract was mixed with more and more sugar water until a group of tasters could no longer feel any heat. The more water that was needed, the hotter the pepper. The Scoville scale is still used today. A sweet bell pepper scores zero, a jalapeño scores a few thousand, and the hottest peppers in the world score more than two million.\n\nIf you eat something too spicy, do not reach for a glass of cold water. Capsaicin mixes with oil but not with water, so water simply spreads it around your mouth. Milk works much better, because a protein in it called casein can hold on to capsaicin and wash it away. Rice and bread can also help by soaking up some of the heat.\n\nInterestingly, birds cannot feel capsaicin at all. They happily eat the hottest peppers and then spread the seeds far away in their droppings. Mammals, on the other hand, would crush the seeds with their teeth, but the burning feeling keeps most of them away. Many scientists believe this is why peppers developed capsaicin in the first place: it protects their seeds from the wrong animals and lets the right ones carry them to new places.", "passage_zh": "咬一口辣椒，你的嘴巴可能會覺得像著火一樣。你的臉會變紅、眼睛泛淚，甚至可能開始流汗。然而辣椒本身其實一點也不燙，它可能才剛從冰箱拿出來。那麼，到底發生了什麼事呢？\n\n答案是一種叫做辣椒素的化學物質，它主要存在於辣椒內部連著種子的白色部分。辣椒素不是由味蕾察覺的，而是附著在口中的痛覺感應器上——這些感應器原本是在東西燙得危險時（例如一杯滾燙的茶）向你發出警告的。當辣椒素碰到它們時，它們就會向大腦傳送「燒起來了！」的訊息。大腦信以為真，便設法讓身體降溫，這就是你會流汗的原因。換句話說，辣其實不是像甜或鹹那樣的味道，而是一種痛覺。\n\n1912年，一位名叫威爾伯・史高維爾的美國藥劑師發明了一種測量辣椒辣度的方法。在他的測試中，辣椒萃取物會加入越來越多的糖水稀釋，直到一群試吃者再也感覺不到任何辣味為止。需要的水越多，代表辣椒越辣。史高維爾辣度指標至今仍在使用。甜椒的分數是零，墨西哥辣椒（jalapeño）有幾千分，而世界上最辣的辣椒則超過兩百萬分。\n\n如果你吃到太辣的東西，別急著拿一杯冰水。辣椒素能溶於油卻不溶於水，所以喝水只會讓它在嘴裡擴散開來。牛奶的效果好得多，因為牛奶中一種叫做酪蛋白的蛋白質能抓住辣椒素，把它帶走。米飯和麵包也能吸收部分辣味，幫助緩解。\n\n有趣的是，鳥類完全感覺不到辣椒素。牠們能開心地吃下最辣的辣椒，再透過糞便把種子散播到遠方。另一方面，哺乳類動物會用牙齒把種子咬碎，但那種灼燒的感覺讓大部分哺乳類不敢靠近。許多科學家相信，這正是辣椒當初演化出辣椒素的原因：它能保護種子不被「不對的」動物吃掉，並讓「對的」動物把種子帶到新的地方。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this passage?", "options": ["To teach readers how to grow their own chili peppers", "To warn readers never to eat spicy food", "To compare cooking styles from different countries", "To explain why chili peppers feel hot and share some related facts"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文說明辣椒素如何讓人產生灼熱感（第二段）、辣度如何測量（第三段）、吃太辣時該怎麼辦（第四段），以及辣椒為何演化出辣椒素（第五段）。(A) 文中沒有教人種辣椒；(B) 文中只建議吃太辣時喝牛奶，並沒有叫人不要吃辣；(C) 文中沒有比較各國烹飪方式。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why do people sweat when they eat hot peppers?", "options": ["Their brains think the body is burning and try to cool it down.", "The peppers are usually served at a very high temperature.", "Their taste buds pick up capsaicin as a salty taste.", "They usually drink too much water during the meal."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：they send the message \"Burning!\" to your brain. Your brain believes it and tries to cool your body down, which is why you sweat. 大腦以為身體被燙到而想降溫，所以會流汗。(B) 第一段說辣椒本身並不燙；(C) 第二段說辣椒素不是由味蕾察覺，辣也不是像鹹那樣的味道；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the Scoville scale?", "options": ["It was created in the early 2000s.", "It measures how much sugar a pepper contains.", "A higher score means more water was needed to remove the heat.", "A jalapeño scores more than two million on it."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：The more water that was needed, the hotter the pepper. 需要越多糖水稀釋才感覺不到辣，代表越辣、分數越高。(A) 它是在 1912 年發明的；(B) 糖水是用來稀釋的，不是測量含糖量；(D) 墨西哥辣椒只有幾千分，超過兩百萬分的是世界上最辣的辣椒。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Your friend has just eaten a very spicy dish and says his mouth is burning. Based on the passage, what is the best advice?", "options": ["Drink a large glass of ice water quickly.", "Have a glass of milk.", "Eat the white part of the pepper.", "Keep your mouth open until the heat is gone."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Milk works much better, because a protein in it called casein can hold on to capsaicin and wash it away. 牛奶中的酪蛋白能抓住辣椒素並把它帶走。(A) 文中說不要喝冷水，因為水只會讓辣椒素在口中擴散；(C) 第二段說辣椒素主要就在白色部分，吃了只會更辣；(D) 文中沒有提到這個方法。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about birds and chili peppers?", "options": ["Birds help pepper plants grow in new places.", "Birds stay away from peppers because of the burning feeling.", "Birds crush pepper seeds before they swallow them.", "Birds can only eat sweet bell peppers."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段說鳥類感覺不到辣椒素，會吃下辣椒並 spread the seeds far away in their droppings，最後一句也說辣椒素讓 the right ones carry them to new places，可推論鳥類幫助辣椒在新的地方生長。(B) 鳥類感覺不到灼熱感，不會因此避開；(C) 文中說用牙齒咬碎種子的是哺乳類；(D) 文中說鳥類能吃最辣的辣椒。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a25", "title": "A Second Chance for Ugly Vegetables", "title_zh": "給醜蔬果第二次機會", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Environment", "Food"], "passage": "On a farm in Yunlin, Mr. Huang pulls up hundreds of carrots every morning. Many are perfect: long, straight and bright orange. Others have two \"legs,\" are bent like a banana or are simply too small. These carrots taste exactly the same, but supermarkets often refuse to buy them because shoppers prefer vegetables that look perfect. In the past, Mr. Huang fed them to his neighbor's pigs or left them in the field to rot.\n\nThis problem is not unusual. According to the United Nations, about one third of all the food produced in the world is lost or wasted. Some is thrown away by families who buy too much, but a large amount never even leaves the farm. When food is wasted, the water, land and energy used to grow it are wasted, too. Rotting food also produces gases that make climate change worse.\n\nLast spring, a group of students from a nearby vocational high school decided to help. With their teacher, they visited five local farms and offered to sell the farmers' \"ugly\" fruit and vegetables at a weekend stall in front of the town's train station. They called it the \"Second Chance Market.\" Everything was about 30 percent cheaper than usual, and each box came with a card explaining why the food looked different.\n\nThe first few weekends were slow. Many shoppers picked up a twisted cucumber, laughed and put it back. So the students got creative. They cooked free samples, such as carrot soup and tomato salad, and posted funny photos online of \"the vegetable of the week,\" like a heart-shaped potato. Soon, people began coming just to see what odd shapes they would find. By the end of the summer, the stall had sold more than three tons of produce that would otherwise have been thrown away.\n\nMr. Huang says the market has changed the way he feels about his work. \"I used to feel sad when I saw good food lying in the field,\" he said. \"Now I know someone will enjoy it.\" The students have learned something, too: what matters most about food is not how it looks, but how it tastes and the hard work that goes into growing it.", "passage_zh": "在雲林的一座農場裡，黃先生每天早上都要拔起好幾百根紅蘿蔔。其中許多長得很完美：又長又直，顏色是鮮豔的橘色。其他的則長出兩隻「腳」、像香蕉一樣彎曲，或是太小。這些紅蘿蔔的味道完全一樣，但超市常常拒絕收購，因為顧客偏好外表完美的蔬菜。過去，黃先生會把它們拿去餵鄰居的豬，或是任由它們在田裡腐爛。\n\n這個問題並不少見。根據聯合國的資料，全世界生產的食物中約有三分之一在過程中損失或被浪費掉。有些是被買太多的家庭丟掉的，但還有大量的食物根本沒有離開過農場。食物被浪費時，用來種植它們的水、土地和能源也跟著浪費了。腐爛的食物還會產生讓氣候變遷更加惡化的氣體。\n\n去年春天，附近一所高職的一群學生決定伸出援手。他們和老師一起拜訪了五座當地農場，提議在週末到鎮上的火車站前擺攤，幫農民販售「醜」蔬果。他們把攤位取名為「第二次機會市集」。所有商品都比平常便宜約三成，而且每一箱都附上一張卡片，說明這些食物為什麼長得不一樣。\n\n最初幾個週末生意很冷清。許多顧客拿起一根扭曲的小黃瓜，笑一笑又放回去。於是學生們發揮了創意。他們煮了紅蘿蔔濃湯、番茄沙拉等免費試吃品，並在網路上張貼「本週蔬菜」的趣味照片，例如一顆心形的馬鈴薯。很快地，人們開始專程前來，只為了看看能找到什麼奇形怪狀的蔬果。到了夏天結束時，這個攤位已經賣出超過三公噸原本會被丟掉的農產品。\n\n黃先生說，這個市集改變了他對自己工作的感受。「以前看到好好的食物躺在田裡，我會很難過，」他說，「現在我知道會有人好好享用它們。」學生們也學到了一課：食物最重要的不是外表，而是它的味道，以及種植它所付出的辛勞。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a student market gives imperfect fruit and vegetables a second chance", "How farmers can grow straighter and bigger carrots", "Why supermarkets sell vegetables at low prices", "How climate change is damaging farms in Yunlin"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文從黃先生賣不出去的醜紅蘿蔔談起，說明食物浪費的問題，再介紹高職學生成立「第二次機會市集」販售醜蔬果的經過與成果（第三、四段）。(B) 文中沒有教人種出漂亮的紅蘿蔔；(C) 超市其實是拒收醜蔬果，打折販售的是學生的攤位；(D) 氣候變遷只在第二段說明食物浪費的影響時順帶一提。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did supermarkets often refuse to buy some of Mr. Huang's carrots?", "options": ["The carrots did not taste as good as the others.", "Shoppers prefer vegetables that look perfect.", "Mr. Huang asked for too high a price.", "The carrots had already started to rot."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：supermarkets often refuse to buy them because shoppers prefer vegetables that look perfect. 超市拒收是因為顧客偏好外表完美的蔬菜。(A) 文中說 These carrots taste exactly the same，味道完全一樣；(C) 文中未提到價格問題；(D) 腐爛是賣不出去之後被留在田裡的結果，不是拒收的原因。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the Second Chance Market?", "options": ["It was open every day in the school's parking lot.", "Its fruit and vegetables cost more than usual.", "Each box came with a card explaining why the food looked different.", "It sold only carrots from Mr. Huang's farm."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：each box came with a card explaining why the food looked different. (A) 市集是週末在火車站前擺攤，不是每天在學校；(B) 價格比平常便宜約三成；(D) 學生拜訪了五座農場，販售各種蔬果（如第四段的小黃瓜、番茄、馬鈴薯），不只有紅蘿蔔。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"odd\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["strange", "cheap", "fresh", "tiny"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：people began coming just to see what odd shapes they would find，前文提到扭曲的小黃瓜、心形的馬鈴薯等，可知 odd 指「奇怪的、不尋常的」，與 strange 意思最接近。(B) 便宜的、(C) 新鮮的、(D) 極小的，都不能用來形容這些讓人好奇而專程來看的形狀。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "How does Mr. Huang most likely feel about the market now?", "options": ["Worried that it will hurt his business", "Angry that his carrots are sold so cheaply", "Not interested in what the students are doing", "Glad that his good food is no longer wasted"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段黃先生說：I used to feel sad when I saw good food lying in the field... Now I know someone will enjoy it. 他以前很難過，現在知道有人會享用這些食物，可知他感到欣慰、高興。(A)(B)(C) 擔心、生氣、不感興趣都與他說話的語氣不符。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a26", "title": "What's for Dinner in Space?", "title_zh": "太空中的晚餐吃什麼？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Food"], "passage": "Eating dinner seems simple on Earth. But imagine trying to eat in a place where your soup floats out of the bowl and your rice drifts around the room. That is the challenge astronauts face on the International Space Station (ISS), where everything is weightless. Over the years, scientists have found clever ways to feed people in space.\n\nThe first space meals, in the 1960s, were not very pleasant. Astronauts squeezed food such as applesauce out of metal tubes, like toothpaste, or ate small, dry cubes of food. Today the menu is much better. Astronauts on the ISS can choose from more than a hundred kinds of food, from scrambled eggs to chicken curry. Much of it is freeze-dried, which means the water has been removed so that it lasts a long time and weighs less. Before eating, astronauts simply add hot water to the package.\n\nSome ordinary foods cause special problems. Bread, for example, makes crumbs, and in space crumbs do not fall to the floor. They float around and could get into an astronaut's eyes or damage important equipment. For this reason, astronauts have eaten tortillas instead of bread since the 1980s. Salt and pepper are a problem for the same reason, so they come in liquid form: the salt is mixed with water, and the pepper is mixed with oil.\n\nAnother surprise is that food tastes weaker in space. In weightlessness, fluids in the body move up toward the head, so astronauts often feel as if they have a stuffy nose, just like when they have a cold. Because smell is an important part of flavor, food seems to lose some of its taste. This is why many astronauts start to love hot sauce and spicy dishes during their missions.\n\nIn the future, astronauts on long trips, such as a journey to Mars, will need to grow some of their own food. Scientists have already started testing this. In 2015, astronauts on the ISS ate red lettuce that they had grown on the station in a special plant box. It was only a small salad, but it was a big step toward farming in space.", "passage_zh": "在地球上，吃晚餐似乎是件簡單的事。但想像一下，在一個湯會從碗裡飄出來、米飯會在房間裡四處漂浮的地方吃飯會是什麼情形。這正是國際太空站（ISS）上的太空人面臨的挑戰，因為那裡的一切都處於失重狀態。多年來，科學家想出了許多巧妙的方法，讓人們在太空中也能吃飯。\n\n1960年代最早的太空餐並不怎麼令人愉快。太空人得像擠牙膏一樣，從金屬管裡擠出蘋果泥之類的食物，或是吃一小塊一小塊乾乾的方塊食物。如今的菜單好多了。國際太空站上的太空人可以從一百多種食物中挑選，從炒蛋到咖哩雞都有。其中很多是冷凍乾燥的，也就是食物中的水分已被去除，因此可以保存很久，重量也比較輕。吃之前，太空人只要在包裝裡加入熱水就可以了。\n\n有些普通的食物會造成特別的問題。例如麵包會產生碎屑，而在太空中，碎屑不會掉到地上，而是四處漂浮，可能會跑進太空人的眼睛或損壞重要的設備。因此，從1980年代起，太空人就改吃墨西哥薄餅而不是麵包。鹽和胡椒也有同樣的問題，所以它們是以液體的形式出現：鹽溶在水裡，胡椒則混在油裡。\n\n另一件令人意外的事是，食物在太空中吃起來味道比較淡。在失重狀態下，體內的液體會往頭部移動，所以太空人常覺得鼻子塞住，就像感冒時一樣。由於嗅覺是風味很重要的一部分，食物似乎就失去了一些味道。這就是為什麼許多太空人在執行任務期間會開始愛上辣醬和辛辣的菜餚。\n\n未來，進行長途旅行（例如前往火星）的太空人將需要自己種植一部分食物。科學家已經開始測試這件事。2015年，國際太空站上的太空人吃了他們在站上一個特殊植物箱裡種出來的紅萵苣。那雖然只是一小份沙拉，卻是邁向太空農業的一大步。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How astronauts eat in space and the problems they must solve", "How people can train to become astronauts", "Why bread is unhealthy for people on Earth", "What daily life will be like for people on Mars"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹太空食物從 1960 年代到現在的變化（第二段）、麵包碎屑與調味料的問題（第三段）、太空中食物變淡的原因（第四段），以及在太空種菜的嘗試（第五段）。(B) 文中沒有提到如何成為太空人；(C) 麵包的問題在於碎屑會漂浮，與健康無關；(D) 火星只在最後一段被當作長途旅行的例子。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why do astronauts eat tortillas instead of bread?", "options": ["Tortillas are cheaper to send into space.", "Bread makes crumbs that float around and may cause damage.", "Bread tastes weaker than tortillas in space.", "Tortillas do not need any water before eating."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Bread, for example, makes crumbs... They float around and could get into an astronaut's eyes or damage important equipment. For this reason, astronauts have eaten tortillas instead of bread. 改吃薄餅是因為麵包屑會漂浮並造成危險。(A)(C)(D) 價格、味道、是否需要加水，文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"stuffy\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["running very fast", "full of nice smells", "blocked so that air cannot pass through easily", "too dry and cold"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說體液往頭部移動，太空人會覺得 a stuffy nose, just like when they have a cold，感冒時鼻子會「塞住、不通」，所以 stuffy 是「堵塞的、不通氣的」。(A) 跑得很快、(D) 太乾太冷，都與感冒鼻塞的情形不符；(B) 下一句說嗅覺因此變差、食物變淡，與「充滿香味」正好相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why do many astronauts start to like spicy food during their missions?", "options": ["Spicy food is the only kind of food on the ISS.", "Their sense of smell is weaker, so food seems to have less taste.", "Spicy food helps them sleep better in space.", "They have to eat it to fight off colds."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Because smell is an important part of flavor, food seems to lose some of its taste. This is why many astronauts start to love hot sauce and spicy dishes. 鼻塞讓嗅覺變弱，食物吃起來變淡，所以喜歡重口味的辣食。(A) 第二段說站上有一百多種食物；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 太空人只是「感覺像感冒」，並非真的需要對抗感冒。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as a way of preparing food for space?", "options": ["Mixing salt with water", "Mixing pepper with oil", "Removing the water from food so that it lasts longer", "Baking fresh bread in a small oven"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段提到鹽溶在水裡 (A)、胡椒混在油裡 (B)；第二段提到冷凍乾燥是把食物中的水分去除以便長期保存 (C)。文中說麵包會產生碎屑，所以太空人改吃薄餅，完全沒有提到在太空烤麵包。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a27", "title": "Slow Down and Enjoy Your Lunch", "title_zh": "慢慢吃，好好享受午餐", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Food"], "passage": "The lunch bell rings, and many students rush to finish their lunch boxes in ten minutes so that they can play basketball, take a nap or review for a quiz. Eating fast may seem like a smart way to save time, but health experts say it can be bad for both your body and your enjoyment of food.\n\nThe main reason is that your stomach and your brain do not communicate right away. When you eat, your stomach stretches and your gut releases hormones, chemical messages that travel through the blood. These signals tell your brain that you are full, but they take about twenty minutes to have a strong effect. If you finish a meal in ten minutes, your brain has not received the message yet, so you may keep eating even though your body already has enough.\n\nOver time, this habit can cause problems. A study in Japan that followed nearly 60,000 people found that fast eaters were more likely to become overweight than slow eaters. Eating quickly can also lead to stomachaches, because large pieces of food that have not been chewed well are harder to digest. In addition, people who rush through meals often enjoy their food less, since they hardly notice how it tastes.\n\nWatching videos or scrolling through your phone while you eat can make things worse. When your attention is on a screen, you pay less attention to how much you have eaten. Several studies have shown that people who eat while distracted tend to eat more, not only during the meal but also later in the day.\n\nLuckily, slowing down is not difficult. Try putting down your chopsticks or spoon between bites, and chew each bite well before you swallow. Take a sip of water now and then, and chat with your friends instead of looking at your phone. If possible, give yourself at least twenty minutes for each meal. You may find that you feel full with less food, and that lunch tastes better than ever.", "passage_zh": "午餐鐘聲一響，許多學生就急著在十分鐘內吃完便當，好去打籃球、午睡或準備小考。吃得快看起來是節省時間的聰明方法，但健康專家表示，這對你的身體和享受食物的樂趣都可能有害。\n\n主要原因在於你的胃和大腦並不會立刻溝通。當你吃東西時，胃會撐開，腸道會釋放荷爾蒙，也就是透過血液傳遞的化學訊息。這些訊號會告訴大腦你已經飽了，但它們大約需要二十分鐘才會產生明顯的作用。如果你十分鐘就吃完一餐，大腦還沒收到訊息，所以即使身體已經吃夠了，你可能還會繼續吃。\n\n久而久之，這個習慣就會造成問題。日本一項追蹤將近六萬人的研究發現，吃飯快的人比吃飯慢的人更容易變得過重。吃得太快也可能導致胃痛，因為沒有好好咀嚼的大塊食物比較難消化。此外，匆忙吃飯的人往往比較無法享受食物，因為他們幾乎沒注意到食物的味道。\n\n邊吃飯邊看影片或滑手機會讓情況更糟。當你的注意力在螢幕上時，就比較不會注意自己吃了多少。好幾項研究顯示，邊吃東西邊分心的人往往會吃得比較多，不只是在那一餐，在當天稍後也是如此。\n\n幸好，放慢速度並不難。試著在每一口之間放下筷子或湯匙，每一口都充分咀嚼再吞下去。不時喝一小口水，並和朋友聊天，而不是看手機。可能的話，每一餐至少給自己二十分鐘。你可能會發現，吃比較少的食物就飽了，而且午餐比以往任何時候都更美味。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Students should skip lunch to have more time to study.", "Eating slowly is better for your health and helps you enjoy food more.", "Japanese people eat more slowly than people in other countries.", "Playing basketball right after lunch is dangerous."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文說明吃太快的壞處（大腦來不及接收飽足訊號、容易過重、胃痛、無法享受食物），並在第五段提供放慢吃飯速度的方法。(A) 文中沒有建議不吃午餐；(C) 第三段日本的研究是比較吃得快和吃得慢的人，不是比較各國；(D) 文中沒有說飯後打籃球危險。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why might people who eat fast eat too much?", "options": ["Their brains have not yet received the signal that they are full.", "Their lunch boxes usually contain too much food.", "The hormones in their gut make them feel hungrier.", "They drink too little water during their meals."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：If you finish a meal in ten minutes, your brain has not received the message yet, so you may keep eating. 飽足訊號約需二十分鐘才明顯，吃太快時大腦還沒收到。(C) 文中說荷爾蒙是告訴大腦「已經飽了」，與「更餓」相反；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"digest\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["cook over a fire", "buy at a store", "break down in the body so that it can be used", "smell before eating"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：large pieces of food that have not been chewed well are harder to digest，前面說吃太快會胃痛，可知 digest 是指食物在體內被「消化」、分解。(A) 烹煮、(B) 購買、(D) 聞，都與「沒嚼好的大塊食物在胃裡造成不適」無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the study in Japan?", "options": ["It followed about 6,000 high school students.", "It found that slow eaters were more likely to be overweight.", "It found that eating quickly helped people lose weight.", "It found that fast eaters were more likely to become overweight."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：A study in Japan that followed nearly 60,000 people found that fast eaters were more likely to become overweight than slow eaters. (A) 研究對象將近六萬人，且文中沒有說是高中生；(B)(C) 都與研究結果相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which student is following the advice in the passage?", "options": ["Ben puts down his spoon between bites and chats with his classmates.", "Kevin watches cartoons on his phone while he eats.", "Amy finishes her lunch in eight minutes so that she can take a nap.", "Lily swallows big bites quickly to get to basketball practice."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段建議 putting down your chopsticks or spoon between bites，以及 chat with your friends instead of looking at your phone，Ben 的做法完全符合。(B) 邊吃邊看手機正是第四段提醒要避免的；(C) 八分鐘吃完不符合「每餐至少二十分鐘」；(D) 沒有好好咀嚼又吃得太快，也違反建議。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a28", "title": "The Cooker That Became Part of the Family", "title_zh": "像家人一樣的大同電鍋", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Culture"], "passage": "In many Taiwanese kitchens, there is one machine that almost everyone recognizes: a round metal pot with a lid and a single switch on the front. It is the Tatung electric cooker, and for more than sixty years it has steamed rice, warmed up leftovers and cooked soup for millions of families. For many people, it is not just a kitchen tool but almost a member of the family.\n\nThe cooker was first sold in 1960 by Tatung, a Taiwanese company. To make it, Tatung worked with the Japanese company Toshiba, which had started selling the world's first automatic electric rice cooker in 1955. At that time, many families in Taiwan still cooked rice on stoves that burned wood or coal, which took time and careful watching. The new cooker made life much easier, because it could turn itself off when the food was ready.\n\nThe secret is a very simple design. The cooker has an outer pot and an inner pot. The cook puts the food in the inner pot, pours some water into the outer pot and presses the switch down. As the water boils, hot steam cooks the food. When all the water has turned into steam, the temperature inside rises quickly, and the switch pops up with a loud \"click,\" turning off the heat. In general, the more water you pour into the outer pot, the longer the food cooks.\n\nBecause of this design, the cooker can do much more than make rice. People use it to steam buns, dumplings and fish, to cook soups and to heat up yesterday's dinner. It has very few parts that can break, so it is not unusual for a family to use the same cooker for twenty or thirty years. Many Taiwanese students who go abroad to study even pack one in their suitcase so that they can cook familiar food in a new country.\n\nToday, there are many modern rice cookers with digital screens and dozens of programs. Still, the Tatung cooker remains popular. It now comes in many bright colors, and young designers have turned its shape into a symbol of Taiwanese daily life. For many older people, its \"click\" brings back warm memories of a mother or father calling, \"Dinner's ready!\"", "passage_zh": "在許多台灣家庭的廚房裡，有一台幾乎人人都認得的機器：一個附有鍋蓋、前面只有一個開關的圓形金屬鍋。它就是大同電鍋。六十多年來，它為數百萬個家庭蒸飯、加熱剩菜、煮湯。對很多人來說，它不只是廚房用具，幾乎就像家中的一分子。\n\n這款電鍋於1960年由台灣的大同公司首度推出。為了製造它，大同與日本的東芝公司合作；東芝在1955年開始販售世界上第一台自動電子鍋。當時，台灣許多家庭仍在燒木柴或煤炭的爐灶上煮飯，既費時又需要小心看顧。新的電鍋讓生活輕鬆了許多，因為食物煮好時它會自動關掉。\n\n它的祕密在於非常簡單的設計。電鍋有外鍋和內鍋。煮飯的人把食物放進內鍋，在外鍋倒一些水，再把開關按下去。水沸騰時，熱蒸氣就會把食物煮熟。當水全部變成蒸氣後，鍋內的溫度會迅速上升，開關就會「喀」的一聲跳起來，切斷加熱。一般來說，外鍋倒的水越多，食物煮的時間就越長。\n\n由於這樣的設計，電鍋能做的遠遠不只是煮飯。人們用它來蒸包子、水餃和魚，煮湯，以及加熱前一天的晚餐。它很少有會故障的零件，所以一個家庭用同一台電鍋用上二、三十年並不稀奇。許多出國留學的台灣學生甚至會把一台電鍋放進行李箱，好在新的國家煮熟悉的家鄉菜。\n\n如今市面上有許多附數位螢幕、有數十種功能的新式電子鍋。儘管如此，大同電鍋依然很受歡迎。它現在有許多鮮豔的顏色，年輕設計師也把它的造型變成台灣日常生活的象徵。對許多年長的人來說，它那「喀」的一聲，會勾起爸爸或媽媽呼喊「吃飯囉！」的溫暖回憶。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to cook perfect rice in any kind of pot", "Why Japanese kitchen products are better than Taiwanese ones", "The history, design and importance of a well-known Taiwanese cooker", "How to choose a modern rice cooker with a digital screen"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹大同電鍋的由來（第二段）、運作原理（第三段）、多種用途與耐用（第四段），以及它在台灣人生活和記憶中的地位（第一、五段）。(A) 文中沒有教人用各種鍋子煮飯；(B) 文中只說大同與東芝合作，沒有比較優劣；(D) 新式電子鍋只在第五段順帶提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, which of the following is true?", "options": ["Tatung invented the world's first automatic rice cooker.", "Tatung worked with a Japanese company to make its cooker.", "The Tatung cooker was first sold in 1955.", "Most Taiwanese families had electric stoves in 1960."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：To make it, Tatung worked with the Japanese company Toshiba. (A) 推出世界第一台自動電子鍋的是東芝，不是大同；(C) 1955 年是東芝開始販售電子鍋的年份，大同電鍋是 1960 年推出；(D) 文中說當時許多家庭仍用燒木柴或煤炭的爐灶煮飯。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What makes the switch on the cooker pop up?", "options": ["The inner pot becomes too full of rice.", "The cook presses the switch a second time.", "All the water in the outer pot has turned into steam.", "A timer rings after exactly twenty minutes."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：When all the water has turned into steam, the temperature inside rises quickly, and the switch pops up. 外鍋的水全部變成蒸氣、溫度上升，開關就會跳起。(A)(D) 文中未提及內鍋太滿或計時器；(B) 按下開關是開始煮的動作，不會讓開關跳起。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Mrs. Lin wants to cook some beef soup in her Tatung cooker for a longer time. Based on the passage, what should she do?", "options": ["Use a smaller inner pot.", "Press the switch down more quickly.", "Put less water in the outer pot.", "Pour more water into the outer pot."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段最後一句：the more water you pour into the outer pot, the longer the food cooks. 外鍋水越多，煮的時間越長。(C) 水較少反而煮得較短；(A)(B) 內鍋大小和按開關的速度，文中沒有說會影響烹煮時間。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 4, the word \"one\" in \"pack one in their suitcase\" refers to ______.", "options": ["a Tatung cooker", "a new country", "a Taiwanese student", "a dish of familiar food"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Many Taiwanese students who go abroad to study even pack one in their suitcase so that they can cook familiar food，能放進行李箱、又能用來煮家鄉菜的，就是本文介紹的大同電鍋。(B)(C) 國家和學生都不可能放進行李箱；(D) 熟悉的食物是用電鍋「煮」出來的，不是被裝進行李箱的東西。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a29", "title": "A Recipe Without Numbers", "title_zh": "沒有數字的食譜", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Life", "Food"], "passage": "One evening during last winter vacation, my mother had to work late, so my grandmother asked me to help her cook dinner. I agreed, mainly because I wanted to learn her three-cup chicken, which has been my favorite dish since I was little. I took out my phone, ready to type the recipe. \"How much soy sauce?\" I asked. Grandma just laughed. \"Enough,\" she said.\n\nThat was my first lesson: Grandma never measures anything. She learned to cook by watching her own mother, not by reading cookbooks. She told me that the dish got its name from the old way of making it, with one cup each of sesame oil, rice wine and soy sauce. But nobody uses three full cups today, she said, because the dish would be far too oily and salty. \"You cook with your eyes, your nose and your ears.\"\n\nI soon understood what she meant. First, she heated sesame oil in a clay pot and added thick slices of ginger. She did not use a timer. Instead, she waited until the edges of the ginger curled and turned light brown and the kitchen filled with a warm smell. Then she added the chicken and listened. \"Hear that loud sizzle? The pot is hot enough.\" When the sauce became thick and shiny, she threw in a handful of fresh basil, put the lid on and turned off the heat.\n\nOf course, I wanted to cook the dish without her help. So the next weekend, I measured each sauce before Grandma poured it in and wrote everything down in a notebook. A week later, I made three-cup chicken by myself for the whole family. It was good, but it was not quite Grandma's. The chicken was a little dry, because I had followed my notes exactly instead of watching the sauce.\n\nNow I understand that a recipe is only a starting point. Numbers help a beginner, but a good cook also pays attention to colors, smells and sounds. I still keep my notebook, and next to the numbers I have added Grandma's tips, such as \"Wait for the ginger to curl\" and \"Listen for the sizzle.\" Someday, I hope to teach this dish to my own grandchildren, with numbers and without them.", "passage_zh": "去年寒假的某個晚上，媽媽要加班到很晚，所以奶奶要我幫她煮晚餐。我答應了，主要是因為我想學她的三杯雞，那是我從小最愛的一道菜。我拿出手機，準備把食譜打下來。「醬油要放多少？」我問。奶奶只是笑了笑，說：「放夠就好。」\n\n這是我學到的第一課：奶奶從來不量任何東西。她是看著自己的媽媽做菜學會的，而不是看食譜學的。她告訴我，這道菜的名字來自傳統的做法：麻油、米酒和醬油各放一杯。但她說，現在沒有人真的會用滿滿三杯，因為那樣菜會太油又太鹹。「要用眼睛、鼻子和耳朵來煮菜。」\n\n我很快就明白她的意思了。首先，她在砂鍋裡把麻油燒熱，放進厚厚的薑片。她沒有用計時器，而是等到薑片的邊緣捲起、變成淡褐色，廚房裡充滿溫暖的香氣。接著她放入雞肉，仔細聆聽。「聽到那大聲的滋滋聲了嗎？鍋子夠熱了。」當醬汁變得濃稠又油亮時，她丟進一把新鮮的九層塔，蓋上鍋蓋，然後關火。\n\n當然，我希望自己不靠奶奶幫忙也能煮這道菜。所以下一個週末，在奶奶倒入每一種醬料之前，我都先量一量，並把所有東西都寫在筆記本裡。一週後，我自己為全家人做了三杯雞。味道不錯，但還是比不上奶奶做的。雞肉有點乾，因為我完全照著筆記做，而沒有留意醬汁的狀況。\n\n現在我明白，食譜只是一個起點。數字能幫助初學者，但好的廚師還會留意顏色、香氣和聲音。我仍然保留著那本筆記本，並在數字旁邊加上奶奶的訣竅，例如「等薑片捲起來」和「聽滋滋聲」。希望有一天，我能把這道菜教給我自己的孫子孫女——有數字的版本，也有沒有數字的版本。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main lesson the writer learned?", "options": ["Recipes are useless, so beginners should never use them.", "Good cooking needs careful attention, not just exact numbers.", "Grandparents should learn to use smartphones in the kitchen.", "Three-cup chicken must be made with three full cups of each sauce."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：a recipe is only a starting point. Numbers help a beginner, but a good cook also pays attention to colors, smells and sounds. 作者學到做菜除了數字，還要用感官細心觀察。(A) 作者說數字能幫助初學者，並仍保留筆記本，不是說食譜沒用；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第二段奶奶說現在沒有人真的用滿三杯。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Where does the name \"three-cup chicken\" come from?", "options": ["The dish was once served in three small cups.", "The chicken had to be cooked for three hours.", "In the old recipe, one cup each of three sauces was used.", "Three kinds of chicken were used to make the dish."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：the dish got its name from the old way of making it, with one cup each of sesame oil, rice wine and soy sauce. 名稱來自傳統做法中麻油、米酒、醬油各一杯。(A)(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How did Grandma know the pot was hot enough for the chicken?", "options": ["She listened for a loud sizzling sound.", "She set a timer on her phone.", "She touched the side of the clay pot.", "She checked the oil with a thermometer."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Then she added the chicken and listened. \"Hear that loud sizzle? The pot is hot enough.\" 奶奶是聽滋滋聲來判斷鍋子夠熱。(B) 文中說 She did not use a timer；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why was the writer's three-cup chicken a little dry?", "options": ["The writer forgot to add the fresh basil.", "The writer used much more soy sauce than Grandma did.", "The writer's family did not like the taste of ginger.", "The writer followed the notes exactly instead of watching the sauce."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The chicken was a little dry, because I had followed my notes exactly instead of watching the sauce. 雞肉偏乾是因為作者只照筆記上的數字做，沒有觀察醬汁的狀況。(A)(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "When Grandma answered \"Enough\" in paragraph 1, what did she most likely mean?", "options": ["She did not want to teach the writer how to cook.", "There was not enough soy sauce left in the kitchen.", "The right amount depends on the cook's judgment, not an exact number.", "The writer had asked too many questions that day."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段接著說 Grandma never measures anything，又說 You cook with your eyes, your nose and your ears，可知奶奶說「放夠就好」，是指份量要靠經驗和感官判斷，而不是固定的數字。(A) 奶奶後來仍一步步示範，並非不想教；(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到醬油不夠或問題太多。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a30", "title": "Pickleball: The Backyard Game That Brought a Family Together", "title_zh": "匹克球：讓一家人更親近的後院遊戲", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "Life"], "passage": "Last summer, sixteen-year-old Kai Lin noticed something unusual in his neighborhood park. Every morning, a group of retired people gathered on a small court and hit a plastic ball back and forth with flat paddles. One of them was his own grandmother. \"It's called pickleball,\" she told him. \"Come and try it. I bet you'll lose.\"\n\nPickleball was invented in 1965 on Bainbridge Island, near Seattle in the United States. Joel Pritchard and his friend Bill Bell came home from a game of golf and found their children bored. There was an old badminton court in Pritchard's backyard, but nobody could find a full set of rackets. So the fathers gave the kids table tennis paddles and a light plastic ball full of holes, and they lowered the net. People still argue about the strange name. Some say it came from the family dog, Pickles; others say it came from \"pickle boat,\" a rowing term for a boat filled with leftover rowers.\n\nThe game is easy to learn. The court is the same size as a doubles badminton court, much smaller than a tennis court, so players do not need to run very far. The ball is slow, and every serve must be hit underhand. There is one special rule: players may not hit the ball in the air while standing in the area right next to the net. This area is called the \"kitchen,\" and it stops tall, strong players from simply smashing every ball. As a result, good pickleball depends more on control and patience than on power.\n\nThese features help explain why the sport has spread so quickly. In the United States, it has been named the fastest-growing sport for several years in a row, and in 2022 it became the official state sport of Washington. Courts have also appeared in Taiwan, often in parks and school gyms.\n\nKai did lose his first game to his grandmother, 11 to 4. But he kept coming back. Now they play together twice a week, and he has noticed that the sport gives them more than exercise. \"At home, we don't always have much to talk about,\" he says. \"On the court, we never stop talking.\"", "passage_zh": "去年夏天，十六歲的林凱在住家附近的公園注意到一件不尋常的事。每天早上，一群退休人士會聚集在一座小球場上，用扁平的球拍把一顆塑膠球打來打去。其中一位竟然就是他自己的奶奶。「這叫做匹克球，」她告訴他，「來試試看吧。我敢說你會輸。」\n\n匹克球是在 1965 年於美國西雅圖附近的班布里奇島發明的。喬爾‧普里查德和他的朋友比爾‧貝爾打完高爾夫球回到家，發現孩子們很無聊。普里查德家的後院有一座舊的羽毛球場，但沒有人找得到一整套球拍。於是兩位父親給孩子們桌球拍和一顆布滿小洞的輕塑膠球，並把球網降低。至於這個奇怪的名字，人們到現在還在爭論。有人說它來自家裡的狗「Pickles」；也有人說它來自「pickle boat」，這是划船界的用語，指由其他船挑剩的划手所組成的船。\n\n這項運動很容易學。球場和羽毛球雙打場地一樣大，比網球場小得多，所以球員不需要跑很遠。球速很慢，而且每次發球都必須低手發球。它有一條特別的規則：球員站在緊鄰球網的區域時，不可以在球落地前於空中擊球。這個區域被稱為「廚房」，它能防止高大有力的球員一味地扣殺每一顆球。因此，打好匹克球靠的是控球和耐心，而不是力量。\n\n這些特點有助於說明這項運動為什麼擴展得這麼快。在美國，它已連續好幾年被評為成長最快的運動；2022 年，它更成為華盛頓州的官方州運動。台灣也出現了匹克球場，通常設在公園和學校體育館裡。\n\n林凱的第一場比賽確實以 4 比 11 輸給了奶奶。但他一次又一次回到球場。現在他們每週一起打兩次球，而他發現這項運動帶給他們的不只是運動而已。「在家裡，我們不一定有很多話可以聊，」他說，「但在球場上，我們從來沒停止過聊天。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How young players can easily beat older players at pickleball", "The history, rules and appeal of pickleball, seen through one family's experience", "Why tennis is losing players to newer sports in Taiwan", "A rowing team that invented a new sport near Seattle"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文以林凱和奶奶打匹克球的經驗開頭與結尾，中間介紹匹克球的起源（第二段）、規則（第三段）和受歡迎的原因（第四段）。(A) 與第五段林凱第一場就輸給奶奶不符；(C) 文中沒有提到網球人口減少；(D) 匹克球是兩位父親為孩子發明的，「pickle boat」只是名字來源的說法之一，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did the fathers give the children table tennis paddles?", "options": ["They could not find a full set of badminton rackets.", "Table tennis paddles were cheaper than tennis rackets.", "The children were too young to play tennis.", "They wanted the children to play a game indoors."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：There was an old badminton court in Pritchard's backyard, but nobody could find a full set of rackets. So the fathers gave the kids table tennis paddles... 因為找不到整套羽毛球拍，才改用桌球拍。(B)(C)(D) 價錢、年紀、室內遊戲文中皆未提及，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In pickleball, what is the \"kitchen\"?", "options": ["A place beside the court where players rest and eat snacks", "The area at the back of the court where every serve begins", "A small shop in the park that sells paddles and balls", "The area next to the net where players may not hit the ball in the air"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：players may not hit the ball in the air while standing in the area right next to the net. This area is called the \"kitchen\"。「廚房」指緊鄰球網、不可在球落地前截擊的區域，並非真的廚房或休息區。(A)(B)(C) 都是望文生義的錯誤解讀，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT true about pickleball according to the passage?", "options": ["Every serve must be hit underhand.", "Its court is larger than a tennis court.", "It was invented in 1965.", "It is the official state sport of Washington."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段說球場和羽毛球雙打場地一樣大，much smaller than a tennis court（比網球場小得多），所以 (B)「比網球場大」與文意不符。(A) 見第三段 every serve must be hit underhand；(C) 見第二段 invented in 1965；(D) 見第四段 official state sport of Washington，三者皆正確，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does Kai mean when he says, \"On the court, we never stop talking\"?", "options": ["Pickleball games are too noisy for him to enjoy.", "His grandmother talks during games to make him lose.", "Playing pickleball has brought him closer to his grandmother.", "He would rather chat with his grandmother than play the game."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：林凱說在家不一定有話可聊，在球場上卻一直聊天，前一句也提到這項運動帶給他們 more than exercise。可推知打匹克球拉近了祖孫的距離。(A)(B) 文中沒有抱怨吵鬧或奶奶故意干擾；(D) 他每週和奶奶打兩次球，並非比較想聊天而不打球，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a31", "title": "Breaking: From Street Parties to the Olympic Stage", "title_zh": "霹靂舞：從街頭派對到奧運舞台", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "Arts"], "passage": "In August 2024, a new sport appeared at the Paris Olympics. There was no ball, no net and no finish line. Instead, two dancers took turns in the center of a stage while a DJ played music. They spun on their heads, froze in difficult positions and moved their feet so fast that it was hard to follow them. This was breaking, often called breakdancing, and it was making its Olympic debut.\n\nBreaking began in the Bronx, a part of New York City, in the 1970s. At neighborhood parties, DJs noticed that people danced hardest during the \"break,\" the short part of a song where the singing stops and only the drums play. A DJ known as Kool Herc began playing these breaks again and again so that the dancing could continue. The young people who danced to them became known as b-boys and b-girls. Many of them grew up in poor neighborhoods, and a dance battle gave them a way to win respect without fighting.\n\nAt the Olympics, breaking took the form of one-on-one battles. The dancers did not know which songs the DJ would play, so they had to make up many of their moves on the spot. A group of judges scored each round on technique, variety, musicality, originality and how cleanly the moves were performed. In other words, copying someone else's routine was not enough; the best breakers showed a personal style. Ami Yuasa of Japan won the first gold medal for women, and Phil Wizard of Canada won the men's event.\n\nNot everyone was happy to see breaking at the Olympics. Some fans felt that a street art should not be scored like gymnastics, and they worried that strict rules would take away its freedom. In fact, breaking will not be part of the 2028 Games in Los Angeles. Still, many young dancers say Paris gave them something important. For the first time, their parents and teachers saw that breaking needs years of training, strength and discipline.\n\nA dance that started at parties in one neighborhood has now traveled to stages around the world. Whether or not it returns to the Olympics, breaking will keep living wherever there is music and a little open floor.", "passage_zh": "2024 年 8 月，巴黎奧運出現了一項新運動。場上沒有球、沒有網子，也沒有終點線。取而代之的是，兩名舞者在 DJ 播放音樂時輪流站上舞台中央。他們用頭旋轉、以高難度的姿勢定格，雙腳移動得快到讓人幾乎看不清楚。這就是霹靂舞（breaking，常被稱為 breakdancing），而這是它第一次在奧運登場。\n\n霹靂舞在 1970 年代起源於紐約市的布朗克斯區。在社區派對上，DJ 們注意到，人們在歌曲的「break」（間奏）時跳得最起勁；那是歌曲中歌聲停止、只剩鼓聲的短短片段。一位名叫 Kool Herc 的 DJ 開始一再重複播放這些間奏，讓大家可以繼續跳舞。隨著這些間奏起舞的年輕人，後來被稱為 b-boy 和 b-girl。他們當中許多人在貧困的社區長大，而尬舞讓他們不必打架，也能贏得尊重。\n\n在奧運中，霹靂舞採用一對一對決的形式。舞者事先不知道 DJ 會播放哪些歌曲，所以許多動作必須當場即興發揮。一群評審針對技巧、動作多樣性、音樂性、原創性以及動作完成的俐落程度，為每一回合評分。換句話說，光是模仿別人的套路是不夠的；最好的舞者會展現出個人風格。日本的湯淺亞實贏得了史上第一面女子組金牌，加拿大的 Phil Wizard 則贏得男子組冠軍。\n\n並不是每個人都樂見霹靂舞進入奧運。有些舞迷認為，一種街頭藝術不應該像體操一樣被打分數，他們也擔心嚴格的規則會奪走它的自由。事實上，霹靂舞將不會出現在 2028 年的洛杉磯奧運。儘管如此，許多年輕舞者說，巴黎奧運帶給了他們一樣重要的東西。他們的父母和老師第一次看到，霹靂舞需要多年的訓練、力量和自律。\n\n一種從某個社區的派對中誕生的舞蹈，如今已走上世界各地的舞台。無論它是否會重返奧運，只要有音樂和一小塊空地，霹靂舞就會一直延續下去。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How judges are trained to score Olympic events", "Why breaking should take the place of gymnastics", "Where breaking came from and how it appeared at the Olympics", "The life and music of a famous New York DJ"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段介紹霹靂舞在 2024 巴黎奧運首次登場，第二段說明它在 1970 年代紐約布朗克斯區的起源，第三、四段描述奧運比賽方式與各方看法。(A) 評審只是第三段的細節；(B) 文中沒有主張取代體操；(D) Kool Herc 只在第二段被提到一次，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"on the spot\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["in one fixed place on the stage", "right at that moment, without planning", "after many weeks of careful practice", "under the brightest light"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The dancers did not know which songs the DJ would play, so they had to make up many of their moves on the spot. 舞者事先不知道音樂，只能「當場、臨時」編出動作，on the spot 意思是 right at that moment, without planning。(A) 是字面上的「在某個地點」，不合上下文；(C) 與「事先不知道音樂」矛盾；(D) 與文意無關，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what is the \"break\" in a song?", "options": ["The short part where the singing stops and only the drums play", "The loudest part, when the singer sings the chorus", "The silence between the end of one song and the start of another", "The first minute of a song, before the dancers begin"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：the \"break,\" the short part of a song where the singing stops and only the drums play。break 是歌聲停止、只剩鼓聲的短短片段。(B) 與「歌聲停止」相反；(C)(D) 文中未如此定義，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why were some fans unhappy about breaking becoming an Olympic sport?", "options": ["They thought the moves were too dangerous for young dancers.", "They felt the music played at the Olympics was too loud.", "They wanted the Olympics to spend more money on gymnastics.", "They worried that strict rules might take away its freedom."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：Some fans felt that a street art should not be scored like gymnastics, and they worried that strict rules would take away its freedom. 舞迷擔心嚴格規則會奪走這種街頭藝術的自由。(A)(B)(C) 危險性、音樂太大聲、經費問題文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true according to the passage?", "options": ["Breaking will be one of the sports at the 2028 Los Angeles Games.", "Olympic breakers practiced with the DJ's songs before each battle.", "A dancer from Japan won the first Olympic gold medal for women.", "Breaking was first created by dancers in Paris in the 1970s."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：Ami Yuasa of Japan won the first gold medal for women. 日本選手湯淺亞實贏得第一面女子組金牌。(A) 第四段說 breaking will not be part of the 2028 Games；(B) 第三段說舞者不知道 DJ 會放哪些歌；(D) 第二段說霹靂舞起源於紐約布朗克斯區，不是巴黎，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a32", "title": "Hitting the Wall: The Marathon's 30-Kilometer Problem", "title_zh": "撞牆期：馬拉松三十公里的難關", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "Science"], "passage": "For the first three hours of her first marathon, Ya-ting felt wonderful. She passed other runners and waved at people cheering by the road. Then, at around the 32-kilometer mark, everything changed. Her legs felt as heavy as stone, her mind became foggy, and she suddenly wanted to cry. She had \"hit the wall,\" an experience that many marathon runners know too well.\n\nHitting the wall is not just in a runner's head; it has a clear scientific cause. Our muscles get most of their quick energy from glycogen, a form of sugar stored in the muscles and the liver. The body can store only a limited amount, often enough for roughly 30 kilometers of hard running. When glycogen runs low, the body has to depend more on fat. Fat contains plenty of energy, but the body turns it into fuel much more slowly. As a result, the runner is forced to slow down, sometimes to a walk. Because the brain also needs sugar, runners may feel confused, weak and emotional.\n\nThis is why the marathon can feel so cruel. The race is 42.195 kilometers long, so the wall usually appears with about ten kilometers still to go. Interestingly, this exact distance comes from the 1908 London Olympics, where the course was planned to start at Windsor Castle and finish in front of the royal family's seats in the stadium.\n\nThe good news is that the wall can usually be avoided. First, in the two or three days before a race, runners eat more rice, noodles and bread so that their bodies can store extra glycogen. Second, during the race, they take in sugar through sports drinks, bananas or energy gels before they feel tired. Third, they start at a steady pace. Running too fast in the early kilometers burns glycogen quickly and brings the wall closer. Finally, long training runs teach the body to use fat more efficiently.\n\nYa-ting walked and jogged the last ten kilometers and finished in just over five hours. Six months later, she ran her second marathon with a better plan, and this time she never met the wall at all. \"The wall is real,\" she says, \"but you can prepare for it.\"", "passage_zh": "在人生第一場馬拉松的前三個小時，雅婷感覺棒極了。她超越了其他跑者，也向路邊加油的民眾揮手。然而，大約跑到 32 公里處時，一切都變了。她的雙腿重得像石頭，腦袋變得昏昏沉沉，還突然很想哭。她「撞牆」了──這是許多馬拉松跑者都再熟悉不過的經驗。\n\n撞牆並不只是跑者的心理作用，它有明確的科學原因。我們的肌肉主要從肝醣獲得快速的能量；肝醣是一種儲存在肌肉和肝臟裡的醣類。身體能儲存的量有限，通常大約只夠以比賽強度跑 30 公里左右。當肝醣快要用完時，身體就必須更依賴脂肪。脂肪含有大量能量，但身體把它轉換成燃料的速度慢得多。因此，跑者會被迫放慢速度，有時甚至只能用走的。又因為大腦也需要醣類，跑者可能會感到思緒混亂、虛弱，情緒也變得激動。\n\n這就是為什麼馬拉松會讓人覺得如此殘酷。比賽全長 42.195 公里，所以撞牆通常出現在還剩大約十公里的時候。有趣的是，這個精確的距離來自 1908 年的倫敦奧運，當時的路線被規劃為從溫莎城堡出發，終點設在體育場內王室成員的座位前方。\n\n好消息是，撞牆通常是可以避免的。第一，在賽前兩三天，跑者會多吃米飯、麵條和麵包，好讓身體儲存額外的肝醣。第二，比賽途中，他們會在感到疲累之前，就先透過運動飲料、香蕉或能量膠補充醣分。第三，他們會以穩定的配速起跑。在前幾公里跑得太快，會讓肝醣很快耗盡，使撞牆提早到來。最後，長距離的訓練能讓身體更有效率地利用脂肪。\n\n雅婷在最後十公里邊走邊慢跑，以五個多小時完賽。六個月後，她帶著更好的計畫跑了第二場馬拉松，而這一次她完全沒有撞牆。「撞牆是真的，」她說，「但你可以事先做好準備。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the 1908 London Olympics changed the rules of running", "How Ya-ting trained to become a professional runner", "Why marathon runners hit the wall and how they can avoid it", "Why eating too much fat is bad for long-distance runners"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文以雅婷的經驗為例，第二段解釋撞牆的科學原因（肝醣不足），第四段說明避免撞牆的方法。(A) 1908 年倫敦奧運只是第三段提到馬拉松距離的由來；(B) 雅婷是一般跑者，文中沒說她要當職業選手；(D) 文中沒說吃脂肪不好，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why are runners forced to slow down when they hit the wall?", "options": ["Their bodies must depend more on fat, which is turned into energy slowly.", "Their muscles have used up all the water in their bodies.", "They have eaten too much rice and bread before the race.", "Their legs have been hurt by running on hard roads."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：When glycogen runs low, the body has to depend more on fat... the body turns it into fuel much more slowly. As a result, the runner is forced to slow down. 肝醣不足時，身體改靠轉換較慢的脂肪供能，所以速度被迫變慢。(B)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 賽前多吃米飯麵包反而是第四段建議的預防方法，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer say that the marathon \"can feel so cruel\"?", "options": ["Because the first marathon was held only for the royal family.", "Because most marathons are held in very hot weather.", "Because few runners are able to finish their first marathon.", "Because runners often still have about ten kilometers left when their glycogen runs low."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：The race is 42.195 kilometers long, so the wall usually appears with about ten kilometers still to go. 肝醣約在 30 公里左右耗盡，但全程有 42 公里，撞牆後還要再跑大約十公里，所以說它殘酷。(A) 終點設在王室座位前，不代表只為王室舉辦；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT suggested in the passage as a way to avoid hitting the wall?", "options": ["Eating more rice, noodles and bread a few days before the race", "Running as fast as possible in the early kilometers to save time", "Taking in sugar from sports drinks or bananas during the race", "Doing long training runs before the race day"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段建議以穩定配速起跑，並指出 Running too fast in the early kilometers burns glycogen quickly and brings the wall closer（前幾公里跑太快會讓撞牆提早到來），所以 (B) 不是建議的方法。(A)(C)(D) 分別是第四段提到的賽前多吃澱粉、賽中補充醣分、長距離訓練，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"steady\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["even and not changing", "very fast and exciting", "slow and lazy", "difficult and painful"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段 Third, they start at a steady pace，下一句接著說一開始跑太快會讓撞牆提早到來，可知 steady pace 是「穩定、不忽快忽慢的配速」，意思接近 even and not changing。(B) 與「不要跑太快」相反；(C)(D) 不合文意，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a33", "title": "Is 10,000 Steps a Day Really the Magic Number?", "title_zh": "一天一萬步真的是神奇數字嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "Many smartwatches and phone apps give their users the same daily goal: 10,000 steps. When people reach it, the watch may shake gently or show fireworks on the screen. The number sounds so scientific that most people never question it. But where did it actually come from?\n\nThe answer is not a laboratory but an advertising campaign. In 1965, shortly after the Tokyo Olympics, a Japanese company began selling a simple step counter called the manpo-kei, which means \"10,000-step meter.\" Interest in health and exercise was growing in Japan at that time, and a round, easy-to-remember number helped sell the product. Some people also point out that the Japanese character for 10,000, 万, looks a little like a person walking. The goal soon spread around the world, even though no study had proved that it was the right number.\n\nIn recent years, scientists have finally tested the idea. In a 2019 study of more than 16,000 older women in the United States, those who walked about 4,400 steps a day were less likely to die during the following years than those who walked only 2,700. The benefits kept rising as the number of steps went up, but they leveled off at around 7,500 steps a day. Later research that combined many studies found similar results. For people over 60, the benefits stopped growing at 6,000 to 8,000 steps, while younger adults kept gaining up to about 8,000 to 10,000.\n\nDoes this mean 10,000 steps is a bad goal? Not at all. For young, healthy people, walking more is still good for the heart and the mind. However, the research sends an important message to people who hardly exercise: you do not need to reach a huge number to improve your health. Moving from 3,000 to 5,000 steps a day can already make a real difference.\n\nExperts also remind us that steps are not the only thing that counts. Walking fast enough to breathe a little harder, climbing stairs and playing sports all bring extra benefits. So if your watch says you have reached only 6,000 steps today, do not feel like a failure. The best number is the one you can keep up, day after day.", "passage_zh": "許多智慧手錶和手機 app 都為使用者設定了相同的每日目標：一萬步。當人們達成目標時，手錶可能會輕輕震動，或在螢幕上放出煙火。這個數字聽起來很科學，以至於大多數人從來沒有懷疑過它。但它究竟是從哪裡來的呢？\n\n答案不是來自實驗室，而是來自一場廣告宣傳。1965 年，東京奧運結束後不久，一家日本公司開始販售一種簡單的計步器，名叫「萬步計」，意思就是「一萬步的計數器」。當時日本人對健康和運動的興趣日漸提高，而一個整齊好記的整數有助於推銷這項產品。也有人指出，日文中代表一萬的「万」字，看起來有點像一個正在走路的人。這個目標很快就傳遍了全世界，儘管當時並沒有任何研究證明這是正確的數字。\n\n近年來，科學家終於開始檢驗這個說法。在 2019 年一項針對美國一萬六千多名年長女性的研究中，每天走大約 4,400 步的人，在接下來幾年中死亡的機率比每天只走 2,700 步的人來得低。步數增加時，好處也持續增加，但到了每天大約 7,500 步時，好處就不再上升。後來綜合多項研究的分析也發現了類似的結果：對 60 歲以上的人來說，好處在 6,000 到 8,000 步時就停止增加；較年輕的成年人則一直到大約 8,000 到 10,000 步都還能持續獲益。\n\n這是否表示一萬步是個不好的目標呢？完全不是。對年輕健康的人來說，多走路對心臟和心理仍然有益。不過，這些研究對平常很少運動的人傳達了一個重要的訊息：你不需要達到一個很大的數字，也能改善健康。從每天 3,000 步增加到 5,000 步，就已經能帶來真正的改變。\n\n專家也提醒我們，步數並不是唯一重要的事。走路快到讓呼吸稍微變喘、爬樓梯以及從事運動，都能帶來額外的好處。所以，如果你的手錶顯示你今天只走了 6,000 步，別覺得自己很失敗。最好的數字，是你能夠日復一日持續下去的那一個。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To advertise a new kind of smartwatch for walkers", "To prove that walking is harmful for older people", "To describe how Japan prepared for the 1964 Tokyo Olympics", "To explain where the 10,000-step goal came from and what research says about it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段說明一萬步的目標來自 1965 年日本計步器的廣告宣傳，第三到五段介紹科學研究的結果與建議。(A) 文中沒有推銷任何產品；(B) 研究顯示走路對年長者有益，與選項相反；(C) 東京奧運只是第二段的時間背景，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why was the number 10,000 chosen for the step counter?", "options": ["It was a round, easy-to-remember number that helped sell the product.", "A study had proved that it was the best number for health.", "Olympic athletes in Tokyo walked that many steps every day.", "Japanese doctors told everyone to walk that far each day."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：a round, easy-to-remember number helped sell the product. 一萬是好記的整數，有助於產品銷售。(B) 與第二段最後一句 no study had proved that it was the right number 相反；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In the 2019 study, at about how many steps a day did the benefits stop increasing?", "options": ["2,700", "4,400", "7,500", "10,000"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：The benefits kept rising as the number of steps went up, but they leveled off at around 7,500 steps a day. 好處在每天約 7,500 步時就不再增加。(A) 2,700 和 (B) 4,400 是研究中拿來比較的步數；(D) 10,000 是廣告設定的目標，並非研究結果，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What would the writer most likely say to a person who walks only 3,000 steps a day?", "options": ["\"Walking is useless unless you reach 10,000 steps.\"", "\"Try adding a couple of thousand steps; it can already help.\"", "\"You should buy a smartwatch before you start walking.\"", "\"Walking slowly is healthier than walking fast.\""], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Moving from 3,000 to 5,000 steps a day can already make a real difference. 作者認為每天多走兩千步左右就能帶來改變。(A) 與第四段 you do not need to reach a huge number 相反；(C) 文中未建議買手錶；(D) 第五段說走快一點、讓呼吸稍喘反而有額外好處，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"leveled off\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["stopped going up", "dropped suddenly", "started again", "became unfair"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 The benefits kept rising... but they leveled off at around 7,500 steps，「but」表示轉折：好處原本持續上升，到 7,500 步就「趨於平緩、不再增加」，同段下一句也用 stopped growing 換句話說，故 leveled off 意思接近 stopped going up。(B) 文中沒說好處突然下降；(C)(D) 不合文意，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a34", "title": "Winter Guests in Tainan: My First Black-faced Spoonbills", "title_zh": "臺南的冬季嬌客：我第一次看到黑面琵鷺", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Nature", "Life"], "passage": "When Pei-shan's biology teacher invited the class on a birdwatching trip, she almost said no. Getting up at five on a Saturday to look at birds did not sound like fun. But on a cold December morning, standing quietly beside a wetland in Qigu, Tainan, she saw a group of tall white birds in the shallow water. Each one had a long, flat black bill shaped like a spoon. \"Those are black-faced spoonbills,\" her teacher whispered. \"Some of them flew here all the way from Korea.\"\n\nThe black-faced spoonbill is an endangered bird. In the late 1980s, scientists could find only about 300 of them in the world. Their wetlands were being turned into farms, factories and roads, and the species seemed close to disappearing. Since then, countries in East Asia have worked together to protect them. Today there are more than 6,000, and every winter a large share of them come to Taiwan, especially to the coast of Tainan. They usually arrive around October and fly north again in spring.\n\nSpoonbills feed in an unusual way. Instead of looking for food with their eyes, they sweep their open bills from side to side under the water. When a small fish or shrimp touches the inside of the bill, it snaps shut. Because they feed by touch, they can find food in muddy water and even in the dark. In fact, they often feed at night and spend much of the day resting.\n\nThat morning changed how Pei-shan spends her weekends. She has since joined a local birdwatching club and learned some basic rules. Birdwatchers should wear clothes in dull colors, such as green or brown, and keep their voices low. They should stay on the paths and use binoculars instead of walking closer. Feeding wild birds or using a camera flash is not allowed, because it can frighten or harm them.\n\nBirdwatching, Pei-shan says, has taught her patience. You cannot order a bird to appear; you can only wait, look carefully and enjoy whatever comes. It has also changed how she sees wetlands, which she once thought were just empty, muddy land. \"Now when I look at a wetland,\" she says, \"I see a home.\"", "passage_zh": "當佩珊的生物老師邀請全班參加賞鳥活動時，她差點就拒絕了。星期六早上五點起床去看鳥，聽起來一點也不好玩。但在十二月一個寒冷的早晨，她靜靜地站在臺南七股的一片溼地旁，看到一群高大的白色鳥兒站在淺水中。每一隻都有又長又扁、形狀像湯匙的黑色鳥喙。「那些是黑面琵鷺，」老師小聲地說，「有些是大老遠從韓國飛過來的。」\n\n黑面琵鷺是一種瀕臨絕種的鳥類。在 1980 年代末期，科學家在全世界只找得到大約 300 隻。牠們的溼地被改建成農田、工廠和道路，這個物種似乎就快要消失了。從那時起，東亞各國便攜手合作保護牠們。如今牠們的數量已超過六千隻，而每年冬天都有很大一部分會來到臺灣，尤其是臺南沿海。牠們通常在十月左右抵達，到了春天再飛回北方。\n\n琵鷺覓食的方式很特別。牠們不是用眼睛找食物，而是把張開的喙放在水中左右掃動。當小魚或小蝦碰到喙的內側時，喙就會迅速閉合。因為牠們靠觸覺覓食，所以在混濁的水中、甚至在黑暗中都能找到食物。事實上，牠們經常在夜間覓食，白天則大多在休息。\n\n那個早晨改變了佩珊度過週末的方式。她後來加入了當地的賞鳥社團，並學到一些基本規則。賞鳥的人應該穿著綠色或咖啡色等暗色系的衣服，並且壓低音量。他們應該待在步道上，用望遠鏡觀察，而不是走近鳥兒。餵食野鳥或使用相機閃光燈是不被允許的，因為這樣可能會驚嚇或傷害牠們。\n\n佩珊說，賞鳥教會了她耐心。你無法命令一隻鳥出現；你只能等待、仔細觀察，然後享受眼前出現的一切。賞鳥也改變了她看待溼地的方式；她以前認為溼地只是空蕩蕩、泥濘的土地。「現在當我看著一片溼地，」她說，「我看到的是一個家。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a student became interested in birdwatching through the black-faced spoonbill", "Why students should not go on school trips early in the morning", "How farmers in Tainan turned wetlands into farms and factories", "Why Korea is the best place in the world to watch birds"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文從佩珊第一次在七股看到黑面琵鷺寫起，介紹這種鳥的處境與覓食方式，再說明她加入賞鳥社團、學會耐心。(B) 她雖不想早起，但最後愛上賞鳥；(C) 溼地被開發只是第二段的背景；(D) 韓國只是部分黑面琵鷺飛來的地方，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did the number of black-faced spoonbills fall so low in the 1980s?", "options": ["Too many people caught them for food.", "Winters in East Asia became much colder.", "Their wetlands were turned into farms, factories and roads.", "Birdwatchers often used camera flashes and frightened them."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Their wetlands were being turned into farms, factories and roads, and the species seemed close to disappearing. 溼地被開發成農田、工廠和道路，使牠們幾乎消失。(A)(B) 文中未提及；(D) 閃光燈是第四段的賞鳥規則，並非數量減少的原因，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why can spoonbills find food in muddy water?", "options": ["They have very sharp eyes that can see through mud.", "They feed by feeling for food with their bills.", "They wait for the water to become clear in the daytime.", "They follow other birds that can find fish easily."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Because they feed by touch, they can find food in muddy water and even in the dark. 牠們把喙放在水中左右掃動，靠觸覺感受小魚小蝦。(A) 第三段明說牠們 instead of looking for food with their eyes；(C) 牠們常在夜間覓食，白天多在休息；(D) 文中未提及，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would a careful birdwatcher do?", "options": ["Wear a bright red jacket so that friends can find him easily", "Throw some bread into the water to bring the birds closer", "Use a camera flash to take clearer photos of the birds", "Stay on the path and watch the birds through binoculars"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：They should stay on the paths and use binoculars instead of walking closer. 待在步道上用望遠鏡觀察才是正確做法。(A) 規則是穿暗色系衣服；(B) 不可餵食野鳥；(C) 不可使用閃光燈，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What does Pei-shan mean when she says, \"I see a home\"?", "options": ["She wants to build a house near the wetland.", "She now understands that wetlands are where many animals live.", "She thinks wetlands look like the village she grew up in.", "She feels more comfortable in a wetland than in her own home."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說她以前認為溼地只是 empty, muddy land（空蕩蕩、泥濘的土地），現在卻看到「一個家」，可推知她明白了溼地是黑面琵鷺等野生動物生活的地方。(A)(C)(D) 都把 home 誤解為人的住所，與上下文不符，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a35", "title": "Twenty Moves or Fewer: My Year with the Rubik's Cube", "title_zh": "二十步以內：我與魔術方塊的一年", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Life", "Science"], "passage": "I got my first Rubik's Cube as a birthday present when I was fifteen. For a week, I turned it in every direction. I could finish one side, but whenever I tried to fix the second side, the first one fell apart. At the end of the week, I put the cube in a drawer and decided that it was a toy for geniuses only.\n\nMonths later, I learned that even its inventor had trouble at first. Ernő Rubik, a Hungarian professor of architecture, created the cube in 1974 to help his students understand how objects move in three dimensions. After he mixed it up for the first time, it took him about a month to solve it. The cube went on sale around the world in 1980 and became one of the best-selling toys in history.\n\nWhat surprised me most was the math behind it. A standard cube can be mixed into more than 43 quintillion different positions. That is 43 followed by eighteen zeros. Yet in 2010, a team of researchers used powerful computers to prove that every position can be solved in twenty moves or fewer, and that some positions need the full twenty. Cube fans call this number \"God's Number.\"\n\nOf course, I am not a computer. I took the cube out of the drawer again and watched online videos about the \"beginner's method.\" Instead of solving the whole cube at once, you solve it layer by layer, from the bottom to the top. For each step, you learn a short, fixed series of moves called an algorithm. At first I wrote the algorithms on cards and carried them everywhere. After two weeks, I could solve the cube in four minutes. Then my hands started to remember the moves, and my time dropped below one minute.\n\nToday my best time is 38 seconds. That is slow compared with top \"speedcubers,\" who can finish in under five seconds at official competitions, but I am proud of it. More importantly, the cube taught me something about learning. When a problem looks impossible, it often helps to break it into smaller steps and practice each one until it feels easy.", "passage_zh": "我十五歲生日時收到了人生第一顆魔術方塊。整整一個星期，我把它往各個方向轉來轉去。我可以完成一面，但每當我想拼好第二面時，第一面就又被打亂了。那個星期結束時，我把方塊收進抽屜，認定它是只有天才才能玩的玩具。\n\n幾個月後，我才知道就連它的發明者一開始也碰到了困難。匈牙利建築學教授魯比克‧艾爾諾在 1974 年發明了這個方塊，目的是幫助學生理解物體在三度空間中如何移動。他第一次把方塊轉亂之後，花了大約一個月才把它還原。1980 年，魔術方塊開始在全世界販售，成為史上最暢銷的玩具之一。\n\n最讓我驚訝的是它背後的數學。一顆標準的魔術方塊可以被轉成超過 4,300 京種不同的排列，也就是 43 後面再加上十八個零。然而在 2010 年，一群研究人員利用強大的電腦證明了：任何一種排列都能在二十步以內還原，而且有些排列確實需要整整二十步。方塊迷把這個數字稱為「上帝之數」。\n\n當然，我不是電腦。我又把方塊從抽屜裡拿出來，觀看介紹「初學者解法」的網路影片。這個方法不是一次解開整顆方塊，而是從底部到頂部，一層一層地解。每一個步驟，你都要學會一組簡短、固定的轉動順序，這叫做「公式」（algorithm）。一開始，我把公式寫在卡片上，走到哪裡都帶著。兩個星期後，我能在四分鐘內把方塊還原。後來我的雙手開始記住這些動作，我的時間也降到了一分鐘以內。\n\n如今我的最佳紀錄是 38 秒。和頂尖的「速解玩家」相比，這樣的速度很慢，他們在正式比賽中可以在五秒內完成，但我仍然以此為傲。更重要的是，魔術方塊教會了我一些關於學習的道理。當一個問題看起來不可能解決時，把它拆成幾個較小的步驟，再把每一步練習到駕輕就熟，往往會很有幫助。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The life story of a Hungarian professor of architecture", "How computers are used to win speedcubing competitions", "How the writer learned to solve the Rubik's Cube and what it taught the writer", "Why the Rubik's Cube is too difficult for most teenagers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文從作者十五歲時放棄方塊寫起，接著介紹方塊的由來與數學，再描述作者如何用初學者解法學會還原，最後說明從中學到的學習方法。(A) 魯比克只出現在第二段；(B) 電腦是用來證明上帝之數，不是用來比賽；(D) 與作者最後成功解開的經驗相反，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Ernő Rubik create the cube?", "options": ["To help his students understand how objects move in three dimensions", "To sell a new toy to children around the world", "To test which of his students were geniuses", "To prove that any puzzle can be solved in twenty moves"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：created the cube in 1974 to help his students understand how objects move in three dimensions. 他發明方塊是為了幫助學生理解物體在三度空間中的移動。(B) 1980 年才在全世界販售，並非發明的目的；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 二十步是 2010 年研究人員證明的，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is \"God's Number\"?", "options": ["The total number of positions a cube can be mixed into", "The largest number of moves needed to solve any position of the cube", "The writer's best time for solving the cube", "The number of algorithms in the beginner's method"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：every position can be solved in twenty moves or fewer, and that some positions need the full twenty. Cube fans call this number \"God's Number.\" 任何排列最多只要二十步就能還原，而且有些排列正好需要二十步，所以二十就是還原任何排列「最多需要的步數」，也就是上帝之數。(A) 是 43 quintillion 種排列；(C) 作者的紀錄是 38 秒；(D) 文中沒有說初學者解法有幾組公式，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 4, the word \"algorithm\" refers to ______.", "options": ["a video that teaches beginners", "a card that the writer carried everywhere", "one layer of the cube", "a fixed series of moves for one step"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：For each step, you learn a short, fixed series of moves called an algorithm. algorithm 在這裡指解某一步時要用的一組固定轉動順序（公式）。(A) 影片是學習的管道；(B) 卡片是作者用來寫公式的工具；(C) layer 是方塊的「一層」，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What lesson does the writer most likely want readers to learn?", "options": ["Only people who are good at math can enjoy puzzles.", "Computers will always solve problems better than people.", "A hard problem becomes easier when you divide it into small steps and practice.", "It is better to give up early when something looks impossible."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段最後一句：When a problem looks impossible, it often helps to break it into smaller steps and practice each one until it feels easy. 作者想傳達：把難題拆成小步驟並反覆練習，就會變得容易。(A)(B) 文中沒有這樣的主張；(D) 作者先放棄後又重新學習並成功，與此相反，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a36", "title": "Thin Air on Jade Mountain", "title_zh": "玉山上的稀薄空氣", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "Travel"], "passage": "At 3,952 meters, Jade Mountain, or Yushan, is the highest mountain in Taiwan, even higher than Japan's Mount Fuji. Every year, thousands of hikers climb it to watch the sun rise over a sea of clouds. Last spring, seventeen-year-old Hsin-yi and her father were two of them. They had trained for months by climbing stairs and hiking smaller mountains near Taipei. Still, the mountain had a surprise for them.\n\nThe most popular route starts at Tataka, at about 2,600 meters. From there, hikers walk 8.5 kilometers to Paiyun Lodge, at 3,402 meters, and spend the night. At around two or three in the morning, they get up and climb the last 2.4 kilometers in the dark to reach the top before sunrise.\n\nOn the first afternoon, Hsin-yi felt fine. But that evening at the lodge, she developed a terrible headache. She lost her appetite and could not sleep. Her father recognized the signs at once: altitude sickness. As people climb higher, the air gets thinner, so each breath brings in less oxygen, and the body needs time to adjust. Above about 2,500 meters, some people, even young and fit ones, get headaches, feel sick to their stomachs or have trouble sleeping. Being strong does not protect you; what matters most is how fast you go up.\n\nMountain guides give simple advice. Walk slowly, even if you feel strong. Drink plenty of water and rest when you need to. Tell your group as soon as you feel unwell instead of hiding it. Most importantly, if the symptoms get worse (for example, if a headache does not go away after rest or the person becomes confused), the only real cure is to go down to a lower altitude. No summit is worth risking your life for.\n\nHsin-yi drank some warm water, rested and told the lodge staff how she felt. By two in the morning, her headache had disappeared, so she set off slowly toward the peak with her father. They reached the summit just as the sky turned orange. \"I almost gave up,\" she said later. \"But I learned that the mountain decides how fast you can go, not you.\"", "passage_zh": "玉山海拔 3,952 公尺，是台灣最高的山，甚至比日本的富士山還要高。每年都有成千上萬的登山客攀登玉山，為的是看太陽從雲海中升起。去年春天，十七歲的欣怡和她的爸爸也是其中兩位。他們花了好幾個月的時間，靠爬樓梯和攀登台北附近較小的山來鍛鍊體能。即使如此，這座山還是給了他們一個意外。\n\n最熱門的路線從海拔約 2,600 公尺的塔塔加出發。登山客從那裡步行 8.5 公里，到海拔 3,402 公尺的排雲山莊過夜。大約凌晨兩三點，他們起床，在黑暗中走完最後的 2.4 公里，趕在日出前登頂。\n\n第一天下午，欣怡感覺還好。但那天晚上在山莊裡，她開始劇烈頭痛。她沒有食慾，也睡不著。她的爸爸立刻認出這些徵兆：高山症。人爬得越高，空氣就越稀薄，每一次呼吸吸進的氧氣也越少，而身體需要時間適應。在大約 2,500 公尺以上，有些人──即使是年輕、體能好的人──會頭痛、反胃或難以入睡。身體強壯並不能保護你；最重要的是你上升的速度有多快。\n\n登山嚮導的建議很簡單。就算覺得自己體力很好，也要慢慢走。多喝水，需要時就休息。一感到不舒服就要告訴隊友，不要隱瞞。最重要的是，如果症狀變得更嚴重，例如休息後頭痛仍然沒有消失，或是意識變得混亂，唯一真正的解方就是下撤到較低的海拔。沒有任何一座山頂值得你拿生命去冒險。\n\n欣怡喝了一些溫水、休息，並告訴山莊工作人員她的狀況。到了凌晨兩點，她的頭痛已經消失了，於是她和爸爸一起慢慢地朝山頂出發。他們抵達山頂時，天空正好染成一片橘紅。「我差點就放棄了，」她事後說，「但我學到了：能走多快，是由山決定的，不是由你決定。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To describe a climb up Jade Mountain and explain how to deal with altitude sickness", "To compare Jade Mountain with Mount Fuji in Japan", "To advertise mountain-climbing classes near Taipei", "To explain why the sky turns orange at sunrise"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以欣怡和爸爸攀登玉山的經過為主軸，第三段說明高山症的原因與症狀，第四段提出預防與處理的建議。(B) 富士山只在第一段比較高度時提到；(C) 台北附近的山只是欣怡事前訓練的地點，文中沒有推銷課程；(D) 天空變橘紅只是第五段登頂時的描寫，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "How far do hikers walk from Paiyun Lodge to the summit of Jade Mountain?", "options": ["8.5 kilometers", "3.4 kilometers", "2.4 kilometers", "10.9 kilometers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：they get up and climb the last 2.4 kilometers in the dark to reach the top. 從排雲山莊到山頂是最後的 2.4 公里。(A) 8.5 公里是塔塔加到排雲山莊的距離；(B) 3,402 是山莊的海拔高度（公尺），不是距離；(D) 10.9 公里是塔塔加到山頂的總距離（8.5＋2.4），故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a sign of altitude sickness mentioned in the passage?", "options": ["A sore throat", "Pain in the knees", "A high fever", "A headache and a loss of appetite"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：she developed a terrible headache. She lost her appetite and could not sleep. Her father recognized the signs at once: altitude sickness. 頭痛和沒有食慾都是高山症的徵兆。(A)(B)(C) 喉嚨痛、膝蓋痛、發高燒文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "At Paiyun Lodge, a hiker's headache does not go away after a long rest, and he begins to feel confused. What should he do?", "options": ["Climb faster so that he can reach the summit sooner", "Go down to a lower altitude as soon as possible", "Keep quiet so that his group will not worry", "Drink coffee and continue toward the peak"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：if a headache does not go away after rest or the person becomes confused, the only real cure is to go down to a lower altitude. 症狀惡化時唯一的解方是下撤到較低海拔。(A)(D) 繼續往上只會更危險；(C) 第四段明說要 tell your group... instead of hiding it，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does Hsin-yi mean when she says, \"the mountain decides how fast you can go, not you\"?", "options": ["Hikers must follow the speed that guides set for every group.", "The trail on Jade Mountain is too narrow for hikers to walk quickly.", "Climbers should adjust their speed to how their bodies react to the altitude.", "Strong hikers should always walk in front of weaker ones."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段指出 Being strong does not protect you; what matters most is how fast you go up，欣怡雖然事前認真訓練仍得了高山症，必須休息、放慢腳步才能登頂。可推知她的意思是：上山的速度要配合身體對高海拔的反應，而不是照自己的計畫或體力。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a37", "title": "The Spinning Bell: Our School's Diabolo Team", "title_zh": "旋轉的鈴：我們學校的扯鈴隊", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Sports"], "passage": "Every afternoon at four, the basketball court behind Hsing-hua Senior High School fills with a low humming sound. It comes from twenty diabolos spinning fast on strings tied between pairs of sticks. The school's diabolo team is practicing for its biggest show of the year: the school's 60th anniversary celebration, in front of more than a thousand students, parents and guests.\n\nThe diabolo has been played in China for hundreds of years. Traditional ones were made of bamboo, with small holes cut in the sides. As the toy spun, air rushed through the holes and made a whistling sound, which is why it was often compared to a bell. In the early 1900s, a French engineer made a new version and gave it the name \"diabolo.\" It quickly became a craze in Europe, where adults and children played it in parks. Today most diabolos are made of plastic, which makes them lighter and stronger.\n\nHow does a diabolo stay balanced on a thin string? The secret is spinning. A fast-spinning object tends to keep pointing in the same direction, which is also why a spinning top can stand on its tip. That is why players keep pulling the string to make the diabolo spin faster. When it slows down, it begins to tilt, and the player must correct it by moving the sticks forward or backward.\n\nThe team's coach, Mr. Kao, says the hobby teaches more than balance. \"Every new trick means dropping the diabolo a hundred times,\" he says. \"Students learn that failing is part of learning.\" Team captain Yu-chen agrees. When she joined in her first year, she could barely keep the diabolo spinning for ten seconds. Now she can throw it ten meters into the air, turn around once and catch it on the string.\n\nThe night before the show, Yu-chen admits she is nervous. \"If I drop it in front of everyone, I'll want to disappear,\" she says with a laugh. Then she picks up her sticks again. After all, she has dropped the diabolo thousands of times, and each time, she has picked it up.", "passage_zh": "每天下午四點，興華高中後方的籃球場就會響起一陣低沉的嗡嗡聲。這聲音來自二十顆扯鈴，它們在兩根棍子之間的繩子上快速旋轉。學校的扯鈴隊正在為一年中最盛大的表演練習：學校的六十週年校慶，屆時台下將有超過一千名學生、家長和來賓。\n\n扯鈴在中國已經流傳了好幾百年。傳統的扯鈴是用竹子做的，側邊開了小孔。扯鈴旋轉時，空氣從小孔衝出，發出像哨子般的聲響，所以常被比喻成鈴鐺。二十世紀初，一位法國工程師做出了新版本，並把它命名為「diabolo」。它很快就在歐洲風靡一時，大人和小孩都在公園裡玩。如今大多數的扯鈴是塑膠製的，因此更輕也更堅固。\n\n扯鈴是怎麼在細細的繩子上保持平衡的呢？祕訣就在於旋轉。快速旋轉的物體會傾向維持指向同一個方向，這也是陀螺能用尖端立起來的原因。所以玩家會不斷拉動繩子，讓扯鈴轉得更快。當它轉速變慢時，就會開始傾斜，玩家必須把棍子往前或往後移動來修正。\n\n扯鈴隊的高教練說，這項嗜好教會學生的不只是平衡。「每學一個新招式，都要讓扯鈴掉下來上百次，」他說，「學生會學到，失敗是學習的一部分。」隊長宇辰也同意這一點。她高一剛入隊時，幾乎沒辦法讓扯鈴持續轉上十秒鐘。現在她能把扯鈴拋到十公尺高，原地轉一圈，再用繩子接住。\n\n表演前一晚，宇辰承認她很緊張。「如果我在大家面前掉了扯鈴，我會想找個地洞鑽進去，」她笑著說。接著，她又拿起了扯鈴棍。畢竟，她已經讓扯鈴掉下來好幾千次了，而每一次，她都把它撿了起來。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to build a diabolo out of bamboo at home", "Why French engineers changed traditional Chinese toys", "Why students should not perform in front of large crowds", "A school diabolo team, the history of the toy and how it works"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段介紹興華高中扯鈴隊準備校慶表演，第二段說明扯鈴的歷史，第三段解釋它保持平衡的原理，第四、五段描寫教練和隊長的練習與心情。(A) 文中沒有教人自製扯鈴；(B) 法國工程師只是第二段的一個細節；(C) 與文意無關，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did traditional diabolos make a whistling sound?", "options": ["They had small bells tied inside them.", "Air rushed through holes in their sides as they spun.", "Players blew into them while playing.", "Their strings were made of thin metal."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：As the toy spun, air rushed through the holes and made a whistling sound. 傳統竹製扯鈴側邊有小孔，旋轉時空氣從孔中衝出而發出聲音。(A) 「像鈴鐺」只是比喻，裡面並沒有鈴鐺；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what keeps a diabolo balanced on the string?", "options": ["Spinning fast", "Being made of plastic", "Using a very thin string", "Holding the sticks very still"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：How does a diabolo stay balanced on a thin string? The secret is spinning. 讓扯鈴保持平衡的祕訣是快速旋轉。(B) 塑膠材質只讓它更輕、更堅固（第二段）；(C) 繩子細正是平衡困難的原因；(D) 第三段說扯鈴傾斜時玩家要前後移動棍子修正，並非靜止不動，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Yu-chen?", "options": ["She has never dropped the diabolo during practice.", "She joined the team only a few weeks ago.", "She has improved greatly through a lot of practice.", "She is the coach of the school's diabolo team."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說宇辰剛入隊時幾乎無法讓扯鈴轉上十秒，現在卻能拋到十公尺高再接住；第五段也提到她已經掉過好幾千次扯鈴。可推知她靠大量練習進步很多。(A) 與「掉了好幾千次」相反；(B) 她是高一入隊，現在已是隊長；(D) 教練是高教練，宇辰是隊長，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"barely\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["easily", "hardly", "happily", "often"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段 she could barely keep the diabolo spinning for ten seconds，這句描述她剛入隊時程度很差，與後面「現在能拋到十公尺高」形成對比，barely 意思是「幾乎不能、勉強」，與 hardly 最接近。(A) easily 意思相反；(C)(D) 不合文意，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a38", "title": "Wishes in the Night Sky: The Sky Lanterns of Pingxi", "title_zh": "夜空中的心願：平溪天燈", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Environment"], "passage": "Every year on the night of the Lantern Festival, the fifteenth day of the first month of the lunar calendar, thousands of glowing lanterns rise slowly into the dark sky over Pingxi, a small mountain district in New Taipei City. Visitors from Taiwan and abroad come here to watch the show and to send up wishes of their own.\n\nThe tradition goes back more than a hundred years. In the past, Pingxi was a poor area surrounded by mountains, and groups of robbers sometimes attacked the villages. When that happened, many villagers ran away to hide in the hills. After the danger had passed, the people who stayed behind would release lanterns into the sky. The lanterns told the families in the hills that it was safe to come home. For this reason, sky lanterns are sometimes called \"peace lanterns.\"\n\nA sky lantern works like a small hot-air balloon. It is made of thin paper on a light frame, with a piece of paper soaked in oil tied to the bottom. When the paper is lit, the air inside the lantern becomes hot. Hot air is lighter than the cooler air around it, so the lantern begins to rise. Before letting it go, visitors use brushes to write their wishes on the four sides, such as \"pass the exam,\" \"good health\" or \"a happy family.\"\n\nToday, the most popular place to send up a lantern is Shifen Old Street. Here, the tracks of the Pingxi Line run right through the middle of the street, with shops on both sides. Between trains, visitors stand on the tracks to light their lanterns. When a train is coming, shopkeepers shout a warning, and everyone quickly steps aside.\n\nHowever, not everyone is happy about the tradition. What goes up must come down, and burned-out lanterns often land in forests, rivers and farms. Some people worry that they could start fires or harm wild animals. To deal with the problem, the local government has offered small rewards to people who collect fallen lanterns, and some shops now sell lanterns made of materials that break down more easily in nature. In this way, Pingxi hopes to keep its tradition while protecting nature.", "passage_zh": "每年元宵節晚上，也就是農曆正月十五日，成千上萬盞發光的天燈會緩緩升上平溪的夜空。平溪是新北市一個位於山區的小行政區。來自台灣各地和國外的遊客都會來到這裡觀賞這場盛會，也親手放飛自己的心願。\n\n這項傳統可以追溯到一百多年前。過去，平溪是一個四面環山的貧窮地區，村莊不時會遭到成群的盜匪襲擊。每當這種情況發生，許多村民就會逃到山裡躲藏。等危險過去後，留在村裡的人就會把燈放上天空，用這些燈告訴躲在山裡的家人：現在可以安全回家了。因此，天燈有時被稱為「平安燈」。\n\n天燈的原理就像一個小型熱氣球。它是用薄紙糊在輕巧的骨架上做成的，底部綁著一張浸過油的紙。紙點燃後，燈內的空氣就會變熱。熱空氣比周圍較冷的空氣輕，所以天燈便開始上升。放手之前，遊客會用毛筆在天燈的四面寫下心願，例如「考試順利」、「身體健康」或「家庭幸福」。\n\n如今，最熱門的放天燈地點是十分老街。在這裡，平溪線的鐵軌就從街道正中央穿過，兩旁都是商店。趁兩班火車之間的空檔，遊客會站在鐵軌上點燃天燈。火車快來的時候，店家會大聲提醒，大家便趕緊退到一旁。\n\n然而，並不是每個人都喜歡這項傳統。有升就有落，燒完的天燈常常掉進森林、河流和農田裡。有些人擔心天燈可能引發火災或傷害野生動物。為了解決這個問題，當地政府提供小獎勵給撿拾掉落天燈的人，有些店家也開始販售用在大自然中較容易分解的材料做成的天燈。平溪希望藉由這些方法，既能保留這項傳統，也能保護大自然。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why robbers often attacked villages in Pingxi long ago", "The history, science and problems of Pingxi's sky lanterns", "How the Pingxi Line was built through Shifen Old Street", "Why visitors should stop sending up sky lanterns"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文依序介紹平溪天燈的由來（第二段）、飛上天的原理（第三段）、熱門地點（第四段），以及帶來的環境問題與解決方法（第五段）。(A) 盜匪只是第二段說明由來的背景；(C) 第四段只提到鐵軌穿過老街，並未說明鐵路如何興建；(D) 第五段說平溪希望保留傳統並保護環境，並沒有主張停止放天燈。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did people in Pingxi first send sky lanterns into the sky?", "options": ["To celebrate a good harvest of rice", "To frighten the robbers away from the village", "To ask the gods to help them pass exams", "To tell villagers hiding in the hills that it was safe to return"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：After the danger had passed, the people who stayed behind would release lanterns... The lanterns told the families in the hills that it was safe to come home. 天燈最初是用來通知躲在山裡的家人可以平安回家。(A) 文中未提及；(B) 天燈是在危險過去後才放，並非用來嚇走盜匪；(C) 祈求考試順利是現代遊客寫的心願（第三段），不是天燈的由來。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"release\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["let go of", "pick up", "paint", "repair"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段 the people who stayed behind would release lanterns into the sky，是把天燈「放」上天空；第三段 Before letting it go 描述的也是同一個動作，所以 release 與 let go of（放開、放走）意思最接近。(B) 撿起、(C) 彩繪、(D) 修理都不符合文意。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Based on paragraph 3, what would most likely happen if the air inside a sky lantern did not get hot?", "options": ["It would rise even faster.", "It would burn more brightly.", "It would not be able to rise.", "It would carry more wishes."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說明 Hot air is lighter than the cooler air around it, so the lantern begins to rise. 天燈是靠燈內空氣變熱、比外面的空氣輕才能上升，可推知空氣若沒有變熱，天燈就飛不起來。(A) 與原理相反；(B)(D) 都與第三段的原理無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the last paragraph, what is one way people are dealing with the problems caused by sky lanterns?", "options": ["Allowing lanterns to be sent up only from the train tracks", "Offering small rewards to people who collect fallen lanterns", "Letting people send up lanterns only on the night of the Lantern Festival", "Asking visitors to write shorter wishes on their lanterns"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：the local government has offered small rewards to people who collect fallen lanterns，另外也有店家販售較容易分解的天燈。(A) 第四段只說十分老街的遊客在鐵軌上放天燈，並不是規定；(C)(D) 文中均未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a39", "title": "Nine Days on Foot: Walking with Mazu", "title_zh": "徒步九天：與媽祖同行", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Society"], "passage": "Every spring, a goddess goes on a long walk through central Taiwan, and hundreds of thousands of people follow her. This is the Dajia Mazu Pilgrimage, a religious journey and one of the biggest festivals in Taiwan. For nine days and eight nights, a statue of Mazu rides in a sedan chair, a seat on poles carried by several people, from Jenn Lann Temple in Dajia, Taichung, to a temple in Chiayi County and back again. The whole route is more than 300 kilometers long and passes through four cities and counties.\n\nMazu is a goddess of the sea. According to legend, she was a young woman named Lin Mo who lived on an island off the coast of China about a thousand years ago and helped save fishermen in storms. Because early Chinese settlers had to cross the dangerous Taiwan Strait to reach Taiwan, many of them prayed to Mazu for a safe trip. Today, there are hundreds of Mazu temples across the island.\n\nThe dates of the pilgrimage change every year. On the night of the Lantern Festival, temple leaders ask Mazu to choose them by throwing moon blocks, two curved wooden pieces that give a \"yes\" or \"no\" answer depending on how they land. Once the dates are announced, believers start making plans to join.\n\nLast spring, seventeen-year-old Hsu Yi-ting walked with Mazu for two days. \"My feet hurt after the first ten kilometers,\" she said, \"but I couldn't believe how kind people were.\" Along the road, local families and shops handed out free water, rice balls and snacks, and some volunteers even offered foot massages. Yi-ting also saw people kneel in a long line on the road so that Mazu's sedan chair could pass over them. They believe this brings blessings and good health.\n\nThe pilgrimage has also changed with the times. These days, Mazu's location is tracked by GPS and shown online, so followers can check on their phones where she is and when she will reach their town. For Yi-ting, though, technology was not the most important part. \"Walking with so many strangers made me feel that we were all one big family,\" she said. \"After I graduate, I want to walk the whole way.\"", "passage_zh": "每年春天，都有一位女神在中台灣展開一趟漫長的步行旅程，而且有數十萬人跟隨著她。這就是大甲媽祖遶境進香，它是一趟宗教之旅，也是台灣規模最大的節慶之一。在九天八夜之中，媽祖神像坐在神轎上——也就是由好幾個人用長桿抬著的座椅——從台中大甲的鎮瀾宮出發，前往嘉義縣的一座廟宇，再折返回來。全程超過三百公里，途經四個縣市。\n\n媽祖是海上的女神。根據傳說，她本名林默，是大約一千年前住在中國沿海一座島上的年輕女子，曾在暴風雨中幫忙拯救漁民。由於早期的中國移民必須橫渡危險的台灣海峽才能抵達台灣，所以許多人會向媽祖祈求一路平安。如今，全台各地有數百座媽祖廟。\n\n遶境進香的日期每年都不同。在元宵節晚上，廟方負責人會擲筊請媽祖決定日期。筊是兩塊彎月形的木片，依照落地的方式來表示「是」或「否」。日期一公布，信徒們就開始計畫參加。\n\n去年春天，十七歲的徐以婷跟著媽祖走了兩天。「才走了十公里我的腳就開始痛了，」她說，「但我不敢相信大家竟然這麼親切。」沿路上，當地的住家和商店免費發送開水、飯糰和點心，有些志工甚至提供腳底按摩。以婷還看到人們在路上跪成一長排，讓媽祖的神轎從他們上方經過。他們相信這樣做能帶來保佑和健康。\n\n遶境進香也隨著時代改變。現在，媽祖的位置會以 GPS 追蹤並公布在網路上，信徒可以用手機查看她的位置，以及什麼時候會抵達自己的城鎮。不過對以婷來說，科技並不是最重要的部分。「跟這麼多陌生人一起走路，讓我覺得大家就像一個大家庭，」她說，「畢業以後，我想要走完全程。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The life story of a young woman named Lin Mo", "How temples use GPS to keep track of their visitors", "A long religious walk in honor of Mazu and the people who join it", "Why moon blocks are used in Taiwanese temples"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹大甲媽祖遶境進香：路線與天數（第一段）、媽祖的由來（第二段）、日期的決定方式（第三段），以及參加者以婷的經驗（第四、五段）。(A) 林默只在第二段簡短提及；(B) GPS 追蹤的是媽祖的位置，不是遊客，而且只是第五段的細節；(D) 擲筊只是第三段的細節。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How long does the Dajia Mazu Pilgrimage last?", "options": ["Two days", "Eight days", "Ten days", "Nine days"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：For nine days and eight nights, a statue of Mazu rides in a sedan chair... 遶境進香共九天八夜。(A) 兩天是以婷參加的天數（第四段）；(B)「八」是夜晚的數目；(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did many early Chinese settlers pray to Mazu?", "options": ["They had to cross a dangerous sea to get to Taiwan.", "They wanted her to choose the dates of the pilgrimage.", "They hoped she would help them find jobs in Taiwan.", "They believed she could stop robbers from attacking them."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Because early Chinese settlers had to cross the dangerous Taiwan Strait to reach Taiwan, many of them prayed to Mazu for a safe trip. 早期移民要橫渡危險的台灣海峽，所以向海上女神祈求平安。(B) 擲筊選日期是現代遶境的儀式（第三段）；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 3, what does \"they\" in \"depending on how they land\" refer to?", "options": ["The temple leaders", "The moon blocks", "The believers", "The dates"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段 throwing moon blocks, two curved wooden pieces that give a \"yes\" or \"no\" answer depending on how they land，會「落地」並依落地方式表示是或否的，是被擲出的筊，因此 they 指 moon blocks。(A) 廟方負責人是擲筊的人；(C) 信徒在下一句才出現；(D) 日期不會落地。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Yi-ting from the last two paragraphs?", "options": ["She has already walked the whole route once.", "She found the walk easy and not tiring at all.", "She was touched by people's kindness and hopes to join again.", "She thinks the GPS was the best part of the trip."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段她說 I couldn't believe how kind people were，第五段又說和陌生人同行讓她覺得 we were all one big family，並表示 After I graduate, I want to walk the whole way，可推知她被沿途的善意感動，並希望再次參加。(A) 她這次只走了兩天；(B) 她說走了十公里腳就痛了；(D) 第五段說 technology was not the most important part。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a40", "title": "Back on Track: The Alishan Forest Railway", "title_zh": "重返軌道：阿里山林業鐵路", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "History"], "passage": "In July 2024, a train full of passengers pulled into Alishan Station, high in the mountains of Chiayi County, and the people waiting there cheered loudly. It was the first time in fifteen years that a train had made the whole trip from Chiayi City up to Alishan. For many people in Taiwan, it felt as if an old friend had finally come home.\n\nThe railway was built during the period of Japanese rule, and trains began running on it in the early 1910s. At first, its main job was not to carry tourists but to bring timber down from the mountains. Alishan was covered with huge, ancient cypress trees, and their strong, sweet-smelling wood was very valuable for building. For decades, trains carried logs down the mountain, and large areas of forest were cut down. As the old forests disappeared, the railway slowly became a line for visitors instead.\n\nTrain lovers around the world know this railway well. In about 70 kilometers, it climbs from around 30 meters above sea level to more than 2,000 meters. Since a train cannot climb a very steep slope directly, the builders used clever designs. At one mountain, the track goes around and around like a spring, circling it three times as it rises. In other places, the train climbs in a Z shape: it moves forward, stops, and then goes backward up the next part of the track. Through the windows, passengers can watch the forest change from bamboo and other warm-weather plants to cool, misty forests of tall trees.\n\nIn August 2009, Typhoon Morakot brought record amounts of rain to southern Taiwan. Landslides destroyed bridges and tunnels and buried parts of the track. Repairs were slow and difficult because the mountains are steep and earthquakes and storms happen often. Section by section, workers rebuilt the line until the whole railway could finally open again.\n\nToday, visitors ride the train to watch the sunrise over a \"sea of clouds,\" walk among giant trees that are more than a thousand years old, and taste the famous high-mountain tea grown nearby. On weekends and holidays, seats often sell out quickly, so travelers are advised to book their tickets early.", "passage_zh": "2024 年 7 月，一列載滿乘客的火車駛進位於嘉義縣高山上的阿里山車站，在那裡等候的人們大聲歡呼。這是十五年來第一次有火車從嘉義市一路開上阿里山，跑完全程。對許多台灣人來說，這種感覺就像一位老朋友終於回家了。\n\n這條鐵路建於日治時期，在 1910 年代初期開始通車。起初，它的主要任務不是載運觀光客，而是把木材從山上運下來。當時阿里山上長滿了巨大的古老檜木，這些木材堅固又帶有香氣，是非常珍貴的建材。數十年間，火車不斷把原木運下山，大片森林也因此遭到砍伐。隨著古老的森林逐漸消失，這條鐵路也慢慢轉變成載運遊客的路線。\n\n世界各地的鐵道迷都很熟悉這條鐵路。在大約七十公里的路程中，它從海拔約三十公尺爬升到兩千多公尺。由於火車無法直接爬上非常陡的坡，建造者採用了巧妙的設計。在某座山上，鐵軌像彈簧一樣一圈又一圈地環繞著山，繞了三圈慢慢往上爬。在其他路段，火車則以 Z 字形爬升：先往前開，停下來，再倒退開上下一段鐵軌。乘客可以從車窗看著外面的森林，從竹林和其他喜歡溫暖氣候的植物，逐漸變成涼爽、雲霧繚繞的高大森林。\n\n2009 年 8 月，莫拉克颱風為南台灣帶來破紀錄的雨量。山崩摧毀了橋梁和隧道，也掩埋了部分鐵軌。由於山勢陡峭，地震和暴風雨又經常發生，修復工程既緩慢又艱難。工人們一段一段地重建鐵路，直到全線終於能夠重新通車。\n\n如今，遊客搭火車上山，觀賞「雲海」上的日出、漫步在樹齡超過一千年的巨木之間，並品嚐附近種植的知名高山茶。週末和假日的座位常常很快就賣完，因此建議旅客及早訂票。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history, design and return of a famous mountain railway", "How high-mountain tea is grown in Alishan", "Why Typhoon Morakot was the worst storm in Taiwan's history", "The daily life of workers who cut down trees in Alishan"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹阿里山林業鐵路：2024 年全線恢復通車（第一段）、興建的歷史（第二段）、爬山的特殊設計（第三段）、颱風損毀與修復（第四段）以及今日的觀光（第五段）。(B) 高山茶只在第五段順帶提到；(C) 文中只說莫拉克颱風帶來破紀錄的雨量，沒說它是史上最嚴重的風災；(D) 文中沒有描述伐木工人的生活。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What was the railway's main purpose when it was first built?", "options": ["To take tourists to see the sunrise", "To carry soldiers into the mountains", "To bring tea leaves to Chiayi City", "To carry wood down from the mountains"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：At first, its main job was not to carry tourists but to bring timber down from the mountains. 起初主要是把木材運下山。(A) 載觀光客是後來才有的用途；(B)(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"timber\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["fresh fruit", "wood used for building", "heavy rain", "wild animals"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段接著說阿里山檜木 their strong, sweet-smelling wood was very valuable for building，而且火車把 logs（原木）運下山，可知 timber 指「木材」，即 wood used for building。(A)(C)(D) 都與上下文無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, how does the train climb the mountain in a Z shape?", "options": ["It circles the same mountain three times.", "It goes through a long tunnel under the mountain.", "It moves forward, stops, and then goes backward up the next part of the track.", "It is pulled up the slope by a strong rope."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：the train climbs in a Z shape: it moves forward, stops, and then goes backward up the next part of the track. (A) 繞山三圈是另一種像彈簧的「螺旋」設計，不是 Z 字形；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 4?", "options": ["The railway was repaired in only a few months.", "Earthquakes caused more damage to the railway than Typhoon Morakot.", "Workers decided to build a completely new railway somewhere else.", "The railway was closed for a long time because the damage was serious and hard to repair."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說莫拉克颱風引起的山崩沖毀橋梁、隧道並掩埋鐵軌，而且 Repairs were slow and difficult；再對照第一段「十五年來第一次」跑完全程，可推知鐵路因損壞嚴重、修復困難而停駛很久。(A) 與「十五年」不符；(B) 文中沒有比較；(C) 工人是 Section by section... rebuilt the line，並非另建新鐵路。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a41", "title": "Hearts in the Sea: Penghu's Stone Fish Traps", "title_zh": "海中的愛心：澎湖的石滬", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Nature"], "passage": "If you stand on a cliff on Qimei Island in Penghu at low tide, you will see one of the most photographed sights in Taiwan: two heart shapes made of stone walls, lying side by side in the shallow sea. This is the Twin Hearts Stone Weir. It looks like a romantic work of art, but it was actually built as a clever trap for catching fish.\n\nA stone weir is a low wall of stones built in the sea near the shore. The idea is simple. When the tide comes in, seawater covers the walls, and fish swim over them to look for food. When the tide goes out, the water flows away through the gaps between the stones, but the fish are trapped inside the walls. Fishermen can then walk in and catch them with nets or even by hand. No boats, no fuel and no bait are needed.\n\nPenghu has more than 500 stone weirs, one of the largest numbers in the world. They were built with two kinds of stone found on the islands: dark basalt, a hard rock formed from volcanic lava, and coral stone, which is light and full of small holes. Building a weir took a lot of work, so it was usually shared by several families. The families took turns collecting the fish, and they all worked together to repair the walls after storms.\n\nIn the past, a weir could catch large numbers of fish during a good season. However, as modern fishing boats caught more and more fish, fewer fish came near the shore. At the same time, many young people moved to big cities on the main island of Taiwan to find jobs. With no one to look after them, many weirs were slowly broken down by the waves.\n\nToday, local people are trying to save this part of their history. Older fishermen are teaching volunteers how to fit the stones together without cement, and some weirs have been rebuilt. Visitors can also join guided tours and walk inside a weir at low tide. If you plan to see the Twin Hearts, remember to check the tide times first. At high tide, the famous hearts disappear under the water.", "passage_zh": "如果你在退潮時站在澎湖七美島的懸崖上，就能看到台灣最常被拍攝的景點之一：兩個由石牆圍成的愛心，並排躺在淺淺的海水中。這就是雙心石滬。它看起來像一件浪漫的藝術品，但其實是一個用來捕魚的巧妙陷阱。\n\n石滬是建在海岸附近海中的低矮石牆。原理很簡單。漲潮時，海水淹過石牆，魚兒會游過牆來覓食。退潮時，海水從石頭之間的縫隙流走，魚卻被困在石牆裡面。漁民接著就可以走進去，用網子甚至徒手把魚抓起來。不需要船、不需要燃料，也不需要魚餌。\n\n澎湖有五百多座石滬，數量名列世界前茅。石滬是用島上的兩種石頭砌成的：一種是深色的玄武岩，是由火山熔岩形成的堅硬岩石；另一種是珊瑚石，重量輕，上面布滿小孔。蓋一座石滬需要花費很多功夫，所以石滬通常由好幾戶人家共有。這些家庭輪流收魚，暴風雨過後也一起合力修補石牆。\n\n過去，在漁獲好的季節，一座石滬可以捕到大量的魚。然而，隨著現代漁船捕撈的魚越來越多，靠近岸邊的魚就變少了。同時，許多年輕人搬到台灣本島的大城市找工作。由於沒有人照顧，許多石滬就慢慢被海浪沖毀了。\n\n如今，當地人正努力保存這段歷史。年長的漁民教導志工如何不用水泥就把石頭堆疊在一起，有些石滬已經重建完成。遊客也可以參加導覽行程，在退潮時走進石滬裡。如果你打算去看雙心石滬，記得先查好潮汐時間。漲潮時，著名的雙心就會消失在海面下。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Penghu is the best place in Taiwan to go fishing by boat.", "Stone weirs are an old and clever way of fishing that people are now trying to protect.", "Young people in Penghu would rather work as fishermen than move away.", "The Twin Hearts Stone Weir was built as a work of art for tourists."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文介紹石滬的捕魚原理（第二段）、建造與共同管理的方式（第三段）、沒落的原因（第四段），以及當地人如何保存修復（第五段）。(A) 石滬捕魚 No boats... are needed，不是坐船捕魚；(C) 第四段說許多年輕人搬到大城市找工作；(D) 第一段說它看起來像藝術品，但其實是捕魚的陷阱。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How does a stone weir catch fish?", "options": ["Fishermen put bait inside the walls to attract fish.", "Boats push the fish toward the stone walls.", "Fish are caught in nets hanging on the walls.", "Fish swim in at high tide and cannot get out when the water goes down."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：When the tide comes in... fish swim over them... When the tide goes out... the fish are trapped inside the walls. 魚在漲潮時游進來，退潮時被困住。(A)(B) 與 No boats, no fuel and no bait are needed 矛盾；(C) 網子是漁民走進去抓魚時用的，不是掛在牆上的。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"shallow\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["not deep", "very cold", "dirty", "far away"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段說退潮時可以看到石牆 lying side by side in the shallow sea，第二段也說漁民可以直接 walk in 走進石滬抓魚，可知那裡的海水很淺，shallow 即 not deep。(B)(C)(D) 文中均無相關線索。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why were many stone weirs broken down?", "options": ["The government told the families to stop using them.", "Tourists climbed on the walls to take photos.", "Fewer fish came near the shore and young people left, so no one took care of the weirs.", "Strong earthquakes destroyed most of the walls."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：fewer fish came near the shore... many young people moved to big cities... With no one to look after them, many weirs were slowly broken down by the waves. 魚變少、年輕人離開，石滬沒人照顧而被海浪沖毀。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Based on the passage, what should a visitor do in order to see the Twin Hearts clearly?", "options": ["Check the tide times and go when the tide is low", "Rent a boat and sail around Qimei Island", "Go at high tide when the water is deepest", "Join the volunteers who rebuild the walls"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：remember to check the tide times first. At high tide, the famous hearts disappear under the water. 第一段也說要在 low tide 時從懸崖上觀看，所以應先查潮汐，在退潮時前往。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 漲潮時雙心會消失在水面下；(D) 參加修復與能否看清雙心無關。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a42", "title": "A Town Shaped by Clay: Yingge", "title_zh": "陶土塑造的小鎮：鶯歌", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Arts", "Culture"], "passage": "When Chen Kai-wen's art class visited Yingge last spring, he expected an easy day. Their teacher had promised that everyone could make a bowl on a pottery wheel. \"How hard can it be?\" Kai-wen said. Twenty minutes later, his third bowl had fallen apart, and his hands and shirt were covered in wet clay.\n\nYingge, a district in New Taipei City, is often called Taiwan's \"pottery town.\" Its history with clay began about 200 years ago, when settlers from Fujian, China, found good clay in the area. There was also coal nearby, which could be burned to heat kilns, the very hot ovens used to harden pottery. With both clay and fuel close at hand, more and more workshops opened. By the 1970s and 1980s, Yingge's factories were producing huge numbers of bowls, cups, tiles and vases, and many of them were sold overseas.\n\nLater, as costs rose, many factories moved to other countries, and the town had to find a new way forward. Instead of making cheap goods in large numbers, Yingge turned to art and tourism. In 2000, the Yingge Ceramics Museum opened. It was the first museum in Taiwan built to tell the story of ceramics, and it shows everything from simple farm jars to modern works of art. Nearby, shops on Yingge Old Street sell handmade teapots and cups, and many let visitors try making pottery themselves.\n\nThe town's name comes from a large rock on a hill above it. People thought the rock looked like a parrot, a bird that can be called \"yingge\" in Chinese. According to an old story, the bird was once a monster that breathed out thick fog so that soldiers got lost in the hills. The famous general Koxinga fired a cannon at it, and the monster turned into stone. Visitors can still hike up the hill to see the rock today.\n\nIn the end, Kai-wen managed to make one small, slightly uneven bowl. It had to be dried and then fired in a kiln, so it was mailed to his home a month later. \"It's not perfect,\" he said, \"but now whenever I see a cup in a shop, I think about how much skill it takes to make one.\"", "passage_zh": "去年春天，陳凱文的美術班到鶯歌校外教學時，他原本以為這會是輕鬆的一天。老師答應大家，每個人都可以用拉坯機做一個碗。「能有多難？」凱文說。二十分鐘後，他做的第三個碗也垮了，雙手和上衣都沾滿了濕濕的陶土。\n\n鶯歌是新北市的一個行政區，常被稱為台灣的「陶瓷之鎮」。它和陶土的淵源始於大約兩百年前，當時從中國福建來的移民在這一帶發現了優質的黏土。附近還產煤，可以拿來燒窯；窯是用來把陶器燒硬的高溫爐子。黏土和燃料都近在手邊，於是開設的工坊越來越多。到了 1970 和 1980 年代，鶯歌的工廠大量生產碗、杯子、磁磚和花瓶，其中許多都外銷到國外。\n\n後來，隨著成本上升，許多工廠遷到其他國家，小鎮必須另尋出路。鶯歌不再大量生產便宜的商品，而是轉向藝術和觀光發展。2000 年，鶯歌陶瓷博物館開幕。這是台灣第一座專門講述陶瓷故事的博物館，展出的作品從樸素的農家陶甕到現代藝術品都有。附近鶯歌老街上的商店販售手工茶壺和杯子，許多店家也讓遊客親手體驗做陶。\n\n這個小鎮的名字來自山丘上的一塊大石頭。人們覺得這塊石頭長得像鸚鵡，而鸚鵡在中文裡也可以叫做「鸚哥」。根據古老的傳說，這隻鳥曾經是一頭怪物，會吐出濃霧讓士兵在山中迷路。著名的將領鄭成功朝它發射大砲，怪物就變成了石頭。今天，遊客仍然可以爬上山丘去看這塊石頭。\n\n最後，凱文終於做出一個小小的、有點歪的碗。這個碗必須先晾乾，再放進窯裡燒製，所以一個月後才寄到他家。「它不完美，」他說，「但現在每當我在店裡看到一個杯子，我就會想到做一個杯子需要多少功夫。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to make a perfect bowl on a pottery wheel", "Why many factories in Taiwan moved overseas", "A pottery town's history and a student's visit there", "How Koxinga turned a monster into stone"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文以凱文到鶯歌做陶的經驗開頭與結尾（第一、五段），中間介紹鶯歌陶瓷業的起源（第二段）、轉型發展藝術與觀光（第三段）以及地名的由來（第四段）。(A) 凱文最後只做出一個 slightly uneven bowl，文章並沒有教人做出完美的碗；(B) 工廠外移只是第三段的背景；(D) 鄭成功的傳說只是第四段說明地名由來的細節。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did pottery workshops grow in Yingge about 200 years ago?", "options": ["Good clay and coal for fuel could both be found nearby.", "The government built a large museum there.", "Many tourists wanted to buy handmade teapots.", "Factories moved there from other countries."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：settlers... found good clay in the area. There was also coal nearby... With both clay and fuel close at hand, more and more workshops opened. 當地同時有黏土和燃料。(B) 博物館到 2000 年才開幕；(C) 觀光是後來才有的發展；(D) 第三段說的是工廠遷往其他國家，方向正好相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "How did Yingge change after many factories moved away?", "options": ["It started producing even more cheap goods.", "It became a center for coal mining.", "It closed all of its pottery shops.", "It focused on art and tourism instead."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Instead of making cheap goods in large numbers, Yingge turned to art and tourism. (A) 與原文相反；(B) 煤只在第二段作為燃料提及；(C) 老街上仍有許多陶瓷商店。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"fired\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["made to leave a job", "heated to become hard", "shot with a gun", "painted with colors"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段 It had to be dried and then fired in a kiln，對照第二段 kilns, the very hot ovens used to harden pottery，可知 fired 指在窯中「燒製」，讓陶器變硬。(A)「被開除」和 (C)「開槍」都是 fire 的其他意思，但不符合此處上下文；(D) 文中未提及上色。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How does Kai-wen most likely feel at the end of the passage?", "options": ["He respects the skill that pottery makers have.", "He is angry that his bowl is not perfect.", "He thinks making pottery is a waste of time.", "He wants to open a pottery factory overseas."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段凱文說 now whenever I see a cup in a shop, I think about how much skill it takes to make one，可見他體會到做陶需要高超的技術，對製陶的人多了一份敬意。(B) 他承認碗不完美，但語氣並不生氣；(C)(D) 文中沒有線索。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a43", "title": "Snow in May: The Tung Blossoms of Hakka Country", "title_zh": "五月雪：客家庄的桐花", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Nature"], "passage": "Snow in May? In most of Taiwan, that sounds impossible. Yet every April and May, parts of the hills in northern and central Taiwan turn white. Small white flowers fall from the trees and cover hiking paths, roads and streams like a thin layer of snow. People call this sight \"May snow.\" The flowers come from the tung tree, which has become a symbol of Hakka culture.\n\nTung trees are not native to Taiwan. They were brought over from southern China, and many were planted during the period of Japanese rule because their seeds produce a very useful oil. Tung oil dries into a hard, waterproof surface, so it was used in paint and to protect wood, boats and paper umbrellas. Many Hakka families lived in the hills where the trees grew, and collecting the seeds became an important way for them to earn money. The seeds, however, are poisonous and must never be eaten.\n\nIn the decades after World War II, cheaper man-made products took the place of tung oil, and the trees were no longer worth much. Since nobody bothered to cut them down, they kept growing on the hillsides. Then, in 2002, the government department in charge of Hakka affairs decided to celebrate the flowers with a festival. The Hakka Tung Blossom Festival was a great success. Today, it brings large numbers of visitors every spring to Hakka towns in places such as Miaoli, Hsinchu and Taoyuan.\n\nThe festival is about more than flowers. Visitors can enjoy Hakka music, walk on trails under the blooming trees, and taste Hakka food such as lei cha, a drink made by grinding tea leaves, nuts and seeds into a powder and adding hot water. Local farmers and shops also sell crafts and snacks, so the festival helps small hill towns earn more income.\n\nIf you plan to go, remember a few simple rules. The best time to see the falling flowers is usually from mid-April to early May. Stay on the paths, and never shake the trees to make the flowers fall faster. The flowers on the ground should be left where they are, so that other visitors can enjoy the \"snow,\" too.", "passage_zh": "五月下雪？在台灣大部分地區，這聽起來根本不可能。然而每年四、五月，台灣北部和中部的部分山丘都會變成一片雪白。小小的白花從樹上飄落，覆蓋在登山步道、道路和溪流上，就像一層薄薄的雪。人們稱這幅景象為「五月雪」。這些花來自油桐樹，而油桐樹已經成為客家文化的象徵。\n\n油桐樹並不是台灣原生的樹種。它們是從中國南方引進的，許多是在日治時期種下的，因為它們的種子可以榨出非常有用的油。桐油乾了以後會形成堅硬又防水的表層，所以被用在油漆中，也用來保護木材、船隻和紙傘。許多客家家庭住在油桐樹生長的山區，採集桐籽便成為他們重要的收入來源。不過，桐籽有毒，千萬不能吃。\n\n第二次世界大戰後的數十年間，較便宜的人造產品取代了桐油，油桐樹也就不再值錢了。由於沒有人費心去砍，它們就繼續在山坡上生長。後來在 2002 年，負責客家事務的政府機關決定為這些花舉辦一個節慶。客家桐花祭非常成功，如今每年春天都為苗栗、新竹和桃園等地的客家城鎮帶來大批遊客。\n\n桐花祭不只是賞花而已。遊客可以欣賞客家音樂、在盛開的油桐樹下沿著步道散步，還可以品嚐客家美食，例如擂茶。擂茶是把茶葉、堅果和種子磨成粉，再加入熱水做成的飲品。當地的農民和商店也會販賣手工藝品和點心，因此桐花祭能幫助山區小鎮增加收入。\n\n如果你打算前往，請記得幾個簡單的規則。觀賞落花的最佳時間通常是四月中旬到五月初。請走在步道上，千萬不要為了讓花快點掉下來而搖晃樹木。地上的落花應該留在原處，讓其他遊客也能欣賞這片「雪景」。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history of tung trees and the Hakka festival for their flowers", "The way tung oil is made from poisonous seeds", "The reason it sometimes snows in Taiwan's hills in May", "The traditional Hakka drink made from tea and nuts"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹油桐花「五月雪」的景象（第一段）、油桐樹引進台灣的經過與桐油的用途（第二段）、客家桐花祭的由來與活動（第三、四段），以及賞花須知（第五段）。(B) 第二段只提到桐籽能產油而且有毒，並未說明製油方法；(C) 「五月雪」指的是像雪一樣飄落的白色桐花，並不是真的下雪；(D) 擂茶只是第四段提到的一種客家美食。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what was tung oil used for?", "options": ["Cooking Hakka food", "Making medicine for colds", "Protecting things from water", "Lighting lanterns during festivals"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Tung oil dries into a hard, waterproof surface, so it was used in paint and to protect wood, boats and paper umbrellas. 桐油用來保護物品、讓東西防水。(A) 第二段特別提醒桐籽有毒，不能吃；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did tung trees stop being valuable?", "options": ["The trees stopped producing seeds.", "Cheaper man-made products replaced tung oil.", "The government cut most of them down.", "Hakka families moved away from the hills."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：cheaper man-made products took the place of tung oil, and the trees were no longer worth much. (A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 第三段說 nobody bothered to cut them down，與此相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"native\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["originally growing in a place", "very expensive", "easy to plant", "dangerous to eat"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段 Tung trees are not native to Taiwan. They were brought over from southern China，後一句說油桐是從中國南方引進的，可知 native 指「原生的、本來就生長在某地的」。(B)(C) 文中沒有相關線索；(D) 有毒的是桐籽，與 native 的意思無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which visitor is following the advice in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Amy shakes a tree so her friend can take a photo of the falling flowers.", "Ben fills a bag with flowers from the ground to take home.", "Cindy leaves the path to get closer to the tallest tree.", "David stays on the path and leaves the fallen flowers where they are."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段提醒：Stay on the paths, and never shake the trees... The flowers on the ground should be left where they are. 只有 David 走在步道上，並讓落花留在原處。(A) 搖樹、(B) 把落花帶回家、(C) 離開步道，都違反了建議。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a44", "title": "Lights on the Water: The Keelung Ghost Festival", "title_zh": "水上的燈火：基隆中元祭", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "History"], "passage": "In many cultures, people fear ghosts. In Taiwan, however, people often treat them more like guests who need to be looked after. According to Taiwanese folk beliefs, the gates of the world of the dead open on the first day of the seventh lunar month, and spirits return to the world of the living for a whole month. This time is known as Ghost Month, and one of its most famous celebrations takes place in the port city of Keelung.\n\nThe Keelung Ghost Festival began in the 1850s after a terrible event. At that time, two groups of Chinese settlers, one from Zhangzhou and one from Quanzhou, fought bloody battles over land and other resources, and many people were killed. Afterward, local leaders buried the dead and held ceremonies for their spirits. To keep the fighting from happening again, they decided that groups of families with the same last name would take turns hosting the festival each year. Instead of fighting with weapons, they would compete to hold the best celebration.\n\nThe highlight of the festival comes on the fourteenth night of the month. First, a long parade with colorful floats, music and dancers moves through the streets. Then the crowd follows it to Badouzi, by the sea. There, people place water lanterns, paper lanterns shaped like small houses, onto the waves and set them on fire. The lanterns are believed to light the way for spirits who died at sea and invite them to enjoy the food offered to them the next day.\n\nMany people believe that the farther a lantern floats before it burns out, the better luck its family group will have in the coming year. So the crowd watches closely as the burning lanterns drift away into the dark sea.\n\nEven outside Keelung, Ghost Month affects daily life across Taiwan. Many families and shops set out tables of food and drinks as offerings to the spirits. Some people avoid swimming, moving house or getting married during the month, and grandparents may warn children not to stay out late at night. Whether or not people believe in ghosts, the Keelung festival reminds everyone of an important lesson from its history: it is far better to compete in peace than to fight.", "passage_zh": "在許多文化中，人們都害怕鬼。然而在台灣，人們往往把祂們當成需要好好招待的客人。根據台灣的民間信仰，農曆七月初一陰間的大門會打開，亡魂會回到陽間停留整整一個月。這段時間被稱為「鬼月」，而其中最有名的祭典之一就在港口城市基隆舉行。\n\n基隆中元祭始於 1850 年代的一場悲劇。當時，分別來自漳州和泉州的兩群中國移民為了爭奪土地和其他資源，爆發了血腥的械鬥，許多人因此喪命。事後，地方領袖埋葬了死者，並為亡魂舉行祭典。為了避免再次發生衝突，他們決定由同姓的宗親團體每年輪流主辦祭典。各團體不再拿著武器打鬥，而是比賽誰能把祭典辦得最好。\n\n祭典的最高潮在農曆七月十四日晚上。首先，一支由五彩花車、音樂和舞者組成的長長遊行隊伍會穿越大街小巷。接著，人群會跟著隊伍來到海邊的八斗子。在那裡，人們把水燈——也就是做成小房子形狀的紙燈——放到海浪上，並點火燃燒。人們相信這些水燈能為在海上喪生的亡魂照亮道路，邀請祂們隔天前來享用為祂們準備的祭品。\n\n許多人相信，水燈在燒完之前漂得越遠，該宗親團體來年的運氣就越好。因此，人群都會目不轉睛地看著燃燒的水燈漂向漆黑的大海。\n\n即使在基隆以外的地方，鬼月也影響著全台灣人的日常生活。許多家庭和商店會擺出一桌桌的食物和飲料，作為獻給亡魂的祭品。有些人會在這個月避免游泳、搬家或結婚，祖父母也可能會提醒孩子晚上不要在外面待太晚。無論人們是否相信有鬼，基隆中元祭都提醒大家一個來自歷史的重要教訓：和平地競爭遠比打鬥好得多。", "questions": [{"q": "According to Taiwanese folk beliefs, what happens during Ghost Month?", "options": ["People travel to Keelung to fight with other groups.", "Families move into new houses for good luck.", "Fishermen go out to sea to catch more fish.", "Spirits of the dead come back to the world of the living."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：the gates of the world of the dead open on the first day of the seventh lunar month, and spirits return to the world of the living for a whole month. (A) 械鬥是 1850 年代的歷史事件，不是鬼月習俗；(B) 第五段說有些人在鬼月會避免搬家，與此相反；(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did local leaders decide that family groups would take turns hosting the festival?", "options": ["To make the festival bigger than those in other cities", "To replace fighting with peaceful competition", "To collect money for building new temples", "To decide which group could own the land"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：To keep the fighting from happening again... Instead of fighting with weapons, they would compete to hold the best celebration. 輪流主辦是為了用和平的競爭取代械鬥。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 土地是當初械鬥爭奪的對象，並不是輪流主辦的目的。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"highlight\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the beginning", "the lowest point", "the most exciting part", "the cheapest ticket"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段描述七月十四日晚上的大遊行和放水燈，是整個祭典中最精彩、最受矚目的活動，所以 highlight 指「最精彩的部分」。(A) 鬼月從農曆七月初一就開始了，十四日並不是開頭；(B)(D) 都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What happens at Badouzi on the fourteenth night?", "options": ["Burning water lanterns are sent out onto the sea.", "Food is offered to the spirits at the seaside.", "Two groups of settlers fight over land.", "A parade of floats starts at the harbor."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Then the crowd follows it to Badouzi... people place water lanterns... onto the waves and set them on fire. (B) 祭品是在 the next day（隔天）供奉；(C) 是 1850 年代的歷史事件；(D) 遊行是在市區街道上進行，之後人群才跟著到八斗子。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's main message in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Children should never go out during Ghost Month.", "Everyone in Taiwan believes in ghosts.", "The festival teaches people that peace is better than fighting.", "Ghost Month is only celebrated in Keelung."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段最後一句：the Keelung festival reminds everyone of an important lesson from its history: it is far better to compete in peace than to fight. (A) 文中只說祖父母可能提醒孩子晚上別在外面待太晚，並非重點；(B) Whether or not people believe in ghosts 表示並非人人都相信有鬼；(D) 第五段開頭說鬼月影響全台灣。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a45", "title": "The Lions That Fight the Wind: Kinmen's Wind Lion Gods", "title_zh": "與風對抗的獅子：金門風獅爺", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Culture"], "passage": "On Kinmen, a group of islands just a few kilometers off the coast of China, visitors often notice something unusual at the edge of villages: stone lions that stand on two legs like people, some of them wearing red capes. These are the wind lion gods. For hundreds of years, local people have believed that these lions protect their villages from strong winds and bad luck.\n\nWhy would an island need protection from the wind? According to local history, Kinmen was once covered with forests. In the 1600s, however, most of its trees were cut down, partly to build warships. Without trees, nothing could slow down the strong northeast winds that blow from autumn to spring. The winds carried sand across the island, burying farmland and making it very hard to grow crops.\n\nSince people could not control the weather, they turned to their beliefs. They made lion statues out of stone or clay and placed them at village entrances, often facing the direction the wind came from. They believed the lions would block the wind and keep evil spirits away. Today, there are more than 60 traditional wind lion gods on Kinmen, and no two are the same. Some are tall and serious, some are small with big smiles, and some are painted in bright colors. Villagers still offer them food and incense, and on special days they dress them in new red capes.\n\nKinmen looks very different today. After 1949, the islands became an important military base, and the army planted huge numbers of trees to protect the land from the wind and to hide soldiers and equipment. Now, much of Kinmen is green again. The wind lion gods are no longer needed to fight sandstorms, but they have become a symbol of the islands. Their faces appear on signs, T-shirts and souvenirs, and many visitors spend a day riding bicycles around Kinmen to find as many of them as they can.\n\nTo many people, the wind lion gods show how people in the past held on to hope during hard times. As one local guide puts it, \"The lions could not really stop the wind, but they gave farmers the courage to keep going.\"", "passage_zh": "金門是距離中國沿岸只有幾公里的一群島嶼。遊客在這裡的村落邊緣常會注意到一些不尋常的東西：像人一樣用兩條腿站立的石獅子，有些還披著紅色披風。這些就是風獅爺。數百年來，當地人一直相信這些獅子能保護村莊，抵擋強風和厄運。\n\n為什麼一座島嶼會需要抵擋風呢？根據當地的歷史，金門曾經森林茂密。然而在 1600 年代，島上大部分的樹木都被砍伐，其中一部分是為了建造戰船。沒有了樹木，就沒有東西能減緩從秋天吹到春天的強勁東北風。強風把沙子吹過整座島，掩埋了農田，使農作物非常難以生長。\n\n由於人們無法控制天氣，便轉而向信仰尋求幫助。他們用石頭或陶土製作獅子像，擺在村莊入口，通常面向風吹來的方向。他們相信這些獅子能擋住強風，並趕走邪靈。如今金門有六十多尊傳統風獅爺，而且沒有兩尊是一模一樣的。有的高大嚴肅，有的矮小且笑容滿面，有的還塗上了鮮豔的色彩。村民至今仍會用食物和香來祭拜它們，在特別的日子還會幫它們換上新的紅披風。\n\n今天的金門看起來大不相同。1949 年以後，金門成為重要的軍事基地，軍隊種下了大量的樹木，以保護土地免受強風侵襲，並隱藏士兵和裝備。如今，金門大部分地區又恢復了一片綠意。風獅爺已經不再需要對抗風沙，卻成了金門的象徵。它們的臉出現在招牌、T 恤和紀念品上，許多遊客也會花一整天騎腳踏車環島，盡可能找到更多的風獅爺。\n\n對許多人來說，風獅爺展現了過去的人們如何在艱困的時期中懷抱希望。正如一位當地導覽員所說：「獅子其實擋不住風，但它們給了農民繼續努力下去的勇氣。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the army made Kinmen green again after 1949", "Kinmen's wind lion gods, their history and meaning", "Why Kinmen's forests were cut down in the 1600s", "The best way to tour Kinmen by bicycle"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文介紹金門風獅爺：外觀與用途（第一段）、島上樹木被砍光而飽受風沙之苦的背景（第二段）、村民製作與祭拜風獅爺的方式（第三段），以及它們今日成為金門象徵、帶給人們希望的意義（第四、五段）。(A) 軍隊植樹只是第四段的細節；(C) 1600 年代砍樹只是第二段說明風害由來的背景；(D) 騎腳踏車尋找風獅爺只在第四段末順帶一提。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did strong winds cause so many problems on Kinmen?", "options": ["Most of the trees had been cut down, so nothing could slow the wind.", "Warships brought storms to the islands.", "Farmers built their houses too close to the sea.", "The wind blew only in summer, when crops were growing."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：most of its trees were cut down... Without trees, nothing could slow down the strong northeast winds. 樹木被砍光後，強風無法被減緩，沙子因此掩埋農田。(B)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 東北風是 from autumn to spring，不是只在夏天吹。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why did the army plant trees on Kinmen?", "options": ["To build new warships", "To make wind lion gods out of wood", "To sell the wood to other countries", "To protect the land from the wind and to hide soldiers"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：the army planted huge numbers of trees to protect the land from the wind and to hide soldiers and equipment. (A) 為了造戰船而砍樹是 1600 年代的事（第二段）；(B) 風獅爺是用石頭或陶土製作的（第三段）；(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"symbol\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a kind of weapon", "a dangerous storm", "something that represents a place or an idea", "a gift for soldiers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說風獅爺雖然不再需要對抗風沙，但 they have become a symbol of the islands，而且它們的臉出現在招牌、T 恤和紀念品上，可知 symbol 指「代表某地或某種概念的事物」，也就是象徵。(A)(B)(D) 都不符合上下文。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the local guide mean in the last paragraph?", "options": ["The lions really did stop the wind from blowing.", "The lions helped people stay hopeful, even though they had no real power over the weather.", "Farmers should stop believing in the wind lion gods.", "The lions were made to welcome tourists to Kinmen."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段導覽員說 The lions could not really stop the wind, but they gave farmers the courage to keep going，意思是獅子雖然無法真的擋風，卻帶給人們希望與勇氣。(A) 與 could not really stop the wind 相反；(C) 文中沒有這個意思；(D) 風獅爺原本是為了擋風、驅邪而設，不是為了歡迎觀光客。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a46", "title": "\"Your Package Is Waiting\"... or Is It?", "title_zh": "「您的包裹正在等您」……真的嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Life"], "passage": "Last Tuesday, Wen-ting, a second-year student at a vocational high school in Taichung, received a text message while she was waiting for the bus. \"Your package could not be delivered because the address was incomplete. Please update your information within 24 hours, or the package will be returned,\" it said. Below the message was a link. Wen-ting had ordered a pair of sneakers online a few days earlier, so the message did not seem strange at all.\n\nShe clicked the link and found a website that looked just like a real delivery company's page. It asked for her name, address and phone number, and she typed them in. Then it asked her to pay a \"redelivery fee\" of NT$25 by credit card. Wen-ting did not have a credit card, so she asked her older brother for help. As soon as he saw the website, he told her to stop. \"Look at the web address,\" he said. \"It's a long string of strange letters, not the company's real website.\"\n\nWen-ting had almost become the victim of a scam called \"smishing,\" a word made from \"SMS\" (text messages) and \"phishing.\" In phishing, criminals pretend to be a company people trust so that they will give away personal information. The small fee is only bait. What the criminals really want is the card number, which they can use to spend much larger amounts of money later.\n\nThese messages work because they are designed to make people act quickly. They often use words such as \"urgent,\" \"final notice\" or \"within 24 hours.\" They also arrive at a time when many people really are waiting for things they have ordered online.\n\nPolice in Taiwan suggest a few simple rules. First, never click links in messages you did not expect. Instead, open the delivery company's official app or check your order on the shopping website. Second, remember that real companies rarely ask for card information through a text message. Third, if you are not sure, call the 165 Anti-Fraud Hotline. Wen-ting now tells her friends, \"When a message rushes you, slow down.\"", "passage_zh": "上週二，臺中一所高職二年級的學生文婷在等公車時收到一則簡訊：「您的包裹因地址不完整無法配送。請於 24 小時內更新資料，否則包裹將被退回。」訊息下方附有一個連結。文婷幾天前才在網路上買了一雙運動鞋，所以這則訊息看起來一點也不奇怪。\n\n她點了連結，進入一個看起來跟真的物流公司網頁一模一樣的網站。網站要她填寫姓名、地址和電話號碼，她都輸入了。接著網站要求她用信用卡支付新臺幣 25 元的「重新配送費」。文婷沒有信用卡，於是請哥哥幫忙。哥哥一看到這個網站，就叫她停下來。「你看網址，」他說，「那是一長串奇怪的字母，不是那家公司真正的網站。」\n\n文婷差一點就成了一種叫做「smishing（簡訊釣魚）」詐騙的受害者，這個字是由「SMS」（簡訊）和「phishing」（網路釣魚）組合而成。在網路釣魚詐騙中，歹徒會假冒大家信任的公司，讓人交出個人資料。那筆小額費用只是誘餌，歹徒真正想要的是信用卡卡號，之後他們就能用它刷更大筆的金額。\n\n這類訊息之所以有效，是因為它們刻意設計成讓人急著行動。它們常使用「緊急」、「最後通知」或「24 小時內」這類字眼。而且它們出現的時機，正好是許多人真的在等網購商品送達的時候。\n\n臺灣警方建議幾個簡單的原則。第一，絕對不要點選你沒有預期會收到的訊息中的連結，而是打開物流公司的官方 App，或到購物網站查詢訂單。第二，記住真正的公司很少透過簡訊要求提供信用卡資料。第三，如果不確定，就撥打 165 反詐騙諮詢專線。文婷現在常告訴朋友：「當訊息催你的時候，就放慢腳步。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to order sneakers online safely and cheaply", "A common text message scam and how to avoid it", "Why delivery companies charge redelivery fees", "How Wen-ting's brother started working for the police"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從文婷收到假的包裹簡訊說起，第三段說明這是「簡訊釣魚」詐騙，第四、五段說明它為何有效以及如何防範，所以主旨是一種常見的簡訊詐騙與避免方法。(A) 網購運動鞋只是故事背景；(C) 重新配送費是歹徒的誘餌，文章並未說明物流公司為何收費；(D) 文中沒有提到哥哥去當警察。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did the text message NOT seem strange to Wen-ting at first?", "options": ["It came from her brother's phone number.", "It asked her to pay a very large amount of money.", "She was really waiting for something she had bought online.", "She had received the same message many times before."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段最後一句：Wen-ting had ordered a pair of sneakers online a few days earlier, so the message did not seem strange at all. 她剛好在等網購的運動鞋，所以不覺得奇怪。(A) 簡訊不是哥哥傳的；(B) 簡訊本身並沒有要求付錢，後來網站要求的「重新配送費」也只有 NT$25，並非大筆金額；(D) 文中沒有說她以前收過同樣的簡訊。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"bait\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a reward for honest customers", "something used to attract and trick people", "a fee that every delivery company must charge", "a mistake made by careless criminals"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The small fee is only bait. What the criminals really want is the card number... 小額費用只是用來引人上鉤的「誘餌」，真正目的是騙取卡號，所以 bait 指用來吸引並欺騙人的東西。(A) 不是給誠實顧客的獎勵；(C) 這筆費用是假的，並非物流公司必收的費用；(D) 這是歹徒刻意的設計，不是失誤。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Based on paragraph 4, which of the following messages is most likely a smishing message?", "options": ["\"FINAL NOTICE: Your account will be closed today. Click here now to keep it.\"", "\"Thank you for shopping with us. Your order will arrive next week.\"", "\"Hi, it's Mom. I'll be home late tonight. Dinner is in the fridge.\"", "\"Your library book is due on Friday. Please return it at the front desk.\""], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段指出詐騙簡訊會讓人急著行動，常用 \"urgent\"、\"final notice\"、\"within 24 hours\" 等字眼，(A) 同時有「最後通知」、「今天就會關閉」和「立刻點這裡」，最符合這些特徵。(B)(C)(D) 都沒有催促點連結或提供資料，只是一般通知或家人訊息。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "If you receive a message like the one Wen-ting got, what should you do according to the police?", "options": ["Click the link quickly before the 24 hours are over.", "Reply to the message and ask for the company's address.", "Pay the fee with a family member's credit card.", "Check your order in the official app or call the 165 hotline."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段警方建議：不要點陌生訊息的連結，改用物流公司的官方 App 或購物網站查詢訂單；不確定時撥打 165 反詐騙諮詢專線。(A) 正是歹徒希望你做的事；(B) 回覆詐騙訊息並非警方建議；(C) 用家人的信用卡付款會讓卡號被盜。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a47", "title": "Three Random Words: Building a Better Password", "title_zh": "三個隨機單字：打造更好的密碼", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Instructions"], "passage": "Every year, security companies publish lists of the passwords people use most often. And every year, the same sad results appear near the top: \"123456,\" \"password\" and \"qwerty,\" which is simply the first six letters on the top row of a keyboard. Passwords like these are easy to remember, but they are also easy to guess. Criminals use computer programs that can try millions of common passwords in a very short time, so a weak password protects almost nothing.\n\nMany people think that a strong password must look like \"T7#q!z2P,\" a mix of capital letters, numbers and symbols. Such passwords are hard to guess, but they are also hard to remember. As a result, people write them on sticky notes or use the same one everywhere. Experts now say that length matters more than strange symbols. Every extra character makes a password much harder for a computer to crack, or break into.\n\nThat is why the UK's National Cyber Security Centre suggests a simple method: choose three random words and put them together. A password like \"purplecactusbicycle\" is long, but it is easy to remember because your brain can turn it into a picture, perhaps a purple cactus riding a bicycle. The words should be truly random. Your pet's name, your birthday or your favorite team are poor choices, because people can often find this information on your social media.\n\nEven a good password should be used for only one account. When a website is hacked, criminals often try the stolen email addresses and passwords on many other websites. If you use the same password for your game account, your email and your bank app, one leak can open all of them.\n\nFinally, turn on two-step verification wherever you can. With this feature, logging in needs both your password and a second proof, such as a code sent to your phone. Even if someone steals your password, they still cannot get in without your phone. It takes a few extra seconds, but those seconds may save you a lot of trouble.", "passage_zh": "每年，資安公司都會公布人們最常使用的密碼排行榜。而每一年，排在前面的都是同樣令人遺憾的結果：「123456」、「password」，以及「qwerty」──這只不過是鍵盤最上排的前六個字母。這類密碼容易記，但也容易被猜到。歹徒使用的電腦程式能在極短的時間內試遍數百萬組常見密碼，所以弱密碼幾乎什麼都保護不了。\n\n很多人以為，強密碼一定要長得像「T7#q!z2P」，混合大寫字母、數字和符號。這樣的密碼很難猜，但也很難記。結果，人們就把密碼寫在便利貼上，或是每個地方都用同一組。專家現在表示，長度比奇怪的符號更重要。每多一個字元，電腦要破解（也就是闖入）這組密碼就會困難許多。\n\n這就是為什麼英國國家網路安全中心建議一個簡單的方法：選三個隨機的單字，把它們連在一起。像「purplecactusbicycle」（紫色仙人掌腳踏車）這樣的密碼很長，卻很好記，因為你的大腦可以把它變成一幅畫面──也許是一株紫色的仙人掌在騎腳踏車。這些單字必須真的是隨機的。寵物的名字、你的生日或你最喜歡的球隊都是不好的選擇，因為別人常常可以在你的社群媒體上找到這些資料。\n\n即使是好密碼，也應該只用在一個帳號上。當某個網站遭駭時，歹徒常會拿偷來的電子郵件地址和密碼到許多其他網站嘗試登入。如果你的遊戲帳號、電子信箱和銀行 App 都用同一組密碼，一次外洩就可能讓所有帳號都被打開。\n\n最後，只要可以，就開啟二階段驗證。有了這個功能，登入時除了密碼之外，還需要第二道證明，例如傳送到你手機的驗證碼。就算有人偷了你的密碼，沒有你的手機還是無法登入。這只多花幾秒鐘，但這幾秒鐘可能幫你省下許多麻煩。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain why people often forget their passwords", "To give advice on keeping online accounts safe", "To compare different kinds of computer keyboards", "To show how criminals build their computer programs"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文說明弱密碼的危險，並建議使用三個隨機單字組成的長密碼、一個帳號一組密碼，以及開啟二階段驗證，目的是教讀者保護網路帳號。(A) 文中只提到複雜密碼難記，並非全文重點；(C) 鍵盤只在第一段解釋 qwerty 時出現；(D) 文中沒有說明歹徒如何寫程式。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what is a problem with passwords like \"T7#q!z2P\"?", "options": ["Computers can guess them in a few seconds.", "Most websites do not allow symbols in passwords.", "They are hard to remember, so people write them down or reuse them.", "They are too long to type on a phone."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Such passwords are hard to guess, but they are also hard to remember. As a result, people write them on sticky notes or use the same one everywhere. 這類密碼難記，導致人們把它寫下來或到處重複使用。(A) 文中說這種密碼「很難猜」；(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"leak\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a time when private information is stolen or made public", "a password that is easy to remember", "a game account that has been closed", "a small hole in a water pipe"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段前文提到 When a website is hacked, criminals often try the stolen email addresses and passwords...，接著說 one leak can open all of them，可知 leak 指帳號密碼等私人資料被偷或外洩。(B)(C) 與上下文無關；(D) 是 leak「漏水的破洞」的字面意思，不符合本文語境。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following passwords would the National Cyber Security Centre most likely recommend?", "options": ["123456789", "Lucky0815 (your dog's name and your birthday)", "qwerty2025", "lanternpotatoriver"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段建議選三個「真正隨機」的單字連在一起，lanternpotatoriver（燈籠＋馬鈴薯＋河流）正符合。(A)(C) 是第一段提到的常見弱密碼加上數字；(B) 用寵物名字和生日，第三段說這是 poor choices，因為別人可能從社群媒體查到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How does two-step verification help protect an account?", "options": ["It changes your password automatically every day.", "It stops criminals from making computer programs.", "It asks for a second proof, such as a code sent to your phone.", "It lets you use one password for all of your accounts."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：logging in needs both your password and a second proof, such as a code sent to your phone. 登入時除了密碼還需要第二道證明，所以即使密碼被偷也登不進去。(A)(B) 文中沒有提到；(D) 與第四段「一組密碼只用在一個帳號」的建議相反。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a48", "title": "What One Photo Told a Stranger", "title_zh": "一張照片告訴陌生人的事", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Society"], "passage": "When Yi-chen got a kitten for her sixteenth birthday, she did what many teenagers would do. She took dozens of photos and posted the cutest one on her public social media account. In the picture, the gray kitten was sleeping by the living room window. Within an hour, the photo had more than a hundred likes.\n\nThree days later, Yi-chen got a private message from someone she did not know. \"Cute cat! Your home is near the Lucky Mart on Minsheng Road, right? I walk past there every day.\" Yi-chen felt cold all over. She had never told anyone online where she lived. She deleted the message, blocked the account and told her parents right away.\n\nThe next week, Yi-chen shared the story with her computer teacher, Mr. Lai, and he used her photo, with her permission, as a lesson for the whole class. He asked the students to look carefully at the picture. Through the window, part of a store sign could be seen, showing the name of the branch. On the sofa lay her school jacket, with the school's name printed on it. \"Put these small clues together,\" Mr. Lai said, \"and a stranger can find your neighborhood in a few minutes.\"\n\nHe also explained that a photo can carry information you cannot see. Many smartphones save the exact place where a photo was taken inside the photo file. Most big social media apps remove this hidden data when you upload a picture, but it may stay in a photo that you send as a file or post on some other websites.\n\nMr. Lai then gave the class a short checklist. Check the background for signs, house numbers and uniforms. Turn off location saving in your camera settings. Post travel photos after you get home, not while you are still there. Most importantly, ask yourself, \"Would I be happy if a stranger saw this?\" Yi-chen still posts photos of her kitten, but now she takes them in front of a plain wall.", "passage_zh": "怡辰十六歲生日時得到一隻小貓，她做了許多青少年都會做的事：拍了好幾十張照片，然後把最可愛的一張貼到她公開的社群媒體帳號上。照片裡，那隻灰色小貓睡在客廳的窗邊。不到一個小時，這張照片就有一百多個讚。\n\n三天後，怡辰收到一則陌生人傳來的私訊：「好可愛的貓！你家在民生路那家幸運超商附近，對吧？我每天都會經過那裡。」怡辰全身發冷。她從來沒有在網路上告訴任何人她住在哪裡。她刪除了訊息、封鎖了那個帳號，並立刻告訴爸媽。\n\n隔週，怡辰把這件事告訴電腦老師賴老師，老師在徵得她同意後，用她的照片給全班上了一課。他請學生仔細看這張照片。從窗戶望出去，可以看到一部分商店招牌，上面寫著分店的名稱。沙發上放著她的學校外套，上面印著校名。「把這些小線索拼在一起，」賴老師說，「陌生人幾分鐘內就能找到你住的社區。」\n\n他也解釋，照片可能帶有你看不見的資料。許多智慧型手機會把拍照的確切地點存在照片檔案裡。大部分大型社群媒體 App 會在你上傳照片時移除這些隱藏的資料，但如果你把照片當作檔案傳送，或貼到其他某些網站上，這些資料可能還留在裡面。\n\n接著，賴老師給了全班一份簡短的檢查清單：檢查背景有沒有招牌、門牌號碼和制服；在相機設定中關閉位置儲存功能；旅遊照片等回到家再貼，不要人還在當地就貼出來。最重要的是，問問自己：「如果陌生人看到這張照片，我會覺得沒關係嗎？」怡辰現在還是會貼小貓的照片，不過都改在一面素色的牆前面拍。", "questions": [{"q": "How did the stranger most likely find out where Yi-chen lived?", "options": ["Yi-chen's parents told him.", "He noticed clues in the background of her photo.", "Yi-chen wrote her address under the photo.", "He followed her home from school one day."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段賴老師指出照片中窗外露出寫有分店名稱的商店招牌，沙發上還有印著校名的外套，並說 Put these small clues together... a stranger can find your neighborhood，可知陌生人是從照片背景的線索推測出她家位置。(A)(D) 文中沒有提到；(C) 第二段說她從未在網路上透露住處。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What did Yi-chen do right after she got the stranger's message?", "options": ["She replied and asked who the person was.", "She posted another photo of her kitten.", "She deleted the message, blocked the account and told her parents.", "She moved her kitten to a different room."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段最後一句：She deleted the message, blocked the account and told her parents right away. (A) 她沒有回覆對方；(B) 文中沒有說她立刻又貼照片；(D) 文中沒有提到她把貓移到別的房間。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 4, what does \"this hidden data\" refer to?", "options": ["The number of likes a photo receives", "The name printed on Yi-chen's school jacket", "The private messages sent by strangers", "Information about the place where a photo was taken"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段前一句：Many smartphones save the exact place where a photo was taken inside the photo file. 緊接著的 this hidden data 指的就是存在檔案中、看不見的拍攝地點資料。(A) 按讚數是公開的；(B) 外套上的校名是照片中看得見的線索，出現在第三段；(C) 私訊與照片檔案無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Kai is on a trip to Kenting with his family. According to Mr. Lai's checklist, what should he do with his beach photos?", "options": ["Post them after he returns home.", "Post them right away so his friends can join him.", "Send them as files to people he does not know.", "Turn on location saving so he can remember the places."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段檢查清單：Post travel photos after you get home, not while you are still there. 旅遊照片應該回家後再貼。(B) 正好是清單提醒不要做的事；(C) 第四段說以檔案傳送的照片可能保留位置資料，更不該傳給陌生人；(D) 清單建議關閉位置儲存功能。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the last sentence of the passage?", "options": ["Yi-chen has stopped using social media completely.", "Yi-chen's kitten no longer likes the living room.", "Yi-chen has learned to be careful about what her photos show.", "Yi-chen thinks plain walls make photos more beautiful."], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一句說怡辰仍會貼小貓照片，但現在都在素色牆面前拍，背景不會再露出招牌或制服等線索，可推知她已學會注意照片透露的資訊。(A) 她仍在貼照片，並未停用社群媒體；(B) 文中沒有提到貓的喜好；(D) 她換背景是為了安全，不是為了美觀。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a49", "title": "A Familiar Voice, a Fake Call", "title_zh": "熟悉的聲音，假冒的電話", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Society"], "passage": "One evening, Mrs. Lin's phone rang. It was her daughter, Pei-shan, a university student in Tainan, or at least it sounded like her. \"Mom, I'm in trouble,\" the voice cried. \"I knocked over a man's scooter, and he says I have to pay NT$50,000 today, or he'll call the police. Please don't tell Dad. Just send the money to this account.\" The voice had Pei-shan's soft accent and even her habit of saying \"Mom\" twice when she was upset.\n\nMrs. Lin grabbed her bag and started toward the door. Then she remembered something her family had agreed on a few months earlier. She took a deep breath and asked, \"What was the name of our first dog?\" There was a short silence, and then the line went dead. With shaking hands, she called Pei-shan's number. Her daughter answered after two rings. She was studying in the library and had no idea what her mother was talking about.\n\nThe caller was not Pei-shan at all. It was probably a criminal using an AI voice tool. Today, such tools can copy a person's voice from a short recording, sometimes only a few seconds long. Criminals can easily find these recordings in videos that people post on social media. Then they simply type what they want the fake voice to say.\n\nAlthough the technology is new, the trick is an old one. Calls like this usually share three warning signs: there is an emergency, you are asked for money right away, and you are told to keep it a secret. These signs are designed to stop you from thinking clearly.\n\nExperts suggest two simple defenses. First, if a call makes you nervous, hang up and call the person back on a number you already know. Second, choose a family \"safe word\" or question, something that has never appeared online, and use it to check who is really calling. Mrs. Lin's family had chosen theirs almost as a joke. Now they are very glad they did.", "passage_zh": "某天晚上，林太太的手機響了。打來的是她在臺南讀大學的女兒佩珊，至少聽起來像是她。「媽，我出事了，」那個聲音哭著說，「我把一個男人的機車撞倒了，他說我今天一定要賠新臺幣五萬元，不然他就要報警。拜託不要告訴爸爸，直接把錢匯到這個帳戶就好。」那個聲音有佩珊柔和的口音，甚至連她一難過就會連叫兩聲「媽」的習慣都一樣。\n\n林太太抓起包包，正要往門口走去。這時她想起全家人幾個月前約定好的一件事。她深吸一口氣，問道：「我們家第一隻狗叫什麼名字？」電話那頭沉默了一下，接著就斷線了。她雙手發抖，撥了佩珊的號碼。女兒響兩聲就接起電話。她正在圖書館念書，完全不知道媽媽在說什麼。\n\n打電話來的根本不是佩珊，很可能是歹徒利用 AI 語音工具假冒的。如今，這類工具只需要一小段錄音，有時只要幾秒鐘，就能複製一個人的聲音。歹徒很容易就能從人們發布在社群媒體上的影片裡找到這些錄音。接著，他們只要打出想讓假聲音說的話就行了。\n\n雖然這項科技是新的，但這種騙術其實很老套。這類電話通常有三個共同的警訊：發生了緊急狀況、要你立刻付錢，以及要你保守祕密。這些手法就是為了讓你無法冷靜思考。\n\n專家建議兩個簡單的防範方法。第一，如果某通電話讓你緊張，就先掛斷，再用你原本就知道的號碼回撥給對方。第二，全家一起約定一個「安全暗語」或問題，必須是從來沒有出現在網路上的內容，用它來確認打來的到底是誰。林太太一家當初選定暗語時，幾乎只是當成玩笑。現在，他們非常慶幸當初這麼做了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a university student paid for a scooter accident", "A phone scam that uses a copied voice, and how to protect yourself", "Why parents should not let their children study far from home", "How AI tools help people make funny videos"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先描述林太太接到假冒女兒聲音的詐騙電話，第三段說明歹徒用 AI 複製聲音，第四、五段提出警訊與防範方法。(A) 撞倒機車是歹徒編的故事，佩珊並沒有出事；(C) 文中沒有討論子女是否該離家讀書；(D) 文中的 AI 工具是被用來詐騙，不是做搞笑影片。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did Mrs. Lin believe at first that the caller was her daughter?", "options": ["The call came from Pei-shan's own phone number.", "The caller knew the name of the family's first dog.", "The voice sounded like Pei-shan and even had her speaking habit.", "Pei-shan had told her about the accident earlier that day."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段最後一句：The voice had Pei-shan's soft accent and even her habit of saying \"Mom\" twice when she was upset. 聲音和說話習慣都很像女兒。(A) 文中沒有提到來電號碼；(B) 對方答不出狗的名字就掛斷了；(D) 佩珊根本不知道這件事。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 2, \"the line went dead\" means that ______.", "options": ["the phone call suddenly ended", "the caller started to cry", "the caller gave the right answer", "someone in the family died"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段林太太問了只有家人知道的問題後，There was a short silence, and then the line went dead，接著她就自己撥電話給佩珊，可知對方掛斷、通話突然中斷。(B)(C) 對方只是沉默，沒有哭也沒有回答；(D) 是 dead 字面上「死亡」的意思，不符合本文語境。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the caller most likely hang up after Mrs. Lin's question?", "options": ["The caller was angry that Mrs. Lin had told Pei-shan's father.", "The caller had already received the money.", "The caller wanted Mrs. Lin to call back on another number.", "The caller could not answer because he was not really Pei-shan."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說 The caller was not Pei-shan at all，是用 AI 複製聲音的歹徒；家庭暗語問題（第一隻狗的名字）只有真正的家人知道，歹徒答不出來只好掛斷。(A) 林太太並沒有告訴爸爸；(B) 她還沒有匯款；(C) 文中沒有這樣的情節。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, which of the following would be the best family \"safe word\"?", "options": ["A pet's name that appears in many of your public posts", "A word your family chose together and has never shared online", "Your mother's phone number", "The name of the school you go to"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：choose a family \"safe word\" or question, something that has never appeared online. 安全暗語必須是家人約定、從未在網路上出現的內容。(A) 已公開在貼文中，歹徒查得到；(C)(D) 電話號碼和校名都容易被別人知道。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a50", "title": "From Car Parts to Bubble Tea: The Story of the QR Code", "title_zh": "從汽車零件到珍珠奶茶：QR Code 的故事", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "History"], "passage": "Today, we scan QR codes to order bubble tea, pay bills and open websites. But these little black-and-white squares were not invented for any of these things. They were born in a car parts factory in Japan in 1994.\n\nAt that time, factories used barcodes to keep track of parts. A barcode could hold only about 20 characters, so workers sometimes had to scan many barcodes on a single box. Masahiro Hara, an engineer at Denso, a company that makes car parts, was asked to design something better. According to Hara, the idea came to him while he was playing Go, a board game with black and white stones on a grid. What if information could be stored in two directions, across and down, instead of in a single line?\n\nThe result was the QR code, and \"QR\" stands for \"Quick Response.\" One code can hold up to 7,089 numbers. The three large squares in its corners tell the scanner where the code is and which way it is turned, so it can be read quickly from any angle. A QR code can even be read when part of it is dirty or damaged. Just as importantly, Denso decided not to charge anyone for using the design. This allowed companies around the world to adopt it freely.\n\nFor years, few people outside Japan used QR codes in their daily lives. Things changed when smartphones with good cameras became common. During the COVID-19 pandemic, the codes became part of daily life almost everywhere. In 2021, for example, people in Taiwan scanned a QR code to send a text message before entering a shop or restaurant.\n\nTheir popularity, however, has also attracted criminals. Because people cannot read a QR code with their eyes, a fake one looks exactly like a real one. Some criminals stick their own codes over real ones on posters or parking machines, leading people to fake payment pages. So before you scan, check whether the code is a sticker placed on top of something else, and look at the web address before you enter any information.", "passage_zh": "如今，我們會掃描 QR Code 來點珍珠奶茶、繳帳單和開啟網站。但這些黑白相間的小方塊，當初並不是為了這些用途而發明的。它們誕生於 1994 年日本的一間汽車零件工廠。\n\n當時，工廠用條碼來追蹤零件。一個條碼只能存大約 20 個字元，所以工人有時必須在同一個箱子上掃描許多條碼。汽車零件製造商電裝（Denso）的工程師原昌宏受命設計出更好的東西。根據原昌宏的說法，這個點子是他在下圍棋時想到的；圍棋是一種在格狀棋盤上擺放黑白棋子的遊戲。如果資訊能以橫向和縱向兩個方向儲存，而不是只排成一條線，會怎麼樣呢？\n\n成果就是 QR Code，「QR」代表「Quick Response」（快速回應）。一個 QR Code 最多可以存 7,089 個數字。角落的三個大方塊會告訴掃描器條碼的位置和方向，因此不論從哪個角度都能快速讀取。即使 QR Code 有一部分髒了或損壞，仍然可以讀取。同樣重要的是，電裝決定不向任何人收取使用這項設計的費用。這讓世界各地的公司都能自由採用它。\n\n多年來，日本以外的地方很少有人在日常生活中使用 QR Code。當配備好相機的智慧型手機普及之後，情況就改變了。在新冠肺炎疫情期間，這些條碼幾乎在世界各地都成了日常生活的一部分。例如在 2021 年，臺灣民眾進入商店或餐廳前，要先掃描 QR Code 傳送一則簡訊。\n\n然而，QR Code 的普及也引來了歹徒。因為人無法用眼睛讀出 QR Code 的內容，假的看起來和真的一模一樣。有些歹徒會把自己的條碼貼在海報或停車繳費機上原本的條碼上面，把人引導到假的付款網頁。所以在掃描之前，要先檢查這個條碼是不是貼在其他東西上面的貼紙，並且在輸入任何資料前先看清楚網址。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the passage mainly discuss?", "options": ["The history of the QR code, its uses and a risk it brings", "How to play the board game Go", "Why barcodes are better than QR codes", "How Japanese car factories became successful"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹 QR Code 在 1994 年的誕生經過（第一至三段）、在日常生活中的普及（第四段），以及假 QR Code 帶來的詐騙風險（第五段）。(B) 圍棋只是發明靈感的來源；(C) 文中說條碼容量小，QR Code 是改良版，與選項相反；(D) 文中沒有討論日本車廠如何成功。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why was the QR code first designed?", "options": ["To help customers pay for drinks", "To help people check in at restaurants", "To store more information than barcodes for tracking car parts", "To stop criminals from making fake payment pages"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：factories used barcodes to keep track of parts. A barcode could hold only about 20 characters... 原昌宏被要求設計更好的東西，可知 QR Code 最初是為了在汽車零件工廠中存放比條碼更多的資訊。(A)(B) 是後來的用途（第一、四段）；(D) 假 QR Code 是第五段提到的新問題，不是發明目的。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what is the job of the three large squares in a QR code?", "options": ["They store the address of a website.", "They show the scanner where the code is and which way it is turned.", "They prove that the code is not a fake one.", "They make the code look like a Go board."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The three large squares in its corners tell the scanner where the code is and which way it is turned, so it can be read quickly from any angle. (A) 文中沒有說網址存在大方塊裡；(C) 第五段說假的 QR Code 看起來跟真的一模一樣，大方塊無法證明真假；(D) 圍棋只是靈感來源，不是大方塊的功能。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from Denso's decision not to charge anyone for using the QR code design?", "options": ["Denso lost a lot of money and stopped making car parts.", "QR codes could only be used inside Japan.", "Very few companies were interested in QR codes.", "It helped the QR code spread widely around the world."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Denso decided not to charge anyone for using the design. This allowed companies around the world to adopt it freely. 免費使用讓世界各地的公司都能採用，可推知這有助於 QR Code 在全球普及。(A) 文中沒有提到電裝虧損或停產；(B)(C) 都與「世界各地的公司自由採用」相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the last paragraph, what is the safest thing to do before scanning a QR code on a parking machine?", "options": ["Scan it quickly before someone else uses it.", "Enter your card number to see if the page is real.", "Check whether it is a sticker covering the original code.", "Clean the code so it can be read from any angle."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：before you scan, check whether the code is a sticker placed on top of something else. 歹徒會把假條碼貼在停車繳費機原本的條碼上，所以掃描前要先檢查是否為覆蓋上去的貼紙。(A) 匆忙掃描反而危險；(B) 文中提醒輸入資料前要先確認網址，不能用卡號測試；(D) 清潔條碼與防範詐騙無關。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a51", "title": "Can I Take My Power Bank on the Plane?", "title_zh": "行動電源可以帶上飛機嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Travel"], "passage": "Before his first trip abroad, Jun-hao packed a large power bank in his suitcase, thinking it was the safest place for it. At the check-in counter, a member of the airline staff asked him to open the suitcase and take it out. \"Power banks must go in your carry-on bag,\" she explained. Jun-hao was surprised, but there is a good reason behind the rule.\n\nPower banks use lithium-ion batteries, the same kind found in phones and laptops. These batteries store a lot of energy in a small space. If one is damaged, badly made or overheated, it can catch fire, and such fires are hard to put out. In the cabin, crew members can notice smoke quickly and deal with it. In the cargo hold under the floor, however, a fire might grow for a long time before anyone knows. That is why spare batteries are never allowed in checked luggage.\n\nSize matters, too. Airlines measure a battery's energy in watt-hours (Wh), but most power banks only show milliamp-hours (mAh) and voltage (V). To find the watt-hours, multiply the two numbers and divide by 1,000. A common 10,000 mAh power bank at 3.7 V holds about 37 Wh. Power banks up to 100 Wh are usually allowed. Those between 100 and 160 Wh need the airline's approval, and anything larger cannot be taken on a passenger plane at all.\n\nRecently, the rules have become even stricter. In early 2025, a fire broke out on a plane in South Korea, and investigators believed a power bank had started it. Since then, many airlines in Asia, including those in Taiwan, have stopped allowing passengers to use or charge power banks during flights.\n\nSo, before you fly, check the label on your power bank. If its capacity is not shown, it may be refused at security. Keep it in your carry-on bag, and remember to charge your phone before boarding. Jun-hao only lost a few minutes at the check-in counter, but since then, he has checked his bags carefully before every trip.", "passage_zh": "第一次出國前，俊豪把一個大型行動電源放進行李箱，以為那是最安全的地方。到了報到櫃檯，一位航空公司人員請他打開行李箱，把行動電源拿出來。「行動電源一定要放在手提行李裡，」她解釋。俊豪很驚訝，但這項規定背後有充分的理由。\n\n行動電源使用鋰離子電池，和手機、筆記型電腦裡的電池是同一種。這種電池在小小的空間裡儲存了大量的能量。如果電池受損、製造不良或過熱，就可能起火，而這種火很難撲滅。在客艙裡，空服員能很快察覺煙霧並加以處理。然而在地板下方的貨艙裡，火勢可能延燒很久都沒有人發現。這就是為什麼備用電池絕對不能放在託運行李中。\n\n容量大小也很重要。航空公司以瓦特小時（Wh）來計算電池的能量，但大部分行動電源只標示毫安培小時（mAh）和電壓（V）。要算出瓦特小時，就把這兩個數字相乘，再除以 1,000。一個常見的 10,000 mAh、3.7 V 的行動電源大約是 37 Wh。100 Wh 以下的行動電源通常可以攜帶；介於 100 到 160 Wh 之間的需要航空公司核准；超過 160 Wh 的則完全不能帶上客機。\n\n最近，規定變得更加嚴格。2025 年初，南韓一架飛機發生火災，調查人員認為起火原因是一個行動電源。從那之後，亞洲許多航空公司，包括臺灣的航空公司，都不再允許乘客在飛行途中使用行動電源或替行動電源充電。\n\n所以，搭飛機前要先檢查行動電源上的標籤。如果上面沒有標示容量，安檢時可能會被拒絕。把它放在手提行李中，並記得在登機前先把手機充好電。俊豪只在報到櫃檯耽誤了幾分鐘，但從那次之後，他每次出門旅行前都會仔細檢查自己的行李。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain the rules for taking power banks on planes and the reasons for them", "To advertise a new kind of power bank for travelers", "To describe everything that happened on Jun-hao's trip abroad", "To compare lithium-ion batteries with other kinds of batteries"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以俊豪的經驗開頭，接著說明行動電源必須放手提行李的原因（第二段）、容量限制（第三段）、2025 年後的新規定（第四段），以及搭機前該注意的事（第五段）。(B) 文中沒有推銷任何產品；(C) 只提到他在報到櫃檯的經過，並非整趟旅程；(D) 文中沒有比較不同種類的電池。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why are power banks NOT allowed in checked luggage?", "options": ["They are too heavy to put in suitcases.", "A fire in the cargo hold might not be noticed quickly.", "Passengers might forget them at the airport.", "Crew members need them to charge their own phones."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：In the cargo hold under the floor, however, a fire might grow for a long time before anyone knows. That is why spare batteries are never allowed in checked luggage. 貨艙裡起火不易被發現。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Mei has a 30,000 mAh power bank that works at 3.7 V. According to paragraph 3, what should she do?", "options": ["Put it in her suitcase and check it in.", "Nothing special, because it is under 100 Wh.", "Leave it at home, because it can never be taken on a plane.", "Get the airline's approval before taking it on board."], "answer": 3, "explain": "依第三段公式：30,000 × 3.7 ÷ 1,000 = 111 Wh，介於 100 到 160 Wh 之間，need the airline's approval，必須先取得航空公司核准。(A) 第二段說備用電池不能託運；(B) 111 Wh 已超過 100 Wh；(C) 只有超過 160 Wh 才完全不能帶上客機。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What has changed on many airlines in Asia since 2025?", "options": ["Passengers may not use or charge power banks during flights.", "Power banks must now be put in checked luggage.", "Power banks larger than 37 Wh are no longer allowed.", "Passengers must buy their power banks at the airport."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：many airlines in Asia, including those in Taiwan, have stopped allowing passengers to use or charge power banks during flights. (B) 與第二段「不能放託運行李」相反；(C) 37 Wh 只是第三段計算的例子，100 Wh 以下通常可攜帶；(D) 文中沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"refused\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["charged", "repaired", "not accepted", "weighed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：If its capacity is not shown, it may be refused at security. 沒有標示容量的行動電源在安檢時可能「被拒絕、不被接受」，因為人員無法確認它是否符合規定。(A)「充電」、(B)「修理」、(D)「秤重」都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a52", "title": "Why Does My Phone Know What I Like?", "title_zh": "為什麼我的手機知道我喜歡什麼？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Society"], "passage": "Have you ever watched one funny cat video and then found your screen full of cats for the rest of the night? That is not luck. Behind almost every video app and social media site is a recommendation system, a computer program that decides what to show you next.\n\nTo see how fast it works, Ms. Chiang, a teacher in Kaohsiung, tried an experiment with her class. Students made brand-new accounts on a short-video app using school tablets. One group watched every basketball video to the end and skipped everything else. Another group did the same with cooking videos. After only thirty minutes, the two groups' screens looked completely different. \"It's like the app read our minds,\" said one student.\n\nIn fact, the app was not reading minds; it was reading behavior. Recommendation systems notice what you click on, how long you watch, what you like or share, and what you skip after one second. The more you use an app, the more it learns about you. Its main goal is simple: to keep you watching, because the longer people stay, the more ads the company can show them.\n\nThis can be helpful. It is nice to find songs, recipes or study tips that match your interests. But there is a downside. The writer Eli Pariser called it a \"filter bubble.\" When you only see things you already agree with or enjoy, you may start to believe that everyone thinks the same way you do. Also, because the app always offers \"just one more video,\" it is easy to lose track of time.\n\nYou do not have to delete your apps to stay in control. Search for topics outside your usual interests now and then. Use the \"Not interested\" button when you see something you do not want more of. Follow people with different opinions, and set a time limit for yourself. Remember: the app is working hard to know you, so make sure you also know how the app works.", "passage_zh": "你有沒有過這種經驗：只看了一部好笑的貓咪影片，結果那天晚上整個螢幕都是貓？這可不是運氣。幾乎每個影音 App 和社群網站背後，都有一套推薦系統，也就是決定接下來要給你看什麼內容的電腦程式。\n\n為了看看它運作得有多快，高雄的江老師和班上同學做了一個實驗。學生們用學校的平板電腦，在一個短影音 App 上註冊全新的帳號。其中一組把每一部籃球影片都看到最後，其他影片一律跳過；另一組則對烹飪影片做同樣的事。才過了三十分鐘，兩組的螢幕畫面就完全不同了。「就好像這個 App 能看穿我們的心思一樣，」一位學生說。\n\n事實上，這個 App 讀的不是人心，而是行為。推薦系統會注意你點了什麼、看了多久、對什麼按讚或分享，以及什麼內容你看一秒就滑走。你使用 App 的時間越多，它對你的了解就越多。它的主要目標很簡單：讓你一直看下去，因為人們停留得越久，公司就能給他們看越多廣告。\n\n這可能很有幫助。能找到符合你興趣的歌曲、食譜或讀書技巧是很棒的事。但它也有缺點。作家伊萊．帕理澤把這種現象稱為「過濾泡泡」（filter bubble）。當你只看到自己本來就認同或喜歡的東西，你可能會開始以為每個人的想法都跟你一樣。此外，因為 App 總是提供「再看一部就好」的影片，人很容易忘了時間。\n\n你不必刪除 App 也能保有主控權。偶爾搜尋一些你平常不感興趣的主題。看到不想再看到的內容時，就按「不感興趣」。追蹤一些意見和你不同的人，並替自己設定使用時間上限。記住：App 正努力地了解你，所以你也要確定自己了解 App 是怎麼運作的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to make funny cat videos that many people will watch", "How recommendation systems choose what we see, and how to stay in control", "Why teachers should use tablets in class", "How companies make basketball and cooking videos"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段點出主題「推薦系統」，第二、三段以實驗說明它如何根據使用者行為決定內容，第四段談優缺點，第五段建議如何保有主控權。(A) 貓咪影片只是開頭的例子；(C) 平板只是實驗工具；(D) 籃球和烹飪影片是實驗內容，文章並未說明如何製作影片。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What did Ms. Chiang's experiment show?", "options": ["Apps can really read their users' thoughts.", "Students prefer cooking videos to basketball videos.", "School tablets work more slowly than personal phones.", "A video app can quickly change what it shows based on how people use it."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：只看了三十分鐘，看籃球影片和看烹飪影片的兩組，畫面就 looked completely different，說明 App 會依使用行為很快改變推薦內容。(A) 第三段明說 the app was not reading minds; it was reading behavior，學生的話只是比喻；(B)(C) 實驗並未比較這些。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why do the companies want people to keep watching?", "options": ["So that they can show people more ads", "So that people can find more study tips", "So that their computer programs can take a rest", "So that people will make more new accounts"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段最後一句：to keep you watching, because the longer people stay, the more ads the company can show them. 人們停留越久，公司就能播放越多廣告。(B) 讀書技巧是第四段提到推薦系統的好處，不是公司的目的；(C)(D) 文中沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"downside\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a good point", "a lower floor", "a disadvantage", "a hidden button"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段先說 This can be helpful（好處），接著用 But there is a downside 轉折，後面描述「過濾泡泡」和容易忘了時間等問題，可知 downside 是「缺點、壞處」。(A) 與 But 的轉折相反；(B) 是字面上「下方」的聯想；(D) 與文意無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would the writer most likely suggest?", "options": ["Delete all video apps right away.", "Search for new topics sometimes and set a time limit.", "Watch only the kinds of videos you already enjoy.", "Press \"like\" on every video you see."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段建議偶爾搜尋平常不感興趣的主題、使用「不感興趣」按鈕、追蹤意見不同的人，並 set a time limit for yourself。(A) 第五段第一句說 You do not have to delete your apps；(C) 正是第四段「過濾泡泡」的問題；(D) 每部影片都按讚只會讓推薦系統收集更多你的行為資料，文中並沒有這樣建議。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a53", "title": "Grandpa's Morning Messages", "title_zh": "爺爺的早安訊息", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Society", "Technology"], "passage": "Every morning at six, my grandpa sends a message to our family LINE group. Usually it is a picture of flowers with the words \"Good morning! Have a nice day!\" We all reply with a sticker, and that is the end of it. But last month, one of his messages started a small family storm.\n\n\"Doctors say that drinking hot lemon water every morning can kill cancer cells!\" the message said. \"Share this with everyone you love!\" Within minutes, my aunt had forwarded it to her friends. I knew it was not true, because our health teacher had talked about this very rumor in class. I started typing, \"Grandpa, this is fake news,\" but my mom saw what I was doing and stopped me before I could press \"Send.\" \"Don't correct him in front of the whole family,\" she said. \"He'll feel embarrassed.\"\n\nSo that weekend, I visited Grandpa and sat down next to him with his phone. I didn't tell him he was wrong. Instead, I asked, \"Shall we check where this came from?\" I showed him how to forward a message to Cofacts, a LINE chatbot run by volunteers in Taiwan. A few seconds later, it replied with a link to an article explaining that there is no scientific evidence for the claim. Grandpa read it twice and laughed. \"I thought I was helping everyone,\" he said.\n\nThen we talked about how to recognize suspicious messages. Many of them do not say which doctor or which study the information comes from. They try to make you feel afraid or excited. And they almost always ask you to share them quickly. Grandpa wrote these three warning signs on a piece of paper and stuck it next to his desk.\n\nNow Grandpa checks every surprising message before he shares it. He has even taught his friends at the community center how to use the chatbot, and they call him \"Detective Chou.\" His good-morning flowers still arrive at six o'clock every day, but the fake health tips have stopped. I learned something too: the best way to fight false information is often with patience, not anger.", "passage_zh": "每天早上六點，我爺爺都會在我們家族的 LINE 群組裡傳一則訊息。通常是一張花的圖片，配上「早安！祝你有美好的一天！」這幾個字。我們大家都回一個貼圖，事情就這樣結束了。但上個月，他的一則訊息在家裡掀起了一場小風暴。\n\n「醫生說，每天早上喝熱檸檬水可以殺死癌細胞！」訊息上寫著，「請分享給你愛的每一個人！」幾分鐘內，我阿姨就已經把它轉傳給她的朋友了。我知道這不是真的，因為我們的健康教育老師上課時才談過這個謠言。我開始打字：「爺爺，這是假新聞。」但媽媽看到我在做什麼，在我按下「傳送」之前就阻止了我。「不要在全家人面前糾正他，」她說，「他會覺得很難為情。」\n\n所以那個週末，我去探望爺爺，拿著他的手機坐到他身旁。我沒有說他錯了，而是問他：「我們要不要查查看這是從哪裡來的？」我教他怎麼把訊息轉傳給 Cofacts（真的假的），這是一個由臺灣志工經營的 LINE 聊天機器人。幾秒鐘後，它回覆了一篇文章的連結，說明這個說法並沒有科學證據。爺爺讀了兩遍，然後笑了。「我還以為我是在幫大家呢，」他說。\n\n接著我們聊到如何辨認可疑的訊息。很多這類訊息都沒有說明資料是來自哪位醫生或哪項研究。它們會試圖讓你感到害怕或興奮。而且它們幾乎總是要你趕快分享出去。爺爺把這三個警訊寫在一張紙上，貼在他的書桌旁邊。\n\n現在，爺爺每次看到令人驚訝的訊息，都會先查證再分享。他甚至教社區活動中心的朋友們使用這個聊天機器人，大家都叫他「周偵探」。他的早安花朵圖片仍然每天六點準時送達，但假的健康小撇步已經不見了。我也學到了一件事：對抗錯誤資訊最好的方法，往往是耐心，而不是憤怒。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the story mainly about?", "options": ["How the writer helped Grandpa learn to check messages before sharing them", "Why drinking lemon water is good for people's health", "How to send stickers in a family LINE group", "Why the writer's aunt stopped using LINE"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文描述作者發現爺爺轉傳假的健康訊息後，私下教他用 Cofacts 查證並辨認可疑訊息，最後爺爺養成先查證再分享的習慣。(B) 文中說「熱檸檬水殺癌細胞」沒有科學證據；(C) 貼圖只是第一段的生活細節；(D) 文中沒有說阿姨停用 LINE。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the writer's mom stop the writer from replying in the family group?", "options": ["She believed the message about lemon water was true.", "She did not want Grandpa to feel embarrassed in front of everyone.", "She thought the writer should be studying instead.", "She wanted to check the message by herself first."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段媽媽說：\"Don't correct him in front of the whole family... He'll feel embarrassed.\" 她不希望爺爺在全家人面前難堪。(A) 她並沒有說訊息是真的；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"suspicious\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["funny", "long", "possibly false or dishonest", "polite"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段在談如何辨認 suspicious messages，後面列出的特徵是沒有來源、刻意引起恐懼或興奮、要你趕快分享，都是假訊息的警訊，所以 suspicious 指「可疑的、可能不實的」。(A)「好笑的」、(B)「長的」、(D)「有禮貌的」都與這些警訊無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of the warning signs that Grandpa wrote down?", "options": ["The message does not say where its information comes from.", "The message tries to make you feel afraid or excited.", "The message asks you to share it quickly.", "The message is sent early in the morning."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段列出三個警訊：沒有說明資料來自哪位醫生或哪項研究、試圖讓你害怕或興奮、要你趕快分享，分別對應 (A)(B)(C)。「早上傳送」並不是警訊，爺爺每天早上六點傳的早安圖就不是假訊息。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Grandpa from the last paragraph?", "options": ["He has stopped sending messages to the family group.", "He has become someone who helps others check information.", "He no longer trusts any health information at all.", "He has started working as a real police detective."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說爺爺會先查證再分享，還教社區活動中心的朋友使用聊天機器人，大家叫他 \"Detective Chou\"，可推知他成了幫助別人查證資訊的人。(A) 他的早安圖仍每天六點送達；(C) 文中只說假的健康小撇步不見了，沒說他不相信任何健康資訊；(D)「偵探」只是朋友給他的綽號。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a54", "title": "Bells on Our Backpacks: Sharing the Mountains with Taiwan's Black Bear", "title_zh": "背包上的鈴鐺：與臺灣黑熊共享山林", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Nature", "Environment"], "passage": "Last summer, our school hiking club spent three days on the Batongguan Historic Trail in Yushan National Park. Before we set off, a park ranger named Mr. Hu gave each of us a small bell to tie to our backpacks. \"These are not for decoration,\" he said. \"They tell the bears that you are coming.\" We laughed, but he was serious.\n\nThe Formosan black bear is the only kind of bear native to Taiwan. It is easy to recognize by the white V-shaped mark on its chest, which looks like a crescent moon. Adult males can weigh more than 100 kilograms. The bears live mainly in mountain forests between 1,000 and 3,500 meters above sea level. No one knows exactly how many are left, but their numbers are small, so the bear is protected by law.\n\nAlthough bears look frightening, Mr. Hu explained that they are actually shy and usually avoid people. Most of their food comes from plants. In autumn, they climb oak trees to eat acorns, the small nuts that help them store fat for the winter. They also eat fruit, insects and sometimes small animals. \"A bear that hears you will usually walk away,\" he said. \"Trouble happens when you surprise one at close range.\"\n\nHe also told us what to do if we met a bear. Stay calm and never run, because running may make the bear chase you. Instead, keep facing the bear and slowly back away while speaking in a low, steady voice. Never feed a bear or move closer to take a photo. At night, we packed our food and trash tightly so that the smell would not attract visitors.\n\nThe bigger danger, however, is people hurting bears. Some hunters set traps called snares to catch wild boars and deer, and bears sometimes step into them by mistake. Several rescued bears have lost toes or even whole paws this way. On our last day, we found fresh claw marks on a tree beside the trail, but no bear. I was a little disappointed, yet I also felt glad. Somewhere in the forest, a bear had heard our bells and chosen to stay out of our way.", "passage_zh": "去年夏天，我們學校的登山社在玉山國家公園的八通關古道走了三天。出發前，一位姓胡的保育巡查員發給我們每人一個小鈴鐺，要我們綁在背包上。「這些可不是裝飾品，」他說，「它們會告訴黑熊你們來了。」我們都笑了，但他是認真的。\n\n臺灣黑熊是臺灣唯一的原生熊類。牠很容易辨認，因為胸前有一塊白色的 V 字形斑紋，看起來像一彎新月。成年公熊的體重可以超過一百公斤。黑熊主要生活在海拔一千到三千五百公尺之間的山區森林。沒有人確切知道還剩下多少隻，但牠們的數量很少，因此受到法律保護。\n\n胡先生解釋，雖然黑熊看起來很可怕，但牠們其實很害羞，通常會避開人類。牠們大部分的食物來自植物。秋天時，牠們會爬上橡樹吃橡實，這種小小的堅果能幫助牠們為冬天儲存脂肪。牠們也吃水果、昆蟲，有時也吃小動物。「聽到你們聲音的熊通常會自己走開，」他說，「麻煩往往發生在你近距離嚇到牠的時候。」\n\n他也告訴我們萬一遇到黑熊該怎麼做。保持冷靜，千萬不要跑，因為逃跑可能會讓熊追你。你應該面對著熊，慢慢往後退，同時用低沉而平穩的聲音說話。絕對不要餵熊，也不要為了拍照而靠近。晚上，我們把食物和垃圾都包得緊緊的，這樣氣味才不會引來「訪客」。\n\n然而，更大的危險其實是人類傷害黑熊。有些獵人會設置一種叫做「吊索」的陷阱來捕捉山豬和鹿，而黑熊有時會不小心踩進去。好幾隻被救援的黑熊就因此失去了腳趾，甚至整隻熊掌。最後一天，我們在步道旁的一棵樹上發現了新鮮的爪痕，卻沒有看到熊。我有一點失望，但也覺得很高興。在森林的某個地方，有一隻熊聽到了我們的鈴聲，選擇避開了我們。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why hikers should stay away from Yushan National Park", "What the writer learned about Taiwan's black bears and how to share the mountains with them", "How park rangers train black bears to stay away from people", "Why the writer's hiking club failed to take a photo of a black bear"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文以作者在八通關古道登山的經驗為主軸，依序介紹臺灣黑熊的外型與棲地（第二段）、個性與食性（第三段）、遇熊時的應對方法（第四段）以及牠們面臨的威脅（第五段），也就是如何與黑熊共享山林。(A) 文中沒有勸人別去玉山國家公園；(C) 胡先生教的是登山客，並非訓練黑熊；(D) 文中只說不要為了拍照而靠近熊，從未提到登山社想拍熊；沒看到熊只是結尾的細節。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to Mr. Hu, what is the purpose of the bells?", "options": ["To call for help if a hiker gets lost", "To keep bears away from the hikers' food at night", "To let bears know that people are coming", "To decorate the hikers' backpacks"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段胡先生說 \"They tell the bears that you are coming.\"，鈴鐺是讓熊知道有人來了；第三段他也說聽到人聲的熊通常會自己走開。(A) 文中沒有提到求救；(B) 晚上是靠把食物和垃圾包緊來避免氣味吸引動物，與鈴鐺無關；(D) 胡先生明白說 \"These are not for decoration\"。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what should a hiker do if he or she meets a black bear?", "options": ["Face the bear and move back slowly while talking calmly", "Turn around and run away as fast as possible", "Throw some food to the bear so that it will leave", "Stay still and quietly take a photo of the bear"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：keep facing the bear and slowly back away while speaking in a low, steady voice，也就是面對熊、慢慢後退並用平穩的聲音說話。(B) 文中說 never run；(C) 文中說 Never feed a bear；(D) 文中說不要為了拍照而靠近熊。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"snares\" in the last paragraph refers to ______.", "options": ["bears that have been rescued", "marks that bears leave on trees", "bells that hikers tie to their bags", "traps used to catch wild animals"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：Some hunters set traps called snares to catch wild boars and deer，snares 就是獵人用來捕捉野生動物的陷阱（吊索）。(A) 被救援的熊是受害者；(B) 熊留在樹上的是 claw marks（爪痕）；(C) 鈴鐺是 bells。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did the writer feel glad at the end of the trip?", "options": ["Because the hikers finally saw a bear up close", "Because the claw marks proved that bears are not dangerous", "Because it seemed that a bear had avoided them, just as Mr. Hu said", "Because the trip was over and the writer could go home"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段結尾說 a bear had heard our bells and chosen to stay out of our way，對照第三段胡先生所說「聽到人聲的熊通常會走開」，作者高興是因為熊似乎真的聽到鈴聲而避開了他們。(A) 他們並沒有看到熊；(B) 爪痕只表示有熊來過，不能證明熊不危險；(D) 文中沒有提到作者想回家。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a55", "title": "More Than a Day Off: Understanding Typhoons", "title_zh": "不只是放假：認識颱風", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "For many students in Taiwan, the word \"typhoon\" brings one question to mind: Will there be a day off tomorrow? Every summer, families stay up late waiting for the news. But behind these announcements is a powerful storm that deserves our respect, not just our excitement.\n\nA typhoon is born over warm tropical seas. When the surface of the ocean reaches about 26.5 degrees Celsius or higher, large amounts of warm, wet air rise into the sky. As the air rises, it cools and forms huge clouds, and more air rushes in from below to take its place. Because the Earth is turning, this moving air begins to spin. In the Northern Hemisphere, typhoons spin counterclockwise, the opposite direction to the way a clock's hands move.\n\nAt the center of a strong typhoon is the \"eye,\" an area of calm air that can be dozens of kilometers wide. Inside the eye, the wind is light and the sky may even be blue. This can fool people. When the eye passes over a town, some people think the storm is over and go outside, only to be caught by violent winds again when the other side of the storm arrives.\n\nTaiwan lies right in the common path of typhoons crossing the western Pacific, and most of them arrive between July and October. The Central Mountain Range often weakens a typhoon's winds, but the mountains also force the wet air upward, squeezing heavy rain out of the clouds. In August 2009, Typhoon Morakot dropped about 3,000 millimeters of rain on some mountain areas in just a few days, more than many places receive in a whole year. Floods and landslides destroyed entire villages.\n\nStill, typhoons are not only bad news. Their rain fills the reservoirs that supply water to homes, farms and factories. So the next time a typhoon day off is announced, remember that the decision is made for safety, not for fun. Stay indoors, keep away from rivers, mountains and the coast, and be thankful for the water the storm brings.", "passage_zh": "對許多臺灣學生來說，「颱風」這個詞會讓人立刻想到一個問題：明天會放假嗎？每年夏天，許多家庭都會熬夜等待消息。但在這些宣布的背後，是一個值得我們敬畏、而不只是讓人興奮的強大風暴。\n\n颱風誕生在溫暖的熱帶海洋上。當海面水溫達到約攝氏 26.5 度以上時，大量溫暖潮濕的空氣會上升到天空中。空氣上升時會冷卻並形成巨大的雲，同時更多空氣從下方湧入，填補原本的位置。由於地球在自轉，這些流動的空氣便開始旋轉。在北半球，颱風以逆時針方向旋轉，也就是和時鐘指針轉動的方向相反。\n\n強烈颱風的中心是「颱風眼」，這是一片空氣平靜的區域，寬度可達數十公里。在颱風眼裡，風很小，天空甚至可能是藍色的。這可能會騙過人們。當颱風眼經過某個城鎮時，有些人以為颱風已經結束而走到戶外，結果當風暴的另一側來到時，又遭到猛烈的強風侵襲。\n\n臺灣正好位在颱風橫越西太平洋時常走的路徑上，而大多數颱風在七月到十月之間來襲。中央山脈常會減弱颱風的風力，但山脈也會迫使潮濕的空氣往上升，從雲層中「擠」出大量雨水。2009 年 8 月，莫拉克颱風在短短幾天內就在部分山區降下約 3,000 毫米的雨量，比許多地方一整年的雨量還多。洪水和山崩摧毀了整座村莊。\n\n不過，颱風並不全是壞消息。颱風帶來的雨水會填滿水庫，供應家庭、農田和工廠用水。所以下次宣布放颱風假時，請記得這個決定是為了安全，而不是為了讓你玩樂。待在室內，遠離河川、山區和海邊，並且感謝風暴帶來的水。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in this passage?", "options": ["To help readers understand typhoons and take them seriously", "To explain how local governments decide on typhoon days off", "To describe the damage caused by Typhoon Morakot in detail", "To encourage students to have fun on typhoon days off"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段指出颱風假背後是「值得我們敬畏」的強大風暴，接著說明颱風如何形成（第二段）、颱風眼（第三段）、對臺灣的影響（第四段），最後提醒放颱風假是為了安全，可見目的在讓讀者認識並重視颱風。(B) 文中沒有說明政府如何決定是否放假；(C) 莫拉克颱風只是第四段的一個例子；(D) 與最後一段 the decision is made for safety, not for fun 相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, which of the following is needed for a typhoon to form?", "options": ["Cold air coming down from the mountains", "Strong winds blowing in a clockwise direction", "A large area of dry land with high temperatures", "Ocean water that is about 26.5°C or warmer"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：When the surface of the ocean reaches about 26.5 degrees Celsius or higher, large amounts of warm, wet air rise into the sky. 颱風需要約攝氏 26.5 度以上的溫暖海水。(A) 文中沒有提到山上的冷空氣；(B) 北半球的颱風是逆時針旋轉；(C) 颱風誕生在海洋上，不是乾燥的陸地。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why can the eye of a typhoon be dangerous?", "options": ["It brings the heaviest rain of the whole storm.", "People may think the storm has ended and go outside too early.", "It is so wide that it covers the whole of Taiwan.", "Its winds spin faster than those in any other part of the storm."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：some people think the storm is over and go outside, only to be caught by violent winds again when the other side of the storm arrives. 颱風眼風小、天空可能放晴，會讓人誤以為颱風已經過去而出門。(A) 文中沒有說颱風眼的雨最大；(C) 颱風眼寬約數十公里，不可能涵蓋全臺；(D) 文中說颱風眼裡 the wind is light，與此相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"violent\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["gentle", "cool", "extremely strong", "unusual"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段 caught by violent winds again 指颱風的另一側來到時，人們又遭到「猛烈的」強風侵襲，violent 意為「劇烈的、極強的」，與 extremely strong 最接近。(A) gentle「溫和的」意思相反；(B) cool「涼爽的」與 (D) unusual「不尋常的」都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?", "options": ["Most typhoons hit Taiwan between January and April.", "The Central Mountain Range usually makes typhoon winds stronger.", "Typhoons bring nothing but damage to Taiwan.", "Typhoon rain helps fill the reservoirs that people depend on."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：Their rain fills the reservoirs that supply water to homes, farms and factories. 颱風的雨水能填滿供應用水的水庫。(A) 第四段說大多數颱風在七月到十月來襲；(B) 第四段說中央山脈常會「減弱」颱風的風力；(C) 第五段說 typhoons are not only bad news。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a56", "title": "The Great Escape of Inky the Octopus", "title_zh": "章魚 Inky 的大逃亡", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "One night in 2016, workers at the National Aquarium of New Zealand left the lid of a tank slightly open. When they returned the next morning, Inky, the octopus who lived in that tank, was gone. He had squeezed through the small gap, slid across the floor and found a narrow drainpipe that led to the sea. Inky never came back, and his escape quickly became news around the world.\n\nHow could an animal do something so clever? Part of the answer is its body. An octopus has no bones at all. The only hard part is its beak, a sharp mouth shaped like a parrot's. This means it can pass through almost any opening larger than its beak. Even a fairly large octopus can get through a hole about the size of a coin.\n\nIts brain is also unusual. An octopus has about 500 million nerve cells, far more than most other animals without a backbone. However, around two-thirds of them are not in its head but in its eight arms. As a result, each arm can touch, taste and even make simple decisions on its own. In aquariums, octopuses have learned to open jars, recognize their keepers and even spray water at people they do not like.\n\nOctopuses are also masters of disguise. Special cells in their skin let them change color in less than a second, and some can even change the texture of their skin to look like rocks or seaweed. If an enemy still comes close, they shoot out a cloud of dark ink and swim away. Inside, their bodies are just as strange: an octopus has three hearts and blue blood.\n\nSurprisingly, such a smart animal lives a very short life. Most octopuses live only one or two years, and they grow up alone, without parents to teach them. Everything they know, they must learn by themselves. Perhaps that is why scientists find them so fascinating. Inky's escape was more than a funny news story. It was a reminder that intelligence on Earth can take forms very different from our own.", "passage_zh": "2016 年的某天晚上，紐西蘭國家水族館的工作人員沒有把一個水族箱的蓋子完全蓋好。隔天早上他們回來時，住在那個水族箱裡的章魚 Inky 已經不見了。牠從那道小縫鑽了出去，滑過地板，找到一根通往大海的細窄排水管。Inky 再也沒有回來，牠的逃亡很快就成了全世界的新聞。\n\n動物怎麼可能做出這麼聰明的事？部分答案在於牠的身體。章魚全身完全沒有骨頭，唯一堅硬的部分是牠的喙，也就是一張形狀像鸚鵡嘴的尖銳嘴巴。這表示只要開口比牠的喙大，牠幾乎都能穿過去。即使是體型相當大的章魚，也能鑽過一個大約硬幣大小的洞。\n\n牠的大腦也很特別。一隻章魚大約有五億個神經細胞，遠多於其他大多數無脊椎動物。然而，其中大約三分之二並不在頭部，而是在牠的八隻腕足裡。因此，每一隻腕足都能觸摸、品嚐，甚至自己做出簡單的決定。在水族館裡，章魚曾學會打開瓶罐、認出牠們的飼育員，甚至會朝自己不喜歡的人噴水。\n\n章魚也是偽裝高手。牠們皮膚裡的特殊細胞能讓牠們在不到一秒內改變顏色，有些甚至能改變皮膚的質感，看起來就像岩石或海藻。如果敵人仍然靠近，牠們就會噴出一團黑色墨汁，然後游走。牠們的身體內部同樣奇特：章魚有三顆心臟，而且血液是藍色的。\n\n令人意外的是，這麼聰明的動物壽命卻非常短。大多數章魚只能活一到兩年，而且牠們獨自長大，沒有父母教導。牠們所知道的一切都必須靠自己學會。也許這就是科學家覺得牠們如此迷人的原因。Inky 的逃亡不只是一則有趣的新聞，它也提醒我們：地球上的智慧，可以有和我們截然不同的形式。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How aquariums can stop their animals from escaping", "Why octopuses are in danger in the world's oceans", "The special features that make octopuses clever and unusual animals", "How Inky was finally found and brought back to the aquarium"], "answer": 2, "explain": "文章以 Inky 逃亡事件開頭，接著介紹章魚沒有骨頭的身體（第二段）、特殊的神經系統（第三段）、偽裝能力與三顆心臟（第四段）以及短暫的一生（第五段），主旨是章魚聰明又奇特的各種特徵。(A) 文中沒有談水族館如何防止動物逃脫；(B) 文中沒有提到章魚面臨危險；(D) 第一段說 Inky never came back。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How did Inky get out of the aquarium?", "options": ["He squeezed through a gap and escaped through a drainpipe.", "A worker carried him to the sea by mistake.", "He broke the glass of his tank with his beak.", "He hid in a jar and was taken out by a visitor."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段：had squeezed through the small gap, slid across the floor and found a narrow drainpipe that led to the sea，Inky 從縫隙鑽出，經由排水管逃到海裡。(B) 不是工作人員帶走的；(C) 文中沒有打破玻璃；(D) 打開瓶罐是第三段提到章魚的其他能力，與這次逃脫無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why can an octopus get through very small holes?", "options": ["Its arms can make decisions by themselves.", "It can change the color of its skin.", "It can shoot out ink to make a hole bigger.", "Its body has no bones, and only its beak is hard."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：An octopus has no bones at all. The only hard part is its beak... it can pass through almost any opening larger than its beak. 因為沒有骨頭、只有喙是硬的，所以能鑽過小洞。(A) 腕足能自己做決定是第三段的內容；(B) 變色是用來偽裝；(C) 墨汁是用來擺脫敵人，不能把洞變大。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"disguise\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["fighting enemies with strong arms", "changing one's appearance in order to hide", "swimming very fast in deep water", "making friends with other animals"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說章魚能改變顏色和皮膚的質感，看起來像岩石或海藻，可見 masters of disguise 指「偽裝高手」，disguise 是改變外表來隱藏自己。(A)(C)(D) 都與改變外觀、躲藏起來無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the last paragraph?", "options": ["Young octopuses do not learn their skills from their parents.", "Octopuses are smarter than all other animals.", "Octopuses live longer in aquariums than in the sea.", "Scientists do not think octopuses are worth studying."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：they grow up alone, without parents to teach them. Everything they know, they must learn by themselves. 可推知章魚的本領不是向父母學來的。(B) 文中只說章魚聰明，沒有說牠比所有動物都聰明；(C) 文中沒有比較水族館和海中的壽命；(D) 與 scientists find them so fascinating 相反。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a57", "title": "A Highway That Makes Room for Butterflies", "title_zh": "為蝴蝶讓路的高速公路", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Environment", "Nature"], "passage": "Every spring, drivers on National Freeway No. 3 near Linnei, Yunlin County, may notice something unusual: one lane is closed, not for road work, but for butterflies. On the busiest days, thousands of purple crow butterflies may fly across the highway in a single hour, and the government has decided to make room for them.\n\nPurple crow butterflies have dark brown wings that shine blue and purple in the sunlight. Four kinds of them live in Taiwan. In winter, they gather in warm, sheltered valleys in the south, such as those in Maolin, Kaohsiung, where the mountains block the cold northeast wind. From about November to March, huge numbers of them rest on the trees there, forming what people call \"butterfly valleys.\" It is often said that Taiwan and Mexico, where monarch butterflies spend the winter, are the only two places in the world where butterflies gather in such large groups.\n\nWhen the weather warms up in spring, the butterflies leave the valleys and head north. Many of them travel along the western side of the Central Mountain Range, and near Linnei they must cross the busy highway. Because they fly low, many used to be hit by cars and trucks. Volunteers who studied the butterflies found large numbers of dead ones on the road every spring.\n\nIn 2007, after volunteers and scientists asked for help, the freeway authorities took action. They put up tall nets beside the road, which make the butterflies rise above the traffic. When large numbers are crossing, they also close the outside lane and warn drivers to slow down. Thanks to these steps, far fewer butterflies are killed on the road today.\n\nThis story shows that protecting nature does not always require huge changes. Sometimes it only takes one closed lane, some nets and drivers who are willing to wait a few extra minutes. For a few weeks each spring, a busy highway becomes a road shared by people and butterflies.", "passage_zh": "每年春天，行經雲林縣林內附近國道 3 號的駕駛人，可能會注意到一件不尋常的事：有一條車道被封閉了，原因不是道路施工，而是為了蝴蝶。在最繁忙的日子裡，一小時內可能就有數千隻紫斑蝶飛越這條高速公路，而政府決定為牠們讓出空間。\n\n紫斑蝶的翅膀是深褐色的，在陽光下會閃耀出藍紫色的光澤。臺灣共有四種紫斑蝶。冬天時，牠們會聚集在南部溫暖、有遮蔽的山谷中，例如高雄茂林一帶，那裡的山脈擋住了寒冷的東北風。大約從十一月到隔年三月，大批紫斑蝶停棲在那裡的樹上，形成人們所說的「蝴蝶谷」。常有人說，臺灣和帝王斑蝶過冬的墨西哥，是全世界僅有的兩個蝴蝶會如此大規模聚集的地方。\n\n春天天氣變暖時，紫斑蝶便離開山谷向北飛。許多紫斑蝶沿著中央山脈西側前進，到了林內附近就必須飛越繁忙的高速公路。因為牠們飛得很低，過去有許多紫斑蝶被汽車和卡車撞死。研究紫斑蝶的志工每年春天都在路面上發現大量死去的蝴蝶。\n\n2007 年，在志工和科學家請求協助之後，高速公路主管機關採取了行動。他們在路旁架設高高的網子，讓蝴蝶飛升到車流上方。當大量蝴蝶正在飛越時，他們也會封閉外側車道，並提醒駕駛人減速慢行。多虧了這些措施，如今在路上死亡的蝴蝶已經大幅減少。\n\n這個故事告訴我們，保護大自然不一定需要巨大的改變。有時只需要封閉一條車道、架設一些網子，再加上願意多等幾分鐘的駕駛人。每年春天有幾個星期，一條繁忙的高速公路會成為人類與蝴蝶共享的道路。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why purple crow butterflies are more beautiful than monarch butterflies", "How engineers build highways through high mountains", "Why drivers in Yunlin often get stuck in traffic jams", "How a highway in Taiwan was changed to protect traveling butterflies"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文說明紫斑蝶春天北返時必須飛越國道 3 號，以及高速公路架設網子、封閉外側車道來保護牠們，主旨是臺灣一條高速公路為了保護遷徙的蝴蝶而做出改變。(A) 文中沒有比較兩種蝴蝶的美；(B) 文中沒有談如何建造公路；(C) 文中沒有提到塞車問題。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, when can people see large numbers of purple crow butterflies in the butterfly valleys?", "options": ["From about July to October", "From about November to March", "Only for a few days in early summer", "All year round"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：From about November to March, huge numbers of them rest on the trees there, forming what people call \"butterfly valleys.\" 大約從十一月到隔年三月可以看到大批紫斑蝶。(A)(C)(D) 的時間都與文中不符；而且第三段說春天變暖後牠們就會離開山谷。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the main purpose of the tall nets beside the highway?", "options": ["To make the butterflies fly higher than the cars and trucks", "To catch the butterflies so that scientists can study them", "To stop drivers from leaving the highway", "To keep the cold northeast wind away from the valleys"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：They put up tall nets beside the road, which make the butterflies rise above the traffic. 網子是讓蝴蝶飛高、越過車流。(B) 網子不是用來捕捉蝴蝶的；(C) 文中沒有提到；(D) 擋住東北風的是南部山谷周圍的山脈（第二段），不是網子。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"sheltered\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["crowded", "dry", "protected from wind and bad weather", "empty"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段 warm, sheltered valleys... where the mountains block the cold northeast wind，山脈擋住了寒風，可知 sheltered 意為「有遮蔽的、受保護的」。(A) crowded「擁擠的」、(B) dry「乾燥的」、(D) empty「空的」都不符合上下文。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward the steps taken on the highway?", "options": ["Worried", "Approving", "Doubtful", "Uninterested"], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一段作者說保護自然不一定需要巨大的改變，並形容這條高速公路成為 a road shared by people and butterflies，語氣正面，可見作者贊同這些措施。(A) worried「擔心的」、(C) doubtful「懷疑的」、(D) uninterested「不感興趣的」都與文中肯定的語氣不符。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a58", "title": "When Thunder Roars: Three Myths About Lightning", "title_zh": "雷聲響起時：關於閃電的三個迷思", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "On summer afternoons in Taiwan, a bright, sunny morning can quickly turn into a thunderstorm. The hot sun heats the ground, warm wet air rises fast, and by three or four o'clock, dark clouds and flashes of lightning fill the sky. A single flash can heat the air around it to about 30,000 degrees Celsius, roughly five times hotter than the surface of the sun. Lightning is amazing to watch, but many people still believe things about it that are simply not true.\n\nMyth 1: Lightning never strikes the same place twice. In fact, lightning often hits the same place again and again, especially tall, pointed objects. The Empire State Building in New York, for example, is struck more than 20 times in an average year. Lightning does not remember where it has been. It simply follows the easiest path to the ground.\n\nMyth 2: The rubber tires of a car protect you. A car with a hard metal roof is a fairly safe place during a storm, but the tires have nothing to do with it. If lightning hits the car, the metal body carries the electricity around the people inside and down into the ground. A car with a soft roof, or a motorcycle, offers no such protection.\n\nMyth 3: If it isn't raining, you are safe. Lightning can strike more than 15 kilometers away from the storm that produces it, so if you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be hit. To judge the distance, count the seconds between the flash and the thunder. Because sound travels about one kilometer every three seconds, a nine-second gap means the storm is only about three kilometers away.\n\nSo what should you do? Remember the saying \"When thunder roars, go indoors.\" A building with electric wires and water pipes, or a hard-topped car, is the best shelter. Do not stand under a tall tree, and stay away from open fields, rooftops and water. After the storm seems to pass, wait at least 30 minutes after the last thunder before going outside again. A little patience is a small price to pay for your safety.", "passage_zh": "在臺灣的夏天，一個晴朗明亮的早晨可能很快就變成雷雨天。炎熱的太陽曬熱地面，溫暖潮濕的空氣迅速上升，到了下午三、四點，天空就布滿烏雲和閃電。一道閃電能把周圍的空氣加熱到大約攝氏三萬度，約是太陽表面溫度的五倍。閃電看起來令人驚嘆，但許多人至今仍相信一些關於閃電、其實根本不正確的說法。\n\n迷思一：閃電不會兩次打在同一個地方。事實上，閃電常常一次又一次地打在同一個地方，尤其是高聳、尖銳的物體。例如，紐約的帝國大廈平均每年會被閃電擊中二十次以上。閃電不會記得自己去過哪裡，它只是沿著通往地面最容易的路徑前進。\n\n迷思二：汽車的橡膠輪胎能保護你。有堅硬金屬車頂的汽車在暴風雨中是相當安全的地方，但這和輪胎毫無關係。如果閃電擊中汽車，金屬車身會把電流從車內乘客的周圍導開，一路導入地面。軟頂的汽車或機車則無法提供這樣的保護。\n\n迷思三：沒有下雨就很安全。閃電可能打在距離產生它的暴風雨 15 公里以上的地方，所以只要你聽得到雷聲，你就近到可能被擊中。要判斷距離，可以計算從看到閃光到聽到雷聲之間的秒數。因為聲音大約每三秒傳播一公里，相隔九秒就表示暴風雨只在大約三公里外。\n\n那麼你該怎麼做呢？記住這句話：「雷聲響，進室內。」有電線和水管的建築物，或是有硬頂的汽車，是最好的避難處。不要站在高大的樹下，並遠離空曠的田野、屋頂和水邊。暴風雨似乎過去之後，也要在最後一次雷聲後等待至少 30 分鐘，再回到戶外。多一點耐心，對你的安全來說只是很小的代價。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain why Taiwan has more thunderstorms than New York", "To correct some wrong ideas about lightning and explain how to stay safe", "To describe how the Empire State Building was built", "To compare the temperature of lightning with that of the sun"], "answer": 1, "explain": "文章列出三個關於閃電的迷思並一一說明真相（第二至四段），最後一段提供雷雨時的安全建議，目的是糾正錯誤觀念並說明如何保持安全。(A) 文中沒有比較臺灣和紐約的雷雨次數；(C) 帝國大廈只是第二段的一個例子；(D) 溫度的比較只是第一段的一個細節。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why is a car with a hard metal roof a fairly safe place during a thunderstorm?", "options": ["Its metal body carries the electricity around the passengers and into the ground.", "Its rubber tires stop the electricity from reaching the ground.", "Lightning never strikes cars that are moving.", "Its windows are strong enough to block lightning."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：the metal body carries the electricity around the people inside and down into the ground，汽車安全是因為金屬車身把電流從乘客周圍導入地面。(B) 文中明白說 the tires have nothing to do with it；(C) 文中沒有這種說法；(D) 文中沒有提到車窗。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Kevin sees a flash of lightning and hears the thunder six seconds later. About how far away is the storm?", "options": ["About 6 kilometers", "About 3 kilometers", "About 2 kilometers", "About 18 kilometers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：sound travels about one kilometer every three seconds，相隔六秒表示距離約 6 ÷ 3 = 2 公里。(A) 誤把秒數當成公里數；(B) 是相隔九秒時的距離；(D) 是把秒數乘以三的錯誤算法。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Amy is playing basketball on an outdoor court. It is not raining, but she hears thunder. According to the passage, what should she do?", "options": ["Keep playing until it starts to rain", "Wait under a tall tree beside the court", "Go up to the school rooftop to see where the storm is", "Go into a nearby building right away"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說只要聽得到雷聲就可能被閃電擊中，沒下雨也不安全；第五段說 \"When thunder roars, go indoors.\"，所以應該立刻進入附近的建築物。(A) 正是迷思三的錯誤觀念；(B) 第五段說不要站在高大的樹下；(C) 第五段說要遠離屋頂。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 2, the word \"It\" in \"It simply follows the easiest path to the ground\" refers to ______.", "options": ["lightning", "the Empire State Building", "the same place", "an average year"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Lightning does not remember where it has been. It simply follows the easiest path to the ground. 前一句的主詞是 Lightning，沿著最容易的路徑到達地面的也是閃電，所以 It 指閃電。(B)(C)(D) 都不會「沿著路徑前往地面」。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a59", "title": "Water Bears: Small but Almost Unbreakable", "title_zh": "水熊蟲：渺小卻幾乎打不倒", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "When Ms. Lai's biology class put a drop of water from some wet moss under a microscope, the students did not expect to see anything special. Instead, they found a tiny creature slowly walking across the glass on eight short legs. It looked like a fat little bear without ears. \"That,\" said Ms. Lai, \"is a tardigrade, also called a water bear. It may be the toughest animal on Earth.\"\n\nTardigrades are very small. Most are less than one millimeter long, about the size of a grain of sand, so you need a microscope to see them clearly. Scientists have found more than 1,000 kinds, and they live almost everywhere: in moss and soil, at the bottom of the sea, in hot springs, on high mountains and even in Antarctica.\n\nWhat makes them famous is their ability to survive. When their home dries up, a tardigrade pulls in its head and legs, loses almost all the water in its body and becomes a dry little ball called a \"tun.\" In this state, its body almost stops working. It can stay this way for years, and when water returns, it comes back to life within a few hours. In one famous study, Japanese scientists brought back to life tardigrades that had been frozen in Antarctic moss for over 30 years.\n\nIn the tun state, tardigrades can survive conditions that would kill almost any other animal. Some have survived temperatures close to -273°C, the coldest temperature possible, and others have lived through heat of over 150°C for a few minutes. In 2007, scientists even sent tardigrades into space and left them outside a spacecraft for ten days. After returning to Earth, some were still alive and later laid healthy eggs.\n\nHowever, water bears are not superheroes. When they are active, they are actually quite delicate and can be killed easily. Their special power only works when they have time to dry out slowly. Scientists hope that studying them may one day help keep medicines safe without refrigerators, or protect astronauts on long space trips. Not bad for an animal you could easily miss with your own eyes.", "passage_zh": "賴老師生物課的學生把一滴從潮濕苔蘚取來的水放在顯微鏡下觀察時，並不期待會看到什麼特別的東西。沒想到，他們發現一隻微小的生物正用八隻短短的腳，慢慢地走過玻璃片。牠看起來像一隻沒有耳朵的胖小熊。「那是緩步動物，」賴老師說，「也叫做水熊蟲。牠可能是地球上最強悍的動物。」\n\n水熊蟲非常小。大多數不到一公釐長，大約是一粒沙子的大小，所以需要用顯微鏡才能看清楚。科學家已經發現了一千多種水熊蟲，牠們幾乎無所不在：苔蘚和土壤裡、海底、溫泉中、高山上，甚至南極洲都有。\n\n牠們出名的原因是驚人的生存能力。當棲息的環境變乾時，水熊蟲會把頭和腳縮起來，失去身體裡幾乎所有的水分，變成一顆叫做「桶狀體」的乾燥小球。在這種狀態下，牠的身體幾乎完全停止運作。牠可以這樣維持好幾年，等水再次出現時，幾個小時內就能「復活」。在一項著名的研究中，日本科學家讓在南極苔蘚中冷凍了三十多年的水熊蟲重新活了過來。\n\n在桶狀體狀態下，水熊蟲能在幾乎會殺死所有其他動物的環境中存活。有些曾在接近攝氏零下 273 度，也就是可能存在的最低溫度下存活，也有些曾在超過攝氏 150 度的高溫中撐過幾分鐘。2007 年，科學家甚至把水熊蟲送上太空，讓牠們在太空船外待了十天。回到地球後，有些水熊蟲仍然活著，之後還產下了健康的卵。\n\n不過，水熊蟲並不是超級英雄。在活動狀態時，牠們其實相當脆弱，很容易死亡。牠們的特殊能力只有在有時間慢慢變乾時才能發揮。科學家希望，研究牠們有一天能幫助我們在沒有冰箱的情況下保存藥品，或是在長途太空旅行中保護太空人。對一種用肉眼很容易就錯過的動物來說，這已經很了不起了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["A tiny animal that can survive in extreme conditions", "How to use a microscope in a biology class", "Why bears are able to live in Antarctica", "How astronauts protect themselves on long space trips"], "answer": 0, "explain": "文章介紹水熊蟲的大小與分布（第二段）、乾燥成桶狀體的生存方式（第三段）、能熬過極冷、極熱和太空環境（第四段），主旨是一種能在極端環境中存活的微小動物。(B) 顯微鏡只是第一段的情境；(C) 水熊蟲並不是真正的熊；(D) 太空人只是最後一段提到的可能應用。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is a \"tun\"?", "options": ["A kind of moss where tardigrades like to live", "The eggs that tardigrades laid after returning from space", "The dry, ball-like state a tardigrade enters when its home dries up", "A machine that scientists use to freeze tardigrades"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：When their home dries up, a tardigrade... becomes a dry little ball called a \"tun.\" 桶狀體是水熊蟲在環境變乾時變成的乾燥小球。(A) 苔蘚是牠們的棲地；(B) 產卵是第四段的內容；(D) 文中沒有提到這種機器。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, what happened to the tardigrades that were sent into space in 2007?", "options": ["All of them died because of the cold.", "Some of them survived and later produced healthy eggs.", "They stayed in space for more than 30 years.", "They were kept safely inside the spacecraft."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：After returning to Earth, some were still alive and later laid healthy eggs. 有些水熊蟲存活下來，之後還產下健康的卵。(A) 與 some were still alive 不符；(C) 牠們在太空中只待了十天，三十多年是第三段冷凍研究的時間；(D) 牠們是被放在太空船「外面」。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"delicate\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["delicious", "powerful", "common", "easily harmed"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：When they are active, they are actually quite delicate and can be killed easily. 後半句說明牠們很容易死亡，所以 delicate 意為「脆弱的、容易受傷的」。(A) delicious「美味的」只是字形相近；(B) powerful「強大的」意思相反；(C) common「常見的」不符合文意。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why are scientists interested in studying tardigrades?", "options": ["They want to use tardigrades as food for astronauts.", "They hope to make tardigrades grow as large as real bears.", "They might learn how to keep medicines safe without refrigerators.", "They want to stop tardigrades from spreading to Antarctica."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：Scientists hope that studying them may one day help keep medicines safe without refrigerators... (A) 文中沒有提到把水熊蟲當作食物；(B) 文中沒有要把牠們養得像熊一樣大；(D) 第二段說南極本來就有水熊蟲，科學家並沒有想阻止牠們擴散。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a60", "title": "Never Make a Crow Angry", "title_zh": "千萬別惹烏鴉生氣", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "Imagine you are walking across a university campus when, suddenly, several black birds start diving at your head and crying loudly. You have done nothing wrong today. But years ago, someone wearing the same face did something the birds have never forgotten. This is not the beginning of a horror movie. It is what happened in a famous experiment with American crows.\n\nIn 2006, scientists at the University of Washington in Seattle wanted to find out whether crows could recognize human faces. Wearing a rubber caveman mask, a few researchers caught a small group of wild crows on campus. They put identification rings on the birds' legs and then let them go. The crows were not hurt, but they clearly did not enjoy the experience.\n\nLater, the researchers walked around campus wearing the same caveman mask. Many crows began to \"scold\" them at once, calling loudly and flying close to their heads. When the same people walked the same paths wearing a different mask, the crows ignored them. The birds were not reacting to the people themselves but to the face they had learned to connect with danger.\n\nThe most surprising part came later. Over the next few years, the number of crows scolding the caveman mask actually grew. Most of these birds had never been caught, and some had not even been born when the experiment began. It seems that crows \"tell\" each other about dangerous faces, and young birds learn by watching how their parents and neighbors react. Years after the first crows were caught, the mask could still cause an uproar of angry calls.\n\nCrows belong to the same bird family as ravens and magpies, a group famous for its intelligence. Birds in this family can solve puzzles, remember where they have hidden food, and in some cases even use sticks as tools. The Seattle experiment shows one more skill: a long memory for faces. So the next time a crow watches you from a power line, be polite. It may remember you for much longer than you think.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，你正走在一所大學的校園裡，突然有好幾隻黑色的鳥開始朝你的頭俯衝，還大聲鳴叫。你今天什麼壞事也沒做。但在多年前，有個戴著同一張臉的人做了一件這些鳥從未忘記的事。這不是恐怖電影的開頭，而是一項以美國烏鴉為對象的著名實驗中真實發生的事。\n\n2006 年，西雅圖華盛頓大學的科學家想知道烏鴉是否能認出人類的臉。幾位研究人員戴著橡膠製的原始人面具，在校園裡捕捉了一小群野生烏鴉。他們在這些鳥的腳上套上識別腳環，然後把牠們放走。這些烏鴉並沒有受傷，但顯然很不喜歡這次的經驗。\n\n後來，研究人員戴著同樣的原始人面具在校園裡走動。許多烏鴉立刻開始「責罵」他們，大聲鳴叫，並飛到他們頭部附近。當同樣的人戴著另一種面具走在同樣的路上時，烏鴉卻不理會他們。這些鳥並不是對那些人本身起反應，而是對牠們已經學會和危險聯想在一起的那張臉起反應。\n\n最令人驚訝的部分還在後頭。在接下來的幾年裡，對原始人面具發出責罵的烏鴉數量反而增加了。這些鳥大多從來沒有被抓過，有些甚至在實驗開始時還沒出生。看來烏鴉會互相「告知」危險的臉孔，而幼鳥則是藉由觀察父母和鄰居的反應來學習。在第一批烏鴉被捕捉多年之後，那張面具仍然能引來一陣憤怒的叫聲，造成一片騷動。\n\n烏鴉和渡鴉、喜鵲同屬一個鳥類家族，這個家族以聰明著稱。這個家族的鳥類會解決難題、記得自己把食物藏在哪裡，有些甚至會用樹枝當作工具。西雅圖的實驗則顯示了牠們的另一項本領：對臉孔的長久記憶。所以下次有烏鴉從電線上看著你時，請客氣一點。牠記得你的時間，可能比你想像的久得多。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Crows are dangerous birds that often attack people.", "Scientists should not catch wild birds for experiments.", "Crows are the only birds that can use tools.", "Crows can remember human faces and share this information with other crows."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文說明西雅圖的實驗：烏鴉能認出曾經捕捉牠們的那張「臉」（第三段），並把這個資訊傳給從未被抓過的烏鴉（第四段），最後指出烏鴉對臉孔有長久的記憶。(A) 烏鴉只對特定的面具有反應，並不是常常攻擊人；(B) 文中沒有反對這類實驗；(C) 第五段說這個家族的鳥類有些會使用工具，並沒有說只有烏鴉會。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why did the crows scold the researchers?", "options": ["The researchers were carrying food.", "The researchers were wearing the mask connected with catching crows.", "The researchers walked too close to the crows' nests.", "The crows were hungry and wanted to be fed."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The birds were not reacting to the people themselves but to the face they had learned to connect with danger. 研究人員戴著當初捕捉烏鴉時的原始人面具，烏鴉把這張臉和危險聯想在一起。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did the same people also walk the same paths wearing a different mask?", "options": ["To make the crows feel more comfortable on campus", "To catch more crows without being noticed", "To check whether the crows were reacting to the face rather than the people", "To scare the crows away from the university"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：When the same people walked the same paths wearing a different mask, the crows ignored them. The birds were not reacting to the people themselves but to the face... 換戴另一種面具，是為了確認烏鴉反應的是那張臉，而不是人本身。(A)(B)(D) 都不是這樣做的目的。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"uproar\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a loud, noisy reaction", "a quiet moment", "a long journey", "a friendly greeting"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段 the mask could still cause an uproar of angry calls，面具仍會引起一陣憤怒的叫聲，uproar 意為「騷動、喧鬧」。(B) a quiet moment「安靜的片刻」意思相反；(C) a long journey「長途旅行」與 (D) a friendly greeting「友善的問候」都不符合文意。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What was the most surprising result of the experiment?", "options": ["The crows forgot the caveman mask after a few days.", "Crows that had never been caught also scolded the caveman mask.", "The crows attacked anyone who walked on campus.", "The crows were seriously hurt when they were caught."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：The most surprising part came later... Most of these birds had never been caught, and some had not even been born... 最令人驚訝的是，從未被抓過的烏鴉也會責罵那張面具。(A) 烏鴉多年後仍記得那張面具；(C) 第三段說烏鴉會忽略戴其他面具的人；(D) 第二段說 The crows were not hurt。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a61", "title": "Lights in the Dark: My First Firefly Night", "title_zh": "黑暗中的光點：我的第一次賞螢之夜", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Nature", "Life"], "passage": "Last April, my aunt took me on a firefly-watching tour at Green Hill Farm in the mountains of Miaoli. I had only seen fireflies in pictures and cartoons, so I was very excited. When we arrived at seven o'clock, the sky was already dark, and our guide, a cheerful woman named Ms. Peng, gathered our group at the start of a forest path.\n\nBefore we started, Ms. Peng explained the rules. \"First, turn off the flashlights on your phones, or cover them with red paper. Strong light confuses the fireflies. Second, no flash photography. Third, stay on the path, because young fireflies live in the grass and soil, and you might step on them. And of course, don't catch them. Let them stay where they belong.\"\n\nAfter ten minutes of walking, I noticed one small yellow-green light, then another, and soon hundreds more. They floated above the bushes like tiny stars that had come down to Earth. Ms. Peng told us that Taiwan has more than 60 kinds of fireflies, and spring is the best time to see many of them. Each kind flashes in its own pattern. The flashes work like a secret language that males and females use to find each other.\n\nI asked whether the fireflies' lights were hot. Ms. Peng smiled and shook her head. A firefly makes light through a chemical reaction inside its body, and this reaction gives off very little heat. That is why scientists call it \"cold light.\" An old-fashioned light bulb, by contrast, wastes most of its energy as heat.\n\nOn the way back, Ms. Peng told us that fireflies are in trouble in many places. Bright streetlights make it hard for them to see each other's signals, and chemicals used on farms can kill them. Adult fireflies live for only about two weeks, so every night matters. Before getting into the car, I turned around for one last look. The forest was still full of tiny blinking lights, and I quietly promised myself to keep my flashlight off.", "passage_zh": "去年四月，阿姨帶我到苗栗山區的綠丘農場參加賞螢活動。我只在圖片和卡通裡看過螢火蟲，所以非常興奮。我們七點抵達時，天色已經暗了，我們的導覽員是一位名叫彭小姐的開朗女士，她把大家集合在一條森林步道的入口。\n\n出發前，彭小姐說明了規則。「第一，請關掉手機的手電筒，或是用紅色的紙蓋住。強光會讓螢火蟲混亂。第二，禁止使用閃光燈拍照。第三，請待在步道上，因為螢火蟲的幼蟲住在草地和土壤裡，你們可能會踩到牠們。當然，也不要抓牠們。讓牠們留在屬於牠們的地方。」\n\n走了十分鐘後，我注意到一個小小的黃綠色光點，接著又一個，很快就出現了好幾百個。它們飄浮在灌木叢上方，就像降落到地面上的小星星。彭小姐告訴我們，臺灣有六十多種螢火蟲，而春天是觀賞許多種螢火蟲的最佳時節。每一種螢火蟲都以自己獨特的模式閃光。這些閃光就像一種祕密語言，雄蟲和雌蟲用它來找到彼此。\n\n我問螢火蟲的光是不是燙的。彭小姐笑著搖搖頭。螢火蟲是透過體內的化學反應發光，而這種反應幾乎不會產生熱。這就是為什麼科學家稱它為「冷光」。相較之下，傳統的燈泡則把大部分的能量浪費在發熱上。\n\n回程時，彭小姐告訴我們，螢火蟲在許多地方正面臨危機。明亮的路燈讓牠們很難看見彼此的訊號，而農田使用的化學藥劑也可能殺死牠們。成年的螢火蟲只能活大約兩個星期，所以每一個夜晚都很重要。上車前，我轉身看了最後一眼。森林裡仍然滿是一閃一閃的小光點，我悄悄地對自己許下承諾：要讓手電筒保持關閉。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to take good photos of fireflies at night", "Why the writer's aunt works as a firefly guide", "The writer's first firefly-watching trip and what the writer learned", "How farmers in Miaoli use chemicals on their farms"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文描述作者第一次到苗栗參加賞螢活動的經過，以及從導覽員那裡學到的規則、螢火蟲閃光的意義、冷光和牠們面臨的危機。(A) 文中反而說禁止使用閃光燈拍照；(B) 導覽員是彭小姐，不是阿姨；(D) 農田的化學藥劑只是最後一段提到的一項威脅。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Ms. Peng ask visitors to stay on the path?", "options": ["Because the forest is full of dangerous snakes", "Because the farm closes at seven o'clock", "Because the fireflies only fly above the path", "Because visitors might step on young fireflies in the grass and soil"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：stay on the path, because young fireflies live in the grass and soil, and you might step on them. (A) 文中沒有提到蛇；(B) 七點是他們抵達農場的時間；(C) 文中沒有說螢火蟲只在步道上方飛。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why do fireflies flash?", "options": ["To help males and females find each other", "To scare away visitors with flashlights", "To keep their bodies warm at night", "To show that spring is coming"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：The flashes work like a secret language that males and females use to find each other. 閃光是雄蟲和雌蟲尋找彼此的方式。(B)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到；而且第四段說螢火蟲的光幾乎不產生熱，不可能用來保暖。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does \"cold light\" in paragraph 4 mean?", "options": ["Light that can only be seen in cold weather", "Light that produces very little heat", "Light that comes from streetlights", "Light that makes people feel cold"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：this reaction gives off very little heat. That is why scientists call it \"cold light.\" 冷光指的是幾乎不產生熱的光。(A) 與天氣冷熱無關；(C) 路燈是第五段提到的威脅；(D) 並不是讓人覺得冷。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the writer's promise at the end?", "options": ["The writer plans to catch some fireflies next time.", "The writer will never visit the farm again.", "The writer is afraid of walking in the dark forest.", "The writer wants to protect fireflies by not disturbing them with light."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段說強光會讓螢火蟲混亂，第五段也說明亮的路燈讓牠們看不見彼此的訊號，因此作者承諾「讓手電筒保持關閉」，可推知作者想藉由不用光線打擾牠們來保護螢火蟲。(A) 規則明說不要抓螢火蟲；(B) 文中沒有這種說法；(C) 作者並沒有表現出害怕。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a62", "title": "Three Words My Father Never Said", "title_zh": "爸爸從沒說出口的三個字", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Life"], "passage": "My father has never said \"I love you\" to me. He didn't say it on my birthdays, or even when I was in the hospital with a broken leg. For a long time, I thought this meant he was a cold person. Parents in movies and online videos hugged their children and said those three words all the time. Why couldn't my dad?\n\nLast winter, I found the answer in an unexpected place: a plate of guava. I was studying late for a mock exam when Dad knocked on my door. He didn't say a word. He just put a plate on my desk and left. The guava was cut into perfect slices, with a little plum powder on the side, just the way I like it. Suddenly I realized that he had done this almost every night for years. I had just never noticed.\n\nThe next day, I told my homeroom teacher, Ms. Lai, about it. She smiled. \"Many fathers of your parents' generation are not used to expressing their feelings in words,\" she explained. \"They show love through actions instead: cutting fruit, picking you up from cram school, or asking 'Have you eaten yet?'\" She called it \"a quiet language of love.\" Once you learn to read it, she said, you start to see it everywhere.\n\nAfter that, I began to pay attention. Every morning, Dad checked the weather before I left for school and put an umbrella by the door if it was going to rain. When I came home late from club practice, the living room light was always on, though he pretended to be watching TV. He had been talking to me all along. I just hadn't understood his language.\n\nStill, I wanted to say something back. So one night, when he brought me a plate of sliced apples, I didn't just say \"Thanks.\" I said, \"Dad, the guava last week was really sweet. You always pick the best ones.\" He looked surprised, then a little shy. \"The man at the market saves them for me,\" he said softly, and hurried out. But I saw him smiling. Maybe we will never say those three words to each other. That's OK. We have found our own way to say them.", "passage_zh": "我爸爸從來沒有對我說過「我愛你」。我過生日時他沒說，甚至我摔斷腿住院時，他也沒說。很長一段時間，我以為這代表他是個冷漠的人。電影和網路影片裡的父母總是擁抱孩子，把這三個字掛在嘴邊。為什麼我爸爸就是說不出口呢？\n\n去年冬天，我在一個意想不到的地方找到了答案：一盤芭樂。那天我為了模擬考熬夜念書，爸爸來敲我的房門。他一句話也沒說，只是把一個盤子放在我的書桌上就離開了。芭樂被切成整整齊齊的片狀，旁邊還附上一點梅粉，正是我喜歡的吃法。我突然意識到，好幾年來他幾乎每天晚上都這麼做，只是我從來沒有注意到。\n\n隔天，我把這件事告訴了班導賴老師。她笑了笑，解釋說：「很多跟你爸媽同一代的父親，不習慣用言語表達自己的感受。他們改用行動來表達愛：切水果、去補習班接你下課，或是問你『吃飽了沒？』」她把這稱為「一種安靜的愛的語言」。她說，一旦你學會解讀它，就會發現它無所不在。\n\n從那之後，我開始留意。每天早上，爸爸都會在我出門上學前查看天氣，如果會下雨，就在門邊放一把傘。當我參加社團練習很晚才回家時，客廳的燈總是亮著，雖然他假裝在看電視。原來他一直都在跟我說話，只是我之前聽不懂他的語言。\n\n不過，我還是想回應些什麼。於是有一天晚上，當他端來一盤切好的蘋果時，我不只是說「謝謝」。我說：「爸，上星期的芭樂真的好甜，你總是挑到最好的。」他看起來很驚訝，接著有點害羞。「市場的老闆會特地留給我，」他小聲地說，然後匆匆走了出去。但我看見他在笑。也許我們永遠不會對彼此說出那三個字，但沒關係，我們已經找到屬於自己的說法了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the writer came to understand Dad's quiet way of showing love", "Why fathers in Taiwan should hug their children more often", "How to choose and cut sweet guavas at the market", "Why the writer did poorly on a mock exam"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文描述作者原本以為爸爸從不說「我愛你」是因為個性冷漠，後來從爸爸每晚送來的切好水果、雨天放在門邊的傘等行動，理解到這是他「安靜的愛的語言」。(B) 文中並未主張爸爸應該多擁抱孩子；(C) 芭樂只是故事的引子，並不是在教人挑水果；(D) 文中沒有提到模擬考考差。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what did the writer suddenly realize?", "options": ["Dad was unhappy that the writer studied so late.", "Dad did not know what kind of fruit the writer liked.", "Dad had brought cut fruit almost every night for years.", "Dad wanted the writer to stop eating while studying."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Suddenly I realized that he had done this almost every night for years. 作者突然意識到，爸爸好幾年來幾乎每晚都送來切好的水果。(A) 爸爸沒有表示不高興；(B) 爸爸附上梅粉，「正是我喜歡的吃法」，可見他很清楚作者的喜好；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"expressing\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["hiding", "showing", "forgetting", "changing"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：are not used to expressing their feelings in words... They show love through actions instead. 後句的 instead 表示這些父親「改用」行動來表現愛，可知 expressing 的意思接近 showing（表達、表現）。(A) hiding「隱藏」與文意相反；(C) forgetting「忘記」、(D) changing「改變」都不合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why was the living room light always on when the writer came home late?", "options": ["Dad forgot to turn it off before going to bed.", "Dad was watching his favorite late-night TV show.", "Dad was too busy with his work to go to sleep.", "Dad was waiting for the writer to get home, though he didn't say so."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：the living room light was always on, though he pretended to be watching TV. pretended（假裝）表示爸爸其實不是真的在看電視，而是在等作者平安回家，只是沒說出口；下一句 He had been talking to me all along 也說明這是他表達關心的方式。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 他只是「假裝」在看電視。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How did Dad most likely feel when the writer praised the guava?", "options": ["Angry that the writer had noticed", "Pleased but a little embarrassed", "Worried about the writer's exam", "Bored with the conversation"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段作者稱讚芭樂後，爸爸 looked surprised, then a little shy，說完話就匆匆走出去，但作者 saw him smiling。可見他心裡很高興，只是有點不好意思。(A) 文中沒有任何生氣的描述；(C) 這段對話與考試無關；(D) 他在微笑，並不是覺得無聊。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a63", "title": "How Many Friends Can One Person Really Have?", "title_zh": "一個人真的能有多少朋友？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Society"], "passage": "When Kai turned seventeen, more than two hundred people wished him a happy birthday online. He had over six hundred followers on social media, so he should have felt popular. Instead, he felt strangely lonely. \"I didn't know who I could call if something bad happened,\" he said. Kai's experience raises an interesting question: how many real friends can one person have?\n\nBritish scientist Robin Dunbar has spent years studying this question. In the 1990s, he noticed that monkeys and apes with larger brains lived in larger groups. Based on the size of the human brain, he predicted that people can keep about 150 meaningful relationships at one time. This idea became known as \"Dunbar's number.\" Interestingly, the number seems to appear in real life again and again, from the size of early farming villages to the number of people British families sent Christmas cards to in one study.\n\nHowever, those 150 people are not all equally close. Dunbar describes our relationships as a set of circles. In the center are about five people, the ones you would turn to in a crisis, such as a serious illness or a family emergency. Next come about fifteen good friends, then fifty, and finally 150 people you would still happily greet if you met them at an airport. The bigger the circle, the weaker the relationships in it.\n\nSocial media does not seem to change these limits. In a 2016 study, Dunbar found that Facebook users had about 150 online friends on average, but they felt they could count on only about four of them in a difficult time. Clicking \"follow\" is easy, but real friendship takes time. One American study found that it takes about 50 hours together to turn someone you just met into a casual friend, and more than 200 hours to become close friends.\n\nFor Kai, this was useful news. He stopped worrying about how many likes his posts got and started spending more time with a few teammates from his basketball club. \"I have fewer followers now,\" he says, \"but I have three friends who would come to the hospital at midnight for me. That's the number that matters.\"", "passage_zh": "凱滿十七歲那天，有兩百多人在網路上祝他生日快樂。他在社群媒體上有六百多個追蹤者，照理說應該覺得自己很受歡迎。然而，他卻莫名地感到寂寞。「我不知道如果發生了不好的事，我可以打電話給誰，」他說。凱的經驗帶出了一個有趣的問題：一個人究竟能有多少真正的朋友？\n\n英國科學家羅賓‧鄧巴（Robin Dunbar）多年來一直在研究這個問題。1990 年代，他注意到大腦較大的猴子和猿類，生活的群體也比較大。根據人類大腦的大小，他推測人一次大約能維持 150 段有意義的人際關係。這個概念後來被稱為「鄧巴數字」。有趣的是，這個數字似乎一再出現在現實生活中，從早期農業村落的規模，到某項研究中英國家庭寄送聖誕卡的對象人數，都是如此。\n\n不過，這 150 個人並不是都一樣親近。鄧巴把我們的人際關係描述成一組同心圓。最中心大約有五個人，是你遇到危機時會求助的對象，例如生了重病或家裡發生緊急狀況的時候。接下來是大約十五個好朋友，然後是五十人，最外層則是 150 個你在機場遇到時仍會開心打招呼的人。圈子越大，裡面的關係就越淡。\n\n社群媒體似乎沒有改變這些限制。在 2016 年的一項研究中，鄧巴發現臉書使用者平均約有 150 個網路好友，但他們覺得在困難時真正能依靠的，只有大約四個人。按下「追蹤」很容易，但真正的友誼需要時間。一項美國研究發現，要把剛認識的人變成普通朋友，大約需要一起相處 50 個小時；要成為親密的好友，則需要超過 200 個小時。\n\n對凱來說，這是很有用的消息。他不再擔心自己的貼文得到多少個讚，而是開始花更多時間和籃球社的幾個隊友相處。「我現在的追蹤者變少了，」他說，「但我有三個朋友願意在半夜趕到醫院陪我。那才是真正重要的數字。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why teenagers should stop using social media", "The limits on how many close relationships people can keep", "How monkeys and apes choose their friends", "How to get more followers online"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文以凱的經驗開頭，介紹鄧巴數字、人際關係的同心圓，以及社群媒體無法改變這些限制，主旨是人能維持的親密關係是有限的。(A) 文中沒有叫青少年停用社群媒體；(C) 猴子和猿類只是第二段研究的背景；(D) 與文意相反，凱後來反而不在意追蹤者和按讚數。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to Dunbar, about how many people are in the closest circle of a person's relationships?", "options": ["Five", "Fifteen", "Fifty", "150"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：In the center are about five people, the ones you would turn to in a crisis. 最中心的一圈大約是五個人。(B) 十五人是「好朋友」那一圈；(C) 五十人與 (D) 150 人都是更外層的圈子。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"crisis\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a happy celebration", "a school activity", "a long holiday", "a time of serious trouble"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段 in a crisis 後面舉例 such as a serious illness or a family emergency（生重病或家中發生緊急狀況），可知 crisis 指「危機、遇到嚴重困難的時刻」。(A) 快樂的慶祝、(B) 學校活動、(C) 長假都與這些例子不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the 2016 study in paragraph 4?", "options": ["People with more online friends usually have more close friends.", "Facebook helps people become close friends in less than 50 hours.", "Having many online friends does not mean having many people to depend on.", "Most Facebook users have no real friends at all."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說臉書使用者平均約有 150 個網路好友，但困難時覺得能依靠的只有大約四個人，可推論網路朋友多，不代表可以依靠的人多。(A) 與研究結果相反；(B) 文中說變成普通朋友就要約 50 小時，成為親密好友更要超過 200 小時；(D)「完全沒有真朋友」太過絕對，文中說大約有四個人可以依靠。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In the last paragraph, what does \"That\" in \"That's the number that matters\" refer to?", "options": ["The number of Kai's followers", "The number of likes on Kai's posts", "The number of friends who would really help Kai when he needs them", "Dunbar's number, 150"], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一段凱說追蹤者變少了，但有三個朋友願意半夜趕到醫院陪他，接著說 That's the number that matters，That 指的就是這些在需要時真正會幫他的朋友人數。(A)(B) 追蹤者和按讚數正是他不再在意的數字；(D) 150 是鄧巴數字，並不是凱這句話所指的。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a64", "title": "Sorry Is Not Enough: What Makes a Good Apology", "title_zh": "光說對不起還不夠：怎樣才是好的道歉", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Life", "Science"], "passage": "Pei-shan knew she had made a mistake. She had promised to help her best friend, Wen-ting, practice for a singing contest on Saturday afternoon. Instead, she forgot and went to a movie with some other classmates. Wen-ting found out when she saw their photos online. On Monday, Pei-shan sent her a quick message: \"Sorry! But you know how busy I've been lately.\" Wen-ting never replied. Pei-shan had apologized, so why was her friend still upset?\n\nScientists have studied what makes an apology work. In 2016, researchers at Ohio State University in the U.S. asked 755 people to read different apologies and rate how effective they were. The apologies contained different combinations of six parts: saying you feel sorry, explaining what went wrong, admitting that it was your fault, promising not to do it again, offering to fix the problem, and asking for forgiveness.\n\nThe results were clear. The most important part was admitting responsibility, which means saying \"It was my fault\" without making excuses. The second most important was offering to repair the damage, for example, \"Let me make it up to you.\" Surprisingly, the least important part was asking, \"Will you forgive me?\" The study also found that the more parts an apology included, the better it worked.\n\nLooking back at her message, Pei-shan saw what had gone wrong. Her \"sorry\" was followed by \"but,\" and the rest of the sentence was an excuse. It sounded as if she was blaming her busy schedule instead of herself. When \"but\" comes right after \"sorry,\" an apology often turns into a defense of yourself.\n\nThat evening, Pei-shan called Wen-ting. \"I forgot our practice because I didn't write it down, and that was my fault,\" she said. \"I know how much the contest means to you. Can I come over every day this week to help you get ready?\" After a long pause, Wen-ting laughed. \"Only if you bring snacks.\" Pei-shan learned that a real apology is not just about saying the right word. It is about showing that you understand what you did and that you want to make it right.", "passage_zh": "佩珊知道自己犯了錯。她答應過要在星期六下午幫最好的朋友文婷練習歌唱比賽，結果卻忘得一乾二淨，跑去和其他幾個同學看電影。文婷在網路上看到他們的照片，才發現這件事。星期一，佩珊傳了一則簡短的訊息給她：「抱歉！不過你也知道我最近有多忙。」文婷一直沒有回覆。佩珊已經道歉了，為什麼她的朋友還是很不高興呢？\n\n科學家研究過怎樣的道歉才有效。2016 年，美國俄亥俄州立大學的研究人員請 755 個人閱讀各種不同的道歉，並評估這些道歉有多有效。這些道歉由六個部分以不同的方式組合而成：表達歉意、說明哪裡出了錯、承認是自己的錯、承諾不會再犯、提議彌補問題，以及請求原諒。\n\n結果很清楚。最重要的部分是承擔責任，也就是不找藉口地說出「是我的錯」。第二重要的是提出彌補損害的方法，例如「讓我補償你」。令人意外的是，最不重要的部分是問對方「你願意原諒我嗎？」研究也發現，道歉包含的部分越多，效果就越好。\n\n回頭看自己傳的訊息，佩珊明白了問題出在哪裡。她的「抱歉」後面接著「不過」，而句子剩下的部分全是藉口。聽起來就像是她把錯怪到忙碌的行程上，而不是怪自己。當「不過」緊跟在「抱歉」之後，道歉往往就變成了替自己辯護。\n\n那天晚上，佩珊打電話給文婷。「我會忘了我們的練習，是因為我沒有把它記下來，這是我的錯，」她說，「我知道這場比賽對你有多重要。這個星期我可以每天去你家，幫你準備比賽嗎？」電話那頭沉默了好一陣子，然後文婷笑了。「你要帶零食來才行。」佩珊學到了，真正的道歉不只是說出對的字眼，而是要讓對方知道，你明白自己做了什麼，而且你想要彌補。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain why friends should never go to movies without each other", "To describe how to prepare for a singing contest", "To show what makes an apology effective through a story and a study", "To report that most people refuse to forgive their friends"], "answer": 2, "explain": "文章先以佩珊失敗的道歉為例，再介紹俄亥俄州立大學的研究，最後描述她如何重新道歉，目的在說明怎樣的道歉才有效。(A) 看電影只是故事情節；(B) 文中沒有教人準備歌唱比賽；(D) 文婷最後原諒了佩珊，文中也沒有這樣的報告。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the Ohio State study, which part of an apology was the LEAST important?", "options": ["Admitting that it was your fault", "Offering to fix the problem", "Explaining what went wrong", "Asking for forgiveness"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：the least important part was asking, \"Will you forgive me?\" 最不重要的部分是請求原諒。(A) 承認是自己的錯是最重要的部分；(B) 提議彌補是第二重要的部分；(C) 說明哪裡出了錯並不是文中所說最不重要的部分。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did Wen-ting most likely not reply to Pei-shan's first message?", "options": ["The message gave an excuse instead of taking real responsibility.", "Wen-ting was too busy practicing for the contest.", "Wen-ting did not see the message until much later.", "The message invited her to go to another movie."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段指出佩珊的「抱歉」後面接著「不過」，剩下的部分都是藉口，聽起來像在怪忙碌的行程而不是怪自己；第三段也說最重要的是不找藉口地承認錯誤。可推知文婷不回覆，是因為這則訊息只有藉口，沒有真正承擔責任。(B)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"defense\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a promise to change", "words that protect yourself from blame", "a funny joke between friends", "a request for help"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說「抱歉」後面加上「不過」和藉口，聽起來是在怪行程而不是怪自己，因此道歉變成了 a defense of yourself，也就是替自己開脫、保護自己不被責備的話。(A) 承諾改變、(C) 朋友間的玩笑、(D) 請求幫忙都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which parts of a good apology did Pei-shan's phone call clearly include?", "options": ["Admitting her fault and offering to help repair the damage", "Blaming her schedule and asking for forgiveness", "Promising never to see movies again and giving a gift", "Describing the movie and inviting Wen-ting to the next one"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段佩珊說 that was my fault（承認是自己的錯），又問 Can I come over every day this week to help you get ready?（提出彌補的方法），正好是研究中最重要的兩個部分。(B) 她這次沒有怪行程，也沒有請求原諒；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a65", "title": "Name It to Tame It: Finding Words for Your Feelings", "title_zh": "說出情緒，就能馴服情緒：為感受找到合適的詞語", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "When the school counselor asked Jun-hao how he felt, he gave his usual answer: \"Fine.\" When she asked again, he said, \"Bad, I guess.\" Those were the only words he could find. But \"bad\" was not the whole story. That morning, he had shouted at his little brother for no reason, and he did not understand why he was so angry.\n\nThe counselor showed him a colorful circle called a \"feelings wheel.\" One of the first feelings wheels was designed by a therapist named Gloria Willcox in 1982, and many different versions are used today. In the center are a few basic emotions, such as sad, angry, scared and happy. Moving outward, each one is divided into more specific words. \"Angry,\" for example, may lead to \"frustrated\" or \"jealous.\" With the wheel in front of him, Jun-hao slowly found better words. He felt embarrassed because he had failed a skills test in front of his classmates, and he was worried that his parents would be disappointed.\n\nPutting feelings into words may sound too simple to help, but science suggests that it works. In a 2007 study at the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA), people looked at pictures of angry or frightened faces while their brains were scanned. When they chose a word to describe the emotion on each face, activity in the amygdala, a part of the brain that reacts to danger and strong feelings, went down. Naming a feeling seemed to calm the brain. Some experts sum this up as \"Name it to tame it.\"\n\nPsychologists also say that the more exact your words are, the better. A person who can only say \"I feel bad\" does not know what to do next. But someone who knows they feel \"lonely\" might call a friend, while someone who feels \"exhausted\" might simply need a good night's sleep. Exact words point to exact solutions.\n\nJun-hao now keeps a small notebook. Every night, he writes down a word or two for the strongest feeling of his day and what caused it. \"It's strange,\" he says. \"When I know what I'm feeling, it gets smaller.\" Last week, he apologized to his brother, and this time he could explain why he had been so upset.", "passage_zh": "學校的輔導老師問俊豪感覺如何時，他給了一貫的回答：「還好。」老師再問一次，他說：「很糟吧，我想。」那是他唯一想得到的詞。但「很糟」並不是事情的全貌。那天早上，他無緣無故對弟弟大吼，而他自己也不明白為什麼會那麼生氣。\n\n輔導老師給他看一個色彩繽紛的圓形圖，叫做「情緒輪」。最早的情緒輪之一，是由一位名叫葛洛莉亞‧威爾柯克斯（Gloria Willcox）的心理治療師在 1982 年設計的，現在則有許多不同的版本。圓心是幾種基本情緒，例如難過、生氣、害怕和快樂。越往外圈，每一種情緒都會被細分成更具體的詞語。例如「生氣」可能延伸出「挫折」或「嫉妒」。看著眼前的情緒輪，俊豪慢慢找到了更貼切的詞。他覺得很難堪，因為他在同學面前沒通過技能測驗；他也很擔心爸媽會失望。\n\n把感受化為文字，聽起來簡單得好像沒什麼用，但科學顯示它確實有效。在美國加州大學洛杉磯分校（UCLA）2007 年的一項研究中，受試者一邊看著憤怒或驚恐的臉孔照片，一邊接受腦部掃描。當他們選出一個詞來描述每張臉上的情緒時，杏仁核的活動就下降了；杏仁核是大腦中對危險和強烈情緒產生反應的部位。替情緒命名，似乎能讓大腦平靜下來。有些專家把這個道理歸納成一句話：「說出情緒，就能馴服情緒。」\n\n心理學家也說，用詞越精確越好。一個只會說「我覺得很糟」的人，不知道接下來該怎麼做。但知道自己感到「寂寞」的人，可能會打電話給朋友；而覺得「筋疲力盡」的人，可能只需要好好睡一覺。精確的詞語，能指向精確的解決方法。\n\n俊豪現在有一本小筆記本。每天晚上，他會用一兩個詞寫下當天最強烈的感受，以及造成這種感受的原因。「很奇怪，」他說，「當我知道自己有什麼感受時，那種感覺就變小了。」上星期，他向弟弟道歉，而這一次，他能說清楚自己當時為什麼那麼不高興。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Students should not show their feelings at school.", "Finding exact words for feelings can help people deal with them.", "Brain scans are the best way to understand our emotions.", "Feelings wheels were invented to help students pass tests."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文透過俊豪的經驗、情緒輪、UCLA 的研究和心理學家的說法，說明用精確的詞語說出感受，有助於處理情緒。(A) 與文意無關；(C) 腦部掃描只是研究使用的方法；(D) 情緒輪是用來找出描述感受的詞語，與通過考試無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the UCLA study, what happened when people named the emotions on the faces?", "options": ["They became more frightened of the pictures.", "They forgot the faces more quickly.", "Activity in a brain area that reacts to strong feelings went down.", "They learned to read faces faster than before."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：When they chose a word to describe the emotion on each face, activity in the amygdala... went down. 杏仁核是大腦中對危險和強烈情緒產生反應的部位，它的活動下降了。(A) 與研究結果相反；(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Jun-hao most likely shout at his brother?", "options": ["He felt embarrassed and worried about his failed test but did not understand those feelings.", "His brother had broken his notebook.", "His brother had told their parents about the test.", "He was angry at the school counselor."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段說俊豪無緣無故對弟弟大吼，自己也 did not understand why he was so angry；第二段他藉由情緒輪才找到更貼切的詞：他因為在同學面前沒通過技能測驗而覺得難堪，也擔心爸媽會失望。可推知他大吼其實是因為這些當時還說不出來的難堪與擔心，第五段也說他後來終於能向弟弟解釋自己為什麼那麼不高興。(B) 文中沒有提到筆記本被弄壞；(C) 文中沒有提到弟弟告訴爸媽考試的事；(D) 他是在大吼之後才和輔導老師談話，也沒有對老師生氣。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"exhausted\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["excited", "angry", "bored", "very tired"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說覺得 exhausted 的人 might simply need a good night's sleep（只需要好好睡一覺），可知 exhausted 的意思是「非常疲累、筋疲力盡」。(A) 興奮、(B) 生氣、(C) 無聊，都不是靠睡一覺就能解決的感受。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the feelings wheel?", "options": ["It has basic emotions in the center and more specific words further out.", "It was designed by scientists at UCLA in 2007.", "It contains only four feelings: sad, angry, scared and happy.", "It can only be used by trained doctors."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段說情緒輪的中心是幾種基本情緒，越往外圈就細分成更具體的詞語。(B) 最早的情緒輪之一是心理治療師威爾柯克斯在 1982 年設計的，2007 年的 UCLA 研究與情緒輪無關；(C) such as 只是舉例，而且外圈還有更多詞語；(D) 文中沒有這樣的限制，俊豪自己也能使用。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a66", "title": "The Woman Who Created Mother's Day, and Then Fought Against It", "title_zh": "創立母親節，後來又反對它的女人", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Culture"], "passage": "Every May, flower shops in Taiwan fill up with carnations, and restaurants are fully booked for family dinners. Mother's Day, on the second Sunday of May, is one of the busiest days of the year for many businesses. But the woman who created the holiday would probably hate what it has become.\n\nHer name was Anna Jarvis, and she grew up in the American state of West Virginia. Her mother, Ann Reeves Jarvis, was a community leader who taught local women how to keep their families healthy. During the American Civil War, she also cared for soldiers from both sides. When she died in 1905, Anna wanted to find a way to honor her, and all mothers who had worked so hard for their families.\n\nOn May 10, 1908, the first Mother's Day service was held at a church in Grafton, West Virginia. Anna sent 500 white carnations, her mother's favorite flower, for the people who came. She then wrote thousands of letters to politicians, newspapers and church leaders. Her hard work paid off. In 1914, President Woodrow Wilson officially made the second Sunday in May Mother's Day across the United States.\n\nHowever, Anna soon became unhappy. Flower shops raised the price of carnations, and companies began selling cards, candy and gifts. To Anna, this missed the whole point. She wanted the day to be about personal thanks, not shopping. She once said that sending a printed card only shows that you are too lazy to write to the woman who has done more for you than anyone else in the world. She even insisted on the spelling \"Mother's Day,\" not \"Mothers' Day,\" because she wanted each family to honor its own mother.\n\nFor the rest of her life, Anna fought against the holiday she had started. She spent most of her money on this fight and was even arrested once for protesting at an event where carnations were sold. She died in 1948, without any children of her own.\n\nAnna's story still has a lesson for us. A gift is nice, but what many mothers want most cannot be bought: your time, a handwritten note, or simply a sincere \"Thank you.\"", "passage_zh": "每年五月，臺灣的花店裡擺滿了康乃馨，餐廳也被家庭聚餐訂滿。母親節在五月的第二個星期日，是許多商家一年中最忙碌的日子之一。但創立這個節日的女士，可能會非常討厭它現在的樣子。\n\n她的名字是安娜‧賈維斯（Anna Jarvis），在美國的西維吉尼亞州長大。她的母親安‧瑞夫斯‧賈維斯是一位社區領袖，曾教導當地婦女如何維護家人的健康。美國南北戰爭期間，她也照顧過交戰雙方的士兵。1905 年母親過世後，安娜想找個方式紀念她，也紀念所有為家庭辛苦付出的母親。\n\n1908 年 5 月 10 日，第一場母親節禮拜在西維吉尼亞州格拉夫頓的一間教堂舉行。安娜送去 500 朵白色康乃馨給到場的人，那是她母親最喜歡的花。之後，她寫了數千封信給政治人物、報社和教會領袖。她的努力有了回報。1914 年，美國總統威爾遜正式將五月的第二個星期日訂為全美國的母親節。\n\n然而，安娜很快就感到不滿。花店調漲了康乃馨的價格，各家公司也開始販售卡片、糖果和禮物。對安娜來說，這完全失去了這個節日的意義。她希望這一天是用來親自表達感謝，而不是用來購物。她曾說，寄一張印好的卡片，只代表你懶得寫信給這個世界上為你付出最多的女人。她甚至堅持這個節日要拼成「Mother's Day」，而不是「Mothers' Day」，因為她希望每個家庭都向自己的母親致敬。\n\n安娜的餘生一直在反對這個由她親手創立的節日。她把大部分的錢都花在這場抗爭上，甚至曾因為在一場販售康乃馨的活動中抗議而被逮捕。她在 1948 年過世，自己沒有任何子女。\n\n安娜的故事至今仍值得我們學習。禮物固然不錯，但許多母親最想要的東西是買不到的：你的時間、一張親手寫的字條，或只是一句真誠的「謝謝」。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How flower shops in Taiwan get ready for May", "Why carnations are popular gifts around the world", "The life of President Woodrow Wilson", "How Mother's Day began and why its creator later turned against it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文介紹安娜‧賈維斯為了紀念母親而推動母親節，後來卻因為節日商業化而終生反對它。(A) 花店只是第一段的引言；(B) 康乃馨只是故事中的細節；(C) 威爾遜總統只在第三段出現一次。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did Anna Jarvis send white carnations to the first Mother's Day service?", "options": ["They were her mother's favorite flower.", "They were the cheapest flowers at that time.", "The President asked her to do so.", "White was the official color of West Virginia."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Anna sent 500 white carnations, her mother's favorite flower... 白色康乃馨是她母親最喜歡的花。(B)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 威爾遜總統是在 1914 年才正式訂定母親節，與送花無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"paid off\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["cost a lot of money", "was successful", "was stopped", "was forgotten"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段 Her hard work paid off. 後面接著說 1914 年總統正式訂定母親節，可見她的努力「有了成果、成功了」。(A) pay 雖然有「付錢」的意思，但這裡不是指花了很多錢；(C)(D) 都與母親節成為全國節日的結果相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did Anna Jarvis insist on spelling the holiday \"Mother's Day\"?", "options": ["She thought it looked more beautiful on cards.", "She wanted the day to honor only her own mother.", "She wanted each family to honor its own mother.", "The President chose this spelling in 1914."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：because she wanted each family to honor its own mother. 她堅持使用單數所有格的 Mother's，是希望每個家庭都向自己的母親致敬。(B) 她想紀念的不只是自己的母親，第二段說她也想紀念所有辛苦的母親；(A)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following gifts would Anna Jarvis most likely approve of?", "options": ["An expensive dinner at a crowded restaurant", "A handwritten letter from a daughter to her mother", "A printed card with only a name signed on it", "A large box of chocolates from a department store"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說安娜希望這一天是親自表達感謝，而不是購物，並批評寄印好的卡片代表懶得親手寫信；最後一段也提到母親最想要的是時間、親手寫的字條或真誠的道謝，因此她最可能贊成手寫的信。(A)(D) 都是她反對的商業化消費；(C) 正是她批評的印刷卡片。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a67", "title": "When Your Best Friend Makes a New Friend", "title_zh": "當你的好朋友交了新朋友", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Life", "Society"], "passage": "Yi-chen and Mei had been best friends since third grade. They took the same bus to school, shared earphones and always ate lunch together. So when a new student named Annie moved from Malaysia and joined their class in October, Yi-chen didn't think much about it. Then Mei invited Annie to eat lunch with them. Soon Mei and Annie both joined the dance club, and Yi-chen, who was too shy to dance, suddenly found herself alone after school.\n\nYi-chen began to have a heavy feeling she did not like. When Mei laughed at Annie's jokes, Yi-chen's stomach hurt. When she saw photos of the two of them at dance practice, she wanted to cry. She started giving Mei short, cold answers. One night, she posted a sad song online with the words \"Some people forget you so easily.\" She knew Mei would see it.\n\nWhat Yi-chen was feeling was jealousy, and it is very common in friendships, especially among teenagers. Psychologists explain that jealousy usually comes from fear, the fear of losing someone important to us. In a way, it shows how much a friendship matters. The problem is not the feeling itself but what we do with it. Giving someone the silent treatment or posting hidden messages online usually pushes friends further away.\n\nMei did see the post, and she felt hurt and confused. Luckily, Yi-chen's older sister gave her some advice: \"Tell Mei how you feel, but talk about yourself, not about what she did wrong.\" So the next day, Yi-chen said, \"I've been feeling left out since you joined the dance club. I'm scared you don't need me anymore.\" Mei was quiet for a moment. Then she hugged her. \"I thought you were mad at me! I never stopped wanting to be your friend.\"\n\nThings are different now. Mei and Yi-chen still ride the bus together every morning, and on Fridays the three girls eat noodles together after dance practice. Annie is teaching Yi-chen some Malay, and Yi-chen is showing Annie the best snack shops in town. \"I thought a new friend would take something away from me,\" Yi-chen says. \"In the end, she added something.\"", "passage_zh": "怡辰和小美從三年級起就是最好的朋友。她們搭同一班公車上學，一起分享耳機，午餐也總是一起吃。所以，當一位名叫安妮的新同學在十月從馬來西亞搬來、轉進她們班上時，怡辰並沒有想太多。後來，小美邀請安妮和她們一起吃午餐。不久之後，小美和安妮都加入了舞蹈社，而太害羞、不敢跳舞的怡辰，突然發現自己放學後變得孤單一人。\n\n怡辰開始有一種她不喜歡的沉重感覺。小美聽到安妮的笑話哈哈大笑時，怡辰的胃就痛了起來。看到她們兩人在舞蹈社練習的照片時，她就很想哭。她開始用簡短、冷淡的話回應小美。有一天晚上，她在網路上貼了一首悲傷的歌，還配上一句話：「有些人真的很容易就把你忘了。」她知道小美一定會看到。\n\n怡辰的這種感受就是嫉妒，它在友誼中非常常見，在青少年之間尤其如此。心理學家解釋，嫉妒通常來自恐懼，也就是害怕失去對我們很重要的人。從某個角度來看，它也顯示出這段友誼有多重要。問題不在於這種感受本身，而在於我們如何處理它。跟對方冷戰，或在網路上發表意有所指的訊息，通常只會把朋友推得更遠。\n\n小美確實看到了那則貼文，她覺得既受傷又困惑。幸好，怡辰的姊姊給了她一個建議：「告訴小美你的感受，但要談你自己，不要談她做錯了什麼。」於是隔天，怡辰對小美說：「自從你加入舞蹈社以後，我一直覺得自己被冷落了。我好怕你再也不需要我了。」小美沉默了一下，然後抱住了她。「我還以為你在生我的氣！我從來沒有不想當你的朋友啊。」\n\n現在情況不一樣了。小美和怡辰每天早上仍然一起搭公車，每到星期五，三個女孩會在舞蹈社練習後一起去吃麵。安妮在教怡辰說一些馬來語，而怡辰則帶安妮去鎮上最好吃的小吃店。「我本來以為新朋友會從我身上奪走什麼，」怡辰說，「結果，她反而為我增添了一些東西。」", "questions": [{"q": "What lesson does the story mainly teach?", "options": ["It is better to have only one close friend.", "Shy students should join a dance club.", "Feeling jealous is normal, and talking honestly about it can help.", "New students usually take friends away from others."], "answer": 2, "explain": "故事描述怡辰因為好朋友交了新朋友而嫉妒，第三段說明嫉妒很常見、通常來自害怕失去重要的人，第四段她坦白說出感受後與小美和好，所以主要的啟示是：嫉妒是正常的，誠實說出來會有幫助。(A) 結局是三個人都成了朋友；(B) 文中沒有這樣的建議；(D) 與結尾 she added something 的意思相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why was Yi-chen suddenly alone after school?", "options": ["Mei and Annie had joined the dance club, and Yi-chen was too shy to dance.", "Yi-chen had to take a different bus home.", "Mei had moved to Malaysia with her family.", "Yi-chen's sister did not allow her to go out."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段：Mei and Annie both joined the dance club, and Yi-chen, who was too shy to dance, suddenly found herself alone after school. (B)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 從馬來西亞搬來的是安妮，不是小美。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"left out\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very tired", "lucky", "busy", "not included"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段怡辰說，自從小美加入舞蹈社後，她一直 feeling left out，而且害怕小美不再需要她，可知 left out 指「被排除在外、沒有被納入」。(A) 很累、(B) 幸運、(C) 忙碌都不符合她當時的處境。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What advice did Yi-chen's older sister give her?", "options": ["Stop talking to Mei for a few weeks.", "Talk about her own feelings instead of blaming Mei.", "Post more messages online so that Mei would notice.", "Make friends with Annie before talking to Mei."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段姊姊說：Tell Mei how you feel, but talk about yourself, not about what she did wrong. 也就是說出自己的感受，而不是指責小美。(A)(C) 正是第三段說會把朋友推得更遠的做法；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does Yi-chen mean when she says, \"In the end, she added something\"?", "options": ["Annie helped Yi-chen with her math homework.", "Annie joined the dance club after Mei did.", "Annie took Mei away from Yi-chen.", "Annie's friendship made Yi-chen's life richer."], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段怡辰說，她原本以為新朋友會奪走什麼，結果 she added something；配合前面三個女孩一起吃麵、互相教語言和介紹小吃店，可知安妮的友誼讓她的生活更豐富。(A) 文中沒有提到；(B) 與這句話的意思無關；(C) 與她的話意思相反。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a68", "title": "Pastry Chef or Office Job? Talking to My Parents About the Future", "title_zh": "當甜點師傅還是坐辦公室？和爸媽談未來", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Career", "Life"], "passage": "Ever since he baked his first pineapple cake in junior high, Po-wei has wanted to be a pastry chef. Now a second-year student in the food and beverage department of a vocational high school, he dreams of opening his own bakery one day. There is just one problem: his parents. \"Baking is a nice hobby,\" his father keeps saying, \"but you need a stable job, with regular hours and a steady income. Why not study business and work in an office?\"\n\nPo-wei's situation is common. Many teenagers and their parents disagree about future careers. Usually, the parents are not trying to control their children's lives. They are worried. Po-wei's parents had heard that chefs work long hours, stand all day and earn little money at first. Their worries were not completely wrong, but they did not have the whole picture.\n\nInstead of arguing, Po-wei decided to prepare, as if he were getting ready for an important presentation. With help from his homeroom teacher, he collected information about baking programs at universities of science and technology, the pay of experienced pastry chefs, and the skills certificates he could earn. He also spent his summer vacation working part-time at a local bakery, getting up at five every morning. The job was tiring, but it proved to himself, and to his parents, that he was serious.\n\nWhen the family finally sat down to talk, Po-wei did something important: he listened first. He asked his parents exactly what they were afraid of and wrote down their answers. Then he answered each worry one by one. Because he understood their concern about money, he even offered to take some business classes so that he could run his own bakery well someday.\n\nHis parents did not change their minds overnight. But a week later, his mother came home with a bag of flour and asked him to teach her how to make pineapple cakes. Now his father proudly shares Po-wei's cookies with his coworkers. \"I thought I had to win the argument,\" Po-wei says. \"Actually, I just had to help them stop worrying.\"", "passage_zh": "自從國中烤出第一顆鳳梨酥後，柏維就一直想當甜點師傅。現在他是一所高職餐飲管理科的二年級學生，夢想有一天能開一家屬於自己的烘焙坊。只有一個問題：他的爸媽。「烘焙當興趣很不錯，」爸爸總是這麼說，「但你需要一份安穩的工作，有固定的上班時間和穩定的收入。為什麼不去念商科，以後在辦公室上班呢？」\n\n柏維的處境很常見。許多青少年和爸媽對未來的職業看法不同。通常，父母並不是想控制孩子的人生，而是感到擔心。柏維的爸媽聽說廚師工時很長、要整天站著，而且剛開始賺的錢很少。他們的擔心並不完全是錯的，只是他們沒有看到全貌。\n\n柏維沒有跟爸媽爭吵，而是決定好好準備，就像要準備一場重要的簡報一樣。在班導的協助下，他蒐集了科技大學的烘焙相關科系、資深甜點師傅的薪水，以及他可以考取的技術證照等資料。他還利用暑假到當地一家麵包店打工，每天早上五點就起床。這份工作很累，但也向他自己和爸媽證明了他是認真的。\n\n全家人終於坐下來好好談的時候，柏維做了一件很重要的事：他先傾聽。他問爸媽到底在擔心什麼，並把他們的回答寫下來。接著，他一一回應每一項擔憂。因為他理解爸媽對收入的顧慮，他甚至主動提出要修一些商業課程，以便將來能把自己的烘焙坊經營好。\n\n他的爸媽並沒有在一夜之間就改變想法。但一個星期後，媽媽帶了一袋麵粉回家，請他教她做鳳梨酥。現在，爸爸會驕傲地把柏維做的餅乾分給同事吃。「我原本以為我必須吵贏，」柏維說，「其實，我只需要讓他們不再擔心。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why pastry chefs earn more money than office workers", "How a student dealt with a disagreement with his parents about his future", "How to make pineapple cakes at home", "Why vocational high schools should offer business classes"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文描述柏維想當甜點師傅，但爸媽希望他找安穩的工作；他透過蒐集資料、打工證明決心、先傾聽再回應，化解了與爸媽的意見分歧。(A) 文中沒有比較甜點師傅和上班族的收入；(C) 鳳梨酥只是故事細節；(D) 修商業課程只是柏維提出的做法之一。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what were Po-wei's parents worried about?", "options": ["Opening a bakery in Taiwan costs too much money.", "Po-wei was not good enough at baking.", "Po-wei's grades would drop because of his hobby.", "Chefs work long hours, stand all day and earn little at first."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Po-wei's parents had heard that chefs work long hours, stand all day and earn little money at first. (A)(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"stable\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["exciting", "creative", "steady and safe", "difficult"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段爸爸說 a stable job, with regular hours and a steady income（上班時間固定、收入穩定），後面還建議他到辦公室上班，可知 stable 指「安穩、穩定的」。(A) 刺激的、(B) 有創意的、(D) 困難的都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Po-wei work at a bakery during his summer vacation?", "options": ["To show that he was serious about becoming a pastry chef", "To earn money to buy his own oven", "Because his homeroom teacher made him do it", "Because his parents wanted him to give up baking"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：He also spent his summer vacation working part-time at a local bakery... it proved to himself, and to his parents, that he was serious. 打工是為了證明自己是認真的。(B) 文中沒有提到買烤箱；(C) 班導只協助他蒐集資料；(D) 與文意不符。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from what Po-wei's mother did a week later?", "options": ["She wanted to open a bakery herself.", "She was starting to accept and support his dream.", "She thought his pineapple cakes were not good.", "She wanted him to quit his part-time job."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說爸媽沒有一夜之間就改變想法，但一週後媽媽帶麵粉回家，請他教她做鳳梨酥，接著爸爸也驕傲地把他做的餅乾分給同事，可推知媽媽開始接受並支持他的夢想。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 她是想學做鳳梨酥，並不是覺得不好吃。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a69", "title": "The Thank-You Letter I Almost Didn't Deliver", "title_zh": "差點沒送出的感謝信", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Life"], "passage": "Last month, our health teacher, Mr. Huang, gave us an unusual assignment. \"Think of someone who was very kind to you but whom you never properly thanked,\" he said. \"Write that person a letter. Then, if you can, deliver it in person and read it aloud.\" Some of my classmates groaned. Reading a letter aloud to someone's face sounded terribly embarrassing.\n\nMr. Huang explained that the idea came from Martin Seligman, an American psychologist known as one of the founders of positive psychology, the study of what makes life go well. In a 2005 study, his team asked people to try several different exercises, including this \"gratitude visit.\" The gratitude visit produced the biggest boost in happiness, and the effect lasted for about a month.\n\nI knew right away who I should write to: Auntie Lin, who runs a breakfast shop near my old home. When I was in seventh grade, my mother was in the hospital for three months. My father left for work early and came home late, so I often went to school hungry. Auntie Lin noticed. Every morning, she handed me an egg pancake and a cup of warm soy milk, and she never let me pay. \"Pay me back by studying hard,\" she said.\n\nWriting the letter was easy. Delivering it was not. I almost turned back twice on the bus. When I finally arrived, the shop was busy, and Auntie Lin didn't recognize me at first because I had grown so much. My hands shook as I opened the letter, and by the second paragraph, my voice was shaking too. When I looked up, Auntie Lin was wiping her eyes with her apron.\n\n\"I didn't think you would remember,\" she said quietly. Many children had come through her shop over the years, she told me, but very few ever came back. Before I left, she insisted on making me an egg pancake, and once again, she would not take my money.\n\nMr. Huang was right about the science, but I learned something he hadn't mentioned. Later, I read that people who write thank-you letters usually think the receivers will feel less happy than they really do. I believe it. Auntie Lin may have been even happier than I was.", "passage_zh": "上個月，我們的健康教育老師黃老師出了一份不尋常的作業。「想一個曾經對你非常好，但你從來沒有好好道謝的人，」他說，「寫一封信給那個人。然後，如果可以的話，親自把信送去，並且唸給對方聽。」有些同學發出了哀號。當著別人的面唸信，聽起來實在太難為情了。\n\n黃老師解釋，這個點子來自美國心理學家馬汀‧塞利格曼（Martin Seligman），他被認為是正向心理學的創始人之一；正向心理學研究的是什麼能讓人生過得美好。在 2005 年的一項研究中，他的團隊請受試者嘗試好幾種不同的練習，其中就包括這個「感恩拜訪」。結果，感恩拜訪帶來的幸福感提升最大，而且效果持續了大約一個月。\n\n我馬上就知道要寫給誰：林阿姨，她在我以前住的地方附近開了一家早餐店。我國一的時候，媽媽住院了三個月。爸爸一大早就出門上班，很晚才回家，所以我常常餓著肚子上學。林阿姨注意到了。每天早上，她都會遞給我一份蛋餅和一杯溫豆漿，而且從來不讓我付錢。「好好念書，就是還我了，」她說。\n\n寫信很容易，送信就沒那麼簡單了。在公車上，我有兩次差點就掉頭回家。終於到了的時候，店裡正忙，林阿姨一開始沒認出我，因為我長高了好多。我打開信紙時雙手發抖，唸到第二段時，連聲音也在發抖。當我抬起頭，林阿姨正用圍裙擦著眼淚。\n\n「我沒想到你還記得，」她輕聲說。她告訴我，這些年來有很多孩子在她店裡吃過早餐，但很少有人會再回來。我離開前，她堅持要幫我做一份蛋餅，而且又一次不肯收我的錢。\n\n黃老師說的科學沒有錯，但我還學到了一件他沒提到的事。後來我讀到，寫感謝信的人通常會以為收信的人沒那麼開心，但實際上對方開心得多。我相信這是真的。林阿姨也許比我還要開心。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The writer's experience of thanking someone with a letter, and what the writer learned", "How to run a successful breakfast shop", "Why students should not be given difficult homework", "The life story of the psychologist Martin Seligman"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文描述作者因為一份作業而寫感謝信給林阿姨、親自唸給她聽的經過，以及作者從中學到的事。(B) 早餐店只是故事的背景；(C) 作者並沒有反對這份作業；(D) 塞利格曼只在第二段被提到，用來說明作業的由來。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, which of the following is true about the \"gratitude visit\" in Seligman's study?", "options": ["It was the only exercise the researchers tested.", "It made people happier for about a year.", "It gave people the biggest boost in happiness of all the exercises.", "It worked only when people wrote very long letters."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：The gratitude visit produced the biggest boost in happiness, and the effect lasted for about a month. (A) 文中說研究請受試者嘗試好幾種練習；(B) 效果只持續大約一個月，不是一年；(D) 文中沒有提到信的長度。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Auntie Lin give the writer free breakfast?", "options": ["The writer helped out in her shop every morning.", "The writer's father had asked her to.", "The writer had won a prize at school.", "She noticed that the writer often went to school hungry."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說作者國一時媽媽住院，爸爸早出晚歸，所以 I often went to school hungry. Auntie Lin noticed. 林阿姨注意到作者常餓著肚子上學，才每天送早餐給作者。(A)(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"insisted on\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["refused to think about", "firmly wanted to do, even if the other person said no", "forgot to do", "was afraid of"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段 she insisted on making me an egg pancake, and once again, she would not take my money. 林阿姨堅持要做蛋餅給作者，而且又不肯收錢，表示她非常堅定地要這麼做。(A) 拒絕考慮、(C) 忘記做、(D) 害怕，都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why was Auntie Lin wiping her eyes with her apron?", "options": ["The kitchen was full of smoke.", "She was angry that the writer had come at a busy time.", "She was deeply moved by the writer's letter.", "She was sad that the writer had moved away."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段作者唸信唸到聲音發抖，抬頭時看到林阿姨用圍裙擦眼淚；第五段她輕聲說「我沒想到你還記得」，可知她是被這封信深深感動而落淚。(A) 文中沒有提到煙；(B) 她並沒有生氣；(D) 文中沒有說她因作者搬走而難過。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a70", "title": "The Seven-Minute Miracle", "title_zh": "七分鐘的奇蹟", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "Culture"], "passage": "When a Shinkansen, or bullet train, pulls into Tokyo Station, a line of workers in neat uniforms is already waiting on the platform. As the train stops, they bow together. The passengers step off, and the workers hurry inside. Seven minutes later, the train is spotless and ready for its next trip. Travelers from around the world film this scene, and the media have given it a nickname: the \"seven-minute miracle.\"\n\nThe schedule is extremely tight. Many Shinkansen trains stay at Tokyo Station for only about twelve minutes before leaving again. About two minutes are needed for passengers to get off, and about three for new passengers to get on. That leaves just seven minutes for cleaning. To make this possible, each worker is usually responsible for one car with around 100 seats.\n\nIn that short time, the cleaners do a surprising amount of work. They pick up trash, wipe every table, sweep the floor and check under the seats for lost items such as phones and wallets. They also clean the toilets and change the cloth covers on the headrests. Finally, they turn each row of seats around so that all passengers will face the direction the train is going. When they finish, they step out, line up and bow again before the new passengers board.\n\nThe job was not always so admired. In the past, many cleaners felt that passengers hardly noticed them, and some were even embarrassed to tell people what they did. In the mid-2000s, a new manager tried to change this. He told the workers that they were not just cleaners but part of the travel experience, just like the train crew. Workers were encouraged to share ideas, greet passengers and help travelers who were lost. Slowly, the team began to take pride in its work.\n\nToday, the team is famous far beyond Japan. Business students at Harvard University in the United States have even studied it as an example of excellent teamwork. The lesson is simple: when people feel respected, even an ordinary job can be done in an extraordinary way.", "passage_zh": "當一列新幹線（子彈列車）駛進東京車站時，一排穿著整齊制服的工作人員早已在月台上等候。列車一停下，他們便一起鞠躬。乘客下車後，工作人員立刻快步進入車廂。七分鐘後，列車已經一塵不染，準備好展開下一趟行程。來自世界各地的旅客會拍下這一幕，媒體也替它取了個外號：「七分鐘的奇蹟」。\n\n時間安排極為緊湊。許多新幹線列車在東京車站只停留大約十二分鐘，就會再度出發。乘客下車大約需要兩分鐘，新乘客上車大約需要三分鐘，清潔工作就只剩下七分鐘。為了做到這一點，每位工作人員通常負責一節約有一百個座位的車廂。\n\n在這麼短的時間內，清潔人員完成的工作多得驚人。他們撿拾垃圾、擦拭每一張桌子、掃地，並檢查座位底下是否有手機、錢包等遺失物品。他們也會清潔廁所，並更換頭枕上的布套。最後，他們把每一排座位轉向，讓所有乘客都能面向列車行進的方向。完成後，他們走出車廂、排好隊伍，在新乘客上車前再次鞠躬。\n\n這份工作並非一直都受人欽佩。過去，許多清潔人員覺得乘客幾乎不會注意到他們，有些人甚至不好意思告訴別人自己從事什麼工作。2000 年代中期，一位新來的主管試著改變這種情況。他告訴員工，他們不只是清潔人員，而是旅行體驗的一部分，就像列車上的乘務人員一樣。公司鼓勵員工分享想法、向乘客打招呼，並幫助迷路的旅客。漸漸地，這個團隊開始以自己的工作為榮。\n\n如今，這個團隊的名聲遠遠傳到日本以外。美國哈佛大學的商學院學生甚至把它當作優秀團隊合作的範例來研究。其中的道理很簡單：當人們感到受尊重時，即使是平凡的工作，也能做得不平凡。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why Japan's bullet trains always leave and arrive exactly on time", "How a team of cleaners does a fast, careful job and takes pride in it", "How passengers should behave when they ride the Shinkansen", "Why Tokyo Station has become the busiest train station in Japan"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文描述東京車站新幹線清潔人員如何在七分鐘內完成清潔（第二、三段），以及他們如何開始以自己的工作為榮（第四、五段）。(A) 文中沒有談到列車是否準時；(C) 文中沒有教乘客如何搭車；(D) 文中未提及東京車站是否最繁忙，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why do the workers have only seven minutes to clean a train?", "options": ["They must also clean several other trains on the same platform.", "Japanese law says that cleaning must not take longer than seven minutes.", "Part of the train's short stop is needed for passengers to get off and on.", "Each worker has to clean all the cars of a train alone."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：列車在東京車站只停留約十二分鐘，乘客下車約需兩分鐘、新乘客上車約需三分鐘，That leaves just seven minutes for cleaning。(A)(B) 文中皆未提及；(D) 與第二段「每位工作人員通常負責一節車廂」不符，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the cleaners' work?", "options": ["Turning the rows of seats to face the direction of travel", "Looking for things that passengers have left behind", "Changing the covers on the headrests", "Selling drinks and snacks to new passengers"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段列出清潔人員的工作：撿垃圾、擦桌子、掃地、檢查遺失物品 (B)、清潔廁所、更換頭枕布套 (C)，以及把座位轉向列車行進方向 (A)。文中沒有提到販賣飲料和點心，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"spotless\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very crowded", "completely clean", "perfectly on time", "totally empty"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說清潔人員進入車廂七分鐘後，the train is spotless and ready for its next trip，前後文都在談清潔工作，可知 spotless 指「一塵不染、非常乾淨」。(A) 擁擠、(C) 準時、(D) 空無一人都不符合文意，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraphs 4 and 5?", "options": ["The workers did a better job after they began to feel respected and proud.", "The new manager had once been a student at Harvard University.", "Passengers are now asked to clean their own seats before getting off.", "The cleaners became famous because they were paid much more than before."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說新主管讓員工覺得自己是旅行體驗的一部分，團隊因此 began to take pride in its work；第五段的結論是 when people feel respected, even an ordinary job can be done in an extraordinary way，可推論員工感到受尊重後，工作表現更好。(B) 文中只說哈佛學生研究這個團隊；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a71", "title": "Riding to Work in the Sky: The Cable Cars of La Paz", "title_zh": "搭纜車上班：拉巴斯的空中交通", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "Technology"], "passage": "Imagine riding to work in a small glass cabin that hangs from a cable high above a busy city. For thousands of people in La Paz, Bolivia, this is a normal part of the day. La Paz and its neighbor, El Alto, share the world's largest cable car network used as public transportation.\n\nThe reason is geography. La Paz lies in a deep, bowl-shaped valley about 3,600 meters above sea level. On the flat land above it sits El Alto, whose name means \"the high one\" in Spanish. Many people live in El Alto but work or study in La Paz. For years, most people traveled between the two cities by bus or minibus on steep, narrow roads. Traffic jams were common, and the trip could easily take an hour.\n\nIn 2014, the first cable car line opened. Today there are ten lines, each named after a color, such as Red, Yellow and Green. Each cabin carries up to ten passengers, and a new one arrives every few seconds, so people rarely have to wait long. Some trips that once took an hour now take only ten to fifteen minutes.\n\nRosa Mamani, a nurse who lives in El Alto, rides the cable car to her hospital every morning. \"I used to leave home before six, and I was always tired,\" she says. \"Now I can sleep longer, and I enjoy the view of the mountains on the way.\" Because the cable cars run on electricity, they are also quieter and cleaner than the old buses, which filled the streets with smoke.\n\nOf course, the system is not perfect. It cost a lot of money to build, and some people say the government should have spent more on improving buses and roads instead. A cable car also cannot carry as many people as a subway train. Still, in a city where it is very difficult to build tunnels or wide roads, traveling through the sky has turned out to be a smart solution.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，搭乘一個懸掛在纜繩上、高高掠過繁忙城市上空的小玻璃車廂去上班。對玻利維亞拉巴斯的成千上萬人來說，這是每天再平常不過的事。拉巴斯和鄰近的埃爾阿爾托共同擁有全世界最大、作為大眾運輸使用的纜車路網。\n\n原因在於地理環境。拉巴斯位於一個深深的碗狀山谷中，海拔約三千六百公尺。在它上方的平地上是埃爾阿爾托，這個名字在西班牙文中的意思是「高處的那一個」。許多人住在埃爾阿爾托，卻在拉巴斯工作或上學。多年來，大多數人都是在陡峭狹窄的道路上搭公車或小巴往返兩座城市。塞車是家常便飯，一趟路很容易就要花上一個小時。\n\n2014 年，第一條纜車路線通車。如今共有十條路線，每一條都以顏色命名，例如紅線、黃線和綠線。每個車廂最多可載十名乘客，而且每隔幾秒就有一個新車廂到站，所以人們很少需要等很久。有些過去要花一小時的路程，現在只要十到十五分鐘。\n\n住在埃爾阿爾托的護理師羅莎．馬馬尼每天早上搭纜車到醫院上班。「我以前六點前就得出門，總是覺得很累，」她說，「現在我可以多睡一點，還能在路上欣賞山景。」由於纜車靠電力運行，它們也比那些讓街道充滿廢氣的老舊公車更安靜、更乾淨。\n\n當然，這套系統並不完美。它的建造費用很高，有些人認為政府當初應該把更多錢花在改善公車和道路上。纜車的載客量也比不上一列捷運列車。儘管如此，在一個很難開挖隧道或興建寬闊道路的城市裡，從空中通行後來證明是個聰明的解決辦法。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why tourists should ride cable cars to see the mountains of Bolivia", "How a nurse in El Alto found a better job at a hospital in La Paz", "How cable cars became an important way to travel in La Paz and El Alto", "Why the old buses in La Paz caused serious air pollution and noise"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段點出拉巴斯擁有全世界最大的大眾運輸纜車路網，第二至五段說明興建的原因、運作方式、對居民的幫助與缺點，故主旨是纜車成為當地重要的交通方式。(A) 文中沒有推薦觀光；(B) 羅莎只是第四段的例子，而且文中沒有提到她換工作；(D) 老舊公車的廢氣只是第四段的細節，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why was traveling between El Alto and La Paz difficult in the past?", "options": ["The roads were steep and narrow, and traffic was often heavy.", "There were no buses or minibuses between the two cities.", "People needed a special pass to enter La Paz.", "The weather in the valley was usually too cold for travel."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：most people traveled between the two cities by bus or minibus on steep, narrow roads. Traffic jams were common，道路陡峭狹窄，而且經常塞車。(B) 與文中「大多數人搭公車或小巴往返」相反；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"rarely\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["often", "always", "almost never", "sometimes"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說每隔幾秒就有新車廂到站，so people rarely have to wait long，由前後的因果關係可知人們「幾乎不必」等很久，rarely 意為「很少、幾乎不」。(A) 經常、(B) 總是、(D) 有時都與「車廂很快就來」的文意不符，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Rosa Mamani?", "options": ["She has stopped working at the hospital.", "She now leaves home later in the morning than she did before.", "She moved from El Alto to La Paz to be closer to work.", "She prefers taking the bus because it is cheaper."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段羅莎說 I used to leave home before six... Now I can sleep longer，可推論她現在早上出門的時間比以前晚。(A) 她仍每天搭纜車到醫院上班；(C) 文中說她住在埃爾阿爾托；(D) 文中沒有說她偏好搭公車，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a weakness of the cable car system?", "options": ["It was very expensive to build.", "It cannot carry as many people as a subway.", "Some people think the money should have been spent on buses and roads.", "It often has to stop running in bad weather."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段提到的缺點有：建造費用很高 (A)、有人認為錢應該用來改善公車和道路 (C)、載客量比不上捷運列車 (B)。文中沒有提到纜車常因天氣不好而停駛，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a72", "title": "A Life-Saving Gift: The Story of the Seat Belt", "title_zh": "救命的禮物：安全帶的故事", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Technology"], "passage": "Every time you get into a car, you probably pull a belt across your body and click it into place without thinking. This simple action takes about two seconds, yet it may be the most important thing you do during the whole trip. The kind of seat belt most of us use today was invented in Sweden more than sixty years ago, and its story is about more than clever engineering.\n\nMost early cars had no belts at all. Later, some cars had lap belts, which went across the hips only. In a serious crash, these belts could not stop the upper body from flying forward, so a person's head might still hit the steering wheel or the window. The belt itself could also cause injuries to the stomach. Besides, many drivers found belts uncomfortable and simply did not wear them.\n\nIn 1958, the Swedish car company Volvo hired an engineer named Nils Bohlin. He had previously worked on ejection seats, which shoot pilots out of fighter planes in an emergency, so he understood how the human body moves at high speed. Within a year, he came up with a new design. One strap crossed the hips, and another went across the chest, forming a V shape. Both were joined at a single buckle beside the seat, so the belt could be put on with one hand. In 1959, the first Volvo cars with this \"three-point\" belt went on sale in Sweden.\n\nThen Volvo made an unusual decision. The company held a patent on the design, which meant it could have stopped other companies from copying it or made them pay to use it. Instead, Volvo allowed every car maker in the world to use the idea for free. Its leaders believed that saving lives was more important than making money from the invention.\n\nThat decision changed road safety around the world. Today, three-point belts can be found in almost every car, and Volvo estimates that they have saved more than a million lives. So the next time you buckle up, even in the back seat, remember that the belt across your chest was once a gift.", "passage_zh": "每次坐進車裡，你大概都會不假思索地把安全帶拉過身體、扣好。這個簡單的動作只要大約兩秒鐘，卻可能是你整趟路程中最重要的一件事。我們今天大多使用的這種安全帶，是六十多年前在瑞典發明的，而它的故事不只關於巧妙的工程設計。\n\n大多數早期的汽車根本沒有安全帶。後來，有些汽車裝了只橫過臀部的腰帶式安全帶。發生嚴重車禍時，這種安全帶無法阻止上半身向前衝，所以人的頭部仍可能撞上方向盤或車窗。安全帶本身也可能造成腹部受傷。此外，許多駕駛覺得安全帶不舒服，乾脆就不繫。\n\n1958 年，瑞典汽車公司富豪（Volvo）聘請了一位名叫尼爾斯．博林的工程師。他先前曾參與研發彈射座椅，這種座椅能在緊急時刻把飛行員從戰鬥機中彈射出去，因此他很了解人體在高速下會如何移動。不到一年，他就想出了新的設計：一條帶子橫過臀部，另一條斜過胸前，形成一個 V 字形。兩條帶子在座位旁用同一個扣環扣住，因此只用一隻手就能繫上。1959 年，第一批配備這種「三點式」安全帶的富豪汽車在瑞典上市。\n\n接著，富豪做了一個不尋常的決定。這家公司擁有這項設計的專利，也就是說，它原本可以阻止其他公司仿造，或要求對方付費才能使用。然而，富豪卻讓全世界每一家汽車製造商免費使用這個設計。公司的領導者認為，拯救生命比靠這項發明賺錢更重要。\n\n這個決定改變了全世界的行車安全。如今，幾乎每一輛汽車都裝有三點式安全帶，而富豪估計，它們已經拯救了超過一百萬條生命。所以，下次你扣上安全帶時──即使是坐在後座──請記得，橫過你胸前的這條帶子，曾經是一份禮物。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history of car racing and fast sports cars in Sweden", "How the three-point seat belt was invented and given to the world", "Why fighter pilots need ejection seats in an emergency", "How to choose the safest car and seat belts for your family"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從早期安全帶的問題（第二段），談到博林發明三點式安全帶（第三段），以及富豪免費公開這項設計（第四、五段）。(A) 文中未提到賽車；(C) 彈射座椅只是說明博林背景的細節；(D) 文中沒有教人如何選車，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what was one problem with lap belts?", "options": ["They often broke apart when a car hit something at high speed.", "They were too expensive for most drivers to add to their cars.", "They could not keep the upper body from moving forward in a crash.", "They were made only for the front seats of Swedish cars."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：In a serious crash, these belts could not stop the upper body from flying forward，腰帶式安全帶無法阻止上半身向前衝。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention that Nils Bohlin had worked on ejection seats?", "options": ["To show that he had useful knowledge for designing a seat belt", "To explain why he later left Volvo to work for an airplane maker", "To suggest that cars should also have ejection seats", "To show that he had once been a famous fighter pilot himself"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說他做過彈射座椅，so he understood how the human body moves at high speed，作者提到這點是為了說明他具備設計安全帶所需的知識。(B) 文中沒有說他離開富豪；(C) 文中沒有建議汽車裝彈射座椅；(D) 他是工程師，不是飛行員，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"patent\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a safety test that every new car must pass before it is sold", "a large amount of money paid to an engineer for a new idea", "a special type of buckle that holds two straps together", "an official right to be the only one who can make or use an invention"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段解釋擁有 patent 代表公司 could have stopped other companies from copying it or made them pay to use it，可知 patent 是「專利」，也就是只有自己能製造或使用某項發明的正式權利。(A) 安全測試、(B) 一大筆錢、(C) 扣環都不符合句意，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward Volvo's decision in paragraph 4?", "options": ["Doubtful", "Angry", "Admiring", "Uninterested"], "answer": 2, "explain": "作者在第四段說富豪認為拯救生命比賺錢更重要，第五段又說這個決定 changed road safety around the world，並把安全帶形容為 a gift，可見作者對這個決定抱持讚賞的態度。(A) 懷疑、(B) 生氣、(D) 不感興趣都不符合，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a73", "title": "Why Flying East Feels Worse: Understanding Jet Lag", "title_zh": "為什麼往東飛比較難受？認識時差", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "Health"], "passage": "Last summer, Yu-ting flew from Taipei to Paris for a two-week art camp. France is six hours behind Taiwan in summer, so she expected to feel terrible when she arrived. To her surprise, she felt fine. She was a little sleepy on the first evening, but she went to bed at ten, woke up early the next morning and was ready to explore the city.\n\nThe trip home was a different story. After landing in Taipei, Yu-ting lay awake in bed until three in the morning. In her morning classes, she could hardly keep her eyes open, and she had no appetite at lunch. It took her almost a week to feel normal again. Why was the second half of her trip so much harder?\n\nThe answer lies in the body's internal clock, a group of cells in the brain that controls when we feel sleepy, hungry or alert. This clock normally follows a cycle of about 24 hours. When we fly across several time zones quickly, the clock cannot change at once. It keeps telling the body to sleep or wake up according to the time back home. This problem is called jet lag. In general, the body clock can only move about one hour a day.\n\nDirection matters, too. For most people, the natural body clock runs a little longer than 24 hours, so it is easier to stay up later than to go to sleep earlier. Flying west, as Yu-ting did on her way to Paris, makes the day longer, which suits the body. Flying east makes the day shorter. When she got home, her body still thought that 11 p.m. in Taipei was only 5 p.m., so falling asleep was nearly impossible.\n\nThere are ways to make jet lag easier. Light is the strongest signal for the body clock, so after flying east, get plenty of bright morning sunlight and avoid screens late at night. Try to eat and sleep at local times, and keep any naps under thirty minutes. These steps cannot remove jet lag completely, but they can help your body catch up with the clock on the wall.", "passage_zh": "去年夏天，郁婷從臺北飛到巴黎，參加為期兩週的藝術營。夏天時法國比臺灣慢六個小時，所以她以為抵達後會很不舒服。出乎她意料的是，她覺得精神還不錯。第一天晚上她有點睏，但她十點上床睡覺，隔天一早醒來，就準備好去探索這座城市了。\n\n回家的旅程卻是另一回事。回到臺北後，郁婷躺在床上，直到凌晨三點都睡不著。早上上課時，她幾乎睜不開眼睛，午餐時也沒有胃口。她花了將近一個星期才恢復正常。為什麼旅程的後半段會辛苦這麼多呢？\n\n答案就在於人體的生理時鐘，也就是大腦中一群負責控制我們何時感到想睡、飢餓或清醒的細胞。這個時鐘通常遵循大約二十四小時的週期。當我們在短時間內飛越好幾個時區時，生理時鐘無法立刻調整，仍然依照家鄉的時間告訴身體該睡覺或該醒來。這個問題就叫做時差。一般來說，生理時鐘一天只能調整大約一個小時。\n\n飛行的方向也有影響。對大多數人來說，天生的生理時鐘週期比二十四小時稍長一些，所以晚一點睡比早一點睡容易。像郁婷去巴黎時那樣往西飛，會讓一天變長，比較適合身體；往東飛則會讓一天變短。她回到家時，身體仍然以為臺北的晚上十一點只是下午五點，所以幾乎不可能睡得著。\n\n有一些方法可以減輕時差的影響。光線是影響生理時鐘最強烈的訊號，所以往東飛之後，早上要多曬明亮的陽光，晚上則要避免看螢幕。盡量依照當地時間吃飯和睡覺，小睡也不要超過三十分鐘。這些方法無法完全消除時差，但能幫助你的身體追上牆上的時鐘。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To describe the art camp that Yu-ting joined in Paris", "To suggest that students should not travel abroad during the school year", "To compare the weather in Taiwan and France", "To explain why jet lag is often worse after flying east and how to deal with it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "文章先以郁婷去程輕鬆、回程辛苦的經驗開頭（第一、二段），再說明生理時鐘與飛行方向的關係（第三、四段），最後提供減輕時差的方法（第五段）。(A) 藝術營只是故事背景；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What happened to Yu-ting after she returned to Taipei?", "options": ["She fell asleep early every evening and woke up before sunrise.", "She caught a cold on the plane and missed a week of school.", "She could not sleep until late and felt very sleepy in her morning classes.", "She felt fine and was full of energy at school the next morning."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Yu-ting lay awake in bed until three in the morning. In her morning classes, she could hardly keep her eyes open。(A)(D) 與文意相反；(B) 文中沒有提到感冒或請假，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Yu-ting feel fine when she arrived in Paris?", "options": ["Flying west made her day longer, which is easier for the body clock.", "She took a long nap as soon as she arrived at her hotel.", "Paris and Taipei are in the same time zone during the summer.", "She had slept through the whole flight from Taipei to Paris."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Flying west, as Yu-ting did on her way to Paris, makes the day longer, which suits the body。往西飛讓一天變長，比較符合生理時鐘，所以她到巴黎時覺得還好。(B)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 第一段說夏天時法國比臺灣慢六小時，兩地並不在同一時區，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"internal\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very accurate", "electronic", "from another country", "inside the body"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說 internal clock 是 a group of cells in the brain，也就是存在於體內的時鐘，internal 意為「內部的」。(A) 很準確、(B) 電子的、(C) 來自外國的都不符合文意，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, which is the best advice for someone who has just flown east?", "options": ["Take a long nap in the afternoon to make up for lost sleep.", "Use your phone in bed until you feel sleepy.", "Go outside and get bright sunlight in the morning.", "Eat meals at the same times as in your home country."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：after flying east, get plenty of bright morning sunlight。(A) 文中建議小睡不要超過三十分鐘；(B) 文中建議晚上避免看螢幕；(D) 文中建議依照當地時間吃飯，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a74", "title": "The Mother Road Turns 100", "title_zh": "「母親之路」一百歲了", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Travel"], "passage": "On November 11, 1926, a new highway in the United States was given a number that would one day be famous around the world: 66. Route 66 ran about 3,900 kilometers from Chicago, Illinois, to Santa Monica, California, a seaside city next to Los Angeles on the Pacific coast. Along the way, it crossed eight states and passed through farmland, deserts and mountains. In November 2026, the road turns 100 years old.\n\nIn the 1930s, Route 66 became a road of hope. Years of dry weather and huge dust storms ruined farms in states such as Oklahoma, and thousands of families packed everything they owned into old cars and drove west to look for work in California. The writer John Steinbeck described their difficult journey in his 1939 novel The Grapes of Wrath, where he called Route 66 \"the mother road.\" The name stuck.\n\nAfter World War II, the road became a symbol of fun and freedom. More American families owned cars and had time for vacations. Small businesses appeared along the highway to serve them: gas stations, diners and motels with bright neon signs. Some motels even let guests sleep in rooms shaped like tepees, the cone-shaped tents of some Native American peoples. A popular 1946 song invited travelers to \"get your kicks on Route 66.\"\n\nBut the good times did not last. In 1956, the government began building a network of wide, fast interstate highways. One by one, parts of Route 66 were bypassed, as drivers chose the new roads that went around the small towns. When the last section was replaced in 1984, many towns along the old road lost their customers. The following year, Route 66 was officially removed from the national highway system.\n\nYet the mother road refused to disappear. Local people worked to save old buildings and signs, and \"Historic Route 66\" signs now guide drivers along much of the original path. Visitors from Europe, Asia and beyond come to drive it, and the 2006 animated movie Cars, whose sleepy desert town was inspired by Route 66, introduced the road to a new generation. Today, people don't drive Route 66 to get somewhere quickly. For them, the journey itself is the destination.", "passage_zh": "1926 年 11 月 11 日，美國一條新公路得到了一個日後聞名世界的編號：66。66 號公路從伊利諾州的芝加哥延伸到加州的聖塔莫尼卡──緊鄰洛杉磯、位於太平洋岸邊的一座海濱城市，全長約三千九百公里。沿途它穿越八個州，經過農田、沙漠和山脈。2026 年 11 月，這條公路就滿一百歲了。\n\n1930 年代，66 號公路成了一條希望之路。連年乾旱和巨大的沙塵暴摧毀了奧克拉荷馬州等地的農田，成千上萬的家庭把所有家當塞進老舊的汽車裡，一路向西到加州找工作。作家史坦貝克在 1939 年的小說《憤怒的葡萄》中描寫了他們艱辛的旅程，並把 66 號公路稱為「母親之路」。這個名稱從此流傳下來。\n\n第二次世界大戰後，這條公路成為歡樂與自由的象徵。越來越多美國家庭擁有汽車，也有時間去度假。公路沿線出現許多為他們服務的小商家：加油站、小餐館，以及掛著明亮霓虹燈招牌的汽車旅館。有些汽車旅館甚至讓客人住在圓錐帳篷形狀的房間裡，那是某些美洲原住民傳統的帳篷樣式。1946 年一首流行歌曲還邀請旅人「到 66 號公路上找樂子」。\n\n但好景不常。1956 年，政府開始興建由寬敞、快速的州際公路組成的路網。66 號公路一段接一段地被繞過，因為駕駛人選擇了繞開小鎮的新道路。1984 年最後一段被取代後，舊公路沿線的許多小鎮失去了顧客。隔年，66 號公路正式從國家公路系統中除名。\n\n然而，這條母親之路並沒有就此消失。當地居民努力保存老建築和招牌，如今「歷史 66 號公路」的路標引導駕駛人走過原始路線的大部分路段。來自歐洲、亞洲和其他地方的遊客專程來開這條路，而 2006 年的動畫電影《汽車總動員》中那座寧靜的沙漠小鎮，正是以 66 號公路為靈感，讓新一代認識了這條公路。如今，人們開上 66 號公路不是為了快速抵達某個地方。對他們來說，旅程本身就是目的地。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to plan a road trip across the United States on Route 66", "The life and famous novels of the writer John Steinbeck", "Why interstate highways are better than old roads", "The history of Route 66, from its beginning to the present"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文依時間順序介紹 66 號公路：1926 年誕生（第一段）、1930 年代的希望之路（第二段）、戰後的度假熱潮（第三段）、被州際公路取代（第四段），以及今日的復興（第五段）。(A) 文中沒有教人規劃旅程；(B) 史坦貝克只出現在第二段；(C) 文中沒有比較兩者好壞，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did many families travel on Route 66 in the 1930s?", "options": ["They wanted to visit the beaches of California on vacation.", "Their farms were ruined, so they went west to find work.", "They were moving to Chicago to work in factories.", "They wanted to see the places described in a famous novel."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：dry weather and huge dust storms ruined farms... families... drove west to look for work in California。(A) 他們不是去度假；(C) 他們是往西去加州，不是去芝加哥；(D) 小說是描寫他們的旅程，並非他們出發的原因，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, who gave Route 66 the name \"the mother road\"?", "options": ["A singer in a popular 1946 song", "The makers of the movie Cars", "A writer, in a novel about families traveling west", "The government, when the road was first built"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：John Steinbeck described their difficult journey in his 1939 novel The Grapes of Wrath, where he called Route 66 \"the mother road\"。(A) 1946 年的歌曲是邀請旅人到 66 號公路找樂子；(B) 電影是 2006 年才推出；(D) 文中未提及，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"bypassed\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["repaired and made wider for more traffic", "avoided by new roads that went around them", "sold to private companies to build shops", "closed for a long time because of dust storms"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說 parts of Route 66 were bypassed, as drivers chose the new roads that went around the small towns，可知 bypassed 意為「被（新路）繞過」。(A) 修繕拓寬、(C) 賣給民間公司、(D) 因沙塵暴關閉都不符合文意，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by \"the journey itself is the destination\" in the last paragraph?", "options": ["People drive Route 66 mainly to enjoy the trip, not to arrive somewhere fast.", "Route 66 is now the fastest way to get from Chicago to Los Angeles.", "Most visitors get lost because the old road has no signs.", "Travelers have to drive the whole road in one day."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段前一句說 people don't drive Route 66 to get somewhere quickly，可見人們開這條路是為了享受旅程本身，而不是快速抵達目的地。(B) 與文意相反；(C) 文中說有「歷史 66 號公路」路標指引；(D) 文中未提及，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a75", "title": "Thirsty Ships: The Panama Canal's Water Problem", "title_zh": "口渴的運河：巴拿馬運河的缺水難題", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Environment", "Business"], "passage": "Every year, thousands of ships carrying everything from cars to bananas pass through the Panama Canal, a waterway about 80 kilometers long that connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans. Before the canal opened in 1914, a ship traveling from New York to San Francisco had to sail all the way around the southern tip of South America. The canal made that journey about 13,000 kilometers shorter.\n\nMany people imagine the canal as a long, flat channel of seawater, but it actually works like a giant water staircase. The land in the middle of Panama is too high for ships to sail straight across, so ships enter huge chambers called locks. Gates close behind them, and fresh water from a man-made lake, Gatun Lake, flows in and lifts each ship about 26 meters. After crossing the lake, the ship is lowered in the same way on the other side. The whole trip usually takes eight to ten hours.\n\nThis clever system has one weakness: it depends on rain. No pumps are used. Instead, gravity pulls water down from the lake into the locks, and afterward the water flows out to sea. Each ship that passes through uses about 200 million liters of fresh water, enough to fill around 80 Olympic swimming pools.\n\nIn 2023, Panama had one of the driest years in its history, and the water level of Gatun Lake dropped sharply. The canal had to cut the number of ships allowed to cross each day and limit how much cargo they could carry. Long lines of ships waited at both ends, and some companies paid millions of US dollars to move to the front of the line. Shipping costs rose, and some ships chose much longer routes instead.\n\nThe drought also revealed a bigger problem. Gatun Lake supplies drinking water to about half of Panama's people, so the canal and the country's families depend on the same water. Panama is now planning a new reservoir, a man-made lake for storing water, to collect more rain. As the world's climate changes, even a famous engineering wonder must learn to live with less water.", "passage_zh": "每年都有數千艘船隻通過巴拿馬運河，載運的貨物從汽車到香蕉應有盡有。這條水道長約八十公里，連接大西洋與太平洋。在運河於 1914 年開通之前，從紐約開往舊金山的船必須一路繞過南美洲的最南端。運河讓這段航程縮短了大約一萬三千公里。\n\n許多人把運河想像成一條又長又平、充滿海水的水道，但它其實像一座巨大的「水上階梯」。巴拿馬中部的地勢太高，船隻無法直接航行通過，所以船會駛進名為「船閘」的巨大閘室。閘門在船後關上，來自人工湖加通湖的淡水流入閘室，把每艘船抬高約二十六公尺。橫越湖面後，船再以同樣的方式在另一側降下。整段航程通常需要八到十個小時。\n\n這套巧妙的系統有一個弱點：它得靠下雨。運河不使用抽水機，而是靠重力讓湖水往下流進船閘，之後這些水便流入大海。每艘通過的船大約會用掉兩億公升的淡水，足以裝滿大約八十座奧運標準游泳池。\n\n2023 年，巴拿馬經歷了史上最乾燥的年份之一，加通湖的水位大幅下降。運河當局不得不減少每天允許通行的船隻數量，並限制船隻的載貨量。運河兩端都有長長的船隊在等候，有些公司甚至付出數百萬美元，只為了排到隊伍的最前面。航運成本因此上漲，有些船則改走遠得多的航線。\n\n這場乾旱也揭露了一個更大的問題。加通湖供應巴拿馬約一半人口的飲用水，因此運河和全國的家庭仰賴的是同一批水。巴拿馬目前正計畫興建一座新的水庫，也就是用來儲水的人工湖，以收集更多雨水。隨著全球氣候變遷，即使是著名的工程奇蹟，也必須學會在水更少的情況下運作。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the canal depends on fresh water and what happened when water ran short", "Why ships traveling from New York no longer sail to San Francisco", "How Gatun Lake became a popular place for swimming and boating", "Why Panama decided to build a canal between two oceans in 1914"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一、二段介紹運河的功能與船閘的運作方式，第三段指出運河仰賴雨水，第四、五段說明 2023 年乾旱造成的影響與未來的挑戰，故主旨是運河仰賴淡水，以及缺水時發生的問題。(B) 與第一段相反，運河反而讓這條航線變短；(C) 文中未提到游泳；(D) 1914 年只是第一段的細節，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happens to a ship inside a lock?", "options": ["Pumps push seawater into the lock so that the ship can move faster.", "Fresh water flows in and raises the ship to a higher level.", "Workers unload the cargo to make the ship lighter.", "The ship waits there for eight to ten hours."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：fresh water from a man-made lake, Gatun Lake, flows in and lifts each ship about 26 meters。(A) 第三段明言 No pumps are used，而且流入的是淡水不是海水；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 八到十小時是通過整條運河所需的時間，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "About how much fresh water does one ship use to pass through the canal?", "options": ["26 million liters", "80 million liters", "13,000 million liters", "200 million liters"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Each ship that passes through uses about 200 million liters of fresh water。(A) 26 是船被抬高的公尺數；(B) 80 是可裝滿的游泳池數量；(C) 13,000 是航程縮短的公里數，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did shipping costs rise in 2023?", "options": ["Ships had to pay for all the fresh water they used in the locks.", "Gatun Lake was closed to all ships for the whole year because of the drought.", "Fewer ships could cross each day, so many had to wait or take longer routes.", "The canal was closed for repairs, so ships had to sail around South America."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說運河減少每天通行的船數並限制載貨量，船隻大排長龍，接著說 Shipping costs rose, and some ships chose much longer routes instead，可推論成本上漲是因為通行船數減少，船隻必須等待或繞遠路。(A)(D) 文中皆未提及；(B) 文中只說減少每天允許通行的船數，並沒有全年封閉，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"revealed\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["hid", "showed", "solved", "caused"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說乾旱 revealed a bigger problem，接著說明加通湖同時供應運河用水和全國約一半人口的飲用水，可知 revealed 意為「顯示、揭露」出原本存在的問題。(A) 隱藏、(C) 解決、(D) 造成都不符合文意，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a76", "title": "The Map That Is Wrong on Purpose", "title_zh": "故意「畫錯」的地圖", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Arts"], "passage": "Imagine standing on a subway platform in a city you have never visited. You look at the map on the wall, and within seconds you know which line to take and where to change trains. This easy experience owes a lot to a man named Harry Beck and his map of the London Underground, the world's oldest subway system, which opened in 1863.\n\nBefore Beck, the Underground's maps tried to show exactly where every station was. The lines were drawn on top of the real streets, so they twisted and turned. In the city center, stations were packed so closely together that their names were hard to read, while stations in the suburbs were spread far apart near the edges of the map. As more lines were built, the maps became more and more confusing.\n\nBeck worked for the Underground as an engineering draftsman, a person who makes technical drawings. In 1931, he had a simple but brilliant idea. Passengers traveling underground cannot see the streets above them, so they do not need to know real distances. They only need to know the order of the stations and where the lines meet. In his free time, Beck drew a new map using only straight lines, which ran up and down, from side to side, or at 45-degree angles. He placed the stations at almost equal distances and gave the crowded city center more space. The result looked a little like a diagram of an electrical circuit.\n\nAt first, his bosses thought the design was too strange. But in 1933, they printed a small number of pocket maps as a test. Passengers loved them, and the map was soon printed in large numbers. Beck continued to improve it for more than twenty years.\n\nBecause Beck's map is \"wrong\" on purpose, it sometimes fools people. On the map, Leicester Square and Covent Garden stations look just as far apart as many other stations, but they are actually less than 300 meters from each other. Many tourists take the train for a trip that would be quicker on foot. Still, Beck's idea of making a map clear rather than exact spread around the world. Today, subway maps from Tokyo to Taipei follow his basic rules.", "passage_zh": "想像你站在一座從未去過的城市的捷運月台上。你看了看牆上的路線圖，幾秒鐘內就知道該搭哪一條線、在哪裡轉車。這種輕鬆的體驗，很大一部分要歸功於一位名叫哈利．貝克的人，以及他為倫敦地鐵繪製的路線圖。倫敦地鐵於 1863 年通車，是全世界最古老的地下鐵系統。\n\n在貝克之前，倫敦地鐵的路線圖試圖標出每個車站的確切位置。路線是畫在真實的街道上，所以彎來彎去。在市中心，車站擠得太近，站名很難看清楚；郊區的車站則在地圖邊緣分散得很開。隨著越來越多路線興建，路線圖也變得越來越令人困惑。\n\n貝克在倫敦地鐵擔任工程製圖員，也就是繪製技術圖面的人。1931 年，他想到一個簡單卻絕妙的點子：在地底下搭車的乘客看不到上方的街道，所以他們不需要知道真實的距離，只需要知道車站的順序，以及各條路線在哪裡交會。貝克利用空閒時間畫了一張新地圖，只使用直線──上下垂直、左右水平，或是四十五度的斜線。他把車站之間的距離畫得幾乎相等，並給擁擠的市中心更多空間。畫出來的成果看起來有點像電路圖。\n\n起初，他的上司覺得這個設計太奇怪了。但在 1933 年，他們試印了少量的口袋地圖。乘客非常喜愛，這張地圖很快就大量印製。貝克持續改良它長達二十多年。\n\n因為貝克的地圖是刻意「畫錯」的，它有時會讓人上當。在地圖上，萊斯特廣場站和柯芬園站之間的距離看起來和許多其他車站一樣，但實際上兩站相距不到三百公尺。許多觀光客會搭車走一段其實步行更快的路程。儘管如此，貝克「清楚比精確更重要」的想法傳遍了全世界。如今，從東京到臺北，各地的捷運路線圖都遵循他的基本原則。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the London Underground became the world's oldest subway system", "How Harry Beck made a clear subway map that ignores real distances", "Why tourists in London should walk instead of taking the train", "How electrical circuits are designed and drawn by engineers"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段引出貝克的倫敦地鐵路線圖，第二段說明舊地圖的問題，第三、四段描述貝克如何設計新地圖並獲得成功，第五段談它的影響，故主旨是貝克設計出不呈現真實距離、卻清楚易懂的路線圖。(A) 1863 年通車只是第一段的細節；(C) 是第五段的例子；(D) 電路圖只是附帶提及，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what was a problem with the older Underground maps?", "options": ["They did not show the real streets of London above the tunnels.", "They were printed only in black and white, so the lines looked the same.", "Stations in the city center were so crowded that their names were hard to read.", "They showed only the stations in the suburbs, not those in the city center."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：In the city center, stations were packed so closely together that their names were hard to read。(A) 舊地圖正是把路線畫在真實街道上；(B) 文中未提及顏色；(D) 第二段說舊地圖市中心和郊區的車站都有標示，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Beck believe that real distances were not important on a subway map?", "options": ["Passengers underground cannot see the streets, so they only need the order of stations.", "Most passengers already knew the streets of London very well and did not need help.", "The real distances between all the stations on each line were almost the same.", "His bosses told him to make the map as small as possible so it could fit in a pocket."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Passengers traveling underground cannot see the streets above them, so they do not need to know real distances. They only need to know the order of the stations and where the lines meet。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 與第五段「萊斯特廣場站和柯芬園站實際相距不到三百公尺」的說法不符，可見各站真實距離並不相同；(D) 第四段只說上司起初覺得設計太奇怪，沒有說是上司要求縮小地圖，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"suburbs\" in paragraph 2 refers to ______.", "options": ["the busiest shopping streets in the middle of the city", "the deep tunnels that run under the River Thames", "famous places that tourists from abroad like to visit", "the areas at the edge of a city, away from the center"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段把 the city center 和 the suburbs 對比：市中心的車站擠在一起，郊區的車站則 spread far apart near the edges of the map，可知 suburbs 指「市郊，遠離市中心的地區」。(A) 與市中心的意思相反；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why do many tourists take the train between Leicester Square and Covent Garden?", "options": ["The walk between the two stations is long and dangerous at night.", "The map makes the two stations look farther apart than they really are.", "There is no road or sidewalk that connects the two stations.", "Tickets for very short trips like this one are free for tourists."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：On the map, Leicester Square and Covent Garden stations look just as far apart as many other stations, but they are actually less than 300 meters from each other，地圖讓兩站看起來比實際遠，所以觀光客會搭車。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及；而且第五段說這段路 would be quicker on foot，步行反而更快，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a77", "title": "The King of the Road: Jeepneys in the Philippines", "title_zh": "道路之王：菲律賓的吉普尼", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Society"], "passage": "On the busy streets of Manila, the capital of the Philippines, it is hard to miss a jeepney. These long, colorful vehicles are covered with bright paintings, shiny metal decorations and names such as \"Lucky Star\" or \"Queen of the South.\" For millions of Filipinos, jeepneys are the cheapest way to get to school or work, and they are so common that people call them the \"King of the Road.\"\n\nThe jeepney's story began after World War II. When American soldiers left the Philippines, they left behind many military jeeps. Filipinos turned these into something new. They made the jeeps longer, added roofs and two long benches at the back, and painted them in eye-catching colors. A vehicle built for war became a symbol of everyday life.\n\nRiding a jeepney is a lesson in trust. Passengers climb in at the back and sit facing each other. To pay, you hand your money to the person next to you and say \"Bayad po,\" a polite way of saying \"Here's my fare.\" The money is passed from hand to hand until it reaches the driver, and your change comes back the same way. When you want to get off, you simply call out \"Para po!\" and the driver pulls over.\n\nHowever, the King of the Road is in trouble. Many jeepneys are decades old, and their old diesel engines fill the air with black smoke. In 2017, the government started a program to replace them with modern vehicles that cause less pollution, have air conditioning and are safer. Ramon Cruz, who has driven the same jeepney in Manila for thirty years, understands the reasons but is still worried. \"A new one costs more than two million pesos,\" he says. \"How can a driver like me pay for that?\"\n\nMany drivers have protested, and parts of the program have been delayed again and again. Some people want the old jeepneys to disappear quickly for the sake of cleaner air. Others fear that a piece of Filipino culture, along with many drivers' jobs, will be lost. Perhaps the best answer is a new jeepney that is clean and safe but still carries the bright colors and friendly spirit of the old one.", "passage_zh": "在菲律賓首都馬尼拉繁忙的街道上，吉普尼很難不引人注意。這些車身長、色彩繽紛的車輛上布滿鮮豔的彩繪、閃亮的金屬裝飾，還有像「幸運星」或「南方女王」這樣的名字。對數以百萬計的菲律賓人來說，吉普尼是上學或上班最便宜的交通方式，而且它們隨處可見，因此人們稱它們為「道路之王」。\n\n吉普尼的故事始於第二次世界大戰之後。美軍離開菲律賓時，留下了許多軍用吉普車。菲律賓人把它們改造成全新的交通工具：他們把吉普車加長，加上車頂，在後方裝上兩排長椅，再漆上醒目的顏色。一輛為戰爭而打造的車，就這樣成了日常生活的象徵。\n\n搭乘吉普尼是一堂關於信任的課。乘客從車尾上車，面對面坐著。付錢時，你把錢交給旁邊的人，並說「Bayad po」，這是「這是我的車資」的禮貌說法。錢會一手傳一手，直到交到司機手上，找的零錢也會用同樣的方式傳回來。想下車時，只要喊一聲「Para po！」，司機就會靠邊停車。\n\n然而，這位道路之王正面臨困境。許多吉普尼已經有幾十年的車齡，老舊的柴油引擎讓空氣中充滿黑煙。2017 年，政府推動一項計畫，要以汙染較少、有冷氣而且更安全的新式車輛取代它們。在馬尼拉開同一輛吉普尼三十年的拉蒙．克魯茲明白其中的理由，卻仍然很擔心。「一輛新車要價超過兩百萬披索，」他說，「像我這樣的司機怎麼付得起？」\n\n許多司機發起抗議，這項計畫的部分內容也一再延後。有些人為了讓空氣更乾淨，希望老吉普尼盡快消失；另一些人則擔心，一部分的菲律賓文化會隨著許多司機的工作一起消失。或許最好的答案是一種新的吉普尼：它乾淨又安全，卻依然保有老吉普尼鮮豔的色彩和友善的精神。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to drive a jeepney safely on the busy streets of Manila", "Why American soldiers came to the Philippines during World War II", "The history and culture of jeepneys, and the challenge they now face", "How much money jeepney drivers earn each day and how they spend it"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一至三段介紹吉普尼的外觀、起源和搭乘文化，第四、五段說明它因汰換計畫而面臨的困境。(A) 文中沒有教人開車；(B) 美軍只是第二段的背景；(D) 文中沒有提到司機每天的收入，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how did jeepneys begin?", "options": ["Filipinos changed the military jeeps that American soldiers left behind.", "A Japanese company designed them for the Philippine government.", "They were first built as small buses to carry tourists around Manila.", "The government bought them from the United States in 2017."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：When American soldiers left the Philippines, they left behind many military jeeps. Filipinos turned these into something new。(B)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 2017 年是政府推動汰換計畫的時間，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "If you are riding a jeepney and want to get off, what should you do?", "options": ["Pass your money to the driver.", "Say \"Bayad po\" to the person next to you.", "Ring the bell above the window.", "Call out \"Para po!\""], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：When you want to get off, you simply call out \"Para po!\" and the driver pulls over。(A)(B) 是付車資的方式；(C) 文中沒有提到按鈴，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer say that \"riding a jeepney is a lesson in trust\"?", "options": ["Drivers let passengers ride for free if they are polite.", "Passengers depend on strangers to pass their money and change correctly.", "Passengers must trust the driver to choose the fastest route.", "Jeepneys have no seats, so passengers must hold on to each other."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段說乘客把錢交給旁邊的人，一手傳一手送到司機手上，零錢也用同樣方式傳回來，可見乘客必須信任陌生人會把錢和零錢正確傳遞。(A)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 文中說乘客坐在兩排長椅上，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How does Ramon Cruz feel about the government's program?", "options": ["He is excited because he can finally buy a new vehicle.", "He does not care because he plans to stop driving soon.", "He understands why it started, but he is worried about the cost.", "He is angry because he thinks old jeepneys cause no pollution."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：Ramon Cruz... understands the reasons but is still worried，他擔心新車要價超過兩百萬披索，自己付不起。(A) 與他的擔憂相反；(B) 文中沒有說他打算不再開車；(D) 文中沒有說他否認汙染問題，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a78", "title": "The Missing Holiday Pay: Reading My First Payslip", "title_zh": "消失的國定假日工資：讀懂我的第一張薪資單", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Money", "Career"], "passage": "Last fall, I started my first part-time job at a bubble tea shop. I was paid NT$200 an hour. When my payslip for October arrived, I was so excited that I only looked at the final number and smiled. My older brother, who works as an accountant, told me to read it more carefully. \"A payslip is a record of your work,\" he said, \"and records can have mistakes.\"\n\nSo we read it together. The first line showed my working hours: 64 hours in total. Next to it was my pay before deductions, NT$12,800. Deductions are amounts taken out of your pay before you receive it. Mine included a small amount for labor insurance. My brother explained that in Taiwan, part-time workers also have the right to labor insurance. The employer pays 70 percent of the cost, the government pays 10 percent, and the worker pays only 20 percent. He added that my boss had to put at least 6 percent of my pay into a retirement account in my name.\n\nThen my brother noticed something. \"Didn't you work on October 10?\" he asked. I did. National Day is a public holiday, but the shop was open, and I worked a six-hour shift that day. By law, if a worker agrees to work on a national holiday, the employer must pay double for those hours. On my payslip, however, those six hours were paid at the normal rate.\n\nI felt nervous about talking to my boss. I was only seventeen, and I didn't want to seem rude. My brother suggested that I bring photos of the work schedule and ask a polite question instead of making a complaint. The next day, I said, \"Could you check my pay for October 10? I think holiday hours are paid double.\" My manager looked at the payslip, apologized, and said the computer system had not been set up correctly. The missing money was added to my next payment.\n\nNow I know that every worker, even a teenager with a part-time job, should understand their payslip. Keep a record of your hours, learn your basic rights, and don't be afraid to ask. Reading a payslip takes five minutes, but it can protect money you worked hard for.", "passage_zh": "去年秋天，我在一家手搖飲料店開始了人生第一份打工，時薪是新臺幣 200 元。十月的薪資單送來時，我興奮得只看了最後的金額就笑了。我哥哥是會計師，他要我讀得仔細一點。他說：「薪資單是你工作的紀錄，而紀錄是有可能出錯的。」\n\n於是我們一起讀。第一行列出我的工作時數：總共 64 小時。旁邊是扣款前的薪資，新臺幣 12,800 元。「扣款」是指在你拿到薪水之前，先從薪資中扣掉的金額。我的扣款包括一小筆勞保費。哥哥解釋，在臺灣，兼職人員也有參加勞工保險的權利。保費由雇主負擔百分之七十、政府負擔百分之十，勞工自己只付百分之二十。他還告訴我，老闆必須另外提撥至少相當於我薪資百分之六的錢，存進我名下的退休金帳戶。\n\n接著哥哥發現了一件事。他問：「你十月十日不是有上班嗎？」我確實有。國慶日是國定假日，但店裡照常營業，我那天上了六個小時的班。依照法律規定，如果勞工同意在國定假日上班，雇主必須以加倍的工資支付這些時數。然而在我的薪資單上，這六個小時卻是以平常的時薪計算。\n\n要跟老闆談這件事，我覺得很緊張。我才十七歲，不想顯得沒禮貌。哥哥建議我帶著班表的照片，用禮貌的方式提出疑問，而不是去抱怨。隔天我說：「可以請您幫我確認一下十月十日的薪水嗎？我記得國定假日的工時是加倍計薪的。」店長看了薪資單後向我道歉，說是電腦系統沒有設定好。少給的錢後來加進了我下一次的薪水裡。\n\n現在我知道，每一位勞工，即使是打工的青少年，都應該看懂自己的薪資單。記錄自己的工作時數、了解基本權益，而且不要害怕發問。讀一張薪資單只要五分鐘，卻能保護你辛苦工作賺來的錢。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in this passage?", "options": ["To explain why bubble tea shops often need part-time workers", "To show why young workers should read and understand their payslips", "To complain that the shop did not pay the writer on time", "To encourage teenagers to work on national holidays for more money"], "answer": 1, "explain": "作者以自己發現國定假日工資少算的經驗，在最後一段總結：every worker... should understand their payslip，並提醒讀者記錄工時、了解權益，故主旨是說明年輕勞工應讀懂薪資單。(A) 文中未談飲料店為何需要人手；(C) 問題是少算加倍工資，不是遲發薪水；(D) 作者並未鼓勵大家在假日上班賺錢。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "According to the passage, how much of the cost of labor insurance does the worker pay?", "options": ["6 percent", "10 percent", "70 percent", "20 percent"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：The employer pays 70 percent of the cost, the government pays 10 percent, and the worker pays only 20 percent. 勞工只付百分之二十。(A) 6 percent 是雇主提撥退休金的比例；(B) 是政府負擔的比例；(C) 是雇主負擔的比例。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "If the October 10 shift had been paid correctly, what would the writer's pay before deductions have been?", "options": ["NT$12,800", "NT$13,400", "NT$14,000", "NT$15,200"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段說 64 小時的扣款前薪資為 12,800 元（64 × 200），其中國慶日的 6 小時只以平常的時薪計算。第三段指出國定假日工時應加倍，所以這 6 小時還要再多付 6 × 200 = 1,200 元，正確金額為 12,800 + 1,200 = 14,000 元。(A) 是原本算錯的金額；(B) 13,400 元只多加了 600 元（半倍）；(D) 15,200 元是把 6 小時的加倍工資 2,400 元整筆再加上去，重複計算了已付的 1,200 元。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "The word \"rate\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the amount of money paid for each hour", "the speed at which someone works", "the number of customers in a shop", "the time when a shift begins"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 those six hours were paid at the normal rate，指這六小時是用「平常的計薪標準」計算，而法律規定應加倍，可知 rate 指每小時支付的金額（時薪）。(B) 工作速度、(C) 客人數量、(D) 上班時間都與「計薪」的上下文不符。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the writer's manager?", "options": ["The manager refused to pay the writer for the holiday shift.", "The manager probably did not make the mistake on purpose.", "The manager asked the writer to work fewer hours in November.", "The manager thought the writer was rude to ask about the pay."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說店長看了薪資單後 apologized（道歉），並解釋是 the computer system had not been set up correctly，之後也把少給的錢補進下一次的薪水，可推知店長應該不是故意少付。(A) 與「錢補進下次薪水」相反；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 店長向作者道歉，並沒有認為作者無禮。故選 (B)"}]}, {"id": "a79", "title": "Paid to Shop? The Secret Work of Mystery Shoppers", "title_zh": "逛街也能賺錢？神秘客的秘密工作", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Business", "Career"], "passage": "Last Saturday, Lin Mei-ling ordered a sandwich and an iced coffee at a fast-food restaurant in Taichung. She looked like any other customer, but she wasn't. While she waited, she quietly noticed how long it took for someone to greet her, whether the tables were clean, and if the cashier repeated her order correctly. Mei-ling, a 20-year-old university student, is a mystery shopper.\n\nMystery shoppers are people hired to visit shops, restaurants, banks or hotels as ordinary customers and report on their experience. Companies use them because managers cannot be everywhere at once. A restaurant chain with two hundred branches, for example, needs to know whether a customer in a small town gets the same service as one in the city center. The idea is not new. In the 1940s, some American banks and stores hired people to pose as customers to check whether their workers were honest.\n\nThe job sounds like fun, but it takes skill. Before each visit, Mei-ling receives a list of things to check. She cannot take notes in front of the staff, so she types everything into her phone as soon as she leaves. Her report has to be fair and exact. \"The cashier was friendly\" is not enough; she must write down the time, what was said, and how long she waited. \"I'm not there to catch people,\" she explains. \"I'm there to describe what really happened.\"\n\nThe pay is not high. Mei-ling earns NT$300 to NT$500 per visit, and the company pays back the cost of her meal. For her, the real value is what she learns. \"Now I notice things most customers don't, like whether a worker looks up when the door opens,\" she says. She hopes to work in hotel management, and these visits are good training.\n\nHowever, she warns teenagers to be careful. Some online ads offering high pay for \"secret shopping\" are scams. In a common trick, the \"company\" sends you money, asks you to test a money transfer service by sending most of it to another person, and later the first payment turns out to be fake. Real mystery shopping companies never ask you to send or pay money. If a job sounds too good to be true, it probably is.", "passage_zh": "上星期六，林美玲在臺中一家速食店點了一份三明治和一杯冰咖啡。她看起來跟其他客人沒什麼兩樣，但她並不是一般的客人。在等餐的時候，她悄悄留意店員過了多久才來招呼她、桌子乾不乾淨，以及收銀員有沒有正確複誦她點的餐。二十歲的大學生美玲，是一名神秘客。\n\n神秘客是受雇以一般顧客身分造訪商店、餐廳、銀行或飯店，再回報自身消費經驗的人。公司之所以聘用他們，是因為主管不可能同時出現在每個地方。舉例來說，一家有兩百間分店的連鎖餐廳，需要知道小鎮上的顧客是否得到和市中心顧客一樣的服務。這個做法並不新。早在 1940 年代，美國就有一些銀行和商店雇人假扮成顧客，檢查員工是否誠實。\n\n這份工作聽起來很好玩，但需要技巧。每次造訪之前，美玲會收到一份要檢查的項目清單。她不能在店員面前做筆記，所以一離開店裡，就馬上把所有細節輸入手機。她的報告必須公正又精確。光寫「收銀員很友善」是不夠的，她必須記下時間、對方說了什麼，以及她等了多久。她解釋：「我不是去抓別人的錯，而是去描述真正發生的事。」\n\n這份工作的報酬並不高。美玲每次造訪可以賺新臺幣 300 到 500 元，公司也會支付她的餐費。對她來說，真正的價值在於學到的東西。她說：「現在我會注意到大多數客人不會注意的事，例如門打開時，店員有沒有抬頭看。」她希望將來能從事飯店管理的工作，而這些造訪正是很好的訓練。\n\n不過，她提醒青少年要小心。有些以高薪招募「秘密購物員」的網路廣告，其實是詐騙。一種常見的手法是，「公司」先匯一筆錢給你，要你把其中大部分轉給另一個人，藉此「測試」某個匯款服務，結果後來發現第一筆款項是假的。真正的神秘客公司絕不會要你匯錢或付錢。如果一份工作好得令人難以置信，那它多半就不是真的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How fast-food restaurants in Taichung train their cashiers", "Why university students should work in hotel management", "What mystery shoppers do and what people should know about the job", "How banks in the 1940s stopped their workers from stealing money"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文以美玲為例，介紹神秘客的工作內容（第一、二段）、需要的技巧（第三段）、報酬與收穫（第四段），以及要小心的詐騙（第五段）。(A) 文中未談速食店如何訓練收銀員；(B) 飯店管理只是美玲個人的志向；(D) 1940 年代的銀行只是第二段提到的歷史背景，並非主旨。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why do companies hire mystery shoppers?", "options": ["Their managers cannot check every shop or branch themselves.", "They want customers to spend more money in their shops.", "They need extra workers on busy weekends.", "Their own workers are usually dishonest."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Companies use them because managers cannot be everywhere at once. 主管無法同時出現在每個地方，所以需要神秘客去檢查各分店的服務。(B)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第二段只說 1940 年代有銀行用來檢查員工是否誠實，並沒有說員工通常不誠實。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The phrase \"pose as\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["complain to", "take pictures of", "work together with", "pretend to be"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段 hired people to pose as customers to check whether their workers were honest，這些人其實不是真正的客人，而是「假扮成」客人去觀察員工，pose as 意思接近 pretend to be。(A) 向……抱怨、(B) 拍照、(C) 合作，都不符合神秘客的做法。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "Which of the following would Mei-ling most likely write in her report?", "options": ["\"The staff seemed nice, and I liked the coffee a lot.\"", "\"At 2:15 p.m., the cashier said 'Welcome!' about 40 seconds after I came in.\"", "\"The cashier was lazy and should be fired right away.\"", "\"I think this restaurant is better than the one near my school.\""], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段說報告必須 fair and exact，只寫 \"The cashier was friendly\" 不夠，must write down the time, what was said, and how long she waited。(B) 同時包含時間、說了什麼和等了多久，最符合要求。(A)(D) 是個人感受，不夠精確；(C) 帶有批評與處罰的意味，違反她說的 \"I'm not there to catch people\"。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "According to the last paragraph, which job ad should a teenager be most careful about?", "options": ["One that pays NT$400 for each visit to a restaurant", "One that gives you a list of things to check before each visit", "One that asks you to send part of a payment to another person", "One that pays back the cost of the meal you order"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段說常見的詐騙是「公司」先匯錢給你，再要你把大部分轉給別人，並強調 Real mystery shopping companies never ask you to send or pay money，所以要你轉錢給別人的廣告最可疑。(A)(B)(D) 都是第三、四段描述的正常神秘客工作內容。故選 (C)"}]}, {"id": "a80", "title": "A New Kind of Farmer: Flying Drones over the Rice Fields", "title_zh": "新一代農夫：在稻田上空飛無人機", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Career"], "passage": "When Chen Kuan-yu finished college in Taipei, everyone expected him to find an office job in the city. Instead, he moved back to his family's village in Yunlin County and became a farmer. But Kuan-yu does not spend his days bent over in the rice fields with a heavy sprayer on his back. He works with a remote control in his hands, flying a large drone over the crops.\n\nHis drone carries a tank of fertilizer or pesticide, a chemical that protects plants from insects and disease. It flies about two meters above the rice and sprays evenly as it moves. Spraying one hectare by hand can take a farmer several hours under the hot sun, but the drone can finish the same area in about fifteen minutes. Because the drone does the spraying, the farmer also stays away from the chemicals and does not breathe them in.\n\nKuan-yu's services are in high demand. Many farmers in Taiwan are now over sixty, and such tiring work is hard for them. Young people who could help are often working in the cities. So older farmers in the area pay Kuan-yu to spray their fields. During the busiest months, he flies from early morning until the wind gets too strong. Drones cannot fly safely in strong wind or heavy rain, and each battery lasts only about ten minutes, so he always brings several charged ones.\n\nBecoming a drone pilot was not as easy as playing a video game. Kuan-yu had to pass a written test and a flying test to get a government license. He also had to learn about different crops, the right amount of each chemical, and the rules for flying near roads and houses. \"One mistake can damage a neighbor's vegetables or hurt someone,\" he says. \"Being careful is the most important part of the job.\"\n\nKuan-yu believes technology can make farming attractive to young people again. \"People think farming means hard work and low pay,\" he says. \"But farms today need people who understand machines, data and plants. My grandfather taught me how to grow rice. Now I teach him how to read the field maps on my tablet.\"", "passage_zh": "陳冠宇在臺北讀完大學時，大家都以為他會在城市裡找一份辦公室的工作。結果他卻搬回雲林縣家鄉的村子，當起了農夫。不過，冠宇並不是整天彎著腰、背著沉重的噴藥桶在稻田裡工作。他工作時手裡拿的是遙控器，操控一架大型無人機在農作物上空飛行。\n\n他的無人機載著一桶肥料或農藥，農藥是一種保護植物不受昆蟲和疾病侵害的化學藥劑。無人機在稻子上方大約兩公尺處飛行，一邊移動一邊均勻地噴灑。用人工噴灑一公頃的田地，農夫可能得在大太陽底下忙上好幾個小時，但無人機大約十五分鐘就能完成同樣的面積。因為由無人機負責噴灑，農夫也能遠離這些化學藥劑，不會把它們吸進體內。\n\n冠宇的服務非常搶手。臺灣有許多農夫如今都已超過六十歲，這麼辛苦的工作對他們來說很吃力。而原本可以幫忙的年輕人，往往都在城市裡工作。因此，當地年紀較大的農夫會付錢請冠宇幫忙噴灑他們的田。在最忙碌的那幾個月，他從一大早一直飛到風變得太大為止。無人機在強風或大雨中無法安全飛行，而且每顆電池只能撐大約十分鐘，所以他總是帶著好幾顆充好電的電池。\n\n成為無人機操作員可不像打電動那麼簡單。冠宇必須通過筆試和飛行術科測驗，才能取得政府核發的執照。他也必須學習各種農作物的知識、每種化學藥劑的正確用量，以及在道路和房屋附近飛行的規定。他說：「一個失誤就可能毀了鄰居的菜，甚至傷到人。小心謹慎是這份工作最重要的部分。」\n\n冠宇相信，科技能讓年輕人重新覺得務農是有吸引力的。他說：「大家都以為務農就是工作辛苦、收入又低。但現在的農場需要懂機器、懂數據、也懂植物的人。我爺爺教我怎麼種稻，現在換我教他怎麼看懂我平板上的農田地圖。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why most young people in Taiwan do not want to live in the countryside", "How farmers in Yunlin grow rice without using any chemicals", "The dangers of playing video games for too many hours", "A young man who uses drones to make farm work easier and safer"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文介紹冠宇回鄉用無人機替農夫噴灑農藥，說明無人機省時、讓農夫遠離化學藥劑（第二段），以及這份工作需要的技能與他對務農的看法。(A) 年輕人在城市工作只是第三段的一個細節；(B) 與文中使用肥料與農藥的內容不符；(C) 第四段只是用打電動來比喻，並非主題。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "According to the passage, about how long does the drone take to spray one hectare?", "options": ["Ten minutes", "Fifteen minutes", "Several hours", "From early morning until the wind gets strong"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：the drone can finish the same area in about fifteen minutes，同樣一公頃的面積無人機大約十五分鐘完成。(A) 十分鐘是第三段提到的電池續航時間；(C) 好幾個小時是人工噴灑所需的時間；(D) 是他在最忙碌月份的工作時間。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Why do older farmers in the area pay Kuan-yu to spray their fields?", "options": ["The work is too tiring for them, and young people are often away in the cities.", "The government does not allow farmers over sixty to use pesticide.", "Kuan-yu sells his services at a lower price than other workers.", "Their own drones cannot fly in strong wind or heavy rain."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說許多農夫已超過六十歲，such tiring work is hard for them，而且 Young people who could help are often working in the cities，所以他們付錢請冠宇代為噴灑。(B)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 強風大雨無法飛行是所有無人機的限制，文中也沒說這些農夫有自己的無人機。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The phrase \"in high demand\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very expensive", "hard to understand", "wanted by many people", "available only in summer"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段 Kuan-yu's services are in high demand 之後說明農夫年紀大、年輕人不在身邊，所以 older farmers in the area pay Kuan-yu to spray their fields，可知很多人需要他的服務，in high demand 意思是「需求量大、很搶手」。(A) 文中沒有提到價格；(B)(D) 與上下文無關。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "What does Kuan-yu mean when he says, \"Now I teach him how to read the field maps on my tablet\"?", "options": ["His grandfather no longer wants to grow rice.", "Tablets are more useful than real experience on a farm.", "His grandfather taught him nothing important about farming.", "Old farming knowledge and new technology can both be shared in a family."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段冠宇說 My grandfather taught me how to grow rice. Now I teach him how to read the field maps on my tablet. 爺爺傳授種稻的經驗，他則教爺爺使用新科技，表示傳統知識和新技術可以在家人之間互相分享。(A)(C) 與「爺爺教他種稻」不符；(B) 文中並沒有說平板比實際經驗更有用，他反而強調需要懂機器、數據和植物的人。故選 (D)"}]}, {"id": "a81", "title": "The Interview Started Before I Sat Down", "title_zh": "還沒坐下，面試就開始了", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Career", "Life"], "passage": "Last July, I had my first real job interview, for a summer position at the Harbor Science Museum in Kaohsiung. The job was to help children at the hands-on exhibits. I prepared for days. I practiced my answers in front of a mirror, ironed my best shirt and printed three copies of my résumé. I thought I was ready for every question.\n\nI arrived twenty minutes early. In the lobby, an older man in a gray uniform was trying to carry a big box of paper cups through the door, and some cups had fallen onto the floor. I was nervous and wanted to review my notes, but I put my bag down, held the door and helped him pick up the cups. Then I asked the woman at the front desk where the interview would be. She smiled, wrote down my name and told me to wait.\n\nThe interview itself went well, I thought. The manager, Ms. Hsu, asked why I wanted the job and how I would handle a crying child. At the end, she said something surprising: \"Mr. Lo told me you helped him with the cups, and Amy at the front desk said you were very polite. That tells me more than your answers do.\" Mr. Lo, it turned out, was the head of the museum's cleaning team, and Ms. Hsu always asked him and the receptionist about every candidate.\n\nI got the job. Weeks later, I asked Ms. Hsu why she cared so much about the lobby. She explained that in an interview, everyone tries to show their best side. \"But in the lobby, people think nobody important is watching,\" she said. \"How you treat someone who can't give you the job shows how you will treat our visitors and your coworkers.\"\n\nSince then, I have learned that many employers do something similar. Some ask the receptionist for an opinion; others notice how candidates treat the waiter during a lunch meeting. So if you have an interview soon, remember that it may begin the moment you walk through the door. Be kind to everyone, not because someone might be watching, but because it is the right thing to do.", "passage_zh": "去年七月，我參加了人生第一場正式的工作面試，應徵高雄港灣科學博物館的暑期職缺，工作內容是在動手操作的展品區協助小朋友。我準備了好幾天：對著鏡子練習回答、把最好的一件襯衫燙平，還印了三份履歷。我以為自己已經準備好面對任何問題了。\n\n我提早二十分鐘到達。在大廳裡，一位穿著灰色制服的年長男士正想把一大箱紙杯搬進門，有一些紙杯掉到了地上。我很緊張，原本想再看一下筆記，但我還是放下包包，幫他扶著門，並幫他把紙杯撿起來。接著我向櫃檯的小姐詢問面試地點。她微笑著寫下我的名字，請我稍候。\n\n我覺得面試本身進行得很順利。經理許小姐問我為什麼想要這份工作，以及遇到哭鬧的小孩時會怎麼處理。面試最後，她說了一段令我意外的話：「羅先生跟我說你幫他撿了紙杯，櫃檯的 Amy 也說你非常有禮貌。這比你的回答更能讓我了解你。」原來，羅先生是博物館清潔團隊的主管，而許小姐每次都會向他和櫃檯人員詢問每一位應徵者的表現。\n\n我錄取了。幾個星期後，我問許小姐為什麼這麼在意大廳裡發生的事。她解釋，面試的時候，每個人都會努力表現出自己最好的一面。她說：「但在大廳裡，大家會以為沒有重要的人在看。你怎麼對待一個無法給你工作的人，就能看出你將來會怎麼對待我們的參觀者和你的同事。」\n\n從那之後，我才知道許多雇主也會做類似的事。有些人會詢問櫃檯人員的意見，有些人則會在午餐會談時，留意應徵者怎麼對待服務生。所以，如果你最近要參加面試，請記得：從你走進門的那一刻起，面試可能就已經開始了。對每個人都要親切友善，不是因為可能有人在看，而是因為這是應該做的事。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main lesson of this story?", "options": ["Arriving early is the best way to get any job.", "Science museums prefer workers who are good with children.", "How you treat everyone around you can matter as much as your interview answers.", "Candidates should spend their waiting time reviewing their notes."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段許小姐說作者幫忙撿紙杯、對櫃檯有禮貌 tells me more than your answers do，最後一段也提醒讀者面試可能從走進門就開始，要 Be kind to everyone。(A) 提早到只是故事細節，並非重點；(B) 文中沒有比較博物館的用人偏好；(D) 作者正是放下筆記去幫忙，才得到好評價，與此選項相反。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "Who was Mr. Lo?", "options": ["The head of the museum's cleaning team", "The manager who interviewed the writer", "Another student who wanted the same job", "A visitor who was bringing his children to the museum"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Mr. Lo, it turned out, was the head of the museum's cleaning team. 羅先生是博物館清潔團隊的主管，也就是第二段在大廳搬紙杯的那位男士。(B) 面試作者的是經理許小姐；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "According to Ms. Hsu, why does she care about how candidates act in the lobby?", "options": ["The lobby is the place where most visitors complain.", "She wants to know whether candidates can carry heavy boxes.", "She does not have enough time to ask many questions in the interview.", "People show their real behavior when they think no one important is watching."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段許小姐說 in the lobby, people think nobody important is watching，而且 How you treat someone who can't give you the job shows how you will treat our visitors and your coworkers，也就是在大廳的表現更能看出一個人真實的樣子。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 她在意的是待人態度，不是搬東西的能力。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "The word \"candidate\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a person who cleans a building", "a person who is trying to get a job", "a person who visits a museum", "a person who works at the front desk"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段 Ms. Hsu always asked him and the receptionist about every candidate，許小姐向清潔主管和櫃檯詢問的是每一位來面試的人，第五段 how candidates treat the waiter 也是指應徵者，candidate 意思是「應徵者、候選人」。(A) 是羅先生的工作；(C) 是參觀者 visitor；(D) 是 receptionist。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "In paragraph 3, what does \"That\" in \"That tells me more than your answers do\" refer to?", "options": ["The writer's kind and polite behavior before the interview", "The three copies of the writer's résumé", "The writer's answer about how to handle a crying child", "The writer's plan to arrive twenty minutes early"], "answer": 0, "explain": "That 前面一句是 Mr. Lo told me you helped him with the cups, and Amy at the front desk said you were very polite，指作者在面試前幫忙撿紙杯、對櫃檯有禮貌的表現，這比面試回答更能讓經理了解作者。(B)(D) 與上下文無關；(C) 正是 That 拿來比較的 your answers，不會是 That 本身。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a82", "title": "A Year Down Under: My Cousin's Working Holiday", "title_zh": "在南半球的一年：表姊的打工度假", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "Career"], "passage": "When my cousin Ya-ting turned twenty-four, she surprised the whole family. She quit her job at a bank in Taichung and flew to Australia on a working holiday visa. This kind of visa lets young people travel in another country and take jobs there to pay for their trip. Australia accepts Taiwanese applicants aged 18 to 30, and they can stay for up to twelve months.\n\nHer first weeks in Melbourne were hard. Rent was expensive, and nobody called her back after she handed out her résumé at dozens of cafés. \"I thought my English was good,\" she told me on a video call. \"But Australians speak fast and use a lot of slang. When a customer asked for a 'flat white,' I had no idea it was a kind of coffee!\" After a month, she finally found work washing dishes in a small restaurant.\n\nLater, she decided to stay a second year. To do this, she had to work for about three months in certain jobs in regional Australia, such as on farms. So she left the city for a small farm town in Queensland and spent the season picking strawberries. On some farms, workers were paid by the bucket instead of by the hour. Fast pickers did well, but slow pickers like Ya-ting earned very little at first. She learned to read every job offer carefully before saying yes.\n\nLooking back, Ya-ting says the year taught her more than any class. She learned to cook for herself, manage her money and solve problems in a second language. She also made friends from Korea, Germany and Brazil. However, she does not think a working holiday is right for everyone. \"Some people think it's an easy way to get rich. It isn't,\" she says. \"You need a plan, some savings and the courage to ask for help.\"\n\nNow back in Taiwan, Ya-ting works for a travel company that plans trips for Australian visitors. Her English and her experience helped her get the job. \"My year down under wasn't a break from my career,\" she says, using a popular nickname for Australia. \"It was the beginning of it.\"", "passage_zh": "表姊雅婷二十四歲那年，讓全家人都嚇了一跳。她辭掉在臺中一家銀行的工作，拿著打工度假簽證飛到了澳洲。這種簽證讓年輕人可以在另一個國家旅行，並在當地工作來支付旅費。澳洲接受十八到三十歲的臺灣人申請，最長可以停留十二個月。\n\n她在墨爾本的頭幾個星期很辛苦。房租很貴，她在幾十家咖啡店投了履歷，卻沒有人回電。她在視訊通話中告訴我：「我本來以為自己的英文不錯，但澳洲人講話很快，又用很多俚語。有位客人點了一杯『flat white』，我完全不知道那是一種咖啡！」過了一個月，她終於在一家小餐廳找到洗碗的工作。\n\n後來，她決定再多待一年。要做到這一點，她必須在澳洲的偏遠地區從事特定工作大約三個月，例如在農場工作。於是她離開城市，搬到昆士蘭的一個農業小鎮，花了一整季採草莓。有些農場不是按小時計薪，而是按桶計酬。採得快的人收入不錯，但像雅婷這樣採得慢的人，一開始賺得非常少。她因此學會在答應之前，仔細閱讀每一份工作條件。\n\n回想起來，雅婷說那一年教會她的比任何一堂課都多。她學會自己煮飯、管理金錢，並用第二語言解決問題。她也交到了來自韓國、德國和巴西的朋友。不過，她並不認為打工度假適合每一個人。她說：「有些人以為這是輕鬆致富的方法，其實不是。你需要一個計畫、一些存款，還有開口求助的勇氣。」\n\n現在雅婷回到臺灣，在一家為澳洲旅客規劃行程的旅行社工作。她的英文能力和這段經歷幫助她得到了這份工作。她用了一個常用來稱呼澳洲的暱稱「down under」說：「我在 down under 的那一年並不是職涯的中斷，而是職涯的起點。」", "questions": [{"q": "According to the passage, which of the following people could apply for a working holiday visa to Australia?", "options": ["A 16-year-old Taiwanese high school student", "A 27-year-old Taiwanese office worker", "A 33-year-old Taiwanese engineer", "A 40-year-old Taiwanese teacher"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：Australia accepts Taiwanese applicants aged 18 to 30，只有十八到三十歲的臺灣人可以申請，二十七歲符合條件。(A) 未滿十八歲；(C)(D) 都超過三十歲。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Why did Ya-ting move to a farm town in Queensland?", "options": ["Rent in the farm town was cheaper than in Melbourne.", "She could not find any job in the city.", "She wanted to learn how to grow her own food.", "Working there allowed her to stay in Australia for a second year."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：she decided to stay a second year. To do this, she had to work for about three months in certain jobs in regional Australia, such as on farms. 她是為了能多留一年才去農場工作。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 第二段說她一個月後已在餐廳找到洗碗的工作。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "The word \"regional\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["away from the big cities", "near the beach", "full of tourists", "close to the airport"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 in regional Australia, such as on farms，接著說 she left the city for a small farm town，可知 regional 指「大城市以外的地區、地方」。(B)(C)(D) 在文中都找不到依據。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about being \"paid by the bucket\"?", "options": ["Every worker gets the same amount of money each day.", "Workers have to buy their own buckets before they start.", "The more fruit a worker picks, the more money he or she earns.", "It is the best way for new workers to make money."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說按桶計酬時 Fast pickers did well, but slow pickers like Ya-ting earned very little，可推知收入取決於採收的數量，採得越多賺得越多。(A) 若每人收入相同，就不會有快慢的差別；(B) 文中未提及；(D) 雅婷剛開始採得慢、賺得很少，與此相反。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "What is Ya-ting's attitude toward working holidays?", "options": ["She thinks they are a waste of time for young people.", "She thinks they are valuable but require careful preparation.", "She believes they are the fastest way to become rich.", "She feels they are only suitable for people with perfect English."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段雅婷說那一年 taught her more than any class，但也提醒 a working holiday is not right for everyone，需要 a plan, some savings and the courage to ask for help；第五段更說那是職涯的起點，可見她認為打工度假很有價值，但需要好好準備。(A) 與她的正面評價相反；(C) 她明確說打工度假並不是輕鬆致富的方法（It isn't）；(D) 她自己的英文一開始也不完美。故選 (B)"}]}, {"id": "a83", "title": "More Than Selling Snacks: Behind the Convenience Store Counter", "title_zh": "不只是賣零食：便利商店櫃檯後的工作", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Career", "Society"], "passage": "At 6:55 a.m., Huang Po-wei puts on his uniform and steps behind the counter of a convenience store in New Taipei. In the next eight hours, he will do much more than sell drinks and rice balls. \"People think this job is easy,\" says the 19-year-old, who works full-time and takes classes in the evening. \"Then they watch me for ten minutes and change their minds.\"\n\nTaiwan has more than 13,000 convenience stores, one of the highest numbers per person in the world. They are popular because they offer so many services. Customers can pay electricity and phone bills, pick up packages they ordered online, send parcels, print documents, add money to their transit cards and buy train or concert tickets. Each service has its own steps, and the clerk has to know all of them. During his first month, Po-wei carried a small notebook full of instructions in his pocket.\n\nThe morning rush is the hardest part. Office workers want coffee, students want breakfast, and everyone is in a hurry. In just a few minutes, Po-wei may heat a sandwich, make three lattes, scan a pile of bills and search the back room for a customer's package. He also has to check that no food on the shelves is out of date. Because he must handle so many tasks at once, his manager says the most important skill is not speed but staying calm.\n\nNot every customer is kind. Some shout when the line is long, and others blame the clerk for problems with online shops. Po-wei has learned to listen first and then explain clearly. \"Usually, people are angry at the situation, not at me,\" he says. On the other hand, many regular customers greet him by name, and one elderly man brings him a bag of oranges every Lunar New Year.\n\nPo-wei does not plan to stay at the counter forever. He is studying logistics, the business of moving goods from one place to another, and hopes to manage a delivery center someday. He believes the store has prepared him well. \"Here I learned how packages travel, how to deal with people and how to solve problems fast,\" he says. \"That's a lot to learn from a job people call simple.\"", "passage_zh": "早上六點五十五分，黃柏維穿上制服，走進新北市一家便利商店的櫃檯後方。接下來的八個小時，他要做的事遠遠不只是賣飲料和飯糰。這位十九歲的年輕人白天做全職工作，晚上去上課。他說：「大家都以為這份工作很簡單，但只要看我做十分鐘，他們就會改變想法。」\n\n臺灣有超過一萬三千家便利商店，以人口比例來說是全世界最多的地區之一。便利商店受歡迎，是因為它們提供非常多種服務。顧客可以繳電費和電話費、領取網購的包裹、寄送包裹、列印文件、替交通卡加值，還能買火車票或演唱會門票。每一種服務都有自己的操作步驟，店員必須全部都會。剛上班的第一個月，柏維口袋裡總是放著一本寫滿操作說明的小筆記本。\n\n早上的尖峰時段是最辛苦的。上班族要買咖啡，學生要買早餐，而且每個人都在趕時間。短短幾分鐘內，柏維可能要加熱一份三明治、做三杯拿鐵、刷一疊帳單，還要到後面的倉庫找顧客的包裹。他也必須檢查貨架上的食物有沒有過期。因為他必須同時處理這麼多工作，店長說最重要的能力不是速度，而是保持冷靜。\n\n並不是每位顧客都很客氣。有些人在排隊很長時大吼大叫，也有人把網路商店的問題怪到店員頭上。柏維學會先傾聽，再清楚地說明。他說：「通常大家氣的是當下的狀況，而不是我。」另一方面，許多常客會叫他的名字跟他打招呼，還有一位老先生每年農曆新年都會帶一袋橘子給他。\n\n柏維並不打算一直待在櫃檯後面。他正在學習物流，也就是把貨物從一個地方運送到另一個地方的行業，希望將來能管理一個配送中心。他認為這家店讓他做好了準備。他說：「我在這裡學到包裹是怎麼運送的、怎麼跟人相處，還有怎麼快速解決問題。能從一份大家口中『很簡單』的工作學到這麼多，收穫真的不少。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this passage?", "options": ["To show that working in a convenience store is harder than many people think", "To explain how to order products from online shops", "To advertise a new convenience store in New Taipei", "To compare convenience stores in Taiwan with those in other countries"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段柏維說 People think this job is easy... Then they watch me for ten minutes and change their minds，接著各段描述店員要會的各種服務、尖峰時段的忙碌和面對顧客的壓力，目的在說明這份工作比一般人想的困難。(B) 文中未教人網購；(C) 不是廣告；(D) 第二段只提到臺灣便利商店數量多，並沒有和其他國家詳細比較。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "Which of the following services is NOT mentioned in the passage?", "options": ["Paying phone bills", "Sending parcels", "Adding money to transit cards", "Opening a bank account"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段列出的服務有 pay electricity and phone bills、pick up packages、send parcels、print documents、add money to their transit cards、buy train or concert tickets，並沒有提到開銀行帳戶。(A)(B)(C) 都在第二段中出現。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "According to Po-wei's manager, what is the most important skill for the job?", "options": ["Working very fast", "Making good coffee", "Staying calm", "Remembering customers' names"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段最後一句：his manager says the most important skill is not speed but staying calm. 店長認為最重要的是保持冷靜，而不是速度，所以 (A) 正好相反；(B)(D) 雖然是工作中的細節，但店長沒有說它們最重要。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "The phrase \"regular customers\" in paragraph 4 refers to customers who ______.", "options": ["always pay with cash", "come to the store often", "buy only ordinary products", "shout when the line is long"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段 many regular customers greet him by name，這些客人會叫出柏維的名字，表示他們常常來店裡、彼此熟悉，regular customers 指「常客」。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 是同段前面提到的不友善顧客，並非常客的意思。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "What does Po-wei mean when he says, \"Usually, people are angry at the situation, not at me\"?", "options": ["He thinks most customers are rude people.", "He believes the store should hire more clerks.", "He does not take customers' anger personally.", "He wants angry customers to complain to his manager."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說有些顧客會因排隊太久大吼，或把網路商店的問題怪到店員頭上，柏維卻認為大家氣的是「狀況」而不是他本人，表示他不把顧客的怒氣當成針對自己，因此能先傾聽再說明。(A) 他並沒有批評顧客；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)"}]}, {"id": "a84", "title": "Gold Medals for Skilled Hands: The WorldSkills Competition", "title_zh": "給巧手的金牌：國際技能競賽", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Career", "Education"], "passage": "Imagine cutting and coloring hair while a team of judges watches your every move and a clock counts down above your head. One wrong cut could cost you a medal. For young people at the WorldSkills Competition, this is not a bad dream. It is the chance of a lifetime.\n\nWorldSkills is sometimes called the \"Olympics of skills.\" It began in Spain in 1950 as a contest between young workers from Spain and Portugal. Today, it is held every two years in a different city, and more than eighty countries and regions are members. Competitors take part in over fifty skills, from cooking, bricklaying and car painting to web design and robotics. Most competitors must be 22 years old or younger.\n\nKao Jun-hao, a hairdressing student from a vocational high school in Tainan, was one of them. To win a place on his national team, he first had to do well in Taiwan's own skills competitions. Then came more than a year of training. He practiced on mannequin heads, the plastic heads with real hair used in hairdressing schools, for up to ten hours a day. \"My fingers hurt, and some days I wanted to quit,\" he says. \"But every haircut made me a little faster.\"\n\nAt the competition, judges from different countries give points for precision, creativity and safety. A haircut that is only two millimeters too short or a few minutes too late can lose valuable points. Besides gold, silver and bronze medals, competitors who reach a very high international standard receive a Medallion for Excellence. Jun-hao did not win gold, but he came home with a Medallion and great pride.\n\nSupporters say the competition sends an important message: skilled workers are just as valuable as office workers. Many countries need more electricians, mechanics and chefs, and WorldSkills helps young people see these jobs as careers to be proud of. Today, Jun-hao works in a busy salon in Kaohsiung and trains younger students in his free time. \"A medal lasts forever,\" he says, \"but skills are something you use every single day.\"", "passage_zh": "想像一下：你正在剪髮、染髮，一群評審盯著你的每個動作，頭頂上的時鐘還在倒數計時。只要剪錯一刀，就可能與獎牌擦身而過。對國際技能競賽的年輕選手來說，這不是一場惡夢，而是一生難得的機會。\n\n國際技能競賽有時被稱為「技能奧運」。它於 1950 年在西班牙開始，當時是西班牙和葡萄牙年輕勞工之間的比賽。如今這項競賽每兩年在不同的城市舉辦一次，會員國家和地區超過八十個。選手們參加的職類超過五十種，從烹飪、砌磚、汽車噴漆，到網頁設計和機器人都有。大多數選手的年齡必須在二十二歲以下。\n\n來自臺南一所高職、主修美髮的高俊豪就是其中一員。為了進入國家代表隊，他必須先在臺灣本身的技能競賽中取得好成績，接著是一年多的訓練。他在假人頭上練習，也就是美髮學校使用、裝有真髮的塑膠頭模，一天最多練習十個小時。他說：「我的手指好痛，有些日子真的很想放棄。但每剪一次頭髮，我就變得更快一點。」\n\n比賽中，來自不同國家的評審會依照精準度、創意和安全來評分。剪出來的頭髮只要短了兩公釐，或晚了幾分鐘完成，都可能失去寶貴的分數。除了金、銀、銅牌之外，達到極高國際水準的選手還能獲得「優勝獎」。俊豪雖然沒有拿到金牌，卻帶著優勝獎章和滿滿的驕傲回國。\n\n支持者認為，這項競賽傳達了一個重要的訊息：技術人員和辦公室上班族一樣有價值。許多國家都需要更多的電工、技師和廚師，而國際技能競賽幫助年輕人把這些工作看成值得驕傲的職業。現在，俊豪在高雄一家生意興隆的髮廊工作，空閒時還會指導學弟妹。他說：「獎牌可以永遠保存，但技能是你每天都會用到的東西。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to become a famous hairdresser in Kaohsiung", "An international contest that celebrates young people's job skills", "The history of sports competitions between Spain and Portugal", "Why office jobs are better than skilled jobs"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文介紹國際技能競賽的由來與規則（第二、四段），以高俊豪為例說明選手的訓練（第三段），最後說明這項比賽讓人看見技術工作的價值（第五段）。(A) 俊豪在高雄工作只是細節；(C) 西班牙和葡萄牙只是比賽的起源，而且比的是技能不是運動；(D) 與第五段「技術人員和辦公室上班族一樣有價值」不符。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "According to the passage, when and where did WorldSkills begin?", "options": ["In Portugal, in 1950", "In Taiwan, after the first national skills competition", "In Spain, in 1950", "In a different city every two years"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：It began in Spain in 1950 as a contest between young workers from Spain and Portugal. 比賽於 1950 年在西班牙開始。(A) 葡萄牙只是當年參賽的國家之一；(B) 文中未提及；(D) 每兩年換城市舉辦是現在的情況，不是起源。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "Based on the passage, which of the following people could most likely compete at WorldSkills?", "options": ["A 21-year-old car painting student", "A 30-year-old chef who owns a restaurant", "A 45-year-old electrician with many years of experience", "A 26-year-old office worker who likes robots"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Most competitors must be 22 years old or younger. 大多數選手必須在二十二歲以下，而 car painting 也是第二段列出的比賽職類之一，所以二十一歲學汽車噴漆的學生最可能參賽。(B)(C)(D) 年齡都超過二十二歲。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The word \"precision\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["speed", "price", "kindness", "exactness"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段 judges... give points for precision 後面接著說 A haircut that is only two millimeters too short... can lose valuable points，只差兩公釐都會扣分，可知 precision 指「精確、精準」，與 exactness 最接近。(A) 速度是同句後半「晚幾分鐘」才相關，並非 precision 的意思；(B)(C) 與上下文無關。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the Medallion for Excellence?", "options": ["It allows competitors without a gold, silver or bronze medal to be honored for their high level.", "It is given to every competitor who finishes all the tasks in the competition.", "It is only for older competitors who have worked in their skill for years.", "It is worth more than a gold medal and is given to only one person."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說除了金、銀、銅牌之外，competitors who reach a very high international standard receive a Medallion for Excellence，而俊豪沒拿到金牌仍獲得此獎，可推知沒拿到前三名的選手只要達到很高水準，也能獲得肯定。(B) 必須達到很高的國際標準，並非人人都有；(C) 文中未提及，且選手大多在二十二歲以下；(D) 文中沒有說它比金牌更有價值或只頒給一人。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a85", "title": "Four Days a Week: Can a Shorter Work Week Really Work?", "title_zh": "一週只上四天班：縮短工時真的可行嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Business", "Society"], "passage": "Imagine working only four days a week and still getting the same pay. In many countries, including Taiwan, the normal work week is five days, or about forty hours. But in recent years, some companies and governments have started to ask a surprising question: what if people could do the same amount of work in less time?\n\nOne of the best-known experiments took place in Iceland. Between 2015 and 2019, about 2,500 workers in offices, preschools, hospitals and other public workplaces cut their hours from 40 to 35 or 36 a week, with no cut in pay. Researchers found that in most workplaces, the amount and quality of work stayed the same or even improved. Workers said they felt less stressed and had more time for their families and hobbies.\n\nIn 2022, a trial took place in the United Kingdom. Sixty-one companies, with about 2,900 workers, tried a four-day week for six months. The trial followed the \"100-80-100\" model: workers receive 100 percent of their pay for 80 percent of the time, as long as they keep 100 percent of their output. To make this possible, many teams shortened meetings and found ways to waste less time. At the end of the trial, 56 of the 61 companies decided to continue the shorter week.\n\nHowever, a four-day week is not easy for every job. A restaurant, a hospital or a bus company cannot simply close for an extra day, so these workplaces need more staff or careful planning. Some workers in the trials also felt pressure to fit five days of work into four, which made their workdays more tiring. Critics point out another problem: the companies in the trials chose to join, so they may have been more likely to succeed than an average company.\n\nWill the four-day week become normal one day? Nobody knows yet. But the experiments have changed the way many people think about work. Perhaps the most important question is not how many hours people work, but what they achieve in those hours, and whether they have enough energy left for the rest of their lives.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，一週只上四天班，卻還能領到一樣的薪水。在許多國家，包括臺灣，正常的工作週是五天，也就是大約四十個小時。但近幾年來，有些公司和政府開始提出一個令人意外的問題：如果人們能用更少的時間完成同樣多的工作，那會怎麼樣？\n\n最有名的實驗之一發生在冰島。2015 年到 2019 年間，大約 2,500 名在辦公室、幼兒園、醫院和其他公家單位工作的員工，把每週工時從 40 小時減少到 35 或 36 小時，而且薪水沒有減少。研究人員發現，在大多數的工作場所，工作的數量和品質都維持不變，甚至還有進步。員工們表示，他們覺得壓力變小了，也有更多時間陪伴家人和從事嗜好。\n\n2022 年，英國也進行了一項試驗。六十一家公司、大約 2,900 名員工試行了六個月的四天工作週。這項試驗採用「100-80-100」模式：只要員工維持百分之百的工作產出，就能用百分之八十的工作時間，領取百分之百的薪水。為了做到這一點，許多團隊縮短了會議時間，並設法減少浪費的時間。試驗結束時，六十一家公司中有五十六家決定繼續實施較短的工作週。\n\n然而，四天工作週並不適用於每一種工作。餐廳、醫院或公車公司不能說多休一天就多休一天，所以這些工作場所需要更多人手或仔細的安排。在試驗中，也有些員工感到壓力，因為他們必須把五天的工作塞進四天裡完成，讓每個工作天變得更累。批評者還指出另一個問題：參加試驗的公司都是自己選擇加入的，所以它們可能比一般公司更容易成功。\n\n四天工作週有一天會成為常態嗎？目前還沒有人知道。不過，這些實驗已經改變了許多人對工作的看法。也許最重要的問題不在於人們工作了多少小時，而在於他們在這些時間裡完成了什麼，以及他們是否還留有足夠的精力過生活中其他的部分。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why Taiwan should change its work week to four days right away", "How workers in Iceland spend their free time with their families", "What experiments with shorter work weeks found, and the problems they may face", "Why hospitals and restaurants need more workers than other companies"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二、三段介紹冰島和英國縮短工時試驗的結果，第四段說明四天工作週的困難與批評，第五段則反思工作的意義。(A) 文中只提到臺灣一般每週工作四十小時，並未主張臺灣立刻改變；(B) 只是第二段的細節；(D) 醫院、餐廳只是第四段舉的例子。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "In the Iceland experiment, how many hours a week did the workers work after the change?", "options": ["35 or 36 hours", "About 32 hours", "40 hours", "Four days of 10 hours"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：cut their hours from 40 to 35 or 36 a week，冰島的員工把每週工時從 40 小時減為 35 或 36 小時。(C) 40 小時是減少之前的工時；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "In the \"100-80-100\" model, what does the number \"80\" refer to?", "options": ["The percentage of pay that workers receive", "The number of companies in the trial", "The percentage of output that workers must keep", "The percentage of the usual working time"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：workers receive 100 percent of their pay for 80 percent of the time, as long as they keep 100 percent of their output，80 指的是一般工作時間的百分之八十。(A) 薪水是 100%；(C) 產出也必須維持 100%；(B) 參加試驗的公司有 61 家。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why might the results of the trials look better than they really are?", "options": ["Many workers did not tell the truth to the researchers.", "The companies that took part chose to join, so they may have been more likely to succeed.", "Most companies in the trials were restaurants and bus companies.", "The trials lasted only one week, which was too short."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段最後一句：the companies in the trials chose to join, so they may have been more likely to succeed than an average company. 參加的公司是自願加入的，本來就可能比一般公司更容易成功，所以結果可能看起來比較好。(A) 文中未提及；(C) 餐廳、公車公司是用來說明「不易實施」的例子，並未說它們參加試驗；(D) 英國試驗為期六個月，冰島更長達數年。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "The word \"output\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the amount of work that is produced", "the money that a company spends", "the time a worker spends in meetings", "the number of days in a work week"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 as long as they keep 100 percent of their output，指員工即使工時減少，也要維持原本的「工作成果、產出」，接著說團隊縮短會議、減少浪費時間，就是為了完成同樣多的工作，故 output 指產出的工作量。(B)(C)(D) 都不是員工必須維持的「工作成果」。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a86", "title": "The Glue That Didn't Stick: How the Post-it Note Was Born", "title_zh": "黏不牢的膠水：便利貼的誕生", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Business", "Science"], "passage": "In 1968, a scientist named Spencer Silver was working in a laboratory at 3M, an American company. He was trying to make a very strong glue for building airplanes. Instead, he made something strange: a glue that was sticky but weak. It could hold a piece of paper in place, yet the paper could be pulled off easily without tearing and then stuck down again. Nobody could think of a use for it, so for years this \"failed\" glue just sat on the shelf.\n\nSilver did not give up. He kept telling people at 3M about it, and one of them was Art Fry, another scientist there. Fry sang in his church choir every week, and he had a small but annoying problem. He marked the songs in his songbook with little pieces of paper, but they kept falling out. In 1974, he had an idea: what if he put some of Silver's glue on the paper? The bookmark would stay in place without damaging the pages.\n\nFry soon realized that his sticky bookmark could do much more. One day, he stuck one with a question on it to a report for his boss, and his boss wrote an answer on the same little note. At that moment, Fry understood that the sticky paper was not just a bookmark. It was a new way to share short messages.\n\nEven so, success did not come quickly. When 3M first tested the product in four cities in 1977, few people bought it, because they did not understand what it was for. So in 1978, the company gave away free samples in Boise, a city in the state of Idaho. Once people had tried the notes, most of them wanted to buy more. In 1980, the product went on sale across the United States as the Post-it Note.\n\nEven the famous yellow color was an accident. When the team was making early samples, the lab next door happened to have only yellow scrap paper, so that is what they used. Today, the notes come in many colors and sizes and are used all over the world. Their story reminds us that a \"mistake\" is sometimes just an idea waiting for the right problem.", "passage_zh": "1968 年，一位名叫史賓塞・席佛的科學家在美國 3M 公司的實驗室工作。他原本想做出一種非常強力、可以用來製造飛機的膠水，結果卻做出了一種奇怪的東西：一種有黏性、但黏力很弱的膠水。它能把一張紙固定在原位，但紙可以輕易撕下而不會破掉，之後還能再黏回去。沒有人想得出它能拿來做什麼，所以好幾年來，這個「失敗」的膠水就一直被擱在架子上。\n\n席佛並沒有放棄。他不斷向 3M 的同事介紹這項發明，其中一位聽眾是同樣在那裡工作的科學家亞特・傅萊。傅萊每個星期都在教會的唱詩班唱歌，而他有一個小小卻惱人的問題：他用小紙片在歌本裡標記要唱的歌，但紙片老是掉出來。1974 年，他想到一個點子：如果在紙片上塗一些席佛的膠水呢？這樣書籤就能固定不動，又不會損壞書頁。\n\n傅萊很快就發現，他的黏性書籤能做的事遠不止於此。有一天，他把一張寫了問題的書籤貼在要交給上司的報告上，上司就在同一張小紙條上寫下了回答。就在那一刻，傅萊明白這種會黏的紙不只是書籤，而是一種分享簡短訊息的新方法。\n\n即使如此，成功並沒有很快到來。1977 年 3M 第一次在四個城市試賣這項產品時，買的人很少，因為大家不明白它是做什麼用的。於是在 1978 年，公司在愛達荷州的波夕市免費發送樣品。人們一旦試用過，大多數都想再多買一些。1980 年，這項產品以「Post-it 便利貼」的名稱在全美上市。\n\n就連那著名的黃色也是意外。團隊在製作早期樣品時，隔壁實驗室剛好只有黃色的廢紙，於是他們就用了黃色。如今，便利貼有各種顏色和尺寸，全世界都在使用。它的故事提醒我們：一個「錯誤」有時只是一個還在等待合適問題出現的點子。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why 3M stopped making strong glue for airplanes.", "How a weak glue that seemed useless became a popular product.", "How Art Fry became a famous church singer.", "Why yellow is the most popular color for office products."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從第一段席佛做出黏力很弱、看似失敗的膠水，到傅萊想到用途、3M 推廣上市，說明便利貼如何誕生。(A) 文中沒說 3M 停止製造強力膠；(C) 唱詩班只是傅萊想到點子的背景；(D) 第五段只說黃色是意外，並未說它是最受歡迎的顏色。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Why did Art Fry want a new kind of bookmark?", "options": ["His boss asked him to design one for a report.", "His songbook was too heavy to carry to church.", "He wanted to sell bookmarks to the people in his choir.", "The paper bookmarks in his songbook kept falling out."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：He marked the songs in his songbook with little pieces of paper, but they kept falling out. 他用來標記的紙片老是從歌本掉出來。(A) 上司是第三段他用便條紙詢問的對象，並沒有要他設計書籤；(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What happened after 3M gave away free samples in Boise in 1978?", "options": ["Most people who tried the notes wanted to buy more.", "People still did not understand what the notes were for.", "3M decided to stop selling the product in other cities.", "The company changed the color of the notes to yellow."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Once people had tried the notes, most of them wanted to buy more. 試用過的人大多想再買。(B) 是 1977 年四個城市試賣時的情形；(C) 與 1980 年在全美上市的事實相反；(D) 第五段說黃色是製作早期樣品時就用了，並非此時才改。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The word \"annoying\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["exciting", "dangerous", "irritating", "expensive"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段 a small but annoying problem 指紙片一直掉出來這個「小小卻惱人」的問題，annoying（令人煩惱的）與 irritating（令人惱火的）意思最接近。(A) 令人興奮的、(B) 危險的、(D) 昂貴的，都不符合紙片掉落這種小麻煩的語意。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "What lesson does the writer want readers to learn from the story?", "options": ["Scientists should always follow their original plans.", "A failure may become useful when it meets the right problem.", "New products sell well as soon as they are put in stores.", "Free samples are the only way to make people buy something."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段最後一句：a \"mistake\" is sometimes just an idea waiting for the right problem，看似錯誤的發明遇上合適的問題就能變得有用。(A) 席佛的膠水正是偏離原計畫的成果；(C) 與第四段「成功沒有很快到來」相反；(D) 說法太絕對，文中並未這樣主張。故選 (B)"}]}, {"id": "a87", "title": "Six Dots That Opened a World: Louis Braille", "title_zh": "打開世界的六個點：路易・布萊爾", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "History"], "passage": "Every day, millions of blind people around the world read books, check medicine labels and find the right elevator button by touching small raised dots. This reading system is called braille. Surprisingly, it was invented by a teenager.\n\nLouis Braille was born in 1809 in a small village near Paris, France. His father made leather goods such as saddles for horses. When Louis was three, he was playing with a sharp tool in his father's workshop when it slipped and hurt one of his eyes. The wound became infected, and the infection soon spread to his other eye. By the age of five, he was completely blind.\n\nLouis was a bright child, and at ten he won a place at a special school for blind children in Paris. The school had a few books with large raised letters, but they were heavy, and reading them letter by letter was very slow. Then, in 1821, a former army captain named Charles Barbier visited the school. He had created \"night writing,\" a system of raised dots that let soldiers read messages in the dark without using a light. However, his system used up to twelve dots for each sound, so each symbol was too large to feel with a single fingertip.\n\nLouis saw the problem and set to work. By 1824, when he was only fifteen, he had created a much simpler system. Each letter was made from a \"cell\" of just six dots, arranged in two columns of three. Because one cell could be felt with a single touch, readers could move their fingers quickly along a line. Later, Louis also used the dots to write numbers and music.\n\nHowever, his system was not accepted right away. Some sighted teachers preferred the old raised letters, and for a time the new system was even banned at the school. But Louis, who became a teacher there himself, and his students kept using it. He died in 1852, at the age of 43. Two years later, the school finally made braille its official system.\n\nToday braille is used in many languages, including Chinese. Louis Braille's life shows that the people who face a problem are often the best ones to solve it.", "passage_zh": "每天，世界各地有數百萬名視障者靠著觸摸小小的凸點來讀書、確認藥品標籤，以及找到正確的電梯按鈕。這種閱讀系統叫做點字。令人驚訝的是，它是一位青少年發明的。\n\n路易・布萊爾 1809 年出生於法國巴黎附近的一個小村莊。他的父親製作馬鞍等皮革製品。路易三歲時，在父親的工作坊裡玩一件尖銳的工具，工具一滑，傷到了他的一隻眼睛。傷口受到感染，感染很快又擴散到另一隻眼睛。到了五歲，他已經完全失明。\n\n路易是個聰明的孩子，十歲時獲得巴黎一所盲童學校的入學資格。學校裡有幾本印著大型凸起字母的書，但這些書很重，而且一個字母一個字母地摸讀非常緩慢。後來，在 1821 年，一位名叫查爾斯・巴比耶的前陸軍上尉來到學校參訪。他發明了一種「夜間書寫」，是用凸點組成的系統，讓士兵不必點燈也能在黑暗中讀取訊息。不過，他的系統每個聲音最多要用到十二個點，因此每個符號都太大，無法用一個指尖一次摸完。\n\n路易看出了問題所在，便開始動手研究。到了 1824 年，年僅十五歲的他已經創造出一套簡單許多的系統。每個字母都由一個只有六個點的「點字格」組成，排成兩直行、每行三個點。因為一格只要指尖一碰就能摸出來，讀者的手指可以沿著一行字快速移動。後來，路易也用這些點來書寫數字和樂譜。\n\n然而，他的系統並沒有立刻被接受。有些看得見的老師比較喜歡舊式的凸起字母，這套新系統甚至一度在學校裡被禁止使用。但後來也在這所學校任教的路易和他的學生們，仍然繼續使用它。他在 1852 年過世，享年 43 歲。兩年後，學校終於將點字定為正式使用的系統。\n\n如今，點字已運用於包括中文在內的許多語言。路易・布萊爾的一生告訴我們：面對問題的人，往往正是最適合解決問題的人。", "questions": [{"q": "How did Louis Braille lose his sight?", "options": ["He was born blind in a small village near Paris.", "He became sick after reading in the dark for too long.", "An accident with a sharp tool led to an infection that spread to both eyes.", "He was hurt by a soldier while visiting an army camp."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：他三歲時在父親的工作坊玩尖銳工具，傷到一隻眼睛，The wound became infected, and the infection soon spread to his other eye. 傷口感染後擴散到另一隻眼睛，五歲時完全失明。(A) 他並非天生失明；(B)(D) 文中沒有提到。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "Why did Charles Barbier create \"night writing\"?", "options": ["To let soldiers read messages in the dark without a light.", "To help blind children learn to write music.", "To make books with large raised letters lighter.", "To teach Louis Braille how to read and write."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：a system of raised dots that let soldiers read messages in the dark without using a light，夜間書寫是讓士兵不必點燈就能在黑暗中讀取訊息。(B) 用點字寫樂譜是布萊爾後來做的事；(C) 文中沒有提到；(D) 巴比耶只是來學校參訪，並非為了教布萊爾而發明。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "Why was Louis Braille's system easier to use than Barbier's?", "options": ["It used large raised letters instead of dots.", "It was written on lighter and cheaper paper.", "It used twelve dots so that each sound was clearer.", "Each cell had only six dots and could be felt with one touch."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：Each letter was made from a \"cell\" of just six dots... Because one cell could be felt with a single touch, readers could move their fingers quickly，六點一格、一摸就能讀出，所以讀得快。(A) 大型凸起字母是舊式書本的做法；(B) 文中未提及；(C) 十二點正是巴比耶系統的缺點。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "The word \"banned\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["praised", "not allowed", "taught", "made easier"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說有些老師偏好舊式字母，新系統 was even banned at the school，但學生仍繼續使用，可知 banned 是「被禁止」，與 not allowed 意思最接近。(A) 受到稱讚、(C) 被教授、(D) 被簡化，都與「沒有立刻被接受」的上下文不符。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "What does the writer most likely want to show in the last paragraph?", "options": ["People who face a problem themselves often understand best how to solve it.", "Braille is now used only in European languages.", "Blind students should not try to invent new things.", "Louis Braille became rich because of his invention."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段：the people who face a problem are often the best ones to solve it，布萊爾本身是視障者，最了解問題並解決了它。(B) 與「包括中文在內的許多語言」相反；(C) 與文意相反；(D) 文中沒有提到他因此致富。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a88", "title": "The Doctor Who Told Doctors to Wash Their Hands", "title_zh": "要醫生洗手的醫生", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "History"], "passage": "Today, washing your hands seems like the most basic rule of good health. But less than 200 years ago, even doctors did not think it was important. One of the first people to show that clean hands could save lives was a Hungarian doctor named Ignaz Semmelweis. His story is both inspiring and sad.\n\nIn 1846, Semmelweis began working at a large hospital in Vienna, Austria. The hospital had two clinics where women gave birth, and something about them puzzled him. In the first clinic, where babies were delivered by doctors and medical students, about one in ten mothers died of a terrible illness called childbed fever. In the second clinic, which was run by midwives, women trained to help with births, far fewer mothers died. Some women even begged to be sent to the second clinic.\n\nIn 1847, a doctor friend of Semmelweis was cut by a student's knife while examining a dead body. He soon became sick and died, and his illness looked just like childbed fever. Semmelweis then noticed something important. The doctors and students often came straight from examining dead bodies to help women give birth, but the midwives never did this. Perhaps the doctors were carrying something deadly on their hands.\n\nAt that time, nobody knew about germs. Still, Semmelweis ordered every doctor and student to wash their hands in a chlorine solution before examining patients. The result was amazing. Within months, deaths in the first clinic fell sharply, to about one or two mothers in a hundred.\n\nYou might think other doctors thanked him. Instead, many of them were offended. They did not like the idea that they themselves were causing their patients' deaths. Semmelweis was also slow to publish his results, and he often argued angrily with people who disagreed. He lost his job in Vienna and later returned to Hungary. He died in 1865, at only 47, before his idea was widely accepted.\n\nIn the years that followed, scientists such as Louis Pasteur and Joseph Lister showed that tiny germs cause many diseases. The world finally understood that Semmelweis had been right. Today, he is remembered as a pioneer of handwashing, a simple habit that still protects millions of people every day.", "passage_zh": "今天，洗手似乎是維持健康最基本的規則。但在不到兩百年前，就連醫生也不認為洗手很重要。最早證明乾淨的雙手能拯救生命的人之一，是一位名叫伊格納茲・塞麥爾維斯的匈牙利醫生。他的故事既激勵人心，又令人難過。\n\n1846 年，塞麥爾維斯開始在奧地利維也納的一家大醫院工作。這家醫院有兩間讓婦女生產的產科病房，其中有件事讓他百思不解。在第一病房，接生的是醫生和醫學生，大約每十位產婦就有一位死於一種叫做「產褥熱」的可怕疾病。而在由助產士（受過訓練、協助生產的女性）負責的第二病房，死亡的產婦卻少得多。有些婦女甚至哀求院方把她們送到第二病房。\n\n1847 年，塞麥爾維斯的一位醫生朋友在檢查遺體時，被學生的刀子割傷。他很快就病倒並過世了，而他的病症看起來和產褥熱一模一樣。塞麥爾維斯這時注意到一件重要的事：醫生和學生常常剛檢查完遺體，就直接去幫婦女接生，但助產士從來不做這種事。也許醫生的手上帶著某種致命的東西。\n\n當時沒有人知道細菌的存在。儘管如此，塞麥爾維斯還是要求每一位醫生和學生在檢查病人之前，都要用含氯的溶液洗手。結果令人驚訝：短短幾個月內，第一病房的死亡人數大幅下降，降到大約每一百位產婦中只有一到兩位死亡。\n\n你也許以為其他醫生會感謝他。相反地，許多人覺得受到冒犯。他們不喜歡「是自己害死了病人」這種說法。塞麥爾維斯也遲遲沒有發表研究結果，還常常和意見不同的人激烈爭吵。他失去了在維也納的工作，後來回到匈牙利。他在 1865 年過世，年僅 47 歲，生前沒能看到自己的觀念被普遍接受。\n\n在接下來的幾年裡，巴斯德和李斯特等科學家證明了許多疾病是由微小的細菌引起的。世人終於明白塞麥爾維斯是對的。如今，他被視為推廣洗手的先驅，而洗手這個簡單的習慣，至今每天仍保護著數百萬人。", "questions": [{"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did some women beg to be sent to the second clinic?", "options": ["The second clinic was closer to their homes.", "The doctors in the second clinic were more famous.", "The second clinic was cleaner and more comfortable.", "Far fewer mothers died in the second clinic."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：In the second clinic... far fewer mothers died. Some women even begged to be sent to the second clinic. 第二病房死亡的產婦少得多，所以婦女哀求被送去那裡。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 第二病房是由助產士負責，不是醫生。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What made Semmelweis think that the doctors' hands might be the problem?", "options": ["The midwives told him that the doctors never washed their hands.", "His friend died of a similar illness after being cut while examining a dead body.", "He found germs on the doctors' hands with a microscope.", "Louis Pasteur wrote him a letter about germs."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：他的朋友在檢查遺體時被刀割傷，病逝時的症狀 looked just like childbed fever，他因此聯想到醫生剛摸過遺體就去接生。(A) 文中沒有提到；(C) 第四段說 At that time, nobody knew about germs，當時沒人知道細菌；(D) 最後一段說巴斯德等人是在後來才證明細菌致病，文中也沒有提到巴斯德寫信給他。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "The word \"offended\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["grateful", "confused", "upset", "frightened"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：You might think other doctors thanked him. Instead, many of them were offended，Instead 表示與「感謝」相反，且他們不喜歡「自己害死病人」的說法，可知 offended 是「被冒犯而不高興的」，與 upset 意思最接近。(A) 感激的與文意相反；(B) 困惑的、(D) 害怕的都不符合上下文。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 5, why did many doctors refuse to accept Semmelweis's idea?", "options": ["They did not want to believe that they were causing their patients' deaths.", "Washing their hands in chlorine took too much time and money.", "The number of deaths in the first clinic did not change.", "Semmelweis had already lost his job in Vienna."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：They did not like the idea that they themselves were causing their patients' deaths. 醫生們不願接受自己就是病人死亡的原因。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 與第四段死亡人數大幅下降相反；(D) 他失去工作是被反對之後的結果，不是原因。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward Semmelweis?", "options": ["Critical, because he caused the deaths of many mothers.", "Doubtful, because his results were never proven.", "Respectful, and sorry that he was not recognized in his lifetime.", "Amused, because his idea about handwashing seems silly today."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段說他的故事 both inspiring and sad，最後一段稱他為 a pioneer of handwashing（洗手的先驅），並惋惜他生前觀念未被接受，可見作者對他是尊敬且同情的。(A) 害死產婦的是當時不洗手的做法，並非他；(B) 最後一段說世人證明他是對的；(D) 作者並未覺得洗手的觀念可笑。故選 (C)"}]}, {"id": "a89", "title": "Light from the Wind: William Kamkwamba's Windmill", "title_zh": "風帶來的光：威廉・坎寬巴的風車", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Education"], "passage": "In 2001, a terrible drought hit Malawi, a small country in southeastern Africa. With almost no rain, the corn died in the fields, and millions of people went hungry. William Kamkwamba, a 14-year-old boy from a farming family, ate just one small meal a day. Worse, his parents could no longer pay his school fees, so he had to leave school.\n\nBut William did not want to stop learning. He began visiting a small library at a nearby primary school. His English was not very good, so he studied the pictures and diagrams in the science books carefully. One book, called Using Energy, had a picture of a windmill on its cover. William learned that wind could turn blades, and that turning blades could produce electricity. He thought, \"If I build a windmill, my family can have light at night.\"\n\nThere was no money to buy parts, so William searched a scrapyard, a place where old and broken machines are thrown away. He collected a broken bicycle, the fan from an old tractor, plastic pipes and a small generator from a bicycle light. For the tower, he cut down tall, straight trees. Many villagers laughed at him and called him crazy as he carried home piles of junk.\n\nIn 2002, William finally finished his windmill. When the wind blew, the blades turned, and a small light bulb began to glow. Soon his home had electric lights and a working radio, things very few families in his village had. Later, he built more windmills, including one that pumped water for his family's fields.\n\nNews of the boy who brought power to his village spread. Journalists wrote about him, and he was invited to tell his story at international meetings. With help from supporters, William returned to school and later graduated from Dartmouth College in the United States in 2014. His story became a book and a film, both called The Boy Who Harnessed the Wind, meaning the boy who caught the wind's power and put it to work.\n\nIn one of his talks, William gave this advice to young people facing hard times: \"Trust yourself and believe. Whatever happens, don't give up.\"", "passage_zh": "2001 年，一場嚴重的乾旱襲擊了非洲東南部的小國馬拉威。由於幾乎沒有下雨，田裡的玉米都枯死了，數百萬人因此挨餓。十四歲的威廉・坎寬巴出身農家，每天只吃得到一小餐。更糟的是，他的父母再也付不起他的學費，他只好離開學校。\n\n但威廉不想停止學習。他開始到附近一所小學的小圖書館看書。他的英文不太好，所以他仔細研究科學書裡的圖片和圖解。其中一本叫做《使用能源》的書，封面上有一座風車的圖片。威廉從書中得知，風能轉動葉片，而轉動的葉片能產生電力。他心想：「如果我能做出一座風車，我們家晚上就能有燈光了。」\n\n因為沒有錢買零件，威廉就到廢料場去找，那是丟棄老舊、損壞機器的地方。他收集了一輛壞掉的腳踏車、一台舊曳引機的風扇、幾根塑膠管，以及腳踏車車燈用的小型發電機。至於塔架，他砍下又高又直的樹木來搭建。他把一堆堆破銅爛鐵搬回家時，許多村民都嘲笑他，說他瘋了。\n\n2002 年，威廉終於完成了他的風車。風一吹，葉片就轉動，一個小燈泡亮了起來。很快地，他家就有了電燈和能用的收音機，這些都是村裡很少人家擁有的東西。後來，他又做了更多風車，其中一座能抽水灌溉他家的田地。\n\n這個男孩為村子帶來電力的消息很快傳開了。記者報導他的故事，他也受邀到國際會議上分享經歷。在支持者的幫助下，威廉重返校園，後來在 2014 年從美國的達特茅斯學院畢業。他的故事被寫成書，也拍成電影，名稱都叫《馭風男孩》（The Boy Who Harnessed the Wind），意思是捕捉風的力量、讓它為人所用的男孩。\n\n在一次演講中，威廉給正面臨困境的年輕人這樣的建議：「相信自己，懷抱信念。無論發生什麼事，都不要放棄。」", "questions": [{"q": "Why did William Kamkwamba have to leave school?", "options": ["His family could not afford his school fees after the drought.", "He wanted to spend all his time building windmills.", "His school was closed because there was no electricity.", "His English was too poor for him to pass his classes."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段：旱災造成饑荒，his parents could no longer pay his school fees, so he had to leave school，父母付不起學費。(B) 他是離開學校之後才開始研究風車；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第二段雖說他英文不好，但那不是他離開學校的原因。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "How did William learn about windmills even though his English was not very good?", "options": ["A teacher at the primary school explained the science to him.", "He watched videos about windmills on the Internet.", "He carefully studied the pictures and diagrams in library books.", "Villagers who had built windmills before taught him."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：His English was not very good, so he studied the pictures and diagrams in the science books carefully. 他靠仔細研究圖書館書中的圖片和圖解來學習。(A)(B) 文中沒有提到；(D) 第三段說村民嘲笑他，並沒有教他。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "Which of the following did William NOT use to build his first windmill?", "options": ["The fan from an old tractor", "A car engine", "Plastic pipes", "A broken bicycle"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段列出他用的材料：a broken bicycle, the fan from an old tractor, plastic pipes and a small generator from a bicycle light，另外用樹木做塔架，其中並沒有汽車引擎。(A)(C)(D) 都是文中提到的材料。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "In paragraph 5, the word \"harnessed\" in the book title The Boy Who Harnessed the Wind is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["was afraid of", "ran faster than", "complained about", "controlled and used"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段在書名後解釋 meaning the boy who caught the wind's power and put it to work，即捕捉風的力量並加以利用，所以 harnessed 是「控制並利用」，選 controlled and used。(A) 害怕、(B) 跑得比……快、(C) 抱怨，都不符合他用風力發電的故事。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What is the main purpose of this passage?", "options": ["To explain how farmers in Malawi grow corn.", "To warn readers about the dangers of collecting junk.", "To compare different ways of producing electricity.", "To show how a boy's curiosity and hard work changed his life and village."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文敘述威廉在饑荒中失學，卻靠自學和不放棄的精神做出風車，為家鄉帶來電力，最後重返校園，旨在展現好奇心與努力如何改變他和村子。(A) 玉米只是第一段的背景；(B) 文中沒有警告撿廢料的危險；(C) 文中只談風力，沒有比較發電方式。故選 (D)"}]}, {"id": "a90", "title": "The Vegetable Seller Who Gave Away Millions", "title_zh": "捐出千萬的賣菜阿嬤", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Society", "Money"], "passage": "In 2010, the American magazine Time published its yearly list of the 100 most influential people in the world. The list included presidents, business leaders and movie stars. It also included a woman who sold vegetables at a market in Taitung, in eastern Taiwan. Her name was Chen Shu-chu.\n\nChen was born in 1950 into a poor family. When she was thirteen, her mother became seriously ill, but the family could not pay the hospital in time, and her mother died. After finishing elementary school, Chen gave up her studies to help her father at the family's vegetable stall. For decades after that, she worked at the market almost every day, from early in the morning until evening.\n\nChen lived a very simple life. She ate plain meals, wore simple clothes and rarely bought anything for herself. Her vegetables were cheap, so her profit on each sale was only a few dollars. Still, she saved carefully, one coin at a time. Over the years, these small amounts slowly added up.\n\nThen she did something that surprised many people: she gave most of her savings away. She donated money to help poor children and to support a children's home. She also gave NT$4.5 million to her old elementary school to build a new library, so that the children there could enjoy reading. In total, she donated more than NT$10 million, an amazing amount for someone who earned so little each day.\n\nChen never wanted to be famous. She seemed uncomfortable with all the attention, and she did not think she had done anything special. When people asked why she gave so much, her answer was simple: money is only useful when it is given to people who need it. Her story shows that you do not have to be rich to make a difference. Small amounts, saved patiently and shared kindly, can change many lives.", "passage_zh": "2010 年，美國《時代》雜誌公布了年度「全球百大最具影響力人物」名單。名單上有總統、企業領袖和電影明星，也有一位在臺灣東部臺東的市場賣菜的婦人。她的名字是陳樹菊。\n\n陳樹菊 1950 年出生於一個貧窮的家庭。她十三歲時，母親生了重病，但家裡沒辦法及時付錢給醫院，母親因此過世。小學畢業後，陳樹菊放棄升學，到家裡的菜攤幫父親的忙。此後的幾十年，她幾乎每天都在市場工作，從清晨一直忙到傍晚。\n\n陳樹菊過著非常簡樸的生活。她吃得簡單，穿著樸素，很少為自己買任何東西。她的菜賣得很便宜，所以每筆生意只賺得到幾塊錢。即使如此，她仍然一塊錢一塊錢地仔細存起來。多年下來，這些小錢慢慢累積成一大筆錢。\n\n接著，她做了一件讓許多人驚訝的事：她把大部分的積蓄都捐了出去。她捐錢幫助貧困的兒童，也支持一所兒童之家。她還捐了新臺幣 450 萬元給她就讀過的小學興建新的圖書館，讓那裡的孩子能享受閱讀。她總共捐出了超過新臺幣一千萬元，對一位每天收入那麼少的人來說，這是一筆驚人的數目。\n\n陳樹菊從來不想出名。面對這麼多關注，她似乎很不自在，也不認為自己做了什麼特別的事。當別人問她為什麼捐這麼多錢時，她的回答很簡單：錢要給需要的人才有用。她的故事告訴我們，不必是有錢人，也能帶來改變。耐心存下、真心分享的小錢，能改變許多人的生命。", "questions": [{"q": "Why was it surprising that Chen Shu-chu was on Time's list in 2010?", "options": ["She had never left Taiwan before.", "She was an ordinary vegetable seller, not a famous or powerful person.", "She was the youngest person on the list.", "She had worked for the magazine as a reporter."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說名單上多是總統、企業領袖和電影明星，It also included a woman who sold vegetables at a market in Taitung，一位市場賣菜的婦人出現在名單上，與其他名人形成對比。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "What happened when Chen was thirteen?", "options": ["Her mother died after the family could not pay the hospital in time.", "She opened her own vegetable stall in Taipei.", "She won a prize for being the best student in her school.", "Her father gave her money to build a library."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：When she was thirteen, her mother became seriously ill, but the family could not pay the hospital in time, and her mother died. (B) 她是在臺東幫父親顧菜攤，不是在臺北自己開店；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 捐錢蓋圖書館的是她自己，且是多年以後的事。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "How was Chen able to donate so much money?", "options": ["She sold her vegetables at very high prices.", "She received a large amount of money from Time magazine.", "She lived simply and saved small amounts over many years.", "Her old elementary school helped her raise the money."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：她生活簡樸、很少為自己買東西，she saved carefully, one coin at a time. Over the years, these small amounts slowly added up. (A) 與「她的菜賣得很便宜」相反；(B) 文中沒說雜誌給她錢；(D) 是她捐錢給母校，而不是學校幫她籌錢。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "The word \"profit\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the price that customers pay for vegetables", "the number of customers she has each day", "the money she gives to other people", "the money she earns after paying her costs"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Her vegetables were cheap, so her profit on each sale was only a few dollars. 菜價便宜，所以每筆生意「賺到的錢」只有幾塊，profit 指扣除成本後的利潤。(A) 是售價而非利潤；(B) 是顧客人數；(C) 是捐款，與這句的語意不合。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What is the main message of the passage?", "options": ["Selling vegetables is the best way to become rich.", "You do not need to be rich to make a difference in other people's lives.", "Famous magazines only choose people who want attention.", "Children should leave school early to help their families."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：Her story shows that you do not have to be rich to make a difference. 全文以陳樹菊省吃儉用、捐出積蓄的故事，說明不必富有也能幫助別人。(A) 文中沒有這種說法；(C) 與第五段「她從來不想出名」相反；(D) 她失學是因家境所迫，作者並沒有鼓勵學生輟學。故選 (B)"}]}, {"id": "a91", "title": "The Little Ramp That Helps Everyone", "title_zh": "幫助每個人的小斜坡", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Society", "History"], "passage": "The next time you pull a suitcase down the street or push a baby stroller, look down at the corner of the sidewalk. You will probably see a gentle slope that connects the sidewalk to the road. This simple design is called a curb cut. Most people never notice it, but it has a surprising history.\n\nFor a long time, most sidewalks ended in a sharp step, called a curb. For people in wheelchairs, even a curb about fifteen centimeters high was like a wall. Without help, they could not cross the street. After World War II, many soldiers returned home with serious injuries, and a few American cities, such as Kalamazoo, Michigan, built small ramps for them. However, such ramps remained rare for many years.\n\nThings began to change in the early 1970s in Berkeley, California. A group of disabled students and activists there, including Ed Roberts, wanted to live independent lives. They wanted to go to class, go shopping and visit friends on their own. They asked the city again and again for curb cuts. According to a famous story, some of them grew tired of waiting. One night, they went out with hammers and cement and made rough ramps themselves. Soon after, Berkeley began building official curb cuts at street corners.\n\nThen something unexpected happened. The ramps were made for wheelchair users, but almost everyone started to use them. Parents with strollers, travelers with heavy suitcases, delivery workers with carts, cyclists and older people who walked slowly all found that the ramps made life easier. Today, laws in the United States and many other places require ramps like these on new sidewalks.\n\nThis is now called the \"curb-cut effect\": when something is designed to help one group of people, it often ends up helping many others too. You can see the same effect in other places. Subtitles were first created for viewers who cannot hear well, but today many people turn them on when they watch videos on a noisy bus. A small change for a few can become a big change for all.", "passage_zh": "下次你在街上拖著行李箱或推著嬰兒車時，不妨低頭看看人行道的轉角。你很可能會看到一道和緩的斜坡，把人行道和馬路連接起來。這種簡單的設計叫做「緣石坡道」。大多數人從來沒注意過它，但它有一段令人意想不到的歷史。\n\n很長一段時間，大部分的人行道邊緣都是一道陡直的台階，叫做「緣石」。對坐輪椅的人來說，即使只有大約十五公分高的緣石，也像一道牆。沒有別人幫忙，他們就無法過馬路。第二次世界大戰後，許多士兵身負重傷返鄉，於是美國有少數城市，例如密西根州的卡拉馬祖，為他們建造了小斜坡。不過，這樣的斜坡在之後許多年裡仍然很少見。\n\n情況在 1970 年代初期的加州柏克萊開始改變。當地有一群身心障礙學生和社運人士，包括艾德・羅伯茲在內，希望過獨立自主的生活。他們想自己去上課、去購物、去拜訪朋友。他們一次又一次地要求市政府設置緣石坡道。根據一個著名的故事，其中有些人等得不耐煩了，某天晚上，他們帶著鐵鎚和水泥出門，自己做出了粗糙的斜坡。不久之後，柏克萊便開始在街角興建正式的緣石坡道。\n\n接著，意想不到的事情發生了。這些斜坡原本是為輪椅使用者設計的，結果幾乎每個人都開始使用。推嬰兒車的父母、拖著沉重行李箱的旅客、推著推車的送貨員、騎腳踏車的人，以及走路緩慢的長者，都發現斜坡讓生活變得更方便。如今，美國和許多其他地方的法律，都規定新建的人行道必須設置這樣的斜坡。\n\n這種現象現在被稱為「緣石坡道效應」（curb-cut effect）：為了幫助某一群人而做的設計，最後往往也幫助了其他許多人。在其他地方也能看到同樣的效應。字幕最初是為聽力不好的觀眾而設計，但現在許多人在嘈雜的公車上看影片時也會打開字幕。為少數人做的小改變，可以成為造福所有人的大改變。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why sidewalks in the United States are higher than those in other countries.", "How soldiers in World War II built roads in American cities.", "How a design made for wheelchair users ended up helping many other people.", "Why people should not use hammers and cement on city streets."], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹緣石坡道的歷史：原本為輪椅使用者設計，後來推嬰兒車的父母、旅客等人都受惠，並由此說明「緣石坡道效應」（curb-cut effect）。(A) 文中沒有比較各國人行道高度；(B) 士兵是斜坡的受益者，並非建路的人；(D) 只是第三段故事的細節，並非主旨。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did Kalamazoo build small ramps after World War II?", "options": ["To make the city more beautiful for visitors.", "To help delivery workers move their carts.", "To make it easier for children to ride bicycles.", "To help soldiers who came home with serious injuries."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：After World War II, many soldiers returned home with serious injuries, and a few American cities, such as Kalamazoo, Michigan, built small ramps for them. 斜坡是為受傷返鄉的士兵而建。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 送貨員是第四段提到後來受惠的人，並非當初建造的原因。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the activists in Berkeley who made their own ramps?", "options": ["They were tired of the city's slow action and decided to do something themselves.", "They were workers hired by the city to build ramps.", "They wanted to stop people from using the sidewalks.", "They did not really need ramps to cross the street."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：他們一再要求市政府設置斜坡，some of them grew tired of waiting，於是晚上自己帶著鐵鎚和水泥做斜坡，可推知他們對市政府行動緩慢不耐煩，決定自己動手。(B) 他們是學生和社運人士，不是市府僱用的工人；(C) 與他們想方便出門的目的相反；(D) 他們正是需要斜坡的身心障礙者。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The word \"independent\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["busy and tiring", "not needing help from others", "rich and comfortable", "living far away from the city"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：wanted to live independent lives. They wanted to go to class, go shopping and visit friends on their own. 後一句說明他們想「靠自己」去上課、購物、拜訪朋友，所以 independent 是「獨立、不需依靠別人」。(A) 忙碌疲累、(C) 富裕舒適、(D) 住在遠離城市的地方，都不符合上下文。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the best example of the \"curb-cut effect\"?", "options": ["A school builds a new gym that only its basketball team can use.", "A phone company makes an expensive phone just for business people.", "Large, clear signs made for older people also help tourists find their way.", "A city closes a road so that only buses can drive on it."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段定義 curb-cut effect：when something is designed to help one group of people, it often ends up helping many others too。為長者設計的大型清楚標示也幫助了觀光客，正符合「為某群人設計，結果幫助更多人」。(A)(B)(D) 都只服務特定的一群人，並沒有讓其他人也受惠。故選 (C)"}]}, {"id": "a92", "title": "Clearing the Way: Mary Anderson and the Windshield Wiper", "title_zh": "看清前方的路：瑪麗・安德森與雨刷", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "History"], "passage": "On a snowy winter day in 1902, a woman from Alabama named Mary Anderson was visiting New York City. She was riding a streetcar, a kind of electric train that runs on rails along the street. As snow and ice built up on the front window, she noticed that the driver could hardly see. Again and again, he had to open the window or stop the streetcar to clean the glass by hand. Cold air blew in on the passengers, and the trip was slow and uncomfortable.\n\nMost passengers just complained, but Anderson started thinking. Why couldn't the driver clean the window from inside? Back home, she drew a simple design: a rubber blade on the outside of the window, connected to a handle inside. By moving the handle, the driver could make the blade swing back and forth across the glass, pushing away the rain and snow. When the weather was good, the device could be taken off.\n\nIn 1903, Anderson received a patent for her invention. A patent is an official document that says an invention belongs to you, so that others cannot copy it for a number of years. She then tried to sell the rights to a company in Canada, but the company turned her down. It said the device was not useful enough to make money. Some people even worried that the moving blade would distract drivers and cause accidents.\n\nAnderson's patent ran out in 1920. By then, cars had become much more common, and drivers needed to see clearly in all kinds of weather. Companies began to make windshield wipers based on similar ideas, and soon wipers became a normal part of every car. Sadly, because her patent had ended, Anderson never earned any money from her idea.\n\nToday, windshield wipers can be found on almost every car, bus and train. In 2011, more than a hundred years after that snowy streetcar ride, Mary Anderson was added to the National Inventors Hall of Fame in the United States. Her story shows that great ideas often come from paying attention to everyday problems that other people simply accept.", "passage_zh": "1902 年一個下雪的冬日，一位來自阿拉巴馬州、名叫瑪麗・安德森的女士正在紐約市旅遊。她搭乘一輛路面電車，也就是一種沿著街道上的軌道行駛的電動列車。隨著冰雪在前方的車窗上越積越多，她注意到司機幾乎看不見路。司機一次又一次地打開車窗，或是停下電車，用手清理玻璃。冷風灌進來吹在乘客身上，這趟車程既緩慢又難受。\n\n大部分乘客只是抱怨，安德森卻開始思考：為什麼司機不能在車內清理車窗呢？回家後，她畫出了一個簡單的設計：車窗外有一片橡膠刮片，連接到車內的一根把手。司機只要移動把手，就能讓刮片在玻璃上來回擺動，把雨水和雪推開。天氣好的時候，這個裝置還可以拆下來。\n\n1903 年，安德森為她的發明取得了專利。專利是一種官方文件，證明某項發明屬於你，讓別人在一定的年限內不能抄襲。之後，她試著把這項權利賣給加拿大的一家公司，但對方拒絕了她，說這個裝置不夠實用，賺不了錢。有些人甚至擔心，移動的刮片會讓司機分心而造成事故。\n\n安德森的專利在 1920 年到期。那時汽車已經普遍得多，駕駛人在各種天氣下都需要看清楚前方。各家公司開始根據類似的構想製造雨刷，雨刷很快就成了每輛車的基本配備。遺憾的是，由於專利已經到期，安德森從來沒有因為她的點子賺到任何錢。\n\n如今，幾乎每一輛汽車、公車和火車上都看得到雨刷。2011 年，在那趟下雪天的電車之旅一百多年後，瑪麗・安德森被列入美國國家發明家名人堂。她的故事告訴我們，偉大的點子往往來自留意那些別人只是默默接受的日常問題。", "questions": [{"q": "What problem did Mary Anderson notice on the streetcar?", "options": ["The streetcar was too crowded for all the passengers.", "The heater on the streetcar was not working.", "The streetcar driver did not know the way in New York.", "The driver could hardly see because snow and ice covered the window."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：As snow and ice built up on the front window, she noticed that the driver could hardly see. 冰雪堆積在車窗上，司機幾乎看不見路。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 文中說冷風吹進來，是因為司機開窗清玻璃，並非暖氣故障。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how did Anderson's device work?", "options": ["It used hot air to melt the snow on the window.", "The driver moved a handle inside to swing a rubber blade outside.", "A passenger climbed out to clean the window with a brush.", "It worked by itself whenever it started to rain."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：a rubber blade on the outside of the window, connected to a handle inside. By moving the handle, the driver could make the blade swing back and forth，司機在車內移動把手，讓車窗外的橡膠刮片來回擺動。(A) 文中未提到熱風；(C) 文中清理車窗的是司機，而且是用手，並沒有乘客爬出去用刷子清理，這也不是她的裝置；(D) 她的裝置需要司機手動操作，不是自動的。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "The phrase \"turned her down\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["refused her offer", "paid her a lot of money", "helped her improve the design", "asked her to work for them"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：the company turned her down. It said the device was not useful enough to make money. 公司認為這個裝置賺不了錢，可知 turned her down 是「拒絕她」，與 refused her offer 意思最接近。(B)(C)(D) 都是接受或合作的意思，與上下文相反。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "Why did Anderson never earn money from windshield wipers?", "options": ["She gave her idea to a car company for free.", "Her design did not work in heavy snow.", "By the time wipers became popular, her patent had already ended.", "The Canadian company copied her design and sold it secretly."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：她的專利在 1920 年到期，那時汽車才普遍、雨刷才被大量生產，because her patent had ended, Anderson never earned any money from her idea. (A) 她曾想賣掉權利，並非免費送出；(B) 文中沒說設計在大雪中無效；(D) 加拿大公司是拒絕購買，文中沒說對方抄襲。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "What lesson does the writer most likely want readers to learn?", "options": ["Paying attention to everyday problems can lead to useful inventions.", "It is dangerous to ride a streetcar in winter.", "Inventors should always sell their ideas to big companies.", "A good invention will always make its inventor rich."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段：great ideas often come from paying attention to everyday problems that other people simply accept. 留意日常生活中的問題能帶來實用的發明。(B) 只是故事背景；(C) 文中沒有這樣的建議；(D) 與安德森沒有因雨刷賺到錢的事實相反。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a93", "title": "Would You Take the Stairs If They Played Music?", "title_zh": "如果樓梯會彈奏音樂，你會走樓梯嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Society"], "passage": "Everyone knows that walking up stairs is good exercise. Yet when people can choose between the stairs and an escalator, most of them stand on the escalator. Simply telling people to \"be healthier\" usually does not work. So in 2009, a team in Stockholm, Sweden, tried a different idea: what if taking the stairs was just more fun?\n\nThe experiment took place at Odenplan, a busy subway station where a staircase ran right next to an escalator. One night, workers covered each step with black and white panels, so the stairs looked like the keys of a giant piano. Under the steps, they placed sensors, small devices that can notice pressure or movement, and connected them to speakers. When someone stepped on a stair, it played a musical note.\n\nThe next morning, commuters on their way to work were surprised. Some stopped and smiled. Others jumped up and down the steps to play simple tunes, and parents walked up and down with their children again and again. According to the team, 66 percent more people than usual chose the stairs instead of the escalator.\n\nThe piano stairs were part of a project called \"The Fun Theory,\" created for the car company Volkswagen as a kind of advertisement. The idea behind it was that fun is one of the easiest ways to change people's behavior. The project also included a \"bottle bank arcade,\" where people got points like in a video game for recycling glass bottles, and \"the world's deepest trash can,\" which made a long falling sound whenever someone threw rubbish into it.\n\nOf course, the experiment had its limits. It was not a careful scientific study, and the excitement might disappear once people got used to the music. Still, the idea has spread, and piano stairs have since appeared in cities around the world. The lesson is useful for anyone who wants to build a better habit: make it more enjoyable. Study with a friend, play music while you clean your room, or turn your exercise into a game. Small changes like these can make good choices feel easy.", "passage_zh": "大家都知道爬樓梯是很好的運動。然而，當人們可以在樓梯和手扶梯之間選擇時，大多數人還是會站上手扶梯。光是叫大家「要更健康」通常沒什麼效果。所以在 2009 年，瑞典斯德哥爾摩的一個團隊嘗試了一個不同的點子：如果走樓梯變得更好玩呢？\n\n這項實驗在歐登廣場站（Odenplan）進行，那是一個繁忙的捷運站，站內有一座樓梯就緊鄰著手扶梯。某天晚上，工作人員在每一階樓梯貼上黑白相間的面板，讓整座樓梯看起來就像一架巨型鋼琴的琴鍵。他們在階梯底下裝了感應器（一種能偵測壓力或動作的小型裝置），並把它們連接到喇叭。只要有人踩上一階樓梯，它就會發出一個音符。\n\n隔天早上，趕著上班的通勤族都很驚訝。有些人停下腳步露出微笑；有些人在階梯上跳上跳下，彈奏簡單的曲子；還有父母帶著孩子一次又一次地上下走著。根據團隊的說法，選擇走樓梯而不搭手扶梯的人比平常多了百分之六十六。\n\n鋼琴樓梯是一個名為「樂趣理論」計畫的一部分，這項計畫是為福斯汽車公司打造的一種廣告。它背後的想法是：樂趣是改變人們行為最簡單的方法之一。這項計畫還包括一台「回收瓶電玩機」，人們回收玻璃瓶時能像玩電玩一樣得分；以及「世界上最深的垃圾桶」，每當有人把垃圾丟進去，它就會發出一段長長的墜落聲。\n\n當然，這項實驗也有它的限制。它並不是嚴謹的科學研究，而且等人們聽慣了音樂，新鮮感可能就會消失。儘管如此，這個點子已經傳播開來，此後世界各地的城市都出現了鋼琴樓梯。對任何想培養好習慣的人來說，這個啟示都很有用：讓它變得更有趣。和朋友一起讀書、打掃房間時放點音樂，或是把運動變成一場遊戲。像這樣的小改變，能讓好的選擇變得輕而易舉。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Making a healthy choice more fun can change what people do.", "Escalators in Stockholm are often too crowded to use.", "Volkswagen built a new subway station in Sweden.", "Piano lessons are a good way for people to get exercise."], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以斯德哥爾摩的鋼琴樓梯為例，說明 fun is one of the easiest ways to change people's behavior，讓健康的選擇變有趣就能改變人的行為。(B) 文中未提到手扶梯太擠；(C) 福斯只是這個廣告計畫的委託者，並未建造車站；(D) 文中說的是走樓梯，不是學鋼琴。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happened when someone stepped on a stair?", "options": ["The escalator next to it stopped moving.", "A light on the stair turned on.", "The stair played a musical note.", "A video started on a screen in the station."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段最後一句：When someone stepped on a stair, it played a musical note. 踩上樓梯就會發出一個音符。(A)(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "According to the team, how did the piano stairs change people's behavior?", "options": ["66 percent of the passengers stopped using the subway.", "People spent 66 minutes playing music on the stairs.", "The escalator was used 66 percent more than before.", "66 percent more people than usual chose the stairs over the escalator."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：According to the team, 66 percent more people than usual chose the stairs instead of the escalator. 選擇走樓梯的人比平常多了百分之六十六。(A)(B) 與數字的意義不符；(C) 正好相反，是走樓梯的人變多，不是手扶梯。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by \"the experiment had its limits\" in paragraph 5?", "options": ["The stairs could only be used by a few people at a time.", "The results were not scientifically proven and might not last.", "The music was too loud for people at the station.", "The project cost Volkswagen too much money."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段接著說明限制：It was not a careful scientific study, and the excitement might disappear once people got used to the music. 這不是嚴謹的科學研究，效果也可能在人們習慣後消失。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Based on the passage, which advice would the writer most likely give a student who finds studying boring?", "options": ["Stop studying until it feels interesting again.", "Study only on the escalator on the way to school.", "Ask teachers to give less homework.", "Find a way to make studying more enjoyable, such as studying with a friend."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：The lesson is useful for anyone who wants to build a better habit: make it more enjoyable. Study with a friend... 作者主張讓好習慣變得有趣，例如和朋友一起讀書。(A) 停止讀書不是建立好習慣；(B) 文中沒有這種建議；(C) 與文中「讓事情變有趣」的做法無關。故選 (D)"}]}, {"id": "a94", "title": "More Than a Lucky Accident: The Long Road to Penicillin", "title_zh": "不只是幸運的意外：盤尼西林的漫長之路", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "History"], "passage": "In September 1928, the Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming returned to his London laboratory after a summer holiday and noticed something strange. On a glass dish he had left behind, a blue-green mold had begun to grow, and around it the bacteria he had been studying had died. This moment is often called one of the luckiest accidents in scientific history. Yet the full story of penicillin is far longer and more complicated, and it involves many more people than Fleming alone.\n\nFleming deserves credit for recognizing the importance of what he saw. Many researchers would have thrown the spoiled dish away, but he grew the mold, found that it released a substance capable of killing several kinds of harmful bacteria, and named it penicillin. However, he struggled to separate the substance from the liquid in which the mold grew, and the small amounts he produced were unstable and quickly lost their power. After publishing his results in 1929, he turned to other projects, and for nearly a decade his discovery attracted little attention.\n\nThe breakthrough came at the University of Oxford, where a team led by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain began studying penicillin in 1939. Their colleague Norman Heatley designed clever equipment to extract and purify the drug, at times using milk containers and hospital bedpans. In 1940, mice infected with deadly bacteria survived after being treated, while untreated mice died. Early the next year, the team first used it to treat a person, a police officer with a severe infection. He began to improve within a day, but the supply ran out before he was cured, and he died. The case showed that the drug worked; the real problem was producing enough of it.\n\nWith Britain at war, its factories could not make the drug on a large scale, so Florey traveled to the United States for help. Scientists in Peoria, Illinois, developed ways to grow the mold in huge tanks, and a moldy melon from a local market turned out to contain a strain that produced far more penicillin than Fleming's original mold. By the D-Day landings in June 1944, there was enough penicillin to treat wounded Allied soldiers. In 1945, Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.\n\nThe history of penicillin suggests that great discoveries rarely depend on a single moment of genius. The French scientist Louis Pasteur once said that chance favors only the prepared mind, but turning a lucky observation into a life-saving medicine also requires patience, teamwork and money. Fleming himself warned that bacteria could become resistant if the drug was misused. Today, antibiotic resistance is a serious threat to global health, which is why doctors urge patients to use antibiotics only when necessary.", "passage_zh": "1928 年 9 月，蘇格蘭科學家亞歷山大‧弗萊明放完暑假回到倫敦的實驗室，注意到一件奇怪的事。在他離開前留下的一個玻璃培養皿上，長出了一團藍綠色的黴菌，而黴菌周圍他原本在研究的細菌都死了。這一刻常被稱為科學史上最幸運的意外之一。然而，盤尼西林完整的故事要漫長、複雜得多，參與其中的人也遠遠不只弗萊明一個。\n\n弗萊明確實值得肯定，因為他看出了眼前現象的重要性。許多研究人員可能會直接把這個被汙染的培養皿丟掉，但他把黴菌培養起來，發現它會釋放出一種能殺死好幾種有害細菌的物質，並將它命名為盤尼西林。然而，他很難把這種物質從培養黴菌的液體中分離出來，而且他製造出的少量盤尼西林並不穩定，很快就會失去效力。1929 年發表研究結果之後，他便轉向其他研究計畫，接下來將近十年，他的發現幾乎沒有受到關注。\n\n突破出現在牛津大學。1939 年，由霍華德‧弗洛里與恩斯特‧錢恩帶領的團隊開始研究盤尼西林。他們的同事諾曼‧希特利設計了巧妙的設備來萃取並純化這種藥物，有時甚至用上了牛奶容器和醫院的便盆。1940 年，感染致命細菌的老鼠在接受治療後存活下來，而沒有接受治療的老鼠都死了。隔年年初，團隊第一次用它來治療病人，對象是一名嚴重感染的警察。他在一天之內就開始好轉，但藥物在他痊癒之前就用完了，最後他還是過世了。這個病例證明這種藥有效；真正的問題在於如何製造出足夠的數量。\n\n當時英國正處於戰爭之中，國內的工廠無法大量生產這種藥，於是弗洛里前往美國尋求協助。伊利諾州皮奧里亞的科學家發展出在巨大槽桶中培養黴菌的方法，而且從當地市場買來的一顆發霉的甜瓜，竟然含有一種菌株，產出的盤尼西林比弗萊明原本的黴菌多得多。到了 1944 年 6 月諾曼第登陸時，已經有足夠的盤尼西林可以治療受傷的盟軍士兵。1945 年，弗萊明、弗洛里和錢恩共同獲得諾貝爾生理學或醫學獎。\n\n盤尼西林的歷史顯示，偉大的發現很少只靠某個天才的靈光一閃。法國科學家路易‧巴斯德曾說，機會只眷顧有準備的人；但要把一次幸運的觀察變成能救命的藥物，還需要耐心、團隊合作和資金。弗萊明本人就曾警告，如果這種藥被濫用，細菌可能會產生抗藥性。如今，抗生素抗藥性已成為全球健康的嚴重威脅，這也是為什麼醫生呼籲病人只在必要時才使用抗生素。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Fleming's discovery alone turned penicillin into a famous medicine.", "Penicillin became a useful drug only through the long efforts of many people.", "Antibiotics should no longer be used because bacteria have become resistant.", "The Second World War stopped scientists from doing medical research."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段末句指出盤尼西林的故事 far longer and more complicated，且 involves many more people than Fleming alone；第二到四段依序說明弗萊明、牛津團隊與美國科學家的貢獻，第五段也總結偉大發現需要 patience, teamwork and money，故主旨是 (B)。(A) 與第一段末句相反；(C) 第五段只說要在必要時才用抗生素，並非不再使用；(D) 戰爭反而促使英美合作量產，文中未說研究被停止。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what problem did Fleming have with penicillin?", "options": ["He could not make it pure and stable enough to be useful.", "He found that it killed only one kind of bacteria.", "His laboratory refused to let him publish his results.", "The mold died soon after he started to grow it."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：he struggled to separate the substance from the liquid... and the small amounts he produced were unstable and quickly lost their power，意即他無法把盤尼西林分離純化，產物又不穩定，故選 (A)。(B) 與同段 killing several kinds of harmful bacteria 相反；(C) 他在 1929 年已發表結果；(D) 文中說他成功培養了黴菌，並未說黴菌死掉。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention the police officer in paragraph 3?", "options": ["To prove that penicillin was too dangerous to give to humans", "To explain why the Oxford team stopped studying penicillin", "To show that doctors in Oxford made a serious medical mistake", "To show that penicillin worked but could not yet be made in large amounts"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說這位警察 began to improve within a day，但 the supply ran out before he was cured，接著直接點出 The case showed that the drug worked; the real problem was producing enough of it，作者舉此例是要說明藥物有效但產量不足，故選 (D)。(A) 他用藥後是好轉的，並非藥物危險；(B) 團隊沒有停止研究，第四段弗洛里還到美國尋求量產；(C) 他死亡是因為藥用完，文中未指醫療疏失。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"strain\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["pressure", "injury", "type", "container"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段 a moldy melon... turned out to contain a strain that produced far more penicillin than Fleming's original mold，此處 strain 指黴菌的「菌株、品系」，也就是某一種類，與 type 最接近，故選 (C)。strain 雖也有「壓力」(A)、「拉傷」(B) 的意思，但不符合本句談黴菌的語境；(D) container 是第三段提到的牛奶容器，與 strain 無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is TRUE according to the passage?", "options": ["The Oxford team tested penicillin on mice before giving it to a human patient.", "Fleming planned his 1928 experiment carefully in order to find penicillin.", "Penicillin was first produced in huge amounts in British factories.", "Fleming received the 1945 Nobel Prize by himself."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段先說 In 1940, mice infected with deadly bacteria survived after being treated，接著 Early the next year 才第一次用來治療病人，故 (A) 正確。(B) 第一段說這是一場 accident，並非事先計畫；(C) 第四段說英國工廠無法大量生產，是在美國發展出大槽培養法；(D) 第四段說諾貝爾獎由弗萊明、弗洛里和錢恩三人共享。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a95", "title": "The Planet Found with a Pen: How Mathematics Discovered Neptune", "title_zh": "用筆找到的行星：數學如何發現海王星", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "History"], "passage": "Most planets in our solar system were found in the obvious way: someone looked up and noticed a point of light moving slowly against the stars. Neptune is different. Before anyone knowingly saw it, its position had already been worked out on paper, which is why a French scientist of the time said it had been discovered \"with the point of a pen.\"\n\nThe story begins with Uranus, which William Herschel discovered in 1781. Using Isaac Newton's laws of gravity, astronomers calculated the path Uranus should follow around the Sun. Over the following decades, however, the planet refused to stay on schedule. Sometimes it was slightly ahead of its predicted position, and sometimes it fell behind. There seemed to be two likely explanations: either Newton's laws did not work at such great distances, or an unseen object was pulling Uranus off course.\n\nIn 1845, the French mathematician Urbain Le Verrier decided to test the second idea. Working backward from the tiny irregularities in Uranus's motion, he calculated where an unknown planet would have to be in order to cause them. He then sent his prediction to Johann Galle at the Berlin Observatory. Galle received the letter on September 23, 1846, and searched that very night. In less than an hour, he and his assistant found a faint object about one degree from the spot Le Verrier had named. It was Neptune.\n\nThe discovery soon led to an argument. In England, a young Cambridge mathematician, John Couch Adams, had made similar calculations, but British astronomers had been slow to act on them. For a time, Britain and France argued over who deserved the honor. Strangely, Neptune had been seen long before: Galileo recorded it in his notes in 1612 and 1613, but he mistook it for a star.\n\nThe same method, however, does not always succeed. Le Verrier later noticed that Mercury's orbit did not quite match Newton's predictions either, so he proposed another hidden planet, called Vulcan, circling even closer to the Sun. Several observers claimed to have spotted it, but Vulcan does not exist. The real answer came in 1915, when Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity showed that Newton's laws need a small correction near a massive object like the Sun. This time, it was the theory, not the sky, that was missing something.\n\nAstronomers still reason this way today. Some believe that the strange orbits of distant icy objects beyond Neptune may be caused by an undiscovered \"Planet Nine,\" although no one has found it yet. The story of Neptune reminds us that mathematics can point scientists in the right direction, but only careful observation can show whether a prediction is correct.", "passage_zh": "太陽系中大多數的行星都是用最直接的方式發現的：有人抬頭望向天空，注意到一個光點在群星之間緩緩移動。海王星卻不一樣。在任何人有意識地看見它之前，它的位置就已經在紙上被算出來了，這也是為什麼當時一位法國科學家說，它是「用筆尖」發現的。\n\n故事要從天王星說起，它是威廉‧赫歇爾在 1781 年發現的。天文學家運用牛頓的萬有引力定律，算出天王星繞行太陽應該走的路徑。然而在接下來的幾十年裡，這顆行星卻始終不肯照著時間表走。有時它比預測的位置稍微超前，有時又落後。看來最可能的解釋有兩種：要不是牛頓的定律在這麼遙遠的距離下不適用，就是有某個看不見的天體正把天王星拉離軌道。\n\n1845 年，法國數學家奧本‧勒維耶決定驗證第二種想法。他從天王星運行中那些細微的不規則變化往回推算，算出一顆未知的行星必須位在哪裡，才會造成這些變化。接著，他把預測結果寄給柏林天文台的約翰‧伽勒。伽勒在 1846 年 9 月 23 日收到這封信，當天晚上就展開搜尋。不到一個小時，他和助手就在距離勒維耶所指位置約一度的地方，找到一個黯淡的天體。那就是海王星。\n\n這項發現很快就引發了爭議。在英國，一位年輕的劍橋數學家約翰‧庫奇‧亞當斯也做過類似的計算，但英國的天文學家遲遲沒有據此採取行動。有一段時間，英國和法國為了這份榮耀該歸誰而爭論不休。奇妙的是，其實早就有人看過海王星：伽利略在 1612 年和 1613 年的筆記中記錄過它，卻誤以為那是一顆恆星。\n\n然而，同樣的方法並不一定都會成功。勒維耶後來注意到，水星的軌道也和牛頓定律的預測不太吻合，於是他提出另一顆隱藏的行星，稱為「火神星」，在比水星更靠近太陽的地方繞行。好幾位觀測者都聲稱看到了它，但火神星根本不存在。真正的答案在 1915 年才出現：愛因斯坦的廣義相對論指出，在太陽這種質量巨大的天體附近，牛頓的定律需要做些微的修正。這一次，缺了點什麼的是理論，而不是天空。\n\n天文學家至今仍用這種方式推理。有些人認為，海王星外圍一些遙遠冰質天體的奇特軌道，可能是由一顆尚未被發現的「第九行星」所造成，不過至今還沒有人找到它。海王星的故事提醒我們，數學能為科學家指出正確的方向，但只有仔細的觀測，才能證明預測是否正確。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How Galileo discovered Neptune by accident in the 1600s", "Why Newton's laws of gravity turned out to be completely wrong", "How Neptune was predicted by calculation and what this method can teach us", "How astronomers today are searching for a ninth planet"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文從第一段「海王星的位置先在紙上被算出來」開始，說明勒維耶如何從天王星的軌道偏差推算出海王星，再以火神星與第九行星為例討論這種方法的限制與現代應用，故選 (C)。(A) 第四段說伽利略誤以為它是恆星，並沒有發現它；(B) 第五段只說牛頓定律在太陽附近需要 a small correction，並非完全錯誤；(D) 第九行星只是最後一段的例子。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what problem did astronomers notice about Uranus?", "options": ["It was too faint to be seen even with the best telescopes.", "It moved much faster than any other planet in the solar system.", "It appeared to be pulled toward the Sun by Newton's laws.", "Its actual position did not match the path they had calculated."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：the planet refused to stay on schedule. Sometimes it was slightly ahead of its predicted position, and sometimes it fell behind，表示天王星的實際位置和計算出的路徑不符，故選 (D)。(A) 赫歇爾在 1781 年就發現了它，並非看不到；(B) 文中只說它有時超前、有時落後，沒有和其他行星比較速度；(C) 文中說的是有未知天體可能把它 pulling... off course，並非被牛頓定律拉向太陽。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Galileo's observation in paragraph 4?", "options": ["He saw Neptune but did not realize that it was a planet.", "He was the first person to calculate Neptune's position.", "He helped Le Verrier find Neptune in 1846.", "He believed that Neptune was pulling Uranus off course."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Galileo recorded it in his notes in 1612 and 1613, but he mistook it for a star，他看到了海王星，卻誤以為是恆星，可推知他並不知道那是一顆行星，故選 (A)。(B) 計算位置的是勒維耶與亞當斯；(C) 伽利略是 1600 年代的人，不可能參與 1846 年的搜尋；(D) 天王星在 1781 年才被發現，伽利略當時不可能這樣想。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"irregularities\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["careful measurements", "unexpected changes", "serious mistakes", "regular patterns"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段 the tiny irregularities in Uranus's motion 指的就是第二段所說天王星「有時超前、有時落後」、偏離預測路徑的情形，也就是不規則、出乎預期的變化，故選 (B)。(A) 測量是天文學家做的事，不是天王星運動的特徵；(C) 這些偏差不是人為錯誤，而是另一顆行星的引力造成的；(D) regular patterns 與 irregular 意思相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, how was the case of Vulcan different from the case of Neptune?", "options": ["Vulcan was predicted by Adams, while Neptune was predicted by Le Verrier.", "Vulcan was found in Berlin, while Neptune was found in Cambridge.", "Vulcan was seen by Galileo, while Neptune was seen by no one before 1846.", "Vulcan's problem was solved by a better theory, not by a hidden planet."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段說火神星根本不存在，水星軌道的問題是靠 1915 年愛因斯坦的廣義相對論修正牛頓定律才解決，並以 it was the theory, not the sky, that was missing something 作結；而海王星的情況則是真的有一顆隱藏的行星，故選 (D)。(A) 火神星也是勒維耶提出的；(B) 火神星從未被找到，海王星是在柏林發現的；(C) 第四段說伽利略看到的是海王星。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a96", "title": "Glasses for a Telescope: How Hubble Was Rescued", "title_zh": "幫望遠鏡配眼鏡：哈伯如何起死回生", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Technology"], "passage": "When the Hubble Space Telescope was carried into orbit by the space shuttle Discovery in April 1990, astronomers expected the clearest pictures of the universe ever taken. Above the blurring effect of Earth's atmosphere, Hubble was supposed to see distant stars and galaxies in sharp detail. Within weeks, however, it became clear that something was terribly wrong. The first images were fuzzy, and the telescope, which had cost about 1.5 billion US dollars, quickly became a joke on late-night television.\n\nThe problem lay in Hubble's main mirror, which is 2.4 meters wide. It had been ground to the wrong shape: its outer edge was too flat by roughly one-fiftieth of the thickness of a sheet of paper. That tiny error meant that light hitting the edge of the mirror and light hitting the center were focused in slightly different places, so every image was blurred. An investigation later found that an instrument used to test the mirror had been put together incorrectly. Other tests had shown signs of the error, but they were dismissed because engineers trusted the main instrument more.\n\nBringing the telescope back to Earth would have been too risky and expensive, and replacing a mirror in space was impossible. Yet engineers noticed something hopeful. The mirror had been made extremely precisely; it was simply made precisely to the wrong shape. Because the mistake was the same all the way around the mirror, it could be measured exactly, and an exact correction could be designed, in the same way that glasses correct a person's eyesight.\n\nIn December 1993, seven astronauts aboard the space shuttle Endeavour flew up to Hubble. During five long spacewalks, they installed a device called COSTAR, which used small mirrors to correct the light before it reached several instruments, and they replaced Hubble's main camera with a new one that had the correction built into it. When the first new images arrived, the stars were sharp points of light instead of blurry spots.\n\nWhat followed has made Hubble one of the most successful scientific instruments in history. In 1995, it stared at a tiny, seemingly empty patch of sky for ten days and revealed thousands of galaxies. Its observations have also helped scientists estimate the age of the universe at about 13.8 billion years. Later instruments were all designed with the correction included, so COSTAR was removed in 2009 and now sits in a museum.\n\nHubble's early failure is still taught to engineers today. It shows how much depends on careful, independent testing, and how dangerous it is to ignore results that do not fit our expectations. It also proves that a serious mistake, if it is studied honestly, can be turned into a remarkable success.", "passage_zh": "1990 年 4 月，哈伯太空望遠鏡由發現號太空梭送上軌道時，天文學家都期待它能拍出史上最清晰的宇宙照片。哈伯位在地球大氣層之上，不受大氣造成的模糊影響，照理說應該能清楚看見遙遠的恆星和星系。然而不到幾週，大家就發現事情嚴重不對勁。最早傳回的影像模糊不清，這座花費約 15 億美元的望遠鏡，很快就成了深夜電視節目上的笑柄。\n\n問題出在哈伯寬 2.4 公尺的主鏡。這面鏡子被磨成了錯誤的形狀：它的外緣太平了，誤差大約只有一張紙厚度的五十分之一。這個微小的誤差，使得照在鏡子邊緣的光和照在中央的光，聚焦在稍微不同的位置，所以每一張影像都是模糊的。後來的調查發現，一台用來檢測這面鏡子的儀器在組裝時出了錯。其實其他檢測曾顯示出誤差的跡象，但因為工程師比較信任那台主要的儀器，這些結果都被忽略了。\n\n把望遠鏡帶回地球太冒險也太昂貴，而在太空中更換鏡子更是不可能的事。不過，工程師注意到一件令人燃起希望的事。這面鏡子其實做得極為精確，只是精確地被做成了錯誤的形狀。由於整面鏡子的誤差在每個方向都一樣，因此可以被精準地測量出來，並據此設計出精準的修正方式，就像眼鏡能矯正一個人的視力一樣。\n\n1993 年 12 月，七名太空人搭乘奮進號太空梭飛到哈伯身邊。他們在五次漫長的太空漫步中，安裝了一個名為 COSTAR 的裝置，利用幾面小鏡子在光線進入數台儀器之前先加以修正；他們也把哈伯的主相機換成一台內建修正功能的新相機。當第一批新影像傳回來時，恆星成了清晰的光點，而不再是模糊的斑點。\n\n接下來的發展，讓哈伯成為史上最成功的科學儀器之一。1995 年，它花了十天凝視天空中一小塊看似空無一物的區域，結果發現了數千個星系。它的觀測也幫助科學家估算出宇宙的年齡大約是 138 億年。後來的儀器在設計時都已經納入了修正功能，所以 COSTAR 在 2009 年被拆下，如今陳列在博物館中。\n\n直到今天，哈伯早期的失敗仍是工程師要學習的教材。它說明了仔細而獨立的檢測有多麼重要，也說明忽視不符合預期的結果有多麼危險。它同時證明，一個嚴重的錯誤只要能被誠實地檢討，也可以轉變成非凡的成功。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a serious flaw in the Hubble Space Telescope was found and corrected", "Why space telescopes take clearer pictures than telescopes on the ground", "How astronomers used Hubble to calculate the age of the universe", "Why the space shuttle program was stopped after 1993"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文依序說明哈伯升空後影像模糊（第一段）、原因是主鏡形狀錯誤（第二段）、工程師想出修正方法（第三段）、1993 年太空人前往修復（第四段），以及之後的成就與教訓（第五、六段），故選 (A)。(B) 只是第一段的背景；(C) 宇宙年齡只是第五段的一個例子；(D) 文中完全沒有提到太空梭計畫停止。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what caused Hubble's blurry images?", "options": ["The telescope was damaged by the atmosphere during launch.", "The astronauts installed the main camera in the wrong place.", "The mirror was given the wrong shape because a testing instrument was assembled incorrectly.", "The mirror was too small to collect light from distant galaxies."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段說主鏡 had been ground to the wrong shape，外緣太平，使光線聚焦在不同位置；調查發現 an instrument used to test the mirror had been put together incorrectly，故選 (C)。(A) 文中未提到發射時受損，而且哈伯本來就在大氣層之上；(B) 太空人是後來才去修復，並非造成問題的人；(D) 問題在於形狀而非大小。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why were engineers able to design a correction for the mirror?", "options": ["The mirror could easily be brought back to Earth and reshaped.", "The error was regular and could be measured exactly.", "The other tests had already corrected most of the error.", "The mirror's center was too flat, while its edge was perfect."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Because the mistake was the same all the way around the mirror, it could be measured exactly, and an exact correction could be designed，因為誤差是規律一致的，可以精確測量，才能設計出精確的修正，故選 (B)。(A) 同段說帶回地球 too risky and expensive；(C) 第二段說其他檢測的結果被忽略，並沒有修正誤差；(D) 第二段說太平的是外緣 (outer edge)，不是中央。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"dismissed\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["repeated several times", "sent to other scientists", "carefully checked", "treated as unimportant"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Other tests had shown signs of the error, but they were dismissed because engineers trusted the main instrument more. 工程師比較相信主要儀器，所以其他顯示誤差的檢測結果被「不予理會、當作不重要」，故選 (D)。第六段也提到 how dangerous it is to ignore results，可互相印證。(C) 若有仔細檢查就會發現錯誤，與文意相反；(A)(B) 都不符合上下文。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which statement would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["Expensive projects like Hubble are usually a waste of money.", "A mistake can lead to valuable lessons if people examine it honestly.", "Engineers should always trust one main instrument over other tests.", "Hubble's later success was mainly a matter of good luck."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第六段最後一句：a serious mistake, if it is studied honestly, can be turned into a remarkable success，作者認為錯誤若能被誠實檢討，就能帶來寶貴的教訓甚至成功，故選 (B)。(A) 第五段稱哈伯為史上最成功的科學儀器之一，與此相反；(C) 第二、六段正是在批評只相信主要儀器而忽略其他結果；(D) 文中強調的是精確測量與太空人的修復工作，並非運氣。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a97", "title": "A Push in the Right Direction: The Day NASA Hit an Asteroid", "title_zh": "推一把：NASA 撞擊小行星的那一天", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Technology"], "passage": "About 66 million years ago, an asteroid roughly ten kilometers wide struck what is now Mexico and helped wipe out the dinosaurs. Far smaller objects can still cause damage. In 2013, a rock only about 20 meters across exploded above the Russian city of Chelyabinsk, and around 1,500 people were injured, mostly by flying glass from broken windows. These events raise an obvious question: if a dangerous asteroid were heading toward Earth, could we do anything to stop it?\n\nOn September 26, 2022, NASA tried to find out. Its Double Asteroid Redirection Test, or DART, deliberately crashed a spacecraft weighing about 570 kilograms into an asteroid called Dimorphos at more than 20,000 kilometers per hour. Dimorphos, which is about 160 meters wide, travels around a larger asteroid named Didymos. Neither of them poses any threat to our planet. The goal was simply to test whether a fast-moving spacecraft could push an asteroid slightly off its path.\n\nThe choice of target was a clever one. Measuring a small change in an asteroid's journey around the Sun would be extremely difficult. Dimorphos, however, circled Didymos once every 11 hours and 55 minutes, and each time one asteroid passed in front of the other, telescopes on Earth could detect a tiny dip in their combined brightness. By timing these dips before and after the crash, scientists could tell precisely how much the orbit had changed.\n\nThe results were better than expected. NASA had hoped to shorten the orbit by at least 73 seconds, but the impact reduced it by about 32 minutes. The main reason was the enormous cloud of rock and dust that the crash blasted into space. Like gas shooting out of a rocket, this material pushed Dimorphos even harder. The asteroid seems to be a loose pile of rubble rather than one solid rock, which made the effect stronger.\n\nStill, DART does not mean that Earth is safe. A small push only becomes a large miss if it has many years to add up, so the method works only if a dangerous asteroid is spotted long before it arrives. Astronomers have already found most near-Earth asteroids larger than one kilometer, but fewer than half of those 140 meters or more across, which could destroy a city, have been located. For this reason, NASA is building a new space telescope designed to hunt for them.\n\nMeanwhile, the European Space Agency launched a mission called Hera in 2024 to study Dimorphos up close. For the first time in history, humans have changed the motion of a natural object in space. Unlike the dinosaurs, we may one day be able to defend ourselves, as long as we keep watching the sky.", "passage_zh": "大約 6,600 萬年前，一顆寬約十公里的小行星撞擊了今天的墨西哥一帶，並促成了恐龍的滅絕。就算是小得多的天體，也仍然可能造成災害。2013 年，一顆直徑只有大約 20 公尺的岩石在俄羅斯車里雅賓斯克市上空爆炸，造成約 1,500 人受傷，大多是被破碎窗戶的玻璃碎片所傷。這些事件引出一個顯而易見的問題：如果有一顆危險的小行星正朝地球飛來，我們能做些什麼來阻止它嗎？\n\n2022 年 9 月 26 日，NASA 試著找出答案。它的「雙小行星重定向測試」，簡稱 DART，刻意讓一艘重約 570 公斤的太空船，以每小時超過兩萬公里的速度撞上一顆名為「迪莫弗斯」的小行星。迪莫弗斯寬約 160 公尺，繞著一顆較大的小行星「迪蒂莫斯」運行。這兩顆小行星都不會對地球造成任何威脅。這次任務的目的只是要測試：高速飛行的太空船能不能把小行星稍微推離原本的路徑。\n\n選擇這個目標是很聰明的做法。要測量一顆小行星繞太陽運行時的微小變化，是非常困難的事。然而，迪莫弗斯每 11 小時 55 分鐘繞迪蒂莫斯一圈，每當其中一顆小行星從另一顆前方經過時，地球上的望遠鏡就能偵測到兩者總亮度出現微小的下降。只要比較撞擊前後這些亮度下降的時間，科學家就能精確得知軌道改變了多少。\n\n結果比預期的更好。NASA 原本希望讓軌道週期至少縮短 73 秒，但這次撞擊卻讓它縮短了約 32 分鐘。主要原因是撞擊把大量的岩石和塵埃噴射到太空中。就像從火箭噴出的氣體一樣，這些物質把迪莫弗斯推得更用力。這顆小行星似乎是一堆鬆散的碎石，而不是一整塊堅固的岩石，因此讓這種效果更加明顯。\n\n儘管如此，DART 並不代表地球從此就安全了。小小的一推必須經過許多年的累積，才會變成大幅度的偏離，所以這個方法只有在危險的小行星抵達前很久就被發現時才有效。天文學家已經找到大部分直徑超過一公里的近地小行星，但直徑 140 公尺以上、足以摧毀一座城市的小行星，被找到的還不到一半。因此，NASA 正在建造一座專門用來搜尋它們的新太空望遠鏡。\n\n同時，歐洲太空總署也在 2024 年發射了名為「赫拉」的任務，要近距離研究迪莫弗斯。這是人類史上第一次改變太空中天然物體的運動。和恐龍不同，我們也許有一天真的能保護自己，只要我們持續觀察天空。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer mention the dinosaurs and the city of Chelyabinsk in paragraph 1?", "options": ["To prove that asteroids hit Earth every few years", "To explain how scientists first discovered Dimorphos", "To compare the sizes of asteroids in Mexico and Russia", "To show why the danger of asteroids is worth taking seriously"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段先舉恐龍滅絕和車里雅賓斯克有約 1,500 人受傷兩個例子，再提出問題：if a dangerous asteroid were heading toward Earth, could we do anything to stop it? 目的是說明小行星的威脅值得認真看待，引出 DART 任務，故選 (D)。(A) 文中沒有說每隔幾年就有撞擊；(B) 第一段完全沒提到迪莫弗斯；(C) 作者並非要比較大小，而是說明大小不同的天體都可能造成災害。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why did NASA choose Dimorphos as its target?", "options": ["Because changes in its orbit around Didymos could be measured accurately.", "Because it was moving toward Earth at a dangerous speed.", "Because it was the largest asteroid near Earth.", "Because it did not travel around the Sun."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說迪莫弗斯繞著迪蒂莫斯運行，地球上的望遠鏡可以偵測亮度的下降，By timing these dips before and after the crash, scientists could tell precisely how much the orbit had changed，故選 (A)。(B) 第二段明說 Neither of them poses any threat to our planet；(C) 迪莫弗斯只有約 160 公尺寬，比它繞行的迪蒂莫斯還小；(D) 文中只說測量它繞太陽的變化很困難，並非它不繞太陽。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "After the impact, about how long did it take Dimorphos to travel once around Didymos?", "options": ["11 hours and 55 minutes", "12 hours and 27 minutes", "11 hours and 23 minutes", "73 seconds less than before"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說撞擊前迪莫弗斯 circled Didymos once every 11 hours and 55 minutes；第四段說撞擊 reduced it by about 32 minutes。11 小時 55 分減去 32 分鐘約為 11 小時 23 分，故選 (C)。(A) 是撞擊前的時間；(B) 是誤把時間加上 32 分鐘；(D) 73 秒只是 NASA 原本希望達到的最低目標，實際縮短得更多。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 5?", "options": ["Finding dangerous asteroids early is just as important as being able to push them.", "Asteroids smaller than one kilometer cannot cause serious damage.", "A spacecraft like DART can stop any asteroid a few days before it hits.", "NASA has already located every asteroid that could destroy a city."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段說 the method works only if a dangerous asteroid is spotted long before it arrives，又提到足以摧毀城市的小行星被找到的不到一半，所以 NASA 要建新望遠鏡去搜尋，可推知及早發現和推動小行星的能力一樣重要，故選 (A)。(B) 同段說直徑 140 公尺以上的小行星就足以摧毀城市；(C) 與「小小一推需要許多年累積」相反；(D) 與「被找到的還不到一半」相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"deliberately\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["by accident", "on purpose", "very slowly", "without danger"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段說 DART deliberately crashed a spacecraft... into an asteroid，同段最後又說明 The goal was simply to test whether a fast-moving spacecraft could push an asteroid，可見撞擊是事先計畫好的，deliberately 意為「刻意地、故意地」，與 on purpose 相同，故選 (B)。(A) by accident 意思相反；(C) 太空船時速超過兩萬公里，並不慢；(D) without danger 與這個字的意思無關。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a98", "title": "Ripples in Space: The Hundred-Year Hunt for Gravitational Waves", "title_zh": "太空中的漣漪：追尋重力波的百年之旅", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science"], "passage": "In 1916, Albert Einstein made a remarkable prediction. According to his general theory of relativity, massive objects bend space and time around them, and when such objects speed up or crash into each other, they should send ripples spreading through the universe, much as a stone thrown into a pond creates waves. Einstein called these ripples gravitational waves, but he doubted that anyone would ever detect them. By the time they reached Earth, he believed, they would be far too weak to measure.\n\nA century later, scientists proved him half right. The waves were indeed incredibly weak, but they were not impossible to catch. The Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory, or LIGO, has two huge L-shaped detectors in the US states of Washington and Louisiana. Each has two arms four kilometers long. A laser beam is split in two, sent down both arms, reflected by mirrors and then combined again. If a gravitational wave passes through, it stretches one arm and squeezes the other by far less than the width of a single proton, slightly changing the pattern of the returning light.\n\nMeasuring such a tiny change is extremely difficult because almost anything can shake the mirrors: a passing truck, waves crashing on a distant beach, or an earthquake on the other side of the world. To reduce this noise, the mirrors hang from special systems that absorb vibration. Building two detectors about 3,000 kilometers apart also helps. A local vibration will appear in only one of them, but a genuine gravitational wave, traveling at the speed of light, should be recorded by both within a few thousandths of a second.\n\nThat is exactly what happened on September 14, 2015. Both detectors picked up a signal lasting about one-fifth of a second. When it was turned into sound, it became a short \"chirp\" that rose quickly in pitch. Researchers worked out that it had been produced by two black holes, about 36 and 29 times as massive as the Sun, spiraling into each other some 1.3 billion light-years away. The discovery was announced in February 2016, and three of the project's leaders won the 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics.\n\nThe achievement matters because, until then, astronomers had studied the universe almost entirely through light and other forms of radiation. Black holes give off no light at all, yet they can now be \"heard.\" In 2017, detectors caught the collision of two neutron stars, which telescopes then observed as well, showing that such crashes create heavy elements like gold.\n\nToday, detectors in Italy and Japan have joined the search, and hundreds of these cosmic events have been recorded. For most of history, we watched a silent movie of the universe. Now the movie finally has a soundtrack.", "passage_zh": "1916 年，愛因斯坦提出了一個非凡的預測。根據他的廣義相對論，質量巨大的物體會讓周圍的空間和時間彎曲；當這樣的物體加速或彼此相撞時，應該會發出向整個宇宙擴散的漣漪，就像把石頭丟進池塘會激起水波一樣。愛因斯坦把這些漣漪稱為重力波，但他懷疑是否有人能偵測到它們。他認為，等到這些波傳到地球時，早已微弱得無法測量。\n\n一個世紀之後，科學家證明他只說對了一半。這些波確實極為微弱，但並非不可能捕捉。「雷射干涉重力波天文台」，簡稱 LIGO，在美國華盛頓州和路易斯安那州各有一座巨大的 L 形偵測器。每座偵測器都有兩條長四公里的臂。一道雷射光被分成兩道，分別射向兩條臂，經鏡子反射後再重新合在一起。如果有重力波通過，它會把其中一條臂拉長、另一條臂壓縮，變化量比一顆質子的寬度還要小得多，使反射回來的光的圖樣產生些微的改變。\n\n要測量這麼微小的變化極為困難，因為幾乎任何事物都可能讓鏡子晃動：一輛經過的卡車、遠方海灘上拍打的海浪，或是地球另一端發生的地震。為了減少這些干擾，鏡子都懸掛在能吸收震動的特殊裝置上。把兩座偵測器蓋在相距約 3,000 公里的地方也有幫助。局部的震動只會出現在其中一座偵測器中，但真正的重力波以光速傳遞，應該會在千分之幾秒內被兩座偵測器都記錄下來。\n\n2015 年 9 月 14 日，事情正是這樣發生的。兩座偵測器都接收到一個持續約五分之一秒的訊號。把它轉換成聲音後，就成了一聲音調迅速升高的短促「啁啾聲」。研究人員推算出，這個訊號是由兩個分別約為太陽 36 倍和 29 倍質量的黑洞，在大約 13 億光年外互相盤旋、合而為一所產生的。這項發現在 2016 年 2 月公布，計畫的三位領導人也獲得了 2017 年的諾貝爾物理學獎。\n\n這項成就之所以重要，是因為在此之前，天文學家幾乎完全是透過光和其他形式的輻射來研究宇宙。黑洞完全不發光，如今卻能被「聽見」。2017 年，偵測器捕捉到兩顆中子星的碰撞，接著望遠鏡也觀測到了這次事件，證明這類碰撞會產生像金這樣的重元素。\n\n如今，義大利和日本的偵測器也加入了搜尋的行列，已經記錄到數百起這類宇宙事件。在人類歷史的大部分時間裡，我們看的是一部宇宙的無聲電影。如今，這部電影終於有了配樂。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Einstein's theory of relativity has been proved completely wrong.", "Scientists finally detected gravitational waves, giving astronomy a new way to study the universe.", "LIGO was built mainly to measure earthquakes on the other side of the world.", "Black holes are the only objects in the universe that produce gravitational waves."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從愛因斯坦的預測談起，說明 LIGO 如何在 2015 年首次偵測到重力波，並在第五、六段指出它讓天文學家多了一種「聽」宇宙的方法，故選 (B)。(A) 第二段說科學家證明他 half right，重力波確實存在；(C) 地震是第三段提到的干擾來源，並非建造目的；(D) 第五段提到中子星碰撞也被偵測到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why does LIGO use two detectors far away from each other?", "options": ["So that scientists in two states can share the cost equally", "So that one detector can keep working while the other is repaired", "So that the laser beam can travel 3,000 kilometers", "So that scientists can tell a real wave from a local vibration"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：A local vibration will appear in only one of them, but a genuine gravitational wave... should be recorded by both within a few thousandths of a second. 兩座偵測器相距遙遠，是為了分辨真正的重力波和當地的震動，故選 (D)。(A)(B) 文中未提及；(C) 每條臂只有四公里長，3,000 公里是兩座偵測器之間的距離。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what produced the signal detected on September 14, 2015?", "options": ["Two neutron stars that crashed and created gold", "A powerful earthquake on the other side of the world", "Two black holes that spiraled into each other and merged", "A star about 36 times as massive as the Sun exploding"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：it had been produced by two black holes, about 36 and 29 times as massive as the Sun, spiraling into each other some 1.3 billion light-years away，故選 (C)。(A) 中子星碰撞是第五段提到的 2017 年事件；(B) 地震是會干擾偵測器的雜訊，不是訊號來源；(D) 36 倍是其中一個黑洞的質量，並非恆星爆炸。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about black holes from paragraph 5?", "options": ["They could hardly be studied with ordinary telescopes before gravitational waves were detected.", "They are the main source of heavy elements such as gold.", "They give off more light than any other objects in space.", "They were first discovered by LIGO in 2017."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段說過去天文學家幾乎完全靠光和其他輻射研究宇宙，而 Black holes give off no light at all, yet they can now be \"heard\"，可推知在偵測到重力波之前，很難用一般望遠鏡研究黑洞，故選 (A)。(B) 文中說產生金等重元素的是中子星碰撞；(C) 與 give off no light at all 相反；(D) 2017 年偵測到的是中子星碰撞，而且第四段 2015 年的訊號已來自黑洞。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"genuine\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["weak", "local", "dangerous", "real"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段把 A local vibration（局部震動，屬於雜訊）和 a genuine gravitational wave 對比，後者才是科學家要找的真正訊號，genuine 意為「真正的、真實的」，與 real 最接近，故選 (D)。(A) 重力波雖然微弱，但 genuine 並不是這個意思；(B) local 正是句中與 genuine 對比的字；(C) dangerous 不合文意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a99", "title": "The Woman Who Measured the Universe", "title_zh": "丈量宇宙的女性", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "History"], "passage": "How far away is a star? For centuries, this was one of the hardest questions in astronomy. A star that looks bright in the night sky might be close to us and quite dim, or very far away and extremely powerful, and there was no easy way to tell the difference. Surprisingly, the key to solving this puzzle was found by a woman who was not even allowed to use her observatory's large telescopes.\n\nHenrietta Swan Leavitt joined the Harvard College Observatory in the 1890s as one of its \"computers.\" At the time, this word referred not to machines but to women hired to examine photographic glass plates of the night sky, measure the brightness of thousands of stars and record the results by hand. The work was tedious and poorly paid, and women were rarely allowed to operate the big telescopes themselves. Leavitt, who gradually lost much of her hearing, nevertheless worked with extraordinary patience and accuracy.\n\nStudying plates of the Magellanic Clouds, two great star systems visible from the Southern Hemisphere, she identified more than 1,700 variable stars, whose brightness changes over time. She paid special attention to a type called Cepheids, which grow brighter and dimmer in a steady rhythm lasting from a few days to several weeks. In 1912, after comparing Cepheids in the Small Magellanic Cloud, she reported a striking pattern: the brighter a Cepheid was, the longer its cycle took.\n\nThis finding was far more powerful than it might sound. Since all the stars in the Small Magellanic Cloud are roughly the same distance from Earth, differences in how bright they appeared had to reflect real differences in how much light they gave off. Therefore, by timing a Cepheid's cycle, astronomers could work out its true brightness. Comparing that with how bright it looked from Earth then revealed its distance, in the same way that we can judge how far away a lamp is if we know how strong its bulb is.\n\nIn the 1920s, Edwin Hubble found Cepheids in the Andromeda Nebula and used Leavitt's method to measure their distance. The result showed that Andromeda was far too distant to be part of our own Milky Way. It was a separate galaxy, and the universe was suddenly much larger than most scientists had imagined.\n\nLeavitt did not live to see this revolution. She died of cancer in 1921, and in 1925 a Swedish mathematician, unaware of her death, tried to nominate her for the Nobel Prize, which cannot be awarded after a person has died. Today her discovery is often called Leavitt's Law, a lasting reminder that important science is sometimes done by people whose work goes unnoticed for too long.", "passage_zh": "一顆恆星離我們有多遠？好幾個世紀以來，這一直是天文學中最難回答的問題之一。一顆在夜空中看起來很亮的恆星，可能離我們很近、本身其實相當黯淡，也可能離我們非常遙遠、本身極為明亮，而且沒有簡單的方法能分辨。令人意外的是，解開這個謎題的關鍵，竟然是由一位連天文台的大型望遠鏡都不被允許使用的女性找到的。\n\n亨麗埃塔‧斯旺‧勒維特在 1890 年代進入哈佛大學天文台，成為那裡的「計算員」之一。在當時，這個詞指的不是機器，而是受雇來檢視夜空照相玻璃底片的女性，她們要測量數千顆恆星的亮度，再用手把結果記錄下來。這份工作單調乏味，薪水又低，而且女性很少能親自操作大型望遠鏡。勒維特的聽力逐漸喪失了大半，但她仍以非凡的耐心和精確度投入工作。\n\n她在研究麥哲倫星雲的底片時──那是兩個在南半球可以看見的巨大星系統──找出了一千七百多顆變星，也就是亮度會隨時間改變的恆星。她特別注意一種叫做造父變星的恆星，它們會以穩定的節奏變亮又變暗，一個週期從幾天到好幾週不等。1912 年，她比較了小麥哲倫星雲中的造父變星之後，發表了一個驚人的規律：造父變星越亮，它的週期就越長。\n\n這項發現的威力遠比聽起來強大得多。由於小麥哲倫星雲中的所有恆星和地球的距離大致相同，它們看起來亮度上的差異，必定反映出它們實際發出的光有多少的差別。因此，只要測量造父變星的週期，天文學家就能推算出它真正的亮度。再把這個亮度和它從地球上看起來的亮度相比，就能得知它的距離；這就像只要知道一盞燈的燈泡有多強，就能判斷這盞燈離我們有多遠一樣。\n\n1920 年代，愛德溫‧哈伯在仙女座星雲中找到了造父變星，並用勒維特的方法測量它們的距離。結果顯示，仙女座實在太遠了，不可能是我們銀河系的一部分。它是另一個獨立的星系，宇宙一下子變得比大多數科學家想像的還要大得多。\n\n勒維特沒能親眼看到這場革命。她在 1921 年因癌症過世；1925 年，一位不知道她已經過世的瑞典數學家想提名她角逐諾貝爾獎，但這個獎不能頒給已經過世的人。如今，她的發現常被稱為「勒維特定律」，持續提醒著我們：重要的科學成就，有時是由那些長久以來未受重視的人所完成的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why women were not allowed to use telescopes in the 1890s", "How Edwin Hubble discovered the Andromeda galaxy by himself", "How Leavitt's discovery made it possible to measure distances in space", "Why Cepheid stars are brighter than other stars"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段提出「恆星有多遠」這個難題，並說解開關鍵的是一位女性；第三、四段說明勒維特發現造父變星的規律，以及如何用它算出距離；第五段說哈伯用她的方法發現仙女座是另一個星系，故選 (C)。(A) 只是第二段的背景；(B) 第五段說哈伯用的是 Leavitt's method，並非獨力完成；(D) 文中沒有說造父變星比其他恆星亮。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what pattern did Leavitt report in 1912?", "options": ["Brighter Cepheids take longer to complete one cycle.", "Stars in the Southern Hemisphere are brighter than those in the north.", "Variable stars become dimmer as they get older.", "Cepheids change their brightness in random ways."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段最後一句：In 1912, after comparing Cepheids in the Small Magellanic Cloud, she reported a striking pattern: the brighter a Cepheid was, the longer its cycle took. 越亮的造父變星週期越長，故選 (A)。(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到；(D) 同段說造父變星以 a steady rhythm（穩定的節奏）變亮變暗，並非隨機。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why was it important that the Cepheids Leavitt compared were all in the Small Magellanic Cloud?", "options": ["Because these stars could be seen through Harvard's largest telescope", "Because these stars were the closest ones to Earth", "Because these stars never changed in brightness", "Because their differences in brightness could not be caused by different distances"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：Since all the stars in the Small Magellanic Cloud are roughly the same distance from Earth, differences in how bright they appeared had to reflect real differences in how much light they gave off. 因為距離大致相同，亮度差異不可能是距離造成的，而是真實亮度的差別，故選 (D)。(A) 第一、二段說她不被允許使用大型望遠鏡，她研究的是照相底片；(B) 文中沒說它們最近；(C) 她研究的正是亮度會變化的變星。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"tedious\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["dangerous", "boring and repetitive", "exciting and new", "easy to finish"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段描述計算員的工作是檢視玻璃底片、測量數千顆恆星的亮度，再 record the results by hand，可見工作重複又費時，tedious 意為「冗長乏味的」，與 boring and repetitive 最接近，故選 (B)。(C) 與文意相反；(A) 文中沒有提到危險；(D) 測量數千顆恆星並不容易完成。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is TRUE according to the passage?", "options": ["Leavitt won the Nobel Prize in 1925 for her work on Cepheids.", "Leavitt took the photographs of the Small Magellanic Cloud with her own telescope.", "Hubble used Leavitt's method to show that Andromeda lies outside the Milky Way.", "Leavitt's discovery made the universe seem smaller than scientists had believed."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：Edwin Hubble... used Leavitt's method... The result showed that Andromeda was far too distant to be part of our own Milky Way，故 (C) 正確。(A) 第六段說她 1921 年已過世，1925 年的提名無法成立，因為諾貝爾獎不頒給過世的人；(B) 她研究的是天文台的照相底片，而且女性很少能操作大型望遠鏡；(D) 第五段說宇宙 suddenly much larger，與此相反。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a100", "title": "Traffic Jam in Orbit: The Growing Problem of Space Junk", "title_zh": "軌道上的大塞車：日益嚴重的太空垃圾問題", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Technology"], "passage": "Imagine a highway where broken-down cars are never towed away, where pieces of old tires and bumpers keep flying around at deadly speed, and where new vehicles join the traffic every week. That is roughly what the space around our planet has become. Since the Soviet Union launched Sputnik in 1957, humans have left behind dead satellites, used rocket stages and countless fragments. Today, tens of thousands of objects larger than ten centimeters are being tracked, and scientists estimate that there are more than a million pieces larger than one centimeter.\n\nSuch small objects may not sound dangerous, but in low orbit they travel at around 28,000 kilometers per hour, so even a tiny fragment carries enormous energy. In 1983, a fleck of paint chipped a window of a space shuttle, and a one-centimeter piece of metal can disable a satellite completely. The International Space Station has had to change its course dozens of times to avoid debris, and on several occasions astronauts have sheltered in their spacecraft, ready to escape.\n\nThe real worry is that collisions multiply. In 2009, a working American communications satellite crashed into a dead Russian satellite above Siberia, creating more than 2,000 trackable pieces. Missile tests in which several countries deliberately destroyed their own satellites have added thousands more. Every collision produces new fragments, and every new fragment makes another collision more likely.\n\nAs early as 1978, NASA scientist Donald Kessler warned that if orbits became crowded enough, this process could turn into a chain reaction. Pieces would smash into satellites, which would break into more pieces, until certain orbits became too dangerous to use for generations. This idea, now known as the Kessler syndrome, has become more urgent as private companies launch networks of thousands of satellites. Since weather forecasts, navigation and many communications depend on satellites, the consequences would be felt by almost everyone on Earth.\n\nExperts agree that prevention is the first step. Satellites can be designed to fall back and burn up in the atmosphere when their missions end, and in 2022 the United States reduced the time allowed for this from 25 years to five for the satellites it licenses. Engineers are also testing ways to remove existing junk, such as a net that successfully captured a target in orbit in 2018. However, cleanup missions are expensive, and international law does not allow one country to remove another country's satellite without permission.\n\nIn many ways, space junk resembles plastic in the ocean. Each user gains something by using the shared space, while the cost is spread among everyone. Space may seem endless, but the useful orbits around Earth are a limited resource, and protecting them will require cooperation before the traffic jam becomes impossible to clear.", "passage_zh": "想像一條公路，上面拋錨的車子永遠不會被拖走，舊輪胎和保險桿的碎片不斷以致命的速度四處亂飛，而每個星期都還有新的車輛加入車流。這大致就是我們地球周圍的太空如今變成的樣子。自從蘇聯在 1957 年發射史普尼克號以來，人類已經留下了失效的衛星、用過的火箭節段和數不清的碎片。如今，有數萬個大於十公分的物體正受到追蹤，而科學家估計，大於一公分的碎片超過一百萬個。\n\n這麼小的物體聽起來也許不危險，但在低軌道上，它們的速度大約是每小時 28,000 公里，因此就算是很小的碎片也帶有巨大的能量。1983 年，一小片油漆就在太空梭的窗戶上撞出了缺口；而一塊一公分大的金屬碎片，就可能讓一顆衛星完全失去功能。國際太空站已經有數十次必須改變航道來閃避碎片，也有好幾次，太空人必須躲進他們的太空船裡，隨時準備逃生。\n\n真正令人擔心的是，碰撞會不斷倍增。2009 年，一顆運作中的美國通訊衛星在西伯利亞上空撞上一顆已失效的俄羅斯衛星，產生了兩千多個可追蹤的碎片。好幾個國家曾進行飛彈試驗，刻意摧毀自己的衛星，又增加了數千個碎片。每一次碰撞都會產生新的碎片，而每一個新碎片都會讓下一次碰撞更有可能發生。\n\n早在 1978 年，NASA 科學家唐納德‧凱斯勒就提出警告：如果軌道變得夠擁擠，這個過程可能演變成連鎖反應。碎片會撞上衛星，衛星又會碎裂成更多碎片，直到某些軌道變得太危險，好幾個世代都無法使用。這個概念如今被稱為「凱斯勒症候群」；隨著民間公司發射由數千顆衛星組成的衛星網路，這個問題也變得更加迫切。由於天氣預報、導航和許多通訊都仰賴衛星，一旦發生，地球上幾乎每個人都會受到影響。\n\n專家們都同意，預防是第一步。衛星可以設計成在任務結束後落回大氣層並燒毀；2022 年，美國把它所核發執照的衛星可以留在軌道上的期限，從 25 年縮短為五年。工程師也在測試清除現有垃圾的方法，例如在 2018 年，曾有一張網在軌道上成功捕捉到一個目標物。然而，清理任務的費用十分昂貴，而且國際法不允許一個國家在未經許可的情況下，移除另一個國家的衛星。\n\n從許多方面來看，太空垃圾就像海洋中的塑膠。每個使用者都從這個共享的空間中得到好處，代價卻由所有人一起分擔。太空看似無邊無際，但地球周圍有用的軌道其實是有限的資源；要保護它們，就必須在這場大塞車變得無法疏通之前攜手合作。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Private companies should stop launching satellites into space.", "The International Space Station is no longer safe for astronauts.", "Space junk has already made all orbits around Earth impossible to use.", "Space junk is a growing danger that calls for prevention, cleanup and cooperation."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文先說明太空垃圾的數量與危險（第一、二段），再說明碰撞會倍增並可能引發凱斯勒症候群（第三、四段），最後提出預防與清除的方法，並強調需要合作（第五、六段），故選 (D)。(A) 作者只說衛星網路使問題更迫切，沒有主張停止發射；(B) 太空站只是第二段的例子；(C) 第四段說的是「可能」讓某些軌道無法使用，並非已經發生。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what happened in 2009?", "options": ["A missile test destroyed an American communications satellite.", "An active satellite crashed into a dead one, producing over 2,000 trackable pieces.", "A fleck of paint broke a window of the International Space Station.", "Russia removed one of its old satellites from orbit."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：In 2009, a working American communications satellite crashed into a dead Russian satellite above Siberia, creating more than 2,000 trackable pieces. 運作中的美國通訊衛星撞上失效的俄羅斯衛星，故選 (B)。(A) 飛彈試驗是各國摧毀「自己的」衛星，與 2009 年的事件不同；(C) 油漆碎片撞到的是 1983 年的太空梭窗戶；(D) 文中沒有提到俄羅斯移除衛星。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "If the \"Kessler syndrome\" began, what would most likely happen?", "options": ["Some orbits would become too dangerous to use for a very long time.", "All the debris would quickly fall back and burn up in the atmosphere.", "Satellites would be able to avoid one another more easily.", "Weather forecasts would become more accurate than before."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說凱斯勒症候群是一種連鎖反應：碎片撞上衛星、衛星再碎裂成更多碎片，until certain orbits became too dangerous to use for generations，故選 (A)。(B) 與連鎖反應使碎片越來越多的說法相反；(C) 軌道會更擁擠，衛星更難閃避；(D) 同段說天氣預報仰賴衛星，一旦發生會受到負面影響。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"disable\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["speed up", "build again", "stop from working", "follow closely"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段說小碎片 carries enormous energy，a one-centimeter piece of metal can disable a satellite completely，意指一公分的金屬碎片就能讓衛星「完全失去功能」，disable 與 stop from working 最接近，故選 (C)。(A)(B)(D) 都不符合碎片撞擊造成損壞的上下文。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer compare space junk to plastic in the ocean in the last paragraph?", "options": ["To show that most space junk is made of plastic", "To point out that a shared resource can be damaged when users do not share responsibility for it", "To suggest that cleaning the ocean is more important than cleaning space", "To explain how space junk falls into the ocean after burning up"], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一段：Each user gains something by using the shared space, while the cost is spread among everyone，並說有用的軌道是 a limited resource，需要合作保護。作者藉海洋塑膠的例子說明共享資源在大家只顧使用時容易被破壞，故選 (B)。(A) 文中未說太空垃圾多為塑膠；(C) 作者沒有比較兩者何者較重要；(D) 文中說衛星會在大氣層中燒毀，沒有提到落入海洋。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a101", "title": "One Twin in Space, One on Earth: What NASA Learned from the Kelly Brothers", "title_zh": "一個在太空，一個在地球：NASA 從凱利兄弟身上學到的事", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Health"], "passage": "Scientists who study the human body often wish they could compare a person with an exact copy who had lived a different life. In 2015, NASA found the next best thing. Astronaut Scott Kelly spent 340 days aboard the International Space Station, while his identical twin brother Mark, a retired astronaut, stayed on Earth. Because the two men share the same genes, any differences that appeared between them during the mission were likely to have been caused by life in space.\n\nTen research teams collected blood, urine and other samples from both brothers before, during and after the flight, and published their main results in 2019. One of the most surprising findings involved telomeres, the protective caps at the ends of our chromosomes, which are often compared to the plastic tips on shoelaces. Telomeres normally get shorter as people grow older, so scientists expected Scott's to shrink in the harsh conditions of space. Instead, they became longer. Within days of his return, however, most of them shortened again, and some ended up shorter than before the flight.\n\nThe researchers also found that the activity of thousands of Scott's genes changed in orbit, probably in response to stress, radiation and weightlessness. About 90 percent of these changes disappeared within six months of landing, but a few linked to the immune system and to repairing damaged DNA remained. Other tests revealed swelling at the back of his eyes, a thickening of an important artery, and a slight drop in the speed and accuracy of his thinking after he came home.\n\nNot all news coverage got the science right. Some headlines claimed that seven percent of Scott's DNA had changed and that the brothers were no longer identical twins. NASA quickly corrected this mistake. Scott's DNA itself had not changed; what had changed was how strongly certain genes were switched on or off. It is a bit like turning the volume of a song up or down: the sound changes, but the song itself stays the same.\n\nThe study matters because a trip to Mars and back could take two to three years, with far more exposure to radiation than on the space station. On the whole, the results suggest that the human body is remarkably resilient, recovering from most of the changes caused by a year in orbit. Yet some effects clearly need further investigation before astronauts travel that far.\n\nThe researchers themselves are careful not to claim too much. A study of only two people cannot prove what will happen to every astronaut, so they see it as a starting point rather than a final answer. As more people spend longer periods in space, their bodies will help complete the picture that the Kelly twins began.", "passage_zh": "研究人體的科學家常常希望，能拿一個人和另一個過著不同生活、卻一模一樣的「複製人」來比較。2015 年，NASA 找到了最接近這個理想的方法。太空人史考特‧凱利在國際太空站待了 340 天，而他的同卵雙胞胎兄弟、已經退休的太空人馬克則留在地球上。由於兩人擁有相同的基因，任務期間他們之間出現的任何差異，很可能都是太空生活造成的。\n\n十個研究團隊在飛行前、飛行中和飛行後，收集了兩兄弟的血液、尿液和其他樣本，並在 2019 年發表了主要的研究結果。其中最令人意外的發現和端粒有關。端粒是位在染色體末端的保護帽，常被比喻成鞋帶末端的塑膠套。端粒通常會隨著年齡增長而變短，所以科學家原本預期，在太空嚴苛的環境中，史考特的端粒會縮短。結果它們反而變長了。然而，他回到地球後幾天內，大部分的端粒又縮短了，有些甚至變得比飛行前還短。\n\n研究人員也發現，史考特有數千個基因的活動在軌道上發生了變化，可能是為了因應壓力、輻射和失重狀態。在他返回地球後的六個月內，這些變化大約有百分之九十消失了，但仍有少數與免疫系統以及修復受損 DNA 有關的變化持續存在。其他檢查也顯示，他的眼睛後方出現腫脹，一條重要動脈的管壁變厚，而且他回家之後，思考的速度和準確度都略微下降。\n\n並不是所有的新聞報導都正確理解了這項科學。有些標題宣稱史考特有百分之七的 DNA 改變了，兩兄弟也不再是同卵雙胞胎。NASA 很快就更正了這個錯誤。史考特的 DNA 本身並沒有改變；改變的是某些基因被開啟或關閉的程度。這有點像把一首歌的音量調大或調小：聽起來的聲音變了，但歌曲本身還是一樣。\n\n這項研究之所以重要，是因為往返火星一趟可能要花兩到三年，而且受到的輻射比在太空站上多得多。整體而言，研究結果顯示人體的恢復力相當驚人，在軌道上待了一年所造成的大多數變化都能復原。不過，在太空人前往那麼遠的地方之前，有些影響顯然還需要進一步研究。\n\n研究人員自己也很小心，不會過度宣稱研究的意義。一項只有兩個人的研究，無法證明每一位太空人都會發生什麼事，因此他們把它視為一個起點，而不是最終的答案。隨著越來越多人在太空中待得更久，他們的身體將幫助我們把凱利雙胞胎開始拼湊的這幅圖畫拼得更完整。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["What a study of twin astronauts revealed about the effects of a long stay in space", "Why identical twins should not be allowed to travel into space", "How Scott Kelly prepared his body for a trip to Mars", "Why news reports about science are usually wrong"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段介紹 NASA 以凱利雙胞胎進行研究，第二、三段說明史考特在太空中端粒、基因活動與身體的變化，第五、六段討論研究對火星任務的意義與限制，故選 (A)。(B) 文中沒有這種主張；(C) 史考特待的是國際太空站，並未前往火星；(D) 第四段只說「有些」報導有誤，而非新聞通常都錯。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why were the Kelly brothers especially useful for this study?", "options": ["Both of them had spent a year on the space station before.", "Mark was able to collect samples from Scott while he was in orbit.", "Since they share the same genes, differences between them could be linked to spaceflight.", "They were the only astronauts who agreed to give blood samples."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：Because the two men share the same genes, any differences that appeared between them during the mission were likely to have been caused by life in space. 兩人基因相同，出現的差異就能歸因於太空生活，故選 (C)。(A) 文中只說史考特這次待了 340 天；(B) 樣本是由研究團隊收集，馬克留在地球上；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happened to Scott's telomeres?", "options": ["They became shorter in space and stayed short after he returned.", "They became longer in space but mostly shortened again after he returned.", "They did not change at all during the mission.", "They grew longer after he returned to Earth."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：科學家原本預期端粒會縮短，Instead, they became longer. Within days of his return, however, most of them shortened again，即在太空中變長，回到地球後大多又縮短，故選 (B)。(A) 與 Instead, they became longer 相反；(C) 端粒明顯有變化；(D) 變長是發生在太空中，回到地球後反而縮短。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer compare gene activity to the volume of a song in paragraph 4?", "options": ["To show that Scott listened to music to reduce stress in space", "To prove that the brothers were no longer identical twins", "To explain why seven percent of Scott's DNA was damaged", "To make clear that his genes worked differently while his DNA stayed the same"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說 Scott's DNA itself had not changed; what had changed was how strongly certain genes were switched on or off，接著用調整歌曲音量比喻：聲音變了，歌曲本身不變，目的是說明基因活動改變但 DNA 本身沒變，故選 (D)。(A) 文中未提到他聽音樂；(B)(C) 正是 NASA 更正的錯誤報導內容。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"resilient\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["able to recover from difficult conditions", "easily damaged by small changes", "unable to change at all", "likely to become ill"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段 the human body is remarkably resilient, recovering from most of the changes caused by a year in orbit，後半句說明人體能從大多數變化中「復原」，resilient 意為「有恢復力的、能迅速復原的」，故選 (A)。(B)(D) 與「能復原」的意思相反；(C) 第二、三段顯示身體確實有變化，只是大多能恢復。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a102", "title": "Why Cities Run a Fever: The Urban Heat Island", "title_zh": "城市為什麼會發燒：都市熱島效應", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Science"], "passage": "On a summer evening in Taipei, walking from a leafy park onto a busy street lined with concrete buildings can feel like stepping into an oven. This is not just your imagination. Scientists have measured the difference for decades, and they have a name for it: the urban heat island. A city can be several degrees warmer than the countryside around it, and the gap tends to be largest after sunset, when rural areas cool quickly but the city stays stubbornly warm.\n\nThe main reason lies in the materials cities are built from. Asphalt roads, concrete walls and dark rooftops absorb a large share of the sunlight that falls on them and store it as heat. During the day they can become far hotter than the air; at night they slowly release that heat back into the streets. Fields and forests behave differently. Plants release water through their leaves, and as this water evaporates, it carries heat away, much as sweat cools the human body. When soil and trees are replaced by pavement, this natural cooling system disappears.\n\nHuman activity makes the problem worse. Cars, factories and, ironically, air conditioners all give off waste heat. On a hot night, thousands of air-conditioning units push warm air out of buildings and into narrow streets, which encourages people to turn their own machines up even higher. Tall buildings packed closely together also trap heat and block the wind that might otherwise carry it away.\n\nThis extra warmth is more than uncomfortable. During heat waves, high night-time temperatures prevent the body from recovering, which is why elderly people and those who work outdoors face serious health risks. Hotter cities also use more electricity for cooling, putting pressure on power grids at exactly the moment demand is highest.\n\nFortunately, the heat island is one environmental problem that cities can tackle locally. Painting roofs white or covering them with reflective coatings bounces sunlight back into the sky. Street trees provide shade and restore some of the cooling effect of evaporation. Some cities have gone further. In Seoul, a busy elevated highway was torn down in the early 2000s to uncover the Cheonggyecheon stream that had been buried beneath it, and studies found that the area along the restored stream became noticeably cooler than nearby roads.\n\nNone of these measures will stop global warming on its own, but they can make the hottest days more bearable for millions of people. As more of the world's population moves into cities, the way we design streets, roofs and parks may matter just as much as the air conditioners we buy.", "passage_zh": "在臺北的夏日傍晚，從綠樹成蔭的公園走到兩旁林立水泥建築的熱鬧街道上，感覺就像走進了烤箱。這不只是你的錯覺。科學家測量這種溫差已經有數十年，並為它取了一個名字：都市熱島。城市可能比周圍的鄉間高出好幾度，而且這個差距通常在日落之後最大——那時鄉間會迅速降溫，城市卻頑固地維持溫熱。\n\n主要原因在於建造城市的材料。柏油路、水泥牆和深色屋頂會吸收照射在它們上面的大部分陽光，並把它儲存成熱能。白天時，它們的溫度可能遠高於空氣；到了夜晚，它們又慢慢把這些熱釋放回街道上。田野和森林則不一樣。植物會透過葉子釋放水分，而當這些水分蒸發時，就會把熱帶走，就像汗水讓人體降溫一樣。當土壤和樹木被鋪面取代，這套天然的降溫系統也就消失了。\n\n人類的活動讓問題更加嚴重。汽車、工廠，以及諷刺的是，冷氣機，都會排出廢熱。在炎熱的夜晚，成千上萬臺冷氣機把熱空氣從建築物裡推到狹窄的街道上，這又促使人們把自家的冷氣開得更強。緊密相鄰的高樓也會困住熱氣，並擋住原本可能把熱帶走的風。\n\n這些額外的熱不只是讓人不舒服而已。熱浪期間，夜間的高溫讓身體無法恢復，這就是為什麼長者和在戶外工作的人會面臨嚴重的健康風險。較熱的城市也需要用更多電力來降溫，正好在用電需求最高的時刻對電網造成壓力。\n\n幸好，熱島是城市能在地方層級處理的環境問題之一。把屋頂漆成白色或塗上反光塗料，可以把陽光反射回天空。行道樹能提供遮蔭，並恢復部分蒸發所帶來的降溫效果。有些城市做得更多。在首爾，一條繁忙的高架道路在 2000 年代初期被拆除，讓原本被埋在下方的清溪川重見天日；研究發現，沿著這條復原溪流的區域明顯比附近的道路涼爽。\n\n這些措施沒有一項能單獨阻止全球暖化，但它們可以讓數百萬人在最炎熱的日子裡好過一些。隨著世界上越來越多人口移入城市，我們如何設計街道、屋頂和公園，可能和我們買什麼冷氣機一樣重要。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why air conditioners are the main cause of global warming", "Why cities are warmer than the countryside and what can be done about it", "How Seoul became the coolest city in Asia", "Why people should move from cities back to the countryside"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說明城市比鄉間溫暖的「都市熱島」現象，第二、三段解釋原因，第四段說明影響，第五段提出白色屋頂、行道樹、復原溪流等對策，故主旨為 (B)。(A) 冷氣機只是第三段提到的廢熱來源之一，且末段說這些措施無法單獨阻止全球暖化，文中並未說冷氣是暖化主因；(C) 首爾只是一個例子，文中沒說它是亞洲最涼爽的城市；(D) 末段反而提到越來越多人移入城市，並未建議搬回鄉下。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how do plants help keep an area cool?", "options": ["They block all sunlight from reaching the ground.", "They store heat during the day and release it at night.", "Water evaporating from their leaves carries heat away.", "They create wind that blows hot air out of the area."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Plants release water through their leaves, and as this water evaporates, it carries heat away, much as sweat cools the human body. 植物葉子釋出的水分蒸發時會帶走熱，故選 (C)。(B) 白天儲熱、夜晚釋放熱的是柏油路、水泥牆等城市材料，不是植物；(A)(D) 文中皆未提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"ironically\" in paragraph 3 suggests that ______.", "options": ["machines meant to cool people down end up making the streets hotter", "air conditioners are too expensive for most families", "air conditioners give off less heat than cars and factories", "people should never use air conditioners at night"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說汽車、工廠，以及 ironically（諷刺的是）冷氣機都會排出廢熱：冷氣原本是用來降溫的，卻把熱空氣排到街道上，讓街道更熱，形成與原本目的相反的結果，故選 (A)。(B) 文中沒有提到價格；(C) 文中沒有比較冷氣與汽車、工廠排熱量的多寡；(D) 作者沒有要求人們晚上完全不開冷氣。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the Cheonggyecheon project in Seoul?", "options": ["It was built mainly to provide electricity for the city.", "It made the whole city of Seoul cooler than the countryside.", "It replaced an old stream with a modern highway.", "It showed that bringing back water and open space can lower local temperatures."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：首爾拆除高架道路，讓被埋在下方的清溪川重見天日，研究發現 the area along the restored stream became noticeably cooler than nearby roads，可推論恢復水域與開放空間能降低當地氣溫，故選 (D)。(A) 文中未提發電；(B) 變涼的只是溪流沿岸，並非整個首爾比鄉間涼；(C) 方向相反，是拆掉高架道路、恢復溪流。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's attitude toward solutions such as white roofs and street trees?", "options": ["Strongly opposed, because they cost too much", "Uninterested, because only global action matters", "Hopeful but realistic about what they can achieve", "Certain that they will put an end to global warming"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段以 Fortunately 開頭介紹這些對策，末段又說 None of these measures will stop global warming on its own, but they can make the hottest days more bearable，可見作者對其效果抱持希望，但也清楚它們的限制，故選 (C)。(A)(B) 與作者正面的語氣不符；(D) 與末段「無法單獨阻止全球暖化」相反。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a103", "title": "Blue Carbon: The Climate Heroes Growing in the Mud", "title_zh": "藍碳：生長在泥灘裡的氣候英雄", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Nature"], "passage": "When people think of forests that fight climate change, they usually picture the Amazon rainforest or the vast pine forests of the north. Few imagine a muddy coastline where twisted trees stand in salty water. Yet mangrove forests, which grow along tropical and subtropical coasts, are among the most effective natural carbon stores on Earth. Together with salt marshes and seagrass meadows, they hold what scientists call \"blue carbon\": carbon captured by ocean and coastal ecosystems.\n\nLike all plants, mangroves take in carbon dioxide from the air as they grow. What makes them special is what happens underground. Their roots sit in waterlogged mud that contains very little oxygen, so fallen leaves and dead roots break down extremely slowly. Instead of returning to the atmosphere, much of the carbon is buried and can remain locked in the soil for centuries. As a result, a hectare of mangroves can store up to four times as much carbon as a hectare of tropical rainforest, mostly beneath the surface.\n\nMangroves protect people as well as the climate. Their dense, tangled roots slow down waves and reduce the damage caused by storm surges. The shallow water among the roots also serves as a nursery, where young fish, crabs and shrimp can hide from predators until they are large enough to survive in open water. For millions of coastal families, healthy mangroves mean both safer homes and bigger catches.\n\nDespite these benefits, mangroves have been disappearing for decades. Large areas have been cleared for shrimp farms, ports and tourist resorts, while others have been cut down for timber and charcoal. According to the UN Food and Agriculture Organization, roughly a fifth of the world's mangroves were lost between 1980 and 2005. Worse still, destroying a mangrove does not simply stop it from absorbing carbon. When the mud is dug up or dried out, carbon stored over centuries can escape quickly, turning a carbon store into a source of greenhouse gases.\n\nTaiwan offers a small example of what protection can achieve. At the mouth of the Tamsui River grows a forest of a mangrove species known locally as shui bi zai, or \"water pen,\" named after its long, pen-shaped seedlings, which hang from the branches before dropping into the mud. The area has been a nature reserve since 1986, and today visitors can stroll along a wooden boardwalk and watch fiddler crabs waving their oversized claws at low tide.\n\nProtecting mangroves will never replace the need to cut emissions from cars and factories. However, scientists increasingly argue that blue carbon ecosystems deserve as much attention as rainforests. Saving a muddy shoreline may not sound as exciting as saving a jungle, but for the climate, it can be just as important.", "passage_zh": "當人們想到對抗氣候變遷的森林時，腦中浮現的通常是亞馬遜雨林或北方廣大的松樹林。很少人會想到一片泥濘的海岸，上面長著枝幹扭曲、泡在鹹水中的樹木。然而，生長在熱帶與亞熱帶海岸的紅樹林，卻是地球上最有效的天然儲碳場所之一。紅樹林與鹽沼、海草床一起儲存了科學家所說的「藍碳」：也就是由海洋與海岸生態系統所捕捉的碳。\n\n和所有植物一樣，紅樹林在生長時會從空氣中吸收二氧化碳。它們的特別之處在於地底下發生的事。紅樹林的根部位於浸滿水、幾乎不含氧氣的泥土中，所以落葉和死去的根分解得非常緩慢。這些碳大多不會回到大氣中，而是被埋藏起來，可以在土壤中封存好幾個世紀。因此，一公頃紅樹林所儲存的碳，最多可達一公頃熱帶雨林的四倍，而且大部分都藏在地表之下。\n\n紅樹林保護的不只是氣候，也保護人們。它們茂密、糾結的根能減緩海浪，降低暴潮造成的災害。根部之間的淺水區也是一座「育幼場」，幼小的魚、蟹和蝦可以躲在這裡避開掠食者，直到長大到能在開闊海域生存為止。對數百萬個沿海家庭來說，健康的紅樹林代表更安全的家園和更豐富的漁獲。\n\n儘管有這些好處，紅樹林數十年來仍不斷消失。大片紅樹林被砍除，用來興建養蝦場、港口和觀光度假村，還有一些則被砍來做木材和木炭。根據聯合國糧食及農業組織的資料，1980 年到 2005 年之間，全世界大約有五分之一的紅樹林消失了。更糟的是，破壞紅樹林不只是讓它無法再吸收碳而已。當泥土被挖開或變乾時，累積了好幾個世紀的碳可能會迅速釋放出來，使原本的儲碳場所變成溫室氣體的來源。\n\n臺灣提供了一個小小的例子，說明保護可以帶來什麼成果。淡水河口生長著一片紅樹林，樹種在當地被稱為「水筆仔」，名稱來自它細長、像筆一樣的胎生苗——這些幼苗會先掛在枝條上，之後才掉進泥灘裡。這個區域自 1986 年起成為自然保留區，如今遊客可以沿著木棧道漫步，在退潮時觀察招潮蟹揮舞著大得不成比例的螯。\n\n保護紅樹林永遠無法取代減少汽車和工廠排放的必要。不過，越來越多科學家主張，藍碳生態系統應該得到和雨林一樣多的關注。拯救一片泥濘的海岸線聽起來也許不像拯救一片叢林那麼令人興奮，但對氣候來說，它可能同樣重要。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain why mangroves are valuable and why they need protection", "To compare different ways of running a shrimp farm", "To teach readers how to plant a mangrove forest", "To argue that rainforests are no longer important for the climate"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文說明紅樹林的儲碳能力（第二段）、保護海岸與漁業的功能（第三段）、遭破壞的情況（第四段），並以淡水河口為保護的例子，末段呼籲重視藍碳生態系統，故目的為 (A)。(B) 養蝦場只是第四段提到破壞紅樹林的原因之一；(C) 文中未教人種植；(D) 末段說藍碳應與雨林 as much attention，並未說雨林不重要。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why can carbon stay in mangrove soil for a very long time?", "options": ["Mangrove trees live for thousands of years.", "Salt water slowly turns the carbon into rock.", "The waterlogged mud has little oxygen, so dead plant material breaks down slowly.", "Fish and crabs eat the fallen leaves before they can rot."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Their roots sit in waterlogged mud that contains very little oxygen, so fallen leaves and dead roots break down extremely slowly. 泥土缺氧使落葉和死根分解得很慢，碳因此被埋藏好幾個世紀，故選 (C)。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"nursery\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a hospital for injured sea animals", "a farm where shrimp are raised for sale", "a school for the children of fishing families", "a place where young creatures can grow up in safety"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說根部之間的淺水區 serves as a nursery, where young fish, crabs and shrimp can hide from predators until they are large enough to survive，可知 nursery 在此指幼小生物能安全成長的地方（育幼場），故選 (D)。(A) 文中沒有提到受傷的動物；(B) 養蝦場是第四段破壞紅樹林的原因，與此無關；(C) 是 nursery「托兒所」的字面聯想，不合上下文。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 4 about clearing mangroves to build shrimp farms?", "options": ["It is one of the best ways to reduce greenhouse gases.", "It can add carbon to the atmosphere instead of removing it.", "It stopped completely after 2005.", "It has made coastal homes safer from storms."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說紅樹林被砍除以興建養蝦場等，且 When the mud is dug up or dried out, carbon stored over centuries can escape quickly, turning a carbon store into a source of greenhouse gases，可推知開闢養蝦場反而可能把碳釋放到大氣中，故選 (B)。(A) 與此相反；(C) 文中只提到 1980 到 2005 年的損失，未說之後完全停止；(D) 第三段指出紅樹林能保護海岸，砍除反而會讓家園較不安全。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the mangroves at the mouth of the Tamsui River?", "options": ["They were planted by tourists in 1986.", "They have been protected as a nature reserve since 1986.", "Their name comes from the shape of their leaves.", "Visitors are not allowed to enter the area."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：The area has been a nature reserve since 1986，故選 (B)。(A) 1986 年是成為保留區的年份，並非遊客種植；(C) 「水筆仔」的名稱來自 pen-shaped seedlings（筆狀的幼苗），不是葉子；(D) 文中說遊客可以 stroll along a wooden boardwalk（沿著木棧道漫步）。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a104", "title": "Green Labels, Grey Truths: How to Spot Greenwashing", "title_zh": "綠色標籤，灰色真相：如何識破「漂綠」", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Business"], "passage": "Walk down the aisle of any supermarket and you will see the color green everywhere: leaves printed on shampoo bottles, the word \"natural\" on snack bags, and promises that a product is \"eco-friendly\" or \"kind to the planet.\" As more shoppers worry about climate change and plastic pollution, companies have discovered that appearing environmentally responsible is good for sales. Unfortunately, looking green and being green are not the same thing. When a company makes itself or its products seem better for the environment than they really are, the practice is called greenwashing.\n\nThe term was coined in the 1980s by an American environmentalist, Jay Westerveld. He noticed a card in a hotel room asking guests to reuse their towels to \"save the environment.\" He suspected that the hotel cared less about the planet than about cutting its laundry bills, especially as it was busy expanding at the time. Since then, the tricks have become far more sophisticated.\n\nOne common trick is vague language. Words such as \"green,\" \"natural\" and \"eco\" sound positive but have no fixed meaning, so they are almost impossible to check. Another is the hidden trade-off, in which a company highlights one small benefit while ignoring much bigger problems, such as a clothing brand that promotes one collection made from recycled bottles while producing millions of cheap garments. A third is the irrelevant claim. A spray can labeled \"CFC-free\" may sound responsible, but CFCs have been banned in most countries for decades, so every competing product is free of them too.\n\nGreenwashing causes harm in several ways. It misleads shoppers who are genuinely trying to make better choices, and it rewards companies for clever advertising rather than for reducing pollution. It can also make people cynical: if every product claims to be green, consumers may stop believing environmental claims altogether, including honest ones. Governments have started to act. The European Union, for example, has passed rules banning general claims such as \"environmentally friendly\" unless companies can prove them.\n\nShoppers can protect themselves by asking a few simple questions. Is the claim specific, such as \"made from 70 percent recycled plastic,\" or is it just a pleasant-sounding word? Is it supported by a trusted, independent certification, or by a logo the company designed itself? Does the claim cover the whole product, or only a small part of it, like the cap or the box?\n\nNone of this means that every green label is a lie. Many businesses are making real efforts to cut waste and emissions. The point is that the color of a package should never replace evidence. A truly responsible product rarely needs to shout about being natural; it simply shows you the facts.", "passage_zh": "走進任何一家超市的走道，你都會看到到處都是綠色：洗髮精瓶身上印著樹葉，零食包裝上寫著「天然」，還有各種標榜產品「環保」或「對地球友善」的承諾。隨著越來越多消費者擔心氣候變遷和塑膠汙染，企業發現，讓自己看起來對環境負責有助於銷售。不幸的是，「看起來很綠」和「真的很綠」並不是同一回事。當一家公司讓自己或自家產品顯得比實際上更有益於環境時，這種做法就叫做「漂綠」。\n\n這個詞是在 1980 年代由美國環保人士傑伊‧韋斯特維爾德所創。他注意到飯店房間裡的一張卡片，請房客重複使用毛巾以「拯救環境」。他懷疑這家飯店在乎的其實不是地球，而是想減少洗衣費用，尤其當時這家飯店正忙著擴建。從那時起，這類手法已經變得精巧許多。\n\n一種常見的手法是使用含糊的字眼。像「綠色」、「天然」和「環保」這些字聽起來很正面，卻沒有固定的定義，因此幾乎無法查證。另一種是「隱藏的取捨」，也就是公司強調一個小小的優點，卻忽略大得多的問題——例如某個服飾品牌大力宣傳一個用回收寶特瓶製成的系列，同時卻生產數百萬件廉價衣服。第三種則是「無關的聲明」。一罐標示「不含氟氯碳化物」的噴霧聽起來很負責任，但氟氯碳化物在大多數國家早已禁用數十年，所以每一項同類競爭產品其實也都不含它。\n\n漂綠會在好幾個方面造成傷害。它誤導了真心想做出更好選擇的消費者，也讓企業因為巧妙的廣告、而不是因為減少汙染而獲得回報。它還可能讓人變得憤世嫉俗：如果每樣產品都宣稱自己很環保，消費者可能乾脆不再相信任何環保聲明，連誠實的也一併懷疑。各國政府已經開始採取行動。舉例來說，歐盟已通過規定，禁止企業使用「對環境友善」這類籠統的說法，除非能證明其真實性。\n\n消費者可以透過問幾個簡單的問題來保護自己。這項聲明具體嗎？例如「以 70% 回收塑膠製成」，還是只是一個聽起來很舒服的字眼？它有沒有可信的獨立認證支持，還是只有公司自己設計的標誌？這項聲明涵蓋的是整個產品，還是只有其中一小部分，例如瓶蓋或外盒？\n\n這並不代表每一個綠色標籤都是謊言。許多企業確實在努力減少廢棄物和排放。重點在於，包裝的顏色永遠不該取代證據。一項真正負責任的產品很少需要大聲強調自己有多天然；它只會把事實攤在你眼前。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history of environmental laws in the European Union", "Why green packaging always attracts more customers", "What greenwashing is, why it is harmful, and how to recognize it", "How hotels can save money by washing fewer towels"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段定義漂綠，第二段說明名稱由來，第三段介紹常見手法，第四段說明其害處，第五段教消費者如何辨識，故主旨為 (C)。(A) 歐盟規定只是第四段的一個例子；(B) 文中說綠色外觀有助銷售，但重點是它可能誤導人，且並未說「總是」吸引顧客；(D) 毛巾只是第二段說明名稱由來的故事。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Jay Westerveld doubt the message on the hotel card?", "options": ["He thought the hotel mainly wanted to save money on laundry.", "The hotel did not provide any towels for its guests.", "The card was written in a language he could not read.", "The hotel charged guests extra for clean towels."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：He suspected that the hotel cared less about the planet than about cutting its laundry bills，他懷疑飯店真正在意的是減少洗衣費用，而且飯店當時正忙著擴建，故選 (A)。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"cynical\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["careless", "distrustful", "generous", "excited"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：It can also make people cynical: if every product claims to be green, consumers may stop believing environmental claims altogether. 冒號後說明消費者可能什麼環保聲明都不再相信，可知 cynical 指「憤世嫉俗、不信任的」，與 distrustful 最接近，故選 (B)。(A) 粗心的、(C) 慷慨的、(D) 興奮的，都不符合「不再相信」的文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Following the advice in paragraph 5, which label is MOST likely to be a trustworthy environmental claim?", "options": ["\"100% natural and green!\"", "\"Eco-friendly cap\"", "\"CFC-free, for a cleaner planet\"", "\"Made from 70% recycled plastic,\" with a certificate from an independent organization"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段建議檢查聲明是否具體、是否有獨立認證、是否涵蓋整個產品。(D) 有具體數字，又有獨立機構的認證，最符合條件，故選 (D)。(A) 只是含糊的字眼（第三段 vague language）；(B) 只涉及瓶蓋這一小部分；(C) 是第三段所說的「無關的聲明」，因為氟氯碳化物早已禁用。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why is a \"CFC-free\" label an example of an irrelevant claim?", "options": ["CFCs are actually good for the environment.", "CFCs were banned long ago, so similar products are free of them anyway.", "The label is too small for most shoppers to read.", "Only products made in Europe contain CFCs."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：CFCs have been banned in most countries for decades, so every competing product is free of them too. 氟氯碳化物早已禁用，同類產品本來就不含，標榜「不含」並沒有特別意義，故選 (B)。(A) 與事實相反；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a105", "title": "A Backup Copy for the World's Crops: The Svalbard Seed Vault", "title_zh": "為全世界的作物備份：斯瓦巴全球種子庫", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Science"], "passage": "Anyone who has lost an important computer file knows the value of a backup copy. Farmers and scientists face a similar risk on a much larger scale. Over thousands of years, people have developed an enormous variety of crops, from countless types of rice and wheat to beans and potatoes, each adapted to particular soils, climates and diseases. If these varieties disappear, the genetic information they carry is gone forever. To protect it, the world has built what is sometimes called the \"Doomsday Vault.\"\n\nThe Svalbard Global Seed Vault opened in 2008 on a remote Norwegian island in the Arctic Ocean, roughly halfway between mainland Norway and the North Pole. Its location was chosen with great care. The vault is dug deep into a sandstone mountain, high enough above sea level to stay dry even if polar ice melts. The surrounding permafrost, ground that stays frozen all year round, acts as a natural freezer, while cooling machines keep the storage rooms at about minus 18 degrees Celsius, a temperature at which many seeds can survive for decades or even centuries.\n\nAlthough the building belongs to the Norwegian government, the seeds inside do not. Instead, the vault works like a safe-deposit box at a bank. Gene banks around the world send duplicate samples of the seeds they already hold, and only the bank that deposited a box is allowed to take it out again. Today the vault holds well over a million samples from almost every country on Earth.\n\nFor years, many people assumed the vault would only be needed after a global catastrophe. Then, in 2015, the first withdrawal took place for a very human reason: war. A research center that had kept an important gene bank in Aleppo, Syria, could no longer work there safely because of the conflict. It requested its backup seeds, planted them in Lebanon and Morocco, harvested new seeds, and later sent fresh copies back to Svalbard. The system had worked exactly as intended.\n\nClimate change, one of the very threats the vault was designed to guard against, has also tested the vault itself. After an unusually warm and wet autumn in 2016, meltwater from thawing permafrost leaked into the entrance tunnel. The water never reached the seeds, but Norway spent millions of dollars making the entrance more waterproof.\n\nOf course, frozen seeds alone cannot feed the world. Crop varieties also need to be grown in real fields, where they continue to adapt to changing conditions. Still, as rising temperatures, new pests and extreme weather put pressure on agriculture, the quiet rooms beneath the Arctic snow offer humanity something valuable: a second chance.", "passage_zh": "任何曾經弄丟重要電腦檔案的人，都知道備份的價值。農民和科學家面臨著類似的風險，只是規模大得多。數千年來，人類培育出種類極多的作物，從數不清的稻米和小麥品種，到各種豆類和馬鈴薯，每一種都適應了特定的土壤、氣候和病害。如果這些品種消失，它們所攜帶的遺傳資訊就會永遠失去。為了保護這些資訊，世界建造了一座有時被稱為「末日種子庫」的設施。\n\n斯瓦巴全球種子庫於 2008 年啟用，位於北極海中一座偏遠的挪威島嶼上，大約在挪威本土和北極點的中間。它的地點經過審慎挑選。種子庫深深挖進一座砂岩山中，海拔高度足以讓它即使在極地冰層融化時也能保持乾燥。周圍的永凍土——也就是一整年都維持結凍的土地——就像一座天然冷凍庫，而冷卻機器則讓儲藏室保持在大約攝氏零下 18 度，在這個溫度下，許多種子可以存活數十年甚至數百年。\n\n雖然這棟建築屬於挪威政府，裡面的種子卻不是。這座種子庫的運作方式就像銀行的保險箱。世界各地的基因庫把自己已經保存的種子複製一份樣本寄來，而且只有存放箱子的那個基因庫才能把它取出來。如今，種子庫保存了遠超過一百萬份樣本，來自地球上幾乎每一個國家。\n\n多年來，許多人以為只有在全球性的大災難之後才會用到這座種子庫。然而，2015 年第一次提領種子，原因卻非常「人為」：戰爭。一個曾在敘利亞阿勒坡保存重要基因庫的研究中心，因為戰亂而無法再安全地在當地運作。它申請取回備份的種子，在黎巴嫩和摩洛哥種植、收成新的種子，之後再把新的複本送回斯瓦巴。這套系統完全按照原本的設計發揮了作用。\n\n氣候變遷正是這座種子庫原本要防範的威脅之一，而它也考驗了種子庫本身。2016 年一個異常溫暖又潮濕的秋天過後，永凍土融化的水滲進了入口隧道。融水從未碰到種子，但挪威仍花了數百萬美元，讓入口更加防水。\n\n當然，光靠冷凍的種子無法養活全世界。作物品種也需要在真正的田地裡種植，才能持續適應不斷變化的環境。儘管如此，隨著氣溫上升、新的病蟲害和極端天氣對農業造成壓力，北極冰雪下那些安靜的儲藏室為人類提供了一樣寶貴的東西：第二次機會。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer mention losing an important computer file in paragraph 1?", "options": ["To compare the seed vault to a backup that protects something irreplaceable", "To show that seeds can now be stored on computers", "To warn readers about the danger of computer viruses", "To explain how scientists record genetic information"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段先說弄丟電腦檔案的人都知道備份的價值，接著說作物品種一旦消失，遺傳資訊就 gone forever，再引出種子庫，可見作者是以「備份檔案」比喻種子庫保護無法取代的作物資源，故選 (A)。(B)(C)(D) 都誤解了這個比喻，文中並未提到用電腦儲存種子、電腦病毒或記錄遺傳資訊的方法。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason the vault's location was chosen?", "options": ["It is high enough above sea level to stay dry.", "The permafrost around it keeps it naturally cold.", "It is dug deep inside a sandstone mountain.", "It is close to the world's major farming areas."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段提到種子庫 dug deep into a sandstone mountain (C)、high enough above sea level to stay dry (A)，以及周圍的 permafrost acts as a natural freezer (B)。文中說它位於偏遠的北極島嶼，並未說靠近主要農業區，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 3, the vault is compared to a \"safe-deposit box\" at a bank because ______.", "options": ["people must pay a high fee every time they use it", "it keeps other people's property safe, and only the owner can take it out", "it is located inside a bank in Norway", "the seeds inside are sold to whoever offers the most money"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：建築屬於挪威政府，但種子不屬於挪威；各地基因庫寄存種子，而且 only the bank that deposited a box is allowed to take it out again，就像銀行保險箱只有存放者能取出自己的東西，故選 (B)。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，(D) 也與「只有存放者能取出」相矛盾。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What happened after the first withdrawal of seeds in 2015?", "options": ["The seeds were destroyed because of the war in Syria.", "The vault stopped accepting seeds from other gene banks.", "The seeds were grown in Lebanon and Morocco, and new copies were sent back to Svalbard.", "Norway took ownership of the seeds from Syria."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：研究中心取回備份種子後 planted them in Lebanon and Morocco, harvested new seeds, and later sent fresh copies back to Svalbard，故選 (C)。(A) 種子並未被毀，反而成功種植；(B) 文中未提及；(D) 與第三段「種子不屬於挪威」相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the last paragraph?", "options": ["The vault has made growing crops in fields unnecessary.", "The writer sees the vault as valuable but not a complete solution.", "Seeds stored in the vault can adapt to climate change on their own.", "The writer thinks the vault should be moved to a warmer place."], "answer": 1, "explain": "末段先說 frozen seeds alone cannot feed the world，作物仍須在田裡種植以持續適應環境，接著用 Still 轉折，稱種子庫給人類 a second chance，可推論作者認為種子庫很寶貴，但並非萬全的解決辦法，故選 (B)。(A) 與「仍需在田地種植」相反；(C) 文中說的是在田裡生長的作物才能持續適應，不是冷凍的種子；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a106", "title": "The Hole That Is Healing: Lessons from the Ozone Layer", "title_zh": "正在癒合的破洞：臭氧層給我們的啟示", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "History"], "passage": "High above our heads, mostly between 15 and 35 kilometers up, lies a thin layer of gas that makes life on land possible. The ozone layer absorbs most of the sun's harmful ultraviolet (UV) radiation, which can cause skin cancer and eye damage in humans and harm crops and tiny ocean plankton. For most of history, nobody knew this invisible shield existed, let alone that human activity could damage it.\n\nIn 1974, two chemists, Mario Molina and Sherwood Rowland, published a warning. They argued that chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs, chemicals widely used in refrigerators, air conditioners and spray cans, could drift into the upper atmosphere, where sunlight would break them apart and release chlorine. Each chlorine atom could then destroy ozone molecules again and again, and later estimates suggested that one atom could destroy as many as 100,000. Many manufacturers dismissed the idea as alarmist, since CFCs were cheap, stable and seemed perfectly harmless.\n\nThe proof arrived in dramatic form. In 1985, scientists from the British Antarctic Survey reported that ozone levels over Antarctica had been dropping sharply every spring. Satellite images soon revealed a vast area of thinning ozone, which the media nicknamed the \"ozone hole.\" The public was shocked, and suddenly the issue could no longer be ignored.\n\nWhat happened next is often described as the most successful environmental agreement in history. In 1987, countries signed the Montreal Protocol, agreeing to reduce and eventually end the production of CFCs and other ozone-destroying chemicals. The agreement was strengthened several times as the science became clearer, and in 2009 it became the first treaty in United Nations history to be ratified by every member country. Industries that had warned that replacing CFCs would be extremely costly developed substitutes faster than expected. In 1995, Molina, Rowland and the Dutch scientist Paul Crutzen shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for their work.\n\nThe results are now visible. A 2023 assessment backed by the United Nations concluded that the ozone layer is on track to recover to its 1980 levels over most of the world by around 2040, and over Antarctica by around 2066. Because many CFCs are also powerful greenhouse gases, the treaty has also helped to slow global warming.\n\nWhy did the world succeed with ozone when progress on climate change has been so much slower? Part of the answer is that only a limited number of chemicals and industries were involved, and replacements were available. Climate change, by contrast, is linked to almost every part of modern life. Even so, the ozone story offers a hopeful lesson: when the science is clear, the public is concerned and governments act together, even a global environmental problem can be turned around.", "passage_zh": "在我們頭頂上方，大多位於 15 到 35 公里高的地方，有一層薄薄的氣體，讓陸地上的生命得以存在。臭氧層吸收了太陽大部分有害的紫外線，這些紫外線會使人罹患皮膚癌、傷害眼睛，也會損害農作物和海洋中微小的浮游生物。在人類歷史的大部分時間裡，沒有人知道這面看不見的盾牌存在，更別說知道人類活動可能會破壞它。\n\n1974 年，兩位化學家馬里奧‧莫利納和舍伍德‧羅蘭發表了一項警告。他們主張，氟氯碳化物（CFCs）——一種廣泛用於冰箱、冷氣機和噴霧罐的化學物質——可能會飄到高層大氣，在那裡被陽光分解並釋放出氯。每一個氯原子接著就能一再地破壞臭氧分子，後來的估計顯示，一個氯原子就可能破壞多達十萬個臭氧分子。許多製造商認為這種說法是危言聳聽而不予理會，因為氟氯碳化物便宜、穩定，而且看起來完全無害。\n\n證據以戲劇性的方式出現了。1985 年，英國南極調查局的科學家發表報告，指出南極上空的臭氧濃度每年春天都急劇下降。衛星影像很快就顯示出一大片臭氧變稀薄的區域，媒體稱之為「臭氧洞」。大眾大為震驚，這個問題突然再也無法被忽視。\n\n接下來發生的事，常被形容為史上最成功的環境協定。1987 年，各國簽署了《蒙特婁議定書》，同意減少並最終停止生產氟氯碳化物和其他會破壞臭氧的化學物質。隨著科學證據越來越明確，這項協定經過了數次強化；到了 2009 年，它成為聯合國歷史上第一個獲得所有會員國批准的條約。那些曾警告取代氟氯碳化物將非常昂貴的產業，研發替代品的速度比預期快得多。1995 年，莫利納、羅蘭與荷蘭科學家保羅‧克魯岑因為這方面的研究，共同獲得諾貝爾化學獎。\n\n如今成果已經清楚可見。一份由聯合國支持的 2023 年評估報告指出，世界大部分地區上空的臭氧層可望在 2040 年左右恢復到 1980 年的水準，南極上空則約在 2066 年恢復。由於許多氟氯碳化物也是強效的溫室氣體，這項條約也連帶幫助減緩了全球暖化。\n\n為什麼世界在臭氧問題上能成功，在氣候變遷上的進展卻慢了這麼多？部分原因是，臭氧問題只牽涉到數量有限的化學物質和產業，而且已經有替代品可用。相較之下，氣候變遷幾乎與現代生活的每個層面都有關。即使如此，臭氧的故事仍然帶給我們充滿希望的啟示：當科學證據明確、大眾關心，而且各國政府共同行動時，即使是全球性的環境問題也能被扭轉。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["CFCs are still widely used in refrigerators around the world.", "The ozone layer will never recover from the damage caused by humans.", "Protecting the ozone layer shows that global cooperation can solve an environmental problem.", "Climate change is a smaller problem than the ozone hole."], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文依序說明臭氧層的功能、氟氯碳化物的危害、臭氧洞的發現、《蒙特婁議定書》的成功與臭氧層的恢復，末段總結 when the science is clear, the public is concerned and governments act together, even a global environmental problem can be turned around，故選 (C)。(A) 議定書已逐步停止生產氟氯碳化物；(B) 與第五段「可望恢復」相反；(D) 末段反而說氣候變遷牽涉更廣、更難解決。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how do CFCs damage the ozone layer?", "options": ["They block sunlight from reaching the upper atmosphere.", "Sunlight breaks them apart, releasing chlorine that destroys ozone.", "They turn ozone into ultraviolet radiation.", "They kill the plankton that produce ozone."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：CFCs 飄到高層大氣後，sunlight would break them apart and release chlorine，而每個氯原子會一再破壞臭氧分子，故選 (B)。(A)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 第一段只說紫外線會傷害浮游生物，並未說浮游生物製造臭氧。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"dismissed\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["rejected as not worth considering", "explained in great detail", "supported with new evidence", "kept secret from the public"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Many manufacturers dismissed the idea as alarmist, since CFCs were cheap, stable and seemed perfectly harmless. 製造商認為這種說法危言聳聽，又覺得氟氯碳化物無害，可知 dismissed 指「不予理會、駁回」，故選 (A)。(B)(C) 與製造商的態度相反；(D) 這項警告已公開發表，並非被保密。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the 2023 assessment, when is the ozone layer over Antarctica expected to return to its 1980 levels?", "options": ["Around 2009", "Around 2023", "Around 2040", "Around 2066"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：the ozone layer is on track to recover to its 1980 levels over most of the world by around 2040, and over Antarctica by around 2066，南極上空約在 2066 年恢復，故選 (D)。(C) 2040 年是世界大部分地區的恢復時間；(A) 2009 年是條約獲所有會員國批准的年份；(B) 2023 年是評估報告發表的年份。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the last paragraph, why was the ozone problem easier to solve than climate change?", "options": ["Scientists understood climate change much better than ozone.", "It involved fewer chemicals and industries, and substitutes were available.", "Only rich countries were affected by the ozone hole.", "The public has never been interested in climate change."], "answer": 1, "explain": "末段：only a limited number of chemicals and industries were involved, and replacements were available，而氣候變遷則幾乎與現代生活的每個層面相關，故選 (B)。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，(A) 也與作者的推論方向不符。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a107", "title": "Cities That Drink the Rain", "title_zh": "會「喝」雨水的城市", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Society"], "passage": "For most of the twentieth century, engineers treated rainwater in cities as a waste product. The goal was to get rid of it as quickly as possible: rain fell on roofs and roads, ran into drains, and rushed through concrete channels into the nearest river or sea. This worked well enough for ordinary storms. But as climate change brings heavier and more sudden downpours, many cities are discovering that their pipes simply cannot carry water away fast enough.\n\nThe problem is partly caused by the city itself. In a forest or a field, much of the rain soaks into the soil, where it is stored and slowly released. In a modern city, however, most surfaces, such as roofs, roads and parking lots, are impermeable, meaning that water cannot pass through them. During a heavy storm, nearly every drop runs off at once, and drains designed decades ago overflow. Streets turn into rivers and basements fill with water.\n\nA different idea, often called the \"sponge city,\" turns the traditional approach upside down. Instead of fighting rainwater, a sponge city tries to absorb, store and reuse it, much as a natural landscape does. The term became widely known after China launched a national sponge city program in 2015, but similar ideas have shaped cities from Rotterdam to Copenhagen.\n\nThe tools of a sponge city are surprisingly simple. Permeable pavements let rain sink into the ground below. Green roofs, covered with soil and plants, hold water and release it gradually. Rain gardens, which are shallow planted areas beside roads, collect runoff from the street. Even public spaces can be designed to flood on purpose. In Rotterdam, for instance, a \"water square\" serves as a basketball court and meeting place on dry days but fills up like a pond during a storm, holding the water until the drains can cope.\n\nCopenhagen offers a striking example of why such ideas matter. In July 2011, a cloudburst dropped more rain on the city in a couple of hours than it normally receives in two months, causing damage worth hundreds of millions of euros. In response, the city drew up a plan to redesign streets so that they guide floodwater toward parks and the harbor.\n\nSponge cities are not a perfect solution. They need space, careful maintenance and cooperation between planners and residents, and no design can absorb every extreme storm. Yet they offer more than flood protection. Green areas that store water also cool the air, provide homes for birds and insects, and give city dwellers pleasant places to relax. In a warming world, learning to work with the rain rather than against it may be one of the smartest choices a city can make.", "passage_zh": "在二十世紀的大部分時間裡，工程師把城市中的雨水當成一種廢棄物。目標是盡快把它排掉：雨水落在屋頂和道路上，流進排水溝，再沿著水泥渠道奔流到最近的河川或大海。這種做法對一般的暴雨還算有效。但隨著氣候變遷帶來更猛烈、更突然的豪雨，許多城市發現，它們的管線根本來不及把水排走。\n\n這個問題有一部分是城市本身造成的。在森林或田野中，大部分的雨水會滲入土壤，在那裡被儲存起來，再慢慢釋放。然而在現代城市裡，大多數的表面，例如屋頂、道路和停車場，都是不透水的，也就是說水無法穿過它們。下大雨時，幾乎每一滴雨都會同時流走，而數十年前設計的排水溝就會滿溢。街道變成河流，地下室也跟著淹水。\n\n一種常被稱為「海綿城市」的不同理念，把傳統做法完全顛倒過來。海綿城市不和雨水對抗，而是像自然地景一樣，嘗試吸收、儲存並再利用雨水。這個名詞在中國於 2015 年推出全國性的海綿城市計畫後廣為人知，但從鹿特丹到哥本哈根，類似的理念也早已影響了許多城市的樣貌。\n\n海綿城市的工具出奇地簡單。透水鋪面讓雨水能滲入下方的地面。覆蓋著土壤和植物的綠屋頂可以留住雨水，再慢慢釋放。雨水花園——也就是道路旁略為低窪、種有植物的區域——能收集從街道流下來的逕流。就連公共空間也可以刻意設計成會淹水的地方。例如在鹿特丹，一座「水廣場」在晴天時是籃球場和聚會場所，暴雨時則會像池塘一樣蓄滿水，把水留住，直到排水系統能夠應付為止。\n\n哥本哈根提供了一個引人注目的例子，說明這些理念為什麼重要。2011 年 7 月，一場暴雨在短短一兩個小時內降在這座城市的雨量，比它平常兩個月的雨量還多，造成高達數億歐元的損失。為此，市政府擬定了一項計畫，重新設計街道，讓街道把洪水導向公園和港口。\n\n海綿城市並不是完美的解決辦法。它們需要空間、細心的維護，以及規劃者和居民之間的合作，而且沒有任何設計能吸收每一場極端暴雨。然而，它們提供的不只是防洪。能儲水的綠地也能讓空氣降溫、為鳥類和昆蟲提供棲地，並讓城市居民有舒適的地方可以放鬆。在一個逐漸暖化的世界裡，學會與雨水合作而非對抗，可能是一座城市所能做出最聰明的選擇之一。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Old drainage pipes should simply be replaced with bigger ones.", "Cities can reduce flooding by absorbing and storing rainwater instead of only draining it away.", "Rotterdam and Copenhagen have the best public sports facilities in Europe.", "Climate change has made heavy rain less common in cities."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一、二段說明傳統排水方式在豪雨下不敷使用，第三段提出「海綿城市」——吸收、儲存並再利用雨水，第四、五段舉例說明做法，末段評估其優缺點，故主旨為 (B)。(A) 文中並未主張只換更大的管線，而是改變思維；(C) 籃球場只是水廣場的例子；(D) 與第一段 heavier and more sudden downpours 相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"impermeable\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["easy to clean", "very old and damaged", "covered with plants", "not letting water pass through"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：most surfaces... are impermeable, meaning that water cannot pass through them，句中 meaning that 直接解釋了這個字：「水無法穿過」，故選 (D)。(C) 覆蓋植物的是第四段的綠屋頂，正好相反；(A)(B) 與文意無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What is special about the \"water square\" in Rotterdam?", "options": ["It is used for sports on dry days and stores water during storms.", "It pumps rainwater directly into the sea.", "It is open to the public only in winter.", "It was built to provide drinking water for the city."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：a \"water square\" serves as a basketball court and meeting place on dry days but fills up like a pond during a storm，晴天是籃球場，暴雨時則蓄水，故選 (A)。(B) 文中說它把水留住直到排水系統能應付，並非直接抽進海裡；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention the 2011 cloudburst in Copenhagen?", "options": ["To show that sponge cities cannot work in northern Europe", "To prove that cities no longer need drains", "To explain why the city began to plan for extreme rain in a new way", "To compare the weather in Copenhagen with the weather in China"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段開頭說哥本哈根是 a striking example of why such ideas matter，接著描述 2011 年暴雨造成嚴重損失，In response 市政府擬定計畫，重新設計街道把洪水導向公園和港口，可見作者提這場暴雨是為了說明該市為何開始用新方式因應極端降雨，故選 (C)。(A) 與文意相反；(B) 第四段提到仍需等排水系統 cope，並未說不需要排水溝；(D) 文中未比較兩地天氣。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's view of sponge cities in the last paragraph?", "options": ["They are useless because they take up too much space.", "They can completely prevent every kind of flood.", "They have limits, but their benefits make them worth pursuing.", "They should only be built in large Asian cities."], "answer": 2, "explain": "末段先承認海綿城市 not a perfect solution（需要空間、維護與合作，也無法吸收每一場極端暴雨），再用 Yet 轉折，指出它們還能降溫、提供棲地與休憩空間，並稱之為 one of the smartest choices，可見作者認為它有限制但值得推動，故選 (C)。(A) 作者並未說它沒用；(B) 與 no design can absorb every extreme storm 相反；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a108", "title": "Fix It, Don't Bin It: The Fight for the Right to Repair", "title_zh": "修好它，別丟掉：爭取「維修權」的行動", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Technology"], "passage": "When a smartphone screen cracks or the battery no longer lasts a full day, many owners face a frustrating choice. An official repair can cost almost as much as a new phone, and independent repair shops may be unable to get the right parts. Faced with these obstacles, millions of people simply buy a new phone, and the old one ends up in a drawer or a rubbish bin.\n\nMultiplied across the world, these small decisions create an enormous environmental problem. According to a United Nations report, the world produced about 62 million tonnes of electronic waste in 2022, and only around a fifth of it was documented as properly collected and recycled. Discarded electronics contain toxic substances such as lead and mercury, which can leak into soil and water. They also contain valuable metals like gold and copper, which are wasted when devices are dumped or burned. Moreover, most of a smartphone's carbon footprint comes from manufacturing it, not using it, so keeping a phone working for longer is one of the simplest ways to reduce its impact.\n\nThis is why a growing movement is demanding the \"right to repair.\" Its supporters argue that people who buy a product should be able to fix it themselves or take it to any repair shop they choose. To make that possible, manufacturers would need to sell spare parts at reasonable prices, publish repair manuals, and stop using software locks that prevent replacement parts from working unless the company itself installs them.\n\nSome governments have begun to listen. In 2021, France began requiring products such as smartphones and laptops to display a \"repairability index,\" a score out of 10 showing how easy they are to fix. In 2024, the European Union adopted a law requiring manufacturers to offer repairs for certain products, such as washing machines and smartphones, even after the warranty has expired.\n\nManufacturers have often resisted these changes. They argue that opening devices can be dangerous, that poorly trained repairers may damage products, and that sharing detailed designs could reveal trade secrets. Some of these concerns are reasonable; lithium batteries, for example, can catch fire if handled carelessly. Critics point out, however, that companies also earn more when customers replace devices instead of repairing them.\n\nUltimately, the debate is about our relationship with the things we own. For decades, consumers have been encouraged to treat electronics as disposable and to upgrade every year or two. The right-to-repair movement promotes a different attitude, one in which a cracked screen is a small problem to be solved rather than an excuse to start again. If it succeeds, the greenest phone may turn out to be the one already in your pocket.", "passage_zh": "當智慧型手機的螢幕摔裂，或電池撐不到一整天時，許多手機主人都會面臨一個令人沮喪的選擇。官方維修的費用可能幾乎和買一支新手機一樣貴，而獨立維修店又可能拿不到合適的零件。面對這些阻礙，數百萬人乾脆直接買新手機，舊手機則被丟進抽屜或垃圾桶。\n\n這些小小的決定放大到全世界，就形成了一個巨大的環境問題。根據聯合國的一份報告，2022 年全世界產生了大約 6,200 萬公噸的電子廢棄物，其中只有大約五分之一有紀錄顯示經過妥善的回收處理。被丟棄的電子產品含有鉛和汞等有毒物質，可能滲入土壤和水中。它們也含有金和銅等珍貴金屬，當這些裝置被傾倒或焚燒時，這些金屬就浪費掉了。此外，一支智慧型手機的碳足跡大部分來自製造過程，而不是使用過程，所以讓手機用得更久，是減少其影響最簡單的方法之一。\n\n這就是為什麼有越來越多人投入一項運動，要求「維修權」。支持者主張，人們買了一項產品，就應該能夠自己修理，或拿去任何自己選擇的維修店修理。要做到這一點，製造商必須以合理的價格販售備用零件、公開維修手冊，並停止使用軟體鎖——這種鎖會讓更換的零件除非由原廠安裝，否則無法正常運作。\n\n有些政府已經開始聽取這些意見。2021 年，法國開始要求智慧型手機和筆記型電腦等產品必須標示「可維修指數」，以滿分 10 分的分數顯示產品有多容易修理。2024 年，歐盟通過一項法律，要求製造商即使在保固期結束後，也必須為洗衣機、智慧型手機等特定產品提供維修服務。\n\n製造商常常抗拒這些改變。他們主張，拆開裝置可能很危險，訓練不足的維修人員可能會弄壞產品，而且公開詳細的設計可能會洩漏商業機密。其中有些顧慮是合理的；例如鋰電池如果處理不慎，就可能起火。不過，批評者指出，當顧客換新而不是修理裝置時，企業也能賺到更多錢。\n\n說到底，這場爭論關乎我們與自己所擁有的物品之間的關係。數十年來，消費者一直被鼓勵把電子產品當作用過即丟的東西，每一兩年就換新一次。維修權運動提倡一種不同的態度：螢幕裂了只是一個有待解決的小問題，而不是重新買一支的藉口。如果這項運動成功，最環保的手機，可能就是已經在你口袋裡的那一支。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To advertise a new type of long-lasting smartphone battery", "To explain the right-to-repair movement and the debate around it", "To teach readers how to replace a cracked phone screen", "To argue that people should stop using electronic devices"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先說明手機難以維修的情況與電子廢棄物問題，再介紹維修權運動的訴求（第三段）、各國的立法（第四段）、製造商的反對與批評者的回應（第五段），末段總結，故目的為 (B)。(A)(C) 文中沒有推銷電池或教人換螢幕；(D) 作者主張的是讓裝置用得更久，不是停止使用電子產品。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the UN report, about how much of the electronic waste produced in 2022 was documented as properly recycled?", "options": ["About one fifth", "About half", "About 62 percent", "Almost all of it"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：the world produced about 62 million tonnes of electronic waste in 2022, and only around a fifth of it was documented as properly collected and recycled，故選 (A)。(C) 62 是電子廢棄物的總量（6,200 萬公噸），不是百分比；(B)(D) 與 only around a fifth 不符。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT something the right-to-repair movement asks manufacturers to do?", "options": ["Sell spare parts at reasonable prices", "Publish repair manuals", "Stop using software locks on replacement parts", "Give customers a free new phone every two years"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段列出運動的訴求：sell spare parts at reasonable prices (A)、publish repair manuals (B)、stop using software locks (C)。文中從未要求每兩年免費換新手機，這反而與「讓手機用得更久」的主張相反，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"disposable\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["expensive and valuable", "difficult to use", "meant to be thrown away after a short time", "easy to repair at home"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第六段：consumers have been encouraged to treat electronics as disposable and to upgrade every year or two，每一兩年就換新，可知 disposable 指「用過即丟的」，故選 (C)。(D) 與文意相反，正是維修權運動想改變的態度；(A)(B) 與上下文無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by saying \"the greenest phone may turn out to be the one already in your pocket\"?", "options": ["Green-colored phones are better for the environment.", "Phones should be kept in a pocket to protect them from damage.", "Using your current phone for longer is often better for the environment than buying a new one.", "New phones are now made entirely from recycled materials."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段指出手機的碳足跡大多來自製造過程，因此 keeping a phone working for longer 是減少影響最簡單的方法；末段這句話的意思是：繼續使用（並修理）手上的手機，往往比買新手機更環保，故選 (C)。(A) 誤把 greenest（最環保的）當成顏色；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a109", "title": "Too Hot for Boys? Climate Change and the Future of Sea Turtles", "title_zh": "熱到生不出公的？氣候變遷與海龜的未來", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Nature"], "passage": "For humans and most other mammals, whether a baby is male or female is decided by its genes at the moment of conception. Sea turtles follow a very different rule. For them, sex is determined by temperature, specifically the temperature of the sand surrounding the eggs during the middle stage of their development. This unusual system has served sea turtles well for more than a hundred million years, but a warming climate is now putting it under pressure.\n\nThe rule itself is surprisingly simple. Eggs that develop in cooler sand tend to hatch as males, while those in warmer sand tend to become females. For many species, the \"pivotal temperature,\" at which roughly equal numbers of males and females are produced, is around 29 degrees Celsius. Just a degree or two above that, a nest may produce almost nothing but female hatchlings.\n\nIn 2018, researchers studying green turtles on Australia's Great Barrier Reef published a striking result. Among young turtles that had hatched on the warmer beaches of the northern reef, more than 99 percent were female. Older adults from the same population were also mostly female, but far less extremely so, which suggests that the imbalance has grown as temperatures have risen over recent decades. Similar trends have been reported on nesting beaches in other parts of the world.\n\nAt first, a population with more females might not seem like a crisis, since one male can mate with several females. In the short term, it may even increase the number of eggs laid. The danger lies further ahead. If males become too rare, many eggs may never be fertilized. At even higher temperatures, a more serious problem appears: the embryos may not survive at all, so that no turtles of either sex hatch.\n\nConservationists are experimenting with ways to cool things down. On some beaches, volunteers place shade cloth over nests or gently sprinkle them with water to lower the temperature of the sand. Elsewhere, eggs are moved to shaded hatcheries where conditions can be controlled. Taiwan has a part to play as well. Green turtles still come ashore to lay eggs on a few beaches here, most famously on Wang-an Island in Penghu, where nesting sites are protected during the summer breeding season.\n\nThese measures can buy time, but they treat the symptoms rather than the cause. In the past, sea turtles survived major climate shifts, partly by changing when and where they nested. The question is whether they can adapt quickly enough this time, when temperatures are rising unusually fast. Their future, in other words, may depend less on what happens on the beach than on what humans decide to do about greenhouse gas emissions.", "passage_zh": "對人類和大多數其他哺乳動物來說，寶寶是公是母，在受孕的那一刻就由基因決定了。海龜卻遵循一套非常不同的規則。對牠們而言，性別是由溫度決定的——確切地說，是蛋在發育中期時周圍沙子的溫度。這套不尋常的機制讓海龜順利生存了超過一億年，但如今氣候暖化正讓它承受壓力。\n\n這套規則本身出奇地簡單。在較涼的沙中發育的蛋，往往會孵出公海龜；在較溫暖的沙中發育的蛋，則往往會變成母海龜。對許多海龜物種來說，能孵出大約相同數量公、母海龜的「關鍵溫度」約為攝氏 29 度。只要比這個溫度高出一兩度，一窩蛋孵出的可能幾乎全是母海龜。\n\n2018 年，研究人員在澳洲大堡礁研究綠蠵龜，發表了一項驚人的結果。在大堡礁北部較溫暖的海灘上孵化的年輕海龜中，超過 99% 是母的。同一族群中年紀較大的成龜也以母龜居多，但比例遠沒有那麼極端，這顯示隨著近幾十年來氣溫上升，性別失衡的情況愈來愈嚴重。世界其他地區的產卵海灘也有類似趨勢的報告。\n\n乍看之下，一個母龜較多的族群似乎不算危機，因為一隻公龜可以和好幾隻母龜交配。短期內，這甚至可能增加產下的蛋的數量。危險在於更遙遠的未來。如果公龜變得太稀少，許多蛋可能永遠無法受精。而在更高的溫度下，會出現更嚴重的問題：胚胎可能根本無法存活，結果公龜和母龜都孵不出來。\n\n保育人士正在嘗試各種降溫的方法。在一些海灘上，志工會在巢上方架設遮陽網，或輕輕地灑水，以降低沙子的溫度。在其他地方，蛋會被移到有遮蔭、能控制環境條件的孵化場。臺灣也能盡一份心力。綠蠵龜仍會上岸到臺灣的幾處海灘產卵，其中最有名的是澎湖的望安島，那裡的產卵地在夏季繁殖期間會受到保護。\n\n這些措施可以爭取時間，但它們治標不治本。過去，海龜曾經熬過重大的氣候變化，部分原因是牠們改變了築巢的時間和地點。問題在於，這一次在氣溫上升得異常快速的情況下，牠們能否適應得夠快。換句話說，牠們的未來取決於人類決定如何處理溫室氣體排放，可能更甚於海灘上發生的事。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How sea turtles find their way back to the beaches where they were born", "How rising temperatures threaten the balance of male and female sea turtles", "Why green turtles prefer to lay their eggs in Taiwan", "How scientists count the eggs in a sea turtle nest"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一、二段說明海龜的性別由沙子溫度決定，第三段以大堡礁研究說明母龜比例大增，第四段說明其危險，第五、六段談保育措施與根本原因，故主旨為 (B)。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 臺灣望安島只是第五段的一個例子，文中沒說綠蠵龜偏好在臺灣產卵。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what usually happens to eggs that develop in warmer sand?", "options": ["They hatch more quickly as males.", "They produce larger and stronger turtles.", "They tend to hatch as females.", "They produce equal numbers of males and females."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Eggs that develop in cooler sand tend to hatch as males, while those in warmer sand tend to become females. 較溫暖的沙中孵出的多半是母海龜，故選 (C)。(A) 與文意相反，較涼的沙才孵出公海龜；(D) 公母數量大致相等是在約攝氏 29 度的「關鍵溫度」時；(B) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the 2018 study on the Great Barrier Reef suggest?", "options": ["The share of female turtles has increased as the beaches have warmed.", "Most of the older adult turtles in the population were male.", "Turtles on cooler beaches had stopped nesting altogether.", "Green turtles can no longer be found on the Great Barrier Reef."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：北部較暖海灘孵出的年輕海龜超過 99% 是母的，而年紀較大的成龜雖也以母龜居多，但 far less extremely so，這 suggests that the imbalance has grown as temperatures have risen，故選 (A)。(B) 文中說成龜也是 mostly female；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"buy time\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["spend a lot of money", "waste time and effort", "solve a problem forever", "delay a problem for a while"], "answer": 3, "explain": "末段：These measures can buy time, but they treat the symptoms rather than the cause. 遮陽、灑水等措施只能治標、不能治本，所以 buy time 指「爭取時間、暫時延緩問題」，故選 (D)。(C) 與「治標不治本」相反；(A) 是 buy 的字面意思，不合文意；(B) 作者並未認為這些措施是白費力氣。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why could a population with too few males eventually decline?", "options": ["The females would stop coming ashore to lay eggs.", "Many eggs might never be fertilized.", "The males would eat many of the eggs.", "The sand on the beaches would become too cool."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：If males become too rare, many eggs may never be fertilized. 公龜太少時，許多蛋可能無法受精，族群就可能衰退，故選 (B)。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 與全文「沙子愈來愈熱」的情況相反。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a110", "title": "Waiting for the Second Marshmallow: What the Famous Test Really Shows", "title_zh": "等待第二顆棉花糖：著名實驗真正告訴我們的事", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Education"], "passage": "In the late 1960s, the psychologist Walter Mischel and his team at Stanford University invited young children from the university's nursery school into a small room and placed a treat, such as a marshmallow, in front of them. The deal was simple: a child could eat the treat right away, or wait alone until the researcher returned, which could take up to twenty minutes, and then receive two. Some children covered their eyes, turned their chairs around or sang quietly to themselves, while others gave in within seconds.\n\nThe experiment became famous because of what happened years later. When Mischel followed up with some of the original participants as teenagers, he found that those who had waited longer tended to have higher SAT scores, cope better with stress and be described by their parents as more focused. The message seemed clear: self-control at the age of four could predict success later in life. Countless books and teachers repeated the idea, and \"Don't eat the marshmallow!\" became a popular motto for discipline.\n\nHowever, more recent research has complicated this neat story. In 2012, researchers at the University of Rochester added a twist. Before the marshmallow test, one group of children was promised better art supplies, and the adult kept the promise; another group was let down when the adult returned empty-handed. Children who had learned that the adult was reliable waited, on average, about four times longer than those who had been disappointed. For a child who has learned that promises are often broken, eating the treat immediately is not a lack of willpower but a reasonable choice.\n\nThen, in 2018, a larger study tested the original claim with more than 900 children from a much wider range of backgrounds. It did find a link between waiting and later school achievement, but the link was only about half as strong as earlier studies had suggested, and it became much smaller once the researchers took into account factors such as family income and the home environment. In other words, a child's ability to wait may partly reflect his or her circumstances rather than a fixed personality trait.\n\nNone of this means that self-control is unimportant. Rather, the newer findings suggest that it can be shaped by experience and environment, which is encouraging news. Mischel himself argued that the children who succeeded relied on strategies, such as distracting themselves, instead of pure willpower, and strategies can be taught. For students fighting distractions at their desks, the lesson may be not to stare at the marshmallow, but to put it somewhere out of sight.", "passage_zh": "1960 年代後期，心理學家沃爾特‧米歇爾和他在史丹佛大學的團隊，邀請該校附設幼兒園的孩子進入一間小房間，並在他們面前放一樣點心，例如一顆棉花糖。規則很簡單：孩子可以馬上吃掉點心，也可以獨自等待，直到研究人員回來——最久可能要等上二十分鐘——然後就能得到兩份。有些孩子遮住眼睛、把椅子轉過去，或小聲地對自己唱歌，另一些孩子則在幾秒鐘內就忍不住了。\n\n這個實驗之所以出名，是因為多年後發生的事。米歇爾追蹤部分當年的受試者到他們青少年時期，發現當初等得比較久的孩子，SAT 成績往往比較高、比較能應付壓力，父母也說他們比較專注。這個訊息似乎很明確：四歲時的自制力可以預測日後的成功。無數書籍和老師一再重複這個觀念，「別吃棉花糖！」也成了一句常用來勉勵自律的口號。\n\n然而，較新的研究讓這個簡潔的故事變得複雜了。2012 年，羅徹斯特大學的研究人員在實驗中加了一個轉折。在棉花糖測驗之前，大人答應其中一組孩子會給他們更好的美術用品，而且信守承諾；另一組孩子則失望了，因為大人空著手回來。已經體認到大人很可靠的孩子，平均等待的時間大約是失望那組孩子的四倍。對一個已經學到「承諾常常不會兌現」的孩子來說，立刻吃掉點心並不是缺乏意志力，而是合理的選擇。\n\n接著在 2018 年，一項規模更大的研究以九百多名來自更多元背景的孩子，重新檢驗原本的說法。研究確實發現等待能力和日後的學業成就有關，但關聯的強度只有早期研究所顯示的一半左右；而且在研究人員把家庭收入和家庭環境等因素納入考量之後，這個關聯變得小得多。換句話說，孩子能不能等待，可能有一部分反映的是他所處的環境，而不是固定不變的人格特質。\n\n這些都不代表自制力不重要。相反地，新的研究結果顯示，自制力可以受到經驗和環境的塑造，這是令人振奮的消息。米歇爾本人就認為，成功等到的孩子靠的是策略，例如轉移自己的注意力，而不是單純的意志力，而策略是可以教的。對於坐在書桌前和各種讓人分心的事物奮戰的學生來說，啟示或許是：不要盯著棉花糖看，而是把它放到看不見的地方。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How Mischel designed a perfect intelligence test for young children", "Why young children should not be given sweets at school", "How newer research has changed our understanding of a famous self-control study", "Why the University of Rochester proved that Mischel's study was a failure"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一、二段介紹米歇爾的棉花糖實驗及其著名結論，第三、四段說明 2012 年與 2018 年的新研究 complicated this neat story，第五段再說明新發現的意義，故主旨為 (C)。(A) 該實驗測的是自制力，並非智力測驗；(B) 文中未討論學校該不該給孩子甜食；(D) 羅徹斯特大學的研究只是補充新觀點，並未證明原研究失敗，第五段也說自制力仍然重要。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what happened in the 2012 study?", "options": ["Children who had been let down by an adult waited for a much shorter time.", "Children who were promised art supplies refused to eat the marshmallow at all.", "Children who waited longer were given better art supplies as a reward.", "The researchers found that the adult's reliability made no difference."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Children who had learned that the adult was reliable waited, on average, about four times longer than those who had been disappointed，可知被大人失信的孩子等待時間短得多，故選 (A)。(B) 文中沒說他們完全不吃；(C) 美術用品是在測驗「之前」承諾的，並非等待後的獎勵；(D) 與研究結果相反，大人是否可靠影響很大。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"complicated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["proved completely false", "made more famous", "explained clearly", "made less simple"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段首句 more recent research has complicated this neat story，接著說明新研究讓「自制力決定成功」這個簡潔（neat）的結論多了其他因素，因此 complicated 是「使變得複雜、不再那麼單純」，與 made less simple 最接近，故選 (D)。(A) 新研究並未完全推翻原研究，2018 年研究仍發現有關聯；(B)(C) 都不符合句意。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 4 about the earlier follow-up studies?", "options": ["They included more children than the 2018 study did.", "They may have given too much credit to self-control alone.", "They were carried out only with children from poor families.", "They proved that family income has no effect on school results."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段指出 2018 年的研究發現關聯 only about half as strong as earlier studies had suggested，而且考慮家庭收入與家庭環境後關聯變得更小，可推知早期研究可能高估了自制力本身的影響，故選 (B)。(A) 2018 年的研究才是 larger study；(C) 早期受試者來自史丹佛大學附設幼兒園，2018 年研究的背景才 much wider，文中並未說早期受試者來自貧困家庭；(D) 與文意相反，家庭收入會影響結果。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by \"not to stare at the marshmallow, but to put it somewhere out of sight\" in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Students should remove distractions instead of relying only on willpower.", "Students should not eat sweets while they are studying.", "Students should reward themselves with snacks after finishing their work.", "Students should hide their snacks so that classmates cannot take them."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段先說成功的孩子靠的是 strategies, such as distracting themselves, instead of pure willpower，再把這個道理應用到 students fighting distractions at their desks，可知「把棉花糖放到看不見的地方」是比喻：與其硬靠意志力，不如把會讓人分心的東西移開，故選 (A)。(B)(C)(D) 都把棉花糖當成真正的零食，誤解了這個比喻。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a111", "title": "Forget 21 Days: How Long Does It Really Take to Form a Habit?", "title_zh": "忘掉二十一天吧：養成一個習慣到底需要多久？", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Health", "Life"], "passage": "If you have ever decided to start a new habit, you have probably been told that it takes just 21 days. The number appears in countless self-help books, apps and social media posts, and it is attractive because it sounds achievable: three weeks of effort, and the new behavior will run on its own. Unfortunately, the evidence behind this popular claim is surprisingly weak.\n\nThe idea seems to come from Maxwell Maltz, an American plastic surgeon who wrote a best-selling book in 1960. He noticed that his patients needed a minimum of about 21 days to get used to their new faces, and he suggested that people generally need at least that long to adjust to a change. Over the years, his careful word \"minimum\" was quietly dropped, and \"at least 21 days\" turned into \"21 days,\" a neat rule that Maltz had never actually tested.\n\nIn 2009, a research team led by Phillippa Lally at University College London decided to measure habit formation directly. They asked 96 volunteers to choose one simple daily behavior, such as eating a piece of fruit with lunch or going for a fifteen-minute run before dinner, and to perform it in the same situation every day. Each day, the participants reported how automatic the behavior felt; in other words, whether they did it without having to think about it or push themselves.\n\nThe results were far less tidy than the 21-day rule. On average, it took 66 days for a behavior to reach its highest level of automaticity, but the range was enormous: one person needed only 18 days, while for others the researchers estimated that it would take as long as 254 days. Simple actions, like drinking a glass of water, generally became automatic sooner than demanding ones, like exercise. Interestingly, missing a single day did not seriously damage the process, so an occasional slip need not be a reason to give up.\n\nWhat does this mean for students who want to review vocabulary every evening or stop checking their phones during homework? First, expect the process to take months rather than weeks, and do not feel like a failure when day 22 still requires effort. Second, attach the new behavior to a fixed cue, such as \"after I finish dinner, I open my word list,\" because a consistent situation is what allows the brain to link the action to its trigger. Finally, start small. A habit that feels almost too easy at first is more likely to survive long enough to become automatic.", "passage_zh": "如果你曾經下定決心要養成一個新習慣，大概有人告訴過你：只要二十一天就夠了。這個數字出現在無數的勵志書、應用程式和社群媒體貼文裡。它很吸引人，因為聽起來做得到：努力三個星期，新的行為就會自動運作。可惜的是，這個流行說法背後的證據出奇地薄弱。\n\n這個說法似乎來自美國整形外科醫師麥斯威爾‧馬爾茲，他在 1960 年寫了一本暢銷書。他注意到，病人最少需要大約二十一天才能習慣自己的新面孔，並因此提出：人們通常至少需要這麼長的時間來適應一項改變。多年下來，他謹慎使用的「最少」一詞被悄悄拿掉，「至少二十一天」變成了「二十一天」，成了一條馬爾茲本人從未實際驗證過的簡潔規則。\n\n2009 年，倫敦大學學院由菲莉帕‧拉莉帶領的研究團隊，決定直接測量習慣是如何養成的。他們請 96 名志願者各自選擇一個簡單的日常行為，例如午餐時吃一份水果，或晚餐前跑步十五分鐘，並且每天在相同的情境下執行。參與者每天回報這個行為感覺有多「自動」，也就是說，他們是否不必多想、也不必勉強自己就會去做。\n\n研究結果遠不如「二十一天規則」那樣整齊。平均來說，一個行為要花 66 天才會達到最高的自動化程度，但個別差異非常大：有一個人只需要 18 天，而研究人員估計，有些人可能要長達 254 天。簡單的行動，例如喝一杯水，通常比費力的行動（例如運動）更快變得自動。有趣的是，偶爾漏掉一天並不會嚴重破壞這個過程，所以偶爾鬆懈一次，不必成為放棄的理由。\n\n這對想每天晚上複習單字，或想在寫作業時不再一直查看手機的學生來說，代表什麼呢？第一，要有心理準備：這個過程需要的是幾個月而不是幾個星期，到了第二十二天仍然需要努力，也不要覺得自己失敗了。第二，把新的行為和一個固定的提示連結起來，例如「吃完晚餐後，我就打開單字表」，因為一致的情境才能讓大腦把這個行動和它的觸發點連在一起。最後，從小處開始。一個剛開始簡單到幾乎太容易的習慣，比較有可能撐得夠久，直到變成自動自發。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in this passage?", "options": ["To prove that Maxwell Maltz was a dishonest doctor", "To correct a popular belief about habits and give more realistic advice", "To encourage students to try plastic surgery to change their habits", "To explain why exercise is the most important habit for students"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說「二十一天養成習慣」的說法 the evidence behind this popular claim is surprisingly weak，第二到四段說明這個說法的由來與 2009 年研究的真實結果，第五段再提供學生實際可行的建議，故選 (B)。(A) 第二段只說他的「最少」被後人省略，並未指他不誠實；(C) 整形手術只是說法的來源背景；(D) 運動只是研究中的例子之一。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, where did the \"21 days\" idea come from?", "options": ["A study of 96 volunteers at University College London", "A popular app that rewarded users for keeping habits", "An experiment in which patients ate fruit with lunch every day", "A surgeon's observation of how long patients took to get used to their new appearance"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Maxwell Maltz, an American plastic surgeon... noticed that his patients needed a minimum of about 21 days to get used to their new faces，可知這個說法源自整形外科醫師觀察病人適應新面孔的時間，故選 (D)。(A)(C) 是第三段 2009 年拉莉的研究，其結果反而推翻了二十一天之說；(B) 文中只說應用程式會引用這個數字，並非其來源。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is TRUE about the results of the 2009 study?", "options": ["The average time was 66 days, but some people needed far longer.", "Every participant formed a habit within exactly 21 days.", "Exercise habits became automatic sooner than drinking water did.", "Missing one day forced participants to start the process again."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：On average, it took 66 days...but the range was enormous，有人只要 18 天，有人估計要 254 天，故選 (A)。(B) 與研究結果不符；(C) 同段說簡單的行動（喝水）比費力的行動（運動）更快變得自動，正好相反；(D) 同段說 missing a single day did not seriously damage the process。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"automatic\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["done with the help of a machine", "done only once in a while", "done without having to think about it", "done in order to please others"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段在 automatic 之後用 in other words 解釋：whether they did it without having to think about it or push themselves，即「不必思考、不必勉強就會去做」，故選 (C)。(A) 是 automatic 在機器方面的意思，不符本文語境；(B)(D) 與上下文無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which student is following the writer's advice most closely?", "options": ["Ken plans to study for three hours every night and expects the habit to be fixed in three weeks.", "Lily reviews her notes whenever she happens to remember, at different times each day.", "Sam gives up his plan after he forgets to practice the guitar one evening.", "Mei practices five English words every night right after she brushes her teeth."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段建議：要有花上數月的心理準備、把新行為連結到固定提示（fixed cue），並且 start small。Mei 每晚刷牙後（固定提示）練習五個單字（從小處開始），最符合建議，故選 (D)。(A) 一開始就每晚三小時，又期待三週就養成，違反 start small 及「需要數月」的提醒；(B) 時間不固定，缺少一致的情境；(C) 第四段說漏掉一天不必放棄。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a112", "title": "The Waiter Who Never Forgot: Why Unfinished Tasks Stay on Our Minds", "title_zh": "從不忘記的服務生：為什麼未完成的事總是掛在心上", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Education"], "passage": "According to a well-known story, in the 1920s a group of psychologists in Berlin often met at a café near their university. Their teacher, Kurt Lewin, noticed something curious about one of the waiters. The man could remember long, complicated orders for a whole table without writing anything down, yet once the bill had been paid, the details seemed to vanish from his memory completely. One of Lewin's students, a young woman from Lithuania named Bluma Zeigarnik, decided to find out whether this was a general feature of the human mind.\n\nIn her experiments, Zeigarnik gave volunteers around twenty short tasks, such as solving puzzles, doing simple math problems and making small figures out of clay. She allowed them to finish some of the tasks but interrupted the others before they were done. Later, when the volunteers were asked to list the tasks they could remember, they recalled the interrupted ones about twice as often as the completed ones. Zeigarnik published her findings in 1927, and this tendency came to be known as the Zeigarnik effect.\n\nLewin explained the result in terms of tension. When we start a task, he suggested, the mind creates a kind of inner pressure that is released only when the task is complete. Until then, the unfinished task keeps knocking at the door of our thoughts. Later researchers have pointed out that the effect does not appear in every study and depends on how much a person cares about the task. Still, most of us recognize the feeling of a half-written essay that follows us to bed.\n\nTelevision writers have long taken advantage of this tendency. An episode that ends just before a secret is revealed leaves viewers with an unfinished story that is hard to forget, which is why they come back the following week. The same principle can help students. Many people put off a difficult assignment because getting started feels painful. However, if you work on it for just five minutes, the task becomes \"unfinished\" rather than \"not started,\" and that small amount of tension may pull you back to it later.\n\nThe effect also has a downside: too many open tasks can crowd the mind and make it hard to relax or sleep. Fortunately, you do not always have to finish a task to quiet it. In a 2011 study, people who were reminded of an unfinished goal were far less distracted by it once they had written down a specific plan for when and how they would complete it. So, on a busy night, try making a short list of tomorrow's tasks before going to bed. Your brain may then be willing to let them wait until morning.", "passage_zh": "根據一個廣為流傳的故事，1920 年代，柏林有一群心理學家常在大學附近的一家咖啡館聚會。他們的老師庫爾特‧勒溫注意到其中一位服務生有件奇特的事。這個人可以不用寫下任何東西，就記住整桌客人又長又複雜的點餐內容；可是一旦客人結完帳，這些細節似乎就從他的記憶中完全消失了。勒溫的一位學生，一位來自立陶宛、名叫布盧瑪‧蔡格尼克的年輕女子，決定要查明這是否是人類心智普遍具有的特性。\n\n在實驗中，蔡格尼克給志願者大約二十項簡短的任務，例如解謎題、做簡單的數學題，以及用黏土捏小人偶。她讓志願者完成其中一些任務，卻在其他任務完成之前打斷他們。之後，當志願者被要求列出自己記得的任務時，他們想起被打斷的任務的次數，大約是已完成任務的兩倍。蔡格尼克在 1927 年發表了她的研究結果，這種傾向後來就被稱為「蔡格尼克效應」。\n\n勒溫用「緊張」來解釋這個結果。他認為，當我們開始一項任務時，心裡會產生一種內在的壓力，只有在任務完成時才會釋放。在那之前，這項未完成的任務會一直敲著我們思緒的門。後來的研究人員指出，這個效應並不是在每項研究中都會出現，而且取決於一個人有多在乎這項任務。儘管如此，我們大多數人都熟悉那種感覺：一篇寫到一半的作文，會一路跟著你上床睡覺。\n\n電視編劇很早就懂得利用這種傾向。一集在祕密即將揭曉前結束的節目，會讓觀眾心裡留下一個難以忘懷、還沒結束的故事，這就是他們下週會再回來收看的原因。同樣的原理也能幫助學生。很多人會拖延困難的作業，因為開始動手的感覺很痛苦。然而，如果你只先做五分鐘，這項作業就從「還沒開始」變成「尚未完成」，而這一點點緊張感，可能會在之後把你拉回來繼續做。\n\n這個效應也有缺點：太多尚未完成的事會塞滿腦袋，讓人難以放鬆或入睡。幸好，你不一定要把事情做完才能讓它安靜下來。在一項 2011 年的研究中，被提醒有某個目標尚未完成的人，一旦寫下何時、如何完成它的具體計畫，受到它干擾的程度就大幅降低。所以，在忙碌的夜晚，睡前試著列一張明天要做的事的簡短清單吧。這樣一來，你的大腦或許就願意讓它們等到早上了。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer mention the waiter in paragraph 1?", "options": ["To introduce the observation that led to Zeigarnik's research", "To show that waiters in Berlin had unusually good memories", "To complain about the poor service in cafés in the 1920s", "To explain why customers should always pay their bills quickly"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段描述服務生能記住未結帳的點餐，結帳後卻忘記，接著說蔡格尼克 decided to find out whether this was a general feature of the human mind，可知作者用這個故事引出蔡格尼克研究的起點，故選 (A)。(B) 重點不在服務生記憶力特別好，而在「未完成」與「已完成」的差別；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In Zeigarnik's experiments, which tasks did the volunteers remember best?", "options": ["The tasks they found the easiest", "The tasks they had completed first", "The tasks that had been interrupted", "The tasks that involved making clay figures"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：they recalled the interrupted ones about twice as often as the completed ones，被打斷的任務被記住的次數約為完成任務的兩倍，故選 (C)。(A)(B) 文中未提及任務難易或完成順序；(D) 捏黏土只是任務的例子之一，與記憶效果無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"vanish\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["repeat", "disappear", "improve", "change"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段 yet once the bill had been paid, the details seemed to vanish from his memory completely，yet 表示與前面「記得又長又複雜的點餐」相反，所以結帳後細節從記憶中「消失」，vanish 與 disappear 意思最接近，故選 (B)。(A)(C)(D) 都無法和 completely 及前文的轉折語氣搭配。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why might working on a hard assignment for just five minutes be helpful?", "options": ["Five minutes is enough time to finish most school assignments.", "Teachers give extra points to students who start early.", "It allows students to forget about the assignment completely.", "The assignment becomes unfinished, and that tension may draw the student back to it."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：if you work on it for just five minutes, the task becomes \"unfinished\" rather than \"not started,\" and that small amount of tension may pull you back to it later，故選 (D)。(A) 文中沒說五分鐘就能完成作業；(B) 未提及老師加分；(C) 與蔡格尼克效應相反，未完成的事反而更不容易忘記。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "A student cannot fall asleep because she keeps thinking about everything she must do tomorrow. According to the passage, what is the best thing for her to do?", "options": ["Get up and try to finish all of the tasks before morning", "Watch one more episode of a TV drama to relax", "Write down a specific plan for when and how she will do the tasks", "Try hard not to think about any of the tasks at all"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段說不一定要把事情做完才能讓它安靜下來：2011 年的研究中，人們寫下 a specific plan for when and how they would complete it 後就比較不受干擾，並建議睡前列出明天的待辦清單，故選 (C)。(A) 文中說 you do not always have to finish a task；(B) 第四段反而說電視劇的懸念會讓人放不下；(D) 文中未建議硬逼自己不去想。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a113", "title": "25,000 Streets in Your Head: How London Taxi Drivers Changed Their Brains", "title_zh": "腦中裝著兩萬五千條街：倫敦計程車司機如何改變了自己的大腦", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Health"], "passage": "To drive one of London's famous black taxis, it is not enough to have a driver's license. Would-be drivers must pass a demanding exam known as \"the Knowledge,\" which requires them to memorize about 25,000 streets and thousands of landmarks, from hospitals to theaters, within six miles of Charing Cross in central London. Trainees typically spend three to four years riding around the city on scooters, learning routes by heart, and many of them give up before they ever qualify.\n\nFor the neuroscientist Eleanor Maguire of University College London, these drivers offered a rare natural experiment. In a study published in 2000, she and her colleagues used brain scans to compare licensed taxi drivers with men who did not drive taxis. They focused on the hippocampus, a seahorse-shaped structure deep inside the brain that plays a key role in memory and in finding our way around. The rear part of the hippocampus turned out to be larger in the taxi drivers, and the longer a driver had been doing the job, the larger it tended to be.\n\nThere was an obvious objection, however. Perhaps people who are born with a large hippocampus are simply more likely to become taxi drivers. To test this, Maguire and her colleague Katherine Woollett followed 79 trainees for several years, scanning their brains before and after their training, and compared them with a group of people who were not training. In 2011, they reported that only the trainees who passed the Knowledge showed growth in the rear hippocampus; those who failed or gave up, as well as the comparison group, showed no such change. This was strong evidence that intense learning can physically reshape the adult brain, an ability known as neuroplasticity.\n\nThe change, however, may come at a price. Taxi drivers in these studies tended to do worse than comparison groups on certain tests of new visual information, such as remembering a complex drawing. This suggests that the brain may balance a gain in one area against a cost in another, although researchers are still investigating exactly how this works.\n\nThese findings raise an interesting question for the smartphone age. When we follow turn-by-turn directions from a navigation app, we hardly need to build a mental map at all. A 2017 study from the same university found that activity in the hippocampus rose when volunteers planned their own routes through a simulation of London streets, but not when they simply followed instructions. Nobody is suggesting that we throw our phones away. Now and then, however, finding your own way might be good exercise for the brain.", "passage_zh": "想在倫敦開一輛著名的黑色計程車，光有駕照是不夠的。想當司機的人必須通過一項名為「倫敦知識大全」（the Knowledge）的嚴格考試，要背下倫敦市中心查令十字周圍六英里內大約兩萬五千條街道，以及從醫院到劇院等數千個地標。受訓者通常要花三到四年的時間騎著機車在城裡四處跑，把路線牢牢記住，而且很多人還沒取得資格就放棄了。\n\n對倫敦大學學院的神經科學家埃莉諾‧馬圭爾來說，這些司機提供了一個難得的天然實驗。在 2000 年發表的一項研究中，她和同事用腦部掃描比較了持有執照的計程車司機和不開計程車的男性。他們把焦點放在海馬迴上，那是位於大腦深處、形狀像海馬的構造，在記憶和認路方面扮演關鍵角色。結果發現，計程車司機海馬迴的後段比較大，而且司機做這份工作的時間愈久，這個部位往往就愈大。\n\n不過，有一個明顯的質疑。也許天生海馬迴就比較大的人，本來就比較可能成為計程車司機。為了驗證這一點，馬圭爾和她的同事凱瑟琳‧伍利特追蹤了 79 名受訓者好幾年，在他們受訓前後掃描大腦，並與一組沒有受訓的人做比較。2011 年，她們發表報告指出，只有通過考試的受訓者，海馬迴後段才出現增長；沒通過或中途放棄的人，以及對照組，都沒有這種變化。這有力地證明了密集的學習能在實質上重塑成人的大腦，這種能力稱為「神經可塑性」。\n\n然而，這種改變可能要付出代價。在這些研究中，計程車司機在某些關於新視覺資訊的測驗上，例如記住一幅複雜的圖畫，表現往往比對照組差。這顯示大腦可能會在某一方面的收穫和另一方面的損失之間取得平衡，不過研究人員仍在探究其中確切的運作方式。\n\n這些發現在智慧型手機時代引出了一個有趣的問題。當我們照著導航應用程式一個路口接一個路口的指示走，幾乎完全不需要在腦中建立地圖。同一所大學在 2017 年的一項研究發現，當志願者在模擬的倫敦街道中自己規劃路線時，海馬迴的活動會增加；但只是照著指示走的時候就不會。沒有人主張我們應該把手機丟掉。不過，偶爾自己找路，或許是讓大腦運動的好方法。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the passage mainly show?", "options": ["London taxi drivers are more intelligent than other drivers.", "Navigation apps have made the Knowledge exam unnecessary.", "People with a large hippocampus are born to be good drivers.", "Intense learning can change the structure of the adult brain."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文以倫敦計程車司機為例：第二段發現司機的海馬迴後段較大，第三段的追蹤研究證明這是學習造成的，並點出 intense learning can physically reshape the adult brain，故選 (D)。(A) 文中未比較智力，第四段甚至提到司機某些測驗表現較差；(B) 文中未說考試已不必要；(C) 正是第三段被追蹤研究否定的說法。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 1, what is \"the Knowledge\"?", "options": ["A brain scan that all London drivers must take", "An exam that requires drivers to memorize London's streets and landmarks", "A navigation app designed for London's taxi drivers", "A course that teaches trainees how to ride scooters safely"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：a demanding exam known as \"the Knowledge,\" which requires them to memorize about 25,000 streets and thousands of landmarks，故選 (B)。(A) 腦部掃描是第二段研究人員使用的工具；(C) 導航應用程式出現在第五段，與考試無關；(D) 受訓者騎機車是為了熟記路線，不是學騎車。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did Maguire and Woollett follow the trainees for several years?", "options": ["To help the trainees pass the Knowledge more quickly", "To find out how many trainees would give up their training", "To check whether the training itself caused the hippocampus to grow", "To prove that taxi drivers have worse memories than other people"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段先提出質疑：也許天生海馬迴大的人比較會成為計程車司機；To test this，她們追蹤受訓者受訓前後的大腦變化，目的是確認是否是訓練本身造成海馬迴增長，故選 (C)。(A) 研究並未協助受訓者；(B) 有人放棄只是研究結果的一部分，不是研究目的；(D) 記憶較差的部分是第四段提到的其他發現。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"objection\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a reason to doubt something", "a goal to aim for", "an object in the brain", "a result of an exam"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 There was an obvious objection, however. 下一句隨即提出「也許天生海馬迴大的人比較會當上計程車司機」，這是對第二段結論的質疑，因此 objection 指「反對意見、懷疑的理由」，故選 (A)。(B) 目標、(C) 腦中的物體、(D) 考試結果都不符合上下文。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest about navigation apps in the last paragraph?", "options": ["They are dangerous and should be banned for taxi drivers.", "Relying on them all the time may give the hippocampus less work to do.", "They help people build better mental maps than paper maps do.", "They were invented by the scientists at University College London."], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一段說使用導航時 we hardly need to build a mental map at all，2017 年研究也發現只照指示走時海馬迴活動不會增加，作者因此建議偶爾自己找路，可見一直依賴導航可能讓海馬迴少了「運動」的機會，故選 (B)。(A) 作者明說 Nobody is suggesting that we throw our phones away，沒有主張禁止；(C) 與文意相反；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a114", "title": "Twenty-Four Jams: The Puzzle of Choice Overload", "title_zh": "二十四種果醬：「選擇過多」之謎", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Business", "Science"], "passage": "On two Saturdays in a California supermarket, shoppers came across a tasting booth offering free samples of jam. For some hours the table displayed 24 different flavors; at other times, it displayed only six. The psychologists behind the booth, Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper, wanted to know a simple thing: would having more options make people more likely to buy?\n\nThe large display certainly attracted more attention. About 60 percent of passing shoppers stopped to taste when 24 jams were on offer, compared with 40 percent for the smaller display. Yet when it came to actually buying, the pattern reversed dramatically. Only about 3 percent of the people who stopped at the large display went on to purchase a jar, while about 30 percent of those who saw just six flavors did. Published in 2000, the study suggested that too many choices can leave people confused and unable to decide, a problem that came to be called \"choice overload.\"\n\nThe jam study soon became one of the most famous experiments in psychology. In his 2004 book The Paradox of Choice, the psychologist Barry Schwartz argued that endless options in modern life, from breakfast cereals to phone plans, can make people anxious and less satisfied, because every option they reject becomes a possible regret. Businesses took note, and some companies reduced the number of products they offered in the hope of selling more.\n\nHowever, when other researchers tried to repeat the finding, the results were mixed. A 2010 review that combined the results of 50 experiments found that, on average, the choice overload effect was close to zero. In some studies, more options made people less likely to choose; in others, more options made people more likely to choose, or made no difference at all. This did not mean that the original study was wrong, but it did suggest that the effect appears only under certain conditions.\n\nLater research has helped to identify those conditions. Choice overload seems most likely when the options are hard to compare, when people do not already know what they want, and when they feel pressure to make the perfect choice. Someone who already loves strawberry jam will find it in a row of 24 within seconds; someone who has never tasted jam before may simply walk away. For students choosing a college department from a long list, the lesson is useful: deciding on your priorities before you look at the options can turn a frightening list into a manageable one.", "passage_zh": "在加州一家超市裡，連續兩個星期六，購物的人都會經過一個提供果醬免費試吃的攤位。有些時段桌上擺了 24 種不同口味，其他時段則只擺 6 種。設置這個攤位的心理學家希娜‧艾恩嘉和馬克‧萊珀，想知道一件簡單的事：選擇愈多，會不會讓人愈想買？\n\n大型的陳列確實比較吸引目光。擺出 24 種果醬時，約有 60% 經過的顧客停下來試吃；小型陳列則是 40%。然而，說到真正購買，情況卻大逆轉。在大型陳列前停下來的人當中，只有大約 3% 後來買了一罐；而只看到 6 種口味的人當中，約有 30% 買了。這項研究在 2000 年發表，顯示太多選擇可能讓人感到困惑、無法下決定，這個問題後來被稱為「選擇過多」。\n\n這項果醬研究很快就成為心理學界最著名的實驗之一。心理學家貝瑞‧史瓦茲在他 2004 年出版的《選擇的弔詭》（The Paradox of Choice）一書中主張，現代生活中無窮無盡的選項，從早餐穀片到手機資費方案，都可能讓人焦慮、比較不滿足，因為每一個被放棄的選項都可能成為遺憾。企業注意到了這一點，有些公司減少了所販售的產品種類，希望能賣得更多。\n\n然而，當其他研究人員嘗試重現這項結果時，結果卻不一致。一篇在 2010 年綜合了 50 項實驗結果的回顧研究發現，平均而言，「選擇過多」的效應幾乎是零。在某些研究中，選項愈多，人們愈不容易做出選擇；在另一些研究中，選項多反而讓人更願意選擇，或者根本沒有差別。這並不表示原本的研究是錯的，但確實顯示這個效應只會在某些條件下出現。\n\n後來的研究幫忙找出了這些條件。當選項難以互相比較、人們還不清楚自己想要什麼，以及覺得有壓力必須做出完美選擇的時候，似乎最容易發生選擇過多的情況。一個本來就愛吃草莓果醬的人，能在一排 24 種果醬中幾秒內就找到它；從沒嚐過果醬的人，則可能乾脆走開。對於要從一長串名單中選擇大學科系的學生來說，這個啟示很有用：在看選項之前先確定自己重視什麼，就能把一張令人害怕的清單，變成一張可以處理的清單。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How supermarkets can sell more jam by offering free samples", "Why Barry Schwartz's book became a best seller", "A famous finding about having too many options, and how later research has questioned it", "The reasons why strawberry jam is more popular than other flavors"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一、二段介紹果醬實驗與「選擇過多」的概念，第三段說明它的影響，第四、五段則指出後續研究結果不一致、效應只在某些條件下出現，故選 (C)。(A) 試吃只是實驗的方式，並非主旨；(B) 史瓦茲的書只在第三段提及，文中也沒說它是暢銷書；(D) 草莓果醬只是第五段的例子。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Of the shoppers who stopped at the six-jam display, about how many bought a jar?", "options": ["3 percent", "30 percent", "40 percent", "60 percent"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：about 30 percent of those who saw just six flavors did（買了一罐），故選 (B)。(A) 3% 是在 24 種果醬攤位停下的人的購買比例；(C) 40% 是經過小型陳列時停下試吃的比例；(D) 60% 是經過大型陳列時停下試吃的比例。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"reversed\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["stayed the same", "became more popular", "was carefully checked", "turned the other way"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段先說大型陳列吸引較多人試吃，接著說 Yet when it came to actually buying, the pattern reversed dramatically，購買時反而是小型陳列的比例高出許多，可見情況「反過來了」，故選 (D)。(A) 與 Yet 表示的轉折相反；(B)(C) 都不符合上下文。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, what did the 2010 review find?", "options": ["On average, having more options had almost no effect on choosing.", "The original jam study had used false numbers.", "More options always made people less likely to choose.", "Shoppers always prefer stores that offer fewer products."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：A 2010 review... found that, on average, the choice overload effect was close to zero，故選 (A)。(B) 同段明說 This did not mean that the original study was wrong；(C) 同段指出有些研究中選項多反而讓人更願意選擇，並非 always；(D) 文中沒有這樣的結論。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Based on the last paragraph, who is MOST likely to experience choice overload?", "options": ["A boy who always orders the same drink at his favorite tea shop", "A girl who has already decided she wants a red bike under NT$5,000", "A student choosing between two pens that look almost the same", "A tourist who has never tried tea before and must pick one from 40 kinds"], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段指出，選擇過多最容易發生在選項難以比較、且 people do not already know what they want 的時候，就像從沒嚐過果醬的人可能乾脆走開。從沒喝過茶又要從 40 種中挑一種的觀光客最符合，故選 (D)。(A)(B) 都已經清楚自己要什麼；(C) 只有兩個選項，談不上選項過多。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a115", "title": "Is Anyone Going to Help? Rethinking the Bystander Effect", "title_zh": "會有人出手幫忙嗎？重新思考「旁觀者效應」", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Society", "Science"], "passage": "Late one night in March 1964, a young woman named Kitty Genovese was attacked and killed near her apartment building in New York City. Two weeks later, The New York Times reported that 38 of her neighbors had watched or listened to the attack and that none of them had called the police while it was happening. The story shocked the country and became a symbol of the coldness of modern city life.\n\nThe case inspired two psychologists, John Darley and Bibb Latané, to investigate why people fail to help. In a 1968 experiment, college students talked over an intercom with what they thought were other students in separate rooms. Suddenly, one of the voices seemed to have a seizure and called for help. When participants believed they were the only person who could hear, 85 percent went to help before the voice stopped. When they believed four other people were listening, only 31 percent did. The researchers called this a \"diffusion of responsibility\": the more people present, the more each one assumes that someone else will act.\n\nThis \"bystander effect\" became one of the best-known ideas in social psychology, and the Genovese story was told in textbooks for decades. Yet later investigations revealed that the newspaper's account had been seriously exaggerated. Few of the neighbors had actually seen the attack, and most heard only noises they did not understand. At least one person did call the police, and one neighbor rushed out and held Genovese in her arms as she was dying.\n\nMore recent research has also painted a more hopeful picture. In 2019, an international team studied security camera footage of more than 200 real public conflicts in the Netherlands, the United Kingdom and South Africa. In about nine out of ten cases, at least one bystander stepped in, whether by calming someone down or pulling people apart. What is more, the more bystanders there were, the more likely it was that somebody would intervene. Although each person in a crowd may feel less responsible, a larger crowd also contains more potential helpers.\n\nSo what should you do if you ever need help in public, or see someone else who does? Experts suggest breaking the diffusion of responsibility by making the request personal. Instead of shouting, \"Somebody call an ambulance!\", point to a specific person and say, \"You in the blue jacket, please call 119.\" Once a request is directed at one person, it becomes much harder for that person to assume that someone else will take care of it.", "passage_zh": "1964 年 3 月的一個深夜，一位名叫姬蒂‧吉諾維斯的年輕女子在紐約市自家公寓大樓附近遭到攻擊身亡。兩週後，《紐約時報》報導說，她有 38 位鄰居目睹或聽到了這場攻擊，但在事發過程中，沒有任何一個人報警。這則報導震驚全美，也成為現代都市生活冷漠無情的象徵。\n\n這個案件促使兩位心理學家約翰‧達利和比布‧拉塔內去研究人們為什麼不伸出援手。在 1968 年的一項實驗中，大學生透過對講機和他們以為是待在其他房間的學生交談。突然間，其中一個聲音似乎癲癇發作，並大聲求救。當參與者以為只有自己聽得到時，85% 的人在聲音停止之前就去幫忙；當他們以為還有另外四個人也在聽時，只有 31% 的人這麼做。研究人員稱之為「責任分散」：在場的人愈多，每個人就愈會認為別人會採取行動。\n\n這種「旁觀者效應」成為社會心理學中最廣為人知的概念之一，吉諾維斯的故事也在教科書中流傳了數十年。然而，後來的調查發現，那篇報導嚴重誇大了事實。實際目睹攻擊的鄰居很少，大部分人只聽到一些搞不清楚是什麼的聲音。至少有一個人確實報了警，還有一位鄰居衝出去，在吉諾維斯臨終時將她抱在懷裡。\n\n較新的研究也描繪出比較有希望的景象。2019 年，一個國際研究團隊分析了荷蘭、英國和南非兩百多起真實公共衝突的監視器影片。在大約十分之九的案例中，至少有一位旁觀者出手介入，不論是讓某人冷靜下來，還是把人拉開。不僅如此，旁觀者愈多，就愈有可能有人出面介入。雖然人群中的每個人可能覺得自己的責任比較小，但人群愈大，可能出手幫忙的人也愈多。\n\n那麼，如果你在公共場所需要幫助，或看到別人需要幫助時，該怎麼做呢？專家建議，把請求針對特定的人，以打破責任分散。與其大喊「誰來叫救護車！」，不如指著某個特定的人說：「穿藍色外套的那位，請幫忙打 119。」一旦請求是針對某一個人，那個人就很難再認為別人會處理了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["People living in big cities never help strangers in trouble.", "A famous idea about helping has been partly challenged, but it still offers useful lessons.", "The New York Times reported the Genovese case accurately.", "Security cameras have made it unnecessary for bystanders to help."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一、二段介紹吉諾維斯案與旁觀者效應，第三、四段指出原報導被誇大、新研究顯示多數情況下有人出手，第五段再根據「責任分散」提出求助的實用方法，故選 (B)。(A) 正是第四段研究所反駁的觀念；(C) 第三段說報導 had been seriously exaggerated；(D) 監視器只是研究人員收集資料的工具。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In Darley and Latané's experiment, what happened when participants believed four other people could hear the voice?", "options": ["Fewer than one third of them went to help.", "Almost all of them went to help immediately.", "They called the police instead of helping.", "They asked the other listeners what to do."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：When they believed four other people were listening, only 31 percent did（去幫忙），31% 不到三分之一，故選 (A)。(B) 85% 去幫忙是以為只有自己聽到時的結果；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"exaggerated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["kept secret from the public", "written by many different people", "made to seem bigger or worse than it really was", "printed on the front page of the newspaper"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說報導被 seriously exaggerated，接著列出事實：真正目睹的人很少、有人報警、有鄰居衝出去抱住她，可見原報導把鄰居的冷漠說得比實際嚴重，exaggerated 意為「誇大的」，故選 (C)。(A)(B)(D) 都與後文所舉的事實無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the 2019 study described in paragraph 4?", "options": ["People in the Netherlands are kinder than people in other countries.", "Darley and Latané's experiment was completely wrong.", "Most public conflicts end without anyone getting involved.", "Being in a large group does not necessarily make help less likely."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段指出約十分之九的衝突中至少有一位旁觀者介入，而且 the more bystanders there were, the more likely it was that somebody would intervene，可推論身處人多的場合不一定就比較得不到幫助，故選 (D)。(A) 研究並未比較各國善良程度；(B) 同段承認人群中每個人可能覺得責任較小，並未全盤否定舊研究；(C) 與「十分之九有人介入」相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "A classmate suddenly faints on a crowded MRT platform. According to the passage, what is the best thing to do?", "options": ["Point to one particular person and ask him or her to call 119.", "Shout loudly, \"Somebody help us!\" to everyone on the platform.", "Wait quietly, because someone else will surely call for help.", "Take a video of the situation so that the police can see it later."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段建議 point to a specific person，例如「穿藍色外套的那位，請幫忙打 119」，因為請求針對某一個人時，對方就很難推給別人，故選 (A)。(B) 正是文中說「與其……不如」要避免的喊法；(C) 這正是「責任分散」造成的錯誤想法；(D) 文中未建議。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a116", "title": "The Silent Thief: How a Phone You Don't Touch Can Steal Your Focus", "title_zh": "安靜的小偷：沒碰的手機如何偷走你的專注力", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Technology", "Education"], "passage": "Imagine you are about to start an important test. Your phone is switched to silent and lying face down on the desk. You have no intention of touching it, and it does not make a single sound. Surely it cannot affect how well you do? According to a much-discussed 2017 study from the University of Texas at Austin, it may do exactly that.\n\nIn the study, led by the researcher Adrian Ward, about 800 smartphone users completed tasks on a computer that measured how much information they could hold and process in their minds at once. Before starting, participants were randomly told to do one of three things: place their phone face down on the desk, keep it in a pocket or bag, or leave it in another room. In every case, the phones were silenced, so there were no alerts or vibrations to interrupt anyone.\n\nThe results were striking. Participants whose phones were in another room performed significantly better than those whose phones were on the desk, while those with phones in a pocket or bag scored somewhere in between. Remarkably, most participants said that they had not been thinking about their phones at all during the tasks. The effect was also strongest among people who said they depended most heavily on their phones in daily life.\n\nThe researchers called this effect \"brain drain.\" Their explanation is that our attention is a limited resource. Because a phone is so closely linked to messages, friends and entertainment, the brain treats it as something important. Even when we successfully resist the urge to check it, the effort of ignoring it quietly uses up some of the mental capacity we would otherwise devote to the task in front of us. In other words, the problem is not only that the phone interrupts us, but also that the brain has to work to avoid being interrupted.\n\nIt is worth noting that not every later study has found such a clear effect, and scientists are still debating how large it really is. Still, the advice that follows from it is simple and costs nothing. When you need to concentrate, whether on homework, an exam or a long piece of reading, putting your phone face down may not be enough. Leave it in another room, or at least put it away in your bag, out of sight. You may be surprised how much easier it becomes to focus when there is one less thing your brain has to ignore.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，你即將開始一場重要的考試。你的手機已經切換成靜音，正面朝下放在桌上。你完全不打算碰它，它也沒有發出任何聲音。它總不可能影響你的表現吧？根據德州大學奧斯汀分校在 2017 年一項備受討論的研究，它可能真的會。\n\n在這項由研究人員亞德里安‧華德主持的研究中，大約 800 名智慧型手機使用者在電腦上完成一些測驗，這些測驗可以測量他們能同時在腦中記住並處理多少資訊。開始之前，參與者被隨機指示做以下三件事之一：把手機正面朝下放在桌上、放在口袋或包包裡，或是留在另一個房間。在所有情況下，手機都設成靜音，所以不會有任何通知或震動打擾任何人。\n\n結果相當驚人。手機放在另一個房間的參與者，表現明顯優於手機放在桌上的人；而手機放在口袋或包包裡的人，分數則介於兩者之間。值得注意的是，大部分參與者都說，他們在測驗過程中完全沒有想到自己的手機。此外，在那些自認日常生活中最依賴手機的人身上，這個效應最強。\n\n研究人員把這種效應稱為「大腦流失」。他們的解釋是：我們的注意力是有限的資源。因為手機和訊息、朋友、娛樂關係如此密切，大腦會把它當成重要的東西。即使我們成功抗拒了查看手機的衝動，努力忽略它這件事，也會悄悄用掉一部分本來可以投入眼前任務的腦力。換句話說，問題不只是手機會打擾我們，還在於大腦必須費力避免被打擾。\n\n值得一提的是，並不是後來的每一項研究都發現這麼明顯的效應，科學家們仍在爭論它到底有多大。儘管如此，由此得出的建議既簡單又不花一毛錢。當你需要專心的時候，不論是寫作業、考試還是閱讀長篇文章，把手機正面朝下可能還不夠。把它留在另一個房間，或至少收進包包裡，讓自己看不到它。少了一樣大腦必須忽略的東西，你可能會驚訝地發現，專心變得容易多了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main point of the passage?", "options": ["Phones should be banned from all schools and exam rooms.", "Silent phones are safe because they cannot interrupt us.", "Simply having a phone nearby may reduce our ability to focus.", "People who use their phones often are less intelligent than others."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段提出「靜音、正面朝下的手機也可能影響表現」，第二到四段說明研究結果與「大腦流失」的原因，第五段建議專心時把手機放到看不見的地方，故選 (C)。(A) 作者只建議把手機放遠，並未主張全面禁止；(B) 與全文論點相反；(D) 文中討論的是專注力與腦力的分配，並非智力高低。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the study, which group of participants performed the best?", "options": ["Those who kept their phones in a pocket", "Those who placed their phones face down on the desk", "Those who held their phones in their hands", "Those who left their phones in another room"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Participants whose phones were in another room performed significantly better than those whose phones were on the desk，放在口袋或包包裡的人分數介於兩者之間，故選 (D)。(A) 分數居中；(B) 表現最差；(C) 研究中沒有這一組。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which finding does the writer describe as surprising in paragraph 3?", "options": ["Many participants checked their phones secretly during the tasks.", "Most participants said they had not been thinking about their phones.", "Participants with phones in bags did better than everyone else.", "People who seldom used phones were affected the most."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段用 Remarkably（值得注意、令人驚訝的是）帶出：most participants said that they had not been thinking about their phones at all，故選 (B)。(A) 第二段說手機都靜音，文中沒有偷看手機的描述；(C) 放在包包的人分數居中；(D) 與同段 strongest among people who... depended most heavily on their phones 相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"devote\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["give", "lose", "borrow", "refuse"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段 uses up some of the mental capacity we would otherwise devote to the task in front of us，意思是原本可以「投入、用在」眼前任務上的腦力，devote 與 give 最接近，故選 (A)。(B) lose 是忽略手機造成的結果，不是 devote 的意思；(C) borrow、(D) refuse 都不合句意。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward the \"brain drain\" findings in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Completely doubtful, because later studies proved them wrong", "Angry, because phone companies have hidden the truth", "Careful but practical, since the effect is debated but the advice is still useful", "Uninterested, since the findings do not apply to students"], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一段先坦承 not every later study has found such a clear effect，科學家仍在爭論效應大小（態度謹慎），接著用 Still 轉折，說這個建議 simple and costs nothing，並具體建議學生把手機放到別處（態度務實），故選 (C)。(A) 作者只說效應大小仍有爭議，並未說被證明是錯的；(B) 文中未提及手機公司；(D) 作者正是把研究應用在學生的作業與考試上。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a117", "title": "Nobody Is Staring at Your Shirt: The Spotlight Effect", "title_zh": "沒有人盯著你的上衣看：聚光燈效應", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Life"], "passage": "Have you ever spilled soy sauce on your shirt at lunch and spent the rest of the day convinced that everyone was staring at the stain? Or replayed an awkward comment you made in a group chat, certain that your friends were still thinking about it hours later? If so, you have experienced what psychologists call the spotlight effect: the tendency to believe that other people notice our appearance and behavior far more than they actually do.\n\nThe term was made famous by a clever experiment at Cornell University, published in 2000. The psychologist Thomas Gilovich and his colleagues asked students to put on a T-shirt printed with a large picture of Barry Manilow, a singer whom most students at the time considered extremely uncool. Each student then walked into a room where several other students were filling out questionnaires, stayed briefly and left. Afterwards, the shirt wearers were asked to guess how many people in the room had noticed who was on their shirt. They guessed about half. In reality, only about a quarter could say.\n\nWhy do we make this mistake so consistently? One explanation is that each of us is, quite naturally, at the center of our own experience. We know exactly how embarrassed we feel, so we assume our discomfort must be obvious to everyone else. We start from our own point of view and fail to adjust enough for the fact that other people are busy worrying about themselves. The student sitting next to you is probably far more concerned about his own hair than about the stain on your shirt.\n\nA closely related error is the \"illusion of transparency,\" the belief that our inner feelings, such as nervousness, can be clearly seen by others. In one study, people who gave a short speech believed they looked much more nervous than their audience actually thought they did. Encouragingly, when speakers were told about this illusion beforehand, they felt calmer, and their speeches were rated as better.\n\nUnderstanding the spotlight effect will not make embarrassing moments disappear, but it can shrink them to their real size. The next time you trip on the stairs or mispronounce a word during an English presentation, remember that most people will barely notice, and those who do will probably have forgotten about it by lunchtime. The spotlight we feel shining on us is, for the most part, a light we have switched on ourselves.", "passage_zh": "你有沒有在午餐時把醬油灑在上衣上，接下來一整天都認定每個人都在盯著那塊汙漬看？或是一再回想自己在群組聊天裡說過的一句尷尬的話，確信朋友們過了好幾個小時還在想著它？如果有，那你就體驗過心理學家所說的「聚光燈效應」：也就是傾向於認為別人對我們外表和行為的注意，遠比實際上多得多。\n\n這個名詞因康乃爾大學一項巧妙的實驗而出名，該研究於 2000 年發表。心理學家湯瑪斯‧季洛維奇和同事請學生穿上一件印著歌手貝瑞‧曼尼洛巨幅照片的 T 恤，當時大多數學生都認為這位歌手非常俗氣。接著，每位學生走進一間有好幾位學生正在填寫問卷的房間，短暫停留後離開。之後，研究人員請穿 T 恤的學生猜猜看，房間裡有多少人注意到他們衣服上印的是誰。他們猜大約一半。實際上，只有大約四分之一的人說得出來。\n\n為什麼我們總是一再犯這種錯誤？一種解釋是，我們每個人很自然地都是自己經驗的中心。我們清楚知道自己有多尷尬，所以就以為自己的不自在一定也很明顯，每個人都看得出來。我們從自己的觀點出發，卻沒有充分考慮到一個事實：別人正忙著為自己的事操心。坐在你旁邊的同學，在意自己頭髮的程度，大概遠遠超過在意你上衣的汙漬。\n\n一個密切相關的錯誤是「透明度錯覺」，也就是以為我們內心的感受，例如緊張，會被別人看得一清二楚。在一項研究中，進行簡短演講的人以為自己看起來非常緊張，但聽眾實際上感覺到的緊張程度，遠比他們想像的低。令人欣慰的是，當演講者事先得知這種錯覺之後，他們感覺比較平靜，演講也獲得比較好的評價。\n\n了解聚光燈效應並不會讓尷尬的時刻消失，但能把它們縮小到真正的大小。下次你在樓梯上絆了一下，或在英文報告時把一個字念錯，記得大多數人幾乎不會注意到，而注意到的人大概到午餐時間就已經忘了。我們感覺到照在自己身上的聚光燈，大部分其實是我們自己打開的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Students should avoid wearing T-shirts with pictures of singers.", "Giving a speech is the most stressful experience for teenagers.", "Most people are too busy to help others who are embarrassed.", "People tend to overestimate how much others notice them."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段定義聚光燈效應為 the tendency to believe that other people notice our appearance and behavior far more than they actually do，第二到四段以實驗與相關研究說明，第五段勸人不必太在意尷尬時刻，故選 (D)。(A) T 恤只是實驗工具；(B) 演講只是第四段的例子，文中未比較何者壓力最大；(C) 文中說別人忙著擔心自己，但並未談到幫助別人。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In the Cornell experiment, about how many people in the room actually noticed who was on the T-shirt?", "options": ["About a quarter", "About half", "Nearly everyone", "Not a single person"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：They guessed about half. In reality, only about a quarter could say. 穿 T 恤的學生猜約一半，實際上只有約四分之一的人說得出來，故選 (A)。(B) 是穿衣者自己猜測的比例，並非實際結果；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the researchers most likely choose a Barry Manilow T-shirt for the experiment?", "options": ["Because Barry Manilow was a famous psychologist at Cornell", "Because wearing it would make the students feel embarrassed", "Because the students in the room were all his fans", "Because his picture was too small for anyone to recognize"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段說這位歌手是 a singer whom most students at the time considered extremely uncool（當時學生認為非常俗氣），穿著他的 T 恤容易讓學生覺得難為情，正好能測試人們是否高估別人對自己的注意，故選 (B)。(A) 他是歌手，不是心理學家；(C) 文中沒說房間裡的人是他的歌迷；(D) 第二段說照片是 a large picture。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, what happened when speakers learned about the illusion of transparency before their speeches?", "options": ["They refused to give their speeches.", "They looked more nervous to the audience.", "They felt calmer and gave better speeches.", "They spoke for a much longer time than before."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：when speakers were told about this illusion beforehand, they felt calmer, and their speeches were rated as better，故選 (C)。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(B) 與文意相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by \"a light we have switched on ourselves\" in the last sentence?", "options": ["We should turn off the lights to save energy during presentations.", "The attention we feel from others is mostly created by our own minds.", "Only famous people are really followed by spotlights wherever they go.", "We should try hard to become the center of everyone's attention."], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一句是比喻：我們覺得照在自己身上的聚光燈（被別人注意的感覺），大部分是自己打開的，也就是說這種被注視的感覺多半來自自己的想像，呼應第三段「我們是自己經驗的中心」的解釋，故選 (B)。(A)(C) 把聚光燈當成真正的燈光，誤解比喻；(D) 與作者勸人不必在意別人眼光的用意不符。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a118", "title": "Four Easy Payments? The Hidden Cost of Buy Now, Pay Later", "title_zh": "輕鬆分四期？「先買後付」的隱藏代價", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Money", "Technology"], "passage": "When nineteen-year-old Chia-hui saw a pair of wireless headphones priced at NT$6,000, she knew she could not afford them. Then a message popped up at the online checkout: \"Pay just NT$1,500 today. No interest, no credit card needed.\" With one tap, the headphones were hers. Within three months, however, she had used the same service for a jacket, a concert ticket and a new phone case, and she was struggling to keep track of what she owed and when each payment was due.\n\nChia-hui's experience is increasingly common. \"Buy now, pay later\" (BNPL) services, which allow shoppers to split a purchase into several smaller payments, have spread rapidly around the world over the past decade, especially among young people. The most typical plan divides the price into four equal parts paid every two weeks. As long as every payment is made on time, the shopper pays no interest at all, which makes the deal seem almost too good to be true.\n\nSo who pays for it? The answer, mostly, is the store. Shops usually pay the BNPL company a fee on each sale, often higher than the fee they pay for credit card transactions. They are willing to do so because the service encourages customers to buy more, and to choose more expensive items, than they otherwise would. Breaking a large price into small pieces makes paying feel less painful, so a NT$6,000 purchase begins to look like a NT$1,500 one.\n\nThis psychological effect is exactly what worries consumer groups. Surveys in several countries have found that many BNPL users end up spending more than they planned, and a significant number have missed at least one payment. Late fees can quickly add up, and some providers may report missed payments to credit agencies, which could make it harder to borrow money in the future. Another danger is \"loan stacking\": because each purchase is a separate small loan, often taken out through different apps, it is easy to lose sight of the total amount owed.\n\nNone of this means that BNPL is always harmful. Used carefully, it can help someone spread the cost of a genuinely necessary item, such as a laptop for school, without paying credit card interest. The key is to treat it as what it really is: a loan. Before tapping \"pay later,\" experts suggest asking a simple question: \"Would I still buy this if I had to pay the full price today?\" If the honest answer is no, the item is probably not worth the debt.", "passage_zh": "十九歲的佳慧看到一副售價新台幣 6,000 元的無線耳機時，她知道自己買不起。接著，網路結帳頁面跳出一則訊息：「今天只要付 1,500 元，零利率，不需要信用卡。」只按了一下，耳機就成了她的。然而不到三個月，她又用同一個服務買了一件外套、一張演唱會門票和一個新手機殼，結果她開始搞不清楚自己欠了多少錢，以及每一筆款項何時到期。\n\n佳慧的經歷越來越常見。「先買後付」（BNPL）服務讓消費者把一筆消費拆成好幾筆較小的款項支付，過去十年間在全球迅速普及，尤其受到年輕人歡迎。最典型的方案是把價格平分成四期，每兩週付一次。只要每一期都準時繳款，消費者完全不用付利息，這讓這種交易看起來好得幾乎令人難以置信。\n\n那麼，究竟是誰在付這筆錢？答案主要是商店。商家通常每做成一筆交易，就要付給先買後付公司一筆手續費，而且往往高於信用卡交易的手續費。商家願意這麼做，是因為這項服務會鼓勵顧客買得比原本更多，也會選擇更貴的商品。把一個大金額拆成小塊，會讓付錢的感覺沒那麼痛苦，於是一筆 6,000 元的消費，開始看起來像只有 1,500 元。\n\n這種心理效應正是消費者團體所擔心的。多個國家的調查發現，許多先買後付的使用者最後花的錢比原本計畫的多，而且有相當多人至少錯過一次繳款。滯納金可能很快累積起來，有些業者還可能把逾期紀錄通報給信用機構，日後要借錢就可能更加困難。另一個危險是「多重借貸」：因為每筆消費都是一筆獨立的小額貸款，而且常常是透過不同的應用程式借的，所以很容易就忽略了自己總共欠了多少錢。\n\n這並不表示先買後付一定有害。若謹慎使用，它可以幫助人們分攤真正必要物品的費用，例如上學用的筆記型電腦，又不必支付信用卡利息。關鍵在於認清它的本質：它就是一筆貸款。專家建議，在按下「之後再付」之前，先問自己一個簡單的問題：「如果今天就得付全額，我還會買嗎？」如果誠實的答案是「不會」，那件東西大概就不值得你為它背債。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How BNPL companies charge customers very high interest rates", "How BNPL services work and why shoppers should use them with care", "Why young people should stop shopping online", "How Chia-hui saved enough money to buy her headphones"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先以佳慧的例子帶出「先買後付」，第二、三段說明其運作方式與由誰負擔成本，第四段說明風險，第五段建議謹慎使用，故主旨為 (B)。(A) 錯，第二段說只要準時付款就完全不用付利息；(C) 文中並未要求年輕人停止網購；(D) 佳慧並沒有存錢，而是分期付款。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, who mainly pays for the \"no interest\" offer?", "options": ["Credit card companies", "Shoppers who pay on time", "The government", "The stores that offer the service"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段 \"The answer, mostly, is the store. Shops usually pay the BNPL company a fee on each sale\"，指出主要是商店支付手續費。(A) 信用卡只是拿來比較手續費高低；(B) 準時付款的消費者不需付任何利息；(C) 文中未提及政府。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"lose sight of\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["stop being aware of", "refuse to pay", "carefully calculate", "hide from others"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說每筆消費都是透過不同 app 借的小額貸款，所以 \"it is easy to lose sight of the total amount owed\"，意思是很容易「忽略、不再注意到」總欠款，與 (A) stop being aware of 最接近。(B) 拒絕付款、(C) 仔細計算、(D) 對他人隱瞞，皆不符上下文。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer suggest asking, \"Would I still buy this if I had to pay the full price today?\"", "options": ["To show that BNPL services are illegal for teenagers", "To persuade shoppers to pay for everything in cash", "To help shoppers judge whether a purchase is really worth borrowing for", "To explain how stores calculate the fees they pay"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段先說關鍵是把先買後付當成「貸款」，再建議自問若今天要付全額還會不會買，若答案是否定的，就 \"not worth the debt\"，目的是幫助消費者判斷這東西是否值得借錢購買。(A) 文中未說違法；(B) 作者並未要求只用現金，還說謹慎使用可分攤必要物品的費用；(D) 與商店手續費無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned in paragraph 4 as a risk of using BNPL services?", "options": ["Having to pay late fees", "Having personal data stolen by the app", "Spending more than planned", "Losing track of the total debt"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段提到花得比計畫多 (C)、滯納金累積 (A)、因同時有多筆貸款而忽略總欠款 (D)，也提到逾期紀錄可能影響日後借錢；但「個人資料被應用程式竊取」文中完全沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a119", "title": "Same Price, Less Chocolate: The Quiet Trick of Shrinkflation", "title_zh": "價格不變，巧克力變少：「縮水式通膨」的無聲伎倆", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Business", "Money"], "passage": "In November 2016, fans of Toblerone, the Swiss chocolate bar famous for its triangular peaks, noticed something strange about the bars sold in Britain. The peaks were further apart than before, leaving wide, empty gaps between them. The maker explained that rising costs had forced it to reduce the weight of its 400-gram bar to 360 grams while keeping the price the same. Many customers were furious, and the new shape was mocked across social media.\n\nThe Toblerone case is a famous example of what economists call \"shrinkflation,\" a combination of \"shrink\" and \"inflation.\" Instead of raising the price of a product, a company quietly reduces the amount inside the package. A bag of potato chips might contain a few grams less, a roll of toilet paper might have fewer sheets, or a bottle of shampoo might hold slightly less liquid. Because the package often looks almost identical, many shoppers never notice the change.\n\nWhy do companies choose this approach? When the cost of ingredients, energy or transport rises, firms face a difficult decision. Research suggests that consumers are far more sensitive to changes in price than to changes in quantity; a price rise from NT$35 to NT$39 is obvious, but a drop from 50 grams to 45 grams usually goes unnoticed. Shrinkflation therefore allows companies to protect their profits while avoiding the bad publicity that a visible price increase might bring. A related practice, sometimes called \"skimpflation,\" involves replacing ingredients with cheaper ones instead of reducing the size.\n\nCritics argue that this is a form of deception, since it takes advantage of the fact that people rarely read the small print on a package. Some retailers and governments have started to fight back. In 2023, the French supermarket chain Carrefour put warning labels on products whose size had shrunk while their price had not, in an attempt to pressure suppliers. France later introduced rules requiring stores to inform shoppers about such changes.\n\nFor consumers, the best defense is to look beyond the price tag. Checking the unit price, such as the cost per 100 grams, makes it much easier to compare products fairly and to spot when a familiar item has quietly become worse value. It may take a few extra seconds in the supermarket, but at a time of rising prices, those seconds could save a surprising amount of money over a year.", "passage_zh": "2016 年 11 月，Toblerone 的愛好者發現在英國販售的巧克力有些奇怪。Toblerone 是以三角形「山峰」造型聞名的瑞士巧克力，但這時山峰之間的距離比以前更遠，中間留下又寬又空的缺口。製造商解釋，成本上漲迫使他們把 400 公克的巧克力減為 360 公克，價格則維持不變。許多顧客非常憤怒，新的造型也在社群媒體上遭到嘲笑。\n\nToblerone 事件是經濟學家所說「縮水式通膨」（shrinkflation）的著名例子，這個字結合了「縮小」（shrink）與「通貨膨脹」（inflation）。公司不調漲產品價格，而是悄悄減少包裝裡的分量。一包洋芋片可能少了幾公克，一捲衛生紙的張數可能變少，一瓶洗髮精裝的量可能稍微少一點。由於包裝往往看起來幾乎一模一樣，許多消費者從來沒有察覺到這種改變。\n\n公司為什麼選擇這種做法？當原料、能源或運輸成本上漲時，企業面臨艱難的抉擇。研究顯示，消費者對價格變化的敏感程度遠高於對分量變化的敏感程度；價格從新台幣 35 元漲到 39 元一眼就看得出來，但分量從 50 公克減為 45 公克通常沒人注意。因此，縮水式通膨讓公司能保住利潤，同時避免明顯漲價可能帶來的負面形象。另一種相關的做法有時被稱為「偷工式通膨」（skimpflation），也就是不減少分量，而是改用較便宜的原料。\n\n批評者認為這是一種欺騙，因為它利用了人們很少閱讀包裝上小字的事實。一些零售商和政府已經開始反擊。2023 年，法國連鎖超市家樂福在分量縮水、價格卻沒有降低的商品上貼上警示標籤，試圖向供應商施壓。法國後來也制定規定，要求商店告知消費者這類變動。\n\n對消費者來說，最好的防禦方法是不要只看標價。查看單位價格，例如每 100 公克的價格，就能更容易公平地比較商品，並發現熟悉的商品何時悄悄變得不划算。在超市裡這也許要多花幾秒鐘，但在物價上漲的時代，這幾秒鐘一年下來可能幫你省下驚人的一筆錢。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer begin the passage with the story of Toblerone?", "options": ["To compare Swiss chocolate with British chocolate", "To complain about the taste of the new chocolate bar", "To introduce a well-known example of shrinkflation", "To show that social media can force companies to lower prices"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段描述 2016 年 Toblerone 巧克力減重但價格不變，第二段隨即說 \"The Toblerone case is a famous example of what economists call 'shrinkflation'\"，可知作者用它引出縮水式通膨的著名例子。(A) 文中沒有比較兩國巧克力；(B) 民眾不滿的是造型與分量，並非口味；(D) 社群媒體只是嘲笑新造型，並未讓價格下降。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why do companies often prefer shrinkflation to raising prices?", "options": ["Shoppers notice price changes much more easily than changes in quantity.", "Smaller packages are cheaper to advertise on television.", "Governments do not allow companies to raise their prices.", "Customers prefer smaller packages that are easier to carry."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 \"consumers are far more sensitive to changes in price than to changes in quantity\"，並舉例漲 4 元一眼可見，少 5 公克卻沒人注意，說明消費者對價格比對分量敏感得多。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"deception\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["celebration", "competition", "protection", "trickery"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說批評者認為這是一種 deception，因為它 \"takes advantage of the fact that people rarely read the small print\"（利用人們很少看包裝小字的事實），可知是「欺騙」，與 trickery（詐術、欺騙）意思最接近。(A) 慶祝、(B) 競爭、(C) 保護皆不符上下文。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would be an example of \"skimpflation\"?", "options": ["A drink company raises the price of a bottle of tea from NT$25 to NT$30.", "A cookie maker replaces butter with a cheaper oil but keeps the same price and size.", "A supermarket puts a warning label on a smaller bag of chips.", "A shampoo bottle is made smaller and sold at a lower price."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段最後一句說 skimpflation 是 \"replacing ingredients with cheaper ones instead of reducing the size\"，即分量不變而改用較便宜的原料；(B) 把奶油換成較便宜的油、價格與大小不變，正符合定義。(A) 是直接漲價；(C) 是零售商的反制做法；(D) 分量變少但價格也降低，不算隱形漲價。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer advise shoppers to do?", "options": ["Compare the unit prices of products.", "Avoid buying chocolate made by foreign companies.", "Complain to the supermarket about every price rise.", "Buy only products sold in large packages."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段 \"Checking the unit price, such as the cost per 100 grams, makes it much easier to compare products fairly\"，作者建議查看並比較單位價格。(B) 文中沒有叫人避免外國巧克力；(C) 沒有建議向超市抱怨；(D) 沒有建議只買大包裝。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a120", "title": "Easy to Join, Hard to Leave: Inside the Subscription Economy", "title_zh": "加入容易，退出難：走進訂閱經濟", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Business", "Technology"], "passage": "Not long ago, people bought music on CDs, software in boxes and films on DVDs. Once they had paid, the product was theirs to keep. Today, a growing share of what we spend goes to subscriptions instead: monthly fees for streaming music and video, cloud storage, mobile games, language-learning apps and even razors or pet food delivered to the door. Economists call this shift the \"subscription economy,\" and it has changed the relationship between companies and customers in important ways.\n\nFor businesses, the attraction is obvious. A one-time sale brings in money only once, but a subscriber pays again and again, providing a steady and predictable income. When the software company Adobe stopped selling boxed copies of its popular design programs in 2013 and switched to monthly plans, many users complained loudly. Yet the company's income became more stable and eventually grew far larger, and countless other firms have since followed a similar path.\n\nCustomers also gain some benefits. Instead of paying a large sum up front, they can try a service cheaply, receive regular updates and cancel when they no longer need it, at least in theory. In practice, cancelling is not always as simple as signing up. Some services offer a free trial that automatically turns into a paid plan unless the user remembers to cancel. Others hide the \"cancel\" button deep inside account settings, or require customers to make a phone call during office hours.\n\nDesigners have a name for these tricks: \"dark patterns.\" The term was created in 2010 by the British user-experience designer Harry Brignull to describe website and app designs that push people into decisions they might not otherwise make. One of the patterns he identified is the \"roach motel,\" named after a type of insect trap: it is easy to get in, but very hard to get out. Because small monthly fees are easy to forget, many people keep paying for services they hardly use.\n\nGovernments have begun to respond. Germany, for example, has required online businesses since 2022 to provide a clear cancellation button, so that ending a contract is as easy as starting one. Individuals can protect themselves too. Setting a reminder before a free trial ends, checking bank statements every month and asking \"Have I used this in the last thirty days?\" are simple habits that can prevent a few small fees from quietly turning into a large yearly expense.", "passage_zh": "不久以前，人們還是買 CD 聽音樂、買盒裝軟體、買 DVD 看電影。只要付了錢，產品就歸自己所有。如今，我們的花費中有越來越大的比例改用在訂閱上：音樂和影片串流、雲端儲存空間、手機遊戲、語言學習應用程式，甚至是送貨到府的刮鬍刀或寵物飼料，每個月都要付費。經濟學家把這種轉變稱為「訂閱經濟」，它在許多重要方面改變了企業與顧客之間的關係。\n\n對企業來說，吸引力顯而易見。一次性的銷售只帶來一次收入，但訂閱者會一再付費，提供穩定且可預測的收入。2013 年，軟體公司 Adobe 停止販售旗下熱門設計軟體的盒裝版本，改為按月付費的方案，當時許多使用者大聲抱怨。然而，這家公司的收入變得更穩定，最後還大幅成長，此後也有無數企業走上類似的路。\n\n顧客也能得到一些好處。他們不必一開始就付一大筆錢，而可以用便宜的價格試用服務、定期獲得更新，不再需要時就取消——至少理論上是如此。實際上，取消並不總是像註冊那麼簡單。有些服務提供免費試用，除非使用者記得取消，否則會自動轉為付費方案。還有些服務把「取消」按鈕藏在帳號設定的深處，或要求顧客在上班時間打電話辦理。\n\n設計師對這些伎倆有個名稱：「黑暗模式」。這個詞是英國使用者體驗設計師哈利‧布里格納爾在 2010 年提出的，用來描述那些把人推向原本可能不會做的決定的網站與應用程式設計。他指出的模式之一叫做「蟑螂屋」，名稱來自一種捕蟲陷阱：進去很容易，出來卻非常困難。由於每月的小額費用很容易被遺忘，許多人持續為自己幾乎沒在用的服務付錢。\n\n各國政府已開始採取行動。例如德國自 2022 年起，要求線上業者提供清楚的解約按鈕，讓終止合約和簽訂合約一樣容易。個人也可以保護自己。在免費試用結束前設定提醒、每個月檢查銀行對帳單，並問自己「過去三十天我用過這個嗎？」這些簡單的習慣，可以避免幾筆小小的費用悄悄累積成一大筆年度支出。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Subscriptions are always cheaper than buying products outright.", "Adobe was the first company in the world to sell software by subscription.", "Dark patterns are used only by gyms and mobile game companies.", "Subscriptions can benefit both sides, but some companies make it hard for customers to leave."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段介紹訂閱經濟，第二、三段分別說明企業與顧客的好處，第三、四段指出取消困難的「黑暗模式」，第五段提出因應方法，故主旨為 (D)。(A) 文中沒說訂閱一定比較便宜；(B) 文中只說 Adobe 改為按月方案，沒說它是第一家；(C) 文中並未說黑暗模式只用於健身房和遊戲公司。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happened after Adobe switched to monthly plans?", "options": ["It soon started selling boxed copies again.", "Its income became steadier and later grew much larger.", "Most of its users stopped using its programs.", "The government asked it to lower its prices."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段 \"Yet the company's income became more stable and eventually grew far larger\"，即收入更穩定，後來大幅成長。(A) 文中未提及恢復販售盒裝版；(C) 文中只說許多使用者抱怨，並未說多數人停用；(D) 未提及政府要求降價。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The \"roach motel\" mentioned in paragraph 4 refers to a design that ______.", "options": ["lets users try a service for free forever", "shows customers the cheapest plans first", "makes joining easy but leaving difficult", "sends reminders before a payment is due"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段解釋 roach motel 的特點是 \"it is easy to get in, but very hard to get out\"，即加入容易、離開困難。(A)(B) 與定義無關；(D) 反而是第五段建議的自保方法。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of a \"dark pattern\" described in the passage?", "options": ["A free trial that becomes a paid plan unless the user remembers to cancel", "A reminder email sent three days before a free trial ends", "A clear cancel button on a website's main page", "A one-time price for a music CD"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段提到免費試用 \"automatically turns into a paid plan unless the user remembers to cancel\"，第四段接著說 \"Designers have a name for these tricks: 'dark patterns.'\"，可知 (A) 屬於黑暗模式。(B)(C) 都是幫助使用者的設計，與黑暗模式相反；(D) 是第一段提到的傳統一次性購買。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is the purpose of the last paragraph?", "options": ["To explain why Germany's economy has grown quickly since 2022", "To argue that people should stop using subscriptions completely", "To show ways that governments and individuals can deal with the problem", "To advertise a new app that checks bank statements"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段先說政府的做法（德國要求提供清楚的解約按鈕），再說個人可以設定提醒、每月檢查帳單等習慣，目的在說明政府與個人如何因應這個問題。(A) 文中未談德國經濟；(B) 作者並未主張完全停止訂閱；(D) 文中沒有推銷任何應用程式。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a121", "title": "The Snowball Effect: Why Time Is a Young Saver's Best Friend", "title_zh": "雪球效應：為什麼時間是年輕儲蓄者最好的朋友", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Money", "Education"], "passage": "A popular story claims that Albert Einstein once called compound interest \"the eighth wonder of the world.\" There is no evidence that he ever said it, but the idea behind the quotation is worth taking seriously. Compound interest is one of the most powerful forces in personal finance, and understanding it early can make an enormous difference to a person's future.\n\nThe principle is simple. With simple interest, you earn interest only on the money you originally saved. With compound interest, you also earn interest on the interest you have already received. Imagine putting NT$10,000 into an account that pays 10 percent a year. After one year you have NT$11,000. In the second year, however, you earn 10 percent of NT$11,000, not of NT$10,000, so you end up with NT$12,100, and after the third year, NT$13,310. Like a snowball rolling down a hill, the sum grows faster the bigger it becomes.\n\nThe most important ingredient in this process is time. Consider two friends who both invest NT$3,000 a month and earn an average return of 6 percent a year. Amy starts at 20, invests for just ten years and then stops completely. Ben waits until he is 30 and then invests every month for thirty years. By the age of 60, Ben has put in three times as much money as Amy: NT$1,080,000 compared with NT$360,000. Yet the two end up with roughly the same amount, close to NT$3 million each, with Ben only slightly ahead. Amy's early start gave her money ten extra years to multiply.\n\nBenjamin Franklin understood this idea well. When he died in 1790, he left £1,000 each to the cities of Boston and Philadelphia, with instructions that the money be lent to young craftsmen at interest and that part of it be left to grow for a full two hundred years. The funds did not grow as much as Franklin had predicted, but by 1990 they were worth several million dollars in total.\n\nThere is, however, a darker side to compounding. The same mathematics works against people who borrow money, especially on credit cards, where unpaid debt can grow quickly as interest is charged on interest. A useful shortcut called the \"rule of 72\" shows how fast this happens: divide 72 by the interest rate to estimate how many years it takes for an amount to double. At 6 percent, savings double in about twelve years; at 12 percent, a debt doubles in only about six. For young people, the lesson is clear: start saving early, and avoid debt that compounds against you.", "passage_zh": "有個流傳很廣的說法，聲稱愛因斯坦曾把複利稱為「世界第八大奇蹟」。沒有證據顯示他真的說過這句話，但這句話背後的概念值得認真看待。複利是個人理財中最強大的力量之一，及早了解它，可能讓一個人的未來大不相同。\n\n原理很簡單。單利只會以你最初存的錢來計算利息；複利則連你已經得到的利息也會再生利息。想像你把新台幣一萬元存進一個年利率 10% 的帳戶。一年後你有 11,000 元。但到了第二年，你賺的是 11,000 元的 10%，而不是 10,000 元的 10%，所以最後會有 12,100 元，第三年結束後則有 13,310 元。就像雪球從山坡上滾下來，金額越大，增加得越快。\n\n這個過程最重要的元素是時間。想想兩個朋友，他們每個月都投資 3,000 元，平均年報酬率都是 6%。艾咪從 20 歲開始，只投資十年就完全停止；阿斌等到 30 歲才開始，接著連續三十年每個月投資。到了 60 歲，阿斌投入的錢是艾咪的三倍：108 萬元對 36 萬元。然而，兩人最後的金額大致相同，都接近 300 萬元，阿斌只稍微多一點。艾咪提早起步，讓她的錢多了十年的時間不斷倍增。\n\n班傑明‧富蘭克林很了解這個道理。他在 1790 年過世時，分別留給波士頓和費城各 1,000 英鎊，並指示這筆錢要借給年輕工匠收取利息，其中一部分還要整整累積成長兩百年。這兩筆基金的成長雖然不如富蘭克林預測的那麼多，但到了 1990 年，兩者合計已價值數百萬美元。\n\n不過，複利也有黑暗的一面。同樣的數學原理對借錢的人不利，尤其是信用卡，未繳清的欠款因為利息還會再生利息，可能很快就越滾越大。一個叫做「72 法則」的實用捷徑可以看出這速度有多快：用 72 除以利率，就能估算一筆金額需要幾年會翻倍。利率 6% 時，存款大約十二年翻倍；利率 12% 時，債務只要大約六年就會翻倍。對年輕人來說，教訓很清楚：及早開始儲蓄，並避免會讓複利對你不利的債務。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the writer say about the quotation attributed to Einstein?", "options": ["It may not be real, but the idea it expresses is important.", "Einstein said it in a famous speech about banks.", "It proves that compound interest was invented by a scientist.", "It is only a joke and should not be taken seriously."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段 \"There is no evidence that he ever said it, but the idea behind the quotation is worth taking seriously\"，即這句話未必是愛因斯坦說的，但其概念很重要。(B) 文中未提到演講；(C) 文中沒說複利是科學家發明的；(D) 作者明確說值得認真看待，並非玩笑。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "If the account in paragraph 2 kept paying 10 percent a year, how much would there be after the fourth year?", "options": ["NT$14,000", "NT$14,310", "NT$14,641", "NT$16,000"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段說明每年都以前一年的總額計算 10% 利息，第三年結束時有 13,310 元，第四年再加上 13,310 元的 10%（1,331 元），共 14,641 元。(A)(B) 是每年只加固定金額的錯誤算法；(D) 差距太大。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the example of Amy and Ben mainly show?", "options": ["Ben was wiser because he put in more money in total.", "Starting early can matter more than the total amount invested.", "Investing for ten years is always better than investing for thirty.", "A 6 percent return is too low to make any real difference."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段艾咪只投入 36 萬元，阿斌投入 108 萬元，兩人最後的金額卻大致相同，最後一句 \"Amy's early start gave her money ten extra years to multiply\" 說明提早開始比投入的總額更關鍵。(A) 與文意相反；(C) 過度推論，文中阿斌最後仍稍微多一點；(D) 文中未說 6% 太低。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"multiply\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["disappear", "be divided", "be spent", "increase greatly"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段 \"gave her money ten extra years to multiply\"，指錢在多出的十年中不斷「大幅增加」，與 increase greatly 最接近。(A) 消失、(B) 被分割、(C) 被花掉，都與複利讓錢成長的文意相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the \"rule of 72,\" about how long would it take for a debt to double at an interest rate of 9 percent?", "options": ["4 years", "8 years", "9 years", "12 years"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說明 72 法則：用 72 除以利率，可估算金額翻倍所需的年數，72 ÷ 9 = 8 年。(A) 是利率 18% 時的結果；(C) 誤把利率當成年數；(D) 是利率 6% 時的年數。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a122", "title": "Why the Price Keeps Changing: The Rise of Dynamic Pricing", "title_zh": "價格為什麼一直變？動態定價的興起", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Business", "Money"], "passage": "On 31 August 2024, millions of music fans in Britain and Ireland went online to buy tickets for the long-awaited reunion tour of the rock band Oasis. After waiting for hours in online queues, many were shocked to find that some standing tickets first listed at around £148 now cost more than £350. The reason was \"in-demand pricing,\" a system in which prices rise automatically when many people want to buy at the same time. The outcry was so loud that Britain's competition regulator opened an investigation within days.\n\nThis practice, known as dynamic pricing, is far from new. Airlines began using computer programs to adjust fares in the 1980s, and today it is normal for the same seat on the same flight to cost very different amounts depending on when it is booked. Hotels do the same during holidays, and ride-hailing apps such as Uber raise fares during rainstorms or rush hours. What has changed is the scale and speed. With powerful algorithms and huge amounts of data, companies can now adjust prices minute by minute, and some supermarkets have installed electronic shelf labels that can be updated instantly.\n\nSupporters argue that dynamic pricing is simply the law of supply and demand in action. When demand is high, higher prices encourage more supply (more drivers decide to work during a storm, for example) while discouraging people whose need is less urgent. When demand is low, prices fall, filling empty seats and rooms that would otherwise earn nothing. In theory, the people who value a product most highly are the ones who get it.\n\nCritics, however, point out several problems. First, fans of a band or travelers in an emergency often have little choice, so higher prices may feel less like a market at work and more like being taken advantage of. Second, buyers frequently do not know the rules, so they cannot plan ahead or compare offers fairly. Most worrying of all, as companies collect more personal data, there is a risk that prices could be set not by overall demand but by what each individual customer seems willing to pay.\n\nWhether dynamic pricing is fair may depend largely on transparency. Many economists believe that people accept changing prices more easily when they understand in advance how and why those prices will change. For consumers, the practical lesson is to book early when possible, compare prices at different times, and remember that the first price shown is not always the only one available.", "passage_zh": "2024 年 8 月 31 日，英國和愛爾蘭數百萬名樂迷上網搶購搖滾樂團綠洲合唱團（Oasis）期待已久的重組巡迴演唱會門票。在網路上排隊等了好幾個小時後，許多人震驚地發現，有些一開始標價約 148 英鎊的站票，竟然漲到超過 350 英鎊。原因是所謂的「熱門需求定價」，也就是當許多人同時想購買時，價格就會自動上漲的制度。抗議聲浪大到英國的公平競爭主管機關在幾天之內就展開調查。\n\n這種被稱為「動態定價」的做法其實一點也不新。航空公司從 1980 年代就開始用電腦程式調整票價，如今同一班飛機上的同一個座位，依訂票時間不同而價格相差很多，已是常態。飯店在假期期間也會這麼做，而像 Uber 這類叫車應用程式，在暴雨或尖峰時段也會提高車資。改變的是規模與速度。有了強大的演算法和龐大的資料，企業現在可以每分鐘調整價格，有些超市也已安裝能即時更新的電子貨架標籤。\n\n支持者認為，動態定價只不過是供需法則的實際運作。需求高時，較高的價格會鼓勵增加供給（例如有更多司機決定在暴風雨中出來工作），同時讓需求沒那麼急迫的人打退堂鼓。需求低時，價格下降，填滿原本毫無收入的空座位與空房間。理論上，最重視某項產品的人就能得到它。\n\n然而，批評者指出了幾個問題。第一，樂團的歌迷或遇到緊急狀況的旅客往往沒什麼選擇，因此價格上漲給人的感覺，與其說是市場在運作，不如說是被占便宜。第二，買方往往不知道規則，因此無法事先規劃，也無法公平地比較各種方案。最令人擔心的是，隨著企業蒐集越來越多個人資料，價格可能不再由整體需求決定，而是由每位顧客看起來願意付多少錢來決定。\n\n動態定價是否公平，可能很大程度取決於透明度。許多經濟學家認為，如果人們事先了解價格會如何變動、為什麼會變動，就比較容易接受價格的變化。對消費者來說，實用的教訓是：盡可能及早訂購、在不同時間比較價格，並記住第一個看到的價格不一定是唯一的價格。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the first paragraph mainly describe?", "options": ["How Oasis became one of Britain's most popular bands", "Why online ticket queues are often extremely long", "A recent case that made many people angry about dynamic pricing", "The rules for buying standing tickets at large concerts"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段描述 2024 年 Oasis 演唱會門票因 \"in-demand pricing\" 從約 148 英鎊漲到超過 350 英鎊，\"The outcry was so loud...\" 引起強烈抗議，是讓許多人對動態定價感到憤怒的近期事件。(A) 文中未介紹樂團的成名經過；(B) 排隊只是背景細節；(D) 文中未說明購買站票的規則。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned in paragraph 2 as an example of dynamic pricing?", "options": ["Airline fares", "Hotel rooms", "Ride-hailing apps", "Train tickets"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段提到航空公司票價 (A)、假期期間的飯店 (B)、Uber 等叫車應用程式 (C)，以及超市的電子貨架標籤，但沒有提到火車票。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to supporters, how can higher prices during a rainstorm help?", "options": ["They encourage more drivers to work.", "They make rides safer for passengers.", "They reduce the drivers' working hours.", "They allow passengers to get free rides later."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段支持者的說法：\"higher prices encourage more supply (more drivers decide to work during a storm, for example)\"，即較高的價格鼓勵更多司機出來工作。(B) 安全、(C) 減少工時、(D) 免費搭乘，文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What do critics find \"most worrying\" about dynamic pricing?", "options": ["Electronic shelf labels may break down easily.", "Companies might charge each person a different price based on personal data.", "Concerts might be cancelled because demand is too low.", "Airlines might stop selling tickets online."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段 \"Most worrying of all... prices could be set not by overall demand but by what each individual customer seems willing to pay\"，最令人擔心的是企業利用個人資料，依每個人願意付的金額訂出不同價格。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"transparency\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["low cost", "speed", "popularity", "openness"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段說人們若 \"understand in advance how and why those prices will change\" 就比較能接受價格變化，可知 transparency 指規則公開、讓人看得清楚，即「透明度」，與 openness 最接近。(A) 低成本、(B) 速度、(C) 受歡迎程度皆不符上下文。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a123", "title": "What a Burger Can Tell Us About Money: The Big Mac Index", "title_zh": "一個漢堡能告訴我們什麼？大麥克指數", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Money", "Business"], "passage": "Economists often explain difficult ideas with numbers and charts, but in 1986 the British magazine The Economist chose something tastier: a hamburger. That year, it introduced the Big Mac Index, a light-hearted way of comparing currencies around the world by looking at the price of a McDonald's Big Mac in different countries. Decades later, the index is still published regularly and has become one of the best-known tools for teaching basic economics.\n\nThe index is based on a theory called purchasing power parity, or PPP. According to this theory, in the long run, exchange rates should move toward the level at which the same product costs the same everywhere. A Big Mac is a useful example because it is made to almost the same recipe in more than a hundred countries, using local ingredients and local workers in restaurants that pay local rent.\n\nThe calculation is simple. Imagine that a Big Mac costs US$6 in the United States and NT$90 in Taiwan. Dividing 90 by 6 gives an \"implied\" exchange rate of 15 Taiwan dollars to one US dollar. If the real exchange rate is 30 Taiwan dollars to one US dollar, a Taiwanese Big Mac costs only US$3, half the American price. By the logic of the index, the Taiwan dollar would be undervalued by 50 percent. In fact, the index has often suggested that the Taiwan dollar is among the most undervalued currencies in the world.\n\nOf course, the index has obvious weaknesses. The price of a burger depends heavily on local costs such as wages and rent, which are usually much lower in poorer countries, so cheaper burgers there do not necessarily mean that the currency is \"wrong.\" Tastes and eating habits also differ. In India, for example, where many people do not eat beef, McDonald's sells a chicken burger called the Maharaja Mac, which the index uses instead. To deal with some of these problems, The Economist also publishes an adjusted version that takes average income into account.\n\nNobody, not even its creators, claims that the Big Mac Index can accurately predict exchange rates. Its real value lies elsewhere. By turning an abstract theory into something everyone can picture, it makes economics approachable, and it reminds us that a currency's worth is ultimately measured by what it can actually buy.", "passage_zh": "經濟學家常用數字和圖表來解釋艱深的概念，但在 1986 年，英國雜誌《經濟學人》選了一樣比較美味的東西：漢堡。那一年，該雜誌推出了「大麥克指數」，用一種輕鬆的方式，觀察麥當勞大麥克在不同國家的價格，來比較世界各地的貨幣。數十年後，這個指數仍定期公布，並已成為教導基礎經濟學最知名的工具之一。\n\n這個指數是根據一個叫做「購買力平價」（PPP）的理論。根據這個理論，長期來說，匯率應該會朝著同一件產品在各地價格都相同的水準移動。大麥克之所以是個好例子，是因為它在一百多個國家都以幾乎相同的配方製作，而且使用當地的食材和當地的員工，餐廳也支付當地的租金。\n\n計算方式很簡單。假設一個大麥克在美國賣 6 美元，在台灣賣新台幣 90 元。90 除以 6，得到的「隱含」匯率是 15 元新台幣兌 1 美元。如果實際匯率是 30 元新台幣兌 1 美元，那麼台灣的大麥克只要 3 美元，是美國價格的一半。按照這個指數的邏輯，新台幣就被低估了 50%。事實上，這個指數經常顯示新台幣是全世界被低估最多的貨幣之一。\n\n當然，這個指數有明顯的缺點。漢堡的價格很大程度取決於工資和租金等當地成本，而這些成本在較貧窮的國家通常低得多，因此那裡的漢堡比較便宜，並不一定代表貨幣的價值「不對」。各地的口味和飲食習慣也不同。例如在許多人不吃牛肉的印度，麥當勞販售一種叫做「王公麥克」（Maharaja Mac）的雞肉漢堡，指數便改用它來計算。為了處理其中一些問題，《經濟學人》也公布一種將平均所得納入考量的調整版指數。\n\n沒有人——即使是它的創造者——宣稱大麥克指數能準確預測匯率。它真正的價值在別處。它把抽象的理論變成人人都能想像的東西，讓經濟學變得平易近人，也提醒我們：一種貨幣的價值，終究是由它實際能買到什麼來衡量。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why McDonald's has become popular in so many countries", "How a burger price index is used to compare currencies, and what its limits are", "How to calculate the cost of the ingredients in a Big Mac", "Why the Taiwan dollar is the strongest currency in Asia"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段介紹大麥克指數，第二、三段說明其理論基礎與計算方式，第四段指出缺點，第五段說明它真正的價值，故選 (B)。(A) 文中未討論麥當勞受歡迎的原因；(C) 計算的是匯率而非原料成本；(D) 第三段說新台幣常被認為「被低估」，並非最強勢。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the theory of purchasing power parity, what should happen in the long run?", "options": ["Exchange rates should move toward levels at which the same product costs the same everywhere.", "All countries should start using the same currency.", "The price of a Big Mac should rise by the same amount every year.", "Poorer countries should pay higher prices for imported food."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段 \"in the long run, exchange rates should move toward the level at which the same product costs the same everywhere\"，故選 (A)。(B) 文中未說各國應使用同一種貨幣；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Using the method in paragraph 3, if a Big Mac cost US$6 in the United States and 240 \"lums\" in an imaginary country, what would the implied exchange rate be?", "options": ["6 lums to one US dollar", "24 lums to one US dollar", "36 lums to one US dollar", "40 lums to one US dollar"], "answer": 3, "explain": "依第三段的方法，把當地價格除以美國價格即得隱含匯率（90 ÷ 6 = 15），因此 240 ÷ 6 = 40，即 40 lums 兌 1 美元。(A) 6 是美國的售價，並非匯率；(B)(C) 都不是 240 ÷ 6 的結果。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention that wages and rent are lower in poorer countries?", "options": ["To show why McDonald's earns more money in poorer countries", "To prove that the index predicts exchange rates accurately", "To explain why a cheap burger does not always mean a currency is undervalued", "To suggest that a Big Mac should cost the same everywhere"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說工資和租金在較貧窮國家低得多，\"so cheaper burgers there do not necessarily mean that the currency is 'wrong'\"，用來說明便宜的漢堡不一定代表貨幣被低估，這是指數的缺點。(A) 文中未談麥當勞的獲利；(B) 與第五段「沒有人宣稱它能準確預測匯率」相反；(D) 作者並無此主張。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"approachable\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["easy to understand", "expensive to study", "difficult to change", "impossible to predict"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段 \"By turning an abstract theory into something everyone can picture, it makes economics approachable\"，把抽象理論變成人人都能想像的東西，可知 approachable 指「容易理解、平易近人」。(B)(C)(D) 皆與上下文意思不符。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a124", "title": "Why Does the Medium Popcorn Exist? The Decoy Effect", "title_zh": "中桶爆米花為什麼存在？誘餌效應", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Business", "Science"], "passage": "Imagine you are buying popcorn at a cinema. A small bucket costs NT$60, a large one costs NT$120, and you hesitate: is it really worth paying double? Then you notice a medium bucket priced at NT$110. Suddenly the large one looks like a bargain, since for just ten dollars more you get far more popcorn. You walk away with the large bucket, feeling smart. What you may not realize is that the medium option was probably never meant to be chosen at all.\n\nMarketing experts call this the \"decoy effect.\" A decoy is an option that is clearly worse than one of the others, and its main job is to make that other option look attractive by comparison. The idea was first described in a 1982 study by the researchers Joel Huber, John Payne and Christopher Puto, who used the term \"asymmetric dominance.\" In plain language, the decoy is dominated, or beaten in every way, by the target option but not by the remaining one, so it pushes people toward the target.\n\nOne of the best-known demonstrations comes from the behavioral economist Dan Ariely, who described it in his 2008 book Predictably Irrational. He noticed that The Economist magazine had once offered three subscriptions: online only for $59, print only for $125, and print plus online for $125. When he presented these choices to 100 students at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 84 chose the combined package and 16 chose online only; nobody chose print only. When he removed the print-only option, however, the results almost reversed: 68 students chose online only, and just 32 chose the combined package. The option nobody wanted had changed what everyone else did.\n\nWhy does this work? People rarely judge value in absolute terms. Instead, they compare options with each other, especially when they are unsure what something should cost. A decoy provides an easy comparison, giving us a reason to feel confident about a choice that we might otherwise question.\n\nDecoys appear in many places, from phone plans and gym memberships to the sizes of coffee cups. Knowing about them will not stop companies from using them, but it can help you make better decisions. Before choosing, try ignoring the option that seems to exist only for comparison, and ask whether you would still pick the same item if there were only two choices. Sometimes the small popcorn is exactly what you wanted.", "passage_zh": "想像你正在電影院買爆米花。小桶賣新台幣 60 元，大桶賣 120 元，你猶豫了：真的值得付兩倍的錢嗎？接著你注意到中桶標價 110 元。突然間，大桶看起來很划算，因為只要多付十元，就能得到多得多的爆米花。你買了大桶離開，覺得自己很精明。你可能沒意識到的是，中桶這個選項很可能從一開始就不是要讓人選的。\n\n行銷專家把這稱為「誘餌效應」。誘餌是一個明顯比其中某個選項差的選項，它的主要任務是透過比較，讓那個選項看起來很有吸引力。這個概念最早出現在研究人員喬爾‧休伯、約翰‧佩恩和克里斯多福‧普托 1982 年的一項研究中，他們用的術語是「不對稱支配」。說得淺白一點，誘餌被目標選項「支配」，也就是在各方面都輸給目標選項，卻沒有輸給剩下的那個選項，因此它會把人推向目標選項。\n\n最有名的實例之一來自行為經濟學家丹‧艾瑞利，他在 2008 年的著作《誰說人是理性的！》中描述了這個實驗。他注意到《經濟學人》雜誌曾提供三種訂閱方案：只訂電子版 59 美元、只訂紙本 125 美元、紙本加電子版 125 美元。當他把這些選項拿給麻省理工學院的 100 名學生選擇時，84 人選了組合方案，16 人選了只訂電子版，沒有任何人選只訂紙本。然而，當他拿掉只訂紙本的選項後，結果幾乎反轉：68 名學生選擇只訂電子版，只有 32 人選擇組合方案。那個沒人要的選項，改變了其他所有人的選擇。\n\n為什麼這招有效？人們很少用絕對的標準來判斷價值，而是會把選項互相比較，尤其是當他們不確定某樣東西該值多少錢的時候。誘餌提供了一個簡單的比較對象，讓我們有理由對一個原本可能會懷疑的選擇感到有信心。\n\n誘餌出現在許多地方，從手機方案、健身房會員到咖啡杯的大小都有。知道誘餌的存在並不能阻止企業使用它們，但能幫助你做出更好的決定。做選擇之前，試著忽略那個似乎只為了比較而存在的選項，並問問自己：如果只有兩個選擇，你還會挑同一樣東西嗎？有時候，小桶爆米花正是你想要的。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer begin with the example of buying popcorn?", "options": ["To complain that snacks at cinemas are too expensive", "To compare popcorn prices in different countries", "To explain how cinemas make their popcorn", "To show how a decoy can influence an everyday choice"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段以電影院爆米花為例，中桶 110 元的存在讓大桶看起來很划算，最後一句指出中桶 \"was probably never meant to be chosen at all\"，第二段隨即點出這就是誘餌效應。(A) 作者並未抱怨價格；(B) 沒有比較不同國家；(C) 沒有談到爆米花的製作方式。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In Dan Ariely's experiment, which option did no students choose?", "options": ["Online only", "Print plus online", "Print only", "A free trial"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段 \"nobody chose print only\"，沒有學生選擇只訂紙本。(A) 有 16 人選擇；(B) 有 84 人選擇；(D) 文中沒有免費試用這個選項。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the results of Ariely's experiment?", "options": ["Most of the students preferred reading magazines on paper.", "The print-only option made the combined package look like better value.", "Students almost always choose the cheapest option available.", "The magazine lost a lot of money because of the experiment."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段有只訂紙本的選項時，84 人選組合方案；拿掉之後只剩 32 人選組合方案，可推論同樣 125 美元的只訂紙本（誘餌）讓組合方案看起來更划算。(A) 只訂紙本無人選擇，無法推論學生偏好紙本；(C) 有誘餌時多數學生選了較貴的方案；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 2, the word \"dominated\" means that the decoy is ______.", "options": ["worse than the target option in every way", "more popular than any other option", "always the most expensive choice", "controlled by the government"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段 \"the decoy is dominated, or beaten in every way, by the target option\"，用 or 引出的 beaten in every way 正是解釋 dominated，指誘餌在各方面都比目標選項差。(B) 誘餌通常沒人選；(C) 文中未說誘餌一定最貴；(D) 與政府無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Always buy the largest size available.", "Stay away from cinemas and gyms that use decoys.", "Imagine the choice without the option that seems to exist only for comparison.", "Ask the shop staff to point out which option is the decoy."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段 \"try ignoring the option that seems to exist only for comparison, and ask whether you would still pick the same item if there were only two choices\"，建議想像拿掉那個只為比較而存在的選項再做決定。(A) 與最後一句「小桶可能正是你想要的」相反；(B) 作者並未要人避開這些地方；(D) 文中未提及詢問店員。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a125", "title": "Every Receipt a Lottery Ticket: Taiwan's Uniform Invoice Lottery", "title_zh": "每張發票都是彩券：台灣的統一發票對獎", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Money", "Society"], "passage": "Visitors to Taiwan are often puzzled by a small ritual that takes place every other month. On the 25th of each odd-numbered month, people across the island check the newly announced winning numbers, carefully comparing them with the stack of receipts they have collected from convenience stores, restaurants and night-market stalls. Some even keep a special box at home just for receipts. For a lucky few, a single slip of paper from buying a bottle of tea can be worth as much as NT$10 million.\n\nThese receipts are known as uniform invoices, and the system behind them dates back to 1951. At that time, the government faced a serious problem: many businesses were underreporting their sales in order to pay less tax. Since officials had little way of checking how much each shop actually sold, a large amount of tax money was being lost. Requiring every sale to be recorded on an official, numbered invoice was the solution, but only if customers actually asked for one.\n\nThat is where the lottery comes in. By turning each invoice into a free lottery ticket, the government gave ordinary shoppers a personal reason to demand a receipt every time they paid. In effect, millions of consumers became unpaid tax inspectors, making it far more difficult for a shop to hide its income. The prize money comes from tax revenue, but the government considers the cost worthwhile, since the system helps it collect far more tax than it pays out.\n\nThe rules are straightforward. Each draw covers invoices from the previous two months, and winners have three months, starting from the 6th of the following month, to claim their prizes. The top two prizes, of NT$10 million and NT$2 million, require all eight digits to match a particular number, while matching just the last three digits of one of the first-prize numbers wins NT$200. Many people donate their invoices to charities, which collect them in boxes at shop counters and claim any winnings to support their work.\n\nIn recent years, the system has gone digital. Shoppers can now store \"cloud invoices\" by showing a barcode on their phones, and prize money can be transferred automatically to their bank accounts, saving paper and the trouble of checking by hand. The idea has even spread abroad: Portugal, among other countries, has introduced its own receipt lottery. More than seventy years after it began, Taiwan's invoice lottery remains a clever example of how a small reward can change the behavior of millions.", "passage_zh": "來台灣的外國旅客，常對一個每兩個月上演一次的小儀式感到困惑。每逢單數月的 25 日，全台各地的民眾都會查看剛公布的中獎號碼，仔細比對他們從便利商店、餐廳和夜市攤販蒐集來的一疊發票。有些人甚至在家裡準備一個專門放發票的盒子。對少數幸運兒來說，買一瓶茶拿到的一張小紙條，價值可能高達新台幣一千萬元。\n\n這些收據稱為「統一發票」，背後的制度可追溯到 1951 年。當時政府面臨一個嚴重的問題：許多商家為了少繳稅而短報營業額。由於官員幾乎沒有辦法查核每家店實際賣了多少，大量稅收因此流失。解決方法是規定每筆交易都要記錄在正式且有編號的發票上——但前提是顧客真的會索取發票。\n\n這就是對獎發揮作用的地方。政府把每張發票變成一張免費的彩券，讓一般消費者在每次付錢時都有了索取發票的切身理由。實際上，數百萬名消費者成了不支薪的稅務稽查員，讓商店要隱瞞收入變得困難許多。獎金來自稅收，但政府認為這筆花費很值得，因為這個制度幫它收到的稅遠多於它發出的獎金。\n\n規則很簡單。每次開獎涵蓋前兩個月的發票，中獎者從次月 6 日起有三個月的時間可以領獎。最高的兩個獎項分別是一千萬元和兩百萬元，八位數字都必須與特定號碼完全相同；而只要末三碼與任一組頭獎號碼相同，就能獲得 200 元。許多人會把發票捐給慈善團體，這些團體在商店櫃檯放置捐贈箱蒐集發票，並領取中獎獎金來支持他們的工作。\n\n近年來，這個制度已經數位化。消費者現在可以在手機上出示條碼，把發票存成「雲端發票」，獎金還能自動匯入銀行帳戶，既節省紙張，也省去親手對獎的麻煩。這個構想甚至傳到了國外：葡萄牙等國家也推出了自己的收據抽獎制度。實施七十多年後，台灣的發票對獎依然是一個巧妙的例子，說明小小的獎勵如何能改變數百萬人的行為。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain how Taiwan's invoice lottery works and why it was created", "To encourage readers to buy more lottery tickets", "To compare Taiwan's tax system with Portugal's", "To complain about the amount of paper used for receipts"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段描述民眾對發票的情景，第二段說明制度在 1951 年為解決逃漏稅而設立，第三段解釋對獎的作用，第四、五段說明規則與數位化發展，故目的是說明發票對獎如何運作及設立原因。(B) 文中並未鼓勵買彩券；(C) 葡萄牙只在第五段順帶提到；(D) 文中提到雲端發票可節省紙張，但並非抱怨。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what problem was the uniform invoice system designed to solve?", "options": ["Shops were charging customers too much.", "Customers kept losing their receipts.", "There were not enough stores in Taiwan.", "Businesses were hiding part of their sales to pay less tax."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段 \"many businesses were underreporting their sales in order to pay less tax\"，即商家為了少繳稅而短報營業額。(A)(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Jason's invoices from May and June were included in the draw on July 25. From what date can he start claiming a prize?", "options": ["June 25", "July 6", "August 6", "October 6"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段 \"winners have three months, starting from the 6th of the following month, to claim their prizes\"，7 月 25 日開獎，下個月 6 日即 8 月 6 日起可以領獎（至 11 月 5 日止）。(A)(B) 都在開獎之前；(D) 雖在領獎期限內，但不是開始領獎的日期。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by saying that consumers became \"unpaid tax inspectors\"?", "options": ["Shoppers were hired by the government to visit shops.", "By asking for receipts, shoppers helped make sure businesses reported their sales.", "Shoppers had to pay extra tax on every purchase they made.", "Shoppers were fined if they did not keep their receipts."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段說對獎讓消費者有 \"a personal reason to demand a receipt every time they paid\"，因此 \"making it far more difficult for a shop to hide its income\"，即消費者索取發票，等於幫政府確保商家如實申報營業額，就像不支薪的稽查員。(A) 消費者並非受僱於政府；(C)(D) 文中未提到多繳稅或罰款。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true according to the passage?", "options": ["Only people who have lived in Taiwan for over a year can win prizes.", "To win the top prize, only the last three digits need to match.", "Paper invoices are no longer used in Taiwan.", "Prize money for cloud invoices can be sent straight to a bank account."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段 \"prize money can be transferred automatically to their bank accounts\"，雲端發票的獎金可自動匯入銀行帳戶。(A) 文中未提居住期限；(B) 第四段說最高獎項須八位數字全部相同，末三碼相同只能得 200 元；(C) 文中只說可存成雲端發票，第四段仍提到捐贈紙本發票，並未說紙本已停用。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a126", "title": "Move 37: What a Machine Taught the Masters of Go", "title_zh": "第 37 手：一台機器給圍棋高手上的一課", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Technology", "Culture"], "passage": "In March 2016, an estimated 200 million people around the world tuned in as Lee Sedol, one of the greatest Go players of his generation, sat down in a Seoul hotel to face an opponent that could not feel nervous, tired or proud. The opponent was AlphaGo, a computer program developed by DeepMind, a British company owned by Google. Most experts expected Lee to win easily, and Lee himself said he expected to win 5-0, or perhaps 4-1. Go, an ancient board game from China, has more possible positions than there are atoms in the observable universe, so a computer cannot win simply by checking possibilities faster than a human.\n\nInstead of following rules written by programmers, AlphaGo learned from experience. It first studied about 30 million moves from games between strong human players, and then played millions of games against itself, gradually developing its own sense of which moves lead to victory. Then came the moment that made the match famous. In the second game, on its 37th move, AlphaGo placed a stone where almost no professional would consider playing. Commentators assumed it was a mistake, and Lee was so surprised that he took nearly fifteen minutes to respond. Yet as the game went on, it became clear that this strange stone had helped AlphaGo take control.\n\nAlphaGo won the match 4-1. Lee's single victory came in the fourth game, thanks to his own brilliant Move 78, which confused the program. Still, the result changed how people thought about creativity. Move 37 had not been copied from any human game; it was something new. If a machine could produce an idea that experts first dismissed and later admired, could it be called creative?\n\nFor Lee, the experience was painful. In 2019 he retired from professional Go, explaining that even if he became the top player, there would still be \"an entity that cannot be defeated.\" Many feared that the game itself would lose its meaning. However, something unexpected happened. Professional players began using AI programs as training partners, studying their unusual ideas instead of learning only from the old masters. A 2023 study of millions of moves by professional players found that the quality of human decisions improved noticeably after AlphaGo appeared, and that players began making more original moves.\n\nThe story of Move 37 offers a lesson that reaches beyond the board. AI may defeat people at tasks they once believed were uniquely human, but defeat is not the only possible outcome. When people treat these systems as teachers rather than simply as rivals, they can be pushed beyond familiar habits and discover possibilities they had never imagined. The challenge is to keep thinking for ourselves while learning from a machine that thinks very differently.", "passage_zh": "2016 年 3 月，全球估計約有兩億人守在螢幕前，看著李世乭在首爾一家飯店坐下，迎戰一個不會緊張、不會疲倦，也不會驕傲的對手。李世乭是他那一代最偉大的圍棋棋士之一，而他的對手是 AlphaGo——由 Google 旗下的英國公司 DeepMind 開發的電腦程式。大多數專家都預期李世乭會輕鬆獲勝，他本人也表示，預計自己會以五比零、或者四比一獲勝。圍棋是一種源自中國的古老棋類遊戲，可能出現的盤面比可觀測宇宙中的原子還要多，因此電腦無法只靠比人類更快地檢查各種可能性來取勝。\n\nAlphaGo 並不是依照程式設計師寫好的規則下棋，而是從經驗中學習。它先研究了約三千萬手高段人類棋士對弈的棋步，接著和自己下了數百萬盤棋，逐漸培養出自己對「哪些棋步能通往勝利」的判斷力。接著，讓這場比賽聲名大噪的時刻來了。在第二局的第 37 手，AlphaGo 把棋子下在一個幾乎沒有職業棋士會考慮的位置。講評者都以為那是失誤，李世乭則驚訝到花了將近十五分鐘才回應。然而隨著棋局進行，大家逐漸看清楚，正是這顆奇怪的棋子幫助 AlphaGo 掌握了局勢。\n\nAlphaGo 最後以四比一贏得這場比賽。李世乭唯一的勝利出現在第四局，靠的是他自己精彩的「第 78 手」，那一手讓程式陷入混亂。儘管如此，這個結果還是改變了人們對創造力的看法。第 37 手並不是從任何人類棋譜中模仿來的，而是全新的東西。如果一台機器能想出一個專家起初不屑一顧、後來卻讚嘆不已的點子，它能被稱為有創造力嗎？\n\n對李世乭來說，這次經驗很痛苦。2019 年他從職業圍棋界引退，解釋說即使自己成為頂尖棋士，仍然會有「一個無法被打敗的存在」。許多人擔心圍棋本身會因此失去意義。然而，意想不到的事情發生了。職業棋士開始把 AI 程式當成練習對手，研究它們不尋常的想法，而不再只向古代名家學習。一項在 2023 年針對職業棋士數百萬手棋步所做的研究發現，AlphaGo 出現之後，人類下棋決策的品質明顯提升，棋士們也開始下出更多具有原創性的棋步。\n\n第 37 手的故事所帶來的啟示，遠遠超出棋盤之外。AI 也許會在人們曾以為只有人類才能勝任的事情上打敗人類，但被打敗並不是唯一可能的結果。當人們把這些系統當作老師，而不只是對手時，就能被推離熟悉的習慣，發現自己從未想像過的可能性。真正的挑戰在於：一邊向一台思考方式和我們截然不同的機器學習，一邊仍然堅持獨立思考。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How AlphaGo's victory over a Go champion changed the way people play and think about the game", "Why Go is much harder for beginners to learn than chess", "How Lee Sedol prepared for his match against a computer program", "Why professional Go players should stop practicing with AI programs"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文從 2016 年 AlphaGo 與李世乭的對局談起，說明第 37 手引發對創造力的討論（第三段），以及職業棋士開始向 AI 學習、下棋品質提升（第四段），最後歸納出啟示。(B) 文中沒有比較圍棋與西洋棋的學習難度；(C) 文中未描述李世乭如何準備比賽；(D) 與第四、五段鼓勵把 AI 當老師的內容相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 1, why could a computer not win at Go simply by checking possibilities quickly?", "options": ["The rules of Go were kept secret from computer programmers.", "Go players are allowed to change the rules during a game.", "The game has an almost endless number of possible positions.", "Computers in 2016 were too slow to play any board game."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：Go ... has more possible positions than there are atoms in the observable universe, so a computer cannot win simply by checking possibilities faster than a human. 圍棋的盤面變化比宇宙中的原子還多，幾乎無窮無盡。(A)(B) 文中皆未提及；(D) 文中並未說電腦速度太慢，問題在於可能性實在太多。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about AlphaGo's 37th move in the second game?", "options": ["Lee Sedol had expected AlphaGo to play it.", "It was not something AlphaGo had learned by copying human players.", "It was the main reason AlphaGo lost the second game.", "Commentators praised it as brilliant as soon as it was played."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段說 Move 37 had not been copied from any human game; it was something new，第二段也提到幾乎沒有職業棋士會下在那裡，可推知這一手不是模仿人類而學來的。(A) 第二段說李世乭非常驚訝，花了將近十五分鐘才回應；(C) 這一手反而幫助 AlphaGo 掌握局勢，而且由第三段「李世乭唯一的勝利在第四局」可知第二局是 AlphaGo 獲勝；(D) 講評者起初以為那是失誤。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"dismissed\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["remembered", "copied", "explained", "rejected"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段 an idea that experts first dismissed and later admired，dismissed 與 later admired（後來讚嘆）形成對比，再對照第二段講評者原本以為第 37 手是失誤，可知 dismissed 指「認為不重要、不值得考慮而否定」，與 rejected 最接近。(A) 記得、(B) 模仿、(C) 解釋，都無法和「後來讚嘆」形成對比。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which best describes the author's attitude toward AI in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Fearful, because AI will soon replace all human players", "Uninterested, because Go is only a board game", "Hopeful, while reminding readers to keep thinking for themselves", "Angry, because AI forced Lee Sedol to leave professional Go"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段指出被 AI 打敗 is not the only possible outcome，把 AI 當老師能發現新的可能性，最後又提醒 keep thinking for ourselves，態度是樂觀中帶有提醒。(A) 作者並未表示害怕；(B) 作者認為這個故事的啟示 reaches beyond the board，並非不感興趣；(D) 第四段只說李世乭的經驗很痛苦，作者並未表達憤怒。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a127", "title": "Are You a Robot? The Hidden Work Behind CAPTCHAs", "title_zh": "你是機器人嗎？驗證碼背後看不見的工作", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Technology", "History"], "passage": "Almost everyone who uses the Internet has met them: wavy letters that are hard to read, a box that says \"I'm not a robot,\" or a grid of photos with the instruction to select every traffic light. These small tests are called CAPTCHAs, short for \"Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart.\" They may seem like a minor annoyance, but their history reveals a surprising amount about people and artificial intelligence.\n\nThe idea grew out of a real problem. Around 2000, automated programs known as \"bots\" were flooding websites with fake accounts and junk messages. Free e-mail services, for instance, found that bots could sign up for thousands of addresses in minutes. In 2003, a team at Carnegie Mellon University that included a young graduate student named Luis von Ahn introduced the term CAPTCHA. Their reasoning was clever: computers at the time were poor at reading twisted letters, while humans could read them with little effort. A task that was easy for people but hard for machines could therefore act as a gate.\n\nVon Ahn later made a rough calculation that troubled him. People around the world were solving about 200 million CAPTCHAs a day, each taking roughly ten seconds. Together, that added up to hundreds of thousands of hours of human effort every day, simply thrown away. Could this effort be put to good use? His answer was reCAPTCHA, launched in 2007. Instead of random letters, it showed users words from old books and newspapers that scanning software had failed to recognize. Each time someone typed one of these words, they helped turn printed pages into digital text, including old articles from The New York Times. In 2009, Google bought the project.\n\nAs technology changed, so did the puzzles. Today, many CAPTCHAs ask users to identify buses, crosswalks or street signs. Google has openly said that this effort helps to label images and build data sets for machine learning. In other words, when you click on traffic lights, you may be doing a tiny piece of work that teaches computers to understand pictures, a skill needed for digital maps and self-driving cars. Some newer versions ask you to do nothing at all; instead, they quietly study how you move your mouse to judge whether you are human.\n\nThere is an irony in this story. The answers that millions of people provided have helped AI become far better at reading and seeing, so good, in fact, that it can now pass many traditional CAPTCHAs. A 2023 study found that bots solved distorted-text puzzles faster and more accurately than most human volunteers. The humble CAPTCHA, designed to prove that we are not machines, may have helped machines become a little more like us.", "passage_zh": "幾乎每個上網的人都碰過它們：難以辨認的歪扭字母、一個寫著「我不是機器人」的方框，或是一格格的照片，要你選出所有的紅綠燈。這些小測驗叫做 CAPTCHA（驗證碼），是「全自動區分電腦和人類的公開圖靈測試」（Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart）的縮寫。它們看似只是小小的麻煩，但它們的歷史卻透露出許多關於人類與人工智慧的驚人事實。\n\n這個點子源自一個真實的問題。2000 年前後，被稱為「機器人程式」（bots）的自動化程式大量湧入各個網站，建立假帳號、發送垃圾訊息。舉例來說，免費電子郵件服務就發現，機器人程式能在幾分鐘內註冊數千個信箱。2003 年，卡內基美隆大學的一個團隊——其中包括一位名叫路易斯‧馮‧安的年輕研究生——提出了 CAPTCHA 這個名稱。他們的想法很巧妙：當時的電腦不擅長辨認扭曲的字母，人類卻幾乎毫不費力就能讀出來。因此，一個對人來說簡單、對機器來說困難的任務，就可以充當一道關卡。\n\n後來，馮‧安做了一個粗略的計算，結果讓他很困擾。全世界的人每天大約要解兩億個驗證碼，每個大約花十秒鐘。加起來，每天有數十萬小時的人力就這樣白白浪費掉。這些心力能不能拿來做些有用的事呢？他的答案是在 2007 年推出的 reCAPTCHA。它不再顯示隨機的字母，而是讓使用者看舊書和舊報紙上那些掃描軟體辨識不出來的字。每當有人輸入其中一個字，就是在幫忙把印刷的紙頁變成數位文字，其中包括《紐約時報》的舊文章。2009 年，Google 買下了這個計畫。\n\n隨著科技改變，謎題也跟著改變。如今，許多驗證碼會要求使用者找出公車、斑馬線或路標。Google 曾公開表示，這些心力有助於替圖片加上標記，並建立供機器學習使用的資料集。換句話說，當你點選紅綠燈時，你可能正在做一件小小的工作，教電腦看懂圖片——這正是數位地圖和自動駕駛汽車都需要的能力。有些較新的版本甚至什麼都不用你做，而是悄悄分析你移動滑鼠的方式，來判斷你是不是人類。\n\n這個故事裡有一個諷刺之處。數百萬人提供的答案，幫助 AI 在閱讀和辨識影像方面大幅進步；事實上，AI 已經厲害到能通過許多傳統的驗證碼。一項 2023 年的研究發現，機器人程式解扭曲文字謎題的速度和正確率，都比大多數人類志願者還要高。這個不起眼的驗證碼原本是設計來證明我們不是機器，卻可能反而幫助機器變得更像我們一點。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to solve CAPTCHAs more quickly and accurately", "Why Google decided to stop using CAPTCHAs on its websites", "The life story of the computer scientist Luis von Ahn", "How CAPTCHAs developed and how they have been connected to AI"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文介紹 CAPTCHA 的由來（第二段）、reCAPTCHA 如何利用人力把文字數位化（第三段）、圖片驗證碼與機器學習的關係（第四段），以及 AI 反過來能通過驗證碼的諷刺（第五段）。(A) 文中沒有教人解題技巧；(B) 文中未說 Google 停用驗證碼；(C) 馮‧安只出現在第二、三段，並非全文重點。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what was reCAPTCHA originally designed to do?", "options": ["Make use of people's effort to turn printed words into digital text", "Teach Internet users how to type faster and more accurately", "Help bots create free e-mail accounts more easily", "Collect photos of traffic lights for self-driving cars"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：馮‧安認為大量人力被白白浪費，於是推出 reCAPTCHA，讓使用者輸入掃描軟體辨識不出的舊書報文字，they helped turn printed pages into digital text。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 驗證碼的目的正是阻擋機器人程式；(D) 辨識紅綠燈等圖片是第四段所說後來的版本，並非 2007 年推出時的用途。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the author suggest about clicking on pictures of traffic lights?", "options": ["It is the most difficult type of CAPTCHA for most humans.", "It allows websites to look through users' personal photos.", "People's answers may help computers learn to recognize images.", "It was first used to protect the archives of The New York Times."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段提到 Google 表示使用者解驗證碼的心力有助於替圖片加標記、建立機器學習資料集，並說 when you click on traffic lights, you may be doing a tiny piece of work that teaches computers to understand pictures。(A) 文中未比較各種驗證碼的難易度；(B) 文中未提及；(D)《紐約時報》出現在第三段，與舊文章的數位化有關，和紅綠燈圖片無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"humble\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["dangerous", "ordinary", "expensive", "modern"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段 The humble CAPTCHA, designed to prove that we are not machines... 作者用 humble 形容驗證碼這種常見、看似微不足道的小測驗（第一段也說它們 may seem like a minor annoyance），意思接近 ordinary（普通的、不起眼的）。(A) 危險的、(C) 昂貴的、(D) 現代的，都不符合上下文。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the \"irony\" mentioned in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Humans now solve CAPTCHAs much faster than they did in 2003.", "Google bought a project that it had at first refused to support.", "Bots stopped flooding websites soon after CAPTCHAs were invented.", "A tool made to keep machines out has helped machines get better at passing it."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段說人們提供的答案幫助 AI 更會閱讀與辨識影像，so good, in fact, that it can now pass many traditional CAPTCHAs；原本用來擋住機器的工具，反而讓機器更會通過它，這就是諷刺之處。(A) 文中說的是機器人程式比大多數人類更快更準；(B) 文中未提及 Google 曾拒絕支持；(C) 文中沒有說機器人程式從此不再出現。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a128", "title": "Ninety-Nine Phones and a Fake Traffic Jam", "title_zh": "九十九支手機與一場假塞車", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Technology", "Arts"], "passage": "In early 2020, a Berlin artist named Simon Weckert walked slowly along several quiet streets, pulling a small red cart. Inside the cart were 99 second-hand smartphones, each one switched on and running Google Maps. Weckert was not delivering anything. He was carrying out an experiment, and when he posted a video of it online, the result amazed millions of viewers: on the map, the nearly empty streets behind him turned from green to red, the color used to warn drivers of a heavy traffic jam.\n\nTo understand how this trick worked, it helps to know where traffic information comes from. Navigation apps do not have people watching every road. Instead, they rely largely on data from phones. When users allow an app to access their location, their phones regularly send anonymous reports about where they are and how fast they are moving. If many phones on the same road are suddenly crawling along at walking speed, the system concludes that cars are stuck in traffic. It then colors the road red and may suggest other routes to drivers heading that way.\n\nWeckert's cart fooled the system because it produced exactly this pattern. To the app, 99 phones moving at walking pace looked like 99 cars in a slow-moving line. Weckert pointed out that drivers who trusted the map could be sent onto other roads to avoid a jam that did not exist. One of the streets he chose ran past Google's own Berlin office, a playful detail that helped the video spread. Google responded with good humor, saying that it enjoyed seeing creative uses of its maps and was always working to make its traffic data more accurate.\n\nThe experiment was more than a clever prank. Weckert presented it as a work of art that asks how much power digital maps have over the physical world. Millions of people now let an app decide which road to take and how long a trip will last. Such decisions feel objective because they are based on data. Yet data is only a record of signals, not reality itself. When the signals are misleading, whether by accident or on purpose, the decisions built on them can be wrong too. Computer scientists sum up this problem in a simple phrase: \"garbage in, garbage out.\"\n\nMost of the time, crowd-sourced information works remarkably well, because the errors of a few users are outweighed by the accurate data of many. But the system depends on an assumption that each signal comes from a real, ordinary user. As more of daily life is guided by data, from delivery routes to online reviews, it is worth asking a simple question: where does this information come from, and could someone be shaping it?", "passage_zh": "2020 年初，一位名叫西蒙‧韋克特的柏林藝術家拉著一輛紅色小推車，慢慢走過幾條安靜的街道。推車裡裝著 99 支二手智慧型手機，每一支都開著機，並且執行著 Google 地圖。韋克特不是在送貨，而是在進行一項實驗。當他把實驗影片貼到網路上後，結果讓數百萬名觀眾大吃一驚：在地圖上，他身後幾乎沒有車的街道從綠色變成了紅色——也就是用來警告駕駛前方嚴重塞車的顏色。\n\n要了解這個把戲是怎麼辦到的，得先知道路況資訊是從哪裡來的。導航應用程式並沒有派人盯著每一條路，而是大多仰賴來自手機的資料。當使用者允許應用程式取得自己的位置時，手機就會定期傳送匿名報告，說明自己在哪裡、移動得多快。如果同一條路上有許多手機突然以步行的速度緩慢前進，系統就會判斷車輛塞在車陣中，接著把那條路標成紅色，並可能建議正要前往那裡的駕駛改走其他路線。\n\n韋克特的推車之所以能騙過系統，正是因為它製造出了這種模式。對應用程式來說，以步行速度移動的 99 支手機，看起來就像排成一列、緩慢前進的 99 輛車。韋克特指出，相信地圖的駕駛可能會被引導到其他道路，去躲避一場根本不存在的塞車。他挑選的街道中，有一條正好經過 Google 自己在柏林的辦公室，這個俏皮的細節讓影片傳播得更廣。Google 則以幽默的態度回應，表示很樂見大家用有創意的方式使用它的地圖，並說公司一直在努力讓路況資料更準確。\n\n這個實驗不只是一場聰明的惡作劇。韋克特把它當成一件藝術作品來呈現，探討數位地圖對真實世界到底有多大的影響力。如今有數百萬人讓應用程式決定該走哪條路、一趟路程要花多久。這些決定因為以資料為根據，所以感覺很客觀。然而，資料只是訊號的紀錄，並不是現實本身。當訊號具有誤導性時——不論是出於意外還是故意——建立在這些訊號上的決定也可能跟著出錯。電腦科學家用一句簡單的話來概括這個問題：「垃圾進，垃圾出。」\n\n大多數時候，由群眾提供的資訊效果非常好，因為少數使用者的錯誤會被多數人的正確資料蓋過。但這套系統建立在一個假設上：每一個訊號都來自一位真實、普通的使用者。隨著日常生活有愈來愈多部分由資料引導——從送貨路線到網路評論——我們值得問一個簡單的問題：這些資訊是從哪裡來的？會不會有人在背後操弄它？", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To teach readers how to avoid traffic jams in Berlin", "To use an art project to show how data-based systems can be misled", "To criticize Google for collecting people's location data", "To explain why second-hand smartphones are cheaper than new ones"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文以韋克特拉著 99 支手機製造假塞車為例，說明導航系統如何依賴手機資料（第二段），並帶出「資料只是訊號，不等於現實」以及「垃圾進，垃圾出」的道理（第四、五段）。(A) 文章不是在教人避開柏林的塞車；(C) 作者並未批評 Google 蒐集資料，第三段反而提到 Google 幽默回應；(D) 文中未比較手機價格。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how does a navigation app decide that a road is jammed?", "options": ["Workers stand by the road and report the traffic by phone.", "Drivers press a button in the app whenever they are stuck.", "Cameras along the road count the cars that pass by.", "Many phones on the road are moving very slowly at the same time."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：If many phones on the same road are suddenly crawling along at walking speed, the system concludes that cars are stuck in traffic. 系統是根據許多手機同時緩慢移動來判斷塞車。(A) 文中明確說導航程式 do not have people watching every road；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the author mention that one street ran past Google's Berlin office?", "options": ["To point out an amusing detail that helped the video attract attention", "To show that Google had asked Weckert to carry out the experiment", "To explain why that street was full of real traffic", "To prove that Google's workers saw the fake traffic jam themselves"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：One of the streets he chose ran past Google's own Berlin office, a playful detail that helped the video spread. 作者提到這一點，是因為這個俏皮（playful，即 amusing）的細節讓影片廣為流傳、引起注意。(B) 文中未說 Google 委託他做實驗；(C) 第一段說那些街道 nearly empty，並沒有真的塞車；(D) 文中沒有提到 Google 員工是否看見。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"objective\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["difficult to understand", "connected with art", "based on facts rather than feelings", "designed only for drivers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段 Such decisions feel objective because they are based on data. 這些決定因為以資料為根據，所以讓人覺得「客觀」，objective 指根據事實而非個人感覺；下一句 Yet data is only a record of signals 正是在反駁這種客觀的感覺。(A)(B)(D) 都與 based on data 的說明不符。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the phrase \"garbage in, garbage out\" in paragraph 4 mean?", "options": ["People should throw away their old phones in the proper way.", "If the data put into a system is poor, its results will be poor too.", "Computers must be cleaned regularly in order to work well.", "The more data a system collects, the better its decisions become."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段前一句說 When the signals are misleading ... the decisions built on them can be wrong too，接著才以 garbage in, garbage out 總結：輸入的資料有問題，得出的結果也會有問題。(A)(C) 只是從字面上的「垃圾」聯想，與文意無關；(D) 文中強調的是資料的品質，而不是數量。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a129", "title": "The Power of a Number: What \"Likes\" Do to the Teenage Brain", "title_zh": "一個數字的力量：「讚」對青少年大腦的影響", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Society"], "passage": "For many teenagers, posting a photo online is followed by a few nervous minutes of checking. How many likes has it received? Is it doing better or worse than a friend's post? It is easy to dismiss this behavior as vanity, but scientists suggest that the pull of the \"like\" button is rooted in the way the adolescent brain develops.\n\nIn 2016, researchers at the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) carried out one of the first brain-scanning studies of social media use. They invited 32 teenagers aged 13 to 18 to look at nearly 150 photos in an app designed to resemble Instagram while a scanner recorded their brain activity. Some of the photos had been submitted by the teenagers themselves. What the participants did not know was that the number of likes on each picture had been decided by the researchers.\n\nThe results were striking. When the teens saw their own photos with a large number of likes, a region called the nucleus accumbens became especially active. This area is part of the brain's reward system, the same circuit that responds when we eat chocolate or win money. The lead researcher, Lauren Sherman, noted that this circuit is thought to be particularly sensitive during adolescence, which may help explain why teenagers are so strongly drawn to social media.\n\nThe study also revealed how easily people follow the crowd. Participants were much more likely to like a photo if it already seemed popular, even though the likes had been assigned at random. In other words, the number itself shaped their judgment. The researchers also found that when teens viewed photos showing risky behavior, such as smoking or drinking, brain areas linked to self-control were less active. This suggests that what peers appear to approve of online may weaken a young person's caution.\n\nFindings like these have fed into a wider debate about how social media is designed. In 2019, Instagram began testing hidden like counts in several countries, and two years later it allowed all users to choose whether to see these numbers. Supporters hope that removing the visible score will reduce pressure and comparison, although opinions remain divided on how much difference it really makes.\n\nNone of this means that likes are simply harmful. Online approval can also help young people feel connected and supported. The key is awareness. Knowing that a number on a screen can trigger the same reward system as a prize may help users step back and ask themselves whether they are posting to share something they care about or simply chasing a score.", "passage_zh": "對許多青少年來說，在網路上貼出一張照片之後，接下來就是緊張地查看好幾分鐘。這張照片得到多少個讚了？表現得比朋友的貼文好還是差？我們很容易把這種行為看成愛慕虛榮而不當一回事，但科學家認為，「讚」按鈕的吸引力，其實根源於青少年大腦的發展方式。\n\n2016 年，加州大學洛杉磯分校（UCLA）的研究人員進行了最早針對社群媒體使用的腦部掃描研究之一。他們邀請 32 名 13 到 18 歲的青少年，在一個仿照 Instagram 設計的應用程式中觀看將近 150 張照片，同時用掃描器記錄他們的腦部活動。其中有些照片是青少年自己提供的。參與者不知道的是，每張照片上的按讚數其實都是由研究人員決定的。\n\n研究結果相當驚人。當青少年看到自己的照片獲得大量的讚時，大腦中一個叫做「依核」的區域會變得特別活躍。這個區域是大腦獎勵系統的一部分，也就是我們吃巧克力或贏錢時會產生反應的同一個迴路。主要研究者蘿倫‧謝爾曼指出，一般認為這個迴路在青春期特別敏感，這或許有助於解釋為什麼青少年會如此受到社群媒體吸引。\n\n這項研究也顯示出人們有多麼容易從眾。如果一張照片看起來已經很受歡迎，參與者就更有可能替它按讚，即使那些讚是隨機分配的也一樣。換句話說，數字本身就左右了他們的判斷。研究人員還發現，當青少年看到呈現危險行為的照片，例如抽菸或喝酒時，大腦中和自我控制有關的區域活動會比較少。這顯示同儕在網路上看似認可的事物，可能會削弱年輕人的警覺心。\n\n像這樣的研究發現，也被納入了「社群媒體該如何設計」這個更廣泛的討論之中。2019 年，Instagram 開始在幾個國家測試隱藏按讚數，兩年後更讓所有使用者自行選擇是否要看到這些數字。支持者希望，拿掉看得見的分數能減少壓力和比較，不過對於這樣做究竟能帶來多大的改變，各方看法仍然分歧。\n\n這一切並不代表按讚就是單純有害的。網路上的認可也能讓年輕人覺得與他人有所連結、受到支持。關鍵在於覺察。知道螢幕上的一個數字能啟動和獲得獎品一樣的獎勵系統，或許能幫助使用者退一步問問自己：我貼文是為了分享自己在乎的事，還是只是在追逐分數？", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to take photos that will receive more likes on Instagram", "Why teenagers should stop using social media completely", "How likes on social media affect the teenage brain and judgment", "How scientists invented a new kind of brain scanner"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹 UCLA 的腦部掃描研究：按讚會啟動青少年大腦的獎勵系統（第三段）、按讚數會左右判斷（第四段），並提到社群媒體設計的討論與保持覺察的重要（第五、六段）。(A) 文中沒有教人拍照技巧；(B) 第六段明確表示 None of this means that likes are simply harmful；(D) 掃描器只是研究工具，並非文章重點。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What did the participants in the UCLA study NOT know?", "options": ["The researchers had decided how many likes each photo received.", "Some of the photos had been provided by the participants themselves.", "A scanner was recording their brain activity.", "The app they used was designed to look like Instagram."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段最後一句：What the participants did not know was that the number of likes on each picture had been decided by the researchers. 參與者不知道每張照片的按讚數其實是研究人員決定的，與 (A) 相符。(B) 照片是參與者自己提供的，他們當然知道；(C) 他們是在掃描器中接受研究；(D) 應用程式的外觀他們親眼看得到。這三項都不是參與者不知道的事。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"striking\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["violent", "boring", "confusing", "remarkable"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段 The results were striking. 後面接著描述令人印象深刻的發現：看到自己的照片獲得很多讚時，大腦的獎勵系統會特別活躍。striking 在此指「引人注目的、驚人的」，與 remarkable 最接近。(A) violent（暴力的）是從 strike（打擊）字面聯想而來的錯誤選項；(B) 無聊的、(C) 令人困惑的，都與文意不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 4?", "options": ["Teenagers only like photos that were taken by their close friends.", "How popular a photo seems can influence whether others like it.", "Photos of risky behavior make the brain's self-control areas more active.", "The participants could easily tell that the likes were not real."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Participants were much more likely to like a photo if it already seemed popular, even though the likes had been assigned at random... the number itself shaped their judgment. 可推知照片看起來有多受歡迎，會影響別人是否按讚。(A) 文中未提及；(C) 正好相反，文中說自我控制區域 less active；(D) 由第二段可知參與者並不知道按讚數是研究人員安排的。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, which of the following is true about Instagram?", "options": ["It now lets all users decide whether to see like counts.", "It removed like counts in every country in 2016.", "It carried out the brain-scanning study together with UCLA.", "It has proved that hiding likes completely removes pressure."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：In 2019, Instagram began testing hidden like counts in several countries, and two years later it allowed all users to choose whether to see these numbers. Instagram 在兩年後讓所有使用者自行選擇是否要看到按讚數，與 (A) 相符。(B) 2016 年是 UCLA 研究的年份，Instagram 是 2019 年才開始在部分國家測試；(C) 研究是 UCLA 的研究人員做的，文中未說 Instagram 參與；(D) 文中說對於效果 opinions remain divided。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a130", "title": "Can the Internet Forget? The Right to Be Forgotten", "title_zh": "網路會遺忘嗎？談「被遺忘權」", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Society", "Technology"], "passage": "Imagine that a foolish comment you made years ago is still the first thing that appears whenever someone searches your name. Human memory fades over time, but the Internet rarely does. This raises a difficult question for the digital age: should people have the right to make their past harder to find?\n\nA Spanish man named Mario Costeja González helped answer that question. In 1998, a newspaper printed notices announcing that his property would be auctioned to pay off debts he owed to the social security system. The matter was later settled, but more than ten years afterward, a Google search of his name still led straight to those old notices. Believing that the information was no longer relevant, he asked for it to be removed. His case eventually reached the Court of Justice of the European Union.\n\nIn May 2014, the court made a landmark decision. It ruled that search engines such as Google could be required to remove links to information about a person when that information is \"inadequate, irrelevant or no longer relevant, or excessive.\" Importantly, the ruling did not order the newspaper to delete anything. The original notices stayed online; they simply stopped appearing in search results for his name. The idea became widely known as the \"right to be forgotten,\" and a similar \"right to erasure\" later became part of the European Union's data protection law, the GDPR, which took effect in 2018.\n\nSince then, Google has received more than a million requests to remove millions of web addresses from its results. Each request is judged individually, and roughly half of the links have been removed. Requests are more likely to be refused when the information concerns a public figure or a serious crime, because the public has a legitimate interest in knowing about it. A politician, for instance, cannot simply erase reports about how he or she has spent public money.\n\nCritics argue that the right gives private companies too much power to decide what the public may see, and that it could be misused to hide information people deserve to know. Others note that the ruling applies mainly within Europe, so the same results may still be found elsewhere. There is also an irony in Costeja González's story. By going to court to have his old debts forgotten, he made them famous, and his name is now attached to one of the best-known legal cases of the Internet age.\n\nThe debate is far from settled. Yet the case is a reminder that the online world is not just a place we visit but a lasting record of who we have been. A law may help to hide some information later, but it can never guarantee that something is truly forgotten.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，你多年前說過的一句蠢話，至今仍是別人搜尋你的名字時最先出現的結果。人的記憶會隨著時間淡去，網路卻很少遺忘。這為數位時代帶來了一個難題：人們是否應該有權利，讓自己的過去變得比較難被找到？\n\n一位名叫馬里奧‧科斯特哈‧岡薩雷斯的西班牙男子，讓這個問題有了答案。1998 年，一家報紙刊登了公告，宣布他的房產將被拍賣，以償還他積欠社會保險體系的債務。這件事後來已經解決，但十多年後，用 Google 搜尋他的名字，仍然會直接連到那些舊公告。他認為這些資訊早已不再相關，因此要求將它們移除。他的案子最後一路打到了歐盟法院。\n\n2014 年 5 月，法院做出了一項具有里程碑意義的判決。法院裁定，當某人的相關資訊「不適當、不相關或已不再相關，或是過度」時，Google 這類搜尋引擎可以被要求移除連到這些資訊的連結。重要的是，這項判決並沒有命令報社刪除任何內容。原本的公告仍然留在網路上，只是在搜尋他的名字時，不再出現在搜尋結果中。這個概念後來被廣泛稱為「被遺忘權」；之後，類似的「刪除權」也被納入歐盟的資料保護法《一般資料保護規則》（GDPR），該法於 2018 年正式施行。\n\n從那時起，Google 已經收到超過一百萬件請求，要求從搜尋結果中移除數百萬個網址。每一件請求都會個別審查，大約有一半的連結被移除。如果資訊涉及公眾人物或重大犯罪，請求就比較可能被拒絕，因為大眾有正當的理由知道這些事。舉例來說，政治人物就不能隨意抹去有關自己如何花用公帑的報導。\n\n批評者認為，這項權利讓私人企業擁有太大的權力去決定大眾能看到什麼，而且可能被濫用來隱藏民眾理應知道的資訊。也有人指出，這項判決主要只適用於歐洲境內，所以在其他地方仍可能搜尋到同樣的結果。科斯特哈‧岡薩雷斯的故事還有一個諷刺之處：他上法院是為了讓自己過去的債務被遺忘，結果反而讓它們出了名；如今，他的名字和網路時代最著名的法律案件之一緊緊連在一起。\n\n這場爭論還遠遠沒有定論。然而，這個案例提醒我們：網路世界不只是我們造訪的地方，更是一份記錄我們過去模樣的長久檔案。法律也許能在日後幫忙隱藏某些資訊，卻永遠無法保證任何事情真的會被遺忘。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To teach readers how to delete their social media accounts", "To argue that newspapers should remove old articles about people", "To describe how Google became the world's most popular search engine", "To explain the origin of the \"right to be forgotten\" and the debate around it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二、三段說明被遺忘權源自科斯特哈‧岡薩雷斯的案件與 2014 年的判決，第四、五段介紹實際執行的情形與批評意見。(A) 文中沒有教人刪除帳號；(B) 第三段明確指出判決 did not order the newspaper to delete anything；(C) 文中未談 Google 如何成為熱門的搜尋引擎。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the 2014 ruling, what could search engines be required to do?", "options": ["Delete old articles from newspapers' own websites", "Remove certain links from the search results for a person's name", "Pay people whose debts had been reported in the news", "Stop showing any information about private individuals"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：search engines ... could be required to remove links to information about a person when that information is \"inadequate, irrelevant or no longer relevant, or excessive\"；原本的公告仍留在網路上，只是不再出現在搜尋他名字的結果中。(A) 判決並未要求報社刪除任何內容；(C) 文中未提及賠償；(D) 只有符合條件的資訊才可能被移除，並不是全部資訊。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Based on paragraph 4, which of the following requests would Google most likely refuse?", "options": ["A former mayor asking to remove reports about how he spent city money", "A man asking to remove a 20-year-old notice about a debt he has already paid", "A woman asking to remove an outdated story about her family's tiny shop", "A teenager asking to remove a silly comment he made in junior high school"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Requests are more likely to be refused when the information concerns a public figure or a serious crime... A politician ... cannot simply erase reports about how he or she has spent public money. 前任市長屬於公眾人物，花用公款的報導與公共利益有關，最可能被拒絕。(B)(C)(D) 都是一般人的私事，而且已經過時或不再重要，比較符合第三段可以移除連結的條件。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"landmark\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["related to land and buildings", "easy to forget", "very important and marking a turning point", "kept secret from the public"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段 the court made a landmark decision，後文說明這項判決確立了廣為人知的「被遺忘權」，並影響了後來於 2018 年施行的歐盟資料保護法，可知 landmark 指「具有里程碑意義、代表重大轉折的」。(A) 是從 land（土地）字面聯想而來的錯誤選項；(B) 容易忘記的、(D) 對大眾保密的，都與文意不符。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What is ironic about Costeja González's story?", "options": ["He lost his case at the European court.", "His effort to make his past forgotten made it widely known.", "He later became a judge at the Court of Justice.", "Google refused to follow the court's decision."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：By going to court to have his old debts forgotten, he made them famous. 他想讓舊債被遺忘，結果反而讓它們廣為人知。(A) 由第三段可知法院的判決對他有利；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第四段說 Google 會個別審查請求，並移除了大約一半的連結，並沒有拒絕遵守判決。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a131", "title": "The Thirsty Cloud: The Hidden Cost of Artificial Intelligence", "title_zh": "口渴的雲端：人工智慧看不見的代價", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Technology"], "passage": "When we ask a chatbot a question or store photos \"in the cloud,\" it can feel as if the work happens in the air, using no resources at all. In reality, the cloud lives on the ground. It is made up of data centers, huge buildings packed with thousands of computer servers that run day and night. As AI spreads, these buildings are using a growing share of the world's energy.\n\nThe reason lies in the way AI works. Training a large AI model means feeding it enormous amounts of text or images and adjusting billions of internal settings, a process that can keep thousands of powerful chips busy for weeks or months. Even afterward, every question requires more calculation. According to the International Energy Agency (IEA), data centers used about 1.5 percent of the world's electricity in 2024, and their demand could more than double by 2030, reaching a level slightly higher than the total electricity use of Japan today.\n\nElectricity is only part of the story. Computer chips produce a great deal of heat and must be kept cool. Many data centers do this with systems that evaporate water, much as sweat cools the human body. Researchers at the University of California, Riverside, estimated in 2023 that training one well-known AI model may have used around 700,000 liters of fresh water, and that a chat of a few dozen questions could consume about half a liter. Such figures are only rough estimates, but they show that digital activity has a physical cost.\n\nThis cost is not shared equally. Some data centers are built in regions that already suffer from drought. In such areas, residents have complained that technology companies are competing with farms and households for limited water. Rising electricity demand can also strain local power grids and, if the power comes from coal or gas, increase greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nEngineers are searching for solutions. Some companies build data centers in cooler climates, such as northern Europe, where outside air can do much of the cooling. Others use recycled wastewater, or cool chips with liquid in closed systems that lose very little water. In some Nordic cities, waste heat from data centers is even piped into homes to keep them warm in winter. Meanwhile, researchers are designing smaller AI models that need far less computing power.\n\nNone of this means we should stop using AI. It does mean that the technology is not as weightless as it seems. Just as people have learned to think about the energy used by cars and air conditioners, we may need to ask how much power and water each digital service consumes, and whether every task really needs the most powerful AI available.", "passage_zh": "當我們向聊天機器人提問，或把照片存到「雲端」時，會覺得這些工作好像是在空中完成的，完全不消耗任何資源。事實上，雲端是建在地面上的。它由資料中心組成——那是一棟棟巨大的建築物，裡面塞滿了數千台日夜不停運轉的電腦伺服器。隨著 AI 愈來愈普及，這些建築物用掉的能源在全世界所占的比例也愈來愈高。\n\n原因在於 AI 的運作方式。訓練一個大型 AI 模型，必須餵給它極大量的文字或圖片，並調整數十億個內部參數，這個過程可能讓數千顆高效能晶片連續忙上好幾週甚至好幾個月。即使訓練完成之後，每一個問題也都需要更多的運算。根據國際能源總署（IEA）的資料，2024 年資料中心用掉了全世界大約 1.5% 的電力，而到了 2030 年，它們的用電需求可能會增加一倍以上，達到比日本目前全國總用電量還略高的程度。\n\n電力只是問題的一部分。電腦晶片會產生大量的熱，必須保持低溫。許多資料中心靠著讓水蒸發的系統來降溫，原理就跟流汗能讓人體降溫差不多。加州大學河濱分校的研究人員在 2023 年估計，訓練某個知名的 AI 模型可能用掉了大約 70 萬公升的淡水，而一段包含幾十個問題的聊天對話，可能會消耗大約半公升的水。這些數字都只是粗略的估計，但它們顯示出數位活動是有實際代價的。\n\n這種代價並不是由所有人平均分擔的。有些資料中心蓋在原本就飽受乾旱之苦的地區。在這些地方，居民抱怨科技公司正在和農場及一般家庭搶奪有限的水資源。不斷增加的用電需求也可能讓當地電網不堪負荷；如果電力來自燃煤或天然氣，還會增加溫室氣體的排放。\n\n工程師們正在尋找解決辦法。有些公司把資料中心蓋在氣候較涼爽的地方，例如北歐，讓戶外的空氣就能完成大部分的降溫工作。有些則使用回收的廢水，或是在密閉系統中用液體冷卻晶片，幾乎不會損耗水。在一些北歐城市，資料中心排出的廢熱甚至會透過管線送進住家，在冬天為家家戶戶供暖。同時，研究人員也在設計所需運算能力少得多的小型 AI 模型。\n\n這一切並不代表我們應該停止使用 AI，但確實代表這項科技並不像表面上看起來那樣「沒有重量」。就像人們已經學會思考汽車和冷氣機所消耗的能源一樣，我們或許也需要問問：每一項數位服務要消耗多少電力和水？是不是每一項工作都真的需要動用最強大的 AI？", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The large amounts of energy and water used by AI and data centers", "How to store photos safely in the cloud", "Why Japan uses more electricity than most other countries", "How sweating helps to cool the human body"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文說明 AI 背後的資料中心耗用大量電力（第二段）與水（第三段），以及由此造成的影響（第四段）和可能的解決辦法（第五段）。(B) 把照片存到雲端只是第一段的開場例子；(C) 日本只是第二段用來比較用電量的例子；(D) 流汗只是第三段用來比喻蒸發降溫的說法。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the IEA, what may happen to data centers' electricity demand by 2030?", "options": ["It may fall to about 1.5 percent of the world's electricity.", "It may stay about the same as it was in 2024.", "It may more than double and go beyond Japan's current total electricity use.", "It may be fully supplied by waste heat from people's homes."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：their demand could more than double by 2030, reaching a level slightly higher than the total electricity use of Japan today. IEA 預估到 2030 年資料中心的用電需求可能增加一倍以上，並略高於日本目前的全國總用電量，與 (C) 相符。(A) 1.5% 是 2024 年資料中心用電占全球的比例；(B) 與「增加一倍以上」不符；(D) 第五段說的是把資料中心的廢熱送進住家，方向正好相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the author mean by saying that \"the cloud lives on the ground\" in paragraph 1?", "options": ["Cloud services are only available in countries with flat land.", "People should store their data on paper instead of computers.", "Bad weather can easily damage online services.", "Online services depend on real buildings and machines that use resources."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段先說使用雲端時，感覺 the work happens in the air, using no resources at all，接著說 the cloud lives on the ground，並解釋雲端是由塞滿伺服器、日夜運轉的資料中心組成，意即線上服務其實仰賴實體建築和耗用資源的機器。(A)(B)(C) 都是從字面上的「雲」與「地面」做出的錯誤聯想，文中並未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why do many data centers use water?", "options": ["To clean the dust off their computer servers", "To cool down the chips, which produce a lot of heat", "To produce electricity for the buildings", "To provide drinking water for nearby farms"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Computer chips produce a great deal of heat and must be kept cool. Many data centers do this with systems that evaporate water. 資料中心用水是為了替晶片降溫。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第四段反而說資料中心和農場、家庭爭奪有限的水資源。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way to reduce the environmental impact of data centers?", "options": ["Building data centers in places with cooler weather", "Using the waste heat from data centers to warm homes", "Designing AI models that need less computing power", "Limiting how many questions each user may ask a chatbot"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段提到把資料中心蓋在較涼爽的地方 (A)、把廢熱送進住家供暖 (B)、設計所需運算能力較少的小型 AI 模型 (C)，但沒有提到限制使用者提問的次數；第六段也只是建議我們思考每項工作是否真的需要最強大的 AI，並非限制提問。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a132", "title": "The Face the Computer Couldn't See", "title_zh": "電腦看不見的那張臉", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Technology", "Society"], "passage": "As a graduate student at the MIT Media Lab, Joy Buolamwini was building a playful project called the \"Aspire Mirror,\" which used a camera and face-tracking software to place inspiring images over a person's reflection. There was one problem: the software could not detect her face. It worked for her lighter-skinned classmates, but not for her, a Black woman. Only when she put on a plain white mask did the computer finally recognize that a face was there.\n\nRather than treating this as a strange technical glitch, Buolamwini decided to investigate. In 2018, she and the computer scientist Timnit Gebru published a study called \"Gender Shades.\" They tested face-analysis systems sold by three major technology companies, including IBM and Microsoft, asking each system to identify whether the person in a photo was male or female. To make the test fair, they built a new collection of 1,270 photos of politicians from three African and three European countries, carefully balanced by gender and skin color.\n\nThe results were alarming. For lighter-skinned men, the systems made mistakes less than 1 percent of the time. For darker-skinned women, however, the error rate climbed as high as 34.7 percent. In other words, a technology that seemed highly accurate overall performed very poorly for one particular group of people, and this weakness had been hidden inside impressive average scores.\n\nWhy would a computer show such a bias? Machines do not hold opinions, but they learn from examples. Face-analysis systems are trained on huge collections of photos, and if most of those photos show lighter-skinned men, the system becomes skilled at recognizing them and much less reliable with everyone else. The problem is often not a single bad decision by a programmer but a quiet imbalance in the data. Buolamwini calls this the \"coded gaze,\" meaning that the priorities and blind spots of the people who build technology can become built into it.\n\nThe study had real consequences. After the companies were informed of the results, several of them released updated systems with much smaller gaps between groups. The Algorithmic Justice League, an organization she had founded to raise awareness of unfair AI, gained wide attention, and her story was told in the 2020 documentary Coded Bias. Her work also fed into debates about police use of facial recognition, where a wrong match could lead to an innocent person being stopped or even arrested.\n\nThe lesson extends far beyond faces. AI systems now help decide who gets a job interview, a loan or medical attention. A system may look impressive on average while failing the people who are least represented in its data. Asking \"accurate for whom?\" is therefore one of the most important questions we can put to any intelligent machine.", "passage_zh": "喬伊‧布蘭維尼在麻省理工學院媒體實驗室念研究所時，正在製作一個名為「激勵魔鏡」（Aspire Mirror）的趣味作品。這個作品利用攝影機和臉部追蹤軟體，把能激勵人心的影像疊在照鏡子的人的倒影上。但有一個問題：軟體偵測不到她的臉。這套軟體對她膚色較淺的同學都有效，對身為黑人女性的她卻不管用。直到她戴上一個素白的面具，電腦才終於辨認出那裡有一張臉。\n\n布蘭維尼沒有把這件事當成奇怪的技術小故障，而是決定深入調查。2018 年，她和電腦科學家蒂姆尼特‧格布魯發表了一項名為「性別色調」（Gender Shades）的研究。她們測試了 IBM、微軟等三家大型科技公司所販售的臉部分析系統，要求每個系統判斷照片中的人是男性還是女性。為了讓測試公平，她們建立了一套新的照片資料庫，收錄來自三個非洲國家和三個歐洲國家的政治人物共 1,270 張照片，並在性別與膚色上仔細取得平衡。\n\n研究結果令人憂心。對膚色較淺的男性，這些系統出錯的機率不到 1%；但對膚色較深的女性，錯誤率卻高達 34.7%。換句話說，一項整體看來非常準確的科技，用在某一群人身上時卻表現得非常差，而這個弱點一直隱藏在亮眼的平均分數之中。\n\n為什麼電腦會出現這樣的偏差呢？機器本身並沒有成見，但它們會從範例中學習。臉部分析系統是用龐大的照片資料庫訓練出來的，如果其中大部分的照片都是膚色較淺的男性，系統就會很擅長辨認他們，對其他人的辨識則沒那麼可靠。問題往往不是出在某位程式設計師的一個錯誤決定，而是資料中不易察覺的失衡。布蘭維尼把這種現象稱為「編碼凝視」（coded gaze），意思是打造科技的人所重視的事物和他們的盲點，都可能被寫進科技之中。\n\n這項研究帶來了實際的影響。這些公司得知研究結果後，其中好幾家推出了更新版的系統，不同族群之間的差距大幅縮小。她先前創立、致力於讓大眾認識不公平 AI 的組織「演算法正義聯盟」也受到廣泛關注，而她的故事則被拍成 2020 年的紀錄片《編碼偏見》（Coded Bias）。她的研究也成為警方使用臉部辨識技術相關辯論的一部分；在這類情況下，一次錯誤的比對就可能導致無辜的人被攔查，甚至遭到逮捕。\n\n這個教訓遠遠不只適用於人臉。如今，AI 系統會協助決定誰能得到工作面試、貸款或醫療照護。一個系統的平均表現也許看起來很亮眼，卻可能辜負了在它的資料中最缺乏代表性的那群人。因此，「對誰來說準確？」是我們可以向任何智慧機器提出的最重要問題之一。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to build a mirror that shows inspiring pictures", "How a researcher revealed that face-analysis AI was less accurate for some groups", "Why IBM and Microsoft stopped selling their technology to the police", "How politicians in Africa and Europe use facial recognition at work"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從布蘭維尼的臉無法被軟體偵測談起，介紹她的 Gender Shades 研究發現系統對膚色較深的女性錯誤率特別高（第三段），並分析原因（第四段）與後續影響（第五段）。(A) Aspire Mirror 只是第一段的引子；(C) 文中未提到這些公司停止把技術賣給警方；(D) 政治人物的照片只是研究使用的資料。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 1, what happened when Buolamwini put on a white mask?", "options": ["The software stopped working for her classmates.", "Her reflection disappeared from the mirror.", "The camera recorded her name and identity.", "The computer finally detected a face."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段最後一句：Only when she put on a plain white mask did the computer finally recognize that a face was there. 她戴上素白面具後，電腦才終於偵測到那裡有一張臉，與 (D) 相符。(A) 軟體原本就對她膚色較淺的同學有效，文中沒說她戴面具後軟體對同學失效；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"glitch\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a small fault", "a clever idea", "a famous study", "a serious crime"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段 Rather than treating this as a strange technical glitch, Buolamwini decided to investigate. 她沒有把軟體偵測不到她的臉當成「奇怪的技術小問題」輕輕帶過，而是決定深入調查，glitch 意思接近 a small fault（小故障）。(B) 聰明的點子、(C) 著名的研究、(D) 嚴重的罪行，都不符合「沒把它當一回事，而是決定調查」的上下文。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, what is often the main cause of bias in face-analysis systems?", "options": ["Programmers who dislike certain groups of people", "Cameras that are too cheap to take clear photos", "Training photos that do not fairly represent all groups", "Computers that form their own opinions about people"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：if most of those photos show lighter-skinned men, the system becomes skilled at recognizing them and much less reliable with everyone else. The problem is often not a single bad decision by a programmer but a quiet imbalance in the data. 偏差主要來自訓練照片的失衡。(A) 文中說問題往往不是某位程式設計師的錯誤決定；(B) 文中未提及；(D) 文中明確說 Machines do not hold opinions。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the question \"accurate for whom?\" in the last paragraph suggest?", "options": ["AI systems should only be used by trained experts.", "A system's average accuracy may hide poor results for some groups.", "Every AI system is equally accurate for all kinds of people.", "People should stop using AI to make decisions about loans."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第六段：A system may look impressive on average while failing the people who are least represented in its data. 因此要問「對誰來說準確」，提醒我們平均準確率可能掩蓋系統對某些族群表現很差的事實，也呼應第三段的研究結果。(A)(D) 文中並未如此主張；(C) 與全文內容相反。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a133", "title": "Sugar Pills That Work: The Strange Power of the Placebo", "title_zh": "會發揮作用的糖錠：安慰劑的奇妙力量", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "During the Second World War, an American army doctor named Henry Beecher was running out of morphine for wounded soldiers. According to a well-known story, a nurse injected one soldier with nothing but salt water while telling him it was a strong painkiller. To Beecher's astonishment, the man calmed down and his pain seemed to ease. Beecher later studied this puzzling effect, and in 1955 he published a famous paper called \"The Powerful Placebo.\"\n\nA placebo is a treatment with no active ingredient, such as a sugar pill. The word comes from Latin and means \"I shall please.\" Placebos play an essential role in medical research. In drug tests, some volunteers receive the real medicine and others an identical-looking placebo. Neither the patients nor the doctors know who is getting which, so their expectations cannot affect the results. Only a drug that clearly beats the placebo is considered effective.\n\nWhy should a fake pill help at all? The key seems to be expectation. When people believe they are being treated, the brain can release endorphins, the body's natural painkillers. In a 1978 experiment, researchers gave patients a drug that blocks endorphins, and the pain relief produced by a placebo largely disappeared. The placebo effect, in other words, is not merely imagination; it involves real chemical changes. Its power has limits, however. Placebos mainly affect symptoms that the brain helps to shape, such as pain and nausea. A sugar pill cannot shrink a tumor or kill bacteria.\n\nCuriously, the details of a fake treatment matter. Two placebo pills tend to work better than one. In one experiment, volunteers told that a painkiller cost $2.50 per pill reported more relief than those told it had been discounted to ten cents, although everyone received the same sugar pill. The more impressive a treatment seems, the more strongly the brain responds.\n\nEven more surprisingly, a placebo may work when patients know it is fake. In a 2010 study at Harvard, people with a long-term digestive condition were openly told that their pills contained no medicine, yet many still improved more than patients who received no pills. The effect can also work in reverse. When patients are warned about possible side effects, some develop headaches or dizziness even from a placebo. This is called the nocebo effect, from a Latin word meaning \"I shall harm.\"\n\nNone of this means doctors should replace real medicine with sugar pills. Secretly giving patients fake treatment raises serious ethical questions, and dangerous diseases require treatments that truly work. Still, the placebo effect teaches an important lesson: a doctor's clear explanation, a caring attitude and a patient's trust are not merely polite extras. They can become part of the cure.", "passage_zh": "第二次世界大戰期間，一位名叫亨利・畢徹（Henry Beecher）的美國軍醫，用來救治傷兵的嗎啡快要用完了。根據一個廣為流傳的故事，一名護士只替一位士兵注射了食鹽水，卻告訴他那是強效止痛藥。令畢徹大感驚訝的是，這名士兵竟然平靜下來，疼痛似乎也減輕了。畢徹後來研究了這個令人費解的現象，並在 1955 年發表了一篇名為〈強大的安慰劑〉的著名論文。\n\n安慰劑是一種不含任何有效成分的治療，例如糖錠。這個字源自拉丁文，意思是「我將使人愉悅」。安慰劑在醫學研究中扮演不可或缺的角色。在藥物試驗中，一部分志願者服用真正的藥物，另一部分則服用外觀一模一樣的安慰劑。病人和醫生都不知道誰拿到的是哪一種，因此他們的期待不會影響結果。只有效果明顯勝過安慰劑的藥物，才會被認為有效。\n\n假藥丸究竟為什麼會有幫助呢？關鍵似乎在於「期待」。當人們相信自己正在接受治療時，大腦會釋放腦內啡，也就是身體天然的止痛物質。在 1978 年的一項實驗中，研究人員讓病人服用一種會阻斷腦內啡的藥物，結果安慰劑所帶來的止痛效果大致消失了。換句話說，安慰劑效應不只是想像而已，它牽涉到身體裡真實的化學變化。不過，它的力量是有限的。安慰劑主要影響那些由大腦參與形成的症狀，例如疼痛和噁心。一顆糖錠無法讓腫瘤縮小，也無法殺死細菌。\n\n奇妙的是，假治療的細節也很重要。兩顆安慰劑往往比一顆更有效。在一項實驗中，被告知止痛藥每顆要價 2.5 美元的志願者，比被告知藥價已打折到十美分的志願者，回報感受到更多的舒緩，儘管所有人拿到的都是同樣的糖錠。一種治療看起來越令人印象深刻，大腦的反應就越強烈。\n\n更令人驚訝的是，即使病人知道是假藥，安慰劑仍可能發揮作用。在 2010 年哈佛大學的一項研究中，研究人員坦白告訴患有一種長期消化道疾病的病人，他們的藥丸裡完全沒有藥物成分，然而許多人的改善程度仍然超過沒有服用任何藥丸的病人。這種效應也可能反過來發生。當病人被告知可能出現的副作用時，有些人即使只服用安慰劑，也會出現頭痛或頭暈。這稱為「反安慰劑效應」（nocebo effect），名稱來自一個意思是「我將造成傷害」的拉丁字。\n\n這一切並不表示醫生應該用糖錠取代真正的藥物。暗中給病人假治療會引發嚴重的倫理問題，而危險的疾病也需要真正有效的治療。儘管如此，安慰劑效應帶給我們重要的一課：醫生清楚的說明、關懷的態度，以及病人的信任，並不只是禮貌性的附加品，它們也可能成為治療的一部分。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why army doctors ran out of morphine during the Second World War", "How expectations can make fake treatments produce real effects, and what this means for medicine", "Why sugar pills work better than most modern medicines", "How drug companies decide the prices of new painkillers"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從畢徹的故事談起，說明安慰劑在藥物試驗中的角色（第二段）、它如何透過期待產生真實的身體變化（第三至五段），最後指出醫病之間的信任也是治療的一部分（第六段），故主旨是 (B)。(A) 只是第一段的開場背景；(C) 第三段明說糖錠無法讓腫瘤縮小或殺死細菌，第六段也說危險的疾病需要真正有效的治療；(D) 第四段的藥價只是實驗中的設定，並非在談藥廠如何定價。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why do neither the patients nor the doctors know who is receiving the placebo in a drug test?", "options": ["To save money on expensive medicines", "To make the volunteers feel less nervous", "To prevent their expectations from affecting the results", "To make sure every volunteer gets the real drug in the end"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Neither the patients nor the doctors know who is getting which, so their expectations cannot affect the results. 讓雙方都不知情，是為了避免期待影響試驗結果，故選 (C)。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the 1978 experiment described in paragraph 3 suggest?", "options": ["The placebo effect involves real chemical changes in the body.", "Endorphins make pain worse for most patients.", "Placebos work better when they are more expensive.", "Patients who take placebos are only pretending to feel better."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：阻斷腦內啡的藥物使安慰劑的止痛效果 largely disappeared，接著作者說 the placebo effect ... is not merely imagination; it involves real chemical changes，可知安慰劑效應牽涉身體真實的化學變化，故選 (A)。(B) 文中說腦內啡是天然止痛物質，正好相反；(C) 是第四段另一項實驗的發現，與 1978 年的實驗無關；(D) 與「不只是想像」的結論相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"astonishment\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["anger", "disappointment", "great surprise", "boredom"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：士兵只被注射了食鹽水，卻 calmed down and his pain seemed to ease，這完全出乎醫生意料，因此 To Beecher's astonishment 表示「令畢徹非常驚訝」，astonishment 與 great surprise 最接近，故選 (C)。(A) 生氣、(B) 失望、(D) 無聊，都不符合看到意外好轉時的反應。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of the nocebo effect?", "options": ["A girl feels less pain after taking a pill that she believes is a strong painkiller.", "A patient feels better after taking pills that he knows contain no medicine.", "A man recovers from an infection after taking real antibiotics.", "A woman gets a headache after reading about the side effects of a pill that is actually a placebo."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段說明反安慰劑效應：When patients are warned about possible side effects, some develop headaches or dizziness even from a placebo. 被告知副作用後，吃了安慰劑反而出現頭痛，正是 (D) 的情況，故選 (D)。(A) 是一般的安慰劑效應；(B) 是第五段前半提到的「公開的安慰劑」；(C) 是真正藥物的療效，與安慰劑無關。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a134", "title": "The Roll of Paper That Changed Medicine: The Birth of the Stethoscope", "title_zh": "改變醫學的一卷紙：聽診器的誕生", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["History", "Health"], "passage": "In the early 1800s, doctors examining a patient's heart or lungs had only two simple methods. They could tap on the chest and listen to the sounds it made, or they could press an ear directly against the patient's body. Neither worked well. Tapping revealed little about the heart, and putting an ear on a stranger's chest was uncomfortable for both people, not to mention unhygienic in an age when many patients rarely bathed.\n\nIn 1816, a French doctor named René Laennec faced exactly this problem at a hospital in Paris. His patient was a young woman with signs of heart disease. Because she was overweight, tapping told him almost nothing, and because of her age and sex, he felt it would be improper to place his ear against her chest. Then he remembered that the scratch of a pin at one end of a long piece of wood can be heard clearly at the other end. Laennec rolled a few sheets of paper into a tight tube, put one end on the woman's chest and held his ear to the other. To his delight, he could hear her heartbeat more clearly than ever before.\n\nLaennec spent the next three years improving his invention. He eventually settled on a hollow wooden tube about 25 centimeters long and named it the stethoscope, from the Greek words for \"chest\" and \"to examine.\" More importantly, he developed a method for using it. He listened to the chests of hundreds of patients and wrote down what he heard. Later, when some of those patients died, he compared his notes with what he found in their bodies. In this way, he learned which sounds were linked to diseases such as pneumonia and tuberculosis, and in 1819 he published a book that taught doctors to \"read\" the body through the ear.\n\nNot everyone was impressed. Some doctors laughed at the wooden tube, and others doubted that it would ever come into general use. Over the following decades, however, its value became impossible to deny. In the 1850s, a version with flexible tubes and two earpieces appeared, and the stethoscope hanging around a doctor's neck gradually became the most recognizable symbol of medicine.\n\nThe story has a sad twist. Laennec died of tuberculosis in 1826, at the age of only 45, killed by one of the very diseases his invention had helped doctors identify. Today, some experts wonder whether his tool will be replaced by pocket-sized ultrasound machines that show images of the heart instead of just sounds. Yet Laennec's greatest gift was never simply a tube. It was the idea that careful observation, checked against evidence, can turn a vague impression into a reliable diagnosis.", "passage_zh": "十九世紀初，醫生要檢查病人的心臟或肺部，只有兩種簡單的方法。他們可以敲擊胸口，聽聽它發出的聲音；也可以把耳朵直接貼在病人身上。這兩種方法效果都不好。敲擊幾乎無法透露心臟的狀況，而把耳朵貼在陌生人的胸口上，對雙方來說都很不自在，更別提在那個許多病人很少洗澡的年代，這麼做有多不衛生了。\n\n1816 年，一位名叫雷奈克（René Laennec）的法國醫生在巴黎一家醫院裡，正好遇上了這個難題。他的病人是一位出現心臟病徵兆的年輕女子。由於她體型偏胖，敲擊胸口幾乎聽不出什麼；又因為她的年齡與性別，他覺得把耳朵貼在她胸前並不得體。這時他想起，在一根長木頭的一端用別針刮出的聲音，可以在另一端清楚地聽見。雷奈克把幾張紙捲成一根緊實的紙筒，一端放在那位女子的胸口，另一端貼著自己的耳朵。令他欣喜的是，他聽到的心跳聲比以往任何時候都清楚。\n\n接下來三年，雷奈克不斷改良他的發明。他最後選定一根長約 25 公分的空心木管，並將它命名為 stethoscope（聽診器），這個字來自希臘文中的「胸部」與「檢查」。更重要的是，他發展出一套使用這項工具的方法。他聆聽了數百位病人的胸腔，並把聽到的聲音記錄下來。後來，當其中一些病人過世時，他便將筆記與在他們遺體中發現的狀況互相比對。透過這種方式，他了解了哪些聲音與肺炎、肺結核等疾病有關，並在 1819 年出版了一本書，教導醫生如何透過耳朵「讀懂」身體。\n\n並不是每個人都對此印象深刻。有些醫生嘲笑這根木管，也有人懷疑它是否真的會普及。然而在接下來的幾十年裡，它的價值已經無法否認。1850 年代，出現了附有軟管與兩個耳塞的版本，而掛在醫生脖子上的聽診器，也逐漸成為醫學最具代表性的象徵。\n\n這個故事有個令人感傷的轉折。雷奈克在 1826 年死於肺結核，得年僅 45 歲，奪走他生命的，正是他的發明曾幫助醫生辨識出的疾病之一。如今，有些專家懷疑他的這項工具是否會被口袋大小的超音波儀器取代，因為這些儀器能顯示心臟的影像，而不只是聲音。然而，雷奈克最偉大的貢獻從來就不只是一根管子，而是這樣的觀念：仔細的觀察經過證據的檢驗，就能把模糊的印象轉變為可靠的診斷。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why tuberculosis was common in nineteenth-century Paris", "How the stethoscope was invented and why it changed medicine", "How children's games have inspired many medical inventions", "Why modern doctors have stopped using stethoscopes"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文介紹雷奈克在 1816 年如何想出用紙筒聽心跳（第二段）、如何改良並發展出一套診斷方法（第三段）、聽診器如何成為醫學的象徵（第四段），以及他留下的觀念（第五段），故選 (B)。(A) 肺結核只是文中提到的疾病之一；(C) 文中沒有談到兒童遊戲啟發其他發明；(D) 第五段只說有些專家懷疑聽診器將來是否會被取代，並未說醫生已不再使用。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why was tapping on the young woman's chest not useful?", "options": ["She was overweight.", "She refused to let the doctor touch her.", "The hospital was too noisy.", "Laennec had never learned how to do it."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Because she was overweight, tapping told him almost nothing. 因為病人體型偏胖，敲擊胸口幾乎聽不出什麼，故選 (A)。(B)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 第一段說敲擊是當時醫生常用的方法，文中並未說雷奈克不會。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why was it important that Laennec compared his notes with what he found in the bodies of patients who had died?", "options": ["It proved that wooden tubes worked better than paper ones.", "It helped him discover a cure for tuberculosis.", "It allowed him to link particular chest sounds to particular diseases.", "It showed that tapping on the chest was more reliable than listening."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：透過把筆記和遺體中的發現互相比對，he learned which sounds were linked to diseases such as pneumonia and tuberculosis，因此能把特定的聲音和特定的疾病連結起來，故選 (C)。(A) 文中沒有比較紙筒與木管的效果，這也與比對遺體無關；(B) 文中沒有說他找到治療方法，第五段反而說他死於肺結核；(D) 第一段說敲擊效果不好。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"improper\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["too expensive", "physically impossible", "medically dangerous", "not socially acceptable"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：because of her age and sex, he felt it would be improper to place his ear against her chest。因為病人是年輕女性，把耳朵貼在她胸前在當時的社會觀念裡並不得體，再對照第一段「對雙方都很不自在」，可知 improper 指「不合禮儀、不恰當」，故選 (D)。(A) 與金錢無關；(B) 這麼做並非做不到；(C) 文中沒有說這樣做有醫療上的危險。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the last paragraph, what does the author think was Laennec's greatest contribution?", "options": ["A tool that will never be replaced by modern machines", "The idea of making diagnoses through careful observation checked against evidence", "The first pocket-sized ultrasound machine", "A cure for the disease that later killed him"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段最後：Laennec's greatest gift was never simply a tube. It was the idea that careful observation, checked against evidence, can turn a vague impression into a reliable diagnosis. 作者認為他最大的貢獻是「仔細觀察並以證據檢驗」的診斷觀念，故選 (B)。(A) 作者提到聽診器可能被超音波儀器取代，並未說它永遠不會被取代；(C) 超音波儀器是現代的設備，不是雷奈克發明的；(D) 他死於肺結核，並沒有找到治療方法。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a135", "title": "Step Outside and See Better: Taiwan's Battle Against Myopia", "title_zh": "走到戶外，看得更清楚：臺灣對抗近視之戰", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Health", "Education"], "passage": "In almost any Taiwanese senior high school classroom, most students wear glasses. Surveys have found that more than eight out of ten Taiwanese students are nearsighted by the time they finish high school, one of the highest rates in the world. Myopia, or nearsightedness, is often treated as a minor inconvenience that glasses can fix. Doctors, however, are worried, because severe myopia greatly raises the risk of serious eye problems later in life, such as a detached retina, which can cause blindness.\n\nMyopia usually develops because the eyeball grows too long. As a result, light from distant objects comes into focus in front of the retina, the light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye, instead of directly on it, so faraway things look blurry. Once the eye has grown too long, it does not shrink back. The best strategy, therefore, is to prevent or delay myopia during childhood, while the eye is still growing.\n\nPeople have long blamed myopia on reading too closely, and more recently on staring at phones. Close work does seem to play a role, but researchers have found that another factor matters even more: time spent outdoors. In a well-known 2008 study, scientists compared six- and seven-year-old children of Chinese background living in Sydney, Australia, and in Singapore. Only about 3 percent of the Sydney children were nearsighted, compared with about 29 percent in Singapore. The most striking difference was how long they spent outdoors: nearly 14 hours a week in Sydney, but only about 3 hours in Singapore.\n\nWhy would being outside protect the eyes? Scientists believe the key is brightness. Even on a cloudy day, outdoor light is usually several times brighter than classroom lighting, and on a sunny day it can be a hundred times brighter. Bright light appears to trigger the release of dopamine in the retina, a chemical signal that slows the growth of the eyeball.\n\nTaiwan has put this research into practice. In 2010, the government launched a program called \"Tian-Tian 120,\" which encourages schools and parents to give children 120 minutes of outdoor activity every day. Students are also taught the \"3010\" rule: after 30 minutes of close work, rest the eyes for 10 minutes by looking into the distance. After years of steady increases, the rate of poor eyesight among elementary school students has stopped rising and even begun to fall.\n\nFor teenagers who already wear glasses, going outside will not undo their myopia, though eye doctors can offer treatments to slow it down. Still, the lesson is surprisingly pleasant. Protecting your eyes does not mean giving up books or screens. It may simply mean stepping out into the daylight more often and letting your eyes enjoy the view.", "passage_zh": "在臺灣幾乎任何一間高中教室裡，大多數學生都戴著眼鏡。調查發現，臺灣學生到高中畢業時，有超過八成患有近視，是全世界近視比例最高的地區之一。近視常被當成戴副眼鏡就能解決的小麻煩。然而醫生們很擔心，因為高度近視會大幅提高日後發生嚴重眼疾的風險，例如視網膜剝離，而這可能導致失明。\n\n近視通常是因為眼球長得太長。結果，遠處物體的光線會在視網膜（眼睛後方對光敏感的那一層組織）的前方聚焦，而不是剛好落在視網膜上，因此遠處的東西看起來就模糊不清。眼球一旦長得太長，就不會再縮回去。因此，最好的策略是在眼睛仍在發育的童年時期，預防或延緩近視的發生。\n\n人們長久以來把近視歸咎於看書距離太近，近年來則歸咎於盯著手機看。近距離用眼似乎確實有影響，但研究人員發現，另一個因素更加重要：待在戶外的時間。在 2008 年一項著名的研究中，科學家比較了分別住在澳洲雪梨和新加坡、六到七歲的華裔兒童。雪梨的孩子只有大約 3% 近視，新加坡則約有 29%。兩組孩子最明顯的差異，在於待在戶外的時間：雪梨的孩子每週將近 14 小時，新加坡的孩子卻只有約 3 小時。\n\n為什麼待在戶外能保護眼睛呢？科學家認為關鍵在於亮度。即使是陰天，戶外光線通常也比教室的燈光亮好幾倍；到了晴天，更可能亮上一百倍。明亮的光線似乎會促使視網膜釋放多巴胺，這種化學訊號能減緩眼球的生長。\n\n臺灣已經把這項研究付諸實行。2010 年，政府推出名為「天天戶外 120」的計畫，鼓勵學校和家長讓孩子每天有 120 分鐘的戶外活動。學生也被教導「3010」原則：近距離用眼 30 分鐘後，要望向遠方，讓眼睛休息 10 分鐘。在多年持續上升之後，國小學生視力不良的比率已經停止攀升，甚至開始下降。\n\n對已經戴眼鏡的青少年來說，到戶外活動並不能讓近視消失，不過眼科醫師可以提供減緩近視加深的治療。儘管如此，這個啟示出乎意料地令人愉快。保護眼睛並不代表要放棄書本或螢幕，或許只是代表要更常走進陽光裡，讓眼睛好好欣賞外面的風景。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To advertise a new kind of glasses for teenagers", "To explain why myopia is so common and how time outdoors can help prevent it", "To argue that students should stop reading books and using phones", "To compare the school systems of Taiwan, Australia and Singapore"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先說明臺灣近視問題的嚴重性與近視的成因（第一、二段），再介紹戶外時間能預防近視的研究與原因（第三、四段），以及臺灣的推行措施（第五段），故選 (B)。(A) 文中沒有推銷任何眼鏡；(C) 第六段明說保護眼睛 does not mean giving up books or screens；(D) 文中比較的是兒童的戶外時間與近視比例，不是教育制度。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happens in the eye of a nearsighted person?", "options": ["The retina becomes too sensitive to bright light.", "The eyeball becomes too short to focus properly.", "Light from distant objects comes into focus in front of the retina.", "The eye produces too much dopamine."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：the eyeball grows too long. As a result, light from distant objects comes into focus in front of the retina ... instead of directly on it。近視是眼球太長，使遠處光線聚焦在視網膜前方，故選 (C)。(B) 與「眼球太長」相反；(A) 文中未提及；(D) 第四段說多巴胺能減緩眼球生長，並非近視的成因。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the 2008 study described in paragraph 3?", "options": ["Children in Singapore read far more books than children in Sydney.", "The amount of time children spend outdoors seems to be closely linked to myopia.", "Children of Chinese background are naturally more likely to become nearsighted.", "Moving to Australia can cure a child's myopia."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：兩地都是華裔兒童，雪梨孩子近視約 3%，新加坡約 29%，而最明顯的差異是戶外時間（每週將近 14 小時對約 3 小時），可推知戶外時間與近視密切相關，故選 (B)。(A) 文中沒有比較兩地孩子的閱讀量；(C) 兩組都是華裔，近視比例卻相差很大，反而說明不能只用血統解釋；(D) 第二段說眼球長太長後不會縮回，文中也沒有說搬家能治好近視。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the \"3010\" rule, what should a student do after reading for 30 minutes?", "options": ["Rest the eyes for 10 minutes by looking into the distance", "Spend 30 minutes playing outdoors", "Keep reading for 10 more minutes under brighter light", "Have the eyes checked by a doctor every 10 days"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：the \"3010\" rule: after 30 minutes of close work, rest the eyes for 10 minutes by looking into the distance。近距離用眼 30 分鐘後，要望向遠方讓眼睛休息 10 分鐘，故選 (A)。(B) 混淆了「天天戶外 120」與 3010 原則中的數字；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"trigger\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["prevent", "measure", "hide", "cause"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：Bright light appears to trigger the release of dopamine in the retina。明亮的光線使視網膜「釋放」多巴胺，進而減緩眼球生長，因此 trigger 指「引發、促使」，與 cause 最接近，故選 (D)。(A) 阻止，意思相反；(B) 測量、(C) 隱藏，都無法說明光線與多巴胺釋放的因果關係。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a136", "title": "Minutes That Matter: Why Everyone Should Learn CPR", "title_zh": "關鍵的幾分鐘：為什麼人人都該學 CPR", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Health", "Society"], "passage": "Imagine you are waiting for a train when a man nearby suddenly collapses. He is not moving, and he does not seem to be breathing normally. A crowd gathers. Some people take out their phones; others look at one another, hoping someone else knows what to do. The next few minutes will decide whether this man lives or dies, and the outcome depends less on doctors than on the ordinary people standing around him.\n\nThe man has most likely suffered a sudden cardiac arrest. Although people often confuse the two, this is different from a heart attack. A heart attack happens when blood flow to part of the heart is blocked, and the patient is usually awake and in pain. In a cardiac arrest, the heart's electrical system goes wrong, and instead of pumping, the heart begins to quiver uselessly. Blood stops reaching the brain, and the person loses consciousness within seconds.\n\nTime is the enemy. Without oxygen, brain cells begin to die after about four to six minutes, and a person's chance of survival falls by roughly 7 to 10 percent for every minute without help. Even in a big city, an ambulance usually needs several minutes to arrive, so the people already at the scene are the only ones who can act quickly enough.\n\nIn Taiwan, the steps are taught as \"Call, Call, C, D.\" First, call out to the person and tap his shoulders to see whether he responds. If he does not, call 119 and ask someone to bring an automated external defibrillator, or AED. Next comes C, for compressions: push hard and fast in the center of the chest, about 5 to 6 centimeters deep and 100 to 120 times per minute, roughly the beat of the disco song \"Stayin' Alive.\" Finally, D stands for defibrillation: as soon as the AED arrives, switch it on and follow its spoken instructions.\n\nMany people hesitate because they are afraid of doing something wrong. Some worry about breaking the person's ribs. This can happen, but ribs heal, while a heart that never restarts cannot. Others fear that the AED might give a dangerous shock. In fact, the machine checks the heart's rhythm by itself and delivers a shock only when one is needed. If you are unsure what to do, the 119 operator can guide you over the phone, and Taiwan's law protects people who try in good faith to save a life.\n\nLearning these skills takes only a few hours, and many fire departments and schools offer courses. Since most cardiac arrests happen at home, the life you save may well belong to someone you love. Taking out a phone to film is easy. Taking action is harder, but it is the only choice that can make a difference.", "passage_zh": "想像你正在等火車，附近一名男子突然倒下。他一動也不動，呼吸似乎也不正常。人群聚集過來。有些人拿出手機；有些人面面相覷，希望有別人知道該怎麼做。接下來的幾分鐘將決定這名男子的生死，而結果與其說取決於醫生，不如說取決於圍在他身邊的普通人。\n\n這名男子很可能是發生了突發性心跳停止。雖然人們常把兩者混為一談，但這和心臟病發作並不相同。心臟病發作是因為通往心臟某個部位的血流被阻塞，病人通常意識清醒，並感到疼痛。而在心跳停止時，心臟的電訊系統出了問題，心臟不再把血液送出去，而是開始無效地顫動。血液無法送到大腦，患者會在幾秒內失去意識。\n\n時間就是敵人。腦細胞在缺氧大約四到六分鐘後就會開始死亡，而每多過一分鐘得不到救助，存活的機會就會下降大約 7% 到 10%。即使在大城市，救護車通常也需要好幾分鐘才能抵達，因此已經在現場的人，是唯一能夠及時採取行動的人。\n\n在臺灣，急救步驟被教成「叫、叫、C、D」。首先，叫喚患者並拍打他的肩膀，看他是否有反應。如果沒有反應，就打 119 求救，並請人去拿自動體外心臟電擊去顫器，也就是 AED。接下來是 C，代表胸外按壓：在胸口中央用力、快速地按壓，深度約 5 到 6 公分，每分鐘 100 到 120 下，大約就是迪斯可歌曲〈Stayin' Alive〉的節拍。最後，D 代表電擊去顫：AED 一送到，就打開電源，依照它的語音指示操作。\n\n許多人因為害怕做錯而猶豫不決。有些人擔心會壓斷患者的肋骨。這確實可能發生，但肋骨會癒合，一顆再也無法重新跳動的心臟卻不會。也有人擔心 AED 可能會給出危險的電擊。事實上，這台機器會自行分析心律，只有在需要時才會電擊。如果你不確定該怎麼做，119 的執勤人員可以透過電話引導你，而且臺灣的法律也保護出於善意救人的人。\n\n學會這些技能只需要幾個小時，許多消防局和學校都有開設課程。由於大多數的心跳停止發生在家中，你救回的生命很可能就屬於你所愛的人。拿出手機拍影片很容易；採取行動比較難，但那是唯一能帶來改變的選擇。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To encourage ordinary people to learn CPR and use AEDs", "To explain why ambulances in big cities are often late", "To compare heart attacks with other common heart diseases", "To warn readers not to take photos at train stations"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文說明心跳停止時現場民眾的行動決定生死（第一、三段），介紹「叫、叫、C、D」的步驟（第四段），化解大家的顧慮（第五段），最後鼓勵讀者去上課、採取行動（第六段），故選 (A)。(B) 第三段只說救護車需要幾分鐘，並非在批評救護車太慢；(C) 第二段比較心臟病發作與心跳停止只是為了說明，並非全文目的；(D) 文中並未談到在車站拍照的規定。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how is a cardiac arrest different from a heart attack?", "options": ["A cardiac arrest only happens to elderly people.", "In a cardiac arrest, the heart stops pumping properly and the person quickly loses consciousness.", "A heart attack is caused by a problem in the heart's electrical system.", "A person having a cardiac arrest is usually awake and in pain."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：In a cardiac arrest, the heart's electrical system goes wrong, and instead of pumping, the heart begins to quiver uselessly ... the person loses consciousness within seconds. 心跳停止時心臟無法正常送血，患者幾秒內就失去意識，故選 (B)。(C)(D) 把兩者的特徵弄反了：電訊系統出問題的是心跳停止，意識清醒且感到疼痛的是心臟病發作；(A) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "When doing chest compressions correctly, about how many times should a person press in one minute?", "options": ["30 to 50 times", "60 to 80 times", "100 to 120 times", "150 to 180 times"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：push hard and fast in the center of the chest, about 5 to 6 centimeters deep and 100 to 120 times per minute。胸外按壓每分鐘約 100 到 120 下，故選 (C)。其他選項的數字都不符合文中的說明。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "A classmate suddenly collapses and does not respond when you call out to him and tap his shoulders. According to the passage, what should you do next?", "options": ["Wait for a teacher to arrive and decide what to do", "Give him some water to drink", "Call 119 and ask someone to bring an AED", "Move him to a quiet room so that he can rest"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：叫喚並拍打肩膀後，If he does not [respond], call 119 and ask someone to bring an automated external defibrillator, or AED。下一步應該打 119 並請人拿 AED，故選 (C)。(A) 第三段強調現場的人必須立刻行動，不能等待；(B)(D) 文中沒有這些步驟，也會浪費寶貴的時間。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"hesitate\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["move quickly", "complain loudly", "refuse to pay", "pause before acting"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：Many people hesitate because they are afraid of doing something wrong。因為怕做錯而「遲遲不敢行動」，hesitate 即「猶豫」，與 pause before acting 最接近，故選 (D)。(A) 迅速行動，意思相反；(B) 大聲抱怨、(C) 拒絕付錢，都與害怕做錯的情境無關。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a137", "title": "Turning Up the Heat: Why Your Body Gives Itself a Fever", "title_zh": "把溫度調高：身體為什麼要讓自己發燒", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Health"], "passage": "You wake up in the middle of the night shivering. Even under two thick blankets you feel freezing, yet when your mother checks your temperature, the thermometer reads 39°C. How can you feel cold when your body is actually hotter than usual? The answer is that your body is not out of control. It is deliberately turning up the heat, and it has good reasons for doing so.\n\nBody temperature is controlled by the hypothalamus, a small region deep in the brain that works like a thermostat. Normally it keeps the body at around 37°C. When germs invade, however, the immune system releases chemical messengers that travel to the hypothalamus and raise this \"set point\" by a degree or two. Suddenly your normal temperature feels too low, so your body works to warm itself up: blood vessels in the skin narrow to keep heat in, and muscles shiver to produce more. Later, when the set point returns to normal, the opposite happens. You start sweating and throw off the blankets, a moment people describe as the fever \"breaking.\"\n\nWhy would the body choose to feel so miserable? Many viruses and bacteria multiply less efficiently when the temperature rises, while certain white blood cells appear to move faster and attack invaders more effectively. Further evidence comes from an unexpected source: lizards. In the 1970s, scientists infected desert iguanas with bacteria. Unable to produce heat inside their bodies, the lizards moved to warmer spots to raise their temperature. When researchers kept some infected lizards in cooler conditions, more of them died.\n\nFever does have costs. It burns extra energy, causes the body to lose water, and makes people feel terrible. Medicines such as acetaminophen can bring the temperature down, and they are useful when a patient cannot rest or eat. However, many doctors now advise treating the patient rather than the number on the thermometer. A child with a temperature of 38.5°C who is still drinking and playing may not need medicine at all.\n\nIt is also important not to confuse fever with heatstroke. In heatstroke, the brain's thermostat has not been reset; instead, the body's cooling system has been overwhelmed by extreme heat. Fever medicine will not help, and the person must be cooled down immediately.\n\nOf course, a fever is a sign that something is wrong, and it should not be ignored. A fever that lasts more than a few days, an extremely high temperature, or symptoms such as difficulty breathing or confusion all mean it is time to see a doctor. In most cases, though, a fever is not the enemy. It is the body's way of fighting back.", "passage_zh": "你在半夜裡發著抖醒來。即使蓋了兩條厚棉被，你還是覺得冷得要命；然而當媽媽替你量體溫時，溫度計卻顯示 39°C。你的身體明明比平常還熱，為什麼你會覺得冷呢？答案是：你的身體並沒有失控，它是刻意把溫度調高的，而且這麼做有充分的理由。\n\n體溫是由下視丘控制的。下視丘是大腦深處的一個小區域，運作起來就像恆溫器。平常它會讓身體維持在 37°C 左右。然而當病菌入侵時，免疫系統會釋放化學傳訊物質，這些物質傳到下視丘，把這個「設定點」調高一、兩度。突然之間，你正常的體溫感覺起來太低了，於是身體會設法讓自己暖和起來：皮膚的血管收縮，把熱留在體內；肌肉則發抖，產生更多熱量。之後，當設定點回到正常時，情況就會反過來。你開始流汗，把棉被掀開，人們把這一刻形容為燒「退了」。\n\n身體為什麼要選擇讓自己這麼難受呢？許多病毒和細菌在溫度升高時，繁殖的效率會變差；而某些白血球似乎能移動得更快，更有效地攻擊入侵者。另一項證據則來自一個意想不到的地方：蜥蜴。1970 年代，科學家讓沙漠鬣蜥感染細菌。這些蜥蜴無法在體內自行產生熱量，於是牠們移動到較溫暖的地方來提高體溫。當研究人員把一些受感染的蜥蜴放在較涼爽的環境中時，死亡的數量就變多了。\n\n發燒確實也有代價。它會消耗額外的能量，使身體流失水分，還會讓人感覺很糟。乙醯胺酚之類的藥物可以讓體溫下降，當病人無法休息或進食時，這類藥物就很有用。不過，現在許多醫生建議，應該治療的是病人，而不是溫度計上的數字。一個體溫 38.5°C、但仍然會喝水、會玩耍的孩子，可能根本不需要吃藥。\n\n另外很重要的一點是，不要把發燒和中暑混為一談。中暑時，大腦的恆溫器並沒有被重新設定，而是身體的散熱系統被極端的高溫壓垮了。退燒藥幫不上忙，必須立刻讓患者降溫。\n\n當然，發燒表示身體出了問題，不應該置之不理。如果發燒持續好幾天、體溫極高，或者出現呼吸困難、意識混亂等症狀，就表示該去看醫生了。不過在大多數情況下，發燒並不是敵人，而是身體反擊的方式。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["A fever is usually a useful defense that the body creates on purpose.", "People with a fever should always take medicine right away.", "Lizards are more likely than humans to die from infections.", "Heatstroke is the most common cause of a high body temperature."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段說身體是 deliberately turning up the heat，第二、三段說明發燒的機制與好處，最後一段總結 a fever is not the enemy. It is the body's way of fighting back，故選 (A)。(B) 與第四段「治療病人而非溫度計上的數字」、38.5°C 的孩子可能不需要吃藥相反；(C) 蜥蜴實驗只是佐證，並未和人類比較；(D) 第五段只提醒不要把中暑和發燒混淆，並未說中暑最常見。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why does a person with a fever often feel cold at first?", "options": ["The blood vessels in the skin open wider and let heat escape.", "The brain's set point has been raised, so a normal temperature feels too low.", "The germs in the body produce cold chemicals.", "The person has been sweating too much."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：化學傳訊物質使下視丘把 set point 調高，Suddenly your normal temperature feels too low，所以身體會發抖、覺得冷，故選 (B)。(A) 文中說皮膚的血管是 narrow（收縮）以保留熱量，正好相反；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 流汗是設定點回到正常、退燒時才發生的。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the experiment with desert iguanas in paragraph 3 suggest?", "options": ["Lizards can produce fevers inside their bodies just as humans do.", "Cool conditions help lizards fight off bacteria.", "Raising body temperature may help animals survive infections.", "Desert iguanas cannot be infected by bacteria."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：受感染的鬣蜥會主動移到溫暖的地方提高體溫，而被放在較涼爽環境中的蜥蜴 more of them died，可推知提高體溫有助於動物在感染時存活，故選 (C)。(A) 文中說牠們 unable to produce heat inside their bodies；(B) 與實驗結果相反；(D) 科學家正是讓牠們感染了細菌。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"deliberately\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["by accident", "slowly", "painfully", "on purpose"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：your body is not out of control. It is deliberately turning up the heat, and it has good reasons for doing so。身體「沒有失控」且「有充分理由」這麼做，可知 deliberately 指「刻意地、故意地」，與 on purpose 最接近，故選 (D)。(A) 意外地，與「有充分理由」矛盾；(B) 慢慢地、(C) 痛苦地，都不符合上下文。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why will fever medicine not help a person with heatstroke?", "options": ["Heatstroke is caused by bacteria that medicine cannot kill.", "In heatstroke, the brain's set point has not been raised; the body's cooling system has failed.", "People with heatstroke are unable to swallow pills.", "Fever medicine only works on young children."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：In heatstroke, the brain's thermostat has not been reset; instead, the body's cooling system has been overwhelmed by extreme heat. Fever medicine will not help。中暑不是設定點被調高，而是散熱系統被高溫壓垮，所以退燒藥無效，故選 (B)。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a138", "title": "Too Clean for Our Own Good? The Puzzle of Rising Allergies", "title_zh": "太乾淨反而不好？過敏越來越多之謎", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "Between 1997 and 2008, the rate of peanut allergy among American children more than tripled, and today some schools in Western countries ban peanut snacks completely. Over the past several decades, doctors in many developed countries have also seen sharp increases in hay fever, asthma and eczema. Since human genes cannot change that quickly, something in the way we live must have changed.\n\nAn allergy is a mistake made by the immune system. Its job is to defend the body against real threats such as viruses and harmful bacteria. In people with allergies, however, it overreacts to harmless substances like pollen, dust mites or certain foods, treating them as dangerous invaders. The result can be anything from a runny nose to a life-threatening reaction.\n\nIn 1989, a British doctor named David Strachan offered a possible explanation. After studying more than 17,000 British children, he noticed something odd: children with several older brothers and sisters were less likely to develop hay fever. Strachan suggested that the infections older siblings brought home might train a young child's immune system, and that smaller families and cleaner homes had taken away this early training. His idea became known as the \"hygiene hypothesis.\"\n\nLater research has refined the theory. Scientists now believe that what matters is not dirt itself or catching common illnesses, but regular contact with a wide variety of harmless microbes, especially those found on farms, in soil and around animals. A striking 2016 study compared two farming communities in the United States, the Amish and the Hutterites. The groups share similar genetic backgrounds and lifestyles, but the Amish farm in traditional ways and keep animals close to their homes, while the Hutterites use modern machinery. Asthma was about four times more common among Hutterite children. When dust from Amish homes was given to mice, it protected them against asthma-like reactions.\n\nTiming seems to matter as well. For years, parents of babies at high risk of allergies were told to avoid peanuts. Yet a 2015 study in Britain found that high-risk babies who regularly ate small amounts of peanut products from an early age were about 80 percent less likely to develop a peanut allergy by the age of five. Parents should still talk to a doctor before trying this at home.\n\nNone of this means we should stop washing our hands, which remains one of the best ways to prevent dangerous infections. The lesson is rather that the immune system, like a student, learns from experience. Playing outdoors, spending time with animals and eating a varied diet may give it the education it needs to tell real enemies from harmless strangers.", "passage_zh": "1997 年到 2008 年之間，美國兒童對花生過敏的比率成長為原來的三倍以上；如今，西方國家的一些學校甚至完全禁止學生帶花生零食。過去幾十年來，許多已開發國家的醫生也發現，花粉熱、氣喘和濕疹的病例大幅增加。既然人類的基因不可能改變得那麼快，一定是我們的生活方式出現了某些改變。\n\n過敏是免疫系統犯下的錯誤。免疫系統的任務是保護身體，對抗病毒和有害細菌等真正的威脅。然而在過敏的人身上，免疫系統會對花粉、塵蟎或某些食物等無害的物質反應過度，把它們當成危險的入侵者。結果可能從流鼻水到危及性命的反應都有。\n\n1989 年，一位名叫大衛・史特拉坎（David Strachan）的英國醫生提出了一種可能的解釋。在研究了超過一萬七千名英國兒童之後，他注意到一件奇怪的事：有好幾個哥哥姊姊的孩子，比較不容易得到花粉熱。史特拉坎認為，哥哥姊姊帶回家的感染，或許能訓練年幼孩子的免疫系統；而家庭規模變小、居家環境變得更乾淨，則讓孩子失去了這種早期訓練。他的想法後來被稱為「衛生假說」。\n\n後來的研究讓這個理論變得更加精確。科學家現在認為，重要的並不是髒污本身，也不是感染常見的疾病，而是經常接觸各式各樣無害的微生物，尤其是存在於農場、土壤和動物周遭的微生物。2016 年一項引人注目的研究，比較了美國的兩個農業社群：阿米希人（Amish）與哈特派信徒（Hutterites）。這兩個群體的遺傳背景與生活方式相似，但阿米希人以傳統方式務農，並把動物養在住家附近；哈特派信徒則使用現代化的機械。結果，哈特派兒童罹患氣喘的比例大約是阿米希兒童的四倍。當研究人員讓實驗小鼠接觸阿米希人家中的灰塵時，這些灰塵保護了牠們，使牠們不易出現類似氣喘的反應。\n\n時機似乎也很重要。多年來，過敏高風險嬰兒的父母都被告知要避免讓孩子吃花生。然而，英國 2015 年的一項研究發現，從很小的時候就經常吃少量花生製品的高風險嬰兒，到五歲時對花生過敏的機率大約低了 80%。不過，父母在家中嘗試這種做法之前，仍應先和醫生討論。\n\n這一切並不表示我們應該停止洗手，因為洗手仍然是預防危險感染的最佳方法之一。這些研究帶來的啟示其實是：免疫系統就像學生一樣，會從經驗中學習。到戶外玩耍、和動物相處，以及多樣化的飲食，或許能給免疫系統所需要的教育，讓它分辨得出真正的敵人與無害的陌生人。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why washing hands is the best way to prevent allergies", "How early contact with microbes and certain foods may affect the development of allergies", "How the Amish and the Hutterites live and farm in the United States", "Why peanut snacks should be banned in all schools"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從過敏人數增加談起，介紹衛生假說（第三段）、接觸多樣微生物的重要性（第四段）以及及早接觸花生的研究（第五段），說明早期的接觸可能影響過敏的發展，故選 (B)。(A) 第六段只說洗手能預防感染，並沒有說洗手能預防過敏；(C) 阿米希人與哈特派信徒只是第四段的研究例子；(D) 第一段只提到有些學校禁止花生零食，作者並未主張全面禁止。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what did David Strachan notice in his study?", "options": ["Children from cleaner homes were healthier in every way.", "Children who ate peanuts were more likely to develop asthma.", "Children without brothers or sisters caught fewer infections.", "Children with several older siblings were less likely to get hay fever."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：children with several older brothers and sisters were less likely to develop hay fever。有好幾個哥哥姊姊的孩子較不容易得到花粉熱，故選 (D)。(A) 與他的推論相反，他認為環境太乾淨反而少了早期訓練；(B) 花生研究是第五段 2015 年的研究，且與氣喘無關；(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did the researchers in the 2016 study compare the Amish and the Hutterites?", "options": ["The two groups are alike in many ways but differ in how closely they live with farm animals.", "Both groups refuse to see doctors when they are sick.", "The Hutterites keep more animals near their homes than the Amish do.", "The two groups eat completely different kinds of food."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：The groups share similar genetic backgrounds and lifestyles, but the Amish ... keep animals close to their homes, while the Hutterites use modern machinery。兩個群體在許多方面相似，主要差別在於是否以傳統方式務農、與動物接觸，因此適合用來比較環境的影響，故選 (A)。(C) 把兩者弄反了；(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"refined\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["proved completely wrong", "improved and made more exact", "made more popular", "translated into other languages"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Later research has refined the theory. 接著說明科學家現在認為重要的不是髒污本身，而是接觸多樣的無害微生物，等於是把原本的衛生假說修正得更精確，因此 refined 指「改進、使更精確」，故選 (B)。(A) 後來的研究並未完全推翻這個理論；(C)(D) 與上下文無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would the author most likely agree with?", "options": ["Children should stop washing their hands so that their immune systems become stronger.", "Babies at high risk of allergies should never be given peanut products.", "The rise in allergies has been caused mainly by changes in human genes.", "Playing outdoors and spending time with animals may help children's immune systems develop."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第六段：Playing outdoors, spending time with animals and eating a varied diet may give it the education it needs，作者認為這些經驗有助於免疫系統學習，故選 (D)。(A) 第六段明說不應停止洗手；(B) 與第五段「及早吃少量花生製品可降低過敏」的研究結果相反；(C) 第一段說基因不可能改變得那麼快，原因應在生活方式。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a139", "title": "Fighting Mosquitoes with Mosquitoes: A New Weapon Against Dengue", "title_zh": "以蚊制蚊：對抗登革熱的新武器", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Health", "Nature"], "passage": "In 2015, southern Taiwan suffered its worst outbreak of dengue fever in many decades. More than 40,000 people were infected, most of them in Tainan and Kaohsiung. Hospitals filled up, neighborhoods were sprayed with insecticide, and volunteers went from house to house searching for standing water. The enemy behind all this was tiny: the Aedes mosquito.\n\nDengue is caused by a virus that mosquitoes carry from one person to another. Its symptoms include a high fever, a severe headache, pain behind the eyes and aching joints, which explains its old nickname, \"breakbone fever.\" Most patients recover within a week or two, but some develop severe dengue, which can cause internal bleeding and can be fatal. To make matters worse, there are four types of the virus. Recovering from one type protects you only against that type, and a later infection with a different type actually increases the risk of severe illness.\n\nControlling the mosquitoes that spread dengue is not easy. Aedes mosquitoes bite mainly during the day, and they lay their eggs in small containers of clean water, such as flowerpot saucers, old tires and even discarded bottle caps. Their eggs can stick to the inside of a container and survive for months without water. That is why health workers in Taiwan urge people to regularly inspect, empty, clean and scrub anything that might hold water.\n\nIn recent years, scientists have developed a surprising new weapon: more mosquitoes. The idea depends on Wolbachia, a type of bacteria that lives naturally in many insects but not in Aedes aegypti, the main species that spreads dengue. Researchers discovered that when these mosquitoes carry Wolbachia, the dengue virus has great difficulty multiplying inside them, so they are far less likely to pass it on to humans. Better still, females pass Wolbachia to their young, so once Wolbachia mosquitoes are released, the bacteria gradually spread through the local population without the need for constant new releases.\n\nThe results have been encouraging. In a large trial in Yogyakarta, Indonesia, Wolbachia mosquitoes were released in some neighborhoods but not in others. The areas that received them saw 77 percent fewer dengue cases and 86 percent fewer hospital admissions for the disease. Importantly, the mosquitoes are not genetically modified, and the method does not depend on chemicals that may harm other insects.\n\nStill, Wolbachia is not a magic solution. Releasing mosquitoes requires years of planning and the support of local communities. Until such programs become widespread, the best protection remains simple: remove standing water, use insect repellent, and see a doctor quickly if a sudden fever appears after a mosquito bite. In the battle against dengue, everyone has a part to play.", "passage_zh": "2015 年，臺灣南部爆發了數十年來最嚴重的登革熱疫情。超過四萬人受到感染，大多數在臺南和高雄。醫院人滿為患，各個社區噴灑殺蟲劑，志工們挨家挨戶尋找積水。這一切背後的敵人非常微小：斑蚊。\n\n登革熱是由病毒引起的，蚊子會把病毒從一個人傳給另一個人。它的症狀包括高燒、劇烈頭痛、眼窩後方疼痛和關節痠痛，這也解釋了它過去的別名——「斷骨熱」。大多數病人在一到兩週內就會康復，但有些人會發展成重症登革熱，可能造成內出血，甚至致命。更糟的是，這種病毒共有四種型別。從某一型康復後，只能對那一型產生保護力；之後若感染另一型，反而會增加罹患重症的風險。\n\n控制傳播登革熱的蚊子並不容易。斑蚊主要在白天叮人，而且會在裝有乾淨積水的小容器裡產卵，例如花盆底盤、廢輪胎，甚至是被丟棄的瓶蓋。牠們的卵可以黏在容器內壁，在沒有水的情況下存活好幾個月。這就是為什麼臺灣的衛生人員要呼籲民眾，定期對任何可能積水的東西進行「巡、倒、清、刷」。\n\n近年來，科學家發展出一種令人意想不到的新武器：更多的蚊子。這個構想仰賴一種叫做沃爾巴克氏菌（Wolbachia）的細菌。這種細菌天然存在於許多昆蟲體內，卻不存在於傳播登革熱的主要蚊種埃及斑蚊體內。研究人員發現，當埃及斑蚊帶有沃爾巴克氏菌時，登革病毒很難在牠們體內繁殖，因此牠們把病毒傳給人類的可能性就大幅降低。更棒的是，雌蚊會把沃爾巴克氏菌傳給下一代，所以帶菌的蚊子一旦被釋放，這種細菌就會在當地的蚊子族群中逐漸擴散，不需要一再重新釋放。\n\n成果令人振奮。在印尼日惹的一項大規模試驗中，研究人員在部分社區釋放帶有沃爾巴克氏菌的蚊子，其他社區則沒有。釋放蚊子的地區，登革熱病例減少了 77%，因登革熱住院的人數也減少了 86%。重要的是，這些蚊子並沒有經過基因改造，而且這個方法也不必依賴可能傷害其他昆蟲的化學藥劑。\n\n儘管如此，沃爾巴克氏菌並不是萬靈丹。釋放蚊子需要多年的規劃，以及當地社區的支持。在這類計畫普及之前，最好的保護方法依然很簡單：清除積水、使用防蚊液，而且如果被蚊子叮咬後突然發燒，要盡快就醫。在對抗登革熱的戰役中，每個人都有自己的一份責任。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history of dengue fever outbreaks in Taiwan", "How dengue spreads and how a new method using bacteria may help control it", "Why genetically modified mosquitoes are dangerous to other insects", "How doctors treat patients with severe dengue in hospitals"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先介紹登革熱的疫情、症狀與斑蚊的習性（第一至三段），再說明利用沃爾巴克氏菌「以蚊制蚊」的新方法及其成效與限制（第四至六段），故選 (B)。(A) 文中只提到 2015 年的疫情；(C) 第五段說這些蚊子 not genetically modified；(D) 文中沒有說明醫院如何治療重症。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why can a second dengue infection be especially dangerous?", "options": ["The virus becomes resistant to all kinds of medicine.", "Mosquitoes bite more often during the second infection.", "Being infected by a different type of the virus raises the risk of severe illness.", "The first infection has already damaged the patient's joints."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：a later infection with a different type actually increases the risk of severe illness。之後感染不同型別的病毒，反而會增加罹患重症的風險，故選 (C)。(A)(B) 文中皆未提及；(D) 關節痠痛只是症狀之一，文中並未說會造成永久損傷。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would best help stop Aedes mosquitoes from breeding around a home?", "options": ["Keeping the windows closed only at night", "Regularly emptying and scrubbing flowerpot saucers", "Spraying insecticide once a year", "Storing clean water in open buckets"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：斑蚊在裝有乾淨積水的小容器（如花盆底盤）中產卵，卵還會黏在容器內壁，所以要定期 inspect, empty, clean and scrub，故選 (B)。(A) 斑蚊主要在白天叮人，而且關窗無法阻止牠們產卵；(C) 一年噴一次藥無法清除不斷出現的積水與蟲卵；(D) 開放的水桶正是斑蚊產卵的地方。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why is it important that female mosquitoes pass Wolbachia to their young?", "options": ["It allows Wolbachia to spread through the mosquito population without constant new releases.", "It makes the young mosquitoes bite more people.", "It kills the mosquitoes before they can lay eggs.", "It turns the mosquitoes into genetically modified insects."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：females pass Wolbachia to their young, so ... the bacteria gradually spread through the local population without the need for constant new releases。細菌能代代相傳，就會在蚊子族群中自行擴散，不必一再釋放，故選 (A)。(B)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 第五段說這些蚊子 not genetically modified。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"discarded\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["colorful", "expensive", "broken", "thrown away"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：such as flowerpot saucers, old tires and even discarded bottle caps。和 old tires（廢輪胎）並列、會在戶外積水的瓶蓋，指的是「被丟棄的」瓶蓋，discarded 與 thrown away 最接近，故選 (D)。(A) 彩色的、(B) 昂貴的，都與積水無關；(C) 瓶蓋是否破損並非重點，重點是被隨手丟棄在外。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a140", "title": "Three Scripts, One Message: How the Rosetta Stone Was Cracked", "title_zh": "三種文字，同一則訊息：羅塞塔石碑如何被解讀", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["History", "Culture"], "passage": "In July 1799, French soldiers were strengthening an old fort near the town of Rashid, which Europeans called Rosetta, in northern Egypt. While digging, they uncovered a broken slab of dark stone covered in carved writing. The stone carried the same text in three scripts: hieroglyphs at the top, a script called Demotic in the middle and ancient Greek at the bottom. Since scholars could still read Greek, the stone seemed to offer a key to a writing system that no one had understood for more than a thousand years.\n\nThe Greek section explained what the text was: a decree issued in 196 BC by a council of Egyptian priests to honor King Ptolemy V. At that time, Egypt was ruled by a Greek-speaking royal family, so important messages were often written in more than one script. Hieroglyphs were used for sacred purposes, Demotic for everyday documents and Greek for the government. After the British defeated the French in Egypt, the stone was handed over to them in 1801, and it has been in the British Museum since 1802.\n\nHaving a translation, however, did not make the puzzle easy. Many scholars assumed that hieroglyphs were pure symbols, with each picture standing for an idea rather than a sound, a belief that misled them for years. The English scientist Thomas Young made important progress by showing that the oval rings in the text, called cartouches, contained royal names, and he correctly identified several signs in the name Ptolemy. Yet he still believed that sound-based signs were used only to spell foreign names.\n\nThe breakthrough came from Jean-François Champollion, a young Frenchman who knew many ancient languages, including Coptic, a late form of Egyptian still used in church services. By comparing the names Ptolemy and Cleopatra, which share several sounds, he worked out the values of more signs. Then, in 1822, he read the names of Egyptian kings such as Ramesses and Thutmose in copies of temple carvings, names that were clearly not foreign. He realized that the system was a mixture: some signs represented sounds, while others stood for whole words or ideas. Legend says he rushed into his brother's office, shouted \"I've got it!\" and fainted.\n\nChampollion's discovery opened the door to three thousand years of Egyptian history. Today, scholars can read the words of pharaohs, priests and ordinary workers, from love poems to complaints about unpaid wages. The story also shows how knowledge grows: a lucky find, the careful work of many scholars and one person's willingness to question a widely held assumption all played a part. That is why, in English, \"a Rosetta Stone\" now means anything that provides the key to a difficult problem.", "passage_zh": "1799 年 7 月，法國士兵在埃及北部的小鎮拉希德（歐洲人稱之為「羅塞塔」）附近加固一座舊堡壘。挖掘時，他們發現了一塊破損的深色石板，上面刻滿了文字。石板上用三種文字刻著同一段內容：最上方是象形文字，中間是一種稱為「世俗體」的文字，最下方則是古希臘文。由於學者仍然讀得懂希臘文，這塊石碑似乎提供了一把鑰匙，可以解開一套一千多年來無人能懂的文字系統。\n\n希臘文的部分說明了這段文字的內容：這是一群埃及祭司在西元前 196 年頒布的詔令，用來表彰國王托勒密五世。當時埃及由一個說希臘語的王室統治，因此重要的訊息常以不只一種文字書寫：象形文字用於神聖的場合，世俗體用於日常文件，希臘文則用於政府事務。英國在埃及擊敗法國之後，這塊石碑於 1801 年被移交給英國，自 1802 年起便一直存放在大英博物館。\n\n然而，有了譯文並不代表謎題就容易解開。許多學者認定象形文字純粹是符號，每個圖案代表的是一個概念而不是一個聲音，這種看法讓他們多年來走錯了方向。英國科學家湯瑪斯‧楊取得了重要的進展：他證明文中那些橢圓形的圈（稱為「王名圈」）裡寫的是王室成員的名字，並正確辨認出「托勒密」這個名字中的幾個符號。但他仍然認為，表音的符號只用來拼寫外國人的名字。\n\n真正的突破來自讓－法蘭索瓦‧商博良。這位法國年輕人通曉許多古代語言，其中包括科普特語──一種晚期的埃及語，至今仍用於教會禮拜。他比較了「托勒密」和「克麗奧佩脫拉」這兩個有好幾個相同發音的名字，推算出更多符號的讀音。接著在 1822 年，他從神廟雕刻的摹本中讀出了拉美西斯、圖特摩斯等埃及國王的名字，而這些名字顯然不是外國名字。他領悟到這套文字系統是混合式的：有些符號代表聲音，有些則代表整個字詞或概念。傳說他衝進哥哥的辦公室，大喊「我找到答案了！」接著就昏倒了。\n\n商博良的發現開啟了通往三千年埃及歷史的大門。如今，學者能讀懂法老、祭司和平民百姓留下的文字，從情詩到抱怨工資被拖欠的申訴都有。這個故事也顯示了知識是如何累積的：一次幸運的發現、許多學者的細心研究，加上一個人願意質疑普遍被接受的看法，三者都功不可沒。這就是為什麼在英文中，「羅塞塔石碑」如今可以用來指任何能解開難題的關鍵。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why the Rosetta Stone is kept in the British Museum.", "How the Rosetta Stone helped scholars learn to read Egyptian hieroglyphs.", "How French soldiers built forts in northern Egypt.", "Why Ptolemy V was honored by a council of Egyptian priests."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從石碑的發現（第一段）、內容（第二段），談到湯瑪斯‧楊與商博良如何解讀象形文字（第三、四段）及其意義（第五段），主旨是石碑如何幫助學者讀懂象形文字。(A) 只在第二段末順帶提及；(C)(D) 都只是背景細節，並非主旨，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why was the same message written in three scripts?", "options": ["The priests wanted to help future scholars learn to read hieroglyphs.", "French soldiers later added two translations to the stone.", "Hieroglyphs could only be used to spell foreign names.", "Each script served a different purpose in Egypt at that time."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：當時埃及由說希臘語的王室統治，Hieroglyphs were used for sacred purposes, Demotic for everyday documents and Greek for the government，三種文字各有用途。(A) 文中未提及祭司有此用意；(B) 石碑本來就刻有三種文字，與法國士兵無關；(C) 是第三段湯瑪斯‧楊對表音符號的錯誤看法，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Thomas Young?", "options": ["He was the officer who found the stone in 1799.", "He disagreed that the cartouches contained royal names.", "He was on the right track but did not fully understand how hieroglyphs worked.", "He could read Coptic better than Champollion could."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說楊 made important progress，證明王名圈內是王室名字並辨認出 Ptolemy 中的幾個符號，但 Yet he still believed that sound-based signs were used only to spell foreign names，可推論他方向正確卻未完全理解這套文字。(A) 發現石碑的是法國士兵；(B) 與「他證明王名圈內是王室名字」相反；(D) 文中並未比較兩人的科普特語能力，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why was reading the names Ramesses and Thutmose so important for Champollion?", "options": ["It proved that sound-based signs were also used to write Egyptian words.", "It showed that each picture stood for an idea rather than a sound.", "It proved that these two kings had originally come from Greece.", "It finally allowed him to read the Greek section of the stone."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：商博良讀出 Ramesses、Thutmose 等埃及國王的名字，names that were clearly not foreign，因而領悟表音符號不只用於外國名字，這套文字是表音與表意的混合。(B) 正是第三段學者的錯誤假設；(C) 文中說這些名字顯然不是外國名字；(D) 希臘文本來就有學者讀得懂，與此無關，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"assumption\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a careful measurement", "a translation into another language", "a piece of carved stone", "a belief that is accepted as true without proof"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段 one person's willingness to question a widely held assumption，呼應第三段許多學者 assumed（認定）象形文字純粹是表意符號；assumption 指「未經證實就被認定為真的想法、假設」。(A) 仔細的測量、(B) 翻譯、(C) 刻字的石頭，都無法放進「質疑一個普遍被接受的……」的語意中，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a141", "title": "Messages in Knots: The Mystery of the Inca Khipu", "title_zh": "繩結裡的訊息：印加奇普之謎", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["History", "Culture"], "passage": "At its height in the early 1500s, the Inca Empire stretched for more than 4,000 kilometers along the Andes of South America, from what is now Ecuador to Chile. Running an empire of that size required careful records: how many people lived in each village, how much corn was kept in each storehouse, and how many days of work each family owed the state. Yet the Incas had no alphabet and no books. They kept their records in knots.\n\nThese records were called khipu, a word meaning \"knot\" in Quechua, the language of the Incas. A khipu looks like a mop: a main cord, usually made of cotton or llama and alpaca wool, with dozens or even hundreds of thinner cords hanging from it. By tying knots at different heights along each cord, record keepers could store numbers. Knots near the bottom counted ones, those higher up counted tens, then hundreds, and so on, while an empty space stood for zero. In other words, the Incas counted in tens, just as we do today.\n\nMaking and reading khipu was a skilled profession. Trained officials called khipukamayuq kept the accounts of their communities, and relay runners carried khipu along the empire's roads to the capital, Cusco. Spanish writers who arrived after the conquest in the 1530s were astonished to hear these officials \"read\" not only numbers but also histories and laws from their cords. At first, Spanish courts even accepted khipu as evidence. Later, however, church leaders decided that khipu were connected with traditional religion, and in 1583 a church council ordered many of them to be destroyed.\n\nAbout a thousand khipu survive today, but most cannot be fully understood. Researchers have decoded the numbers but still argue about whether some khipu also recorded words. Color seems to have mattered, as did the direction in which knots were tied. In 2017, a researcher examined two khipu kept for centuries by a mountain village in Peru. Local people described them as letters sent during a war, and the cords used so many combinations of color and animal fiber that they may have represented sounds or names rather than just numbers.\n\nThe khipu challenges a common belief that a great civilization must have writing in the form of marks on paper, clay or stone. The Incas built roads, bridges and stone cities that still stand, and governed millions of people without a script like ours. Perhaps the question is not whether they could write, but whether our definition of writing is too narrow. If scholars ever crack the code, the cords may finally let the Incas tell their own story, instead of leaving it to the Spanish who conquered them.", "passage_zh": "十六世紀初的全盛時期，印加帝國沿著南美洲的安地斯山脈綿延超過四千公里，範圍從今天的厄瓜多一直延伸到智利。治理如此龐大的帝國需要詳細的紀錄：每個村莊住了多少人、每座倉庫存放了多少玉米，以及每個家庭欠國家多少天的勞役。然而，印加人既沒有字母，也沒有書本。他們是用繩結來保存紀錄的。\n\n這些紀錄稱為「奇普」（khipu），在印加人的語言克丘亞語中意思是「結」。奇普看起來就像拖把：一條主繩通常以棉線或駱馬、羊駝的毛製成，上面垂掛著數十條甚至數百條較細的繩子。記錄者在每條繩子不同的高度打結，就能儲存數字。靠近底端的結代表個位數，往上依序是十位、百位，以此類推，而空著的位置則代表零。換句話說，印加人和我們今天一樣是以十進位計數。\n\n製作和解讀奇普是一門專業技能。受過訓練、稱為「奇普卡馬尤克」的官員負責管理各自社群的帳目，接力傳遞的信差則沿著帝國的道路把奇普送往首都庫斯科。1530 年代西班牙征服印加之後來到當地的西班牙作家，驚訝地聽到這些官員不僅能從繩子上「讀」出數字，還能讀出歷史與法律。起初，西班牙的法庭甚至接受奇普作為證據。然而後來，教會領袖認定奇普與傳統宗教有關，1583 年一場宗教會議下令銷毀其中許多奇普。\n\n如今約有一千個奇普留存下來，但大多數仍無法被完全解讀。研究人員已經破解了其中的數字，卻仍在爭論有些奇普是否也記錄了語言文字。顏色似乎具有意義，打結的方向也是。2017 年，一位研究人員檢視了祕魯一座山村保存了好幾個世紀的兩個奇普。當地人說它們是戰爭期間送出的信件，而這些繩子所用的顏色與動物纖維組合多得驚人，代表的可能是讀音或名字，而不只是數字。\n\n奇普挑戰了一個普遍的看法：偉大的文明必須擁有寫在紙張、泥板或石頭上的文字。印加人建造了至今仍屹立不搖的道路、橋梁和石造城市，也在沒有我們這類文字的情況下管理了數百萬人。或許問題不在於他們會不會書寫，而在於我們對「文字」的定義是否太狹隘。如果學者有一天能破解這套密碼，這些繩子或許終於能讓印加人說出自己的故事，而不是由征服他們的西班牙人代為述說。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To describe how the Spanish conquered the Inca Empire.", "To compare the Inca number system with modern mathematics.", "To explain how the Incas kept records with knotted cords and why these cords remain a mystery.", "To argue that every great civilization needs an alphabet."], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹奇普的用途與記數方式（第一、二段）、記錄者與遭銷毀的歷史（第三段），以及至今仍無法完全解讀的謎團（第四、五段）。(A) 西班牙征服只是背景；(B) 第二段只用一句話提到與今日相同的十進位；(D) 與第五段「偉大文明未必需要我們這類文字」的觀點相反，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how did a khipu show the number zero?", "options": ["With a knot tied near the bottom of a cord.", "By leaving an empty space where a knot would be.", "With a cord of a special color.", "By cutting one cord shorter than the others."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：Knots near the bottom counted ones... while an empty space stood for zero，該位置不打結、留下空位即代表零。(A) 靠近底端的結代表個位數；(C) 顏色在第四段被提到，與零無關；(D) 文中未提及，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"astonished\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["amazed", "worried", "bored", "annoyed"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：西班牙作家 were astonished to hear these officials \"read\" not only numbers but also histories and laws，聽到繩子竟能「讀」出歷史和法律而感到吃驚，astonished 意為「非常驚訝的」，與 amazed 最接近。(B) 擔心的、(C) 無聊的、(D) 惱怒的都不符合上下文，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the 2017 study described in paragraph 4 suggest?", "options": ["All khipu were used only to record numbers.", "The mountain village had copied its khipu from Spanish letters.", "Researchers can now read every khipu that survives.", "Some khipu may have recorded language, not just numbers."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：兩個奇普的顏色與動物纖維組合多得驚人，they may have represented sounds or names rather than just numbers，暗示有些奇普可能記錄了語言。(A) 與研究結果相反；(B) 文中未提及抄襲西班牙信件；(C) 與 most cannot be fully understood 矛盾，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which statement would the author most likely agree with?", "options": ["The Incas were less advanced because they had no script.", "The Spanish recorded Inca history more accurately than the Incas did.", "Our idea of what counts as writing may be too limited.", "Khipu should no longer be studied because they cannot be decoded."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：Perhaps the question is not whether they could write, but whether our definition of writing is too narrow，作者認為我們對文字的定義可能太狹隘。(A) 作者強調印加人在沒有文字的情況下仍建立偉大文明；(B) 作者希望由印加人「說出自己的故事」，而非由西班牙人代言；(D) 作者期待學者有一天能破解密碼，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a142", "title": "Golden Scars: The Japanese Art of Kintsugi", "title_zh": "金色的傷痕：日本的金繼工藝", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Arts", "Culture"], "passage": "When a favorite bowl breaks, most people either throw it away or glue it back together and hope that no one notices the cracks. In Japan, however, a centuries-old tradition does the opposite. In kintsugi, which means \"golden joinery,\" broken pottery is repaired with a natural lacquer and then decorated with powdered gold. Instead of hiding the damage, the repair turns every crack into a shining line, so that the history of the object becomes part of its beauty.\n\nNo one knows exactly when kintsugi began, but a popular story links it to the fifteenth century. According to the legend, Ashikaga Yoshimasa, a shogun, or military ruler, who loved the tea ceremony, sent a damaged Chinese tea bowl back to China to be fixed. When it returned, it was held together with ugly metal staples. Disappointed, he asked Japanese craftsmen to find a more elegant solution. Whether or not the story is true, kintsugi developed alongside the tea ceremony, in which guests were expected to examine and admire each bowl closely.\n\nThe process requires great patience. The main material is urushi, the sap of a tree that grows in East Asia. Craftsmen use it as glue to join the pieces, to fill in missing areas and to build up smooth layers, and each layer must harden in a warm, humid cabinet before the next one can be added. Only at the very end is fine gold powder applied to the lacquer lines. Because raw urushi can cause painful skin rashes, the work must be done with great care, and a single repair may take several weeks or even months.\n\nKintsugi reflects ideas that run deep in Japanese culture. One is wabi-sabi, the appreciation of beauty in things that are simple, imperfect and marked by time. Another is mottainai, a feeling of regret when something useful is wasted. From this point of view, a cracked bowl is not ruined; it has simply entered a new stage of its life. Some collectors even value a well-repaired piece more highly than an undamaged one, because its golden lines make it unique.\n\nIn recent years, kintsugi has become popular far beyond Japan, often as a symbol of how people can recover from failure, illness or loss. Online videos teach beginners to try it at home, and cheap kits replace real lacquer with quick-drying glue and gold-colored paint. Traditional craftsmen point out that such shortcuts miss the point, and that some of these materials are not safe for dishes that will hold food. Real kintsugi, they say, teaches the same lesson as recovery itself: healing cannot be rushed, and the marks it leaves are nothing to be ashamed of.", "passage_zh": "當心愛的碗破了，大多數人不是把它丟掉，就是用膠水黏回去，然後希望沒有人注意到那些裂痕。然而在日本，有一項流傳了好幾個世紀的傳統卻反其道而行。在意為「金色接合」的「金繼」工藝中，破損的陶瓷器會先用天然漆修補，再以金粉裝飾。修補不是為了掩飾損傷，而是讓每一道裂痕都變成閃閃發亮的線條，使器物的歷史成為它美麗的一部分。\n\n沒有人確切知道金繼始於何時，但一個流傳甚廣的故事將它與十五世紀連結在一起。根據傳說，熱愛茶道的幕府將軍（也就是軍事統治者）足利義政，把一只損壞的中國茶碗送回中國修理。茶碗送回來時，卻是用難看的金屬鋦釘固定的。他大失所望，於是請日本工匠找出更雅緻的修補方法。不論這個故事是否屬實，金繼確實是隨著茶道一同發展起來的；在茶道中，賓客應該仔細端詳、欣賞每一只茶碗。\n\n這個過程需要極大的耐心。主要材料是「漆」，也就是一種生長在東亞的樹木所分泌的汁液。工匠用它當作黏著劑來接合碎片、填補缺損的部分，並一層層塗出平滑的表面；每一層都必須放在溫暖潮濕的櫃子裡乾燥硬化，才能塗上下一層。直到最後，才會在漆線上撒上細緻的金粉。由於生漆可能引起疼痛的皮膚紅疹，整個工作必須格外小心，而一件作品的修補可能要花上好幾週甚至好幾個月。\n\n金繼反映了日本文化中根深柢固的觀念。其一是「侘寂」，也就是欣賞簡樸、不完美、帶有歲月痕跡之物的美。另一個是「勿體無い」（mottainai），指有用的東西被浪費時所產生的惋惜之情。從這個角度來看，有裂痕的碗並沒有毀掉，它只是進入了生命的新階段。有些收藏家甚至認為修補得好的器物比完好無缺的更有價值，因為那些金色線條讓它獨一無二。\n\n近年來，金繼在日本以外的地方也大受歡迎，常被用來象徵人們如何從失敗、疾病或失去之中重新站起來。網路上的影片教初學者在家自己動手做，便宜的材料包則用快乾膠和金色顏料取代真正的漆。傳統工匠指出，這類捷徑忽略了金繼的真義，而且其中有些材料用在盛裝食物的器皿上並不安全。他們說，真正的金繼和復原本身教給我們的道理一樣：療癒急不得，而它留下的痕跡也沒有什麼好羞愧的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to choose a good tea bowl for the tea ceremony.", "A Japanese way of repairing pottery and the values behind it.", "Why Chinese pottery was more valuable than Japanese pottery.", "How to make a kintsugi repair quickly at home."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文介紹金繼是什麼（第一段）、起源傳說（第二段）、製作過程（第三段）、背後的文化觀念（第四段）及現今的流行與爭議（第五段）。(A) 茶道只是背景；(C) 文中未比較中日陶瓷的價值；(D) 第五段提到傳統工匠反對在家速成的做法，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the legend, why did Ashikaga Yoshimasa ask Japanese craftsmen for help?", "options": ["The repair done in China looked ugly to him.", "Chinese craftsmen refused to fix his bowl.", "He wanted to sell the bowl to a collector.", "He had broken the bowl during a tea ceremony."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：茶碗修好送回時 was held together with ugly metal staples，他 Disappointed，於是請日本工匠 find a more elegant solution。(B) 中國方面其實修了，只是不好看；(C) 文中未提及要賣碗；(D) 文中沒有說碗是在茶會中打破的，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does a traditional kintsugi repair take so long?", "options": ["The gold powder has to be imported from abroad.", "Craftsmen are only allowed to work on it during the tea ceremony.", "Each layer of lacquer has to harden before the next one is added.", "The broken pieces must be soaked in water for several months."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：each layer must harden in a warm, humid cabinet before the next one can be added，因此 a single repair may take several weeks or even months。(A)(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"elegant\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["cheap", "strong", "quick", "graceful"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：足利義政不滿意 ugly metal staples（難看的金屬鋦釘），要求工匠找出 a more elegant solution，elegant 與 ugly 相對，意為「優雅的、雅緻的」，與 graceful 最接近。(A) 便宜的、(B) 堅固的、(C) 快速的都不是與「難看」相對的概念，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Traditional craftsmen would most likely agree that ______.", "options": ["kits with quick-drying glue are a smart way to save time", "the slow process is an important part of what kintsugi means", "kintsugi should only be practiced by people in Japan", "bowls repaired with gold should never be used again"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：傳統工匠認為速成材料包 miss the point，並說真正的金繼教人 healing cannot be rushed（療癒急不得），可見緩慢的過程正是金繼的意義所在。(A) 與工匠的看法相反；(C) 文中未主張只能在日本進行；(D) 工匠擔心的是速成材料不適合盛裝食物，並非修補過的碗不能使用，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a143", "title": "Marigolds and Candlelight: Mexico's Day of the Dead", "title_zh": "萬壽菊與燭光：墨西哥的亡靈節", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Culture", "History"], "passage": "In early November, cemeteries across Mexico glow with candlelight. Families clean the graves of their relatives, cover them with orange flowers and sit beside them late into the night, eating, talking and sometimes playing music. To a visitor this may seem strange, even disrespectful, but for the people taking part it is one of the most meaningful events of the year. During Día de Muertos, or the Day of the Dead, the souls of the dead are believed to return to visit the living, and they deserve a warm welcome.\n\nThe festival is a blend of two traditions. Long before the Spanish arrived in the sixteenth century, Indigenous peoples such as the Aztecs held ceremonies to honor their ancestors. Spanish missionaries brought the Catholic holidays of All Saints' Day and All Souls' Day, on November 1 and 2. Over time, the two sets of beliefs mixed, and the celebration settled on these dates. In many places, November 1 is set aside for children who have died, and November 2 for adults.\n\nAt the center of the celebration is the ofrenda, an altar built at home. Families decorate it with photographs, candles, special bread, sugar skulls and the favorite food and drinks of the people they are remembering, perhaps a bowl of spicy soup or a bottle of soda. The bright orange marigold plays a special role: its strong smell and color are said to guide spirits back to their families. Paths of petals sometimes lead from the street to the altar.\n\nOne of the festival's best-known images is La Catrina, an elegant skeleton wearing a large hat decorated with flowers. She was first drawn in the early 1900s by the printmaker José Guadalupe Posada, who used her to make fun of Mexicans who copied European fashions and forgot their roots. Decades later, the painter Diego Rivera placed her in a famous mural, and she became a reminder that death comes to everyone, rich or poor. In 2008, UNESCO added the festival to its list of the world's intangible cultural heritage.\n\nThe tradition, however, continues to change. In 2015, the James Bond film Spectre opened with a huge Day of the Dead parade through Mexico City. No such parade existed, but after the film's success, the city created a real one in 2016, and it now attracts enormous crowds every year. Some people worry that tourism and movies are turning a family ritual into a show. Others answer that the festival has always absorbed new influences, and that what matters most remains unchanged: remembering the dead with love rather than fear.", "passage_zh": "每到十一月初，墨西哥各地的墓園便籠罩在燭光之中。家家戶戶清掃親人的墳墓，在墓上鋪滿橘色的花朵，並在墓旁一直坐到深夜，一邊吃東西、聊天，有時還會演奏音樂。對外地遊客來說，這或許顯得奇怪，甚至有些不敬，但對參與其中的人而言，這是一年之中最有意義的活動之一。在「亡靈節」期間，人們相信亡者的靈魂會回來探望在世的親人，因此應該熱情地迎接他們。\n\n這個節日融合了兩種傳統。早在十六世紀西班牙人到來之前，阿茲特克人等原住民族就會舉行儀式來紀念祖先。西班牙傳教士則帶來了天主教在 11 月 1 日和 2 日的諸聖節與萬靈節。隨著時間推移，兩套信仰逐漸交融，慶祝活動也就固定在這兩天。在許多地方，11 月 1 日是留給過世孩童的日子，11 月 2 日則用來紀念成年人。\n\n慶祝活動的核心是「祭壇」，也就是在家中搭起的供桌。家人會在上面擺放照片、蠟燭、特製的麵包、糖做的骷髏頭，以及被紀念者生前最愛的食物和飲料──也許是一碗辣湯，或是一瓶汽水。鮮豔的橘色萬壽菊扮演特別的角色：據說它濃烈的氣味和顏色能引導亡靈回到家人身邊。有時人們還會用花瓣鋪出一條小路，從街上一路通往祭壇。\n\n這個節日最廣為人知的形象之一是「卡特里娜」──一具戴著飾有花朵的大帽子、打扮優雅的骷髏。她最早是由版畫家何塞‧瓜達盧佩‧波薩達在 1900 年代初期所畫，用來嘲諷那些模仿歐洲時尚、忘了自身根源的墨西哥人。數十年後，畫家迪亞哥‧里維拉把她畫進一幅著名的壁畫中，她也成了一種提醒：死亡會降臨在每個人身上，不分貧富。2008 年，聯合國教科文組織將這個節日列入世界無形文化遺產名錄。\n\n然而，這項傳統仍在持續改變。2015 年，詹姆士‧龐德電影《007：惡魔四伏》以一場穿越墨西哥城的盛大亡靈節遊行作為開場。當時現實中並沒有這樣的遊行，但電影大受歡迎之後，墨西哥城在 2016 年真的辦起了遊行，如今每年都吸引大批人潮。有些人擔心觀光和電影正把家族儀式變成一場表演；也有人回應說，這個節日向來都會吸收新的影響，而最重要的核心始終沒變：以愛而不是恐懼來懷念逝者。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following best describes the passage?", "options": ["A guide to visiting the cemeteries of Mexico City.", "A history of the James Bond films made in Mexico.", "An introduction to a Mexican festival, its origins and how it is changing.", "A comparison between Halloween and the Day of the Dead."], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹亡靈節的活動（第一段）、由來（第二段）、祭壇與萬壽菊（第三段）、卡特里娜的形象（第四段），以及這項傳統的新變化（第五段）。(A) 文中並未提供參觀墓園的指南；(B) 龐德電影只是第五段的一個例子；(D) 文中沒有提到萬聖節，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, where do the dates of the festival come from?", "options": ["Catholic holidays brought to Mexico by Spanish missionaries.", "An Aztec calendar used to plan the corn harvest.", "The birthday of the printmaker José Guadalupe Posada.", "A decision made by UNESCO in 2008."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Spanish missionaries brought the Catholic holidays of All Saints' Day and All Souls' Day, on November 1 and 2... the celebration settled on these dates。(B) 阿茲特克人雖會紀念祖先，但文中未提及收成曆法；(C) 波薩達只與卡特里娜有關；(D) 2008 年是列入無形文化遺產的時間，與日期由來無關，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why are marigolds used in the celebration?", "options": ["They are the cheapest flowers in Mexico in November.", "They keep insects away from the food on the altar.", "They were the favorite flower of the painter Diego Rivera.", "They are believed to help the spirits find their way home."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：its strong smell and color are said to guide spirits back to their families，人們相信萬壽菊的氣味與顏色能引導亡靈回家。(A)(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the story of the Mexico City parade?", "options": ["The parade has been held every year since the time of the Aztecs.", "Popular culture can help create a new part of an old tradition.", "The government stopped the parade because of the large crowds.", "The parade in Spectre was filmed during the first real parade in 2016."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：電影《007：惡魔四伏》中的遊行原本 No such parade existed，電影成功後墨西哥城才在 2016 年舉辦真正的遊行，可見電影等流行文化促成了傳統中的新元素。(A) 遊行是 2016 年才開始的；(C) 文中說遊行每年都吸引大批人潮，並未停辦；(D) 電影在 2015 年就已上映，早於第一次真正的遊行，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"absorbed\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["taken in and made part of itself", "completely rejected", "carefully hidden", "quickly forgotten"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：Others answer that the festival has always absorbed new influences，對照第二段亡靈節本身就是原住民與天主教傳統交融而成，absorbed 指「吸收、融入」新的影響。(B) 完全拒絕、(C) 小心隱藏、(D) 很快忘記，都與「傳統持續吸納新元素」的論點不符，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a144", "title": "More Than a War Dance: The Story of the Haka", "title_zh": "不只是戰舞：哈卡舞的故事", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Culture", "Sports"], "passage": "Before every major match, New Zealand's national rugby team, the All Blacks, lines up to face its opponents and performs a haka. The players stamp their feet, slap their chests and thighs, open their eyes wide and shout in the Māori language. For many people, this is the only haka they have ever seen, and they often describe it simply as a \"war dance.\" That label, however, captures only a small part of what the haka means to the Māori, the Indigenous people of New Zealand.\n\nIn traditional Māori society, haka were indeed performed before battle, both to frighten enemies and to prepare warriors' minds and bodies. But they were also used on many peaceful occasions. Communities performed haka to welcome important guests, to celebrate achievements and to express grief at funerals. Each tribe had its own haka, with words describing its history, its ancestors or the challenges it had faced. A haka is therefore less a dance than a poem performed with the whole body, and the face is as important as the feet.\n\nThe haka most often performed by the All Blacks, \"Ka Mate,\" has a dramatic history. It was composed around 1820 by Te Rauparaha, a chief of the Ngāti Toa tribe, after escaping from enemies who were hunting him. According to tradition, he hid in a pit used for storing sweet potatoes. Its famous opening lines, \"Ka mate! Ka mate! Ka ora! Ka ora!\", mean \"It is death! It is death! It is life! It is life!\" and express his relief at surviving. In other words, the world's most famous haka is not about attacking others but about the joy of staying alive.\n\nAs the haka became famous, questions arose about who owns it. Companies in other countries used it in advertisements without asking, and many Māori felt that a sacred part of their culture was being treated as a marketing tool. After years of discussion, a law passed in 2014 officially recognized Te Rauparaha as the composer of \"Ka Mate.\" The law does not require anyone to pay to perform the haka, but it does require that its origin be acknowledged when it is used for commercial purposes.\n\nToday, haka are more visible than ever in New Zealand. Students perform them at school events and graduations, families perform them at weddings, and communities perform them to honor respected teachers and leaders. Many non-Māori New Zealanders learn haka at school, and women perform them too; the national women's rugby team has its own. For the Māori, each performance is a reminder that the haka is not entertainment for tourists but a living way of expressing identity, respect and strong emotion.", "passage_zh": "每逢重要比賽開打前，紐西蘭國家橄欖球隊「黑衫軍」都會排成一列面對對手，表演一段哈卡舞。球員們跺腳、拍打胸膛和大腿、瞪大雙眼，並用毛利語高聲呼喊。對許多人來說，這是他們唯一看過的哈卡舞，而他們常常只把它形容為「戰舞」。然而，這個標籤只說出了哈卡舞對紐西蘭原住民毛利人所代表意義的一小部分。\n\n在傳統的毛利社會中，哈卡舞確實會在作戰前表演，既是為了震懾敵人，也是為了讓戰士做好身心準備。但哈卡舞也用於許多和平的場合。族人會表演哈卡舞來歡迎貴客、慶祝成就，或在喪禮上表達哀悼。每個部落都有自己的哈卡舞，歌詞描述該部落的歷史、祖先或曾經面對的挑戰。因此，與其說哈卡是一種舞蹈，不如說它是一首用全身演出的詩，而臉部表情和雙腳一樣重要。\n\n黑衫軍最常表演的哈卡舞〈Ka Mate〉有一段戲劇性的歷史。它是恩加蒂托亞部落的酋長特勞帕拉哈在 1820 年左右所創作的，當時他剛從追殺他的敵人手中逃脫。根據傳說，他曾躲在一個用來儲藏番薯的坑洞裡。這首哈卡舞著名的開頭「Ka mate! Ka mate! Ka ora! Ka ora!」意思是「是死亡！是死亡！是生命！是生命！」，表達他死裡逃生、如釋重負的心情。換句話說，世界上最有名的哈卡舞並不是關於攻擊別人，而是關於活下來的喜悅。\n\n隨著哈卡舞聲名大噪，「誰擁有它」的問題也隨之浮現。其他國家的公司未經同意就把它用在廣告中，許多毛利人覺得自己文化中神聖的一部分被當成了行銷工具。經過多年討論，紐西蘭在 2014 年通過一項法律，正式承認特勞帕拉哈是〈Ka Mate〉的作者。這項法律並不要求任何人付費才能表演這段哈卡舞，但規定在商業用途中使用時，必須註明它的出處。\n\n如今，哈卡舞在紐西蘭比以往任何時候都更常見。學生在學校活動和畢業典禮上表演，家人在婚禮上表演，社區也會表演哈卡舞來向受人敬重的老師和領袖致敬。許多非毛利裔的紐西蘭人也在學校學習哈卡舞，女性同樣會表演；紐西蘭國家女子橄欖球隊就有自己的哈卡舞。對毛利人而言，每一次表演都在提醒世人：哈卡舞不是給觀光客看的娛樂，而是一種表達身分認同、敬意與強烈情感的活生生的方式。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the author's main purpose in writing the passage?", "options": ["To teach readers how to perform the haka correctly.", "To explain the rules of rugby in New Zealand.", "To argue that the All Blacks should stop performing the haka.", "To show that the haka has a much wider meaning than a war dance."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段末指出 That label... captures only a small part of what the haka means to the Māori，接著各段說明哈卡舞用於和平場合、〈Ka Mate〉的真正含義、版權爭議與今日的多元用途。(A) 文中沒有教學步驟；(B) 橄欖球只是開頭的引子；(C) 作者並未主張黑衫軍停止表演，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an occasion for performing a haka?", "options": ["Welcoming important guests.", "Celebrating a good sweet potato harvest.", "Expressing sorrow at a funeral.", "Celebrating at a wedding."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段提到 to welcome important guests (A)、to express grief at funerals (C)；第五段提到 families perform them at weddings (D)。番薯只出現在第三段，是特勞帕拉哈躲藏的儲藏坑，文中並未提到慶祝番薯豐收，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What do the opening lines of \"Ka Mate\" express?", "options": ["Te Rauparaha's relief at escaping death.", "A warning to enemies that they are about to be attacked.", "Sadness about the death of a respected chief.", "Thanks to the farmers who stored sweet potatoes."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：開頭幾句意為「是死亡！是死亡！是生命！是生命！」，and express his relief at surviving，並說這首哈卡 is not about attacking others but about the joy of staying alive。(B) 與「並非關於攻擊別人」相反；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, what does the 2014 law require?", "options": ["Anyone who performs \"Ka Mate\" must pay the Ngāti Toa tribe.", "Only Māori people may perform the haka in public.", "The origin of \"Ka Mate\" must be acknowledged when it is used commercially.", "Foreign companies may no longer show the haka in films."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：The law does not require anyone to pay to perform the haka, but it does require that its origin be acknowledged when it is used for commercial purposes。(A) 法律明確不要求付費；(B) 第五段提到許多非毛利人也會表演；(D) 法律只要求註明出處，並未禁止使用，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 1, the word \"label\" refers to ______.", "options": ["the name of New Zealand's national rugby team", "the Māori language used in the performance", "the shouting and stamping of the players", "the description of the haka as a \"war dance\""], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：they often describe it simply as a \"war dance.\" That label, however, captures only a small part...，That label 指的就是前一句把哈卡舞稱為「戰舞」的說法。(A)(B)(C) 都不是被用來「形容」哈卡舞的稱呼，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a145", "title": "Rethinking Easter Island: Collapse or Survival?", "title_zh": "重新認識復活節島：崩潰還是倖存？", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["History", "Environment"], "passage": "Easter Island, known to its people as Rapa Nui, is one of the most isolated inhabited places on Earth, lying about 3,500 kilometers off the coast of Chile. It is famous for its moai, nearly a thousand huge stone figures carved by the island's Polynesian settlers. For decades, however, the island has also been famous for a darker reason. It has often been told as a warning story about how a society can destroy itself.\n\nThe story goes like this. After settling the island around 800 years ago, the islanders became obsessed with building ever larger statues. To move them, they cut down the island's forests, which once held millions of palm trees. Without trees, the soil washed away, crops failed, and the population, perhaps 15,000 people at its peak, fell into war and starvation long before Europeans arrived in 1722. The lesson seemed clear: if we waste our planet's resources, we will share Easter Island's fate.\n\nIn recent years, however, many researchers have questioned this account. The forests did disappear, but rats, which arrived with the first settlers, probably played a large role by eating palm seeds and preventing new trees from growing. Moving the statues may not have required many trees either. Islanders have long said that the moai \"walked\" to their places. In 2012, archaeologists tested this idea with a concrete copy weighing over four tonnes. Using only ropes, a team of eighteen people rocked the upright statue from side to side, \"walking\" it about a hundred meters in under an hour.\n\nNew evidence also suggests that the population never reached the large numbers once imagined. In 2024, one study used satellite images to map the island's \"rock gardens,\" fields where farmers placed broken stones on the soil to keep it moist and add nutrients. The results suggested that, even with fish and other seafood, the island could have supported only about 4,000 people, not 15,000. Another study that year, based on ancient DNA, found no sign of a sudden population crash before Europeans arrived. Rather than destroying their environment, the islanders appear to have adapted cleverly to a difficult one.\n\nThe real disaster came later. European ships brought diseases, and in the 1860s slave traders from Peru kidnapped around 1,500 islanders to work on the mainland. By 1877, only 111 Rapa Nui people remained on the island. Seen in this light, Easter Island still teaches a lesson, but a different one. It is the story not of a foolish people who ruined their home, but of a resilient society that survived for centuries until outsiders nearly destroyed it. It also reminds us to check the evidence before accepting a story simply because its moral seems useful.", "passage_zh": "復活節島在當地人口中稱為「拉帕努伊」，是地球上最與世隔絕的有人居住地之一，距離智利海岸約 3,500 公里。島上以摩艾石像聞名──那是將近一千尊由島上的玻里尼西亞移民所雕刻的巨大石像。然而數十年來，這座島也因為一個較為黑暗的原因而出名：它常被當成一則警世故事，用來說明一個社會如何自我毀滅。\n\n故事是這樣的：大約八百年前在島上定居之後，島民開始執迷於建造越來越大的石像。為了搬運石像，他們砍光了島上曾經長著數百萬棵棕櫚樹的森林。沒有了樹木，土壤被沖刷流失，農作物歉收，巔峰時期或許多達一萬五千人的人口，早在 1722 年歐洲人到來之前，就已陷入戰爭與饑荒。這個教訓似乎再清楚不過：如果我們浪費地球的資源，就會落得和復活節島一樣的下場。\n\n然而近年來，許多研究人員對這個說法提出了質疑。森林確實消失了，但隨著第一批移民來到島上的老鼠可能扮演了重要角色：牠們吃掉棕櫚樹的種子，讓新的樹長不出來。搬運石像或許也不需要用到很多樹木。島民一直以來都說，摩艾是自己「走」到定位的。2012 年，考古學家用一尊重達四公噸以上的混凝土複製品測試這個說法。一支十八人的團隊只靠繩索，讓直立的石像左右搖擺，在不到一小時內就讓它「走」了大約一百公尺。\n\n新的證據也顯示，島上人口從未達到過去想像的那麼多。2024 年，一項研究利用衛星影像繪製島上「岩石園」的分布圖──這是農民把碎石鋪在土壤上，以保持濕潤並增加養分的田地。結果顯示，即使加上魚類和其他海產，這座島最多也只能養活大約四千人，而不是一萬五千人。同年另一項根據古代 DNA 所做的研究，也沒有發現歐洲人到來之前人口突然崩潰的跡象。島民似乎並沒有破壞自己的環境，反而巧妙地適應了一個艱困的環境。\n\n真正的災難發生在後來。歐洲船隻帶來了疾病，而在 1860 年代，來自祕魯的奴隸販子綁走了大約 1,500 名島民，送到南美大陸做苦工。到了 1877 年，島上只剩下 111 名拉帕努伊人。從這個角度來看，復活節島依然能給我們教訓，只是內容不同了。這不是一群愚昧的人毀掉自己家園的故事，而是一個堅韌的社會存續了好幾個世紀，直到外來者幾乎將它摧毀的故事。它也提醒我們：接受一個故事之前應該先檢視證據，不能只因為它的寓意看起來很有用就信以為真。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["New evidence challenges the popular story that the islanders destroyed their own society.", "The moai were carved in order to frighten European visitors.", "Easter Island's forests were cut down by European settlers.", "Rock gardens were the main cause of the island's environmental problems."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段先介紹「島民濫伐導致社會崩潰」的流行說法，第三至五段再以老鼠、石像「行走」實驗、岩石園與古代 DNA 研究及歐洲人帶來的災難提出反證。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 森林消失與歐洲移民無關；(D) 岩石園是島民適應環境的方法，而非問題的原因，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why did the palm forests fail to grow back?", "options": ["The statues were dragged over the young trees.", "The islanders burned the forests to make farmland.", "Rats ate the palm seeds.", "Slave traders shipped all the wood to Peru."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：rats... probably played a large role by eating palm seeds and preventing new trees from growing。(A) 文中說石像可能是用繩索讓它「走」的，並非拖過樹木；(B) 文中未提及燒林；(D) 奴隸販子綁走的是人，與木材無關，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the 2024 study of rock gardens, about how many people could the island have supported?", "options": ["About 1,500.", "About 4,000.", "About 15,000.", "Only 111."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：The results suggested that, even with fish and other seafood, the island could have supported only about 4,000 people, not 15,000，即使加上海產，島上最多也只能養活約四千人。(A) 1,500 是第五段被綁走的島民人數；(C) 15,000 是舊說法估計的巔峰人口，正是這項研究所否定的；(D) 111 是 1877 年島上剩下的人數，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"resilient\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["careless", "wealthy", "isolated", "able to recover from difficulties"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：a resilient society that survived for centuries，對照第四段島民 adapted cleverly to a difficult one（巧妙適應艱困環境），resilient 指「堅韌、能從困境中恢復的」。(A) 粗心的與文意相反；(B) 富有的文中未提及；(C) 孤立的雖符合地理位置，但不能說明「存續數百年」的原因，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What does the last sentence of the passage suggest?", "options": ["Stories that teach a moral are never true.", "Scientists should stop studying Easter Island.", "A story should not be believed just because its lesson seems useful.", "Environmental problems are not worth worrying about."], "answer": 2, "explain": "末句：It also reminds us to check the evidence before accepting a story simply because its moral seems useful，提醒我們不應只因寓意有用就相信一個故事。(A) 說法過於絕對，作者只要求先檢視證據；(B) 文中正是靠持續研究才推翻舊說；(D) 作者並未否定環境問題的重要性，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a146", "title": "Colors That Vanish in Minutes: Why the Terracotta Army Waits Underground", "title_zh": "幾分鐘就消失的色彩：兵馬俑為何仍在地下等待", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["History", "Science"], "passage": "In March 1974, farmers digging a well near Xi'an in northwestern China struck pieces of baked clay. They had stumbled upon one of the greatest archaeological finds of the twentieth century: an underground army created more than 2,200 years ago to guard the tomb of Qin Shi Huang, the first emperor to unite China. The pits around the tomb are thought to contain more than 8,000 life-size soldiers, along with horses, chariots and real bronze weapons. Yet half a century later, most of the army is still buried. Why are archaeologists in no hurry to dig it all up?\n\nPart of the answer is color. Today the soldiers appear a dull gray-brown, but when they were made, they were painted in bright reds, greens, blues and purples. The paint was applied over a layer of lacquer, a natural varnish, which had soaked up moisture during two thousand years underground. Once exposed to dry air, the lacquer can begin to curl within seconds and flake off within minutes, taking the paint with it. Many of the figures uncovered in the 1970s lost their colors almost as soon as they saw daylight.\n\nSince the 1990s, Chinese scientists, working with experts from Germany, have developed ways to protect the paint, such as treating newly exposed surfaces with special solutions that stop them from drying too quickly. These methods have allowed some figures to keep traces of their original colors. But each piece must be treated slowly and by hand, so excavation now moves at a careful pace, often only a few square meters at a time.\n\nAn even bigger mystery lies beneath a large earth mound nearby: the emperor's own tomb, which has never been opened. The historian Sima Qian, writing about a century after the emperor's death, described a burial chamber filled with treasures, with rivers of mercury flowing through a model of the empire and crossbows set to shoot anyone who broke in. For a long time, this sounded like legend. However, scientific tests of the soil on the mound have found unusually high levels of mercury, suggesting that his description may contain some truth.\n\nChinese authorities have decided to leave the tomb closed until they are confident they can protect whatever is inside. This patience reflects a lesson archaeologists everywhere have learned: excavation destroys as well as reveals. Once an object is removed from the ground, information about its position and surroundings is lost forever, and materials that survived for centuries may decay in days. By waiting, archaeologists give future generations with better tools the chance to see what cannot yet be safely uncovered. Sometimes the best way to protect the past is to leave it where it is.", "passage_zh": "1974 年 3 月，中國西北部西安附近的農民在挖井時，挖到了一些燒製過的陶片。他們無意間碰上了二十世紀最偉大的考古發現之一：一支在兩千兩百多年前打造、用來守護秦始皇陵墓的地下大軍，而秦始皇正是第一位統一中國的皇帝。陵墓周圍的坑洞據信埋藏了八千多尊真人大小的士兵俑，以及陶馬、戰車和真正的青銅兵器。然而半個世紀過去了，這支軍隊大部分仍埋在地下。為什麼考古學家一點也不急著把它們全部挖出來？\n\n部分原因在於色彩。今天的兵馬俑呈現暗沉的灰褐色，但剛製成時，它們被漆上了鮮豔的紅、綠、藍、紫等顏色。顏料塗在一層生漆（一種天然塗料）之上，而這層漆在地底兩千年間吸飽了水分。一旦接觸到乾燥的空氣，漆層可能在幾秒內就開始捲曲，幾分鐘內便會剝落，連帶把顏料一起帶走。1970 年代出土的許多陶俑，幾乎一見到天日就失去了色彩。\n\n自 1990 年代起，中國科學家與德國專家合作，研發出保護彩繪的方法，例如用特殊溶液處理剛出土的表面，避免它們乾得太快。這些方法讓一些陶俑得以保留原本色彩的痕跡。但每一件都必須以手工慢慢處理，因此現在的挖掘進度十分謹慎緩慢，往往一次只挖幾平方公尺。\n\n更大的謎團就藏在附近一座巨大的土丘底下：秦始皇本人的陵墓至今從未被打開過。史學家司馬遷在秦始皇死後約一百年寫下的記載中，描述墓室裡堆滿珍寶，有以水銀做成的江河在帝國的模型中流動，還設有弩箭，會射殺任何闖入的人。長久以來，這聽起來就像傳說。然而，對封土堆土壤所做的科學檢測發現，其中的汞含量異常地高，顯示他的記載或許有幾分真實。\n\n中國當局已決定，在確定能保護墓中一切之前，陵墓將保持封閉。這份耐心反映了世界各地考古學家都學到的一課：挖掘在揭露的同時也會造成破壞。物品一旦從地下移出，關於它所在位置與周遭環境的資訊就會永遠消失，而保存了好幾個世紀的材質，可能在幾天內就腐朽。透過等待，考古學家讓擁有更好工具的後代子孫，有機會看見目前還無法安全挖掘的東西。有時候，保護過去最好的方法，就是讓它留在原地。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the farmers near Xi'an became rich after 1974.", "Why the first emperor of China needed such a large army.", "How Chinese and German scientists invented a new kind of lacquer.", "Why archaeologists are moving slowly at the Terracotta Army and the emperor's tomb."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段末提出問題 Why are archaeologists in no hurry to dig it all up?，接著第二、三段說明彩繪容易剝落、需慢慢處理，第四、五段說明皇陵為何仍未開啟，以及「挖掘也是破壞」的道理。(A) 文中未提及；(B) 只是背景；(C) 中德科學家研發的是保護彩繪的方法，並非發明生漆，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happens when the painted lacquer is exposed to dry air?", "options": ["It may curl and flake off within minutes.", "It slowly changes from gray-brown to bright red.", "It absorbs moisture and becomes stronger.", "It becomes too hot for workers to touch."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Once exposed to dry air, the lacquer can begin to curl within seconds and flake off within minutes, taking the paint with it。(B) 文中說陶俑原本色彩鮮豔，接觸空氣後顏料隨漆層剝落才成為灰褐色，並非由灰褐變紅；(C) 漆層是在地底吸收水分，接觸乾燥空氣後反而會剝落；(D) 文中未提及，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does excavation at the site now move so slowly?", "options": ["The government has run out of money for the project.", "Farmers are still growing crops above the pits.", "Each piece has to be treated carefully by hand to protect its paint.", "Mercury in the soil makes the pits too dangerous to enter."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：each piece must be treated slowly and by hand, so excavation now moves at a careful pace。(A)(B) 文中未提及；(D) 高含量的汞是在皇陵封土堆中測到的，與兵馬俑坑的挖掘速度無關，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What do the high levels of mercury in the soil suggest?", "options": ["Farmers once used mercury to protect their crops.", "Sima Qian's description of the tomb may be partly accurate.", "Thieves have already broken into the emperor's tomb.", "The clay soldiers were once coated with mercury."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：司馬遷描述墓中有 rivers of mercury，原本 sounded like legend，但土壤檢測發現 unusually high levels of mercury, suggesting that his description may contain some truth。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 文中說皇陵 has never been opened，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"decay\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["rot or break down", "be stolen", "become more valuable", "be discovered"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：materials that survived for centuries may decay in days，對照第二段彩繪一接觸空氣就剝落，decay 指「腐朽、損壞」。(B) 被偷、(C) 變得更有價值、(D) 被發現，都無法說明為何考古學家寧可等待，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a147", "title": "Snapping Celery and Coconut Hooves: The Hidden Art of Foley", "title_zh": "折斷的芹菜與椰子殼馬蹄聲：電影擬音的幕後藝術", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Arts", "Career"], "passage": "The next time you watch an action movie, try closing your eyes during a fight scene. You will hear the thud of every punch, the creak of a leather jacket and the crunch of footsteps on broken glass. Surprisingly, very few of these sounds were recorded during filming. Most were created weeks later by Foley artists, sound performers who watch the film on a large screen in a studio and recreate each small noise in perfect time with the picture.\n\nThe job is named after Jack Foley, who worked at Universal Studios in the 1920s. When talking pictures became a sensation at the end of that decade, studios rushed to add sound to films that had been shot in silence. One such film was the musical Show Boat, released in 1929. Foley and a small team watched the projected film and performed footsteps, clapping and other noises while the sound was recorded. Because everything was captured in one continuous recording, their timing had to be excellent.\n\nWhy not simply record these sounds on set? One reason is that microphones are positioned to capture the actors' voices, so quieter sounds such as rustling paper or a key turning in a lock are often too weak, or lost among the noise of the crew. Another is that many on-screen actions are fake: actors only pretend to hit each other, and a heavy stone door may really be painted foam. Finally, films sold abroad are often dubbed into other languages. When the original dialogue is removed, every sound recorded along with it disappears too, so filmmakers need a separate effects track for every version.\n\nFoley studios look more like junk shops than recording rooms. Their floors contain shallow \"pits\" filled with gravel, sand, wood or concrete, so artists can walk on whatever surface appears on screen, and their shelves are crowded with old shoes, dishes and car parts. Often the object that sounds right is not the object being shown. Snapping a stick of celery makes a convincing breaking bone, two halves of a coconut shell become a galloping horse, and squeezing a leather pouch filled with cornstarch produces the soft crunch of footsteps in snow.\n\nToday, sound designers can choose from digital libraries of millions of recorded effects, yet Foley has not disappeared, because it is really a form of acting. A nervous teenager, an exhausted soldier and a confident businesswoman all walk differently, and a skilled Foley artist can express those differences through the speed and weight of footsteps alone. Ready-made recordings rarely match one actor's movements so closely. Ironically, the better Foley artists do their job, the less audiences notice it, since the sounds simply feel like part of the world on screen.", "passage_zh": "下次你看動作片的時候，試著在打鬥場面中閉上眼睛。你會聽到每一拳打下去的悶響、皮夾克的嘎吱聲，還有踩在碎玻璃上的腳步聲。令人意外的是，這些聲音很少是在拍攝時錄下來的。它們大多是在幾週之後由擬音師製作出來的；擬音師是一種聲音表演者，他們在錄音室裡看著大銀幕上的影片，配合畫面分秒不差地重現每一個細小的聲響。\n\n這份工作是以傑克‧佛利命名的，他在1920年代任職於環球影業。那個年代末期，有聲電影大受歡迎，各家片廠紛紛急著為原本以無聲方式拍攝的電影加上聲音，1929年上映的音樂劇電影《畫舫璇宮》就是其中之一。佛利和一個小團隊一邊觀看放映中的影片，一邊表演腳步聲、拍手聲和其他聲響，同時將聲音錄製下來。由於所有聲音都是在一次連續的錄音中完成，他們必須把時間點掌握得非常精準。\n\n為什麼不乾脆在拍攝現場錄下這些聲音呢？原因之一是，麥克風是為了收錄演員的說話聲而擺放的，因此像翻動紙張或鑰匙轉動門鎖這類較小的聲音往往太微弱，或是淹沒在工作人員的噪音之中。另一個原因是，銀幕上的許多動作都是假的：演員只是假裝互毆，而一扇沉重的石門其實可能只是上了漆的泡棉。最後，賣到國外的電影常常會配上其他語言。當原本的對白被拿掉時，所有跟著對白一起錄下的聲音也會一併消失，因此電影製作者需要一條獨立的音效音軌，供每一個版本使用。\n\n擬音錄音室看起來比較像舊貨店，而不像錄音間。地板上有一個個裝著碎石、沙子、木頭或水泥的淺坑，讓擬音師可以踩在與畫面相同的地面上走路；架子上則堆滿了舊鞋子、碗盤和汽車零件。能發出正確聲音的東西，往往並不是畫面中出現的那樣東西。折斷一根芹菜，聽起來就像骨頭斷裂一樣逼真；兩半椰子殼可以變成奔馳的馬；擠壓一個裝滿玉米澱粉的皮製小袋，則能發出在雪地上行走時那種輕柔的嘎吱聲。\n\n如今，音效設計師可以從收錄數百萬種音效的數位資料庫中挑選聲音，但擬音並沒有消失，因為它其實是一種表演。一個緊張的青少年、一名筋疲力盡的士兵和一位自信的女企業家，走路的方式都不一樣，而技巧純熟的擬音師光靠腳步聲的快慢與輕重，就能表現出這些差異。現成的錄音很少能如此精準地配合某一位演員的動作。諷刺的是，擬音師的工作做得越好，觀眾就越不會注意到它，因為那些聲音感覺起來就像是銀幕世界中本來就有的一部分。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How Jack Foley became a famous Hollywood actor.", "Why digital sound libraries have now completely replaced human sound performers.", "How performers create sound effects for films and why their work still matters.", "Why microphones on film sets cannot record the actors' voices clearly enough."], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹擬音師如何在錄音室為電影重現聲音（第一段）、這份工作的由來（第二段）、為何不在現場收音（第三段）、使用的道具（第四段），以及這份工作至今仍無法被取代的原因（第五段）。(A) 佛利並非演員；(B) 與第五段 Foley has not disappeared 相反；(D) 第三段說麥克風正是為了收錄演員說話聲而擺放，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why do films sold abroad need a separate effects track?", "options": ["Foreign audiences usually prefer louder and more exciting sound effects than local ones.", "Sounds recorded with the original dialogue are lost when the voices are replaced.", "Other countries have different laws about how loud the sound in films can be.", "Foreign actors must perform all of the film's sounds again in their own language."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：When the original dialogue is removed, every sound recorded along with it disappears too, so filmmakers need a separate effects track for every version. 配音時拿掉原本的對白，跟著一起錄下的聲音也會消失。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 換掉的是對白，不是要外國演員重做音效，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"convincing\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["believable", "frightening", "expensive", "unusual"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Snapping a stick of celery makes a convincing breaking bone，折斷芹菜的聲音聽起來就像真的骨頭斷裂，convincing 意為「令人信服的、逼真的」，與 believable 最接近。(B) 可怕的、(C) 昂貴的、(D) 不尋常的都不符合「聽起來像真的」的意思，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention \"a nervous teenager, an exhausted soldier and a confident businesswoman\" in the last paragraph?", "options": ["To show that Foley artists come from many different backgrounds and age groups.", "To explain why most films are about these three kinds of characters.", "To suggest that actors should learn to walk more naturally.", "To show that Foley sounds must fit each character's personality and feelings."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段說擬音其實是一種表演，這三種人走路方式不同，擬音師能 express those differences through the speed and weight of footsteps alone，舉例是為了說明擬音必須配合角色的個性與心情，這正是現成音效做不到的。(A)(B)(C) 都不是作者舉例的目的，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following can be inferred from the passage?", "options": ["Jack Foley invented the coconut-shell sound for horses in Show Boat.", "In a movie fight, the sound of a punch is usually added after filming.", "Most Foley studios are located inside old junk shops near film studios.", "Show Boat was the first film ever made with sound."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說打鬥場面中的拳頭聲等 very few ... were recorded during filming，第三段又說 actors only pretend to hit each other，可推論拳頭聲通常是拍攝後才加上的。(A) 文中未說椰子殼是佛利發明的；(C) 第四段只說錄音室「看起來像」舊貨店；(D) 第二段說《畫舫璇宮》是片廠趕著補上聲音的電影之一，並非第一部有聲電影，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a148", "title": "The Empty Wall: How a Theft Made the Mona Lisa Famous", "title_zh": "空蕩蕩的牆面：一樁竊案如何讓〈蒙娜麗莎〉聲名大噪", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Arts", "History"], "passage": "Every day, thousands of visitors push through the crowds at the Louvre in Paris to catch a glimpse of a small portrait behind thick bulletproof glass. The Mona Lisa, painted by Leonardo da Vinci in the early sixteenth century, is often called the most famous painting in the world. Yet in 1900 it was admired mainly by art experts and was far from a household name. What changed its fate was not a new discovery about its beauty, but a crime.\n\nOn the morning of Monday, August 21, 1911, while the museum was closed to the public, a man in the white smock worn by Louvre workers lifted the painting off the wall. In a quiet staircase, he removed its frame and glass case, hid the wooden panel under his smock and simply walked out. The thief was Vincenzo Peruggia, an Italian handyman who had once worked at the museum and had even helped to fit protective glass over its paintings.\n\nAstonishingly, the theft went unnoticed for more than a day, as guards assumed the painting had been taken away to be photographed. Once the truth came out, the Louvre closed for a week, and newspapers around the world printed the Mona Lisa's face on their front pages. When the museum reopened, crowds lined up to stare at the empty space where it had hung. Even famous artists fell under suspicion: the poet Guillaume Apollinaire was jailed for several days, and his friend, the young painter Pablo Picasso, was questioned by police.\n\nFor more than two years, the painting lay hidden in a trunk in Peruggia's room in Paris. Then, in late 1913, he took it to Florence and offered it to an art dealer, Alfredo Geri. Suspicious, Geri asked the director of the Uffizi Gallery to examine it. Once they confirmed it was genuine, they contacted the police, and Peruggia was arrested at his hotel.\n\nAt his trial, Peruggia claimed to be a patriot who wanted to return an Italian masterpiece to its homeland, since he believed Napoleon had stolen it. In fact, Leonardo had carried the painting to France himself, and it had passed into the collection of the French king. Many Italians nevertheless saw Peruggia as a hero, and although he was sentenced to just over a year in prison, he was released after about seven months.\n\nThe story suggests that fame in art depends not only on quality but also on stories and the media. Leonardo's skill was exactly the same before and after 1911; what changed was that millions of ordinary people now knew the painting's face and its dramatic history. In a sense, the empty wall did as much for the Mona Lisa as the painting itself.", "passage_zh": "每天都有成千上萬的遊客在巴黎羅浮宮擠過人潮，只為了看一眼厚厚的防彈玻璃後面那幅小小的肖像畫。李奧納多‧達文西在十六世紀初畫下的〈蒙娜麗莎〉，常被稱為世界上最有名的畫作。然而在1900年時，欣賞它的主要是藝術專家，它還遠遠稱不上家喻戶曉。改變它命運的，並不是人們對它的美有了什麼新發現，而是一樁犯罪。\n\n1911年8月21日星期一早上，羅浮宮正值休館、不對外開放，一名身穿羅浮宮工作人員白色工作服的男子把這幅畫從牆上取了下來。他在一處僻靜的樓梯間拆掉畫框和玻璃罩，把木板畫藏在工作服底下，就這麼走了出去。這名竊賊是文森佐‧佩魯賈，一位曾在羅浮宮工作過的義大利雜工，他甚至還曾幫忙為館內的畫作裝上保護玻璃。\n\n令人驚訝的是，這起竊案過了一天多都沒有人察覺，因為警衛以為那幅畫被拿去拍照了。真相大白後，羅浮宮閉館一個星期，世界各地的報紙都把蒙娜麗莎的臉登上了頭版。博物館重新開放時，人們大排長龍，只為了盯著那幅畫原本掛著的空位看。就連知名的藝術家也遭到懷疑：詩人阿波利奈爾被關了好幾天，他的朋友、年輕的畫家畢卡索也被警方約談。\n\n這幅畫藏在佩魯賈巴黎住處的一個大皮箱裡，長達兩年多。後來在1913年底，他把畫帶到佛羅倫斯，向藝術品商人阿爾弗雷多‧傑里兜售。起了疑心的傑里請烏菲茲美術館的館長前來鑑定。他們確認是真跡之後便通知了警方，佩魯賈隨即在下榻的旅館被捕。\n\n在審判中，佩魯賈聲稱自己是個愛國者，想讓一件義大利傑作回到祖國，因為他認為這幅畫是被拿破崙搶走的。事實上，是達文西自己把這幅畫帶到法國的，後來它成了法國國王的收藏。儘管如此，許多義大利人仍把佩魯賈視為英雄；他雖然被判刑一年多，卻在大約七個月後就獲釋了。\n\n這個故事顯示，藝術作品的名氣不僅取決於品質，也取決於故事和媒體。達文西的技藝在1911年前後完全一樣；改變的是，如今有數百萬的普通人認得這幅畫的臉，也知道它那段充滿戲劇性的經歷。從某種意義上來說，那面空蕩蕩的牆為〈蒙娜麗莎〉所做的，和這幅畫本身一樣多。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["The Louvre has always had poor security for its paintings.", "Leonardo da Vinci painted the Mona Lisa as a gift for the king of France.", "Peruggia was a true hero who returned a masterpiece to Italy.", "A famous crime played a major role in making the Mona Lisa world-famous."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段指出改變〈蒙娜麗莎〉命運的 was not a new discovery about its beauty, but a crime，最後一段也說 the empty wall did as much for the Mona Lisa as the painting itself，全文說明竊案如何讓這幅畫舉世聞名。(A) 文中只談這一次竊案；(B) 第五段只說達文西自己把畫帶到法國，後來才成為法國國王的收藏，並未說是為國王而畫的禮物；(C) 第五段指出佩魯賈的說法有誤，作者並未稱他為真英雄，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How did Peruggia get the painting out of the museum?", "options": ["He broke a window at night and climbed out with the painting.", "He paid one of the museum guards to carry it out of the building for him.", "Dressed as a museum worker, he hid it under his smock and walked out.", "He replaced it with a copy while the museum was crowded with visitors."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：a man in the white smock worn by Louvre workers ... hid the wooden panel under his smock and simply walked out。他穿著館員的白色工作服，把畫藏在衣服下走出去。(A) 文中說他是 simply walked out，並非趁夜破窗爬出；(B) 文中沒有提到收買警衛；(D) 第二段說當天 the museum was closed to the public，而且他並沒有放上複製品，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"a household name\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["something kept in people's homes", "something known to almost everyone", "something too expensive to buy", "something painted by a family member"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：1900年時這幅畫 was admired mainly by art experts and was far from a household name，前半句說只有藝術專家欣賞，可知 a household name 指「家喻戶曉的人或物」。(A) 是字面上的誤解；(C)(D) 與上下文無關，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Peruggia's reason for the theft?", "options": ["It was based on a mistaken belief about the painting's history.", "The court accepted it and let him go without punishment.", "It persuaded the Louvre to give the painting to Italy.", "It was invented by newspapers rather than by Peruggia himself."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：佩魯賈 believed Napoleon had stolen it，但 In fact, Leonardo had carried the painting to France himself，可見他的理由建立在錯誤的認知上。(B) 第五段說他仍被判刑一年多（sentenced to just over a year in prison），並非無罪開釋；(C) 第一段說這幅畫如今仍在巴黎羅浮宮展出，可見並沒有因此被交給義大利；(D) 第五段說是他本人在審判中這樣聲稱，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Only the most skillfully painted works become famous.", "Art experts are the people who decide which paintings become popular.", "Thieves should be thanked for making paintings more valuable.", "The stories told about a work of art can matter as much as its quality."], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段：fame in art depends not only on quality but also on stories and the media，達文西的技藝前後不變，改變的是大眾知道了這幅畫和它的戲劇性經歷。(A) 與作者論點相反；(B) 第一段說原本只有專家欣賞，畫作卻是因竊案才出名；(C) 作者並未主張感謝竊賊，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a149", "title": "Can Mozart Make You Smarter? The Rise and Fall of a Musical Myth", "title_zh": "聽莫札特會變聰明嗎？一則音樂迷思的興起與破滅", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Arts", "Science"], "passage": "In 1993, three researchers at the University of California, Irvine, published a short report in the respected science journal Nature. They had asked 36 college students to spend ten minutes listening to a Mozart sonata for two pianos, listening to relaxation instructions, or sitting in silence. Afterwards, the students completed spatial reasoning tasks, such as imagining how a folded and cut piece of paper would look when it was opened. Those who had heard Mozart scored noticeably higher, although the improvement disappeared within about fifteen minutes.\n\nThe researchers never claimed that Mozart made anyone generally more intelligent, but the media quickly turned their modest finding into a simple and exciting message: classical music makes you smarter. The idea became known as the \"Mozart effect.\" Parents bought CDs and videos that promised to boost their babies' brains, and in 1998 the governor of the American state of Georgia proposed spending public money to give every newborn in the state a classical music recording.\n\nUnfortunately, the scientific story was far less exciting. Other laboratories tried to repeat the experiment, and many could not replicate the result at all. In 2010, researchers at the University of Vienna combined the data from about forty studies involving some 3,000 people and concluded that there was little evidence for any special effect of Mozart's music. Besides, the original study had never involved babies, and the benefit it reported was small and brief.\n\nSo why did some people perform better after hearing Mozart? Psychologist Glenn Schellenberg and his colleagues offered a convincing explanation: arousal and mood. Any experience that makes us feel more alert and cheerful can briefly improve our performance on mental tasks. In one experiment, people who preferred a short story by Stephen King did just as well after listening to the story as others did after hearing Mozart. In a large study of British schoolchildren, those who listened to pop songs by bands such as Blur did better than those who heard Mozart. The music itself, it seems, was never magic; enjoying it was what mattered.\n\nThe rise and fall of the Mozart effect offers a useful lesson about how science reaches the public. A cautious result from a small experiment can grow into a confident promise once it passes through headlines, advertisements and political speeches. Before believing such claims, it is worth asking who was actually studied, how large the effect was and whether other scientists found the same thing. As for Mozart, his music needs no scientific excuse. It is worth listening to simply because it is beautiful.", "passage_zh": "1993年，加州大學爾灣分校的三位研究人員在備受推崇的科學期刊《自然》上發表了一篇簡短的報告。他們請36名大學生花十分鐘聆聽一首莫札特的雙鋼琴奏鳴曲、聆聽放鬆指導語，或是靜靜坐著。接著，這些學生進行空間推理測驗，例如想像一張經過摺疊和剪裁的紙打開後會是什麼樣子。聽過莫札特的學生分數明顯較高，不過這種進步在大約十五分鐘內就消失了。\n\n研究人員從未宣稱莫札特能讓人整體變得更聰明，但媒體很快就把這項有限的發現變成一個簡單又令人興奮的訊息：古典音樂讓你變聰明。這個想法後來被稱為「莫札特效應」。父母們紛紛購買標榜能促進寶寶大腦發展的CD和影片；1998年，美國喬治亞州州長甚至提議動用公帑，送給州內每一名新生兒一份古典音樂錄音。\n\n遺憾的是，科學上的真相遠沒有那麼令人興奮。其他實驗室試著重做這項實驗，許多都完全無法複製出相同的結果。2010年，維也納大學的研究人員綜合了約四十項研究、總計約三千人的資料，結論是幾乎沒有證據顯示莫札特的音樂有任何特殊效果。此外，最初的研究根本沒有以嬰兒為對象，而且它所發現的好處既微小又短暫。\n\n那麼，為什麼有些人在聽完莫札特後表現得比較好呢？心理學家格倫‧謝倫伯格和他的同事提出了一個令人信服的解釋：激發程度與心情。任何讓我們感覺更清醒、更愉快的經驗，都能暫時提升我們在心智任務上的表現。在一項實驗中，比較喜歡史蒂芬‧金短篇故事的人，聽完故事後的表現和其他人聽完莫札特後一樣好。在一項針對英國學童的大型研究中，聽了布勒（Blur）等樂團流行歌曲的孩子，表現比聽莫札特的孩子更好。看來，音樂本身從來就沒有魔力；真正重要的是享受它。\n\n莫札特效應的興起與破滅，讓我們對科學如何傳達給大眾上了寶貴的一課。一項小型實驗得出的謹慎結果，一旦經過新聞標題、廣告和政治演說的層層傳播，就可能膨脹成一個信心滿滿的承諾。在相信這類說法之前，值得先問一問：研究的對象到底是誰？效果有多大？其他科學家是否也得到了相同的結果？至於莫札特，他的音樂根本不需要科學理由來背書。它值得聆聽，單純只是因為它很美。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To show how a popular belief grew from limited evidence and was later questioned.", "To encourage parents to play classical music to their babies every day from birth.", "To prove that modern pop music is better than classical music for learning.", "To explain how spatial reasoning tests are designed and scored."], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文先介紹1993年的小型實驗（第一段），再說明媒體如何把它誇大成「莫札特效應」（第二段），接著指出後續研究無法證實（第三、四段），最後提醒讀者檢視證據（第五段）。(B) 作者並不支持這種做法；(C) 第四段的流行歌只是用來說明「心情」的解釋，並非比較音樂優劣；(D) 測驗方式只在第一段簡單帶過，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the 1993 study?", "options": ["It was carried out on newborn babies in Georgia.", "It showed that Mozart permanently raised students' intelligence.", "It found a brief improvement in one kind of mental task.", "It compared Mozart's music with pop songs by Blur."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：受試者是36名大學生，聽完莫札特後在 spatial reasoning tasks（空間推理測驗）上分數較高，但 the improvement disappeared within about fifteen minutes。(A) 第三段明確說 the original study had never involved babies；(B) 效果只維持約十五分鐘，且研究者 never claimed 能讓人整體變聰明；(D) 與 Blur 比較的是第四段另一項英國學童的研究，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"replicate\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["explain", "publish", "reject", "reproduce"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Other laboratories tried to repeat the experiment, and many could not replicate the result，前文說其他實驗室「重做」這項實驗，可知 replicate 是「複製出（相同結果）」的意思，與 reproduce 最接近。(A) 解釋、(B) 發表、(C) 拒絕皆不合上下文，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What do the Stephen King story and the Blur songs in paragraph 4 suggest?", "options": ["Stories are generally more useful than music for raising students' test scores.", "People do better after any experience that they enjoy and that wakes them up.", "Classical music makes most children feel sleepy and bored during tests.", "Mozart's music only works on adults, not on children."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段指出關鍵在 arousal and mood：Any experience that makes us feel more alert and cheerful can briefly improve our performance，喜歡故事的人聽故事、孩子聽流行歌都表現得一樣好或更好，結論是 enjoying it was what mattered。(A) 文中並未說故事比音樂更有效；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward claims like the Mozart effect?", "options": ["Cautious: readers should check the evidence before believing them.", "Enthusiastic: they are a good way to make science popular.", "Uninterested: such claims make no difference to anyone.", "Angry: the original researchers lied about the results of their study."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：Before believing such claims, it is worth asking who was actually studied, how large the effect was and whether other scientists found the same thing，作者提醒讀者要先檢視證據，態度是審慎的。(B) 作者批評的是誇大的宣傳；(C) 第二段提到家長花錢、政府要動用公帑，可見影響不小；(D) 第二段說研究者 never claimed 莫札特讓人變聰明，問題出在媒體的誇大，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a150", "title": "Worth More Than Gold: The Long Search for the Perfect Blue", "title_zh": "比黃金還珍貴：尋找完美藍色的漫長旅程", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Arts", "History"], "passage": "Look around you and you will probably see blue everywhere: on jeans, school uniforms, phone screens and advertisements. It is hard to believe that for most of human history, blue was one of the most difficult colors for artists to obtain. Red and yellow pigments could be made from ordinary earth and clay, but very few natural materials produce a strong, lasting blue. The sky and the sea, after all, cannot be ground into powder.\n\nThe ancient Egyptians found one solution more than 4,500 years ago. By heating sand, limestone and a copper mineral together at very high temperatures, they created a bright substance now known as Egyptian blue, often described as the world's first synthetic pigment. It decorated tombs, statues and jewelry for centuries, but after Roman times the method was gradually forgotten in Europe.\n\nFor painters in medieval and Renaissance Europe, the finest blue was ultramarine, made by grinding lapis lazuli, a semi-precious stone mined mainly in the mountains of what is now Afghanistan. Its name comes from Latin words meaning \"beyond the sea,\" because it had to be shipped across the sea to reach Europe. Turning the stone into pure pigment was slow and wasteful, and good ultramarine could cost more than gold. Wealthy patrons therefore sometimes wrote the exact amount to be used into their contracts with artists, so that a cheaper blue could not secretly be used instead. The color was also often reserved for the most important figure in a picture, such as the robe of the Virgin Mary.\n\nCheaper blues eventually arrived, often by accident. In Berlin in the early 1700s, a color maker trying to produce a red pigment ended up with a deep blue instead, which became known as Prussian blue. Affordable and intense, it spread around the world, and more than a century later the Japanese artist Hokusai used it in his famous print The Great Wave off Kanagawa. Then, in the 1820s, a French society offered a prize to anyone who could make artificial ultramarine. A chemist named Jean-Baptiste Guimet won the prize in 1828, and the once-precious color became available to almost every painter.\n\nThe search is not over. In 2009, a chemistry team at Oregon State University was studying new materials for electronics when a sample came out of the furnace a brilliant blue. Named YInMn blue after the elements yttrium, indium and manganese, it was the first new inorganic blue pigment discovered in about two hundred years. It is extremely stable and reflects heat well, which may make it useful for keeping roofs cool, and it even inspired a new crayon color. The story of blue reminds us that the colors artists use are shaped not only by imagination but also by chemistry, trade and lucky mistakes.", "passage_zh": "看看你的周遭，你大概會發現藍色無所不在：牛仔褲、學校制服、手機螢幕和廣告上都有。很難相信，在人類歷史的大部分時間裡，藍色都是藝術家最難取得的顏色之一。紅色和黃色的顏料可以用普通的泥土和黏土製成，但能產生濃烈而持久的藍色的天然材料卻少之又少。畢竟，天空和大海是沒辦法磨成粉的。\n\n古埃及人在四千五百多年前找到了一個解決辦法。他們把沙子、石灰石和一種含銅礦物放在一起，以極高的溫度加熱，製造出一種鮮豔的物質，也就是現在所稱的「埃及藍」，它常被稱為世界上第一種人工合成顏料。好幾個世紀以來，埃及藍被用來裝飾墳墓、雕像和珠寶，但在羅馬時代之後，這種製法在歐洲就漸漸失傳了。\n\n對中世紀和文藝復興時期的歐洲畫家來說，最上等的藍色是「群青」，它是將青金石磨碎製成的；青金石是一種半寶石，主要開採自今日阿富汗一帶的山區。群青的英文名稱源自意為「越過海洋」的拉丁文，因為它必須經由海運才能抵達歐洲。把石頭變成純淨顏料的過程既緩慢又耗損原料，上等的群青甚至可能比黃金還貴。因此，富有的贊助人有時會把顏料的確切用量寫進與畫家簽訂的合約裡，以免畫家偷偷改用較便宜的藍色。此外，這種顏色也往往只保留給畫中最重要的人物使用，例如聖母瑪利亞的長袍。\n\n比較便宜的藍色終於出現了，而且常常是意外的產物。1700年代初期，柏林一位顏料製造者原本想做出紅色顏料，結果卻得到了一種深藍色，後來被稱為「普魯士藍」。這種藍色價格實惠、色澤濃烈，很快就傳遍世界；一百多年後，日本畫家葛飾北齋把它用在他著名的版畫〈神奈川沖浪裏〉中。接著在1820年代，法國的一個協會懸賞徵求能製造人工群青的人。一位名叫尚－巴蒂斯特‧吉美的化學家在1828年贏得了這筆獎金，這種曾經珍貴無比的顏色從此幾乎每位畫家都用得起。\n\n這場追尋還沒有結束。2009年，奧勒岡州立大學的一個化學團隊正在研究電子產品用的新材料，結果有一個樣本從高溫爐中取出時，竟呈現出亮麗的藍色。它以釔、銦、錳這三種元素命名為「YInMn藍」，是大約兩百年來首次發現的新無機藍色顏料。它極為穩定，而且能有效反射熱能，因此或許可以用來讓屋頂保持涼爽；它甚至還啟發了一種新的蠟筆顏色。藍色的故事提醒我們，藝術家所使用的顏色不只是由想像力決定，也受到化學、貿易和幸運的失誤所影響。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why blue is the most popular color in modern advertising and fashion.", "Why Egyptian art was more colorful than European art.", "Why the Virgin Mary is always painted wearing a blue robe in Europe.", "How people over thousands of years found ways to make blue pigments."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文依時間順序介紹人類取得藍色顏料的歷程：古埃及的埃及藍（第二段）、文藝復興時期的群青（第三段）、普魯士藍與人工群青（第四段），以及2009年的 YInMn 藍（第五段）。(A) 廣告只在第一段順帶一提；(B) 文中未比較兩者；(C) 聖母長袍只是第三段的例子，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why is the pigment called \"ultramarine\"?", "options": ["It was first used to paint pictures of the ocean and the sky.", "It was invented by a sailor from Afghanistan.", "It had to be carried across the sea to reach Europe.", "Its color is deeper than the color of the sea."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：Its name comes from Latin words meaning \"beyond the sea,\" because it had to be shipped across the sea to reach Europe。名稱來自「越過海洋」，因為它要經海運才能到達歐洲。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did some wealthy patrons write the amount of ultramarine into their contracts?", "options": ["To make sure the artist really used the costly pigment they had paid for.", "To force artists to use as little of the expensive blue as possible.", "Because ultramarine could not be sold legally without a written contract.", "Because artists did not know how to mix the pigment themselves."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說 good ultramarine could cost more than gold，接著以 therefore 說明贊助人因此把確切用量寫進合約，so that a cheaper blue could not secretly be used instead，目的是確保自己花大錢買的昂貴顏料真的被用在畫上。(B) 寫明用量是為了防止畫家偷換便宜顏料，並非要求少用；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What do Prussian blue and YInMn blue have in common?", "options": ["Both were made by grinding a semi-precious stone called lapis lazuli.", "Both were first produced by chemists working in France.", "Both were discovered while people were trying to make something else.", "Both were used by Hokusai in his famous print of a great wave."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：普魯士藍是 a color maker trying to produce a red pigment 時意外做出的；第五段：YInMn 藍是團隊 studying new materials for electronics 時意外出現的，兩者都是意外發現。(A) 用青金石的是群青；(B) 普魯士藍出自柏林，YInMn 藍出自美國奧勒岡州；(D) 北齋用的是普魯士藍，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"reserved\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["booked in advance", "kept for a special purpose", "quiet and shy", "thrown away"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The color was also often reserved for the most important figure in a picture, such as the robe of the Virgin Mary，因為群青昂貴，只「保留給」畫中最重要的人物使用，與 kept for a special purpose 最接近。(A)「預訂」與 (C)「拘謹害羞」雖然也是 reserved 的意思，但不符合此處上下文；(D) 意思相反，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a151", "title": "Twenty-Four Frames a Second: Why Movies Look Like Movies", "title_zh": "每秒二十四格：電影為什麼看起來「像電影」", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Arts", "Technology"], "passage": "Every movie is an illusion. What looks like smooth, continuous motion is really a series of still pictures, called frames, shown one after another so quickly that our brains blend them together. For almost a hundred years, most films have been shot and projected at 24 frames per second. You might expect such an important standard to be the result of careful scientific research, but it was actually a practical compromise.\n\nIn the silent film era, there was no fixed speed. Many cameras were cranked by hand, and films were often shot at around 16 to 20 frames per second. Projectionists could run a film faster or slower to fit the length of a show, and audiences barely noticed. Everything changed in the late 1920s, when sound arrived. Sound has to be played back at a steady rate, so films now had to run at exactly the same speed every time; otherwise, voices would rise and fall in pitch. Studios settled on 24 frames per second because it was fast enough for acceptable sound quality, yet slow enough to avoid wasting expensive film.\n\nOver the decades, audiences learned to connect the look of 24 frames, including the slight blur when objects move quickly, with the feeling of cinema itself. Television news, sports and daytime dramas, by contrast, were often recorded on video at higher rates, which made movement look sharper and more \"live.\" As a result, many viewers now feel that higher frame rates look cheap, even though they are technically more detailed. Some people call this the \"soap opera effect.\"\n\nA few filmmakers have tried to break the habit. In 2012, director Peter Jackson released The Hobbit: An Unexpected Journey at 48 frames per second, hoping the sharper images would make its 3D scenes more comfortable to watch. Many viewers, however, complained that the film looked strangely artificial, as if they were watching actors on a set rather than characters in another world. Taiwanese-born director Ang Lee went even further, shooting Billy Lynn's Long Halftime Walk in 2016 at 120 frames per second. Few cinemas had the equipment to show it in its full format, and the experiment did not become a new standard.\n\nInterestingly, the same people who reject high frame rates in cinemas often demand them elsewhere. Video gamers happily pay for screens that show 120 frames per second or more, because in a game, smooth motion and quick responses can mean the difference between winning and losing. This contrast suggests that our sense of what \"looks right\" depends less on our eyes than on habit and purpose. A choice made nearly a century ago to match sound and save money has become, for many people, the very definition of a movie.", "passage_zh": "每一部電影都是一種錯覺。看起來流暢連續的動作，其實是一連串稱為「格」的靜止畫面，以極快的速度一張接一張播放，快到讓我們的大腦把它們融合在一起。將近一百年來，大多數電影都是以每秒二十四格來拍攝和放映。你或許以為這麼重要的標準是經過嚴謹的科學研究才訂出來的，但它其實只是一個務實的折衷方案。\n\n在默片時代，並沒有固定的速度。許多攝影機是用手搖的，影片的拍攝速度往往在每秒十六到二十格左右。放映師可以依照節目時間的長短，把影片放快一點或放慢一點，觀眾幾乎察覺不到。到了1920年代末期，有聲電影出現，一切都改變了。聲音必須以穩定的速率播放，因此影片每次放映都得以完全相同的速度運轉；否則，說話聲的音調就會忽高忽低。片廠最後選定每秒二十四格，因為這個速度既快到足以提供還過得去的音質，又慢到不至於浪費昂貴的底片。\n\n幾十年下來，觀眾逐漸把二十四格的畫面質感，包括物體快速移動時那種輕微的模糊，和電影本身的感覺連結在一起。相較之下，電視新聞、體育節目和日間連續劇常以較高的速率錄成錄影帶，讓動作看起來更清晰，也更有「現場感」。結果，許多觀眾如今覺得較高的影格率看起來很廉價，儘管從技術上來說它呈現的細節更多。有人把這種現象稱為「肥皂劇效應」。\n\n有幾位電影工作者曾試圖打破這個習慣。2012年，導演彼得‧傑克森以每秒四十八格推出《哈比人：意外旅程》，希望更清晰的影像能讓片中的3D畫面看起來更舒服。然而許多觀眾抱怨這部片看起來異常不自然，彷彿他們看的是片場裡的演員，而不是另一個世界中的角色。台灣出生的導演李安走得更遠，他在2016年以每秒一百二十格拍攝《比利‧林恩的中場戰事》。很少有戲院具備以完整規格放映這部片的設備，這項實驗也沒有成為新的標準。\n\n有趣的是，在電影院裡排斥高影格率的人，在其他地方卻常常要求高影格率。電玩玩家很樂意花錢購買每秒能顯示一百二十格以上的螢幕，因為在遊戲裡，流暢的動作和快速的反應可能就是輸贏的關鍵。這種對比顯示，我們覺得什麼「看起來才對」，與其說取決於眼睛，不如說取決於習慣和用途。將近一個世紀前，為了配合聲音、節省成本而做出的選擇，如今對許多人來說，已成了「電影」的定義本身。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to choose the best screen for playing fast-moving video games at home.", "How Ang Lee became a world-famous film director.", "Why silent films were more enjoyable to watch than modern sound films.", "Why 24 frames per second became the film standard and why it has lasted."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文說明每秒二十四格的由來（第一、二段）、觀眾為何習慣它（第三段）、提高影格率的嘗試為何沒有成功（第四段），以及習慣如何決定我們的感受（第五段）。(A) 電玩只是第五段的對照例子；(B) 李安只是第四段的例子；(C) 文中未比較默片與現代電影的好壞，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did studios choose 24 frames per second when sound films appeared?", "options": ["Scientists had proved it was the fastest speed the human eye can see.", "It gave acceptable sound quality without using too much costly film.", "Hand-cranked cameras were unable to go any faster.", "Television programs were already using exactly this speed."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段最後一句：24 frames per second ... was fast enough for acceptable sound quality, yet slow enough to avoid wasting expensive film，在音質與底片成本之間取得平衡。(A) 第一段說它並非科學研究的結果；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第三段說電視使用的是較高的速率，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"compromise\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a solution that balances different needs", "a serious mistake that cannot be fixed", "a secret agreement between companies", "an important scientific discovery"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段說這個標準並非 careful scientific research 的結果，而是 a practical compromise；第二段接著說明二十四格是在音質與底片成本之間取捨的結果，因此 compromise 指「兼顧不同需求的折衷方案」。(B)(C) 文中無此意；(D) 與第一段的說法相反，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about why many viewers disliked the 48-frame version of The Hobbit?", "options": ["The story was much longer and slower than most viewers had expected it to be.", "Most cinemas played the film at the wrong speed because of old equipment.", "The sharp, smooth images did not match what they expected a film to look like.", "The 3D glasses given to viewers were too heavy and uncomfortable to wear."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說觀眾習慣把二十四格的畫面和 the feeling of cinema 連結，覺得較高的影格率看起來廉價；第四段觀眾抱怨《哈比人》looked strangely artificial，可推論是清晰流暢的畫面不符合他們對電影的期待。(A) 文中未提及；(B) 第四段說缺乏放映設備的是李安的《比利‧林恩的中場戰事》，並非《哈比人》被以錯誤速度播放；(D) 傑克森反而希望高影格率讓3D更舒服，文中沒有提到眼鏡的問題，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention video gamers in the last paragraph?", "options": ["To show that video games now tell better and more exciting stories than movies.", "To advise readers to buy faster screens for their homes.", "To complain that young people spend too much money on games.", "To show that people's feelings about frame rates depend on habit and purpose."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段指出同一群在電影院排斥高影格率的人，玩遊戲時卻要求高影格率，並說 our sense of what \"looks right\" depends less on our eyes than on habit and purpose，提到玩家是為了說明這個對比。(A)(B)(C) 都不是作者的寫作目的，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a152", "title": "The Font Everyone Loves to Hate: The Story of Comic Sans", "title_zh": "人人都愛嫌棄的字型：Comic Sans 的故事", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Arts", "Technology"], "passage": "Few things in design stir up as much emotion as a single font. Mention Comic Sans to a graphic designer, and you may hear a groan or a joke. Yet the same rounded, playful letters appear on birthday invitations, classroom posters and shop signs around the world. How did one typeface become both so widespread and so widely mocked?\n\nComic Sans was created in 1994 by Vincent Connare, a typeface designer at Microsoft. He was shown an early version of Microsoft Bob, a program meant to make computers friendlier for beginners, in which a cartoon dog gave advice in speech balloons. To Connare, it seemed odd that the dog spoke in Times New Roman, a formal font associated with newspapers. Inspired by the hand-lettering in the comic books in his office, he quickly drew a casual alternative. The font was finished too late for Microsoft Bob, but it was later included with Windows and soon appeared on millions of computers.\n\nThat was where the trouble began. Because it looked friendly and was easy to find, people used Comic Sans for almost everything, including business reports, warning signs and official notices. Designers argued that its childlike style made serious messages look silly, and in 1999 two designers in the United States launched a campaign called \"Ban Comic Sans.\" The font's reputation suffered another blow in 2012, when scientists at CERN, the European physics laboratory, presented evidence for the long-sought Higgs boson on slides written in Comic Sans. The discovery was historic, but many people online seemed more interested in making fun of the font.\n\nComic Sans does have defenders, however. Its letters are simple and clearly different from one another; the lowercase \"b\" and \"d,\" for example, are not exact mirror images, as they are in many fonts. For this reason, some teachers find it helpful for young children who are learning to read, and some organizations that support people with dyslexia, a condition that makes reading difficult, list it among fonts that may be easier to read. Its relaxed, handwritten look can also make a message feel warm and approachable.\n\nThe real lesson of Comic Sans may be that no font is good or bad in itself. Typefaces, like clothes, send messages about tone. A swimsuit is perfect at the beach but strange at a job interview, and in the same way Comic Sans works well on a party flyer but poorly on a court notice. Connare himself has said that he designed the font for a particular purpose and never meant it to be used everywhere. Choosing a typeface, then, is not just decoration; it is about matching the voice of the letters to the message they carry.", "passage_zh": "在設計領域中，很少有東西能像一種字型那樣引發這麼強烈的情緒。向平面設計師提起 Comic Sans，你可能會聽到一聲哀嘆或一句玩笑話。然而，同樣圓潤、俏皮的字母，卻出現在世界各地的生日邀請卡、教室海報和商店招牌上。一種字型怎麼會既如此普遍，又如此廣受嘲笑呢？\n\nComic Sans 是微軟的字型設計師文森‧康納爾在1994年設計的。當時有人給他看了 Microsoft Bob 的早期版本；這是一套為了讓初學者覺得電腦更親切而設計的程式，裡面有一隻卡通狗會用對話框提供建議。康納爾覺得，讓這隻狗用 Times New Roman 說話很奇怪，因為那是一種會讓人聯想到報紙的正式字型。他從辦公室裡那些漫畫書上的手寫字體得到靈感，很快就畫出了一種輕鬆隨興的替代字型。這套字型完成時已經來不及用在 Microsoft Bob 上，但後來被收進 Windows 之中，很快就出現在數百萬台電腦上。\n\n麻煩就是從這裡開始的。由於它看起來很親切又容易取得，人們幾乎什麼都用 Comic Sans，包括商業報告、警告標示和官方公告。設計師認為，它孩子氣的風格會讓嚴肅的訊息顯得很可笑；1999年，兩位美國設計師發起了一項名為「禁用 Comic Sans」的運動。2012年，這套字型的名聲又遭受了一次打擊：歐洲的物理實驗室 CERN 的科學家發表了人們尋找已久的希格斯玻色子的證據，而他們的簡報投影片用的正是 Comic Sans。這項發現具有歷史意義，但許多網友似乎對嘲笑這個字型更感興趣。\n\n不過，Comic Sans 也有擁護者。它的字母簡單，彼此之間的差異也很明顯；舉例來說，小寫的「b」和「d」並不像在許多字型中那樣，是完全對稱的鏡像。因此，有些老師認為它對正在學習閱讀的幼童很有幫助，而一些協助閱讀障礙者的組織，也把它列為可能比較容易閱讀的字型之一；閱讀障礙是一種造成閱讀困難的症狀。它輕鬆的手寫風格，也能讓訊息感覺起來溫暖又平易近人。\n\nComic Sans 真正給我們的啟示，或許是：沒有一種字型本身是好或壞的。字型就像衣服一樣，會傳達出某種語氣。泳裝在海灘上再合適不過，穿去求職面試卻很奇怪；同樣地，Comic Sans 用在派對傳單上效果很好，用在法院公告上就很不恰當。康納爾本人也說過，他設計這套字型是為了特定用途，從來沒打算讓它被用在所有地方。因此，選擇字型不只是裝飾而已，而是要讓字母的「聲音」與它們所承載的訊息相符。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to design a new font that makes computer programs look friendlier.", "Why Microsoft Bob failed as a friendly computer program for beginners.", "Why one font became both popular and criticized, and what it teaches us.", "How scientists at CERN discovered the Higgs boson and shared the news."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段提出問題：一種字型怎麼會 both so widespread and so widely mocked？接著說明它的由來（第二段）、受批評的原因（第三段）、擁護者的看法（第四段），最後歸納出字型要配合用途的啟示（第五段）。(A) 文中並未教人如何設計字型；(B) Microsoft Bob 只是背景；(D) CERN 只是第三段的例子，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Vincent Connare create Comic Sans?", "options": ["He felt a formal newspaper font did not suit a cartoon dog's speech.", "He wanted to help children with reading problems.", "CERN asked him to design a clear font for its science presentations.", "He hoped to replace Times New Roman as the main font in newspapers."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：To Connare, it seemed odd that the dog spoke in Times New Roman, a formal font associated with newspapers，所以他畫出了輕鬆的替代字型。(B) 幫助閱讀是第四段提到的後來用途，並非設計原因；(C) CERN 是2012年的事；(D) 他想替換的是卡通狗對話框裡的字型，不是報紙用字，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"mocked\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["copied", "laughed at", "sold", "designed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說設計師一聽到 Comic Sans 就會 a groan or a joke（哀嘆或開玩笑），可知 mocked 是「被嘲笑」的意思；第三段的 making fun of the font 也是同樣意思，與 laughed at 最接近。(A) 複製、(C) 賣出、(D) 設計皆不合上下文，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest about the CERN presentation in 2012?", "options": ["The discovery it announced was later proved to be wrong.", "The scientists chose Comic Sans because it was easier to read than other fonts.", "The choice of font took some attention away from an important message.", "CERN later apologized and stopped using the font for good."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：The discovery was historic, but many people online seemed more interested in making fun of the font，這項重大發現的焦點反而被字型搶走了。(A) 文中說這項發現 historic，並未說被推翻；(B) 文中未說明 CERN 選用的理由；(D) 文中未提及道歉，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which statement would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["Comic Sans should be removed from all computers as soon as possible.", "Comic Sans is the best choice for every kind of document.", "Only professional designers should be allowed to choose fonts.", "A font should be chosen to suit the purpose and tone of a message."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：no font is good or bad in itself，並以衣服為例，說選擇字型 is about matching the voice of the letters to the message they carry。(A) 是 Ban Comic Sans 運動的立場，作者並不贊同完全否定；(B) 作者說它不適合法院公告；(C) 文中未提及，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a153", "title": "A Mixtape for the Stars: The Voyager Golden Record", "title_zh": "寄給星空的精選輯：航海家號金唱片", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Arts", "Science"], "passage": "In 1977, NASA launched two spacecraft, Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, to study the giant planets of the outer solar system. Scientists knew that after finishing their mission, the probes would keep traveling outward and drift among the stars for millions of years. So each one carries an unusual passenger: a twelve-inch, gold-plated copper record filled with sounds and images from Earth. It is, in effect, a message in a bottle thrown into what the astronomer Carl Sagan called the \"cosmic ocean.\"\n\nA small committee led by Sagan faced the difficult job of deciding what should represent our entire planet. They chose 115 images, from diagrams of DNA to photographs of people eating and running, along with sounds such as thunder, birdsong, whale calls, laughter and a kiss. They also recorded greetings in 55 languages. One of them, in Amoy, a form of Southern Min closely related to Taiwanese, says: \"Friends of space, how are you all? Have you eaten yet? Come visit us if you have time.\"\n\nThe heart of the record, however, is about ninety minutes of music. The committee tried to include as many cultures as possible, so Bach and Beethoven sit beside Javanese gamelan, Peruvian panpipes, a Navajo night chant, Japanese shakuhachi and an ancient Chinese guqin piece called \"Flowing Streams.\" There is also rock and roll. When some argued that Chuck Berry's \"Johnny B. Goode\" was too adolescent for such a serious project, Sagan reportedly replied that there are a lot of adolescents on the planet.\n\nDesigning a record that aliens could actually play was another challenge. Each record comes with a needle, and its aluminum cover is engraved with diagrams explaining how to play it, using a unit of time based on the hydrogen atom. A map on the cover shows the position of our Sun relative to fourteen pulsars, so that anyone who finds it might work out where it came from. The team estimated that the record could survive in space for a billion years.\n\nWill anyone ever hear it? Probably not. Voyager 1 entered interstellar space in 2012, but space is enormous: in about 40,000 years, it will pass within 1.6 light-years of another star, which is still a vast distance. Critics have also noted that the record offers an idealized picture of humanity, with almost no sign of war, poverty or disease. Yet perhaps its real audience was always us. Choosing what to include forced people to think about what makes our species worth knowing, and nearly fifty years later, the Golden Record remains a moving portrait of a planet trying to introduce itself.", "passage_zh": "1977年，美國太空總署發射了「航海家1號」和「航海家2號」兩艘太空船，前往研究外太陽系的巨大行星。科學家知道，這兩艘探測器在完成任務之後，仍會繼續往外飛行，在群星之間漂流數百萬年。因此，它們各自載著一位不尋常的「乘客」：一張十二吋、鍍金的銅製唱片，裡面錄滿了來自地球的聲音和影像。實際上，它就像是一個被丟進天文學家卡爾‧薩根所說的「宇宙海洋」裡的瓶中信。\n\n由薩根帶領的一個小型委員會，肩負起一項艱難的任務：決定該用什麼來代表我們整個星球。他們選了115張圖像，從DNA的示意圖到人們吃東西、跑步的照片都有，另外還有雷聲、鳥鳴、鯨魚的叫聲、笑聲和親吻聲等聲音。他們也錄下了55種語言的問候語。其中一段用的是廈門話，那是一種和台語關係十分密切的閩南語，內容是：「太空的朋友，你們好嗎？吃飽了沒？有空來我們這裡坐坐吧！」\n\n不過，這張唱片的核心是大約九十分鐘的音樂。委員會盡可能納入各種不同的文化，因此巴哈和貝多芬的作品，與爪哇的甘美朗、秘魯的排笛、納瓦霍族的夜歌、日本的尺八，以及一首名為〈流水〉的中國古琴曲並列。唱片裡也有搖滾樂。當有人認為查克‧貝瑞的〈Johnny B. Goode〉對這麼嚴肅的計畫來說太「青少年」了，據說薩根回答：這個星球上可有很多青少年呢。\n\n設計一張外星人真的能播放的唱片，是另一項挑戰。每張唱片都附有一根唱針，鋁製的封套上刻著說明播放方法的圖示，並以一個根據氫原子訂出的時間單位作為基準。封套上還有一幅地圖，標示出太陽相對於十四顆脈衝星的位置，讓任何發現它的人都有可能推算出它來自何方。團隊估計，這張唱片可以在太空中保存十億年。\n\n會有誰聽到它嗎？大概不會。航海家1號在2012年進入了星際空間，但太空實在太遼闊了：大約四萬年後，它才會從距離另一顆恆星1.6光年的地方經過，而這仍是一段極為遙遠的距離。批評者也指出，這張唱片呈現的是理想化的人類形象，幾乎看不到戰爭、貧窮或疾病的痕跡。然而，它真正的聽眾或許一直都是我們自己。決定要放入哪些內容，迫使人們思考人類究竟有什麼值得被認識的地方；將近五十年後，這張金唱片依然是一幅動人的畫像，描繪著一個努力自我介紹的星球。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["A record sent into space to introduce Earth, and what it contains and means.", "How the Voyager spacecraft discovered new planets beyond our solar system.", "Why rock music is more popular than classical music around the world.", "How scientists send radio messages to try to contact aliens."], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹航海家號所攜帶的金唱片：它的由來（第一段）、圖像與問候語（第二段）、音樂（第三段）、播放說明的設計（第四段），以及它的意義（第五段）。(B) 文中未說發現新行星；(C) 搖滾樂只是第三段的一個例子；(D) 文中談的是唱片，不是無線電訊號，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the Golden Record?", "options": ["Greetings in many different languages.", "Sounds such as thunder and whale calls.", "Music from a wide range of cultures.", "Video clips from popular movies."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段提到55種語言的問候語 (A)、雷聲與鯨魚叫聲等聲音 (B)；第三段提到來自各種文化的音樂 (C)。唱片內容是圖像、聲音與音樂，文中從未提到電影片段，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why might Taiwanese readers find the Amoy greeting especially interesting?", "options": ["It is in a language closely related to Taiwanese.", "It was recorded by a famous Taiwanese singer.", "It invites aliens to visit Taiwan in particular.", "It is the only greeting on the record that asks a question."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Amoy, a form of Southern Min closely related to Taiwanese，廈門話是和台語關係密切的閩南語，台灣讀者自然會覺得親切有趣。(B) 文中未提到錄音者；(C) 問候語說的是 Come visit us，並未指名台灣；(D) 文中沒有說它是唯一提問的問候語，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What did Sagan most likely mean when he replied that \"there are a lot of adolescents on the planet\"?", "options": ["Teenagers should not be allowed to listen to rock music like Chuck Berry's.", "Young people are part of humanity, so their music belongs on the record.", "The record was mainly made for teenagers on Earth to enjoy.", "Aliens are probably young and would enjoy rock music more than Bach."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：有人認為搖滾歌曲 too adolescent（太孩子氣）不該收錄，薩根回答地球上有很多青少年，意思是青少年也是人類的一部分，他們的音樂也應該被代表。(A) 與他支持收錄的立場相反；(C) 唱片是送往太空的；(D) 文中沒有推測外星人的年齡，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by saying \"perhaps its real audience was always us\"?", "options": ["NASA secretly planned to bring the record back to Earth one day for people to hear.", "Copies of the record were made mainly to be sold to music fans.", "Making the record helped people think about what is valuable about humanity.", "Scientists have proved that there is no life on other planets."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段緊接著說 Choosing what to include forced people to think about what makes our species worth knowing，外星人大概聽不到，但製作唱片的過程讓人類反思自身的價值。(A)(B) 文中未提及；(D) 作者只說 Probably not 有人會聽到，並未說已證明沒有外星生命，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a154", "title": "Like New for Less: Certified Refurbished Phones", "title_zh": "近乎全新，價格更實惠：認證整新手機", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Technology"], "passage": "SECOND LIFE PHONES\nCertified Refurbished Smartphones\n3F, No. 88, Zhongshan Road, Taichung\n\nWhy pay full price for a new phone? Every phone we sell passes a 40-point check and is carefully cleaned. If its battery holds less than 85% of its original charge, we put in a new one. All phones come with a charger and a 12-month warranty.\n\nChoose Your Grade\nGrade A: Looks like new. No marks can be seen from 30 cm away.\nGrade B: Light scratches on the back or sides. The screen is perfect.\nGrade C: Clear scratches on the screen or body, but the phone works perfectly.\n\nPrices (Grade A / Grade B / Grade C)\nOrbit 10, 128 GB: NT$9,800 / NT$8,600 / NT$7,200\nOrbit 12, 128 GB: NT$13,500 / NT$12,000 / NT$10,400\nOrbit 12, 256 GB: NT$15,900 / NT$14,200 / NT$12,500\n\nSpring Offers (March 1–31)\n• Trade in your old phone and get up to NT$3,000 off. The amount depends on its model and condition.\n• Students with a valid student ID get an extra 5% off any Grade A phone.\n• Add a screen protector and a phone case for just NT$350 (usual price NT$600).\n\nOur Promise\nNot happy with your phone? Bring it back within 14 days for a full refund. The phone must be in the same condition as when you bought it, with everything still in the box. The warranty does not cover water damage or screens cracked by dropping the phone.\n\nOpen Tuesday to Sunday, 11 a.m. to 9 p.m. (closed on Mondays)\nTel: (04) 2225-8810 | LINE: @secondlifephones", "passage_zh": "二度人生手機\n認證整新智慧型手機\n台中市中山路 88 號 3 樓\n\n何必用原價買新手機？我們販售的每一支手機都通過 40 項檢測，並經過仔細清潔；如果電池的蓄電量不到原始容量的 85%，我們會換上新電池。所有手機皆附充電器及 12 個月保固。\n\n選擇您要的等級\nA 級：外觀如新，從 30 公分外看不到任何痕跡。\nB 級：背面或側邊有輕微刮痕，螢幕完美無瑕。\nC 級：螢幕或機身有明顯刮痕，但手機功能完全正常。\n\n價格（A 級／B 級／C 級）\nOrbit 10，128 GB：新台幣 9,800 元／8,600 元／7,200 元\nOrbit 12，128 GB：新台幣 13,500 元／12,000 元／10,400 元\nOrbit 12，256 GB：新台幣 15,900 元／14,200 元／12,500 元\n\n春季優惠（3 月 1 日至 31 日）\n• 舊手機折抵換購，最高可折新台幣 3,000 元，折抵金額依機型及手機狀況而定。\n• 學生出示有效學生證，購買任何 A 級手機可再打九五折。\n• 加購螢幕保護貼和手機殼只要新台幣 350 元（原價 600 元）。\n\n我們的承諾\n對手機不滿意嗎？14 天內帶回即可全額退款。手機狀況必須與購買時相同，盒內物品也必須齊全。保固不包含進水損壞，也不包含因手機摔落造成的螢幕破裂。\n\n營業時間：週二至週日，上午 11 點至晚上 9 點（週一公休）\n電話：(04) 2225-8810｜LINE：@secondlifephones", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this advertisement?", "options": ["To teach people how to repair their old phones", "To sell used phones that have been tested and cleaned", "To ask people to give away phones they no longer use", "To introduce a brand-new phone model"], "answer": 1, "explain": "標題寫著 Certified Refurbished Smartphones（認證整新智慧型手機），第二段說明每支手機都經過 40 項檢測、清潔並視情況換新電池，後面再列出價格與優惠，目的是販售整理過的二手手機。(A) 廣告沒有教人修手機；(C) 舊手機是用來「折抵換購」，不是捐出；(D) 販售的是整新機，並非新機型，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Ting-yu, a high school student, shows her student ID and buys a Grade A Orbit 10. She also adds the screen protector and phone case. How much does she pay in total?", "options": ["NT$9,910", "NT$10,150", "NT$9,660", "NT$9,310"], "answer": 2, "explain": "價格表中 A 級 Orbit 10 為 9,800 元；Spring Offers 第二點說學生出示有效學生證購買 A 級手機可再打九五折（加購配件不在此折扣內）：9,800 × 0.95 = 9,310 元；再加上加購保護貼和手機殼的 350 元，共 9,660 元。(A) 誤用原價 600 元計算加購；(B) 沒有扣學生折扣；(D) 漏算加購的 350 元，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Mr. Lin wants an Orbit 12 with 128 GB. Light scratches on the back or sides are fine with him, but he does not want any clear scratches, and the screen must be perfect. If he wants to spend as little as possible, how much will he pay?", "options": ["NT$10,400", "NT$13,500", "NT$14,200", "NT$12,000"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Choose Your Grade 段落說 B 級只有背面或側邊輕微刮痕、螢幕完美，符合林先生的要求；C 級的螢幕或機身有明顯刮痕，他無法接受。因此最省錢的選擇是 B 級，價格表中 Orbit 12 128 GB 的 B 級為 12,000 元。(A) 是 C 級價格，不符合要求；(B) 是 A 級價格，雖然符合要求但比較貴；(C) 是 256 GB 的 B 級價格，容量不對，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Two months after buying a phone from the shop, a customer drops it and cracks the screen. What can be inferred?", "options": ["The warranty will not pay for the repair.", "She can return the phone for a full refund.", "The shop will give her a new battery for free.", "She should take the phone to the shop on a Monday."], "answer": 0, "explain": "Our Promise 段落最後一句說保固不包含 screens cracked by dropping the phone（因摔落造成的螢幕破裂），所以這次維修無法由保固支付。(B) 全額退款只限 14 天內且狀況與購買時相同，已過兩個月；(C) 換電池是出售前的檢修，與摔破螢幕無關；(D) 該店週一公休，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"valid\" in the Spring Offers section is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["expensive", "officially accepted and not out of date", "borrowed from a friend", "printed in bright colors"], "answer": 1, "explain": "a valid student ID 指「有效的學生證」，也就是正式核發、仍在有效期限內、可以被接受的證件，與 officially accepted and not out of date 意思最接近。(A) 昂貴的、(C) 向朋友借來的、(D) 彩色印刷的，都不符合文意，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a155", "title": "Cold All Day: The TrailMate Steel Bottle", "title_zh": "整天都冰涼：TrailMate 不鏽鋼保溫瓶", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Environment"], "passage": "TrailMate Insulated Steel Bottle\nCold for a day. Hot for half a day. Made to last for years.\n\nHow It Works\nThe TrailMate has two steel walls with a vacuum between them. Because heat cannot travel easily through a vacuum, your drinks stay cold for up to 24 hours and hot for up to 12 hours. The outside of the bottle stays dry and comfortable to hold, even when it is full of ice water or hot tea.\n\nSizes and Prices\n500 ml: NT$690 (weight 330 g)\n750 ml: NT$850 (weight 420 g)\n1,000 ml: NT$990 (weight 510 g)\n\nColors: Forest Green, Sunset Orange and Ocean Blue\nSnow White comes in the 750 ml and 1,000 ml sizes only.\n\nExtras\n• Name engraving: NT$150 (please allow 3 extra working days)\n• Spare straw lid: NT$220\n\nCare Instructions\n• Wash by hand with warm, soapy water. Do not put the bottle in a dishwasher, because the high heat can damage the paint and the vacuum seal.\n• Do not use it for fizzy drinks such as soda or sparkling water. Pressure can build up inside and push the lid open.\n• Never put the bottle in a freezer or a microwave oven.\n\nBetter for the Planet\nOne TrailMate can replace hundreds of plastic bottles a year. Bring yours to any Green Leaf Café and get NT$10 off every drink.\n\nHow to Order\nOrder online at www.trailmate.com.tw. Delivery is free for orders over NT$1,500; otherwise, it costs NT$80. Questions? Email help@trailmate.com.tw.", "passage_zh": "TrailMate 不鏽鋼保溫瓶\n保冷一整天，保溫半天，堅固耐用，一用就是好幾年。\n\n運作原理\nTrailMate 有兩層鋼壁，中間是真空層。由於熱很難通過真空傳遞，您的飲料可以保冷長達 24 小時、保溫長達 12 小時。即使瓶裡裝滿冰水或熱茶，瓶身外側依然乾爽，拿起來也很舒服。\n\n容量與價格\n500 毫升：新台幣 690 元（重量 330 公克）\n750 毫升：新台幣 850 元（重量 420 公克）\n1,000 毫升：新台幣 990 元（重量 510 公克）\n\n顏色：森林綠、夕陽橘、海洋藍\n雪白色只有 750 毫升和 1,000 毫升兩種容量。\n\n加購項目\n• 刻名服務：新台幣 150 元（需多等 3 個工作天）\n• 備用吸管蓋：新台幣 220 元\n\n清潔與保養\n• 請用溫肥皂水手洗。請勿放入洗碗機，因為高溫可能損壞瓶身烤漆和真空密封。\n• 請勿裝汽水或氣泡水等碳酸飲料，瓶內壓力可能升高，把蓋子頂開。\n• 切勿將水瓶放入冷凍庫或微波爐。\n\n對地球更友善\n一支 TrailMate 一年就能取代數百個塑膠瓶。帶著您的水瓶到任何一家綠葉咖啡館，每杯飲料都可折抵新台幣 10 元。\n\n訂購方式\n請上 www.trailmate.com.tw 線上訂購。訂單金額超過新台幣 1,500 元免運費，未達者運費 80 元。有任何問題，請寄電子郵件至 help@trailmate.com.tw。", "questions": [{"q": "According to the product information, how long can the bottle keep a drink hot?", "options": ["Up to 6 hours", "Up to 12 hours", "Up to 24 hours", "Up to 48 hours"], "answer": 1, "explain": "How It Works 段落說 your drinks stay cold for up to 24 hours and hot for up to 12 hours，保溫最長 12 小時；開頭標語 Hot for half a day（溫熱半天）也是同樣意思。(C) 24 小時是「保冷」的時間；(A)(D) 文中未提及，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Jason orders one 750 ml bottle with his name engraved on it. Nothing else is in his order. How much does he pay in total, including delivery?", "options": ["NT$1,000", "NT$930", "NT$1,300", "NT$1,080"], "answer": 3, "explain": "750 毫升水瓶 850 元，加上 Extras 中的刻名服務 150 元，共 1,000 元；How to Order 段落說訂單超過 1,500 元才免運費，否則運費 80 元，所以總共 1,080 元。(A) 漏算運費；(B) 漏算刻名費用；(C) 多算了 220 元的備用吸管蓋，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Amy wants a Snow White bottle, and she wants it to be as light as possible. Which size should she choose?", "options": ["750 ml", "500 ml", "1,000 ml", "Any size, because they all weigh the same"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Colors 段落說雪白色只有 750 毫升和 1,000 毫升兩種；價格表顯示 750 毫升重 420 公克，比 1,000 毫升的 510 公克輕，所以應選 750 毫升。(B) 500 毫升雖然最輕，但沒有雪白色；(C) 比較重；(D) 三種容量重量都不同，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following does the company tell customers NOT to do?", "options": ["Wash the bottle with warm, soapy water", "Use the bottle for hot tea", "Fill the bottle with cola", "Bring the bottle to a café"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Care Instructions 第二點說 Do not use it for fizzy drinks such as soda，可樂屬於碳酸飲料，瓶內壓力可能把蓋子頂開，所以不可以裝可樂。(A) 是建議的清洗方式；(B) 文中說可以裝熱茶保溫；(D) 帶到綠葉咖啡館還可以折抵 10 元，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"fizzy\" in the Care Instructions is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very sweet", "full of small bubbles", "very cold", "made from fresh fruit"], "answer": 1, "explain": "fizzy drinks such as soda or sparkling water 舉的例子是汽水和氣泡水，而且後面說瓶內會產生壓力，可知 fizzy 是「有氣泡的」，與 full of small bubbles 意思最接近。(A) 很甜的、(C) 很冰的、(D) 用新鮮水果做的，都不是這兩種飲料的共同特點，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a156", "title": "No Ropes Needed: A New Climbing Gym Opens", "title_zh": "不需要繩索：新攀岩館開幕", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Sports"], "passage": "BOULDER BOX CLIMBING GYM\nNow Open in Kaohsiung!\n2F, No. 25, Bo'ai 2nd Road, Zuoying District\n\nWhat Is Bouldering?\nBouldering is rock climbing on short walls, no higher than 4.5 meters, without ropes or harnesses. Thick, soft mats cover the floor, so you can simply drop down when you are tired. It's a great workout for your arms, your legs and your brain!\n\nPrices\nDay Pass: NT$450 (students: NT$380, Monday to Friday only)\n10-Visit Card: NT$4,000 (valid for 6 months)\nMonthly Pass (unlimited visits): NT$2,200\nShoe rental: NT$100 | Chalk bag rental: NT$50\n\nGrand Opening Week (November 7–13)\nAll day passes are only NT$250, and shoe rental is free!\n\nBeginner Class\nNever climbed before? Our coaches will teach you how to fall safely, basic moves and how to plan your way up a wall.\nSaturdays 10:00–11:30 a.m. and Sundays 2:00–3:30 p.m.\nNT$600, including a day pass and shoe rental. Up to 8 people per class. Book online at least one day in advance.\n\nBefore You Climb\n• All first-time climbers must fill out a safety form at the front desk.\n• Climbers under 18 need a parent to sign the form.\n• Children under 12 must climb with an adult at all times.\n• Please wear comfortable sports clothes. No jeans!\n\nOpening Hours\nMonday to Friday: 2 p.m.–11 p.m.\nSaturday and Sunday: 9 a.m.–10 p.m.\nwww.boulderbox.com.tw | (07) 555-2468", "passage_zh": "抱石盒子攀岩館\n高雄新開幕！\n左營區博愛二路 25 號 2 樓\n\n什麼是抱石？\n抱石是在不超過 4.5 公尺高的矮牆上攀岩，不需要繩索或吊帶。地面鋪滿厚厚的軟墊，所以累了可以直接跳下來。抱石能好好鍛鍊你的手臂、雙腿，還有大腦！\n\n價格\n單次入場：新台幣 450 元（學生 380 元，僅限週一至週五）\n10 次卡：新台幣 4,000 元（6 個月內有效）\n月票（不限次數）：新台幣 2,200 元\n攀岩鞋租借：100 元｜粉袋租借：50 元\n\n開幕週（11 月 7 日至 13 日）\n所有單次入場票只要新台幣 250 元，攀岩鞋免費租借！\n\n新手課程\n從來沒攀過岩嗎？教練會教你如何安全落地、基本動作，以及怎麼規劃攀上岩牆的路線。\n每週六上午 10:00–11:30、每週日下午 2:00–3:30\n新台幣 600 元，含單次入場及攀岩鞋租借。每班最多 8 人。請至少提前一天上網預約。\n\n攀岩之前\n• 所有第一次來的攀岩者都必須在櫃檯填寫安全同意書。\n• 未滿 18 歲者需由家長在同意書上簽名。\n• 未滿 12 歲的兒童必須全程由成人陪同攀爬。\n• 請穿著舒適的運動服裝，不可穿牛仔褲！\n\n營業時間\n週一至週五：下午 2 點至晚上 11 點\n週六、週日：上午 9 點至晚上 10 點\nwww.boulderbox.com.tw｜(07) 555-2468", "questions": [{"q": "According to the advertisement, what is special about bouldering?", "options": ["It is done outdoors on real mountains.", "Climbers are tied to ropes to stay safe.", "It is done on low walls without ropes.", "It is only for experienced climbers."], "answer": 2, "explain": "What Is Bouldering? 段落說抱石是在不超過 4.5 公尺的矮牆上攀爬，without ropes or harnesses（不用繩索或吊帶）。(A) 廣告介紹的是室內攀岩館；(B) 與「不用繩索」相反；(D) 攀岩館另開設新手課程，歡迎沒攀過岩的人，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "During Grand Opening Week, a student and her father climb at the gym together, and both of them rent shoes. How much do they pay in total?", "options": ["NT$500", "NT$700", "NT$830", "NT$1,030"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Grand Opening Week 段落說開幕週所有單次入場票都是 250 元，而且攀岩鞋免費租借，所以兩人共 250 × 2 = 500 元。(B) 多算了平時的租鞋費 200 元；(C) 用平時的學生價 380 元加一般價 450 元計算；(D) 用平時價格又加上租鞋費，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "After the opening week, Kevin plans to climb about 12 times next month. Which choice will cost him the least?", "options": ["Buying a day pass every time he climbs", "Buying a Monthly Pass", "Buying a 10-Visit Card and two day passes", "Taking the Beginner Class 12 times"], "answer": 1, "explain": "依價格表計算：每次買單次票 450 × 12 = 5,400 元；10 次卡加兩張單次票 4,000 + 900 = 4,900 元；上 12 次新手課 600 × 12 = 7,200 元；月票不限次數只要 2,200 元，最便宜。因此 (A)(C)(D) 都比較貴，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about a 15-year-old girl who comes to the gym for the first time?", "options": ["She must climb with an adult at all times.", "She can get the student price on Saturdays.", "She is not allowed to rent climbing shoes.", "She needs a parent's signature on the safety form."], "answer": 3, "explain": "Before You Climb 段落說第一次來的人都要填安全同意書，且 Climbers under 18 need a parent to sign the form，15 歲未滿 18 歲，所以需要家長簽名。(A) 全程由成人陪同只限未滿 12 歲的兒童；(B) 學生價只限週一至週五；(C) 文中沒有限制青少年租鞋，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the Beginner Class?", "options": ["It is held every weekday evening.", "It costs NT$600 plus NT$100 for shoes.", "Up to eight people can join each class.", "People can sign up for it on the same day."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Beginner Class 段落說 Up to 8 people per class（每班最多 8 人）。(A) 課程只在週六上午和週日下午；(B) 600 元已包含入場和租鞋；(D) 必須至少提前一天上網預約，不能當天報名，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a157", "title": "Share the Moon: Mid-Autumn Gift Boxes", "title_zh": "共享明月：中秋禮盒", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Food"], "passage": "GOLDEN MOON BAKERY\nMid-Autumn Festival Gift Boxes\nHandmade in Tainan since 1978\n\nThis year, share the full moon with the people you love. All our pastries are baked fresh every morning, and we never add preservatives.\n\nOur Gift Boxes\n(1) Classic Box (6 pieces): NT$680\n2 lotus seed paste with salted egg yolk, 2 red bean, 2 mixed nut\n(2) Flaky Pastry Box (8 pieces): NT$880\n4 egg yolk pastries, 4 taro pastries\n(3) Snow Skin Box (6 mini pieces): NT$560\n2 mango, 2 green tea, 2 custard\n\nSnow skin mooncakes are not baked. They must be kept in the refrigerator and are best eaten within 5 days. The Classic Box and the Flaky Pastry Box can be kept at room temperature for up to 10 days.\n\nEarly Bird Offer\nOrder and pay before August 20 to get 10% off all gift boxes.\n\nPick-up and Delivery\n• Pick-up at our shop: September 1–24, 9 a.m.–8 p.m.\n• Delivery anywhere in Taiwan: NT$150 per address. Delivery to one address is free for orders of 10 boxes or more.\n• Snow Skin Boxes are for pick-up only.\n\nPlease Note\n• All our pastries contain eggs, milk and wheat. The Classic Box also contains nuts.\n• Orders cannot be changed or canceled after August 25.\n\nTo order, call (06) 222-8899 or visit our shop at No. 16, Section 2, Minzu Road, West Central District, Tainan.", "passage_zh": "金月餅舖\n中秋節禮盒\n自 1978 年起在台南手工製作\n\n今年，和你所愛的人一起共享滿月吧。我們所有的糕餅都是每天早上新鮮烘焙，絕不添加防腐劑。\n\n禮盒介紹\n(1) 經典禮盒（6 入）：新台幣 680 元\n蓮蓉蛋黃 2 個、紅豆 2 個、綜合堅果 2 個\n(2) 酥皮禮盒（8 入）：新台幣 880 元\n蛋黃酥 4 個、芋頭酥 4 個\n(3) 冰皮禮盒（迷你 6 入）：新台幣 560 元\n芒果 2 個、抹茶 2 個、卡士達 2 個\n\n冰皮月餅不經烘烤，必須冷藏，最好在 5 天內食用完畢。經典禮盒和酥皮禮盒可在室溫下保存最多 10 天。\n\n早鳥優惠\n8 月 20 日前訂購並付款，所有禮盒享九折優惠。\n\n取貨與宅配\n• 門市取貨：9 月 1 日至 24 日，上午 9 點至晚上 8 點。\n• 全台宅配：每個地址運費新台幣 150 元。訂購 10 盒以上，寄送到同一個地址免運費。\n• 冰皮禮盒僅限門市取貨。\n\n注意事項\n• 所有糕餅都含有蛋、牛奶和小麥，經典禮盒另含堅果。\n• 8 月 25 日之後，訂單恕不接受更改或取消。\n\n訂購請電 (06) 222-8899，或親臨門市：台南市中西區民族路二段 16 號。", "questions": [{"q": "Mrs. Wu orders three Classic Boxes and pays on August 15. She will pick them up at the shop herself. How much does she pay?", "options": ["NT$2,040", "NT$1,836", "NT$1,986", "NT$2,190"], "answer": 1, "explain": "經典禮盒每盒 680 元，三盒共 2,040 元；Early Bird Offer 說 8 月 20 日前訂購並付款享九折，8 月 15 日符合條件：2,040 × 0.9 = 1,836 元；她自己到門市取貨，不必付運費。(A) 沒有打折；(C) 多加了 150 元運費；(D) 沒打折又加運費，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Lily is allergic to nuts. She wants the bakery to send a gift box to her grandmother in Taichung. Which box should she choose?", "options": ["The Classic Box", "The Snow Skin Box", "The Flaky Pastry Box", "None of them, because all the boxes contain nuts"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Please Note 段落說只有經典禮盒另含堅果，所以 (A) 不適合；Pick-up and Delivery 段落說冰皮禮盒僅限門市取貨，無法宅配到台中，所以 (B) 不行；(D) 錯在並非所有禮盒都含堅果。酥皮禮盒不含堅果又可以宅配，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What is true about the Snow Skin Box?", "options": ["It can be kept at room temperature for 10 days.", "It has to be stored in a refrigerator.", "It can be delivered for NT$150.", "It is the most expensive box."], "answer": 1, "explain": "禮盒介紹之後的段落說冰皮月餅 must be kept in the refrigerator（必須冷藏）。(A) 室溫保存 10 天的是經典禮盒和酥皮禮盒；(C) 冰皮禮盒僅限門市取貨，不能宅配；(D) 冰皮禮盒 560 元是三種中最便宜的，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "A customer placed an order on August 10. On August 28, she wants to change it from five boxes to three. What will most likely happen?", "options": ["She can change it if she pays an extra NT$150.", "She will get 10% off the new order.", "She can change it by visiting the shop.", "The bakery will not accept the change."], "answer": 3, "explain": "Please Note 第二點說 Orders cannot be changed or canceled after August 25，8 月 28 日已超過期限，所以麵包店不會接受更改。(A) 150 元是運費，不是改單費；(B) 九折是早鳥優惠，與改單無關；(C) 期限後無論用什麼方式都不能更改，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"preservatives\" in the introduction probably means ______.", "options": ["things added to food to stop it from going bad", "special boxes used for gifts", "people who bake bread and cakes", "sweet fillings made from beans"], "answer": 0, "explain": "店名下方的開頭介紹（This year, share the full moon...）說糕餅每天早上新鮮烘焙，而且 we never add preservatives（絕不添加……），後文也提到冰皮月餅最好 5 天內吃完，可推知 preservatives 是「防腐劑」，也就是加進食物中防止變質的東西。(B) 禮盒、(C) 烘焙師傅、(D) 豆類甜餡都不是會「添加」到糕餅裡讓它保存更久的東西，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a158", "title": "Fifteen Minutes a Day: Choosing a Language App Plan", "title_zh": "每天十五分鐘：選擇語言學習 App 方案", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Education"], "passage": "LingoLeaf\nLearn a new language in 15 minutes a day\n\nStudy Japanese, Korean, Spanish, French, German or English with short lessons, fun games and real-life conversations. More than five million learners already use LingoLeaf!\n\nChoose Your Plan\n\nFREE\n• One new lesson per day\n• Ads after every lesson\n• Basic vocabulary review\n\nPLUS: NT$270 per month or NT$2,280 per year\n• Unlimited lessons with no ads\n• Download lessons and study offline\n• Speaking practice with an AI tutor that checks your pronunciation\n• Monthly progress reports\n\nFAMILY: NT$3,600 per year\n• All Plus features for up to 6 people\n• Each member gets a separate account and progress report\n\nStudent Discount\nStudents get 30% off the yearly Plus plan. Just sign up with your school email address.\n\nTry Plus Free for 14 Days\nYour free trial ends exactly 14 days after you sign up. If you do not want to pay, you must cancel at least 24 hours before the trial ends. If you don't, you will be charged for the plan you chose. You can cancel at any time in Settings > My Plan.\n\nDownload LingoLeaf from the App Store or Google Play.\nNeed help? Visit help.lingoleaf.com or email support@lingoleaf.com.", "passage_zh": "LingoLeaf\n每天 15 分鐘，學會一種新語言\n\n透過簡短的課程、有趣的遊戲和真實生活對話，學習日語、韓語、西班牙語、法語、德語或英語。已經有超過五百萬名學習者在使用 LingoLeaf！\n\n選擇您的方案\n\n免費版\n• 每天一堂新課程\n• 每堂課結束後會播放廣告\n• 基礎單字複習\n\nPLUS 版：每月新台幣 270 元，或每年新台幣 2,280 元\n• 課程無限制，而且沒有廣告\n• 可下載課程離線學習\n• 由 AI 家教陪你練習口說，並檢查發音\n• 每月學習進度報告\n\n家庭版：每年新台幣 3,600 元\n• 最多 6 人可使用 Plus 版所有功能\n• 每位成員都有各自的帳號和進度報告\n\n學生優惠\n學生購買 Plus 年繳方案享七折優惠，只要用學校的電子郵件信箱註冊即可。\n\nPlus 版 14 天免費試用\n免費試用期在您註冊後整整 14 天結束。如果不想付費，必須在試用期結束前至少 24 小時取消，否則系統會依您選擇的方案收費。您可以隨時在「設定 > 我的方案」中取消。\n\n請到 App Store 或 Google Play 下載 LingoLeaf。\n需要協助嗎？請上 help.lingoleaf.com，或寄電子郵件至 support@lingoleaf.com。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following can learners do ONLY if they pay?", "options": ["Take one new lesson every day", "Review basic vocabulary", "Download lessons and study without the Internet", "Choose one of six languages to study"], "answer": 2, "explain": "PLUS 方案列出 Download lessons and study offline（下載課程離線學習），免費版沒有這項功能，必須付費才能使用。(A)(B) 是免費版就有的功能；(D) 第二段說所有學習者都能從六種語言中選擇，並非付費才有，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How much can a learner save in one year by paying for Plus yearly instead of monthly?", "options": ["NT$270", "NT$960", "NT$3,240", "NT$2,280"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Plus 月繳每月 270 元，一年共 270 × 12 = 3,240 元；年繳只要 2,280 元，可省下 3,240 − 2,280 = 960 元。(A) 只是一個月的費用；(C) 是月繳一整年的總金額；(D) 是年繳的價格，都不是省下的金額，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "A senior high school student signs up for the yearly Plus plan with her school email address. How much will she pay?", "options": ["NT$1,596", "NT$684", "NT$2,280", "NT$2,268"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Student Discount 段落說學生購買 Plus 年繳方案享 30% off（七折），2,280 × 0.7 = 1,596 元。(B) 684 元只是折扣的金額；(C) 沒有扣除學生折扣；(D) 誤用月繳一年的總價 3,240 元去打七折，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Kevin starts his free trial at 8 p.m. on May 3. He decides that he does not want to pay. By when must he cancel?", "options": ["By 8 p.m. on May 17", "By 8 p.m. on May 4", "By 8 p.m. on May 18", "By 8 p.m. on May 16"], "answer": 3, "explain": "試用段落說試用期在註冊後整整 14 天結束：5 月 3 日晚上 8 點加 14 天，是 5 月 17 日晚上 8 點；又必須在結束前至少 24 小時取消，所以最晚要在 5 月 16 日晚上 8 點以前取消。(A) 是試用期結束的時間，已經太晚；(B) 誤以為是註冊後 24 小時內取消；(C) 已超過試用期，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"charged\" in the free trial section is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["given more power", "made to pay money", "allowed to study more", "sent a free gift"], "answer": 1, "explain": "If you do not want to pay, you must cancel...; If you don't, you will be charged for the plan you chose. 前文提到「不想付費就要取消」，所以沒取消就會被「收費」，charged 意思接近 made to pay money。(A) 是 charge 作「充電」時的聯想，與文意不合；(C)(D) 都不是不取消的後果，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a159", "title": "Go Camping Without Buying a Tent", "title_zh": "不用買帳篷也能去露營", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Travel"], "passage": "TRAILHEAD CAMPING RENTALS\nGo camping without buying everything!\nNo. 52, Guangming 6th Road, Zhubei City, Hsinchu County\n\nRental Packages (price for one weekend trip, Friday to Sunday)\nCouple Package: NT$900\n2-person tent, 2 sleeping bags, 2 sleeping mats\nFamily Package: NT$1,800\n4-person tent, sunshade, 4 sleeping bags, 4 sleeping mats, a folding table and 4 chairs\n\nAdd-ons\nGas stove with 2 gas cans: NT$250\nLED lantern: NT$100\nCooler box: NT$150\n\nHow It Works\n1. Book online at least 5 days before your trip.\n2. Pick up your gear after 3 p.m. on the first day of your trip. Bring your ID card, and pay the rental fee plus a NT$2,000 deposit for each package.\n3. Return everything by 12 noon on the day after your trip ends. You will get your deposit back if nothing is broken or missing.\n\nImportant\n• Late returns cost NT$300 per day.\n• Please dry the tent before you return it. If it comes back wet or very dirty, we will keep NT$500 of your deposit for cleaning.\n• Never use the gas stove inside a tent.\n\nWeekday Special\nCamping from Monday to Thursday? Get 20% off any package. (Add-ons are not included.)\n\nDelivery\nNo car? We can deliver your gear to most campsites in Hsinchu County for NT$400 each way.\n\nQuestions? Call 03-555-1203 or message us on LINE: @trailheadcamp", "passage_zh": "登山口露營用品出租\n不必樣樣都買，也能去露營！\n新竹縣竹北市光明六路 52 號\n\n租借套組（週末一趟的價格，週五至週日）\n雙人套組：新台幣 900 元\n雙人帳篷、睡袋 2 個、睡墊 2 張\n家庭套組：新台幣 1,800 元\n四人帳篷、遮陽天幕、睡袋 4 個、睡墊 4 張、折疊桌 1 張和椅子 4 張\n\n加購項目\n瓦斯爐（附 2 罐瓦斯）：新台幣 250 元\nLED 營燈：新台幣 100 元\n保冰箱：新台幣 150 元\n\n租借流程\n1. 請至少在出發前 5 天上網預約。\n2. 出發當天下午 3 點後取件。請攜帶身分證，並支付租金及每個套組新台幣 2,000 元的押金。\n3. 請在行程結束隔天的中午 12 點前歸還所有物品。若沒有任何損壞或遺失，押金將全額退還。\n\n重要事項\n• 逾期歸還每天收取新台幣 300 元。\n• 歸還前請先把帳篷晾乾。若歸還時帳篷潮濕或非常髒，我們會從押金中扣除 500 元清潔費。\n• 切勿在帳篷內使用瓦斯爐。\n\n平日特惠\n週一到週四去露營？任何套組都打八折（加購項目不適用）。\n\n送貨服務\n沒有車嗎？我們可以把裝備送到新竹縣大部分的露營區，單程運費新台幣 400 元。\n\n有任何問題，請撥 03-555-1203，或在 LINE 上傳訊息給我們：@trailheadcamp", "questions": [{"q": "The Lin family rents the Family Package with a gas stove and a lantern for a weekend trip. How much do they need to pay when they pick up the gear?", "options": ["NT$2,150", "NT$4,150", "NT$3,800", "NT$4,550"], "answer": 1, "explain": "家庭套組 1,800 元，加購瓦斯爐 250 元和營燈 100 元，租金共 2,150 元；How It Works 第 2 點說取件時要付租金 plus a NT$2,000 deposit（加上 2,000 元押金），所以共 4,150 元。(A) 漏算押金；(C) 漏算加購項目；(D) 多算了 400 元的單程送貨費，而且題目沒有提到要送貨，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Hao and his girlfriend rent the Couple Package for a trip from Tuesday to Thursday. How much is the rental fee for the package?", "options": ["NT$720", "NT$900", "NT$180", "NT$1,440"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Weekday Special 段落說週一到週四露營，任何套組都打八折：雙人套組 900 × 0.8 = 720 元。(B) 是沒有打折的週末價；(C) 只是折扣的金額；(D) 是家庭套組打八折的價格，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "It rained during the Lins' trip, and their tent is still wet on Sunday night. What should they do to get their whole deposit back?", "options": ["Return the tent as soon as possible on Sunday night", "Pay NT$300 for a late return", "Dry the tent and return it by noon on Monday", "Ask the shop to deliver a new tent to the campsite"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Important 第二點說帳篷若潮濕歸還，會從押金扣 500 元清潔費；How It Works 第 3 點說在行程結束隔天中午 12 點前歸還即可，週末行程就是週一中午前。所以應先晾乾帳篷，再於週一中午前歸還。(A) 帳篷仍濕會被扣清潔費；(B) 逾期費與帳篷潮濕無關，也不能拿回全部押金；(D) 文中沒有這項服務，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the advertisement, which of the following is NOT allowed?", "options": ["Returning the gear at 10 a.m. on Monday after a weekend trip", "Cooking with the gas stove inside the tent", "Booking a package one week before the trip", "Asking the shop to bring the gear to a campsite"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Important 第三點說 Never use the gas stove inside a tent（切勿在帳篷內使用瓦斯爐）。(A) 週末行程只要在行程結束隔天（週一）中午 12 點前歸還都可以；(C) 至少提前 5 天預約即可，一週前符合規定；(D) 店家提供新竹縣露營區的送貨服務，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"deposit\" in How It Works is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a fee for cleaning tents", "a place to store camping gear", "a discount for weekday trips", "money you pay first and get back later if nothing goes wrong"], "answer": 3, "explain": "How It Works 第 2、3 點說取件時要付 2,000 元 deposit，若歸還時沒有損壞或遺失，You will get your deposit back，可知 deposit 是「押金」，先付、事後可退還。(A) 清潔費是從押金中扣除的錢，不是押金本身；(B) 存放裝備的地方、(C) 平日折扣都不符合文意，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a160", "title": "Read Before You Rub: A Sunscreen Label", "title_zh": "塗抹前先看清楚：防曬乳標籤", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Health"], "passage": "SUNSHIELD SPORT\nSunscreen Lotion SPF 50+ PA++++\nWater Resistant (80 Minutes) | 50 ml\n\nMade for active days outdoors: school sports days, beach trips, cycling and hiking. It is light, not sticky, and does not leave your skin white.\n\nWhat the Labels Mean\nSPF 50+: Strong protection against UVB rays, which cause sunburn.\nPA++++: The highest level of protection against UVA rays, which cause early skin aging such as wrinkles and dark spots.\n\nDirections\n1. Shake well before use.\n2. Apply generously to all skin that will be in the sun 15 minutes before going outside. Don't forget your ears, neck and the tops of your feet!\n3. Reapply at least every 2 hours.\n4. Reapply after 80 minutes of swimming or sweating, and right after drying yourself with a towel.\n\nWarnings\n• For use on the skin only. Keep away from the eyes. If the lotion gets into your eyes, rinse them well with water.\n• Stop using the lotion and see a doctor if a rash appears.\n• For babies under 6 months old, ask a doctor before use.\n\nStorage\nKeep below 30°C. Do not leave the bottle in a hot car or in direct sunlight. Use within 12 months after opening.\n\nPrice\nSingle bottle (50 ml): NT$420\nTwin Pack (2 × 50 ml): NT$760\nAvailable at all Daily Health pharmacies and online at www.sunshieldsport.com.tw.", "passage_zh": "SUNSHIELD SPORT 運動防曬\n防曬乳液 SPF 50+ PA++++\n抗水（80 分鐘）｜50 毫升\n\n專為戶外活動的日子設計：學校運動會、海邊出遊、騎單車和健行都適用。質地輕盈、不黏膩，也不會讓皮膚泛白。\n\n標示說明\nSPF 50+：對會造成曬傷的 UVB 紫外線提供強效防護。\nPA++++：對 UVA 紫外線的最高等級防護；UVA 會造成皺紋、黑斑等皮膚提早老化現象。\n\n使用方法\n1. 使用前請充分搖勻。\n2. 出門前 15 分鐘，在所有會曬到太陽的皮膚上塗抹足量。別忘了耳朵、脖子和腳背！\n3. 至少每 2 小時補擦一次。\n4. 游泳或流汗 80 分鐘後請補擦；用毛巾擦乾身體後也請立即補擦。\n\n警告\n• 僅供皮膚外用。避免接觸眼睛，若不慎進入眼睛，請用清水充分沖洗。\n• 若出現疹子，請停止使用並就醫。\n• 未滿 6 個月的嬰兒使用前請先諮詢醫師。\n\n保存方式\n請存放於攝氏 30 度以下。請勿將瓶子留在高溫的車內或放在陽光直射處。開封後請於 12 個月內使用完畢。\n\n價格\n單瓶（50 毫升）：新台幣 420 元\n兩入組（50 毫升 × 2）：新台幣 760 元\n各家每日健康藥局皆有販售，也可上 www.sunshieldsport.com.tw 線上購買。", "questions": [{"q": "According to the label, what does \"PA++++\" tell users?", "options": ["The lotion protects strongly against sunburn.", "The lotion can be used for four hours without reapplying.", "The lotion gives the highest level of protection against UVA rays.", "The lotion is safe for babies of any age."], "answer": 2, "explain": "What the Labels Mean 段落說 PA++++ 是 The highest level of protection against UVA rays（對 UVA 的最高等級防護）。(A) 防曬傷是 SPF 50+ 的意思；(B) 使用方法說至少每 2 小時要補擦；(D) 警告說未滿 6 個月的嬰兒要先問醫師，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Ming puts on the sunscreen at 8:30 a.m. and starts swimming at 8:45 a.m. He keeps swimming all morning. By what time should he reapply it?", "options": ["10:05 a.m.", "10:30 a.m.", "9:50 a.m.", "10:45 a.m."], "answer": 0, "explain": "使用方法第 4 點說游泳 80 分鐘後要補擦。小明 8:45 開始游泳，8:45 加 80 分鐘是 10:05。(B) 是從 8:30 起算 2 小時，但游泳時要依 80 分鐘的規定；(C) 錯把起算時間當成擦防曬的 8:30；(D) 是從 8:45 起算 2 小時，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "A student finds a red rash on her arm after using the lotion. What should she do?", "options": ["Put more lotion on the rash", "Rinse her eyes with water", "Keep using it, but only every two hours", "Stop using it and see a doctor"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Warnings 第二點說 Stop using the lotion and see a doctor if a rash appears（出現疹子要停用並就醫）。(A)(C) 都是繼續使用，與警告相反；(B) 用清水沖洗是乳液進入眼睛時的處理方式，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would the maker most likely tell customers NOT to do?", "options": ["Use a bottle that was opened eight months ago", "Leave the bottle in a car parked in the summer sun", "Put the lotion on their ears and neck", "Shake the bottle before using it"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Storage 段落說請存放於攝氏 30 度以下，Do not leave the bottle in a hot car or in direct sunlight，夏天停在太陽下的車內溫度很高，所以廠商不希望顧客這麼做。(A) 開封後 12 個月內都可使用，8 個月仍在期限內；(C)(D) 都是使用方法中建議的步驟，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How much can a customer save by buying the Twin Pack instead of two single bottles?", "options": ["NT$40", "NT$80", "NT$340", "NT$420"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Price 段落中單瓶 420 元，買兩瓶要 420 × 2 = 840 元；兩入組只要 760 元，可省下 840 − 760 = 80 元。(A) 只是平均每瓶省下的金額；(C) 是用 760 減 420 得到的數字；(D) 是單瓶的價格，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a161", "title": "Everything Must Go: A Furniture Moving Sale", "title_zh": "全面出清：家具行搬遷大拍賣", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Money"], "passage": "OAK & PINE FURNITURE\nBIG MOVING SALE: Everything Must Go!\n\nAfter 15 years on Wenhua Road, we are moving to a bigger store. Instead of moving our old stock, we are selling it at the lowest prices we have ever offered.\n\nSale Dates: November 20 – December 5\nHours: 10 a.m. – 9 p.m. every day\n\nDiscounts\nSofas: 40% off\nBeds and mattresses: 35% off\nDining tables and chairs: 30% off\nDesks and office chairs: 25% off\nDisplay items (marked with a red tag): 50% off\n\nDelivery and Assembly\n• Free delivery within Chiayi City for orders of NT$10,000 or more (after discounts). Smaller orders: NT$800.\n• Delivery outside Chiayi City: please ask our staff.\n• Assembly service: NT$500 per item.\n\nPlease Note\n• Display items are sold as is. They may have small scratches or marks, and they cannot be returned or exchanged.\n• All other items can be returned within 7 days if you keep the receipt.\n• We accept cash, credit cards and LINE Pay.\n\nOur New Home\nFrom December 15, visit us at No. 300, Xinmin Road, West District, Chiayi City. The first 100 customers on opening day will receive a free cushion!\nTel: (05) 238-6655 | www.oakandpine.com.tw", "passage_zh": "橡木與松木家具行\n搬遷大拍賣：全面出清！\n\n我們在文化路經營了 15 年，現在即將搬到更大的店面。與其把舊庫存搬過去，我們決定以史上最低價出清。\n\n拍賣期間：11 月 20 日至 12 月 5 日\n營業時間：每天上午 10 點至晚上 9 點\n\n折扣\n沙發：六折\n床架與床墊：六五折\n餐桌椅：七折\n書桌與辦公椅：七五折\n展示品（貼有紅色標籤）：五折\n\n運送與組裝\n• 嘉義市區內，折扣後訂單滿新台幣 10,000 元免運費；未滿者運費 800 元。\n• 嘉義市以外地區的運送，請洽店員。\n• 組裝服務：每件新台幣 500 元。\n\n注意事項\n• 展示品以現況出售，可能有小刮痕或污漬，恕不退換。\n• 其他商品只要保留收據，7 天內皆可退貨。\n• 我們接受現金、信用卡和 LINE Pay。\n\n我們的新家\n12 月 15 日起，歡迎到嘉義市西區新民路 300 號找我們。開幕當天前 100 名顧客可獲贈抱枕一個！\n電話：(05) 238-6655｜www.oakandpine.com.tw", "questions": [{"q": "Why is Oak & Pine Furniture having this sale?", "options": ["It is celebrating its 15th birthday.", "It is closing down for good.", "It is moving to a bigger store.", "It wants to introduce a new kind of sofa."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段說 we are moving to a bigger store，與其搬運舊庫存，不如以最低價出清，所以拍賣的原因是要搬到更大的店。(A) 15 年是在文化路經營的時間，並非慶祝週年；(B) 店家 12 月 15 日會在新址開幕，不是永久歇業；(D) 文中沒有提到新款沙發，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Mr. Huang lives in Chiayi City. He buys a desk (usual price NT$8,000) and an office chair (usual price NT$4,000), and he wants them delivered. He does not need assembly. How much does he pay in total?", "options": ["NT$9,800", "NT$9,000", "NT$12,800", "NT$10,800"], "answer": 0, "explain": "書桌與辦公椅打七五折：(8,000 + 4,000) × 0.75 = 9,000 元；運送規定是折扣後滿 10,000 元才免運，9,000 元未達標準，要加運費 800 元，共 9,800 元。(B) 漏算運費；(C) 沒有打折；(D) 多加了兩件的組裝費 2 × 500 = 1,000 元，但黃先生不需要組裝，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "A sofa with a red tag usually costs NT$30,000. How much does it cost during the sale?", "options": ["NT$18,000", "NT$12,000", "NT$19,500", "NT$15,000"], "answer": 3, "explain": "貼紅色標籤的是展示品，Discounts 段落說展示品打五折（50% off）：30,000 × 0.5 = 15,000 元。(A) 是一般沙發打六折的價格，但這張沙發是紅標展示品；(B) 只是一般沙發的折扣金額；(C) 是打六五折的價格，那是床架與床墊的折扣，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the red-tag items?", "options": ["They are brand-new and have never been used.", "They can be returned within 7 days.", "They cannot be returned, even with a receipt.", "They will be moved to the new store."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Please Note 第一點說展示品 cannot be returned or exchanged（恕不退換），而第二點的「7 天內憑收據退貨」只適用於 All other items（其他商品），所以紅標商品即使有收據也不能退。(A) 展示品可能有小刮痕或污漬，不是全新；(B) 是其他商品的規定；(D) 店家就是要出清舊庫存，不打算搬走，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"sold as is\" in the Please Note section is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["sold with a free gift", "sold in its present condition, with no promise to fix it", "sold only to members of the store", "sold at the usual price"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Display items are sold as is. 後面接著說展示品 may have small scratches or marks（可能有小刮痕或污漬），而且不能退換，可知 as is 是「依現況」出售，店家不會修好或保證。(A) 贈品是開幕當天送抱枕，與展示品無關；(C) 文中沒有會員限定；(D) 展示品打五折，不是原價，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a162", "title": "Fill Your Buckets: A Water Outage Notice", "title_zh": "先儲好水：社區停水通知", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Notice", "Life"], "passage": "NOTICE TO ALL RESIDENTS\nSunshine Garden Apartments, Buildings A and B\nWater Supply Interruption\n\nDear Residents,\nThe water tanks on the roof and in the basement of our buildings will be cleaned and checked. This must be done every six months to keep our water safe and clean. During the work, there will be no running water in any unit in Buildings A and B.\n\nDate: Thursday, November 12\nTime: 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.\n\nBefore the work starts:\n• Fill some buckets or bottles with water for drinking, cooking and flushing toilets.\n• Turn off your water heater and washing machine. They may be damaged if they run without water.\n• Close all taps, or water may suddenly come out when the supply returns.\n\nAfter the work:\nWhen the water comes back, it may look a little yellow or cloudy. This is normal. Run the cold water tap in your kitchen for about three minutes until the water is clear. Do not drink it before then.\n\nPlease note:\nThe shower rooms in the gym on the first floor of Building A will also be closed all day. The parking garage and the elevators will not be affected.\n\nIf the work finishes early, we will post a message in the building's LINE group. If you have any questions, please contact the Management Office.\n\nManagement Office, Sunshine Garden Apartments\nTel: (02) 2765-4321 (8 a.m.–8 p.m.)\nPosted: November 2", "passage_zh": "全體住戶公告\n陽光花園社區 A、B 棟\n停水通知\n\n親愛的住戶您好：\n本社區屋頂及地下室的水塔將進行清洗與檢查。這項工作每六個月必須進行一次，以確保我們的用水安全乾淨。施工期間，A、B 兩棟所有住戶都將停止供水。\n\n日期：11 月 12 日（星期四）\n時間：上午 9:00 至下午 5:00\n\n施工開始前：\n• 請用水桶或瓶子預先儲水，供飲用、煮飯及沖馬桶使用。\n• 請關閉熱水器和洗衣機，機器在沒有水的狀態下運轉可能會損壞。\n• 請關緊所有水龍頭，否則恢復供水時水可能會突然流出來。\n\n施工結束後：\n恢復供水時，水可能看起來有點黃或混濁，這是正常現象。請打開廚房的冷水水龍頭，讓水流約三分鐘，直到水變清澈為止。在那之前請勿飲用。\n\n請注意：\nA 棟一樓健身房的淋浴間當天也將全天關閉。停車場和電梯則不受影響。\n\n若工程提早完成，我們會在社區的 LINE 群組發布訊息。如有任何問題，請洽管理室。\n\n陽光花園社區管理室\n電話：(02) 2765-4321（上午 8 點至晚上 8 點）\n公告日期：11 月 2 日", "questions": [{"q": "Why will the water supply be stopped?", "options": ["A water pipe under the street is broken.", "The buildings' water tanks will be cleaned and checked.", "The city is trying to save water during a dry season.", "New water heaters are being put into every unit."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段說屋頂和地下室的水塔 will be cleaned and checked（將進行清洗與檢查），而且每六個月要做一次以確保水質安全。(A) 文中沒有提到街上的水管破裂；(C) 沒有提到乾旱或節約用水；(D) 熱水器只出現在「施工前請關閉熱水器」的提醒中，並不是停水的原因，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "For how many hours will there be no water on November 12?", "options": ["Five hours", "Seven hours", "Eight hours", "Ten hours"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段寫明停水時間為上午 9:00 至下午 5:00，從 9 點到 17 點共 8 小時。(A) 是誤把結束時間「5 點」當成時數；(B)(D) 時數計算錯誤，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What should residents do before 9:00 a.m. on November 12?", "options": ["Turn off their water heaters.", "Run the kitchen tap for three minutes.", "Move their cars out of the parking garage.", "Call the Management Office to sign up."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段 Before the work starts 列出施工前要做的事，其中一項是關閉熱水器和洗衣機，以免機器在無水狀態下運轉而損壞。(B) 讓冷水流三分鐘是第五段「恢復供水後」才要做的事；(C) 第六段說停車場不受影響，不必移車；(D) 文中沒有要求住戶打電話登記，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Mr. Lin gets home at 6 p.m. on November 12 and finds that the water from his kitchen tap looks cloudy. What should he do?", "options": ["Call the Management Office right away.", "Boil the water and drink it at once.", "Wait until the next morning before using any water.", "Let the cold water run for a few minutes until it is clear."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段 After the work 說恢復供水時水可能有點黃或混濁，This is normal（這是正常的），只要打開廚房冷水龍頭讓水流約三分鐘，直到水變清澈即可。(A) 這是正常現象，不需要立刻聯絡管理室；(B) 文中說水變清澈前不要喝，也沒有提到煮沸；(C) 文中沒有要求等到隔天，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"affected\" in the \"Please note\" part is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["cleaned", "influenced", "opened", "repaired"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Please note 部分先說健身房淋浴間當天會關閉，接著說 The parking garage and the elevators will not be affected，意思是停車場和電梯「不會受到停水影響」，affected 意為「受影響的」，與 influenced 最接近。(A) cleaned「被清洗」、(C) opened「被打開」、(D) repaired「被修理」都不符合文意，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a163", "title": "Drop, Cover, Hold On: A School Earthquake Drill", "title_zh": "趴下、掩護、穩住：校園地震演練", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Notice", "Instructions"], "passage": "HSIN-MING SENIOR HIGH SCHOOL\nNotice: Campus Earthquake Drill\n\nSeptember 21 is Taiwan's National Disaster Prevention Day. It reminds us of the 921 Earthquake of 1999, which killed more than 2,400 people. To help everyone stay safe in a real earthquake, our school will hold a drill for all students and staff.\n\nDate: Monday, September 21\nTime: 9:21 a.m. (during the second class period)\n\nWhat Will Happen\n1. At 9:21, a long warning sound will be played through the school speakers.\n2. Stay where you are and follow three steps: DROP to the floor, take COVER under your desk, and HOLD ON to the desk legs until the shaking stops.\n3. After about one minute, a teacher will announce that it is time to leave the buildings. Walk quickly to the sports field by the route shown on the map beside your classroom door. Protect your head with your schoolbag or a book.\n4. Line up with your class at the sign with your class number. Class leaders will count the students and report to the homeroom teacher.\n\nRemember\nDo not run, push or talk. Never use the elevators. Do not go back for your belongings.\n\nStudents who are in the library, labs or bathrooms at 9:21 should follow the same steps there, then go to the field and find their own class.\n\nStudents who cannot take part because of an injury must tell their homeroom teacher by Friday, September 18. They will wait in the Health Center during the drill.\n\nThe drill will end at about 9:45 a.m., and the second class period will continue as usual.\nOffice of Student Affairs", "passage_zh": "新明高級中學\n公告：校園地震演練\n\n9 月 21 日是台灣的國家防災日，提醒我們記住 1999 年造成超過 2,400 人罹難的九二一大地震。為了幫助大家在真正發生地震時保護自身安全，本校將舉行全校師生地震演練。\n\n日期：9 月 21 日（星期一）\n時間：上午 9:21（第二節課期間）\n\n演練流程\n1. 9:21 時，校園廣播會播放一段長長的地震警報聲。\n2. 留在原地，並依照三個步驟：「趴下」到地上、躲在桌子下「掩護」、抓緊桌腳「穩住」，直到搖晃停止。\n3. 大約一分鐘後，老師會廣播宣布離開建築物。請依照教室門邊地圖上標示的路線，快步走到操場，並用書包或書本保護頭部。\n4. 到操場後，在寫有自己班級號碼的牌子前與全班一起排隊。班長清點人數後向導師回報。\n\n請記住\n不奔跑、不推擠、不說話。絕對不要搭乘電梯。不要回頭拿個人物品。\n\n9:21 時若人在圖書館、實驗室或廁所，請在原地依照相同步驟進行，然後到操場找到自己的班級。\n\n因受傷而無法參加演練的同學，必須在 9 月 18 日（星期五）前告知導師。演練期間請在健康中心等候。\n\n演練約於上午 9:45 結束，之後第二節課照常進行。\n學生事務處", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this notice?", "options": ["To explain why earthquakes happen so often in Taiwan", "To tell students when the drill will be held and what to do", "To ask students to stay home on National Disaster Prevention Day", "To report the damage caused by a recent earthquake"], "answer": 1, "explain": "公告列出演練的日期與時間（第三段），並在 What Will Happen、Remember 等段落說明演練時該怎麼做，目的是告訴學生演練何時舉行以及應採取的行動。(A) 文中沒有解釋地震成因；(C) 演練在上課時間舉行，學生不是待在家；(D) 九二一大地震發生於 1999 年，只是說明防災日的由來，並非報導最近的災情，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the notice, what should students do first when they hear the warning sound?", "options": ["Run to the sports field as fast as they can", "Take the elevator down to the first floor", "Drop to the floor, get under their desks and hold on", "Go back to their classroom to get their schoolbags"], "answer": 2, "explain": "What Will Happen 第 2 點說聽到警報後要 Stay where you are，並依照 DROP、COVER、HOLD ON 三個步驟，趴下、躲到桌下、抓緊桌腳。(A) 要等約一分鐘後老師廣播才離開，而且 Remember 段說不可奔跑；(B) 絕對不可搭電梯；(D) 不可回頭拿個人物品，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Jia-hui is in the chemistry lab when the warning sound starts. What should she do?", "options": ["Go back to her classroom first, and then follow her class to the field.", "Wait in the lab until her homeroom teacher comes to find her.", "Go to the Health Center and stay there during the drill.", "Follow the same steps in the lab, and then join her class on the field."], "answer": 3, "explain": "倒數第三段說 9:21 時在圖書館、實驗室或廁所的學生，要在原地依照相同步驟進行，then go to the field and find their own class（再到操場找自己的班級）。(A) 不必先回教室；(B) 文中沒有說要在實驗室等導師；(C) 待在健康中心的是因受傷無法參加演練的學生，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Kai-wen hurt his leg playing basketball and cannot take part in the drill. What should he do?", "options": ["Tell his homeroom teacher by September 18.", "Ask his class leader to count him on the field.", "Bring a doctor's note on the day of the drill.", "Stay alone in his classroom during the drill."], "answer": 0, "explain": "倒數第二段說因受傷無法參加的學生 must tell their homeroom teacher by Friday, September 18（必須在 9 月 18 日前告知導師），演練時在健康中心等候。(B) 班長清點的是到操場的同學；(C) 文中沒有提到醫生證明；(D) 他應該在健康中心等候，而不是獨自待在教室，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"belongings\" in the \"Remember\" part is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["classmates", "things that you own", "safe places", "school rules"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Remember 部分說 Do not go back for your belongings，前文也提到可用 schoolbag or a book 保護頭部，可知 belongings 指書包等「個人物品、自己擁有的東西」，與 things that you own 意思最接近。(A) classmates「同學」、(C) safe places「安全的地方」、(D) school rules「校規」都不符合「回去拿」的文意，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a164", "title": "Is This Yours? Lost and Found Last Call", "title_zh": "這是你的嗎？失物招領最後通知", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Notice", "Education"], "passage": "LOST AND FOUND: LAST CALL!\nHua-Kang Vocational High School, Office of General Affairs\n\nOur lost-and-found shelves are full! Since the start of the semester, we have collected more than 150 items, including:\n• 37 water bottles\n• 26 jackets and school sweaters\n• 19 umbrellas\n• 12 calculators\n• 8 pairs of earphones\n• lunch boxes, pencil cases, sports shoes and much more\n\nWhen and Where to Claim Your Things\nDates: Monday, December 7 to Friday, December 18\nTime: lunch break (12:10–12:50) and after school (4:10–5:00)\nPlace: Room 105, next to the school store\n\nHow to Claim\nBring your student ID card and describe the item (its color, its brand and where you lost it). For phones, wallets and keys, you must also prove that the item is yours, for example by unlocking the phone.\n\nWhat Happens Next?\nItems that are not claimed by December 18 will be handled as follows:\n• Clothes, umbrellas and bottles in good condition will be washed and donated to the Rainbow Children's Home.\n• Calculators and stationery will be put on the student council's free \"Share Shelf.\"\n• Broken or very dirty items will be recycled or thrown away.\n\nPhones, wallets and keys will not be donated. They will be kept in the office for one more month and then handed over to the local police station.\n\nTip: Write your name and class on your things! Last semester, 90% of the items with names on them were returned to their owners within two days.\n\nQuestions? Contact Ms. Huang at ext. 213.", "passage_zh": "失物招領：最後通知！\n華岡高級職業學校 總務處\n\n我們的失物招領架已經滿了！本學期開學以來，我們已經收集了超過 150 件物品，包括：\n• 水壺 37 個\n• 外套及學校毛衣 26 件\n• 雨傘 19 把\n• 計算機 12 台\n• 耳機 8 副\n• 便當盒、鉛筆盒、運動鞋等等\n\n認領時間與地點\n日期：12 月 7 日（星期一）至 12 月 18 日（星期五）\n時間：午休時間（12:10–12:50）及放學後（4:10–5:00）\n地點：105 室，福利社旁\n\n認領方式\n請攜帶學生證，並說出物品的特徵（顏色、品牌及遺失地點）。若是手機、錢包和鑰匙，還必須證明物品是你的，例如當場解鎖手機。\n\n之後會怎麼處理？\n12 月 18 日前無人認領的物品將依下列方式處理：\n• 狀況良好的衣物、雨傘和水壺會清洗後捐贈給彩虹兒童之家。\n• 計算機和文具會放到學生會的免費「分享架」上。\n• 損壞或非常髒的物品將回收或丟棄。\n\n手機、錢包和鑰匙不會捐出。這些物品會在辦公室再保管一個月，之後送交當地派出所。\n\n小提醒：請在你的物品上寫上姓名和班級！上學期，有寫名字的物品中有 90% 在兩天內就回到了主人手上。\n\n有任何問題，請洽黃小姐，分機 213。", "questions": [{"q": "According to the notice, which kind of item was found most often this semester?", "options": ["Umbrellas", "Jackets and school sweaters", "Water bottles", "Calculators"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段的清單中，水壺有 37 個，數量最多。(A) 雨傘 19 把；(B) 外套及毛衣 26 件；(D) 計算機 12 台，都比水壺少，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Ming-jie lost his umbrella in October. What is the latest time he can pick it up at Room 105?", "options": ["12:50 p.m. on Friday, December 18", "5:00 p.m. on Monday, December 7", "4:10 p.m. on Friday, December 18", "5:00 p.m. on Friday, December 18"], "answer": 3, "explain": "When and Where 段落說認領日期到 12 月 18 日（星期五）為止，放學後的認領時間是 4:10–5:00，所以最晚是 12 月 18 日下午 5:00。(A) 12:50 是午休時段的結束時間，當天放學後還能領；(B) 12 月 7 日是第一天，不是最後一天；(C) 4:10 是放學後時段的開始時間，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Pei-yu thinks one of the phones in the office is hers. Besides showing her student ID card, what must she do to get it back?", "options": ["Pay a small fee to the office.", "Show that the phone is hers, for example by unlocking it.", "Come back with one of her parents.", "Go to the local police station to ask for it."], "answer": 1, "explain": "How to Claim 段落說手機、錢包和鑰匙除了學生證和描述之外，you must also prove that the item is yours, for example by unlocking the phone（還必須證明是自己的，例如解鎖手機）。(A)(C) 文中沒有提到付費或家長陪同；(D) 手機要再保管一個月後才會送到派出所，現在應到 105 室認領，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What will happen to a calculator that no one claims by December 18?", "options": ["It will be donated to the Rainbow Children's Home.", "It will be handed over to the local police station.", "It will be put on a shelf where students can take it for free.", "It will be kept in the office for one more month."], "answer": 2, "explain": "What Happens Next? 段落說 Calculators and stationery will be put on the student council's free \"Share Shelf\"（計算機和文具會放到學生會的免費分享架）。(A) 捐給兒童之家的是衣物、雨傘和水壺；(B)(D) 再保管一個月後送交派出所的是手機、錢包和鑰匙，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can we learn from the tip at the end of the notice?", "options": ["Items with names on them usually find their owners quickly.", "Students will be punished if they do not write their names on their things.", "Most lost items last semester were never returned.", "Only 10% of students wrote their names on their things last semester."], "answer": 0, "explain": "Tip 段落說上學期有寫名字的物品中，90% 在兩天內就還給了主人，可知寫上名字的物品通常很快就能找到主人。(B) 文中只是建議，沒有提到處罰；(C) 文中沒有提到大部分失物都沒被領回；(D) 90% 指的是「有名字的物品」被歸還的比例，不能推出只有 10% 的學生寫名字，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a165", "title": "Rules for Happy Tails: A Dog Park Sign", "title_zh": "快樂狗狗守則：狗公園告示牌", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Notice", "Life"], "passage": "WELCOME TO RIVERBEND DOG PARK\nRun, play and make new friends, on four legs or two!\n\nOpening Hours\nTuesday to Sunday: 6:00 a.m.–9:00 p.m.\nClosed on Mondays for cleaning and grass care.\nThe park may also close after heavy rain. Please check our Facebook page before you come.\n\nPlay Areas\nBig Dog Area: for dogs over 15 kg\nSmall Dog Area: for dogs 15 kg and under\nPuppy Corner: for puppies 4 to 6 months old (open 8:00–10:00 a.m. only)\n\nPark Rules\n1. All dogs must be microchipped and have an up-to-date rabies vaccination. Staff may ask to see your dog's records.\n2. Puppies under 4 months old and sick dogs are not allowed.\n3. Keep your dog on a leash until you are inside the double gate. Then you may take the leash off.\n4. Pick up after your dog. Free bags and bins are at every gate.\n5. Each adult may bring no more than two dogs.\n6. Children under 12 must stay with an adult at all times.\n7. No dog food or toys in the play areas, because they can start fights. You may feed your dog in the picnic area outside the fence.\n8. If your dog becomes aggressive, please take it out of the park right away.\n\nWater stations are next to each gate. Please do not let your dog swim in the pond.\n\nOwners who break these rules will be asked to leave.\nPark Office: (04) 2233-8899", "passage_zh": "歡迎來到河灣狗狗公園\n不管是四條腿還是兩條腿，都來這裡奔跑、玩耍、交新朋友吧！\n\n開放時間\n週二至週日：上午 6:00 至晚上 9:00\n每週一休園，進行清潔及草地養護。\n大雨過後公園也可能關閉，來之前請先查看我們的臉書粉絲專頁。\n\n遊戲區\n大型犬區：體重超過 15 公斤的狗狗\n小型犬區：體重 15 公斤以下（含）的狗狗\n幼犬區：4 到 6 個月大的幼犬（僅於上午 8:00–10:00 開放）\n\n公園規則\n1. 所有狗狗都必須植入晶片，並已按時施打狂犬病疫苗。工作人員可能會要求查看狗狗的紀錄。\n2. 未滿 4 個月的幼犬及生病的狗狗不得入園。\n3. 進入雙層門之前，狗狗必須繫上牽繩，進入後才可解開牽繩。\n4. 請清理狗狗的排泄物。每個入口都有免費的袋子和垃圾桶。\n5. 每位成人最多只能帶兩隻狗。\n6. 未滿 12 歲的兒童必須全程由成人陪同。\n7. 遊戲區內禁止攜帶狗食或玩具，因為可能引發打架。可以在圍欄外的野餐區餵食狗狗。\n8. 如果您的狗狗變得具有攻擊性，請立即帶牠離開公園。\n\n每個入口旁都有飲水站。請勿讓狗狗在池塘裡游泳。\n\n違反規則的飼主將被請離公園。\n公園管理處：(04) 2233-8899", "questions": [{"q": "Mrs. Tsai's dog weighs 22 kilograms. Where should it play?", "options": ["In the Small Dog Area", "In the Big Dog Area", "In the Puppy Corner", "In the picnic area"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Play Areas 段落說 Big Dog Area 是給 dogs over 15 kg（超過 15 公斤的狗），22 公斤的狗應到大型犬區。(A) 小型犬區只限 15 公斤以下；(C) 幼犬區只限 4 到 6 個月大的幼犬；(D) 野餐區在圍欄外，是餵食的地方，不是遊戲區，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Mr. Chen wants to bring his five-month-old puppy to the Puppy Corner. When can he go?", "options": ["Saturday at 3:00 p.m.", "Monday at 9:00 a.m.", "Sunday at 7:00 p.m.", "Wednesday at 9:00 a.m."], "answer": 3, "explain": "Play Areas 段落說幼犬區只在上午 8:00–10:00 開放，Opening Hours 段落又說公園週一休園，所以只有週二到週日的上午 8 點到 10 點可以去，星期三上午 9 點符合。(A)(C) 時間不在幼犬區開放時段內；(B) 星期一公園休園，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Lily, an adult, has three dogs. What should she do if she wants to bring all of them to the park?", "options": ["Come with another adult.", "Keep one of the dogs on a leash the whole time.", "Visit the park only in the early morning.", "Leave one dog in the picnic area."], "answer": 0, "explain": "規則第 5 條說 Each adult may bring no more than two dogs（每位成人最多帶兩隻狗），所以要帶三隻狗，就需要另一位成人同行。(B) 繫著牽繩並不能讓一位成人多帶一隻狗；(C) 每人最多帶兩隻狗的規定與到訪時段無關；(D) 文中沒有說可以把狗留在野餐區，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is allowed at Riverbend Dog Park?", "options": ["Throwing a ball for your dog in the Big Dog Area", "Letting your dog cool off in the pond", "Feeding your dog in the picnic area outside the fence", "Bringing a three-month-old puppy to the Puppy Corner"], "answer": 2, "explain": "規則第 7 條說遊戲區禁止狗食和玩具，但 You may feed your dog in the picnic area outside the fence（可以在圍欄外的野餐區餵狗）。(A) 球是玩具，遊戲區內禁止；(B) 倒數第二段說不可讓狗在池塘游泳；(D) 規則第 2 條說未滿 4 個月的幼犬不得入園，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"aggressive\" in Rule 8 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["ready to fight or attack", "tired and sleepy", "hungry and thirsty", "sick and weak"], "answer": 0, "explain": "規則第 7 條提到狗食和玩具 can start fights（可能引發打架），第 8 條接著說狗若變得 aggressive 就要立刻帶離公園，可知 aggressive 指「有攻擊性的、想打架的」，與 ready to fight or attack 最接近。(B)「疲倦想睡」、(C)「又餓又渴」、(D)「生病虛弱」都不是需要立刻離開以免傷害其他狗的情況，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a166", "title": "A Second Life for School Uniforms", "title_zh": "讓制服重獲新生：二手制服義賣", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Notice", "Money"], "passage": "CHING-FENG SENIOR HIGH SCHOOL STUDENT COUNCIL\nUniform Swap & Sale: Give Your Old Uniform a Second Life!\n\nGrowing fast? Graduating soon? Don't throw away your old uniforms! The Student Council is collecting used school uniforms and selling them at low prices. All the money raised will go to the school's emergency fund for students in need.\n\nPART 1: Donate (March 2–13)\nBring clean uniforms to the Student Council Office (Room 210) during lunch break.\nWe accept: summer and winter shirts, skirts, trousers, sports clothes and school jackets.\nWe do not accept: items with holes, stains or broken zippers.\nFor every three items you donate, you will get one coupon for the sale!\n\nPART 2: Sale (Thursday, March 19 and Friday, March 20)\nTime: 12:10–1:00 p.m.\nPlace: Student Activity Center, 1F\nPrices:\n• Shirts and sports T-shirts: NT$50\n• Skirts, trousers and sports pants: NT$80\n• School jackets: NT$150 (A new one costs NT$750 at the school store!)\nOne coupon = one free shirt or sports T-shirt\n\nRules\n• Each student may buy up to five items.\n• Cash only. Please bring small bills.\n• No returns or exchanges, but you may try clothes on in Room 110.\n• Items left after the sale will be given free to students who need them.\n\nQuestions? Send us a message on Instagram (@cfhs_council) or visit Room 210.", "passage_zh": "青峰高中學生會\n制服交換暨義賣：讓你的舊制服重獲新生！\n\n長得太快？快畢業了？別把舊制服丟掉！學生會正在收集二手制服，並以低價出售。所得款項將全數捐入學校的急難救助基金，幫助有需要的同學。\n\n第一部分：捐贈（3 月 2 日至 13 日）\n請在午休時間將乾淨的制服送到學生會辦公室（210 室）。\n接受：夏季及冬季制服上衣、裙子、長褲、運動服和學校外套。\n不接受：有破洞、污漬或拉鍊損壞的衣物。\n每捐贈三件，即可獲得一張義賣兌換券！\n\n第二部分：義賣（3 月 19 日星期四及 3 月 20 日星期五）\n時間：中午 12:10 至下午 1:00\n地點：學生活動中心一樓\n價格：\n• 制服上衣及運動 T 恤：50 元\n• 裙子、長褲及運動褲：80 元\n• 學校外套：150 元（在福利社買新的要 750 元！）\n一張兌換券＝免費兌換一件制服上衣或運動 T 恤\n\n規則\n• 每位同學最多可購買五件。\n• 僅收現金，請盡量準備小額鈔票。\n• 恕不退換，但可以在 110 室試穿。\n• 義賣結束後剩下的衣物，將免費送給有需要的同學。\n\n有問題嗎？歡迎在 Instagram（@cfhs_council）傳訊息給我們，或到 210 室詢問。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the Uniform Swap & Sale?", "options": ["To raise money for students in need by selling used uniforms", "To sell new uniforms to first-year students at lower prices", "To collect old uniforms and make them into new clothes", "To choose a new design for the school uniform"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段說學生會收集二手制服並低價出售，All the money raised will go to the school's emergency fund for students in need（所得全數捐入急難救助基金，幫助有需要的同學）。(B) 賣的是二手制服，不是新制服；(C) 衣物是直接再賣出，不是重新做成新衣；(D) 文中沒有提到更換制服設計，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Ting donated six items in Part 1. At the sale, she gets two shirts, one pair of trousers and one school jacket, and she uses all of her coupons. How much does she pay?", "options": ["NT$330", "NT$280", "NT$230", "NT$750"], "answer": 2, "explain": "PART 1 說每捐三件可得一張兌換券，捐六件可得兩張；PART 2 說一張券可免費換一件上衣。兩件上衣用券免費，只需付長褲 80 元和外套 150 元，共 230 元。(A) 是完全沒用券的價格（50×2＋80＋150＝330）；(B) 只用了一張券；(D) 是福利社新外套的價格，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which item will the Student Council NOT accept?", "options": ["A clean winter shirt", "A pair of sports pants", "A school jacket that is a little old", "A skirt with a broken zipper"], "answer": 3, "explain": "PART 1 寫明 We do not accept: items with holes, stains or broken zippers（不接受有破洞、污漬或拉鍊損壞的衣物），拉鍊壞掉的裙子不會被接受。(A)(B)(C) 冬季上衣、運動服和學校外套都在接受清單上，舊一點但乾淨完整的衣物也可以捐，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How much money can a student save by buying a used school jacket at the sale instead of a new one at the school store?", "options": ["NT$150", "NT$600", "NT$750", "NT$900"], "answer": 1, "explain": "PART 2 價格表說二手學校外套 150 元，而福利社的新外套要 750 元，可省下 750－150＝600 元。(A) 是二手外套的售價；(C) 是新外套的價格；(D) 是把兩個價格相加，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"raised\" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["lifted up", "spent", "collected", "lost"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段 All the money raised will go to the school's emergency fund，指義賣「募得、籌到」的錢會捐入急難救助基金，raise money 是「募款」，與 collected 意思最接近。(A) lifted up 是 raise「舉起」的另一個意思，不合文意；(B) spent「花掉」、(D) lost「遺失」都與文意不符，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a167", "title": "No Trucks on Day One: A Holiday Garbage Schedule", "title_zh": "初一停收：春節垃圾清運公告", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Notice", "Environment"], "passage": "GREENHILL DISTRICT CLEANING TEAM\nLunar New Year Garbage Collection Schedule\n\nDuring the Lunar New Year holiday, garbage trucks will follow the special schedule below. Please plan ahead, and never leave garbage bags on the street or at the truck stops.\n\nFeb. 4 (Thu.): Normal collection\nFeb. 5 (Fri., New Year's Eve): Extra afternoon collection (2:00–4:00 p.m.) and normal evening collection\nFeb. 6 (Sat., Day 1): No collection\nFeb. 7 (Sun., Day 2): No collection\nFeb. 8 (Mon., Day 3): Evening collection, general garbage and food waste only\nFeb. 9 (Tue., Day 4): Normal collection begins again\n\nPlease Note\n• Recycling (paper, plastic, glass and cans) will not be collected on Day 3. Please keep it at home until Day 4.\n• Large items such as old sofas, mattresses and cabinets: Call us to book a free pickup at least three days in advance. The last pickup day before the holiday is February 3.\n• Red envelopes, gift boxes and wrapping paper can be recycled as paper, but please remove plastic windows and ribbons first.\n• Ashes from burning paper money must be completely cool before you put them in a general garbage bag.\n\nTip: Many families do a big house cleaning just before the New Year, so there may be long lines at the evening trucks on New Year's Eve. Taking your garbage out at the afternoon collection can save you a long wait.\n\nCleaning Team Hotline: (03) 335-6789\n8:30 a.m.–5:30 p.m. every day (closed Feb. 5–8)", "passage_zh": "綠丘區清潔隊\n春節垃圾清運時間表\n\n春節連假期間，垃圾車將依照下列特別時間表收運。請事先做好安排，絕對不要把垃圾袋丟在路邊或垃圾車停靠點。\n\n2 月 4 日（四）：正常收運\n2 月 5 日（五，除夕）：加開下午收運（下午 2:00–4:00），晚上照常收運\n2 月 6 日（六，初一）：停收\n2 月 7 日（日，初二）：停收\n2 月 8 日（一，初三）：晚上收運，僅收一般垃圾及廚餘\n2 月 9 日（二，初四）：恢復正常收運\n\n注意事項\n• 初三不收資源回收物（紙類、塑膠、玻璃及鐵鋁罐），請先留在家中，等初四再拿出來。\n• 舊沙發、床墊、櫃子等大型家具：請至少提前三天打電話預約免費清運。連假前最後一個清運日為 2 月 3 日。\n• 紅包袋、禮盒和包裝紙可當作紙類回收，但請先拆除塑膠窗和緞帶。\n• 燒完紙錢的灰燼必須完全冷卻後，才能裝進一般垃圾袋。\n\n小提醒：許多家庭會在過年前大掃除，因此除夕晚上的垃圾車可能大排長龍。利用下午加開的時段倒垃圾，可以省下長時間的等待。\n\n清潔隊服務專線：(03) 335-6789\n每天上午 8:30 至下午 5:30（2 月 5 日至 8 日暫停服務）", "questions": [{"q": "On which days will there be no garbage collection at all?", "options": ["February 4 and 5", "February 5 and 6", "February 6 and 7", "February 8 and 9"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段的時間表中，2 月 6 日（初一）和 2 月 7 日（初二）都寫著 No collection（停收）。(A) 2 月 4 日正常收運，5 日還加開下午班；(B) 2 月 5 日除夕有收運；(D) 2 月 8 日晚上收一般垃圾和廚餘，9 日恢復正常，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Mr. Lee has a big bag of plastic bottles on February 8. What should he do?", "options": ["Put it out with the general garbage that evening.", "Leave it at the truck stop overnight.", "Call the hotline to book a pickup.", "Keep it at home and take it out on February 9."], "answer": 3, "explain": "時間表說 2 月 8 日（初三）只收一般垃圾和廚餘，Please Note 第一點也說初三不收回收物，please keep it at home until Day 4（請留在家中等初四），也就是 2 月 9 日。(A) 寶特瓶是回收物，不能當一般垃圾丟；(B) 第二段說絕對不可把垃圾留在停靠點；(C) 預約清運是給沙發等大型家具，而且專線 2 月 8 日暫停服務，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Mrs. Chang wants the cleaning team to take away her old sofa before the holiday. What is the latest date she can call to book a pickup?", "options": ["January 31", "February 1", "February 3", "February 4"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Please Note 第二點說大型家具要 at least three days in advance（至少提前三天）預約，連假前最後一個清運日是 2 月 3 日，往前推三天就是 1 月 31 日。(B) 只提前兩天；(C) 2 月 3 日是清運日本身；(D) 已經過了最後清運日，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "How should people throw away a gift box with a plastic window?", "options": ["Put the whole box in a general garbage bag.", "Take off the plastic window and recycle the box as paper.", "Keep it at home until the holiday is over.", "Burn it together with paper money."], "answer": 1, "explain": "Please Note 第三點說紅包袋、禮盒和包裝紙 can be recycled as paper, but please remove plastic windows and ribbons first（可當紙類回收，但要先拆除塑膠窗和緞帶）。(A) 禮盒可以回收，不必整個丟進一般垃圾；(C) 只有初三不收回收物，不必等到連假結束；(D) 文中沒有說可以把禮盒拿去燒，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why does the notice suggest taking garbage out in the afternoon on New Year's Eve?", "options": ["The evening trucks will not come that day.", "Food waste is collected only in the afternoon.", "There may be long lines at the evening trucks.", "Recycling is not collected in the evening."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Tip 段落說許多家庭在過年前大掃除，so there may be long lines at the evening trucks on New Year's Eve（除夕晚上的垃圾車可能大排長龍），下午倒垃圾可省下等待時間。(A) 時間表寫明除夕晚上照常收運；(B)(D) 文中沒有說廚餘只在下午收，也沒有說除夕晚上不收回收物，晚上是 normal evening collection（照常收運），故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a168", "title": "Roads Closed for the Big Race", "title_zh": "大賽封路：馬拉松交通管制公告", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Notice", "Travel"], "passage": "TRAFFIC NOTICE\nHarbor City Marathon: Sunday, December 13\n\nAbout 12,000 runners will take part in this year's Harbor City Marathon. To keep them safe, several roads will be closed to cars, scooters and bicycles on race day.\n\nRoad Closures\n• Seaside Avenue (the whole road): 4:00 a.m.–1:00 p.m.\n• Zhongshan Road (Sections 1–3): 5:00 a.m.–10:30 a.m.\n• Harbor Bridge: 5:30 a.m.–9:00 a.m.\nRoads may reopen earlier if the last runners pass before these times.\n\nBus and MRT Changes\n• Buses 26 and 88 will not stop at the Seaside Park, Lighthouse and Fish Market stops until 1:30 p.m. Please take the MRT from Harbor Station instead.\n• Bus 15 will take a different route along Minzu Road.\n• The MRT will start running at 5:00 a.m., one hour earlier than usual, with extra trains until noon.\n\nParking\nParking on the closed roads is not allowed from 10:00 p.m. on Saturday, December 12. Cars left there will be towed away. Owners will have to pay a towing fee of NT$1,000, plus NT$200 for each day the car is kept at the tow yard.\n\nResidents\nPeople living on Seaside Avenue may drive in or out only through Lane 52, with help from the police. If you need to go to a hospital or a train station, please allow extra time.\n\nFor the latest news, visit the Harbor City Government website or call the traffic hotline at (07) 331-5000. Thank you for your patience, and come out to cheer for the runners!", "passage_zh": "交通管制公告\n海港市馬拉松：12 月 13 日（星期日）\n\n今年的海港市馬拉松將有約 12,000 名跑者參加。為了確保跑者安全，比賽當天多條道路將禁止汽車、機車及腳踏車通行。\n\n道路封閉\n• 海濱大道（全線）：上午 4:00 至下午 1:00\n• 中山路（一至三段）：上午 5:00 至上午 10:30\n• 港灣大橋：上午 5:30 至上午 9:00\n若最後一批跑者提早通過，道路可能提前開放。\n\n公車與捷運異動\n• 26 路及 88 路公車在下午 1:30 前不停靠海濱公園、燈塔及魚市場站，請改到港口站搭乘捷運。\n• 15 路公車將改道行駛民族路。\n• 捷運將於上午 5:00 開始營運，比平常提早一小時，並加開班次至中午。\n\n停車\n自 12 月 12 日（星期六）晚上 10:00 起，封閉路段禁止停車。違規停放的車輛將被拖吊，車主須支付拖吊費 1,000 元，另加每天 200 元的保管費。\n\n居民\n住在海濱大道的居民，只能在警察協助下經由 52 巷開車進出。如需前往醫院或火車站，請預留更多時間。\n\n最新消息請上海港市政府網站查詢，或撥打交通專線 (07) 331-5000。感謝您耐心配合，也歡迎一起出來為跑者加油！", "questions": [{"q": "Which road will be closed for the longest time on race day?", "options": ["Seaside Avenue", "Zhongshan Road", "Harbor Bridge", "Minzu Road"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Road Closures 段落中，海濱大道從上午 4:00 封到下午 1:00，共 9 小時，時間最長。(B) 中山路 5:00 到 10:30，共 5.5 小時；(C) 港灣大橋 5:30 到 9:00，共 3.5 小時；(D) 民族路並未封閉，只是 15 路公車改走這條路，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Ms. Kao usually takes Bus 26 from the Lighthouse stop on Sunday mornings. What should she do at 8 a.m. on December 13?", "options": ["Wait for Bus 26 at the Lighthouse stop as usual.", "Take Bus 15 along Seaside Avenue.", "Ride her scooter over Harbor Bridge.", "Take the MRT from Harbor Station."], "answer": 3, "explain": "Bus and MRT Changes 段落說 26 路公車下午 1:30 前不停靠燈塔站，Please take the MRT from Harbor Station instead（請改到港口站搭捷運）。(A) 當天早上 26 路不停燈塔站；(B) 15 路公車改走民族路，而且海濱大道封閉；(C) 港灣大橋 5:30 到 9:00 禁止機車通行，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Mr. Wu parked his car on Zhongshan Road on Saturday night, and it was towed away. If the car is kept at the tow yard for three days, how much will he pay in total?", "options": ["NT$600", "NT$1,200", "NT$1,600", "NT$3,000"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Parking 段落說拖吊費 NT$1,000，另加每天 NT$200 的保管費。保管三天是 200×3＝600 元，總共 1,000＋600＝1,600 元。(A) 只算保管費，漏掉拖吊費；(B) 只算了一天保管費；(D) 是把 1,000 元的拖吊費誤當成每天收取（1,000×3），故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the MRT?", "options": ["It will be closed until the race is over.", "It will stop running at noon on race day.", "It usually starts running at 6:00 a.m.", "It will not stop at Harbor Station on race day."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Bus and MRT Changes 段落說比賽當天捷運 5:00 開始營運，one hour earlier than usual（比平常早一小時），可推知平常是早上 6:00 開始營運。(A) 捷運當天提早營運，並未關閉；(B) 中午是加開班次結束的時間，不是停駛；(D) 公告還建議乘客到港口站搭捷運，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The words \"towed away\" in the \"Parking\" part are closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["washed and cleaned", "pulled away by a truck", "given a parking ticket", "locked in place"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Parking 段落說違規停在封閉路段的車 will be towed away，之後車主要付拖吊費，並為車子 kept at the tow yard（放在拖吊場）的天數付費，可知 towed away 是「被拖吊車拖走」，與 pulled away by a truck 最接近。(A)「被清洗」、(C)「被開罰單」、(D)「被鎖在原地」都不會讓車子出現在拖吊場，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a169", "title": "Your School, Your Vote: A Student Council Election", "title_zh": "你的學校，你的一票：學生會選舉", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Notice", "Society"], "passage": "FENG-LIN SENIOR HIGH SCHOOL\nStudent Council Election Notice\nWant to make our school better? Run for Student Council President, or help choose who will!\n\n1. Who Can Run\n• Students in Grade 10 or Grade 11\n• Students with no major demerits on their school records\n• Each candidate needs the signatures of at least 50 students who support him or her.\n\n2. Important Dates\n• Sign-up: April 7–10, at the Office of Student Affairs. Bring your signature list and a 2-inch photo.\n• Campaign period: April 13–24. Posters may only be put on the notice boards in each building, not on walls, windows or doors.\n• Candidate Debate: Wednesday, April 22, 12:20–1:00 p.m., in the school auditorium. It will also be streamed live on the school's YouTube channel.\n• Voting Day: Monday, April 27\n• Results: announced at the morning assembly on April 28\n\n3. How to Vote\nAll students may vote online through the school's student system from 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. on Voting Day. Log in with your student ID number and password. Each student has one vote. If you forget your password, go to the Computer Center (Room 302) before April 24 to get a new one.\n\n4. Rules for Candidates\n• Each candidate may spend no more than NT$2,000 on the campaign. Keep all receipts.\n• Do not give gifts, snacks or drinks to voters.\n• Each campaign team may have up to six members.\n\nThe candidate with the most votes wins. However, if fewer than 30% of all students vote, a second vote will be held the following week.\nOffice of Student Affairs, ext. 1105", "passage_zh": "楓林高級中學\n學生會選舉公告\n想讓我們的學校變得更好嗎？出來競選學生會會長，或幫忙選出最適合的人選吧！\n\n一、參選資格\n• 高一或高二學生\n• 在校紀錄中沒有大過的學生\n• 每位候選人須取得至少 50 位同學的連署支持。\n\n二、重要日期\n• 登記：4 月 7 日至 10 日，於學務處辦理。請攜帶連署名單及二吋照片一張。\n• 競選期間：4 月 13 日至 24 日。海報只能張貼在各大樓的公布欄，不可貼在牆壁、窗戶或門上。\n• 候選人政見辯論會：4 月 22 日（星期三）中午 12:20 至下午 1:00，於學校禮堂舉行，並同步在學校的 YouTube 頻道直播。\n• 投票日：4 月 27 日（星期一）\n• 開票結果：4 月 28 日於朝會宣布\n\n三、投票方式\n投票日當天上午 8:00 至下午 5:00，全體學生皆可透過學校的學生系統上網投票。請以學號及密碼登入。每位學生只有一票。如果忘記密碼，請在 4 月 24 日前到電腦中心（302 室）重新設定。\n\n四、候選人規則\n• 每位候選人的競選花費不得超過 2,000 元，並須保留所有收據。\n• 不得贈送禮物、點心或飲料給選民。\n• 每個競選團隊最多六名成員。\n\n得票最多的候選人當選。但若投票人數不到全校學生的 30%，將於下一週重新投票。\n學務處，分機 1105", "questions": [{"q": "Who is NOT allowed to run for Student Council President?", "options": ["A Grade 10 student with 60 signatures", "A Grade 11 student with a team of five members", "A Grade 12 student with excellent grades", "A Grade 11 student who has no major demerits"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Who Can Run 段落說只有 Students in Grade 10 or Grade 11（高一或高二學生）可以參選，高三學生即使成績優秀也不符合資格。(A) 50 個以上連署即可；(B) 競選團隊最多六人，五人符合規定；(D) 沒有大過正是參選條件之一，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Where may candidates put up their campaign posters?", "options": ["On classroom doors", "On the notice boards in each building", "On the windows of the library", "On any wall in the school"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Important Dates 段落的 Campaign period 說 Posters may only be put on the notice boards in each building, not on walls, windows or doors（海報只能貼在各大樓公布欄，不可貼在牆壁、窗戶或門上）。(A)(C)(D) 門、窗戶和牆壁都明文禁止，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Yu-ting has forgotten her password for the student system. What should she do so that she can vote?", "options": ["Go to Room 302 before April 24 to get a new password.", "Vote on paper at the Office of Student Affairs on April 27.", "Ask a friend to log in and vote for her.", "Call ext. 1105 on the morning of Voting Day."], "answer": 0, "explain": "How to Vote 段落說 If you forget your password, go to the Computer Center (Room 302) before April 24 to get a new one（忘記密碼請在 4 月 24 日前到 302 室電腦中心重新設定）。(B) 投票方式是上網投票，文中沒有紙本投票；(C) 公告要求用自己的學號和密碼登入，每人只有一票，不能請別人代為投票；(D) 文中沒有說投票日當天可打分機處理密碼，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Feng-Lin Senior High School has 1,500 students. If only 400 students vote on April 27, what will happen?", "options": ["The candidate with the most votes will become president.", "The school will cancel this year's election.", "The candidates will hold another debate in the auditorium.", "Students will vote again the following week."], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段說若投票人數 fewer than 30% of all students（不到全校學生的 30%），就要在下一週重新投票。1,500 人的 30% 是 450 人，只有 400 人投票，未達門檻，所以要重新投票。(A) 投票率未達 30%，得票最多者不能直接當選；(B) 文中說會重新投票，並沒有取消選舉；(C) 文中沒有提到再辦一次辯論會，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following candidates is breaking the rules?", "options": ["Ming spends NT$1,800 on his campaign and keeps all the receipts.", "Annie asks six friends to join her campaign team.", "Jason gives free bubble tea to students who promise to vote for him.", "Kelly puts her posters on the notice board in the science building."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Rules for Candidates 段落說 Do not give gifts, snacks or drinks to voters（不得贈送禮物、點心或飲料給選民），Jason 請同學喝珍珠奶茶違反規定。(A) 花費未超過 2,000 元且保留收據；(B) 團隊最多六人，符合規定；(D) 海報本來就應貼在各大樓的公布欄，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a170", "title": "Booking a Museum Tour for the Science Club", "title_zh": "為科學社預約博物館導覽", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Email", "Education"], "passage": "From: Lin Yu-ting\nTo: Education Office, Harbor Light Science Museum\nDate: March 3\nSubject: Group Visit Request\n\nDear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am the leader of the Science Club at Nanhai Senior High School. Our club would like to visit your museum on Saturday, March 21. There will be 28 students and 2 teachers in our group.\n\nWe are especially interested in your new exhibition, \"Life in the Deep Sea.\" Could you arrange a guided tour of it for us? I would also like to know whether students can get cheaper tickets and whether there is a place where we can eat our packed lunches.\n\nThank you for your help.\n\nSincerely,\nLin Yu-ting\n\nFrom: Education Office, Harbor Light Science Museum\nTo: Lin Yu-ting\nDate: March 4\nSubject: RE: Group Visit Request\n\nDear Yu-ting,\n\nThank you for your interest in our museum. On March 21, guided tours of \"Life in the Deep Sea\" start at 10:00 a.m. and 2:00 p.m., and each lasts about 50 minutes. Because the exhibition rooms are small, one guide can take no more than 15 visitors, so your group will be divided into two smaller groups with two guides. The fee is NT$600 per guide.\n\nStudent tickets cost NT$100 each (regular price: NT$250). Teachers who come with a school group can enter for free. Please remember to bring student ID cards, or students will have to pay the regular price.\n\nFood and drinks are not allowed in the exhibition areas, but your group may use the Picnic Room on the first floor between 11:30 a.m. and 1:30 p.m. at no cost. Please let us know if you need it.\n\nPlease confirm which tour time you would like by March 14. You can pay at the ticket counter on the day of your visit.\n\nBest wishes,\nWu Ya-chi\nEducation Office", "passage_zh": "寄件者：林語婷\n收件者：海光科學博物館教育推廣組\n日期：3 月 3 日\n主旨：團體參觀申請\n\n敬啟者：\n\n我是南海高中科學社的社長。本社希望在 3 月 21 日星期六到貴館參觀，團體共有 28 名學生和 2 名老師。\n\n我們對貴館的新展覽「深海生命」特別有興趣。能否請您為我們安排這個展覽的導覽？我也想知道學生是否能買到較便宜的門票，以及館內是否有地方可以讓我們吃自備的午餐。\n\n感謝您的協助。\n\n林語婷 敬上\n\n寄件者：海光科學博物館教育推廣組\n收件者：林語婷\n日期：3 月 4 日\n主旨：回覆：團體參觀申請\n\n語婷您好：\n\n感謝您對本館的關注。3 月 21 日當天，「深海生命」的導覽於上午 10 點和下午 2 點開始，每場約 50 分鐘。由於展覽室空間較小，每位導覽員最多只能帶 15 位參觀者，因此你們的團體將分成兩個小組，由兩位導覽員帶領。導覽費用為每位導覽員新台幣 600 元。\n\n學生票每張新台幣 100 元（全票 250 元）。隨學校團體前來的老師可免費入場。請記得攜帶學生證，否則學生須購買全票。\n\n展覽區內禁止飲食，但你們可以在上午 11 點 30 分至下午 1 點 30 分之間免費使用一樓的野餐室。如有需要，請告知我們。\n\n請於 3 月 14 日前向我們確認你們想參加哪一個導覽時段。參觀當天可在售票處付款。\n\n祝 順心\n教育推廣組 吳雅琪", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of Yu-ting's email?", "options": ["To complain that the museum's tickets are too expensive", "To ask about arranging a group visit with a guided tour", "To invite a museum guide to give a talk at her school", "To ask for a part-time job at the museum's Education Office"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一封信第一段說社團想在 3 月 21 日到博物館參觀，第二段請館方安排「深海生命」的導覽，並詢問學生票和用餐地點，可知目的是詢問團體參觀與導覽的安排。(A) 她只是詢問有沒有優惠，並未抱怨票價；(C)(D) 信中完全沒有提到邀請演講或應徵工作，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "If all the students bring their ID cards, how much will the group pay in total for tickets and guides?", "options": ["NT$3,400", "NT$3,000", "NT$4,200", "NT$4,000"], "answer": 3, "explain": "回信第二段：學生票每張 100 元，老師免費，28 × 100 = 2,800 元；第一段：團體要分成兩組、由兩位導覽員帶領，每位 600 元，共 1,200 元。合計 2,800 + 1,200 = 4,000 元。(A) 只算了一位導覽員；(B) 把兩位老師也算成學生票，又漏了導覽費；(C) 把老師也算進門票，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why will Yu-ting's group be divided into two smaller groups?", "options": ["Because the exhibition rooms are too small for large groups.", "Because teachers and students must join different tours.", "Because the museum offers only short tours on Saturdays.", "Because some students want to eat lunch in the Picnic Room."], "answer": 0, "explain": "回信第一段：Because the exhibition rooms are small, one guide can take no more than 15 visitors，因為展覽室空間小，一位導覽員最多只能帶 15 人，所以 30 人的團體要分成兩組。(B) 文中沒有說師生要分開；(C) 導覽每場約 50 分鐘，與分組無關；(D) 野餐室是用餐地點，不是分組的原因，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What should Yu-ting do by March 14?", "options": ["Pay for the tickets and the guides online.", "Send the museum a list of her members' names.", "Tell the museum which tour time her group wants.", "Book a table at the museum's restaurant."], "answer": 2, "explain": "回信最後一段：Please confirm which tour time you would like by March 14，要在 3 月 14 日前告知想參加的導覽時段。(A) 同段說參觀當天在售票處付款，不是線上付款；(B) 館方沒有要求名單；(D) 館方提供的是免費的野餐室，文中沒有提到餐廳訂位，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"arrange\" in Yu-ting's email is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["cancel", "organize", "repeat", "describe"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Could you arrange a guided tour of it for us? 是請館方「安排」一場導覽，arrange 意思接近 organize（籌劃、安排）。(A) cancel 是「取消」，(C) repeat 是「重複」，(D) describe 是「描述」，放回句中都不合語意，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a171", "title": "Drilling at 7:30 a.m.: A Letter About Renovation Noise", "title_zh": "早上七點半就在鑽牆：一封關於裝修噪音的信", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Letter", "Life"], "passage": "May 2\nTo: Management Committee, Sunrise Garden Apartments\n\nDear Committee Members,\n\nI live in Apartment 7B, and I am writing to complain about the noise from the renovation work in Apartment 8B, which is right above mine. The work began on April 20. Since then, the workers have often started drilling as early as 7:30 a.m., and last Sunday they worked all morning.\n\nI understand that people need to repair their homes, but the noise has become unbearable. My grandmother, who lives with us, cannot get enough rest, and I am preparing for an important exam in June. Could you please remind the owner of 8B of the building rules? I would also like to know how long the work will continue.\n\nYours faithfully,\nChang Wei-ling\n\nMay 4\nTo: Chang Wei-ling, Apartment 7B\n\nDear Ms. Chang,\n\nThank you for your letter, and we are sorry for the trouble you have had. Under our building rules, renovation work is allowed only from 9:00 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. and from 1:30 p.m. to 5:30 p.m., Monday to Saturday. No work is allowed on Sundays or national holidays.\n\nYesterday we spoke with the owner of 8B and the renovation company. They apologized and promised to follow these hours from now on. According to the company, the noisiest part of the job, breaking up the old floor tiles, will be finished by May 10. After that, the work will mainly be painting and putting in furniture. The whole project is expected to end on May 31.\n\nIf the workers break the rules again, please call the management office at (02) 2758-3302, extension 100, while it is happening. Our guard will go upstairs and check right away. An owner who breaks the rules three times must pay a fine of NT$3,000.\n\nSincerely,\nLo Chih-hao\nManagement Office, Sunrise Garden Apartments", "passage_zh": "5 月 2 日\n致：日昇花園社區管理委員會\n\n各位委員好：\n\n我住在 7B，寫這封信是要反映正樓上 8B 裝修工程的噪音問題。工程從 4 月 20 日開始。從那時起，工人經常早上 7 點半就開始鑽牆，上週日甚至施工了一整個上午。\n\n我能理解大家需要整修房子，但噪音已經讓人無法忍受。和我們同住的奶奶沒辦法好好休息，而我正在準備 6 月的一場重要考試。能否請您提醒 8B 的屋主遵守社區規約？我也想知道工程還會持續多久。\n\n張薇玲 敬上\n\n5 月 4 日\n致：7B 張薇玲小姐\n\n張小姐您好：\n\n謝謝您的來信，造成您的困擾，我們深感抱歉。依照本社區規約，裝修工程只能在週一至週六的上午 9 點至中午 12 點，以及下午 1 點半至 5 點半進行，週日及國定假日一律不得施工。\n\n我們昨天已和 8B 的屋主及裝修公司談過。他們表示歉意，並承諾從現在起會遵守施工時間。根據裝修公司的說法，工程中最吵的部分——敲除舊地磚——將在 5 月 10 日前完成。之後主要是油漆和安裝家具。整個工程預計在 5 月 31 日結束。\n\n如果工人再次違規，請在施工當下撥打管理室電話 (02) 2758-3302 分機 100，我們的警衛會立刻上樓查看。屋主違規三次須繳納新台幣 3,000 元罰款。\n\n日昇花園社區管理室\n羅志豪 敬上", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of Wei-ling's letter?", "options": ["To ask the committee to change the building rules", "To complain about renovation noise and ask for help", "To apologize for the noise her own family has made", "To ask for permission to renovate her apartment"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一封信第一段 I am writing to complain about the noise from the renovation work in Apartment 8B，第二段又請委員會提醒 8B 屋主遵守規約，可知目的是抱怨裝修噪音並請求協助。(A) 她是請委員會執行現有規約，不是修改規約；(C) 製造噪音的是 8B，不是她家；(D) 要裝修的是樓上住戶，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the building rules, when could the workers drill?", "options": ["At 8:30 a.m. on a Monday", "At 1:00 p.m. on a Wednesday", "At 3:00 p.m. on a Saturday", "At 10:00 a.m. on a Sunday"], "answer": 2, "explain": "回信第一段：施工時間是週一至週六的 9:00–12:00 和 1:30–5:30，週六下午 3 點在允許範圍內。(A) 早上 8 點半早於 9 點；(B) 下午 1 點是午休時段，1 點半才能施工；(D) 週日一律不得施工，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the renovation work after May 10?", "options": ["It will stop completely.", "It will be allowed on Sundays.", "It will be done by a different company.", "It will probably make less noise."], "answer": 3, "explain": "回信第二段說最吵的部分（敲除舊地磚）會在 5 月 10 日前完成，之後主要是油漆和安裝家具，可推論噪音會變小。(A) 整個工程要到 5 月 31 日才結束，不會完全停止；(B) 規約規定週日不得施工；(C) 文中沒有提到換公司，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "If the workers start drilling at 7:30 a.m. again, what should Wei-ling do?", "options": ["Call the management office at once.", "Write another letter to the committee.", "Go upstairs and argue with the workers herself.", "Pay a fine of NT$3,000 to the committee."], "answer": 0, "explain": "回信最後一段：If the workers break the rules again, please call the management office ... while it is happening，違規時應在當下打電話到管理室，警衛會立刻上樓查看。(B) 寫信太慢，管理室要的是當下來電；(C) 上樓查看是警衛的工作；(D) 罰款是由違規的屋主繳納，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"unbearable\" in the second paragraph of Wei-ling's letter is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["hard to notice", "too bad to put up with", "easy to fix", "quite exciting"], "answer": 1, "explain": "the noise has become unbearable 後面接著說奶奶無法休息、她無法專心準備考試，可知噪音已經「讓人無法忍受」，unbearable 意思接近 too bad to put up with。(A)「不易察覺」與文意相反；(C)「容易修理」、(D)「相當刺激」都不符合抱怨的語氣，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a172", "title": "The Bus That Drove Right Past Me", "title_zh": "公車從我面前直接開走了", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Email", "Travel"], "passage": "From: Chen Kai-wen\nTo: Customer Service, Greenway Bus Company\nDate: October 7\nSubject: Bus Did Not Stop for Me\n\nDear Customer Service,\n\nI am writing about a problem I had yesterday morning, October 6. I was waiting at the Zhongshan Elementary School stop for the Route 236 bus. When the 6:52 a.m. bus came, I waved my hand clearly, but the bus did not stop. It just drove past me.\n\nThe next bus did not come until 20 minutes later. As a result, I was late for school and missed a math quiz. I take this bus every school day, and this has never happened before. I hope you can look into this matter and make sure it does not happen again.\n\nRegards,\nChen Kai-wen\n\nFrom: Customer Service, Greenway Bus Company\nTo: Chen Kai-wen\nDate: October 9\nSubject: RE: Bus Did Not Stop for Me\n\nDear Kai-wen,\n\nWe are very sorry for what happened. After checking the GPS records and the camera on the bus, we found that a large truck was parked in front of the bus stop at that time. It blocked the driver's view, so he did not see you waving. We have reminded all our drivers on Route 236 to slow down and look carefully at this stop.\n\nTo solve the problem, the stop will be moved 15 meters forward, to the front of the post office, starting November 1. We have also asked the police to stop trucks from parking there.\n\nAs an apology, we have mailed you two free ride coupons. They can be used on any Greenway bus until December 31. You can also download our \"Greenway Live\" app to see where your bus is in real time.\n\nIf you have any more questions, please call us at 0800-236-236 (8 a.m. to 8 p.m. daily).\n\nSincerely,\nHsu An-mei\nCustomer Service Team", "passage_zh": "寄件者：陳凱文\n收件者：綠道客運客服中心\n日期：10 月 7 日\n主旨：公車過站不停\n\n客服人員您好：\n\n我寫信是要反映昨天早上（10 月 6 日）遇到的問題。當時我在中山國小站等 236 路公車。早上 6 點 52 分的公車來時，我很清楚地揮手招車，但公車沒有停，直接從我面前開走了。\n\n下一班公車過了 20 分鐘才來，結果我上學遲到，還錯過了一場數學小考。我每天上學都搭這班公車，以前從來沒發生過這種事。希望貴公司能調查這件事，並確保不再發生。\n\n陳凱文 敬上\n\n寄件者：綠道客運客服中心\n收件者：陳凱文\n日期：10 月 9 日\n主旨：回覆：公車過站不停\n\n凱文您好：\n\n對於這次發生的事，我們深感抱歉。檢查 GPS 紀錄和車上的攝影機畫面後，我們發現當時有一輛大卡車停在公車站前，擋住了司機的視線，所以他沒有看到您在招手。我們已提醒 236 路的所有司機，經過這一站時要放慢速度、仔細查看。\n\n為了解決這個問題，從 11 月 1 日起，這個站牌將往前移 15 公尺，改設在郵局前面。我們也已請警方取締在該處停放的卡車。\n\n為表歉意，我們已寄給您兩張免費乘車券，在 12 月 31 日前可搭乘任何一輛綠道客運的公車。您也可以下載我們的「綠道即時」App，查看公車的即時位置。\n\n如有其他問題，請撥打 0800-236-236（每天上午 8 點至晚上 8 點）。\n\n客服團隊 許安美 敬上", "questions": [{"q": "Why didn't the 6:52 a.m. bus stop for Kai-wen?", "options": ["The driver was in a hurry because the bus was late.", "The bus was already too full to take more passengers.", "A parked truck blocked the driver's view of the stop.", "Kai-wen was waiting at the wrong bus stop."], "answer": 2, "explain": "回信第一段：a large truck was parked in front of the bus stop ... It blocked the driver's view, so he did not see you waving，是停在站前的大卡車擋住司機視線。(A)(B) 文中沒有提到公車誤點或客滿；(D) 凱文每天都在這一站搭車，並沒有站錯，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "At what time did the next Route 236 bus arrive that morning?", "options": ["7:12 a.m.", "7:02 a.m.", "7:20 a.m.", "6:32 a.m."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一封信第一段說沒停的是 6:52 的公車，第二段說 The next bus did not come until 20 minutes later，6:52 再過 20 分鐘是 7:12。(B) 只加了 10 分鐘；(C) 把「20 分鐘」誤當成時刻；(D) 是往前減 20 分鐘，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What will change starting November 1?", "options": ["Route 236 buses will come every ten minutes.", "The bus stop will be in a slightly different place.", "Kai-wen's ride coupons will stop working.", "A new driver will drive the 6:52 a.m. bus."], "answer": 1, "explain": "回信第二段：the stop will be moved 15 meters forward, to the front of the post office, starting November 1，站牌會往前移 15 公尺到郵局前。(A) 文中沒有提到增加班次；(C) 乘車券可用到 12 月 31 日；(D) 公司只是提醒司機注意，並未換人，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"look into\" in Kai-wen's email is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["forget about", "complain about", "pay for", "find out the facts about"], "answer": 3, "explain": "I hope you can look into this matter and make sure it does not happen again. 凱文希望公司「調查」這件事，回信中公司也確實查看了 GPS 紀錄和攝影機畫面，所以 look into 接近 find out the facts about。(A)「忘記」與他的期望相反；(B) 抱怨的是凱文本人，不是要公司去抱怨；(C)「付錢」不合語意，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the ride coupons?", "options": ["They can only be used on Route 236.", "Kai-wen can pick them up at the bus stop.", "They can be used until the end of the year.", "He must download an app to use them."], "answer": 2, "explain": "回信第三段：They can be used on any Greenway bus until December 31，乘車券可用到 12 月 31 日，也就是年底。(A) 可搭乘任何一輛綠道客運公車，不限 236 路；(B) 公司說 we have mailed you，是用郵寄的；(D) App 是用來查公車位置的，與乘車券無關，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a173", "title": "Asking a Hotel for a Summer Internship", "title_zh": "向飯店詢問暑期實習機會", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Email", "Career"], "passage": "From: Wang Pei-shan\nTo: Human Resources, Harborview Hotel Hualien\nDate: March 10\nSubject: Summer Internship at the Front Desk\n\nDear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am a second-year student in the Hospitality Program at Ji'an Vocational High School. I would like to ask whether your hotel offers summer internships for high school students.\n\nI am especially interested in working at the front desk. I have practiced check-in and check-out procedures in class, and I can speak Chinese, English and some Japanese. I believe an internship at your hotel would help me learn how a real hotel works.\n\nCould you tell me how to apply? Thank you very much.\n\nBest regards,\nWang Pei-shan\n\nFrom: Human Resources, Harborview Hotel Hualien\nTo: Wang Pei-shan\nDate: March 12\nSubject: RE: Summer Internship at the Front Desk\n\nDear Pei-shan,\n\nThank you for your interest in our hotel. We offer a six-week summer internship from July 6 to August 14. Interns work five days a week, eight hours a day, including some weekends, and are paid NT$200 per hour. Free meals are provided during each shift.\n\nThis year we have three positions at the front desk and five in the restaurant. To apply, please send us the following by April 15:\n1. a completed application form (download it from our website)\n2. your résumé with a recent photo\n3. a recommendation letter from one of your teachers\n\nSelected students will be invited to an interview on April 26. Because many of our guests come from overseas, part of the interview for front desk positions will be in English.\n\nPlease note that all interns must be at least 16 years old, and students under 18 must also send a permission form signed by a parent.\n\nWe look forward to receiving your application.\n\nKind regards,\nLai Yi-chun\nHuman Resources Department", "passage_zh": "寄件者：王佩珊\n收件者：花蓮港景飯店人力資源部\n日期：3 月 10 日\n主旨：櫃檯暑期實習\n\n敬啟者：\n\n我是吉安高職觀光餐旅科二年級的學生，想請問貴飯店是否提供高中職學生暑期實習的機會。\n\n我對櫃檯工作特別有興趣。我在課堂上練習過辦理入住和退房的流程，也會說中文、英文和一些日文。我相信在貴飯店實習，能幫助我了解一家真正的飯店是如何運作的。\n\n能否請您告訴我該如何申請？非常感謝。\n\n王佩珊 敬上\n\n寄件者：花蓮港景飯店人力資源部\n收件者：王佩珊\n日期：3 月 12 日\n主旨：回覆：櫃檯暑期實習\n\n佩珊您好：\n\n感謝您對本飯店的關注。我們提供為期六週的暑期實習，時間從 7 月 6 日到 8 月 14 日。實習生每週工作五天，每天八小時，包含部分週末，時薪新台幣 200 元。每個班次都提供免費餐點。\n\n今年櫃檯有三個名額，餐廳有五個名額。如欲申請，請在 4 月 15 日前寄給我們以下資料：\n1. 填寫完整的申請表（請至本飯店網站下載）\n2. 附近照的履歷表\n3. 一封由您的老師撰寫的推薦信\n\n獲選的學生將受邀參加 4 月 26 日的面試。由於本飯店有許多來自海外的房客，櫃檯職缺的面試有一部分會以英文進行。\n\n請注意，所有實習生須年滿 16 歲，未滿 18 歲的學生還須另外寄送家長簽名的同意書。\n\n期待收到您的申請。\n\n人力資源部 賴怡君 敬上", "questions": [{"q": "Why did Pei-shan write the first email?", "options": ["To book a room for her family's summer trip", "To ask how she can apply for a summer internship", "To complain about the service at the hotel's front desk", "To invite the hotel staff to give a talk at her school"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一封信第一段問飯店是否提供高中職學生暑期實習，第三段又問 Could you tell me how to apply?，可知目的是詢問如何申請暑期實習。(A) 她不是要訂房；(C) 她對櫃檯工作有興趣，並沒有抱怨；(D) 信中沒有邀請演講，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How much will an intern earn in one full week of work, not counting the free meals?", "options": ["NT$8,000", "NT$1,600", "NT$10,000", "NT$48,000"], "answer": 0, "explain": "回信第一段：每週工作五天、每天八小時、時薪 200 元，5 × 8 × 200 = 8,000 元。(B) 只是一天的收入（8 × 200）；(C) 是以每週工作 50 小時計算；(D) 是六週的總收入，不是一週，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Pei-shan is 17 years old. Besides the three items in the list, what else must she send?", "options": ["A copy of her student ID card", "A permission form signed by a parent", "A score report from an English test", "A letter from a hotel she has worked at"], "answer": 1, "explain": "回信第四段：students under 18 must also send a permission form signed by a parent，佩珊 17 歲未滿 18 歲，必須另附家長簽名的同意書。(A)(C)(D) 學生證、英文檢定成績和前雇主的信，飯店都沒有要求，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Pei-shan's interview if she is chosen?", "options": ["It will take place on April 15.", "It will be held entirely in Japanese.", "She should be ready to answer some questions in English.", "Only students who are over 18 can take part in it."], "answer": 2, "explain": "佩珊申請的是櫃檯職位，回信第三段說 part of the interview for front desk positions will be in English，所以她應準備用英文回答部分問題。(A) 4 月 15 日是寄資料的截止日，面試在 4 月 26 日；(B) 文中說的是部分用英文，沒有提到全程日文；(D) 年滿 16 歲即可申請，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"positions\" in the second email is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["opinions", "rooms", "seats", "jobs"], "answer": 3, "explain": "we have three positions at the front desk and five in the restaurant 是說櫃檯有三個、餐廳有五個實習「職缺」，position 在此指工作職位，接近 jobs。(A)「意見」、(B)「房間」、(C)「座位」放回句中都說不通，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a174", "title": "Still Paying for the Gym After I Quit", "title_zh": "退出健身房後還在被扣款", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Email", "Money"], "passage": "From: Tsai Min-hui\nTo: Member Services, FitZone Gym\nDate: October 6\nSubject: Charged After Cancelling My Membership\n\nDear Member Services,\n\nI cancelled my monthly membership (Member No. FZ-58213) at the front desk of your Xinyi branch on August 28. A staff member gave me a copy of the cancellation form, which I still have.\n\nHowever, NT$1,299 was taken from my credit card on September 5, and again on October 5. I have not used the gym at all since I cancelled. I would like a full refund of NT$2,598. I have attached a photo of the cancellation form.\n\nI look forward to your quick reply.\n\nTsai Min-hui\n\nFrom: Member Services, FitZone Gym\nTo: Tsai Min-hui\nDate: October 8\nSubject: RE: Charged After Cancelling My Membership\n\nDear Ms. Tsai,\n\nThank you for contacting us, and we apologize for the trouble. We have checked your account and the photo you sent.\n\nAccording to Rule 7 of your membership contract, a cancellation must be made at least 10 days before the next payment date. Payments are taken on the 5th of each month. Since you cancelled on August 28, only eight days before September 5, the September payment was correctly charged, and your membership remained active until October 4.\n\nHowever, the October payment was our mistake. Our staff did not enter your cancellation into the computer system in time. We will return NT$1,299 to your credit card within 7 to 14 working days.\n\nTo make up for the trouble, we would like to offer you two free one-day passes, which you can use at any FitZone branch before the end of the year. If you decide to rejoin within three months, we will also waive the NT$500 joining fee.\n\nBest regards,\nHo Chia-ling\nMember Services, FitZone Gym", "passage_zh": "寄件者：蔡敏慧\n收件者：FitZone 健身房會員服務部\n日期：10 月 6 日\n主旨：取消會員後仍被扣款\n\n會員服務部您好：\n\n我在 8 月 28 日於貴公司信義分店的櫃檯取消了月費會員（會員編號 FZ-58213）。當時一位工作人員給了我一份取消申請表的副本，我目前仍保留著。\n\n然而，我的信用卡在 9 月 5 日被扣了新台幣 1,299 元，10 月 5 日又被扣了一次。取消之後我完全沒有再去過健身房。我希望能全額退還新台幣 2,598 元。隨信附上取消申請表的照片。\n\n期待您盡快回覆。\n\n蔡敏慧\n\n寄件者：FitZone 健身房會員服務部\n收件者：蔡敏慧\n日期：10 月 8 日\n主旨：回覆：取消會員後仍被扣款\n\n蔡小姐您好：\n\n感謝您與我們聯繫，造成您的困擾，我們深感抱歉。我們已查看您的帳戶以及您寄來的照片。\n\n根據會員合約第 7 條，取消會員須在下次扣款日至少 10 天前提出。每月扣款日為 5 日。您於 8 月 28 日取消，距離 9 月 5 日只有八天，因此 9 月的扣款是正確的，您的會員資格也一直有效到 10 月 4 日。\n\n不過，10 月的扣款是我們的錯誤。我們的工作人員沒有及時將您的取消申請輸入電腦系統。我們將在 7 到 14 個工作天內，將新台幣 1,299 元退回您的信用卡。\n\n為了彌補給您帶來的困擾，我們想送您兩張免費單日入場券，年底前可在任何一家 FitZone 分店使用。如果您決定在三個月內重新加入，我們也將免收新台幣 500 元的入會費。\n\nFitZone 健身房會員服務部\n何佳玲 敬上", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of Ms. Tsai's email?", "options": ["To cancel her gym membership", "To ask for her money back for two payments", "To complain about the gym's old equipment", "To ask how she can rejoin the gym"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一封信第二段說她在 9 月 5 日和 10 月 5 日各被扣了 1,299 元，並表示 I would like a full refund of NT$2,598，目的是要求退還兩筆扣款。(A) 她在 8 月 28 日就已取消會員；(C) 信中沒有提到器材；(D) 重新入會是回信中健身房提出的優惠，不是她的要求，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why was the September payment correctly charged?", "options": ["Ms. Tsai used the gym several times in September.", "The staff forgot to enter her cancellation into the system.", "The monthly fee went up at the beginning of September.", "She cancelled less than 10 days before the payment date."], "answer": 3, "explain": "回信第二段：合約第 7 條規定須在扣款日至少 10 天前取消，她 8 月 28 日取消，距離 9 月 5 日只有八天，所以 9 月的扣款是正確的。(A) 她說取消後完全沒去過；(B) 這是 10 月扣款出錯的原因；(C) 文中沒有提到漲價，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How much money will FitZone return to Ms. Tsai's credit card?", "options": ["NT$1,299", "NT$2,598", "NT$1,799", "NT$500"], "answer": 0, "explain": "回信第三段：只有 10 月的扣款是健身房的錯，We will return NT$1,299 to your credit card。(B) 是她原本要求的金額，但 9 月的扣款依合約不退；(C) 是把 1,299 加上 500 元入會費；(D) 500 元是重新入會時免收的入會費，不是退款，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the reply, which of the following is true?", "options": ["The free passes can only be used at the Xinyi branch.", "Ms. Tsai forgot to fill in a cancellation form.", "Ms. Tsai could still use the gym until October 4.", "Payments are taken on the 28th of each month."], "answer": 2, "explain": "回信第二段：your membership remained active until October 4，她的會員資格有效到 10 月 4 日，在那之前仍可使用健身房。(A) 入場券可在 any FitZone branch 使用；(B) 她有填寫取消申請表並保留副本；(D) 扣款日是每月 5 日，28 日是她取消的日期，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"waive\" in the last paragraph of the reply is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["double", "not charge", "explain", "pay back later"], "answer": 1, "explain": "we will also waive the NT$500 joining fee 出現在健身房提供的補償方案中，意思是重新入會時「免收」500 元入會費，waive 接近 not charge。(A)「加倍收取」不可能是補償；(C)「說明」不合語意；(D) 免收是一開始就不用付，不是先付再退，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a175", "title": "Happy 8th Birthday, Grandpa? A Cake Mix-Up", "title_zh": "祝爺爺八歲生日快樂？一場蛋糕烏龍", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Email", "Food"], "passage": "From: Lee Hsiao-wen\nTo: Sweet Corner Bakery\nDate: June 16\nSubject: Wrong Birthday Cake (Order No. 7731)\n\nDear Sweet Corner Bakery,\n\nOn June 10, I ordered an eight-inch mango cream cake for my grandfather's 80th birthday party. I asked for the words \"Happy 80th Birthday, Grandpa\" to be written on it. I paid NT$1,050 in advance and picked up the cake yesterday at 5 p.m.\n\nWhen we opened the box at the party, we found a chocolate cake that said \"Happy 8th Birthday, Lily\"! My grandfather laughed, but the rest of the family was disappointed, and my cousin, who is allergic to chocolate, could not eat any of it.\n\nI hope you can explain what went wrong and tell me what you can do about it.\n\nLee Hsiao-wen\n\nFrom: Sweet Corner Bakery\nTo: Lee Hsiao-wen\nDate: June 17\nSubject: RE: Wrong Birthday Cake (Order No. 7731)\n\nDear Hsiao-wen,\n\nWe are so sorry that we spoiled such an important day. After checking our records, we found that two cakes were put in the wrong boxes on June 15. Lily's family received your mango cake, and you received their chocolate cake. We have apologized to them as well.\n\nTo make things right, please choose one of the following:\nOption 1: A full refund of NT$1,050 to the card you paid with.\nOption 2: A new eight-inch cake of any flavor, free of charge, to be picked up on any day before July 31. Please order at least three days in advance. If you would like a ten-inch cake instead, you only need to pay the difference of NT$400.\n\nWith either option, we will also give you a NT$200 coupon for your next visit.\n\nPlease reply by June 24 to let us know your choice. From now on, our staff will check the words on every cake with the customer before it leaves the shop.\n\nWith our apologies,\nChen Ya-ru\nStore Manager, Sweet Corner Bakery", "passage_zh": "寄件者：李曉雯\n收件者：甜蜜角落烘焙坊\n日期：6 月 16 日\n主旨：生日蛋糕拿錯了（訂單編號 7731）\n\n甜蜜角落烘焙坊您好：\n\n我在 6 月 10 日訂了一個八吋芒果鮮奶油蛋糕，要在爺爺的 80 歲生日派對上用。我請你們在蛋糕上寫「爺爺 80 歲生日快樂」。我已事先付了新台幣 1,050 元，並在昨天下午 5 點取貨。\n\n我們在派對上打開盒子時，看到的卻是一個寫著「莉莉 8 歲生日快樂」的巧克力蛋糕！爺爺笑了出來，但其他家人都很失望，而我那位對巧克力過敏的表弟更是一口都不能吃。\n\n希望你們能說明是哪裡出了錯，並告訴我你們打算如何處理。\n\n李曉雯\n\n寄件者：甜蜜角落烘焙坊\n收件者：李曉雯\n日期：6 月 17 日\n主旨：回覆：生日蛋糕拿錯了（訂單編號 7731）\n\n曉雯您好：\n\n這麼重要的日子被我們搞砸了，真的非常抱歉。查看紀錄後，我們發現 6 月 15 日有兩個蛋糕被裝錯了盒子：莉莉的家人拿到了您的芒果蛋糕，而您拿到了他們的巧克力蛋糕。我們也已經向他們道歉了。\n\n為了彌補，請從以下方案中選擇一項：\n方案一：全額退還新台幣 1,050 元至您付款時使用的卡片。\n方案二：免費提供一個任選口味的八吋新蛋糕，可在 7 月 31 日前的任何一天取貨，請至少提前三天訂購。如果您想改成十吋蛋糕，只需補差價新台幣 400 元。\n\n無論選擇哪個方案，我們都會再送您一張新台幣 200 元的折價券，供您下次光臨時使用。\n\n請在 6 月 24 日前回覆告知您的選擇。從現在起，我們的店員在每個蛋糕離開店面之前，都會和顧客核對蛋糕上的文字。\n\n再次致上歉意\n甜蜜角落烘焙坊店長 陳雅如", "questions": [{"q": "What went wrong with Hsiao-wen's order?", "options": ["The cake was not ready when she arrived at 5 p.m.", "The words on her cake were spelled wrong.", "The cake was too small for the whole family.", "She was given a cake that another customer had ordered."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一封信第二段說盒子裡是寫著「莉莉 8 歲生日快樂」的巧克力蛋糕；回信第一段也說明兩個蛋糕裝錯了盒子，她拿到的是莉莉家訂的蛋糕。(A) 她 5 點順利取貨；(B) 不是拼錯字，而是整個蛋糕拿錯；(C) 文中沒有抱怨尺寸，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Hsiao-wen chooses Option 2 but asks for a ten-inch cake. How much will she pay?", "options": ["Nothing", "NT$400", "NT$1,050", "NT$1,450"], "answer": 1, "explain": "回信第二段方案二：八吋蛋糕免費，若改成十吋 you only need to pay the difference of NT$400，只要補差價 400 元。(A) 只有八吋才完全免費；(C) 是原本訂單的金額；(D) 是把原價和差價加在一起，但新蛋糕本身是免費的，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Hsiao-wen has told the bakery that she chooses Option 2. If she wants to pick up the new cake on July 20, what is the latest date she can order it?", "options": ["July 17", "July 20", "June 24", "July 31"], "answer": 0, "explain": "回信第二段方案二規定 Please order at least three days in advance，要在取貨日至少三天前訂購，7 月 20 日往前三天是 7 月 17 日。(B) 當天訂購來不及；(C) 6 月 24 日是回覆選擇方案的期限，不是最晚的訂購日；(D) 7 月 31 日是取貨的最後期限，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What will the bakery do from now on to stop this problem from happening again?", "options": ["Give every customer a NT$200 coupon.", "Stop writing words on birthday cakes.", "Have staff check the words on each cake with the customer.", "Deliver all birthday cakes to customers' homes."], "answer": 2, "explain": "回信第四段：From now on, our staff will check the words on every cake with the customer before it leaves the shop，以後店員會在蛋糕出店前和顧客核對文字。(A) 200 元折價券只是給曉雯的補償；(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"spoiled\" in the bakery's reply is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["ruined", "planned", "shared", "remembered"], "answer": 0, "explain": "We are so sorry that we spoiled such an important day. 蛋糕店為拿錯蛋糕道歉，表示他們「搞砸、破壞」了爺爺的大日子，spoiled 接近 ruined。(B)「計畫」、(C)「分享」、(D)「記得」都不是需要道歉的事，放回句中不合語意，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a176", "title": "A Dark Road Home: Asking for Streetlight Repairs", "title_zh": "回家的路太暗了：請求修理路燈", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Letter", "Society"], "passage": "November 5\nTo: Public Works Section, Linhai District Office\n\nDear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am the president of the Student Council at Linhai Senior High School. I am writing to ask for your help with the streetlights on Heping Lane, the 600-meter road between our school and Linhai MRT Station.\n\nThere are 12 streetlights on this road, but four of them have not worked for about three weeks. Many of our students walk or ride bikes along this road after evening study ends at 9 p.m. Because parts of the road are now very dark, it does not feel safe. Last Thursday, a first-year student fell off his bike after hitting a hole he could not see.\n\nWe would be grateful if you could repair the lights as soon as possible. We would also like to suggest putting up a traffic mirror at the sharp corner near the park.\n\nYours faithfully,\nHuang Yi-jie\nStudent Council President\n\nNovember 12\nTo: Huang Yi-jie, Linhai Senior High School\n\nDear Yi-jie,\n\nThank you for your letter. Our staff checked Heping Lane on November 8 and found that five lights were not working, not four. The problem was caused by an old underground cable. New parts have been ordered, and all the lights should be working again by November 25. Until then, we have set up two temporary lights in the darkest section. The hole in the road was filled on November 10.\n\nAs for the traffic mirror, we need to study the traffic at the corner first. If the mirror is approved, it will be put up in December.\n\nIn the future, the fastest way to report a broken streetlight is to call the 1999 hotline. Every streetlight pole has a small metal plate with a number on it, such as \"HP-07.\" Giving us this number helps our workers find the light quickly.\n\nSincerely,\nLin Shu-fen\nPublic Works Section, Linhai District Office", "passage_zh": "11 月 5 日\n致：臨海區公所工務課\n\n敬啟者：\n\n我是臨海高中學生會會長，寫這封信是想請您協助處理和平巷的路燈問題。和平巷是連接本校與臨海捷運站、長約 600 公尺的道路。\n\n這條路上共有 12 盞路燈，但其中四盞已經壞了大約三個星期。我們有許多學生在晚上 9 點晚自習結束後，沿著這條路步行或騎腳踏車回家。由於部分路段現在非常暗，走起來讓人覺得不安全。上週四，一名一年級學生騎車撞上一個看不見的坑洞而摔車。\n\n若您能盡快修好這些路燈，我們將不勝感激。我們也想建議在公園附近的急轉彎處設置一面道路反射鏡。\n\n學生會會長\n黃奕傑 敬上\n\n11 月 12 日\n致：臨海高中 黃奕傑同學\n\n奕傑同學您好：\n\n謝謝您的來信。我們的同仁在 11 月 8 日檢查了和平巷，發現故障的路燈有五盞，而不是四盞。問題出在一條老舊的地下電纜。新零件已經訂購，所有路燈預計在 11 月 25 日前恢復正常。在那之前，我們已在最暗的路段架設了兩盞臨時照明燈。路面的坑洞也已在 11 月 10 日填補完成。\n\n至於道路反射鏡，我們需要先研究該轉角的交通狀況。如果核准設置，將在 12 月安裝。\n\n日後若要通報路燈故障，最快的方式是撥打 1999 專線。每根路燈桿上都有一塊寫著編號的小金屬牌，例如「HP-07」。提供這個編號，能幫助我們的工作人員快速找到那盞路燈。\n\n臨海區公所工務課\n林淑芬 敬上", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of Yi-jie's letter?", "options": ["To ask the district office to build a new MRT station", "To report that a student was hurt in a traffic accident", "To ask the district office to fix the broken streetlights", "To thank the district office for repairing a road"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一封信第一段說 I am writing to ask for your help with the streetlights on Heping Lane，第三段又請區公所 repair the lights as soon as possible，目的是請求修理故障的路燈。(A) 捷運站本來就有；(B) 學生摔車只是說明危險的例子，不是寫信的主要目的；(D) 他是提出請求，不是道謝，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "When the staff checked Heping Lane on November 8, how many streetlights were working?", "options": ["Four", "Seven", "Eight", "Twelve"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一封信第二段說這條路共有 12 盞路燈；回信第一段說 11 月 8 日檢查時發現 five lights were not working，所以正常的有 12 − 5 = 7 盞。(A) 四盞是奕傑原本以為壞掉的數量；(C) 是用 12 − 4 計算，但實際壞了五盞；(D) 是路燈總數，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the reply, what has the district office already done?", "options": ["Filled the hole in the road.", "Put up a traffic mirror at the corner.", "Replaced the old underground cable.", "Repaired all of the broken lights."], "answer": 0, "explain": "回信第一段最後一句：The hole in the road was filled on November 10，坑洞已在 11 月 10 日填補。(B) 反射鏡要先研究交通狀況，核准後才會在 12 月設置；(C)(D) 新零件才剛訂購，路燈預計 11 月 25 日前才會修好，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "If Yi-jie finds another broken streetlight in the future, what is the best thing for him to do?", "options": ["Write a letter to the school principal.", "Wait until the district office checks the road again.", "Put a temporary light next to the pole himself.", "Call the 1999 hotline and give the number on the pole."], "answer": 3, "explain": "回信最後一段說最快的通報方式是 call the 1999 hotline，並提供路燈桿上金屬牌的編號，方便工作人員快速找到。(A)(B) 都比打專線慢，也不是區公所建議的方式；(C) 臨時照明燈是區公所架設的，不是要學生自己裝，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"temporary\" in the reply is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very bright", "expensive", "used for a short time only", "broken"], "answer": 2, "explain": "回信第一段說路燈預計 11 月 25 日前修好，Until then（在那之前）先架設兩盞 temporary lights，可知這些燈只是「暫時」使用，接近 used for a short time only。(A)(B) 文中沒有提到燈的亮度或價格；(D) 這些燈是用來補足照明的，不是壞掉的燈，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a177", "title": "A Seat for Grandma: Booking Wheelchair Space at a Concert", "title_zh": "幫奶奶找個好位子：預訂音樂會輪椅席", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Email", "Arts"], "passage": "From: Kuo Ting-yu\nTo: Box Office, Starlight Concert Hall\nDate: September 2\nSubject: Wheelchair Seating for \"Songs of the Island\"\n\nHello,\n\nMy family would like to see the \"Songs of the Island\" concert on Saturday, October 3, at 7:30 p.m. My grandmother uses a wheelchair, so I am writing to ask about accessible seating.\n\nThere will be three of us: my grandmother, my mother and me. We hope to sit together. My grandmother has a disability card. Can she get a discount? Also, is there a place where we can park close to the entrance?\n\nThank you,\nKuo Ting-yu\n\nFrom: Box Office, Starlight Concert Hall\nTo: Kuo Ting-yu\nDate: September 3\nSubject: RE: Wheelchair Seating for \"Songs of the Island\"\n\nDear Ting-yu,\n\nThank you for your email. We have eight wheelchair spaces in Row M on the first floor. Each wheelchair space has a regular seat next to it for a companion, and other seats in the same row can also be booked, so your family can sit together. Tickets in Row M cost NT$1,200 each.\n\nA person with a disability card and one companion can each buy tickets at half price. Your grandmother will need to show her card at the entrance, so please remember to bring it.\n\nWheelchair spaces cannot be booked online. Please call our box office at (04) 2251-7788 between 10 a.m. and 6 p.m., Tuesday to Sunday. Tickets must be paid for within three days of booking.\n\nAccessible parking spaces are on the B1 level of our parking garage, right next to Elevator 3, which goes directly to the first-floor lobby. We suggest arriving at least 30 minutes early, as the lobby gets very crowded before the show.\n\nWe look forward to welcoming your family.\n\nBest regards,\nBox Office Team\nStarlight Concert Hall", "passage_zh": "寄件者：郭庭妤\n收件者：星光音樂廳售票處\n日期：9 月 2 日\n主旨：「島嶼之歌」音樂會輪椅座位\n\n您好：\n\n我們一家人想觀賞 10 月 3 日星期六晚上 7 點 30 分的「島嶼之歌」音樂會。我奶奶需要坐輪椅，所以想詢問無障礙座位的相關事宜。\n\n我們一共三個人：奶奶、媽媽和我，希望能坐在一起。我奶奶持有身心障礙證明，請問她可以享有優惠嗎？另外，入口附近是否有地方可以停車？\n\n謝謝您。\n郭庭妤\n\n寄件者：星光音樂廳售票處\n收件者：郭庭妤\n日期：9 月 3 日\n主旨：回覆：「島嶼之歌」音樂會輪椅座位\n\n庭妤您好：\n\n感謝您的來信。本廳一樓 M 排設有八個輪椅席位。每個輪椅席旁邊都有一個供陪同者使用的一般座位，同一排的其他座位也可以預訂，所以您的家人可以坐在一起。M 排的票價每張新台幣 1,200 元。\n\n持有身心障礙證明者本人及一名陪同者，均可購買半價票。您的奶奶入場時需出示證明，請記得攜帶。\n\n輪椅席位無法在網路上預訂，請於週二至週日上午 10 點到下午 6 點之間，撥打售票處電話 (04) 2251-7788 訂位。訂位後須在三天內完成付款。\n\n無障礙停車位位於停車場地下一樓，就在 3 號電梯旁，搭電梯可直達一樓大廳。由於演出前大廳會非常擁擠，建議您至少提早 30 分鐘抵達。\n\n期待您全家光臨。\n\n星光音樂廳售票處團隊 敬上", "questions": [{"q": "Why did Ting-yu write to the concert hall?", "options": ["To ask about seats and services for her grandmother, who uses a wheelchair", "To complain that the concert hall has no elevator", "To ask the concert hall to change the date of the concert", "To ask for a job at the concert hall's box office"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一封信第一段說奶奶需要坐輪椅，I am writing to ask about accessible seating，第二段又詢問能否坐在一起、有沒有優惠和停車位，目的是詢問奶奶的座位與相關服務。(B) 回信中提到有 3 號電梯，她也沒有抱怨；(C)(D) 信中都沒有提到，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "If the family buys three tickets in Row M, how much will they pay in total?", "options": ["NT$1,800", "NT$2,400", "NT$3,000", "NT$3,600"], "answer": 1, "explain": "回信第一、二段：M 排每張 1,200 元，持身心障礙證明者本人及一名陪同者可買半價票。奶奶 600 元 + 一位陪同者 600 元 + 另一人全票 1,200 元 = 2,400 元。(A) 誤以為三人都半價；(C) 只算了奶奶半價；(D) 完全沒有打折，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about booking a wheelchair space?", "options": ["It can be done on the concert hall's website.", "It can be done by phone on Monday mornings.", "Tickets can be paid for on the night of the concert.", "It must be done by phone during box office hours."], "answer": 3, "explain": "回信第三段：Wheelchair spaces cannot be booked online，必須在週二至週日上午 10 點到下午 6 點打電話到售票處訂位。(A) 輪椅席不能網路訂位；(B) 售票處週一不接受電話；(C) 訂位後三天內就要付款，不能等到演出當晚，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What should Ting-yu's grandmother bring to the concert?", "options": ["A letter from her doctor", "Her parking ticket from the box office", "Her disability card", "A printed copy of this email"], "answer": 2, "explain": "回信第二段：Your grandmother will need to show her card at the entrance, so please remember to bring it.，奶奶入場時要出示身心障礙證明，所以要記得攜帶。(A)(B)(D) 回信中都沒有要求攜帶這些東西，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"companion\" in the reply is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a person who sells tickets", "a person who performs on stage", "a person who drives a car", "a person who goes somewhere with another person"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Each wheelchair space has a regular seat next to it for a companion，輪椅席旁的座位是給「陪同者」坐的，也就是陪著奶奶一起來的人，companion 意思接近 a person who goes somewhere with another person。(A) 是售票員，(B) 是表演者，(C) 是司機，都不是坐在輪椅席旁的人，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a178", "title": "Catch the Last Boat Back: An Island Ferry Timetable", "title_zh": "別錯過最後一班船：離島渡輪時刻表", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Schedule", "Travel"], "passage": "BLUE TURTLE FERRY\nDonggang ⇄ Xiaoliuqiu (Baisha Port)\nWinter Timetable: November 1 – March 31\n\nThe trip takes about 25 minutes. Please arrive at the pier at least 20 minutes before your boat leaves. Boarding closes 5 minutes before departure, and late passengers will not be allowed on.\n\nDonggang → Xiaoliuqiu\n7:00 | 8:30 | 10:00 | 11:30 | 13:30 | 15:00 | 16:30*\n\nXiaoliuqiu → Donggang\n7:40 | 9:10 | 10:40 | 12:10 | 14:10 | 15:40 | 17:10*\n\n*Saturdays, Sundays and national holidays only\n\nTicket Prices (NT$)\nAdult: one way 230 / round trip 410\nChild (6–12) and senior (65+): one way 120 / round trip 220\nChildren under 6: free (one per paying adult)\nBicycle: 100 each way\n\nGood to Know\n• A round-trip ticket is good only on the date printed on it. Your return trip is not fixed to one boat: just show your ticket at the pier and take any boat back that day. Seats are first come, first served.\n• Book online at least one day ahead and save NT$20 on each round-trip ticket.\n• Boats may be cancelled because of strong winds or typhoon warnings. Before you leave home, check our Facebook page or call the ticket office.\n• Sea turtles live in the waters around the island. Please enjoy them from a distance, and never touch or feed them.\n\nTicket Office: Donggang Pier, Building B\nOpen 6:30 a.m.–5:30 p.m. daily | Tel: (08) 833-5126", "passage_zh": "藍海龜渡輪\n東港 ⇄ 小琉球（白沙港）\n冬季時刻表：11 月 1 日至 3 月 31 日\n\n航程約 25 分鐘。請至少在開船前 20 分鐘抵達碼頭。開船前 5 分鐘停止登船，遲到的旅客將無法上船。\n\n東港 → 小琉球\n7:00 | 8:30 | 10:00 | 11:30 | 13:30 | 15:00 | 16:30*\n\n小琉球 → 東港\n7:40 | 9:10 | 10:40 | 12:10 | 14:10 | 15:40 | 17:10*\n\n*僅週六、週日及國定假日行駛\n\n票價（新臺幣）\n全票：單程 230／來回 410\n孩童（6–12 歲）及敬老（65 歲以上）：單程 120／來回 220\n未滿 6 歲兒童：免費（每位付費成人限帶一名）\n自行車：每趟 100\n\n注意事項\n• 來回票僅限票面上印的日期使用。回程不限定班次：當天只要在碼頭出示船票，就可以搭任何一班船回來。座位先到先坐。\n• 至少提前一天上網訂票，每張來回票可省 NT$20。\n• 遇到強風或颱風警報時，船班可能取消。出門前請先查看我們的臉書粉絲專頁，或打電話到售票處詢問。\n• 島嶼周圍的海域有海龜棲息。請保持距離欣賞，絕對不要觸摸或餵食牠們。\n\n售票處：東港碼頭 B 棟\n每日上午 6:30 至下午 5:30 營業｜電話：(08) 833-5126", "questions": [{"q": "Mr. Huang is spending Thursday, December 10, on Xiaoliuqiu. What is the latest boat he can take back to Donggang that day?", "options": ["The 14:10 boat", "The 15:40 boat", "The 16:30 boat", "The 17:10 boat"], "answer": 1, "explain": "「小琉球 → 東港」時刻表中最晚的 17:10 標有 *，而星號說明為 Saturdays, Sundays and national holidays only（僅週六、週日及國定假日行駛）。12 月 10 日是平日的星期四，因此最晚只能搭 15:40 的船。(A) 14:10 不是最晚的一班；(C) 16:30 是東港開往小琉球的船；(D) 17:10 平日不開，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Mrs. Lin buys round-trip tickets at the ticket office for herself, her husband, their 10-year-old daughter and their 4-year-old son. How much does she pay?", "options": ["NT$1,040", "NT$980", "NT$1,260", "NT$1,640"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Ticket Prices 段：兩位大人來回票 410 × 2 = 820；10 歲女兒屬於 Child (6–12)，來回 220；4 歲兒子未滿 6 歲，由付費大人帶著免費。合計 820 + 220 = 1,040 元。(B) 是誤用網路訂票優惠的金額，但她是在售票處買票；(C) 多算了兒子的 220 元；(D) 是把四人都算成全票，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "A visitor wants to take the 10:00 boat from Donggang. Following the ferry company's advice, by what time should he arrive at the pier?", "options": ["9:30", "9:35", "9:40", "9:55"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段說 Please arrive at the pier at least 20 minutes before your boat leaves（至少在開船前 20 分鐘抵達碼頭），10:00 往前推 20 分鐘是 9:40。(D) 9:55 是停止登船的時間，並非建議抵達的時間；(A)(B) 雖然更早抵達也可以，但題目問的是最晚應在幾點前抵達，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about round-trip tickets?", "options": ["Passengers must come back on a boat at a fixed time.", "They can be used on any day within one month.", "They are cheaper if they are booked online a day or more ahead.", "They include free transport for one bicycle."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Good to Know 第二點說 Book online at least one day ahead and save NT$20 on each round-trip ticket（至少提前一天上網訂票，每張來回票省 20 元）。(A) 第一點說回程 is not fixed to one boat，當天任何一班都能搭；(B) 第一點說只限票面日期使用；(D) 票價表寫自行車每趟另收 100 元，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "A typhoon warning has been announced on the morning of Ms. Chen's trip. What should she do first?", "options": ["Go to the pier earlier than usual to get a seat.", "Buy her tickets online for the same day.", "Wait for the weekend boat at 16:30.", "Check the ferry's Facebook page or call the ticket office before leaving home."], "answer": 3, "explain": "Good to Know 第三點說遇強風或颱風警報時船班可能取消，Before you leave home, check our Facebook page or call the ticket office（出門前先查看臉書粉絲專頁或打電話到售票處）。(A) 船班可能取消，提早去碼頭沒有幫助；(B) 網路訂票須提前一天，與颱風無關；(C) 16:30 的船只在週末及假日行駛，文中也未提及颱風時要改搭，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a179", "title": "One Hundred Minutes to Eat: A Hot Pot Buffet Price List", "title_zh": "限時一百分鐘：火鍋吃到飽價目表", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Food", "Money"], "passage": "GOLDEN POT HOT POT BUFFET\nAll You Can Eat in 100 Minutes\n\nPrices per Person (NT$)\nWeekday Lunch (Mon.–Fri., 11:30 a.m.–4:00 p.m.): 480\nWeekday Dinner (Mon.–Fri., 5:00–10:00 p.m.): 580\nWeekend & Holiday Lunch: 580\nWeekend & Holiday Dinner: 640\n\nChildren's Prices (by height)\nUnder 100 cm: free\n100–140 cm: half price\nOver 140 cm: adult price\n\nA 10% service charge will be added to every bill.\n\nYour Price Includes\n• Your own small pot with one soup base: spicy, tomato, mushroom or herbal\n• Self-service bar: vegetables, tofu, seafood, noodles, sauces, drinks and ice cream\n• One plate of pork, beef or lamb per person each time you order. To order more meat, use the tablet on your table.\n\nExtras\nWagyu beef plate: NT$280\nTwo-flavor pot (two soups in one pot): +NT$50 per pot\n\nHouse Rules\n• Your 100 minutes start when you sit down. Last orders are taken 15 minutes before your time is up.\n• Please take only what you can finish. If more than half a plate of food is left uneaten, we will charge NT$100.\n• Outside food and drinks are not allowed, but birthday cakes are welcome.\n• Birthday Special: On weekdays, one guest per table whose birthday is in that month eats for half price (ID required).\n\nReservations\nCall (02) 2736-8810 or book on our website. We will hold your table for 10 minutes after your booking time.", "passage_zh": "金鍋火鍋吃到飽\n100 分鐘無限享用\n\n每人價格（新臺幣）\n平日午餐（週一至週五，上午 11:30 至下午 4:00）：480\n平日晚餐（週一至週五，下午 5:00 至晚上 10:00）：580\n週末及假日午餐：580\n週末及假日晚餐：640\n\n兒童價格（依身高計算）\n未滿 100 公分：免費\n100 至 140 公分：半價\n超過 140 公分：大人價格\n\n每筆帳單另加收一成服務費。\n\n價格包含\n• 個人小鍋一份，湯底四選一：麻辣、番茄、菇菇或藥膳\n• 自助吧：蔬菜、豆腐、海鮮、麵條、醬料、飲料和冰淇淋\n• 每次點餐，每人可點豬肉、牛肉或羊肉一盤。想加點肉品，請使用桌上的平板。\n\n加購項目\n和牛肉盤：NT$280\n鴛鴦鍋（一鍋兩種湯底）：每鍋加 NT$50\n\n用餐規定\n• 100 分鐘從入座時開始計算。用餐時間結束前 15 分鐘停止點餐。\n• 請適量取用。若剩下超過半盤沒吃完的食物，將加收 NT$100。\n• 禁止攜帶外食和飲料，但歡迎自備生日蛋糕。\n• 壽星優惠：平日用餐，每桌一位當月壽星享半價優惠（須出示證件）。\n\n訂位\n請撥 (02) 2736-8810 或上官網訂位。訂位時間過後，座位將為您保留 10 分鐘。", "questions": [{"q": "Mr. and Mrs. Wang and their son, who is 120 cm tall, have dinner at Golden Pot on a Sunday. How much is their bill, including the service charge?", "options": ["NT$1,600", "NT$2,112", "NT$1,595", "NT$1,760"], "answer": 3, "explain": "週日晚餐屬於 Weekend & Holiday Dinner，大人每人 640 元，兩人 1,280 元；兒子 120 公分，屬於 100–140 cm 的半價，320 元，小計 1,600 元。再依 A 10% service charge will be added to every bill 加一成服務費：1,600 × 1.1 = 1,760 元。(A) 沒加服務費；(B) 把兒子算成全價；(C) 誤用平日晚餐價格，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How can customers get more meat after their first plate?", "options": ["By ordering it on the tablet at their table", "By taking it from the self-service bar", "By paying NT$280 for each extra plate", "By asking for a two-flavor pot"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Your Price Includes 第三點說 To order more meat, use the tablet on your table（想加點肉品，請使用桌上的平板）。(B) 自助吧提供的是蔬菜、豆腐、海鮮等，並沒有提到肉品；(C) NT$280 是加購和牛肉盤的價格，一般肉品不必另外付費；(D) 鴛鴦鍋是湯底，與加肉無關，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "A group of friends sits down at 6:10 p.m. What is the latest time they can order more food?", "options": ["7:20 p.m.", "7:35 p.m.", "7:50 p.m.", "8:05 p.m."], "answer": 1, "explain": "House Rules 第一點：100 分鐘從入座時開始計算，6:10 加 100 分鐘是 7:50 用餐結束；Last orders are taken 15 minutes before your time is up（結束前 15 分鐘停止點餐），所以最後點餐時間是 7:35。(C) 7:50 是用餐時間結束；(A)(D) 計算錯誤，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What will happen if a customer leaves most of a plate of shrimp uneaten?", "options": ["The shrimp will be packed for the customer to take home.", "The customer's time limit will be cut by 15 minutes.", "The customer will have to pay an extra NT$100.", "The customer will no longer be allowed to use the self-service bar."], "answer": 2, "explain": "House Rules 第二點說 If more than half a plate of food is left uneaten, we will charge NT$100（剩下超過半盤沒吃完，將加收 100 元）。剩下大半盤蝦子就符合這個情況。(A)(B)(D) 文中都沒有這些規定，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"hold\" in the last line is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["carry", "keep", "clean", "order"], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一句 We will hold your table for 10 minutes after your booking time，意思是訂位時間過後，餐廳會把座位「保留」十分鐘，hold 在此等於 keep（保留）。(A) carry 是「攜帶、搬運」，是 hold 的另一個意思，但不符合這裡的文意；(C) clean「清理」、(D) order「點餐」都不符合句意，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a180", "title": "Before You Dive In: Swimming Pool Hours and Tickets", "title_zh": "下水之前先看看：游泳池開放時間與票價", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Sports", "Schedule"], "passage": "RIVERSIDE SPORTS CENTER\nIndoor Swimming Pool: Hours and Tickets\n\nOpening Hours (Tuesday–Sunday)\nMorning: 6:00 a.m.–12:00 noon\nAfternoon: 1:00–5:00 p.m.\nEvening: 6:00–9:30 p.m.\nThe pool is closed from 12:00 to 1:00 p.m. and from 5:00 to 6:00 p.m. for water testing and cleaning. All swimmers must leave the water at these times.\nClosed every Monday. When a Monday is a national holiday, the pool opens that day and closes on Tuesday instead.\n\nLanes\nLanes 1–2: Fast swimmers\nLanes 3–5: Slow and normal swimmers\nLane 6: Swimming lessons (closed to the public on weekday afternoons)\n\nTicket Prices (NT$)\nSingle Ticket (one session only)\nAdult: 110\nStudent (with ID) and senior (65+): 80\nChildren under 110 cm: free with a paying adult\nEarly Bird (enter before 7:00 a.m.): 70 for everyone\n10-Visit Card: 900 (valid for 6 months; can be shared with family members)\nMonthly Pass: 1,500 (for one person only; photo required)\n\nPool Rules\n• Swim caps must be worn in the pool. Caps are sold at the front desk for NT$60.\n• Take a shower before you get into the water.\n• Children under 12 must swim with an adult.\n• Only bottled water is allowed in the pool area.\n• Lockers take NT$10 coins. Your coin comes back when you open the locker.\n\nFront Desk: (04) 2258-3317", "passage_zh": "河濱運動中心\n室內游泳池：開放時間與票價\n\n開放時間（週二至週日）\n上午場：上午 6:00 至中午 12:00\n下午場：下午 1:00 至 5:00\n晚上場：晚上 6:00 至 9:30\n中午 12:00 至下午 1:00，以及下午 5:00 至 6:00，為水質檢測及清潔時間，泳池暫停開放。這些時段所有泳客都必須離開水中。\n每週一休館。如遇週一為國定假日，當天照常開放，改為週二休館。\n\n水道\n第 1–2 道：快速泳道\n第 3–5 道：慢速及一般泳道\n第 6 道：游泳教學（平日下午不開放民眾使用）\n\n票價（新臺幣）\n單次票（限用一個時段）\n全票：110\n學生（須出示學生證）及敬老（65 歲以上）：80\n身高未滿 110 公分兒童：由購票大人陪同免費入場\n早鳥票（上午 7:00 前入場）：一律 70\n十次卡：900（6 個月內有效；可與家人共用）\n月票：1,500（限本人使用，須附照片）\n\n泳池規定\n• 在泳池內必須戴泳帽。服務櫃檯有販售泳帽，每頂 NT$60。\n• 下水前請先淋浴。\n• 未滿 12 歲兒童必須由大人陪同游泳。\n• 泳池區只能攜帶瓶裝水。\n• 置物櫃使用 10 元硬幣，打開櫃子時硬幣會退還。\n\n服務櫃檯：(04) 2258-3317", "questions": [{"q": "At 10 a.m. on a Saturday, Kevin, a senior-high student with his student ID, goes swimming with his mother and his 68-year-old grandfather. They each buy a single ticket. How much do they pay in total?", "options": ["NT$210", "NT$270", "NT$300", "NT$330"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Ticket Prices 段：Kevin 是帶學生證的學生，80 元；68 歲的爺爺屬於 senior (65+) 也是 80 元，媽媽是全票 110 元，合計 80 + 80 + 110 = 270 元。(A) 是三張早鳥票的價格，但早鳥票限上午 7:00 前入場；(C) 把爺爺算成全票；(D) 是三張全票的價格，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Mrs. Wu wants to buy a ticket that both she and her husband can use. Which ticket should she buy?", "options": ["Monthly Pass", "Single Ticket", "Early Bird Ticket", "10-Visit Card"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Ticket Prices 段說 10-Visit Card 可 be shared with family members（可與家人共用），符合夫妻共用的需求。(A) 月票 for one person only（限本人使用）；(B)(C) 單次票和早鳥票都只能用一次，不是兩人可以重複使用的票，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Jason arrives at the pool at 12:20 p.m. on a Wednesday. What will probably happen?", "options": ["He will have to wait about 40 minutes before he can swim.", "He will be able to swim in Lane 6 until 1:00 p.m.", "He will be able to buy an Early Bird ticket.", "He will find the pool closed for the whole day."], "answer": 0, "explain": "Opening Hours 段說 The pool is closed from 12:00 to 1:00 p.m. for water testing and cleaning，所以 12:20 抵達要等到 1:00 才能下水，約 40 分鐘。(B) 這段時間所有人都必須離開水中，而且第 6 道平日下午供游泳課使用，不開放民眾；(C) 早鳥票限上午 7:00 前入場；(D) 星期三照常開放，只是中午暫停一小時，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In a week when Monday is a national holiday, on which day will the pool be closed?", "options": ["Sunday", "Monday", "Tuesday", "It will not close that week."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Opening Hours 段最後一句：When a Monday is a national holiday, the pool opens that day and closes on Tuesday instead（週一遇國定假日則照常開放，改為週二休館）。(A) 週日一向開放；(B) 國定假日的週一照常開放；(D) 文中說改在週二休館，並非整週不休，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"valid\" in the line about the 10-Visit Card is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["able to be used", "sold at a lower price", "shared with others", "kept in a safe place"], "answer": 0, "explain": "10-Visit Card: 900 (valid for 6 months...)，意思是十次卡在六個月內「有效、可以使用」，valid 即 able to be used。(B) 是價格，與 valid 無關；(C) 共用是同一行後半句 can be shared 的意思，不是 valid 的意思；(D) 與文意不符，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a181", "title": "Which Doctor, Which Day? A Clinic Timetable", "title_zh": "哪天找哪位醫師？診所門診時間表", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Health", "Schedule"], "passage": "SUNRISE FAMILY CLINIC\nConsultation Hours\n\nSessions\nMorning: 8:30 a.m.–12:00 noon\nAfternoon: 2:30–5:30 p.m.\nEvening: 6:30–9:00 p.m.\n\nDoctor Schedule\nMonday: Morning – Dr. Lin | Afternoon – Dr. Chou | Evening – Dr. Lin\nTuesday: Morning – Dr. Lin | Afternoon – closed | Evening – Dr. Chou\nWednesday: Morning – Dr. Chou | Afternoon – Dr. Chou | Evening – closed\nThursday: Morning – Dr. Lin | Afternoon – Dr. Chou | Evening – Dr. Lin\nFriday: Morning – Dr. Chou | Afternoon – Dr. Lin | Evening – Dr. Chou\nSaturday: Morning – Dr. Lin | Afternoon and evening – closed\nClosed on Sundays and national holidays.\n\nOur Doctors\nDr. Lin Mei-hua: family medicine, children's health\nDr. Chou Wen-chieh: family medicine, skin problems\n\nRegistration\n• Registration closes 30 minutes before the end of each session.\n• You can book up to 7 days ahead on our website or app. If you book online, please arrive at least 10 minutes before your appointment time.\n• Registration fee: NT$150\n• Always bring your NHI (National Health Insurance) card. If you forget it, you will pay a NT$500 deposit. We will return the money if you bring your card to us within 7 days.\n\nFlu Shots\nAvailable from October 1, Monday to Friday mornings only. Please book by phone.\n\nNotice\nDr. Chou will be attending a medical conference from October 27 to 29. On those days, Dr. Lin will take over his morning sessions, and his afternoon and evening sessions will be cancelled.\n\nTel: (06) 275-4418", "passage_zh": "晨光家庭醫學科診所\n門診時間\n\n看診時段\n上午診：上午 8:30 至中午 12:00\n下午診：下午 2:30 至 5:30\n夜診：晚上 6:30 至 9:00\n\n醫師門診表\n星期一：上午－林醫師｜下午－周醫師｜晚上－林醫師\n星期二：上午－林醫師｜下午－休診｜晚上－周醫師\n星期三：上午－周醫師｜下午－周醫師｜晚上－休診\n星期四：上午－林醫師｜下午－周醫師｜晚上－林醫師\n星期五：上午－周醫師｜下午－林醫師｜晚上－周醫師\n星期六：上午－林醫師｜下午及晚上－休診\n週日及國定假日休診。\n\n本院醫師\n林美華醫師：家庭醫學、兒童保健\n周文傑醫師：家庭醫學、皮膚問題\n\n掛號須知\n• 每個時段結束前 30 分鐘停止掛號。\n• 可透過官網或 App 預約七天內的門診。網路預約者，請在預約時間前至少 10 分鐘到達。\n• 掛號費：NT$150\n• 請務必攜帶健保卡。如果忘了帶，須先繳交 NT$500 押金；只要在七天內把健保卡帶來診所，我們就會退還這筆錢。\n\n流感疫苗\n10 月 1 日起開始施打，僅限週一至週五上午。請以電話預約。\n\n公告\n周醫師將於 10 月 27 日至 29 日參加醫學研討會。這幾天他的上午診由林醫師代診，下午診和夜診則暫停看診。\n\n電話：(06) 275-4418", "questions": [{"q": "Lucy has a skin problem, and she can only come to the clinic after 6 p.m. Which day should she choose?", "options": ["Monday", "Wednesday", "Thursday", "Friday"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Our Doctors 段說周醫師專長是 skin problems（皮膚問題）；醫師門診表中，周醫師看夜診的是星期二和星期五，選項中只有星期五符合。(A)(C) 星期一、四的夜診是林醫師；(B) 星期三晚上休診，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What is the latest time a patient can register for an afternoon session?", "options": ["4:30 p.m.", "5:00 p.m.", "5:30 p.m.", "6:00 p.m."], "answer": 1, "explain": "下午診是 2:30–5:30 p.m.，Registration 第一點說 Registration closes 30 minutes before the end of each session（每個時段結束前 30 分鐘停止掛號），5:30 往前推 30 分鐘是 5:00。(C) 5:30 是下午診結束的時間；(A)(D) 計算錯誤，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Wednesday, October 28?", "options": ["Patients can see Dr. Lin in the morning.", "Dr. Chou will see patients in the afternoon.", "The clinic will be open in the evening.", "The clinic will be closed all day."], "answer": 0, "explain": "10 月 28 日在 Notice 所說的 10 月 27 日至 29 日之間。星期三原本上午、下午都是周醫師看診，公告說 Dr. Lin will take over his morning sessions（上午診由林醫師代診），下午診則取消，所以當天上午可以看林醫師。(B) 周醫師的下午診取消；(C) 星期三晚上本來就休診；(D) 上午仍有門診，並非全天休診，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What happens if a patient forgets to bring the NHI card?", "options": ["The patient cannot see a doctor that day.", "The patient pays NT$500, which can be returned later.", "The patient only needs to pay the NT$150 registration fee.", "The patient has to book again online."], "answer": 1, "explain": "Registration 最後一點：If you forget it, you will pay a NT$500 deposit. We will return the money if you bring your card to us within 7 days（忘了帶健保卡要先付 500 元押金，七天內帶卡來就退還）。(A) 文中沒有說不能看診；(C) 除了掛號費還要付押金；(D) 文中沒有這項規定，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Mrs. Ho wants her son to get a flu shot at the clinic. What should she do?", "options": ["Bring him on Saturday morning without booking.", "Book a time online for a weekday evening.", "Call the clinic to book a time on a weekday morning.", "Bring him to the clinic before October 1."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Flu Shots 段說疫苗 Monday to Friday mornings only（只在週一至週五上午施打），而且 Please book by phone（請以電話預約）。(A) 星期六不施打，也須事先預約；(B) 只限平日上午，且要用電話預約；(D) 疫苗從 10 月 1 日才開始施打，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a182", "title": "Coins, Blankets and Sneakers: A Self-Service Laundry Price List", "title_zh": "硬幣、毛毯和球鞋：自助洗衣店價目表", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Money", "Life"], "passage": "BUBBLES 24-HOUR SELF-SERVICE LAUNDRY\nPrice List\n\nWashers (detergent added automatically, free of charge)\nSmall (10 kg), for everyday clothes: NT$50, about 35 minutes\nLarge (18 kg), for blankets and comforters: NT$80, about 45 minutes\nFabric softener: +NT$10\n\nDryers\nNT$10 for every 8 minutes\nWe suggest: 40 minutes for clothes (NT$50); 56 minutes for blankets (NT$70)\n\nShoe Washer (sneakers only, up to 4 pairs): NT$50\nShoe Dryer: NT$20 for 20 minutes\n\nHow to Pay\nAll machines take NT$10 coins only. Use the change machine by the door to change NT$100 bills. It does not accept NT$500 or NT$1,000 bills, but the convenience store next door can give you change for them.\n\nPlease Remember\n• Check your pockets before washing. A single pen or tissue can ruin a whole load.\n• Do not put shoes or pet beds in the clothes washers. Use the shoe washer for sneakers.\n• Please collect your laundry promptly. If clothes are left in a finished machine for more than 15 minutes, other customers may move them into the baskets on the shelf.\n• Items left for more than 3 days will be given to charity.\n• Security cameras watch this shop 24 hours a day.\n\nMachine not working? Write down the machine number and call 0912-468-357 (8:00 a.m.–11:00 p.m.).", "passage_zh": "泡泡 24 小時自助洗衣店\n價目表\n\n洗衣機（自動加入洗衣精，不另收費）\n小型（10 公斤），適合日常衣物：NT$50，約 35 分鐘\n大型（18 公斤），適合毛毯和棉被：NT$80，約 45 分鐘\n柔軟精：加 NT$10\n\n烘衣機\n每 8 分鐘 NT$10\n建議：衣物烘 40 分鐘（NT$50）；毛毯烘 56 分鐘（NT$70）\n\n洗鞋機（限運動鞋，最多 4 雙）：NT$50\n烘鞋機：每 20 分鐘 NT$20\n\n付款方式\n所有機器只收 10 元硬幣。請使用門邊的兌幣機兌換百元鈔。兌幣機不收五百元和千元鈔，但隔壁的便利商店可以幫你換零錢。\n\n注意事項\n• 洗衣前請檢查口袋。一支原子筆或一張面紙就可能毀了整桶衣物。\n• 請勿將鞋子或寵物睡墊放進洗衣機。運動鞋請使用洗鞋機。\n• 請盡快取走衣物。洗好的衣物若在機器裡放超過 15 分鐘，可能會被其他顧客移到架上的籃子裡。\n• 超過三天未取回的物品將捐給慈善機構。\n• 本店 24 小時都有監視器。\n\n機器故障了嗎？請記下機器號碼，並撥打 0912-468-357（上午 8:00 至晚上 11:00）。", "questions": [{"q": "Mrs. Tsai washes a blanket in a large washer with fabric softener and then dries it for the suggested time. How much does she spend?", "options": ["NT$150", "NT$160", "NT$140", "NT$130"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Washers 段：大型洗衣機 NT$80，加柔軟精 +NT$10；Dryers 段建議毛毯烘 56 分鐘，NT$70。合計 80 + 10 + 70 = 160 元。(A) 漏算柔軟精；(C) 用了衣物的烘乾費 50 元（80 + 10 + 50）；(D) 用了小型洗衣機的價格（50 + 10 + 70），故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Tom arrives at the laundry with only a NT$500 bill. What should he do first?", "options": ["Put the bill into the change machine by the door.", "Ask a staff member at the counter for coins.", "Call 0912-468-357 for help.", "Get change at the convenience store next door."], "answer": 3, "explain": "How to Pay 段說兌幣機 does not accept NT$500 or NT$1,000 bills, but the convenience store next door can give you change for them（不收五百元和千元鈔，但隔壁便利商店可以換零錢）。(A) 兌幣機不收五百元鈔；(B) 這是自助洗衣店，文中沒有提到櫃檯人員；(C) 電話是機器故障時才打的，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Amy's clothes finished washing, but she does not come back for half an hour. What may happen?", "options": ["Another customer may move her clothes into a basket.", "Her clothes will be given to charity.", "She will have to pay for another wash.", "The machine will start drying her clothes automatically."], "answer": 0, "explain": "Please Remember 第三點說洗好的衣物在機器裡放超過 15 分鐘，other customers may move them into the baskets on the shelf（其他顧客可能把衣物移到架上的籃子裡）。半小時已超過 15 分鐘。(B) 要超過三天才會捐出；(C)(D) 文中都沒有這樣的規定，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"promptly\" in the \"Please Remember\" section is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["carefully", "quietly", "without delay", "one by one"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Please collect your laundry promptly. 後面接著說衣物放超過 15 分鐘就可能被別人移走，可知 promptly 是「立刻、不拖延」的意思，與 without delay 最接近。(A) carefully「小心地」、(B) quietly「安靜地」、(D) one by one「一個接一個」都與上下文不符，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about Bubbles Laundry?", "options": ["Sneakers should be cleaned in the shoe washer.", "Customers have to pay extra for detergent.", "The shop is open only from 8 a.m. to 11 p.m.", "Things left behind are thrown away after one day."], "answer": 0, "explain": "Please Remember 第二點說 Use the shoe washer for sneakers（運動鞋請使用洗鞋機）。(B) Washers 標題寫明洗衣精自動加入、free of charge（不另收費）；(C) 店名就是 24-HOUR，上午 8 點到晚上 11 點是故障專線的服務時間；(D) 遺留物品超過三天才會捐給慈善機構，並非一天後丟掉，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a183", "title": "A Desk of Your Own: A Study Center Price List", "title_zh": "專屬於你的書桌：K 書中心價目表", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Education", "Money"], "passage": "QUIET OWL STUDY CENTER\nOpen 7:00 a.m.–12:00 midnight, every day of the year\n\nSeat Prices (NT$)\nHourly: 35 per hour\nDay Pass (any time during opening hours): 250\nMonthly Pass – Open Seat (sit at any free desk): 3,200\nMonthly Pass – Fixed Seat (your own desk with a drawer and a lamp): 4,200\nStudent Discount: NT$400 off any monthly pass with a valid senior-high or college student ID\n\nExtras\nLocker: NT$300 per month\nDiscussion Room (up to 6 people): NT$200 per hour for the whole room; book at the front desk\nPrinting: NT$2 per page (black and white)\n\nFree for Everyone\nWi-Fi, drinking water, a power socket at every desk, and a lounge with a microwave\n\nQuiet Rules\n• The study rooms are silent areas. Set your phone to silent and take calls in the hallway.\n• Food may be eaten only in the lounge. Drinks with lids are allowed at your desk.\n• Open seats: If you leave your desk for more than 2 hours, our staff will move your things to the front desk so that others can use the seat.\n• Please do not save seats for friends.\n\nExam Season Offer\nBuy a monthly pass between March 1 and March 31 and get one extra week free.\n\nFront Desk: (02) 2365-7091", "passage_zh": "安靜貓頭鷹 K 書中心\n開放時間：上午 7:00 至午夜 12:00，全年無休\n\n座位價格（新臺幣）\n計時：每小時 35\n日票（營業時間內任何時段皆可使用）：250\n月票－自由座（任選空位）：3,200\n月票－固定座（專屬書桌，附抽屜和檯燈）：4,200\n學生優惠：持有效的高中職或大專學生證，購買任何月票皆折抵 NT$400\n\n加購項目\n置物櫃：每月 NT$300\n討論室（最多 6 人）：整間每小時 NT$200，請至櫃檯預約\n列印：黑白每頁 NT$2\n\n免費提供\nWi-Fi、飲用水、每張書桌都有插座，以及附微波爐的休息區\n\n安靜守則\n• 自習室為靜音區。請將手機調成靜音，並到走廊接聽電話。\n• 只能在休息區吃東西。有蓋的飲料可以放在書桌上。\n• 自由座：離開座位超過兩小時，工作人員會把您的物品移到櫃檯，讓其他人使用座位。\n• 請勿替朋友佔位。\n\n考季優惠\n3 月 1 日至 3 月 31 日期間購買月票，加贈一週。\n\n服務櫃檯：(02) 2365-7091", "questions": [{"q": "Amy plans to study at the center from 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. this Saturday only. Which is the cheapest choice for her?", "options": ["Paying by the hour", "Buying a Day Pass", "Buying an Open Seat monthly pass", "Buying a Fixed Seat monthly pass"], "answer": 1, "explain": "上午 9 點到下午 5 點共 8 小時，依 Seat Prices 計時每小時 35 元，要 35 × 8 = 280 元；Day Pass 只要 250 元，比較便宜。(A) 280 元較貴；(C)(D) 月票要三、四千元，只讀一天並不划算，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Ken, a senior-high student, shows his student ID, buys a Fixed Seat monthly pass and rents a locker for the month. How much does he pay?", "options": ["NT$3,800", "NT$4,500", "NT$4,100", "NT$3,100"], "answer": 2, "explain": "固定座月票 4,200 元，持學生證折抵 400 元（Student Discount），再加置物櫃每月 300 元：4,200 − 400 + 300 = 4,100 元。(A) 漏算置物櫃；(B) 沒有扣學生優惠；(D) 誤用自由座月票的價格，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Four classmates want to use the discussion room for two hours to practice a presentation. How much will they pay in total?", "options": ["NT$200", "NT$800", "NT$1,600", "NT$400"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Extras 段：Discussion Room (up to 6 people): NT$200 per hour for the whole room（整間每小時 200 元），不是按人數計價，四人未超過六人上限，用兩小時共 200 × 2 = 400 元。(A) 只算了一小時；(B)(C) 誤把價格當成每人計算，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Joyce has an open seat. She leaves her desk for three hours to have lunch and see a movie. What will happen?", "options": ["Her things will be moved to the front desk.", "She will have to pay for a Day Pass again.", "Her friend will save the seat for her.", "She will lose her monthly pass."], "answer": 0, "explain": "Quiet Rules 第三點：Open seats: If you leave your desk for more than 2 hours, our staff will move your things to the front desk（自由座離開超過兩小時，工作人員會把物品移到櫃檯）。三小時已超過兩小時。(B)(D) 文中沒有這些規定；(C) 第四點說 Please do not save seats for friends（請勿替朋友佔位），故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the Quiet Rules, which of the following is allowed?", "options": ["Eating a lunch box at your desk", "Answering a phone call in the hallway", "Saving a seat for a friend who is coming later", "Leaving your phone's ringer on in the study room"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Quiet Rules 第一點說 Set your phone to silent and take calls in the hallway（手機調成靜音，到走廊接電話），所以在走廊接電話是允許的。(A) 第二點說只能在休息區吃東西；(C) 第四點禁止替朋友佔位；(D) 自習室是靜音區，手機須調成靜音，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a184", "title": "Feeding Time! An Aquarium's Daily Program", "title_zh": "餵食時間到！水族館每日節目表", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Nature", "Schedule"], "passage": "CORAL BAY AQUARIUM\nToday's Program\n\nOpening Hours: 9:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m. (last entry 4:00 p.m.)\n\nDaily Shows and Activities\n10:00 a.m. Penguin Breakfast | Penguin Coast (2F) | 15 min\n10:30 a.m. Diver Feeding | Big Ocean Tank (1F) | 20 min\n11:00 a.m. Sea Lion Show | Ocean Theater (3F) | 25 min\n1:30 p.m. Touch Pool Time | Tide Pool Area (1F) | 30 min\n2:30 p.m. Sea Lion Show | Ocean Theater (3F) | 25 min\n3:30 p.m. Shark Talk | Shark Tunnel (B1) | 20 min\n4:15 p.m. Penguin Dinner | Penguin Coast (2F) | 15 min\n\nGood to Know\n• Ocean Theater: The theater seats 300 people. Doors open 15 minutes before each show. Rows 1–3 are the \"Splash Zone,\" so you may get wet!\n• Touch Pool Time: Only 20 children (ages 4–12) can join each session. Free tickets are given out at the Information Desk from 9:00 a.m. Children gently touch sea stars and sea cucumbers with two fingers and wash their hands before and after.\n• No flash photography in the Jellyfish Gallery or at Penguin Coast. Bright flashes can stress the animals.\n• Did you know? Sea stars can grow back lost arms, and sharks keep growing new teeth all their lives.\n\nTickets\nAdult: NT$450 | Child (3–12) and senior (65+): NT$300 | Under 3: free", "passage_zh": "珊瑚灣水族館\n今日節目表\n\n開放時間：上午 9:00 至下午 5:00（最後入館時間為下午 4:00）\n\n每日表演與活動\n上午 10:00　企鵝早餐｜企鵝海岸（2 樓）｜15 分鐘\n上午 10:30　潛水員餵食秀｜大洋池（1 樓）｜20 分鐘\n上午 11:00　海獅表演｜海洋劇場（3 樓）｜25 分鐘\n下午 1:30　觸摸池時間｜潮間帶區（1 樓）｜30 分鐘\n下午 2:30　海獅表演｜海洋劇場（3 樓）｜25 分鐘\n下午 3:30　鯊魚解說｜鯊魚隧道（地下 1 樓）｜20 分鐘\n下午 4:15　企鵝晚餐｜企鵝海岸（2 樓）｜15 分鐘\n\n注意事項\n• 海洋劇場：劇場可容納 300 人。每場表演前 15 分鐘開放入場。第 1–3 排是「潑水區」，可能會被淋濕喔！\n• 觸摸池時間：每場只限 20 名兒童（4–12 歲）參加。上午 9:00 起在服務台發放免費入場券。孩子們用兩根手指輕輕觸摸海星和海參，觸摸前後都要洗手。\n• 水母展區和企鵝海岸禁止使用閃光燈拍照。強烈的閃光可能讓動物感到緊張不安。\n• 你知道嗎？海星斷掉的腕可以再長回來，而鯊魚一生都會不斷長出新牙齒。\n\n門票\n全票：NT$450｜兒童（3–12 歲）及敬老（65 歲以上）：NT$300｜未滿 3 歲：免費", "questions": [{"q": "The Lee family arrives at the aquarium at noon and wants to see the sea lions. What time is the next Sea Lion Show?", "options": ["11:00 a.m.", "1:30 p.m.", "2:30 p.m.", "3:30 p.m."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Daily Shows and Activities 表中，海獅表演在 11:00 a.m. 和 2:30 p.m. 兩場。中午抵達時 11:00 那場已經結束，下一場是 2:30 p.m.。(A) 已經結束；(B) 1:30 p.m. 是觸摸池時間；(D) 3:30 p.m. 是鯊魚解說，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Where should visitors go to watch divers feed the fish?", "options": ["The Big Ocean Tank on 1F", "The Ocean Theater on 3F", "Penguin Coast on 2F", "The Shark Tunnel on B1"], "answer": 0, "explain": "表中 10:30 a.m. 的 Diver Feeding（潛水員餵食秀）地點是 Big Ocean Tank (1F)。(B) 海洋劇場是海獅表演；(C) 企鵝海岸是企鵝早餐和晚餐；(D) 鯊魚隧道是鯊魚解說，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Two parents visit the aquarium with their 8-year-old son and 2-year-old daughter. How much do their tickets cost in total?", "options": ["NT$1,500", "NT$1,200", "NT$1,800", "NT$1,050"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Tickets 段：大人 NT$450 × 2 = 900；8 歲兒子屬於 Child (3–12)，NT$300；2 歲女兒 Under 3: free（免費）。合計 900 + 300 = 1,200 元。(A) 多算了女兒的 300 元；(C) 把四人都算成全票；(D) 只算了一位大人，又把女兒也算成兒童票（450 + 300 + 300），故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Seven-year-old Mia wants to join Touch Pool Time. What should her parents do?", "options": ["Buy a special ticket online the day before.", "Wait at the Tide Pool Area at 1:30 p.m. without a ticket.", "Ask a diver at the Big Ocean Tank to save a place for her.", "Get a free ticket at the Information Desk, which starts giving them out at 9:00 a.m."], "answer": 3, "explain": "Good to Know 第二點：每場只限 20 名兒童，Free tickets are given out at the Information Desk from 9:00 a.m.（上午 9:00 起在服務台發放免費入場券），所以應去服務台領券，名額有限最好早點去。(A) 入場券免費，且文中沒有提到網路購票；(B) 沒有入場券可能無法參加；(C) 文中沒有這種做法，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Grandma Chen does not want to get wet at the Sea Lion Show. What should she do?", "options": ["Sit in Row 4 or farther back.", "Arrive after the show has started.", "Go to the 11:00 show instead of the 2:30 show.", "Sit in one of the first three rows."], "answer": 0, "explain": "Good to Know 第一點說 Rows 1–3 are the \"Splash Zone,\" so you may get wet（第 1–3 排是潑水區，可能會被淋濕），所以不想被淋濕就應坐第 4 排以後。(B) 晚到與會不會被淋濕無關；(C) 兩場表演都有潑水區；(D) 前三排正是潑水區，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a185", "title": "Which Film, What Time, How Much? A Week of Movie Showtimes", "title_zh": "看哪部？幾點？多少錢？電影院一週場次表", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Arts", "Schedule"], "passage": "STARLIGHT CINEMA\nShowtimes: Friday, October 16 – Thursday, October 22\n\nPaper Moon Express (Animation | G | 1 hr 40 min)\nHall 1: 10:20 | 12:30 | 14:40 | 16:50\n\nMy Robot Brother (Family Comedy | P | 1 hr 35 min)\nHall 2: 11:00 | 13:10 | 15:20\n\nDeep Blue Rescue (Action | PG-12 | 2 hr 10 min)\nHall 3: 11:30 | 14:20 | 17:10 | 20:00 | 22:40\n\nThe Last Lighthouse (Drama | PG-15 | 1 hr 55 min)\nHall 2: 18:00 | 20:30\n\nRatings\nG: for all ages\nP: age 6 and up; children aged 6–11 must come with an adult\nPG-12: age 12 and up\nPG-15: age 15 and up\n\nTicket Prices (NT$)\nRegular: 300\nStudent (with student ID; Mon.–Fri., shows starting before 18:00): 260\nChild (3–11) and senior (65+): 230\nEarly Bird (all shows starting before 12:00): 220 for everyone\nOnly one discount can be used for each ticket.\n\nSnack Combo\nMedium popcorn + one drink: NT$150 (save NT$40)\n\nPlease arrive at least 10 minutes before the film starts. Latecomers may be asked to wait for a suitable break before they are shown to their seats. Phones must be switched off or set to silent during the film.\n\nTicket Hotline: (07) 551-2093", "passage_zh": "星光影城\n本週場次：10 月 16 日（五）至 10 月 22 日（四）\n\n《紙月亮特快車》（動畫｜普遍級｜1 小時 40 分）\n第 1 廳：10:20｜12:30｜14:40｜16:50\n\n《我的機器人哥哥》（家庭喜劇｜保護級｜1 小時 35 分）\n第 2 廳：11:00｜13:10｜15:20\n\n《深藍救援》（動作｜輔 12 級｜2 小時 10 分）\n第 3 廳：11:30｜14:20｜17:10｜20:00｜22:40\n\n《最後的燈塔》（劇情｜輔 15 級｜1 小時 55 分）\n第 2 廳：18:00｜20:30\n\n電影分級\n普遍級（G）：所有年齡皆可觀賞\n保護級（P）：6 歲以上可觀賞；6–11 歲兒童須由大人陪同\n輔 12 級（PG-12）：12 歲以上可觀賞\n輔 15 級（PG-15）：15 歲以上可觀賞\n\n票價（新臺幣）\n全票：300\n學生票（須出示學生證；限週一至週五 18:00 前開演的場次）：260\n兒童票（3–11 歲）及敬老票（65 歲以上）：230\n早場票（12:00 前開演的所有場次）：一律 220\n每張票限用一種優惠。\n\n套餐\n中份爆米花＋飲料一杯：NT$150（省 NT$40）\n\n請至少在電影開演前 10 分鐘抵達。遲到的觀眾可能須在適當的空檔才會被帶位入座。電影放映期間，手機須關機或調成靜音。\n\n訂票專線：(07) 551-2093", "questions": [{"q": "Thirteen-year-old Leo is going to the cinema with two friends of the same age, without any adults. Which film are they NOT allowed to see?", "options": ["Paper Moon Express", "The Last Lighthouse", "Deep Blue Rescue", "My Robot Brother"], "answer": 1, "explain": "《The Last Lighthouse》是 PG-15，Ratings 段說 PG-15: age 15 and up（15 歲以上才能看），13 歲的 Leo 不能看。(A) G 級所有年齡皆可；(C) PG-12 為 12 歲以上，13 歲可以看；(D) P 級只規定 6–11 歲兒童須由大人陪同，13 歲不受此限，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "On Saturday, October 17, a senior-high student and her 70-year-old grandmother see the 10:20 showing of Paper Moon Express. How much do they pay for their tickets?", "options": ["NT$440", "NT$460", "NT$490", "NT$600"], "answer": 0, "explain": "10:20 開演，符合 Early Bird (all shows starting before 12:00): 220 for everyone，兩人都適用早場票，共 220 × 2 = 440 元；且每張票只能用一種優惠，選最便宜的早場票最划算。(B) 是把兩人都以 230 元的敬老票計算，但早場票更便宜；(C) 學生票限週一至週五，週六不能用，且也比早場票貴；(D) 是兩張全票，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "At about what time will the last showing of Deep Blue Rescue end?", "options": ["00:10", "00:30", "00:50", "01:10"], "answer": 2, "explain": "《Deep Blue Rescue》最後一場是 22:40，片長 2 hr 10 min（2 小時 10 分），22:40 加 2 小時 10 分是隔天凌晨 00:50。(A)(B)(D) 皆為計算錯誤，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Nine-year-old Ben wants to see My Robot Brother. Which of the following is true?", "options": ["He can watch it alone if he has a ticket.", "He is too young to watch it.", "He can only watch it at the 11:00 show.", "He must watch it with an adult."], "answer": 3, "explain": "《My Robot Brother》是 P 級，Ratings 段說 P: age 6 and up; children aged 6–11 must come with an adult（6–11 歲兒童須由大人陪同）。9 歲的 Ben 可以看，但必須有大人陪同。(A) 不能自己一個人看；(B) 6 歲以上就能看，他並不會太小；(C) 文中沒有限制他只能看哪一場，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Wei, a 16-year-old senior-high student, sees The Last Lighthouse at 20:30 on a Wednesday. How much is his ticket?", "options": ["NT$220", "NT$230", "NT$300", "NT$260"], "answer": 2, "explain": "學生票限 Mon.–Fri., shows starting before 18:00（週一至週五 18:00 前開演的場次），20:30 的場次不適用；早場票限 12:00 前開演；16 歲也不適用兒童票。因此他只能買全票 NT$300。(A) 早場票價；(B) 兒童及敬老票價；(D) 學生票價，此場次不能使用，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a186", "title": "Eyes on the Water: Summer Lifeguards Wanted", "title_zh": "緊盯水面：誠徵暑期救生員", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Career", "Sports"], "passage": "SUMMER LIFEGUARDS WANTED\nGreenhill Municipal Swimming Pool\n\nAre you a strong swimmer who enjoys working with people? Greenhill Pool is looking for six part-time lifeguards for the summer season, from July 1 to August 31.\n\nRequirements\n• At least 17 years old by July 1\n• A valid lifeguard certificate (CPR training included)\n• Pass our swim test: swim 400 meters in under 9 minutes, then tread water for 2 minutes without using your hands\n\nShifts and Pay\nMorning shift: 6:00 a.m. – 12:00 p.m.\nAfternoon shift: 12:00 p.m. – 6:00 p.m.\nEvening shift: 5:00 p.m. – 10:00 p.m.\nPay: NT$220 per hour (NT$250 per hour for evening shifts)\nEvery lifeguard works at least three shifts a week. Uniforms, sunscreen and drinking water are provided.\n\nDuties\nLifeguards watch the pools, help swimmers in trouble, give first aid when needed, and make sure that everyone follows the pool rules. They also help clean the pool deck at the end of each shift. Every 30 minutes, lifeguards move to a different chair so that they can stay alert.\n\nHow to Apply\nFill in the application form at our front desk or on our website, and attach a copy of your lifeguard certificate. Applications close on May 20. Swim tests will be held on May 25 and May 26 at 8:00 a.m. Please bring your swimsuit, goggles and a photo ID.\n\nQuestions? Call Coach Lin at (02) 2736-5180 or email jobs@greenhillpool.org.tw.", "passage_zh": "誠徵暑期救生員\n綠丘市立游泳池\n\n你泳技高超，又喜歡和人打交道嗎？綠丘游泳池正在招募六名暑期兼職救生員，工作期間為 7 月 1 日至 8 月 31 日。\n\n應徵條件\n• 7 月 1 日前年滿 17 歲\n• 持有效的救生員證照（含 CPR 訓練）\n• 通過本池游泳測驗：9 分鐘內游完 400 公尺，接著不用手踩水 2 分鐘\n\n班別與薪資\n早班：上午 6:00 – 中午 12:00\n午班：中午 12:00 – 下午 6:00\n晚班：下午 5:00 – 晚上 10:00\n薪資：時薪新台幣 220 元（晚班時薪新台幣 250 元）\n每位救生員每週至少值三個班。本池提供制服、防曬乳及飲用水。\n\n工作內容\n救生員負責看守泳池、協助遇到危險的泳客、必要時進行急救，並確保每個人都遵守泳池規定。每班結束時也要協助清潔池畔。救生員每 30 分鐘會換到另一張救生椅，以保持警覺。\n\n申請方式\n請至本池櫃檯或網站填寫申請表，並附上救生員證照影本。申請於 5 月 20 日截止。游泳測驗將於 5 月 25 日及 5 月 26 日上午 8:00 舉行，請攜帶泳衣、泳鏡及附照片的身分證件。\n\n有任何問題嗎？請致電 (02) 2736-5180 找林教練，或寄電子郵件至 jobs@greenhillpool.org.tw。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this notice?", "options": ["To invite people to take swimming lessons in the summer", "To find people to work as lifeguards at a swimming pool", "To tell swimmers about the new rules of the pool", "To announce a swimming race on May 25 and May 26"], "answer": 1, "explain": "標題 SUMMER LIFEGUARDS WANTED 及第二段 Greenhill Pool is looking for six part-time lifeguards 都說明這是招募暑期救生員的徵才啟事。(A) 文中沒有開設游泳課；(C) 泳池規定只在工作內容中順帶提到；(D) 5 月 25、26 日是應徵者的游泳測驗，不是比賽，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Mia is a strong swimmer with a lifeguard certificate. She will turn 17 on August 5. What can be inferred?", "options": ["She can apply if she passes the swim test.", "She can work only the evening shifts.", "She must take a CPR class before May 20.", "She cannot get this job this summer."], "answer": 3, "explain": "應徵條件第一項規定 At least 17 years old by July 1（7 月 1 日前須年滿 17 歲），Mia 到 8 月 5 日才滿 17 歲，不符合資格，所以今年夏天無法得到這份工作。(A) 即使通過測驗，年齡仍不符；(B) 文中沒有「未滿 17 歲只能上晚班」的規定；(C) 她已有證照，且問題在年齡而非 CPR，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Ken works two morning shifts and one evening shift in one week. How much will he earn that week?", "options": ["NT$3,740", "NT$3,890", "NT$3,960", "NT$4,140"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Shifts and Pay 段：早班 6:00–12:00 共 6 小時，時薪 220 元，兩班為 6 × 220 × 2 = 2,640 元；晚班 5:00–10:00 共 5 小時，時薪 250 元，為 5 × 250 = 1,250 元，合計 3,890 元。(A) 把晚班也算成時薪 220 元；(C) 把三班都算成 6 小時、時薪 220 元；(D) 把晚班算成 6 小時，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What should an applicant bring to the swim test?", "options": ["A swimsuit, goggles and a photo ID", "A uniform, sunscreen and drinking water", "A copy of a lifeguard certificate and a photo", "The application form and a letter from a coach"], "answer": 0, "explain": "How to Apply 段最後一句：Please bring your swimsuit, goggles and a photo ID. 測驗時要帶泳衣、泳鏡及附照片的證件。(B) 制服、防曬乳和飲用水是錄取後泳池提供的；(C) 證照影本是填寫申請表時附上的；(D) 文中沒有要求教練的信，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"valid\" in the Requirements section is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["expensive", "handwritten", "official and still usable", "difficult to get"], "answer": 2, "explain": "A valid lifeguard certificate 指「有效的」救生員證照，也就是正式核發且尚未過期、仍可使用的證照，最接近 official and still usable。(A)「昂貴的」、(B)「手寫的」、(D)「難以取得的」都不是 valid 的意思，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a187", "title": "Lighting the Way: A Lantern Festival Volunteer Form", "title_zh": "點亮旅程：燈會志工報名表", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Society", "Culture"], "passage": "Harbor City Lantern Festival\nVOLUNTEER APPLICATION FORM\nFestival dates: February 12 – February 26\nPlease return this form to the City Hall Information Desk by January 10.\n\nSection A: Personal Information\nName: Chen Yu-ting     Age: 16\nSchool: Seaside Commercial Vocational High School\nMobile: 0912-468-305\nT-shirt size: ☐ S  ☑ M  ☐ L  ☐ XL\n\nSection B: Job Choices (Choose up to TWO. Write 1 for your first choice and 2 for your second choice.)\n[1] Information Booth – answer visitors' questions and hand out maps (must speak English or Japanese)\n[  ] Lost Child Center – take care of lost children until their parents arrive (must be 18 or older)\n[2] Recycling Team – help visitors sort their trash\n[  ] Photo Spot Helper – take photos for visitors and keep the lines moving\n\nSection C: Availability (Each shift is 4 hours. Volunteers must work at least 3 shifts.)\nWeekday evenings (6:00 – 10:00 p.m.): ☑ Feb 15  ☑ Feb 17\nWeekend afternoons (1:00 – 5:00 p.m.): ☑ Feb 13  ☑ Feb 20  ☑ Feb 21\n\nSection D: Languages\nChinese (native), Taiwanese (good), English (intermediate; GEPT Intermediate Level passed), Japanese (a little)\n\nSection E: Emergency Contact\nName: Chen Ming-hui     Relationship: Mother\nPhone: 0928-117-640\n\nVolunteers under 18 must have a parent or guardian sign below.\nSignature of Parent/Guardian: Chen Ming-hui\n\nImportant Information\nAll volunteers must attend one training session on January 30, 9:00 a.m. – 12:00 p.m., at City Hall, Room 305. A dinner box is provided on every evening shift. Volunteers who work 20 hours or more will receive a volunteer certificate and a festival gift bag.", "passage_zh": "港城燈會\n志工報名表\n燈會日期：2 月 12 日至 2 月 26 日\n請於 1 月 10 日前將本表交回市政府服務台。\n\nA 部分：個人資料\n姓名：陳宥婷　　年齡：16\n學校：海濱高級商業職業學校\n手機：0912-468-305\nT 恤尺寸：☐ S　☑ M　☐ L　☐ XL\n\nB 部分：工作志願（最多選兩項。第一志願填 1，第二志願填 2。）\n[1] 服務台：回答遊客問題並發放地圖（須會說英文或日文）\n[  ] 走失兒童中心：照顧走失的孩童，直到家長抵達（須年滿 18 歲）\n[2] 資源回收組：協助遊客將垃圾分類\n[  ] 拍照點小幫手：幫遊客拍照並維持排隊動線順暢\n\nC 部分：可服務時段（每班 4 小時，志工至少須服務 3 班。）\n平日晚上（晚上 6:00 – 10:00）：☑ 2 月 15 日　☑ 2 月 17 日\n週末下午（下午 1:00 – 5:00）：☑ 2 月 13 日　☑ 2 月 20 日　☑ 2 月 21 日\n\nD 部分：語言能力\n中文（母語）、台語（良好）、英文（中級；已通過全民英檢中級）、日文（略懂）\n\nE 部分：緊急聯絡人\n姓名：陳明惠　　關係：母親\n電話：0928-117-640\n\n未滿 18 歲的志工須由家長或監護人於下方簽名。\n家長／監護人簽名：陳明惠\n\n重要事項\n所有志工都必須參加一次訓練課程，時間為 1 月 30 日上午 9:00 至中午 12:00，地點在市政府 305 室。每個晚上班次都提供晚餐便當。服務滿 20 小時（含）以上的志工，將獲得志工服務證明及一份燈會紀念禮袋。", "questions": [{"q": "Why did Yu-ting NOT choose the Lost Child Center?", "options": ["She is too young for that job.", "She does not speak Japanese well.", "She is free only on weekend afternoons.", "That job does not provide a dinner box."], "answer": 0, "explain": "Section B 寫明 Lost Child Center 須 must be 18 or older（年滿 18 歲），而 Section A 顯示宥婷只有 16 歲，所以她不能選這項工作。(B) 會日文是服務台的條件，與走失兒童中心無關；(C) 她也勾選了平日晚上；(D) 晚餐便當是依晚上班次提供，與工作項目無關，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "If Yu-ting works all the shifts she has checked, what will she get?", "options": ["Only a dinner box on every shift", "A gift bag, but no certificate", "Nothing, because she has not worked enough hours", "A volunteer certificate and a festival gift bag"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Section C 她勾選了 5 個班次，每班 4 小時，共 5 × 4 = 20 小時；Important Information 說 Volunteers who work 20 hours or more will receive a volunteer certificate and a festival gift bag，所以她可以拿到服務證明和紀念禮袋。(A) 晚餐便當只在晚上班次提供，並非每班都有，而且她還能得到其他獎勵；(B) 證明和禮袋是一起發的；(C) 她剛好達到 20 小時，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which job would Yu-ting most like to do at the festival?", "options": ["Helping visitors sort their trash", "Answering visitors' questions and handing out maps", "Taking photos for visitors", "Looking after children who are lost"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Section B 規定第一志願填 1，宥婷在 Information Booth 前填了 [1]，所以她最想做的是回答遊客問題、發放地圖的服務台工作；她的英文程度也符合要求。(A) 資源回收組是她的第二志願；(C)(D) 她沒有選，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Who is Chen Ming-hui?", "options": ["A worker at the City Hall Information Desk", "The teacher who will lead the training session", "Yu-ting's mother, who has agreed to her joining", "Another student who will volunteer with Yu-ting"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Section E 緊急聯絡人 Chen Ming-hui 的 Relationship 是 Mother，而且表格最後的家長／監護人簽名也是她，表示她是宥婷的母親並同意她參加。(A)(B)(D) 表中都沒有這樣的資訊，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What must Yu-ting do before the festival begins?", "options": ["Attend a training session at City Hall on January 30", "Pass an English test at City Hall", "Buy a T-shirt in size M from the festival", "Hand in this form at the festival on February 12"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Important Information 說所有志工都必須在 1 月 30 日到市政府 305 室參加訓練課程，燈會 2 月 12 日才開始。(B) 她已通過全民英檢中級，表中也沒有要求再考試；(C) 表上只是填 T 恤尺寸，沒有說要購買；(D) 表格須在 1 月 10 日前交到市政府服務台，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a188", "title": "Inside the Bear Suit: Mascot Performers Wanted", "title_zh": "熊裝裡的人：誠徵吉祥物表演人員", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Career"], "passage": "NOW HIRING: MASCOT PERFORMERS\nWonder Valley Theme Park, Taichung\n\nOur famous mascot, Bobo the Bear, needs more friends! We are looking for energetic people to wear our character costumes and bring smiles to thousands of visitors every day. You won't say a word on the job, but you will make a big difference.\n\nWhat You'll Do\n• Greet visitors and pose for photos at the park entrance\n• Take part in our 11:00 a.m. and 3:00 p.m. parades\n• Dance in short shows on the Rainbow Stage\n\nWhat We Need\n• Age 18 or above\n• Height between 155 and 170 cm (so that you fit our costumes)\n• Good health: you must be able to walk and dance in a heavy costume\n• Dance experience is a plus, but not required\n\nWorking Conditions\nPerformers work five days a week, including some weekends and holidays. Because the costumes are very hot, performers wear them for no more than 30 minutes at a time, followed by a 20-minute rest in an air-conditioned room. A cooling vest is worn under every costume. Each performer always works with a helper, who guides them through the crowds, since it is hard to see clearly from inside the costume.\n\nPay and Benefits\n• NT$1,800 per day (8-hour workday)\n• NT$200 bonus for each parade\n• Free park tickets for two family members every month\n• A two-week paid training course before you start\n\nHow to Apply\nSend your résumé and a one-minute video of yourself dancing to jobs@wondervalley.com.tw by March 31. Auditions will be held on April 8 at the Rainbow Stage. Please wear comfortable clothes and sports shoes.", "passage_zh": "現正招募：吉祥物表演人員\n台中奇幻谷主題樂園\n\n我們的人氣吉祥物「波波熊」需要更多朋友！我們正在尋找充滿活力的人，穿上我們的角色玩偶裝，每天為數千名遊客帶來笑容。工作時你一句話都不用說，卻能帶來很大的改變。\n\n工作內容\n• 在樂園入口迎接遊客並和他們拍照\n• 參加上午 11:00 和下午 3:00 的遊行\n• 在彩虹舞台上表演簡短的舞蹈秀\n\n應徵條件\n• 年滿 18 歲\n• 身高介於 155 至 170 公分之間（以符合玩偶裝尺寸）\n• 身體健康：必須能穿著厚重的玩偶裝走路和跳舞\n• 有舞蹈經驗者佳，但非必要\n\n工作條件\n表演人員每週工作五天，包含部分週末及假日。由於玩偶裝非常悶熱，表演人員每次穿著不超過 30 分鐘，接著要在冷氣房休息 20 分鐘。每套玩偶裝裡面都會穿上降溫背心。每位表演人員一定會搭配一名助手，帶領他們穿越人群，因為從玩偶裝裡面很難看清楚外面。\n\n薪資與福利\n• 日薪新台幣 1,800 元（每天工作 8 小時）\n• 每參加一場遊行，另加獎金新台幣 200 元\n• 每月提供兩名家人樂園免費門票\n• 正式上班前有兩週的帶薪訓練課程\n\n申請方式\n請在 3 月 31 日前，將履歷表和一段一分鐘的個人跳舞影片寄到 jobs@wondervalley.com.tw。甄選將於 4 月 8 日在彩虹舞台舉行，請穿著舒適的服裝和運動鞋。", "questions": [{"q": "What is this advertisement mainly about?", "options": ["A job that involves wearing a costume and entertaining visitors", "A dance competition for young people at a theme park", "Cheap tickets for families who visit a theme park", "A new parade that a theme park has just started"], "answer": 0, "explain": "標題 NOW HIRING: MASCOT PERFORMERS 及第二段 We are looking for energetic people to wear our character costumes 都說明這是招募穿玩偶裝娛樂遊客的工作廣告。(B) 4 月 8 日是應徵甄選，不是舞蹈比賽；(C) 免費門票只是員工福利之一；(D) 遊行只是工作內容的一部分，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does each performer work with a helper?", "options": ["The helper takes photos of visitors with the mascot.", "The helper teaches the performer new dances.", "It is difficult for the performer to see well in the costume.", "The helper puts the cooling vest on the performer."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Working Conditions 段最後一句：Each performer always works with a helper, who guides them through the crowds, since it is hard to see clearly from inside the costume. 因為從玩偶裝裡很難看清楚，所以需要助手帶路。(A)(B)(D) 文中都沒有說助手負責這些事，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "On Saturday, Jenny works a full day and takes part in both parades. How much will she earn that day?", "options": ["NT$1,800", "NT$2,200", "NT$2,000", "NT$2,600"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Pay and Benefits 段：日薪 1,800 元，每場遊行另加 200 元獎金。她參加 11:00 和 3:00 兩場遊行，所以是 1,800 + 200 × 2 = 2,200 元。(A) 沒有算獎金；(C) 只算了一場遊行；(D) 多算了遊行獎金，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following people is LEAST likely to get this job?", "options": ["A 19-year-old student who is 160 cm tall and has never danced", "A 25-year-old dancer who is 165 cm tall", "An 18-year-old who is 158 cm tall and has worked at a café", "A 20-year-old basketball player who is 182 cm tall"], "answer": 3, "explain": "What We Need 規定身高須介於 155 到 170 公分之間，才穿得下玩偶裝，182 公分的籃球員超出範圍，最不可能被錄取。(A) 符合年齡與身高，且舞蹈經驗 not required；(B)(C) 年齡和身高也都符合條件，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In the sentence \"Dance experience is a plus, but not required,\" the word \"plus\" is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["problem", "advantage", "rule", "number"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Dance experience is a plus, but not required 意思是「有舞蹈經驗是加分條件，但不是必要條件」，plus 在此指「優點、有利條件」，最接近 advantage。(A)「問題」意思相反；(C)「規定」與 not required 矛盾；(D)「數字」是 plus 作為加號的聯想，放回句中不通，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a189", "title": "Three Jobs, One Summer: Reading the School Job Board", "title_zh": "一個暑假，三份工作：看懂學校徵才看板", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Career", "Money"], "passage": "Lakeview Vocational High School Career Center\nSUMMER JOB BOARD\nAll jobs below have been checked by the Career Center. Students under 18 need a parent permission form, signed by a parent, before they start work.\n\nJOB 1: Robot Camp Helper – Bright Gear Kids Academy\nHelp children aged 7 to 10 build simple robots, and set up and clean the classrooms.\nDates: July 6 – July 31, Monday to Friday\nHours: 9:00 a.m. – 4:00 p.m. (one-hour lunch break, unpaid; free lunch provided)\nPay: NT$210/hour\nRequirement: Interest in technology; must have taken a basic programming class\n\nJOB 2: Packing Staff – Sunfield Mango Farm, Tainan\nSort mangoes by size and pack them into boxes for delivery.\nDates: June 29 – July 24, Monday to Saturday\nHours: 6:00 a.m. – 11:00 a.m.\nPay: NT$220/hour, plus one free box of mangoes every week\nRequirement: Able to lift 15-kilogram boxes; a free shuttle bus leaves Tainan Station at 5:30 a.m.\n\nJOB 3: Customer Service Assistant (English) – Island Hop Travel\nAnswer emails and phone calls from foreign travelers.\nDates: July 4 – August 30, Saturdays and Sundays only\nHours: 10:00 a.m. – 6:00 p.m. (one-hour break, unpaid)\nPay: NT$240/hour\nRequirement: GEPT Intermediate Level or above; at least 17 years old\n\nHOW TO APPLY\nPick up an application form at the Career Center (Room 108) and return it to Ms. Huang by June 12. Each student may apply for no more than two jobs. Interviews will be arranged by the employers.", "passage_zh": "湖景高職就業輔導中心\n暑期工作看板\n以下所有工作都經過就業輔導中心審核。未滿 18 歲的學生在開始工作前，須繳交由家長簽名的家長同意書。\n\n工作一：機器人營隊助理－亮齒輪兒童學苑\n協助 7 至 10 歲的孩童組裝簡單的機器人，並負責布置及清潔教室。\n日期：7 月 6 日至 7 月 31 日，週一至週五\n時間：上午 9:00 – 下午 4:00（午休一小時，不支薪；提供免費午餐）\n薪資：時薪新台幣 210 元\n條件：對科技有興趣；須修過基礎程式設計課程\n\n工作二：包裝人員－台南陽光芒果農場\n依大小將芒果分類，並裝箱以便出貨。\n日期：6 月 29 日至 7 月 24 日，週一至週六\n時間：上午 6:00 – 11:00\n薪資：時薪新台幣 220 元，每週另送一箱芒果\n條件：能搬動 15 公斤的箱子；每天上午 5:30 有免費接駁車從台南車站出發\n\n工作三：客服助理（英文）－跳島旅行社\n回覆外國旅客的電子郵件和電話。\n日期：7 月 4 日至 8 月 30 日，僅限週六、週日\n時間：上午 10:00 – 下午 6:00（休息一小時，不支薪）\n薪資：時薪新台幣 240 元\n條件：全民英檢中級（含）以上；年滿 17 歲\n\n申請方式\n請至就業輔導中心（108 室）領取申請表，並於 6 月 12 日前交給黃老師。每位學生最多只能申請兩份工作。面試時間將由雇主安排。", "questions": [{"q": "How much will a student earn for one day of work at the robot camp?", "options": ["NT$1,470", "NT$1,680", "NT$1,260", "NT$1,050"], "answer": 2, "explain": "工作一的時間是上午 9:00 到下午 4:00，共 7 小時，但其中一小時午休 unpaid（不支薪），所以只算 6 小時，6 × 210 = 1,260 元。(A) 把午休也算進去（7 × 210）；(B) 以 8 小時計算；(D) 只算了 5 小時，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Kai, 17, helps at his family's shop on weekdays, so he can work only on weekends. He passed the GEPT Intermediate Level last year. Which job is right for him?", "options": ["Robot Camp Helper", "Packing Staff", "Both Robot Camp Helper and Packing Staff", "Customer Service Assistant"], "answer": 3, "explain": "工作三 Saturdays and Sundays only（只在週末上班），條件是全民英檢中級以上、年滿 17 歲，凱都符合。(A) 機器人營隊是週一至週五；(B) 包裝工作是週一至週六，平日也要上班；(C) 兩者都需要平日上班，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about students who take the packing job at the mango farm?", "options": ["They will have to get up very early in the morning.", "They will work every day, including Sundays.", "They will be given a free lunch every day.", "They must have taken a basic programming class."], "answer": 0, "explain": "工作二的上班時間是上午 6:00 到 11:00，而免費接駁車上午 5:30 就從台南車站出發，可推知做這份工作的學生必須一大早就起床。(B) 工作日期是週一至週六，週日不用上班；(C) 提供免費午餐的是工作一的機器人營隊；(D) 修過基礎程式設計課程是工作一的條件，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true according to the job board?", "options": ["Students may apply for all three jobs if they want to.", "A 16-year-old must get a parent's signature before starting work.", "Robot camp helpers are paid during their lunch break.", "Packing staff must find their own way to the mango farm."], "answer": 1, "explain": "看板開頭說 Students under 18 need a parent permission form, signed by a parent, before they start work，16 歲的學生須先取得家長簽名。(A) 最後一段說每人 no more than two jobs；(C) 午休 unpaid；(D) 每天有免費接駁車從台南車站出發，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In Job 2, the word \"sort\" is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["wash", "sell", "separate into groups", "cut into pieces"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Sort mangoes by size and pack them into boxes 是指「依大小把芒果分類」再裝箱，sort 在此是「分類、分門別類」，最接近 separate into groups。(A)「清洗」、(B)「販賣」、(D)「切塊」都不符合 by size 的意思，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a190", "title": "Dad, Can You Sign This? A Part-Time Work Permission Form", "title_zh": "爸，可以幫我簽名嗎？學生打工同意書", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Education", "Career"], "passage": "Eastbridge Senior High School\nSTUDENT PART-TIME WORK PERMISSION FORM\nReturn to: Student Affairs Office, Room 102 (open 7:30 a.m. – 5:00 p.m.)\n\nPART A: STUDENT (to be filled in by the student)\nName: Lo Wei-chen     Class: 2-3     Age: 17\nEmployer: Happy Paws Pet Hotel\nAddress: 58 Minsheng Road, 2F\nJob: Walking dogs and cleaning pet rooms\nWork schedule: Saturdays and Sundays, 1:00 p.m. – 6:00 p.m.\nPay: NT$215 per hour\nStart date: September 20\n\nPART B: SCHOOL RULES FOR STUDENT WORKERS\n1. Students may work no more than 16 hours a week during the school term.\n2. Students may not work after 10:00 p.m. on any day.\n3. Students may not take jobs that involve driving, selling alcohol or using dangerous machines.\n4. Students who fail more than two subjects in a semester must stop working until their grades improve.\n\nPART C: PARENT OR GUARDIAN\nI know where my child will work, and I agree to the job described above. I understand that the school may ask my child to stop working if any of the rules above are broken.\nParent/Guardian name: Lo Kuo-hua     Relationship: Father\nPhone: 0935-206-718\nSignature: ______________     Date: ______________\n\nPART D: FOR OFFICE USE ONLY\nReceived by: ______________     Approved: ☐ Yes  ☐ No\n\nNOTE: Please attach a copy of the employer's business registration. Forms must be handed in at least five school days before the start date. Students who start work without the school's permission may receive a warning.", "passage_zh": "東橋高級中學\n學生打工同意書\n繳交地點：學務處，102 室（開放時間：上午 7:30 – 下午 5:00）\n\nA 部分：學生（由學生填寫）\n姓名：羅偉辰　　班級：2-3　　年齡：17\n雇主：快樂肉球寵物旅館\n地址：民生路 58 號 2 樓\n工作內容：遛狗及清潔寵物房間\n工作時間：每週六、週日下午 1:00 – 6:00\n薪資：時薪新台幣 215 元\n開始日期：9 月 20 日\n\nB 部分：學生打工校規\n1. 學期中，學生每週工作時數不得超過 16 小時。\n2. 學生任何一天都不得於晚上 10:00 以後工作。\n3. 學生不得從事需要開車、販賣酒類或操作危險機械的工作。\n4. 一學期有超過兩科不及格的學生，必須停止打工，直到成績進步為止。\n\nC 部分：家長或監護人\n本人知道孩子的工作地點，並同意上述工作。本人了解若違反以上任何一項規定，學校得要求孩子停止打工。\n家長／監護人姓名：羅國華　　關係：父親\n電話：0935-206-718\n簽名：______________　　日期：______________\n\nD 部分：以下由學校填寫\n收件人：______________　　核准：☐ 是　☐ 否\n\n注意：請附上雇主的商業登記影本。本表最晚須於開始工作日前五個上課日繳交。未經學校同意就開始打工的學生，可能會受到警告處分。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this form?", "options": ["To help students find part-time jobs near the school", "To let employers report students who break the rules", "To tell parents about a student's grades in each subject", "To get permission from a parent and the school for a student's job"], "answer": 3, "explain": "表格名稱是 STUDENT PART-TIME WORK PERMISSION FORM，Part C 由家長簽名同意，Part D 由學校決定是否核准，可知目的是取得家長和學校對學生打工的同意。(A) 表上的工作是學生自己找的；(B) 沒有讓雇主檢舉學生的欄位；(C) 成績只出現在校規第 4 條，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How many hours will Wei-chen work each week, and how much will he earn?", "options": ["10 hours; NT$2,150", "5 hours; NT$1,075", "12 hours; NT$2,580", "10 hours; NT$1,950"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Part A：他每週六、日下午 1:00 到 6:00 工作，一天 5 小時，一週 10 小時；時薪 215 元，10 × 215 = 2,150 元。(B) 只算了一天；(C) 把一天算成 6 小時；(D) 時數對，但金額是以時薪 195 元計算，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following changes to Wei-chen's job would break the school rules?", "options": ["Getting a raise to NT$230 per hour", "Also working on Wednesdays from 4:00 to 7:00 p.m.", "Also working on Fridays from 7:00 to 11:00 p.m.", "Walking the dogs in a park instead of on the street"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Part B 第 2 條規定學生 may not work after 10:00 p.m.，週五做到晚上 11 點就違反校規。(B) 每週工作 10 + 3 = 13 小時，未超過 16 小時，也沒有超過晚上 10 點；(A)(D) 加薪和換遛狗地點都不違反任何規定，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Who will most likely fill in Part D?", "options": ["Wei-chen himself", "A staff member at the Student Affairs Office", "Wei-chen's father, Lo Kuo-hua", "The manager of Happy Paws Pet Hotel"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Part D 標示 FOR OFFICE USE ONLY（以下由學校填寫），而表格要交回 Student Affairs Office（學務處），所以最可能由學務處人員填寫收件人並決定是否核准。(A) 偉辰填 Part A；(C) 父親填 Part C；(D) 表格不交給雇主，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What should Wei-chen do before he hands in the form?", "options": ["Ask his father to sign Part C and attach a copy of the employer's business registration", "Get a signature from the manager of the pet hotel in Part D", "Wait until he has worked at the pet hotel for five school days", "Write down his grades for each subject in Part B"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Part C 的簽名和日期欄仍是空白，必須請父親簽名；最後的 NOTE 也要求 attach a copy of the employer's business registration。(B) Part D 由學校填寫；(C) 表格須在開始工作日「之前」至少五個上課日繳交，不能先工作；(D) Part B 是校規，不需要填寫成績，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a191", "title": "A Clash of Dates: Rescheduling a Job Interview", "title_zh": "撞期了：更改面試時間", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Email", "Career"], "passage": "From: Lin Chia-hao\nTo: Kao Mei-ling, Spinwell Bicycle Workshop\nDate: May 3\nSubject: Interview on May 9\n\nDear Ms. Kao,\n\nThank you for inviting me to an interview for the Weekend Repair Assistant position on Saturday, May 9, at 2:00 p.m. I am very excited about this opportunity.\n\nUnfortunately, I have just learned that my school has chosen me to take part in a regional skills competition in Kaohsiung on May 9 and 10. Would it be possible to have the interview on another day? I am free any weekday after 4:30 p.m., or at any time on Saturday, May 16.\n\nI am sorry for any trouble this may cause.\n\nBest regards,\nLin Chia-hao\n\nFrom: Kao Mei-ling, Spinwell Bicycle Workshop\nTo: Lin Chia-hao\nDate: May 4\nSubject: RE: Interview on May 9\n\nDear Chia-hao,\n\nCongratulations on being chosen for the competition! We are happy to change your interview. Please choose one of these times:\n• Wednesday, May 13, 5:00 p.m.\n• Saturday, May 16, 10:30 a.m.\n\nThe interview will take about 40 minutes. In the first 15 minutes, we will talk about your experience and why you want this job. After that, you will be asked to fix a flat tire and adjust the brakes on one of our bikes, so please wear clothes that can get dirty. Please also bring your student ID and any certificates you have.\n\nOur workshop is a five-minute walk from Exit 2 of Riverside MRT Station, next to a bakery.\n\nPlease reply by May 8 to let me know which time you prefer. If neither time works for you, please call me at (04) 2251-3368.\n\nBest wishes,\nKao Mei-ling\nManager, Spinwell Bicycle Workshop", "passage_zh": "寄件者：林家豪\n收件者：高美玲，順輪自行車工坊\n日期：5 月 3 日\n主旨：5 月 9 日的面試\n\n高小姐您好：\n\n感謝您邀請我於 5 月 9 日（星期六）下午 2:00 參加「週末維修助理」一職的面試。我對這個機會感到非常興奮。\n\n不巧的是，我剛得知學校選派我參加 5 月 9 日和 10 日在高雄舉行的區域技能競賽。請問面試是否能改到其他日子？我平日下午 4:30 以後都有空，5 月 16 日（星期六）則整天都可以。\n\n很抱歉可能為您帶來不便。\n\n林家豪 敬上\n\n寄件者：高美玲，順輪自行車工坊\n收件者：林家豪\n日期：5 月 4 日\n主旨：回覆：5 月 9 日的面試\n\n家豪你好：\n\n恭喜你獲選參加比賽！我們很樂意為你更改面試時間。請從以下時段中選擇一個：\n• 5 月 13 日（星期三）下午 5:00\n• 5 月 16 日（星期六）上午 10:30\n\n面試約需 40 分鐘。前 15 分鐘我們會聊聊你的經驗，以及你想做這份工作的原因。之後，你需要為我們的一輛腳踏車補破胎並調整煞車，所以請穿可以弄髒的衣服。另外也請攜帶學生證，以及你擁有的任何證照。\n\n我們的工坊距離河濱捷運站 2 號出口步行五分鐘，就在一家麵包店旁邊。\n\n請在 5 月 8 日前回信告訴我你偏好哪個時段。如果兩個時段都不方便，請打 (04) 2251-3368 給我。\n\n順輪自行車工坊經理\n高美玲 敬上", "questions": [{"q": "Why did Chia-hao write the first email?", "options": ["To thank Ms. Kao for giving him the job", "To ask how to get to the bicycle workshop", "To ask if his interview could be moved to another day", "To invite Ms. Kao to watch his skills competition"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一封信第二段：Would it be possible to have the interview on another day? 家豪因為要參加比賽，寫信詢問能否更改面試日期。(A) 他還沒被錄取，只是受邀面試；(B) 交通資訊是高小姐回信才提供的；(D) 他沒有邀請對方觀賽，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why can't Chia-hao go to the interview on May 9?", "options": ["He will be at a competition for his school.", "He has to take an exam at school that day.", "He has to work at another bicycle shop.", "He will be visiting his family in Kaohsiung."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一封信第二段：my school has chosen me to take part in a regional skills competition in Kaohsiung on May 9 and 10，他當天要代表學校參加技能競賽。(B)(C) 文中沒有提到考試或其他工作；(D) 他去高雄是為了比賽，不是探親，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "About how long will the hands-on part of the interview, which comes after the talk, last?", "options": ["15 minutes", "25 minutes", "40 minutes", "55 minutes"], "answer": 1, "explain": "回信第二段：面試約 40 分鐘，前 15 分鐘是談話，之後才是補胎和調整煞車的實作，所以實作約 40 − 15 = 25 分鐘。(A) 是談話的時間；(C) 是整場面試的時間；(D) 是把兩個數字相加，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What should Chia-hao do by May 8?", "options": ["Call Ms. Kao to ask for a third interview time", "Visit the workshop to see where it is", "Send Ms. Kao a copy of his certificates", "Reply to Ms. Kao with the time he chooses"], "answer": 3, "explain": "回信最後一段：Please reply by May 8 to let me know which time you prefer. 他要在 5 月 8 日前回信告知選擇的時段；兩個時段都符合他提到的空檔，所以不需要另外打電話。(A) 只有兩個時段都不行時才打電話；(B) 文中沒有要求先去工坊；(C) 證照是面試當天帶去，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In the second email, the word \"adjust\" is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["remove completely", "paint a new color", "change slightly so that something works well", "buy a new one"], "answer": 2, "explain": "fix a flat tire and adjust the brakes 指補好破胎並「調整」煞車，adjust 是稍做改變使東西運作良好，最接近 change slightly so that something works well。(A)「完全拆除」、(B)「重新上漆」、(D)「買新的」都不是調整的意思，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a192", "title": "Click, Choose, Wait: Applying for a City Summer Job Program", "title_zh": "點選、填志願、等抽籤：申請市府暑期工讀計畫", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Instructions", "Career"], "passage": "Riverbend City Youth Office\nSUMMER WORK PROGRAM FOR STUDENTS\nHow to Apply Online\n\nEvery summer, the Youth Office offers 300 paid jobs in city libraries, parks, museums and offices. Students aged 16 to 24 who live or study in Riverbend City may apply. All jobs last four weeks, from July 13 to August 7. Students work Monday to Friday, six hours a day, and are paid NT$215 per hour.\n\nStep 1: Create an Account (April 1 – April 20)\nGo to www.riverbendyouth.tw and sign up with your mobile phone number. You will receive a six-digit code by text message. Enter the code within 10 minutes, or you will have to ask for a new one.\n\nStep 2: Fill in the Form and Choose Your Jobs\nEnter your personal information and choose up to three jobs in order of preference. You cannot change your choices after you click \"Submit,\" so check them carefully.\n\nStep 3: Upload Your Documents\n• Your student ID card (front and back)\n• A photo of yourself taken within the last six months\n• Students under 18: a consent form signed by a parent\nAll files must be in JPG or PDF format and no larger than 2 MB each.\n\nStep 4: Wait for the Lottery\nThere are always more applicants than jobs, so places are given out by a computer lottery on May 5. Results will be sent by text message and posted on our website the same day. Students who are chosen must attend a three-hour training session on June 27. Anyone who misses the training will lose his or her place.\n\nNeed help? Call the Youth Office at (03) 425-6610 (weekdays, 9:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m.).", "passage_zh": "河灣市青年事務局\n學生暑期工讀計畫\n線上申請說明\n\n每年暑假，青年事務局都會在市立圖書館、公園、博物館和各機關提供 300 個有薪工作。住在河灣市或在河灣市就學的 16 至 24 歲學生皆可申請。所有工作為期四週，從 7 月 13 日至 8 月 7 日。學生週一至週五上班，每天六小時，時薪新台幣 215 元。\n\n步驟一：建立帳號（4 月 1 日至 4 月 20 日）\n請上 www.riverbendyouth.tw 以手機號碼註冊。你會收到一封含六位數驗證碼的簡訊，請在 10 分鐘內輸入驗證碼，否則就必須重新索取驗證碼。\n\n步驟二：填寫表單並選擇工作\n輸入個人資料，並依喜好順序選擇最多三個工作志願。點選「送出」後就無法更改志願，所以請仔細檢查。\n\n步驟三：上傳文件\n• 學生證（正面和背面）\n• 最近六個月內拍攝的本人照片\n• 未滿 18 歲者：由家長簽名的同意書\n所有檔案須為 JPG 或 PDF 格式，每個檔案不得超過 2 MB。\n\n步驟四：等待抽籤\n申請人數總是多於工作名額，因此名額將於 5 月 5 日以電腦抽籤決定。結果會在當天以簡訊通知，並公布在本局網站上。獲選的學生必須參加 6 月 27 日為時三小時的訓練課程，未出席訓練者將失去錄取資格。\n\n需要協助嗎？請於平日上午 9:00 至下午 5:00 來電青年事務局：(03) 425-6610。", "questions": [{"q": "According to the guide, how are students chosen for the jobs?", "options": ["By a computer lottery", "By an interview at the Youth Office", "By their school grades", "By the order in which they apply"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Step 4：places are given out by a computer lottery on May 5，名額以電腦抽籤決定。(B) 文中沒有面試；(C) 沒有提到成績；(D) 申請先後順序不影響結果，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "If a chosen student works every working day of the program, how much will he or she earn in total?", "options": ["NT$6,450", "NT$25,800", "NT$21,500", "NT$30,960"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：工作四週，每週一至週五共 5 天，每天 6 小時，時薪 215 元，4 × 5 × 6 × 215 = 25,800 元。(A) 只是一週的薪水；(C) 把每天算成 5 小時；(D) 把每週算成 6 天，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Amy is 17 years old. Which of the following does she NOT need to upload?", "options": ["Both sides of her student ID card", "A recent photo of herself", "A consent form signed by a parent", "Her report card from last semester"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Step 3 列出的文件是學生證正反面、六個月內拍攝的本人照片，以及未滿 18 歲者須附的家長同意書。(A)(B) 每位申請者都要上傳；(C) Amy 17 歲，未滿 18 歲，所以也要上傳家長同意書；(D) 成績單並不在清單中，不需上傳，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What will happen to a chosen student who does not attend the training on June 27?", "options": ["He or she will be paid less per hour.", "He or she will have to attend training on May 5 instead.", "He or she will lose the place in the program.", "He or she will be given a different job."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Step 4 最後一句：Anyone who misses the training will lose his or her place. 沒參加訓練就會失去錄取資格。(A)(D) 文中沒有減薪或換工作的說法；(B) 5 月 5 日是抽籤日，不是訓練日，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In Step 2, \"in order of preference\" means you should list the jobs ______.", "options": ["from the closest to the farthest", "from the one you want most to the one you want least", "from the highest pay to the lowest pay", "in the order shown on the website"], "answer": 1, "explain": "preference 是「偏好、喜愛」，choose up to three jobs in order of preference 指依自己的喜好順序填寫志願，也就是從最想做的排到最不想做的。(A) 與距離無關；(C) 所有工作時薪相同，都是 215 元；(D) 不是照網站上的順序，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a193", "title": "Too Good to Be True: Spotting a Fake Job Ad", "title_zh": "好得不像真的：識破假徵才廣告", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Notice", "Career"], "passage": "Westgate Vocational High School – Career Center\nWARNING: IS THIS JOB REAL?\n\nLast week, several of our students received the following message on social media:\n\n★ EASY JOB, BIG MONEY! ★\nWork from home with just your phone! No experience needed. Earn NT$3,000 a day for only 2 hours of work. Students welcome! To start, simply send us a photo of your ID card and your bank account information. Pay a NT$2,000 training fee today and start earning tomorrow! Only 5 places left. Add our chat ID now: easymoney88\n\nThis is NOT a real job. Here are four warning signs:\n1. The pay is too high. Most part-time jobs pay about NT$200 an hour, but this ad promises NT$3,000 for just two hours.\n2. A real employer will never ask you to pay money before you start working.\n3. The ad asks for your ID card and bank account. Never give these to strangers. Criminals may use your account to move stolen money, and you could be held responsible by the police.\n4. The ad pushes you to act fast. Scammers want you to decide before you have time to think or ask anyone for advice.\n\nWhat should you do if you get a message like this?\n• Do not reply, and do not click any links.\n• Take a screenshot and show it to a teacher or your parents.\n• Call the 165 Anti-Fraud Hotline to report it.\n\nLooking for a safe part-time job? Visit the Career Center (Room 210) from 12:10 to 1:00 p.m. on any school day. Every job on our job board has been checked by our staff.\n\nMr. Tsai, Career Counselor", "passage_zh": "西門高職就業輔導中心\n警告：這份工作是真的嗎？\n\n上週，本校有幾位同學在社群媒體上收到以下訊息：\n\n★ 輕鬆工作，賺大錢！ ★\n只要一支手機就能在家工作！不需經驗。每天只要工作 2 小時，就能賺新台幣 3,000 元。歡迎學生！只要傳給我們一張身分證照片和你的銀行帳戶資料，就能開始。今天繳交新台幣 2,000 元訓練費，明天就能開始賺錢！只剩 5 個名額，快加我們的聊天帳號：easymoney88\n\n這「不是」真的工作。以下是四個警訊：\n1. 薪水高得離譜。大多數兼職工作的時薪約新台幣 200 元，這則廣告卻承諾只要兩小時就能拿到 3,000 元。\n2. 真正的雇主絕不會要你在開始工作前先付錢。\n3. 廣告要求提供身分證和銀行帳戶。千萬不要把這些交給陌生人。歹徒可能利用你的帳戶轉移贓款，你也可能因此被警方追究責任。\n4. 廣告催促你趕快行動。詐騙集團就是要你在還來不及思考或詢問任何人的意見之前就做決定。\n\n如果收到這類訊息，你該怎麼做？\n• 不要回覆，也不要點選任何連結。\n• 截圖後拿給老師或爸媽看。\n• 撥打 165 反詐騙專線檢舉。\n\n想找安全的兼職工作嗎？每個上課日中午 12:10 至 1:00，歡迎到就業輔導中心（210 室）。我們看板上的每份工作都經過本中心人員審核。\n\n就業輔導老師 蔡老師", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this notice?", "options": ["To advertise a new part-time job for students", "To explain how to open a bank account", "To warn students about a fake job offer and tell them what to do", "To invite students to a training course on social media"], "answer": 2, "explain": "標題 WARNING: IS THIS JOB REAL? 以及 This is NOT a real job 之後列出四個警訊和處理方法，可知目的是提醒學生小心假徵才廣告並告訴他們該怎麼做。(A) 文中的廣告是被拿來當作反面例子；(B) 沒有說明開戶方式；(D) 訓練費是詐騙廣告的內容，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How much does the ad promise to pay for one hour of work?", "options": ["NT$200", "NT$1,500", "NT$2,000", "NT$3,000"], "answer": 1, "explain": "廣告說 Earn NT$3,000 a day for only 2 hours of work，每小時是 3,000 ÷ 2 = 1,500 元。(A) 是警訊 1 提到的大多數兼職工作的時薪；(C) 是廣告要求先繳的訓練費；(D) 是一天兩小時的總金額，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the notice, why is it dangerous to give your bank account information to strangers?", "options": ["Criminals may use the account for stolen money, and you could get into trouble.", "The bank will close your account and keep all your money.", "You will have to pay a training fee every month.", "Your school will not let you look for a part-time job."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段警訊 3：Criminals may use your account to move stolen money, and you could be held responsible by the police. 歹徒可能用你的帳戶轉移贓款，你也可能被警方追究責任。(B)(C)(D) 文中都沒有這些說法，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Hsin-yi received a similar message yesterday. What should she do?", "options": ["Pay the fee quickly before all the places are gone", "Reply to the message to ask for more details", "Send a photo of her ID card to check if the job is real", "Take a screenshot and show it to a teacher or her parents"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段 What should you do? 的建議：Take a screenshot and show it to a teacher or your parents，並撥打 165 檢舉。(A) 先付錢正是警訊 2 所說的詐騙手法；(B) 文中說 Do not reply；(C) 警訊 3 說千萬不要把身分證交給陌生人，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the ad most likely say \"Only 5 places left\"?", "options": ["To make readers act quickly without thinking carefully", "To show that the company is very small", "To tell readers that the job is almost finished", "To explain why the training fee is so cheap"], "answer": 0, "explain": "警訊 4 說 The ad pushes you to act fast. Scammers want you to decide before you have time to think，「只剩 5 個名額」就是製造急迫感，讓人來不及思考就行動。(B)(C)(D) 都不是這句話的目的，文中也沒有相關說明，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a194", "title": "Quiet After Eleven: House Rules at a City Hostel", "title_zh": "十一點後請保持安靜：青年旅館住宿須知", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Travel", "Notice"], "passage": "WELCOME TO OLD TOWN HOSTEL\nNo. 18, Lane 6, Zhongyi Road, Tainan | Tel: (06) 221-7085\n\nCheck-in and Check-out\nCheck-in: 3:00 p.m.–10:00 p.m. | Check-out: by 11:00 a.m.\nArriving after 10:00 p.m.? Send us a message before 8:00 p.m., and we will leave your key in the lock box by the front door.\nLate check-out until 1:00 p.m. costs NT$150, if no other guest has booked your bed.\n\nYour Bed and Locker\n• Every guest gets one locker. Bring your own lock, or rent one at the front desk for NT$50 (plus a NT$100 deposit, which you get back when you return the lock).\n• Bed sheets are free and changed every three days. Towels cost NT$30 each.\n• Please do not sit on other guests' beds or move any furniture.\n\nQuiet Hours: 11:00 p.m.–7:00 a.m.\nThe lights in the dorm rooms go off at 11:00 p.m. If you want to chat, make a phone call or watch videos during quiet hours, please use the lounge on the first floor.\n\nShared Kitchen (open 7:00 a.m.–10:00 p.m.)\n• Wash, dry and put away everything you use.\n• Write your name and check-out date on any food you keep in the fridge. Food without a label is thrown away every Sunday night.\n• Free tea and coffee are served in the lounge all day.\n\nLaundry\nWashing machine: NT$40 | Dryer: NT$40 for 40 minutes\nBuy tokens at the front desk (open 8:00 a.m.–10:00 p.m.).\n\nNot Allowed\nSmoking anywhere in the building (fine: NT$2,000), pets, and visitors in the dorm rooms. Guests under 16 must stay with a parent or guardian.\n\nEnjoy your stay, and ask us anything about Tainan!", "passage_zh": "歡迎光臨老城青年旅館\n臺南市忠義路 6 巷 18 號｜電話：(06) 221-7085\n\n入住與退房\n入住時間：下午 3:00 至晚上 10:00｜退房時間：上午 11:00 前\n晚上 10:00 以後才會抵達嗎？請在晚上 8:00 前傳訊息給我們，我們會把鑰匙放在大門旁的鑰匙盒裡。\n延後退房至下午 1:00 須付 NT$150，前提是您的床位沒有其他房客預訂。\n\n床位與置物櫃\n• 每位房客有一個置物櫃。請自備鎖頭，或在櫃檯租借，租金 NT$50（另收 NT$100 押金，歸還鎖頭時退還）。\n• 床單免費提供，每三天更換一次。毛巾每條 NT$30。\n• 請勿坐在其他房客的床上，也請勿移動任何家具。\n\n安靜時段：晚上 11:00 至上午 7:00\n宿舍房間的燈會在晚上 11:00 關閉。安靜時段內若想聊天、講電話或看影片，請到一樓的交誼廳。\n\n共用廚房（開放時間：上午 7:00 至晚上 10:00）\n• 使用過的所有物品請洗淨、擦乾並歸位。\n• 放進冰箱的食物請寫上姓名和退房日期。沒有標示的食物會在每週日晚上丟掉。\n• 交誼廳全天免費供應茶和咖啡。\n\n洗衣\n洗衣機：NT$40｜烘衣機：40 分鐘 NT$40\n代幣請在櫃檯購買（櫃檯服務時間：上午 8:00 至晚上 10:00）。\n\n禁止事項\n館內全面禁菸（罰款 NT$2,000），禁止攜帶寵物，訪客不得進入宿舍房間。未滿 16 歲的房客必須與父母或監護人同住。\n\n祝您住宿愉快，有任何關於臺南的問題都歡迎問我們！", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this text?", "options": ["To tell guests about the rules and services of a hostel", "To invite travelers to work part-time at a hostel", "To introduce the famous places to visit in Tainan", "To explain why a hostel has to raise its prices"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以標題分段說明入住退房時間、置物櫃、安靜時段、廚房、洗衣及禁止事項，是給房客看的住宿規定與服務說明。(B) 文中沒有徵人訊息；(C) 最後一句只說歡迎詢問臺南的事，並未介紹景點；(D) 文中沒有提到漲價，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Hana rents a lock and one towel, and she washes and dries her clothes once. Not counting the deposit, how much does she pay in total?", "options": ["NT$130", "NT$200", "NT$260", "NT$160"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Your Bed and Locker 段：租鎖 NT$50、毛巾 NT$30；Laundry 段：洗衣 NT$40、烘衣 NT$40。合計 50 + 30 + 40 + 40 = 160 元。(C) 260 元是把會退還的押金 NT$100 也算進去；(A) 130 元少算了毛巾的 NT$30；(B) 200 元多算了一次 NT$40 的洗衣或烘衣費，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Mr. Ito's train will reach Tainan late, and he will get to the hostel at 11:30 p.m. What should he do?", "options": ["Call the hostel after he arrives at the front door.", "Send the hostel a message before 8:00 p.m. that day.", "Pay NT$150 for a late check-in at the front desk.", "Wait in the lounge until the front desk opens the next morning."], "answer": 1, "explain": "Check-in and Check-out 段：Arriving after 10:00 p.m.? Send us a message before 8:00 p.m.（晚上 10 點後才抵達，請在晚上 8 點前傳訊息），旅館會把鑰匙放在門邊的鎖盒裡。(A) 到了才打電話已太晚；(C) NT$150 是「延後退房」的費用，不是晚到入住；(D) 文中沒有這樣的安排，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"deposit\" in the \"Your Bed and Locker\" part is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["money you pay as a fine for breaking a rule", "money you pay first and get back later", "a discount for guests who book early", "a gift that the hostel gives to every guest"], "answer": 1, "explain": "原句說 a NT$100 deposit, which you get back when you return the lock（NT$100 押金，歸還鎖頭時退還），可知 deposit 是「先付、之後會拿回來的錢」，也就是押金。(A) 罰款是 Not Allowed 段的 fine；(C)(D) 文中沒有提到折扣或贈品，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following guests is breaking the hostel's rules?", "options": ["A guest who keeps a bottle of milk with her name and check-out date in the fridge", "A guest who cooks noodles in the kitchen at 9:00 p.m.", "A 15-year-old student who travels alone and stays in a dorm room", "A guest who drinks a cup of free coffee in the lounge at 10:30 p.m."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Not Allowed 段最後一句：Guests under 16 must stay with a parent or guardian（未滿 16 歲須與父母或監護人同住），15 歲學生獨自入住違反規定。(A) 有寫姓名和退房日期，符合規定；(B) 廚房開放到晚上 10:00；(D) 交誼廳全天供應免費茶和咖啡，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a195", "title": "Seven Kilos and Not One More: A Budget Airline's Baggage Rules", "title_zh": "七公斤，一公克都不能多：廉價航空的行李規定", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Travel", "Instructions"], "passage": "SKYLARK AIR\nBaggage Guide for All Flights\n\nOur fares are low because you pay only for what you need. Before you pack, please read these rules carefully.\n\nCarry-on Baggage (included in every ticket)\n• One bag of up to 7 kg and no larger than 56 × 36 × 23 cm, plus one small item such as a handbag or laptop bag.\n• Carry-on bags are weighed at the gate. A bag that is too heavy must be checked in there, and the fee is NT$1,500.\n• Liquids must be in bottles of 100 ml or less, and all bottles must fit in one clear plastic bag of up to 1 liter.\n• Power banks and spare batteries must be carried in your carry-on bag. They are not allowed in checked baggage.\n\nChecked Baggage (not included)\nBuy online at least 4 hours before departure:\n15 kg: NT$700 | 20 kg: NT$900 | 25 kg: NT$1,100 | 30 kg: NT$1,400\nIf you buy it at the airport counter instead, each weight costs NT$600 more.\nIf your bag is heavier than the weight you paid for, you will pay NT$300 for each extra kilogram at the airport.\n\nSports Equipment\nSurfboards, bicycles and golf bags cost NT$1,200 each way. They must be booked at least 24 hours before departure, and each one may weigh up to 20 kg.\n\nCheck-in\nOnline check-in opens 48 hours before departure and closes 1 hour before. Airport check-in counters close 50 minutes before departure. Passengers who miss these times cannot fly, and their tickets will not be refunded.\n\nNeed help? Use the \"Help\" page in the Skylark Air app or call (02) 2715-8800 (8:00 a.m.–9:00 p.m. daily).", "passage_zh": "雲雀航空\n所有航班行李須知\n\n我們的票價低廉，因為您只需為自己需要的服務付費。打包前，請仔細閱讀以下規定。\n\n手提行李（每張機票皆含）\n• 一件重量不超過 7 公斤、尺寸不超過 56 × 36 × 23 公分的行李，另可攜帶一件小型隨身物品，例如手提包或筆電包。\n• 手提行李會在登機門過磅。超重的行李必須在登機門改為託運，費用為 NT$1,500。\n• 液體必須裝在 100 毫升以下的瓶子裡，而且所有瓶子必須放進一個容量不超過 1 公升的透明塑膠袋。\n• 行動電源和備用電池必須放在手提行李中，不可放在託運行李裡。\n\n託運行李（不含在機票內）\n請在起飛前至少 4 小時上網購買：\n15 公斤：NT$700｜20 公斤：NT$900｜25 公斤：NT$1,100｜30 公斤：NT$1,400\n若改在機場櫃檯購買，每種重量都要多付 NT$600。\n如果行李超過您已購買的重量，在機場每超重 1 公斤須付 NT$300。\n\n運動器材\n衝浪板、自行車及高爾夫球袋每趟收費 NT$1,200。須在起飛前至少 24 小時預訂，每件重量上限為 20 公斤。\n\n報到\n線上報到於起飛前 48 小時開放，起飛前 1 小時截止。機場報到櫃檯於起飛前 50 分鐘關閉。錯過以上時間的旅客將無法登機，機票也不予退款。\n\n需要協助嗎？請使用雲雀航空 App 中的「協助」頁面，或撥打 (02) 2715-8800（每日上午 8:00 至晚上 9:00）。", "questions": [{"q": "Ben bought 20 kg of checked baggage online. At the airport, his bag weighs 23 kg. How much more must he pay?", "options": ["NT$300", "NT$600", "NT$900", "NT$1,500"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Checked Baggage 段最後一句：超過已購買的重量，每多 1 公斤付 NT$300。23 − 20 = 3 公斤，3 × 300 = 900 元。(A) 只算了 1 公斤；(B) NT$600 是櫃檯購買比網路多付的金額；(D) NT$1,500 是手提行李在登機門改託運的費用，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Amy forgot to buy checked baggage online. At the airport counter, she buys 15 kg for her suitcase. How much does she pay?", "options": ["NT$700", "NT$1,300", "NT$1,500", "NT$600"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Checked Baggage 段：15 公斤網路價 NT$700，If you buy it at the airport counter instead, each weight costs NT$600 more（在機場櫃檯買要多付 NT$600），700 + 600 = 1,300 元。(A) 是網路價；(D) 只是加價的部分；(C) 是手提行李超重的費用，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would NOT be allowed by Skylark Air?", "options": ["A laptop bag carried together with a 7 kg carry-on bag", "A power bank packed in a carry-on bag", "A 50 ml bottle of shampoo in a clear plastic bag", "A 150 ml bottle of sunscreen in a carry-on bag"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Carry-on Baggage 段第三點：Liquids must be in bottles of 100 ml or less（液體瓶子須在 100 毫升以下），150 毫升的防曬乳不能放在手提行李中。(A) 第一點允許另帶一件小物品如筆電包；(B) 第四點規定行動電源必須放手提行李；(C) 50 毫升符合規定，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Kevin's flight to Okinawa leaves at 2:00 p.m. on Saturday, and he wants to take his surfboard. What is the latest time he can book it?", "options": ["2:00 p.m. on Friday", "10:00 a.m. on Saturday", "1:00 p.m. on Saturday", "2:00 p.m. on Thursday"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Sports Equipment 段：衝浪板等運動器材 must be booked at least 24 hours before departure（須在起飛前至少 24 小時預訂），週六下午 2:00 往前推 24 小時是週五下午 2:00，這就是最晚的期限。(B) 起飛前 4 小時是購買託運行李的期限；(C) 起飛前 1 小時是線上報到截止時間；(D) 週四下午 2:00 預訂雖然也可以，但不是「最晚」期限，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Mrs. Wu has a suitcase to check in. She reaches the check-in counter 40 minutes before her flight. What will most likely happen?", "options": ["She will pay NT$600 more and then check in.", "She can still check in online on her phone.", "She cannot take the flight, and she will not get her money back.", "Her bag will be weighed at the gate for free."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Check-in 段：機場櫃檯於起飛前 50 分鐘關閉，Passengers who miss these times cannot fly, and their tickets will not be refunded（錯過時間的旅客不能登機，也不退款）。她提前 40 分鐘才到，已過櫃檯關閉時間；線上報到也在起飛前 1 小時截止，所以 (B) 錯；(A)(D) 文中沒有這樣的補救方式，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a196", "title": "Scan, Ride, Return: How to Use a Public Bike", "title_zh": "掃碼、騎乘、歸還：公共自行車使用說明", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Instructions", "Travel"], "passage": "GREENWHEEL CITY BIKES\nRide in Four Easy Steps\n\nStep 1: Get Ready\nDownload the GreenWheel app and sign up with your phone number. You can also register a transit card at the screen of any GreenWheel station. Riders must be at least 12 years old.\n\nStep 2: Rent a Bike\nScan the QR code on the bike's small screen with the app, or tap your transit card on the card reader. When the light turns green and you hear a beep, pull the bike out of the dock.\n\nStep 3: Ride Safely\nCheck the brakes, bell and tires before you leave. Ride in bike lanes or on sidewalks marked for bikes. Do not hold a phone while riding, and never carry a passenger.\n\nStep 4: Return the Bike\nPush the bike firmly into an empty dock at any GreenWheel station until you hear a beep. Your ride is not finished until the bike's screen shows \"Returned.\" If the station is full, open the app to find the nearest station with empty docks. You will get 15 extra minutes free to ride there.\n\nPrices\nFirst 30 minutes: NT$10\nFrom 30 minutes to 4 hours: NT$10 for every 30 minutes\nAfter 4 hours: NT$20 for every 30 minutes\nPay with your transit card or a credit card linked to the app.\n\nBroken Bike?\nReturn it as usual, then press the red \"Repair\" button on the dock so that the next rider will not take it. Riders who lose a bike or do not return it may have to pay up to NT$10,000.\n\n24-hour Service Line: (04) 2358-6600", "passage_zh": "綠輪城市單車\n四個簡單步驟就能騎\n\n步驟一：準備\n下載綠輪 App，用手機號碼註冊。也可以在任何一個綠輪站點的螢幕上登記交通卡。騎乘者必須年滿 12 歲。\n\n步驟二：租車\n用 App 掃描車上小螢幕的 QR code，或把交通卡靠近感應器。燈號變綠並聽到「嗶」一聲後，就可以把車從車柱拉出來。\n\n步驟三：安全騎乘\n出發前先檢查煞車、車鈴和輪胎。請騎在自行車道上，或騎在標示可以騎腳踏車的人行道上。騎車時不要手拿手機，也絕對不可以載人。\n\n步驟四：還車\n把車用力推進任何一個綠輪站點的空車柱，直到聽見「嗶」一聲。車上的螢幕顯示「已歸還」才算完成還車。如果站點已滿，請打開 App 尋找最近、有空車柱的站點，系統會多給您 15 分鐘免費騎過去。\n\n費率\n前 30 分鐘：NT$10\n30 分鐘至 4 小時：每 30 分鐘 NT$10\n超過 4 小時：每 30 分鐘 NT$20\n可用交通卡或綁定在 App 的信用卡付款。\n\n車子壞了嗎？\n請照常還車，再按下車柱上的紅色「報修」按鈕，以免下一位騎乘者借到它。遺失車輛或未歸還車輛的騎乘者，最高可能須賠償 NT$10,000。\n\n24 小時客服專線：(04) 2358-6600", "questions": [{"q": "Jason rides a GreenWheel bike for exactly 5 hours. How much does he pay?", "options": ["NT$100", "NT$120", "NT$80", "NT$200"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Prices 段：前 4 小時每 30 分鐘 NT$10，共 8 × 10 = 80 元；超過 4 小時後每 30 分鐘 NT$20，第 5 個小時有 2 個 30 分鐘，2 × 20 = 40 元，合計 120 元。(A) 是全程都用 NT$10 計算；(C) 只算了前 4 小時；(D) 是全程都用 NT$20 計算，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Who is NOT allowed to rent a GreenWheel bike?", "options": ["A 12-year-old girl who uses a registered transit card", "A college student who pays with a credit card in the app", "A 70-year-old man who signs up with his phone number", "An 11-year-old boy who wants to ride to the park with his friends"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Step 1 最後一句：Riders must be at least 12 years old（騎乘者必須年滿 12 歲），11 歲的男孩不能租車。(A) 剛好 12 歲，符合規定；(B)(C) 文中沒有其他年齡或身分限制，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Mia arrives at a station to return her bike, but every dock is full. What should she do?", "options": ["Use the app to find a nearby station with empty docks and ride there.", "Leave the bike next to the station and press the red button.", "Call the service line and wait for someone to take the bike.", "Lock the bike to a tree and return it the next day."], "answer": 0, "explain": "Step 4 最後兩句：If the station is full, open the app to find the nearest station with empty docks（站點已滿時，用 App 找最近有空位的站點），還會多給 15 分鐘免費騎過去。(B) 紅色按鈕是用來報修故障車輛的；(C)(D) 文中沒有這樣的做法，而且未歸還車輛可能要賠錢，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the instructions, when is a ride finished?", "options": ["When the rider hears a beep at the card reader", "When the light on the bike turns green", "When the screen on the bike shows \"Returned\"", "When the rider presses the red \"Repair\" button"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Step 4 第二句：Your ride is not finished until the bike's screen shows \"Returned.\"（車上螢幕顯示「已歸還」才算完成還車）。(A)(B) 是 Step 2 租車時的訊號；(D) 紅色按鈕是車子故障時才需要按的，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"firmly\" in Step 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["slowly and carefully", "strongly, so that it stays in place", "quietly, without making a sound", "quickly, without stopping"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Step 4 說 Push the bike firmly into an empty dock ... until you hear a beep，要「用力」把車推進車柱直到卡好、聽到嗶聲，firmly 意思是「牢固地、用力地」。(A) 小心慢慢推不一定能卡好；(C) 文中反而要求聽到嗶聲；(D) 與「穩固」無關，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a197", "title": "Tall Enough to Ride? Theme Park Tickets and Ride Rules", "title_zh": "身高夠了嗎？主題樂園門票與設施搭乘規定", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Travel", "Money"], "passage": "RAINBOW HILLS ADVENTURE PARK\nOpen daily 9:30 a.m.–5:30 p.m.\n(Saturdays in July and August: open until 9:00 p.m.)\n\nOne-Day Tickets (NT$)\nAdult (18–64): 899\nStudent (with a student ID card): 749\nChild (110 cm to under 140 cm): 599\nSenior (65 and over): 449\nChildren under 110 cm: free\nNight Ticket (enter after 4:00 p.m.): 450 for everyone\nBirthday Gift: Free entry on your birthday. Just show an ID card with your date of birth.\n\nRide Height Rules\nThunder Dragon roller coaster: 140 cm or taller\nPirate Ship: 120 cm or taller\nWater Rapids: 110 cm or taller (riders under 120 cm must sit with an adult)\nMagic Carousel: open to everyone (riders under 100 cm must sit with an adult)\nHeight is checked at the entrance of each ride. For your safety, our staff will not let anyone ride who is even 1 cm too short.\n\nFast Pass\nSkip the line at Thunder Dragon and Water Rapids as many times as you like for NT$300 per person per day. Fast Passes are sold at Guest Service near the main gate, and only 500 are sold each day.\n\nRain and Lightning\nOutdoor rides may stop during heavy rain or when there is lightning. If the whole park has to close before 1:00 p.m., keep your ticket and come back once for free within 30 days.\n\nGuest Service: (03) 587-2468", "passage_zh": "彩虹山冒險樂園\n每日上午 9:30 至下午 5:30 開放\n（7 月與 8 月的週六：開放至晚上 9:00）\n\n一日票（新臺幣）\n全票（18–64 歲）：899\n學生票（須出示學生證）：749\n兒童票（身高 110 公分以上、未滿 140 公分）：599\n敬老票（65 歲以上）：449\n身高未滿 110 公分的兒童：免費\n夜間票（下午 4:00 後入園）：一律 450\n生日禮：生日當天免費入園，只要出示有出生日期的證件即可。\n\n設施身高限制\n雷霆飛龍雲霄飛車：140 公分以上\n海盜船：120 公分以上\n激流泛舟：110 公分以上（未滿 120 公分者須由大人陪同乘坐）\n魔法旋轉木馬：所有人皆可搭乘（未滿 100 公分者須由大人陪同乘坐）\n每項設施入口都會測量身高。為了您的安全，即使只差 1 公分，工作人員也不會讓您搭乘。\n\n快速通關券\n雷霆飛龍和激流泛舟可不限次數免排隊，每人每天 NT$300。快速通關券在正門附近的遊客服務中心販售，每天只賣 500 張。\n\n下雨與打雷\n大雨或打雷時，戶外設施可能暫停運轉。如果整座樂園在下午 1:00 前必須關閉，請保留門票，30 天內可免費再入園一次。\n\n遊客服務中心：(03) 587-2468", "questions": [{"q": "Mr. and Mrs. Lee (both 45) visit the park in the morning with their son, a 16-year-old high school student who is 172 cm tall and brings his student ID, and their daughter, who is 125 cm tall. How much do they pay for tickets?", "options": ["NT$3,146", "NT$2,996", "NT$3,296", "NT$2,547"], "answer": 0, "explain": "One-Day Tickets 段：兩位大人 899 × 2 = 1,798；兒子 172 公分，不能買兒童票，但有學生證可買學生票 749；女兒 125 公分屬於 110 至未滿 140 公分的兒童票 599。合計 1,798 + 749 + 599 = 3,146 元。(B) 把兒子算成兒童票；(C) 把兒子算成全票；(D) 漏算女兒的票，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following can a 115-cm-tall boy do at the park?", "options": ["Ride the Pirate Ship with his father", "Ride the Water Rapids with an adult", "Ride the Water Rapids by himself", "Ride the Thunder Dragon if he buys a Fast Pass"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Ride Height Rules 段：激流泛舟 110 公分以上可搭，但 riders under 120 cm must sit with an adult（未滿 120 公分須由大人陪同），所以 115 公分的男孩可以和大人一起搭。(A) 海盜船要 120 公分以上，有大人陪也不行；(C) 他未滿 120 公分，不能自己搭；(D) 雲霄飛車要 140 公分，買快速通關券也不能改變身高限制，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Emma, a college student, can only come to the park at 5:00 p.m. on a Saturday in August. Which ticket is the cheapest choice for her?", "options": ["A Student ticket", "An Adult ticket with a Fast Pass", "A Child ticket", "A Night Ticket"], "answer": 3, "explain": "樂園在 7、8 月的週六開到晚上 9:00；Night Ticket (enter after 4:00 p.m.): 450 for everyone，她下午 5:00 入園可以買 NT$450 的夜間票，比學生票 NT$749 便宜。(A) 較貴；(B) 更貴；(C) 兒童票以身高 110 至未滿 140 公分為準，大學生不適用，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Because of lightning, the whole park closes at 11:30 a.m. What can visitors do?", "options": ["Get their money back at Guest Service right away.", "Exchange their tickets for free Fast Passes.", "Use their tickets to come back once for free within 30 days.", "Stay in the park and wait for the rides to open again at night."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Rain and Lightning 段：If the whole park has to close before 1:00 p.m., keep your ticket and come back once for free within 30 days（整座樂園在下午 1 點前關閉，保留門票 30 天內可免費再來一次）。11:30 早於 1:00，符合條件。(A)(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the Fast Pass?", "options": ["It can be used on every ride in the park.", "It may be sold out on busy days.", "It is free for students and seniors.", "It can be used only once on each ride."], "answer": 1, "explain": "Fast Pass 段：only 500 are sold each day（每天只賣 500 張），可推知遊客多的日子可能會賣完。(A) 只能用在雷霆飛龍和激流泛舟兩項設施；(C) 每人每天 NT$300，沒有提到免費；(D) 原文說 as many times as you like（不限次數），故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a198", "title": "Car 7, Seat 12A: Reading Your Train Ticket", "title_zh": "7 車 12A 座：看懂你的火車票", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Travel", "Money"], "passage": "COASTLINE EXPRESS\nPassenger: Lin Yu-ting\nTrain 517 | Friday, April 3\nTaichung (dep. 14:25) → Kaohsiung (arr. 15:30)\nCar 7, Seat 12A | Standard Class | Adult | NT$790\nTicket No. 0517-2208-4413\n\nFinding Your Seat\nEach row in Standard Class has five seats. Seats A and E are next to the window, Seat B is in the middle, and Seats C and D are next to the aisle. Your car number is shown on the platform floor, so please wait in the right place before the train arrives.\n\nBefore You Board\n• The ticket gates close 2 minutes before the train leaves.\n• This ticket is good only for the train shown above. If you miss your train, you may take a later Coastline Express train on the same day, but only in Cars 10–12, where seats are not reserved.\n• Keep your ticket until the end of your trip. You will need it to go through the exit gates at Kaohsiung Station.\n\nChanging Your Ticket\nYou may change the date or time of your trip once for free. Changes can be made at any ticket window or in the Coastline Express app up to 30 minutes before the train leaves.\n\nRefunds\nMore than 2 days before departure: NT$20 handling fee\nWithin 2 days of departure: 10% of the ticket price (at least NT$20)\nAfter the train has left: no refund\n\nStudents under 26 can save 25% on some trains from Monday to Thursday. Look for the \"Student Deal\" mark in the app.\n\nCustomer Service: (02) 2788-6600 (6:00 a.m.–midnight)", "passage_zh": "海岸線特快車\n旅客：林語婷\n517 次｜4 月 3 日（星期五）\n臺中（14:25 開）→ 高雄（15:30 到）\n7 車 12A 座｜標準車廂｜全票｜NT$790\n票號：0517-2208-4413\n\n找到你的座位\n標準車廂每排有五個座位。A 座和 E 座靠窗，B 座在中間，C 座和 D 座靠走道。車廂號碼標示在月臺地面上，請在列車進站前到正確的位置等候。\n\n上車之前\n• 驗票閘門在列車開出前 2 分鐘關閉。\n• 本車票只限搭乘上方所列的車次。若錯過列車，可改搭當天較晚的海岸線特快車，但只能坐在第 10 至 12 車的自由座車廂。\n• 請保留車票直到旅程結束。在高雄站出站時，需要用車票通過出站閘門。\n\n更改車票\n旅程的日期或時間可免費更改一次。最晚可在列車開出前 30 分鐘，到任何售票窗口或在海岸線特快車 App 上辦理。\n\n退票\n發車前 2 天以上：手續費 NT$20\n發車前 2 天內：票價的 10%（最低 NT$20）\n列車開出後：不予退票\n\n未滿 26 歲的學生，週一至週四搭乘部分車次可享 75 折優惠。請在 App 中尋找「學生優惠」標示。\n\n客服專線：(02) 2788-6600（上午 6:00 至午夜 12:00）", "questions": [{"q": "What kind of seat does Lin Yu-ting have?", "options": ["An aisle seat in a car with no reserved seats", "A window seat in Standard Class", "A middle seat in Car 12", "An aisle seat in Car 7"], "answer": 1, "explain": "車票上寫 Car 7, Seat 12A | Standard Class，而 Finding Your Seat 段說 Seats A and E are next to the window（A 座和 E 座靠窗），所以她坐的是標準車廂的靠窗座位。(A)(D) 靠走道的是 C、D 座；(C) 中間的是 B 座，而且她在第 7 車，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Yu-ting cancels her trip on April 2. How much money will she get back?", "options": ["NT$711", "NT$770", "NT$790", "NT$79"], "answer": 0, "explain": "4 月 2 日在發車前 2 天內，Refunds 段規定手續費為票價的 10%：790 × 10% = 79 元，可退回 790 − 79 = 711 元。(B) 是誤用發車前 2 天以上的手續費 NT$20；(C) 沒有扣手續費；(D) 是手續費的金額，不是退回的錢，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is the latest time Yu-ting can change her ticket to another time for free?", "options": ["14:23", "14:25", "13:55", "12:25"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Changing Your Ticket 段：可免費更改 up to 30 minutes before the train leaves（最晚在開車前 30 分鐘），14:25 往前推 30 分鐘是 13:55。(A) 14:23 是驗票閘門關閉的時間；(B) 是開車時間；(D) 是提前 2 小時，不是最晚的時間，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Yu-ting gets to Taichung Station at 14:40 and misses her train. What can she do?", "options": ["Ask for a full refund at the ticket window.", "Take any later train that day and sit in Seat 12A.", "Change her ticket for free in the app.", "Take a later Coastline Express train that day and sit in Cars 10–12."], "answer": 3, "explain": "Before You Board 段第二點：If you miss your train, you may take a later Coastline Express train on the same day, but only in Cars 10–12（錯過列車可改搭當天較晚的班次，但只能坐 10 至 12 車的自由座）。(A) 列車開出後不予退票；(B) 不能坐原本的對號座；(C) 更改須在開車前 30 分鐘辦理，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"aisle\" in the \"Finding Your Seat\" part means ______.", "options": ["the place where the train stops for passengers", "the narrow way between rows of seats", "the door at each end of a train car", "the machine that checks tickets at the gate"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Finding Your Seat 段把座位分成 next to the window（靠窗）、in the middle（中間）和 next to the aisle，可知 aisle 是座位之間讓人走動的「走道」。(A) 是 platform（月臺）或 station；(C) 是車門；(D) 是 ticket gate（閘門），故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a199", "title": "Pencils Ready, Phones Away: Rules for the Mock Exam", "title_zh": "鉛筆備好、手機收好：模擬考應試規則", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Education", "Instructions"], "passage": "GUANGHUA VOCATIONAL HIGH SCHOOL\nAcademic Affairs Office\nThird-Year Mock Exam: What You Need to Know\n\nDates: Tuesday, December 8 and Wednesday, December 9\nPlace: Your assigned exam room. The room list will be posted on the board outside the library from December 1.\n\nSchedule\nDay 1: Chinese 8:20–9:30 | English 10:00–11:10 | Math 13:00–14:20\nDay 2: Professional Subject (1) 8:20–9:40 | Professional Subject (2) 10:10–11:30\n\nWhat to Bring\n• Your student ID card. If you forget it, get a free one-day pass from the Academic Affairs Office before 8:00 a.m.\n• A 2B pencil, an eraser and a black pen. Answers on the answer card must be filled in with a 2B pencil, or the machine cannot read them.\n• Calculators are allowed only in Professional Subject (2).\n\nArriving Late and Leaving Early\n• You may enter the exam room up to 20 minutes after a test starts. After that, you will not be allowed to take that test.\n• You may not leave the room during the first 40 minutes of a test.\n\nPhones and Watches\nTurn off your phone and put it in your bag at the front of the room. Smartwatches are not allowed. If your phone rings or makes any sound during a test, 5 points will be taken off your score for that subject.\n\nScores\nScores will be posted on the school website on December 18. If you think there is a mistake in your score, fill in a re-check form at the Academic Affairs Office by 4:00 p.m. on December 21.\n\nTreat this mock exam like the real one. Use it to practice, and good luck!", "passage_zh": "光華高級職業學校\n教務處\n三年級模擬考：應試須知\n\n日期：12 月 8 日（星期二）及 12 月 9 日（星期三）\n地點：各自被分配的試場。試場名單將於 12 月 1 日起張貼在圖書館外的公告欄。\n\n考試時間表\n第一天：國文 8:20–9:30｜英文 10:00–11:10｜數學 13:00–14:20\n第二天：專業科目（一）8:20–9:40｜專業科目（二）10:10–11:30\n\n應攜帶物品\n• 學生證。如果忘了帶，請在上午 8:00 前到教務處領取免費的一日臨時證。\n• 2B 鉛筆、橡皮擦和黑色原子筆。答案卡必須用 2B 鉛筆畫記，否則機器無法讀取。\n• 只有專業科目（二）可以使用計算機。\n\n遲到與提早離場\n• 考試開始後 20 分鐘內可以進入試場；超過時間，該科就不能應考。\n• 考試開始後的前 40 分鐘內不得離開試場。\n\n手機與手錶\n請將手機關機，放進書包並置於教室前方。不得配戴智慧型手錶。考試中如果手機響起或發出任何聲音，該科成績扣 5 分。\n\n成績\n成績將於 12 月 18 日公布在學校網站上。如果認為成績有誤，請在 12 月 21 日下午 4:00 前到教務處填寫複查申請表。\n\n請把這次模擬考當作正式考試來看待。好好利用這次機會練習，祝大家考試順利！", "questions": [{"q": "Who is this notice mainly written for?", "options": ["Teachers who will watch the exam rooms", "Parents who want to visit the school", "Third-year students who will take the mock exam", "Librarians who will post the room list"], "answer": 2, "explain": "標題為 Third-Year Mock Exam: What You Need to Know，內容說明考試日期、要帶的物品、遲到規定、手機規定和成績複查，最後還祝考生 good luck，可知是寫給要參加模擬考的三年級學生。(A)(B)(D) 文中雖提到試場和圖書館，但都不是這份公告的對象，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Ming-hao's bus is late on December 8. What is the latest time he can enter the room for the Math test?", "options": ["13:00", "13:20", "13:40", "12:40"], "answer": 1, "explain": "數學考試在第一天 13:00 開始，Arriving Late 段說 You may enter the exam room up to 20 minutes after a test starts（開考後 20 分鐘內可以入場），13:00 加 20 分鐘是 13:20。(A) 是開考時間；(C) 13:40 是開考後 40 分鐘才能離場的時間；(D) 早於開考時間，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "During the English test, Pei-shan's phone rang in her bag. Without any penalty, her English score would be 72. What will her final English score be?", "options": ["67", "72", "62", "70"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Phones and Watches 段：If your phone rings or makes any sound during a test, 5 points will be taken off your score for that subject（考試中手機發出聲音，該科扣 5 分），原本 72 分扣 5 分，72 − 5 = 67 分。(B) 沒有扣分；(C) 扣了 10 分；(D) 扣了 2 分，都與規定不符，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "On the morning of December 9, Jun realizes that he has left his student ID card at home. What should he do?", "options": ["Go home and get it during the first 40 minutes of the test.", "Take the test and show his ID card on December 21.", "Ask a classmate to lend him a student ID card.", "Get a one-day pass from the Academic Affairs Office before 8:00 a.m."], "answer": 3, "explain": "What to Bring 段第一點：If you forget it, get a free one-day pass from the Academic Affairs Office before 8:00 a.m.（忘記帶學生證，請在上午 8 點前到教務處領取一日臨時證）。(A) 前 40 分鐘不得離場；(B) 12 月 21 日是成績複查的期限；(C) 借用他人證件不合理，文中也沒有提到，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true according to the notice?", "options": ["Students may answer with a black pen on the answer card.", "Students may use a calculator in only one of the five tests.", "Students can find out their exam rooms on the school website.", "Students may leave the room as soon as they finish a test."], "answer": 1, "explain": "What to Bring 段第三點：Calculators are allowed only in Professional Subject (2)，五科中只有專業科目（二）可以用計算機。(A) 答案卡必須用 2B 鉛筆；(C) 試場名單張貼在圖書館外的公告欄，網站公布的是成績；(D) 開考後前 40 分鐘不得離場，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a200", "title": "Dolphins Ahead! Booking a Whale-Watching Trip", "title_zh": "前方有海豚！賞鯨行程預約須知", "level": "Reading · Practical", "tags": ["Nature", "Travel"], "passage": "BLUE HORIZON WHALE WATCHING\nHualien Port | Season: April 1 – October 31\n\nSeveral kinds of dolphins live in the deep waters off Hualien's coast, and on lucky days, whales can be seen too. Join us for a two-hour trip and meet them in the wild!\n\nTrip Times\n7:00 a.m. (Saturdays and Sundays only) | 9:30 a.m. | 1:00 p.m. | 3:30 p.m.\nThe sea is usually calmer in the morning, so morning trips are a good choice for first-timers.\n\nPrices (NT$)\nAdult: 900 | Student (with ID): 800 | Child (3–12): 500\nFor safety reasons, children under 3 are not allowed on board.\nBook online at least 3 days ahead and get 10% off.\n\nOur Promise\nIf no dolphins or whales are seen on your trip, you may join another trip for free within one year. Just show your ticket when you book.\n\nBefore You Go\n• Check in at our counter (Counter 3) at the whale-watching pier in Hualien Port 30 minutes before your trip.\n• If you get seasick easily, take seasickness medicine 30 to 60 minutes before getting on the boat, and eat only a light meal.\n• Bring a hat, sunscreen and a light jacket, because it can be windy at sea.\n• Stay seated while the boat is moving. Never throw anything into the sea, and do not feed the animals.\n\nCancellations\nIf a trip is cancelled because of high waves or a sea warning, you can get a full refund or choose another date. If you cancel your own booking, you will get a full refund up to 2 days before the trip, but only 50% after that.\n\nCall (03) 823-5566 or message us on LINE: @bluehorizon", "passage_zh": "藍色地平線賞鯨\n花蓮港｜航季：4 月 1 日至 10 月 31 日\n\n花蓮外海的深水區住著好幾種海豚，運氣好的日子還能看到鯨魚。快來參加我們兩小時的航程，在大自然中與牠們相遇！\n\n出航時間\n上午 7:00（僅限週六、週日）｜上午 9:30｜下午 1:00｜下午 3:30\n早上的海面通常比較平靜，所以第一次參加的人很適合選擇早上的航班。\n\n票價（新臺幣）\n成人：900｜學生（須出示學生證）：800｜兒童（3–12 歲）：500\n基於安全考量，未滿 3 歲的兒童不得上船。\n至少提前 3 天上網預約，可享九折優惠。\n\n我們的承諾\n如果航程中沒有看到海豚或鯨魚，一年內可免費再參加一次。預約時只要出示船票即可。\n\n出發前須知\n• 請在出航前 30 分鐘到花蓮港賞鯨碼頭的本公司櫃檯（3 號櫃檯）報到。\n• 容易暈船的人，請在上船前 30 到 60 分鐘服用暈船藥，並且只吃清淡的餐點。\n• 請攜帶帽子、防曬乳和輕便外套，因為海上風可能很大。\n• 船隻行駛時請坐在座位上。絕對不要往海裡丟任何東西，也不要餵食動物。\n\n取消規定\n如果因為浪太大或發布海上警報而取消航程，可以全額退費或改選其他日期。如果是您自行取消預約，出航前 2 天以前取消可全額退費，之後取消則只退 50%。\n\n請撥打 (03) 823-5566，或透過 LINE 傳訊息給我們：@bluehorizon", "questions": [{"q": "Mr. and Mrs. Chang book a trip online one week ahead for themselves, their 15-year-old son (a student with an ID card) and their 8-year-old daughter. How much do they pay?", "options": ["NT$2,790", "NT$3,100", "NT$2,520", "NT$2,880"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Prices 段：成人 900 × 2 = 1,800，學生 800，兒童 500，合計 3,100 元；提前一週上網預約符合 at least 3 days ahead，可打九折，3,100 × 0.9 = 2,790 元。(B) 沒有打折；(C) 把兒子算成兒童票（2,800 × 0.9）；(D) 把兒子算成成人票（3,200 × 0.9），故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Alice wants to go whale watching on a Wednesday. What is the earliest trip she can take?", "options": ["The 7:00 a.m. trip", "The 1:00 p.m. trip", "The 9:30 a.m. trip", "The 3:30 p.m. trip"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Trip Times 段：7:00 a.m. (Saturdays and Sundays only)，早上 7 點的航班只在週六、週日出航，所以週三最早的是上午 9:30 的航班。(A) 平日沒有開；(B)(D) 都比 9:30 晚，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about a family with a two-year-old baby?", "options": ["The baby can join the trip for free.", "The whole family cannot go on the boat together.", "The baby has to pay the child price of NT$500.", "The family must take the 7:00 a.m. trip."], "answer": 1, "explain": "Prices 段：For safety reasons, children under 3 are not allowed on board（未滿 3 歲的兒童不得上船），兩歲的寶寶不能上船，所以全家無法一起搭船出海。(A)(C) 寶寶根本不能上船，不是免費或付費的問題；(D) 文中沒有這樣的規定，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Kai went on a trip in July, but no dolphins or whales appeared. What can he do?", "options": ["Ask for a 50% refund at Counter 3.", "Get 10% off his next online booking.", "Change his ticket for a trip to another port.", "Book another trip for free within one year by showing his ticket."], "answer": 3, "explain": "Our Promise 段：If no dolphins or whales are seen on your trip, you may join another trip for free within one year. Just show your ticket when you book.（沒看到海豚或鯨魚，一年內出示船票可免費再參加一次）。(A) 50% 退費是自行在出航前 2 天內取消的規定；(B) 九折是提前 3 天上網預約的優惠；(C) 文中沒有提到其他港口，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What advice does the text give to people who get seasick easily?", "options": ["Take the afternoon trips because the sea is calmer then.", "Take medicine before boarding and eat only a light meal.", "Stand at the front of the boat to enjoy the fresh air.", "Eat a big meal at the port before the trip."], "answer": 1, "explain": "Before You Go 段第二點：take seasickness medicine 30 to 60 minutes before getting on the boat, and eat only a light meal（上船前 30 到 60 分鐘吃暈船藥，只吃清淡的餐點）。(A) 文中說早上的海面通常比較平靜，不是下午；(C) 船行駛時應坐在座位上；(D) 與「吃清淡」相反，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a201", "title": "Sunday Dumplings at Grandma's", "title_zh": "奶奶家的星期天水餃", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Life", "Food"], "passage": "Every Sunday morning, I go to my grandma's home with my parents and my little brother. Grandma lives in an old apartment near a busy market in Taichung. When we arrive, she is always in the kitchen. On the table, there are bowls of meat and cabbage and a big pile of dumpling skins.\n\nGrandma can make one dumpling in about ten seconds. Her dumplings are small and pretty, and they all look the same. My first dumplings looked terrible. Some were too fat, and some broke open in the hot water. Grandma just laughed and said, \"Use less meat, and press the edges together hard.\"\n\nI practiced every week. Now my dumplings are better, but they are still not as pretty as Grandma's. My little brother does not fold dumplings. His job is to count them. Last Sunday, we made 120!\n\nOur favorite time is Lunar New Year. Before the New Year dinner, Grandma puts a clean coin inside one dumpling. People say the person who finds it will have good luck all year. Last year, my father found the coin. This year, I hope it will be me. But the coin is not the best part. The best part is the time in the kitchen with Grandma, talking and laughing together.", "passage_zh": "每個星期天早上，我都會和爸媽還有弟弟一起去奶奶家。奶奶住在台中一個熱鬧市場附近的老公寓裡。我們到的時候，她總是在廚房裡。桌上有一碗碗的肉和高麗菜，還有一大疊水餃皮。\n\n奶奶大約十秒鐘就能包好一顆水餃。她包的水餃又小又漂亮，而且每一顆看起來都一模一樣。我第一次包的水餃看起來很糟糕：有些太胖，有些在滾水裡破開了。奶奶只是笑著說：「肉少放一點，再把邊緣用力捏緊。」\n\n我每個星期都練習。現在我包的水餃進步了，但還是沒有奶奶包的漂亮。我弟弟不包水餃，他的工作是數水餃。上個星期天，我們包了 120 顆！\n\n我們最喜歡的時候是農曆新年。吃年夜飯之前，奶奶會在其中一顆水餃裡放一枚乾淨的硬幣。大家都說，找到硬幣的人一整年都會有好運。去年找到硬幣的是我爸爸，今年我希望會是我。不過，硬幣並不是最棒的部分；最棒的是和奶奶一起待在廚房裡，邊聊天邊歡笑的時光。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to boil dumplings without breaking them", "A busy market near Grandma's apartment", "The writer's Sundays of making dumplings with Grandma", "Why people put coins in food at Lunar New Year"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文描述作者每個星期天到奶奶家包水餃的經過：從一開始包得很難看、每週練習，到過年時奶奶在水餃裡放硬幣，最後說最棒的是和奶奶一起在廚房的時光。(A) 水餃煮破只是第二段的一個小細節；(B) 市場只在第一段順帶提到；(D) 文中只說奶奶會在一顆水餃裡放硬幣，並沒有說明這個習俗的由來。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What did Grandma tell the writer to do?", "options": ["Make the dumplings bigger", "Use less meat and press the edges together hard", "Put more cabbage in the dumplings", "Cook the dumplings in cold water"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：Grandma just laughed and said, \"Use less meat, and press the edges together hard.\" 奶奶要作者肉少放一點，並把邊緣用力捏緊。(A) 作者的水餃有些已經太胖了，奶奶並沒有要作者包大一點；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer's little brother do on Sundays?", "options": ["He folds the dumplings.", "He buys meat at the market.", "He washes the bowls.", "He counts the dumplings."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：My little brother does not fold dumplings. His job is to count them. 弟弟不包水餃，他的工作是數水餃。(A) 與原文相反；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"pile\" in the first paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a lot of things put on top of one another", "a kind of soup", "a small plastic box", "a sharp kitchen knife"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段 a big pile of dumpling skins 指桌上有「一大疊」水餃皮，pile 是許多東西疊放在一起的一堆。(B)(C)(D) 都不能用來形容一大堆水餃皮，也不符合文意。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is TRUE according to the passage?", "options": ["Grandma lives in an apartment in Taipei.", "The writer found the coin last year.", "The writer's dumplings are now prettier than Grandma's.", "Grandma puts a coin in one dumpling before the New Year dinner."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：Before the New Year dinner, Grandma puts a clean coin inside one dumpling. 吃年夜飯前，奶奶會在一顆水餃裡放硬幣。(A) 奶奶住在台中，不是台北；(B) 去年找到硬幣的是作者的爸爸；(C) 第三段說作者的水餃 still not as pretty as Grandma's，仍然沒有奶奶包的漂亮。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a202", "title": "The Slowest Runner on Our Relay Team", "title_zh": "接力隊裡跑最慢的人", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Education", "Sports"], "passage": "Every November, our school has a sports day. The most exciting event is the class relay race. Each class chooses ten runners, and each runner runs 100 meters. This year, our homeroom teacher, Ms. Lin, chose me. I was very surprised, because I am the slowest runner in my class.\n\n\"Why me?\" I asked. Ms. Lin smiled and said, \"You are not fast, but you never give up. And you are very good at passing the baton.\" In a relay race, the baton is a short stick. Each runner must pass it to the next runner without dropping it.\n\nFor three weeks, our team practiced after school. We did not just run. We practiced passing the baton again and again. Many teams think speed is everything, but a dropped baton can lose a race in one second.\n\nOn sports day, two of the fastest classes dropped their batons. Our team did not. We were not the fastest team, but we finished second! Everyone in my class cheered and jumped. That day, I learned something important. A team needs more than fast legs. It needs people who work together and do their own jobs well.", "passage_zh": "每年十一月，我們學校都會舉辦運動會。最刺激的項目是班際大隊接力。每班選出十位跑者，每位跑者跑 100 公尺。今年，我們的導師林老師選了我。我非常驚訝，因為我是班上跑最慢的人。\n\n「為什麼是我？」我問。林老師笑著說：「你跑得不快，但你從不放棄，而且你很會傳接力棒。」在接力賽中，接力棒是一根短短的棒子，每位跑者都必須把它交給下一位跑者，而且不能讓它掉下去。\n\n有三個星期的時間，我們隊放學後都留下來練習。我們不只練跑步，還一次又一次地練習傳接力棒。很多隊伍以為速度就是一切，但只要掉一次棒，一秒鐘就可能輸掉整場比賽。\n\n運動會那天，跑最快的班級中有兩班掉了棒，我們隊沒有。我們不是最快的隊伍，卻拿到了第二名！全班同學都又叫又跳地歡呼。那天我學到一件重要的事：一個團隊需要的不只是跑得快的腿，還需要能夠合作、並把自己的工作做好的人。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the writer became the fastest runner in the school", "How a class did well in a relay race by practicing and working together", "Why the school has a sports day every November", "Why Ms. Lin is the most popular teacher in the school"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文描述作者雖然跑最慢卻被選進接力隊，全隊反覆練習傳棒，最後因為沒有掉棒而拿到第二名，並說明團隊合作的重要。(A) 作者仍是班上跑最慢的人；(C) 運動會在十一月舉行只是背景，文中沒有說明原因；(D) 文中沒有提到林老師是否最受歡迎。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why was the writer surprised when Ms. Lin chose the writer?", "options": ["The writer was the slowest runner in the class.", "The writer did not want to join sports day.", "The writer was hurt and could not run.", "The writer was new at the school."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段：I was very surprised, because I am the slowest runner in my class. 作者很驚訝，因為自己是班上跑最慢的人。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In the passage, a \"baton\" is ______.", "options": ["a kind of running shoe", "the line at the end of a race", "a short stick that runners pass to each other", "a prize for the winning team"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段直接說明：In a relay race, the baton is a short stick. Each runner must pass it to the next runner... 接力棒是一根短棒，跑者要把它交給下一位跑者。(A) 跑鞋、(B) 終點線、(D) 獎品都不是文中的意思。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Besides running, what did the team practice again and again?", "options": ["Starting the race quickly", "Running more than 100 meters", "Cheering for the other runners", "Passing the baton"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：We did not just run. We practiced passing the baton again and again. 他們除了跑步，還一再練習傳接力棒。(A)(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How did the writer's class do in the relay race?", "options": ["They finished second.", "They won first place.", "They dropped the baton twice.", "They finished last."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：We were not the fastest team, but we finished second! 他們拿到第二名。(B) 他們不是第一名；(C) 掉棒的是另外兩個跑得最快的班級，作者的隊伍沒有掉棒；(D) 與原文不符。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a203", "title": "What Is Inside a Camel's Hump?", "title_zh": "駱駝的駝峰裡裝了什麼？", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Nature", "Science"], "passage": "Many people believe that camels keep water in their humps. This idea sounds right, because camels live in hot, dry places and can go for many days without drinking. But it is not true. A camel's hump is full of fat, not water.\n\nFood is hard to find in the desert. When a camel eats well, it keeps extra fat in its hump. Later, when there is little food, its body uses this fat for energy. If a camel does not eat for a long time, its hump gets small and soft. Sometimes the hump even falls over to one side. When the camel eats well again, the hump becomes big and round again.\n\nSo how can camels live with so little water? Their bodies lose very little water. Also, when they find water, they drink a lot very quickly. A very thirsty camel can drink about 100 liters at one time. That is as much as fifty big bottles of water!\n\nThere are two main kinds of camels. The Arabian camel has one hump, and the Bactrian camel has two. Both kinds have two rows of long eyelashes. These keep sand out of their eyes in sandstorms. Camels can also close their nostrils, so sand cannot get into their noses.", "passage_zh": "很多人以為駱駝把水存在駝峰裡。這個說法聽起來很合理，因為駱駝住在又熱又乾的地方，而且可以好多天不喝水。但這不是真的。駱駝的駝峰裡裝滿的是脂肪，不是水。\n\n在沙漠裡很難找到食物。駱駝吃得好的時候，會把多餘的脂肪存在駝峰裡。之後食物很少時，牠的身體就會使用這些脂肪來產生能量。如果駱駝很久沒有進食，牠的駝峰就會變得又小又軟，有時甚至會倒向一邊。等駱駝再次吃得好，駝峰又會變回又大又圓的樣子。\n\n那麼，駱駝只靠這麼少的水要怎麼生存呢？牠們的身體流失的水分非常少。此外，一找到水，牠們就會很快地喝下大量的水。一隻非常口渴的駱駝一次可以喝下大約 100 公升的水，相當於五十大瓶水！\n\n駱駝主要有兩種：單峰駱駝（阿拉伯駱駝）有一個駝峰，雙峰駱駝則有兩個。兩種駱駝都有兩排長長的睫毛，在沙塵暴中可以擋住沙子，不讓沙子跑進眼睛。駱駝也能把鼻孔閉起來，讓沙子無法進入鼻子。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Camels keep fat in their humps, and their bodies are made for life in the desert.", "Camels need to drink water every day to stay alive.", "Arabian camels are stronger than Bactrian camels.", "Sandstorms in the desert are very dangerous for people."], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文先破除「駝峰裡裝水」的錯誤說法，說明駝峰存的是脂肪，再介紹駱駝如何在缺水的環境生存，以及睫毛、鼻孔如何擋沙，重點是駱駝的身體很適合沙漠生活。(B) 與第一段 can go for many days without drinking 相反；(C) 文中沒有比較兩種駱駝誰比較強壯；(D) 沙塵暴只在最後一段提到，且沒有談到對人的危險。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what is inside a camel's hump?", "options": ["Water", "Sand", "Fat", "Food that the camel has not finished eating"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：A camel's hump is full of fat, not water. 駝峰裡裝的是脂肪，不是水。(A) 正是文中要糾正的錯誤觀念；(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What happens to a camel's hump when the camel does not eat for a long time?", "options": ["It becomes bigger and harder.", "It becomes small and soft.", "It fills up with water.", "It turns a different color."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：If a camel does not eat for a long time, its hump gets small and soft. 駱駝很久沒吃東西時，駝峰會變得又小又軟。(A) 與原文相反；(C) 駝峰存的是脂肪而不是水；(D) 文中未提及顏色改變。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"extra\" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["less than usual", "very dirty", "very old", "more than it needs now"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段 When a camel eats well, it keeps extra fat in its hump. Later, when there is little food, its body uses this fat... 駱駝吃得好時，把「用不完、多出來的」脂肪存起來，等沒有食物時再用，所以 extra 是「多餘的、超出目前所需的」。(A) 意思相反；(B)(C) 與文意無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How do long eyelashes help camels?", "options": ["They keep camels cool on hot days.", "They help camels find water.", "They keep sand out of the camels' eyes.", "They help camels see at night."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：Both kinds have two rows of long eyelashes. These keep sand out of their eyes in sandstorms. 兩排長睫毛能在沙塵暴中擋住沙子，不讓沙子進入眼睛。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a204", "title": "A Dog Named Mango", "title_zh": "一隻叫芒果的狗", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Life", "Nature"], "passage": "Last year, I really wanted a dog. I asked my parents to buy a cute puppy from a pet shop. But my dad said, \"Let's visit the animal shelter first. Many dogs there need a home.\" So one Saturday, we went to the shelter near our town.\n\nThere were many dogs at the shelter. Some barked loudly, and some jumped up and down. But one yellow dog sat quietly in the corner. He was three years old, and he looked very sad. A worker told us that his first owner did not want him anymore. We decided to adopt him and take him home. Because of his yellow fur, we named him Mango.\n\nAt first, Mango was very shy. He hid under the table and did not eat much for two days. My mom said, \"Give him time.\" So every evening, I sat near him and read my storybooks out loud. After two weeks, he came out and put his head on my leg. After that, he followed me everywhere!\n\nNow I walk Mango every morning and evening. He is not a puppy, but he is the best dog in the world. I am glad we went to the shelter. Older dogs need a family, too.", "passage_zh": "去年，我非常想養一隻狗。我請爸媽從寵物店買一隻可愛的小狗給我。但爸爸說：「我們先去動物收容所看看吧。那裡有很多狗需要一個家。」於是在某個星期六，我們去了離我們鎮上不遠的收容所。\n\n收容所裡有很多狗。有些大聲吠叫，有些跳上跳下。但有一隻黃色的狗安靜地坐在角落。牠三歲了，看起來非常難過。一位工作人員告訴我們，牠的第一個主人不想要牠了。我們決定領養牠，帶牠回家。因為牠有一身黃色的毛，我們就幫牠取名叫芒果。\n\n一開始，芒果非常害羞。牠躲在桌子底下，兩天都沒吃什麼東西。媽媽說：「給牠一點時間。」所以每天傍晚，我都坐在牠旁邊，大聲念我的故事書。兩個星期後，牠終於走出來，把頭靠在我的腿上。從那之後，我走到哪裡，牠就跟到哪裡！\n\n現在，我每天早上和傍晚都會帶芒果去散步。牠已經不是小狗了，但牠是全世界最棒的狗。我很高興我們當初去了收容所。年紀比較大的狗也需要一個家。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to choose a puppy at a pet shop", "Why yellow dogs are better than other dogs", "How to train a dog to walk with you", "How the writer's family adopted a shy dog that became part of the family"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文描述作者一家沒有去寵物店買小狗，而是到收容所領養了一隻害羞的三歲黃狗芒果，經過耐心陪伴，牠成了家中的一份子。(A) 他們最後沒有去寵物店；(B) 文中沒有比較不同顏色的狗；(C) 文中沒有介紹訓練狗的方法。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did the family name the dog Mango?", "options": ["Because of the color of his fur", "Because he liked to eat mangoes", "Because his first owner gave him the name", "Because they found him under a mango tree"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Because of his yellow fur, we named him Mango. 因為牠的毛是黃色的，所以取名叫芒果。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What did Mango do when he first came home?", "options": ["He barked at everyone loudly.", "He ran out of the house.", "He hid under the table and did not eat much.", "He jumped up and down on the sofa."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：At first, Mango was very shy. He hid under the table and did not eat much for two days. 芒果剛到家時很害羞，躲在桌子底下，兩天都沒吃什麼。(A)(D) 大聲吠叫、跳上跳下的是收容所裡的其他狗；(B) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"adopt\" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["sell an animal to another person", "take an animal or a child into your home and care for it as your own", "take a sick animal to see a doctor", "give an animal a bath"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段 We decided to adopt him and take him home. 他們決定把芒果「領養」回家，adopt 就是把動物或孩子帶回家，當成自己的家人照顧。(A) 賣掉、(C) 看醫生、(D) 洗澡都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What did the writer do to help Mango feel safe?", "options": ["The writer bought him new toys every day.", "The writer sat near him and read storybooks out loud every evening.", "The writer took him back to the shelter for a visit.", "The writer gave him more food than usual."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：So every evening, I sat near him and read my storybooks out loud. 作者每天傍晚坐在芒果旁邊大聲念故事書，兩週後芒果才走出來。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a205", "title": "Good Morning, World: Breakfast in Five Countries", "title_zh": "早安，世界：五個國家的早餐", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Food", "Culture"], "passage": "In Taiwan, many students buy breakfast on their way to school. Breakfast shops open very early, and they sell many kinds of food, such as egg pancakes, rice balls, sandwiches, and soy milk. But what do people in other countries eat in the morning?\n\nIn Japan, a traditional breakfast is a healthy meal of rice, miso soup, and grilled fish. Today, many Japanese people eat bread in the morning, too. In the UK, people sometimes eat a \"full English breakfast.\" It has eggs, bacon, sausages, beans, tomatoes, and toast. This big meal is quite heavy, so many people eat it only on weekends or holidays. On weekdays, they usually have cereal or toast.\n\nIn France, breakfast is usually light and sweet. People often have bread with butter and jam, or a croissant, with coffee or hot chocolate. In Mexico, one popular breakfast is chilaquiles. These are pieces of fried tortilla in a red or green sauce, often with cheese or an egg on top.\n\nBreakfast is different from country to country, but one thing is the same everywhere. A good breakfast gives your body energy for the day. So don't skip it, even when you are in a hurry!", "passage_zh": "在台灣，很多學生會在上學途中買早餐。早餐店很早就開門，賣各式各樣的食物，例如蛋餅、飯糰、三明治和豆漿。但是，其他國家的人早上都吃些什麼呢？\n\n在日本，傳統的早餐是一頓健康的餐點，有白飯、味噌湯和烤魚。現在，很多日本人早上也會吃麵包。在英國，人們有時會吃「全套英式早餐」，裡面有蛋、培根、香腸、豆子、番茄和吐司。這頓大餐吃起來相當有份量，所以很多人只在週末或假日才吃。平日他們通常吃穀片或吐司。\n\n在法國，早餐通常清淡又偏甜。人們常吃塗了奶油和果醬的麵包，或是一個可頌，再配上咖啡或熱可可。在墨西哥，一種很受歡迎的早餐是 chilaquiles（辣醬玉米片）：一小塊一小塊炸過的墨西哥玉米薄餅，淋上紅色或綠色的醬汁，上面常常還會放起司或一顆蛋。\n\n每個國家的早餐都不一樣，但有一件事是到處都相同的：一份好的早餐能為你的身體提供一整天的能量。所以，即使在趕時間，也不要不吃早餐！", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to make a healthy breakfast at home", "What people in different countries eat for breakfast", "Why breakfast shops in Taiwan open early", "The history of the full English breakfast"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文依序介紹台灣、日本、英國、法國和墨西哥的早餐，最後提醒大家不要不吃早餐，主旨是各國人民早餐吃什麼。(A) 文中沒有教人在家做早餐；(C) 台灣早餐店只是開頭的例子；(D) 文中沒有介紹英式早餐的歷史。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which food is part of a traditional Japanese breakfast?", "options": ["Chilaquiles", "Sausages and beans", "A croissant", "Miso soup"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：In Japan, a traditional breakfast is a healthy meal of rice, miso soup, and grilled fish. 日本傳統早餐有白飯、味噌湯和烤魚。(A) 是墨西哥早餐；(B) 是英式早餐的內容；(C) 是法國早餐。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why do many people in the UK eat a full English breakfast only on weekends or holidays?", "options": ["It is a big and heavy meal.", "It is too expensive.", "Shops sell it only on weekends.", "Children do not like it."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：This big meal is quite heavy, so many people eat it only on weekends or holidays. 因為這一餐份量大、吃起來很有負擔，所以很多人只在週末或假日吃。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What are chilaquiles?", "options": ["Sweet bread that people eat with hot chocolate", "A kind of soup made with fish", "Pieces of fried tortilla in a red or green sauce", "Rice balls with an egg inside"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：These are pieces of fried tortilla in a red or green sauce, often with cheese or an egg on top. chilaquiles 是一小塊一小塊炸過的墨西哥玉米薄餅，淋上紅色或綠色的醬汁。(A) 較接近法國早餐；(B) 魚出現在日本早餐，但不是湯；(D) 飯糰是台灣早餐。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"skip\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["cook quickly", "share with others", "eat slowly", "not have; miss"], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段 A good breakfast gives your body energy for the day. So don't skip it, even when you are in a hurry! 早餐能提供一天的能量，所以即使趕時間也不要「跳過、不吃」它，skip 在此指略過不吃。(A)(B)(C) 都不符合「趕時間也別……」的文意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a206", "title": "Cherry Tomatoes on My Balcony", "title_zh": "我家陽台的小番茄", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Life", "Nature"], "passage": "My family lives in an apartment in New Taipei City. We do not have a garden, but we have a small balcony. Last spring, I bought a little cherry tomato plant at a flower market. I put it in a big pot on the balcony and watered it every day.\n\nAfter two weeks, some of the leaves turned yellow. I was worried, so I asked our neighbor, Mrs. Chen. She grows a lot of plants. \"You're giving it too much water,\" she said. \"Only water it when the soil on top is dry. And tomatoes need a lot of sun, at least six hours a day.\" So I moved the pot to the sunny side of the balcony and watered it less.\n\nSoon the plant grew taller and stronger. Small yellow flowers came out, and then little green tomatoes. One by one, they turned red. But one morning, I saw a bird eating my tomatoes! I put a net over the plant to keep the birds away.\n\nIn the end, I picked about forty cherry tomatoes. They were sweeter than the tomatoes from the supermarket. I gave a bowl of them to Mrs. Chen to say thank you. This year, I want to grow basil, too.", "passage_zh": "我家住在新北市的一間公寓。我們沒有花園，但有一個小陽台。去年春天，我在花市買了一小株小番茄苗。我把它種在一個大盆子裡，放在陽台上，每天都幫它澆水。\n\n兩個星期後，有些葉子變黃了。我很擔心，所以去問了鄰居陳太太。她種了很多植物。「你水澆太多了，」她說，「只有在上層的土乾了才澆水。還有，番茄需要很多陽光，一天至少要六個小時。」於是我把盆子搬到陽台有陽光的那一邊，也少澆一點水。\n\n很快地，這株植物長得更高、更壯了。先是開出黃色的小花，接著長出綠色的小番茄，然後它們一顆一顆變紅了。可是有一天早上，我看見一隻鳥在吃我的番茄！我就在植物上蓋了一張網，不讓鳥靠近。\n\n最後，我總共摘了大約四十顆小番茄。它們比超市賣的番茄還甜。我送了一碗給陳太太，向她道謝。今年，我還想種九層塔。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why birds like to eat red fruit", "How to sell vegetables at a flower market", "How the writer learned to grow cherry tomatoes on a balcony", "Why tomatoes from the supermarket are not sweet"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文描述作者在公寓陽台種小番茄的經過：一開始水澆太多、葉子變黃，聽了鄰居建議後改善，最後收成約四十顆。(A) 鳥吃番茄只是第三段的一個小插曲；(B) 作者是在花市買植物，不是賣菜；(D) 文中只說自己種的比超市的甜，並沒有說明超市番茄為什麼不甜。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did some of the leaves turn yellow?", "options": ["The writer gave the plant too much water.", "A bird ate the leaves.", "The pot was too big.", "The writer forgot to water the plant."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：陳太太說 \"You're giving it too much water.\" 葉子變黃是因為水澆太多。(B) 鳥吃的是番茄，而且是後來才發生的事；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 與原文相反，作者每天都澆水。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What did the writer do because of Mrs. Chen's advice?", "options": ["The writer bought a new plant.", "The writer put a net over the plant.", "The writer gave the plant more water.", "The writer moved the pot to the sunny side and watered it less."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段末：So I moved the pot to the sunny side of the balcony and watered it less. 作者聽了陳太太的建議，把盆子搬到有陽光的一邊，並減少澆水。(A) 文中未提及；(B) 蓋網子是後來為了防鳥，不是因為陳太太的建議；(C) 與陳太太的建議相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"soil\" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the top part of a flower", "the earth that plants grow in", "a kind of plant food from a store", "the water at the bottom of a pot"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段 Only water it when the soil on top is dry. 等盆子上層的 soil 乾了再澆水，soil 指植物生長的「泥土」。(A) 花朵、(C) 肥料、(D) 盆底的水都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is TRUE according to the passage?", "options": ["The writer gave some tomatoes to Mrs. Chen.", "The tomato plant had white flowers.", "The writer picked about fourteen tomatoes.", "The writer's family has a big garden."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：I gave a bowl of them to Mrs. Chen to say thank you. 作者送了一碗小番茄給陳太太道謝。(B) 第三段說開的是黃色小花；(C) 作者摘了大約四十（forty）顆，不是十四顆；(D) 第一段說他們家沒有花園，只有小陽台。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a207", "title": "Snow, Ice, and Hot Ramen: Our Trip to Sapporo", "title_zh": "雪、冰和熱拉麵：我們的札幌之旅", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Travel", "Culture"], "passage": "Last February, my family went to Sapporo, a city in the north of Japan. The flight from Taiwan took about four hours. I live in Kaohsiung, and it never snows there. When we left the airport, everything was white. I took off my glove and touched the snow. It was soft, light, and very cold!\n\nWe went to Sapporo in February because of the Sapporo Snow Festival. It happens every year and lasts for about a week. In a long park in the city center, there were huge statues made of snow and ice. Some looked like famous buildings, and others looked like cartoon characters. A few were taller than a house! At night, colorful lights made them even more beautiful.\n\nOur tour guide told us an interesting story. The festival started in 1950. In that first year, some local middle school and high school students made six snow statues in the park. Now about two million people visit the festival every year.\n\nSapporo was very cold. It was often below zero, and I fell on the icy road twice. But I always felt warm after a bowl of hot miso ramen. Miso ramen is a famous food in Sapporo. I hope we can go back next winter.", "passage_zh": "去年二月，我們全家去了札幌，那是日本北部的一座城市。從台灣搭飛機大約要四個小時。我住在高雄，那裡從來不下雪。我們走出機場時，到處都是白茫茫的一片。我脫下手套摸了摸雪，雪又軟又輕，而且非常冰！\n\n我們選在二月去札幌，是為了札幌雪祭。雪祭每年舉辦，為期大約一個星期。在市中心一座長長的公園裡，有許多用雪和冰做成的巨大雕像。有些看起來像有名的建築，有些則像卡通人物，還有幾座比房子還高！到了晚上，彩色的燈光讓它們變得更漂亮。\n\n我們的導遊說了一個有趣的故事。雪祭開始於 1950 年。在第一年，一些當地的國中生和高中生在公園裡做了六座雪雕。現在每年大約有兩百萬人來參觀雪祭。\n\n札幌非常冷，氣溫常常在零度以下，我還在結冰的路上跌倒了兩次。不過，每次吃完一碗熱騰騰的味噌拉麵，我總是覺得全身暖和起來。味噌拉麵是札幌有名的食物。我希望明年冬天我們還能再去。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The writer's family trip to see snow and the Snow Festival in Sapporo", "How to make a snow statue", "Why it never snows in Kaohsiung", "How to cook miso ramen at home"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文描述作者一家二月到札幌旅行：第一次摸到雪、參觀札幌雪祭、聽導遊介紹雪祭的由來，以及在寒冷中吃熱拉麵。(B) 文中沒有教人做雪雕；(C) 只提到高雄不下雪，沒有說明原因；(D) 只說味噌拉麵是札幌名產，沒有教做法。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why was snow new to the writer?", "options": ["The writer's family did not travel much.", "It never snows in Kaohsiung, where the writer lives.", "The writer was too young to remember snow.", "It did not snow in Sapporo that year."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：I live in Kaohsiung, and it never snows there. 作者住在從不下雪的高雄，所以第一次摸到雪。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 與原文相反，札幌到處都是白雪。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Who made the first snow statues in 1950?", "options": ["Famous artists from other countries", "Tour guides in Sapporo", "Workers from the city government", "Some local middle school and high school students"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：In that first year, some local middle school and high school students made six snow statues in the park. 1950 年第一屆雪祭的六座雪雕是當地的國中生和高中生做的。(A)(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"huge\" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very cold", "very old", "very big", "very cheap"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段 there were huge statues made of snow and ice... A few were taller than a house! 後文說有些雕像比房子還高，可知 huge 是「巨大的」。(A)(B)(D) 都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is TRUE about the trip?", "options": ["The flight from Taiwan took about eight hours.", "The writer fell on the icy road two times.", "The family visited Sapporo in December.", "The writer did not like the ramen in Sapporo."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：I fell on the icy road twice. 作者在結冰的路上跌倒了兩次。(A) 第一段說飛行時間大約四個小時；(C) 他們是二月去的；(D) 作者說吃了熱拉麵總是覺得很溫暖，並沒有不喜歡。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a208", "title": "Seven Stops to Taipei 101", "title_zh": "到台北 101 的七個站", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Life", "Travel"], "passage": "Last Saturday, I was at Taipei Main Station on my way to meet my friends. Near the MRT ticket machines, I saw a woman with a big backpack. She was looking at a map, and she looked worried. I wanted to help her, but I was nervous. My English was not very good, and I was afraid of making mistakes.\n\nThen I remembered my English teacher's words: \"Mistakes are OK. Just try.\" So I walked over and said, \"Excuse me. Can I help you?\" The woman smiled. Her name was Kate, and she was from Australia. She wanted to go to Taipei 101.\n\nI showed her the MRT map. \"Take the red line toward Xiangshan,\" I said slowly. \"Get off at Taipei 101/World Trade Center Station. It is seven stops from here.\" I also helped her buy a ticket from the machine. The ticket was a small blue plastic coin, not a paper ticket. Kate thought it was very cute.\n\nBefore she left, Kate gave me a small koala key ring from Australia. \"Your English is great!\" she said. I felt very happy. That day, I learned that English is not just a school subject. It is a way to help people and make new friends.", "passage_zh": "上星期六，我在台北車站，正要去和朋友見面。在捷運售票機附近，我看到一位背著大背包的女士。她正在看地圖，看起來很擔心。我想幫她，但我很緊張。我的英文不太好，而且我很怕犯錯。\n\n接著，我想起英文老師說過的話：「犯錯沒關係，試試看就對了。」於是我走過去說：「不好意思，需要幫忙嗎？」那位女士笑了。她叫凱特，來自澳洲，她想去台北 101。\n\n我拿捷運路線圖給她看。「搭往象山方向的紅線，」我慢慢地說，「在台北 101／世貿站下車，從這裡過去是七站。」我還幫她用售票機買了票。那張票是一枚藍色的圓形塑膠代幣，不是紙做的車票。凱特覺得它很可愛。\n\n她離開前，送了我一個從澳洲帶來的無尾熊小鑰匙圈。「你的英文很棒！」她說。我覺得非常開心。那天我學到，英文不只是學校的一個科目，更是幫助別人、交新朋友的方法。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to buy an MRT card in Taipei", "Why Taipei 101 is famous", "A trip the writer took to Australia", "How the writer used English to help a tourist"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文描述作者在台北車站鼓起勇氣用英文幫助一位來自澳洲的遊客搭捷運去台北 101，並因此學到英文是幫助別人、交朋友的方法。(A) 買票只是其中一個細節；(B) 文中沒有說明台北 101 為何有名；(C) 作者並沒有去澳洲，鑰匙圈是凱特送的。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why was the writer nervous at first?", "options": ["The writer was late to meet friends.", "The woman looked angry.", "The writer was afraid of making mistakes in English.", "The writer did not know the way to Taipei 101."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：My English was not very good, and I was afraid of making mistakes. 作者覺得自己英文不好，怕說錯。(A) 文中沒有說作者遲到；(B) 那位女士看起來是擔心，不是生氣；(D) 作者後來清楚地告訴凱特怎麼去，可見作者知道路。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, how could Kate get to Taipei 101?", "options": ["By taking the red line toward Xiangshan and getting off after seven stops", "By taking the blue line and changing trains twice", "By taking a taxi from the station", "By walking for about seven minutes"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：\"Take the red line toward Xiangshan... Get off at Taipei 101/World Trade Center Station. It is seven stops from here.\" 搭往象山方向的紅線，坐七站到台北 101／世貿站下車。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What was Kate's MRT ticket like?", "options": ["A paper ticket with a map on it", "A small blue plastic coin", "A card with a koala on it", "A ticket on her phone"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The ticket was a small blue plastic coin, not a paper ticket. 那張票是一枚藍色的圓形塑膠代幣，不是紙票。(A) 與原文相反；(C) 無尾熊是凱特送給作者的鑰匙圈；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"nervous\" in the first paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["bored", "hungry", "a little afraid and worried", "very angry"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段 I wanted to help her, but I was nervous... I was afraid of making mistakes. 作者想幫忙卻害怕說錯，可知 nervous 是「緊張的、有點害怕不安的」。(A) 無聊的、(B) 飢餓的、(D) 生氣的都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a209", "title": "One Thousand Pieces", "title_zh": "一千片拼圖", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Life"], "passage": "During the winter vacation, my mom brought home a big box. There were one thousand puzzle pieces inside. The picture on the box showed beautiful hot-air balloons over green fields in Taitung. We put the pieces on our dining table and made one rule. Anyone can add pieces at any time, but no one can move the puzzle off the table.\n\nEveryone had a different way to work. My dad always started with the edges. He looked for pieces with one straight side and made the frame first. My little sister found pieces by color. She put all the red pieces in one pile and all the yellow pieces in another. I worked on the sky. It was the hardest part, because almost every piece was the same light blue.\n\nWe ate dinner on the sofa for three weeks! Finally, there was only one piece left, but we could not find it. We looked under the table, in the box, and even in the trash can. Then my sister saw our cat, Mochi, playing with something under the sofa. It was the last piece!\n\nWe put the last piece in together and cheered. Then my dad glued the puzzle onto a board, and now it hangs on our living room wall. Every time I look at it, I remember that happy winter vacation.", "passage_zh": "寒假的時候，媽媽帶回家一個大盒子，裡面有一千片拼圖。盒子上的圖片是台東綠色田野上空的美麗熱氣球。我們把拼圖片倒在餐桌上，並訂了一條規則：任何人隨時都可以拼上幾片，但誰都不可以把拼圖從桌上移走。\n\n每個人都有不同的拼法。爸爸總是從邊緣開始。他會找有一邊是直線的拼圖片，先把外框拼好。妹妹則是按照顏色找拼圖片，她把所有紅色的放成一堆，黃色的放成另一堆。我負責拼天空，那是最難的部分，因為幾乎每一片都是一樣的淺藍色。\n\n我們在沙發上吃了三個星期的晚餐！終於，只剩下最後一片，但我們怎麼找都找不到。我們找了桌子底下、盒子裡，甚至連垃圾桶都找過了。後來妹妹看到我們家的貓麻糬在沙發底下玩著某個東西——那正是最後一片拼圖！\n\n我們一起把最後一片放上去，大家都歡呼起來。接著爸爸把拼圖黏在一塊板子上，現在它就掛在我們家客廳的牆上。每次看到它，我都會想起那個快樂的寒假。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to choose a good puzzle for children", "How the writer's family finished a 1,000-piece puzzle together", "Why hot-air balloons are popular in Taitung", "How the family's cat learned new tricks"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文描述作者一家在寒假一起拼一千片拼圖的過程：每個人用不同的方法拼、最後一片不見了，結果發現是家裡的貓在沙發底下玩著它，完成後還把拼圖掛在客廳牆上。(A) 文中沒有教人怎麼挑拼圖；(C) 熱氣球只是拼圖上的圖案；(D) 貓只是在沙發下玩那片拼圖，文中沒有提到學把戲。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How did the writer's dad start the puzzle?", "options": ["He looked for all the red pieces.", "He worked on the sky first.", "He put the pieces in piles by color.", "He made the frame with the edge pieces first."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：My dad always started with the edges. He looked for pieces with one straight side and made the frame first. 爸爸先找有直邊的拼圖片，把外框拼好。(A)(C) 是妹妹的拼法；(B) 拼天空的是作者。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why was the sky the hardest part?", "options": ["It had too many balloons in it.", "Some of the sky pieces were missing.", "Almost every piece was the same light blue.", "The writer did not like the color blue."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：It was the hardest part, because almost every piece was the same light blue. 天空最難拼，因為幾乎每一片都是同樣的淺藍色。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(B) 只有最後一片不見，而且文中沒有說那是天空的拼圖片。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Where did the family find the last piece?", "options": ["Under the sofa, where the cat was playing with it", "In the trash can", "Inside the puzzle box", "Under the dining table"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Then my sister saw our cat, Mochi, playing with something under the sofa. It was the last piece! 最後一片在沙發底下，是貓麻糬在玩的東西。(B)(C)(D) 都是他們找過卻沒找到的地方。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the family eat dinner on the sofa for three weeks?", "options": ["The dining table was broken.", "Their cat liked to sit on the dining table.", "They wanted to watch TV while they ate.", "The puzzle was on the dining table."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段說他們把拼圖放在餐桌上，而且規定 no one can move the puzzle off the table（誰都不能把拼圖從桌上移走），所以拼圖完成前的三個星期只好在沙發上吃晚餐。(A)(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a210", "title": "My Week Without a Phone", "title_zh": "沒有手機的一星期", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Life", "Technology"], "passage": "Last month, I dropped my phone on the floor of an MRT station, and the screen broke. The man at the repair shop said, \"It will take one week to fix.\" One week without a phone! At first, I thought it would be the worst week of my life.\n\nThe first two days were hard. I kept putting my hand in my pocket, but my phone was not there. I could not check my class group chat, so I had to call my friend from our home phone to ask about homework. I also did not know the time, so I started wearing my old watch again.\n\nBut then, something changed. On the bus to school, I looked out of the window and saw a new bakery on my street. At dinner, I talked with my grandma for a long time, and she told me funny stories about my dad as a boy. At night, I finished a whole novel. I also fell asleep earlier, because I did not use my phone in bed.\n\nWhen I got my phone back, I was happy. But I also made two new rules for myself. First, no phone at dinner. Second, my phone stays outside my bedroom at night. Now I sleep better, and I feel less tired at school.", "passage_zh": "上個月，我的手機掉在捷運站的地上，螢幕摔破了。修理店的人說：「要一個星期才能修好。」一個星期沒有手機！一開始，我以為那會是我人生中最糟糕的一個星期。\n\n前兩天很難熬。我一直把手伸進口袋，但手機不在那裡。我沒辦法看班級群組的訊息，只好用家裡的電話打給朋友問功課。我也不知道現在幾點，所以我又開始戴我的舊手錶。\n\n不過，後來情況有了變化。坐公車上學時，我看著窗外，發現我家那條街上開了一間新的麵包店。吃晚餐時，我和奶奶聊了很久，她告訴我很多爸爸小時候的趣事。晚上，我讀完了一整本小說。我也比較早睡著，因為我沒有在床上用手機。\n\n拿回手機的時候，我很開心。但我也替自己訂了兩條新規則：第一，吃晚餐時不用手機；第二，晚上手機要放在房間外面。現在我睡得比較好，在學校也覺得沒那麼累了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to fix a broken phone screen", "Why the writer's grandma does not like phones", "What the writer learned during a week without a phone", "How to choose a good novel"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文描述作者手機摔壞送修的一個星期：一開始很不習慣，後來發現了新的麵包店、和奶奶聊天、讀完小說、也更早睡，拿回手機後還替自己訂了新規則。(A) 文中沒有教人修手機；(B) 文中沒有提到奶奶不喜歡手機；(D) 讀小說只是其中一個細節。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did the writer have no phone for a week?", "options": ["The screen broke, and the phone was being fixed.", "The writer's parents took it away.", "The writer lost it on the bus.", "The writer gave it to a friend."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段：I dropped my phone on the floor of an MRT station, and the screen broke... \"It will take one week to fix.\" 手機螢幕摔破，送修需要一個星期。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "How did the writer ask about homework without a phone?", "options": ["By sending an email", "By calling a friend from the home phone", "By going to a friend's house", "By asking the teacher the next day"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：I could not check my class group chat, so I had to call my friend from our home phone to ask about homework. 作者用家裡的電話打給朋友問功課。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What did the writer notice on the bus to school?", "options": ["An old watch", "A friend from class", "A new bakery on the street", "A lost phone"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：On the bus to school, I looked out of the window and saw a new bakery on my street. 作者在公車上看向窗外，發現街上開了一家新的麵包店。(A) 舊手錶是作者自己重新戴上的；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What is one of the writer's new rules?", "options": ["The phone stays outside the bedroom at night.", "The phone can only be used on the bus.", "The writer will not use the phone for one week every month.", "The writer must read a novel before using the phone."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Second, my phone stays outside my bedroom at night. 作者的第二條規則是晚上手機要放在房間外面（第一條是吃晚餐時不用手機）。(B)(C)(D) 都不是作者訂的規則。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a211", "title": "Three Kids, One Gift: Planning Father's Day by Text", "title_zh": "三個孩子，一份禮物：傳訊息討論父親節", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Life", "Money"], "passage": "Mei: Hi, you two! Father's Day is on August 8, and that's next Saturday. Any ideas for Dad's present?\n\nHao: Dad always says his old running shoes hurt his feet. Let's buy him a new pair!\n\nBobo: Good idea! But shoes are expensive. I only have NT$500.\n\nMei: I saw some nice ones at the sports shop near the MRT station. They usually cost NT$2,700, but this week they are NT$300 off.\n\nHao: So they're NT$2,400 now. If we all pay the same amount, that's NT$800 each.\n\nBobo: Sorry, I can't pay that much.\n\nMei: Don't worry. You can pay NT$400, and Hao and I will share the rest.\n\nHao: OK. But what size does Dad wear?\n\nMei: I'm not sure. I'll look inside his old shoes tonight when he's asleep. Don't tell Mom! She can never keep a secret.\n\nBobo: Haha, that's true. Can I make a card for Dad, too?\n\nMei: Of course! He'll love it. Hao, can you meet me at the shop on Thursday afternoon?\n\nHao: Sure. See you there at 4:30!", "passage_zh": "小美：嗨，你們兩個！父親節是八月八日，就是下個星期六。送爸爸的禮物，你們有什麼想法嗎？\n\n阿豪：爸爸老是說他的舊跑鞋穿起來腳會痛。我們買一雙新的給他吧！\n\n波波：好主意！可是鞋子很貴，我只有新台幣 500 元。\n\n小美：我在捷運站附近的運動用品店看到一雙不錯的。原價是新台幣 2,700 元，不過這個星期折價 300 元。\n\n阿豪：所以現在是 2,400 元。如果我們每個人出一樣多，一人就是 800 元。\n\n波波：抱歉，我出不了那麼多。\n\n小美：別擔心。你出 400 元，剩下的我和阿豪一起分攤。\n\n阿豪：好。可是爸爸穿幾號的鞋子？\n\n小美：我不確定。我今天晚上等他睡著以後，看看他舊鞋子裡面標的尺寸。不要告訴媽媽！她從來都藏不住祕密。\n\n波波：哈哈，真的。我也可以做一張卡片送爸爸嗎？\n\n小美：當然可以！他一定會很喜歡。阿豪，星期四下午你可以跟我在那家店碰面嗎？\n\n阿豪：沒問題。四點半在那裡見！", "questions": [{"q": "What are Mei, Hao and Bobo mainly talking about?", "options": ["Where to have dinner on Father's Day", "What to buy for their dad and how to pay for it", "How to keep a secret from their mom", "Which sports shop near the MRT station is the cheapest"], "answer": 1, "explain": "三人一開始討論要送爸爸什麼父親節禮物，決定買跑鞋後，又討論每個人要出多少錢、怎麼知道爸爸的鞋子尺寸。(A) 對話沒有提到吃晚餐；(C) 不要告訴媽媽只是對話中的一小部分，不是主旨；(D) 小美只提到捷運站附近的一家店，並沒有比較哪家店最便宜。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why does Hao want to buy running shoes for Dad?", "options": ["Dad has just started running every morning.", "Hao saw a nice pair on the Internet.", "Running shoes are cheaper than other presents.", "Dad's old running shoes hurt his feet."], "answer": 3, "explain": "阿豪說：Dad always says his old running shoes hurt his feet. 爸爸老是說舊跑鞋讓他的腳很痛，所以想買一雙新的給他。(A) 對話沒有說爸爸剛開始每天跑步；(B) 看到鞋子的是小美，而且是在運動用品店，不是網路上；(C) 波波反而說鞋子很貴。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Bobo pays NT$400. If Hao and Mei pay the same amount for the rest, how much will Hao pay?", "options": ["NT$400", "NT$800", "NT$1,000", "NT$1,200"], "answer": 2, "explain": "鞋子原價 2,700 元，折價 300 元後是 2,400 元。波波出 400 元，剩下 2,400 − 400 = 2,000 元由小美和阿豪各出一半，2,000 ÷ 2 = 1,000 元。(A) 是波波要出的錢；(B) 是三人平分時每人要出的錢；(D) 是把 2,400 元直接分成兩份，忘了先扣掉波波出的 400 元。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In Mei's message, \"the rest\" means ______.", "options": ["the money that is still needed", "a short break after school", "the other presents for Dad", "the people who are not in the chat"], "answer": 0, "explain": "小美說 You can pay NT$400, and Hao and I will share the rest. 波波出 400 元，「剩下的部分」由小美和阿豪分攤，所以 the rest 指的是還需要支付的其餘金額。(B) rest 雖然也有「休息」的意思，但不符合這裡的語意；(C)(D) 與上下文無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can we learn about Mom from the messages?", "options": ["She will help pay for the shoes.", "She often tells other people's secrets.", "She knows what size shoes Dad wears.", "She will go to the shop with Mei on Thursday."], "answer": 1, "explain": "小美說 Don't tell Mom! She can never keep a secret. 媽媽從來藏不住祕密，波波也回答 that's true，可知媽媽常常會把祕密說出去。(A) 出錢買鞋的是三個孩子；(C) 對話沒有說媽媽知道爸爸的尺寸，小美打算自己看舊鞋；(D) 星期四要去店裡的是小美和阿豪。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a212", "title": "Build Your Own Bowl: A Shaved Ice Shop Menu", "title_zh": "自己配一碗：剉冰店菜單", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Food", "Money"], "passage": "SNOW MOUNTAIN ICE SHOP\nOpen 11:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m. (Closed on Wednesdays)\nEach customer must order at least one item.\n\nSTEP 1: CHOOSE YOUR ICE\nShaved ice NT$40\nMilk snowflake ice NT$60\nLarge bowl: add NT$20\n\nSTEP 2: CHOOSE YOUR TOPPINGS\nEvery bowl comes with 3 free toppings. Extra toppings are NT$10 each.\nRed beans | Green beans | Peanuts | Taro balls | Tapioca pearls | Grass jelly | Pudding | Pineapple\n\nSTEP 3: CHOOSE A SAUCE (free)\nBrown sugar syrup | Condensed milk\n\nSUMMER SPECIAL (June to August only)\nFresh Mango Ice NT$150\nMilk snowflake ice with lots of fresh mango and one scoop of mango ice cream\n\nWANT MORE?\nAdd a scoop of ice cream to any bowl for NT$25.\n\nOur taro balls are made by hand in our shop every morning. When they are sold out, we cannot make more until the next day. Come early and try them!", "passage_zh": "雪山冰店\n營業時間：上午 11:00 至晚上 10:00（星期三公休）\n每位客人至少須點一份餐點。\n\n步驟一：選擇冰品\n剉冰 40 元\n牛奶雪花冰 60 元\n大碗：加 20 元\n\n步驟二：選擇配料\n每碗附 3 種免費配料，額外配料每種 10 元。\n紅豆｜綠豆｜花生｜芋圓｜粉圓｜仙草｜布丁｜鳳梨\n\n步驟三：選擇淋醬（免費）\n黑糖漿｜煉乳\n\n夏季限定（僅限 6 月至 8 月）\n新鮮芒果冰 150 元\n牛奶雪花冰加上滿滿的新鮮芒果和一球芒果冰淇淋\n\n還想多一點嗎？\n任何一碗冰都可以加一球冰淇淋，加 25 元。\n\n我們的芋圓每天早上在店裡手工製作。賣完之後，要等到隔天才能再做。早點來嚐嚐看吧！", "questions": [{"q": "At which of these times can you NOT buy shaved ice at this shop?", "options": ["Wednesday at 3:00 p.m.", "Thursday at 9:30 p.m.", "Saturday at 11:30 a.m.", "Monday at 2:00 p.m."], "answer": 0, "explain": "菜單上寫 Open 11:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m. (Closed on Wednesdays)，營業時間是上午 11 點到晚上 10 點，星期三公休，所以星期三下午 3 點買不到剉冰。(B)(C)(D) 都在營業日的營業時間內。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Wei-lun orders a large bowl of shaved ice with five toppings. How much does he pay?", "options": ["NT$70", "NT$80", "NT$90", "NT$110"], "answer": 1, "explain": "剉冰 40 元，大碗加 20 元；每碗附 3 種免費配料，他點了 5 種，多出 2 種，每種 10 元，共 20 元。40 + 20 + 20 = 80 元。(A) 是把多出的配料只算一種；(C) 是把多出的配料算成三種；(D) 是把 5 種配料都算錢（40 + 20 + 50）。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Yu-chen wants to eat Fresh Mango Ice. When can she order it?", "options": ["Any time of the year", "Only on weekends", "Only in the summer months", "Only in the morning"], "answer": 2, "explain": "菜單寫 SUMMER SPECIAL (June to August only)，新鮮芒果冰是夏季限定，只有 6 月到 8 月才有。(A) 不是一整年都有；(B)(D) 菜單沒有限定週末或早上，而且店家上午 11 點才開門。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the shop?", "options": ["Customers must pay NT$10 for the sauce.", "Ice cream comes free with every large bowl.", "The shop opens at 9:00 in the morning.", "Every customer has to order something."], "answer": 3, "explain": "菜單第三行寫 Each customer must order at least one item. 每位客人至少要點一份，也就是每個人都必須點東西。(A) 淋醬是免費的（free）；(B) 加一球冰淇淋要 25 元，不是免費；(C) 店家上午 11 點才開門。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In \"Extra toppings are NT$10 each,\" the word \"extra\" means ______.", "options": ["free", "very small", "more than the usual number", "cold"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前一句說 Every bowl comes with 3 free toppings，每碗附 3 種免費配料；超過這 3 種、「額外多加」的配料，每種要 10 元。所以 extra 意思是「超出平常數量的、額外的」。(A) 免費的配料只有 3 種，額外的要付錢；(B) 很小的；(D) 冰的，皆不符文意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a213", "title": "Pick Your Own: A Strawberry Farm Shuttle and Price List", "title_zh": "自己動手採：草莓園接駁車與價目表", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Travel", "Schedule"], "passage": "SWEET HILL STRAWBERRY FARM\nDahu, Miaoli: Pick your own strawberries!\nSeason: December to April | Open every day, 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.\n\nFREE WEEKEND SHUTTLE BUS (Saturdays and Sundays only)\nDahu Bus Stop to the farm: 9:00, 10:30, 12:00, 1:30, 3:00\nThe farm to Dahu Bus Stop: 9:30, 11:00, 12:30, 2:00, 3:30, 4:30\nThe ride takes about 15 minutes.\n\nPRICES\nEntry ticket: NT$100 per person (children under 110 cm: free)\nEvery entry ticket comes with a basket and a pair of scissors.\nStrawberries: NT$70 for every 100 grams you pick\n\nTIPS FOR PICKERS\n- Come in the morning. Our best strawberries are often gone by noon.\n- Choose berries that are red all over. Green ones will not get sweeter after you pick them.\n- Cut the stem with your scissors. Do not pull the berry, or you may hurt the plant.\n- Wear shoes that can get dirty.\n- Please do not eat strawberries in the field before you pay for them.", "passage_zh": "甜丘草莓園\n苗栗大湖：歡迎自己動手採草莓！\n產季：12 月至隔年 4 月｜每天開放，上午 8:00 至下午 5:00\n\n週末免費接駁車（僅限星期六、星期日）\n大湖公車站開往草莓園：9:00、10:30、12:00、1:30、3:00\n草莓園開往大湖公車站：9:30、11:00、12:30、2:00、3:30、4:30\n車程約 15 分鐘。\n\n價格\n入園門票：每人 100 元（身高未滿 110 公分的兒童免費）\n每張門票附一個籃子和一把剪刀。\n草莓：每採 100 公克 70 元\n\n採果小提醒\n- 請早上來。我們最好的草莓常常不到中午就被採完了。\n- 請挑選整顆都紅透的草莓。綠色的草莓摘下來以後不會再變甜。\n- 請用剪刀剪斷草莓的梗，不要直接拉扯草莓，否則可能會傷到植株。\n- 請穿可以弄髒的鞋子。\n- 付款之前，請勿在園區裡吃草莓。", "questions": [{"q": "Wen-hao gets to Dahu Bus Stop at 11:15 on Sunday. When can he take the next shuttle bus to the farm?", "options": ["10:30", "12:00", "12:30", "1:30"], "answer": 1, "explain": "從大湖公車站開往草莓園的班次是 9:00、10:30、12:00、1:30、3:00，11:15 之後的下一班是 12:00。(A) 10:30 的車已經開走了；(C) 12:30 是從草莓園開回公車站的班次；(D) 1:30 不是最近的一班。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Mr. and Mrs. Wu visit the farm with their son, who is 125 cm tall, and their daughter, who is 95 cm tall. How much do they pay for entry tickets?", "options": ["NT$100", "NT$200", "NT$300", "NT$400"], "answer": 2, "explain": "門票每人 100 元，身高未滿 110 公分的兒童免費。吳先生、吳太太和身高 125 公分的兒子都要買票，95 公分的女兒免費，100 × 3 = 300 元。(B) 漏算了兒子；(D) 把女兒也算進去了；(A) 只算了一個人。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Ya-ling picks 500 grams of strawberries. How much does she pay for them?", "options": ["NT$70", "NT$140", "NT$280", "NT$350"], "answer": 3, "explain": "草莓每 100 公克 70 元，500 公克是 5 個 100 公克，70 × 5 = 350 元。(A) 只是 100 公克的價錢；(B)(C) 分別是 200 公克和 400 公克的價錢。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the farm tell people to come in the morning?", "options": ["The best berries are often all picked by lunchtime.", "The farm closes at noon on weekends.", "The shuttle bus does not run in the afternoon.", "It is too hot to pick strawberries after lunch."], "answer": 0, "explain": "採果小提醒寫 Come in the morning. Our best strawberries are often gone by noon. 最好的草莓常常不到中午就被採完了，也就是到午餐時間常常已經被採光。(B) 草莓園每天開到下午五點；(C) 下午仍有 1:30、3:00 等班次；(D) 文中沒有提到天氣太熱。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the farm say about green strawberries?", "options": ["They are cheaper than red ones.", "They will not become sweeter after they are picked.", "Visitors can eat them in the field for free.", "They are only good for making jam."], "answer": 1, "explain": "文中寫 Green ones will not get sweeter after you pick them. 綠色的草莓摘下來以後不會再變甜，所以要挑整顆紅透的。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 文中說付款前請不要在園區裡吃草莓。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a214", "title": "Shower First, Then Relax: Rules at a Hot Spring Pool", "title_zh": "先淋浴，再放鬆：溫泉池的規定", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Notice", "Health"], "passage": "WELCOME TO MOONLIGHT HOT SPRING POOLS\nJiaoxi, Yilan\n\nOPENING HOURS\nEvery day, 6:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m.\nClosed for cleaning from 12:00 to 1:00 p.m.\nLast entry: 9:30 p.m.\n\nTICKETS\nAdults: NT$80\nStudents (with student ID) and seniors (65 and over): NT$50\nChildren under 6: free\nTowels: NT$30 each\n\nBEFORE YOU GO IN\n- Take a shower first.\n- Wear a swimsuit. Long hair must be tied up.\n- Take off rings, necklaces and other jewelry.\n\nIN THE POOLS\n- Walk slowly. The floor can be slippery.\n- Do not jump, dive or swim.\n- No food or drinks, except bottled water.\n\nFOR YOUR HEALTH\n- Stay in the hot water for no more than 15 minutes at a time. Then get out and rest.\n- Drink some water before and after your bath.\n- Do not go in right after a big meal or after drinking alcohol.\n- If you have heart problems, please ask your doctor before you come.", "passage_zh": "歡迎光臨月光溫泉池\n宜蘭礁溪\n\n開放時間\n每天上午 6:00 至晚上 10:00\n中午 12:00 至下午 1:00 為清潔時間，暫停開放\n最後入場時間：晚上 9:30\n\n票價\n成人：80 元\n學生（持學生證）及年長者（65 歲以上）：50 元\n6 歲以下兒童：免費\n毛巾：每條 30 元\n\n入池前\n- 請先淋浴。\n- 請穿著泳衣。長髮必須綁起來。\n- 請取下戒指、項鍊及其他首飾。\n\n池內規定\n- 請慢慢走，地板可能很滑。\n- 禁止跳水、潛水或游泳。\n- 除了瓶裝水以外，禁止飲食。\n\n為了您的健康\n- 每次泡在熱水中請勿超過 15 分鐘，之後請起身休息。\n- 泡湯前後請喝點水。\n- 請勿在吃飽飯或喝酒後立刻入池。\n- 如果您有心臟方面的疾病，來之前請先詢問醫生。", "questions": [{"q": "Ms. Chang arrives at the pools at 12:20 p.m. What will happen?", "options": ["She can go in right away.", "She has to wait about 40 minutes.", "She has to come back the next day.", "She can go in for free."], "answer": 1, "explain": "告示寫 Closed for cleaning from 12:00 to 1:00 p.m.，中午 12 點到下午 1 點是清潔時間，暫停開放。張小姐 12:20 到達，要等到下午 1 點才能進去，也就是大約要等 40 分鐘。(A) 清潔時間不能入池；(C) 下午 1 點就重新開放，不必隔天再來；(D) 只有 6 歲以下兒童免費。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Mr. Kao visits the pools with his 17-year-old son and his 70-year-old mother. His son brings his student ID. How much do they pay for tickets?", "options": ["NT$130", "NT$150", "NT$180", "NT$240"], "answer": 2, "explain": "高先生是成人，票價 80 元；兒子持學生證，50 元；70 歲的母親屬於 65 歲以上的年長者，也是 50 元。80 + 50 + 50 = 180 元。(A) 漏算了一個人；(B) 是把高先生也算成 50 元；(D) 是三個人都以成人票 80 元計算。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of these is allowed in the pool area?", "options": ["Eating some snacks", "Swimming from one side to the other", "Wearing a necklace", "Drinking bottled water"], "answer": 3, "explain": "告示寫 No food or drinks, except bottled water. 除了瓶裝水以外禁止飲食，所以可以喝瓶裝水。(A) 吃零食屬於禁止的飲食；(B) 告示寫 Do not jump, dive or swim.，禁止游泳；(C) 入池前必須取下項鍊等首飾。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What should people do after 15 minutes in the hot water?", "options": ["Get out of the water and rest", "Take another shower", "Move to a hotter pool", "Eat a big meal"], "answer": 0, "explain": "告示寫 Stay in the hot water for no more than 15 minutes at a time. Then get out and rest. 每次泡在熱水中不要超過 15 分鐘，之後要起身休息。(B) 淋浴是入池前要做的事；(C) 文中沒有提到更熱的池子；(D) 告示提醒不要在吃飽飯後立刻泡湯，與題意無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "On the sign, \"seniors\" are ______.", "options": ["older people", "high school students", "young children", "workers at the pools"], "answer": 0, "explain": "票價寫 Students (with student ID) and seniors (65 and over): NT$50，括號說明 seniors 是 65 歲以上的人，所以 seniors 指「年長者」。(B) 學生已經另外列出；(C) 6 歲以下兒童另有規定；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a215", "title": "Welcome to Tainan! An Email to an Exchange Student", "title_zh": "歡迎來到台南！寫給交換學生的電子郵件", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Email", "Travel"], "passage": "From: Chen Yi-ting\nTo: Lucas Martin\nSubject: Your first weekend in Tainan\n\nHi Lucas,\n\nMy family and I are very happy that you will stay with us this year! My mom asked me to tell you about our plans for your first weekend.\n\nYour plane lands at Kaohsiung International Airport at 2:40 p.m. on Friday. My dad and I will wait for you in the arrival hall. It takes about an hour to drive from the airport to our home in Tainan.\n\nOn Saturday morning, we will visit Anping Old Fort. The Dutch started to build it in 1624, so it is about 400 years old. After that, we can try shrimp rolls, a famous snack in Anping.\n\nOn Sunday, my grandparents will come for lunch. Grandma will cook her milkfish soup. I think it's the best in Tainan! My grandparents don't speak English, but don't worry. I'll help you, and you can say \"Ni hao\" to them.\n\nPlease bring comfortable shoes and a hat. It is still very hot here in October. Also, is there any food you can't eat? Please tell me before Wednesday, so Grandma can plan the menu.\n\nSee you soon!\nYi-ting", "passage_zh": "寄件者：陳怡婷\n收件者：盧卡斯‧馬丁\n主旨：你在台南的第一個週末\n\n嗨，盧卡斯：\n\n我們全家都非常高興你今年要住在我們家！我媽媽要我告訴你，我們為你的第一個週末安排了哪些活動。\n\n你的班機星期五下午 2:40 抵達高雄國際機場。我爸爸和我會在入境大廳等你。從機場開車到我們在台南的家大約要一個小時。\n\n星期六早上，我們會去參觀安平古堡。荷蘭人在 1624 年開始建造它，所以它已經有大約 400 年的歷史了。參觀完之後，我們可以去吃蝦捲，那是安平有名的小吃。\n\n星期日，我的爺爺奶奶會來吃午餐。奶奶會煮她拿手的虱目魚湯，我覺得那是全台南最好喝的！我爺爺奶奶不會說英文，不過別擔心，我會幫你，你也可以用中文跟他們說「你好」。\n\n請帶一雙好走的鞋子和一頂帽子，這裡十月還是很熱。另外，有沒有什麼食物是你不能吃的？請在星期三之前告訴我，這樣奶奶才能安排菜色。\n\n很快就見面囉！\n怡婷", "questions": [{"q": "Why did Yi-ting write this email?", "options": ["To ask Lucas to bring a present for her grandma", "To tell Lucas how to get to Tainan by himself", "To tell Lucas about the plans for his first weekend", "To invite Lucas to a cooking class in Anping"], "answer": 2, "explain": "信的開頭寫 My mom asked me to tell you about our plans for your first weekend. 怡婷寫信是要告訴盧卡斯第一個週末的安排。(A) 信中沒有請他帶禮物；(B) 怡婷和爸爸會去機場接他，他不用自己去台南；(D) 信中沒有提到烹飪課。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Who will meet Lucas at the airport?", "options": ["Yi-ting and her father", "Yi-ting and her mother", "Yi-ting's grandparents", "A teacher from Yi-ting's school"], "answer": 0, "explain": "談到接機時，信中寫 My dad and I will wait for you in the arrival hall. 怡婷和爸爸會在入境大廳等他。(B) 媽媽只是請怡婷寫信；(C) 爺爺奶奶星期日才會來吃午餐；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the email, about how old is Anping Old Fort?", "options": ["100 years old", "200 years old", "300 years old", "400 years old"], "answer": 3, "explain": "談到星期六的行程時，信中寫 The Dutch started to build it in 1624, so it is about 400 years old. 荷蘭人在 1624 年開始建造安平古堡，所以它大約有 400 年的歷史。其他選項的年數都與信中所寫不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What do we know about Yi-ting's grandparents?", "options": ["They live in Kaohsiung.", "They cannot speak English.", "They will take Lucas to Anping.", "They own a restaurant."], "answer": 1, "explain": "談到星期日的午餐時，信中寫 My grandparents don't speak English. 爺爺奶奶不會說英文。(A) 信中沒有說他們住在哪裡；(C) 信中沒有說爺爺奶奶會帶盧卡斯去安平；(D) 奶奶只是會煮虱目魚湯，信中沒有說他們開餐廳。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does Yi-ting want Lucas to do before Wednesday?", "options": ["Buy a new hat", "Learn to cook milkfish soup", "Send her his flight number", "Tell her about any food he cannot eat"], "answer": 3, "explain": "署名前的最後一段寫 Also, is there any food you can't eat? Please tell me before Wednesday, so Grandma can plan the menu. 怡婷請盧卡斯在星期三前告訴她有沒有不能吃的食物。(A) 信中只請他帶帽子，沒有要他買新的；(B) 煮湯的是奶奶；(C) 信中已經寫了班機抵達的時間，沒有要他傳班機號碼。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a216", "title": "Take the Other Elevator: A Repair Notice", "title_zh": "請改搭另一部電梯：電梯維修公告", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Notice", "Life"], "passage": "NOTICE TO ALL RESIDENTS\nSunshine Garden Apartments\n\nElevator A will be closed for repairs from Monday, November 9, to Wednesday, November 11. During these three days, please use Elevator B.\n\nElevator B is smaller, and it will be very busy between 7:00 and 8:30 in the morning. Please leave home a few minutes earlier, or take the stairs if you live on a low floor.\n\nPlease do not move furniture or other large items during the repairs. We need to keep Elevator B free for everyone.\n\nDelivery workers will leave all packages at the front desk on the first floor. You can pick them up there between 8:00 a.m. and 9:00 p.m.\n\nDo you have trouble walking, or do you need to carry heavy bags? Please call the front desk. Our staff will be happy to help you.\n\nElevator A will be ready again at 7:00 a.m. on Thursday, November 12. We are sorry for the trouble, and thank you for your patience.\n\nBuilding Management Office\nNovember 2", "passage_zh": "全體住戶公告\n陽光花園大廈\n\nA 電梯將於 11 月 9 日（星期一）至 11 月 11 日（星期三）停用維修。這三天期間，請改搭 B 電梯。\n\nB 電梯比較小，早上 7:00 到 8:30 之間會非常擁擠。請提早幾分鐘出門；如果您住在低樓層，也可以走樓梯。\n\n維修期間，請勿搬運家具或其他大型物品。我們需要讓 B 電梯保持暢通，方便大家使用。\n\n送貨人員會把所有包裹放在一樓的管理櫃檯。您可以在早上 8:00 到晚上 9:00 之間到櫃檯領取。\n\n您行動不便，或是需要提很重的東西嗎？請打電話到管理櫃檯，我們的工作人員很樂意協助您。\n\nA 電梯將於 11 月 12 日（星期四）早上 7:00 恢復使用。造成不便，敬請見諒，謝謝您耐心配合。\n\n大樓管理中心\n11 月 2 日", "questions": [{"q": "What is the notice mainly about?", "options": ["One of the elevators will be closed for a few days.", "The building will get a new front desk.", "Residents must take the stairs every morning.", "Delivery workers can no longer enter the building."], "answer": 0, "explain": "公告主要說明 A 電梯將停用維修三天，請住戶改搭 B 電梯，並說明維修期間的注意事項。(B) 文中沒有提到新的櫃檯；(C) 只建議住低樓層的人可以走樓梯，並沒有規定大家每天早上都要走樓梯；(D) 送貨人員仍會把包裹送到一樓櫃檯。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why should residents leave home earlier in the morning?", "options": ["The front desk opens at 8:00 a.m.", "The workers will start the repairs at 7:00 a.m.", "Elevator B will be crowded at that time.", "The stairs will be closed until 8:30 a.m."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Elevator B is smaller, and it will be very busy between 7:00 and 8:30 in the morning. Please leave home a few minutes earlier... B 電梯比較小，早上會很擁擠，所以請住戶提早出門。(A) 櫃檯的時間與提早出門無關；(B) 文中沒有說工人幾點開始維修；(D) 文中沒有說樓梯關閉，反而建議住低樓層的人走樓梯。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Mr. Huang has bought a new sofa. On which of these days should it be brought into the building?", "options": ["Monday, November 9", "Tuesday, November 10", "Wednesday, November 11", "Thursday, November 12"], "answer": 3, "explain": "公告說維修期間（11 月 9 日至 11 日）請勿搬運家具等大型物品，而 A 電梯在 11 月 12 日（星期四）早上 7:00 恢復使用，所以沙發應該在星期四再搬進大樓。(A)(B)(C) 都在維修期間內。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Ms. Lin gets home at 9:30 p.m. on Tuesday and wants to get her package. What will happen?", "options": ["A worker will bring it to her door.", "She will have to wait until the next morning.", "She can find it outside her door.", "She can pick it up in Elevator B."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Delivery workers will leave all packages at the front desk... You can pick them up there between 8:00 a.m. and 9:00 p.m. 包裹只能在早上 8:00 到晚上 9:00 之間到櫃檯領取，林小姐晚上 9:30 才到家，所以要等到隔天早上才能領。(A)(C) 包裹放在一樓櫃檯，不會送到門口；(D) 文中沒有這種說法。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In the notice, \"residents\" are ______.", "options": ["workers who repair elevators", "people who deliver packages", "people who live in the building", "visitors who come for a short time"], "answer": 2, "explain": "公告標題是 NOTICE TO ALL RESIDENTS，內容又提到 leave home 和 if you live on a low floor，可知 residents 指「住在這棟大樓的人」，也就是住戶。(A) 修電梯的工人；(B) 送包裹的人；(D) 短暫來訪的訪客，皆不符文意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a217", "title": "Stars over Hehuanshan: A Night Sky Tour", "title_zh": "合歡山上的星空：夜空觀星之旅", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Advertisement", "Science"], "passage": "STARS OVER HEHUANSHAN\nA Night Sky Tour for Families and Students\n\nHehuanshan is more than 3,000 meters high, and there is very little light from cities around it. That makes it one of the best places in Taiwan to see the stars. Join us for a night you will never forget!\n\nDates: Every Saturday in October and November\nBus leaves: Taichung Train Station at 1:00 p.m.\nBus returns: Taichung Train Station at about 3:30 a.m. on Sunday\n\nPRICE (bus, dinner, hot drinks and a guide)\nAdults: NT$1,800\nStudents: NT$1,500\nGroups of 4 or more: NT$100 off for each person\n\nWHAT YOU WILL DO\n- Watch the sunset from the mountain.\n- Learn to find stars and planets with our guide.\n- Look through our big telescopes.\n\nWHAT TO BRING\n- A warm jacket, a hat and gloves. In autumn, nights on the mountain can be close to 0°C.\n- A flashlight with red light. White light makes it harder for everyone's eyes to see the stars.\n\nBAD WEATHER?\nIf it is cloudy or rainy, we will cancel the tour and tell you by 10:00 a.m. on Saturday. You will get all your money back.", "passage_zh": "合歡山上的星空\n適合家庭與學生的夜空之旅\n\n合歡山海拔超過 3,000 公尺，附近幾乎沒有城市的燈光，因此是台灣最適合看星星的地方之一。和我們一起度過一個難忘的夜晚吧！\n\n日期：十月和十一月的每個星期六\n去程：下午 1:00 從台中火車站出發\n回程：星期日凌晨約 3:30 回到台中火車站\n\n費用（含車資、晚餐、熱飲及導覽員）\n成人：1,800 元\n學生：1,500 元\n4 人以上團體：每人折價 100 元\n\n活動內容\n- 在山上欣賞夕陽。\n- 跟著導覽員學習尋找星星和行星。\n- 用我們的大型望遠鏡觀察星空。\n\n攜帶物品\n- 保暖外套、帽子和手套。秋天時，山上夜晚的氣溫可能接近 0°C。\n- 一支紅光手電筒。白光會讓大家的眼睛更難看清楚星星。\n\n天氣不好怎麼辦？\n如果天空多雲或下雨，我們會取消行程，並在星期六上午 10:00 前通知您。費用將全額退還。", "questions": [{"q": "What is this ad for?", "options": ["A cooking class in Taichung", "A new hotel on Hehuanshan", "A shop that sells telescopes", "A night trip to look at the stars"], "answer": 3, "explain": "廣告標題是 STARS OVER HEHUANSHAN，副標題是 A Night Sky Tour for Families and Students，內容介紹搭車上合歡山看夕陽、認識星星、用望遠鏡觀星，是一個夜間觀星行程。(A) 晚餐只是行程內容之一，不是烹飪課；(B) 文中沒有提到旅館；(C) 望遠鏡是活動時使用的，不是要賣。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the ad, why is Hehuanshan a good place to see stars?", "options": ["It is close to Taichung Train Station.", "It has the biggest telescope in Taiwan.", "It is very high and has little light from cities.", "It never rains there in autumn."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Hehuanshan is more than 3,000 meters high, and there is very little light from cities around it. That makes it one of the best places in Taiwan to see the stars. 合歡山很高，附近幾乎沒有城市燈光，所以適合看星星。(A) 從台中出發要坐很久的車，文中沒有說很近；(B) 文中沒有說那裡有全台最大的望遠鏡；(D) 廣告最後提到可能因下雨取消行程，可見不是不會下雨。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Four students go on the tour together. How much does each of them pay?", "options": ["NT$1,400", "NT$1,500", "NT$1,700", "NT$5,600"], "answer": 0, "explain": "學生票 1,500 元，4 人以上的團體每人折價 100 元，所以每人付 1,500 − 100 = 1,400 元。(B) 沒有扣掉團體折扣；(C) 是成人票扣掉折扣後的價錢；(D) 是四個人合計的總金額（1,400 × 4），不是每人要付的錢。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why should people bring a flashlight with red light?", "options": ["Red light helps the bus driver see the road.", "White light makes it harder to see the stars.", "The guide uses red light to find planets.", "Red flashlights keep people warm at night."], "answer": 1, "explain": "攜帶物品寫 A flashlight with red light. White light makes it harder for everyone's eyes to see the stars. 白光會讓大家的眼睛更難看清楚星星，所以要帶紅光手電筒。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What will happen if it rains on the day of the tour?", "options": ["The tour will start one hour later.", "People will watch a movie about stars on the bus.", "People can get half of their money back.", "The tour will be canceled, and people will get all their money back."], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段：If it is cloudy or rainy, we will cancel the tour... You will get all your money back. 如果多雲或下雨，行程會取消，並全額退費。(A)(B) 文中未提及；(C) 是全額退費，不是退一半。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a218", "title": "The Egg Drop Challenge", "title_zh": "護蛋大挑戰", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Education", "Science"], "passage": "Last Monday, our physics teacher, Mr. Tsai, gave us a fun challenge. \"Each team will get one raw egg, twenty straws, one meter of tape and a plastic bag,\" he said. \"Build something to keep your egg safe. On Friday, I will drop it from the third floor.\"\n\nMy team had three people: Kai, Ruby and me. Kai wanted to build a big box of straws around the egg. Ruby wanted to make a parachute from the plastic bag. We argued for two days. In the end, we used both ideas. We put the egg in a small straw box and tied the box to the bag.\n\nOn Friday, the whole class went outside. Mr. Tsai stood at a window on the third floor and dropped the eggs one by one. The first two eggs broke, and everyone screamed.\n\nThen it was our turn. Our parachute opened, and the box came down slowly. When it landed on the grass, we ran to it. Ruby opened the box carefully. The egg was perfect!\n\nMr. Tsai smiled. \"Do you know why it worked?\" he asked. \"The parachute slowed it down, and the straws took the hit.\"\n\nThat day, I learned some physics. I also learned that two good ideas can be better than one.", "passage_zh": "上星期一，我們的物理老師蔡老師給了我們一個有趣的挑戰。「每一組會拿到一顆生雞蛋、二十根吸管、一公尺長的膠帶和一個塑膠袋。」他說：「做出一個能保護雞蛋的東西。星期五，我會把它從三樓丟下去。」\n\n我們這組有三個人：凱、露比和我。凱想用吸管在雞蛋外面做一個大箱子，露比則想用塑膠袋做降落傘。我們爭論了兩天。最後，我們把兩個點子都用上了：把雞蛋放進一個小小的吸管盒子裡，再把盒子綁在塑膠袋上。\n\n星期五，全班都到了戶外。蔡老師站在三樓的一扇窗戶旁，一顆一顆地把雞蛋丟下來。前兩顆蛋都破了，大家都尖叫起來。\n\n接著輪到我們了。我們的降落傘張開了，盒子慢慢地落下。它一落在草地上，我們就跑了過去。露比小心翼翼地打開盒子——雞蛋完好無缺！\n\n蔡老師笑了。「你們知道為什麼會成功嗎？」他問：「降落傘讓它慢了下來，吸管則承受了撞擊。」\n\n那天，我學到了一些物理知識，也學到了兩個好點子加在一起，可能比一個點子更好。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the story mainly about?", "options": ["A teacher who drops his phone out of a window", "A team that keeps an egg safe in a class challenge", "A student who wants to change to another team", "A class that learns how to cook eggs"], "answer": 1, "explain": "故事描述物理老師出了一個護蛋挑戰，作者這組結合兩個點子，成功讓雞蛋從三樓落下而沒有破。(A) 老師丟的是雞蛋，不是手機；(C) 文中沒有人想換組；(D) 文中沒有煮蛋。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did the team argue for two days?", "options": ["They both wanted to be the team leader.", "Ruby broke Kai's egg by mistake.", "Kai did not want to do any work.", "They had different ideas about how to protect the egg."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Kai wanted to build a big box of straws around the egg. Ruby wanted to make a parachute from the plastic bag. We argued for two days. 凱想做吸管箱子，露比想做降落傘，兩人想法不同，所以全組爭論了兩天。(A)(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How did the team finally build their project?", "options": ["They only used a big box of straws.", "They only made a parachute from the bag.", "They put the egg in a straw box and tied the box to a parachute.", "They asked Mr. Tsai to build it for them."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：In the end, we used both ideas. We put the egg in a small straw box and tied the box to the bag. 最後他們把兩個點子都用上，把雞蛋放進吸管盒，再把盒子綁在塑膠袋做的降落傘上。(A)(B) 只用了其中一個點子；(D) 文中沒有請老師幫忙。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In the story, a \"raw\" egg is an egg that ______.", "options": ["has not been cooked", "is very small", "has been painted", "is already broken"], "answer": 0, "explain": "raw egg 指「生雞蛋」，也就是還沒煮熟的蛋。生蛋從高處掉下來很容易破，所以後文才會說前兩顆蛋都破了。(B) 很小的；(C) 塗了顏色的；(D) 已經破掉的，都不符合文意，而且老師發蛋時蛋還是完好的。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What did the writer learn from the challenge?", "options": ["Building things with straws is very easy.", "Two good ideas together can be better than one.", "It is always better to work alone.", "A parachute cannot protect an egg."], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一段：I also learned that two good ideas can be better than one. 作者學到兩個好點子結合起來，可能比一個更好。(A) 文中沒有說用吸管做東西很簡單；(C) 他們是靠團隊合作成功的，與文意相反；(D) 降落傘讓盒子慢慢落下，幫助保護了雞蛋，與文意相反。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a219", "title": "The Long-Tailed Mountain Lady: Taiwan's Blue Magpie", "title_zh": "長尾山娘：台灣藍鵲", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Nature"], "passage": "In the forests of Taiwan's low mountains, you may see a beautiful bird with a very long tail flying through the trees. This is the Taiwan blue magpie. It lives only in Taiwan and nowhere else in the world.\n\nYou can't miss this bird. Most of its body is bright blue, and its head and neck are black. It has a red beak, red feet and yellow eyes. From its beak to the end of its tail, it is about 65 centimeters long, and more than half of that is its tail. In Taiwanese, people call it the \"long-tailed mountain lady.\"\n\nBlue magpies live in family groups. They often fly from tree to tree one after another, like a long blue train in the sky. When the parents have babies, young birds from the year before help to feed them.\n\nThese birds eat almost anything, such as insects, fruit, frogs and even small snakes. They sometimes hide extra food under leaves and come back for it later.\n\nBlue magpies fight hard to protect their nests. If people get too close to a nest, the birds may fly at their heads.\n\nIn 2007, there was a public vote for Taiwan's national bird, and the Taiwan blue magpie got the most votes.", "passage_zh": "在台灣低海拔山區的森林裡，你可能會看到一種尾巴很長的美麗鳥類在樹林間飛翔。牠就是台灣藍鵲。牠只生活在台灣，世界上其他地方都沒有。\n\n這種鳥很好認。牠身體大部分是亮藍色的，頭和脖子是黑色的，還有紅色的嘴、紅色的腳和黃色的眼睛。從嘴巴到尾巴末端，牠大約長 65 公分，其中一半以上是尾巴。在台語裡，人們叫牠「長尾山娘」。\n\n台灣藍鵲以家族為單位一起生活。牠們常常一隻接著一隻地從一棵樹飛到另一棵樹，就像天空中一列長長的藍色火車。當鳥爸爸、鳥媽媽生下寶寶時，前一年出生的年輕藍鵲會幫忙餵食。\n\n這種鳥幾乎什麼都吃，例如昆蟲、水果、青蛙，甚至小蛇。牠們有時會把多出來的食物藏在落葉下，之後再回來吃。\n\n台灣藍鵲會拚命保護自己的巢。如果有人太靠近鳥巢，牠們可能會朝那個人的頭飛撲過去。\n\n2007 年，台灣舉辦了一場國鳥票選活動，台灣藍鵲得到了最多票。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to take good photos of birds in the mountains", "Why some birds in Taiwan are disappearing", "What a special bird of Taiwan looks like and how it lives", "How people in Taiwan choose their national flower"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹台灣藍鵲：只住在台灣、外表特徵、家族生活方式、吃什麼、如何保護鳥巢，以及國鳥票選。(A) 文中沒有教人拍照；(B) 文中沒有提到鳥類減少；(D) 文中提到的是國鳥票選，不是國花。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of these describes the Taiwan blue magpie?", "options": ["A blue body, a black head and a red beak", "A black body, a blue head and a yellow beak", "A red body, a blue tail and black feet", "A blue body, a red head and yellow feet"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Most of its body is bright blue, and its head and neck are black. It has a red beak, red feet and yellow eyes. 身體大多是藍色、頭是黑色、嘴是紅色。(B) 顏色剛好相反，而且黃色的是眼睛，不是嘴；(C) 身體不是紅色；(D) 頭是黑色，腳是紅色。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Who helps the parents feed the baby birds?", "options": ["Other kinds of birds in the forest", "People who live near the mountains", "Only the mother bird", "Young birds that were born the year before"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：When the parents have babies, young birds from the year before help to feed them. 前一年出生的年輕藍鵲會幫忙餵寶寶。(A)(B) 文中未提及；(C) 文中說有家族成員幫忙，不是只有鳥媽媽。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What do blue magpies sometimes do with extra food?", "options": ["They hide it under leaves and eat it later.", "They give it to other kinds of birds.", "They carry it to people in the mountains.", "They drop it into rivers."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：They sometimes hide extra food under leaves and come back for it later. 牠們有時會把多出來的食物藏在落葉下，之後再回來吃。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer say a group of blue magpies looks like \"a long blue train in the sky\"?", "options": ["They make loud noises like a train.", "They fly one after another in a line.", "They fly as fast as a train.", "They often build nests near train stations."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：They often fly from tree to tree one after another, like a long blue train in the sky. 藍鵲常常一隻接著一隻地飛，排成一長列，看起來就像一列長長的火車。(A) 文中沒有提到叫聲；(C) 文中沒有比較飛行速度；(D) 文中沒有提到火車站。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a220", "title": "Sun at Midnight, Dark at Noon: Life in Tromsø", "title_zh": "半夜有太陽，中午是黑夜：特羅姆瑟的生活", "level": "Reading · A2", "tags": ["Nature", "Travel"], "passage": "Tromsø is a small city in the far north of Norway. It is north of the Arctic Circle, so the sun does some strange things there.\n\nFrom about May 20 to July 22, the sun never goes down. This is called the \"midnight sun.\" At 12 o'clock at night, the sky is still bright, and you can read a book outside without a lamp. Children play soccer late in the evening, and many people go hiking after dinner. But sleeping can be hard, so most homes have thick, dark curtains to keep the light out.\n\nIn winter, everything changes. From late November to the middle of January, the sun does not come up at all. This time is called the \"polar night.\" But it is not completely black all day. For a few hours around noon, the sky turns a soft blue. Still, people often feel tired, and many of them light candles at home to feel warm and happy.\n\nThe dark winter brings one special gift: the northern lights. On clear nights, green lights dance across the sky, and visitors from all over the world come to Tromsø to see them.\n\nWhen the sun comes back in January, people are very happy. Some say the first sunlight of the year feels like a warm hello from an old friend.", "passage_zh": "特羅姆瑟是位於挪威極北地區的一座小城市。它在北極圈以北，所以太陽在那裡會出現一些奇特的現象。\n\n大約從 5 月 20 日到 7 月 22 日，太陽一直都不會下山，這叫做「午夜太陽」。到了半夜十二點，天空仍然很亮，你不用開燈就能在戶外看書。孩子們到了很晚還在踢足球，許多人吃完晚餐後去健行。但是這時要睡覺可能很困難，所以大部分的家庭都裝了厚厚的深色窗簾來擋住光線。\n\n到了冬天，一切都變了。從十一月下旬到一月中旬，太陽完全不會升起，這段時間叫做「極夜」。不過，並不是整天都一片漆黑。中午前後的幾個小時，天空會變成柔和的藍色。儘管如此，人們還是常常覺得疲倦，許多人會在家裡點蠟燭，讓自己感到溫暖又開心。\n\n黑暗的冬天也帶來一份特別的禮物：北極光。在晴朗的夜晚，綠色的光在天空中舞動，世界各地的遊客都會來到特羅姆瑟觀賞。\n\n當太陽在一月回來時，人們都非常開心。有些人說，一年中的第一道陽光，就像老朋友溫暖的問候。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to take photos of the northern lights", "Why Norway is a good place to play soccer", "How people in Tromsø make candles at home", "How the sun changes life in Tromsø in summer and winter"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文說明特羅姆瑟位在北極圈以北，夏天有「午夜太陽」、冬天有「極夜」，以及當地人如何因應這些變化。(A) 文中沒有教人拍北極光；(B) 踢足球只是夏天的一個例子；(C) 文中只說人們會點蠟燭，沒有說怎麼做蠟燭。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why do most homes in Tromsø have thick, dark curtains?", "options": ["To keep the light out so people can sleep in summer", "To keep the cold wind out in winter", "To make the rooms look beautiful for visitors", "To see the northern lights more clearly"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：But sleeping can be hard, so most homes have thick, dark curtains to keep the light out. 夏天太陽不下山，晚上很難睡覺，所以要用厚窗簾擋住光線。(B)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 拉上窗簾反而看不到北極光，而且北極光是在冬天出現。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In which month can you see the sun at midnight in Tromsø?", "options": ["January", "March", "June", "November"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：From about May 20 to July 22, the sun never goes down. 大約從 5 月 20 日到 7 月 22 日太陽都不下山，六月正好在這段期間內。(A)(D) 一月和十一月是冬天，部分日子甚至是太陽不會升起的極夜，半夜當然看不到太陽；(B) 三月不在 5 月 20 日到 7 月 22 日之間。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the polar night?", "options": ["It is completely dark for twenty-four hours every day.", "The sky turns a soft blue for a few hours around noon.", "It lasts from May to July.", "People feel more awake than in summer."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：But it is not completely black all day. For a few hours around noon, the sky turns a soft blue. 極夜時並不是整天全黑，中午前後的幾個小時天空會變成柔和的藍色。(A) 與文中說法相反；(C) 五月到七月是午夜太陽的期間；(D) 文中說人們常常覺得疲倦。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why do many visitors come to Tromsø in winter?", "options": ["To go hiking late at night", "To read books outside without a lamp", "To see the northern lights", "To watch the midnight sun"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：The dark winter brings one special gift: the northern lights... visitors from all over the world come to Tromsø to see them. 冬天有北極光，世界各地的遊客都會來觀賞。(A)(B)(D) 都是夏天「午夜太陽」期間才有的情況。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a221", "title": "Smashed or Hit? How a Single Word Can Rewrite a Memory", "title_zh": "「猛撞」還是「碰撞」？一個字如何改寫記憶", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Science", "Society"], "passage": "Most of us treat our memories as if they were recordings: imperfect, perhaps, but faithful to what actually happened. Were that true, an eyewitness who sincerely remembers a face would be as reliable as a security camera. Decades of research by the American psychologist Elizabeth Loftus suggest otherwise. Memory, her work shows, is less a recording than a reconstruction, rebuilt each time we recall it and vulnerable to whatever information happens to be around at the time.\n\nIn a now-classic 1974 study, Loftus and her colleague John Palmer showed students short films of car accidents and then asked them how fast the cars had been going. The only thing that varied was a single verb. Participants asked how fast the cars were going when they \"smashed into\" each other estimated an average of about 41 miles per hour; those who heard \"hit\" gave an average of 34. More striking still was a follow-up experiment. Asked a week after watching the film whether they had seen any broken glass (there had been none), roughly a third of the \"smashed\" group said yes, compared with about one in seven of those who had heard \"hit.\" The word had not merely shaped their answer; it appeared to have quietly altered the memory itself.\n\nHaving shown that details could be distorted, Loftus went further, asking whether an entire event could be planted. In the 1990s, she and her colleagues gave volunteers short written accounts of childhood experiences, supposedly supplied by their relatives. Most were true, but one, getting lost in a shopping mall at around the age of five, had never happened. After being interviewed about the stories, about a quarter of the participants came to remember the invented episode, some of them describing it in vivid detail. Later studies using faked photographs produced similar results.\n\nWhy should a system as sophisticated as the human brain be so easily misled? The answer, many researchers argue, is that memory did not evolve to serve as an archive. Its job is to help us make sense of the present and predict the future, and for that purpose a flexible memory, one that blends past experience with new information, is generally more useful than a rigid one. The price of that flexibility is that we cannot always tell where an original experience ends and a later suggestion begins. Crucially, false memories do not feel false; confidence, it turns out, is a poor guide to accuracy.\n\nNowhere do these findings matter more than in the courtroom. Of the hundreds of wrongly convicted people in the United States who have later been cleared by DNA evidence, a majority were convicted at least partly on the strength of mistaken eyewitness testimony. Partly as a result of research like Loftus's, many police departments have changed the way they run identification lineups, for instance by having them conducted by officers who do not know which person is the suspect, so that they cannot unintentionally steer the witness.\n\nNone of this means that our memories are worthless. Most of what we remember is broadly accurate, especially the gist of events. But Loftus's work offers a humbling reminder to anyone who has ever argued with a friend about who said what at a party: the vivid scene playing in your mind may be partly of your own making.", "passage_zh": "我們大多數人都把記憶當成錄影：也許不完美，卻忠實呈現實際發生過的事。如果真是如此，一位真心記得某張臉孔的目擊者，就會和監視攝影機一樣可靠。然而，美國心理學家伊莉莎白‧羅芙特斯數十年的研究卻顯示並非如此。她的研究指出，記憶與其說是錄影，不如說是一種重建：每次回想時都會重新組合，而且很容易受到當下周遭任何資訊的影響。\n\n在 1974 年一項如今已成經典的研究中，羅芙特斯和同事約翰‧帕爾默讓學生觀看車禍短片，接著問他們車子當時開得多快。各組之間唯一的差別，只有一個動詞。被問到兩車「猛撞」（smashed into）彼此時車速多快的受試者，平均估計約為每小時 41 英哩；聽到「碰撞」（hit）的人，平均估計則是 34 英哩。更驚人的是一項後續實驗的結果。受試者在看完影片一週後被問到是否看見任何碎玻璃（影片中其實根本沒有），結果「猛撞」組大約有三分之一的人說有，而聽到「碰撞」的人當中，只有大約七分之一這麼說。這個字不只影響了他們的回答，似乎還悄悄改變了記憶本身。\n\n在證明細節可以被扭曲之後，羅芙特斯更進一步，探討一整件事情是否也能被植入記憶。1990 年代，她和同事給志願者看幾段簡短的童年經歷文字，據稱是由他們的親人提供的。其中大部分是真的，但有一段——大約五歲時在購物中心走失——從來沒有發生過。在針對這些故事接受訪談之後，大約四分之一的受試者開始「記得」這段捏造的經歷，有些人甚至能生動地描述細節。後來使用偽造照片的研究，也得到了類似的結果。\n\n像人腦這麼精密的系統，為什麼會這麼容易被誤導？許多研究者認為，答案在於記憶演化出來的目的，並不是當作檔案庫。它的任務是幫助我們理解現在、預測未來；為了這個目的，一個有彈性、能把過去經驗與新資訊融合在一起的記憶，通常比僵化的記憶更有用。這種彈性的代價是，我們不一定分辨得出原本的經驗在哪裡結束、後來的暗示又從哪裡開始。關鍵在於，錯誤的記憶感覺起來並不假；事實證明，自信程度並不是判斷記憶是否準確的好指標。\n\n這些發現影響最大的地方，莫過於法庭。在美國，有數百名遭到錯誤定罪、後來因 DNA 證據而洗刷冤屈的人，其中大多數人至少有部分是因為錯誤的目擊證詞而被定罪。部分由於羅芙特斯這類研究的影響，許多警察局已經改變了指認嫌犯的程序，例如改由不知道誰是嫌犯的員警來主持指認，讓他們無法在無意間引導目擊者。\n\n這一切並不代表我們的記憶毫無價值。我們記得的事情大多大致正確，尤其是事件的要點。不過，對任何曾經和朋友爭論派對上誰說了什麼話的人來說，羅芙特斯的研究都是一個令人謙卑的提醒：在你腦海中播放的那個生動畫面，可能有一部分是你自己製造出來的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main point the writer makes about memory?", "options": ["It works like a recording that slowly fades over time.", "It is rebuilt each time it is recalled and can be changed by later information.", "It is reliable only when people feel highly confident about it.", "It is accurate for recent events but not for childhood experiences."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文主旨見第一段：Memory... is less a recording than a reconstruction, rebuilt each time we recall it and vulnerable to whatever information happens to be around at the time. 記憶不像錄影，而是每次回想時重新建構，容易受到後來資訊的影響；之後的車禍實驗與購物中心走失實驗都在支持這個論點。(A) 正是作者要推翻的看法；(C) 第四段指出自信並不能保證準確，與此相反；(D) 文中並未比較近期事件與童年記憶的準確度。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the broken-glass result in Loftus and Palmer's study?", "options": ["The word \"smashed\" led some viewers to add a detail that was never in the film.", "The films showed broken glass in only some of the accidents.", "Students in the \"hit\" group paid less attention to the film.", "Most students could no longer remember the film after a week."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段指出影片中其實沒有碎玻璃（there had been none），但聽到 smashed 的一組約有三分之一說看到了，遠多於聽到 hit 的一組，可推知 smashed 這個字讓部分觀眾在記憶中加入了影片裡沒有的細節。(B) 與 there had been none 矛盾；(C) 文中未提及專注程度；(D) 受試者一週後仍能回答問題，文中並未說大多數人忘了影片。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"planted\" in the third paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["grown slowly in someone's mind over many years", "hidden carefully from other people", "placed in someone's mind by an outside source", "erased completely from someone's memory"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段 whether an entire event could be planted 之後，描述研究者讓受試者「記得」一段從未發生過的走失經歷，可知 planted 指「由外界植入（某人腦中）」。(A) 取 plant「種植」的字面聯想，但這段記憶是研究者在短時間內刻意放進去的，並非多年慢慢長成；(B) 小心地對別人隱藏、(D) 從記憶中完全抹除，皆不符文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer point out that false memories \"do not feel false\"?", "options": ["To argue that people who report false memories are usually lying", "To explain why a witness's confidence cannot prove that a memory is accurate", "To suggest that false memories are easy to detect with careful questioning", "To show that memory evolved mainly to store the past"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：false memories do not feel false; confidence, it turns out, is a poor guide to accuracy. 作者說錯誤記憶「感覺起來並不假」，是為了說明目擊者再有自信，也不能證明記憶正確，這也連結到第五段錯誤目擊證詞造成冤案的討論。(A) 錯誤記憶是當事人真心相信的，並非說謊；(C) 正好相反，錯誤記憶很難察覺；(D) 第四段說記憶演化的目的是幫助理解現在與預測未來，而不是當作檔案庫。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's conclusion in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Memory is so unreliable that it should never be trusted.", "Courts should stop using eyewitnesses altogether.", "Arguments between friends are usually caused by false memories.", "Memory is generally useful, but we should be humble about how accurate it is."], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段先說 None of this means that our memories are worthless. Most of what we remember is broadly accurate，再提出 a humbling reminder（令人謙卑的提醒）。可知作者認為記憶大致可靠，但我們不該對它過度自信。(A) 與 not worthless 相反；(B) 第五段只提到改進指認程序，並未主張完全不用目擊證人；(C) 和朋友爭論只是舉例，文中並未說爭論通常由錯誤記憶造成。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a222", "title": "The Enemy of Our Enemy: Viruses That Hunt Bacteria", "title_zh": "敵人的敵人：獵殺細菌的病毒", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "In late 2015, an American professor named Tom Patterson fell ill while on holiday in Egypt. The cause turned out to be Acinetobacter baumannii, a bacterium that had become resistant to nearly every antibiotic his doctors tried. Within weeks he had been flown back to San Diego, where he slipped into a coma. With conventional medicine running out of options, his wife, the epidemiologist Steffanie Strathdee, turned to an idea that most Western doctors had long dismissed: treating him with viruses.\n\nThe viruses in question are bacteriophages, or simply \"phages,\" a name that means \"eaters of bacteria.\" They were discovered independently by the British scientist Frederick Twort in 1915 and the French-Canadian microbiologist Félix d'Hérelle in 1917. Unlike the viruses that cause colds or flu, phages cannot infect human cells. Instead, each type attacks only particular strains of bacteria, injecting its genetic material and turning the cell into a factory for new phages until it bursts. They are almost unimaginably common: phages are thought to be the most abundant biological entities on Earth, outnumbering even bacteria.\n\nD'Hérelle quickly saw the medical potential of his discovery, and by the 1930s phage therapy was being used in several countries. In the West, however, it was soon abandoned. Early results were inconsistent, partly because the biology was poorly understood, and when penicillin and other antibiotics arrived in the 1940s, they offered something phages could not: a single drug that worked against a wide range of bacteria. Only in the Soviet Union and parts of Eastern Europe, most notably at a research institute in Tbilisi, Georgia, founded with d'Hérelle's help, did the practice continue.\n\nThat very specificity, once a weakness, has now become an advantage. Antibiotics are blunt instruments: they kill helpful bacteria along with harmful ones, and their overuse has encouraged the spread of \"superbugs\" that shrug them off. The World Health Organization counts antimicrobial resistance among the greatest threats to global health, linking it directly to more than a million deaths a year. Phages, by contrast, can be matched to a single strain, leaving the body's useful microbes largely untouched.\n\nThat is precisely what made Patterson's treatment so demanding. Researchers at several American laboratories, including one run by the US Navy, searched their collections for phages capable of killing his particular strain, then purified them and prepared them for injection. In March 2016, with emergency approval from the US Food and Drug Administration, the first doses were given. Within days, Patterson began to wake from his coma, and he eventually made a full recovery.\n\nPatterson's case did not prove that phage therapy works; a single recovery, however dramatic, cannot do that. Bacteria can evolve resistance to phages too, and the need to tailor treatments to individual patients fits awkwardly with the way new drugs are normally tested and approved. Still, his survival helped revive serious interest in the approach, and clinical trials are now under way in several countries. A century after its discovery, an old idea may yet find its moment.", "passage_zh": "2015 年底，一位名叫湯姆‧派特森的美國教授在埃及度假時病倒了。病因是鮑氏不動桿菌，這種細菌對醫生嘗試過的幾乎每一種抗生素都已產生抗藥性。幾週之內，他就被送回聖地牙哥，並在那裡陷入昏迷。在傳統醫學幾乎無計可施的情況下，他的妻子——流行病學家史蒂芬妮‧斯特拉斯迪——轉向一個大多數西方醫生早已不屑一顧的想法：用病毒來治療他。\n\n這裡所說的病毒是噬菌體（bacteriophage，簡稱 phage），這個名稱的意思是「細菌的吞食者」。英國科學家弗德瑞克‧特沃特在 1915 年、法裔加拿大微生物學家費利克斯‧德赫雷爾在 1917 年，分別獨立發現了它們。和引起感冒或流感的病毒不同，噬菌體無法感染人類細胞。每一種噬菌體只會攻擊特定菌株的細菌：它把自己的遺傳物質注入細菌，把細胞變成製造新噬菌體的工廠，直到細胞破裂為止。噬菌體多得令人難以想像：一般認為它們是地球上數量最多的生物實體，甚至比細菌還多。\n\n德赫雷爾很快就看出這項發現在醫學上的潛力，到了 1930 年代，已有好幾個國家使用噬菌體療法。然而在西方，這種療法很快就遭到放棄。早期的療效時好時壞，部分原因是當時對其生物學原理所知甚少；而當盤尼西林等抗生素在 1940 年代問世時，它們提供了噬菌體做不到的東西：單一種藥物就能對付多種細菌。只有在蘇聯和東歐部分地區，尤其是在喬治亞的第比利斯一間由德赫雷爾協助創立的研究所，這種療法才得以延續下來。\n\n這種專一性過去是缺點，如今卻成了優勢。抗生素就像不夠精準的鈍器：它們會連同有害細菌一起殺死有益細菌，而過度使用也助長了根本不把抗生素放在眼裡的「超級細菌」擴散。世界衛生組織將抗微生物藥物抗藥性列為全球健康最大的威脅之一，並指出它每年直接造成超過一百萬人死亡。相較之下，噬菌體可以針對單一菌株，讓體內有用的微生物大致不受影響。\n\n而這正是派特森的治療如此艱鉅的原因。美國好幾間實驗室——包括一間由美國海軍經營的實驗室——在各自收藏的噬菌體中，搜尋能殺死他身上那種菌株的噬菌體，再加以純化，製成注射用的製劑。2016 年 3 月，在美國食品藥物管理局的緊急核准下，他接受了第一劑治療。短短幾天內，派特森就開始從昏迷中甦醒，最後完全康復。\n\n派特森的病例並不能證明噬菌體療法有效；單一病例的康復，無論多麼戲劇性，都無法證明這一點。細菌也可能演化出對噬菌體的抗性，而且必須針對個別病人量身打造療法，這一點也很難配合新藥一般的測試與核准方式。儘管如此，他的存活讓人們重新認真看待這個方法，目前已有好幾個國家正在進行臨床試驗。在被發現一個世紀之後，這個老點子或許終於要迎來屬於它的時刻。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To describe how a long-neglected treatment is attracting new interest", "To argue that antibiotics should be replaced by phages", "To tell the life story of Félix d'Hérelle", "To explain how Tom Patterson caught an infection in Egypt"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文從派特森的病例談起，回顧噬菌體的發現、在西方遭到放棄的原因，以及在抗藥性問題下重新受到重視的經過，最後一段說 his survival helped revive serious interest in the approach。可知主旨是介紹一種長期被冷落的療法如何重新受到關注。(B) 作者並未主張以噬菌體取代抗生素，最後一段還提到其限制；(C) 德赫雷爾只是文中的一部分；(D) 派特森在埃及感染只是開頭的引子。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why was phage therapy largely abandoned in the West?", "options": ["Doctors found that phages often infected human cells.", "The Soviet Union refused to share its research.", "Early results were unreliable, and antibiotics worked against many kinds of bacteria.", "Phages were too rare to be collected in large numbers."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：Early results were inconsistent... and when penicillin and other antibiotics arrived..., they offered something phages could not: a single drug that worked against a wide range of bacteria. 早期療效不穩定，加上抗生素能對付多種細菌，因此西方放棄了噬菌體療法。(A) 第二段明言噬菌體無法感染人類細胞；(B) 文中未提及；(D) 第二段說噬菌體是地球上數量最多的生物實體，與此相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"shrug them off\" in the fourth paragraph most likely means ______.", "options": ["are easily destroyed by them", "are not seriously affected by them", "hide from them inside human cells", "depend on them to survive"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段 the spread of \"superbugs\" that shrug them off，superbugs 指對抗生素有抗藥性的細菌，them 指抗生素，可知 shrug them off 意為「不把它們當一回事、不受其影響」。(A) 與抗藥性的意思相反；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what made Patterson's treatment \"so demanding\"?", "options": ["Patients must be in a coma before phages can be used.", "Phages can only be produced in Georgia.", "Each dose of phages kills the helpful bacteria in the body.", "The phages had to be carefully matched to the exact strain infecting him."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段開頭 That is precisely what made Patterson's treatment so demanding，That 指上一段所說噬菌體只能針對單一菌株；因此研究人員必須搜尋能殺死他身上那種特定菌株的噬菌體，再純化製成注射劑。(A) 昏迷是他的病情，並非使用噬菌體的條件；(B) 治療用的噬菌體來自美國的實驗室；(C) 第四段說噬菌體大致不會傷害有益微生物，會殺死有益菌的是抗生素。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which best describes the writer's attitude toward phage therapy in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Enthusiastic, believing it has already been proven to cure resistant infections", "Cautiously hopeful, recognizing both its promise and its limits", "Dismissive, seeing Patterson's recovery as nothing more than luck", "Indifferent, since antibiotics remain effective against most bacteria"], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一段一方面指出單一病例無法證明療效、細菌也可能產生抗性，另一方面又說臨床試驗正在進行、an old idea may yet find its moment，可見作者的態度是審慎樂觀，同時看到它的潛力與限制。(A) 作者明言這個病例 did not prove that phage therapy works；(C) 作者並未認為康復只是運氣；(D) 第四段指出抗藥性是重大威脅，作者並非漠不關心。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a223", "title": "An Itch Where Nothing Is: Phantom Limbs and the Mirror Box", "title_zh": "癢在不存在的地方：幻肢與鏡箱", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "For centuries, people who had lost an arm or a leg reported something that seemed impossible: they could still feel it. The missing limb might itch, tingle or, worst of all, ache, sometimes for decades. The American Civil War surgeon Silas Weir Mitchell, who coined the term \"phantom limb\" in the 1870s, saw so many cases among wounded soldiers that he could hardly dismiss them as imagination. Yet for a long time doctors had little to offer, and some patients were quietly suspected of exaggerating their pain.\n\nThe key to the puzzle, it turned out, lay not in the stump but in the brain. In the 1930s and 1940s, the Canadian neurosurgeon Wilder Penfield, stimulating the exposed brains of conscious patients during surgery, showed that the surface of the body is represented by a kind of map along a strip of the cortex. Each region of skin corresponds to a particular patch of this map. Curiously, the map does not follow the layout of the body exactly: the area devoted to the face lies right next to the area devoted to the hand.\n\nIn the early 1990s, the neuroscientist V. S. Ramachandran, working in California, wondered what would happen to the hand area once the hand itself was gone. Would it simply fall silent? To find out, he lightly touched different parts of the bodies of patients who had lost an arm while they kept their eyes closed. When he stroked their cheeks, some reported feeling the touch on their missing fingers as well. The explanation he proposed was striking: deprived of its usual input, the hand area had been taken over by signals from the neighboring face area. The brain's map, long assumed to be fixed in adulthood, had redrawn itself.\n\nRamachandran also had a theory about why phantom limbs so often hurt. Many patients felt that their missing hand was frozen in an awkward or painful position, clenched so tightly that they could feel the nails digging into the palm. Because the brain kept sending commands to move the hand and never received any feedback, he suspected, it was trapped in a loop, unable to \"unlock\" the cramped fist. What the brain needed, he reasoned, was the illusion of movement.\n\nHis solution was disarmingly simple: a cardboard box with a mirror standing upright in the middle. A patient places the intact arm on one side of the mirror and the stump on the other, then looks at the reflection. Seen from the right angle, the reflected arm appears to occupy the space where the missing one should be. When the patient moves the healthy hand, the brain \"sees\" the phantom moving too. Some of Ramachandran's patients reported feeling the phantom hand unclench, and their pain eased.\n\nMirror therapy is no miracle cure. Larger clinical studies have produced mixed results: it helps some patients considerably and others hardly at all, and researchers still debate exactly how it works. But its influence has spread well beyond amputees, to stroke rehabilitation and certain chronic pain conditions. More fundamentally, the phantom limb has changed how scientists think about the body itself. The arm you feel, it seems, is not simply the arm you have; it is a model constructed by the brain, one that can outlast the limb it represents and that, with a little ingenuity, can be persuaded to change.", "passage_zh": "幾百年來，失去手臂或腿的人一直描述一件看似不可能的事：他們仍然感覺得到那隻肢體。失去的肢體可能會癢、會發麻，最糟的是會痛，有時長達數十年。美國南北戰爭時期的外科醫生塞拉斯‧威爾‧米契爾在 1870 年代創造了「幻肢」一詞；他在受傷的士兵身上看到太多這樣的病例，實在無法把它們當成想像而不予理會。然而，很長一段時間裡，醫生能做的很少，有些病人甚至被暗中懷疑在誇大自己的疼痛。\n\n原來，解開謎團的關鍵不在殘肢，而在大腦。1930 和 1940 年代，加拿大神經外科醫生懷爾德‧潘菲爾德在手術中刺激清醒病人裸露的大腦，發現身體的表面在大腦皮質的一條帶狀區域上，以一種類似地圖的方式呈現。皮膚的每個區域，都對應到這張地圖上的某一小塊。奇妙的是，這張地圖並沒有完全依照身體的位置排列：負責臉部的區域，就緊鄰著負責手部的區域。\n\n1990 年代初期，在加州工作的神經科學家 V. S. 拉瑪錢德朗好奇：當手本身已經不在了，大腦中負責手的區域會怎麼樣？它會就此沉寂嗎？為了找出答案，他請失去一隻手臂的病人閉上眼睛，然後輕觸他們身體的不同部位。當他輕撫病人的臉頰時，有些人表示，連失去的手指也感覺到了這個觸碰。他提出的解釋相當驚人：手部區域失去了平常的訊號輸入之後，被相鄰臉部區域傳來的訊號占據了。這張長久以來被認為成年後就固定不變的大腦地圖，竟然重新繪製了自己。\n\n拉瑪錢德朗對幻肢為什麼常常會痛，也有一套理論。許多病人覺得失去的手被凍結在一個彆扭或疼痛的姿勢，拳頭握得非常緊，緊到能感覺指甲掐進掌心。他推測，由於大腦不斷發出移動那隻手的指令，卻始終得不到任何回饋，因此陷入了一個迴圈，無法「解開」那隻抽筋般緊握的拳頭。他推論，大腦需要的是一種手在移動的錯覺。\n\n他的解決辦法簡單得令人驚嘆：一個中間直立著一面鏡子的紙箱。病人把完好的手臂放在鏡子的一側，殘肢放在另一側，然後看著鏡中的倒影。從適當的角度看過去，鏡中的手臂就像出現在失去的那隻手臂原本所在的位置。當病人移動健康的手時，大腦就「看見」幻肢也在動。拉瑪錢德朗的一些病人表示，他們感覺到緊握的幻肢手鬆開了，疼痛也減輕了。\n\n鏡像療法並不是萬靈丹。規模較大的臨床研究結果好壞參半：它對某些病人幫助很大，對另一些人卻幾乎沒有效果，研究人員至今仍在爭論它確切的作用原理。不過，它的影響早已擴及截肢者以外的領域，被運用在中風復健和某些慢性疼痛的治療上。更根本的是，幻肢改變了科學家對身體本身的看法。看來，你感覺到的手臂並不只是你擁有的那隻手臂；它是大腦建構出來的模型——這個模型可以比它所代表的肢體存在得更久，而且只要一點巧思，就能說服它改變。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the first paragraph suggest about early attitudes toward phantom limb pain?", "options": ["Doctors understood its causes but lacked the medicine to treat it.", "It was believed to affect only soldiers wounded in war.", "It was poorly understood, so patients' reports were not always taken seriously.", "Surgeons blamed it on infections in the stump."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段說醫生 had little to offer（能做的很少），而且 some patients were quietly suspected of exaggerating their pain（有些病人被暗中懷疑誇大疼痛），可見當時對幻肢痛了解不多，病人的描述不一定被認真看待。(A) 當時醫生並不了解成因，第二段才說明關鍵在大腦；(B) 米契爾在士兵身上看到很多病例，但文中沒有說只有士兵會有；(D) 文中未提及感染。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why was Ramachandran's cheek experiment significant?", "options": ["It proved that the face and the hand share the same nerves.", "It suggested that the body map in the adult brain can reorganize itself.", "It showed that people can feel touch without any brain activity.", "It explained why phantom limbs usually itch rather than hurt."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The brain's map, long assumed to be fixed in adulthood, had redrawn itself. 輕觸臉頰卻在失去的手指上也有感覺，顯示手部區域被臉部區域的訊號占據，代表成年人大腦中的身體地圖能夠重組。(A) 文中說的是大腦地圖上兩區相鄰，而不是共用神經；(C) 與文意相反，感覺正是來自大腦的活動；(D) 這個實驗與幻肢為何會癢無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In the fourth paragraph, the word \"it\" in \"it was trapped in a loop\" refers to ______.", "options": ["the missing hand", "the brain", "the feedback", "the painful position"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段 Because the brain kept sending commands to move the hand and never received any feedback, he suspected, it was trapped in a loop... 不斷發出指令、得不到回饋而陷入迴圈的主體是 the brain，所以 it 指大腦。(A) 手已經不存在，是要被「解開」的對象；(C)(D) 都不是陷入迴圈的主體。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"disarmingly\" in the fifth paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["surprisingly, in a way that wins people over", "dangerously, in a way that could harm patients", "carelessly, without enough planning", "temporarily, as a short-term measure"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段 His solution was disarmingly simple 之後描述的只是一個中間立著鏡子的紙箱，卻能減輕病人的疼痛，可知 disarmingly 是「（簡單得）出人意料、令人折服地」，帶有讚嘆的語氣。(B) 文中沒有提到鏡箱有任何危險；(C) 作者是在稱讚這個設計，並非批評它草率；(D) 文中沒有說它只是暫時的權宜之計。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the last paragraph?", "options": ["Mirror therapy has replaced all other treatments for chronic pain.", "Scientists now agree on exactly how mirror therapy works.", "Phantom limbs prove that amputees only imagine their pain.", "Our sense of our own body is a model built by the brain, and it can be changed."], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段的重點在：The arm you feel... is not simply the arm you have; it is a model constructed by the brain... that... can be persuaded to change. 我們對身體的感覺是大腦建構的模型，而且可以被改變。(A) 文中只說鏡像療法也用於中風復健與某些慢性疼痛，並未取代所有療法；(B) 文中說研究人員仍在爭論它如何發揮作用；(C) 全文說明幻肢痛源自大腦，並不是病人想像出來的。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a224", "title": "Molecular Scissors: From a Bacterial Defense to a Treatment for Sickle Cell Disease", "title_zh": "分子剪刀：從細菌的防禦機制到鐮刀型貧血症的療法", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Science", "Health"], "passage": "Scientific revolutions rarely begin with a grand plan. In 1987, a team of Japanese researchers led by Yoshizumi Ishino, studying a gene in the common gut bacterium E. coli, noticed something odd nearby: a short stretch of DNA repeated again and again, with unique \"spacer\" sequences in between. They had no idea what it was for, and for well over a decade, neither did anyone else. The pattern was eventually given a suitably unwieldy name, clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats, or CRISPR.\n\nThe breakthrough came when scientists realized that the spacers matched the DNA of viruses that infect bacteria. CRISPR, it emerged, was a kind of immune memory. When a bacterium survives a viral attack, it stores a snippet of the invader's DNA in its own genome. If the same virus returns, the cell produces a guide molecule copied from that snippet, which leads a cutting enzyme, most famously one called Cas9, to the matching viral DNA and slices it apart.\n\nIn 2012, Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier showed that this system could be reprogrammed. By supplying a guide of their own design, they could direct Cas9 to cut almost any DNA sequence they chose. Because cells naturally try to repair such cuts, researchers could use them to disable a faulty gene or, with more difficulty, to rewrite it. Compared with earlier gene-editing methods, which were expensive and painstaking, CRISPR was cheap, precise and remarkably easy to use. Within a few years it had spread to laboratories across the world, and in 2020 Doudna and Charpentier were awarded the Nobel Prize in Chemistry.\n\nThe first major medical payoff arrived in late 2023, when regulators in the United Kingdom and then the United States approved a CRISPR-based treatment for sickle cell disease. In this inherited disorder, a single error in a gene that helps make hemoglobin causes red blood cells to become stiff and curved, blocking blood vessels and causing episodes of severe pain. The treatment works in an indirect but ingenious way. Doctors remove a patient's blood-forming stem cells and use CRISPR to switch off a gene that normally suppresses fetal hemoglobin, a form of the protein that the body makes before birth but stops producing in infancy. Once returned to the patient, the edited cells produce fetal hemoglobin, which compensates for the faulty adult version. In clinical trials, the vast majority of patients were free of pain crises for at least a year.\n\nYet the treatment also illustrates CRISPR's limits. It requires weeks in hospital and harsh chemotherapy to clear space in the bone marrow for the edited cells, and its price in the United States is more than two million dollars per patient. Most people with sickle cell disease live in sub-Saharan Africa and India, where such costs are far out of reach.\n\nDeeper concerns surround the editing of human embryos, since such changes would be passed on to future generations. In 2018, the Chinese scientist He Jiankui shocked the world by announcing the birth of twin girls whose embryos he had edited, supposedly to make them resistant to HIV. The experiment was condemned almost universally as reckless and unethical, and he was later sentenced to prison. The episode served as a stark reminder that the question is no longer only what CRISPR can do, but what we ought to let it do.", "passage_zh": "科學革命很少從宏大的計畫開始。1987 年，由石野良純領導的一支日本研究團隊在研究常見腸道細菌大腸桿菌的某個基因時，注意到附近有個奇怪的現象：一小段 DNA 一再重複出現，中間夾著各不相同的「間隔」序列。他們完全不知道這是做什麼用的，而在之後的十多年裡，其他人也一樣不知道。這個模式後來被取了一個相當冗長拗口、倒也恰如其分的名字：clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats（群聚且規律間隔的短回文重複序列），簡稱 CRISPR。\n\n突破來自科學家發現，這些間隔序列與感染細菌的病毒的 DNA 相符。原來，CRISPR 是一種免疫記憶。當細菌在病毒攻擊中存活下來，它會把入侵者的一小段 DNA 存進自己的基因體。如果同一種病毒再度來襲，細胞就會根據這一小段序列複製出一種引導分子，帶領一種切割酵素——其中最有名的叫做 Cas9——找到相符的病毒 DNA，並把它切斷。\n\n2012 年，珍妮佛‧道納和艾曼紐‧夏彭提耶證明了這套系統可以重新設定。只要提供自行設計的引導分子，她們就能指揮 Cas9 切開幾乎任何她們選定的 DNA 序列。由於細胞天生會設法修補這種切口，研究人員便能利用它來讓有缺陷的基因失去作用，或是——難度較高——改寫這個基因。和過去昂貴又費工的基因編輯方法相比，CRISPR 便宜、精準，而且非常容易使用。短短幾年內，它就普及到全世界的實驗室，而道納和夏彭提耶也在 2020 年獲頒諾貝爾化學獎。\n\n第一個重大的醫學成果出現在 2023 年底，英國和美國的主管機關先後核准了一種以 CRISPR 為基礎的鐮刀型貧血症療法。在這種遺傳疾病中，一個與製造血紅素有關的基因上的單一錯誤，會讓紅血球變得僵硬而彎曲，堵塞血管，引發劇烈疼痛的發作。這種療法的運作方式間接卻巧妙。醫生先取出病人的造血幹細胞，再用 CRISPR 關閉一個平常會抑制胎兒血紅素的基因；胎兒血紅素是人體在出生前製造、但到了嬰兒期就停止製造的一種血紅素。這些經過編輯的細胞送回病人體內後，就會製造胎兒血紅素，彌補有缺陷的成人血紅素。在臨床試驗中，絕大多數病人至少一年沒有再出現劇烈疼痛的發作。\n\n然而，這種療法也顯示出 CRISPR 的侷限。病人需要住院好幾週，還要接受強烈的化療，以便在骨髓中騰出空間給經過編輯的細胞；在美國，每位病人的治療費用超過兩百萬美元。而大多數鐮刀型貧血症患者住在撒哈拉以南的非洲和印度，那裡的人根本負擔不起這樣的費用。\n\n更深層的疑慮圍繞在編輯人類胚胎上，因為這種改變會遺傳給後代。2018 年，中國科學家賀建奎宣布一對雙胞胎女嬰誕生，震驚全世界；他聲稱編輯了她們的胚胎，目的是讓她們能抵抗愛滋病毒。這項實驗幾乎受到全世界一致譴責，被認為魯莽又違反倫理，他後來也被判刑入獄。這起事件清楚而嚴厲地提醒我們：問題已經不只是 CRISPR 能做什麼，而是我們應該允許它做什麼。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the first paragraph suggest about how CRISPR was discovered?", "options": ["It was the result of a long-term project to develop gene therapy.", "Its name was chosen to make it easy for the public to remember.", "It was first found in human cells in 1987.", "It was noticed by chance, long before anyone understood its purpose."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：Scientific revolutions rarely begin with a grand plan. 石野良純的團隊在研究另一個基因時才「注意到」奇怪的重複序列，而且 They had no idea what it was for... for well over a decade，可知 CRISPR 是意外發現的，過了很久才有人知道其功能。(A) 與 rarely begin with a grand plan 相反；(B) suitably unwieldy 指名稱冗長拗口，並不好記；(C) 是在大腸桿菌中發現的，不是人類細胞。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what is the natural function of CRISPR in bacteria?", "options": ["It helps bacteria digest food in the human gut.", "It allows bacteria to repair damage caused by antibiotics.", "It lets bacteria recognize and destroy viruses that have attacked them before.", "It helps bacteria exchange genes with neighboring cells."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：CRISPR, it emerged, was a kind of immune memory... If the same virus returns... slices it apart. 細菌儲存病毒的 DNA 片段，同一種病毒再次入侵時便能認出並切斷它。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How does the sickle cell treatment described in the passage work?", "options": ["It replaces the patient's red blood cells with blood from a donor.", "It edits embryos before birth so that the disease never develops.", "It directly repairs the faulty gene in every cell of the patient's body.", "It makes the patient's cells produce a type of hemoglobin normally made before birth."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：use CRISPR to switch off a gene that normally suppresses fetal hemoglobin... the edited cells produce fetal hemoglobin, which compensates for the faulty adult version. 療法是關閉抑制胎兒血紅素的基因，讓細胞重新製造出生前才有的胎兒血紅素。(A) 用的是病人自己的造血幹細胞，不是捐贈者的血；(B) 編輯胚胎是最後一段賀建奎的爭議，與此療法無關；(C) 文中說療法是 indirect（間接的），並非直接修復有缺陷的基因，而且只編輯取出的造血幹細胞。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"stark\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["clear and harsh", "quiet and easy to overlook", "confusing and unclear", "hopeful and encouraging"], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段 The episode served as a stark reminder，指賀建奎事件是一個「鮮明而嚴厲的」提醒，stark 意為明顯、嚴峻的。前文說這項實驗震驚全世界（shocked the world），並 was condemned almost universally，語氣十分嚴肅。(B) 這起事件舉世震驚，並非不起眼、容易被忽略；(C) 它傳達的訊息很清楚，並不令人困惑；(D) 文中語氣嚴肅，並非充滿希望、令人鼓舞。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention the cost of the treatment and where most patients live?", "options": ["To show that CRISPR has failed as a medical technology", "To point out that the treatment may not reach most of the people who need it", "To explain why the treatment was approved in the UK before the US", "To argue that patients in Africa and India should be treated first"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段指出這項療法在美國每人費用超過兩百萬美元，而大多數患者住在撒哈拉以南的非洲和印度，far out of reach（遠遠負擔不起）。作者藉此說明這項療法的好處可能無法照顧到大多數需要的人。(A) 作者稱它為 the first major medical payoff，並未說它失敗；(C) 文中並未說明核准先後的原因；(D) 文中沒有提出應該優先治療誰的主張。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a225", "title": "Can We Trust the Textbook? Psychology's Replication Crisis", "title_zh": "教科書還能信嗎？心理學的再現性危機", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Science", "Education"], "passage": "Science is often described as self-correcting: if a finding is wrong, other researchers will eventually try to repeat the experiment, fail, and set the record straight. For much of the twentieth century, however, few scientists bothered. Journals preferred exciting new discoveries to careful repetitions of old ones, and a researcher who spent a year confirming someone else's result could expect little credit for it. As a consequence, many findings that made their way into textbooks had been tested only once.\n\nIn 2011, that complacency was shaken. A respected psychologist published a series of experiments in a leading journal that appeared to show that people could sense future events before they happened. The paper followed the standard methods of the field to the letter. That was precisely the problem: if accepted methods could \"prove\" something that almost no scientist believed possible, how much confidence could be placed in everything else those methods had produced?\n\nA few years later, a group of some 270 researchers led by Brian Nosek of the University of Virginia set out to answer that question directly. They tried to repeat 100 studies published in 2008 in three major psychology journals, often consulting the original authors to make sure that the experiments were reproduced faithfully. The results, published in the journal Science in 2015, were sobering. Although 97 percent of the original studies had reported statistically significant results, only 36 percent of the repetitions did, and on average the effects they found were about half as strong. Studies in social psychology fared especially badly.\n\nThe causes were less a matter of fraud than of everyday habits. Many studies relied on small samples, which make chance results more likely. Researchers, often without realizing it, made dozens of small decisions, such as which participants to exclude, which measurements to report and when to stop collecting data, that nudged their results toward significance. Journals, in turn, rarely published studies that found nothing, so unsuccessful experiments disappeared into file drawers while lucky flukes were printed and cited.\n\nPsychology's response has been, in many ways, admirable. Increasingly, researchers \"preregister\" their studies, publicly recording their hypotheses and planned analyses before collecting any data so that they cannot quietly shift the goalposts afterward. Some journals now agree to publish a study on the basis of its design, whatever the outcome. Large teams spread across dozens of countries pool their participants to achieve samples far bigger than any single laboratory could gather.\n\nNor is the problem unique to psychology. Similar concerns have been raised in fields ranging from cancer biology to economics, and psychologists deserve some credit for having confronted the issue openly. For students, the lesson is not that science is untrustworthy, but that any single study, however eye-catching, is a starting point rather than a conclusion. Science corrects itself only when scientists take the trouble to check.", "passage_zh": "科學常被形容為能夠自我修正：如果某個發現是錯的，其他研究者遲早會嘗試重做實驗、失敗，然後還原真相。然而，在二十世紀的大部分時間裡，很少有科學家願意費這個工夫。期刊偏好令人興奮的新發現，而不是仔細重做舊的實驗；一個花了一年時間確認別人研究結果的研究者，幾乎得不到什麼肯定。結果，許多寫進教科書的發現，其實只被檢驗過一次。\n\n2011 年，這種自滿受到了衝擊。一位備受敬重的心理學家在一份頂尖期刊上發表了一系列實驗，似乎顯示人能在未來的事件發生之前就感知到它們。這篇論文完全遵照該領域的標準方法進行。問題正出在這裡：如果公認的方法可以「證明」一件幾乎沒有科學家相信可能發生的事，那麼這些方法所產生的其他所有結果，又有多少可信度呢？\n\n幾年後，由維吉尼亞大學的布萊恩‧諾塞克領導、大約 270 名研究者組成的團隊，決定直接回答這個問題。他們嘗試重做 2008 年發表在三份主要心理學期刊上的 100 項研究，並經常諮詢原作者，以確保實驗被忠實重現。2015 年發表在《科學》期刊上的結果令人警醒。雖然原始研究中有 97% 報告了統計上顯著的結果，但重做的研究中只有 36% 得到顯著結果，而且平均來說，它們發現的效果大約只有原本的一半強。社會心理學的研究表現尤其差。\n\n造成問題的原因，與其說是造假，不如說是日常的研究習慣。許多研究依賴小樣本，而小樣本讓偶然出現的結果更容易發生。研究者常常在不自覺的情況下做出幾十個小決定，例如要排除哪些受試者、要報告哪些測量結果、何時停止蒐集資料，而這些決定把結果一點一點推向顯著。期刊則很少刊登什麼都沒發現的研究，於是不成功的實驗消失在抽屜裡，僥倖得到的結果卻被刊出並被引用。\n\n心理學界的回應，在許多方面都值得肯定。越來越多研究者會「預先登記」他們的研究，也就是在蒐集任何資料之前，就公開記錄他們的假設和預定的分析方法，讓自己事後無法偷偷更改標準。有些期刊現在會根據研究的設計決定是否刊登，不論結果如何。分布在數十個國家的大型團隊也會集合各自的受試者，達到任何單一實驗室都無法蒐集到的大樣本。\n\n這個問題也不是心理學獨有的。從癌症生物學到經濟學，許多領域都出現了類似的疑慮，而心理學家公開面對這個問題，值得給予一些肯定。對學生來說，這個教訓並不是科學不可信，而是任何單一研究，無論多麼引人注目，都只是起點，而不是結論。只有當科學家願意費心檢查時，科學才會自我修正。", "questions": [{"q": "The word \"complacency\" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["careless satisfaction with the way things are", "strong disagreement among experts", "fear of making mistakes in public", "eagerness to try new methods"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段說期刊偏好新發現、重做實驗的人得不到肯定，所以很少科學家去檢驗舊結果；第二段 that complacency 即指這種「滿足於現狀、疏於檢查」的態度。(B) 文中沒有提到專家之間意見分歧；(C)(D) 都與當時不願重做實驗的態度不符。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the 2011 paper worry many psychologists?", "options": ["Its author admitted to inventing his data.", "It used methods that were very different from normal practice.", "It proved beyond doubt that future events can be predicted.", "It reached an unbelievable conclusion by following the field's standard methods."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：The paper followed the standard methods of the field to the letter. That was precisely the problem: if accepted methods could \"prove\" something that almost no scientist believed possible... 這篇論文用標準方法得出令人難以置信的結論，讓人懷疑這些方法本身是否可靠。(A) 文中未說作者造假；(B) 正好相反，論文完全遵照標準方法；(C) prove 加上引號，表示作者並不認為真的證明了。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the fourth paragraph, which of the following is NOT presented as a main cause of the problem?", "options": ["Studies with too few participants", "Researchers' small, often unconscious choices during analysis", "Journals' reluctance to publish studies that found nothing", "Deliberate fraud by researchers"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The causes were less a matter of fraud than of everyday habits. 作者明言問題主要不是造假，而是日常的研究習慣。(A) 小樣本、(B) 研究者不自覺的分析決定、(C) 期刊不刊登沒有發現的研究，都是第四段列出的原因。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What does the phrase \"shift the goalposts\" in the fifth paragraph refer to?", "options": ["Moving their laboratories to other countries", "Changing their hypotheses or analyses after seeing the data", "Giving up research when the results are disappointing", "Asking journals to publish their studies faster"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說研究者在蒐集資料之前就公開登記假設和分析方法，so that they cannot quietly shift the goalposts afterward，可知 shift the goalposts 指看到資料之後才偷偷更改假設或分析方式（如同比賽中移動球門）。(A)(C)(D) 皆與預先登記的目的無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's main message in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Psychology is less scientific than biology and economics.", "Students should not believe anything they read in textbooks.", "A single study should be treated with caution, and checking results is essential.", "The replication crisis has now been completely solved."], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一段：any single study, however eye-catching, is a starting point rather than a conclusion. Science corrects itself only when scientists take the trouble to check. 作者提醒單一研究應謹慎看待，而檢驗結果不可或缺。(A) 作者說其他領域也有類似問題，並未說心理學比較不科學；(B) 作者明言 the lesson is not that science is untrustworthy；(D) 文中只說心理學界正在改進，並未說問題已完全解決。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a226", "title": "Twice as Many Every Two Years: The Rise and Slowdown of Moore's Law", "title_zh": "每兩年翻一倍：摩爾定律的崛起與減速", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Technology", "Business"], "passage": "In April 1965, an engineer named Gordon Moore was asked by a trade magazine to predict what would happen in the electronics industry over the next ten years. Looking at the few years of data then available, he noticed that the number of components that could be squeezed onto a single chip had been doubling roughly every year. Assuming the trend would continue, he forecast that chips would contain around 65,000 components by 1975, a figure that struck many readers as absurd. Moore himself later admitted that the prediction had been \"a wild extrapolation.\"\n\nRemarkably, it turned out to be roughly right. In 1975, Moore, by then a co-founder of Intel, revised his estimate to a doubling every two years, and that pace held with surprising consistency for decades. Intel's first microprocessor, released in 1971, contained about 2,300 transistors; the most advanced chips today contain tens of billions. Few predictions in the history of technology have proved so durable, and the observation came to be known as Moore's law, although it was never a law of nature but a description of what engineers and investors managed to achieve.\n\nIn fact, Moore's law was partly self-fulfilling. Because the entire industry expected chips to improve at this rate, companies planned their research, their factories and their products around it, investing whatever was necessary to keep up. Falling behind the curve meant losing customers to competitors who had not. The prediction thus became a target, and the target drove the very progress it had predicted.\n\nTaiwan has played a central part in this story. In 1987, Morris Chang founded the Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company (TSMC) on what was then an unusual idea: instead of designing its own chips, it would manufacture chips designed by other companies. The model allowed firms such as Apple and Nvidia to concentrate on design without building factories that now cost tens of billions of dollars each. TSMC today produces the great majority of the world's most advanced chips, which is one reason Taiwan is so often described as indispensable to the global economy.\n\nYet the law is running into the limits of physics. Some parts of today's transistors are only a few dozen atoms across, and at such scales electrons can \"tunnel\" through barriers that are supposed to block them, wasting power and generating heat. Since the mid-2000s, the speed at which processors run has barely increased, because pushing it higher would make chips too hot to cool. Even the names of new manufacturing processes, such as \"3 nanometers,\" no longer refer to any actual measurement on the chip; they are essentially marketing labels.\n\nEngineers have responded with ingenuity rather than surrender. Instead of merely making transistors smaller, they now stack them vertically, combine several smaller chips into a single package, and design specialized processors for tasks such as artificial intelligence. Whether one calls this the continuation of Moore's law or the start of something new is partly a matter of definition. What is certain is that the steady, almost automatic improvement that a generation of consumers took for granted can no longer be assumed.", "passage_zh": "1965 年 4 月，一位名叫高登‧摩爾的工程師應一本業界雜誌之邀，預測電子產業在接下來十年會有什麼發展。他觀察當時僅有的幾年資料，注意到單一晶片上能塞進的元件數量，大約每年就增加一倍。假設這個趨勢持續下去，他預測到了 1975 年，一片晶片將容納大約六萬五千個元件——許多讀者都覺得這個數字荒謬至極。摩爾自己後來也承認，這個預測是「天馬行空的推斷」。\n\n令人驚訝的是，這個預測大致上是對的。1975 年，已經成為英特爾共同創辦人的摩爾，把他的估計修正為每兩年增加一倍，而這個速度在之後數十年間維持得出奇穩定。英特爾在 1971 年推出的第一款微處理器大約只有 2,300 個電晶體；而今天最先進的晶片則有數百億個。在科技史上，很少有預測能經得起這麼長時間的考驗。這個觀察後來被稱為「摩爾定律」，雖然它從來不是自然定律，而是描述工程師和投資人努力達成的成果。\n\n事實上，摩爾定律有一部分是自我實現的。由於整個產業都預期晶片會以這個速度進步，各家公司便依此規劃研究、工廠和產品，並投入一切必要的資金以跟上腳步。落後於這條曲線，就意味著顧客會被沒有落後的競爭對手搶走。於是，這個預測變成了目標，而這個目標又推動了它所預測的進步。\n\n台灣在這段故事中扮演了核心角色。1987 年，張忠謀以一個在當時相當罕見的構想創立了台灣積體電路製造公司（台積電）：公司不設計自己的晶片，而是替其他公司製造他們設計的晶片。這種模式讓蘋果和輝達等公司能夠專心設計，不必自己興建如今每座造價高達數百億美元的工廠。今天，全世界最先進的晶片絕大多數由台積電生產，這也是台灣常被形容為全球經濟中不可或缺的原因之一。\n\n然而，這條定律正逐漸碰上物理的極限。現今電晶體的某些部分，寬度只有幾十個原子；在這麼小的尺度下，電子會「穿隧」通過原本應該擋住它們的屏障，浪費電力並產生熱。從 2000 年代中期以來，處理器的時脈速度幾乎沒有再提高，因為再往上推，晶片就會熱到無法散熱。就連新製程的名稱，例如「3 奈米」，也已經不再對應晶片上任何實際的尺寸，基本上只是行銷用的標籤。\n\n工程師以巧思回應，而不是就此投降。他們不再只是把電晶體做得更小，而是把電晶體垂直堆疊、把幾顆較小的晶片整合在同一個封裝裡，並為人工智慧等特定任務設計專用的處理器。要把這稱為摩爾定律的延續，還是某種新事物的開端，有一部分取決於如何定義。可以確定的是，一整個世代的消費者視為理所當然、穩定且幾乎自動發生的進步，已經不能再被視為必然。", "questions": [{"q": "Why did many readers find Moore's 1965 forecast \"absurd\"?", "options": ["Moore had no experience in the electronics industry.", "The number he predicted seemed impossibly large at the time.", "He predicted that chip production would stop by 1975.", "The magazine had published many false predictions before."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：he forecast that chips would contain around 65,000 components by 1975, a figure that struck many readers as absurd. 當時一片晶片上的元件還很少，六萬五千這個數字在讀者看來大得不可能。(A) 摩爾本身就是工程師；(C) 他預測的是元件數量增加，不是停止生產；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by saying that Moore's law was \"partly self-fulfilling\"?", "options": ["Moore secretly controlled the companies that made chips.", "The law was based on a natural property of silicon.", "The industry's belief in the prediction pushed it to make the prediction come true.", "Chips kept improving by themselves without any investment."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說明 self-fulfilling：整個產業都預期晶片會以這個速度進步，於是依此規劃並大量投資，The prediction thus became a target, and the target drove the very progress it had predicted. 也就是業界相信這個預測，反而努力讓它成真。(A) 文中未提及；(B) 第二段明言它 was never a law of nature；(D) 與 investing whatever was necessary 矛盾。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what was unusual about TSMC's business model when the company was founded?", "options": ["It would manufacture chips designed by other companies instead of its own.", "It would build all of its factories outside Taiwan.", "It would design chips but leave the manufacturing to others.", "It would sell chips only to Taiwanese companies."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：instead of designing its own chips, it would manufacture chips designed by other companies. 台積電不設計自己的晶片，而是替其他公司製造它們設計的晶片。(B)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 與原文正好相反，專心設計的是蘋果、輝達等客戶。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In the fifth paragraph, the word \"they\" in \"they are essentially marketing labels\" refers to ______.", "options": ["transistors", "electrons", "processors", "the names of manufacturing processes"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段 Even the names of new manufacturing processes, such as \"3 nanometers,\" no longer refer to any actual measurement on the chip; they are essentially marketing labels. 前半句的主詞是 the names of new manufacturing processes，they 即指這些製程名稱。(A)(B)(C) 都不是被稱為行銷標籤的東西。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which best describes the tone of the last paragraph?", "options": ["Balanced, noting both engineers' creativity and the end of easy progress", "Mocking, suggesting that engineers have wasted their efforts", "Alarmed, warning that the computer industry is about to collapse", "Nostalgic, wishing for the days before computers existed"], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段先肯定工程師 responded with ingenuity rather than surrender（以巧思回應而非投降），再指出過去穩定、幾乎自動的進步 can no longer be assumed（不能再被視為理所當然），語氣持平，兼顧兩面。(B) 作者肯定工程師的努力，沒有嘲諷之意；(C) 作者並未說產業即將崩潰；(D) 文中沒有懷念電腦出現之前的時代。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a227", "title": "The Scientist Who Would Not Give Up on mRNA", "title_zh": "不肯放棄 mRNA 的科學家", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "When the first COVID-19 vaccines were authorized in December 2020, less than a year after the virus had been identified, many people assumed that the technology behind them had been developed in a hurry. In reality, the two front-runners rested on decades of unglamorous work, much of it carried out by a Hungarian-born biochemist named Katalin Karikó, whose ideas had been ignored for most of her career.\n\nThe principle she was pursuing was elegant. Our cells constantly use molecules called messenger RNA, or mRNA, as temporary instructions: each one carries a recipe copied from a gene to the cell's protein-making machinery. If scientists could deliver mRNA of their own design into the body, Karikó reasoned, they could turn a patient's cells into tiny factories, producing whatever protein was needed, perhaps a missing enzyme or a harmless piece of a virus that would teach the immune system to recognize the real thing.\n\nThe obstacles were formidable. Synthetic mRNA was fragile and, worse, when injected into animals it triggered violent inflammation, as the immune system mistook it for an invader. Funding agencies were unconvinced, and in the mid-1990s, after repeated grant rejections, Karikó was demoted at the University of Pennsylvania. Most scientists would have moved on to a more promising field. She did not.\n\nHer persistence paid off after a chance meeting at a photocopier with the immunologist Drew Weissman, who was interested in developing vaccines. Working together, they discovered why synthetic mRNA set off such an alarm. Natural RNA in our cells contains chemically modified building blocks, while the version made in the laboratory did not. When they swapped one building block, uridine, for a modified form called pseudouridine, the inflammation largely disappeared, and later work showed that the modified mRNA also produced far more of the desired protein. Their key paper appeared in 2005. It attracted little attention.\n\nOne further problem remained: how to get the fragile molecules into cells intact. The answer came from other researchers, who developed tiny bubbles of fat, known as lipid nanoparticles, to wrap and protect the mRNA. By the late 2010s, companies such as BioNTech and Moderna were testing the approach against cancer and infectious diseases. When the genetic code of the new coronavirus was published in January 2020, they were able to design vaccine candidates within days.\n\nIn 2023, Karikó and Weissman were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Karikó's story is sometimes told as a simple tale of a lone genius proven right, but it also raises uncomfortable questions about how science is funded. Systems that reward safe, fashionable projects may overlook ideas that need many years to mature. How many other promising lines of research, one wonders, have been abandoned not because they were wrong, but because they were early?", "passage_zh": "2020 年 12 月，第一批新冠肺炎疫苗獲得授權使用，距離這種病毒被確認還不到一年，因此許多人以為疫苗背後的技術是倉促開發出來的。事實上，最領先的兩款疫苗是建立在數十年默默無聞的研究之上，而其中許多研究是由一位出生於匈牙利的生化學家卡塔林‧卡莉科完成的；在她職業生涯的大部分時間裡，她的想法都被忽視。\n\n她所追求的原理相當巧妙。我們的細胞不斷使用一種叫做傳訊 RNA（mRNA）的分子作為暫時的指令：每一個 mRNA 都帶著一份從基因複製而來的配方，送到細胞製造蛋白質的機器那裡。卡莉科推論，如果科學家能把自己設計的 mRNA 送進人體，就能把病人的細胞變成小型工廠，製造出任何需要的蛋白質——也許是一種缺少的酵素，或是病毒身上一小段無害的部分，讓免疫系統學會辨認真正的病毒。\n\n但阻礙非常大。人工合成的 mRNA 很脆弱，更糟的是，把它注射到動物體內時會引發劇烈的發炎反應，因為免疫系統把它誤認為入侵者。提供研究經費的機構並不買帳；1990 年代中期，在經費申請一再被拒之後，卡莉科在賓州大學遭到降職。大多數科學家會轉往更有前景的領域，但她沒有。\n\n她的堅持在一次影印機旁的偶遇之後得到了回報。她遇見了有意研發疫苗的免疫學家德魯‧魏斯曼。兩人合作，找出了人工合成的 mRNA 為什麼會引發這麼強烈的警報。我們細胞中的天然 RNA 含有經過化學修飾的組成單元，但實驗室製造的版本沒有。當他們把其中一種組成單元「尿苷」換成經過修飾的「假尿苷」時，發炎反應大多消失了；後來的研究也發現，經過修飾的 mRNA 能讓細胞製造出多得多的目標蛋白質。他們的關鍵論文在 2005 年發表，卻幾乎沒有引起注意。\n\n還剩下一個問題：如何讓脆弱的分子完整地進入細胞。答案來自其他研究者，他們開發出一種叫做脂質奈米顆粒的微小脂肪泡泡，用來包覆並保護 mRNA。到了 2010 年代後期，BioNTech 和莫德納等公司已經在測試用這種方法對抗癌症和傳染病。當新型冠狀病毒的基因序列在 2020 年 1 月公布時，他們在短短幾天內就設計出了候選疫苗。\n\n2023 年，卡莉科和魏斯曼獲頒諾貝爾生理學或醫學獎。卡莉科的經歷有時被說成一個簡單的故事：孤獨的天才最終被證明是對的。但這段經歷也引出了一些令人不安的問題：科學研究的經費究竟是如何分配的？偏好穩當、熱門研究計畫的制度，可能會忽略那些需要許多年才能成熟的構想。不禁令人想問：還有多少有前景的研究方向被放棄，不是因為它們錯了，而是因為它們出現得太早？", "questions": [{"q": "What mistaken belief does the first paragraph correct?", "options": ["That the first COVID-19 vaccines were not effective", "That Katalin Karikó was born in the United States", "That the technology behind the first COVID-19 vaccines was developed very quickly", "That the virus was first identified in December 2020"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：many people assumed that the technology behind them had been developed in a hurry. In reality, the two front-runners rested on decades of unglamorous work. 作者要糾正的是「疫苗技術是倉促開發出來的」這個誤解。(A) 文中並未質疑疫苗的效果；(B) 卡莉科出生於匈牙利，文中也沒有說一般人誤以為她生於美國；(D) 2020 年 12 月是疫苗獲得授權的時間，不是病毒被確認的時間。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what was the main problem with early synthetic mRNA?", "options": ["It was fragile and caused severe inflammation in the body.", "It could only be made in Hungarian laboratories.", "It was too expensive to produce in large amounts.", "It produced too much protein too quickly."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Synthetic mRNA was fragile and, worse, when injected into animals it triggered violent inflammation. 人工合成的 mRNA 很脆弱，又會引發劇烈發炎。(B)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第四段說修飾後的 mRNA 能製造更多蛋白質，這是優點而非問題。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the short sentence \"She did not\" at the end of the third paragraph emphasize?", "options": ["Karikó's determination to continue despite setbacks", "Karikó's lack of interest in other fields of science", "Karikó's refusal to work with other scientists", "Karikó's decision to leave the university"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段末 Most scientists would have moved on to a more promising field. She did not. 簡短的 She did not 與「大多數科學家會轉換領域」形成對比，強調卡莉科在經費被拒、遭到降職之後仍不放棄的決心。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 第四段她還與魏斯曼合作，與此相反；(D) 她並沒有離開這項研究。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the 2005 paper from the sentence \"It attracted little attention\"?", "options": ["It contained serious errors that were later corrected.", "It was published in a newspaper rather than a scientific journal.", "Its importance was not widely recognized until much later.", "Weissman did not want other scientists to read it."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說這篇 2005 年的關鍵論文 attracted little attention，而它的發現後來成為新冠疫苗的基礎，兩人並在 2023 年獲得諾貝爾獎，可推知這篇論文的重要性過了很久才被廣泛認可。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆無根據。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What point does the writer make in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Nobel Prizes should be given only to scientists who work alone.", "Fashionable research projects are always more successful in the end.", "Funding agencies were right to reject Karikó's grant applications.", "Research systems may fail to support valuable ideas that take a long time to develop."], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段：Systems that reward safe, fashionable projects may overlook ideas that need many years to mature. 作者指出偏好穩當、熱門研究的經費制度，可能忽略需要長時間才能成熟的好構想。(A) 作者反而認為「孤獨天才」的說法太過簡化，而且獎項是兩人合得的；(B) 與文意相反；(C) 作者對拒絕給予經費的制度提出質疑，並非表示贊同。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a228", "title": "One Dollar for a Lie: The Uncomfortable Logic of Cognitive Dissonance", "title_zh": "一美元的謊言：認知失調令人不安的邏輯", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Science", "Life"], "passage": "Imagine spending an hour on the dullest task you can think of: placing spools on a tray, emptying the tray and filling it again, then turning dozens of square pegs a quarter turn, one after another. This is what a group of male students at Stanford University were asked to do in a 1959 experiment. When they had finished, the researcher made an unusual request. The next participant, a young woman, was waiting outside, and the assistant who normally briefed newcomers was absent; would the student mind telling her that the task had been interesting and fun?\n\nSome students were offered one dollar to tell this lie; others were offered twenty, a considerable sum at the time. Afterward, all of them were asked how much they had actually enjoyed the task. Common sense suggests that the better-paid group, having been more generously rewarded, would remember the experience more fondly. The opposite happened. Those who had been paid only one dollar rated the task as significantly more enjoyable than those who had received twenty.\n\nThe psychologists behind the study, Leon Festinger and James Carlsmith, explained the result with a theory Festinger had proposed two years earlier. When we hold two beliefs that clash, or when our behavior clashes with our beliefs, we experience an unpleasant tension that Festinger called cognitive dissonance, and we are motivated to reduce it. Students paid twenty dollars had a perfectly good reason for lying: the money. Those paid one dollar did not. Unable to justify their behavior by pointing to the reward, they resolved the tension in another way, by persuading themselves that the task had not been so boring after all.\n\nFestinger had observed a more dramatic version of the same process a few years earlier, when he and his colleagues quietly joined a small group in the American Midwest whose leader had predicted that a great flood would destroy the world on a particular day in December 1954. When the day passed uneventfully, some of the most committed members did not abandon their beliefs. Instead, they announced that their faith had saved the world and began, for the first time, actively trying to win new followers. Having given up jobs, possessions and relationships for the prophecy, they found it easier to reinterpret reality than to admit they had been wrong.\n\nDissonance helps explain many familiar habits. A student who has worked hard to get into a particular school may become convinced that it is far better than its rivals. A smoker who knows the risks may insist that the evidence is exaggerated. A shopper who has just paid a great deal for a new phone tends to seek out favorable reviews and ignore critical ones. In each case, the mind protects us not from the truth but from the discomfort of admitting that a choice we made was a poor one.\n\nYet dissonance need not only lead us astray. Some psychologists have used it to encourage more responsible behavior, for instance by having people publicly urge others to save water and then reminding them of times they had wasted it, which led them to take shorter showers. Understanding the mechanism also offers a modest form of self-defense. When you find yourself defending a decision with unusual passion, it may be worth asking whether you are responding to the evidence, or simply to the cost of having been wrong.", "passage_zh": "想像你花一個小時做一件你想得到最無聊的工作：把線軸放到托盤上，倒空托盤，再重新裝滿；接著把幾十根方形木栓一根接一根地轉四分之一圈。這就是 1959 年一項實驗中，一群史丹佛大學男學生被要求做的事。他們做完之後，研究人員提出了一個不尋常的請求：下一位受試者——一位年輕女子——正在外面等候，而平常負責向新受試者說明的助理今天剛好不在；學生是否願意告訴她，這項工作既有趣又好玩？\n\n有些學生得到一美元來說這個謊，其他人則得到二十美元，這在當時是一筆可觀的金額。之後，所有人都被問到他們實際上有多喜歡這項工作。照常理推想，報酬較高的那一組既然獲得比較優渥的獎賞，對這段經驗的印象應該會比較好。結果卻正好相反：只拿到一美元的人，給這項工作的有趣程度評分明顯高於拿到二十美元的人。\n\n進行這項研究的心理學家利昂‧費斯汀格和詹姆斯‧卡爾史密斯，用費斯汀格兩年前提出的理論來解釋這個結果。當我們同時抱持兩個互相衝突的信念，或是當我們的行為與信念相衝突時，就會感到一種不愉快的緊張，費斯汀格稱之為「認知失調」，而我們會有動機去降低它。拿到二十美元的學生有一個再好不過的說謊理由：錢。拿到一美元的學生卻沒有。由於無法用報酬來合理化自己的行為，他們用另一種方式化解這種緊張：說服自己這項工作其實沒那麼無聊。\n\n幾年前，費斯汀格就曾觀察到同一種心理過程更戲劇化的版本。當時他和同事悄悄加入美國中西部的一個小團體，該團體的領袖預言，一場大洪水會在 1954 年 12 月的某一天毀滅世界。當那一天平靜地過去之後，一些最投入的成員並沒有放棄他們的信念。相反地，他們宣稱是他們的信仰拯救了世界，並且第一次開始積極地吸收新信徒。這些人為了這個預言放棄了工作、財產和人際關係，對他們來說，重新詮釋現實比承認自己錯了更容易。\n\n認知失調可以解釋許多常見的習慣。一個努力考進某所學校的學生，可能會深信這所學校遠比其他學校好。一個知道風險的吸菸者，可能會堅稱相關證據被誇大了。一個剛花大錢買了新手機的消費者，往往會去找好評，而忽略負評。在這些情況中，我們的心智保護我們免於面對的，並不是真相，而是不得不承認自己做了錯誤選擇的那種不舒服。\n\n然而，認知失調不一定只會讓我們誤入歧途。有些心理學家曾利用它來鼓勵更負責任的行為，例如請人公開呼籲別人節約用水，再提醒他們自己曾經浪費水的時候，結果這些人洗澡的時間變短了。了解這個機制也能提供一種小小的自我防衛。當你發現自己以異常的熱情為某個決定辯護時，或許值得問問自己：你是在回應證據，還是只是在回應「承認自己錯了」所要付出的代價？", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer mention what \"common sense suggests\" in the second paragraph?", "options": ["To show that the result of the experiment went against what most people would expect", "To prove that money always improves people's memories", "To criticize the students for being greedy", "To explain why twenty dollars was a large amount of money"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Common sense suggests that the better-paid group... would remember the experience more fondly. The opposite happened. 作者先提出一般人依常識的預期，再說結果正好相反，目的是凸顯實驗結果違反多數人的預期。(B) 文中並未討論記憶力；(C) 作者沒有批評學生貪心；(D) 二十美元是一筆大錢只是附帶說明，並非提到常識的目的。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to cognitive dissonance theory, why did the one-dollar group rate the task as more enjoyable?", "options": ["They were less intelligent than the twenty-dollar group.", "They lacked an outside reason for lying, so they changed their attitude instead.", "They had actually been given a more interesting task.", "They hoped to be paid more if they gave a high rating."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Those paid one dollar did not [have a good reason for lying]. Unable to justify their behavior by pointing to the reward, they resolved the tension... by persuading themselves that the task had not been so boring after all. 一美元的學生缺乏說謊的外在理由，只好改變自己對這項工作的態度。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 兩組做的是同樣無聊的工作。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What happened after the predicted flood failed to occur?", "options": ["All the members admitted they had been wrong and left the group.", "Some committed members became even more eager to spread their beliefs.", "The group's leader blamed Festinger for the failure.", "The members returned to their old jobs and relationships."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：some of the most committed members did not abandon their beliefs. Instead, they announced that their faith had saved the world and began, for the first time, actively trying to win new followers. 一些最投入的成員反而更積極地傳播信仰。(A) 與原文相反；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 文中說他們早已為預言放棄工作和人際關係，並未說他們回到原來的生活。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"lead us astray\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["cause us to make mistakes", "help us find the truth", "make us travel far from home", "make us feel guilty about our choices"], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段 Yet dissonance need not only lead us astray 之後，舉出利用認知失調鼓勵節約用水的正面例子，與前一段那些自我欺騙的例子相對，可知 lead us astray 指「使我們犯錯、誤入歧途」。(B) 意思正好相反；(C) 是 astray「走離正路」的字面聯想，不合文意；(D) 認知失調雖然令人不舒服，但 lead us astray 指把我們引向錯誤，而不是讓我們感到內疚。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following situations is the best example of cognitive dissonance at work?", "options": ["After getting a low test score, a student decides to study harder.", "A girl compares two similar backpacks and buys the cheaper one.", "After paying a lot for a dull concert, a man convinces himself it was one of the best nights of his life.", "A boy asks his teacher to explain a difficult math problem."], "answer": 2, "explain": "認知失調指行為與信念衝突時，人會改變想法來減輕不適，例如第五段花大錢買手機的人只去找好評。(C) 花了大錢卻聽到一場無聊的音樂會，為了不承認錢白花了，便說服自己那是人生中最美好的夜晚之一，正是認知失調的例子。(A) 考差後更用功是一般的行為調整；(B) 比較後買便宜的背包只是合理的選擇；(D) 向老師請教問題與信念衝突無關。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a229", "title": "Two Minds in One Head? What Split-Brain Patients Revealed", "title_zh": "一顆頭裡有兩個心智？裂腦病人揭露的祕密", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Science", "Health"], "passage": "The human brain is divided into two hemispheres, linked by a thick band of some 200 million nerve fibers called the corpus callosum. For a long time, the purpose of this bridge was a mystery. In the 1940s, surgeons began cutting it in a handful of patients with severe epilepsy, hoping to stop seizures from spreading from one side of the brain to the other. The operation often worked, and to the doctors' surprise, the patients seemed largely unchanged. They talked, walked and went about their lives much as before.\n\nIt took carefully designed experiments to show that something extraordinary had in fact happened. Beginning in the early 1960s, the neurobiologist Roger Sperry and his young graduate student Michael Gazzaniga, working at the California Institute of Technology, took advantage of a quirk in the body's wiring. Information from the left half of what we see goes to the right hemisphere, while information from the right half goes to the left; similarly, each hemisphere controls the hand on the opposite side. In most people, moreover, language is handled mainly by the left hemisphere. By flashing images briefly to one side of a patient's field of vision, the researchers could communicate with each hemisphere separately.\n\nThe results were startling. When a picture of a spoon was shown to the left hemisphere, the patient could easily name it. When the same picture was shown to the right hemisphere, the patient insisted that he had seen nothing at all, yet if asked to reach under a screen and pick out the matching object by touch, his left hand would promptly select the spoon. One half of the brain knew something that the other half, the half able to speak, did not.\n\nSome of the most revealing findings came later. In one famous test, Gazzaniga showed a patient's left hemisphere a picture of a chicken's foot and his right hemisphere a snowy scene, then asked him to choose related pictures from a set of cards. His right hand pointed to a chicken; his left hand pointed to a shovel. When asked why, the patient, speaking from his left hemisphere, which knew nothing about the snow, answered without hesitation: the chicken claw went with the chicken, and you need a shovel to clean out the chicken shed.\n\nGazzaniga concluded that the left hemisphere contains what he called an \"interpreter,\" a system that constantly produces explanations for our actions and feelings, even when it has no access to their real causes. Crucially, the patient was not lying; his explanation simply felt true to him. Many researchers suspect that the same process operates in all of us, smoothly turning scattered mental activity into a single, coherent story about who we are and why we do what we do.\n\nSperry shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1981 for this work, and the experiments raised questions that philosophers still debate. Does a split-brain patient have two minds? Is our own sense of being a single, unified self partly an illusion constructed after the fact? Today the operation is rarely performed, since better drugs and more targeted surgery have become available. But the handful of patients who agreed to be studied left behind a lasting insight: the voice in our heads that explains our behavior may not always know as much as it sounds.", "passage_zh": "人類的大腦分成兩個半球，由一條含有約兩億條神經纖維、名為胼胝體的粗帶連接。很長一段時間，這座橋梁的用途一直是個謎。1940 年代，外科醫生開始為少數嚴重癲癇的病人切斷胼胝體，希望阻止癲癇發作從大腦的一側擴散到另一側。手術往往有效，而且令醫生驚訝的是，病人似乎大致沒有改變。他們能說話、走路，生活也和從前差不多。\n\n要靠精心設計的實驗，才能顯示其實發生了非比尋常的變化。從 1960 年代初期開始，神經生物學家羅傑‧斯佩利和他年輕的研究生麥可‧葛詹尼加在加州理工學院，利用了人體神經連接方式的一個特點：我們視野左半邊的資訊會傳到右半腦，右半邊的資訊則傳到左半腦；同樣地，每個半腦控制的是對側的手。此外，大多數人的語言主要由左半腦負責。研究人員只要把圖片短暫地閃現在病人視野的一側，就能分別和每一個半腦溝通。\n\n結果令人震驚。當一張湯匙的圖片呈現給左半腦時，病人能輕易說出它的名稱。當同一張圖片呈現給右半腦時，病人卻堅稱自己什麼都沒看到——然而，如果請他把手伸到隔板下方，憑觸覺挑出相符的物品，他的左手會立刻選出湯匙。大腦的一半知道某件事，而另一半，也就是能說話的那一半，卻不知道。\n\n一些最具啟發性的發現出現得更晚。在一項著名的測試中，葛詹尼加讓病人的左半腦看一張雞爪的圖片，讓右半腦看一幅雪景，然後請他從一組卡片中挑出相關的圖片。他的右手指向一隻雞，左手則指向一把鏟子。被問到原因時，病人透過對雪景一無所知的左半腦發言，毫不猶豫地回答：雞爪和雞是一組的，而清理雞舍需要一把鏟子。\n\n葛詹尼加的結論是，左半腦中有一個他稱為「詮釋者」的系統：它會不斷為我們的行為和感受提出解釋，即使它根本無從得知真正的原因。關鍵在於，這位病人並沒有說謊；他的解釋在他自己聽來就是真的。許多研究者懷疑，同樣的過程也在我們每個人身上運作，順暢地把零散的心智活動，轉化成一個關於我們是誰、為什麼會做這些事的完整而連貫的故事。\n\n斯佩利因這項研究在 1981 年與他人共同獲得諾貝爾生理學或醫學獎，而這些實驗所引發的問題，哲學家至今仍在爭論。裂腦病人有兩個心智嗎？我們覺得自己是一個單一、完整的自我，這種感覺是否有一部分是事後建構出來的錯覺？如今，由於有了更好的藥物和更精準的手術，這種手術已經很少進行。但那些同意接受研究的少數病人留下了一個深遠的啟示：我們腦中那個解釋自己行為的聲音，所知道的未必像它聽起來那麼多。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the second paragraph suggest by saying that \"something extraordinary had in fact happened\"?", "options": ["The operation had failed to stop the patients' seizures.", "The surgeons had made serious mistakes during the operation.", "The patients had become better at speaking than before.", "The patients' normal everyday behavior hid important effects of the surgery."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段說病人術後 seemed largely unchanged（似乎大致沒有改變），第二段卻說 It took carefully designed experiments to show that something extraordinary had in fact happened，表示病人日常的正常表現掩蓋了手術造成的重要影響，要靠精心設計的實驗才看得出來。(A) 第一段說手術往往有效；(B) 文中沒有說醫生犯錯；(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why could the patient not name the spoon when its picture was shown to his right hemisphere?", "options": ["The right hemisphere is unable to process images.", "His left hand was hidden under a screen.", "The picture was flashed too quickly for either hemisphere to see.", "The right hemisphere saw it, but the left hemisphere, which controls speech, did not."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段說明語言主要由左半腦負責，而兩個半腦之間的連結已被切斷；第三段圖片只呈現給右半腦時，病人說沒看到，卻能用左手挑出湯匙。可見右半腦看到了，但負責說話的左半腦不知道。(A) 右半腦能辨認圖片，左手才能選出湯匙；(B) 把手伸到隔板下是之後挑選物品的方式，並非無法說出名稱的原因；(C) 圖片呈現給左半腦時病人就能說出名稱，可見並非快到看不見。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The patient's explanation for choosing the shovel shows that ______.", "options": ["the left hemisphere made up a reasonable-sounding reason without knowing the real one", "the patient was trying to trick the researchers", "the right hemisphere could speak after all", "the patient had often cleaned chicken sheds in the snow"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說發言的左半腦 knew nothing about the snow，卻 answered without hesitation，編出「清理雞舍需要鏟子」的理由；第五段稱之為 interpreter，即使不知道真正原因也會提出解釋。(B) 第五段明言 the patient was not lying；(C) 回答問題的是左半腦；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"coherent\" in the fifth paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["widely shared", "logical and connected", "emotionally powerful", "short and simple"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說詮釋者會把 scattered mental activity（零散的心智活動）轉化成 a single, coherent story，與 scattered 相對，coherent 意為「合乎邏輯、前後連貫的」。(A) 廣為人知的、(C) 情感強烈的、(D) 簡短的，雖然都能用來形容故事，但都無法和 scattered（零散的）形成對比。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["Split-brain surgery is now the most common treatment for epilepsy.", "Every person is either \"left-brained\" or \"right-brained\" in personality.", "Our explanations of our own behavior can be confident and still be wrong.", "The two hemispheres of a healthy brain never share information."], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一段的結論是 the voice in our heads that explains our behavior may not always know as much as it sounds，第五段也說許多研究者認為詮釋者在每個人身上運作，可知作者會同意：我們對自己行為的解釋可能很有自信，卻是錯的。(A) 文中說這種手術如今很少進行；(B) 文中沒有提到左腦型、右腦型人格的說法；(D) 健康的大腦有胼胝體連接兩個半球，兩邊會交流資訊。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a230", "title": "Thought into Words: The Promise and Risks of Brain-Computer Interfaces", "title_zh": "化念頭為語言：腦機介面的希望與風險", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Technology", "Health"], "passage": "In April 2011, a woman named Cathy Hutchinson, who had been unable to move her arms or legs for nearly fifteen years after a stroke, picked up a bottle of coffee and took a sip through a straw. She did it not with her own hand but with a robotic arm, controlled by a tiny array of electrodes implanted in her brain. The moment, captured on video and made public the following year, was a landmark for a field that had long seemed closer to science fiction than to medicine.\n\nBrain-computer interfaces, or BCIs, rest on a simple observation: even when injury or disease cuts the connection between the brain and the body, the brain often continues to generate the signals that once controlled movement. Electrodes placed on or in the brain can record this activity, and software can learn which patterns correspond to which intended actions, such as moving a cursor to the left or closing a hand. Users need not learn a new skill so much as keep trying to do what they always did.\n\nIn recent years, progress has accelerated, largely thanks to advances in artificial intelligence. In 2023, two research teams in California reported systems that could decode attempted speech directly from brain activity. One allowed a woman who had lost the ability to speak after a stroke to communicate at nearly 80 words a minute through a digital avatar, which even reproduced her own voice, rebuilt from a recording of a speech she had given at her wedding. The other enabled a woman with ALS, a disease that gradually destroys the nerves controlling movement, to produce text at more than 60 words a minute. Natural conversation runs at about 160 words a minute, so a gap remains, but earlier devices had managed only a small fraction of that speed.\n\nCommercial interest has followed. Several companies are now running trials of implanted devices, and others are developing headsets that read brain signals without surgery, though far less precisely. Their ambitions extend beyond medicine: advocates imagine a future in which healthy people type, play games or search the internet by thought alone.\n\nIt is this prospect that troubles ethicists. Brain data are unlike other personal information. They may reveal not only what we do but potentially what we intend, feel or prefer, perhaps before we are fully aware of it ourselves. If such data can be stored, sold or demanded by employers and governments, the last private space, the inside of one's own head, could be opened to inspection. In 2021, Chile became the first country to amend its constitution to protect brain activity and the information derived from it, and other governments have begun to consider similar measures.\n\nFor now, the benefits for people who have lost the ability to move or speak are real and, for those individuals, life-changing. The challenge will be to ensure that a technology designed to give people back their voice does not end up taking away their privacy. History suggests that it is far easier to build safeguards into a technology before it becomes part of everyday life than to add them afterward.", "passage_zh": "2011 年 4 月，凱西‧哈欽森——一位中風後已將近十五年無法移動手腳的女性——拿起一瓶咖啡，用吸管啜了一口。她靠的不是自己的手，而是一隻機械手臂，由植入她大腦的一組微小電極陣列控制。這一刻被拍成影片，並在隔年公開；對一個長久以來看似更接近科幻小說、而非醫學的領域來說，這是劃時代的一刻。\n\n腦機介面（BCI）建立在一個簡單的觀察上：即使傷病切斷了大腦與身體之間的連結，大腦往往仍會繼續產生過去用來控制動作的訊號。放在大腦表面或植入大腦內的電極可以記錄這些活動，而軟體則能學會哪些模式對應到哪些想要做的動作，例如把游標往左移，或是握起一隻手。使用者與其說需要學習一項新技能，不如說只需要持續嘗試做他們一直以來在做的事。\n\n近年來，這方面的進展加快了，這主要歸功於人工智慧的進步。2023 年，加州的兩個研究團隊發表了能直接從大腦活動解讀「試圖說出的話」的系統。其中一套系統讓一位中風後失去說話能力的女性，透過一個數位虛擬化身，以每分鐘將近 80 個字的速度溝通；這個化身甚至重現了她本人的聲音，是根據她在婚禮上致詞的錄音重建的。另一套系統則讓一位罹患漸凍症（一種會逐漸破壞控制動作之神經的疾病）的女性，以每分鐘超過 60 個字的速度產生文字。自然對話的速度大約是每分鐘 160 個字，因此差距仍在，但先前的裝置只能達到這個速度的一小部分。\n\n商業界的興趣也隨之而來。目前已有幾家公司正在進行植入式裝置的試驗，另一些公司則在開發不需手術就能讀取腦部訊號的頭戴裝置，不過精準度差得多。他們的野心不只在醫學上：支持者想像在未來，健康的人只靠念頭就能打字、玩遊戲或上網搜尋。\n\n正是這樣的前景讓倫理學家憂心。大腦資料不同於其他個人資訊。它們揭露的可能不只是我們做了什麼，還可能包括我們的意圖、感受或偏好，甚至在我們自己完全察覺之前就已顯露。如果這類資料可以被儲存、販賣，或被雇主和政府要求提供，那麼最後一個私人空間——我們自己的腦袋裡——就可能被打開來接受檢查。2021 年，智利成為第一個修改憲法、保護大腦活動及其衍生資訊的國家，其他國家的政府也已開始考慮類似的措施。\n\n就目前而言，腦機介面為失去行動或說話能力的人帶來的好處是真實的，對這些人來說更足以改變人生。挑戰在於，要確保一項為了讓人重新找回聲音而設計的技術，最後不會奪走他們的隱私。歷史告訴我們，在一項技術成為日常生活的一部分之前就建立防護措施，遠比事後再補上容易得多。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer begin with the story of Cathy Hutchinson?", "options": ["To warn readers about the dangers of brain surgery", "To illustrate an important achievement that showed what BCIs could do", "To explain how strokes damage the brain", "To advertise a company that makes robotic arms"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段描述哈欽森靠植入大腦的電極控制機械手臂喝咖啡，並說這一刻是 a landmark（劃時代的一刻），作者以此開頭，是為了用一項重大成就說明腦機介面能做到什麼。(A) 文中並未警告腦部手術的危險；(C) 中風只是背景，文中沒有說明其原理；(D) 文中沒有替任何公司做廣告。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the second paragraph, why can BCIs work for people who are paralyzed?", "options": ["Their muscles become stronger after the electrodes are implanted.", "Their brains often keep producing the signals for movement.", "They learn an entirely new language to control the computer.", "The software is able to control their thoughts directly."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：even when injury or disease cuts the connection between the brain and the body, the brain often continues to generate the signals that once controlled movement. 即使大腦和身體的連結被切斷，大腦仍會產生控制動作的訊號，所以腦機介面能夠運作。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 第二段末說使用者不必學習新技能，只要持續嘗試原本的動作。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the comparison with natural conversation in the third paragraph?", "options": ["The 2023 systems were faster than normal human speech.", "Speech BCIs are no longer needed by patients.", "The 2023 systems were a big step forward but still slower than ordinary speech.", "People with ALS naturally speak faster than stroke patients."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段指出 2023 年的系統每分鐘約 60 至 80 個字，比先前的裝置快得多，但自然對話約每分鐘 160 個字，so a gap remains。可推知新系統進步很大，但仍比一般說話慢。(A) 與文中數字不符；(B) 文中沒有這樣的說法；(D) 兩人速度不同是因為使用不同的系統，無法推論出病症與說話速度的關係，而且較快的反而是中風患者那一套。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In the fifth paragraph, \"the last private space\" refers to ______.", "options": ["a person's home", "a person's medical records", "a person's own mind and thoughts", "the internet"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段 the last private space, the inside of one's own head, could be opened to inspection，同位語 the inside of one's own head 就是在說明 the last private space，指一個人自己的內心與思想。(A)(B)(D) 都不是文中所指的空間。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which best describes the writer's attitude toward BCIs?", "options": ["Supportive of their medical use but concerned about protecting privacy", "Completely opposed, believing that they should be banned", "Uninterested, since they help only a small number of people", "Uncritically excited about using them for games and shopping"], "answer": 0, "explain": "作者在最後一段肯定腦機介面為失去行動或說話能力的人帶來 real and... life-changing 的好處，但也強調要確保它 does not end up taking away their privacy，第五段也討論了大腦資料的隱私疑慮。可知作者支持其醫療用途，但關心隱私保護。(B) 作者並未主張禁止；(C) 作者認為它能改變這些人的人生，並非不感興趣；(D) 作者對非醫療用途抱持疑慮，並非毫無保留地興奮。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a231", "title": "The Right Cure for the Wrong Reason: London's Great Stink", "title_zh": "對的解方，錯的理由：倫敦大惡臭", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["History", "Science"], "passage": "In the summer of 1858, London held its nose. Weeks of unusual heat had turned the River Thames, which received the waste of well over two million people, into an open sewer, and the smell rising from the water grew so overpowering that the episode went down in history as \"the Great Stink.\" Nowhere was it felt more keenly than in the Houses of Parliament, which stand directly on the riverbank. Curtains were soaked in chemicals in a desperate attempt to block the stench, and lawmakers seriously discussed moving elsewhere.\n\nWhat alarmed them was not mere unpleasantness. Most doctors at the time accepted the \"miasma\" theory, which held that diseases such as cholera were carried by foul-smelling air. If bad air caused disease, a river that smelled this bad had to be deadly. One physician, John Snow, had argued otherwise. Having mapped the deaths in an 1854 cholera outbreak in Soho, he traced them to a single public water pump and concluded that the disease spread through contaminated water. His ideas, however, won few supporters, and he died that very June, while the city was choking on air he had never considered the real danger.\n\nSpurred on by the smell, Parliament acted with remarkable speed, passing a law within weeks that gave the engineer Joseph Bazalgette the money and authority to rebuild London's drainage. His plan was enormous: around 130 kilometers of large \"intercepting\" sewers running parallel to the river, fed by some 1,800 kilometers of smaller street sewers, would carry the city's waste eastward and release it far downstream. Bazalgette is said to have calculated the pipe sizes he needed and then doubled them, reasoning that a job like this would only ever be done once. Had he been less cautious, the system would almost certainly have been overwhelmed as the city kept growing.\n\nThe new sewers did more than remove the smell; they removed cholera, too, though not for the reasons their builders believed. By keeping sewage out of the water that Londoners drank, the system cut off the very route by which the disease traveled. Confirmation came in 1866, when cholera broke out once more. It struck hardest in East London, where the new sewers were not yet complete, and investigators traced the deaths to water from a contaminated reservoir. Even William Farr, a leading government statistician who had long favored the miasma theory, now accepted that Snow had been right.\n\nIt is tempting to read all this as a simple success story, but its lesson is subtler. Good engineering, it turns out, can outrun good science: the right solution was built on the wrong theory. Nor did the right solution last forever. Because rainwater and sewage still share many of the same Victorian pipes, heavy storms push untreated waste into the Thames, which is why London has recently built a 25-kilometer \"super sewer\" deep beneath the river. Bazalgette's caution bought the city more than a century and a half. Yet even the most farsighted engineer can only build for a future he is forced to guess at.", "passage_zh": "1858 年夏天，倫敦人都捏著鼻子過日子。連續數週的異常高溫，讓承接了遠超過兩百萬人排泄物的泰晤士河變成一條露天的汙水溝，河面飄出的臭味強烈到令人無法忍受，這段時期因此以「大惡臭」之名載入史冊。感受最深的莫過於緊鄰河岸而建的國會大廈。為了擋住惡臭，人們不顧一切地把窗簾浸泡在化學藥劑裡，議員們甚至認真討論要不要搬到別處開會。\n\n讓他們驚恐的不只是難聞而已。當時大多數醫師都接受「瘴氣說」，認為霍亂等疾病是由惡臭的空氣傳播的。如果臭氣會致病，那麼一條臭成這樣的河必定致命。然而，有一位醫師約翰‧斯諾持不同看法。他曾把 1854 年蘇活區霍亂疫情的死亡病例標示在地圖上，追查出這些病例都與一座公共抽水機有關，並由此推論這種疾病是經由受汙染的水傳播。不過，他的觀點幾乎沒有人支持；他就在那年六月去世，而當時整座城市正被他從不認為是真正危險所在的臭氣嗆得喘不過氣。\n\n在惡臭的催逼之下，國會以驚人的速度採取行動，在短短幾週內就通過一項法律，讓工程師約瑟夫‧巴澤爾傑特獲得重建倫敦排水系統所需的經費與權力。他的計畫規模龐大：沿著河流平行興建長約 130 km 的大型「截流」下水道，再由總長約 1,800 km 的較小街道下水道匯入，將全市的汙水往東輸送，在遠離市區的下游排放。據說巴澤爾傑特算出所需的管徑後，又把它加倍，理由是這樣的工程只會做這麼一次。要是他當初沒那麼謹慎，隨著城市不斷擴張，這套系統幾乎一定會不堪負荷。\n\n新的下水道不只消除了臭味，也消除了霍亂，只是原因並不是建造者所以為的那樣。由於汙水不再流進倫敦人飲用的水中，這套系統正好切斷了霍亂傳播的途徑。1866 年霍亂再度爆發，證實了這一點。疫情在新下水道尚未完工的東倫敦最為嚴重，調查人員也將死亡病例追溯到一座受汙染水庫供應的用水。就連長期支持瘴氣說、地位舉足輕重的政府統計學家威廉‧法爾，這時也承認斯諾是對的。\n\n我們很容易把這一切看成一個單純的成功故事，但其中的啟示其實更為微妙。事實證明，好的工程可以跑在好的科學前面：正確的解決方法，竟是建立在錯誤的理論之上。而正確的解決方法也並非永遠有效。由於許多維多利亞時代的管線至今仍同時排放雨水與汙水，大雨時未經處理的汙水依然會被沖進泰晤士河，這正是倫敦最近在河床深處建造一條長達 25 km 的「超級下水道」的原因。巴澤爾傑特的謹慎為這座城市爭取了超過一個半世紀的時間。然而，即使是最有遠見的工程師，也只能為一個他不得不去猜測的未來而建造。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["London's lawmakers ignored the Great Stink until a cholera outbreak forced them to act.", "London's sewer system defeated a deadly disease even though it was built on a mistaken theory.", "John Snow designed the sewer system that finally ended cholera in London.", "Victorian engineers understood exactly how cholera spread from person to person."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文說明倫敦因「大惡臭」而興建下水道，當時的人相信疾病是由臭氣傳播（瘴氣說），但下水道真正有效的原因是讓汙水不再進入飲用水；第五段也直接點出 the right solution was built on the wrong theory。(A) 第三段說國會在幾週內就迅速立法，並非等到霍亂爆發才行動；(C) 下水道是巴澤爾傑特設計的，斯諾是提出霍亂經由水傳播的醫師；(D) 第四段說下水道防治霍亂的原因 not for the reasons their builders believed，與選項相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention that John Snow died in June 1858?", "options": ["To suggest that he, too, became ill from breathing the air near the Thames", "To explain why Parliament refused to pay for his research", "To point out the irony that he died just as the city was panicking over what he saw as the wrong danger", "To show that the Great Stink caused a sharp rise in deaths among doctors"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段指出斯諾認為霍亂是經由受汙染的「水」傳播，而他在 1858 年六月去世時，整座城市正因「臭氣」而恐慌；作者寫 choking on air he had never considered the real danger，凸顯大家擔心的正是他認為並非真正危險的東西，充滿諷刺意味。(A) 文中沒有說他因臭氣而生病，這也與他的理論相矛盾；(B) 文中未提及國會拒絕資助他；(D) 文中未提及醫師的死亡人數。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the sentence \"Had he been less cautious, the system would almost certainly have been overwhelmed\" suggest?", "options": ["Bazalgette's system broke down soon after it was completed.", "Bazalgette was criticized for wasting money on unnecessarily wide pipes.", "Bazalgette secretly doubted that his sewers would ever work.", "Making the pipes larger than necessary allowed the system to cope with London's later growth."], "answer": 3, "explain": "此句為省略 if 的倒裝假設句（= If he had been less cautious, ...），意思是「要是他當初沒那麼謹慎，系統幾乎一定早就不堪負荷」，言下之意是他把管徑加倍的做法，讓下水道得以應付城市日後的擴張。(A) 與句意相反；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why was the cholera outbreak of 1866 important?", "options": ["It provided strong evidence that cholera spread through water rather than air.", "It proved that Bazalgette's sewers had been badly designed.", "It forced Parliament to pass the law that paid for the new sewers.", "It showed that the miasma theory had been correct after all."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：1866 年霍亂再度爆發，疫情在下水道尚未完工的東倫敦最嚴重，調查人員把死亡病例追溯到受汙染水庫的用水，連長期支持瘴氣說的法爾也承認斯諾是對的。(B) 疫情集中在下水道「尚未完工」的地區，反而證明下水道有效；(C) 相關法律在 1858 年就已通過；(D) 與文意相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"outrun\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["replace completely", "get ahead of", "be defeated by", "depend on"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Good engineering can outrun good science 意指好的工程可以「跑在好的科學前面」，冒號後的 the right solution was built on the wrong theory 加以說明：在科學還沒弄清楚霍亂成因之前，工程師就已經做出了正確的解決方法。outrun 在此表示「超越、領先」。(A) 完全取代；(C) 被…打敗；(D) 依賴，皆不合文意。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a232", "title": "No Left, No Right: A Language That Points North", "title_zh": "沒有左，也沒有右：一個永遠指向北方的語言", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Culture", "Science"], "passage": "When Captain James Cook's ship, the Endeavour, was damaged on the Great Barrier Reef in 1770, his crew spent several weeks repairing it beside a river in what is now northern Queensland. There they met the Guugu Yimithirr people and wrote down one of their words, gangurru, for a large gray animal that hopped. Thus \"kangaroo\" entered English. Far more remarkable, however, is a feature of the language that went largely unnoticed for the next two centuries: it has no ordinary way of saying \"left\" or \"right.\"\n\nSpeakers of Guugu Yimithirr do not describe space in relation to their own bodies. Instead, they use the four points of the compass. Someone might mention that there is an ant on your northern leg, or ask you to move a little to the east to make room on a bench. When recounting a film, speakers have been observed describing the characters' movements according to the direction the television was facing. To speak this way, one must know at every moment where north is, and fluent speakers do, even in unfamiliar places and even indoors.\n\nHow do they manage it? The answer seems to lie in practice that begins in infancy. Because a child who cannot use compass directions simply cannot be understood, the habit of tracking orientation is built in so early that it becomes as automatic as noticing whether it is day or night. Researchers who tested speakers found that they could point toward distant places with an accuracy that would astonish most city dwellers, many of whom can barely locate north on a sunny afternoon.\n\nThe language also appears to shape memory. In one well-known experiment, people were shown a row of toy animals on a table, then turned around to face the opposite direction and asked to rebuild the row on a second table. English or Dutch speakers typically kept the same left-to-right order, so that the animal on their left stayed on their left. Guugu Yimithirr speakers, by contrast, preserved the compass order, which meant that from their own point of view the row had been reversed. Neither group had been told which kind of \"the same\" was wanted.\n\nSuch findings have revived an old and controversial idea. In its strongest form, which holds that language determines thought and that we cannot think what our language cannot say, the idea has long been abandoned; Guugu Yimithirr speakers, after all, know perfectly well which hand is which. What the language seems to do is subtler. As the linguist Roman Jakobson observed, languages differ less in what they allow their speakers to say than in what they force them to say. A language that obliges you to mention north in almost every other sentence trains your attention in a way that no textbook could.\n\nThere is a sad footnote to this story. Like many Indigenous Australian languages, Guugu Yimithirr is spoken by a shrinking number of people, and more and more of the younger generation are growing up with English. If the language disappears, it will take with it not just a vocabulary but a way of standing in the world.", "passage_zh": "1770 年，詹姆斯‧庫克船長的船「奮進號」在大堡礁觸礁受損，船員們在今日昆士蘭北部的一條河邊花了好幾個星期修船。他們在那裡遇見了古古伊米德希爾族人，並記下了這個民族稱呼一種會跳躍的大型灰色動物的字：gangurru。「袋鼠」（kangaroo）一詞就這樣進入了英語。然而，這個語言還有一個更引人注目的特點，在接下來的兩個世紀裡幾乎沒有人注意到：它沒有一般用來表達「左」或「右」的說法。\n\n說古古伊米德希爾語的人並不以自己的身體為基準來描述空間，而是使用東南西北四個方位。有人可能會告訴你，你「北邊那條腿」上有一隻螞蟻，或請你往東邊挪一點，好在長椅上讓出空位。研究人員也觀察到，他們在轉述一部影片時，會依照當時電視機面對的方向，來描述片中人物往哪裡移動。要這樣說話，一個人必須隨時都知道北方在哪裡，而流利的使用者確實知道，即使身處陌生的地方、即使在室內也一樣。\n\n他們是怎麼辦到的？答案似乎在於從嬰兒時期就開始的練習。由於一個不會使用方位詞的孩子根本無法讓別人聽懂，留意方向的習慣很早就深植心中，變得就像察覺現在是白天還是黑夜一樣自然。測試過這些使用者的研究人員發現，他們指出遠方地點方向的準確度，足以讓大多數都市人大吃一驚；許多都市人即使在晴朗的午後，也幾乎找不出北方在哪裡。\n\n這個語言似乎也會影響記憶。在一個著名的實驗中，研究人員先讓受試者看桌上排成一列的玩具動物，再請他們轉身面向相反方向，在第二張桌子上把那一列動物重新排出來。說英語或荷蘭語的人通常會保持相同的左右順序，原本在他們左手邊的動物，依然擺在左手邊。相較之下，說古古伊米德希爾語的人保留的是方位上的順序，也就是說，從他們自己的角度來看，那一列動物的順序被顛倒了。兩組人事先都沒有被告知，實驗要的是哪一種「相同」。\n\n這類研究結果讓一個古老而具爭議的觀點再度受到重視。這個觀點最強烈的版本主張語言決定思想，認為我們無法思考自己的語言說不出來的東西，這種說法早已被揚棄；畢竟，說古古伊米德希爾語的人完全分得清楚哪隻手是哪隻。語言的作用似乎更為微妙。正如語言學家羅曼‧雅各布森所指出的，各種語言的差別，與其說在於它們「允許」使用者說什麼，不如說在於它們「強迫」使用者說什麼。一個要你幾乎每隔一句話就提到北方的語言，會以任何課本都做不到的方式訓練你的注意力。\n\n這個故事有一段令人感傷的後記。和許多澳洲原住民語言一樣，會說古古伊米德希爾語的人越來越少，越來越多年輕一代是在英語環境中長大的。如果這個語言消失了，隨之消失的將不只是一套詞彙，還有一種立足於世界的方式。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer begin with the story of Captain Cook and the word \"kangaroo\"?", "options": ["To argue that European explorers misunderstood Aboriginal languages", "To explain how the Guugu Yimithirr people first learned English", "To introduce the language through a familiar fact before turning to a more surprising feature", "To show that Cook's crew were the first to notice that the language lacked words for left and right"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段先以大家熟悉的 kangaroo 一字的由來引出古古伊米德希爾語，再用 Far more remarkable, however, ... 轉入本文真正的重點：這個語言沒有一般表達「左」「右」的說法。(A)(B) 文中皆未提及；(D) 文中說這個特點在之後兩百年幾乎沒人注意到（went largely unnoticed），可見庫克的船員並沒有發現。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "A Guugu Yimithirr speaker is sitting on a bench facing south when a bird lands on the ground to his right. How would he most likely tell a friend where the bird is?", "options": ["\"There's a bird on my right.\"", "\"There's a bird to the west of me.\"", "\"There's a bird to the east of me.\"", "\"There's a bird in front of me.\""], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段指出，說古古伊米德希爾語的人不以自己的身體（左右、前後）為基準描述空間，而是使用東南西北（例如 an ant on your northern leg、move a little to the east）。面向南方時，右手邊正是西方，所以他會說鳥在自己的西邊。(A)(D) 是以自己身體為基準的說法，正是這個語言不用的方式；(C) 面向南方時東方在左手邊，方向弄反了。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In the toy animal experiment, what did Guugu Yimithirr speakers do after turning around?", "options": ["They kept the animals in the same compass order, so that the row looked reversed from their own point of view.", "They kept the animal that had been on their left on their left.", "They refused to finish the task because the instructions were unclear.", "They arranged the animals in a random order."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Guugu Yimithirr speakers, by contrast, preserved the compass order, which meant that from their own point of view the row had been reversed. 他們保留方位上的順序，從自己的角度看順序反而顛倒。(B) 是說英語或荷蘭語者的做法；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["People are unable to think about ideas that their language has no words for.", "Guugu Yimithirr speakers cannot tell their left hand from their right.", "City dwellers could point north accurately if they studied geography textbooks harder.", "The language people speak can train what they habitually pay attention to."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段指出「語言決定思想」的強烈說法早已被揚棄，語言的作用較為微妙：語言的差別在於它「強迫」使用者表達什麼，一個要你時時提到北方的語言會訓練你的注意力。(A) 正是作者說已被揚棄的強烈說法；(B) 文中說他們完全分得清哪隻手是哪隻；(C) 作者說這種訓練是 no textbook could 做到的，與選項相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by saying that the language would take with it \"not just a vocabulary but a way of standing in the world\"?", "options": ["Younger people would no longer know the traditional ways to sit and stand.", "Speakers would be forced to leave the land where their ancestors lived.", "A distinctive way of perceiving and orienting oneself in space would be lost along with the words.", "Researchers would no longer be able to find out where the word \"kangaroo\" came from."], "answer": 2, "explain": "a way of standing in the world 是比喻用法，並非字面上「站立的姿勢」。全文說明這個語言讓使用者隨時留意方位、影響他們的記憶，並訓練他們的注意力（第二至五段）；因此語言消失時，失去的不只是一套詞彙，還有一種感知世界、為自己在空間中定位的方式。(A) 把 standing 誤解為字面上的站立；(B) 文中只說年輕一代在英語環境中長大，未提到被迫離開土地；(D) kangaroo 一字早已進入英語，與語言消失無關。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a233", "title": "Beyond the Tragedy: Elinor Ostrom and the Art of Sharing", "title_zh": "超越悲劇：歐斯壯與共享的藝術", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Society", "Environment"], "passage": "In 1968, the ecologist Garrett Hardin published an essay with a memorable title: \"The Tragedy of the Commons.\" Imagine, he wrote, a pasture open to all. Each herder gains the full benefit of adding one more animal to the field, while the cost, slightly less grass for everyone, is shared by all. Acting rationally, every herder therefore keeps adding animals until the pasture is destroyed. \"Freedom in a commons,\" Hardin concluded, \"brings ruin to all.\" The only escapes, it seemed, were to place the land under government control or to divide it into private property.\n\nThe argument was so elegant that it shaped environmental policy for decades. Yet it took an American political scientist, Elinor Ostrom, to ask the obvious question its admirers had overlooked: what actually happens when real people share real resources? Rather than building models, she went to look. What she found, in fishing grounds, forests and irrigation systems around the world, was that communities frequently avoided the tragedy altogether, not through government orders or private ownership, but through rules they had designed themselves.\n\nConsider Törbel, a village in the Swiss Alps whose farmers have shared high mountain meadows for centuries. As early as 1517, the villagers agreed that no one could send more cows to the summer pasture than he could feed through the winter, a rule that neatly tied each family's use of the commons to its own resources. In Valencia, Spain, farmers who share irrigation water have settled their disputes for centuries at a \"Water Tribunal\" that still meets every Thursday outside the city's cathedral. And in Nepal, studies found that canals run by farmers themselves often delivered water more reliably than those built and managed by government agencies.\n\nFrom such cases, Ostrom drew a set of principles that long-lasting commons tend to share. The boundaries of the resource, and of who may use it, are clearly defined. The people affected by the rules have a say in making them. Monitoring is carried out by the users themselves or by people answerable to them, and those who break the rules face penalties that begin small and grow more serious. Not every community gets this right, of course, and Ostrom never claimed that cooperation was guaranteed. Her point was that it was possible, and that the conditions under which it succeeds could be studied.\n\nIn 2009, Ostrom became the first woman to receive the Nobel Prize in economics, an honor all the more striking given that she had been trained not as an economist but as a political scientist. Her work did not so much disprove Hardin as reveal how narrow his assumptions were; Hardin himself later conceded that his essay should have been titled \"The Tragedy of the Unmanaged Commons.\" His imaginary herders never spoke to one another. Real people do, and it is precisely in that conversation, Ostrom showed, that the way out of the tragedy is often found.", "passage_zh": "1968 年，生態學家蓋瑞特‧哈丁發表了一篇標題令人印象深刻的文章：〈公地悲劇〉。他寫道，想像有一片人人都能使用的牧場。每位牧人多放一頭牲畜到草地上，好處完全歸自己，而代價（每個人能分到的草稍微少了一些）卻由所有人共同分擔。因此，每位牧人在理性考量下都會不斷增加牲畜，直到牧場被徹底破壞為止。哈丁的結論是：「在公地上的自由，會為所有人帶來毀滅。」看來，唯一的出路不是把土地交由政府管理，就是將它劃分成私有財產。\n\n這個論點如此簡潔優美，以至於影響了往後數十年的環境政策。然而，直到美國政治學家伊莉諾‧歐斯壯出現，才有人提出那個再明顯不過、卻被這套理論的推崇者所忽略的問題：當真實的人共享真實的資源時，實際上會發生什麼事？她沒有去建立模型，而是親自去觀察。她在世界各地的漁場、森林和灌溉系統中發現，許多社群往往完全避開了這場悲劇，靠的既不是政府的命令，也不是私有制，而是他們自己制定的規則。\n\n以瑞士阿爾卑斯山區的村莊托貝爾為例，那裡的農民數百年來一直共用高山草地。早在 1517 年，村民就約定，任何人送上夏季牧場的牛隻，都不得超過他自己在冬天養得起的數量；這條規則巧妙地把每戶人家對公地的使用，與這戶人家本身的資源連結在一起。在西班牙的瓦倫西亞，共用灌溉用水的農民數百年來都在「水法庭」解決彼此的爭端，這個法庭至今仍在每週四於城裡的主教座堂外開庭。而在尼泊爾，研究發現由農民自行管理的水圳，供水往往比政府機關興建、管理的水圳更穩定可靠。\n\n歐斯壯從這些案例中，歸納出能長久維持的公共資源通常具備的一套原則：資源的範圍以及誰可以使用它，都有清楚的界定；受規則影響的人，在規則的制定上有發言權；監督工作由使用者自己，或由必須向使用者負責的人來執行；違反規則的人會受到處罰，而處罰從輕微開始，逐步加重。當然，並非每個社群都能做到這些，歐斯壯也從未宣稱合作一定會成功。她的重點在於：合作是可能的，而合作在什麼條件下能夠成功，是可以研究的。\n\n2009 年，歐斯壯成為第一位獲得諾貝爾經濟學獎的女性；考慮到她所受的訓練並非經濟學，而是政治學，這項殊榮更顯得非比尋常。她的研究與其說推翻了哈丁的理論，不如說揭示了他的假設有多麼狹隘；哈丁本人後來也承認，那篇文章應該取名為〈無人管理的公地悲劇〉。他想像中的牧人從不彼此交談，但真實的人會；而歐斯壯證明了，走出悲劇的方法，往往正是在這樣的對話中找到的。", "questions": [{"q": "According to Hardin's argument, why would herders keep adding animals to a shared pasture?", "options": ["The government paid them to raise as many animals as possible.", "Each herder enjoyed the whole benefit of an extra animal, while the cost was spread among everyone.", "They did not realize that the grass would grow back slowly.", "They expected the pasture to be divided into private plots soon."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：Each herder gains the full benefit of adding one more animal to the field, while the cost ... is shared by all. 多養一頭牲畜的好處由自己獨享，代價卻由大家分攤，所以每個人都會一直增加牲畜。(A)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 劃分為私有財產是當時被視為「出路」的解決辦法之一，並非牧人增加牲畜的原因。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer imply by saying that Ostrom asked \"the obvious question its admirers had overlooked\"?", "options": ["Hardin's supporters had already tested his theory in many countries.", "Ostrom's question was too simple to deserve serious attention.", "Ostrom believed that shared pastures could never be destroyed.", "Many people had accepted Hardin's idea without checking how real communities behave."], "answer": 3, "explain": "作者說這個問題再明顯不過（obvious），推崇者卻忽略了（overlooked），暗示許多人因為哈丁的論點簡潔優美就接受它，卻從未實際查看真實的社群如何管理共享資源；下一句 Rather than building models, she went to look 也呼應這一點。(A) 與「忽略」的意思相反；(B) 作者對這個問題持肯定態度；(C) 第四段說歐斯壯 never claimed that cooperation was guaranteed，她並未主張公地不可能被破壞。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What was the purpose of the rule adopted in Törbel in 1517?", "options": ["To link the number of cows each family could send to the pasture to the number it could feed in winter", "To give the richest families the right to use the best meadows", "To prevent outsiders from buying land in the village", "To force farmers to sell their cows before winter came"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：no one could send more cows to the summer pasture than he could feed through the winter, a rule that neatly tied each family's use of the commons to its own resources. 這條規則把每戶能放牧的牛隻數量，與其冬天養得起的數量連結在一起。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a principle shared by long-lasting commons?", "options": ["Clearly defined boundaries for the resource and its users", "Penalties for rule-breakers that start small and grow more serious", "Monitoring by the users or by people answerable to them", "An equal share of the resource for every household"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段列出的原則包括：資源與使用者的界線清楚 (A)、受影響的人參與訂定規則、由使用者或向使用者負責的人監督 (C)、罰則由輕而重 (B)。文中從未提到每戶平均分配資源；第三段托貝爾的規則反而是依各戶冬天養得起的牛隻數量來決定能放牧多少，並非人人均分。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's attitude toward Hardin's essay?", "options": ["Dismissive, because the essay had no influence on policy", "Uncritical, because its logic is perfect", "Respectful of its elegance but aware that its assumptions were too narrow", "Angry, because it caused the destruction of many pastures"], "answer": 2, "explain": "作者稱哈丁的論點 so elegant（簡潔優美），並說它影響了數十年的環境政策，但最後一段也指出歐斯壯的研究 reveal how narrow his assumptions were（揭示其假設有多狹隘）。(A) 作者明說它影響政策數十年；(B) 作者對其假設有所批評；(D) 文中沒有憤怒的語氣，也沒有說它造成牧場遭到破壞。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a234", "title": "Let the Mammoth Rest: The Case Against De-extinction", "title_zh": "讓長毛象安息吧：反對「復活滅絕物種」的理由", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Environment", "Science"], "passage": "Few scientific promises are as seductive as the idea of bringing extinct animals back to life. Companies have raised hundreds of millions of dollars on the strength of it, and in 2025 one of them announced the birth of what it called \"dire wolves,\" a species that vanished more than ten thousand years ago. Headlines celebrated. The pups, critics soon pointed out, were essentially gray wolves carrying about twenty genetic edits. Before we cheer the return of the mammoth, we ought to ask whether de-extinction is a goal worth pursuing at all. I believe it is not.\n\nThe first problem is that a recreated animal is not the species that was lost. Even if every gene could be copied perfectly, a species is more than its DNA. Many animals learn crucial behavior from their parents: where to migrate, what to eat, how to avoid predators. A mammoth carried by an elephant mother and raised by humans would have no herd to teach it how to be a mammoth. What we would produce, in other words, is not a resurrection but a new and lonely creature built to resemble an old one.\n\nThe second problem concerns animal welfare. The first time a truly extinct animal was cloned, the result was heartbreaking. In 2003, scientists cloned a Pyrenean ibex, a wild goat whose last member had died three years earlier. The newborn lived for only a few minutes before a defect in its lungs killed it. Should such experiments be repeated with large, intelligent and highly social animals, the suffering involved could be considerable, and it would be borne by creatures that never chose to take part.\n\nMost serious of all is the cost to conservation. Money spent on reviving one lost species is money not spent on saving the thousands now at risk, many of which could be protected relatively cheaply by guarding their habitats. Worse, the promise of de-extinction risks creating a false sense of comfort. If extinction comes to be seen as reversible, the urgency that drives conservation will weaken. Why fight to protect a forest today, some might ask, when science can bring its animals back tomorrow?\n\nSupporters respond that the same tools could help living species, and here they have a point. In 2020, American scientists cloned a black-footed ferret from cells that had been frozen in 1988, adding much-needed genetic diversity to one of North America's most endangered mammals. But this example actually strengthens my case. It is in protecting species that still exist, by storing their cells, widening their gene pools and restoring their homes, that this technology does the most good.\n\nThe mammoth belongs to a world that has disappeared; the northern white rhinoceros, of which only two females remain, belongs to ours. If we are to spend our scientific talent and our limited funds anywhere, let it be on the animals we can still save, before they too become the subject of an expensive and uncertain experiment.", "passage_zh": "很少有科學上的承諾，能像讓滅絕動物起死回生這個想法一樣誘人。好幾家公司憑著這個構想募得了數億美元的資金，其中一家公司更在 2025 年宣布，他們所謂的「恐狼」誕生了，而這種動物早在一萬多年前就已消失。新聞標題一片歡騰。然而批評者很快就指出，這些幼狼基本上只是帶有大約二十處基因編輯的灰狼。在我們為長毛象的回歸歡呼之前，應該先問問：「復活滅絕物種」究竟是不是一個值得追求的目標？我認為不是。\n\n第一個問題是，重新製造出來的動物，並不是那個已經消失的物種。即使每一個基因都能完美複製，一個物種也不只是它的 DNA 而已。許多動物要向父母學習關鍵的行為：往哪裡遷徙、吃些什麼、如何躲避掠食者。一頭由大象母親懷胎、再由人類養大的長毛象，不會有任何象群教牠如何當一頭長毛象。換句話說，我們製造出來的並不是一個復活的物種，而是一種為了模仿舊物種而造出來、孤零零的新生物。\n\n第二個問題與動物福利有關。第一次有真正滅絕的動物被複製出來時，結果令人心碎。2003 年，科學家複製了一頭生活在法國與西班牙交界山區的野山羊（Pyrenean ibex），這種野山羊的最後一隻在三年前就已死亡。這頭新生的小羊只活了短短幾分鐘，就因為肺部缺陷而死去。如果這類實驗用在體型龐大、聰明而且高度群居的動物身上，所造成的痛苦可能相當可觀，而承受這些痛苦的，是從來沒有選擇要參與實驗的生物。\n\n最嚴重的是，這會對保育工作造成損失。花在復活一個已消失物種上的錢，就是沒有用來拯救目前數以千計瀕危物種的錢，而其中許多物種只要保護好棲地，就能以相對低廉的成本受到保護。更糟的是，復活滅絕物種的承諾可能會帶來一種虛假的安心感。如果滅絕被視為可以逆轉，推動保育的急迫感就會減弱。有些人可能會問：既然科學明天就能讓森林裡的動物回來，今天又何必努力保護這片森林？\n\n支持者回應說，同樣的技術也可以幫助現存的物種，這一點確實有道理。2020 年，美國科學家利用 1988 年冷凍保存的細胞複製出一隻黑足鼬，為這種北美洲最瀕危的哺乳動物之一增添了亟需的遺傳多樣性。但這個例子其實更加強了我的論點。這項技術最能發揮作用的地方，正是在於保護仍然存在的物種：保存牠們的細胞、擴大牠們的基因庫，並修復牠們的家園。\n\n長毛象屬於一個已經消失的世界；而只剩下兩頭雌性的北方白犀牛，則屬於我們的世界。如果我們要把科學人才和有限的經費花在某個地方，就讓它花在我們仍然救得了的動物身上吧，別等到牠們也成為一場昂貴又沒有把握的實驗的對象。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer mention the \"dire wolves\" in the first paragraph?", "options": ["To prove that de-extinction has already succeeded", "To show that claims about de-extinction can be exaggerated", "To criticize newspapers for ignoring the birth of the pups", "To explain why wolves became extinct"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說媒體大肆慶祝「恐狼」誕生，但批評者指出這些幼狼 essentially gray wolves carrying about twenty genetic edits（基本上只是經過基因編輯的灰狼），作者藉此說明「復活滅絕物種」的宣傳可能言過其實。(A) 與作者觀點相反；(C) 文中說 Headlines celebrated，媒體並未忽略此事；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the second paragraph, why would a recreated mammoth not truly be a mammoth?", "options": ["Its genes would be completely different from those of a real mammoth.", "An elephant mother would refuse to give birth to it.", "It would have no way of learning the behavior that mammoths once passed from parents to young.", "It would be too lonely to survive in a zoo."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：a species is more than its DNA ... A mammoth ... would have no herd to teach it how to be a mammoth. 物種不只是 DNA，許多關鍵行為要向上一代學習，而復活的長毛象沒有象群可以教牠。(A) 作者的假設是即使基因能完美複製也不夠；(B) 文中說由大象母親懷胎，沒有說大象會拒絕；(D) 文中雖用 lonely 形容，但沒有提到動物園或無法存活。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the question \"Why fight to protect a forest today ... when science can bring its animals back tomorrow?\"", "options": ["The writer believes science will soon be able to bring back any animal.", "The writer thinks forests are less important than the animals living in them.", "The writer wants governments to stop paying for scientific research.", "The writer fears that the promise of de-extinction could make people less committed to conservation."], "answer": 3, "explain": "這個問句緊接在 the promise of de-extinction risks creating a false sense of comfort ... the urgency that drives conservation will weaken 之後，是作者模擬某些人可能出現的想法，用來說明「以為滅絕可以逆轉」會削弱保育的急迫感。(A) 作者並不相信這種說法；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How does the writer deal with the opposing view in the fifth paragraph?", "options": ["By admitting that it is partly right and then using it to support the writer's own position", "By ignoring it and changing the subject", "By claiming that the ferret experiment was a failure", "By arguing that all cloning should be banned"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段先承認支持者的說法有道理（here they have a point），並舉出複製黑足鼬的成功例子，接著說 this example actually strengthens my case：這項技術最大的用處在於保護現存物種，反而支持了作者的立場。(B) 作者有正面回應；(C) 文中說黑足鼬的複製增加了遺傳多樣性，是成功的；(D) 作者並未主張全面禁止複製。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"seductive\" in the first paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["frightening and risky", "widely discussed", "attractive and tempting", "scientifically proven"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段說復活滅絕動物的承諾讓企業募得數億美元、新聞標題一片歡騰，可見這個想法十分「誘人」；作者接著卻要大家先冷靜思考它是否值得追求。seductive 意為「有吸引力的、誘人的」，常暗示讓人不加思索就受吸引。(A) 可怕且有風險；(B) 廣受討論，雖然這個想法確實引起許多報導，但並非 seductive 的字義；(D) 經科學證實，與作者「這只是承諾」的看法不符。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a235", "title": "Words on the Move: Why Language Change Is Not Decay", "title_zh": "流動的字詞：為什麼語言的改變不是衰退", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Culture", "Education"], "passage": "Every generation, it seems, is convinced that it is witnessing the decline of its language. Teachers despair over the spelling in text messages; newspaper columnists complain that young people use \"literally\" to mean its opposite. Such complaints are understandable, but they rest on a misunderstanding. Change is not a sign that a language is decaying. It is a sign that the language is alive, and the history of English proves it.\n\nConsider how many respectable words began life as \"mistakes.\" \"Nice\" comes from a Latin word meaning \"ignorant,\" and for centuries it meant \"foolish\" before drifting through senses such as \"precise\" toward the pleasant meaning we know today. \"Silly\" once meant \"blessed.\" \"Decimate\" originally described a brutal Roman punishment in which one soldier in every ten was killed, yet few people today would insist on that meaning when describing what a typhoon did to a city's trees. Had the critics of each age succeeded in freezing English, we would still be calling foolish people \"nice.\"\n\nAttempts to freeze it have, in fact, been made. In 1712, the writer Jonathan Swift proposed an official academy to \"fix\" the English language so that it would stop changing. Samuel Johnson, who set out to compile his famous dictionary a few decades later, began with similar hopes, but by the time it was published in 1755 he had abandoned them, comparing the effort to control a language to trying \"to lash the wind.\" Johnson understood what many critics still do not: a dictionary can record how people speak, but it cannot command them.\n\nEven the changes that most irritate critics often turn out to be older than they appear. Using \"they\" for a single person whose gender is unknown is frequently attacked as modern carelessness, yet it can be found in the works of Chaucer, Shakespeare and Jane Austen. When Merriam-Webster chose \"they\" as its Word of the Year in 2019, it was not approving a new invention but recognizing an old usage that had taken on a new purpose. Borrowing works the same way: English happily absorbed \"ketchup,\" which is widely believed to come from a Hokkien word for fish sauce, and nobody now considers it a threat.\n\nNone of this means that anything goes. Clear writing still matters, and students must learn the conventions expected in exams, job applications and formal letters. But knowing which conventions a particular situation demands is quite different from believing that those conventions are permanent laws. It is the first, not the second, that education ought to provide.\n\nThe real danger, then, lies not in the changes themselves but in the anxiety they provoke. A student who is told that the way she speaks with friends is simply \"wrong\" may learn to distrust her own voice, when what she actually needs is the ability to move between registers as easily as she moves between chatting with classmates and writing to her principal. The only languages that never change, after all, are the ones that nobody speaks anymore. English, happily, is in no danger of joining them.", "passage_zh": "似乎每一個世代都深信，自己正目睹著所使用的語言走向衰敗。老師們對簡訊裡的拼字感到絕望；報紙專欄作家則抱怨，年輕人用 literally（原意是「照字面意義地」）來表示與原意相反的意思。這些抱怨可以理解，但它們建立在一個誤解之上。改變並不代表一個語言正在衰退，而是代表這個語言是活的，英語的歷史正好證明了這一點。\n\n想想看，有多少正經體面的字，一開始其實是「錯誤」的用法。nice 源自一個意思是「無知的」拉丁字，有好幾個世紀它的意思是「愚蠢的」，後來才逐漸轉變，歷經「精確的」等意思，演變成今天我們所熟知的「美好的」。silly（愚蠢的）曾經的意思是「受祝福的」。decimate 原本是指古羅馬一種殘酷的刑罰：每十名士兵中處死一人；然而今天在描述颱風對一座城市的樹木造成的破壞時，幾乎沒有人會堅持這個字的原意。如果每個時代的批評者都成功地把英語凍結起來，我們現在還會用 nice 來形容愚蠢的人。\n\n事實上，確實有人試圖凍結英語。1712 年，作家強納森‧史威夫特提議成立一個官方學會，把英語「固定」下來，讓它不再改變。數十年後，著手編纂那部著名字典的山繆‧約翰遜一開始也抱持類似的期望，但到了字典在 1755 年出版時，他已經放棄了這個想法，並把控制語言的努力比喻為試圖「鞭打風」。約翰遜明白了許多批評者至今仍不明白的道理：字典可以記錄人們怎麼說話，卻無法命令人們怎麼說話。\n\n就連最讓批評者惱火的那些改變，往往也比表面上看起來更古老。用 they 來指稱一位性別不明的單一人物，常被抨擊是現代人的粗心大意，但這種用法在喬叟、莎士比亞和珍‧奧斯汀的作品中都找得到。2019 年，《韋氏字典》選出 they 作為年度代表字時，並不是在認可一項新發明，而是在肯定一種被賦予了新用途的古老用法。外來語的借用也是如此：英語欣然吸收了 ketchup（番茄醬）這個字，一般認為它源自閩南語中指魚露的詞，而如今沒有人會把它視為一種威脅。\n\n這並不表示怎麼用都可以。清楚的寫作仍然很重要，學生也必須學會考試、求職申請和正式信函中所要求的寫作慣例。但是，知道某個特定場合需要哪些慣例，和相信這些慣例是永恆不變的法則，是截然不同的兩回事。教育應該提供的是前者，而不是後者。\n\n因此，真正的危險不在於改變本身，而在於這些改變所引發的焦慮。一個學生如果被告知，她和朋友說話的方式根本就是「錯的」，可能會學著不信任自己的聲音；然而她真正需要的，是能在不同的語體之間自在轉換，就像她能在和同學聊天與寫信給校長之間自在切換一樣。畢竟，唯一永遠不會改變的語言，是那些已經沒有人在說的語言。幸好，英語完全沒有加入它們行列的危險。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main argument?", "options": ["Teachers should stop correcting students' grammar and spelling.", "Text messaging is causing the decline of the English language.", "Change is a natural sign of a living language rather than a sign of decay.", "Dictionaries should include new slang words as soon as they appear."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段末：Change is not a sign that a language is decaying. It is a sign that the language is alive，全文再以字義演變、史威夫特與約翰遜的例子、單數 they 等加以說明。(A) 第五段說清楚的寫作仍然重要，學生仍須學習寫作慣例；(B) 這是作者所反駁的觀點；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer discuss the words \"nice,\" \"silly\" and \"decimate\"?", "options": ["To show that words now considered normal once had very different meanings", "To prove that Latin is a more precise language than English", "To teach readers the correct meanings they should use in exams", "To argue that the original meanings of these words should be restored"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段以 nice（曾指「愚蠢的」）、silly（曾指「受祝福的」）、decimate（原指十人殺一的刑罰）為例，說明許多現在被認為正常的字，當初的意思截然不同，藉此支持「語言改變並非衰退」的論點。(B)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 與作者觀點相反，作者說若英語被凍結，我們至今還會用 nice 形容愚蠢的人。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest about Samuel Johnson?", "options": ["He succeeded where Swift had failed by fixing English permanently.", "He came to realize that a language could not be controlled.", "He refused to include any new words in his dictionary.", "He persuaded the government to set up Swift's academy."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：約翰遜一開始抱持與史威夫特類似的期望，但到 1755 年字典出版時 he had abandoned them，並把控制語言比喻為「鞭打風」；作者也說他明白 a dictionary can record how people speak, but it cannot command them。(A) 與文意相反；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the main purpose of the fifth paragraph?", "options": ["To admit that the examples given earlier were incorrect", "To introduce another word that has changed its meaning", "To criticize the exam system for being unfair to students", "To limit the argument by acknowledging that conventions still need to be taught"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段以 None of this means that anything goes 開頭，說明清楚的寫作仍然重要，學生仍須學會考試、求職等場合的寫作慣例，只是不應把慣例當成永久不變的法則。這是作者為自己的論點劃定界線，避免被誤解為「怎麼寫都可以」。(A)(B)(C) 皆非本段內容。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In the last paragraph, the phrase \"move between registers\" most likely means ______.", "options": ["switch between different styles of language to suit different situations", "transfer from one school to another", "travel between countries where different languages are spoken", "take turns using different dictionaries"], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段把 move between registers 比作在「和同學聊天」與「寫信給校長」之間切換，可見 register 指依場合而定的語體或語言風格（例如口語與正式用語）。(B) 轉學；(C) 往返於說不同語言的國家之間；(D) 輪流使用不同的字典，皆不合文意。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a236", "title": "Asking for It Back: A Museum's Policy on Returning Cultural Objects", "title_zh": "請求歸還：一座博物館的文物歸還政策", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Culture", "Notice"], "passage": "Harborview Museum of World Cultures. Collections Policy 7: Requests for the Return of Cultural Objects. Approved by the Board of Trustees on 12 January 2026; in effect from 1 March 2026.\n\n1. Purpose. Many objects in the Museum's collection were acquired during periods of colonial rule or armed conflict, and the circumstances in which some of them left their places of origin would not be considered acceptable today. This policy sets out how the Museum will respond when a community, a nation or an individual asks for an object to be returned. It replaces the Museum's previous position, under which no object could be removed from the collection unless it was proven to have been stolen.\n\n2. Who may submit a request. Requests may be made by national governments, by Indigenous and other communities able to demonstrate a cultural connection to an object, or by the descendants of an object's original owners. Requests from dealers or private collectors acting on their own behalf will not be considered.\n\n3. How requests will be assessed. Each request will be reviewed by a Return Review Panel of seven members, at least three of whom must come from outside the Museum. Where a request concerns a particular community, a representative of that community will be invited to take part in the Panel's discussions, though not in its final vote. The Panel will consider (a) how and when the object was acquired, giving particular weight to evidence that it was taken by force or under unequal conditions; (b) whether it left its country of origin after 1970, the year UNESCO adopted its convention against the illegal trade in cultural property, without proper export documents; (c) its religious or cultural significance to the claimant; and (d) whether it can be safely transported and cared for.\n\n4. Timetable. The Museum will acknowledge every request in writing within 30 days of receiving it. The Panel will normally reach a decision within twelve months. Complex cases requiring further research into an object's history may take up to eighteen months, in which case the claimant will be told the reasons for the delay.\n\n5. Possible outcomes. Return is not the only possible result. The Panel may recommend (a) full return of the object; (b) transfer of legal ownership, with the object remaining at the Museum on loan if the claimant so wishes; (c) shared care, under which the object is displayed in both places in turn; or (d) retention by the Museum, in which case a written explanation of the decision will be published on the Museum's website.\n\n6. Costs and appeals. Where a return is approved, the Museum will bear all costs of research, packing and transport. A claimant who disagrees with a decision may appeal once, within 90 days, to the Board of Trustees, whose decision will be final. While a request is under review, the Museum asks that the object remain on public display unless the claimant requests otherwise, for example because the object is sacred and should not be seen by outsiders.", "passage_zh": "港景世界文化博物館　典藏政策第 7 號：文物歸還請求。經董事會於 2026 年 1 月 12 日核准；自 2026 年 3 月 1 日起生效。\n\n一、目的。本館典藏的許多文物，是在殖民統治或武裝衝突期間取得的，其中部分文物離開原屬地的情況，以今日的標準來看並不被接受。本政策說明當某個社群、國家或個人要求歸還文物時，本館將如何回應。本政策取代本館先前的立場；依照先前的立場，除非證實文物遭竊，否則任何文物均不得從館藏中移除。\n\n二、得提出請求者。下列各方得提出請求：國家政府；能證明與文物具有文化淵源的原住民族及其他社群；或文物原所有人的後代。古董商或私人收藏家以個人名義提出的請求，將不予受理。\n\n三、請求之審查方式。每件請求均由七人組成的「歸還審查小組」審查，其中至少三人必須來自館外。若請求涉及特定社群，將邀請該社群的代表參與小組的討論，但不參與最後的表決。小組將考量以下事項：(a) 文物如何及何時取得，並特別重視顯示其係以武力或在不平等條件下取得的證據；(b) 文物是否在 1970 年（聯合國教科文組織通過打擊文化資產非法交易公約之年）以後，未附適當的出口文件而離開原屬國；(c) 文物對請求方的宗教或文化意義；以及 (d) 文物能否安全運送並受到妥善保存。\n\n四、時程。本館將於收到每件請求後 30 天內以書面確認收件。小組通常會在十二個月內作出決定。需要進一步研究文物歷史的複雜案件，最長可能需要十八個月，屆時將告知請求方延後的原因。\n\n五、可能的結果。歸還並非唯一可能的結果。小組可建議：(a) 將文物完整歸還；(b) 移轉法定所有權，若請求方希望，文物可以借展方式繼續留在本館；(c) 共同照管，即文物輪流在雙方所在地展示；或 (d) 由本館保留，在此情況下，本館將於官方網站公布對該決定的書面說明。\n\n六、費用與申訴。若歸還獲准，本館將負擔研究、包裝及運送的全部費用。請求方若不同意決定，得於 90 天內向董事會提出一次申訴，董事會的決定即為最終決定。請求審查期間，除非請求方另有要求（例如該文物屬於聖物，不應讓外人觀看），本館希望該文物繼續公開展示。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this document?", "options": ["To announce that a particular object will be returned to its country of origin", "To explain the procedures the Museum will follow when it is asked to return objects", "To encourage private collectors to donate objects to the Museum", "To apologize for objects that were stolen from the Museum"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第 1 節「目的」明言：This policy sets out how the Museum will respond when a community, a nation or an individual asks for an object to be returned. 全文接著說明誰可提出請求、審查方式、時程、可能結果以及費用與申訴。(A) 文中未提及任何特定文物；(C) 第 2 節反而說不受理私人收藏家的請求，也未提到捐贈；(D) 文中討論的是館藏文物當初如何離開原屬地，並非文物從博物館被偷走。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How does the new policy differ from the Museum's previous position?", "options": ["Previously, objects were removed only if proven stolen; now other circumstances of acquisition can also lead to a return.", "Previously, all requests were accepted; now most of them will be rejected.", "Previously, only communities could make requests; now only governments can.", "Previously, the Museum paid for returns; now claimants must pay."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第 1 節說先前的立場是 no object could be removed from the collection unless it was proven to have been stolen；新政策則會考量文物是否以武力或在不平等條件下取得、是否在 1970 年後未附出口文件離境等因素（第 3 節）。(B)(C) 文中皆無此說法；(D) 第 6 節說歸還獲准時，費用由博物館負擔。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would NOT be able to submit a request under this policy?", "options": ["A national government", "The great-grandson of an object's original owner", "An Indigenous community with a proven cultural link to an object", "An art dealer who hopes to obtain an object and sell it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第 2 節：Requests from dealers or private collectors acting on their own behalf will not be considered. 古董商或私人收藏家以個人名義提出的請求不予受理。(A)(B)(C) 分別是國家政府、原所有人的後代、能證明有文化淵源的原住民族社群，皆可提出請求。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "A community submits a request for a carved mask on 2 April 2026. Which of the following is consistent with the policy?", "options": ["The community's representative casts the deciding vote on the Panel.", "The Panel takes two years to decide and gives no reason for the delay.", "The Museum confirms in writing in April that it has received the request.", "After the return is approved, the community pays to have the mask shipped home."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第 4 節：The Museum will acknowledge every request in writing within 30 days of receiving it. 四月二日提出的請求，在四月內以書面確認收件，符合規定。(A) 第 3 節說社群代表只參與討論，不參與最後表決；(B) 審查最長十八個月，延後時還須告知原因；(D) 第 6 節說歸還獲准時，研究、包裝及運送費用皆由博物館負擔。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "A community wants to become the legal owner of a statue but does not yet have a suitable place to keep it. Under Section 5, which outcome would best meet its needs?", "options": ["Transfer of legal ownership, with the statue staying at the Museum on loan", "Full return of the statue to the community", "Retention by the Museum, with a written explanation published online", "Shared care, with the statue displayed at the Museum and in the community in turn"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第 5 節 (b)：transfer of legal ownership, with the object remaining at the Museum on loan if the claimant so wishes。所有權移轉給請求方，文物則可依其意願以借展方式留在博物館，正好符合「想取得所有權、但暫時沒有合適地方保存」的需求。(B) 完整歸還後必須自行保存文物；(C) 由博物館保留，請求方無法取得所有權；(D) 共同照管要輪流在雙方所在地展示，請求方仍須有地方存放，且文中也未說明所有權歸屬。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a237", "title": "Losing the Dark: The Hidden Cost of a Brighter World", "title_zh": "失去黑夜：一個更明亮世界的隱藏代價", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Environment", "Nature"], "passage": "For almost all of human history, a clear night offered the same spectacle: thousands of stars and the pale band of the Milky Way stretching across the sky. Today, that sight has become a rarity. According to a world atlas of night-sky brightness published in 2016, more than 80 percent of the world's population lives under light-polluted skies, and the Milky Way is hidden from about one-third of humanity, including nearly 80 percent of North Americans. Seldom has so ancient an experience vanished so quickly, and with so little protest.\n\nThe loss is not merely a matter of beauty. Life on Earth evolved with a reliable rhythm of day and night, and countless species depend on darkness to feed, breed and find their way. Moths, which pollinate many plants at night, are drawn to streetlights and circle them until they are exhausted or eaten; one Swiss study found that plants near artificial lights received 62 percent fewer visits from night-time pollinators. Newly hatched sea turtles, which find the ocean by heading toward the brightest horizon, may crawl inland toward roads and hotels instead.\n\nBirds suffer too. Most migratory songbirds travel at night, and bright lights can pull them off course, leaving them circling illuminated buildings until they strike windows or drop from exhaustion. In the United States alone, collisions with buildings are estimated to kill hundreds of millions of birds every year. So powerful is the pull of light that in New York, the twin beams shone into the sky each September in memory of the 2001 attacks are switched off for short periods whenever observers count too many birds trapped inside them.\n\nHumans are hardly immune. Our bodies release melatonin, a hormone that helps regulate sleep, mainly in darkness, and bright light at night can suppress it. Researchers are still investigating how far constant exposure to artificial light contributes to poor sleep and other health problems, but few doubt that a species which evolved under dark skies pays some price for living without them.\n\nUnlike most forms of pollution, however, this one could be reduced almost overnight. Light does not linger in the environment the way plastic or carbon dioxide does; switch off the source, and it is gone. Much of the problem comes from lighting that is badly designed rather than necessary: lamps that shine upward or sideways instead of onto the ground, lights left burning in empty office towers, and harsh blue-white LEDs where warmer tones would serve just as well. Shielding fixtures, dimming them late at night and choosing warmer colors cost little and sacrifice almost nothing in safety.\n\nSome places are already showing what is possible. In 2019, Hehuan Mountain in central Taiwan became Taiwan's first International Dark Sky Park, after local governments, park authorities and residents worked together to reduce stray light in the area. On a clear night there, visitors can once again see what their great-grandparents took for granted. The question is whether the rest of us will decide that such skies are worth reclaiming before we forget what we have lost.", "passage_zh": "在人類歷史的絕大部分時間裡，晴朗的夜晚總會呈現同樣壯觀的景象：成千上萬顆星星，以及橫越天際、淡淡發光的銀河。如今，這樣的景象已經變得罕見。根據 2016 年發表的一份全球夜空亮度地圖集，全世界超過百分之八十的人口生活在受光害影響的天空下，約三分之一的人類看不到銀河，其中包括將近百分之八十的北美洲人。如此古老的體驗，很少像這樣消失得這麼快，卻又幾乎沒有人抗議。\n\n這種損失不只是美不美的問題。地球上的生命是在穩定的晝夜節律中演化而來的，無數物種依靠黑暗來覓食、繁殖和辨認方向。許多植物在夜間靠蛾類授粉，而蛾會被路燈吸引，不停地繞著路燈打轉，直到筋疲力盡或被吃掉；瑞士的一項研究發現，人工光源附近的植物，夜間授粉昆蟲造訪的次數少了百分之六十二。剛孵化的小海龜原本會朝著最明亮的地平線前進、藉此找到大海，如今卻可能轉而爬向內陸的道路和飯店。\n\n鳥類也深受其害。大多數遷徙的鳴禽在夜間飛行，明亮的燈光可能使牠們偏離航道，繞著燈火通明的建築物打轉，直到撞上窗戶或因力竭而墜落。光是在美國，估計每年就有數億隻鳥死於撞擊建築物。燈光的吸引力強大到在紐約，每年九月為紀念 2001 年恐怖攻擊而射向夜空的兩道光束，只要觀察人員計算到有太多鳥被困在光束中，就會暫時關閉一小段時間。\n\n人類也難以倖免。我們的身體主要在黑暗中分泌褪黑激素，這種荷爾蒙有助於調節睡眠，而夜間的強光可能會抑制它的分泌。研究人員仍在探究，長期暴露在人工光線下，對睡眠不佳及其他健康問題的影響究竟有多大；但很少有人懷疑，一個在黑暗夜空下演化而來的物種，在失去黑夜之後必須付出某種代價。\n\n然而，和大多數汙染不同的是，這種汙染幾乎可以在一夕之間減少。光不會像塑膠或二氧化碳那樣滯留在環境中；只要關掉光源，光就消失了。這個問題有很大一部分來自設計不良、而非必要的照明：往上方或側面照射、而不是照向地面的燈具；在空無一人的辦公大樓裡整夜亮著的燈；以及在暖色光就足以勝任的地方，卻使用刺眼的藍白色 LED 燈。為燈具加裝遮罩、在深夜調暗燈光，以及選用較暖的色調，花費不多，在安全上也幾乎沒有任何犧牲。\n\n有些地方已經展現出可能的成果。2019 年，位於台灣中部的合歡山，在地方政府、國家公園管理單位與居民通力合作、減少當地的散逸光線之後，成為台灣第一座國際暗空公園。在晴朗的夜晚，遊客在那裡能再次看見他們的曾祖父母視為理所當然的景象。問題在於，我們其他人是否會在忘記自己失去了什麼之前，認定這樣的夜空值得找回來。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following best summarizes the passage?", "options": ["LED lights are a greater threat to the planet than plastic or carbon dioxide.", "The Milky Way can now be seen only from Hehuan Mountain.", "Artificial light at night harms wildlife and people, but it is a problem that can be reduced fairly easily.", "Scientists have proven that artificial light is the main cause of poor sleep."], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文先說明光害讓多數人看不到銀河，再說明它對蛾、海龜、鳥類與人類的影響（第二至四段），最後指出光害 could be reduced almost overnight，並以合歡山暗空公園為例。(A) 文中只說光不像塑膠或二氧化碳那樣會滯留，並未比較誰的威脅較大；(B) 文中未說只有合歡山看得到銀河；(D) 第四段說研究人員仍在探究（still investigating），並未證實。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the sentence \"Seldom has so ancient an experience vanished so quickly, and with so little protest\" suggest?", "options": ["People have protested strongly against light pollution for many years.", "The loss of starry skies has happened fast and attracted little attention.", "Looking at the stars is a modern hobby that is losing popularity.", "Scientists discovered the Milky Way only recently."], "answer": 1, "explain": "這是否定副詞 seldom 置於句首的倒裝句，意思是「如此古老的體驗，很少消失得這麼快、又這麼少人抗議」，表示星空正迅速消失，卻很少有人注意或反對。(A) 與 so little protest 相反；(C) 文中說觀星是人類歷史上一直都有的體驗，並非現代嗜好；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why are the beams of light in New York sometimes switched off for short periods?", "options": ["To save electricity late at night", "To allow people to see the stars more clearly", "To show respect during a moment of silence", "To free birds that have been drawn into the light and become trapped"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：... are switched off for short periods whenever observers count too many birds trapped inside them. 當觀察人員發現太多鳥被困在光束中時，光束會暫時關閉，好讓鳥兒離開。(A)(B)(C) 文中皆未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer say that light pollution \"could be reduced almost overnight\"?", "options": ["Unlike plastic or carbon dioxide, light disappears as soon as its source is turned off.", "Most countries have already passed laws banning outdoor lighting.", "New kinds of LEDs give off no light at all after midnight.", "Birds and insects quickly get used to bright lights."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：Light does not linger in the environment the way plastic or carbon dioxide does; switch off the source, and it is gone. 光不會滯留在環境中，關掉光源就消失了。(B)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 與文中鳥類和昆蟲受害的描述相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the tone of the last paragraph?", "options": ["Bitter and hopeless", "Amused and light-hearted", "Neutral and purely factual", "Hopeful, but urging readers to act"], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段先以合歡山為例，說明 Some places are already showing what is possible（已有地方展現出可能的成果），語氣帶有希望；最後一句則提醒我們，應在忘記失去了什麼之前，決定是否要找回這樣的夜空，帶有呼籲行動的意味。(A) 作者並不悲觀絕望；(B) 文中沒有輕鬆詼諧的語氣；(C) 最後一句明顯帶有作者的呼籲，並非純粹陳述事實。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a238", "title": "The Wallet Test: Are People More Honest Than We Think?", "title_zh": "錢包測驗：人們比我們以為的更誠實嗎？", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Society", "Science"], "passage": "Imagine that a stranger hands you a wallet and says he found it on the street. Inside are a few business cards, a key, a shopping list and some cash. The owner's email address is printed on the cards. Would you contact the owner? And would the amount of money inside make any difference?\n\nIn one of the largest experiments on honesty ever carried out, a team of economists set out to answer exactly these questions. Research assistants handed in more than 17,000 \"lost\" wallets at banks, hotels, post offices, police stations and other public institutions in 355 cities across 40 countries. Each assistant told the person at the reception desk that he or she had found the wallet nearby and was in a hurry, then left without giving any contact details. The researchers then simply waited to see how many recipients would email the owner. Their findings were published in the journal Science in 2019.\n\nStandard economic theory makes a clear prediction. A purely self-interested person keeps whatever is valuable, so the more money a wallet contains, the less likely it should be to be returned. When the researchers asked hundreds of professional economists to predict the outcome, most expected exactly that. They were wrong. In almost every country, wallets containing money were reported more often than empty ones: overall, the rate rose from 40 percent for wallets with no cash to 51 percent for wallets holding about US$13. In a follow-up conducted in three countries, wallets containing nearly $100 were reported more often still.\n\nWhy would people act against their apparent financial interest? The researchers' analysis pointed to two motives. The first is altruism, a genuine concern for the owner. Tellingly, wallets containing a key, which is valuable to its owner but useless to anyone else, were reported more often than otherwise identical wallets without one. The second motive, which explains why more cash led to more returns, is the wish to avoid seeing oneself as a thief. Keeping an empty wallet costs a person's self-image very little; keeping one full of cash makes the act feel unmistakably like stealing. The more money there is, the higher the psychological price of keeping it.\n\nThe study's findings carry a lesson that reaches well beyond lost property. Economists, and ordinary people as well, tend to underestimate how honest others are. This pessimism matters. Some researchers argue that systems designed for a world of cheats, with endless forms, constant checks and penalties at every turn, may send the message that dishonesty is normal. It is not that rules are unnecessary; rather, rules that assume the worst of people risk encouraging the very behavior they were meant to prevent.\n\nNone of this means that everyone is honest; in some countries, most wallets were never reported at all. Nevertheless, the next time you assume that a stranger would keep your money, remember that in this experiment, at least, the presence of cash made people more, not less, likely to do the right thing.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，有個陌生人遞給你一個錢包，說是他在街上撿到的。錢包裡有幾張名片、一把鑰匙、一張購物清單，還有一些現金。名片上印著失主的電子郵件地址。你會聯絡失主嗎？錢包裡的錢多寡，會不會影響你的決定？\n\n在有史以來規模最大的誠實度實驗之一中，一組經濟學家正是著手要回答這些問題。研究助理們把超過一萬七千個「遺失」的錢包，交給 40 個國家、355 座城市的銀行、旅館、郵局、警察局及其他公共機構。每位助理都告訴櫃檯人員，錢包是自己在附近撿到的，而自己正在趕時間，然後沒有留下任何聯絡方式就離開了。接著，研究人員只要等著看有多少收到錢包的人會寄電子郵件給失主。他們的研究結果在 2019 年發表於《科學》期刊。\n\n傳統經濟學理論對此有明確的預測：一個完全自利的人會把任何有價值的東西據為己有，所以錢包裡的錢越多，被歸還的可能性應該越低。研究人員請數百位專業經濟學家預測實驗結果時，大多數人的預期正是如此。結果他們錯了。幾乎在每一個國家，裝有現金的錢包都比空錢包更常被通報：整體而言，通報率從沒有現金錢包的百分之四十，上升到裝有約 13 美元錢包的百分之五十一。在三個國家進行的後續實驗中，裝有將近 100 美元的錢包，被通報的比率還更高。\n\n為什麼人們的行為會違背自己表面上的經濟利益？研究人員的分析指出了兩個動機。第一個是利他心，也就是真心為失主著想。耐人尋味的是，裝有鑰匙的錢包（鑰匙對失主很重要，對其他人卻毫無用處），比其他條件完全相同、但沒有鑰匙的錢包更常被通報。第二個動機，也就是能解釋為何現金越多、通報越多的原因，是人們不希望把自己看成小偷。把一個空錢包據為己有，對一個人的自我形象幾乎沒有什麼損害；但把一個裝滿現金的錢包留下來，就會讓這個行為明明白白地感覺像是偷竊。錢越多，把它留下來的心理代價就越高。\n\n這項研究的發現所帶來的啟示，遠遠超出遺失物這件事本身。經濟學家和一般人一樣，往往低估了別人的誠實程度。這種悲觀的看法影響重大。有些研究人員認為，為一個滿是騙子的世界所設計的制度，例如沒完沒了的表格、時時刻刻的檢查、處處都有的罰則，可能會傳達出「不誠實是常態」的訊息。這並不是說規則沒有必要，而是說，凡事把人往最壞處想的規則，可能反而會助長它們原本要防止的行為。\n\n這一切並不表示每個人都是誠實的；在某些國家，大部分的錢包從來沒有被通報過。儘管如此，下次當你認定陌生人會把你的錢據為己有時，請記得：至少在這個實驗裡，錢包裡有現金，反而讓人們更有可能、而不是更不可能，做出正確的事。", "questions": [{"q": "What did most professional economists predict about the experiment?", "options": ["Wallets would be returned more often in rich countries than in poor ones.", "Almost no wallets would be returned at all.", "The amount of money in a wallet would make no difference.", "Wallets containing more money would be reported less often."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：the more money a wallet contains, the less likely it should be to be returned ... most expected exactly that. 大多數經濟學家預期錢越多，被歸還的機率越低。(A)(B) 文中皆未提及；(C) 他們預期金額會有影響，只是方向與實驗結果相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention wallets that contained a key?", "options": ["To show that concern for the owner was one reason people reported the wallets", "To explain how the researchers got into the owners' homes", "To suggest that keys are more valuable than cash", "To prove that people return only items that are useless to them"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說明第一個動機「利他心」時舉例：鑰匙對失主有價值、對別人卻沒有用，而裝有鑰匙的錢包更常被通報，顯示人們確實會為失主著想；Tellingly（很能說明問題地）一字也點出這個例子的作用。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 文中沒有比較鑰匙與現金的價值；(D) 裝有現金的錢包也更常被通報，與此說法矛盾。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what best explains why wallets with more cash were reported more often?", "options": ["People expected to receive a large reward from the owner.", "Keeping a wallet full of money would make people feel like thieves.", "Reception staff were afraid of being caught on security cameras.", "The researchers paid the recipients to report the wallets."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：The second motive, which explains why more cash led to more returns, is the wish to avoid seeing oneself as a thief ... The more money there is, the higher the psychological price of keeping it. 錢越多，留下錢包就越像偷竊，心理代價也越高。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In the fifth paragraph, what does \"This pessimism\" refer to?", "options": ["The belief that rules are completely unnecessary", "The fear of losing one's wallet in a foreign country", "The tendency to underestimate how honest other people are", "The view that economists themselves are dishonest"], "answer": 2, "explain": "This pessimism 指的是前一句 Economists, and ordinary people as well, tend to underestimate how honest others are，也就是低估他人誠實程度的悲觀看法。(A) 作者明說 It is not that rules are unnecessary；(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Based on the researchers' explanation of people's motives, which of the following wallets would most likely be reported most often?", "options": ["A wallet containing a key and about US$100", "A wallet containing a key but no money", "A wallet containing about US$100 but no key", "A wallet containing neither money nor a key"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段指出兩個動機：一是利他心，所以裝有鑰匙（對失主重要、對別人沒用）的錢包更常被通報；二是不想把自己看成小偷，所以錢越多，被通報的比率越高（第三段也說裝有近 100 美元的錢包被通報得更多）。同時有鑰匙和大筆現金的錢包，兩種動機都最強。(B) 只有鑰匙、沒有現金；(C) 有現金卻沒有鑰匙；(D) 兩者皆無，最不可能被通報。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a239", "title": "Gold Medals for Poems: When the Olympics Judged Art", "title_zh": "詩作也能拿金牌：奧運評比藝術的年代", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Sports", "Arts"], "passage": "At the 1912 Olympic Games in Stockholm, the gold medal for literature went to an \"Ode to Sport\" submitted by two authors named Georges Hohrod and M. Eschbach. Neither man existed. Behind both names stood Baron Pierre de Coubertin, the founder of the modern Olympics, who had entered the competition himself, reportedly because he feared that too few genuine writers would take part.\n\nThe episode is less strange than it sounds. Coubertin saw the Olympics as a revival of ancient Greek ideals, according to which a cultivated person should develop the mind as well as the body. From 1912 to 1948, medals were therefore awarded not only for running and swimming but also for architecture, literature, music, painting and sculpture, provided that the works had been inspired by sport. Paintings of boxers, poems about runners and designs for stadiums competed for the same gold, silver and bronze as the athletes.\n\nA few competitors excelled in both arenas. Walter Winans, an American marksman who had already won a shooting gold in 1908, took the 1912 gold for sculpture with a small bronze of a horse pulling a light racing cart. Alfréd Hajós, the Hungarian swimmer who had won two races at the first modern Games in 1896, earned a silver medal in 1924 for a stadium he had designed with a partner. Careers like these were precisely what Coubertin had dreamed of.\n\nYet the art competitions never achieved the prestige of the sporting events. Judges struggled to compare entries of very different kinds, and in several years they declined to award a gold medal in some categories at all, judging no entry good enough. Many leading artists stayed away, unwilling to risk their reputations or simply uninterested in painting athletes. The deeper problem, however, was money. The Olympic movement at the time insisted on strict amateurism: athletes who earned a living from their sport were barred from competing. Artists, by contrast, were almost by definition professionals who sold their work. To let paid painters compete while forbidding paid runners struck many officials as inconsistent.\n\nIn 1949, the International Olympic Committee decided to replace the competitions with exhibitions at which no medals would be awarded, and since 1952 a cultural program without prizes has accompanied the Games instead. The medals already won were eventually removed from the official records, and they no longer count toward any country's total.\n\nWas the experiment a failure? Perhaps, but it was a revealing one. It exposed how hard it is to measure artistic excellence, and how strongly the ideal of amateurism once shaped the Games, an ideal the Olympics themselves have long since abandoned, now that professional athletes compete openly. Were the art competitions to be revived today, the old objection about professionals would hardly apply. Whether anyone could agree on who had painted the best picture, however, is another matter entirely.", "passage_zh": "在 1912 年的斯德哥爾摩奧運會上，文學類金牌頒給了一首〈運動頌〉，署名的作者是喬治‧霍羅德和 M‧埃施巴赫兩人。但這兩個人根本不存在。站在這兩個名字背後的，是現代奧運的創辦人古柏坦男爵；據說他之所以親自參賽，是因為擔心真正參賽的作家太少。\n\n這件事其實沒有聽起來那麼奇怪。古柏坦認為奧運是古希臘理想的復興；依照這種理想，一個有教養的人應該心智與身體並重發展。因此，從 1912 年到 1948 年，奧運不只為賽跑和游泳頒發獎牌，也為建築、文學、音樂、繪畫和雕塑頒獎，條件是作品必須以運動為靈感。描繪拳擊手的畫作、歌詠跑者的詩，以及體育場的設計圖，都和運動員一樣角逐金、銀、銅牌。\n\n有少數參賽者在兩個領域都表現傑出。美國神射手華特‧威南斯在 1908 年就已贏得射擊金牌，1912 年又以一座小型銅像（一匹馬拉著輕便的賽馬車）奪得雕塑類金牌。曾在 1896 年第一屆現代奧運贏得兩項游泳比賽的匈牙利選手阿爾弗雷德‧哈約什，則在 1924 年以他和夥伴共同設計的一座體育場，獲得一面銀牌。這樣的經歷，正是古柏坦夢寐以求的。\n\n然而，藝術競賽始終沒有獲得和體育項目一樣的聲望。評審很難比較性質迥異的作品，有好幾屆，他們甚至在某些類別完全不頒發金牌，認為沒有任何作品夠水準。許多頂尖藝術家也不願參加，有的是不想拿自己的名聲冒險，有的則根本對畫運動員不感興趣。不過，更深層的問題在於金錢。當時的奧林匹克運動堅持嚴格的業餘主義：靠運動維生的運動員不得參賽。相較之下，藝術家幾乎按定義就是出售自己作品的職業人士。讓領酬勞的畫家參賽，卻禁止領酬勞的跑者參賽，在許多官員看來標準並不一致。\n\n1949 年，國際奧林匹克委員會決定以不頒發獎牌的展覽取代這些競賽；自 1952 年起，伴隨奧運舉行的改為不設獎項的文化活動。已經頒出的獎牌，後來也從官方紀錄中被移除，不再計入任何國家的獎牌總數。\n\n這項實驗失敗了嗎？或許是吧，但這是一次很有啟發性的失敗。它揭露了衡量藝術成就有多麼困難，也顯示出業餘主義的理想曾經多麼深刻地影響奧運；而如今職業運動員已經公開參賽，奧運本身也早已放棄了這個理想。如果今天要恢復藝術競賽，過去關於職業人士的反對理由幾乎不再適用。然而，大家能不能對誰畫出了最好的作品達成共識，就完全是另一回事了。", "questions": [{"q": "Why did Coubertin enter the 1912 literature competition under invented names?", "options": ["He wanted to win a medal for his country without anyone noticing.", "He reportedly feared that not enough real writers would take part.", "The rules did not allow members of the nobility to compete.", "He wanted to test whether the judges could recognize good poetry."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：who had entered the competition himself, reportedly because he feared that too few genuine writers would take part. 據說他擔心真正參賽的作家太少，所以親自以假名參賽。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following entries would have met the main condition for the Olympic art competitions?", "options": ["A landscape painting by a famous professional artist", "A marble statue of an ancient Greek philosopher", "A poem describing the final lap of a cycling race", "A piece of music written to celebrate a royal wedding"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：medals were ... awarded ... for architecture, literature, music, painting and sculpture, provided that the works had been inspired by sport. 作品唯一的主要條件是「以運動為靈感」，描寫自行車賽最後一圈的詩符合條件。(A) 風景畫與運動無關（是否為職業藝術家並非參賽條件）；(B) 古柏坦崇尚的是古希臘「身心並重」的理想，並不表示古希臘題材就能參賽；(D) 為王室婚禮而寫的音樂也與運動無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention Walter Winans and Alfréd Hajós?", "options": ["To give examples of the all-round achievement that Coubertin hoped the Games would encourage", "To show that only athletes were allowed to enter the art competitions", "To explain why the art competitions were finally ended", "To prove that the judges favored American and Hungarian artists"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說這兩人在體育與藝術兩個領域都獲得獎牌，並以 Careers like these were precisely what Coubertin had dreamed of 作結，呼應第二段古柏坦「心智與身體並重」的理想。(B) 文中未說只有運動員能參加藝術競賽；(C) 停辦原因在第四、五段，與兩人無關；(D) 文中未提及評審偏袒。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what was the deeper reason the art competitions were dropped?", "options": ["Too few people came to see the paintings and sculptures.", "The judges refused to award any medals after 1948.", "Coubertin decided that art had nothing to do with sport.", "Allowing professional artists to compete clashed with the rule that athletes had to be amateurs."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The deeper problem, however, was money. 當時奧運堅持業餘主義，靠運動維生的運動員不得參賽，但藝術家幾乎都是出售作品的職業人士；讓領酬勞的畫家參賽卻禁止領酬勞的跑者參賽，許多官員認為標準不一。(A) 文中未提及觀眾人數；(B) 文中只說有幾屆在某些類別未頒金牌；(C) 古柏坦正是推動藝術競賽的人。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer imply in the last paragraph?", "options": ["The art competitions should be brought back as soon as possible.", "Although the old objection no longer applies, judging art would still be very difficult.", "Professional athletes are still banned from the Olympics today.", "The art competitions failed because the artworks were of poor quality."], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一段以假設語氣的倒裝句 Were the art competitions to be revived today 指出，如今職業運動員已公開參賽，過去反對職業藝術家的理由幾乎不再適用；但接著說大家能否對誰畫得最好達成共識 is another matter entirely，暗示評判藝術依舊困難。(A) 作者並未主張恢復藝術競賽；(C) 文中說 professional athletes compete openly，與選項相反；(D) 文中未說作品品質差是失敗的原因。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a240", "title": "Was Tulip Mania Really So Mad? Rethinking a Famous Bubble", "title_zh": "鬱金香狂熱真的那麼瘋狂嗎？重新檢視一場著名的泡沫", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["History", "Money"], "passage": "Ask an economist for an example of a financial bubble, and you may well hear about tulips. According to the familiar story, the Dutch Republic in the 1630s was swept by a madness so complete that people sold their houses to buy single flower bulbs. When prices collapsed in February 1637, fortunes vanished overnight, ruined merchants threw themselves into canals, and one of the richest economies in Europe was shaken to its foundations. It is a vivid tale. It is also, according to a number of historians, largely untrue.\n\nThe basic facts are not in dispute. Tulips, which had reached Europe from the Ottoman Empire in the sixteenth century, had become luxury goods among wealthy Dutch collectors. The most prized varieties had petals marked with dramatic flame-like streaks, a pattern that growers could not reliably reproduce. Only in the twentieth century did scientists discover why: the streaks were caused by a virus, which also weakened the bulbs and made them multiply slowly, keeping them rare. During the winter of 1636-37, prices for some bulbs rose to extraordinary levels, with a single bulb reportedly selling for more than ten times the yearly income of a skilled craftsman. Then, in early February, buyers stopped appearing, and prices fell sharply.\n\nWhat is disputed is how much damage followed. Much of the popular story can be traced to a book published in 1841 by the Scottish journalist Charles Mackay, who relied heavily on moralizing pamphlets printed shortly after the crash, pamphlets written precisely to warn readers against greed. When the historian Anne Goldgar examined Dutch archives for her 2007 study, she found little evidence of widespread ruin. She could not identify a single person who had gone bankrupt because of tulips, and she found no record of the suicides that later writers described.\n\nPart of the explanation lies in who was trading. The buyers were mostly prosperous merchants and skilled artisans, not poor laborers gambling their savings. Moreover, much of the trade took the form of contracts for bulbs that were still in the ground, to be delivered after they were dug up in the summer. When prices fell, many of these contracts were simply never honored, and the disputes dragged on for months. People certainly lost money they had expected to make, but losing a paper profit is not the same as losing one's house.\n\nIf tulip mania was less dramatic than legend suggests, why has the legend lasted so long? One reason is that it offers a satisfying moral lesson: greed leads to ruin. Another is that commentators have found it useful whenever a new bubble, in railway shares, internet companies or cryptocurrencies, seemed to call for a historical warning. The story survived, in other words, not because it was accurate but because it was useful.\n\nNone of this means that markets are always rational, or that bubbles never happen; they clearly do. What the tulips teach us is something subtler: even our cautionary tales about foolish crowds can spread in foolish ways. That is worth remembering the next time we are tempted to repeat one.", "passage_zh": "請一位經濟學家舉個金融泡沫的例子，你很可能會聽到鬱金香的故事。根據大家耳熟能詳的說法，1630 年代的荷蘭共和國陷入一場全面的瘋狂，人們甚至賣掉房子，只為了買一顆花的球莖。1637 年 2 月價格崩盤時，大筆財富一夜之間化為烏有，破產的商人紛紛跳進運河自盡，而歐洲最富裕的經濟體之一也因此根基動搖。這是個生動的故事。然而根據多位歷史學家的看法，它大部分並非事實。\n\n基本的事實並沒有爭議。鬱金香在十六世紀從鄂圖曼帝國傳入歐洲，後來成為荷蘭富有收藏家之間的奢侈品。最珍貴的品種，花瓣上有著醒目的火焰狀條紋，而種植者無法穩定地培育出這種花紋。直到二十世紀，科學家才發現原因：這些條紋是由一種病毒造成的，這種病毒同時也會削弱球莖，使它們繁殖得很慢，因而一直保持稀有。在 1636 到 1637 年的冬天，某些球莖的價格漲到驚人的程度，據說單單一顆球莖，就賣出了超過一名熟練工匠年收入十倍的價錢。接著，到了二月初，買家不再出現，價格隨即暴跌。\n\n有爭議的是隨之而來的損害究竟有多大。大眾所熟知的說法，很大一部分可以追溯到蘇格蘭記者查爾斯‧麥凱在 1841 年出版的一本書，而他大量引用的，是崩盤後不久印行的說教小冊子，這些小冊子寫作的目的，正是要警告讀者不要貪婪。歷史學家安妮‧戈德加為了她在 2007 年發表的研究查閱荷蘭的檔案資料時，幾乎找不到大範圍破產的證據。她找不到任何一個因鬱金香而破產的人，也找不到後來的作家所描述的那些自殺事件的紀錄。\n\n部分原因在於參與交易的是哪些人。買家大多是富裕的商人和技術純熟的工匠，而不是拿積蓄去賭的窮苦勞工。此外，大部分的交易形式，是買賣仍埋在土裡的球莖的合約，球莖要等到夏天挖出來之後才交貨。價格下跌時，許多這類合約乾脆就沒有履行，相關的糾紛也拖延了好幾個月。人們確實損失了原本預期能賺到的錢，但失去帳面上的利潤，和失去自己的房子並不是同一回事。\n\n如果鬱金香狂熱並不像傳說中那樣戲劇化，為什麼這個傳說能流傳這麼久？原因之一是它提供了一個令人滿意的道德教訓：貪婪會導致毀滅。另一個原因是，每當新的泡沫出現，無論是鐵路股票、網路公司還是加密貨幣，似乎需要一則歷史警訊時，評論家都發現這個故事很好用。換句話說，這個故事之所以流傳下來，不是因為它正確，而是因為它有用。\n\n這一切並不表示市場總是理性的，也不表示泡沫從來不會發生；泡沫顯然確實存在。鬱金香給我們的教訓更為微妙：就連我們用來警告世人「群眾很愚蠢」的故事，也可能以愚蠢的方式散播開來。下次當我們忍不住想重述這樣一個故事時，值得記住這一點。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in this passage?", "options": ["To warn readers not to invest in cryptocurrencies", "To challenge the popular account of tulip mania using historical evidence", "To explain how tulips were brought from the Ottoman Empire to Europe", "To prove that financial bubbles have never really existed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段先敘述大眾熟知的鬱金香狂熱故事，再說 It is also ... largely untrue，接著以戈德加查閱檔案的研究等證據，說明這個傳說誇大了損害。(A) 加密貨幣只是第五段舉的例子；(C) 只是第二段的背景資料；(D) 最後一段明說泡沫 clearly do（確實會發生）。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what caused the flame-like streaks on the most valuable tulips?", "options": ["Special methods used by skilled Dutch growers", "The cold winter of 1636-37", "A virus that also made the bulbs weaker and slower to multiply", "Mixing tulips from the Ottoman Empire with Dutch flowers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：the streaks were caused by a virus, which also weakened the bulbs and made them multiply slowly. 條紋是病毒造成的，這種病毒也會削弱球莖、使其繁殖緩慢。(A) 文中說種植者無法穩定培育出這種花紋；(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer point out that Mackay relied on pamphlets \"written precisely to warn readers against greed\"?", "options": ["To praise Mackay for his careful research", "To show that the pamphlets were the most reliable sources available", "To explain why Mackay's book sold so many copies", "To suggest that Mackay's sources may have exaggerated the damage in order to teach a moral lesson"], "answer": 3, "explain": "作者強調麥凱所依據的小冊子是為了「警告讀者不要貪婪」而寫的，暗示這些資料帶有說教目的，可能誇大了災情；下一句也指出戈德加查閱檔案後，幾乎找不到大範圍破產的證據。(A)(B) 與作者的質疑態度相反；(C) 文中未提及書的銷量。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In the fourth paragraph, \"losing a paper profit\" refers to ______.", "options": ["losing important documents during the crash", "paying too much for printed contracts", "failing to receive money that one had expected to make from a deal", "selling a house in order to buy tulip bulbs"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說價格下跌時許多合約沒有被履行，人們 lost money they had expected to make（損失了原本預期能賺到的錢）；paper profit 即「帳面上的利潤」，指尚未真正到手的獲利。(A)(B) 誤解了 paper 在此的意思；(D) 正是作者說和「失去帳面利潤」不同的情況（losing one's house）。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the writer, why has the legend of tulip mania lasted so long?", "options": ["It has been carefully proven by economists.", "It teaches a satisfying moral lesson and provides a handy warning whenever a new bubble appears.", "Dutch schools still teach it as part of their national history.", "Mackay's book is still the only source on the subject."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：it offers a satisfying moral lesson ... commentators have found it useful whenever a new bubble ... seemed to call for a historical warning ... not because it was accurate but because it was useful. (A) 與文意相反；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 文中還提到戈德加 2007 年的研究，麥凱的書並非唯一的資料來源。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a241", "title": "The Paradox in the Coal Cellar: Jevons and the Limits of Efficiency", "title_zh": "煤窖裡的悖論：傑文斯與效率的極限", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Environment", "History"], "passage": "In 1865 a twenty-nine-year-old economist named William Stanley Jevons published a book with an unglamorous title, The Coal Question, and an alarming thesis. Britain's industrial supremacy, he argued, rested on cheap coal, and cheap coal would not last: as the shallower seams were exhausted and miners were forced ever deeper, costs would climb, and the nation's lead would slip away to rivals with richer deposits. The book won him admirers in Parliament. Yet what has outlived its forecast is a single chapter, almost incidental to the whole, in which Jevons took aim at a remedy that most of his contemporaries regarded as self-evident.\n\nThe remedy was efficiency. If coal was scarce, surely the answer was to burn less of it for each unit of work, and British engineers had been doing exactly that for a century. Jevons thought the reasoning not merely mistaken but backwards. It was, he wrote, a confusion of ideas to suppose that the economical use of fuel amounted to a diminished consumption; the very contrary was the truth. His evidence was history itself. Each improvement to the steam engine, from James Watt's onward, had made steam power cheaper, and cheaper power had not been hoarded but spent—on more mills, more mines, more locomotives—until the total quantity of coal consumed far exceeded anything the cruder engines had ever devoured. Efficiency, far from rescuing a finite resource, had widened the circle of its uses.\n\nStated so baldly, the argument sounds like a paradox designed to provoke, and for a long time it was treated as little more than one. Not until the oil shocks of the 1970s, when governments began to promote energy efficiency as a matter of policy, did economists return to it in earnest. What they found was less dramatic than Jevons's version but no less awkward. When a car uses less fuel per kilometer, the cost of driving falls, and people tend to drive somewhat more; when lighting becomes cheaper, rooms, streets and shop windows are lit more brightly and for longer. Economists now call this the rebound effect. They distinguish its direct form, in which the savings are spent on more of the same service, from its indirect form, in which money saved on fuel is spent on other goods that themselves require energy to produce.\n\nThe crucial question is one of size, and here intellectual honesty demands a qualification that enthusiasts on both sides are inclined to omit. Most studies of households in wealthy countries suggest that the rebound claws back only a fraction of the expected savings—enough to embarrass engineers' projections, rarely enough to cancel them. Jevons's full paradox, in which greater efficiency actually raises total consumption, seems most likely where energy accounts for a large share of a product's cost and where demand for that product is still far from satisfied: precisely the conditions of nineteenth-century Britain, with its hungry and expanding industries. That the paradox held for steam does not oblige it to hold for refrigerators.\n\nIt would be convenient, then, to file Jevons away as a man who generalized too boldly from a single extraordinary century. Convenient, but not quite fair. The deeper point of his chapter was not that efficiency is useless but that it is not, by itself, a policy for consuming less. A more efficient technology makes a resource more useful, and a more useful resource is, other things being equal, one that people will want more of. If the aim is to reduce total consumption—of coal then, of carbon now—efficiency has to be paired with something that keeps the savings from simply being spent: a price, a limit, or a deliberate choice.\n\nThere is a final irony that Jevons did not live to see. Britain never ran out of coal. Its output peaked in 1913 and declined thereafter, not because the seams were exhausted but largely because, over the decades that followed, oil, and later gas and other sources, did much of the same work more cheaply. The prophet of scarcity was wrong about scarcity; the observation he made almost in passing has proved the more durable insight. Predictions about resources, it seems, age badly. Arguments about human behavior, when they are good ones, have a way of lasting.", "passage_zh": "1865 年，一位名叫威廉‧史坦利‧傑文斯、年方二十九歲的經濟學家出版了一本書，書名平淡無奇——《煤炭問題》——論點卻令人憂心。他主張，英國的工業霸權建立在廉價的煤炭之上，而廉價的煤炭不會長久：隨著較淺的煤層逐漸採盡、礦工被迫越挖越深，成本將不斷攀升，英國的領先地位也將拱手讓給礦藏更豐富的對手。這本書為他在國會中贏得了一些欣賞者。然而，比書中預測更經得起時間考驗的，卻是其中單單一章——就全書而言幾乎只是順帶一提——傑文斯在這一章裡，把矛頭指向一個當時多數人都認為不證自明的解方。\n\n這個解方就是效率。如果煤炭稀缺，答案想必就是讓每一單位的工作少燒一點煤，而英國的工程師一百年來做的正是這件事。傑文斯卻認為這種推理不只是錯誤，而且根本是本末倒置。他寫道，以為節約使用燃料就等於減少消耗，是一種觀念上的混淆；事實恰恰相反。他的證據就是歷史本身。從詹姆斯‧瓦特的改良開始，蒸汽機的每一次進步都讓蒸汽動力變得更便宜，而更便宜的動力並沒有被省下來，而是被花掉了——用在更多的工廠、更多的礦場、更多的火車頭上——直到消耗的煤炭總量，遠遠超過那些較粗糙的引擎所曾吞噬的任何數量。效率非但沒有拯救一種有限的資源，反而擴大了它的用途範圍。\n\n說得這麼直白，這個論點聽起來像是一個故意挑釁的悖論，而在很長一段時間裡，人們也確實只把它當成這樣的東西。直到 1970 年代的石油危機，各國政府開始把提升能源效率當作政策來推動，經濟學家才認真地重新研究它。他們的發現沒有傑文斯的版本那麼戲劇化，但同樣令人尷尬。當一輛車每公里耗油更少，開車的成本就下降，人們往往會開得多一些；當照明變得更便宜，房間、街道和商店櫥窗就會被照得更亮、更久。經濟學家現在稱之為「反彈效應」。他們區分了它的直接形式——省下的錢被花在更多同樣的服務上——以及間接形式——在燃料上省下的錢被花在其他本身也需要能源來生產的商品上。\n\n關鍵問題在於規模，而在這一點上，知識上的誠實要求我們加上一個雙方熱衷者都傾向於略去的但書。針對富裕國家家庭所做的大多數研究顯示，反彈只會把預期節省的一小部分又吃回去——足以讓工程師的預估難堪，卻很少足以把節省完全抵銷。傑文斯那種完整的悖論，也就是效率提高反而使總消耗增加，似乎最可能發生在能源占產品成本很大比例、而且市場對該產品的需求還遠未被滿足的情況下：而這恰恰就是十九世紀英國的情況，那時它的產業對能源如飢似渴、不斷擴張。這個悖論在蒸汽機身上成立，並不表示它在冰箱身上也必然成立。\n\n這麼一來，我們大可以省事地把傑文斯歸類為一個僅憑一個非凡的世紀，就過於大膽地歸納出通則的人。省事是省事，卻不太公平。他那一章更深層的重點並不是效率無用，而是效率本身並不構成一套減少消耗的政策。更有效率的技術讓一種資源變得更有用，而在其他條件不變的情況下，更有用的資源就是人們會想要更多的資源。如果目標是減少總消耗——當年是煤，如今是碳——效率就必須搭配某種能防止省下來的部分被直接花掉的東西：一個價格、一個上限，或是一個刻意的選擇。\n\n最後還有一個傑文斯沒能活著看到的諷刺。英國從未把煤用完。英國的煤產量在 1913 年達到高峰，此後便逐漸下滑；原因不是煤層採盡，而主要是在接下來的數十年間，石油，以及後來的天然氣和其他能源，能以更低的成本完成許多相同的工作。這位預言稀缺的人在稀缺這件事上錯了；他幾乎是順帶提出的觀察，反而成了更經久的洞見。看來，關於資源的預測往往經不起時間的考驗；而關於人類行為的論證，只要是好的論證，往往能歷久不衰。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To prove that improving energy efficiency is a waste of time and money", "To show that an argument Jevons made almost in passing remains relevant, though in a more limited form than he stated it", "To explain why Britain's coal output began to decline after 1913", "To criticize nineteenth-century engineers for wasting Britain's coal"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段 what has outlived its forecast is a single chapter, almost incidental to the whole，接著介紹傑文斯「效率反而增加消耗」的論點；第四段指出 Most studies... suggest that the rebound claws back only a fraction，說明完整的悖論只在特定條件下成立；第六段再說這個順帶提出的觀察 has proved the more durable insight。可見作者旨在說明此論點至今仍有意義，但適用範圍比傑文斯所說的有限。(A) 太極端，第五段明言重點 not that efficiency is useless；(C) 1913 年煤產量達到高峰只是第六段的細節；(D) 文中並未批評工程師浪費煤炭，反而說他們一直在提高效率。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, under which conditions is greater efficiency most likely to increase total consumption?", "options": ["When households in wealthy countries buy more efficient appliances", "When governments set a firm limit on how much energy may be used", "When the money saved on fuel is spent on unrelated goods", "When energy makes up a large share of a product's cost and demand for the product is far from satisfied"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：Jevons's full paradox... seems most likely where energy accounts for a large share of a product's cost and where demand for that product is still far from satisfied。(A) 同段指出針對富裕國家家庭的研究顯示，反彈只抵銷 only a fraction，並未使總消耗增加；(B) 第五段說價格、上限等正是防止節省被花掉的方法，與題意相反；(C) 這是第三段所說的間接反彈效應，文中並未說它最可能使總消耗增加。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What is the function of the sentence \"That the paradox held for steam does not oblige it to hold for refrigerators\" in paragraph 4?", "options": ["It warns against assuming that what was true in one historical setting must be true everywhere.", "It suggests that refrigerators are more efficient than steam engines.", "It claims that Jevons's paradox has been completely disproved.", "It explains why modern households own more refrigerators than in the past."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段先說完整的悖論最可能出現在十九世紀英國那樣的條件下，再以 That the paradox held for steam does not oblige it to hold for refrigerators 作結，意思是悖論在蒸汽機時代成立，不代表在今天的冰箱等家電上也必然成立，提醒讀者不要把特定歷史情境的結論任意推廣。(B) 文中並未比較兩者的效率；(C) 太極端，作者只是限縮其適用範圍，並未說它已被完全推翻；(D) 文中未提及家庭擁有冰箱的數量。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"claws back\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["greatly exaggerates", "permanently destroys", "takes away again", "secretly records"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：the rebound claws back only a fraction of the expected savings—enough to embarrass engineers' projections, rarely enough to cancel them。反彈效應把預期省下的一部分「又拿回去」，所以足以讓工程師的預估難堪，卻很少完全抵銷，claw back 意為「收回、奪回」。(A) 誇大與文意無關；(B) permanently destroys 語氣過強，且文中說只有 a fraction；(D) 祕密記錄與文意無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's attitude toward Jevons?", "options": ["Dismissive: because his forecast about coal was wrong, his ideas deserve little attention", "Appreciative but qualified: his central insight has lasted, though his strongest claim needs limits", "Uncritically admiring: everything he predicted has come true", "Indifferent: the writer is mainly interested in modern household appliances"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段 It would be convenient... to file Jevons away... Convenient, but not quite fair，表示作者不認同輕易否定他；第六段又說他的觀察 has proved the more durable insight，可見作者肯定他的核心洞見。但第四段也指出完整的悖論只在特定條件下成立，替他最強烈的主張設下限制。(A) 與第五段相反；(C) 第六段明說他對煤炭稀缺的預測是錯的；(D) 冰箱只是第四段的一個例子，並非作者關注的重點。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a242", "title": "The Staircase That Isn't: The Ironies of Automation", "title_zh": "不存在的階梯：自動化的反諷", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Technology", "Career"], "passage": "There is a story we like to tell about automation, and it has the reassuring shape of a staircase. First the machine takes over the dull and dangerous tasks; then it takes over the routine judgments; and at each step the human being climbs a little higher, freed to supervise, to plan, to think. In this telling, the person left beside a highly automated system has the easiest job of all. He or she merely watches, ready to step in on the rare occasions when something goes wrong.\n\nIn 1983 the psychologist Lisanne Bainbridge published a short paper whose title, \"Ironies of Automation,\" has since become something of a proverb among engineers, and whose argument dismantles the staircase story with unsettling economy. Her first irony concerns motive. Designers automate a process because they regard human operators as unreliable; yet whatever they cannot work out how to automate, they leave to those same operators. The human, in other words, is assigned a residue of tasks defined not by what people do well but by what machines happen to do badly—typically the strangest and least predictable situations a system can encounter.\n\nThe second irony is subtler. Monitoring is not the restful activity it appears to be. Research on vigilance had long shown that people find it remarkably hard to sustain attention on a display in which almost nothing happens; the mind drifts precisely because the system is so dependable. And when the moment of crisis finally arrives, the operator must take over from a machine that has, almost by definition, met something it cannot handle. Manual skills, meanwhile, decay with disuse. A pilot who has spent years watching the autopilot fly is being asked to perform at the peak of their expertise at the very moment that expertise has had the least practice.\n\nDefenders of automation will reply, with some justice, that the record speaks for itself. Commercial aviation, among the most heavily automated activities on earth, has become extraordinarily safe. For every accident in which a bewildered crew mishandled a failure, there are countless flights on which automation quietly prevented errors that tired or distracted humans would otherwise have made. To dwell on the ironies, the argument runs, is to mistake rare exceptions for the rule.\n\nThe objection is fair, but it answers a question Bainbridge did not ask. She never claimed that automation makes systems less safe overall; her point was that its benefits come bundled with new and less visible kinds of fragility, which grow as the human role shrinks. Nor is the concern merely theoretical. Since the 2010s, aviation regulators have encouraged airlines to give pilots more opportunities to fly by hand, precisely so that skills seldom needed do not wither away. The lesson has been absorbed, at least in the cockpit: the rarer the intervention, the more deliberately the ability to intervene must be maintained.\n\nWhether that lesson travels beyond aviation is less certain. Many cars can now steer, brake and keep to their lane on highways while requiring the driver to remain ready to take control at a moment's notice—a description that might have been lifted from Bainbridge's paper. Software that drafts reports and writes code invites a similar arrangement, in which the human is cast as the reviewer of work they no longer practice producing. None of this is an argument for refusing such tools. It is an argument against the staircase story, and for designing systems on a less flattering assumption: that the skill required of the human does not disappear as the machine improves. It merely becomes rarer, harder, and easier to lose.", "passage_zh": "關於自動化，我們喜歡講一個故事，而這個故事有著令人安心的階梯形狀。首先，機器接手枯燥而危險的工作；接著，它接手例行性的判斷；而每往前一步，人類就往上爬高一點，得以騰出手來監督、規劃、思考。照這種說法，留在高度自動化系統旁邊的人，擁有所有工作中最輕鬆的一份。他或她只需要看著，在偶爾出問題的時候準備介入即可。\n\n1983 年，心理學家莉珊‧班布里奇發表了一篇短文，其標題〈自動化的反諷〉後來幾乎成了工程師之間的一句諺語，而文中的論證則以令人不安的簡潔，拆解了這個階梯故事。她的第一個反諷與動機有關。設計者之所以將某個流程自動化，是因為他們認為人類操作員不可靠；然而，凡是他們想不出辦法自動化的部分，卻又留給同一批操作員處理。換句話說，人類被分配到的是一堆剩餘的工作，而界定這些工作的，不是人擅長什麼，而是機器碰巧不擅長什麼——通常是系統可能遇到的最怪異、最難以預測的狀況。\n\n第二個反諷更為微妙。監控並不是表面上看起來那種輕鬆的活動。關於警覺性的研究早已顯示，人很難把注意力持續放在一個幾乎沒有任何變化的顯示畫面上；正因為系統如此可靠，心思才會飄走。而當危機時刻終於來臨，操作員必須從一部機器手中接管控制，而這部機器之所以需要人接手，幾乎可說必然是因為它遇上了自己無法處理的狀況。與此同時，手動操作的技能會因為不用而退化。一位多年來都看著自動駕駛儀飛行的飛行員，被要求在他的專業能力最缺乏練習的那一刻，發揮出最頂尖的專業水準。\n\n自動化的擁護者會回應——而且不無道理——說紀錄本身就說明了一切。商業航空是全世界自動化程度最高的活動之一，如今已變得極為安全。每有一起機組人員手足無措、錯誤處理故障的事故，就有無數次航班是靠自動化默默防止了疲倦或分心的人類原本會犯下的錯誤。這種論點認為，執著於那些反諷，就是把罕見的例外誤當成常態。\n\n這個反駁很公道，但它回答的是班布里奇並沒有提出的問題。她從未主張自動化會使系統整體上變得比較不安全；她的重點在於，自動化的好處是和一些新的、較不顯眼的脆弱性綁在一起的，而人類扮演的角色越縮小，這些脆弱性就越大。這種憂慮也不只是理論上的。自 2010 年代以來，航空主管機關一直鼓勵航空公司給飛行員更多手動飛行的機會，為的正是不讓那些很少用到的技能逐漸萎縮。至少在駕駛艙裡，這個教訓已經被吸收了：需要介入的情況越罕見，介入的能力就越需要刻意地維持。\n\n這個教訓能否推廣到航空以外的領域，就比較難說了。如今許多汽車在高速公路上已經能自行轉向、煞車並維持在車道內，同時要求駕駛人隨時準備好接手控制——這段描述簡直像是從班布里奇的論文裡直接搬出來的。能撰寫報告和程式碼的軟體，也引來類似的安排：人類被設定為審查者，審查的卻是自己已經不再練習產出的工作。這一切都不構成拒絕使用這類工具的理由。它反對的是那個階梯故事，並主張在設計系統時採用一個不那麼討人喜歡的假設：人類所需要的技能，並不會因為機器的進步而消失。它只是變得更少用到、更困難，也更容易失去。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main argument?", "options": ["Automation should be resisted because it makes complex systems less safe.", "Today's pilots are less skilled than pilots were in the years before automation.", "Automation does not remove the need for human skill; it makes that skill rarer, harder and easier to lose.", "Engineers should automate only dull and dangerous tasks and leave all judgment to humans."], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一段：the skill required of the human does not disappear as the machine improves. It merely becomes rarer, harder, and easier to lose，這正是全文透過班布里奇的「反諷」所要建立的論點。(A) 第五段說 She never claimed that automation makes systems less safe overall，最後一段也說 None of this is an argument for refusing such tools；(B) 文中只說技能不用會退化，並未比較今昔飛行員的整體水準；(D) 作者從未提出這樣的建議，第一段甚至描述機器也會接手 routine judgments，作者的重點並不在劃分哪些工作該自動化。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What is the main function of paragraph 4?", "options": ["To present an objection to the writer's argument, which the writer then answers", "To provide evidence that automation causes most aviation accidents", "To summarize the second of Bainbridge's ironies", "To show that the writer has changed his or her mind about automation"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段以 Defenders of automation will reply, with some justice, that the record speaks for itself 開頭，提出支持自動化者的反駁：航空業高度自動化，卻極為安全。第五段隨即以 The objection is fair, but it answers a question Bainbridge did not ask 回應。可見第四段的作用是提出反方意見，再由作者加以回應。(B) 第四段說的正好相反，自動化防止了許多錯誤；(C) 第二個反諷在第三段；(D) 作者在第五、六段仍堅持原本的論點。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the \"staircase\" story described in paragraph 1?", "options": ["It was first proposed by Lisanne Bainbridge in 1983.", "It is supported by decades of research on vigilance.", "It correctly predicts that monitoring is the easiest job of all.", "It underestimates how demanding the human's remaining role can be."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段的階梯故事認為留在自動化系統旁的人 has the easiest job of all，只需看著即可；第二段說班布里奇的論點 dismantles the staircase story，第三段更指出 Monitoring is not the restful activity it appears to be，危機時還得在技能最缺乏練習時接手。可推知這個故事低估了人類剩下的角色有多困難。(A) 班布里奇是拆解而非提出這個故事；(B) 警覺性研究反而顯示監控很困難；(C) 這正是作者要反駁的觀點。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"residue\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a reward", "what is left over", "a dangerous substance", "a carefully chosen set"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：whatever they cannot work out how to automate, they leave to those same operators. The human... is assigned a residue of tasks defined not by what people do well but by what machines happen to do badly。設計者把無法自動化的部分留給人，所以 residue 指「剩下的部分」。(A) 這些工作是負擔而非獎賞；(C) 是 residue 在化學上「殘留物」的聯想，不合文意；(D) 文中強調這些工作不是因為人擅長而被挑選，而是機器做不好才剩下來的。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How does the writer regard software that drafts reports and writes code?", "options": ["Such tools may be used, but systems should be designed on the assumption that human skill still matters.", "Such tools should be refused because they make people lazy.", "Such tools are more dangerous than cars that can partly drive themselves.", "Such tools have already solved the problems Bainbridge described."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段提到撰寫報告與程式碼的軟體會讓人成為 the reviewer of work they no longer practice producing，接著說 None of this is an argument for refusing such tools. It is an argument... for designing systems on a less flattering assumption: that the skill required of the human does not disappear。可見作者並不反對使用，而是主張設計時要假定人類的技能依然重要。(B) 與 None of this is an argument for refusing such tools 相反；(C) 文中並未比較兩者的危險程度；(D) 作者認為這類軟體正重現班布里奇所描述的情況，而非已經解決。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a243", "title": "Four Players, Two Centuries: The Stubborn Arithmetic of Baumol's Cost Disease", "title_zh": "四位樂手，兩個世紀：鮑莫爾成本病頑固的算術", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Money", "Society"], "passage": "Ask the manager of a concert hall why ticket prices seem to rise faster than the price of almost everything else, and you may hear a weary half-joke about a string quartet. A quartet written two centuries ago, the joke goes, still requires four musicians and roughly the same length of time to perform. No technological breakthrough has allowed it to be played by three people, or in half the time, without ceasing to be the same piece. In the language of economics, the productivity of the string quartet has not grown at all since the day it was composed.\n\nThis observation, which sounds like a footnote, became the foundation of one of the more consequential ideas in modern economics. In 1966 the economists William Baumol and William Bowen, in a study of the performing arts, drew attention to a puzzle that would later bear Baumol's name. In manufacturing, productivity rises relentlessly: a factory worker today turns out vastly more cars or shirts per hour than her great-grandparents did, and wages rise accordingly. Musicians, however, cannot go on being paid what musicians earned a century ago; if their wages fell too far behind those in other occupations, they would abandon the concert hall for better-paid work. So their pay rises too, even though their output per hour does not. The result is that the relative cost of a live performance climbs, decade after decade, with something close to inevitability.\n\nWhat makes the idea powerful is how far it travels beyond the concert hall. Any service whose value lies largely in human time and attention is exposed to the same arithmetic: a teacher marking essays, a nurse turning a patient, a barber cutting hair, a care worker helping an elderly man to dress. Such work can be improved at the edges, but its core resists acceleration, and attempts to speed it up often degrade the very thing being purchased. A nurse who sees twice as many patients an hour has not necessarily become twice as productive; she may simply be providing half as much care.\n\nThe label that stuck to the idea, the \"cost disease,\" has proved somewhat misleading, and Baumol's later writings went out of their way to dispel the gloom it implied. On his account, the rising cost of education and health care is not primarily a symptom of waste, greed or mismanagement, though all three may be present. It is, in large part, the shadow cast by spectacular gains elsewhere. And the very gains that make labor-intensive services relatively expensive also make a society rich enough to pay for them. A country whose factories and farms grow ever more productive can, in principle, afford more teachers and nurses, not fewer. What it cannot do is have them at the old relative price.\n\nNot everyone is persuaded that the disease is incurable. Critics point out that technology does seep into supposedly stagnant sectors: recordings allow a single performance to reach millions, diagnostic software can spare doctors hours of routine work, and online courses can, in theory at least, multiply a good teacher's audience. Others observe that productivity in services is notoriously hard to measure; a hospital that charges more but cures conditions once thought hopeless is hardly standing still. These objections are real, yet they tend to refine Baumol's argument rather than overturn it. A recording is a different product from a concert, and few parents would accept a video, however brilliant, as a full substitute for a teacher who knows their child's name.\n\nThe idea's most practical consequence may be political. Because the cost of labor-intensive services rises faster than average, budgets for schools, hospitals and care homes will appear to swell year after year even when nothing is being squandered, and every such increase invites the accusation of inefficiency. Baumol's analysis does not tell governments how much to spend. It does, however, counsel a certain caution before concluding that a service has grown worse merely because it has grown dearer, and a certain skepticism toward any reform that promises to make caring for people as cheap as making phones.", "passage_zh": "如果你去問一位音樂廳經理，為什麼票價上漲的速度似乎比幾乎所有其他東西都快，你可能會聽到一個帶著倦意、半開玩笑的說法，跟弦樂四重奏有關。這個玩笑是這麼說的：一首兩百年前寫成的四重奏，到今天仍然需要四位樂手、大致相同的時間來演奏。沒有任何技術突破，能讓它改由三個人演奏，或在一半的時間內奏完，而又不至於變成另一首曲子。用經濟學的語言來說，弦樂四重奏的生產力從它被創作出來的那天起，就完全沒有成長。\n\n這個聽起來像是註腳的觀察，後來卻成了現代經濟學中一個影響較為深遠的概念的基礎。1966 年，經濟學家威廉‧鮑莫爾和威廉‧鮑恩在一項關於表演藝術的研究中，指出了一個後來以鮑莫爾命名的難題。在製造業，生產力不斷上升：今天的一位工廠工人每小時生產的汽車或襯衫，遠遠多於她的曾祖父母那一代，而薪資也隨之上漲。然而，音樂家不可能一直領著一百年前音樂家的薪水；如果他們的薪資遠遠落後於其他行業，他們就會離開音樂廳，去找待遇更好的工作。所以他們的薪資也會上漲，儘管他們每小時的產出並沒有增加。結果就是，現場演出的相對成本幾乎無可避免地一個十年接著一個十年持續攀升。\n\n這個概念之所以有力，在於它能延伸到音樂廳以外多遠的地方。任何價值主要來自人的時間與心力的服務，都受制於同樣的算術：批改作文的老師、替病人翻身的護理師、為人剪髮的理髮師、幫老先生穿衣的照顧服務員。這類工作可以在枝節上改進，但它的核心難以加速，而試圖加快速度，往往會損害人們所購買的那樣東西本身。一位每小時看的病人數變成兩倍的護理師，未必就是生產力提高了一倍；她可能只是提供了一半的照護而已。\n\n這個概念後來被貼上的標籤——「成本病」——結果有點誤導人，而鮑莫爾後期的著作也特地設法驅散這個名稱所暗示的悲觀。依他的說法，教育和醫療成本的上升，主要並不是浪費、貪婪或管理不善的症狀，儘管這三者都可能存在。在很大程度上，它是其他領域驚人進步所投下的影子。而正是那些讓勞力密集型服務相對變貴的進步，也讓社會富裕到足以負擔這些服務。一個工廠和農場生產力不斷提升的國家，原則上能負擔更多、而不是更少的老師和護理師。它做不到的，是以過去的相對價格擁有他們。\n\n並不是每個人都相信這種病無藥可醫。批評者指出，科技確實會滲入那些被認為停滯不前的部門：錄音讓一場演出能傳到數百萬人耳中，診斷軟體能替醫師省下好幾個小時的例行工作，而線上課程至少在理論上能讓一位好老師的聽眾成倍增加。另一些人則指出，服務業的生產力出了名地難以衡量；一家收費更高、卻能治癒過去被認為無望之疾病的醫院，很難說是在原地踏步。這些反對意見確實有道理，但它們往往是在修正鮑莫爾的論點，而不是推翻它。錄音和音樂會是不同的產品，而且很少有家長會接受用一段影片——無論多麼精彩——完全取代一位叫得出自己孩子名字的老師。\n\n這個概念最實際的影響，或許是在政治上。由於勞力密集型服務的成本上升得比平均快，學校、醫院和照護機構的預算就會年復一年地顯得不斷膨脹，即使根本沒有任何浪費也是如此，而每一次這樣的增加，都會招來效率不彰的指控。鮑莫爾的分析並沒有告訴政府該花多少錢。不過，它確實勸人在只因為一項服務變貴了、就斷定它變差之前，多一分謹慎；也勸人對任何聲稱能讓照顧人跟製造手機一樣便宜的改革，多抱持一分懷疑。", "questions": [{"q": "Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", "options": ["Services that depend heavily on human time tend to become relatively more expensive, largely because productivity rises elsewhere.", "Classical concerts have become too expensive for most people to attend.", "Teachers and nurses should be paid less so that education and health care become affordable.", "Technology will soon make all services as cheap as manufactured goods."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段說明製造業生產力上升使薪資上漲，表演者的薪資也必須跟著漲，於是 the relative cost of a live performance climbs；第三段把這個道理延伸到 Any service whose value lies largely in human time and attention；第四段更指出成本上升是 the shadow cast by spectacular gains elsewhere。(B) 音樂會只是引子，並非重點；(C) 與第四段「社會有能力負擔更多老師和護理師」的論述相反；(D) 這比較接近第五段批評者的看法，而且作者認為他們只是 refine 而非推翻鮑莫爾的論點。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to Baumol and Bowen, why must musicians' pay rise even though their productivity does not?", "options": ["Because concert halls charge higher ticket prices every year", "Because governments require artists to be paid the same as factory workers", "Because otherwise musicians would leave for better-paid work in other occupations", "Because modern pieces of music are longer and harder to play"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：if their wages fell too far behind those in other occupations, they would abandon the concert hall for better-paid work. So their pay rises too。(A) 票價上漲是薪資上漲造成的結果，而不是原因；(B) 文中未提及政府的規定；(D) 文中說的是兩百年前的四重奏至今演奏所需的人數與時間不變，並未說現代曲目更長、更難。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What point is made by the example of the nurse at the end of paragraph 3?", "options": ["Nurses are generally less productive than factory workers.", "Speeding up some kinds of work may reduce the quality of what is actually provided.", "Hospitals should employ twice as many nurses as they do now.", "Nurses deserve higher pay than teachers and barbers."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：attempts to speed it up often degrade the very thing being purchased. A nurse who sees twice as many patients an hour has not necessarily become twice as productive; she may simply be providing half as much care。護理師的例子說明，加快速度可能只是降低照護的品質。(A) 文中並未比較護理師與工廠工人的生產力高低；(C) 文中並未建議醫院雇用兩倍的護理師；(D) 文中並未比較各行業應得的薪資。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"the shadow cast by spectacular gains elsewhere\" in paragraph 4 suggests that rising costs in services are ______.", "options": ["a sign that those services are badly managed", "a temporary problem that will soon disappear", "caused by companies hiding their profits", "a side effect of strong productivity growth in other parts of the economy"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：the rising cost of education and health care is not primarily a symptom of waste, greed or mismanagement... It is, in large part, the shadow cast by spectacular gains elsewhere。影子是伴隨其他東西而產生的，這裡比喻服務業成本上升是其他產業生產力大幅提升所附帶的結果。(A)(C) 正是作者說「主要並非如此」的管理不善、貪婪或浪費；(B) 第二段說成本 decade after decade 持續上升，並非短暫的現象。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How does the writer respond to the critics described in paragraph 5?", "options": ["By dismissing their points as entirely mistaken", "By agreeing that they have disproved Baumol's argument", "By accepting that they have a point while arguing that they refine rather than refute Baumol's idea", "By arguing that recordings are now better than live concerts"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：These objections are real, yet they tend to refine Baumol's argument rather than overturn it。作者承認批評有道理，但認為它們只是修正、而非推翻鮑莫爾的論點。(A) 作者明說 These objections are real；(B) 與 rather than overturn it 相反；(D) 作者說 A recording is a different product from a concert，並未說錄音比現場演出更好。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a244", "title": "Finding Too Much: The Quiet Harm of Overdiagnosis", "title_zh": "發現得太多：過度診斷的隱性傷害", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "Few slogans in medicine command such universal assent as \"early detection saves lives.\" It has the ring of common sense, and in many cases it is true. Over the past few decades, however, a growing number of physicians and epidemiologists have argued that the slogan conceals a problem it has no words for: that looking harder for disease does not only find more of the disease we fear. It also finds a great deal that was never going to harm anyone at all.\n\nThe phenomenon has a name, overdiagnosis, and it is frequently confused with something simpler. A false positive is a mistake: a test suggests cancer, and further investigation shows there is none. Overdiagnosis is stranger, because the diagnosis is, in a narrow sense, correct. The abnormal cells are really there; a pathologist examining them would call them cancer. What is wrong is the prediction the word implies. Some tumors grow so slowly, or not at all, that the people who have them would have lived out their lives and died of something else without ever learning of their existence. Studies of people who died of unrelated causes have long shown that small, silent cancers of the prostate and thyroid, among others, are surprisingly common, particularly in older people.\n\nNowhere has this been demonstrated more vividly than in South Korea. After the launch of a national cancer screening program at the end of the 1990s, many clinics began offering thyroid ultrasound as an inexpensive add-on, and diagnoses of thyroid cancer multiplied many times over within little more than a decade, until it had become the country's most commonly diagnosed cancer. Deaths from the disease, however, remained essentially unchanged. Had screening been catching dangerous cancers early, mortality should eventually have fallen; that it did not suggests that most of the newly discovered tumors had never posed a threat. Large numbers of people, meanwhile, had undergone surgery to remove their thyroid glands, and many would need to take replacement hormones for the rest of their lives.\n\nDefenders of vigorous screening will point, quite rightly, to its successes. Regular screening for cervical cancer, for instance, is widely credited with a substantial decline in deaths from that disease in many countries. They will also note that survival statistics for screened cancers have improved impressively. But survival figures are precisely where the illusion is most persuasive. If a cancer is detected five years earlier than it otherwise would have been, the patient's \"five-year survival\" improves even if the date of death does not move at all, a statistical mirage known as lead-time bias. And if screening adds thousands of harmless tumors to the count, survival rates will soar, since people who were never in danger reliably survive. A treatment can look miraculous when many of those it treats were never at risk.\n\nNone of this amounts to an argument against medicine, or even against screening. It is an argument against a particular kind of arithmetic, one that counts the lives apparently saved and leaves the harms off the ledger: the anxiety of a diagnosis, the side effects of treatment, the quiet conversion of healthy people into patients. Some medical organizations have responded by narrowing their screening recommendations; others now offer \"active surveillance,\" in which a low-risk tumor is monitored rather than immediately removed. Such approaches demand something psychologically difficult for doctors and patients alike: the willingness to know about an abnormality and, for the time being, to leave it alone.\n\nPerhaps the deepest difficulty is that overdiagnosis is invisible at the level of the individual. No one can say which particular patient's tumor would have remained harmless; every person who has been treated is free to believe, and usually does believe, that the treatment saved their life. Overdiagnosis therefore produces grateful patients, satisfied doctors and reassuring statistics, a combination that makes it unusually resistant to correction. The sensible response is not suspicion of every test but a habit of asking, before one is performed, what will be done with the answer.", "passage_zh": "在醫學界，很少有口號能像「早期發現救人命」這樣獲得普遍的認同。它聽起來合乎常理，而且在許多情況下確實如此。然而，過去幾十年來，越來越多的醫師和流行病學家主張，這句口號掩蓋了一個它根本沒有詞彙可以形容的問題：更努力地尋找疾病，找到的不只是更多我們所害怕的疾病，也會找到大量原本永遠不會傷害任何人的東西。\n\n這種現象有個名稱，叫做「過度診斷」，而它常常被誤認為另一件比較單純的事。偽陽性是一種錯誤：檢查結果顯示可能有癌症，進一步檢查後卻發現根本沒有。過度診斷則比較奇怪，因為就狹義而言，這個診斷是正確的。那些異常細胞確實存在；病理科醫師檢查之後，也會稱之為癌症。錯的是這個字眼所隱含的預測。有些腫瘤長得非常慢，甚至根本不長，以致於帶著它們的人原本會過完一生、死於其他原因，而始終不知道它們的存在。針對死於無關原因者所做的研究早已顯示，攝護腺、甲狀腺等部位微小而沒有症狀的癌症出奇地常見，在年長者身上尤其如此。\n\n這一點在南韓得到了再鮮明不過的證明。1990 年代末，南韓推出全國性的癌症篩檢計畫之後，許多診所開始提供價格低廉的甲狀腺超音波作為附加項目，結果在短短十多年內，甲狀腺癌的診斷數量增加了好幾倍，最後成為該國最常被診斷出的癌症。然而，死於這種疾病的人數基本上沒有改變。如果篩檢真的及早揪出了危險的癌症，死亡率最終應該會下降；死亡率沒有下降，顯示大多數新發現的腫瘤從來就不構成威脅。與此同時，已有大量民眾接受手術切除甲狀腺，其中許多人必須終生服用替代荷爾蒙。\n\n積極篩檢的擁護者會指出——而且很有道理——篩檢的成功之處。例如，在許多國家，子宮頸癌的定期篩檢被普遍認為使該疾病的死亡人數大幅下降。他們也會指出，經篩檢發現之癌症的存活統計已有令人印象深刻的改善。但存活數字正是這種錯覺最有說服力的地方。如果一個癌症比原本早五年被發現，即使病人死亡的日期完全沒有改變，他的「五年存活率」也會提高——這是一種被稱為「領先時間偏差」的統計假象。而如果篩檢把數以千計無害的腫瘤加進統計，存活率就會飆升，因為那些從來沒有危險的人當然都會存活。當許多接受治療的人其實從未面臨風險時，一種療法看起來就可能像奇蹟一樣。\n\n這一切都不構成反對醫學、甚至反對篩檢的論點。它反對的是一種特定的算法：只計算表面上挽救了多少生命，卻把傷害排除在帳本之外——診斷帶來的焦慮、治療的副作用，以及悄悄地把健康的人變成病人。有些醫學組織的回應是縮小篩檢建議的範圍；另一些則開始提供「主動監測」，也就是對低風險的腫瘤持續追蹤觀察，而不是立即切除。這些做法要求醫師和病人都做到一件心理上很困難的事：願意知道某個異常的存在，卻暫時放著不去處理它。\n\n也許最根本的困難在於，從個人的層次來看，過度診斷是看不見的。沒有人能說出哪一位病人的腫瘤原本會一直無害；每一位接受過治療的人都可以相信——而且通常確實相信——是治療救了他一命。因此，過度診斷帶來的是心懷感激的病人、感到滿意的醫師，以及令人安心的統計數字——這樣的組合使它格外難以被矯正。明智的回應不是懷疑每一項檢查，而是養成一個習慣：在檢查之前先問，拿到結果之後要怎麼處理。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main point of the passage?", "options": ["Screening tests are usually inaccurate and should be abandoned.", "South Korea's health system performs worse than those of other countries.", "False positives are the most serious problem in modern medicine.", "Searching harder for disease can lead to diagnoses of conditions that would never have caused harm, a cost that is easily overlooked."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：looking harder for disease... also finds a great deal that was never going to harm anyone at all。全文接著解釋過度診斷、以南韓為例，第五段指出一般的算法 leaves the harms off the ledger，第六段說它 invisible，因此難以被矯正。(A) 第五段明言 None of this amounts to an argument against... screening，而且過度診斷的診斷本身是正確的，並非檢查不準；(B) 南韓只是用來說明的例子；(C) 第二段特別區分偽陽性與過度診斷，文章的重點是後者。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how does overdiagnosis differ from a false positive?", "options": ["Overdiagnosis occurs only in elderly patients.", "In overdiagnosis, the abnormality really exists, but it would never have harmed the person.", "A false positive is far more upsetting for the patient.", "Overdiagnosis results from careless mistakes by pathologists."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：A false positive is a mistake... Overdiagnosis is stranger, because the diagnosis is, in a narrow sense, correct. The abnormal cells are really there... What is wrong is the prediction the word implies，也就是這些腫瘤原本不會危害當事人。(A) 文中說 particularly in older people，並非 only；(C) 文中並未比較兩者對病人情緒的影響；(D) 文中說病理科醫師稱之為癌症並沒有錯。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer conclude from the fact that deaths from thyroid cancer in South Korea stayed about the same?", "options": ["The ultrasound machines used in the screening were faulty.", "Doctors had begun treating the cancers too late.", "Most of the extra tumors found through screening had never been dangerous.", "Thyroid cancer had become more common for environmental reasons."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：Had screening been catching dangerous cancers early, mortality should eventually have fallen; that it did not suggests that most of the newly discovered tumors had never posed a threat。(A) 文中並未提及儀器故障；(B) 篩檢讓癌症更早、而不是更晚被發現；(D) 診斷數暴增是因為篩檢變得普及，作者並未歸因於環境因素。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which situation best illustrates \"lead-time bias\" as described in paragraph 4?", "options": ["Screening finds a woman's disease three years earlier, but she dies at the same age she otherwise would have, so she appears to have survived longer after diagnosis.", "A test wrongly indicates that a healthy man has cancer.", "A patient refuses treatment and lives longer than her doctors expected.", "A new drug allows patients to live five years longer than patients did before."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：If a cancer is detected five years earlier..., the patient's \"five-year survival\" improves even if the date of death does not move at all，這就是 lead-time bias。(A) 疾病提早發現、死亡年齡卻不變，診斷後的存活時間看起來變長了，正是這種統計假象。(B) 是第二段所說的偽陽性；(C) 與診斷時間點造成的統計假象無關；(D) 是真正延長了壽命，並非統計假象。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which best describes the writer's attitude toward medical screening?", "options": ["Enthusiastic: the more screening, the better", "Hostile: all screening does more harm than good", "Indifferent: the writer leaves such decisions entirely to doctors", "Cautiously critical: screening has real benefits, but its harms should be weighed and its purpose questioned"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段作者承認 Defenders of vigorous screening will point, quite rightly, to its successes，例如子宮頸癌篩檢；第五段說 None of this amounts to an argument against... screening，反對的是 leaves the harms off the ledger 的算法；第六段則建議 not suspicion of every test but a habit of asking... what will be done with the answer。可見作者是審慎地批判，而非全盤肯定或否定。(A) 與全文論點相反；(B) 太極端；(C) 作者建議人們主動提問，並非漠不關心。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a245", "title": "The Sea My Grandfather Remembered", "title_zh": "祖父記憶中的海", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Environment", "Nature"], "passage": "My grandfather, who fished the waters off a small harbor town for most of his working life, liked to say that when he was a boy the fish had been so thick beneath the pier that you could have lowered a basket and lifted out your dinner. I did not believe him. Nobody in the family did. It was the kind of thing old men say, we thought, in the same category as the winters that were colder and the summers that were longer and the rice that used to taste of something. By the time I was old enough to fish beside him, the pier yielded a few small fish on a good afternoon, and I was perfectly content with them. They were, as far as I was concerned, what the sea was like.\n\nIt was years later, reading about fisheries for reasons that had nothing to do with him, that I came across a phrase that rearranged my memory of those afternoons. In 1995 the fisheries scientist Daniel Pauly described what he called the \"shifting baseline syndrome.\" Each generation of researchers, he observed, tends to accept as normal the state of an ecosystem at the beginning of its own career, and to measure decline from that point. When stocks fall further, the next generation adopts the diminished numbers as its baseline, and so on. The result is a gradual accommodation to loss in which no one is exactly lying and no one quite notices, because the reference point keeps sliding downward along with the thing being measured.\n\nPauly was writing about scientists, but the syndrome is hardly confined to them. One of the most arresting illustrations I know comes from a researcher who gathered decades of photographs taken at a dock in Florida, where anglers returning from charter trips posed beside the day's best catch. Laid side by side, the pictures tell an unambiguous story. In the earliest images, the fish hanging from the board are enormous, some nearly as large as the men beside them; in the most recent, they are a fraction of that size. What does not change is the expression on the anglers' faces. The grins are every bit as wide. Each generation was delighted, and each had every reason to be, since each was measuring its catch against the only sea it had known.\n\nI find that last detail more unsettling than any graph. It suggests that the problem is not a lack of information but something closer to a feature of how we are built. We are calibrated by our childhoods. The rivers we swam in, the birdsong we woke to, the number of insects smeared on the windshield after a summer drive: these become, without our consent, the definition of normal. Whatever came before is legend; whatever comes after is decline. My grandfather's basket of fish was not, I now suspect, an exaggeration so much as a measurement taken with an instrument I did not possess.\n\nThere is, admittedly, a risk in leaping to the opposite conclusion and treating every old person's reminiscence as data. Nostalgia distorts in its own direction; the past was not uniformly abundant, and memory has a way of gilding whatever it touches. The remedy is not to trust anecdotes blindly but to take them seriously enough to check. That is the work of what is sometimes called historical ecology, whose practitioners comb through ships' logs, old market records, restaurant menus and faded photographs in search of the baselines that living memory has lost. Their findings do not always confirm the old stories. Often enough, though, they reveal that the old stories were, if anything, too modest.\n\nMy grandfather has been dead for some years now, and the harbor town has a new seawall and a shop that rents snorkeling gear to visitors. Children paddle in the shallows and squeal at the occasional silver flash beneath them. I watch them with a feeling I find difficult to name. They are, I know, laying down their own baseline, and in thirty years they will tell their grandchildren about the fish that used to swim here. I hope someone believes them. More than that, I hope that by then the sea will have given those grandchildren reason to find the stories unremarkable.", "passage_zh": "我的祖父大半輩子都在一個小港鎮外的海域捕魚，他總愛說，在他小時候，碼頭底下的魚多得不得了，只要放下一個籃子，就能撈起一頓晚餐。我不相信他。家裡也沒有人相信。我們以為，那是老人家常說的那種話，跟以前的冬天比較冷、夏天比較長、米飯比較有味道是同一類的說法。等到我大到可以在他身旁釣魚的時候，在運氣好的下午，碼頭也只釣得到幾條小魚，而我對此心滿意足。在我看來，海本來就是這個樣子。\n\n多年以後，我因為跟他毫無關係的原因閱讀有關漁業的資料，偶然讀到一個說法，徹底重組了我對那些午後的記憶。1995 年，漁業科學家丹尼爾‧鮑利描述了一種他稱為「基準線偏移症候群」的現象。他觀察到，每一代研究人員往往把自己職業生涯剛開始時生態系的狀態視為正常，並以那個時間點為基準來衡量衰退。當魚群數量進一步減少，下一代就把已經縮減的數字當作自己的基準線，如此循環下去。結果是對損失的一種逐步適應：沒有人真的在說謊，也沒有人真正察覺，因為參考點本身也跟著被衡量的事物一起不斷往下滑。\n\n鮑利寫的是科學家，但這種症候群絕不只限於科學家。我所知道最令人震撼的例證之一，來自一位研究人員：這位研究人員蒐集了佛羅里達州某個碼頭數十年來拍攝的照片——包船出海歸來的釣客，會在那裡與當天最好的漁獲合影。把這些照片並排在一起，它們訴說的故事再清楚不過。在最早期的照片裡，掛在板子上的魚十分巨大，有些幾乎和身旁的男人一樣大；在最近期的照片裡，魚的大小只剩當年的一小部分。不變的是釣客臉上的表情。他們的笑容一樣燦爛。每一代人都很開心，而且每一代人都完全有理由開心，因為每一代人都是拿自己的漁獲，跟自己唯一認識過的那片海相比。\n\n我覺得最後這個細節比任何圖表都更令人不安。它顯示問題不在於缺乏資訊，而比較接近我們與生俱來的某種特性。我們都被自己的童年校準過。我們游過泳的河流、伴我們醒來的鳥鳴、夏日開車兜風後擋風玻璃上沾滿的昆蟲數量——這些都在未經我們同意的情況下，成了「正常」的定義。在那之前的一切都是傳說；在那之後的一切都是衰退。現在我懷疑，祖父那一籃子的魚與其說是誇大，不如說是一次測量，而用的是一件我所沒有的儀器。\n\n誠然，跳到相反的結論、把每一位老人的回憶都當成數據，也有風險。懷舊之情會朝另一個方向扭曲事實；過去並不是處處豐饒，而記憶總會替它碰觸到的一切鍍上一層金。解方不是盲目地相信軼事，而是認真看待它們，認真到願意去查證。這正是有時被稱為「歷史生態學」的工作：從事這門學問的人仔細翻閱船隻的航海日誌、舊的市場紀錄、餐廳菜單和褪色的照片，尋找那些活人記憶已經遺失的基準線。他們的發現並不總是證實那些老故事。不過，他們常常發現，那些老故事如果說有什麼問題，反倒是說得太保守了。\n\n祖父已經過世好幾年了，那個港鎮如今有了一道新的海堤，還有一家出租浮潛裝備給遊客的店。孩子們在淺水處划水，偶爾看見腳下閃過一道銀光，就興奮地尖叫。我看著他們，心裡湧起一種難以名狀的感覺。我知道，他們正在奠定自己的基準線，三十年後，他們會跟自己的孫子說起以前在這裡游來游去的魚。我希望有人會相信他們。更重要的是，我希望到了那時候，大海已經讓那些孫子有理由覺得這些故事平淡無奇。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?", "options": ["To show that the writer's grandfather was an unusually skilled fisherman", "To reflect on how each generation's idea of what is normal in nature can hide long-term loss", "To argue that old people's memories are always more reliable than scientific data", "To describe how a small harbor town became popular with tourists"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文以祖父的回憶開頭，第二段介紹「基準線偏移症候群」——每一代都把自己最初所見的狀態當成正常，以致 no one quite notices 損失；第四段說 We are calibrated by our childhoods；最後一段想到今天的孩子也正在 laying down their own baseline。可見作者旨在反思「正常」的標準如何隨世代下滑而掩蓋長期的損失。(A) 文中並未談論祖父的捕魚技術；(C) 第五段明確警告不要 treating every old person's reminiscence as data，太極端；(D) 港鎮的變化只是最後一段的背景。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the detail about the anglers' unchanging grins in paragraph 3 suggest?", "options": ["People judge what they see against their own experience, so they may fail to notice long-term decline.", "Fishing became more enjoyable as the fish became smaller.", "The later photographs were taken by less skilled photographers.", "Recent anglers lied about the size of their catches."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Each generation was delighted, and each had every reason to be, since each was measuring its catch against the only sea it had known。釣客的笑容不變，是因為每一代都拿自己唯一見過的海作比較，所以察覺不到魚變小了。(B) 文中說的是笑容一樣燦爛，並非變得更開心；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"calibrated\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["damaged", "trained in science", "made sentimental", "given a standard to measure against"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：We are calibrated by our childhoods. The rivers we swam in, the birdsong we woke to...: these become, without our consent, the definition of normal。calibrate 原指「校準儀器」，這裡指童年經驗成了我們衡量一切的標準。(A) 文中並非說童年損害了我們；(B) 與科學訓練無關；(C) 懷舊之情是第五段才談到的另一回事，並非 calibrated 的意思。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What is the main function of paragraph 5?", "options": ["To show that historical ecology has proved every old story true", "To admit that the writer's grandfather had been exaggerating after all", "To qualify the argument by warning against trusting memory uncritically, and to suggest how memories can be checked", "To introduce a new topic unrelated to shifting baselines"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段以 There is, admittedly, a risk in leaping to the opposite conclusion 開頭，承認懷舊會扭曲記憶，接著提出 The remedy is not to trust anecdotes blindly but to take them seriously enough to check，並介紹歷史生態學如何查證。可見這一段是在替作者的論點加上限定，並提出查證的方法。(A) 同段說 Their findings do not always confirm the old stories；(B) 第四段作者已表示祖父的話 not... an exaggeration；(D) 歷史生態學正是要找回失落的基準線，與主題密切相關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which best describes the tone of the final paragraph?", "options": ["Wistful but quietly hopeful", "Angry and accusing", "Detached and strictly scientific", "Carefree and amused"], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段作者想起已過世的祖父，看著孩子們時 with a feeling I find difficult to name，帶著淡淡的感傷；結尾 I hope that by then the sea will have given those grandchildren reason to find the stories unremarkable，則含蓄地表達希望海洋能夠恢復，語氣是惆悵中帶著期盼。(B) 文中沒有指責任何人；(C) 這一段是個人抒懷，並非科學論述；(D) 與「難以名狀的感覺」不符。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a246", "title": "Locked In or Merely Good Enough? The Contested Case of QWERTY", "title_zh": "被鎖定，還是只是夠好用？QWERTY 這樁爭議案例", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Technology", "History"], "passage": "Few objects are as familiar, or as little examined, as the keyboard on which this sentence was typed. Its top row of letters (Q, W, E, R, T, Y) gives the layout its name, and the layout itself dates from the 1870s. It emerged from the workshop of Christopher Latham Sholes, a Milwaukee newspaperman and inventor, and reached the public on a typewriter manufactured by E. Remington and Sons, a company then better known for firearms. A century and a half later, the same arrangement greets users of laptops and phones whose inner workings Sholes could not have imagined, and whose keys, unlike his, could never jam.\n\nWhy this particular arrangement? The popular answer, that Sholes deliberately scrambled the letters to slow typists down and so keep the machine's metal type bars from colliding, is at best a simplification. The layout evolved through years of trial and adjustment, and the reasons behind specific choices remain disputed among historians; separating certain frequently paired letters to reduce jamming seems to have been one consideration among several. What is not disputed is that QWERTY was shaped by a mechanical problem that no longer exists.\n\nThat fact made QWERTY irresistible to economists. In 1985 the economic historian Paul David published an influential paper that used the keyboard to illustrate what he called path dependence: the idea that small, even accidental choices early in a technology's history can become locked in, persisting long after better alternatives appear. The mechanism was easy to grasp. Typists learned QWERTY because employers' machines used it; manufacturers built QWERTY machines because typists knew it; and each new adopter made switching costlier for everyone else. David's chief exhibit of a superior road not taken was the Dvorak Simplified Keyboard, patented in 1936, which placed the most common letters on the middle row and was said to allow markedly faster typing. If a demonstrably better design could fail to dislodge an inferior one, the assumption that markets reliably select the best technology looked decidedly shaky.\n\nThen the story itself came under scrutiny. In 1990 the economists Stan Liebowitz and Stephen Margolis re-examined the evidence for Dvorak's superiority and found it surprisingly thin. Several of the most impressive early results, they pointed out, came from studies associated with Dvorak himself, who was hardly a disinterested party; later and more carefully controlled comparisons found the benefit of retraining to be small, too small to justify its cost. On their reading, QWERTY persisted not because the market was trapped but because the alternative was not much better. The parable of lock-in, they suggested, had been built on a foundation that could not bear its weight.\n\nThe dispute has never been entirely settled, and both sides have scored points. David's defenders note that even a modest advantage might have been worth adopting had coordination been easy, and that the very difficulty of conducting a fair comparison, when nearly every experienced typist has spent years on QWERTY, is itself a symptom of lock-in. Skeptics reply that an idea so important ought to be illustrated by an example that survives inspection. What both camps accept is the more general point: that standards, once widely adopted, are sustained by habit, training and compatibility as much as by merit. The width of much of the world's railway track, inherited from the horse-drawn wagonways of English coal mines, is seldom defended as ideal; it is simply what everyone uses.\n\nThere is something fitting in the fate of the QWERTY story. Repeated in textbooks and lectures for decades, it became the standard example of a standard that persists because it is standard: an illustration locked in, one might say, by its own convenience. That it may not prove what it was enlisted to prove does not make path dependence an illusion. It does suggest that tidy parables deserve the same scrutiny as the technologies they describe, and that the phrase \"everyone knows\" should prompt a historian, at least, to start checking.", "passage_zh": "很少有東西像打出這句話的鍵盤一樣，既如此熟悉，又如此少被仔細審視。它最上排的字母（Q、W、E、R、T、Y）為這種排列方式取了名字，而這種排列本身可以追溯到 1870 年代。它誕生於克里斯多福‧萊瑟姆‧肖爾斯的工作室——肖爾斯是密爾瓦基的一位報人兼發明家——並透過雷明頓父子公司製造的打字機進入大眾生活，而這家公司當時以製造槍械更為人所知。一個半世紀之後，同樣的排列迎接著筆電和手機的使用者；這些裝置的內部運作是肖爾斯無法想像的，而且它們的按鍵和他的不同，永遠不可能卡住。\n\n為什麼是這種特定的排列？流行的答案是：肖爾斯故意把字母打亂，好讓打字員慢下來，以免機器的金屬字桿互相碰撞——這個說法充其量只是一種簡化。這種排列是經過多年的嘗試與調整才逐漸成形的，而某些特定安排背後的理由，史學家至今仍有爭議；把某些經常相連出現的字母分開以減少卡鍵，似乎只是諸多考量之一。沒有爭議的是，QWERTY 是因應一個如今已不存在的機械問題而形成的。\n\n這個事實讓 QWERTY 對經濟學家具有難以抗拒的吸引力。1985 年，經濟史學家保羅‧大衛發表了一篇影響深遠的論文，用鍵盤來說明他所謂的「路徑依賴」：一項技術發展初期一些微小、甚至偶然的選擇，可能會被鎖定，在更好的替代方案出現之後仍長久存續。這個機制很容易理解。打字員學 QWERTY，是因為雇主的機器採用它；製造商生產 QWERTY 的機器，是因為打字員會用它；而每多一個人採用，就讓其他所有人轉換的成本更高。大衛用來說明「一條更好卻沒被選上的路」的主要例證，是 1936 年取得專利的德沃夏克簡化鍵盤，它把最常用的字母放在中間那一排，據說能讓打字速度明顯加快。如果一個明顯更好的設計，都無法把一個較差的設計擠下原本的位置，那麼「市場總能可靠地選出最好的技術」這個假設，看起來就相當站不住腳了。\n\n接著，這個故事本身受到了檢驗。1990 年，經濟學家史坦‧李伯維茲和史蒂芬‧馬戈利斯重新檢視了德沃夏克鍵盤較優越的證據，發現它出奇地薄弱。他們指出，早期最令人印象深刻的幾項結果，出自與德沃夏克本人有關的研究，而他很難說是一個沒有利害關係的人；後來控制得更嚴謹的比較則發現，重新訓練的好處很小，小到不足以抵銷所需的成本。照他們的解讀，QWERTY 之所以能延續下來，不是因為市場被困住了，而是因為另一個選擇也沒有好多少。他們認為，「鎖定」這則寓言建立在一個承受不住其重量的基礎上。\n\n這場爭論始終沒有完全平息，雙方也都各有斬獲。支持大衛的人指出，如果協調容易的話，即使只是些微的優勢，也可能值得採用；而且，在幾乎每一位有經驗的打字員都已經用了 QWERTY 好多年的情況下，要進行公平的比較極為困難，這一點本身正是鎖定的一種症狀。懷疑者則反駁說，一個如此重要的概念，應該用一個經得起檢驗的例子來說明。兩個陣營都接受的，是那個更普遍的觀點：標準一旦被廣泛採用，支撐它的除了優點之外，同樣還有習慣、訓練與相容性。世界上很大一部分鐵路軌道的寬度，承襲自英國煤礦裡用馬拉的貨車軌道，很少有人會辯稱它是最理想的；它只不過是大家都在用的寬度而已。\n\nQWERTY 這個故事的命運，有某種恰如其分之處。它在教科書和課堂上被重複了幾十年，成了「一個因為是標準、所以繼續存在的標準」的標準例子——或許可以說，它是一個因為自身的方便而被鎖定的例證。它可能證明不了人們徵召它來證明的東西，但這並不代表路徑依賴是一種錯覺。它確實顯示，工整漂亮的寓言應該和它們所描述的技術一樣接受仔細的審視，而「大家都知道」這句話，至少應該促使一位史學家開始查證。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How Christopher Latham Sholes came to invent the typewriter", "Why the Dvorak keyboard is clearly superior to QWERTY", "How QWERTY became a famous example of lock-in, and how that example has been challenged", "Why phone makers should abandon the QWERTY layout"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段介紹保羅‧大衛以 QWERTY 說明 path dependence 與 lock-in；第四段 Then the story itself came under scrutiny，說明李伯維茲和馬戈利斯的質疑；第五、六段討論這場爭議及其啟示。(A) 肖爾斯只在第一段作為背景出現；(B) 第四段指出德沃夏克鍵盤較優越的證據 surprisingly thin；(D) 文中並未建議廢除 QWERTY。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's view of the popular claim that QWERTY was designed to slow typists down?", "options": ["It has been confirmed by historians.", "It was first put forward by Paul David.", "It explains why the Dvorak keyboard failed.", "It oversimplifies a history whose reasons are still disputed."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：The popular answer, that Sholes deliberately scrambled the letters to slow typists down..., is at best a simplification... the reasons behind specific choices remain disputed among historians。(A) 與 remain disputed 相反；(B) 文中並未說這個說法出自保羅‧大衛；(C) 德沃夏克鍵盤未被普遍採用的原因在第三、四段討論，與這個說法無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer point out that some early studies were \"associated with Dvorak himself\"?", "options": ["To show that Dvorak was a skilled and careful scientist", "To suggest that those results may have been biased, since Dvorak had a stake in the outcome", "To prove that Dvorak could type faster than anyone else", "To explain why the Dvorak keyboard was eventually adopted"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：came from studies associated with Dvorak himself, who was hardly a disinterested party; later and more carefully controlled comparisons found the benefit... to be small。disinterested 意為「沒有利害關係的」，作者暗示德沃夏克本人與結果有利害關係，研究可能有偏差。(A) 作者是在質疑而非稱讚這些研究；(C) 文中未提及他本人的打字速度；(D) 德沃夏克鍵盤並未被普遍採用。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"dislodge\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["force out of an established position", "copy closely", "improve slightly", "advertise widely"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：If a demonstrably better design could fail to dislodge an inferior one, the assumption that markets reliably select the best technology looked decidedly shaky。較好的設計無法把已經站穩地位的較差設計「擠掉、取代」，所以 dislodge 意為「把……從原有位置移開」。(B)(C)(D) 都無法說明為何這會動搖「市場能選出最好技術」的假設。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is the main point of the final paragraph?", "options": ["Path dependence has now been shown to be an illusion.", "Historians should stop using examples from the history of technology.", "Doubts about the QWERTY story do not disprove path dependence, but they show that neat parables need to be checked.", "QWERTY will soon be replaced because it no longer serves any purpose."], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一段：That it may not prove what it was enlisted to prove does not make path dependence an illusion. It does suggest that tidy parables deserve the same scrutiny as the technologies they describe。(A) 與 does not make path dependence an illusion 相反；(B) 作者只是主張要查證，並未要求停止使用科技史的例子；(D) 文中並未預測 QWERTY 會被取代。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a247", "title": "Did the Wolves Really Move the Rivers?", "title_zh": "狼群真的改變了河流嗎？", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Nature", "Science"], "passage": "Midway through \"Where the Willows Wait,\" a handsome new documentary about Yellowstone National Park, the camera lifts slowly from a riverbank thick with young willows and climbs until the whole valley is visible, braided with silver water, while the narrator explains that none of this would exist without the wolves. It is a glorious shot. It is also, in miniature, both the film's great strength and its central problem. The pictures are true. The sentence beneath them is something rather less than that.\n\nThe facts on which the film rests are not in dispute. Gray wolves had been eliminated from Yellowstone by the 1920s, hunted as vermin in an age that regarded predators as obstacles to good management. In their absence, elk multiplied and browsed heavily on the young willows and aspens along the park's streams. After wolves were reintroduced in the mid-1990s, elk numbers fell substantially, and in parts of the park willows and aspens began to recover. Beaver colonies, scarce for decades, increased. Ecologists have a name for chains of influence of this kind, in which a predator at the top of a food web affects the plants at its base: a trophic cascade.\n\nWhere the documentary goes astray is in the elegance it imposes on that cascade. In the film's account, wolves frightened the elk out of valleys and gorges, vegetation sprang back, beavers returned to build their dams, and the rivers themselves altered their courses, a sequence presented with the tidy inevitability of falling dominoes. The scientific literature is a good deal messier. Elk numbers were also driven down by human hunting outside the park, by drought and hard winters, and by growing populations of bears and cougars. Studies have disagreed about how far elk actually changed their behavior in response to the risk of wolves, as opposed to simply being eaten. And the recovery of willows has been strikingly patchy, partly because decades without beavers allowed some streams to cut deeper channels, lowering the water table so that willows struggle to regrow even where elk now leave them alone.\n\nNone of this complexity makes the wolves unimportant. Few ecologists doubt that their return has had real and, in some places, striking effects, and the film is at its best when it simply watches them: a pack moving in single file across a snowfield, an elk herd lifting its heads in unison at a sound the audience cannot hear. These sequences alone are worth the price of a ticket, and the patience behind them is evident in every frame. One leaves the theater with a vivid sense of what the return of a large predator looks like.\n\nWhat one does not leave with is any sense of how scientists know what they know, or how much remains uncertain. That omission matters more than it might seem. Stories of a single heroic species restoring an entire ecosystem are enormously appealing, and they have been put to good use in campaigns for conservation. But they also set up expectations that real landscapes may fail to meet. If wolves are sold as a cure-all, the eventual discovery that they are not risks discrediting the more modest, and more defensible, case for bringing them back. A landscape shaped by many forces at once is harder to film than a row of dominoes. It is also, arguably, a more interesting subject.\n\nThe film's makers will no doubt protest that a documentary is not a scientific review, and that a ninety-minute film for general audiences must simplify. That is fair as far as it goes. Simplification, though, is not the same as substitution, and \"Where the Willows Wait\" too often substitutes a fable for an explanation. The wolves of Yellowstone have accomplished a great deal. They have simply been asked, in this telling, to carry rather more than their share.", "passage_zh": "《柳樹等待之處》是一部以黃石國家公園為題、拍得十分精美的新紀錄片。播到一半時，鏡頭從一片長滿幼嫩柳樹的河岸緩緩升起，一路攀升，直到整座河谷盡收眼底，銀色的水流在其間交織如辮，而旁白此時解釋說，若沒有狼，這一切都不會存在。這是一個壯麗的鏡頭。它同時也具體而微地呈現了這部片最大的優點和它的核心問題。畫面是真實的。畫面底下的那句話，卻遠沒有那麼真實。\n\n這部片所依據的事實並沒有爭議。灰狼在 1920 年代就已經從黃石公園消失了；在那個把掠食者視為良好管理之障礙的年代，牠們被當作害獸獵殺。少了狼，麋鹿大量繁殖，大肆啃食公園溪流沿岸的幼嫩柳樹和白楊。1990 年代中期狼群被重新引入之後，麋鹿數量大幅下降，公園部分地區的柳樹和白楊也開始恢復。幾十年來十分稀少的河狸群落也增加了。生態學家為這類影響鏈取了一個名稱——位於食物網頂端的掠食者，影響到位於底層的植物——叫做「營養級聯」。\n\n這部紀錄片誤入歧途的地方，在於它硬加在這個級聯上的那份工整。在片中的敘述裡，狼把麋鹿嚇出了山谷和峽谷，植被隨之迅速恢復，河狸回來築壩，連河流本身也改變了流向——整個過程被呈現得像一排倒下的骨牌那樣整齊而必然。科學文獻所呈現的情況則雜亂得多。麋鹿數量下降，也是因為公園外的人類狩獵、乾旱與嚴冬，以及熊和美洲獅族群的增加。對於麋鹿究竟在多大程度上因為狼的威脅而改變行為——而不只是被吃掉——各項研究的結論並不一致。此外，柳樹的恢復情況參差不齊得驚人，部分原因是在沒有河狸的幾十年間，有些溪流把河道切割得更深，使地下水位下降，以致於即使在麋鹿已不再啃食的地方，柳樹也難以重新生長。\n\n這些複雜之處並不會讓狼變得無足輕重。很少有生態學家懷疑狼群的回歸確實產生了實質的影響，在某些地方甚至相當顯著；而這部片在單純觀察牠們的時候表現得最好：一群狼排成一列縱隊越過雪原；一群麋鹿聽到某個觀眾聽不見的聲音，同時抬起頭來。光是這些片段就值回票價，而其背後的耐心，在每一個畫面中都清晰可見。觀眾離開戲院時，會對大型掠食者的回歸是什麼模樣，留下鮮明的印象。\n\n然而，觀眾離開時，對於科學家是如何得知這些事的、又還有多少事情仍不確定，卻毫無概念。這個遺漏比表面上看起來更要緊。單一英雄物種拯救整個生態系的故事極具吸引力，也曾在保育運動中發揮良好的作用。但這類故事也會設下真實的地景可能無法達到的期望。如果把狼包裝成萬靈丹來推銷，那麼當人們終究發現牠們並不是萬靈丹時，就可能連那個比較保守、也比較站得住腳的讓狼回歸的理由，都一併失去公信力。一片同時受到多種力量塑造的地景，比一排骨牌更難拍攝。但或許可以說，它也是一個更有趣的題材。\n\n這部片的製作團隊無疑會抗議說，紀錄片不是科學評論，一部拍給一般觀眾看的九十分鐘影片必須簡化。就這一點而言，這話不無道理。然而，簡化不等於替換，而《柳樹等待之處》太常用一則寓言來替換一個解釋。黃石公園的狼確實成就了許多事。只是，在這個版本的敘述中，牠們被要求扛起了遠超過自己份內的重擔。", "questions": [{"q": "Which best summarizes the reviewer's overall opinion of the documentary?", "options": ["It is visually impressive, but it presents a complex scientific story as much simpler than it is.", "It is scientifically accurate but dull to watch.", "It wrongly claims that wolves were never removed from Yellowstone.", "It should never have been made, since wolves have had no effect on the park."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段稱那個鏡頭 It is a glorious shot，第四段稱讚觀察狼群的片段 worth the price of a ticket；但第三段批評它 the elegance it imposes on that cascade，最後一段說它 too often substitutes a fable for an explanation。可見評論者認為影像出色，卻把複雜的科學故事過度簡化。(B) 正好相反；(C) 第二段說狼在 1920 年代被消滅是無爭議的事實，影片並未否認；(D) 第四段明言 None of this complexity makes the wolves unimportant。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the reviewer mean by \"The pictures are true. The sentence beneath them is something rather less than that\" in paragraph 1?", "options": ["Some of the film's images were digitally altered.", "The footage is genuine, but the narrator's claim that the landscape depends entirely on wolves is overstated.", "The narrator speaks too quietly to be heard clearly.", "The film's subtitles contain spelling mistakes."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段旁白說 none of this would exist without the wolves；評論者說畫面是真的，但「畫面底下的那句話」——也就是旁白——並不完全真實。第三段接著說明河谷的變化還有其他許多原因，證明旁白言過其實。(A) 評論者明說 The pictures are true；(C)(D) 文中並未提及音量或字幕，sentence 指的是旁白所說的那句話。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the review, which is one reason willows have not recovered everywhere?", "options": ["Wolves have begun to feed on young willow shoots.", "Tourists have trampled the riverbanks.", "Elk have returned in greater numbers than before.", "Some streams cut deeper channels while beavers were absent, lowering the water table."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：the recovery of willows has been strikingly patchy, partly because decades without beavers allowed some streams to cut deeper channels, lowering the water table so that willows struggle to regrow。(A) 文中並未提及狼吃柳樹；(B) 文中並未提及遊客；(C) 第二段說狼回歸後麋鹿數量 fell substantially。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"cure-all\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a dangerous predator", "an expensive medicine", "a single solution to every problem", "a temporary measure"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：Stories of a single heroic species restoring an entire ecosystem are enormously appealing... If wolves are sold as a cure-all, the eventual discovery that they are not risks discrediting the more modest... case。cure-all 指「萬靈丹」，即能解決所有問題的單一方法，呼應前面「單一英雄物種拯救整個生態系」的說法。(A) 狼雖是掠食者，但與 cure-all 的意思無關；(B) 是從字面 cure「治療」產生的聯想；(D) 與「能解決一切」的意思不符。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the reviewer think the film's oversimplification matters?", "options": ["It may encourage people to start hunting wolves again.", "It will make the film less popular with general audiences.", "It shows that the filmmakers never actually visited Yellowstone.", "Exaggerated claims may later be exposed, undermining the more modest case for restoring wolves."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：That omission matters more than it might seem... If wolves are sold as a cure-all, the eventual discovery that they are not risks discrediting the more modest, and more defensible, case for bringing them back。(A) 文中並未提及重新獵狼；(B) 評論者反而說這類故事 enormously appealing；(C) 第四段稱讚拍攝的耐心 evident in every frame，顯示團隊確實在現場拍攝。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a248", "title": "The Medicine We Hope Never to Use: The Broken Market for New Antibiotics", "title_zh": "我們希望永遠用不到的藥：新抗生素失靈的市場", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Health", "Business"], "passage": "Imagine a company that spends a decade or more and a small fortune developing a product, wins approval from regulators, and is then told by the world's leading experts that its product should be used as rarely as possible. This is not a parable. It is, in rough outline, the position of any firm that succeeds in bringing a genuinely new antibiotic to market, and it goes a long way toward explaining why so few now try.\n\nThe scientific case for new antibiotics is overwhelming. Bacteria evolve resistance to the drugs used against them, and the more a drug is used, the faster resistance tends to spread. Infections that were once routinely cured have become, in some patients, difficult or impossible to treat, and public health authorities have for years described antimicrobial resistance as one of the gravest threats facing modern medicine. Without effective antibiotics, procedures now regarded as routine, from caesarean sections to hip replacements to chemotherapy, would carry a far greater risk of fatal infection.\n\nThe economic case, by contrast, is dismal. Most medicines earn their keep by being sold in volume, often to patients who take them daily for years. Antibiotics are typically taken for days. Worse, a new antibiotic that works against resistant bacteria is precisely the drug that doctors are urged to hold in reserve, deploying it only when older and cheaper drugs have failed, so as to slow the emergence of resistance to it in turn. Such restraint, which physicians call stewardship, is sound medicine. It is also commercially ruinous. In the late 2010s, at least one small company went bankrupt within about a year of winning approval for a new antibiotic, its product praised by clinicians and largely unbought. Many large pharmaceutical firms, observing such outcomes, have quietly withdrawn from the field.\n\nThe resulting predicament resembles one that societies solved long ago in another domain. Nobody expects a fire brigade to be paid per fire extinguished; a town that did so would be rewarding its firefighters for disasters, and would struggle to keep a station open in a quiet year. Instead, fire services are funded for being ready. A growing number of economists argue that new antibiotics should be treated in the same way: not as products to be sold, but as a form of insurance to be maintained.\n\nSeveral proposals follow from this logic. The most discussed is to \"delink\" a company's revenue from the number of doses sold, paying it a fixed annual sum for access to a valuable antibiotic regardless of how much is actually used. England has introduced such an arrangement, often likened to a subscription, for a small number of drugs. Other schemes would offer large rewards for the successful development of antibiotics that meet specified needs, or would fund the costly early stages of research so that fewer promising projects are abandoned before they reach patients.\n\nNone of these remedies is free of difficulty, and their advocates are not always candid about it. A subscription is expensive, and it obliges a government to pay handsomely for a drug that, if all goes well, will sit largely unused, a prospect that is hard to defend to taxpayers when budgets are tight. Deciding which drugs deserve such treatment requires judgments about future threats that may prove mistaken. And because resistant bacteria respect no borders, a scheme adopted by one country confers benefits on all the others, which are tempted to let it carry the cost alone. Antibiotics, in this sense, are a global public good, and global public goods are notoriously underfunded.\n\nStill, the alternative is hardly reassuring. The market as it stands is doing exactly what it was designed to do: rewarding the sale of products, not their availability. That works well enough for most goods. It works poorly for a drug whose greatest success would be to remain, for as long as possible, on the shelf.", "passage_zh": "想像有一家公司，花了十年以上的時間和一大筆錢研發一項產品，獲得主管機關核准上市，接著卻被世界頂尖的專家告知：這項產品應該盡可能少用。這不是寓言。大致而言，這正是任何一家成功把真正全新的抗生素推上市場的公司所面臨的處境，而這在很大程度上解釋了為什麼現在願意嘗試的公司如此之少。\n\n從科學的角度來看，開發新抗生素的理由再充分不過。細菌會對用來對付它們的藥物演化出抗藥性，而一種藥用得越多，抗藥性往往就擴散得越快。有些過去能夠輕易治癒的感染，在某些病人身上已經變得難以治療，甚至無藥可醫；多年來，公共衛生機構一直把抗微生物藥物抗藥性形容為現代醫學所面臨最嚴重的威脅之一。少了有效的抗生素，從剖腹產、髖關節置換到化學治療，這些如今被視為例行的醫療程序，都會面臨高得多的致命感染風險。\n\n相較之下，從經濟的角度來看，前景則相當黯淡。大多數藥物是靠大量銷售來回本，購買者往往是每天服用、一吃就是好幾年的病人。抗生素通常只服用幾天。更糟的是，一種能對付抗藥性細菌的新抗生素，正是醫師被敦促要保留備用的藥物——只有在較舊、較便宜的藥物都失效時才使用，以減緩細菌對它也產生抗藥性的速度。這種克制——醫師稱之為「抗生素管理」——在醫學上是正確的，在商業上卻是毀滅性的。2010 年代後期，至少有一家小公司在新抗生素獲准上市後大約一年內就宣告破產，它的產品受到臨床醫師讚揚，卻幾乎沒人購買。許多大型藥廠看到這樣的結果，已經悄悄退出了這個領域。\n\n由此產生的困境，類似於社會很久以前就在另一個領域解決過的問題。沒有人期望消防隊按撲滅的火災次數領錢；一個這麼做的城鎮，等於是在為災難獎勵消防員，而且在平靜無事的年頭，恐怕很難讓消防隊繼續運作。相反地，消防單位拿到經費，是因為他們隨時待命。越來越多的經濟學家主張，新抗生素也應該用同樣的方式對待：不是當作要銷售的產品，而是當作一種需要維持的保險。\n\n從這個邏輯出發，產生了好幾種提案。最常被討論的，是把公司的收入與售出的劑量「脫鉤」：不論實際用了多少，每年支付公司一筆固定金額，以換取取得某種珍貴抗生素的權利。英格蘭已經針對少數幾種藥物採行這種常被比喻為「訂閱制」的安排。其他方案則會為成功研發出符合特定需求之抗生素的團隊提供高額獎勵，或是資助成本高昂的早期研究階段，讓有前景的計畫不致於那麼常在送到病人手上之前就遭到放棄。\n\n這些解方沒有一個是毫無困難的，而提倡者也不見得總是坦白說明這些困難。訂閱制所費不貲，而且它迫使政府為一種藥物支付高額費用，而這種藥如果一切順利，大部分時間都會擱著不用——在預算吃緊時，這種前景很難向納稅人交代。決定哪些藥物值得這樣的待遇，需要對未來的威脅做出判斷，而這些判斷可能會出錯。此外，由於抗藥性細菌不受國界限制，一個國家所採行的方案會讓其他所有國家受惠，而這些國家就會想讓那個國家獨自承擔成本。就這個意義而言，抗生素是一種全球公共財，而全球公共財向來出了名地經費不足。\n\n儘管如此，另一種選擇也很難讓人安心。目前的市場正在做它被設計來做的事：獎勵產品的銷售，而不是產品的可取得性。對大多數商品來說，這樣運作得還算不錯。但對於一種最大的成功在於盡可能長久地留在架上的藥物而言，這樣的機制就運作得很差。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the central problem described in the passage?", "options": ["Doctors prescribe new antibiotics far too often.", "Bacteria have become resistant to every known antibiotic.", "Regulators refuse to approve new antibiotics.", "The usual way of paying for medicines gives companies little reason to develop new antibiotics that are meant to be used sparingly."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段點出公司研發成功後卻被要求 used as rarely as possible，這 goes a long way toward explaining why so few now try；第三段說明藥物 earn their keep by being sold in volume，而新抗生素卻被保留備用，因此 commercially ruinous；最後一段總結市場 rewarding the sale of products, not their availability。(A) 第三段說醫師被要求保留使用，與此相反；(B) 太極端，第二段只說某些感染 difficult or impossible to treat；(C) 第一段說產品 wins approval from regulators。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 3, \"stewardship\" refers to ______.", "options": ["the process of getting a drug approved by regulators", "selling antibiotics at reduced prices", "using a valuable drug carefully and sparingly to slow the spread of resistance", "replacing older antibiotics with new ones as quickly as possible"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：doctors are urged to hold in reserve, deploying it only when older and cheaper drugs have failed, so as to slow the emergence of resistance... Such restraint, which physicians call stewardship。可知 stewardship 指為了延緩抗藥性而審慎、節制地使用珍貴的藥物。(A) 核准上市與此無關；(B) 文中並未提到降價；(D) 與「只在舊藥失效時才使用」相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention fire brigades in paragraph 4?", "options": ["To illustrate a service that is paid for being ready rather than for how often it is used", "To suggest that firefighters should be trained to give antibiotics", "To argue that fire services are also badly underfunded", "To prove that paying per use always leads to disaster"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Nobody expects a fire brigade to be paid per fire extinguished... Instead, fire services are funded for being ready，接著說新抗生素也應被視為 a form of insurance to be maintained。作者用消防隊類比「因隨時待命、而非因使用次數而獲得報酬」的服務。(B) 文中並未提及；(C) 經費不足是第六段談全球公共財時才提到的，與消防隊無關；(D) always 太極端，作者只是用類比來說明。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the \"subscription\" arrangement mentioned in paragraph 5?", "options": ["It pays companies more when more doses are used.", "It is meant to reward companies even if their antibiotic is rarely prescribed.", "It has already solved the problem of antibiotic resistance worldwide.", "England now uses it to pay for all new medicines."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：paying it a fixed annual sum for access to a valuable antibiotic regardless of how much is actually used，英格蘭採行的正是 such an arrangement, often likened to a subscription。可推知這種制度讓公司即使藥物很少被使用，也能獲得報酬。(A) 與 regardless of how much is actually used 相反；(C) 第六段說這些解方都有困難，並未說問題已經解決；(D) 文中說只用於 a small number of drugs。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which best describes the writer's attitude toward the proposed remedies?", "options": ["Fully confident that they will succeed", "Sympathetic to their logic but frank about their costs and difficulties", "Opposed to them because they waste taxpayers' money", "Uninterested, since the market will correct itself"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段作者認同把抗生素當成保險的邏輯；第六段卻指出 None of these remedies is free of difficulty，例如費用高昂、判斷可能出錯、其他國家搭便車；第七段又說 the alternative is hardly reassuring。可見作者支持其邏輯，但坦白指出困難。(A) 與第六段所列的困難不符；(C) 作者提到納稅人難以接受，但並未因此反對；(D) 最後一段說現行市場 works poorly，並不認為市場會自我修正。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a249", "title": "Living by the Numbers: Confessions of a Reformed Self-Tracker", "title_zh": "活在數字裡：一位改過自新的自我追蹤者的告白", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Health", "Technology"], "passage": "For a little over a year, I knew more about my own body than any of my ancestors could have dreamed of knowing, or so I believed. A band on my wrist counted my steps, estimated my heart rate every few minutes and claimed to know how long I had spent in each stage of sleep. One app tallied what I ate; another logged my moods on a five-point scale. Each morning began with a small verdict delivered to my phone, a \"readiness score\" and a sleep grade, and I came, by degrees, to consult it before consulting myself.\n\nI was hardly alone, and I do not wish to sound like the victim of some sinister plot. The idea that self-knowledge might be pursued through measurement has an honorable lineage; as a young man, Benjamin Franklin kept a chart on which he marked his daily lapses from thirteen virtues. The movement that gave the practice its modern name, the Quantified Self, emerged around 2007 among technology enthusiasts with genuinely curious motives. Tracking can be illuminating. People with diabetes rely on frequent measurement for good reason, and others have discovered, through patient logging, the food that triggered their headaches or the late coffee that was sabotaging their nights. For many, a number on a screen is the nudge that finally gets them out of a chair.\n\nMy own difficulties began, as such things often do, with success. Having discovered that I could raise my sleep score by going to bed earlier, I began going to bed earlier in order to raise my sleep score, an apparently trivial inversion that turned out to matter a great deal. The number had ceased to describe my rest and become its purpose. I lay awake worrying about how my lying awake would look in the morning's report. Sleep researchers, I later learned, have coined a word for this condition: orthosomnia, a preoccupation with perfect sleep data that can itself make sleep worse. That a device intended to improve my nights should have been costing me them struck me, eventually, as funny. It took several months to strike me at all.\n\nWhat I had stumbled into was a private version of a principle familiar to economists and named after one of them, Charles Goodhart, usually paraphrased as \"when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.\" Applied to institutions, it helps explain why a school judged by test scores may begin teaching to the test. Applied to a single person, it describes something subtler and more corrosive. The tracker did not merely misrepresent my health; it gradually reorganized my attention around whatever it happened to be able to count. Steps, which are easy to measure, came to matter more than stretching, which is not. A slow walk with a friend counted for less than a solitary march around the block at a brisker pace.\n\nThere is also the matter of accuracy, about which such devices are less forthcoming than their advertising. Wrist-worn trackers are reasonably good at counting steps and at measuring heart rate at rest; their estimates of sleep stages, calories burned and \"stress\" are considerably shakier, the product of algorithms that infer a great deal from a little. I had been taking advice, in other words, from an instrument whose confidence exceeded its knowledge, a quality I would not tolerate in a friend.\n\nI have not thrown the band away. It lives in a drawer, and I take it out occasionally when there is a specific question I want answered, after which I put it back. That, I have come to think, is roughly the right relationship to have with such devices: one in which the person asks the questions and the instrument answers them, rather than the reverse. The body is not a dashboard. It is a remarkably sophisticated instrument in its own right, one that evolved to report on itself through hunger, fatigue and a hundred subtler signals. I had simply stopped listening to it, because something else was talking more loudly.", "passage_zh": "有一年多的時間，我對自己身體的了解，多過我任何一位祖先所能夢想的程度——至少我是這麼以為的。手腕上的一條手環會計算我的步數，每隔幾分鐘估算一次我的心率，還聲稱知道我在每個睡眠階段各待了多久。一個應用程式記錄我吃了什麼；另一個則用五分量表記錄我的心情。每天早上，都以手機傳來的一個小小判決揭開序幕——一個「準備度分數」和一個睡眠評等——而我也漸漸養成先問它、再問自己的習慣。\n\n我絕非唯一的一個，而我也不想讓自己聽起來像是某個陰險陰謀的受害者。透過測量來追求自我認識，這個想法有著可敬的淵源：班傑明‧富蘭克林年輕時就曾製作一張表格，每天記下自己在十三項美德上的缺失。替這種做法取了現代名稱的「量化自我」運動，大約在 2007 年興起於一群動機真誠、充滿好奇心的科技愛好者之間。追蹤可以帶來啟發。糖尿病患者有充分的理由仰賴頻繁的測量；也有人透過耐心的記錄，找出了引發自己頭痛的食物，或是一直在破壞自己睡眠的那杯晚間咖啡。對許多人來說，螢幕上的一個數字，就是終於讓他們從椅子上站起來的那一點推力。\n\n我自己的麻煩，就像這類事情常有的情況一樣，是從成功開始的。發現早點上床可以提高睡眠分數之後，我開始為了提高睡眠分數而早點上床——這是一個看似微不足道的顛倒，結果卻影響重大。這個數字不再是對我休息狀況的描述，而變成了休息的目的。我躺在床上睡不著，擔心自己睡不著這件事會讓早上的報告看起來如何。後來我才知道，睡眠研究人員已經為這種狀況創造了一個詞：「完美睡眠焦慮」（orthosomnia），指的是一種對完美睡眠數據的執著，而這種執著本身反而可能讓睡眠變差。一個原本用來改善我夜晚的裝置，竟然一直在讓我失去夜晚，最後我覺得這件事很好笑。只是，光是要意識到這件事，就花了我好幾個月。\n\n我誤打誤撞陷入的，是經濟學家所熟知的一條原則的個人版本；這條原則以其中一位經濟學家查爾斯‧古德哈特命名，通常被轉述為：「當一項指標變成目標，它就不再是一項好的指標。」套用在機構上，它有助於解釋為什麼一所以考試成績來評鑑的學校，可能會開始為考試而教學。套用在個人身上，它描述的則是某種更微妙、也更具侵蝕性的東西。這個追蹤器不只是錯誤地呈現了我的健康狀況；它還逐漸讓我的注意力，重新圍繞著它碰巧能夠計算的那些東西打轉。容易測量的步數，變得比難以測量的伸展運動更重要。和朋友一起慢慢散步，算起來還不如獨自一人以較快的步伐繞著街區疾走。\n\n還有準確度的問題，對於這一點，這類裝置可遠不像它們的廣告那樣大方坦白。戴在手腕上的追蹤器在計算步數和測量靜止心率方面相當準確；但它們對睡眠階段、燃燒的卡路里和「壓力」的估算就不可靠得多了，那些都是演算法從少量資訊推論出大量結論的產物。換句話說，我一直在聽從一件自信超過其知識的儀器所給的建議——這種特質，換作是朋友，我可無法容忍。\n\n我並沒有把手環丟掉。它待在抽屜裡，偶爾當我有某個具體的問題想得到答案時，我會把它拿出來，之後再放回去。我逐漸認為，這大致就是與這類裝置相處的正確關係：由人提出問題，由儀器回答，而不是反過來。身體不是一個儀表板。它本身就是一件極為精密的儀器，經過演化，能透過飢餓、疲倦和上百種更細微的訊號來回報自身的狀況。我只是不再傾聽它，因為另一個東西講話更大聲。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main point?", "options": ["Fitness trackers are useless and should be thrown away.", "Self-tracking can be useful, but when the numbers become goals in themselves, they can distort how we live.", "Sleep scores are the most accurate information wearable devices provide.", "The Quantified Self movement was created mainly to sell expensive devices."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段承認 Tracking can be illuminating；第三、四段說明當數字 had ceased to describe my rest and become its purpose，就如古德哈特原則所說，指標一旦變成目標便會扭曲注意力；最後一段主張由人提問、由儀器回答的關係。(A) 作者並未丟掉手環，仍會在有具體問題時使用；(C) 第五段說睡眠階段的估算 considerably shakier；(D) 第二段說這個運動的參與者 with genuinely curious motives。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by \"an apparently trivial inversion\" in paragraph 3?", "options": ["The writer began sleeping during the day and staying awake at night.", "The writer turned the tracker upside down to get better readings.", "The writer started going to bed later than before.", "Instead of describing the writer's sleep, the score became the reason for the writer's behavior."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Having discovered that I could raise my sleep score by going to bed earlier, I began going to bed earlier in order to raise my sleep score... The number had ceased to describe my rest and become its purpose。因果關係顛倒了：原本是分數反映睡眠，後來卻是為了分數而睡。(A)(B) 是從 inversion「倒轉」字面產生的聯想；(C) 作者是更早而非更晚上床。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer's comment at the end of paragraph 3 reveal?", "options": ["The writer still does not understand what orthosomnia means.", "The writer is angry with sleep researchers.", "The writer can now see the irony of the situation, although it took a long time to notice it.", "The writer believes the device was deliberately designed to cause sleeplessness."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段結尾：That a device intended to improve my nights should have been costing me them struck me, eventually, as funny. It took several months to strike me at all。作者如今看出「用來改善睡眠的裝置反而讓自己失眠」的諷刺，但當初花了好幾個月才察覺。(A) 作者在同段已解釋這個詞；(B) 作者並未對研究人員生氣；(D) 文中說裝置是 intended to improve my nights，並非故意害人失眠。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the example of steps and stretching in paragraph 4 illustrate?", "options": ["Activities that are easy to measure can come to seem more important than those that are not.", "Stretching is less beneficial to health than walking.", "Walking with a friend is a waste of time.", "Trackers measure stretching more accurately than steps."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：it gradually reorganized my attention around whatever it happened to be able to count. Steps, which are easy to measure, came to matter more than stretching, which is not。這個例子說明，容易測量的活動會變得比難以測量的活動更受重視。(B) 作者並未比較兩者對健康的實際益處；(C) 和朋友散步「算起來」較不值得，正是作者所批評的扭曲，並非作者本身的看法；(D) 與 stretching, which is not [easy to measure] 相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 5, which of the following are wrist-worn trackers reasonably good at measuring?", "options": ["Steps and resting heart rate", "Sleep stages and stress", "Calories burned and mood", "Stress and resting heart rate"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：Wrist-worn trackers are reasonably good at counting steps and at measuring heart rate at rest; their estimates of sleep stages, calories burned and \"stress\" are considerably shakier。(B) 睡眠階段和壓力都屬於較不可靠的項目；(C) 卡路里的估算較不可靠，而心情是另一個應用程式記錄的，並非手環測量的；(D) 靜止心率雖然準確，但壓力的估算並不可靠。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a250", "title": "Quieter Evenings: A Policy on Messages After Working Hours", "title_zh": "更安靜的夜晚：關於下班後訊息的政策", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Notice", "Career"], "passage": "Dear colleagues,\n\nFollowing the consultation conducted this autumn, and after extensive discussion with the staff council, I am writing to set out the company's new policy on communication outside working hours, which will take effect on 1 March. I should say at the outset what the policy is not. It is not a prohibition on sending messages in the evening, nor a signal that the company regards flexible working with suspicion. It is, rather, an attempt to remove an expectation that has grown up among us without anyone ever having decided that it should: namely, that a message sent at any hour deserves a reply at any hour.\n\nThe reasons for the change will be familiar to many of you, not least because a number of you raised them during the consultation. Smartphones have made it possible to be reachable almost everywhere, and what is possible has a way of becoming what is assumed. Several colleagues described checking their inboxes late at night not because anything urgent was expected, but because they feared appearing less committed than those who did. Research on work and well-being, while not unanimous on every point, broadly supports their experience: an inability to detach mentally from work is associated with poorer sleep and greater exhaustion. Nor are we alone in taking such concerns seriously. France, one of the first countries to act, has since 2017 required larger employers to negotiate arrangements of this kind, and a number of other governments and companies have since followed suit.\n\nWith effect from 1 March, therefore, the following principles will apply. No employee will be expected to read or respond to work-related messages outside their contracted hours, and no employee will suffer any disadvantage, whether in appraisals, promotion or the allocation of work, for declining to do so. Managers are asked to refrain from sending non-urgent messages to their teams outside working hours; where it is more convenient to draft a message in the evening, the scheduled-send function should be used so that it arrives the following morning. Those who choose, for reasons of their own, to work at unconventional times remain free to do so, provided that they do not expect others to keep pace with them.\n\nIt would be naive to pretend that urgency never arises. Genuine emergencies, such as a serious threat to the safety of people or premises, a failure of systems on which clients depend, or an immovable deadline imposed by an external authority, will continue to justify contact out of hours. In such cases, contact should be made by telephone rather than by message, precisely so that the exceptional nature of the request is unmistakable. Each department will maintain a voluntary on-call rota for these situations, and those who serve on it will be compensated in accordance with the arrangements set out in the attached annex.\n\nColleagues who work with partners in other time zones have, understandably, asked how the policy applies to them. Teams in this position are asked to agree, by the end of February, on a \"core overlap\" of no more than two hours during which members can be reached by colleagues abroad, and to arrange their schedules so that this overlap falls within, rather than in addition to, their working day. Where this proves impracticable, the matter should be referred to Human Resources, who will seek a solution case by case.\n\nNo policy of this kind can succeed by decree alone. Habits formed over years will not dissolve because a memo has been circulated, and I am conscious that the most powerful signal in any organization is the conduct of those who lead it. I have therefore asked the senior management team, myself included, to observe the policy with particular care. We will review its operation in September, drawing on an anonymous staff survey, and I would encourage anyone who encounters difficulties before then to raise them with their line manager or with the staff council. With thanks for your many contributions to the consultation, and in the hope that our evenings may become a little quieter,\n\nThe Managing Director", "passage_zh": "各位同仁：\n\n在今年秋天進行的意見徵詢之後，並經過與員工代表會的充分討論，我謹以此函說明公司關於下班時間通訊的新政策，該政策將自 3 月 1 日起生效。首先我要說明這項政策「不是」什麼。它並非禁止在晚上發送訊息，也不表示公司對彈性工作抱持懷疑。相反地，它試圖消除一種在我們之間逐漸形成、卻從來沒有任何人決定它應該存在的期待：也就是，任何時間發出的訊息，都理應在任何時間得到回覆。\n\n許多同仁對這次改變的理由應該都不陌生，尤其因為有不少人在意見徵詢期間就提出過這些理由。智慧型手機讓人幾乎在任何地方都能被聯絡到，而「可能做到」的事，往往會變成「理所當然」的事。有幾位同事描述，他們深夜查看收件匣，並不是因為預期有什麼緊急的事，而是害怕自己看起來不如那些會這麼做的人投入。關於工作與身心健康的研究，雖然並非在每一點上都意見一致，但大致上支持他們的經驗：無法在心理上與工作抽離，與睡眠品質較差和更嚴重的疲憊感有關。認真看待這類問題的，也不只我們。法國是最早採取行動的國家之一，自 2017 年起便要求規模較大的雇主就這類安排進行協商，此後也有其他一些政府和公司起而效尤。\n\n因此，自 3 月 1 日起，將適用以下原則。公司不會期待任何員工在合約規定的工作時間以外閱讀或回覆與工作相關的訊息，任何員工也不會因為不這麼做，而在考績、升遷或工作分配上蒙受任何不利。請各級主管避免在下班時間向團隊成員發送非緊急訊息；若在晚上撰寫訊息較為方便，應使用排程傳送功能，讓訊息在隔天早上送達。基於個人原因而選擇在非一般時段工作的同仁，仍可自由這麼做，前提是不要期待其他人配合他們的步調。\n\n假裝緊急情況永遠不會發生，未免太過天真。真正的緊急情況——例如人員或場所的安全受到嚴重威脅、客戶所仰賴的系統發生故障，或外部主管機關訂下無法更動的期限——仍然構成在下班時間聯絡的正當理由。在這類情況下，應以電話而非訊息聯絡，目的正是要讓這項請求的特殊性質清楚無誤。各部門將為這類情況維持一份自願參加的值班輪值表，參與值班者將依照附件所列的辦法獲得補償。\n\n與其他時區夥伴合作的同仁，可以理解地詢問了這項政策如何適用於他們。處於這種情況的團隊，請於 2 月底前議定一段不超過兩小時的「核心重疊時段」，讓國外的同事在這段時間內能聯絡到團隊成員，並請安排各自的工作時間，使這段重疊時段落在工作日之內，而不是額外加在工作日之外。若這種做法實際上窒礙難行，應將此事轉交人力資源部門，由其依個案尋求解決之道。\n\n這類政策不可能僅靠一紙命令就獲致成功。多年養成的習慣，不會因為一份備忘錄的傳閱就消失；我也深知，任何組織中最有力的訊號，就是其領導者的行為。因此，我已要求高階管理團隊——包括我自己在內——特別用心地遵守這項政策。我們將於 9 月根據一份匿名員工問卷，檢討這項政策的施行情形；在那之前，若有任何人遇到困難，也歡迎向自己的直屬主管或員工代表會提出。感謝大家在意見徵詢期間提供的許多寶貴意見，並期盼我們的夜晚能變得稍微安靜一些。\n\n總經理", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this notice?", "options": ["To ban all staff from sending work messages in the evening", "To announce that the company will no longer allow flexible working", "To introduce a policy that removes the expectation that staff reply to work messages outside working hours", "To ask staff to vote on whether a new policy should be adopted"], "answer": 2, "explain": "通知開頭說明是要 set out the company's new policy on communication outside working hours，並強調它是 an attempt to remove an expectation... that a message sent at any hour deserves a reply at any hour。(A) 同段明言 It is not a prohibition on sending messages in the evening；(B) 同段說政策並非 a signal that the company regards flexible working with suspicion；(D) 意見徵詢已經結束，政策將自 3 月 1 日生效，並非要員工投票。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the notice, what should a manager who finds it convenient to write a non-urgent message in the evening do?", "options": ["Schedule it so that it is delivered the following morning", "Send it immediately but mark it as unimportant", "Telephone the employee instead", "Forward it to Human Resources"], "answer": 0, "explain": "說明各項原則的那一段：where it is more convenient to draft a message in the evening, the scheduled-send function should be used so that it arrives the following morning。(B) 通知並未提到標示為不重要；(C) 打電話是真正緊急時才使用的方式；(D) 人力資源部門處理的是跨時區安排窒礙難行的情況。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the notice require contact in a genuine emergency to be made by telephone?", "options": ["Telephone calls are cheaper than messages.", "It makes clear that the request is truly exceptional.", "Messages sent at night are automatically deleted.", "Calls can be recorded for the review in September."], "answer": 1, "explain": "談緊急情況的那一段：contact should be made by telephone rather than by message, precisely so that the exceptional nature of the request is unmistakable。用電話聯絡，是為了讓對方清楚知道這是特殊情況。(A)(C)(D) 通知中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"impracticable\" in the paragraph about colleagues in other time zones is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["unpopular", "against the law", "unnecessary", "not possible to carry out in practice"], "answer": 3, "explain": "談跨時區同仁的那一段，要求團隊議定不超過兩小時的核心重疊時段，接著說 Where this proves impracticable, the matter should be referred to Human Resources, who will seek a solution case by case。需要人資依個案另尋解決之道，表示這個安排「實際上行不通」。(A) 不受歡迎未必需要另尋解決方法；(B) 與法律無關；(C) 若只是不必要，就不需要轉交人資處理。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Near the end of the notice, why does the writer ask senior managers to observe the policy \"with particular care\"?", "options": ["Senior managers have complained most about late-night messages.", "The policy applies only to senior managers.", "Senior managers will be paid extra for following it.", "Staff are more likely to follow what leaders actually do than what a memo says."], "answer": 3, "explain": "通知接近結尾的段落：Habits formed over years will not dissolve because a memo has been circulated, and I am conscious that the most powerful signal in any organization is the conduct of those who lead it. I have therefore asked the senior management team... to observe the policy with particular care。作者認為領導者的實際行為才是最有力的訊號。(A) 通知中並未提到主管抱怨；(B) 政策適用於全體員工；(C) 額外補償是針對自願值班者，而非高階主管遵守政策。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a251", "title": "When Noon Came by Telegraph: The Railways and the Invention of Standard Time", "title_zh": "當正午由電報送達：鐵路與標準時間的誕生", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["History", "Society"], "passage": "There was a time, not so very long ago, when every town owned its noon. Noon was simply the moment at which the sun stood highest over the local church tower, and since the sun crosses the sky from east to west, that moment arrived a little later with every mile one traveled westward. Bristol, a little over a hundred miles west of London, kept its clocks roughly ten minutes behind the capital's, and nobody thought this odd, because nobody could travel between the two cities quickly enough for the discrepancy to matter. A clock in those days was less an authority than a witness: it testified to what the sun was doing overhead.\n\nIt was the railway that rendered this arrangement untenable. A timetable is a promise made across distance, and a promise that means one thing at the departure platform and another at the destination is scarcely a promise at all. Passengers missed connections; worse, on lines where trains ran in both directions along a single track, a confusion of a few minutes could prove fatal. In 1840 the Great Western Railway took the then radical step of running all its services on London time, and other companies soon followed. By the mid-1850s the great majority of public clocks in Britain had been brought into line with Greenwich, though it was not until 1880 that Parliament got around to making Greenwich Mean Time the legal standard, a reminder that the law, as so often, arrived to ratify what commerce had long since settled.\n\nThe change did not go unresisted. Some townspeople regarded the new time as a metropolitan imposition, an arrogant claim by London to own the sun. Certain public clocks were fitted with two minute hands, one for local time and one for \"railway time\", an arrangement that can still be seen on the old Corn Exchange in Bristol. To dismiss such objections as mere provincial stubbornness would be to miss what was at stake. For centuries, time had been something one read from nature; now it was something one received from an institution. Indeed, from the 1850s it was quite literally transmitted: electrical signals sent along telegraph wires from the Royal Observatory at Greenwich allowed distant stations to set their clocks within seconds of the official hour.\n\nIn the United States, where distances were vaster and railway companies more numerous, the confusion was correspondingly worse. Each company tended to run on the time of its headquarters or of some major city along its line, so that a large station might display several clocks, each telling a different time. The railways' solution, adopted on 18 November 1883, was to divide the continent into a handful of broad time zones, each an hour apart. In many towns noon arrived twice that day, once by the sun and once by the new standard, which is why the occasion is sometimes remembered as \"the day of two noons\". The following year, an international conference in Washington agreed to take the meridian through Greenwich as the starting point for measuring longitude, laying the groundwork for today's global system of time zones. In the United States, as in Britain, the law lagged behind practice: standard time did not become federal law until 1918.\n\nWhat is striking, in retrospect, is how swiftly the artificial became the natural. Within a generation few people would have thought to consult the sun; the clock had ceased to report the time and begun to define it. So thoroughly do we live with the consequences of that inversion that we barely notice them. We speak of \"losing an hour\" when the clocks change for summer, as though time itself had been confiscated, and we feel obscurely cheated when a time-zone boundary places sunset at an hour that seems to belong to some other place.\n\nNone of this is to romanticize the age of local noon, which was, after all, an age in which most people seldom traveled far from home. Standard time was a genuine achievement, and the coordination it made possible, first of trains and telegraphs, later of broadcasts, flights and the global networks on which our phones depend, would be unthinkable without it. Still, it is worth remembering that the hours displayed on our screens are a convention, negotiated by railway managers and ratified by governments, not a fact handed down from the heavens. The sun, for its part, continues to reach its highest point over each town at its own moment, entirely indifferent to the agreement we have made to ignore it.", "passage_zh": "曾經有一段時間——其實距今並不算太久——每個城鎮都擁有屬於自己的正午。所謂正午，不過是太陽升到當地教堂鐘樓上空最高點的那一刻；由於太陽自東向西橫越天空，人每往西走一哩，這一刻就會來得稍晚一些。位於倫敦以西一百多哩的布里斯托，時鐘大約比首都慢十分鐘，卻沒有人覺得這有什麼奇怪，因為沒有人能在兩座城市之間移動得夠快，快到讓這點差距變得要緊。在那個年代，時鐘與其說是權威，不如說是證人：它見證的是太陽在頭頂上的動向。\n\n是鐵路讓這種安排再也行不通。時刻表是一種跨越距離所做的承諾，而一個在出發月臺代表一回事、到了目的地又代表另一回事的承諾，幾乎算不上承諾。旅客錯過轉乘班車；更糟的是，在列車雙向共用單一軌道的路線上，幾分鐘的混淆就可能釀成致命的後果。1840 年，大西部鐵路公司採取了在當時相當激進的做法，讓旗下所有班次都依倫敦時間行駛，其他公司不久也紛紛跟進。到了 1850 年代中期，英國絕大多數的公共時鐘都已經與格林威治對齊，然而直到 1880 年，國會才終於著手把格林威治標準時間定為法定標準——這提醒我們，法律一如往常，往往是事後才來追認商業早已拍板定案的事。\n\n這項改變並非沒有遭遇抵抗。有些鎮民認為新的時間是首都強加於人的，是倫敦傲慢地宣稱連太陽都歸它所有。某些公共時鐘被裝上兩根分針，一根指示當地時間，一根指示「鐵路時間」，這種設計至今仍可在布里斯托的舊穀物交易所看到。若把這些反對意見斥為不過是鄉下人的固執，就會忽略其中真正利害攸關之處。數百年來，時間一直是人們從自然中讀取的東西；如今它卻成了從某個機構那裡接收的東西。事實上，從 1850 年代起，時間確確實實是被「傳送」出來的：格林威治皇家天文臺透過電報線發出電訊號，讓遠方的車站能把時鐘校準到與官方時刻相差不過數秒。\n\n在美國，距離更加遼闊，鐵路公司也更多，混亂的程度也就相應更嚴重。每家公司往往依照總部所在地或沿線某座大城市的時間營運，結果一座大車站可能掛著好幾面時鐘，各自顯示不同的時間。鐵路業者的解決辦法於 1883 年 11 月 18 日開始實施：把整個大陸劃分為少數幾個寬廣的時區，彼此相差一小時。那一天，許多城鎮的正午來了兩次，一次依太陽，一次依新標準，這也是為什麼這一天有時被稱為「兩個正午的日子」。隔年，在華盛頓召開的一場國際會議同意以通過格林威治的子午線作為測量經度的起點，為今日的全球時區制度奠定了基礎。在美國，法律也和在英國一樣落後於實際做法：標準時間直到 1918 年才成為聯邦法律。\n\n回頭看來，令人訝異的是人為的東西多麼快就變成了自然的東西。不到一個世代，已經很少有人會想到去看太陽；時鐘不再報告時間，而是開始定義時間。我們與這種顛倒所帶來的後果共處得如此徹底，以至於幾乎察覺不到它們。夏令時間調整時鐘時，我們說自己「少了一個小時」，彷彿時間本身被沒收了；而當時區界線把日落安排在一個似乎屬於別處的時刻時，我們也會隱隱覺得自己吃了虧。\n\n這一切並不是要把「地方正午」的時代浪漫化，畢竟在那個時代，大多數人很少離家遠行。標準時間是一項貨真價實的成就，它所促成的協調——先是火車與電報，後來是廣播、航班，以及我們的手機所仰賴的全球網路——若沒有它，簡直無法想像。儘管如此，我們仍值得記住：螢幕上顯示的鐘點是一種約定，由鐵路經理人協商出來、再由政府追認，而不是上天賜下的事實。至於太陽，它依然在各自的時刻升到每座城鎮上空的最高點，對我們約定好要忽視它這件事，完全不以為意。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?", "options": ["To argue that local solar time was more accurate than standard time and ought to be restored", "To trace how the needs of the railways produced standard time and to reflect on how a convention came to feel natural", "To explain the scientific reasons why the sun reaches its highest point at different moments in different places", "To praise the Great Western Railway for creating the international system of time zones"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從各地自有正午談起，第二至四段說明鐵路如何促成英美的標準時間，第五、六段則反思 how swiftly the artificial became the natural 以及鐘點只是 a convention。(A) 太過極端，末段明言 None of this is to romanticize the age of local noon，且稱標準時間是 a genuine achievement；(C) 太陽何時最高只在第一段作為背景，並非主旨；(D) 大西部鐵路只在第二段提到，且它只是改用倫敦時間；為全球時區制度奠定基礎的是第四段提到的華盛頓國際會議。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In the first paragraph, the writer says a clock was then \"less an authority than a witness.\" This means that a clock at that time ______.", "options": ["was often inaccurate and therefore could not be trusted", "was usually kept in a church, where it reminded people of their religious duties", "was regarded as more important than the sun itself", "recorded the position of the sun over a particular place rather than imposing a time of its own"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：A clock in those days was less an authority than a witness: it testified to what the sun was doing overhead. 冒號後說明時鐘只是「見證」太陽在當地頭頂的位置，而不是自行規定時間的權威，與第五段 the clock had ceased to report the time and begun to define it 形成對照。(A) 文中沒有說時鐘不準確；(B) church tower 只是用來說明正午的位置，與宗教義務無關；(C) 意思正好相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer point out that Parliament did not make Greenwich Mean Time the legal standard until 1880?", "options": ["To suggest that the law merely confirmed a change that business had already brought about", "To blame Parliament's delay for the railway accidents described earlier", "To show that most British towns refused to accept railway time until 1880", "To show that the Great Western Railway had broken the law in 1840"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：it was not until 1880 that Parliament got around to making Greenwich Mean Time the legal standard, a reminder that the law... arrived to ratify what commerce had long since settled. 作者藉此指出法律只是事後追認鐵路業者早已完成的改變。(B) 作者只說時間混淆可能致命，並未把事故歸咎於國會；(C) 與原文矛盾，到 1850 年代中期 the great majority of public clocks 已改用格林威治時間；(D) 文中完全沒有提到違法。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "How does the writer regard the townspeople who objected to railway time?", "options": ["As foolish provincials who were simply afraid of modern technology", "As people whose complaints were justified because London time was less accurate", "As people who sensed, perhaps rightly, that something important about the nature of time was changing", "As a tiny and unimportant group whose resistance the writer finds amusing"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：To dismiss such objections as mere provincial stubbornness would be to miss what was at stake. 接著說時間從 something one read from nature 變成 something one received from an institution，可見作者認為反對者察覺到時間本質上的重大轉變。(A) 正是作者認為不該採取的看法；(B) 文中沒有說倫敦時間比較不準確；(D) 作者並未嘲笑他們，反而認真看待其反對。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, which of the following is true of the changes in the United States?", "options": ["The federal government introduced time zones in 1883 in order to reduce railway accidents.", "Time zones were introduced by the railway companies decades before they became law.", "Before 1883, every American station kept a single clock set to the time of Washington.", "The international conference in Washington was held before the railways adopted standard time."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：The railways' solution, adopted on 18 November 1883... the law lagged behind practice: standard time did not become federal law until 1918. 時區由鐵路公司於 1883 年實施，三十多年後才成為聯邦法律。(A) 1883 年實施的是鐵路業者而非聯邦政府；(C) 原文說大車站可能掛著 several clocks, each telling a different time；(D) 原文說國際會議是在 The following year，即鐵路改制的隔年才舉行。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a252", "title": "Wallpaper with Feelings: A Night Inside an Immersive Van Gogh Show", "title_zh": "有情緒的壁紙：沉浸式梵谷展的一夜", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Arts"], "passage": "Let me begin with a confession that will do my credibility no favors: I enjoyed myself. For roughly forty minutes I sat on a cushion in a converted warehouse while the walls, the floor and very nearly the ceiling dissolved into swirling blue, and the stars of Van Gogh's night sky drifted past my shoulders to the accompaniment of strings swelling at precisely the moments when strings are expected to swell. Children ran through fields of projected sunflowers. A couple beside me photographed each other bathed in yellow. Nobody, as far as I could tell, was bored, which is more than can be said for most afternoons in most museums.\n\nThe show, one of several near-identical productions that have toured the world's cities in recent years, makes no secret of its ambitions. It does not want you to look at paintings; it wants you to be inside them. Brushstrokes are enlarged to the size of a door, set gently in motion and layered over one another, so that a self-portrait melts into a café terrace, which melts in turn into a branch of almond blossom. Between the images, quotations from the painter's letters to his brother scroll across the walls in elegant type, lest anyone miss the point that we are in the presence of a suffering genius.\n\nIt would be easy, and more than a little smug, to sneer. The organizers can fairly claim to have brought Van Gogh to people who would never willingly cross the threshold of an art museum, and there is no shortage of museums that seem to have done everything in their power to keep such people out: the hushed rooms, the watchful guards, the wall labels written in a dialect that only curators speak. If a teenager who has never heard of Saint-Rémy leaves the warehouse curious about the man who painted those stars, something has been gained.\n\nAnd yet what has been gained is not, I think, quite what is advertised. A painting is not merely an image; it is an object of a particular size, made by a particular hand, with a surface that rewards study. Van Gogh often laid on his paint so thickly that it stands up from the canvas in ridges, and much of the pleasure of seeing one of his pictures in person lies in the slow discovery of how those ridges catch the light differently as you shift your position. Projected thirty feet high, the ridges become flat smears of color; animated, the deliberate stroke becomes a special effect, and the painting itself a kind of wallpaper with feelings. The show offers immersion in place of attention, and the two are not the same. Attention is something you give; immersion is something done to you.\n\nThere is also an irony, which the gift shop does nothing to dispel, in the spectacle of an artist who sold almost nothing in his lifetime being converted into an inexhaustible source of revenue: sunflower umbrellas, starry-night socks, a cocktail whose name I will charitably decline to repeat. One need not be a purist to feel that the swirls of a picture painted in an asylum, by a man in considerable distress, sit oddly on a souvenir mug. The soundtrack, too, tells you at every turn what to feel, as though the paintings could not be trusted to manage that on their own.\n\nNone of which makes the experience worthless, only misnamed. What these productions sell is not Van Gogh but a mood borrowed from him, much as a film adaptation sells a mood borrowed from a novel. My advice, for what it is worth, is to go, enjoy the cushions, and then, if you possibly can, seek out a single real canvas by the same painter, or by anyone at all, and stand in front of it for longer than feels comfortable. It will not move, and nobody will play music to tell you how to respond. That, far from being a shortcoming, is precisely what it has to offer.", "passage_zh": "容我先坦白一件不太能替我的公信力加分的事：我玩得很開心。大約四十分鐘裡，我坐在一座由倉庫改建的場館中的坐墊上，四周的牆壁、地板，連天花板也幾乎一併融化成漩渦般的藍色，梵谷夜空中的星星從我肩旁飄過，背景的弦樂恰好在大家預期弦樂該揚起的那一刻揚起。孩子們在投影出來的向日葵田裡奔跑。我身旁的一對情侶互相拍下對方沐浴在黃光中的樣子。就我所見，沒有一個人覺得無聊——這一點，大多數博物館在大多數的午後可是做不到的。\n\n這場展覽是近年來在世界各大城市巡迴的幾個幾乎一模一樣的製作之一，它毫不掩飾自己的企圖。它不希望你去「看」畫，而是要你置身於畫「之中」。筆觸被放大到一扇門那麼大，被輕輕地賦予動態，再一層層疊合，於是一幅自畫像融化成一座咖啡館露臺，而咖啡館露臺又接著融化成一枝盛開的杏花。在畫面與畫面之間，畫家寫給弟弟的信件摘句以優雅的字體在牆面上捲動，唯恐有人沒領會到：我們正置身於一位受苦天才的面前。\n\n要冷嘲熱諷很容易，但這麼做也未免太自鳴得意了。主辦單位大可理直氣壯地宣稱，他們把梵谷帶到了那些絕不會心甘情願踏進美術館門檻的人面前；而許多美術館似乎也確實想盡辦法把這些人拒於門外：靜悄悄的展廳、虎視眈眈的警衛，還有用一種只有策展人才懂的「方言」寫成的展品說明牌。如果一個從沒聽過聖雷米的青少年走出倉庫時，對這位畫出那些星星的人產生了好奇，那麼總算是有所收穫。\n\n然而，我認為所得到的收穫並不完全是廣告所宣稱的那樣。一幅畫不僅僅是一個影像；它是一件有特定尺寸、出自特定之手、表面值得細細端詳的物件。梵谷常常把顏料塗得極厚，厚到在畫布上隆起一道道稜脊，而親眼看他的畫作，樂趣有很大一部分就在於慢慢發現：當你移動位置時，這些稜脊捕捉光線的方式也隨之不同。投影到三十呎高時，稜脊變成平板的色塊；一旦動畫化，刻意下的筆觸便淪為一種特效，畫作本身則成了某種有情緒的壁紙。這場展覽以「沉浸」取代了「專注」，而兩者並不相同。專注是你主動付出的；沉浸則是別人對你施加的。\n\n此外還有一種諷刺，而禮品店對於化解它毫無幫助：一位生前幾乎沒賣出過任何作品的藝術家，如今被轉化為取之不盡的財源——向日葵雨傘、星夜襪子，還有一款雞尾酒，其名稱我就厚道地不再重複了。不必是個純粹主義者也會覺得，一幅在精神療養院裡、由一個身處極大痛苦中的人所畫出的作品，它的漩渦印在紀念馬克杯上，顯得格格不入。配樂也一樣，每到一處就告訴你該有什麼感受，彷彿這些畫作無法憑自己做到這一點。\n\n這一切並不代表這個體驗毫無價值，只是名不副實。這些製作販賣的並不是梵谷，而是向他借來的一種氛圍，就像一部改編電影販賣的是向小說借來的氛圍。我的建議——如果還算有點參考價值的話——是：去吧，好好享受那些坐墊；然後，如果你辦得到，去找一幅真正的畫作——同一位畫家的也好，任何一位畫家的也罷——在它面前站上一段久到讓你有點不自在的時間。它不會動，也不會有人放音樂告訴你該如何反應。而這一點，絕非缺點，恰恰正是它所能給予的。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the reviewer begin by admitting to having \"enjoyed\" the show?", "options": ["To make clear that the review will be entirely positive", "To suggest that the show is superior to every art museum", "To acknowledge the show's genuine appeal before raising doubts about it", "To apologize for having criticized the show in an earlier review"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段以 a confession that will do my credibility no favors: I enjoyed myself 開頭，承認展覽確實吸引人；但第四段起 And yet what has been gained is not... quite what is advertised，轉而提出質疑，可見開頭是「先讓步、後批評」。(A) 全文後半多有批評，並非完全正面；(B) 作者只說觀眾不無聊這點勝過多數博物館，並非全面優於所有美術館，過於極端；(D) 文中沒有提到先前的評論。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the reviewer imply by saying that some wall labels are \"written in a dialect that only curators speak\"?", "options": ["Museums often describe art in language that ordinary visitors find hard to follow.", "Museums should translate their wall labels into more foreign languages.", "Curators usually come from a different region from most of their visitors.", "The immersive show relies too heavily on quotations from Van Gogh's letters."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段把 the hushed rooms, the watchful guards, the wall labels written in a dialect that only curators speak 列為美術館把一般人 keep out 的原因。dialect 是比喻，暗指說明牌使用艱澀的專業術語，一般觀眾看不懂。(B)(C) 把 dialect 誤解為字面上的外語或地方方言；(D) 信件摘句出現在第二段，與說明牌無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the fourth paragraph, what is chiefly lost when Van Gogh's paintings are projected and animated?", "options": ["The original colors, which projectors cannot reproduce accurately", "The story of the painter's life, which the show leaves out", "The audience's enjoyment, since people soon grow bored", "The physical surface and scale of the works, which reward close and active looking"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：A painting is... an object of a particular size... with a surface that rewards study，並說顏料隆起的 ridges 在現場會隨觀看角度捕捉不同光線，投影後卻 become flat smears of color。可見失去的是畫作的實體表面與尺寸，以及由此而來的專注觀看。(A) 作者沒有說投影機無法重現顏色；(B) 第二段說展覽引用了畫家的信件，並未略去其生平；(C) 與第一段 Nobody... was bored 矛盾。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In the fifth paragraph, the reviewer's tone toward the gift shop and its merchandise is best described as ______.", "options": ["enthusiastic and admiring", "wryly disapproving", "furious and contemptuous", "neutral and purely factual"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段指出 an irony：生前幾乎賣不出畫的藝術家如今成了 an inexhaustible source of revenue，又用 a cocktail whose name I will charitably decline to repeat 這種帶有挖苦意味的說法，並說畫作印在馬克杯上 sit oddly，屬於帶著揶揄的不以為然。(A) 顯然不是讚賞；(C) 太過極端，作者語氣克制，還說 One need not be a purist；(D) 作者明顯表達了立場，並非中立。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which statement best summarizes the reviewer's final judgment?", "options": ["The show is pleasant, but it is no substitute for the quiet, demanding experience of standing before a real painting.", "The show is a fraud, and readers should avoid it altogether.", "Real paintings are boring, so immersive shows represent the future of art.", "The show would be improved by louder music and faster-moving images."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段：None of which makes the experience worthless, only misnamed，並建議讀者 go, enjoy the cushions，之後再去真正的畫作前久站，因為畫作不動、沒有音樂引導，這 is precisely what it has to offer。(B) 作者明明建議讀者去看，並非叫人避開；(C) 與作者推崇真實畫作的立場相反；(D) 作者批評配樂主導情緒，不可能希望音樂更大聲。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a253", "title": "No Word for It? The Romance of the Untranslatable", "title_zh": "找不到對應的字？關於「無法翻譯」的浪漫想像", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Culture"], "passage": "Every few months, a new list makes its way around the internet: twenty, thirty or fifty \"untranslatable\" words from the world's languages, each accompanied by a soft-focus photograph. There is the German Schadenfreude, the pleasure taken in another person's misfortune; the Portuguese saudade, a longing for something absent that may never return; the Japanese komorebi, sunlight filtering through the leaves of trees; the Danish hygge, a kind of snug, candlelit contentment. The lists are charming, and they usually come with a claim, sometimes stated and sometimes merely implied, that is considerably less so: that because a language has a word for something, its speakers inhabit a world that the rest of us cannot reach.\n\nThe claim deserves a hearing, not least because it is not altogether foolish. Languages do carve up experience differently. Russian obliges its speakers to distinguish between a lighter and a darker blue in a way that English does not, and a number of languages require the speaker to mark, in the very grammar of a sentence, whether an event was witnessed personally or merely heard about. Anyone who has learned a second language well knows the curious sensation of a thought that seems to arrive more readily in one tongue than in the other. It would be strange indeed if the words we have at hand made no difference whatever to what we notice.\n\nBut consider what the lists themselves do. Each \"untranslatable\" word is followed, almost without exception, by a perfectly serviceable English translation: a phrase rather than a single word, admittedly, but a translation nonetheless. If komorebi can be rendered as \"sunlight filtering through leaves\", then English speakers are plainly capable of perceiving the phenomenon; they simply lack a one-word label for it. To confuse the absence of a word with the absence of a concept is rather like concluding that, because English lacks a single everyday word for the day after tomorrow, English speakers are incapable of making plans for it. German, as it happens, has übermorgen; English speakers nonetheless seem to keep their appointments.\n\nIndeed, the lists tend to undermine their own premise. English absorbed Schadenfreude long ago, and has more recently taken up hygge with an enthusiasm bordering on the commercial; neither borrowing required its new users to undergo some profound reorganization of their inner lives. Words travel precisely because the experiences they name are already familiar to the people who borrow them. What a borrowed word supplies is not a new feeling but a convenient handle for an old one, together with a dash of foreign glamour that the plain English equivalent lacks.\n\nHerein, I suspect, lies the real appeal of the untranslatable, and it is not an altogether flattering one. To present saudade as a mysterious essence available only to Portuguese speakers is to turn a living language, used by millions of people who also complain about traffic and argue about football in it, into a museum of exquisite moods. It is a compliment of sorts, but it is the compliment a tourist pays: admiring, distant and faintly condescending. Whole cultures are reduced to a single word, and the word to a mood that can be purchased, along with the scented candles, at the nearest homeware store.\n\nNone of this is to say that translation is easy, or that nothing is lost in it. What gets lost, however, is rarely the bare meaning. It is the rhythm of a phrase, the echo of a proverb, the social weight of a particular choice of word: whether it is formal or intimate, old-fashioned or slangy, tender or ironic. A translator confronts not a wall of impossibility but a series of trade-offs, sacrificing one quality in order to preserve another. Seen in this light, every word is partly untranslatable, and none entirely so. That is a less romantic conclusion than the lists would have us accept, but it has the merit of taking both languages, and the people who speak them, seriously.", "passage_zh": "每隔幾個月，就會有一份新的清單在網路上流傳：二十、三十或五十個來自世界各種語言的「無法翻譯」的字，每個字都配上一張柔焦照片。有德文的 Schadenfreude，指因他人的不幸而感到的快樂；有葡萄牙文的 saudade，指對某個已經不在、或許永遠不會回來的事物的思念；有日文的 komorebi，指從樹葉間灑落的陽光；還有丹麥文的 hygge，一種溫暖舒適、燭光搖曳的滿足感。這些清單很迷人，但它們通常伴隨著一個主張——有時明說，有時只是暗示——而這個主張可就遠沒有那麼迷人了：既然某種語言有一個字來指稱某件事物，那麼說這種語言的人就生活在一個我們其他人無法觸及的世界裡。\n\n這個主張值得我們聽一聽，尤其因為它並非全然愚蠢。不同語言確實以不同的方式切分經驗。俄語要求說話者區分較淺的藍與較深的藍，英語則沒有這種要求；而有些語言要求說話者在句子的文法本身中標明，某件事是親眼目睹的，還是只是聽說的。任何把第二語言學得很好的人，都熟悉那種奇妙的感覺：某個想法似乎用其中一種語言比用另一種更容易浮現。如果我們手邊有哪些字，對我們會注意到什麼毫無影響，那才真是奇怪。\n\n但請想想這些清單本身做了什麼。每一個「無法翻譯」的字後面，幾乎無一例外都附上了一個完全堪用的英文翻譯：誠然是一個片語而非單一的字，但終究是個翻譯。如果 komorebi 可以譯成「從樹葉間灑落的陽光」，那麼說英語的人顯然能夠察覺這個現象，他們只是缺少一個單字的標籤而已。把「沒有某個字」與「沒有某個概念」混為一談，就好比因為英文沒有一個常用的單字表示「後天」，就斷定說英語的人無法為後天做計畫。湊巧的是，德文就有 übermorgen 這個字；但說英語的人似乎照樣能準時赴約。\n\n事實上，這些清單往往自己推翻了自己的前提。英語很久以前就吸收了 Schadenfreude，近年來又以近乎商業化的熱情接納了 hygge；這兩次借用都不需要新的使用者對自己的內心生活進行什麼深刻的重組。字詞之所以能夠流傳，正是因為它們所指稱的經驗，對借用它們的人來說本來就很熟悉。一個借來的字所提供的，並不是一種新的感受，而是為一種早已熟悉的感受提供一個方便的稱呼，外加一點平實的英文對應說法所缺少的異國魅力。\n\n我猜想，「無法翻譯」真正的吸引力就在這裡，而且這並不是什麼特別光彩的吸引力。把 saudade 說成只有葡萄牙語使用者才能領會的神祕本質，就是把一種活生生的語言——數以百萬計的人也用它來抱怨交通、爭論足球——變成一座陳列精緻情緒的博物館。這算是一種恭維，但那是觀光客式的恭維：讚嘆、疏遠，又帶著一絲高高在上。整個文化被化約成單一個字，而這個字又被化約成一種情調，可以在最近的居家用品店裡，連同香氛蠟燭一起買到。\n\n這一切並不是說翻譯很容易，或者翻譯中沒有任何損失。然而，損失的很少是字面上的基本意義。損失的是一個片語的節奏、一句諺語的回聲、選用某個字所帶有的社會分量：它是正式還是親暱、老派還是俚俗、溫柔還是反諷。譯者面對的不是一堵「不可能」的高牆，而是一連串的取捨，犧牲某種特質以保全另一種特質。從這個角度來看，每個字都有一部分無法翻譯，卻沒有一個字是完全無法翻譯的。這個結論不如那些清單要我們接受的說法那麼浪漫，但它的好處是，認真看待了兩種語言，以及說這兩種語言的人。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following best expresses the writer's main argument?", "options": ["Some words truly cannot be translated, so learners should study them only in the original language.", "The language people speak has no effect at all on what they notice or think.", "English is a poorer language than German, Portuguese or Japanese because it lacks many words.", "The popular idea of \"untranslatable\" words exaggerates how far languages shut their speakers off from one another."], "answer": 3, "explain": "作者在第三、四段指出清單上的字其實都附有 a perfectly serviceable English translation，並說 the lists tend to undermine their own premise；末段結論 every word is partly untranslatable, and none entirely so。主旨是「無法翻譯」的說法誇大了語言之間的隔閡。(A) 與作者的論點相反；(B) 太過極端，第二段承認 Languages do carve up experience differently；(C) 作者從未貶低英語，反而說缺少單字不代表缺少概念。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What is the main function of the second paragraph?", "options": ["To offer conclusive proof that the lists of untranslatable words are correct", "To concede that the opposing view contains some truth before the writer challenges it", "To introduce a new topic, the history of the Russian language", "To show that grammar matters more than vocabulary in learning a language"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段開頭 The claim deserves a hearing, not least because it is not altogether foolish，接著舉俄語區分深淺藍色等例子承認語言確實影響我們注意的事物；第三段卻以 But consider what the lists themselves do 轉折反駁。可見第二段是先讓步承認對方有部分道理。(A) 作者並不認為清單正確，conclusive proof 太過頭；(C) 俄語只是一個例子，並非新主題；(D) 文中沒有比較文法與字彙在學習上的重要性。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention that German has a single word for \"the day after tomorrow\"?", "options": ["To show that German speakers think more carefully about the future than English speakers do", "To prove that German has a larger vocabulary than English", "To show, with gentle humor, that lacking a single word does not mean lacking the concept", "To give an example of a German word that English has borrowed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：To confuse the absence of a word with the absence of a concept is rather like concluding that... English speakers are incapable of making plans for it. 接著說德文雖有 übermorgen，English speakers nonetheless seem to keep their appointments，以輕鬆幽默的方式說明沒有單字不等於沒有概念。(A) 正是作者所嘲諷的推論；(B) 文中沒有比較字彙量；(D) 英語借用的例子是第四段的 Schadenfreude 和 hygge，不是 übermorgen。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest in the fifth paragraph about presenting saudade as \"a mysterious essence available only to Portuguese speakers\"?", "options": ["It appears to praise Portuguese culture but in fact views it from a superior outsider's distance.", "It accurately describes how most Portuguese speakers feel in their daily lives.", "It is a problem caused mainly by careless professional translators.", "It encourages people to learn Portuguese, which the writer welcomes."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段說這種說法 is a compliment of sorts, but it is the compliment a tourist pays: admiring, distant and faintly condescending，並指出它把活生生的語言變成 a museum of exquisite moods。表面上是讚美，實際上是以局外人居高臨下的眼光看待對方文化。(B) 作者強調葡語使用者也用這種語言抱怨交通、爭論足球，並非整天沉浸在神祕情緒中；(C) 文中批評的是網路清單，不是專業譯者；(D) 作者沒有提到學習葡萄牙語。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the final paragraph, what is usually lost in translation?", "options": ["The basic meaning of most words", "Nothing at all, provided the translator is skilled enough", "Qualities such as rhythm, associations and the degree of formality of a word", "The grammar of the original sentence, which can never be reproduced"], "answer": 2, "explain": "末段：What gets lost, however, is rarely the bare meaning. It is the rhythm of a phrase, the echo of a proverb, the social weight of a particular choice of word: whether it is formal or intimate... 損失的是節奏、聯想與正式程度等特質。(A) 原文明說 rarely the bare meaning；(B) 與 None of this is to say... that nothing is lost in it 矛盾；(D) 文中並未提到文法無法重現。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a254", "title": "The Same River Twice: On Rereading a Children's Book", "title_zh": "兩度踏入同一條河：重讀一本童書", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Arts", "Life"], "passage": "The first time I read The Little Prince I was nine, and I read it, as children read most things, for the plot. A pilot crashes in the desert; a small boy with golden hair appears and asks him to draw a sheep; the boy, it emerges, has come from a tiny planet with three volcanoes and a single, rather vain rose. I remember being indignant at the grown-ups who, shown the pilot's childhood drawing of a boa constrictor digesting an elephant, insisted that it was a hat. I knew exactly whose side I was on.\n\nI read it again at nineteen, in a mood of self-conscious sophistication, and was faintly embarrassed by it. The sentiment seemed laid on with a trowel; the aphorisms, which I had once thought profound, now struck me as the sort of thing printed on greeting cards. That I had become precisely the kind of grown-up the book gently mocks did not occur to me at the time. It is a peculiarity of that age that one can be perfectly lucid about everyone's limitations except one's own.\n\nThe third reading came in my thirties, at a hospital, the book picked up more or less at random from a shelf in a waiting room. This time it was neither the boy nor the aphorisms that held me but the fox, who asks to be tamed and then, when the moment of parting comes, admits that he will weep. The boy protests that the friendship has done the fox no good, since it ends only in grief. The fox's reply, that he has gained the color of the wheat fields, which will from now on remind him of the boy's golden hair, struck me with a force out of all proportion to its simplicity. I had read that page twice before. I had never, in any meaningful sense, read it at all.\n\nThe Greek philosopher Heraclitus is credited with the observation that no one steps into the same river twice, since neither the river nor the person is the same. Rereading is that observation made tangible, with one telling difference: here it is only the person who has changed. The words on the page do not alter, which is, after all, the whole point of printing them, and so they serve as a kind of fixed instrument against which the reader's own changes can be measured. What I noticed at nine, what I disdained at nineteen, what undid me in my thirties: these are not, strictly speaking, facts about the book. They are a record, kept in the margins of a children's story, of the person who happened to be holding it.\n\nIt would be pleasant to conclude from this that every rereading is a revelation, but honesty forbids it. Some books, revisited, simply shrink. An adventure novel that once kept me awake past midnight turned out, when I returned to it at thirty, to be written in clichés I had been too young to recognize as such, and no amount of reflection on my own growth could redeem it. Nor is rereading always a matter of progress. There are things the nine-year-old saw, the plain injustice of grown-ups who could not see the elephant inside the snake, that the nineteen-year-old had lost and that the adult has had to work, with only partial success, to recover.\n\nPerhaps the truest thing one can say about returning to a book, then, is that it is less a matter of understanding more than of understanding differently, and that the losses are as instructive as the gains. Nobody would claim that reading a book once is a waste of time. But a book read only once is a little like a city visited only once, in a single season: one has seen it, certainly, but one has not yet seen what it becomes when the light changes, nor what one becomes in it.", "passage_zh": "我第一次讀《小王子》是九歲，而且我讀它的方式就像孩子讀大多數東西一樣：為了情節。一名飛行員在沙漠中墜機；一個有著金色頭髮的小男孩出現，請他畫一隻綿羊；後來才知道，這男孩來自一顆小小的星球，星球上有三座火山和一朵相當愛慕虛榮的玫瑰。我記得自己曾對那些大人憤憤不平：飛行員把他小時候畫的「蟒蛇正在消化一頭大象」拿給他們看，他們卻堅持那是一頂帽子。我很清楚自己站在哪一邊。\n\n十九歲時我又讀了一次，當時的我帶著一種刻意顯得世故的心態，結果讀得有點難為情。書中的情感似乎渲染得過了頭；那些我曾經覺得深奧的格言，如今在我看來就像印在賀卡上的句子。我當時完全沒有想到，自己已經變成了這本書溫和嘲諷的那種大人。那個年紀有個特點：對每個人的侷限都能看得一清二楚，唯獨看不見自己的。\n\n第三次閱讀是在我三十幾歲時，在一家醫院裡，書是我從候診室的書架上隨手拿起的。這一次吸引我的既不是男孩，也不是那些格言，而是狐狸：牠請求被馴養，接著在分離的時刻來臨時，坦承自己會哭。男孩抗議說，這段友誼對狐狸毫無益處，因為它只以悲傷收場。狐狸回答，牠得到了麥田的顏色，從此以後麥田會讓牠想起男孩的金髮——這個回答以一種與其簡單程度完全不成比例的力量擊中了我。那一頁我之前讀過兩次。但就任何有意義的層面而言，我從來不曾真正讀過它。\n\n希臘哲學家赫拉克利特據說曾指出，沒有人能兩次踏入同一條河，因為河已不是原來的河，人也已不是原來的人。重讀讓這句話變得具體可感，只是有一個耐人尋味的差別：在這裡，改變的只有人。書頁上的文字不會改變——畢竟，這正是把它們印刷出來的全部用意——因此它們成了一種固定的量尺，可以用來測量讀者自身的變化。我九歲時注意到的、十九歲時不屑一顧的、三十幾歲時令我潰堤的，嚴格說來都不是關於這本書的事實。它們是一份紀錄，記在一本童書的頁邊空白處，記錄的是碰巧捧著這本書的那個人。\n\n若能由此得出「每次重讀都是一次啟示」的結論，那該有多好，但誠實不容許我這麼說。有些書，重讀之後就是縮水了。一本曾讓我看到過了午夜還捨不得睡的冒險小說，在我三十歲重讀時，才發現是用一堆陳腔濫調寫成的，只是我當年太年輕，認不出那是陳腔濫調；無論我如何反思自己的成長，都挽救不了它。重讀也並不總是一種進步。有些東西是九歲的我看見了——大人們看不見蛇肚子裡那頭大象，這是再明白不過的不公——而十九歲的我卻已失去，成年的我則得費盡力氣才能找回，而且只找回了一部分。\n\n那麼，關於重返一本書，或許最真切的說法是：重讀與其說是理解得更多，不如說是理解得不同；而其中的失去，與獲得一樣具有啟發性。沒有人會說，一本書只讀一次是在浪費時間。但一本只讀過一次的書，有點像一座只在單一季節造訪過一次的城市：你當然見過它了，但你還沒看見當光線改變時它會變成什麼模樣，也還沒看見置身其中的自己會變成什麼模樣。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How rereading a book at different ages reveals as much about the reader as about the book", "Why The Little Prince is the greatest children's book ever written", "Why adults should stop reading books that were written for children", "How the writer's opinion of the fox changed from dislike to admiration"], "answer": 0, "explain": "作者描述九歲、十九歲與三十幾歲三次閱讀《小王子》的不同感受，第四段點出主旨：these are not... facts about the book. They are a record... of the person who happened to be holding it，即重讀所反映的是讀者本身的變化。(B) 太過極端，作者從未如此評價；(C) 與全文旨趣相反；(D) 作者前兩次只是沒注意到狐狸，並非討厭牠。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest about his or her nineteen-year-old self?", "options": ["At nineteen, the writer understood the book better than at any other age.", "The writer was right at nineteen to dismiss the book as sentimental.", "Without realizing it, the writer had become like the adults the book criticizes.", "The writer disliked the book at nineteen because it was too difficult."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：That I had become precisely the kind of grown-up the book gently mocks did not occur to me at the time. 又說那個年紀的人看得清每個人的侷限 except one's own，可見作者暗示自己當時已不知不覺變成書中嘲諷的大人。(A) 第五段說十九歲的自己已失去九歲時看得見的東西；(B) 作者帶著自嘲回顧當時的態度，並不認為自己是對的；(D) 作者覺得的是 embarrassed，原因是內容煽情，並非太難。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"laid on with a trowel\" in the second paragraph suggests that the book's sentiment seemed ______.", "options": ["carefully hidden beneath the plot", "excessive and heavy-handed", "fresh and original", "entirely absent"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：The sentiment seemed laid on with a trowel; the aphorisms... now struck me as the sort of thing printed on greeting cards. trowel 是抹泥刀，lay it on with a trowel 比喻「塗得太厚、過分誇張」；後半句說格言像賀卡上的句子，也呼應「煽情過度」之意。(A)(D) 與「塗得很厚」的意象相反；(C) 作者此時覺得內容俗套，並不新穎。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In the fourth paragraph, why does the writer describe the printed words as \"a kind of fixed instrument\"?", "options": ["Because the book had been published in many different editions", "Because the writer used the book to measure the width of its margins", "Because later editors had changed some of the book's words", "Because the unchanging text makes it possible to notice how the reader has changed"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The words on the page do not alter... and so they serve as a kind of fixed instrument against which the reader's own changes can be measured. 文字固定不變，因此能像量尺一樣反映讀者的改變。(A) 文中未提版本；(B) 把 margins 和 measured 作字面理解，誤解了比喻；(C) 與 do not alter 矛盾。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What is the main purpose of the fifth paragraph?", "options": ["To prove that the way children read is always superior to the way adults read", "To recommend adventure novels to young readers", "To admit that the writer no longer enjoys reading at all", "To qualify the essay's argument by admitting that rereading can disappoint and that growing older brings losses as well as gains"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段開頭 It would be pleasant to conclude... that every rereading is a revelation, but honesty forbids it，接著舉冒險小說重讀後 simply shrink 的例子，又說 Nor is rereading always a matter of progress，九歲時看得見的東西後來反而失去。此段是在修正、限定前文的論點。(A) always superior 太過極端，作者只說有些東西失去了；(B) 作者提到冒險小說是說它重讀後令人失望，並非推薦；(C) 文中沒有這種說法。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a255", "title": "In Defense of the Runaway Trolley", "title_zh": "為失控的電車辯護", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Society"], "passage": "Few thought experiments have been mocked as relentlessly as the trolley problem. In its best-known form, which grew out of a case described by the British philosopher Philippa Foot in 1967 and was later developed and named by the American philosopher Judith Jarvis Thomson, a runaway trolley is hurtling toward five people on the track. You can pull a lever that will divert it onto a side track, where it will kill one person instead. Should you? The case has spawned countless variations, a cottage industry of academic papers and, more recently, an inexhaustible supply of internet jokes, most of which involve ever more baroque arrangements of tracks, levers and victims.\n\nThe critics' objections are not without force, and it would be disingenuous to pretend otherwise. The scenario, they point out, is wildly artificial: in real life one never knows with certainty that the trolley will kill five people, that the lever will work, or that there is no third option, such as shouting a warning. Real moral life, they add, is seldom a matter of dramatic one-off choices made among strangers; it consists far more often in the slow, unglamorous business of keeping promises, telling the truth and paying attention to the people around us. A discipline that lavishes so much energy on imaginary trolleys, the argument concludes, has lost touch with the questions that actually trouble ordinary people.\n\nYet the charge of artificiality, I would argue, mistakes the purpose of the exercise. A thought experiment is artificial in exactly the way that a laboratory experiment is artificial: it deliberately strips away the complications of the real world so that a single variable can be examined in isolation. No physicist imagines that frictionless surfaces exist; she uses them to understand what friction does. The trolley problem is no more a model of moral life than a frictionless surface is a model of a car journey. It is a tool for isolating a question that real situations tend to blur.\n\nAnd the question it isolates is a genuinely puzzling one. Most people, when asked, say that they would pull the lever. Yet when the case is altered so that the only way to stop the trolley is to push a large stranger off a footbridge into its path, most people recoil, even though the arithmetic, one life for five, is identical. Why should it matter whether one redirects a threat or uses a person as the means of stopping it? Is there a moral difference between harm one intends and harm one merely foresees, or between doing and allowing? These are not idle puzzles. They lie beneath debates about medical triage, about the conduct of war, and about how public money should be divided between those whose suffering is visible and those whose suffering is merely statistical.\n\nIt is sometimes said that the arrival of self-driving cars has made the trolley problem newly practical, since engineers must decide how a vehicle should behave when a collision is unavoidable. This, I suspect, overstates the case: such dilemmas are likely to be vanishingly rare compared with the ordinary work of avoiding accidents altogether, and an engineer would do better to make the brakes reliable than to rank potential victims. But the defense of the trolley problem does not rest on its having direct applications. Its value lies less in the answers it yields than in the discomfort it produces, in the way it forces us to notice that our intuitions, which feel so solid, can point in contradictory directions.\n\nThe jokes, in the end, are something of a tribute. Nobody bothers to parody an idea that has failed to get under their skin. That so many people who will never read a word of moral philosophy find themselves arguing about levers and footbridges suggests that the trolley has touched something real: the uneasy suspicion that being a good person may require more than good intentions, and that thinking clearly about ethics is not a task we can simply leave to our instincts.", "passage_zh": "很少有思想實驗像「電車難題」這樣被無情地嘲弄。它最廣為人知的版本，源自英國哲學家菲莉帕‧芙特在 1967 年描述的一個案例，後來由美國哲學家茱蒂絲‧賈維斯‧湯姆森加以發展並命名：一輛失控的電車正朝著軌道上的五個人疾駛而去。你可以拉下一根拉桿，讓電車轉向一條支線，電車在那裡則只會撞死一個人。你該這麼做嗎？這個案例衍生出無數變化版本、多到自成一門小產業的學術論文，而近年來更衍生出源源不絕的網路笑話，其中大多涉及越來越繁複誇張的軌道、拉桿與受害者配置。\n\n批評者的反對意見並非毫無道理，假裝它們沒有分量是不誠實的。他們指出，這個情境極其不自然：在現實生活中，人永遠無法確定電車一定會撞死五個人、拉桿一定有效，或者沒有第三種選擇，例如大聲示警。他們還說，真實的道德生活很少是陌生人之間戲劇性的一次性抉擇；它更常是由緩慢而平淡無奇的瑣事所構成：信守承諾、說實話、留心身邊的人。他們的論證最後的結論是：一門把這麼多精力揮霍在想像出來的電車上的學問，已經與真正困擾一般人的問題脫節了。\n\n然而我認為，「不自然」這項指控誤解了這個練習的目的。思想實驗之不自然，恰恰就像實驗室實驗之不自然：它刻意剝除現實世界的種種複雜因素，好讓單一變項能被獨立檢視。沒有哪位物理學家會以為無摩擦的平面真的存在；她用它來理解摩擦力的作用。電車難題不是道德生活的模型，正如無摩擦平面不是一趟汽車旅程的模型。它是一種工具，用來把現實情境往往模糊掉的問題分離出來。\n\n而它所分離出來的問題，確實令人困惑。大多數人被問到時，都說自己會拉下拉桿。然而，當案例改成唯一能讓電車停下的方法，是把一個身材高大的陌生人從天橋上推下去擋住電車時，大多數人卻會退縮——儘管算術完全相同，都是以一命換五命。為什麼「讓威脅轉向」與「把一個人當作阻止威脅的工具」會有差別？蓄意造成的傷害與僅僅預見到的傷害之間、「做」與「任其發生」之間，是否存在道德上的差異？這些並不是無關緊要的謎題。它們潛藏在種種爭論的底層：關於醫療檢傷分類、關於戰爭中的行為，以及關於公共資金應該如何分配給苦難清晰可見的人，和苦難只是統計數字的人。\n\n有人說，自動駕駛汽車的出現讓電車難題變得具有新的實用性，因為工程師必須決定，當碰撞無可避免時，車輛應該如何反應。我懷疑這種說法言過其實：與避免事故發生這項日常工作相比，這類兩難恐怕極為罕見；工程師與其替潛在的受害者排序，不如把煞車做得可靠一點。但為電車難題辯護，並不需要仰賴它有直接的應用。它的價值與其說在於它得出的答案，不如說在於它所引起的不安——它迫使我們注意到，我們那些感覺如此堅定的直覺，可能指向彼此矛盾的方向。\n\n說到底，那些笑話其實也算是一種致敬。沒有人會費心去戲仿一個從未真正觸動自己的想法。這麼多一輩子連一個字的道德哲學都不會讀的人，竟然爭論起拉桿和天橋，這顯示電車觸及了某種真實的東西：一種令人不安的懷疑——做一個好人，或許需要的不只是善意；而清楚地思考倫理，不是一件我們可以單純交給本能去處理的事。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main position on the trolley problem?", "options": ["It is a pointless puzzle that philosophers ought to abandon.", "Despite its artificiality, it is a valuable tool for exposing tensions in our moral intuitions.", "Its main value today is in programming self-driving cars.", "It proves that pushing a stranger off a footbridge can be morally acceptable."], "answer": 1, "explain": "作者在第三段說 the charge of artificiality... mistakes the purpose of the exercise，第五段又說 Its value lies... in the discomfort it produces... our intuitions... can point in contradictory directions。作者承認它不自然，但認為它能揭露道德直覺之間的矛盾。(A) 是第二段批評者的看法；(C) 第五段作者認為這種說法 overstates the case；(D) 作者只提出推人下橋的案例引人思考，並未做出此結論。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the purpose of the second paragraph?", "options": ["To present the critics' case fairly before responding to it", "To show that the writer agrees completely with the critics", "To describe how the trolley problem was invented", "To give practical advice about keeping promises and telling the truth"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段開頭 The critics' objections are not without force, and it would be disingenuous to pretend otherwise，接著完整陳述批評者的論點；第三段則以 Yet the charge of artificiality... mistakes the purpose of the exercise 加以反駁。可見第二段是先公允地呈現對方立場。(B) 作者隨即反駁，並非完全同意；(C) 起源是第一段的內容；(D) 守信、說實話只是批評者用來說明真實道德生活的例子，不是建議。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention a physicist's \"frictionless surfaces\"?", "options": ["To show that physics is a more reliable discipline than philosophy", "To suggest that thought experiments are useful only in the natural sciences", "To argue that the trolley problem should be tested in a laboratory", "To illustrate that leaving out real-world details can be a deliberate method rather than a flaw"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：A thought experiment is artificial in exactly the way that a laboratory experiment is artificial: it deliberately strips away the complications... No physicist imagines that frictionless surfaces exist; she uses them to understand what friction does. 作者以物理學的例子說明，刻意簡化是一種研究方法，而非缺陷。(A) 作者並未比較兩門學科的可靠性；(B) 作者正是要說明這種方法也適用於倫理學；(C) 把 laboratory 作字面理解，文中沒有這種主張。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what does the footbridge version of the problem suggest?", "options": ["Most people are unwilling to save five lives under any circumstances.", "People make moral judgments purely by counting how many lives are saved.", "People's judgments depend on how harm is caused, not only on how many die.", "Most people would push the stranger if they were certain it would work."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：most people recoil, even though the arithmetic, one life for five, is identical. 兩種情境的傷亡數字相同，人們的判斷卻不同，接著作者追問 whether one redirects a threat or uses a person as the means，可見判斷取決於傷害是如何造成的。(A) 大多數人願意在第一種情境拉下拉桿；(B) 正好與「算術相同、判斷卻不同」相反；(D) 文中說大多數人 recoil，並無此說。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How does the writer respond to the claim that self-driving cars have made the trolley problem \"newly practical\"?", "options": ["With skepticism, arguing that such dilemmas will be rare and are not why the problem matters", "With enthusiasm, since the claim proves that philosophy is useful to engineers", "With anger, because engineers have ignored the work of philosophers", "With full agreement, adding that engineers must learn to rank potential victims"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：This, I suspect, overstates the case: such dilemmas are likely to be vanishingly rare...，又說 the defense of the trolley problem does not rest on its having direct applications。作者對此說法存疑，並認為電車難題的價值不在實際應用。(B) 作者並不熱衷於這種說法；(C) 文中沒有憤怒的語氣，也沒有指責工程師；(D) 作者說工程師 would do better to make the brakes reliable than to rank potential victims，與此相反。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a256", "title": "Eyes on the Street, Rents Through the Roof: Jane Jacobs Reconsidered", "title_zh": "街道之眼與天價房租：重新審視珍‧雅各", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Society", "History"], "passage": "In 1961 a writer with no formal training in urban planning published a book that would do more to change the way people think about cities than any number of official reports. Jane Jacobs's The Death and Life of Great American Cities was an assault on the orthodoxy of its day, which held that the crowded, untidy neighborhoods of older cities were slums to be cleared and replaced with orderly towers set in open green space and linked by expressways. Jacobs, who had observed the life of her own street in New York's Greenwich Village with a novelist's attention, argued that this was precisely backwards. The apparent disorder of a busy street, with shops on the ground floor, apartments above and people coming and going at all hours, was not chaos but a complex and fragile order, a \"sidewalk ballet\" in which every participant had a part to play.\n\nAmong her most influential ideas was that of \"eyes on the street\". A street that is used throughout the day, she observed, is watched over less by the police than by the shopkeepers, residents and passers-by who have reason to be there, and it is this informal, almost unconscious surveillance that keeps it safe. Deserted plazas and blank walls, however handsome on an architect's drawing, offer no such protection. Hence her insistence on short blocks, mixed uses and a stock of old buildings alongside the new: diversity of purpose, she believed, produced diversity of people, and diversity of people produced the continuous presence on which a city's safety and vitality depended.\n\nJacobs did not confine herself to argument. When Robert Moses, the formidable official who had reshaped much of New York with bridges, parks and highways, pressed for an expressway that would have cut across Lower Manhattan, she helped lead the campaign against it, and the plan was eventually abandoned. That a loosely organized group of residents could defeat the most powerful builder in the city's history has since become a kind of founding legend, retold whenever neighbors gather to resist a project imposed from above.\n\nSix decades on, her victory looks more complete than she could have imagined, and also more ambiguous. Her principles have become the conventional wisdom of planning schools; few architects today would dare to propose the windswept plazas she ridiculed. Yet many of the neighborhoods she championed have since become some of the most expensive places to live anywhere. The very qualities she celebrated, walkable streets, small shops and handsome old buildings, turned out to be exactly what wealthy buyers were willing to pay for, and the mixture of incomes that had made her street so lively has been steadily priced out.\n\nIt would be unfair to lay the blame for this at her door. Jacobs was well aware that old buildings mattered partly because they were cheap, offering low rents to new businesses and to people who could not afford new construction, and she warned that a successful district could be undone by its own popularity. Still, there is an awkward irony in the spectacle of developers invoking her name to market luxury apartments in the kind of neighborhood that, under their management, will soon house no one who resembles her original neighbors.\n\nThe lesson, perhaps, is not that Jacobs was wrong but that she has been read selectively. Her admirers have inherited her taste for lively streets more readily than her concern for who gets to live on them. A city can, it turns out, preserve every brick of a beloved neighborhood and still lose the very thing that made it worth preserving. To take her seriously today would mean attending not only to how a street looks but to whether the baker, the student and the retired factory worker can still afford to watch it.", "passage_zh": "1961 年，一位從未受過正式都市計畫訓練的作家出版了一本書，這本書對人們思考城市方式的改變之大，是再多官方報告也比不上的。珍‧雅各的《偉大城市的誕生與衰亡》是對當時主流正統觀念的一次猛烈抨擊；那種觀念認為，舊城區那些擁擠、雜亂的街坊是應該清除的貧民窟，應該改建成矗立在開闊綠地中、以快速道路相連的整齊高樓。雅各曾以小說家般的細膩目光，觀察她自己在紐約格林威治村所住的那條街上的生活，她主張這種觀念完全本末倒置。一條熱鬧街道表面上的雜亂——一樓是商店、樓上是住家、人們全天候來來去去——並不是混亂，而是一種複雜而脆弱的秩序，一場每位參與者都有其角色的「人行道芭蕾」。\n\n她最具影響力的觀念之一是「街道之眼」。她觀察到，一條整天都有人使用的街道，與其說是由警察看守，不如說是由那些有理由待在那裡的店主、居民和路人看守著；正是這種非正式、近乎無意識的監看，讓街道保持安全。空蕩蕩的廣場和毫無開口的牆面，無論在建築師的圖面上多麼好看，都提供不了這樣的保護。因此她堅持街廓要短、用途要混合，而且新建築旁要保留一批老建築：她相信，用途的多樣帶來人的多樣，而人的多樣則帶來持續不斷的人潮，城市的安全與活力正有賴於此。\n\n雅各並不只是坐而論道。當羅伯特‧摩西斯——這位以橋梁、公園和公路大幅改造了紐約面貌的強勢官員——力推一條將橫切曼哈頓下城的快速道路時，她協助領導了反對運動，而這項計畫最終被放棄。一群組織鬆散的居民竟能擊敗這座城市史上最有權勢的建造者，這件事從此成了一則奠基傳奇，每當鄰里聚集起來抵抗由上而下強加的建設案時，就會被重新傳述。\n\n六十年過去了，她的勝利看起來比她所能想像的更加徹底，卻也更加曖昧。她的原則已成為都市計畫學院的常識；如今很少有建築師敢提出她所嘲笑的那種狂風呼嘯的廣場。然而，她所捍衛的許多街坊，後來都成了全世界居住成本最高的地方之一。她所讚揚的那些特質——適合步行的街道、小商店、美觀的老建築——結果正是富有的買家願意花錢購買的東西，而當初讓她那條街如此生氣蓬勃的各種收入階層混居，已逐漸因價格高漲而被排擠出去。\n\n把這件事歸咎於她是不公平的。雅各很清楚，老建築之所以重要，部分原因正在於它們便宜，能以低廉的租金提供給新創的店家，以及負擔不起新建房屋的人；她也曾警告，一個成功的街區可能毀於自身的受歡迎程度。儘管如此，看到開發商援引她的名字來推銷豪華公寓，而這類街坊在他們的經營下，很快就不會再住著任何像她當年那些鄰居的人，其中仍有一種令人尷尬的諷刺。\n\n或許，教訓並不在於雅各錯了，而在於她一直被選擇性地解讀。她的追隨者繼承她對熱鬧街道的喜好，遠比繼承她對「誰能住在這些街道上」的關切來得積極。事實證明，一座城市可以保存一個備受喜愛的街坊的每一塊磚，卻仍然失去讓它值得保存的那樣東西。今天若要認真看待她，就意味著不只要留意一條街看起來如何，還要留意麵包師傅、學生和退休的工廠工人，是否仍然負擔得起住在那裡、看著這條街。", "questions": [{"q": "According to the passage, what did the planning orthodoxy of Jacobs's time recommend?", "options": ["Placing small shops beneath apartments to keep streets busy", "Protecting old buildings because they offered low rents", "Clearing crowded older neighborhoods and replacing them with towers, green space and expressways", "Encouraging residents to watch over their own streets"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：the orthodoxy of its day... held that the crowded, untidy neighborhoods of older cities were slums to be cleared and replaced with orderly towers set in open green space and linked by expressways. (A)(B)(D) 都是雅各本人的主張（見第一、二、五段），正是她用來反駁當時正統觀念的看法。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"eyes on the street\" refers to ______.", "options": ["security cameras installed by the city government", "police officers who patrol neighborhoods on foot", "architects who carefully study how buildings are used", "the informal watchfulness of people who are naturally present on a busy street"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：A street that is used throughout the day... is watched over less by the police than by the shopkeepers, residents and passers-by who have reason to be there... this informal, almost unconscious surveillance... keeps it safe. 「街道之眼」指的是自然出現在街上的人所形成的非正式監看。(A) 文中未提監視器；(B) 原文明說 less by the police；(C) 建築師的圖面只是拿來對比，與此概念無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by saying that Jacobs's victory looks \"more complete than she could have imagined, and also more ambiguous\"?", "options": ["Her ideas were accepted at first but have since been rejected by most planners.", "Her ideas are now widely accepted, but their success has had consequences she would not have welcomed.", "She won her battle against Moses, but the expressway was built anyway.", "Historians are uncertain whether she really opposed the expressway at all."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Her principles have become the conventional wisdom of planning schools（勝利很徹底），Yet... neighborhoods she championed have since become some of the most expensive places，原本混居的各收入階層 has been steadily priced out（結果曖昧）。(A) 與 conventional wisdom 矛盾；(C) 第三段說計畫 was eventually abandoned；(D) 第三段明說她 helped lead the campaign against it。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the main function of the fifth paragraph?", "options": ["To defend Jacobs against blame while pointing out how her name is now being misused", "To argue that developers have faithfully followed all of Jacobs's ideas", "To show that Jacobs never considered the cost of housing", "To criticize Jacobs for failing to foresee rising rents"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段開頭 It would be unfair to lay the blame for this at her door，並指出她早已了解老建築便宜的重要，也警告過成功的街區可能毀於受歡迎；接著以 Still, there is an awkward irony 批評開發商 invoking her name to market luxury apartments。此段先為雅各辯護，再指出她的名字遭到濫用。(B) 作者認為開發商違背了她的關懷；(C)(D) 與 Jacobs was well aware... and she warned... 矛盾。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would the writer most likely support?", "options": ["Replacing old neighborhoods with modern towers in order to lower rents", "Planning policies that help lively neighborhoods remain affordable to people with different incomes", "Banning all new construction in historic districts", "Setting aside Jacobs's ideas because they are now out of date"], "answer": 1, "explain": "末段：To take her seriously today would mean attending not only to how a street looks but to whether the baker, the student and the retired factory worker can still afford to watch it. 作者重視讓不同收入的人都住得起熱鬧的街坊。(A) 正是雅各反對的做法；(C) 太過極端，雅各主張新舊建築並存，作者也未提出禁建；(D) 作者說 not that Jacobs was wrong，並未認為她的觀念過時。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a257", "title": "Four Minutes of Not Playing: What John Cage's Silence Is For", "title_zh": "四分多鐘的不演奏：約翰‧凱吉的寂靜有何用處", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Arts"], "passage": "On an August evening in 1952, in a concert hall in the woods near Woodstock, New York, the pianist David Tudor walked on stage, sat down at the piano and closed the lid over the keys. He consulted a stopwatch. After a while he raised the lid, then closed it again to mark the start of a second movement, and later a third. At no point did he play a note. When the piece was over, roughly four and a half minutes after it had begun, he stood and bowed. The work was John Cage's 4'33\", and it has been provoking a mixture of reverence, bafflement and irritation ever since.\n\nThe irritation is easy enough to understand. To many listeners the piece has always looked like the most brazen of confidence tricks: a composer claiming credit for nothing at all. It is the kind of work that practically writes its own jokes, about critics, say, who complain that the pianist rushed the second movement. And by Cage's own later account, not everyone at the first performance was charmed; some listeners began whispering among themselves, and a few walked out before the end.\n\nYet the title of the piece, with its curious precision about minutes and seconds, already hints that something other than nothing is going on. Cage did not regard 4'33\" as silent at all. At that first performance, as he later recalled, the audience could hear the wind stirring in the trees outside during the first movement, rain pattering on the roof during the second, and, during the third, the sounds they themselves made as they murmured and shifted in their seats. The piece consists of whatever happens to occur in the room while the performer refrains from filling it. Its material, in other words, is not silence but attention.\n\nCage had arrived at this idea by more than one route. Shortly beforehand he had visited an anechoic chamber at Harvard University, a room engineered to absorb all echoes, expecting to experience perfect silence. Instead he heard two sounds, one high and one low, and was told afterwards that they were his nervous system and his blood in circulation. Whether or not that explanation was physiologically sound, the conclusion he drew from the experience was decisive: as long as one is alive, there is no such thing as silence. He had also seen the all-white paintings of his friend Robert Rauschenberg, canvases that were never truly blank, since they caught the shadows of passing viewers and the changing light of the room.\n\nSeen in this light, 4'33\" is less a piece of music than a frame, and a frame, as any gallery-goer knows, changes what we notice inside it. The coughs and creaking chairs that we normally edit out of a concert, treating them as interference between ourselves and the music, become for four and a half minutes the music itself. Whether one finds this revelatory or merely clever may depend on temperament. What is harder to deny is that few people who have sat through a performance in good faith come away hearing a room in quite the same way.\n\nThere is, admittedly, something self-defeating about the piece's fame. A gesture that was startling in 1952 cannot be startling twice, and audiences today frequently arrive knowing exactly what will not happen, ready to chuckle at the appropriate moment. The work thus risks becoming what Cage least intended: an anecdote to be retold rather than an experience to be undergone. But perhaps that is a reason to listen to it rather than merely to know about it. Of all the works in the repertoire, it may be the only one that no recording can quite reproduce, since what it asks us to hear is, by definition, whatever is around us now.", "passage_zh": "1952 年八月的一個傍晚，在紐約州伍德斯托克附近林間的一座音樂廳裡，鋼琴家大衛‧都鐸走上舞臺，在鋼琴前坐下，然後闔上了琴鍵蓋。他看了看碼錶。過了一會兒，他掀起琴蓋，接著又再度闔上，標示第二樂章的開始，稍後又標示了第三樂章。他自始至終沒有彈奏任何一個音。樂曲結束時——距開始大約四分半鐘——他起身鞠躬。這部作品就是約翰‧凱吉的《4分33秒》，從那時起，它便一直引發崇敬、困惑與惱怒交雜的反應。\n\n惱怒的反應很容易理解。在許多聽眾看來，這首曲子向來就像是最厚顏無恥的騙局：一位作曲家為「什麼都沒有」邀功。這是那種幾乎自己就會生出笑話的作品，比如說，有樂評抱怨鋼琴家把第二樂章彈得太趕。而根據凱吉本人日後的說法，首演時並不是每個人都為之傾倒；有些聽眾開始彼此竊竊私語，還有幾個人在結束前就離席了。\n\n然而，這首曲子的標題對分與秒的精確堅持頗為奇特，這已經暗示了其中發生的並不是「什麼都沒有」。凱吉根本不認為《4分33秒》是無聲的。據他後來回憶，在那場首演中，聽眾在第一樂章時聽得到外頭的風吹動樹林，第二樂章時聽得到雨點打在屋頂上，而到了第三樂章，則聽得到他們自己在座位上低聲交談、挪動身子所發出的聲響。這首曲子的內容，就是在演奏者克制自己不去填滿這個空間時，房間裡碰巧發生的一切聲音。換句話說，它的素材不是寂靜，而是專注。\n\n凱吉是循著不只一條路徑得出這個想法的。在此之前不久，他參觀了哈佛大學的一間無響室——一個經過設計、能吸收所有回音的房間——原本期待體驗到完全的寂靜。結果他卻聽到兩個聲音，一高一低，事後有人告訴他，那是他的神經系統和血液循環的聲音。無論這個解釋在生理學上是否站得住腳，他從這次經驗所得出的結論都具有決定性：只要人還活著，就不存在寂靜這回事。他也看過友人羅伯特‧勞森伯格的全白畫作，那些畫布從來不是真正空白的，因為它們會映出經過的觀者的影子，以及房間裡不斷變化的光線。\n\n從這個角度來看，《4分33秒》與其說是一首樂曲，不如說是一個畫框；而任何常逛畫廊的人都知道，畫框會改變我們在框內注意到的東西。咳嗽聲、椅子的嘎吱聲，平常在音樂會中都被我們剔除在外，視為我們與音樂之間的干擾，如今在四分半鐘裡，它們本身就成了音樂。人們覺得這是啟示還是只是耍小聰明，或許取決於個人的性情。比較難否認的是，很少有人在真心誠意地坐完一場演出後，聽一個房間的方式還能和從前完全一樣。\n\n誠然，這首曲子的名氣本身就帶有某種自我拆臺的成分。一個在 1952 年令人震驚的舉動，不可能令人震驚第二次；如今的聽眾往往在進場前就清楚知道什麼「不會」發生，準備在該笑的時候輕笑幾聲。這部作品因此面臨變成凱吉最不樂見的東西的風險：一則被反覆傳述的軼事，而不是一段被親身經歷的體驗。但這或許正是我們應該去聆聽它、而不只是知道它的理由。在所有的曲目中，它或許是唯一一首任何錄音都無法真正重現的作品，因為它要我們聽的，依其定義，就是此刻環繞在我們周遭的一切。", "questions": [{"q": "What did David Tudor do during the first performance of 4'33\"?", "options": ["He played very softly so that the audience could hear the rain outside.", "He played the same short passage three times.", "He explained the meaning of the piece to the audience before sitting down.", "He closed and raised the piano lid to mark the movements but played nothing."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：sat down at the piano and closed the lid over the keys... he raised the lid, then closed it again to mark the start of a second movement, and later a third. At no point did he play a note. (A)(B) 與 At no point did he play a note 矛盾；(C) 文中沒有提到他事先向觀眾解說。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention jokes about the piece and the whispering listeners in the second paragraph?", "options": ["To prove that 4'33\" really is a confidence trick", "To show that Cage was angry with his audience", "To acknowledge why many people have found the piece irritating before explaining its purpose", "To suggest that the first performance was poorly played"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段開頭 The irritation is easy enough to understand，接著舉出笑話與首演時觀眾竊竊私語、離席的情形；第三段卻以 Yet the title... already hints that something other than nothing is going on 轉而說明作品的用意。可見第二段是先承認許多人覺得它惱人。(A) 作者只說它在許多人看來 looked like 騙局，自己並不這麼認為；(B) 文中沒有提到凱吉生氣；(D) 鋼琴家把第二樂章彈得太趕只是笑話內容，並非事實。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the writer, what is the real \"material\" of 4'33\"?", "options": ["The sounds that happen to occur in the room, which listeners are led to notice", "Complete silence, which Cage believed could be achieved in a concert hall", "Recordings of wind and rain played through hidden speakers", "The pianist's own breathing and heartbeat"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：The piece consists of whatever happens to occur in the room while the performer refrains from filling it. Its material, in other words, is not silence but attention. 作品的素材是現場自然發生、聽眾被引導去注意的聲音。(B) 與 Cage did not regard 4'33\" as silent at all 及第四段 there is no such thing as silence 矛盾；(C) 風聲雨聲是真實的環境聲，並非錄音；(D) 神經和血液的聲音是凱吉在無響室裡聽到的，與鋼琴家無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the writer's remark \"Whether or not that explanation was physiologically sound\"?", "options": ["The writer believes Cage made up his visit to the anechoic chamber.", "The writer is not fully convinced by the explanation Cage was given, but thinks Cage's conclusion matters more.", "The writer thinks anechoic chambers do not really absorb echoes.", "The writer believes Cage had been trained as a scientist."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Whether or not that explanation was physiologically sound, the conclusion he drew from the experience was decisive. 作者對那兩個聲音是否真是神經與血液的聲音有所保留，但認為重點在於凱吉由此得出「沒有真正的寂靜」這個結論。(A) 作者並未質疑這次參觀是否真有其事；(C) 作者把無響室描述為 engineered to absorb all echoes，並未質疑其功能；(D) 文中沒有這種說法，那個解釋是別人 told 他的。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which best describes the writer's overall attitude toward 4'33\"?", "options": ["Dismissive: it is a clever joke that has been taken far too seriously", "Worshipful: it is the greatest piece of music ever composed", "Indifferent: the writer has no particular view of it", "Sympathetic but not uncritical: the writer defends its idea while admitting that its fame works against it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "作者在第三至五段說明作品的用意，認為它像畫框一樣改變我們聆聽的方式，few people... come away hearing a room in quite the same way；末段卻承認 something self-defeating about the piece's fame，名氣讓它可能淪為軼事。整體態度是同情、支持但並非毫無批判。(A) 作者為作品辯護，並不輕視它；(B) 太過極端，作者還承認有人覺得它 merely clever；(C) 作者有明確立場。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a258", "title": "The Tyranny of the Toenail: Video Review and the Spirit of Sport", "title_zh": "腳趾甲的暴政：影像輔助判決與運動精神", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Sports"], "passage": "For most of football's history, a goal was an event that happened in an instant. The ball crossed the line, the net bulged, and tens of thousands of people leapt to their feet in the same heartbeat. Since the introduction of the video assistant referee, or VAR, which was used at the men's World Cup for the first time in 2018 and has since been adopted by most major leagues, that instant has acquired an asterisk. Players now celebrate with one eye on the referee, who may be pressing a finger to his earpiece, and supporters have learned to hold their joy in reserve, like a check that might yet bounce, while officials in a distant room examine the replay frame by frame.\n\nThe case for the technology is not difficult to make, and its advocates make it with understandable conviction. Refereeing errors have decided matches, careers and occasionally whole tournaments. Fans of a certain age can still recite their grievances: the goal Diego Maradona scored with his hand against England at the 1986 World Cup, say, or the shot by Frank Lampard against Germany in 2010 that bounced well over the line and was not given. The latter, indeed, helped persuade the sport's governing body to embrace goal-line technology, whose value few now dispute. If cameras can prevent injustices of that order, it seems perverse to refuse their help out of mere sentiment.\n\nI have no quarrel with goal-line technology, which answers a yes-or-no question quickly and almost invisibly. My difficulty is with what came next. Having been handed a tool designed to correct glaring mistakes, the game has increasingly used it to adjudicate differences that no human eye could perceive and no spectator would ever have complained about. Goals are now disallowed because an attacker's shoulder, or the tip of his boot, was judged to have been a few centimeters beyond the second-last defender at the moment the ball was played, a moment that must itself be estimated, since even broadcast cameras capture only a limited number of frames per second.\n\nThis, it seems to me, misunderstands what the offside rule is for. The rule exists to stop attackers from loitering beside the goal in wait for a long ball; it was never intended to turn the width of a shoulder into a matter of judicial precision. When the rule was applied by human officials, the margin of error was, in effect, part of the rule, and a tight call that went against one's team was accepted, however grudgingly, as part of the texture of the game. To pursue accuracy to the millimeter is not to enforce the rule more faithfully; it is to enforce a different and stricter rule that nobody ever deliberately chose.\n\nThere is also a cost that the champions of accuracy are reluctant to count. Sport is not merely a mechanism for determining who has won; it is a shared emotional experience, and its peculiar power lies in spontaneity, in the fact that thousands of strangers can be swept up in exactly the same feeling at exactly the same moment. A goal celebrated three minutes after the fact, once a replay has been studied and a line drawn across a screen, is a different kind of event. The joy is real, but it arrives as a verdict rather than an eruption.\n\nNone of this argues for abolishing the cameras altogether. A sensible compromise would confine video review to clear and obvious errors, the kind a reasonable spectator could spot on a single replay, and would accept that a game played by human beings at speed will always contain marginal calls that might have gone either way. Perfect justice, after all, is not the purpose of a football match. Had the game been designed to eliminate every possible error, it would hardly have been designed as a game at all.", "passage_zh": "在足球史的大部分時間裡，進球是一件在一瞬間發生的事。球越過門線，球網鼓起，數以萬計的人在同一個心跳間一躍而起。自從「影像助理裁判」（VAR）問世以來——它在 2018 年首度用於男子世界盃，此後也被大多數主要聯賽採用——那一瞬間便多了一個但書。球員如今慶祝時，總有一隻眼睛盯著裁判，而裁判可能正用手指按著耳機；球迷也學會了先把喜悅保留起來，就像一張可能還會跳票的支票，同時遠方某個房間裡的裁判人員正一格一格地檢視重播畫面。\n\n支持這項科技的理由不難提出，而支持者在提出這些理由時，堅定的態度也情有可原。裁判的錯誤曾經決定比賽、球員生涯，偶爾甚至決定整個賽事的結果。上了一點年紀的球迷至今仍能細數他們的不平：比如說，馬拉度納在 1986 年世界盃對英格蘭時用手打進的那一球，或是 2010 年蘭帕德對德國的那一記射門，球明明彈過門線一大截，卻沒被判進球。事實上，後者正是促使這項運動的管理機構接受門線科技的因素之一，而如今很少有人質疑門線科技的價值。如果攝影機能防止那種程度的不公，那麼僅僅出於情感就拒絕它的幫助，似乎是有悖常理的。\n\n我對門線科技沒有意見，它能迅速、幾乎不著痕跡地回答一個「是或否」的問題。我的疑慮在於後來的發展。這項運動拿到一個原本用來糾正明顯錯誤的工具之後，卻越來越常用它來裁決那些人眼無法察覺、也沒有任何觀眾會抱怨的差距。如今，進球會因為進攻球員的肩膀或鞋尖在傳球的那一刻被判定超過倒數第二名防守球員幾公分而被取消——而「傳球的那一刻」本身也必須靠估計，因為即使是轉播攝影機，每秒能捕捉的畫面格數也有限。\n\n在我看來，這誤解了越位規則的用意。這條規則的存在，是為了防止進攻球員在球門邊閒晃，坐等一記長傳；它從來就不是要把一個肩膀的寬度變成需要司法般精確裁定的事。當這條規則由人類裁判執行時，誤差範圍實際上就是規則的一部分；一個對自己球隊不利的險判，無論多麼不情願，大家都會把它當作比賽本身的一部分而接受。追求精確到公釐，並不是更忠實地執行這條規則，而是在執行一條從來沒有人刻意選擇過的、不同且更嚴格的規則。\n\n此外還有一種代價，是那些擁護精確性的人不願去計算的。運動不僅僅是判定誰贏了的機制；它是一種共享的情感經驗，而它獨特的力量在於自發性——在於成千上萬互不相識的人，能在完全相同的時刻被捲入完全相同的情緒之中。一個在事發三分鐘後、等到重播被仔細研究、螢幕上畫出一條線之後才得以慶祝的進球，是另一種性質的事件。喜悅是真的，但它是以「判決」而非「爆發」的形式到來。\n\n這一切並不是在主張完全廢除攝影機。一個合理的折衷辦法，是把影像審查限定在清楚而明顯的錯誤上——也就是一個理智的觀眾看一次重播就能發現的那種錯誤——並且接受：一場由人類高速進行的比賽，永遠會有一些兩可的邊緣判決。畢竟，完美的公正並不是一場足球比賽的目的。倘若這項運動的設計是要消除每一個可能的錯誤，那它當初就根本不會被設計成一場「比賽」了。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the comparison \"like a check that might yet bounce\" in the first paragraph suggest?", "options": ["Fans hesitate to celebrate fully because a goal may still be canceled.", "Fans are worried about how much their tickets cost.", "Referees are paid more than they deserve.", "Goals scored from long distance are often disallowed."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段：supporters have learned to hold their joy in reserve, like a check that might yet bounce, while officials... examine the replay. 支票可能跳票，比喻進球仍可能在重播審查後被取消，因此球迷不敢盡情慶祝。(B)(C) 把 check、bounce 與金錢作字面聯想，文中並未討論票價或裁判薪水；(D) 文中沒有提到遠射。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention the goals involving Maradona and Lampard?", "options": ["To argue that VAR should never have been introduced", "To acknowledge that serious refereeing mistakes give real support to the case for technology", "To show that England has been treated unfairly at every World Cup", "To prove that goal-line technology is unreliable"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段開頭 The case for the technology is not difficult to make，接著以這兩個著名誤判為例，說明 Refereeing errors have decided matches，並指出 Lampard 事件促成門線科技。作者藉此承認支持科技的一方確有道理。(A) 作者在此段是為對方論點舉證，末段也說不主張廢除攝影機；(C) 太過極端，文中只舉出兩次誤判，並未說英格蘭在每屆世界盃都受到不公平對待；(D) 作者說門線科技的價值 few now dispute。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's main objection to the way VAR is used?", "options": ["It is too expensive for smaller leagues to afford.", "It is used too rarely to correct obvious mistakes.", "It is used to judge tiny margins that the rules were never meant to decide.", "It has made decisions about whether the ball crossed the goal line less accurate."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：the game has increasingly used it to adjudicate differences that no human eye could perceive...；第四段又說越位規則 was never intended to turn the width of a shoulder into a matter of judicial precision。作者反對的是用 VAR 裁決規則原本無意判定的微小差距。(A) 文中未提成本；(B) 正好相反，作者認為它用得太細；(D) 作者明言 I have no quarrel with goal-line technology。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by saying that \"the margin of error was, in effect, part of the rule\"?", "options": ["Referees were allowed to ignore the offside rule whenever they wished.", "The offside rule was officially rewritten to include a margin of error.", "Human referees made so many mistakes that the rule became meaningless.", "Since officials could not judge tiny distances, the rule was in practice applied with a tolerance that people accepted."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：When the rule was applied by human officials, the margin of error was, in effect, part of the rule, and a tight call... was accepted... as part of the texture of the game. in effect 意為「實際上」，指人類裁判無法判斷極小的距離，因此規則在實際執行上帶有大家都能接受的容許範圍。(A) 裁判並非可以任意忽略規則；(B) in effect 表示「實質上」而非正式修改條文；(C) 作者並未說規則因此失去意義。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following does the writer propose?", "options": ["Abolishing video technology in football altogether", "Adding more cameras so that offside can be measured even more precisely", "Limiting video review to clear errors that can be seen on a single replay", "Letting fans in the stadium vote on disputed decisions"], "answer": 2, "explain": "末段：A sensible compromise would confine video review to clear and obvious errors, the kind a reasonable spectator could spot on a single replay. (A) 原文明說 None of this argues for abolishing the cameras altogether；(B) 與作者反對追求公釐精確的立場相反；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a259", "title": "No More Numbers on the Notice Board: A Principal's Letter on Class Rankings", "title_zh": "公告欄上不再有名次：校長談班級排名的一封信", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Letter", "Education"], "passage": "Dear Parents, Guardians and Students,\n\nFollowing a year of consultation with teachers, the Parents' Association and the Student Council, the school has decided that, with effect from the coming academic year, class and year-group rankings will no longer be printed on report cards or displayed on notice boards. I am writing to set out the reasons for this decision, to explain what will and will not change, and to address a number of concerns that were raised, often with considerable force, in the course of the consultation.\n\nThe case against published rankings rests on a simple observation: a rank conveys a student's position, not his or her progress. A student who improves substantially over a semester may find that, because classmates have improved as well, his or her rank has not changed at all, while a student whose work has stagnated may rise several places owing to nothing more than the misfortunes of others. A ranking is, moreover, a zero-sum measure, in that one student can move up only if another moves down. Teachers reported, and many students confirmed, that this arithmetic discourages precisely the habits we most wish to foster, such as sharing notes, explaining difficult material to a classmate or working through a problem together. There is also the matter of the students at the foot of the list. Several of them told us, in terms that were not easy to hear, that seeing their names in the same position semester after semester had persuaded them that effort was futile, a conclusion that no school should wish its students to draw.\n\nWe are conscious that many families regard rankings as a clear and objective benchmark, particularly with the entrance examinations in view, and that for some students the prospect of climbing a list is a genuine source of motivation. These views were put to us repeatedly, and we do not dismiss them. Nor is it our contention that competition is in itself harmful; it is, after all, an inescapable feature of the examinations for which we are preparing our students. Our concern is a narrower one: that a single number, publicly displayed, has come to overshadow information of far greater use.\n\nThat information will remain fully available. Report cards will continue to show each student's mark in every subject, together with the class average and the distribution of marks, so that students can judge where they stand without being assigned a place in a queue. Each report will also include written comments from subject teachers identifying particular strengths and areas for improvement, and the results of mock examinations will likewise be reported in score bands rather than as positions. Where a ranking is required by an external application procedure, the school will continue to calculate it and will provide it confidentially to the student concerned on request.\n\nThe new arrangement will be reviewed at the end of its second year in the light of the views of students, parents and teachers, as well as of students' examination results, by a committee that will include representatives of both the Parents' Association and the Student Council. Should the evidence suggest that the change has done more harm than good, we will not hesitate to reconsider it. A meeting at which families may raise questions in person will be held in the school auditorium early next month; details will follow in a separate notice.\n\nFinally, I would like to thank all those who took part in the consultation, including those who argued most forcefully against the proposal. Their objections have made the policy considerably better than it would otherwise have been, and we hope to keep hearing from them.\n\nYours sincerely,\nThe Principal", "passage_zh": "各位家長、監護人及同學：\n\n經過一年來與教師、家長會及學生會的意見徵詢，本校決定自下一學年起，成績單上將不再列印班級排名與全年級排名，公告欄上也不再張貼。謹以此信說明作出此一決定的理由，解釋哪些事項將會改變、哪些不會改變，並回應在徵詢過程中所提出、而且往往措辭相當強烈的若干疑慮。\n\n反對公布排名的理由，建立在一項簡單的觀察上：名次所傳達的是學生的位置，而不是他或她的進步。一位在一學期中大幅進步的學生，可能會發現由於同學也都進步了，自己的名次竟然毫無變動；而一位學業停滯不前的學生，卻可能僅僅因為別人表現失常而前進好幾名。此外，排名是一種零和的衡量方式，因為一位學生要往上爬，就必然有另一位學生往下掉。教師們反映，許多學生也證實，這樣的算術恰恰抑制了我們最希望培養的習慣，例如分享筆記、向同學講解困難的教材，或一起解決一道難題。此外，還有名單最後面那些學生的問題。其中有幾位用讓人聽了很不好受的話告訴我們，學期復一學期看著自己的名字停在同樣的位置，已經讓他們相信努力是徒勞的——這是任何學校都不應希望學生得出的結論。\n\n我們明白，許多家庭把排名視為清楚而客觀的基準，尤其是考量到升學考試的時候；而對某些學生來說，在名單上往上爬的願景，確實是一股真正的動力。這些看法曾一再向我們提出，我們並未對其置之不理。我們也並不主張競爭本身是有害的；畢竟，競爭是我們正在幫助學生準備的那些考試中無可避免的一部分。我們所關切的範圍較為有限：一個公開展示的單一數字，已經掩蓋了遠比它更有用的資訊。\n\n而那些資訊仍將完整提供。成績單將繼續列出每位學生各科的分數，以及班級平均和分數分布情形，讓學生不必被排進一個隊伍裡的某個位置，也能判斷自己的程度如何。每份成績單也將附上各科教師的書面評語，指出學生具體的優點與需要加強之處；模擬考的成績也同樣將以分數級距呈現，而不再列出名次。若有校外的申請程序需要提供排名，本校仍會計算，並在當事學生提出要求時，以保密方式提供給該生。\n\n這項新安排將在實施第二年結束時，由一個包含家長會與學生會代表的委員會，參酌學生、家長與教師的意見以及學生的考試成績進行檢討。倘若證據顯示這項改變弊大於利，我們將毫不猶豫地重新考慮。下個月初將在學校禮堂舉行說明會，讓家長與同學可以當面提問；詳細資訊將另行公告。\n\n最後，我要感謝所有參與意見徵詢的人，包括那些最強烈反對此一提案的人。他們的異議讓這項政策比原本會有的樣子好上許多，我們也希望能繼續聽到他們的聲音。\n\n敬祝 安好\n校長 敬上", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the letter?", "options": ["To ask families to vote on whether class rankings should be abolished", "To announce that report cards will no longer include students' marks", "To announce and justify a decision to stop publishing rankings, and to explain what information will still be provided", "To apologize for mistakes in the previous semester's rankings"], "answer": 2, "explain": "正文第一段：the school has decided that... rankings will no longer be printed on report cards or displayed on notice boards. I am writing to set out the reasons for this decision, to explain what will and will not change... 信的目的在宣布並說明停止公布排名的決定，以及仍會提供哪些資訊。(A) 決定已經作出（has decided），並非請家長投票；(B) 與正文第四段 Report cards will continue to show each student's mark 矛盾；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the letter, which of the following will students still receive on their report cards?", "options": ["Their marks in each subject, together with the class average and the distribution of marks", "Their position in the class, printed beside each subject mark", "A list of the classmates who scored higher than they did", "A prediction of their entrance examination results"], "answer": 0, "explain": "正文第四段：Report cards will continue to show each student's mark in every subject, together with the class average and the distribution of marks. (B) 排名正是要取消的項目；(C) 文中未提及，且與取消公開比較的用意相反；(D) 文中沒有預測升學考試成績的說法。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In the letter, a ranking is described as \"a zero-sum measure.\" This means that ______.", "options": ["a student who scores zero on a test cannot be given a rank", "one student's rise in rank must be matched by another student's fall", "the total of all the students' marks always stays the same", "rankings are of no value to students preparing for examinations"], "answer": 1, "explain": "正文第二段：A ranking is, moreover, a zero-sum measure, in that one student can move up only if another moves down. 「零和」指一方之得必為另一方之失。(A) 把 zero 作字面理解；(C) 名次的增減相抵，並非指分數總和不變，分數可以全體一起提高；(D) 作者承認排名對部分學生有激勵作用，並未說毫無價值。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the main purpose of the paragraph beginning \"We are conscious that many families...\"?", "options": ["To announce that the school may reverse its decision next month", "To argue that competition is always harmful to students", "To criticize the parents who complained during the consultation", "To recognize the arguments in favor of rankings while making clear how limited the school's objection is"], "answer": 3, "explain": "正文第三段承認許多家庭視排名為 a clear and objective benchmark，對某些學生也是 a genuine source of motivation，並說 we do not dismiss them；接著以 Our concern is a narrower one 說明學校的反對只針對公開展示的單一數字。(A) 重新考慮是正文第五段提到的檢討機制，且要在第二年結束時才檢討；(B) 與 Nor is it our contention that competition is in itself harmful 矛盾；(C) 校長並未批評家長，末段甚至感謝反對者。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "A student applying to a program that requires his or her class ranking would most likely ______.", "options": ["be unable to apply, since the school will no longer calculate rankings", "ask the school, which will provide the ranking privately", "find the ranking posted on the class notice board", "have to work out the ranking from the class average alone"], "answer": 1, "explain": "正文第四段：Where a ranking is required by an external application procedure, the school will continue to calculate it and will provide it confidentially to the student concerned on request. 學校仍會計算排名，並在學生提出要求時保密提供。(A) 與 will continue to calculate it 矛盾；(C) 排名將不再張貼於公告欄；(D) 學校會直接提供，不必自行推算，且僅憑平均分數也無法得知名次。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a260", "title": "The Flat Earth That Never Was: How a Myth About the Middle Ages Was Made", "title_zh": "從未存在的平坦大地：一則關於中世紀的迷思如何形成", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["History", "Culture"], "passage": "Ask a random selection of people what Christopher Columbus proved in 1492, and a good many will tell you that he showed the Earth to be round, in defiance of a medieval Church that insisted it was flat. It is a satisfying story, pitting a bold individual against hidebound authority and casting the Middle Ages as a long intermission of ignorance between the achievements of the ancient world and the enlightenment of the modern one. It has only one drawback, which is that almost none of it is true.\n\nThat the Earth is a sphere had been accepted among educated Greeks well over a thousand years before Columbus set sail. Aristotle, in the fourth century BCE, pointed out that the shadow the Earth casts on the Moon during an eclipse is always curved, and that travelers moving north or south see different stars overhead. In the following century Eratosthenes, by comparing the angle of the sun's rays at two cities in Egypt, estimated the planet's circumference with a precision that remains impressive. Nor was this knowledge lost when the classical world declined. The English monk Bede, writing in the eighth century, described the Earth as a globe, and the most widely used astronomy textbook in medieval universities, written by Johannes de Sacrobosco in the thirteenth century, bore the title On the Sphere of the World and set out the reasons for regarding the Earth as round.\n\nThere were dissenters, to be sure. A handful of early Christian writers, most notably Lactantius in the fourth century, ridiculed the idea of people living upside down on the far side of the globe. But such voices were marginal, and to treat them as representative of medieval thought is rather like treating a present-day conspiracy theorist as representative of modern science. Columbus's real dispute with the scholars who doubted his plan concerned not the shape of the Earth but its size. He had persuaded himself, by a selective reading of his sources, that Asia lay only a few thousand miles west of Spain. His critics, whose estimates were considerably closer to the truth, judged that no ship of the day could carry enough provisions for such a voyage. On this point they were right; Columbus survived only because a continent unknown to Europeans happened to lie in the way.\n\nHow, then, did the myth arise? Historians who have traced it point largely to the nineteenth century. In 1828 the American author Washington Irving published a popular biography of Columbus that included a dramatic, and largely invented, scene in which the explorer confronts a council of churchmen who cite scripture against the roundness of the Earth. Later in the century the story was enlisted in a broader polemic. Writers such as John William Draper and Andrew Dickson White, eager to portray the history of science as a long war against religious obstruction, found the flat-earth Middle Ages too useful to examine closely, and the claim passed from their books into school textbooks, where it settled comfortably for generations.\n\nThat the myth has proved so durable says less about the Middle Ages than about ourselves. It flatters the present to imagine that our ancestors were not merely mistaken in ways we can forgive, but foolish in ways we could never be. A people convinced that the Earth was flat would be comically, reassuringly distant from us; a people who knew it was round and nonetheless misjudged its size are uncomfortably like us, capable of sophisticated knowledge and stubborn error at one and the same time.\n\nThere is a certain irony, then, in the fact that a story told to celebrate the triumph of evidence over prejudice should itself have spread on the strength of prejudice rather than evidence. Myths about the past are rarely dislodged merely by being disproved; they persist because they serve a purpose. The flat-earth story served the purpose of making modernity look like a clean break with darkness. Abandoning it means accepting something less tidy: that knowledge does not advance in a straight line from ignorance to light, and that every age, our own included, holds some of its beliefs with a confidence it has not entirely earned.", "passage_zh": "隨便找一群人問問，哥倫布在 1492 年證明了什麼，相當多人會告訴你，他違抗了堅稱大地是平的中世紀教會，證明了地球是圓的。這是個令人滿意的故事：它讓一位勇敢的個人對抗守舊的權威，又把中世紀描繪成夾在古代世界的成就與現代世界的啟蒙之間、一段漫長的蒙昧空窗期。它只有一個缺點：其中幾乎沒有一件事是真的。\n\n早在哥倫布揚帆出航的一千多年前，受過教育的希臘人就已經接受地球是球體。西元前四世紀的亞里斯多德指出，月食時地球投射在月亮上的影子總是彎曲的，而且往北或往南旅行的人，看到頭頂上的星星並不相同。到了下一個世紀，埃拉托斯特尼比較埃及兩座城市的太陽光線角度，估算出地球的周長，其精確程度至今仍令人讚嘆。古典世界衰落之後，這項知識也沒有失傳。八世紀的英格蘭修士比德在著作中把地球描述為一個球體；而中世紀大學裡使用最廣的天文學教科書，是十三世紀的薩克羅博斯科所寫，書名就叫《論世界之球體》，書中闡述了認定地球是圓的種種理由。\n\n當然，也有持不同意見的人。少數早期的基督教作家，其中最著名的是四世紀的拉克坦提烏斯，曾嘲笑「地球另一側住著頭下腳上的人」這種想法。但這類聲音處於邊緣，把它們當成中世紀思想的代表，就好比把當今的陰謀論者當成現代科學的代表。哥倫布與那些懷疑他計畫的學者真正的爭議，不在於地球的形狀，而在於地球的大小。他根據對資料來源的選擇性解讀，說服自己相信亞洲就位在西班牙以西僅僅幾千哩處。他的批評者所估算的數字遠比他的更接近事實，他們判斷當時沒有任何船隻能裝載足夠的補給完成這樣的航程。在這一點上他們是對的；哥倫布之所以能生還，只是因為一塊歐洲人所不知道的大陸碰巧擋在中途。\n\n那麼，這個迷思是怎麼產生的？追溯過其源頭的歷史學家大多指向十九世紀。1828 年，美國作家華盛頓‧歐文出版了一部廣受歡迎的哥倫布傳記，書中有一幕戲劇性、而且大體上是虛構的場景：這位探險家面對一群神職人員組成的會議，他們引用經文反對地球是圓的。到了該世紀後期，這個故事被拉進一場範圍更廣的論戰。像約翰‧威廉‧德雷柏和安德魯‧迪克森‧懷特這樣的作家，急於把科學史描繪成一場對抗宗教阻撓的長期戰爭，他們發現「相信大地是平的中世紀」實在太好用了，好用到他們根本不願仔細查證；於是這個說法從他們的書中流入學校教科書，並在那裡安穩地待了好幾個世代。\n\n這個迷思之所以如此經久不衰，所反映的與其說是中世紀，不如說是我們自己。想像我們的祖先不只是犯了我們可以原諒的錯，而且愚蠢到我們絕不可能那樣愚蠢，這種想像讓現代人感到受用。一群深信大地是平的人，與我們的距離會遠得可笑、遠得令人安心；而一群知道地球是圓的、卻仍然錯估了它大小的人，則與我們像得令人不安——既擁有精深的知識，又會固執地犯錯。\n\n因此，這件事有一種諷刺：一個被用來頌揚證據戰勝偏見的故事，本身竟是憑著偏見、而非證據流傳開來的。關於過去的迷思，很少僅僅因為被證明是錯的就被推翻；它們之所以延續，是因為它們有某種用處。平坦大地的故事所發揮的用處，是讓現代看起來像是與黑暗徹底決裂。拋棄這個故事，意味著接受一個不那麼整齊的事實：知識並不是從無知到光明沿著一條直線前進，而每個時代——包括我們自己的時代——對自己的某些信念，都抱持著一種並非全然有憑有據的自信。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Columbus was the first European to prove that the Earth is round.", "The medieval Church tried to prevent scholars from studying astronomy.", "Ancient Greek scientists were far more intelligent than medieval scholars.", "The belief that medieval people thought the Earth was flat is itself a later myth that has served particular purposes."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段指出哥倫布證明地球是圓的這個故事 almost none of it is true，第二、三段說明中世紀學者早知地球是球體，第四至六段則分析這個迷思如何在十九世紀形成、又為何延續（they persist because they serve a purpose）。(A)(B) 正是文章要推翻的迷思；(C) 第二段強調古希臘的知識在中世紀並未失傳，作者沒有比較兩者的聰明程度。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what was the real disagreement between Columbus and the scholars who doubted his plan?", "options": ["How far a ship would have to sail westward from Spain to reach Asia", "Whether the Earth was round or flat", "Whether people could live on the far side of the globe", "Whether the Spanish crown should pay for the voyage"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Columbus's real dispute with the scholars... concerned not the shape of the Earth but its size. He had persuaded himself... that Asia lay only a few thousand miles west of Spain. 爭議在於地球的大小，也就是從西班牙往西航行到亞洲的距離。(B) 原文明說 not the shape of the Earth；(C) 是拉克坦提烏斯嘲笑的觀念，與哥倫布的爭議無關；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer compare Lactantius to \"a present-day conspiracy theorist\"?", "options": ["To suggest that Lactantius invented the flat-earth myth in the nineteenth century", "To show that conspiracy theories were widespread in the Middle Ages", "To argue that a few marginal views should not be taken to represent the thinking of a whole era", "To prove that Lactantius was the most influential scholar of the Middle Ages"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：such voices were marginal, and to treat them as representative of medieval thought is rather like treating a present-day conspiracy theorist as representative of modern science. 作者以此類比說明，少數邊緣的看法不能代表整個時代。(A) 拉克坦提烏斯是四世紀的人，迷思形成於十九世紀；(B) 文中沒有說陰謀論在中世紀很普遍；(D) 與 marginal 的描述相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer imply about John William Draper and Andrew Dickson White?", "options": ["They studied medieval sources carefully and uncovered the truth about the flat Earth.", "They accepted the flat-earth story without checking it because it suited the argument they wanted to make.", "They were medieval churchmen who opposed the study of science.", "They wrote the first popular biography of Columbus."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：eager to portray the history of science as a long war against religious obstruction, found the flat-earth Middle Ages too useful to examine closely. 暗示他們因為這個故事有利於自己的論點，便不加查證地採用。(A) 與 too useful to examine closely 相反；(C) 他們是十九世紀的作家，不是中世紀的神職人員；(D) 寫哥倫布傳記的是華盛頓‧歐文。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the \"certain irony\" that the writer points out in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Columbus never reached Asia, even though that had been his goal.", "Modern textbooks turn out to be more accurate than medieval ones.", "Medieval scholars knew more about the Earth than most people do today.", "A story meant to celebrate evidence over prejudice spread because of prejudice rather than evidence."], "answer": 3, "explain": "末段：There is a certain irony... in the fact that a story told to celebrate the triumph of evidence over prejudice should itself have spread on the strength of prejudice rather than evidence. (A) 雖然符合史實，但並非作者在末段所指的諷刺；(B) 第四段說錯誤說法正是透過教科書流傳，與此相反；(C) 文中沒有比較中世紀學者與現代人的知識。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a261", "title": "From a Backyard Shed to the World: The Story of Instant Noodles", "title_zh": "從後院小屋走向世界：泡麵誕生的故事", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Food"], "passage": "Every day, people around the world eat hundreds of millions of bowls of instant noodles. They are cheap and easy: just add hot water and wait a few minutes. But few people know that this simple food was first created by one man working alone in a small shed behind his house. His name was Momofuku Ando.\n\nAndo was born in 1910 in Taiwan, then under Japanese rule, and later moved to Japan to do business. After World War II, food was hard to find in Japan. In Osaka, he once saw a long line of hungry people standing in the cold outside a ramen stall. He never forgot that sight. He felt that people needed food before they could have peace, and he wondered if he could make noodles that anyone could cook at home in minutes.\n\nIn 1957, a financial company that Ando helped to run failed, and he lost almost everything he owned. In his late forties, he could have given up. Instead, he built a simple shed behind his home in Ikeda, Osaka, and went to work. He had no team and very little equipment. For about a year, he worked from morning until late at night, and one test after another ended in failure.\n\nThe answer finally came from the kitchen. One day, Ando watched his wife frying tempura and noticed how the hot oil pushed water out of the flour coating. So, after seasoning noodles with chicken soup, he fried them quickly in hot oil. This flash-frying dried the noodles and left tiny holes in them. Later, hot water could fill these holes and make the noodles soft in minutes. In 1958, Ando began selling his invention, Chicken Ramen.\n\nAndo did not stop there. On a trip to the United States, he saw a supermarket manager break Chicken Ramen into a paper cup, pour in hot water and eat it with a fork. This gave him the idea for Cup Noodles, which went on sale in 1971. Today, Ando is remembered not only for his noodles but also for his spirit. He lost nearly everything, failed again and again, and still changed the way the world eats.", "passage_zh": "每天，世界各地的人會吃掉數億碗泡麵。泡麵便宜又方便：只要加入熱水，等幾分鐘就好。但很少人知道，這種簡單的食物最初是由一個人獨自在自家後面的小屋裡發明出來的。他的名字是安藤百福。\n\n安藤百福於 1910 年出生在當時受日本統治的台灣，後來到日本經商。第二次世界大戰後，日本的糧食十分短缺。有一次，他在大阪看到一群飢餓的人站在寒風中，在一家拉麵攤外排著長長的隊伍。他始終忘不了那一幕。他認為人們要先有東西吃，才能擁有和平；於是他開始思考，能不能做出一種任何人在家裡幾分鐘就能煮好的麵。\n\n1957 年，安藤參與經營的一家金融機構倒閉，他幾乎失去了所有財產。當時他已年近五十，大可就此放棄。但他沒有，而是在大阪池田市的自家後面蓋了一間簡陋的小屋，開始埋頭研究。他沒有團隊，手邊的設備也很少。大約一年的時間裡，他從早忙到深夜，一次又一次的試驗都以失敗收場。\n\n答案最後來自廚房。有一天，安藤看著妻子炸天婦羅，注意到熱油會把麵衣裡的水分逼出來。於是，他先用雞湯替麵條調味，再把麵條放進熱油裡快速油炸。這種瞬間油炸的方法讓麵條變乾，並在麵條上留下許多細小的孔洞。之後，熱水就能填進這些小孔，讓麵條在幾分鐘內變軟。1958 年，安藤開始販售他的發明——「雞汁拉麵」（Chicken Ramen）。\n\n安藤並沒有就此停下腳步。在一次美國之行中，他看到一位超市經理把雞汁拉麵掰碎放進紙杯，倒入熱水，再用叉子來吃。這讓他想出了杯麵（Cup Noodles）的點子，杯麵於 1971 年上市。如今，人們記得安藤，不只是因為他的麵，也是因為他的精神。他幾乎失去一切，一次又一次地失敗，卻仍然改變了全世界的飲食方式。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How Momofuku Ando created instant noodles and later Cup Noodles", "Why ramen became the favorite food in Japan after the war", "How instant noodles are made in large factories today", "Why Ando decided to leave Taiwan and move to Japan"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹安藤百福的故事：第二段說明他想發明泡麵的動機，第三、四段描述他在後院小屋中歷經失敗、最後發明「雞汁拉麵」，第五段再提到他想出杯麵（Cup Noodles）。(B) 第二段只提到戰後有人排隊吃拉麵，並未說拉麵成為日本人最愛的食物；(C) 文中沒有介紹現代工廠的生產方式，反而強調他一個人在小屋裡研究；(D) 第二段只用一句話帶過他後來到日本經商，並非全文重點。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what first made Ando think about creating a new kind of noodles?", "options": ["A visit to a supermarket in the United States", "A long line of hungry people waiting for ramen", "A request from the Japanese government after the war", "His wife's complaint that cooking took too much time"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：In Osaka, he once saw a long line of hungry people standing in the cold outside a ramen stall. He never forgot that sight. 接著又說 he wondered if he could make noodles that anyone could cook at home in minutes。看到飢餓的人在寒風中排隊等拉麵，讓他開始思考如何做出在家幾分鐘就能煮好的麵。(A) 美國超市的經驗出現在第五段，是發明泡麵之後想出杯麵的靈感；(C) 文中沒有提到日本政府的要求；(D) 第四段只說他看妻子炸天婦羅而得到油炸的靈感，並沒有提到妻子抱怨做飯太花時間。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, how did flash-frying help Ando's noodles?", "options": ["It gave the noodles the taste of tempura.", "It made the noodles cheaper than restaurant ramen.", "It dried the noodles so hot water could soften them later.", "It made the noodles soft enough to eat right away."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：This flash-frying dried the noodles and left tiny holes in them. Later, hot water could fill these holes and make the noodles soft in minutes. 瞬間油炸讓麵條變乾並留下細小的孔洞，之後熱水能填進小孔，讓麵條很快變軟。(A) 天婦羅只是給了他靈感，麵條的味道來自雞湯調味，文中並未說麵條吃起來像天婦羅；(B) 文中沒有比較價格；(D) 油炸後的麵條是乾的，要加熱水才會變軟，並非立刻就軟到可以吃。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"seasoning\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["washing", "cutting", "cooling", "flavoring"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：after seasoning noodles with chicken soup, he fried them quickly in hot oil. 用雞湯替麵條「調味」，season 在此當動詞，意為「給（食物）調味」，與 flavoring 意思最接近，而不是名詞「季節」的意思。(A) washing「清洗」、(B) cutting「切」、(C) cooling「冷卻」都無法說明雞湯對麵條的作用，不符合文意。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What lesson does the writer most likely want readers to learn from Ando's story?", "options": ["Most great inventions come from large companies with many workers.", "Failure does not have to stop a person from achieving something great.", "People should not try to start something new after the age of forty.", "Good ideas usually come quickly and easily to people who are smart."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段結尾：Today, Ando is remembered not only for his noodles but also for his spirit. He lost nearly everything, failed again and again, and still changed the way the world eats. 作者強調安藤雖然幾乎失去一切、屢次失敗，仍然改變了世界的飲食方式，可見作者想傳達「失敗不一定會阻止一個人成就大事」。(A) 第三段說 He had no team and very little equipment，他是獨自一人、靠很少的設備完成發明的；(C) 安藤年近五十才重新出發，與此說法相反；(D) 第三段說他花了大約一年、經歷一次又一次的失敗，好點子並非輕易得來。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a262", "title": "Hidden in the Green: Why Leaves Change Color in Autumn", "title_zh": "藏在綠色底下：秋天的葉子為什麼會變色", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Nature", "Science"], "passage": "Every autumn, in places like Japan, Korea and Canada, hills that were green all summer turn red, orange and gold. Many people travel a long way just to see them. But where do these colors come from? The answer is surprising: some of them have been inside the leaves all year long.\n\nIn spring and summer, leaves are green because they are full of chlorophyll. This green substance helps a tree use sunlight to turn water and air into sugar. Chlorophyll wears out quickly, so the tree keeps making more of it all summer. But when days grow shorter and nights become cooler in autumn, the tree slowly stops making new chlorophyll. The green that is left begins to break down and fade.\n\nAs the green disappears, yellow and orange colors appear. They come from carotenoids, the same kind of natural coloring found in carrots. These colors were in the leaves the whole time, but in summer there was so much green that it covered them up. Red is different. Some trees, including many kinds of maples, make a new red coloring called anthocyanin in autumn. Scientists think it may protect the leaves from strong sunlight while the tree moves useful materials out of them before they fall.\n\nWeather plays a big part in how bright the colors are each year. The best reds usually come after a period of sunny days and cool nights. Sunny days help leaves make plenty of sugar, and cool nights keep that sugar inside the leaves, where it is used to make red coloring. If autumn stays too warm, or if an early, hard frost arrives, the reds are often weaker.\n\nIn Taiwan's lowlands, the weather stays fairly warm, and many trees keep their green leaves all year. To see red leaves, people usually go up into the mountains, where nights are much colder. One well-known place is Aowanda in Nantou. When the weather turns cold, its hills fill with red, orange and green leaves. Next time you see a red tree, remember: it shows a mix of old colors that were hidden and new ones that were just made.", "passage_zh": "每年秋天，在日本、韓國和加拿大等地，整個夏天都綠油油的山丘會變成紅色、橙色和金黃色。許多人不遠千里，只為了一睹這片景色。但這些顏色是從哪裡來的呢？答案令人驚訝：其中有些顏色，一整年都藏在葉子裡。\n\n春天和夏天時，葉子是綠色的，因為裡面充滿了葉綠素。這種綠色物質能幫助樹木利用陽光，把水和空氣轉變成糖分。葉綠素耗損得很快，所以樹木整個夏天都不斷製造新的葉綠素。但到了秋天，白天變短、夜晚轉涼，樹木就會漸漸停止製造新的葉綠素。剩下的綠色也開始分解、褪去。\n\n綠色消失之後，黃色和橙色就顯現出來了。這些顏色來自類胡蘿蔔素，也就是胡蘿蔔裡含有的那一類天然色素。它們其實一直都在葉子裡，只是夏天時綠色太多，把它們遮住了。紅色則不一樣。有些樹木，包括許多種類的楓樹，會在秋天製造一種新的紅色色素，叫做花青素。科學家認為，在葉子掉落之前，樹木會把葉子裡有用的物質移走，而花青素或許能在這段期間保護葉子不受強烈陽光的傷害。\n\n天氣對每年葉子顏色的鮮豔程度有很大的影響。最美的紅葉通常出現在一段晴朗的白天和涼爽的夜晚之後。晴天讓葉子製造大量糖分，涼爽的夜晚則把這些糖分留在葉子裡，用來製造紅色色素。如果秋天一直太溫暖，或是提早出現嚴重的霜害，紅色往往就會比較黯淡。\n\n在台灣的平地，天氣大多相當溫暖，許多樹木一年四季都保持綠葉。想看紅葉，人們通常要上山，因為山上的夜晚冷得多。南投的奧萬大就是其中一個知名的去處。天氣轉冷時，那裡的山坡會布滿紅、橙、綠交錯的葉子。下次你看到一棵紅色的樹時，別忘了：你看到的，是過去被藏起來的舊顏色，加上剛剛製造出來的新顏色。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To tell readers the best time of year to visit Aowanda", "To compare autumn weather in Taiwan with that in Canada", "To explain where the colors of autumn leaves come from", "To show how trees protect themselves during cold winters"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段提出問題 But where do these colors come from?，第二到四段接著說明葉綠素分解、類胡蘿蔔素顯現、花青素生成，以及天氣對顏色的影響，可知本文目的在說明秋葉顏色的來源。(A) 第五段只提到奧萬大是知名的賞紅葉地點，並未說明一年中哪個時候去最好；(B) 文中沒有比較台灣和加拿大的天氣；(D) 第三段只說花青素可能保護葉子不受強光傷害，並未談樹木如何度過寒冬。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why do leaves show yellow and orange in autumn?", "options": ["These colors are no longer hidden by the green coloring.", "Trees make these colors from sugar on cool nights.", "Strong autumn sunlight slowly turns green leaves yellow.", "The leaves take in these colors from the soil."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：These colors were in the leaves the whole time, but in summer there was so much green that it covered them up. 黃色和橙色一直都在葉子裡，秋天葉綠素分解後，它們不再被綠色遮住，才顯現出來。(B) 第四段提到利用糖分製造的是紅色色素，不是黃色和橙色；(C) 文中沒有說陽光會把葉子變黃；(D) 文中沒有提到顏色來自土壤。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which kind of weather would most likely produce the brightest red leaves?", "options": ["A long autumn that stays warm day and night", "An early autumn with a sudden, hard frost", "Cloudy days with very little sunshine", "Sunny days followed by cool nights"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The best reds usually come after a period of sunny days and cool nights. 晴天讓葉子製造大量糖分，涼爽的夜晚把糖分留在葉子裡，用來製造紅色色素。(A)(B) 同段指出 If autumn stays too warm, or if an early, hard frost arrives, the reds are often weaker；(C) 缺少陽光，葉子就無法製造足夠的糖分，與文中「晴天有助於產生紅色」的說法相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"fade\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["become brighter", "slowly disappear", "spread quickly", "change shape"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：The green that is left begins to break down and fade. 樹木停止製造葉綠素後，剩下的綠色開始分解、「褪去」，第三段開頭也接著說 As the green disappears，可知 fade 意為「逐漸消失、褪色」。(A) become brighter「變得更鮮豔」意思相反；(C) spread quickly「迅速擴散」、(D) change shape「改變形狀」都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following can be inferred from the passage?", "options": ["A leaf that turns yellow in autumn already had yellow coloring in summer.", "The red color of maple leaves was hidden under the green all summer.", "Trees in Taiwan's lowlands are unable to make any chlorophyll.", "Leaves usually make more chlorophyll in autumn than in summer."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說黃色和橙色 were in the leaves the whole time，只是夏天被大量的綠色遮住，可推知秋天變黃的葉子，夏天時就已經含有黃色色素。(B) 第三段說 Red is different，紅色的花青素是秋天才新製造的，並非整個夏天都藏在葉子裡；(C) 第五段說台灣平地許多樹木全年保持綠葉，表示它們一直有葉綠素，並非無法製造；(D) 第二段說樹木到了秋天會漸漸停止製造新的葉綠素。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a263", "title": "Fighting Sound with Sound: Inside Noise-Canceling Headphones", "title_zh": "以聲制聲：抗噪耳機的祕密", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Science"], "passage": "You are on a long flight, and the engines roar without stopping. Then you put on a pair of noise-canceling headphones, and the noise seems to fade away. It feels almost like magic, but it is really clever science. These headphones do not just block sound. They fight sound with more sound.\n\nSound travels through the air as waves, a bit like waves on water. Each wave has high points and low points. Noise-canceling headphones have tiny microphones that listen to the sounds around you. A small chip inside quickly studies these sounds and creates a new sound wave that is the exact opposite of the noise. Where the noise has a high point, the new wave has a low point.\n\nThe headphones then play this opposite wave through their speakers, along with your music. When the two waves meet, the high points and low points cancel each other out, much like adding 1 and -1 to get zero. As a result, much of the noise never reaches your ears. The ear cushions also help by blocking some sound, just as covering your ears with your hands would.\n\nHowever, the technology works better on some sounds than on others. It is best at steady, low sounds, such as the hum of an airplane engine, the rumble of a train or the noise of an air conditioner. These sounds stay almost the same from one moment to the next, so the headphones can easily predict them. Sudden or high sounds, such as voices, a crying baby or a barking dog, change too quickly. That is why you may still hear people talking nearby.\n\nBecause these headphones are so good at shutting out the world, they must be used with care. On a busy street, or while riding a bicycle or scooter, you need to hear car horns, sirens and people shouting warnings. Wearing these headphones there can be dangerous, so the safest choice is to take them off. Used in the right places, however, they can make a noisy trip much more peaceful. And with less noise around you, you may not need to turn your music up so loud.", "passage_zh": "你正在搭乘長途班機，引擎不停地轟隆作響。接著你戴上一副抗噪耳機，噪音彷彿就漸漸消失了。這感覺幾乎像魔法一樣，但其實是巧妙的科學。這種耳機不只是把聲音擋住，而是用更多的聲音來對抗聲音。\n\n聲音是以波的形式在空氣中傳遞，有點像水面上的波浪。每個波都有高點和低點。抗噪耳機上裝有微小的麥克風，負責收聽你周圍的聲音。耳機裡的小晶片會迅速分析這些聲音，並製造出一個和噪音完全相反的新聲波：噪音出現高點的地方，新聲波就是低點。\n\n接著，耳機會透過喇叭，把這個相反的聲波連同你的音樂一起播放出來。兩個聲波相遇時，高點和低點會互相抵消，就像 1 加上 -1 等於零一樣。因此，大部分的噪音根本傳不到你的耳朵。耳罩上的軟墊也能擋住一部分聲音，就像用雙手摀住耳朵一樣。\n\n不過，這項技術對某些聲音的效果比較好，對另一些聲音則比較差。它最擅長處理穩定、低沉的聲音，例如飛機引擎的嗡嗡聲、火車的隆隆聲或冷氣機的運轉聲。這些聲音每一刻幾乎都一樣，所以耳機很容易預測。突然出現或高音的聲音，例如說話聲、嬰兒的哭聲或狗叫聲，則變化得太快。這就是為什麼你可能還是聽得到附近有人在說話。\n\n正因為這種耳機隔絕外界的效果這麼好，使用時必須特別小心。在車水馬龍的街道上，或是騎腳踏車、騎機車時，你需要聽到汽車喇叭聲、警笛聲，以及別人大聲發出的警告。在這些地方戴著這種耳機可能很危險，所以最安全的做法就是把它拿下來。不過，只要在適當的場合使用，它就能讓吵雜的旅程變得寧靜許多。而且周圍的噪音變少了，你可能也不需要把音樂開得那麼大聲。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following is the best title for the passage?", "options": ["The Best Headphones to Buy for Long Flights", "How Headphones Use Sound to Fight Noise", "Why Airplanes Are Louder Than Trains", "The Danger of Listening to Loud Music"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文說明抗噪耳機的原理：第一段點出 They fight sound with more sound，第二、三段解釋耳機如何製造相反的聲波來抵消噪音，第四段說明它對哪些聲音較有效，第五段提醒使用安全。(A) 文中沒有推薦購買哪一種耳機；(C) 飛機和火車只是第四段舉的例子，文中沒有比較兩者的音量；(D) 第五段只順帶提到可以不必把音樂開得那麼大聲，並非全文主旨。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what does the small chip inside the headphones do?", "options": ["It makes your music louder than the noise outside.", "It records your voice so you can make phone calls.", "It turns off the microphones when the room is quiet.", "It produces a sound wave that is the reverse of the noise."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：A small chip inside quickly studies these sounds and creates a new sound wave that is the exact opposite of the noise. 晶片會分析周圍的聲音，製造出和噪音完全相反的聲波，選項中的 the reverse of the noise 即 the exact opposite of the noise。(A) 耳機是靠相反的聲波抵消噪音，而不是把音樂開得比噪音大聲；(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, which sound would noise-canceling headphones reduce most effectively?", "options": ["The steady sound of an air conditioner in an office", "A friend calling your name from across the room", "A dog that suddenly starts barking outside", "A baby crying loudly in the next seat"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：It is best at steady, low sounds, such as ... the noise of an air conditioner. 這類聲音每一刻幾乎都一樣，耳機容易預測並加以抵消。(B)(C)(D) 朋友叫你的說話聲、突然響起的狗叫聲和嬰兒的哭聲，都屬於同段所說的 Sudden or high sounds，變化太快，抗噪效果較差。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"predict\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["forget quickly", "make louder", "know in advance", "record clearly"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：These sounds stay almost the same from one moment to the next, so the headphones can easily predict them. 穩定的聲音幾乎不會改變，所以耳機能輕易「預測」下一刻的聲音，事先製造出相反的聲波。predict 意為「預先知道、預測」。(A) forget quickly「很快忘記」、(B) make louder「使更大聲」、(D) record clearly「清楚錄下」都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward noise-canceling headphones?", "options": ["They are so dangerous that people should stop using them.", "They are useless because they cannot block people's voices.", "They are only worth buying for people who often fly.", "They are helpful, but people should use them in safe places."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段先提醒 they must be used with care，在街上或騎車時最好拿下來；接著又說 Used in the right places, however, they can make a noisy trip much more peaceful. 可見作者認為抗噪耳機很有用，但應該在安全的場合使用。(A) 作者只說在某些場合使用很危險，並未主張完全不用；(B) 第四段雖然說它較難消除說話聲，但對穩定、低沉的聲音很有效，並非沒有用；(C) 文中沒有說只有常搭飛機的人才值得買。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a264", "title": "When Beethoven Calls, Take Out the Trash", "title_zh": "聽見貝多芬，就該倒垃圾了", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Environment", "Culture"], "passage": "In many countries, people leave their trash bags on the street, and a truck picks them up later. In Taiwan, things work very differently. On most evenings, in neighborhoods across the island, a familiar piano tune fills the air. It might be Beethoven's \"Für Elise\" or \"A Maiden's Prayer,\" a piece by a Polish composer. For people in Taiwan, this music means only one thing: the garbage truck is coming.\n\nWhen they hear the music, people hurry out of their homes carrying bags of trash. They wait at set spots along the road, and when the truck stops, they throw their bags into the back themselves. This is because trash is not supposed to touch the ground. Bags left on the street can bring bad smells, insects and rats. Miss the truck, and your garbage stays at home until next time.\n\nA recycling truck often follows the garbage truck. People sort their waste before they go out. Paper, plastic bottles, cans and glass go to the recycling truck, while food scraps go into separate buckets. In Taipei, for example, ordinary trash must be put into special bags that people buy, so throwing away more trash costs more money. Rules like these encourage people to recycle more and waste less.\n\nFor visitors, all of this can be surprising. Some at first think an ice cream truck is coming, since back home, music on the street often means ice cream. Others are surprised to see neighbors chatting while they wait, or someone running down the street in slippers to catch the truck. Visitors who stay for a long time sometimes joke that whenever they hear \"Für Elise,\" they want to grab a bag of trash.\n\nThe system is not always convenient: people must be home at the right time and follow the rules carefully. But it also has clear benefits. The streets stay cleaner, and far more waste is recycled than in the past. In a small way, the trucks also bring neighbors together for a few minutes each day. So if you visit Taiwan and hear Beethoven on the street, don't look for a concert. Look for people with trash bags.", "passage_zh": "在許多國家，人們會把垃圾袋放在路邊，之後再由卡車收走。在台灣，情況卻大不相同。大多數的晚上，全台各地的社區都會響起一段熟悉的鋼琴旋律。可能是貝多芬的〈給愛麗絲〉，也可能是波蘭作曲家的作品〈少女的祈禱〉。對台灣人來說，這段音樂只代表一件事：垃圾車來了。\n\n一聽到音樂，人們就提著一袋袋垃圾匆匆走出家門。他們在路邊固定的地點等候，等垃圾車一停下來，就親手把垃圾袋丟進車子後方。這是因為垃圾不應該落地。放在路邊的垃圾袋會散發臭味，還會引來蚊蟲和老鼠。要是錯過了垃圾車，垃圾就只能留在家裡，等下一次再倒。\n\n垃圾車後面常常會跟著一輛資源回收車。人們出門前會先把垃圾分類：紙類、寶特瓶、鐵鋁罐和玻璃交給資源回收車，廚餘則倒進另外的桶子裡。以台北為例，一般垃圾必須裝進需要付費購買的專用垃圾袋，所以丟的垃圾越多，花的錢就越多。這類規定鼓勵人們多回收、少浪費。\n\n對外國遊客來說，這一切可能相當令人意外。有些人一開始以為是冰淇淋車來了，因為在他們的家鄉，街上傳來音樂通常代表冰淇淋車來了。也有人驚訝地看到鄰居們一邊等車一邊聊天，或是有人穿著拖鞋沿街追垃圾車。在台灣待得比較久的外國人有時會開玩笑說，現在他們只要一聽到〈給愛麗絲〉，就想抓起一袋垃圾。\n\n這套制度並不總是那麼方便：人們必須在對的時間待在家，還要仔細遵守規定。但它也有明顯的好處。街道保持得更乾淨，被回收的垃圾也比過去多得多。垃圾車也以一種小小的方式，讓鄰居們每天能聚在一起幾分鐘。所以，如果你來台灣旅遊，在街上聽到貝多芬的音樂，別急著找音樂會在哪裡，去找找提著垃圾袋的人吧。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The life of Beethoven and his most famous piano music", "Why many visitors to Taiwan dislike the garbage trucks", "How Taiwan's cities have lowered the cost of collecting trash", "How Taiwan's musical garbage trucks work and what they mean to people"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文介紹台灣會播放音樂的垃圾車：第一段說明音樂代表垃圾車來了，第二、三段介紹倒垃圾與資源回收的方式，第四段描述外國遊客的驚訝，第五段說明這套制度的好處與意義。(A) 貝多芬只是第一段提到的樂曲作者，文中並未介紹他的生平；(B) 第四段說遊客感到驚訝，並沒有說他們討厭垃圾車；(C) 文中沒有提到降低收垃圾的成本。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why do people wait for the truck instead of leaving their bags outside?", "options": ["The trucks will not stop unless people are waiting.", "Neighbors enjoy listening to the music together.", "Trash on the ground can smell bad and attract insects and rats.", "The drivers must check every bag before taking it."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：This is because trash is not supposed to touch the ground. Bags left on the street can bring bad smells, insects and rats. 垃圾不應該落地，因為放在路邊的垃圾袋會散發臭味，並引來蚊蟲和老鼠。(A) 文中沒有說垃圾車只為等候的人停車；(B) 音樂只是用來通知垃圾車來了，並不是人們在路邊等候的原因；(D) 文中沒有提到司機要檢查垃圾袋。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, which of the following is true?", "options": ["Food scraps are thrown away together with ordinary trash.", "In Taipei, people who throw away more ordinary trash pay more.", "Recycling must be taken to a center far away from home.", "Each family has to pay for its own recycling truck."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：In Taipei, for example, ordinary trash must be put into special bags that people buy, so throwing away more trash costs more money. 台北的一般垃圾要裝進付費購買的專用垃圾袋，所以丟得越多，花的錢就越多。(A) 同段說廚餘倒進 separate buckets，與一般垃圾分開；(C) 回收物是交給跟在垃圾車後面的資源回收車，不必拿到遠處；(D) 文中沒有提到每戶人家要支付回收車的費用。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"sort\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["separate into groups", "carry outside", "wash clean", "throw away"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：People sort their waste before they go out. 下一句接著說紙類、寶特瓶、鐵鋁罐和玻璃交給資源回收車，廚餘則倒進另外的桶子，可知 sort 是把垃圾「分類」，即分成不同的類別。(B) carry outside「拿到外面」、(C) wash clean「洗乾淨」、(D) throw away「丟掉」都無法說明下一句把垃圾分門別類的意思。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the writer's view of Taiwan's garbage truck system?", "options": ["It is too difficult for most families to follow.", "It should be replaced by trash cans on every street.", "It takes some effort, but it brings real benefits.", "It is mainly a way to teach people classical music."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：The system is not always convenient... But it also has clear benefits. The streets stay cleaner, and far more waste is recycled than in the past. 作者承認這套制度不一定方便，但也帶來街道更乾淨、回收更多、鄰居每天能聚在一起等好處。(A) 作者只說它不總是方便，並未說大多數家庭難以遵守；(B) 文中沒有主張改設路邊垃圾桶，反而說明垃圾不落地的好處；(D) 音樂只是用來通知垃圾車來了，並非用來教人古典音樂。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a265", "title": "Five Days Behind the Front Desk", "title_zh": "櫃檯後的五天", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Career", "Travel"], "passage": "Last summer, I spent six weeks as an intern at a busy hotel near Taichung Station. I study hospitality at a vocational high school, and I had practiced checking guests in at school. However, the \"guests\" there were always my classmates, and nobody was ever in a hurry. On my first morning, a Monday, my supervisor, Ms. Kuo, handed me a name tag that said \"Lin Hsin-yi, Intern\" and told me, \"Today you watch. Tomorrow you talk.\"\n\nAll that first day, I stood next to her and took notes. Checking in looked simple: check the guest's ID or passport, find the booking, make key cards, and explain breakfast hours and the Wi-Fi password. But Ms. Kuo did all this while answering the phone and greeting everyone who passed. She also made me practice smiling in the staff room mirror. \"Your face is the first thing a guest sees,\" she said. By evening, my cheeks hurt.\n\nThe next day, I checked in my first guests, a young couple from Japan, all by myself. My English was slow, but they were patient, and I felt proud. Then, on Wednesday at noon, a businessman rushed to the desk. He had just come off a long flight and wanted his room immediately. Check-in time was three o'clock, and his room was not ready. When I told him this, he raised his voice, and my mind went completely blank.\n\nMs. Kuo stepped in calmly. She let the man finish speaking. Then she apologized, offered to look after his suitcase, and invited him to enjoy a free coffee in the lobby. Meanwhile, she asked the cleaners to prepare his room first. Forty minutes later, he came back for his key card and thanked us both. Afterward, Ms. Kuo told me, \"He wasn't angry at you. He was tired. Listen first, then solve the problem.\"\n\nBy Friday, I could check guests in without notes, and my smile no longer felt forced. I also learned that service work is not about always saying yes. Sometimes you cannot give guests what they want, but you can still show them that you care. I used to think the front desk was about computers and key cards. Now I know it is really about people.", "passage_zh": "去年夏天，我在台中車站附近一家忙碌的飯店實習了六個星期。我在一所高職讀餐旅科，也在學校練習過幫客人辦理入住手續。不過，在學校裡的「客人」都是我的同學，而且從來沒有人趕時間。上班第一天是星期一，那天早上，我的主管郭小姐遞給我一張寫著「實習生 林欣怡」的名牌，並對我說：「今天你先看，明天開始開口。」\n\n第一天一整天，我都站在她旁邊做筆記。幫客人辦入住看起來很簡單：查看客人的身分證件或護照、找出訂房資料、製作房卡，再說明早餐時間和 Wi-Fi 密碼。但郭小姐是一邊接電話、一邊和每位經過的人打招呼，一邊完成這些事。她還要我在員工休息室的鏡子前練習微笑。「你的臉是客人第一眼看到的東西。」她說。到了傍晚，我的臉頰都痠了。\n\n第二天，我第一次獨自幫客人辦理入住，是一對來自日本的年輕夫妻。我的英文說得很慢，但他們很有耐心，我覺得很自豪。接著，星期三中午，一位商務人士匆匆走到櫃檯。他剛下長途班機，想要馬上進房間。但入住時間是下午三點，他的房間還沒準備好。我告訴他這件事時，他提高了音量，我的腦袋頓時一片空白。\n\n郭小姐冷靜地上前處理。她先讓那位先生把話說完。接著她向他道歉，提議幫他保管行李箱，並請他到大廳享用一杯免費咖啡。同時，她請清潔人員優先整理他的房間。四十分鐘後，他回來拿房卡，還向我們兩人道謝。事後郭小姐對我說：「他不是在生你的氣，他只是累了。先傾聽，再解決問題。」\n\n到了星期五，我已經不用看筆記就能幫客人辦理入住，笑容也不再顯得勉強。我還學到，服務業並不是永遠都要說「好」。有時候你無法給客人他們想要的東西，但你仍然可以讓他們感受到你的關心。我以前以為櫃檯工作就是和電腦、房卡打交道，現在我知道，它真正的重點是人。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a student quickly became the manager of a hotel.", "What a student learned about service in her first week at a hotel front desk.", "Why hotels should let guests check in before three o'clock.", "How a student improved her English by talking to guests from Japan."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文描述作者在飯店櫃檯實習第一週的經過：學習辦理入住（第二段）、遇到生氣的客人（第三、四段），最後在第五段領悟 service work is not about always saying yes... Now I know it is really about people. 可見本文重點是作者第一週學到的服務心得。(A) 作者只是實習生，文中沒有提到她成為經理；(C) 文中並沒有主張飯店應該讓客人提早入住；(D) 第三段只提到作者用英文接待日本客人，並非全文重點。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what did Ms. Kuo ask the writer to do?", "options": ["Answer the phone for her during busy hours.", "Write down the names of all the guests.", "Practice smiling in front of a mirror.", "Explain the breakfast hours to guests by herself."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：She also made me practice smiling in the staff room mirror. 郭小姐要作者在員工休息室的鏡子前練習微笑，與 (C) 相符。(A) 接電話的是郭小姐本人，並不是要作者代勞；(B) 作者做的是筆記，文中沒有說要記下所有客人的名字；(D) 第一天作者只是在旁邊看，並沒有被要求獨自向客人說明早餐時間。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why was the businessman upset?", "options": ["The writer could not understand his English.", "The hotel had lost his booking.", "His key card did not open his door.", "He could not get into his room as soon as he arrived."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：He had just come off a long flight and wanted his room immediately. Check-in time was three o'clock, and his room was not ready. 這位商務人士一到飯店就想進房，但房間還沒準備好，所以很不高興。(A) 第三段只提到作者接待日本夫妻時英文說得慢，並沒有說她聽不懂這位商務人士的英文；(B) 文中沒有提到訂房資料遺失；(C) 第四段說他後來回來拿房卡，並沒有提到房卡打不開門。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"blank\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["full of anger", "empty of thoughts", "busy with plans", "calm and relaxed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段 When I told him this, he raised his voice, and my mind went completely blank. 客人一提高音量，作者緊張得「腦袋一片空白」，什麼都想不出來，blank 意為「空白的」，即 empty of thoughts。(A) full of anger「充滿怒氣」是客人的情緒，不是作者的；(C) busy with plans「忙著盤算」與 (D) calm and relaxed「冷靜放鬆」都和作者當時緊張到無法思考的狀態不符。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Ms. Kuo?", "options": ["She believes understanding guests' feelings is an important part of the job.", "She thinks interns should not be allowed to speak to guests.", "She blamed the writer for making the businessman upset.", "She thought the businessman was simply a rude person."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段郭小姐對作者說：He wasn't angry at you. He was tired. Listen first, then solve the problem. 她能體會客人生氣是因為長途旅行太累，並主張先傾聽再解決問題，可推知她認為理解客人的感受是這份工作的重要一環。(B) 第一段她說 Tomorrow you talk，第二天也讓作者獨自接待客人；(C) 她明白告訴作者客人不是在生作者的氣，並沒有責怪作者；(D) 她認為客人只是累了，並不是認為他沒禮貌。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a266", "title": "Lunch Without Scrolling", "title_zh": "不滑手機的午餐", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Education"], "passage": "Last spring, our homeroom teacher, Mr. Hsieh, noticed something strange at lunchtime. Thirty-two students were sitting in one classroom, but the room was almost silent. Nearly everyone was eating with one hand and scrolling with the other. So our class leader, Ya-ting, suggested that for one month, we put our phones in a box during lunch. Mr. Hsieh let the class vote. The idea passed, though not everyone was happy about it.\n\nThe first week was awkward. At 12:10, we dropped our phones into an old shoe box on the teacher's desk and sat down with our lunch boxes. Then nobody knew what to do. I could hear every click of chopsticks and every sip of soup. One boy read the label on his drink bottle three times. A few people ate quickly and put their heads down to wait for nap time.\n\nThings started to change in the second week. Jun-hao brought a deck of cards, and soon six people crowded around his desk. Someone suggested a game called \"Two Truths and a Lie,\" and I learned more about my classmates in one lunch than in a whole year. The girl who sits behind me, it turned out, draws comics, and her family runs a breakfast shop near the night market.\n\nNot everyone was won over, though. Wei-lun said his grandmother lives alone in Hualien, and lunch was when he always sent her a message to make sure she was all right. Pei-chen, one of the quietest people in our class, said the games made her feel she had to talk when she just wanted to rest. Their points were fair, and Mr. Hsieh asked us to take them seriously.\n\nAt the end of the month, we voted again. We decided to keep phone-free lunches, but only on Mondays, Wednesdays and Fridays. On those days, anyone may read a book or eat in peace instead of joining a game, and students who need to contact family can still use their phones for a quick message. On other days, phones are welcome. I don't think phones are the enemy. But the box reminded us of something we had almost forgotten: the people sitting next to us are worth our attention.", "passage_zh": "去年春天，我們的導師謝老師注意到午餐時間有件奇怪的事：三十二個學生坐在同一間教室裡，教室卻幾乎鴉雀無聲。幾乎每個人都是一手吃飯、一手滑手機。於是我們的班長雅婷提議，接下來一個月，午餐時間大家都把手機放進一個箱子裡。謝老師讓全班投票表決。這個提議通過了，不過並不是每個人都樂意。\n\n第一個星期很尷尬。十二點十分，我們把手機丟進講桌上一個舊鞋盒裡，然後拿著便當坐下。接著，沒有人知道該做什麼。我聽得見每一聲筷子的碰撞、每一口喝湯的聲音。有個男生把飲料瓶上的標籤讀了三遍。有幾個人很快吃完，就趴在桌上等午休。\n\n到了第二個星期，情況開始改變。俊豪帶了一副撲克牌來，很快就有六個人圍在他的桌子旁。有人提議玩一個叫「兩真一假」的遊戲，結果我在一次午餐裡對同學的了解，比過去一整年還多。原來坐在我後面的女生會畫漫畫，而她家在夜市附近開早餐店。\n\n不過，並不是每個人都被說服了。偉倫說，他的奶奶獨自住在花蓮，他總是在午餐時傳訊息給她，確認她一切平安。班上最安靜的人之一佩貞則說，那些遊戲讓她覺得自己非說話不可，但她其實只想休息。他們的意見很有道理，謝老師也要我們認真看待。\n\n一個月結束時，我們又投了一次票。我們決定繼續實施「無手機午餐」，但只限星期一、三、五。在這幾天，任何人都可以看書或安靜吃飯，不必參加遊戲；需要聯絡家人的同學也仍然可以用手機很快地傳個訊息。其他日子則歡迎使用手機。我不認為手機是敵人，但那個箱子提醒了我們一件幾乎被遺忘的事：坐在我們身旁的人，值得我們用心關注。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How one class tried phone-free lunches and what it decided in the end.", "Why students should never be allowed to bring phones to school.", "How to play popular card games during lunch breaks.", "Why Mr. Hsieh punished students for using phones in class."], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文依時間順序描述一個班級嘗試一個月「午餐不用手機」的經過：第一週很尷尬（第二段），第二週開始聊天、玩遊戲（第三段），也有人提出不同意見（第四段），最後在第五段全班投票決定 keep phone-free lunches, but only on Mondays, Wednesdays and Fridays。(B) 作者在最後說 I don't think phones are the enemy，並未主張完全禁止帶手機；(C) 玩牌只是第三段的一個細節；(D) 謝老師是讓全班投票，並沒有處罰學生。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happened during the first week?", "options": ["Most students refused to put their phones in the box.", "Students played card games together every day.", "Mr. Hsieh gave a long talk about the dangers of phones.", "Many students were not sure what to do without their phones."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Then nobody knew what to do. 第一週大家把手機放進鞋盒後，都不知道該做什麼，有人反覆讀飲料標籤，有人趕快吃完趴下，與 (D) 相符。(A) 第二段說 we dropped our phones into an old shoe box，並沒有多數人拒絕；(B) 玩牌是第二週才開始的事（第三段）；(C) 文中沒有提到謝老師演講談手機的危險。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why was Wei-lun unhappy with the plan?", "options": ["He wanted to play online games with his friends.", "He used lunchtime to check on a family member.", "He thought the card games were too noisy.", "He was afraid someone would take his phone."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Wei-lun said his grandmother lives alone in Hualien, and lunch was when he always sent her a message to make sure she was all right. 偉倫習慣在午餐時傳訊息，確認獨居的奶奶是否平安，也就是關心家人的狀況，與 (B) 相符。(A) 文中沒有提到他想玩線上遊戲；(C) 覺得遊戲帶來壓力的是佩貞，而且原因並不是太吵；(D) 文中沒有提到擔心手機被拿走。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"awkward\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["uncomfortable", "exciting", "noisy", "relaxing"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段 The first week was awkward. 後面描述大家「不知道該做什麼」，教室安靜到聽得見筷子聲，有人把標籤讀了三遍，有人趕快吃完趴下，可見氣氛很不自在，awkward 意為「尷尬的、不自在的」，即 uncomfortable。(B) exciting「令人興奮的」不符；(C) noisy「吵鬧的」與教室安靜的描述相反；(D) relaxing「令人放鬆的」也和大家不知所措的情況不符。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward phones at the end of the passage?", "options": ["Phones should be banned at school all day.", "Phones are more important than talking with friends.", "Phones are not bad, but time with others also matters.", "Phones are the main reason students get poor grades."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：I don't think phones are the enemy. But the box reminded us of something we had almost forgotten: the people sitting next to us are worth our attention. 作者認為手機本身不是壞東西，但和身邊的人相處也很重要，與 (C) 相符。(A) 全班決定其他日子仍歡迎使用手機，作者也沒有主張整天禁用；(B) 作者強調的是身邊的人值得關注，與此相反；(D) 文中完全沒有提到成績。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a267", "title": "Tears on the Cutting Board: Why Onions Make Us Cry", "title_zh": "砧板上的眼淚：洋蔥為什麼讓我們流淚", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Food"], "passage": "You are helping in the kitchen, and your job sounds easy: chop an onion. But within a minute, your eyes begin to sting, and soon tears are running down your face. You are not sad, so why are you crying? The answer is hidden inside the onion itself, in a clever chemical trick that many scientists believe protects it from hungry animals.\n\nAn onion is made of millions of tiny cells. While the onion is whole, certain chemicals inside the cells are kept apart. Some of them contain sulfur, which gives onions their strong smell. Others are enzymes, substances that speed up chemical changes. When a knife cuts through the onion, it breaks open many cells, and these chemicals mix. Within seconds, they produce a gas that easily spreads into the air.\n\nCarried by the air, this gas soon reaches the surface of your eyes. Nerves there sense that something is bothering them and send a message to the brain. The brain answers by telling your eyes to make more tears. These tears have nothing to do with your feelings. They are simply your body's way of washing the gas away.\n\nPeople have tried many ways to stop the tears, and some seem to work better than others. A sharp knife is probably the most helpful. It cuts cleanly through the cells instead of crushing them, so less gas is likely to be released. Many cooks also chill the onion in the fridge first, hoping the cold will slow the reactions, but tests have not agreed on whether this really works. A fan or a range hood can also help by carrying the gas away from your face. On the other hand, there is little good evidence that holding a piece of bread in your mouth helps. After all, the gas reaches your eyes through the air, not through your mouth.\n\nThis explains why professional chefs often chop onions without a single tear. They keep their knives very sharp, so fewer cells are crushed and less gas is released. Some people may also be less sensitive to the gas. So if onions make you cry, don't worry. Your eyes are just doing their job.", "passage_zh": "你正在廚房幫忙，工作聽起來很簡單：切一顆洋蔥。但不到一分鐘，你的眼睛就開始刺痛，很快地，眼淚便順著臉頰流了下來。你並不傷心，那為什麼會哭呢？答案就藏在洋蔥本身，藏在一種巧妙的化學把戲裡；許多科學家相信，這個把戲能保護洋蔥不被飢餓的動物吃掉。\n\n洋蔥是由數百萬個微小的細胞組成的。洋蔥完整的時候，細胞裡的某些化學物質是彼此分開的。其中一些含有硫，洋蔥強烈的氣味就是由它而來。另一些則是酵素，也就是能加快化學變化的物質。當刀子切過洋蔥時，會弄破許多細胞，這些化學物質便混合在一起。短短幾秒內，它們就會產生一種很容易散布到空氣中的氣體。\n\n這種氣體隨著空氣飄散，很快就到達你眼睛的表面。那裡的神經察覺到有東西在刺激它們，便傳送訊息給大腦。大腦的回應是讓眼睛分泌更多淚水。這些眼淚和你的情緒毫無關係，它們只是身體把氣體沖洗掉的方式。\n\n人們試過許多方法來止住眼淚，其中有些似乎比較有效。鋒利的刀子大概最有幫助。它能俐落地切過細胞，而不是把細胞壓碎，所以釋放出來的氣體可能會比較少。許多廚師也會先把洋蔥放進冰箱冰一下，希望低溫能減緩這些反應，不過這個方法是否真的有效，各項測試的結果並不一致。電風扇或抽油煙機也有幫助，可以把氣體從你的臉前帶走。另一方面，幾乎沒有可靠的證據顯示在嘴裡含一片麵包有用。畢竟，氣體是透過空氣到達你的眼睛，而不是經過你的嘴巴。\n\n這也說明了為什麼專業廚師切洋蔥時常常一滴眼淚也不掉。他們把刀子保持得非常鋒利，所以被壓碎的細胞比較少，釋放出來的氣體也比較少。有些人可能也對這種氣體比較不敏感。所以，如果洋蔥讓你流淚，別擔心，你的眼睛只是在做它該做的事。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To teach readers several different ways to cook onions", "To warn readers that onions can seriously harm their eyes", "To compare the knife skills of chefs and home cooks", "To explain why cutting onions causes tears and what may help"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文先在第二、三段說明切洋蔥時細胞破裂、化學物質混合後產生氣體，眼睛因而分泌淚水把氣體沖走；第四、五段再介紹哪些方法可能有幫助、哪些說法缺乏證據。(A) 文中沒有介紹烹調洋蔥的方法；(B) 第三段說流淚只是 your body's way of washing the gas away，文章並不是在警告洋蔥會嚴重傷害眼睛；(C) 第五段提到專業廚師只是用來說明原理，並不是全文重點。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happens when a knife cuts through an onion?", "options": ["Chemicals that were kept apart mix and form a gas.", "The sulfur in the onion quickly turns into water.", "The enzymes in the cells are all destroyed at once.", "The cells grow larger and fill up with onion juice."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：While the onion is whole, certain chemicals inside the cells are kept apart... When a knife cuts through the onion, it breaks open many cells, and these chemicals mix. Within seconds, they produce a gas... 原本分開的化學物質混合後產生了氣體。(B) 文中沒有說硫會變成水；(C) 酵素並沒有被破壞，而是和含硫的物質混合、加快化學變化；(D) 細胞是被刀子弄破，而不是變大。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why do our eyes make tears when we cut onions?", "options": ["The strong smell of the onion makes us feel sad.", "Juice from the onion jumps directly into our eyes.", "Tears help wash away the gas that reaches our eyes.", "The brain wants to warn us not to eat raw onions."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：These tears have nothing to do with your feelings. They are simply your body's way of washing the gas away. 眼淚是身體用來把到達眼睛的氣體沖洗掉的方式。(A) 文中明說眼淚和情緒無關；(B) 第二、三段說到達眼睛的是散布在空氣中的氣體，而不是洋蔥汁；(D) 文中沒有提到大腦在警告我們不要吃生洋蔥。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"chill\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["wash carefully", "cut into pieces", "cook quickly", "make cold"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段 Many cooks also chill the onion in the fridge first, hoping the cold will slow the reactions，廚師把洋蔥放進冰箱，後面又提到 the cold（低溫），可知 chill 意為「使變冷、冷藏」。(A)「仔細清洗」、(B)「切成小塊」、(C)「快速烹煮」都與放進冰箱、低溫的文意不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following can be inferred from the passage?", "options": ["Holding bread in your mouth is the best way to stop tears.", "Cutting an onion with a dull knife may lead to more tears.", "An onion kept in the fridge will not give off any gas.", "Professional chefs never feel their eyes sting at all."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說鋒利的刀子 cuts cleanly through the cells instead of crushing them, so less gas is likely to be released；由此可推論，鈍的刀子會壓碎較多細胞、釋放較多氣體，可能讓人流更多眼淚。(A) 第四段說 there is little good evidence that holding a piece of bread in your mouth helps，含麵包的做法缺乏可靠證據，更談不上是最好的方法；(C) 第四段只說廚師 hoping the cold will slow the reactions，而且 tests have not agreed on whether this really works，並沒有說冰過的洋蔥完全不會產生氣體；(D) 第五段只說專業廚師 often 切洋蔥不掉淚，用 never 太過絕對。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a268", "title": "The Other Games of 1948", "title_zh": "1948 年的另一場運動會", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "History"], "passage": "On July 29, 1948, the Olympic Games opened in London with a grand ceremony at Wembley Stadium. On the same day, in a village northwest of the city, a much smaller sports event took place on the grounds of Stoke Mandeville Hospital. Sixteen men and women in wheelchairs, all former members of the armed forces, took part in an archery contest. Few people paid attention at the time, but this little event would one day grow into the Paralympic Games.\n\nThe man behind the contest was Dr. Ludwig Guttmann, a German-Jewish doctor and an expert on the brain and spinal cord. After the Nazis came to power, Jewish doctors in Germany were pushed out of most hospitals and faced growing danger. Shortly before the Second World War, Guttmann escaped with his family to Britain. In 1944, he was asked to run a new spinal injuries unit at Stoke Mandeville, where many patients were soldiers badly hurt in the war.\n\nAt that time, people with spinal injuries were given little hope. Many spent most of the day lying in bed, and many did not live long. Guttmann refused to accept this. He made sure patients were turned in bed regularly to prevent painful sores, and he got them sitting up and moving as early as possible. He also believed sport could build strength, lift patients' spirits and give them back their self-respect.\n\nSo sport became part of daily treatment. Patients played team games, including an early form of wheelchair basketball, and archery was popular because it strengthened the arms, shoulders and back. The 1948 contest was held again the next year and kept growing. In 1952, a team from the Netherlands joined, making the event international. In 1960, about 400 athletes took part in the games in Rome, held shortly after the Olympics there. Today, those Rome games are remembered as the first Paralympic Games.\n\nGuttmann's work changed more than sport. By showing that people with disabilities could compete, work and live full lives, he helped change how the world saw them. Today, the Paralympic Games take place soon after the Olympics in the same host city, and thousands of athletes take part. Not bad for an event that started with sixteen archers on a hospital lawn.", "passage_zh": "1948 年 7 月 29 日，奧運在倫敦溫布利球場以盛大的典禮揭開序幕。就在同一天，在倫敦西北方的一座村莊裡，一場規模小得多的運動賽事在斯托克曼德維爾醫院的院區內舉行。十六位坐輪椅的男女參加了一場射箭比賽，他們都曾在軍中服役。當時很少有人注意到這件事，但這場小小的比賽日後將發展成帕拉林匹克運動會（帕運）。\n\n這場比賽的推手是路德維希‧古特曼醫師，他是一位德國猶太裔醫師，也是研究大腦與脊髓的專家。納粹掌權之後，德國的猶太醫師被趕出大多數醫院，處境也愈來愈危險。就在第二次世界大戰爆發前不久，古特曼帶著家人逃到英國。1944 年，他受託主持斯托克曼德維爾醫院新設立的脊髓損傷病房，那裡的許多病人都是在戰爭中受了重傷的軍人。\n\n在那個年代，脊髓損傷的病人幾乎被認為沒有希望。許多人一天中大部分時間都躺在床上，也有許多人活不久。古特曼不肯接受這種情況。他確保病人在床上定時翻身，以免長出疼痛的褥瘡，並讓他們盡早坐起來、活動身體。他也相信，運動能增強體力、提振病人的精神，並讓他們重拾自尊。\n\n於是，運動成了日常治療的一部分。病人們會進行團體運動，包括早期形式的輪椅籃球；而射箭很受歡迎，因為它能鍛鍊手臂、肩膀和背部。1948 年的比賽在隔年再度舉辦，而且規模不斷擴大。1952 年，一支來自荷蘭的隊伍加入，這項賽事從此成為國際性的活動。1960 年，約有四百名選手參加了在羅馬舉行的比賽，時間就在當地奧運結束後不久。如今，那屆羅馬的賽事被視為第一屆帕拉林匹克運動會。\n\n古特曼的貢獻不只改變了運動。他證明身心障礙者也能參加比賽、工作，並過著充實的生活，因而幫助世人改變了對他們的看法。如今，帕運都在奧運結束後不久、於同一個主辦城市舉行，有數千名選手參賽。對一場從醫院草坪上十六名射箭選手開始的賽事來說，這樣的成就真是相當了不起。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the 1948 London Olympics were planned and held.", "Why archery is the most popular Paralympic sport today.", "How a doctor's use of sport in treatment led to the Paralympics.", "Why Ludwig Guttmann had to leave Germany before the war."], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹古特曼醫師如何把運動納入脊髓損傷病人的治療（第三、四段），並說明 1948 年醫院裡的射箭比賽如何一路發展成 1960 年在羅馬舉行、被視為第一屆帕運的賽事（第四段：those Rome games are remembered as the first Paralympic Games）。(A) 倫敦奧運只是第一段的背景；(B) 文中只說射箭在病人之間很受歡迎，沒有說它是當今最受歡迎的帕運項目；(D) 古特曼逃離德國只是第二段的內容，並非全文重點。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did Guttmann move to Britain?", "options": ["The British government invited him to plan the Olympics.", "Life in Germany had become unsafe for Jewish people like him.", "He wanted to learn new ways of treating soldiers there.", "He had been badly hurt as a soldier in the war."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：After the Nazis came to power, Jewish doctors in Germany were pushed out of most hospitals and faced growing danger... Guttmann escaped with his family to Britain. 納粹掌權後，猶太醫師的處境愈來愈危險，身為猶太人的古特曼因此逃往英國，與 (B) 相符。(A) 文中沒有說他受邀籌備奧運；(C) 他是為了逃離危險，而不是為了學習治療方法；(D) 受重傷的軍人是他的病人，不是他本人。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why was archery popular among the patients?", "options": ["It helped them build strength in their upper bodies.", "It was the only sport the hospital could afford.", "It was easier to win than wheelchair basketball.", "It could be done without getting out of bed."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：archery was popular because it strengthened the arms, shoulders and back. 射箭能鍛鍊手臂、肩膀和背部，也就是上半身的力量，與 (A) 相符。(B) 文中沒有提到醫院的經費；(C) 文中沒有比較射箭和輪椅籃球哪個比較容易贏；(D) 第三段說古特曼讓病人盡早坐起來活動，第一段也說參賽者是坐在輪椅上，並不是躺在床上。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"spirits\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["physical strength", "hospital rooms", "feelings and moods", "ghosts and souls"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段 He also believed sport could build strength, lift patients' spirits and give them back their self-respect. 運動能「提振病人的 spirits」，這裡的 spirits 指心情、精神狀態，即 feelings and moods。(A) physical strength「體力」在同一句已用 build strength 表達，spirits 指的是另一件事；(B) hospital rooms「病房」不合文意；(D) ghosts and souls「鬼魂與靈魂」是 spirit 的另一個意思，但不符合此處文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest in the last sentence of the passage?", "options": ["The Paralympics should go back to being small hospital events.", "Archery is still the most important sport at the Paralympics.", "The first contest in 1948 was poorly organized.", "It is impressive how much the small contest has grown."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段最後一句 Not bad for an event that started with sixteen archers on a hospital lawn. 前一句提到如今帕運在奧運的主辦城市舉行、有數千名選手參賽，作者用「對一場從醫院草坪上十六名射箭選手開始的賽事來說還真不錯」的說法，表達對這項賽事成長之多的讚嘆，與 (D) 相符。(A) 作者沒有主張帕運應回到醫院舉辦；(B) 文中沒有說射箭仍是帕運最重要的項目；(C) 文中沒有批評第一次比賽辦得不好。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a269", "title": "Are You Yawning Yet? The Puzzle of Catching a Yawn", "title_zh": "你打哈欠了沒？哈欠傳染之謎", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Health"], "passage": "Think about the last time you yawned. Picture your mouth opening wide, the long, deep breath in, and the shorter breath out. Are you yawning now? If so, you are not alone. Many people yawn just from reading about yawning. A yawn lasts only a few seconds, but it is one of the strangest things our bodies do, and scientists still cannot fully explain it.\n\nYawns can spread in surprising ways. We often yawn after seeing someone else yawn, even a stranger on a bus or a person in a video. Hearing a yawn can be enough, and so can simply thinking about one. Scientists call this \"contagious\" yawning, because it seems to pass from person to person, a little like a cold, though nothing harmful is passed on. Interestingly, very young children rarely catch yawns. This ability seems to develop with age.\n\nBut why do we yawn at all? For many years, people believed a yawn brought extra oxygen to a tired body. However, when scientists tested this idea, they found that breathing extra oxygen did not make people yawn less. Another idea is that yawning may help cool the brain. In one experiment, people holding something cold against their foreheads yawned less while watching videos of others yawning. Temperature may play a part, but this idea is still being studied.\n\nOther researchers believe contagious yawning is connected to our social lives. Some studies have found that people catch yawns more easily from family members and close friends than from strangers. Perhaps catching a yawn is a small sign that we are paying attention to those around us. Still, not every study agrees, and some scientists think other things, such as age, may matter more.\n\nHumans are not the only ones who catch yawns. Studies show that chimpanzees catch yawns from one another, especially members of their own group. Some studies suggest dogs may yawn after seeing or hearing their owners yawn, but the results are mixed. So next time you yawn in class, don't feel bad. You may simply be sharing a moment with the people around you, or your brain may be cooling itself down. Scientists are still trying to decide.", "passage_zh": "回想一下你上一次打哈欠的情景。想像你的嘴巴張得大大的，先深深地吸一口長氣，再短短地吐氣。你現在打哈欠了嗎？如果有，你並不孤單。許多人光是讀到有關打哈欠的文字，就會打起哈欠來。一個哈欠只持續幾秒鐘，卻是我們身體所做最奇特的事情之一，而科學家至今仍無法完全解釋它。\n\n哈欠會以令人驚訝的方式傳開。我們常常在看到別人打哈欠後，自己也跟著打哈欠，即使對方只是公車上的陌生人或影片中的人。聽到別人打哈欠可能就足以引發哈欠，甚至光是想到哈欠也一樣。科學家把這種現象稱為「傳染性」哈欠，因為它似乎會像感冒一樣在人與人之間傳遞，不過並沒有任何有害的東西被傳出去。有趣的是，年紀很小的孩子很少會被哈欠傳染。這種能力似乎是隨著年齡增長才逐漸出現的。\n\n但我們究竟為什麼要打哈欠呢？多年來，人們相信打哈欠能為疲倦的身體帶來額外的氧氣。然而，當科學家測試這個想法時，他們發現吸入額外的氧氣並不會讓人比較少打哈欠。另一種想法是，打哈欠可能有助於讓大腦降溫。在一項實驗中，把冰涼的東西貼在額頭上的人，在觀看別人打哈欠的影片時，打哈欠的次數比較少。溫度可能有一定的影響，但這個想法仍在研究中。\n\n另一些研究人員則認為，傳染性哈欠和我們的社交生活有關。有些研究發現，比起陌生人，人們更容易被家人和好朋友的哈欠傳染。也許被哈欠傳染是一個小小的跡象，表示我們正在留意身邊的人。不過，並非每一項研究的結果都一致，有些科學家認為其他因素，例如年齡，可能更加重要。\n\n會被哈欠傳染的不只有人類。研究顯示，黑猩猩會被彼此的哈欠傳染，尤其是自己群體裡的成員。有些研究指出，狗在看到或聽到主人打哈欠後也可能跟著打哈欠，但研究結果並不一致。所以，下次你在課堂上打哈欠時，不必難為情。你可能只是正在和身邊的人分享同一個時刻，也可能是你的大腦正在替自己降溫。科學家們仍在努力找出答案。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why people should try not to yawn in public", "How doctors use yawning to discover illnesses", "How yawns spread and what scientists know about them so far", "Why animals yawn more often than humans do"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文說明哈欠會因為看到、聽到，甚至讀到或想到而傳染（第二段），接著介紹科學家測試過的幾種解釋——氧氣說、大腦降溫說與社交連結說（第三、四段），再提到黑猩猩和狗（第五段），並指出 scientists still cannot fully explain it。(A) 文中沒有勸人不要在公共場合打哈欠；(B) 文中沒有提到醫生用哈欠診斷疾病；(D) 第五段只說動物也會被哈欠傳染，並未說動物比人類更常打哈欠。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, which of the following can make a person yawn?", "options": ["The smell of food when he or she is hungry", "A glass of cold water on a hot day", "Too much sleep the night before", "The sound of another person yawning"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Hearing a yawn can be enough, and so can simply thinking about one. 光是聽到別人打哈欠就可能引發哈欠，(D)「別人打哈欠的聲音」是此句的改寫。(A) 食物的氣味和 (C) 睡太多，文中都沒有提到；(B) 第三段提到的是額頭貼著冰涼的東西反而讓人較少打哈欠，並沒有說喝冷水會讓人打哈欠。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What did scientists find when they tested the oxygen idea?", "options": ["Tired people took in more oxygen each time they yawned.", "Getting more oxygen did not reduce how often people yawned.", "Yawning helped students stay awake during long classes.", "People yawned less often in rooms full of fresh air."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：when scientists tested this idea, they found that breathing extra oxygen did not make people yawn less. 吸入額外的氧氣並沒有讓人少打哈欠，與 (B)「獲得更多氧氣並未減少打哈欠的次數」相符。(A) 是過去人們相信的說法，實驗並未證實；(C) 文中沒有說打哈欠能讓學生在課堂上保持清醒；(D) 文中沒有提到充滿新鮮空氣的房間。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"contagious\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["able to spread from one person to another", "very dangerous to people's health", "difficult to see or notice", "happening only late at night"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段 Scientists call this \"contagious\" yawning, because it seems to pass from person to person, a little like a cold. 哈欠像感冒一樣在人與人之間傳遞，可知 contagious 意為「有傳染性的」，即 (A)「能從一個人傳到另一個人」。(B)「對健康非常危險」與同一句的 nothing harmful is passed on 不符；(C)「難以察覺」與 (D)「只在深夜發生」都不符合文意。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's attitude toward the explanations for yawning?", "options": ["The writer is certain that yawning cools the brain.", "The writer finds them interesting but not yet proven.", "The writer believes the oxygen idea is the best one.", "The writer feels that studying yawns is a waste of time."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段說大腦降溫的想法 is still being studied，第四段說 not every study agrees，第五段說狗的研究 the results are mixed，最後以 Scientists are still trying to decide. 作結；作者也稱哈欠是 one of the strangest things our bodies do。可見作者覺得這些解釋很有趣，但尚未得到證實。(A) 作者用 may 等保留語氣，並不確定；(C) 第三段指出氧氣說經過測試並不成立；(D) 作者並沒有認為研究哈欠是浪費時間。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a270", "title": "Two Summers a Year: The Amazing Journey of the Arctic Tern", "title_zh": "一年兩個夏天：北極燕鷗的驚人旅程", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Nature"], "passage": "Most of us get one summer each year, but the Arctic tern gets two. This small seabird, which weighs only about 100 grams, spends the northern summer in the far north, where it lays its eggs and raises its young. Then, as the days grow shorter, it sets off south to the seas around Antarctica, arriving just in time for summer there. A few months later, it flies all the way back north.\n\nBecause the bird follows summer around the world, it enjoys long hours of sunlight for much of the year. Near the poles in summer, the sun hardly sets at all. For this reason, the Arctic tern probably sees more daylight than any other animal. It also spends much of its life over the open sea, catching small fish by diving into the water from the air.\n\nFor a long time, no one knew exactly how far these birds traveled. Then scientists attached tiny tracking devices, each weighing less than two grams, to the legs of terns nesting in Greenland and Iceland. The devices recorded light levels every day, which allowed the researchers to work out roughly where each bird had been. About a year later, when the birds returned to nest, the scientists caught them again and collected the devices.\n\nThe results were surprising. The birds did not fly in a straight line from north to south. Instead, they followed a winding, S-shaped route over the Atlantic Ocean, most likely using the winds to save energy. Many also stopped in the middle of the North Atlantic to feed. Because of these turns and stops, the average round trip came to roughly 70,000 kilometers. Later studies of terns from other places suggested that some birds fly even farther.\n\nNow think about a whole lifetime. Some Arctic terns live for more than 30 years. If a bird flies about 70,000 kilometers each year, its total over such a long life could be more than two million kilometers. That is farther than a trip to the Moon and back. In fact, it is close to three such trips. Not bad for a bird that weighs less than an apple.", "passage_zh": "我們大多數人每年只過一個夏天，北極燕鷗卻能過兩個。這種體重只有大約 100 公克的小型海鳥，在北半球的夏天待在遙遠的北方，在那裡產卵、養育幼鳥。接著，隨著白天越來越短，牠便往南出發，飛到南極洲周圍的海域，正好趕上那裡的夏天。幾個月後，牠又一路飛回北方。\n\n由於這種鳥追隨著夏天繞行世界，一年之中有很長一段時間都享有長時間的日照。在夏季的兩極附近，太陽幾乎不會落下。因此，北極燕鷗看到的日光可能比任何其他動物都多。牠一生中也有許多時間在開闊的海面上度過，從空中潛入水中捕捉小魚。\n\n很長一段時間以來，沒有人確切知道這些鳥究竟飛了多遠。後來，科學家把微小的追蹤裝置裝在格陵蘭和冰島築巢的燕鷗腳上，每個裝置的重量都不到兩公克。這些裝置每天記錄光線的強弱，讓研究人員能夠大致推算出每隻鳥曾經到過哪裡。大約一年後，當這些鳥回來築巢時，科學家再次捕捉牠們，並取回裝置。\n\n研究結果令人驚訝。這些鳥並不是從北到南直線飛行，而是沿著一條彎彎曲曲、呈 S 形的路線飛越大西洋，很可能是為了利用風來節省體力。許多燕鷗還會在北大西洋中部停下來覓食。由於這些轉彎和停留，平均往返一趟的距離大約達到七萬公里。後來針對其他地區燕鷗所做的研究顯示，有些鳥飛得更遠。\n\n現在，再想想牠的一生。有些北極燕鷗能活超過 30 年。如果一隻鳥每年飛行大約七萬公里，牠在這麼長的一生中飛行的總距離可能超過兩百萬公里。這比到月球來回一趟還要遠。事實上，這幾乎相當於往返月球三趟。對一隻比一顆蘋果還輕的鳥來說，真是了不起。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How scientists protect seabirds in Greenland", "How the weather differs between the two poles", "How seabirds catch fish in the open sea", "The amazingly long yearly journey of the Arctic tern"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文介紹北極燕鷗每年從北方飛到南極洲周圍再飛回來（第一段），科學家用追蹤裝置發現牠們沿著彎曲的路線飛行，平均往返約七萬公里（第三、四段），並推算牠一生的飛行距離（第五段）。(A) 文中沒有提到保護海鳥；(B) 文中沒有比較兩極的天氣；(C) 第二段只簡短提到燕鷗潛水捕魚，並非全文主旨。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraphs 1 and 2, why does the Arctic tern see so much daylight?", "options": ["It flies mostly at night and rests during the day.", "It spends the summer near both the Arctic and Antarctica.", "It stays near the North Pole for the whole year.", "It lives where the sun never sets in any season."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說燕鷗 spends the northern summer in the far north，之後飛到南極洲周圍的海域 arriving just in time for summer there；第二段說 Near the poles in summer, the sun hardly sets at all. 牠在南北兩極附近都度過夏天，所以日照特別多。(A) 文中沒有說牠夜間飛行、白天休息；(C) 牠每年都會飛到南極洲附近，並非整年待在北極；(D) 兩極只有在夏天太陽才幾乎不下山，並不是每個季節都如此。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How did the scientists find out where the terns had traveled?", "options": ["Devices on the birds recorded light, and the data was collected a year later.", "They followed the birds by boat all the way across the Atlantic Ocean.", "They placed cameras along the coasts of Africa and South America.", "They asked fishermen around the world to report every tern they saw."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：The devices recorded light levels every day... About a year later, when the birds returned to nest, the scientists caught them again and collected the devices. 裝置每天記錄光線，一年後再把裝置取回，與 (A) 相符。(B) 坐船跟隨、(C) 在非洲和南美洲海岸設置相機、(D) 請漁民回報，文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"winding\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very windy and cold", "short and direct", "full of turns and curves", "dangerous and crowded"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段 The birds did not fly in a straight line... Instead, they followed a winding, S-shaped route，winding 與 straight line 相對，又是 S 形，可知意為「蜿蜒的、彎彎曲曲的」，即 (C)「充滿轉彎和彎道的」。(A)「風很大又寒冷」是把 winding 誤認為和 wind（風）有關；(B)「短而直接」意思相反；(D)「危險又擁擠」不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention a trip to the Moon in paragraph 5?", "options": ["To help readers imagine how far a tern may fly in its lifetime", "To suggest that terns may one day be able to reach the Moon", "To show that terns fly higher than any other kind of bird", "To compare the weight of a tern with the weight of a rocket"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：燕鷗一生的飛行距離可能 more than two million kilometers，That is farther than a trip to the Moon and back. In fact, it is close to three such trips. 作者用往返月球的距離作比較，幫助讀者想像這個驚人的數字。(B) 作者並不是說燕鷗能飛到月球；(C) 文中沒有比較飛行高度；(D) 文中沒有提到火箭。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a271", "title": "One Extra Day: The Story Behind February 29", "title_zh": "多出來的一天：二月二十九日的由來", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Culture"], "passage": "In most years, February ends on the 28th. But about once every four years, it gets an extra day: February 29. Such a year is called a leap year. This may seem like a strange rule, but there is a good reason for it. Without leap years, our calendar would slowly drift away from the seasons.\n\nA calendar year has 365 days, but the Earth takes about 365 and a quarter days to travel once around the Sun. That extra quarter of a day may not sound like much, but it adds up. After four years, the calendar falls about one day out of step; after 100 years, about 24 days. In several hundred years, Taiwan could have winter in July.\n\nMore than 2,000 years ago, the Roman leader Julius Caesar introduced a calendar with a simple solution: one extra day every four years. For centuries, it seemed to work well. However, a full year of seasons, from one spring to the next, is actually about 11 minutes shorter than 365 and a quarter days. Adding a day every four years was therefore slightly too much. Over many centuries, those extra minutes added up. By the 1500s, the calendar was about ten days out of step with the seasons.\n\nThe Gregorian calendar, introduced in 1582, fixed the error. Ten days were removed from that year to catch up with the seasons. The leap-year rule also changed. A year that can be divided by four is still a leap year, but century years, such as 1700, 1800 and 1900, are skipped unless they can be divided by 400. That is why 1900 was not a leap year, while 2000 was. Today most of the world uses this calendar, which is off by only about one day every few thousand years.\n\nWhat about people born on February 29? Sometimes called \"leaplings,\" they usually see their real birthday on the calendar only once every four years. In other years, many celebrate on February 28 or March 1. A leapling who turns 20, for example, has had only five \"real\" birthdays. It may be an unusual problem, but it reminds us how carefully our calendar has been designed to keep time with the sky.", "passage_zh": "在大多數的年份裡，二月到 28 日就結束了。但大約每四年一次，這個月會多出一天：2 月 29 日。這樣的年份稱為閏年。這看起來或許像是個奇怪的規則，但背後有充分的理由。如果沒有閏年，我們的曆法就會慢慢地與季節脫節。\n\n一個曆年有 365 天，但地球繞太陽一圈大約需要 365 又四分之一天。多出來的這四分之一天聽起來也許不算什麼，但它會不斷累積。四年後，曆法會和季節相差大約一天；一百年後，會相差大約 24 天。幾百年後，臺灣甚至可能在七月過冬天。\n\n兩千多年前，羅馬領袖凱撒推行了一部曆法，採用一個簡單的解決辦法：每四年增加一天。好幾個世紀以來，這個方法似乎都運作得很好。然而，季節完整輪替一次（從一個春天到下一個春天）的時間，其實比 365 又四分之一天短了大約 11 分鐘。因此，每四年加一天其實稍微加多了。經過許多世紀，這些多出來的分鐘累積了起來。到了 1500 年代，曆法已經和季節相差大約十天。\n\n1582 年開始實施的格里曆修正了這個誤差。為了趕上季節，那一年刪去了十天。閏年的規則也跟著改變：能被 4 整除的年份仍然是閏年，但像 1700、1800 和 1900 年這樣的世紀年則不算閏年，除非它們能被 400 整除。這就是為什麼 1900 年不是閏年，而 2000 年是。如今世界上大多數地方都使用這部曆法，而它每過好幾千年才會差大約一天。\n\n那麼，2 月 29 日出生的人又是怎樣呢？他們有時被稱為「閏日寶寶」，真正的生日通常每四年才會在日曆上出現一次。在其他年份，他們許多人會在 2 月 28 日或 3 月 1 日慶祝。舉例來說，一個滿 20 歲的閏日寶寶，只過了五次「真正的」生日。這或許是個不尋常的困擾，但它提醒我們，我們的曆法經過多麼精心的設計，才能與天空的運行保持一致。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain why leap years exist and how their rules developed", "To describe how people born on February 29 celebrate birthdays", "To argue that the calendar we use today should be changed", "To compare the different calendars used around the world"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段點出閏年 there is a good reason for it，第二段說明地球繞太陽一圈約 365 又四分之一天，第三、四段介紹凱撒的曆法以及 1582 年的格里曆如何修正閏年規則，第五段再談 2 月 29 日出生的人。全文主要在說明閏年存在的原因及其規則的演變。(B) 只是第五段的內容；(C) 作者並未主張要再修改曆法，反而在第五段說 how carefully our calendar has been designed；(D) 文中沒有比較世界各地的曆法。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what would happen if there were no leap years?", "options": ["The Earth would travel around the Sun more slowly.", "Every calendar year would have 365 and a quarter days.", "The calendar would gradually stop matching the seasons.", "February would become the longest month of the year."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：Without leap years, our calendar would slowly drift away from the seasons. 第二段也說一百年後會差約 24 天，幾百年後臺灣甚至可能在七月過冬天。可知沒有閏年，曆法會逐漸與季節脫節。(A) 地球繞太陽的速度不會因曆法而改變；(B) 曆年只有 365 天，這正是問題所在；(D) 文中沒有提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Julius Caesar's calendar later need to be corrected?", "options": ["It did not include any leap days at all.", "It added an extra day every two years.", "It was too difficult for ordinary people to use.", "It made the average year a little too long."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：a full year of seasons, from one spring to the next, is actually about 11 minutes shorter than 365 and a quarter days. Adding a day every four years was therefore slightly too much. 凱撒的曆法每四年加一天，使平均一年比季節實際輪替一次的時間略長，日積月累後與季節相差約十天。(A) 凱撒的曆法正是每四年加一天；(B) 是每四年而不是每兩年；(C) 文中沒有提到曆法難以使用。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"skipped\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["made longer", "left out", "celebrated", "repeated"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段 century years, such as 1700, 1800 and 1900, are skipped unless they can be divided by 400. That is why 1900 was not a leap year... 世紀年除非能被 400 整除，否則不算閏年，可知 skipped 意為「略過、省去」，即 (B)「被省略」。(A)「被加長」、(C)「被慶祝」、(D)「被重複」都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Based on the passage, which of the following years is NOT a leap year?", "options": ["2028", "2000", "2400", "2100"], "answer": 3, "explain": "依第四段的規則：A year that can be divided by four is still a leap year, but century years... are skipped unless they can be divided by 400. 2100 年是世紀年，又不能被 400 整除，所以不是閏年。(A) 2028 能被 4 整除且不是世紀年，是閏年；(B) 文中明確說 2000 was（是閏年）；(C) 2400 能被 400 整除，是閏年。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a272", "title": "Blue at Noon, Red at Sunset: The Secret of Sky Colors", "title_zh": "中午湛藍，傍晚火紅：天空顏色的祕密", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "Almost every child asks this question at some point: \"Why is the sky blue?\" A common guess is that the sky reflects the ocean, but that cannot be right, because the sky is just as blue above deserts far from any sea. The real answer begins with sunlight. Sunlight looks white, but it is actually a mix of all the colors of the rainbow.\n\nLight travels in waves, and each color has a different wavelength, the distance from one wave to the next. Red light has the longest waves our eyes can see, while blue and violet have much shorter ones. The air is made mostly of tiny particles of nitrogen and oxygen called molecules. When sunlight hits them, some of the light is scattered, or sent off in all directions. Short waves are scattered far more than long ones; blue light is scattered several times more strongly than red.\n\nAs sunlight pours through the air, blue light keeps bouncing from molecule to molecule across the whole sky. As a result, it reaches us from every direction. Wherever we look, our eyes catch some of this scattered blue, so the whole sky appears blue. Why not violet, since its waves are even shorter? One reason is that sunlight contains less violet, and our eyes are more sensitive to blue.\n\nSunsets follow the same rule. At noon, the sun is high overhead, and its light passes through a fairly thin layer of air. In the evening, however, the sun sits low near the horizon, so its light must travel through much more air to reach us. On this long journey, most of the blue is scattered away. What remains is mainly red and orange light, which paints the sun and the clouds in warm colors.\n\nClouds offer another clue. Their water droplets are much larger than air molecules, so they scatter all colors about equally, and mixed colors look white to us. On the Moon, which has almost no air, there is nothing to scatter sunlight, and astronauts there saw a black sky even in the daytime. So a blue sky and a red sunset come from the same sunlight, simply traveling through different amounts of air.", "passage_zh": "幾乎每個孩子都曾在某個時候問過這個問題：「天空為什麼是藍色的？」一個常見的猜測是天空反映了海洋的顏色，但這不可能是正確的，因為在遠離海洋的沙漠上空，天空也一樣藍。真正的答案要從陽光說起。陽光看起來是白色的，但其實它混合了彩虹中所有的顏色。\n\n光以波的形式前進，每種顏色都有不同的波長，也就是從一個波到下一個波之間的距離。紅光是我們眼睛看得見的光中波長最長的，而藍光和紫光的波長則短得多。空氣主要是由氮和氧的微小粒子組成，這些粒子叫做分子。陽光碰到分子時，有一部分的光會被散射，也就是被往四面八方送出去。短波被散射的程度遠遠超過長波；藍光被散射的強度是紅光的好幾倍。\n\n當陽光灑進空氣中，藍光不斷在分子之間彈來彈去，遍布整個天空。結果，藍光從四面八方來到我們眼前。不論我們往哪裡看，眼睛都會接收到一些被散射的藍光，所以整片天空看起來都是藍色的。既然紫光的波長更短，天空為什麼不是紫色的呢？原因之一是陽光中的紫光比較少，而且我們的眼睛對藍色比較敏感。\n\n夕陽也遵循同樣的規則。中午時，太陽高掛在頭頂，陽光穿過的空氣層相當薄。然而到了傍晚，太陽低低地掛在地平線附近，陽光必須穿過多得多的空氣才能到達我們這裡。在這段漫長的旅程中，大部分的藍光都被散射掉了。剩下來的主要是紅光和橙光，把太陽和雲朵染成溫暖的色彩。\n\n雲朵提供了另一個線索。雲裡的水滴比空氣分子大得多，因此會把所有顏色大致平均地散射出去，而混合在一起的顏色在我們看來就是白色。月球上幾乎沒有空氣，沒有任何東西能散射陽光，所以太空人在那裡即使是白天，看到的也是一片黑色的天空。所以，藍天和紅色的夕陽都來自同樣的陽光，只是穿過了多寡不同的空氣而已。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why the sky reflects the color of the ocean", "How scattered sunlight gives the sky its colors", "Why astronauts prefer the dark sky of the Moon", "How clouds protect Earth from strong sunlight"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文以「陽光被空氣分子散射」這個原理，說明白天的天空為什麼是藍色（第二、三段）、夕陽為什麼是紅色（第四段），以及雲為什麼是白色、月球上的天空為什麼是黑色（第五段）。(A) 第一段說「天空反映海洋」的猜測 cannot be right；(C) 第五段只提到太空人在月球上看到黑色的天空，並沒有說他們比較喜歡；(D) 文中沒有提到雲能保護地球。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happens when sunlight hits air molecules?", "options": ["Short waves like blue are scattered more than long waves.", "Every color is scattered in exactly the same way.", "Red light is scattered the most because of its long waves.", "The molecules take in all the light and turn it into heat."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Short waves are scattered far more than long ones; blue light is scattered several times more strongly than red. 波長短的藍光比波長長的光更容易被散射。(B) 不同顏色被散射的程度並不相同；(C) 紅光波長最長，被散射得最少，與文意相反；(D) 文中沒有提到分子把光變成熱。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why does the sun look red or orange at sunset?", "options": ["The sun itself becomes cooler in the evening.", "The clouds reflect the red color of the ground.", "Its light passes through more air and loses most of its blue.", "Air molecules begin to scatter red light more than blue."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：the sun sits low near the horizon, so its light must travel through much more air to reach us. On this long journey, most of the blue is scattered away. What remains is mainly red and orange light... 傍晚的陽光要穿過更多空氣，藍光大多被散射掉，剩下紅光和橙光。(A)(B) 文中都沒有提到；(D) 第四段說夕陽 follow the same rule，空氣分子仍然是散射較多的藍光，並不是改成散射紅光。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"horizon\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the highest point in the sky", "a thin layer of clouds", "the center of the sun", "the line where the land and sky seem to meet"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段 At noon, the sun is high overhead... In the evening, however, the sun sits low near the horizon，傍晚太陽低低地位於 horizon 附近，與中午高掛頭頂相對，可知 horizon 指「地平線」，也就是天空與地面看起來相接的那條線。(A)「天空的最高點」意思相反；(B)「一層薄雲」、(C)「太陽的中心」都不符合文意。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention deserts in paragraph 1?", "options": ["To show that deserts have the bluest skies on Earth", "To explain why it seldom rains in dry places", "To prove that a common guess about the sky is wrong", "To suggest that sunlight is stronger far from the sea"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：A common guess is that the sky reflects the ocean, but that cannot be right, because the sky is just as blue above deserts far from any sea. 作者提到遠離海洋的沙漠上空也一樣藍，是為了證明「天空反映海洋顏色」這個常見的猜測是錯的。(A) 文中只說 just as blue（一樣藍），並沒有說最藍；(B)(D) 文中沒有提到沙漠很少下雨，也沒有說遠離海洋的陽光比較強。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a273", "title": "Too Good to Throw Away: How Food Banks Work", "title_zh": "丟掉太可惜：食物銀行如何運作", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Society", "Food"], "passage": "When people hear the word \"bank,\" they usually think of money. A food bank, however, stores food. Every day, shops, farms and factories throw away food that is still safe to eat, while some families nearby cannot afford enough healthy meals. A food bank works like a bridge between these two problems. It collects good food before it reaches the trash can and passes it on to people who need it.\n\nWhere does this food come from? A supermarket may have bread left over at closing time, or cans with dented sides. A farm may harvest a surplus of vegetables, more than it can sell to markets. A factory may print the wrong information on a package, even though the food inside is perfectly fine. Instead of paying to throw these products away, many businesses donate them to a food bank for free.\n\nOnce the food arrives, volunteers get to work. They check every item carefully and throw away anything that is spoiled or unsafe. The rest is sorted into groups, such as rice, noodles, canned food and fresh fruit. Then volunteers pack it into boxes and deliver it to families, or pass it to local groups like community centers and temples.\n\nVolunteers also need to understand the dates printed on food. A \"best before\" date is about quality. After this date, cookies may be less crunchy, but the food is usually still safe if it has been stored correctly and the package is not damaged. A \"use by\" date is about safety. It appears on foods that go bad quickly, such as fresh meat and fish, and these should not be eaten after that date, even if they look and smell fine.\n\nStudents can help, too. A class can hold a food drive and collect packaged food that lasts a long time, like rice and canned beans. Just remember to check the dates first. Some food banks also welcome young volunteers on weekends, often with a parent or teacher. At home, you can read labels carefully before throwing food away. A food bank cannot end hunger by itself, but it shows that one person's waste can become another family's dinner.", "passage_zh": "人們聽到「銀行」這個詞，通常會想到錢。然而，食物銀行儲存的卻是食物。每天，商店、農場和工廠都會丟掉一些仍然可以安全食用的食物，而附近卻有些家庭負擔不起足夠的健康餐點。食物銀行就像一座橋，連接起這兩個問題。它在好好的食物被丟進垃圾桶之前把它們收集起來，再轉交給需要的人。\n\n這些食物是從哪裡來的呢？超市可能在打烊時還有剩下的麵包，或是側面凹陷的罐頭。農場可能收成了過剩的蔬菜，多到無法全部賣給市場。工廠可能在包裝上印錯了資訊，但裡面的食物其實完全沒有問題。與其花錢把這些產品丟掉，許多業者選擇把它們免費捐給食物銀行。\n\n食物送到之後，志工們就開始忙碌了。他們會仔細檢查每一樣物品，把任何腐壞或不安全的東西丟掉。其餘的食物則分門別類，例如米、麵條、罐頭食品和新鮮水果。接著，志工把食物裝箱送到各個家庭，或交給社區中心和寺廟等地方團體。\n\n志工也必須了解食品上印的日期。「最佳賞味日期」和品質有關。過了這個日期，餅乾可能沒那麼酥脆，但只要保存得當、包裝沒有破損，食物通常仍然可以安全食用。「食用期限」則和安全有關。它會出現在容易變質的食物上，例如生鮮肉品和魚類；過了這個日期就不應該再吃，即使看起來、聞起來都沒有問題。\n\n學生也可以出一份力。一個班級可以舉辦募集食物的活動，收集米和豆子罐頭等能保存很久的包裝食品。不過別忘了先檢查日期。有些食物銀行也歡迎年輕志工在週末來幫忙，通常會由家長或老師陪同。在家裡，你也可以在丟掉食物之前仔細閱讀標籤。食物銀行無法單靠自己終結飢餓，但它讓我們看到，一個人浪費掉的食物，也能變成另一個家庭的晚餐。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why supermarkets lower their prices at closing time", "How farmers can grow vegetables that look better", "Why food should never be eaten after its \"best before\" date", "How food banks pass safe extra food on to people in need"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文說明食物銀行的運作：第一段指出它 collects good food before it reaches the trash can and passes it on to people who need it；第二段說明食物來自商店、農場和工廠，第三段說明志工如何檢查、分類並送出食物，第四、五段再介紹日期標示和學生能幫忙的方式。(A) 文中沒有談到超市打烊時降價；(B) 第二段只提到農場有過剩的蔬菜，並未談如何種出更好看的蔬菜；(C) 第四段說過了 best before 日期的食物通常仍可安全食用，與此相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why might a factory give food to a food bank?", "options": ["The food inside the package has already gone bad.", "There is a mistake on the package, but the food is fine.", "The government pays factories to give their food away.", "The food bank buys the food from it at a lower price."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：A factory may print the wrong information on a package, even though the food inside is perfectly fine. 工廠可能是包裝印錯，但裡面的食物沒有問題。(A) 已腐壞的食物會被志工丟掉（見第三段），不是捐贈的原因；(C) 文中沒有提到政府付錢；(D) 文中說業者是 donate them to a food bank for free，食物銀行並沒有花錢購買。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what do volunteers do at a food bank?", "options": ["They check the food, sort it and help deliver it.", "They cook hot meals for families in a kitchen.", "They decide which families may receive food.", "They sell the extra food back to local shops."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：They check every item carefully... The rest is sorted into groups... Then volunteers pack it into boxes and deliver it to families. 志工會檢查食物、分類，並裝箱送到各個家庭。(B) 文中沒有提到志工煮飯；(C) 文中沒有說志工決定哪些家庭可以領取食物；(D) 食物是送給需要的家庭或地方團體，而不是賣回給商店。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"surplus\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a shortage", "an extra amount", "a small sample", "a sudden loss"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段 A farm may harvest a surplus of vegetables, more than it can sell to markets，後面的 more than it can sell 說明了 surplus 的意思，即「過剩、多餘的量」。(A) a shortage「短缺」意思相反；(C) a small sample「少量樣品」與 (D) a sudden loss「突然的損失」都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Based on paragraph 4, what would the writer most likely tell someone who finds a box of cookies one week past its \"best before\" date?", "options": ["Throw it away at once, because it is dangerous.", "Take it back to the store and ask for a refund.", "If the box is not damaged, it is probably still safe.", "Stop buying cookies, because they go bad very quickly."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說 best before 日期與品質有關，過了這個日期 the food is usually still safe if it has been stored correctly and the package is not damaged。由此可推知，作者會告訴對方：只要盒子沒有破損，這盒餅乾很可能仍可安全食用。(A) 與作者「通常仍然安全」的說法相反；(B) 文中沒有提到退貨退款；(D) 文中說容易變質的是生鮮肉品和魚類等食物，不是餅乾。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a274", "title": "Riding on Warm Air: How a Hot-Air Balloon Flies", "title_zh": "乘著熱空氣：熱氣球是怎麼飛上天的", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "Science"], "passage": "Imagine standing on a green hill in Taitung just after sunrise. Giant balloons in bright colors rise slowly over the tea fields, with no engines and no wings. How is this possible? The answer is a simple scientific idea that people have used to fly for more than two hundred years.\n\nWhen air is heated, its tiny particles move faster and spread apart. As a result, hot air is lighter than the cooler air around it. A hot-air balloon has a huge fabric bag called the envelope, a basket for passengers, and a burner between them. The burner sends a powerful flame up into the envelope. Once the air inside is hot enough, the whole balloon rises, like a bubble in water.\n\nThe pilot controls the balloon's height with the burner. More heat makes it climb. To come down, the pilot lets the air cool or opens a vent at the top to let some hot air escape. However, there is no way to steer a balloon directly. The wind decides where it goes. Because winds at different heights often blow in different directions, a skilled pilot moves up or down to catch a wind heading the right way.\n\nBalloon flight as we know it began in France with the Montgolfier brothers, whose family made paper. In June 1783, they sent up a balloon with no passengers in their hometown. That September, at the palace of Versailles, a balloon carried a sheep, a duck and a rooster into the air in front of the king, and all three animals landed safely. In November, two men flew over Paris for about 25 minutes, becoming the first people to fly freely in a balloon.\n\nToday, balloon flights usually take place early in the morning, when the winds are calm. Later in the day, the sun heats the ground, creating rising air and stronger, less predictable winds that make flying more dangerous. That is why visitors to the Taiwan International Balloon Festival, held every summer on Luye Highland in Taitung since 2011, often arrive before dawn. Many enjoy a tethered ride, in which the balloon is tied to the ground with ropes. So if you go, set your alarm early!", "passage_zh": "想像你在日出後不久，站在臺東一座翠綠的山丘上。一顆顆色彩鮮豔的巨大氣球在茶園上空緩緩升起，它們既沒有引擎，也沒有翅膀。這怎麼可能呢？答案是一個簡單的科學原理，人類運用它飛上天空已經有兩百多年了。\n\n空氣受熱時，其中微小的粒子會移動得更快、彼此分得更開。因此，熱空氣比周圍的冷空氣還要輕。熱氣球有一個稱為「球囊」的巨大布袋、一個載乘客的吊籃，以及位於兩者之間的燃燒器。燃燒器會把強大的火焰往上噴進球囊裡。一旦裡面的空氣夠熱，整顆氣球就會上升，就像水中的氣泡一樣。\n\n駕駛員用燃燒器來控制氣球的高度。加更多熱，氣球就會往上爬升。要下降時，駕駛員會讓空氣冷卻，或打開頂端的通氣口，讓一些熱空氣散出去。然而，氣球沒有辦法直接操控方向，它要飛往哪裡是由風決定的。由於不同高度的風常常吹往不同方向，經驗豐富的駕駛員會調整高低，去找一股吹往正確方向的風。\n\n我們今天所熟知的氣球飛行，始於法國的孟格菲兄弟，他們的家族以造紙為業。1783 年 6 月，他們在家鄉放飛了一顆沒有載人的氣球。同年 9 月，在凡爾賽宮，一顆氣球當著國王的面，載著一隻綿羊、一隻鴨子和一隻公雞升空，三隻動物都平安降落。到了 11 月，兩名男子在巴黎上空飛行了大約 25 分鐘，成為最早乘坐氣球自由飛行的人。\n\n如今，熱氣球通常在風勢平穩的清晨飛行。到了一天中稍晚的時候，太陽會把地面曬熱，產生上升的氣流，以及更強、更難預測的風，讓飛行變得更危險。這就是為什麼參加臺灣國際熱氣球嘉年華的遊客，常常天還沒亮就到場；這個活動自 2011 年起，每年夏天都在臺東鹿野高台舉辦。許多人會體驗繫留飛行，也就是用繩索把氣球固定在地面上。所以如果你要去，記得把鬧鐘設早一點！", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the Montgolfier family became rich by making paper", "How hot-air balloons fly, how they began and when they fly", "How students in Taiwan can train to become balloon pilots", "Why hot-air balloons are safer than modern airplanes"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先在第二、三段說明熱氣球的原理（熱空氣比周圍的冷空氣輕）與操控方式，第四段介紹孟格菲兄弟 1783 年的早期飛行，第五段說明熱氣球多在清晨飛行，並介紹鹿野高台的熱氣球嘉年華。(A) 第四段只提到兄弟的家族以造紙為業，沒有說他們靠造紙致富；(C) 文中沒有談如何受訓成為駕駛員；(D) 文中沒有拿熱氣球和飛機比較安全性。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, how does a pilot make the balloon go higher?", "options": ["By using the burner to heat the air inside the envelope", "By waiting for a strong wind to lift the basket", "By opening the vent at the top of the envelope", "By letting the air inside the envelope cool down"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：The pilot controls the balloon's height with the burner. More heat makes it climb. 駕駛員用燃燒器加熱球囊內的空氣，讓氣球上升。(B) 文中說風決定的是方向（The wind decides where it goes），不是高度；(C)(D) 打開頂端的通氣口、讓空氣冷卻，都是讓氣球「下降」的方法。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What happened at Versailles in September 1783?", "options": ["Two men flew over Paris for about 25 minutes.", "The brothers sent up a balloon with no passengers in their hometown.", "A balloon carried three animals into the sky before the king.", "A balloon was tied down with ropes so visitors could ride it."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：That September, at the palace of Versailles, a balloon carried a sheep, a duck and a rooster into the air in front of the king. 在凡爾賽宮，一顆氣球當著國王的面載著三隻動物升空。(A) 兩名男子飛越巴黎是 11 月的事；(B) 在家鄉放飛無人氣球是 6 月的事；(D) 繫留飛行是第五段所描述今日鹿野嘉年華的體驗。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"steer\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["control the direction of", "increase the speed of", "repair the parts of", "lower the weight of"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 there is no way to steer a balloon directly. The wind decides where it goes. 後句說明氣球要往哪裡去是由風決定的，可知 steer 是「操控方向」。(B) increase the speed of「加快速度」、(C) repair the parts of「修理零件」、(D) lower the weight of「減輕重量」都不符合上下文。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following can be inferred from the passage?", "options": ["Balloon pilots can usually fly in any direction they like.", "The animals sent up at Versailles were hurt when they landed.", "Most balloons at the Luye festival fly in the afternoon heat.", "A balloon flight at noon on a hot day would likely be less safe."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：Later in the day, the sun heats the ground, creating rising air and stronger, less predictable winds that make flying more dangerous. 可推知在炎熱的中午飛行，很可能比清晨更不安全。(A) 第三段說氣球無法直接操控方向，由風決定；(B) 第四段說 all three animals landed safely；(C) 第五段說飛行多在清晨，遊客常在天亮前抵達，而不是在午後。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a275", "title": "Not Now, Maybe Later: The 30-Day Rule for Spending", "title_zh": "現在先不買，以後再說：花錢的「30 天法則」", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Money", "Life"], "passage": "It is midnight, and you are scrolling through a shopping app. A cool jacket appears on your screen, and with a few taps, it is yours. When the package arrives a week later, you wonder why you bought it. This is impulse buying: buying something because of a sudden feeling instead of a plan. Luckily, there is a simple way to fight it, called the 30-day rule.\n\nThe rule works like this. When you want something you do not really need, write it down with its price and the date. Then wait 30 days. If you still want it after a month and can pay for it without borrowing money, go ahead and buy it. If not, cross it off your list. The rule is for wants, not for necessities such as food or medicine. It does not say \"never.\" It only says \"not yet.\"\n\nWhy is waiting so helpful? Shopping has never been easier. Many online stores save your card details, so paying takes seconds. Ads often seem to follow you from app to app. Messages like \"Only 3 left!\" or \"Sale ends tonight!\" make you feel that you must decide immediately. Waiting gives your careful, thinking mind time to catch up with your excited feelings.\n\nLin Wei-ting, a 17-year-old student, tried the rule last year. She saw a pair of white sneakers for NT$3,200 in a video and wanted them badly. Instead of buying them, she added them to her list. During the first week, she thought about the shoes every day. By the third week, she hardly thought about them, and she noticed that her old sneakers were still comfortable. When the 30 days were over, the shoes no longer seemed special at all.\n\nThe rule is not about never spending money. It is about spending on things that really matter. Wei-ting put the NT$3,200 into her savings, and she is now saving for a secondhand laptop for her design classes. Some items on your list will still feel important after a month, and you will enjoy them more because you chose them carefully. So the next time a \"Buy Now\" button tempts you, write it down and wait.", "passage_zh": "現在是半夜，你正拿著手機滑購物 App。一件很酷的外套出現在螢幕上，只要點幾下，它就是你的了。一週後包裹送到時，你卻納悶自己當初為什麼要買。這就是「衝動購物」：因為一時的感覺，而不是按照計畫去買東西。幸好，有個簡單的方法可以對抗它，叫做「30 天法則」。\n\n這個法則是這樣運作的：當你想要某樣自己並不真正需要的東西時，把它寫下來，連同價格和日期一起記下。然後等 30 天。如果一個月後你仍然想要它，而且不必借錢就付得起，那就放心去買吧。如果不是這樣，就把它從清單上劃掉。這個法則針對的是「想要」的東西，而不是食物或藥品這類生活必需品。它並不是說「永遠別買」，只是說「現在先別買」。\n\n為什麼等待這麼有用呢？購物從來沒有像現在這麼容易。許多網路商店會儲存你的信用卡資料，付款只要幾秒鐘。廣告似乎常常跟著你，從一個應用程式跑到另一個。「只剩 3 件！」或「特價今晚結束！」這類訊息，會讓你覺得必須立刻做決定。而等待能讓你冷靜思考的頭腦，有時間追上興奮的情緒。\n\n17 歲的學生林薇婷去年試過這個法則。她在一支影片中看到一雙售價新臺幣 3,200 元的白色球鞋，非常想要。但她沒有直接買下，而是把它加進清單裡。第一週，她每天都在想那雙鞋。到了第三週，她幾乎不再想起它，也發現自己的舊球鞋其實還很好穿。30 天到期時，那雙鞋看起來已經一點也不特別了。\n\n這個法則並不是要你永遠不花錢，而是要你把錢花在真正重要的東西上。薇婷把那 3,200 元存了起來，現在她正在存錢，想為設計課買一台二手筆電。清單上的某些東西在一個月後仍然會讓你覺得重要，而因為是你仔細挑選的，你會更享受它們。所以，下次當「立即購買」的按鈕在誘惑你時，先把它寫下來，然後等一等。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?", "options": ["To warn readers never to shop online again", "To explain how online stores protect card details", "To introduce a simple way to control impulse buying", "To compare the prices of sneakers in different stores"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段先描述衝動購物的情形，再提出 there is a simple way to fight it, called the 30-day rule；之後說明這個法則的做法、為什麼有效，並以薇婷的例子說明。作者的目的是介紹一個控制衝動購物的簡單方法。(A) 第二段說 It does not say \"never.\" It only says \"not yet.\"，作者並沒有要讀者再也不網購；(B) 第三段提到網路商店會儲存卡片資料，是為了說明付款太容易，不是說明如何保護資料；(D) 文中只提到一雙球鞋的價格，沒有比較不同商店。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what should you do after you write down something you want?", "options": ["Ask your friends whether it is worth the money", "Buy it at once before the price goes up", "Cross it off your list the following morning", "Wait a month before deciding whether to buy it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：write it down with its price and the date. Then wait 30 days. If you still want it after a month... go ahead and buy it. 寫下來之後要等 30 天（一個月）再決定要不要買。(A) 文中沒有提到詢問朋友；(B) 與「等 30 天」的做法相反；(C) 只有在一個月後不再想要時才把它劃掉，而不是隔天早上。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why do messages like \"Sale ends tonight!\" work on shoppers?", "options": ["They show shoppers the real cost of a product.", "They make shoppers feel they must decide right away.", "They help shoppers compare products more carefully.", "They give shoppers extra time to think about prices."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Messages like \"Only 3 left!\" or \"Sale ends tonight!\" make you feel that you must decide immediately. 這類訊息讓人覺得必須立刻做決定。(A) 文中沒有說這些訊息會顯示真正的成本；(C)(D) 這些訊息是要讓人急著決定，與「更仔細比較」、「多給時間思考」正好相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"necessities\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["things you buy just for fun", "things that are on sale today", "things that cost a lot of money", "things you really need in daily life"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段 The rule is for wants, not for necessities such as food or medicine. necessities 與 wants（想要的東西）相對，例子是食物和藥品，可知是「生活必需品」。(A) 為了好玩而買的東西屬於 wants；(B)「今天特價的東西」與 (C)「很貴的東西」都不符合文中的例子。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Wei-ting?", "options": ["Her strong wish for the sneakers faded over time.", "She bought the sneakers as soon as the month ended.", "She felt the 30-day rule was a waste of her time.", "Her old sneakers were too worn out to wear anymore."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：During the first week, she thought about the shoes every day. By the third week, she hardly thought about them... the shoes no longer seemed special at all. 可推知她對球鞋的強烈渴望隨著時間逐漸消退。(B) 第五段說她把 3,200 元存了起來，並沒有買鞋；(C) 她靠這個法則省下錢，並開始存錢買筆電，看不出她認為這個法則浪費時間；(D) 第四段說 her old sneakers were still comfortable。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a276", "title": "Dancing Directions: The Secret Language of Honeybees", "title_zh": "用舞步指路：蜜蜂的祕密語言", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Nature", "Science"], "passage": "A honeybee has just found a field of flowers full of sweet nectar. The field is far from her hive, and she cannot draw a map or speak. Yet soon after she returns home, many of her sisters find their way to the same flowers. How does she tell them where to go? She performs a special \"waggle dance.\"\n\nThe dance takes place on the honeycomb inside the dark hive. The dancer runs forward in a straight line while shaking her body from side to side. This is the \"waggle.\" Then she circles back, turning right one time and left the next, and repeats the run, so her path looks like the number 8. Other bees crowd around, touching her with their antennae because it is too dark to see. They also pick up the scent of the flowers on her body.\n\nThe direction of the straight run tells the bees which way to fly. On the honeycomb, which hangs straight up and down, \"straight up\" stands for the direction of the sun. If the dancer waggles straight up, the flowers are toward the sun. If she waggles straight down, they are in the opposite direction. If she waggles 40 degrees to the right of straight up, the food lies 40 degrees to the right of the sun. As the sun moves across the sky, dancers slowly change their angle to keep the directions correct.\n\nThe length of the waggle tells the bees how far to fly. The longer the dancer waggles during each run, the farther away the food is. A short waggle means a short trip. When food is very near the hive, bees often perform a simpler round dance instead.\n\nMuch of what we know about this dance comes from the Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch, who studied bees for decades. He set out dishes of sugar water, marked visiting bees with dots of paint, and watched them dance inside glass-walled hives. At first, some scientists doubted that an insect could share such detailed information, but later studies supported his main ideas. In 1973, von Frisch shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with two other scientists who studied animal behavior.", "passage_zh": "一隻蜜蜂剛發現一片開滿花朵、充滿甜美花蜜的田野。這片田野離牠的蜂巢很遠，而牠既不會畫地圖，也不會說話。然而，牠回到家後不久，許多姊妹就找到了同一片花叢。牠是怎麼告訴同伴該往哪裡去的呢？答案是：牠會跳一種特別的「搖擺舞」。\n\n這支舞是在黑暗的蜂巢內、蜂巢片上進行的。跳舞的蜜蜂會一邊沿直線往前跑，一邊左右搖擺身體，這就是「搖擺」。接著牠會繞回起點，這一次向右轉、下一次向左轉，再重複這段直線奔跑，所以牠走的路線看起來就像數字 8。其他蜜蜂會圍在牠身邊；由於蜂巢裡太暗看不見，牠們會用觸角碰觸牠。牠們也會從牠身上聞到花朵的氣味。\n\n直線奔跑的方向會告訴其他蜜蜂該往哪裡飛。在垂直懸掛的蜂巢片上，「正上方」代表太陽的方向。如果跳舞的蜜蜂朝正上方搖擺，花朵就在朝向太陽的方向；如果牠朝正下方搖擺，花朵就在相反的方向。如果牠朝正上方偏右 40 度的方向搖擺，食物就位在太陽右方 40 度的地方。隨著太陽在天空中移動，跳舞的蜜蜂會慢慢調整角度，讓方向保持正確。\n\n搖擺的長短則告訴蜜蜂要飛多遠。跳舞的蜜蜂在每一段直線奔跑中搖擺得越久，食物就越遠。短暫的搖擺就表示路程很短。當食物離蜂巢非常近時，蜜蜂常常改跳一種較簡單的「圓舞」。\n\n我們對這種舞蹈的了解，大多來自奧地利科學家卡爾‧馮‧弗里希，他研究蜜蜂長達數十年。他擺出一盤盤糖水，在前來的蜜蜂身上點上油漆作記號，並隔著玻璃壁的觀察蜂箱，看牠們在裡面跳舞。起初，有些科學家懷疑一種昆蟲是否真能傳遞如此詳細的資訊，但後來的研究支持了他的主要觀點。1973 年，馮‧弗里希與另外兩位研究動物行為的科學家共同獲得諾貝爾生理學或醫學獎。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the best title for the passage?", "options": ["How Bees Use Dance to Give Directions", "Why Bees Prefer Flowers Near the Hive", "The Life Story of an Austrian Scientist", "How Honey Is Made Inside the Hive"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文說明蜜蜂如何用搖擺舞告訴同伴花朵的位置：第二段描述舞步，第三段說明直線奔跑的方向代表飛行方向，第四段說明搖擺的長短代表距離，第五段介紹研究這種舞蹈的馮‧弗里希。(B) 第四段只提到食物很近時蜜蜂會改跳圓舞，並未說蜜蜂偏好近處的花；(C) 第五段只介紹馮‧弗里希的研究與得獎，不是他的生平故事，範圍也太窄；(D) 文中沒有說明蜂蜜如何製造。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what does it mean when a bee waggles straight down?", "options": ["The flowers are very close to the hive.", "The flowers are in the same direction as the sun.", "The flowers are in the opposite direction from the sun.", "The flowers no longer have much nectar left in them."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：\"straight up\" stands for the direction of the sun... If she waggles straight down, they are in the opposite direction. 朝正下方搖擺，表示花朵在與太陽相反的方向。(A) 食物很近時蜜蜂常改跳圓舞，見第四段；(B) 朝正上方搖擺才表示花朵在朝向太陽的方向；(D) 文中沒有提到舞蹈能顯示花蜜還剩多少。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, what does the length of the waggle tell other bees?", "options": ["How many flowers there are in the field", "What color the flowers in the field are", "How much nectar the dancer has collected", "How far they need to fly to find the food"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The length of the waggle tells the bees how far to fly. The longer the dancer waggles during each run, the farther away the food is. 搖擺的長短代表食物有多遠。(A) 花的數量、(B) 花的顏色、(C) 跳舞的蜜蜂採了多少花蜜，文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"scent\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["color", "sound", "smell", "shape"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段 They also pick up the scent of the flowers on her body. 前一句說蜂巢裡太暗看不見，其他蜜蜂是靠觸角碰觸跳舞的蜜蜂，並從牠身上感受到花的 scent，可知 scent 是「氣味」。(A) color「顏色」與 (D) shape「形狀」都要用眼睛看，不符合黑暗中的情境；(B) sound「聲音」也不會留在蜜蜂身上。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the waggle dance from the passage?", "options": ["It looks exactly the same at every hour of the day.", "Its directions would be wrong if dancers ignored the sun's movement.", "It can be understood only by bees that see the dancer clearly.", "It is used mainly to warn other bees about enemies nearby."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：As the sun moves across the sky, dancers slowly change their angle to keep the directions correct. 跳舞的蜜蜂必須隨著太陽移動調整角度，方向才會正確；可推知若不考慮太陽的移動，舞蹈所指的方向就會出錯。(A) 角度會隨時間改變，並非每個時刻都一樣；(C) 第二段說蜂巢內太暗看不見，其他蜜蜂是用觸角碰觸跳舞的蜜蜂；(D) 文中說舞蹈是用來告訴同伴食物的位置，沒有提到警告敵人。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a277", "title": "The Waiter on Wheels: Robots in the Dining Room", "title_zh": "裝著輪子的服務生：餐廳裡的送餐機器人", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Career"], "passage": "At a busy hot pot restaurant, a short machine rolls up to your table carrying trays of meat and vegetables. A cartoon face smiles on its screen, and a cheerful voice says, \"Your food is here!\" When you have taken your plates and touched the screen, the robot heads back to the kitchen. Delivery robots like this have become a familiar sight in some restaurants in Taiwan and elsewhere.\n\nThese robots are good at certain jobs. Using cameras and sensors, they find their way around the dining room and stop when something is in front of them. They can carry heavy trays without getting tired and follow the same routes all day long. They never forget a table number. For owners who have trouble hiring enough workers, this help is very welcome.\n\nHowever, the robots also have clear limits. Most cannot climb stairs, so they usually work on only one floor. In a crowded room, they may simply stop and wait when a child runs in front of them. More importantly, robots cannot solve unexpected problems. If someone spills a drink or is upset about a wrong order, a robot has no idea what to do. And no robot can notice that an old man eating alone looks lonely and stop to chat with him.\n\nBecause of these limits, the robots have not replaced servers. Instead, they have changed what servers do. Wang Mei-ling, a 26-year-old server at a family restaurant, has worked with two robots for a year. \"Before, I spent most of my time running back and forth with heavy trays,\" she says. \"Now the robots do the carrying, and I finally have time to talk with customers.\" She explains the menu, helps parents with small children and handles complaints.\n\nMei-ling's experience suggests how new machines can change work. Machines take over tasks that are tiring and repetitive, while people focus on the parts that need judgment and warmth. \"The robot brings the food,\" Mei-ling says with a smile, \"but I'm the one who makes people want to come back.\" Workers who learn to work with machines, instead of fearing them, may find their jobs become more interesting, not less.", "passage_zh": "在一家生意興隆的火鍋店裡，一台矮矮的機器載著一盤盤的肉和蔬菜，緩緩來到你的桌邊。它的螢幕上有一張微笑的卡通臉，還用開朗的聲音說：「您的餐點到了！」等你拿走餐盤、按一下螢幕之後，機器人就會回到廚房去。像這樣的送餐機器人，在台灣和其他地方的一些餐廳裡，已經成為常見的景象。\n\n這些機器人很擅長某些工作。它們利用攝影機和感測器在用餐區裡找路，前方有東西時就會停下來。它們能載著沉重的托盤而不會累，還能整天沿著同樣的路線來回行走。它們永遠不會記錯桌號。對於請不到足夠人手的老闆來說，這樣的幫手非常受歡迎。\n\n然而，這些機器人也有明顯的限制。大多數機器人不會爬樓梯，所以通常只在一個樓層工作。在擁擠的店裡，如果有小孩跑到它們面前，它們可能就只會停下來等待。更重要的是，機器人無法解決意料之外的問題。如果有人打翻飲料，或因為餐點送錯而不高興，機器人根本不知道該怎麼辦。而且，沒有任何機器人能察覺一位獨自用餐的老先生看起來很寂寞，並停下來陪他聊聊天。\n\n由於這些限制，機器人並沒有取代服務生，而是改變了服務生的工作內容。二十六歲的王美玲在一家家庭餐廳當服務生，已經和兩台機器人共事一年了。她說：「以前，我大部分的時間都端著沉重的托盤跑來跑去。現在搬運的工作交給機器人，我終於有時間和客人聊天了。」她會為客人介紹菜單、協助帶著小孩的父母，並處理客人的抱怨。\n\n美玲的經驗說明了新機器可能如何改變工作：機器接手那些累人又重複的工作，人則專心處理需要判斷力與溫暖的部分。美玲笑著說：「機器人負責送餐，但讓客人想再回來的人是我。」願意學著與機器合作、而不是害怕機器的工作者，可能會發現自己的工作變得更有趣，而不是更乏味。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Delivery robots will soon take the place of all restaurant servers.", "Delivery robots carry food but change what servers do rather than replace them.", "Restaurants should stop using robots because they make too many mistakes.", "Robots are friendlier and more patient than human servers."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先介紹送餐機器人擅長的事（第二段）與做不到的事（第三段），再於第四段指出 the robots have not replaced servers. Instead, they have changed what servers do，最後說明機器負責重複的工作、人專注於需要判斷力與溫暖的部分。(A) 與第四段「機器人並沒有取代服務生」相反；(C) 文中沒有主張餐廳應停用機器人，也沒說它們常出錯；(D) 第三段說機器人無法察覺客人的寂寞，溫暖與關懷正是人比機器強的地方。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what is one thing delivery robots do well?", "options": ["They can make the same trips many times without growing tired.", "They can go up and down stairs to reach every floor.", "They can tell which customers need extra attention.", "They can take orders and answer questions about the menu."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：They can carry heavy trays without getting tired and follow the same routes all day long. 機器人能整天重複走同樣的路線而不會累，(A) 是改寫說法。(B) 第三段說 Most cannot climb stairs，大多數不會爬樓梯，通常只在一個樓層工作；(C) 第三段說機器人無法察覺客人的需要，例如寂寞的老先生；(D) 第四段說介紹菜單是服務生美玲的工作，不是機器人的。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what may a robot do when a child runs in front of it?", "options": ["Push the child gently out of its way", "Stay where it is for a while", "Call a server over to help", "Return to the kitchen right away"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：In a crowded room, they may simply stop and wait when a child runs in front of them. 小孩跑到機器人前面時，它可能只會停下來等待，也就是 (B)「暫時待在原地」。(A) 把人推開、(C) 呼叫服務生、(D) 立刻回廚房，文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"repetitive\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["difficult and dangerous", "new and exciting", "done again and again in the same way", "shared by many different people"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段 Machines take over tasks that are tiring and repetitive，對照第二段機器人能 follow the same routes all day long，可知 repetitive 指「重複的、一再做同樣事情的」。(A)「困難又危險」、(B)「新奇又刺激」、(D)「由許多人共享」都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does Mei-ling mean when she says, \"I'm the one who makes people want to come back\"?", "options": ["She cooks the dishes herself, so they taste better.", "She can bring food to the tables faster than the robots.", "She hopes the restaurant will stop using robots soon.", "Her personal care for customers is what makes them return."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說美玲現在有時間 talk with customers，會介紹菜單、協助父母、處理抱怨；第五段指出人負責 the parts that need judgment and warmth。她的意思是：機器人只負責送餐，真正讓客人願意再上門的是她對客人的關心與服務。(A) 文中沒說她負責烹飪；(B) 送餐是機器人的工作；(C) 她對機器人並無不滿，反而說機器人讓她終於有時間和客人聊天。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a278", "title": "The Question I Was Afraid to Ask", "title_zh": "那個我不敢問的問題", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "Life"], "passage": "For most of my first year of senior high school, I had a secret: I did not understand math. When Mr. Huang wrote long problems on the board, I nodded and copied everything down, but the numbers meant nothing to me. Whenever he asked, \"Any questions?\" the room went silent. I had plenty of questions, but I kept my mouth shut. I was afraid my classmates would think I was stupid.\n\nStaying quiet felt safe, but it was not. Math is like building a house: each new lesson is built on the one before it. Because I never understood functions, I could not follow the lessons on graphs. My homework took hours, and I often copied answers I did not understand. On the midterm exam, I got 38 out of 100, my lowest score ever. That night, I hid the paper and cried.\n\nA week later, I waited after class until everyone had left and walked slowly to Mr. Huang's desk. My heart was beating fast. \"I'm sorry,\" I said, \"but I've been lost since chapter two.\" I expected him to be disappointed. Instead, he smiled and pulled up a chair. \"Thank you for telling me,\" he said. \"Let's start where you got lost.\" Using simple drawings, he explained functions again, and for the first time, they made sense.\n\nThen something surprising happened. Two classmates, Yi-chen and Bo-wei, walked in. They had been standing outside the door, too nervous to come in, and they had exactly the same question. Mr. Huang laughed. \"When one student asks, five more are usually wondering the same thing.\" That afternoon, the three of us agreed to meet in the library twice a week to study together. Soon, four more classmates joined us.\n\nOur study group is not perfect. Sometimes we get stuck, and sometimes we chat too much. But we are no longer afraid to say, \"I don't get it.\" We explain things to each other, and when we are all confused, we take our question to Mr. Huang. I got 76 on the final exam, but my biggest lesson is not in any textbook: asking for help is not a sign of weakness. It is the first step toward getting better.", "passage_zh": "高一的大部分時間裡，我都藏著一個祕密：我聽不懂數學。黃老師在黑板上寫下長長的題目時，我總是一邊點頭，一邊把所有內容抄下來，但那些數字對我來說毫無意義。每次他問：「有沒有問題？」教室裡就一片安靜。我其實有一大堆問題，卻始終閉口不說。我怕同學會覺得我很笨。\n\n保持沉默感覺很安全，其實不然。學數學就像蓋房子：每一課都建立在前一課的基礎上。因為我一直沒弄懂函數，所以也跟不上圖形的單元。我的作業要花好幾個小時才寫得完，而且常常抄下自己根本看不懂的答案。期中考我只考了 38 分（滿分 100 分），是我有史以來最低的分數。那天晚上，我把考卷藏起來，哭了一場。\n\n一個星期後，我在下課後等到所有人都離開了，才慢慢走到黃老師的桌前。我的心跳得好快。「對不起，」我說，「從第二章開始我就跟不上了。」我以為他會很失望，沒想到他卻笑了笑，拉了一張椅子過來。「謝謝你告訴我，」他說，「我們就從你跟不上的地方開始吧。」他用簡單的圖畫重新講解函數，那是我第一次真正弄懂它。\n\n接著，令人意外的事發生了。兩位同學——怡辰和柏維——走了進來。原來他們一直站在門外，緊張得不敢進來，而他們的問題跟我一模一樣。黃老師笑著說：「只要有一個學生發問，通常就還有五個人在想同樣的問題。」那天下午，我們三個人約好每週在圖書館碰面兩次，一起讀書。不久之後，又有四位同學加入了我們。\n\n我們的讀書小組並不完美。有時候我們會卡住，有時候又聊得太多。但我們不再害怕說「我不懂」。我們會互相講解，當大家都搞不懂時，就把問題拿去問黃老師。期末考我考了 76 分，但我學到最重要的一課並不在任何課本裡：尋求幫助不是軟弱的表現，而是讓自己進步的第一步。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main message of the story?", "options": ["Asking for help takes courage but leads to improvement.", "Math is the hardest subject for most high school students.", "Students usually get better grades when they study alone.", "Teachers should make their exams easier to pass."], "answer": 0, "explain": "作者因為不敢發問而愈來愈跟不上（第一、二段），鼓起勇氣在下課後問老師之後終於弄懂（第三段），還和同學組成讀書小組，最後一段點出主旨：asking for help is not a sign of weakness. It is the first step toward getting better. (B) 文中只談作者自己的數學困難，沒說數學對多數學生最難；(C) 作者正是靠讀書小組一起學習而進步，與「獨自讀書」相反；(D) 文中沒有要求老師把考試出得簡單一點。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did math become harder and harder for the writer?", "options": ["The writer often missed math class because of illness.", "Mr. Huang taught the new lessons much too quickly.", "New lessons depended on earlier ones the writer did not understand.", "The writer spent too much time chatting with classmates."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Math is like building a house: each new lesson is built on the one before it. Because I never understood functions, I could not follow the lessons on graphs. 新的單元建立在舊的單元上，作者沒弄懂函數，自然也跟不上後面的圖形單元。(A) 文中沒提到作者生病缺課；(B) 沒有說老師教得太快；(D) 第五段提到讀書小組有時聊太多，但那是作者開始進步之後的事，不是數學變難的原因。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why were Yi-chen and Bo-wei standing outside the classroom door?", "options": ["They had the same problem but felt too shy to go in.", "They were waiting to walk home with the writer.", "They wanted to hear what Mr. Huang thought of the writer.", "They had left their books in the classroom by mistake."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：They had been standing outside the door, too nervous to come in, and they had exactly the same question. 他們和作者有一樣的問題，只是太緊張而不敢進教室。(B) 等作者一起回家、(C) 想偷聽老師對作者的看法、(D) 把書忘在教室，文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"stuck\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["bored and with nothing to do", "tired after a long school day", "angry with one another", "unable to make any progress"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段 Sometimes we get stuck... when we are all confused, we take our question to Mr. Huang，讀書時遇到大家都解不開的題目就會「卡住」，只好去問老師，所以 stuck 指「無法繼續進展」。(A)「無聊、無事可做」、(B)「上了一整天課很累」、(C)「彼此生氣」都不符合上下文。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Mr. Huang?", "options": ["He was angry that the writer had failed the midterm.", "He welcomed students' questions instead of being annoyed.", "He thought the study group was a waste of time.", "He usually refused to help students after class."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段作者以為老師會失望，結果 he smiled and pulled up a chair，還說 Thank you for telling me；第四段他笑著說一個人發問時，通常還有五個人有同樣的疑問。可見他很歡迎學生發問。(A) 他並沒有生氣，反而微笑；(C) 文中沒有提到他反對讀書小組，學生遇到難題時仍會一起去問他；(D) 他在下課後主動拉椅子坐下來替作者講解，與「拒絕幫忙」相反。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a279", "title": "Why 42.195? The Royal Story Behind the Marathon", "title_zh": "為什麼是 42.195？馬拉松距離背後的王室故事", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "History"], "passage": "Every year, thousands of runners in cities such as Taipei, Tokyo and Boston line up for a marathon. All of them know the distance: 42.195 kilometers. But why such a strange number? Why not simply 40 or 42 kilometers? The answer takes us from an ancient Greek legend to the British royal family.\n\nThe story begins in 490 BC, when Greek soldiers defeated a Persian army near a town called Marathon. According to a famous legend, a Greek messenger ran all the way from Marathon to Athens, about 40 kilometers, to bring the good news. When he arrived, he shouted that the Greeks had won, and then he fell to the ground and died. Historians are not sure whether this run really happened, but the story has been told for centuries.\n\nWhen the first modern Olympic Games were held in Athens in 1896, the organizers decided to honor this legend with a race from Marathon to the Olympic stadium. The course was about 40 kilometers long. A Greek runner won, and the crowd went wild with joy. The race soon spread to other countries, but it still had no fixed length. Each marathon was simply \"about\" as long as the messenger's run.\n\nThe famous number came from the 1908 Olympics in London. The organizers planned a course that would start at Windsor Castle, a home of the British royal family, and finish inside the Olympic stadium in front of the royal box, where members of the royal family sat. Measured from start to finish, the course came to 26 miles and 385 yards, or 42.195 kilometers. In other words, the exact distance was decided by where the start and the finish had to be, not by the old Greek legend.\n\nEven after 1908, marathon distances still changed for several years. Then, in 1921, the international organization in charge of athletics made 42.195 kilometers the official distance, and it has stayed that way ever since. So the next time you watch runners struggling through the last two kilometers of a marathon, remember that they are running a little extra, partly because of a royal plan made more than a hundred years ago.", "passage_zh": "每年，在台北、東京、波士頓等城市，都有成千上萬的跑者站上馬拉松的起跑線。他們都知道這項比賽的距離：42.195 公里。但為什麼會是這麼奇怪的數字？為什麼不乾脆是 40 或 42 公里呢？答案要從一則古希臘傳說，一路說到英國的王室。\n\n故事從西元前 490 年開始，當時希臘士兵在一個名叫馬拉松的城鎮附近擊敗了波斯軍隊。根據一則著名的傳說，一位希臘信差從馬拉松一路跑到雅典，距離約 40 公里，為的是傳遞這個好消息。他一抵達就大喊希臘人打贏了，接著便倒在地上死去。歷史學家並不確定這段長跑是否真的發生過，但這個故事已經流傳了好幾個世紀。\n\n1896 年，第一屆現代奧運在雅典舉行，主辦單位決定舉辦一場從馬拉松跑到奧運體育場的賽跑，來紀念這則傳說。賽道全長約 40 公里。最後由一位希臘選手奪冠，現場觀眾欣喜若狂。這項比賽很快就傳到其他國家，但當時仍然沒有固定的長度，每場馬拉松都只是「大約」和那位信差跑的距離一樣長。\n\n這個著名的數字來自 1908 年的倫敦奧運。主辦單位規劃了一條賽道：起點設在英國王室的居所之一溫莎城堡，終點則設在奧運體育場內的皇家包廂前方，也就是王室成員觀賽的位置。從起點量到終點，全長共計 26 英里 385 碼，也就是 42.195 公里。換句話說，這個精確的距離是由起點和終點必須設在哪裡來決定的，而不是由那則古希臘傳說決定的。\n\n即使在 1908 年之後，馬拉松的距離仍然變動了好幾年。直到 1921 年，負責管理國際田徑運動的組織才將 42.195 公里定為正式距離，從此一直沿用至今。所以，下次你看到跑者在馬拉松的最後兩公里苦苦奮戰時，別忘了他們多跑的那一小段路，有一部分要歸因於一百多年前一項與王室有關的計畫。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following is the best title for the passage?", "options": ["How to Get Ready for Your First Marathon", "The Greek Messenger Who Saved Athens", "Famous Winners of the Olympic Marathon", "How the Marathon Got Its Exact Distance"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段提出問題 But why such a strange number?，接著依序說明古希臘傳說（第二段）、1896 年第一場奧運馬拉松約 40 公里（第三段）、1908 年倫敦奧運因起終點位置而定出 42.195 公里（第四段），以及 1921 年成為正式距離（第五段），全文都在解釋馬拉松距離的由來。(A) 文中沒有談如何準備比賽；(B) 信差傳說只是第二段的內容，範圍太窄；(C) 文中只提到「一位希臘選手」奪冠，並沒有介紹歷屆冠軍。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what was true of marathon races in the years after 1896?", "options": ["They were held only in the city of Athens.", "They did not all have the same length.", "Only Greek runners were allowed to enter them.", "They always followed the messenger's exact route."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The race soon spread to other countries, but it still had no fixed length. 馬拉松很快傳到其他國家，但還沒有固定的長度，也就是各場比賽的距離不一樣。(A) 比賽已傳到其他國家，不只在雅典舉行；(C) 文中只說由希臘選手奪冠，沒說只有希臘人能參加；(D) 各地比賽只是「大約」和信差跑的距離一樣長，並非都沿著他的路線。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why was the marathon at the 1908 London Olympics 42.195 kilometers long?", "options": ["The runners asked for a longer and harder race.", "The organizers wanted to copy the messenger's route.", "A new rule said every race had to be over 42 kilometers.", "The start and finish were set at places linked to the royal family."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The organizers planned a course that would start at Windsor Castle... and finish... in front of the royal box... the exact distance was decided by where the start and the finish had to be. 起點在王室居所溫莎城堡、終點在皇家包廂前，量出來的距離就是 42.195 公里。(A) 文中沒提到跑者要求加長；(B) 第四段明確說距離 not by the old Greek legend 而定；(C) 第五段說正式距離到 1921 年才訂定，1908 年時並沒有這樣的規定。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"honor\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["show respect for", "forget about", "change completely", "argue against"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 the organizers decided to honor this legend with a race from Marathon to the Olympic stadium，主辦單位特地沿著傳說中信差跑過的路線舉辦賽跑，是為了「紀念、向這則傳說致敬」，所以 honor 意為 show respect for。(B)「忘記」、(C)「徹底改變」、(D)「反對」都與文意不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward the story of the Greek messenger?", "options": ["The writer believes every detail of it is true.", "The writer thinks it should no longer be told.", "The writer sees it as a famous tale that may not be true.", "The writer thinks it explains the exact marathon distance."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段作者用 According to a famous legend 來介紹這個故事，並說 Historians are not sure whether this run really happened，可見作者把它當成一則有名、但未必真實的傳說。(A) 作者明白表示這件事不一定發生過；(B) 作者並沒有反對繼續流傳這個故事；(D) 第四段說精確距離是由起點和終點的位置決定，not by the old Greek legend。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a280", "title": "Tiny Bumps, Big Feelings: Why We Get Goosebumps", "title_zh": "小小疙瘩，大大感受：我們為什麼會起雞皮疙瘩", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Health"], "passage": "Step out of a warm room into a cold winter wind, and you may notice something strange on your arms. Small bumps appear all over your skin, and the tiny hairs there stand straight up. In English, these are called \"goosebumps,\" because the skin looks like that of a goose whose feathers have been pulled out. Chinese speakers use a different bird and compare the bumps to chicken skin. Either way, the bumps tell an interesting story.\n\nGoosebumps are caused by tiny muscles. At the base of each body hair, there is a muscle almost too small to see. When you feel cold or frightened, your nervous system sends a quick signal to these muscles without you even thinking about it. The muscles tighten and pull the hairs upright, pushing up the skin around each hair into a little bump. This happens automatically, so most people cannot control it.\n\nFor animals with thick fur, this reaction is very useful. When a furry animal is cold, its raised hairs trap a layer of air close to the skin. This warm air works like a blanket, holding in body heat. The reaction helps in times of danger, too. A frightened cat raises the fur on its back and tail, making itself look much bigger than it really is. An enemy may think twice before attacking it.\n\nHumans, however, have lost most of their thick body hair. Our short, thin hairs cannot trap much warm air, and raising them does not make us look any more frightening. For this reason, scientists believe goosebumps are a leftover from our hairy ancestors. The reaction has stayed with us, even though it no longer does us much good.\n\nInterestingly, cold and fear are not the only causes. Many people get goosebumps when they hear a moving piece of music, watch a touching movie scene, or listen to a powerful speech. Scientists are still studying why, but strong feelings seem to set off the same system that reacts to cold and danger. Not everyone has this experience, and a song that moves one person may leave another unmoved. So the next time music gives you goosebumps, thank a tiny muscle and a very old reaction.", "passage_zh": "從溫暖的房間走進冬天的寒風中，你可能會注意到手臂上出現奇怪的變化。皮膚上冒出許多小疙瘩，上面細小的汗毛也直直地豎了起來。在英文裡，這種現象叫做 goosebumps（鵝皮疙瘩），因為這時的皮膚看起來就像被拔光羽毛的鵝皮。說中文的人用的是另一種鳥，把這些小疙瘩比作雞皮，稱為「雞皮疙瘩」。不論是哪一種說法，這些小疙瘩都訴說著一個有趣的故事。\n\n雞皮疙瘩是由微小的肌肉造成的。每一根體毛的根部，都有一條小到幾乎看不見的肌肉。當你覺得冷或害怕時，你根本不必多想，神經系統就會迅速傳送訊號給這些肌肉。這些肌肉一收縮，就會把毛髮拉得直立起來，同時把每根毛髮周圍的皮膚往上推，形成一個小疙瘩。這一切都是自動發生的，所以大多數人無法控制它。\n\n對於毛皮濃密的動物來說，這種反應非常有用。毛茸茸的動物覺得冷時，豎起來的毛會在皮膚旁困住一層空氣。這層溫暖的空氣就像一條毯子，能把體溫留住。這種反應在遇到危險時也有幫助。受到驚嚇的貓會豎起背上和尾巴的毛，讓自己看起來比實際上大得多。敵人在攻擊牠之前，可能就會三思。\n\n然而，人類已經失去了大部分濃密的體毛。我們又短又細的汗毛無法困住多少溫暖的空氣，把它們豎起來也不會讓我們看起來更嚇人。因此，科學家認為雞皮疙瘩是我們毛髮濃密的祖先所留下來的反應。雖然它對我們已經沒有多大幫助，這種反應卻一直保留了下來。\n\n有趣的是，寒冷和恐懼並不是唯一的原因。許多人在聽到一首動人的音樂、看到電影中感人的一幕，或是聽到一場激勵人心的演說時，也會起雞皮疙瘩。科學家仍在研究其中的原因，不過強烈的情緒似乎會啟動身體裡那套對寒冷和危險起反應的系統。並不是每個人都有這種經驗，一首讓某人深受感動的歌，可能讓另一個人毫無感覺。所以，下次音樂讓你起雞皮疙瘩時，不妨感謝一下那條小小的肌肉，以及這個非常古老的反應。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How people can keep warm on cold winter days", "Why cats raise their fur when they meet enemies", "What causes goosebumps and why humans still get them", "Why some kinds of music make people feel afraid"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文說明雞皮疙瘩如何形成（第二段）、對毛皮濃密的動物有什麼用處（第三段）、對人類而言是祖先留下來的反應（第四段），以及強烈的情緒也會引起雞皮疙瘩（第五段）。(A) 文中沒有教人如何保暖；(B) 貓豎起毛只是第三段的一個例子；(D) 第五段說的是動人的音樂會引起雞皮疙瘩，並不是讓人害怕。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happens when we get goosebumps?", "options": ["Our body hairs quickly grow longer and thicker.", "Our skin becomes thinner so that heat can escape.", "We choose to tighten the muscles in our arms.", "Tiny muscles pull our body hairs into an upright position."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：The muscles tighten and pull the hairs upright, pushing up the skin around each hair into a little bump. 毛髮根部微小的肌肉收縮，把毛髮拉得直立起來。(A)(B) 文中都沒有提到；(C) 第二段說神經系統送出訊號是 without you even thinking about it，而且 This happens automatically，並不是我們自己選擇收縮肌肉。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How does raised fur help a furry animal in cold weather?", "options": ["It helps the animal run faster to find a warm place.", "It keeps rain and snow from touching the animal's skin.", "It holds a layer of warm air close to the animal's skin.", "It lets cold air reach the skin and cool the body down."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：its raised hairs trap a layer of air close to the skin. This warm air works like a blanket... 豎起來的毛會在皮膚旁困住一層溫暖的空氣，像毯子一樣幫助動物保住體溫。(A)(B) 文中都沒有提到；(D) 與文意相反，豎起的毛是幫助動物保住體溫，而不是讓身體變冷。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"ancestors\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["animals that live in very cold places", "family members who lived long before us", "scientists who study the human body", "children who are born with thick hair"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段 goosebumps are a leftover from our hairy ancestors，雞皮疙瘩是從毛髮濃密的 ancestors 那裡「遺留下來」(leftover) 的反應；再加上前文說人類 have lost most of their thick body hair，可知 ancestors 指很久以前、毛髮還很濃密的「祖先」。(A)「住在寒冷地方的動物」、(C)「研究人體的科學家」、(D)「天生毛髮濃密的孩子」都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["For humans, goosebumps are more a sign of the past than a useful tool.", "People who get goosebumps from music are easily frightened.", "Humans could stay warm in winter by getting more goosebumps.", "Goosebumps show that a person's muscles are weak or tired."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說人類又短又細的汗毛 cannot trap much warm air，雞皮疙瘩是 a leftover from our hairy ancestors，而且 it no longer does us much good；可知作者認為對人類而言，雞皮疙瘩主要是過去留下的痕跡，已經沒有多大用處。(B) 第五段說音樂引起的雞皮疙瘩來自強烈的情緒，並不表示這些人容易受驚嚇；(C) 與第四段的說法相反；(D) 文中沒有提到肌肉無力或疲勞。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a281", "title": "The Oldest Cold Case: What Ötzi the Iceman Keeps Telling Us", "title_zh": "最古老的懸案：冰人奧茲不斷告訴我們的事", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["History", "Science"], "passage": "In September 1991, two German hikers in the Ötztal Alps, on the border between Austria and Italy, noticed a human body sticking out of the melting ice. They assumed it was a climber who had died in an accident, and at first so did the authorities. Scientific dating later showed that the man had died about 5,300 years ago, in the Copper Age. Nicknamed Ötzi after the valley, he had been so well preserved by the ice that even his skin, organs and stomach contents had survived.\n\nFar from being a ragged primitive, Ötzi was well equipped for the mountains. He wore leggings and a coat sewn from animal hides, a fur cap, and leather shoes stuffed with grass for warmth. He carried a flint knife, an unfinished bow and, most remarkably, an axe with a blade of almost pure copper. Because metal was rare and valuable in his time, many researchers suspect that he was a person of some standing in his community.\n\nHis body tells stories of its own. He bears 61 tattoos, made by working charcoal-based pigment into the skin: simple lines and a few crosses rather than pictures. Many lie over joints and the lower back, where his skeleton shows signs of wear that may well have been painful, so some researchers suggest that they were a form of pain treatment, an attractive but unproven idea. A 2018 study of his stomach found that shortly before he died he had eaten a large, very fatty meal of ibex and red deer meat, an early wheat called einkorn, and traces of a fern.\n\nFor a decade, many assumed that Ötzi had simply died of cold in bad weather. Then, in 2001, an X-ray revealed a stone arrowhead lodged in his left shoulder. Later scans suggested that it had torn a major blood vessel, and most researchers now believe that he bled to death fairly quickly. His full stomach adds a puzzle, since a man running for his life is unlikely to stop for a heavy meal. Who shot him, and why, will probably never be known, which is why his death is sometimes called the world's oldest cold case.\n\nThe story keeps changing because the tools used to study him keep improving. A higher-quality analysis of his genetic material, published in 2023, suggested that his skin was darker than earlier reconstructions had shown and that he had a genetic tendency toward baldness. It also indicated that most of his ancestry came from early farmers whose roots lay in Anatolia, in present-day Turkey. Such findings are best treated as provisional rather than final, since newer technology may revise them again.\n\nWhat makes Ötzi so valuable is not only his age but the ordinary moment he preserves. Egyptian mummies were carefully prepared for the afterlife; Ötzi, by contrast, was caught at a single, unplanned instant, still dressed for the mountains and digesting his last meal. Today he lies in a specially cooled chamber in a museum in Bolzano, Italy. More than three decades after he emerged from the ice, he is still answering questions that no one in 1991 knew how to ask.", "passage_zh": "1991 年 9 月，兩名德國健行者在奧地利與義大利交界處的奧茲塔爾阿爾卑斯山上，發現一具人體從正在融化的冰層中露了出來。他們以為那是一名死於意外的登山客，當局起初也這麼認為。後來的科學定年卻顯示，這名男子死於大約 5,300 年前的銅石並用時代（又稱紅銅時代）。人們依發現地的山谷為他取了「奧茲」這個綽號；冰雪把他保存得極為完好，連他的皮膚、器官和胃裡的食物都留存了下來。\n\n奧茲絕非衣衫襤褸的原始人，他為山中的生活做了充分的準備。他穿著用獸皮縫製的綁腿和外套，戴著毛皮帽，腳上的皮鞋裡塞滿乾草來保暖。他隨身帶著一把燧石小刀、一張尚未完成的弓，而最引人注目的，是一把刀刃幾乎由純銅製成的斧頭。由於金屬在他那個時代稀少又珍貴，許多研究者推測他在所屬的群體中具有一定的地位。\n\n他的身體本身也訴說著故事。他身上有 61 處刺青，是將以木炭為主的顏料植入皮膚而形成的；這些刺青不是圖畫，而是簡單的線條和幾個十字。其中許多位在關節上方和下背部，他的骨骼在這些部位顯示出磨損的跡象，很可能曾令他疼痛，因此有些研究者認為這些刺青是一種止痛療法——這個說法很吸引人，但尚未獲得證實。一項在 2018 年發表、針對他胃部內容物的研究發現，他在死前不久吃了一頓分量大、油脂極多的餐點，內容包括羱羊和紅鹿的肉、一種名為「單粒小麥」的早期小麥，以及微量的蕨類植物。\n\n有十年的時間，許多人以為奧茲只是遇上惡劣天候而凍死。然而在 2001 年，一次 X 光檢查發現他的左肩裡卡著一枚石製箭頭。後來的掃描顯示，這枚箭頭可能割破了一條主要血管；如今多數研究者認為，他在相當短的時間內便失血而死。他那裝滿食物的胃又帶來一個謎團：一個正在逃命的人，不太可能停下來飽餐一頓。至於是誰射殺了他、又是為了什麼，大概永遠無從得知，這也是為什麼他的死有時被稱為「世界上最古老的懸案」。\n\n這個故事不斷在改變，因為研究他的工具不斷在進步。一項在 2023 年發表、品質更高的遺傳物質分析顯示，他的膚色比早期的復原像所呈現的更深，而且他在遺傳上有禿頭的傾向。這項分析也指出，他的祖先大多是早期的農民，而這些農民源自安納托利亞，也就是今天的土耳其一帶。這類發現最好被視為暫時的結論，而非最終定論，因為更新的技術可能又會修正它們。\n\n奧茲之所以如此珍貴，不只在於他年代久遠，更在於他所保存下來的那個平凡時刻。埃及的木乃伊是為了來世而經過精心處理的；相較之下，奧茲卻是在一個毫無預期的瞬間被定格下來——他仍穿著上山的衣物，胃裡還在消化最後一餐。如今，他躺在義大利波札諾一間博物館裡特別冷藏的房間中。在他從冰層中現身三十多年後，他仍在回答那些 1991 年時沒有人知道該怎麼提出的問題。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["What an ancient body has revealed about one man, and how that picture keeps changing", "Why two hikers mistook an ancient body for the victim of a modern climbing accident", "How people in the Copper Age made their clothing, weapons and metal tools", "Why scientists now agree that Ötzi's tattoos were used to treat his pain"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文主旨：第一段介紹 1991 年發現、距今約 5,300 年的冰人，第二至四段依序說明他的衣著與工具、刺青、最後一餐和死因，第五段則指出 The story keeps changing because the tools used to study him keep improving.，可知本文主要在談這具遺體透露了這名男子的哪些生平與死亡細節，以及這些認識如何不斷改變。(B) 健行者誤以為是登山意外，只是第一段的開場；(C) 文中只描述奧茲本人的衣物與工具，並非全面介紹銅器時代的製作技術；(D) 第三段說刺青是止痛療法只是 an attractive but unproven idea，科學家並未達成共識。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the passage say about Ötzi's tattoos?", "options": ["They show animals and scenes from his daily life.", "Many are found on parts of the body that may have hurt.", "They were added after his death as part of a ritual.", "They prove that he worked as a healer in his community."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Many lie over joints and the lower back, where his skeleton shows signs of wear that may well have been painful. 許多刺青位在關節和下背部，而這些部位的骨骼有磨損、很可能會疼痛，與 (B)「許多刺青位在可能會痛的身體部位」相符。(A) 文中說刺青是 simple lines and a few crosses rather than pictures，並非動物或生活場景的圖畫；(C) 文中未提到刺青是死後為了儀式才刺上的；(D) 刺青是止痛療法的說法仍是 unproven（尚未證實），更無法證明他是治療者。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How did the X-ray taken in 2001 change the understanding of Ötzi's death?", "options": ["It confirmed that he had frozen to death in a sudden storm.", "It showed that a fall on the ice had broken his left shoulder.", "It pointed to a violent death rather than one caused by the weather.", "It revealed who had attacked him and why the attack took place."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：For a decade, many assumed that Ötzi had simply died of cold in bad weather. Then, in 2001, an X-ray revealed a stone arrowhead lodged in his left shoulder. 原本許多人以為他是遇上惡劣天候而凍死，2001 年的 X 光卻發現他肩膀裡有箭頭，顯示他很可能死於暴力攻擊，而非天候所致。(A) 正好相反，X 光推翻了凍死的看法；(B) 文中說的是箭頭卡在肩膀裡，並非跌倒摔斷肩膀；(D) 文中說 Who shot him, and why, will probably never be known，兇手和動機並未揭曉。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"standing\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the ability to stay upright on steep ground", "a long period spent waiting in one place", "skill in making tools out of stone", "rank or importance within a group"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段 Because metal was rare and valuable in his time, many researchers suspect that he was a person of some standing in his community. 由於金屬在當時稀少又珍貴，擁有銅斧暗示他在群體中有一定的「地位」，故 standing 在此指「身分、地位」，即 rank or importance within a group。(A) 取 stand「站立」的字面聯想，在陡坡上站穩的能力不符文意；(B) 長時間在一處等待、(C) 製作石器的技巧，都與「金屬稀少珍貴」所引出的推論無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the fact that Ötzi ate a large meal shortly before he died?", "options": ["He had stopped to eat while running away from his attackers.", "He probably did not feel he was in immediate danger at the time.", "He was killed by the fern in his food rather than by the arrow.", "He had shared the meal with the people who later attacked him."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：His full stomach adds a puzzle, since a man running for his life is unlikely to stop for a heavy meal. 一個正在逃命的人不太可能停下來飽餐一頓，由此可推知奧茲吃這頓飯時，大概並不覺得自己正處於立即的危險之中。(A) 與這個推論正好相反；(C) 文中說他是 bled to death（失血而死），蕨類只是胃中的微量成分；(D) 文中說 Who shot him, and why, will probably never be known，無從推知他曾與攻擊者一同用餐。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a282", "title": "Too Ignorant to Know It? The Dunning–Kruger Effect Under Scrutiny", "title_zh": "無知到不自知？檢視鄧寧－克魯格效應", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Science", "Education"], "passage": "Few findings in psychology have escaped the laboratory as completely as the Dunning–Kruger effect. Online, the phrase is used to dismiss almost anyone who seems confidently wrong, usually with the claim that ignorant people are too ignorant to know it. Like many ideas that become slogans, however, the effect is both more modest and more contested than its fame suggests, and its story offers a useful lesson in how statistics can shape what we think we see.\n\nIn 1999, two Cornell University psychologists, Justin Kruger and David Dunning, published a paper titled \"Unskilled and Unaware of It.\" In four studies, undergraduates took tests of humor, logical reasoning and grammar, then estimated how well they had done compared with their peers. Those in the bottom quarter, who scored on average around the 12th percentile, believed they had performed at roughly the 62nd. The strongest performers, by contrast, tended to underestimate their standing. Kruger and Dunning proposed a \"dual burden\": the knowledge needed to answer correctly is the same knowledge needed to recognize a correct answer, so those who lack it cannot see their own mistakes.\n\nMuch of the effect's popularity, however, rests on a picture found nowhere in the paper. A widely shared graph shows confidence soaring to a peak, often labeled \"Mount Stupid,\" once someone learns a little about a subject, then collapsing before slowly recovering as real expertise develops, often without ever returning to that early height. The original data look quite different. Kruger and Dunning compared groups at a single point in time rather than following anyone's confidence as they learned. Moreover, self-estimates still rose with actual skill: the weakest students thought they were above average, but not better than the strongest.\n\nA deeper challenge comes from statistics. Imagine a group whose self-assessments are pure guesses, unrelated to their actual scores. Sorted by real performance, those at the bottom will on average place themselves near the middle, because random guesses cluster there, and so they will appear to overestimate wildly; those at the top will appear to underestimate. This is a form of regression to the mean, and simulations using random numbers reproduce much of the familiar pattern. Add the better-than-average effect, the tendency of most people to rate themselves above average on almost any desirable quality, and every estimate shifts upward, making the least skilled look even more deluded and the most skilled look roughly accurate.\n\nDunning and his colleagues have replied that the pattern survives even after such artifacts are taken into account. The debate is not settled, but a defensible core remains. People are often poor judges of their own performance; most of us overrate ourselves; and in skills such as grammar or logic, it is genuinely hard to spot mistakes one does not know how to avoid. What is less certain is whether incompetence produces a special blindness beyond what random error and general optimism would predict.\n\nThere is a certain irony in all this. A finding about people overrating their own understanding has often been repeated, with great confidence, in a form its authors never claimed. Perhaps the most useful lesson is not that other people are fools, but that everyone, including the person quoting the effect, has limited insight into what they do not know.", "passage_zh": "在心理學的研究發現中，很少有像「鄧寧－克魯格效應」這樣徹底走出實驗室的。在網路上，這個詞幾乎被拿來貶低任何看似「錯得理直氣壯」的人，而且通常還伴隨著一種說法：無知的人無知到連自己無知都不知道。然而，就像許多變成口號的觀念一樣，這個效應其實比它的名氣所暗示的更有限，也更具爭議；它的故事也提供了寶貴的一課，讓我們看到統計方法如何左右我們自以為看見的東西。\n\n1999 年，康乃爾大學的兩位心理學家賈斯汀‧克魯格（Justin Kruger）與大衛‧鄧寧（David Dunning）發表了一篇題為〈不熟練，卻不自知〉的論文。在四項研究中，大學生接受了幽默感、邏輯推理和文法的測驗，接著估計自己和同儕相比表現得如何。成績落在最後四分之一的學生，平均分數大約位於第 12 百分位，他們卻認為自己的表現約在第 62 百分位。相較之下，表現最好的學生則傾向於低估自己的排名。克魯格和鄧寧提出了「雙重負擔」的說法：答對題目所需的知識，正是辨認正確答案所需的知識，因此缺乏這種知識的人，看不見自己的錯誤。\n\n然而，這個效應之所以廣為流行，很大程度上是靠著一張在論文中根本找不到的圖。這張廣為流傳的圖表顯示：一個人對某個主題才學到一點皮毛，信心就飆升到頂峰（常被稱為「愚昧山峰」），接著崩跌，然後隨著真正的專業能力逐漸成形而慢慢回升，而且往往再也回不到當初的高點。原始資料呈現的樣貌卻大不相同。克魯格和鄧寧比較的是同一時間點的不同群體，並沒有追蹤任何人在學習過程中的信心變化。此外，自我評估仍會隨著實際能力而提高：能力最弱的學生認為自己高於平均，但並不認為自己比能力最強的學生還好。\n\n更深層的挑戰來自統計學。想像有一群人，他們的自我評估純粹是瞎猜，和實際分數毫無關係。如果按照真實表現排序，排在最底層的人平均而言會把自己放在中間附近，因為隨機的猜測本來就集中在中間，所以他們看起來會嚴重高估自己；而排在最頂端的人，看起來則會低估自己。這是一種「趨中迴歸」現象，而用隨機數字所做的模擬，也重現了這個熟悉模式的大部分特徵。再加上「優於平均效應」，也就是大多數人在幾乎任何正面特質上，都會把自己評得高於平均的傾向，所有的估計值都會往上移，使能力最差的人看起來更加自欺，而能力最強的人看起來則大致準確。\n\n鄧寧和他的同事則回應說，即使把這類統計假象納入考量，這個模式依然存在。這場辯論尚未定論，但仍有一個站得住腳的核心：人們往往不太會評估自己的表現；我們大多數人都會高估自己；而在文法或邏輯這類技能上，要察覺那些自己根本不知道該如何避免的錯誤，確實很困難。比較不確定的是：能力不足是否會造成一種特殊的盲點，超出隨機誤差與一般樂觀心態所能預測的程度。\n\n這一切帶著幾分諷刺。一項關於「人們高估自己理解程度」的研究發現，常常被人信心滿滿地轉述，而轉述的版本卻是原作者從未主張過的。或許最有用的啟示並不是「別人都是傻瓜」，而是每個人，包括正在引用這個效應的人，對於自己所不知道的事，洞察力都很有限。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?", "options": ["To show that the Dunning–Kruger study was based on invented data", "To explain why beginners always feel more confident than experts", "To compare what the original study found with later myths and criticisms", "To teach students how to avoid overconfidence before taking exams"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段指出這個效應 more modest and more contested than its fame suggests；第二段說明原始研究的發現，第三段指出流行的「愚昧山峰」圖是一種誤解，第四、五段介紹統計學上的批評與作者的回應，並歸納出仍站得住腳的部分。可知作者的目的是把原始研究與後來的迷思、批評加以對照。(A) 文中從未說資料是捏造的；(B) 正是第三段所指出的誤解，原始資料並未顯示初學者比專家更有自信；(D) 文中沒有提供考試前避免過度自信的方法。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how did Kruger and Dunning explain their findings?", "options": ["The knowledge needed to perform a task is also needed to judge that performance.", "Weak students deliberately exaggerated their scores to impress their classmates.", "The tests of humor and grammar were too difficult for most of the students.", "Strong students were too modest to admit how well they had really done."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：the knowledge needed to answer correctly is the same knowledge needed to recognize a correct answer, so those who lack it cannot see their own mistakes. 兩位作者提出「雙重負擔」：做好一件事所需的知識，也正是判斷自己表現好壞所需的知識，與 (A) 相符。(B) 文中沒有說學生故意誇大分數；(C) 文中未提到測驗太難；(D) 文中只說表現最好的學生傾向低估自己，並未把原因解釋為謙虛。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, in what way does the popular \"Mount Stupid\" graph misrepresent the original research?", "options": ["It suggests that the study tested experts instead of students.", "It hides the fact that weak performers overestimated themselves.", "It shows confidence rising steadily as people gain more skill.", "It implies that the least skilled rated themselves above the most skilled."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段描述，流行的圖中初學者的信心衝上「愚昧山峰」，之後雖然慢慢回升，卻 often without ever returning to that early height，彷彿能力最弱的人比專家更有自信；但原始資料顯示 self-estimates still rose with actual skill: the weakest students thought they were above average, but not better than the strongest，也就是能力最弱的人並沒有把自己評得比最強的人還高。(A) 文中的受試者是大學生，那張圖並未涉及此點；(B) 那張圖是誇大而非隱藏弱者高估自己的現象；(C) 自我評估隨能力上升，是原始資料呈現的結果，而不是那張圖的內容（圖中信心是先飆升、再崩跌、後回升）。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"artifacts\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["ancient objects made by hand", "false patterns produced by the method of analysis", "mistakes made on purpose by researchers", "results confirmed by many later studies"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段 the pattern survives even after such artifacts are taken into account 中的 such artifacts，指的是第四段所說的趨中迴歸與優於平均效應，也就是因統計分析方法本身而產生、並非真實存在的「假象」。(A) 是 artifact「手工藝品、古物」的字面意思，不合文意；(C) 文中沒有說研究者故意犯錯；(D) 與「假象」的意思相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest by pointing out \"a certain irony\" in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Many who cite the effect may be overconfident about understanding it.", "Dunning and Kruger later regretted publishing their original paper.", "The effect applies mainly to people who spend a lot of time online.", "Readers should never discuss research they have not carried out."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段：A finding about people overrating their own understanding has often been repeated, with great confidence, in a form its authors never claimed. 諷刺之處在於：一項談「高估自己理解程度」的研究，卻常被人自信滿滿地錯誤轉述，暗示許多引用者本身就高估了自己對這個效應的理解；作者也提醒 including the person quoting the effect。(B) 文中未提及兩位作者後悔；(C) 第一段只說這個詞常在網路上被使用，並未說效應主要適用於網路使用者；(D) 作者並未主張不可討論別人的研究。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a283", "title": "Lunch by Rail: How Mumbai's Dabbawalas Deliver Without Computers", "title_zh": "搭火車的午餐：孟買達巴瓦拉如何不靠電腦送便當", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Business", "Culture"], "passage": "Every working day in Mumbai, India's financial capital, millions of people squeeze onto crowded suburban trains to reach their offices. In the middle of the morning, a second, quieter journey begins. Men in white caps collect metal lunchboxes from homes across the city and set off toward the business districts. They are the dabbawalas, a name meaning roughly \"box carriers,\" and for more than a century they have delivered home-cooked lunches to people at work, then brought the empty boxes back the same afternoon.\n\nThe service is usually traced to the late nineteenth century, when the city was growing rapidly as a center of trade. Then, as now, many office workers lived far from their workplaces and left home early. Many preferred food cooked by their own families, for reasons of taste, cost, health or religion, yet carrying a hot meal on a packed train was hardly practical. The dabbawalas solved the problem neatly: lunch could be cooked after the worker had left and still reach the office in time for the midday break.\n\nThe daily routine is a carefully timed relay. Around mid-morning, a dabbawala picks up boxes from homes in his neighborhood and takes them to the nearest railway station. There, boxes from many collectors are sorted by destination and loaded onto trains. At the other end, they are sorted again and carried the last stretch by bicycle, handcart or on foot, reaching offices by lunchtime. In the afternoon the process runs in reverse, so that each empty box returns to the kitchen it came from.\n\nWhat makes this possible is not technology but a simple code. The lid of each box carries a few colored marks, letters and numbers showing where it was collected, which station it is heading for, and the building and floor where it should end up. Because many dabbawalas have had little formal schooling, the code is designed to be understood at a glance rather than read like a document. The workers are organized in small groups, each responsible for a particular area.\n\nSuch efficiency has made the dabbawalas famous. Estimates vary, but the network is usually said to involve several thousand workers and to move hundreds of thousands of lunches every working day, and business schools have studied it as a model of reliable service. The most famous claim made about it, however, deserves caution. It is widely repeated that the dabbawalas make only one mistake in millions of deliveries, but the figure seems to have come from a rough estimate given in a magazine interview, not from any formal audit, and it is hard to verify. It is perhaps best treated as a tribute rather than a statistic.\n\nNone of this makes the system any less impressive. Its real lesson may be that reliability can come from simple rules, clear responsibilities and workers who take pride in what they do, rather than from expensive machines. The dabbawalas now face serious competition from office canteens and food delivery apps, and the closure of offices during the COVID-19 pandemic hit them hard. Whether the service will survive in its present form is uncertain, but it remains a reminder that efficiency does not have to be high-tech.", "passage_zh": "在印度的金融中心孟買，每個工作天都有數以百萬計的人擠上擁擠的通勤火車去上班。到了上午過半，另一趟較不引人注意的旅程也開始了：頭戴白帽的男子從城市各處的住家收取金屬便當盒，接著朝商業區出發。他們就是「達巴瓦拉」（dabbawala），意思大致是「送盒子的人」。一百多年來，他們把家裡煮好的午餐送到上班族手中，並在同一天下午把空便當盒送回去。\n\n這項服務的起源通常可追溯到十九世紀末，當時孟買正迅速發展為貿易中心。和現在一樣，那時許多上班族住得離工作地點很遠，很早就得出門。許多人偏好吃自家人煮的飯菜，原因可能是口味、花費、健康或宗教；然而，要帶著一份熱騰騰的飯菜擠上滿載的火車，實在不太實際。達巴瓦拉巧妙地解決了這個問題：午餐可以等上班族出門之後才開始煮，卻仍能在中午休息時間及時送到辦公室。\n\n每天的工作就像一場精準計時的接力賽。大約上午過半時，達巴瓦拉會到自己負責區域的住家收取便當盒，送到最近的火車站。在車站裡，由許多人收來的便當盒會依目的地分類，再搬上火車。到了另一端，便當盒會再分類一次，接著以腳踏車、手推車或步行送完最後一段路，在午餐時間前抵達辦公室。到了下午，整個流程倒過來進行，讓每個空便當盒都回到原本的廚房。\n\n讓這一切得以實現的不是科技，而是一套簡單的代碼。每個便當盒的蓋子上只標著幾個彩色記號、字母和數字，標示它在哪裡收取、要送往哪一站，以及最後該送到哪棟大樓的哪一層樓。由於許多達巴瓦拉受過的正規教育不多，這套代碼的設計是讓人一眼就能看懂，而不必像讀文件那樣逐字閱讀。這些工作者分成好幾個小組，每組各自負責一個特定的區域。\n\n如此高的效率讓達巴瓦拉聲名遠播。各方估計不一，但一般認為這個網絡有數千名工作者，每個工作天運送數十萬份午餐；商學院也曾把它當作可靠服務的典範加以研究。然而，關於它最有名的說法，卻值得我們審慎看待。人們常說達巴瓦拉在數百萬次的遞送中只會出錯一次，但這個數字似乎來自一次雜誌訪談中的粗略估計，而非任何正式的稽核，而且很難查證。或許最好把它看成一種讚美，而不是一項統計數據。\n\n但這一切絲毫無損這套系統令人讚嘆之處。它真正的啟示或許在於：可靠性可以來自簡單的規則、明確的分工，以及以自己工作為榮的人，而不一定要靠昂貴的機器。如今，達巴瓦拉面臨公司員工餐廳與外送應用程式的激烈競爭，而新冠肺炎疫情期間辦公室紛紛關閉，更讓他們受到重創。這項服務能否以現在的形式延續下去，仍是未知數，但它始終提醒著我們：效率並不一定要靠高科技。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?", "options": ["To persuade office workers to use delivery apps instead of dabbawalas", "To explain how a low-tech delivery network works and question a claim about it", "To describe how Mumbai's railway system grew in the nineteenth century", "To prove that the dabbawalas almost never make a single mistake"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先在第一至四段說明達巴瓦拉的起源、運作流程，以及便當盒上的簡單代碼（What makes this possible is not technology but a simple code），第五段再指出「數百萬次只錯一次」這個著名說法 deserves caution，而且 hard to verify。可知作者的目的是說明這個低科技的遞送網絡如何運作，並質疑一項關於它的說法。(A) 作者並未勸人改用外送 app，外送 app 只是第六段提到的挑戰之一；(C) 火車只是運送工具，文中並未描述孟買鐵路的發展史；(D) 正好相反，作者認為那個數字很難查證。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why did the service first develop?", "options": ["The railways did not allow passengers to eat on the trains.", "Families wanted a way to send daily messages to workers.", "Office workers were not given any break for lunch.", "Workers wanted home-cooked food but could not easily bring it."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Many preferred food cooked by their own families... yet carrying a hot meal on a packed train was hardly practical. 上班族想吃家裡煮的飯菜，卻很難帶著熱騰騰的飯菜擠上火車，因此需要這項服務，與 (D) 相符。(A) 文中說的是帶飯搭車不實際，並未說鐵路禁止乘客在車上吃東西；(B) 文中未提到家人想傳遞訊息；(C) 文中說午餐會 reach the office in time for the midday break，可見上班族有中午休息時間。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the marks on each lunchbox?", "options": ["They show where a box comes from and where it must go.", "They are scanned by computers at each railway station.", "They are written in full sentences that anyone can read.", "They are changed by a group leader every single morning."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：the lid of each box carries a few colored marks, letters and numbers showing where it was collected, which station it is heading for, and the building and floor where it should end up. 這些記號標示了便當盒的收取地點與最終的送達位置，與 (A) 相符。(B) 文中說 What makes this possible is not technology but a simple code，並不靠電腦掃描；(C) 文中說代碼 designed to be understood at a glance rather than read like a document，並非寫成完整的句子；(D) 文中未提到組長每天早上更改記號。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"tribute\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a payment made to a ruler", "a careful scientific measurement", "an expression of admiration", "a complaint about poor service"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段 It is perhaps best treated as a tribute rather than a statistic. 作者認為「數百萬次只錯一次」這個數字無法查證，與其當作統計數據，不如看成人們對達巴瓦拉的讚美，因此 tribute 在此指「表達敬佩的話」，即 an expression of admiration。(A)「向統治者繳納的貢品」是 tribute 的另一個意思，但不符此處文意；(B) 仔細的科學測量正是作者說它不是的東西（rather than a statistic）；(D) 對服務品質的抱怨，與稱讚的語氣相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which best describes the writer's attitude toward the dabbawalas?", "options": ["Suspicious, believing their system is far less reliable than people think", "Pessimistic, believing their service is certain to disappear very soon", "Dismissive, believing low-tech services have little to teach modern firms", "Admiring of their system, though doubtful about one precise figure"], "answer": 3, "explain": "作者在第五段指出那個著名數字 deserves caution、hard to verify，但第六段隨即說 None of this makes the system any less impressive，並認為它的啟示在於簡單的規則、明確的分工與以工作為榮的人。可知作者欣賞這套系統，只是懷疑其中一個精確的數字。(A) 作者懷疑的是那個數字，而非系統本身的可靠性；(B) 第六段說能否延續 is uncertain，並未斷言很快就會消失；(C) 作者認為它提醒我們 efficiency does not have to be high-tech，並非認為它對現代企業毫無啟發。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a284", "title": "Where Did the Year Go? Why Time Seems to Speed Up as We Age", "title_zh": "這一年跑哪去了？為什麼年紀越大，時間過得越快", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Science", "Life"], "passage": "Ask almost any adult and you will hear the same complaint: the years go by faster and faster. Birthdays arrive before we are ready, and a summer that felt endless at the age of eight now vanishes in what seems like a few weeks. Clocks, of course, have not changed their pace. Yet the feeling is so common that researchers have proposed several explanations. None is settled, but together they suggest that the answer may lie less in time itself than in the way we remember it.\n\nOne of the oldest explanations is mathematical. To a five-year-old, a single year is a fifth of her whole life; to a fifty-year-old, it is only a fiftieth. If we judge each year against all the years before it, each new one must seem smaller. The idea is neat, but critics point out that it describes the feeling rather than explaining it. Few of us consciously compare this year with our entire life, and the theory cannot account for why some periods of adult life feel long while others vanish.\n\nA second idea focuses on novelty. Childhood and youth are crowded with firsts: the first day at school, the first trip without parents, the first job. The brain seems to store new events in rich detail, while familiar routines leave fainter traces. For many adults, one week looks much like the next: the same commute, the same desk, the same evening on the sofa. Looking back on such a year, a person finds relatively few distinct memories to fill it, so the year seems to have passed in a flash.\n\nThis explanation rests on a distinction between how time feels while it is passing and how long it seems in hindsight. The so-called holiday paradox illustrates the difference. A week-long trip abroad, full of new streets, foods and faces, often seems to rush by while we are on it. Once we are home, however, it seems to have lasted far longer than an ordinary week at work. The reverse can also happen: a dull afternoon spent waiting may drag at the time, yet leave almost nothing behind in memory.\n\nIf this account is broadly right, some researchers suggest, we may be able to push back a little against the speeding years. The usual advice is to seek out novelty: to travel, learn a new skill, vary our routines, even take a different route to work, and pay closer attention to ordinary moments instead of drifting through them. None of this can add an hour to the day, and the ideas behind it are still being tested. Still, a year filled with new experiences may well feel fuller in hindsight.\n\nPerhaps the most interesting implication is that the speeding up of time is not entirely fixed; it may partly be a side effect of a life that has settled into comfortable patterns. Routine has real value: it saves effort and frees the mind for other things. But if its price is years that vanish without a trace, it may be worth breaking now and then, so that there is more to remember when we ask, once again, where the year went.", "passage_zh": "隨便問一個大人，你大概都會聽到同樣的抱怨：歲月過得越來越快。生日總在我們還沒準備好時就來了；八歲時感覺永無止境的暑假，如今彷彿短短幾週就消失無蹤。當然，時鐘的步調並沒有改變。然而，這種感覺如此普遍，研究者已經提出了好幾種解釋。這些解釋都還沒有定論，但綜合起來看，答案或許不在時間本身，而在於我們記憶時間的方式。\n\n最古老的解釋之一來自數學。對一個五歲的孩子來說，一年是她整個人生的五分之一；對一個五十歲的人來說，一年卻只占五十分之一。如果我們拿每一年和之前所有的歲月相比，每個新的一年必然顯得更短。這個想法很簡潔，但批評者指出，它只是在描述這種感覺，而不是解釋它。我們很少有人會有意識地拿今年和自己的整個人生相比，而且這個理論也無法說明，為什麼成年生活中有些時期感覺很漫長，有些卻轉眼就消失了。\n\n第二種看法著眼於新奇感。童年和青少年時期充滿了各種「第一次」：第一天上學、第一次沒有父母陪伴的旅行、第一份工作。大腦似乎會把新的事件詳細地儲存下來，而熟悉的例行公事留下的痕跡則比較淡。對許多大人而言，這一週和下一週看起來沒什麼兩樣：同樣的通勤、同樣的辦公桌、同樣窩在沙發上的夜晚。回顧這樣的一年時，人們會發覺能填滿這一年的獨特記憶相對很少，於是這一年就好像一眨眼就過去了。\n\n這種解釋建立在一個區別上：時間正在流逝時給人的感覺，和事後回想時覺得它有多長，是兩回事。所謂的「假期悖論」正好說明了這種差異。一趟為期一週的出國旅行，充滿了新的街道、食物與面孔，身在其中時往往覺得時間飛逝。然而一旦回到家，這趟旅行卻好像比平常上班的一週長得多。反過來的情況也可能發生：一個在等待中度過的無聊下午，當下也許讓人覺得時間過得很慢，卻幾乎沒有在記憶中留下任何東西。\n\n一些研究者認為，如果這種說法大致正確，我們或許能稍微抵擋歲月加速的感覺。常見的建議是去追求新奇：去旅行、學習新技能、改變例行的生活，甚至換一條路線去上班，並且更用心地留意平凡的時刻，而不是漫不經心地度過。這些做法都無法為一天多添一個小時，而它們背後的觀點也仍在檢驗之中。儘管如此，一個充滿新經驗的年頭，在我們回顧時很可能會顯得更加充實。\n\n或許最耐人尋味的啟示是：時間的加速並非完全無法改變，它有一部分可能是生活落入舒適的固定模式之後所產生的副作用。例行公事確實有其價值：它能節省力氣，讓頭腦騰出空間去處理其他事情。但如果它的代價是一年又一年不留痕跡地消失，那麼偶爾打破它或許是值得的；如此一來，當我們再次問起「這一年跑哪去了？」時，就會有更多值得回憶的事。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Evidence that the brain's internal clock slows down with age", "Advice on planning holidays that seem to pass more slowly", "Proof that children judge time more accurately than adults", "Possible reasons why time seems to pass faster as people age"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段指出「年紀越大越覺得時間過得快」的感覺很普遍，researchers have proposed several explanations；第二至四段依序介紹比例的數學解釋、新經驗與記憶的解釋，以及假期悖論，第五、六段再談如何讓時間感覺更充實。可知本文主要在探討時間為何似乎隨年齡增長而加快的幾種可能原因。(A) 文中沒有討論大腦內部時鐘變慢；(B) 假期只是第四段用來說明的例子，而且文中說旅行當下往往覺得時間飛逝，並非教人規劃過得慢的假期；(C) 文中並未比較孩子和大人估計時間的準確度。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What weakness of the mathematical explanation does the writer point out?", "options": ["It applies only to people over the age of fifty.", "It has been disproved by recent studies of the brain.", "It fails to explain why some periods of adult life seem long.", "It ignores the fact that clocks always run at the same speed."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：the theory cannot account for why some periods of adult life feel long while others vanish. 這個數學理論無法說明為何成年生活中有些時期感覺很長、有些卻轉眼即逝，與 (C) 相符。(A) 文中以五歲和五十歲為例說明，並未說只適用於五十歲以上的人；(B) 文中只說批評者認為它是在描述而非解釋這種感覺，並未提到被腦部研究推翻；(D) 第一段雖提到時鐘的步調沒有改變，但那不是作者對數學解釋的批評。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the novelty explanation, why does a routine year seem short in hindsight?", "options": ["Adults are usually too busy to notice how much time has passed.", "It leaves relatively few distinct memories to look back on.", "The brain deletes old memories to make room for new ones.", "Adults compare every year with the long summers of childhood."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Looking back on such a year, a person finds relatively few distinct memories to fill it, so the year seems to have passed in a flash. 一成不變的一年留下的獨特記憶相對很少，所以回顧時覺得很短，與 (B) 相符。(A) 文中未說大人太忙而沒注意時間；(C) 文中說的是熟悉的例行公事 leave fainter traces（留下的痕跡較淡），並非大腦刪除舊記憶；(D) 拿每一年和過去相比是第二段數學解釋的內容，而且文中並未說大人會拿童年的暑假來比較。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"drag\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["seem to pass very slowly", "be pulled along the ground", "be remembered in detail", "come to a sudden end"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段 a dull afternoon spent waiting may drag at the time, yet leave almost nothing behind in memory. 此句與前面「旅行時覺得時間飛逝」（seems to rush by）形成對比，指無聊的等待在當下讓人覺得時間過得很慢，故 drag 在此意為「（時間）似乎過得很慢」。(B)「被拖著在地上走」是 drag 的字面意思，不符文意；(C) 句中說它 leave almost nothing behind in memory，與「被詳細記住」相反；(D) 突然結束與「拖得很長」的感覺正好相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Based on the passage, which of the following would most likely seem long when remembered later?", "options": ["A month of nearly identical days at the same office", "An afternoon spent quietly waiting for a delayed train", "A first week of study in a new city abroad", "A year of following the same weekly routine at home"], "answer": 2, "explain": "根據第三、四段，新的經驗會在記憶中留下豐富的痕跡（The brain seems to store new events in rich detail），所以事後回想時那段時間會顯得較長，就像假期悖論中的旅行 seems to have lasted far longer than an ordinary week。在國外新城市展開的第一週求學生活充滿新的人事物，回想時最可能覺得漫長。(A)、(D) 都是一成不變的例行生活，依第三段的說法，回顧時反而顯得短暫；(B) 第四段指出無聊的等待 leave almost nothing behind in memory，事後回想也不會覺得長。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a285", "title": "Do Trees Really Talk? Second Thoughts About the Wood Wide Web", "title_zh": "樹木真的會說話嗎？重新思考「樹聯網」", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Nature", "Science"], "passage": "Walk through a quiet forest and it is easy to see each tree as a lonely individual, competing with its neighbors for light and water. In recent decades, however, a very different picture has captured the public imagination: trees linked underground by threads of fungus into a vast network, through which they share food, send warnings and even care for their young. It is a beautiful idea. The harder question is how much of it the evidence supports.\n\nThe story usually begins with a 1997 paper in Nature by the Canadian forest ecologist Suzanne Simard and her colleagues. Many trees live in partnership with mycorrhizal fungi, which grow on or into their roots and supply water and minerals in exchange for sugars. Working in a forest in British Columbia, Simard's team exposed seedlings of paper birch and Douglas fir to carbon dioxide containing traceable carbon, then detected that carbon in nearby seedlings of the other species. Their conclusion, that carbon had passed between trees through shared fungal connections, was striking, and the catchy label \"wood wide web\" quickly caught on.\n\nOver the following years, the metaphor grew. Simard came to describe the largest, oldest trees as \"mother trees,\" hubs that she argued could feed shaded seedlings, favor their own offspring and pass on chemical warnings. Best-selling books, a popular TED talk and a Pulitzer Prize-winning novel carried this vision to millions of people, many of whom came away convinced that trees cooperate in ways once thought possible only for animals.\n\nIn 2023, however, a review led by Justine Karst examined the field evidence for three popular claims: that such networks are widespread in forests, that resources moving through them help seedlings grow, and that mature trees favor their own offspring when sending resources or warnings. The authors judged the evidence for the first two claims insufficient and found no peer-reviewed field evidence at all for the third. One difficulty, they explained, is that carbon can travel between plants by other routes, such as through the soil, so finding it in a neighbor does not prove it moved along fungal threads. Tellingly, one co-author had worked on Simard's 1997 study herself.\n\nJust as revealing was what the review found in the scientific literature. Karst and her colleagues reported that later papers often cited earlier studies as stronger support for the network story than those studies actually provided, a pattern they linked to \"positive citation bias.\" Simard and other supporters have pushed back, arguing that the critics read the evidence too narrowly. Yet the debate is not about whether mycorrhizal fungi matter; no one doubts that they are vital to forest health. It is about how far the evidence can be stretched.\n\nThe episode carries a lesson that reaches far beyond forests. Scientific stories spread fastest when they are simple, emotional and easy to picture, and few images are more appealing than a mother caring for her children. Yet each step from data to headline invites a little exaggeration, as a cautious \"may suggest\" quietly becomes \"proves.\" None of this means the original research was worthless or that trees are not connected in surprising ways. It means that the most charming version of a scientific story is not always the best-supported one.", "passage_zh": "走過一座寧靜的森林，人們很容易把每一棵樹看成孤單的個體，與鄰近的樹競爭陽光和水分。然而在最近幾十年間，一幅截然不同的景象擄獲了大眾的想像：樹木在地底下透過真菌的細絲相連，形成一張龐大的網絡，並藉由它分享食物、傳遞警訊，甚至照顧自己的下一代。這是個美麗的想法。比較難回答的問題是：其中究竟有多少是證據所支持的？\n\n這個故事通常從 1997 年說起。那一年，加拿大森林生態學家蘇珊‧希瑪爾（Suzanne Simard）與同事在《自然》期刊發表了一篇論文。許多樹木與菌根真菌共生，這些真菌長在樹根表面或深入樹根內部，為樹木提供水分和礦物質，以換取醣類。希瑪爾的團隊在加拿大英屬哥倫比亞省的一片森林中，讓紙樺和花旗松的幼苗吸收含有可追蹤碳元素的二氧化碳，之後在附近另一種樹的幼苗體內偵測到了這些碳。他們的結論是：碳透過共享的真菌連結在樹木之間傳遞。這個結論十分引人注目，「樹聯網」這個響亮的稱呼也很快流行起來。\n\n在接下來的幾年裡，這個比喻的內容不斷擴大。希瑪爾開始把最大、最老的樹稱為「母樹」，她主張這些樹是網絡的中心，能夠餵養被遮蔭的幼苗、偏袒自己的後代，並傳遞化學警訊。暢銷書、一場廣受歡迎的 TED 演講，以及一部獲得普立茲獎的小說，把這幅景象帶給了數以百萬計的人，其中許多人因此深信，樹木會以過去被認為只有動物才做得到的方式互相合作。\n\n然而在 2023 年，一篇由賈絲汀‧卡斯特（Justine Karst）主導的回顧論文，檢視了三個流行說法的野外證據：這類網絡在森林中十分普遍；透過網絡移動的資源能幫助幼苗生長；成熟的樹木在傳送資源或警訊時會偏袒自己的後代。作者認為，前兩個說法的證據不足，第三個說法則完全找不到經同儕審查的野外證據。他們解釋，其中一個困難在於碳也可能經由其他途徑在植物之間移動，例如透過土壤，因此在鄰近植物體內發現碳，並不能證明碳是沿著真菌細絲傳過去的。耐人尋味的是，這篇論文的一位共同作者，當年也親自參與過希瑪爾 1997 年的研究。\n\n同樣發人深省的，是這篇回顧在科學文獻中發現的現象。卡斯特和同事指出，後來的論文引用早期研究時，常常把它們說成比實際上更有力地支持網絡理論，他們認為這與「正向引用偏誤」有關。希瑪爾和其他支持者則提出反駁，認為批評者對證據的解讀太過狹隘。不過，這場辯論的重點並不是菌根真菌重不重要；沒有人懷疑它們對森林健康至關重要。爭議在於，這些證據究竟能被延伸到什麼程度。\n\n這段插曲帶來的教訓，遠遠超出森林的範圍。簡單、動人又容易想像的科學故事傳播得最快，而很少有畫面比母親照顧孩子更打動人心。然而，從數據到新聞標題的每一步，都可能引來些許誇大，謹慎的「可能顯示」悄悄變成了「證明」。這一切並不代表原本的研究毫無價值，也不代表樹木之間沒有令人驚奇的連結。它的意思是，一個科學故事最迷人的版本，未必是證據最充分的版本。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How fungi help trees survive dry seasons by supplying them with water", "How a popular story about connected, cooperating trees has grown beyond its evidence", "Why Suzanne Simard's 1997 experiment was shown to be dishonest", "How mother trees decide which seedlings should receive their food"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文主旨：第一段提出「樹木透過真菌網絡分享食物、傳遞警訊、照顧後代」這個美麗的想法，接著問 how much of it the evidence supports；第二、三段說明這個故事如何從 1997 年的研究擴大成「母樹」的比喻；第四、五段介紹 2023 年的回顧論文認為證據不足；第六段指出 the most charming version of a scientific story is not always the best-supported one。可知本文在說明一個流行的說法如何超出了證據所能支持的範圍。(A) 真菌提供水分只是第二段的背景說明；(C) 文中從未說該研究造假，第六段還說 None of this means the original research was worthless；(D) 第四段指出「母樹偏袒後代」的說法缺乏證據，文章並未說明母樹如何做決定。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what did Simard's 1997 study report?", "options": ["Large old trees sent chemical warnings to young trees growing nearby.", "Fungi took sugars from trees without giving them anything in return.", "Carbon taken in by one seedling later turned up in a nearby seedling of another species.", "Paper birch seedlings grew faster when planted far away from Douglas firs."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Simard's team exposed seedlings of paper birch and Douglas fir to carbon dioxide containing traceable carbon, then detected that carbon in nearby seedlings of the other species. 也就是一棵幼苗吸收的碳，後來出現在附近另一種樹的幼苗體內。(A) 母樹傳遞化學警訊，是第三段描述後來擴大的說法，並非 1997 年研究的結果；(B) 與第二段 supply water and minerals in exchange for sugars 相反，真菌與樹木是互相交換的；(D) 文中未提到幼苗種植距離與生長速度的關係。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the 2023 review, why does finding carbon in a neighboring plant NOT prove that a fungal network carried it there?", "options": ["The carbon used in such experiments cannot be traced accurately.", "All of the earlier studies were carried out in laboratories, not forests.", "Mycorrhizal fungi are unable to grow into the roots of young trees.", "The carbon may have reached the neighbor by another route, such as the soil."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：carbon can travel between plants by other routes, such as through the soil, so finding it in a neighbor does not prove it moved along fungal threads. 碳可能經由土壤等其他途徑到達鄰近植物。(A) 第二段明說實驗使用的是 traceable carbon（可追蹤的碳）；(B) 1997 年的研究是在英屬哥倫比亞的森林中進行的，回顧論文檢視的也是 field evidence（野外證據）；(C) 與第二段 fungi, which grow on or into their roots 矛盾。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"stretched\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["measured with greater accuracy", "pushed to support more than it shows", "kept hidden from the public", "gathered through field work"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：the debate is not about whether mycorrhizal fungi matter... It is about how far the evidence can be stretched. 同段前文提到後來的論文常把早期研究說成 stronger support for the network story than those studies actually provided，可知 stretched 指證據被「延伸、引申」去支持超過它實際所能證明的內容。(A) 更精確地測量、(C) 對大眾隱瞞、(D) 透過野外工作蒐集，皆不符文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["An appealing image can make a scientific claim seem more certain than the data allow.", "Scientists should never use comparisons or metaphors to describe their work.", "Simard's 1997 study should not have been published in a major journal.", "A scientific idea can be trusted once it appears in best-selling books."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第六段：Scientific stories spread fastest when they are simple, emotional and easy to picture... each step from data to headline invites a little exaggeration. 作者認為「母親照顧孩子」這類動人的畫面，容易讓科學說法聽起來比數據所能支持的更確定。(B) 作者只提醒比喻可能讓故事被誇大，並未主張完全不用比喻，說法太極端；(C) 與 None of this means the original research was worthless 相反；(D) 第三段提到暢銷書把這個說法帶給大眾，但全文正是在說明廣為流傳不等於證據充分。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a286", "title": "The Humble Box That Shrank the World", "title_zh": "讓世界變小的平凡鐵箱", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["History", "Business"], "passage": "Look at almost anything in your room, from your phone to your shoes, and there is a good chance it spent part of its life inside a steel box. Shipping containers are so ordinary that most people never notice them, yet many historians rank the container among the developments that made the modern global economy possible. Its importance lies less in the box itself than in the system that grew up around it.\n\nBefore containers, most packaged goods traveled as \"break-bulk\": sacks of coffee, barrels of wine and crates of machinery, each handled separately. Gangs of dockworkers lifted goods aboard in nets or slings and packed them by hand into the ship's hold, fitting them together like pieces of a puzzle. The work was slow, exhausting and often dangerous. A ship could spend days or even weeks in port, and theft and damage were common. Much of the cost of shipping goods was spent not at sea but at the docks.\n\nThe man usually credited with changing this was not a sailor but a trucking businessman. Malcom McLean, who ran a trucking company in the American South, knew how much time his drivers lost waiting at crowded docks. His idea was simple: why not lift a truck's whole trailer body onto a ship instead of unloading it piece by piece? In April 1956, a converted oil tanker, the Ideal X, sailed from Newark, New Jersey, to Houston, Texas, carrying 58 such boxes on its deck. The voyage is widely regarded as the birth of modern container shipping, and loading costs fell dramatically.\n\nThe box alone, however, was not enough. At first, each shipping company used its own container sizes, which might not fit another firm's ships or cranes. During the 1960s, industry groups and standards organizations agreed on standard dimensions and standard corner fittings that allowed containers to be lifted, stacked and locked together. The resulting 20-foot and 40-foot boxes could move from ship to train to truck without ever being opened, their contents untouched from factory to warehouse. This seamless movement between different kinds of transport, more than the box itself, is what made the container revolutionary.\n\nThe effects spread far beyond the docks. As moving goods became cheaper, companies could make parts in one country, assemble them in another and sell the products worldwide, a pattern many economists believe helped East Asian manufacturing grow so quickly. The map of ports changed too. Container terminals needed huge open spaces for cranes and stacks of boxes, so crowded old waterfronts such as those in Manhattan and London declined, while new terminals grew up where there was room. Kaohsiung in Taiwan, meanwhile, became one of Asia's major container hubs.\n\nNot everyone gained. Containerization swept away the jobs of many dockworkers: where large gangs had once been needed to load a ship, a small crew operating cranes could now do it far faster, and the close-knit neighborhoods around the old docks faded. Unions fought hard over the change, and in some ports they won payments or income guarantees in exchange for accepting the new methods. The container's story is a reminder that some powerful technologies look remarkably dull, and that their benefits and costs are rarely shared equally.", "passage_zh": "看看你房間裡的幾乎任何東西，從手機到鞋子，它們很可能都曾在一個鋼鐵箱子裡度過一段時光。貨櫃普通到大多數人從不曾留意，然而許多歷史學家都把貨櫃列為促成現代全球經濟的重要發展之一。它的重要性與其說在於箱子本身，不如說在於圍繞著它發展出來的整套系統。\n\n在貨櫃出現以前，大多數經過包裝的貨物都以所謂「散雜貨」的方式運送：一袋袋咖啡、一桶桶葡萄酒和一箱箱機器，每一件都要分別處理。一組組碼頭工人用網子或吊索把貨物吊上船，再用人工把它們塞進船艙，像拼圖一樣把貨物彼此卡好。這份工作緩慢、累人，而且常常很危險。一艘船可能要在港口停留好幾天，甚至好幾個星期，貨物遭竊或損壞也是常有的事。運送貨物的成本有很大一部分不是花在海上，而是花在碼頭。\n\n一般認為改變這一切的人，不是水手，而是一位卡車貨運業者。在美國南方經營卡車貨運公司的麥爾坎‧麥克林（Malcom McLean），很清楚自家司機在擁擠的碼頭上等待時浪費了多少時間。他的想法很簡單：與其把卡車上的貨物一件件卸下來，何不把整個拖車車廂直接吊上船？1956 年 4 月，一艘由油輪改裝而成的「理想 X 號」（Ideal X），從紐澤西州的紐華克駛往德州休士頓，甲板上載著 58 個這樣的箱子。這趟航行普遍被視為現代貨櫃航運的誕生，裝貨成本也大幅下降。\n\n然而，光有箱子還不夠。起初，每家航運公司都使用自己的貨櫃尺寸，未必適用於別家公司的船隻或起重機。1960 年代，產業團體和標準組織議定了標準尺寸，以及裝在貨櫃邊角的標準配件（角件），讓貨櫃能被吊起、堆疊並互相鎖緊。由此產生的 20 呎和 40 呎貨櫃，可以從船轉到火車、再轉到卡車，全程完全不必打開，裡面的貨物從工廠到倉庫都不會被動到。真正讓貨櫃具有革命性的，是這種在不同運輸方式之間無縫接軌的移動，而不只是箱子本身。\n\n它的影響遠遠超出了碼頭。隨著運貨變得便宜，企業可以在一個國家製造零件、在另一個國家組裝，再把產品銷往世界各地；許多經濟學家認為，這種模式促成了東亞製造業的快速成長。港口的版圖也隨之改變。貨櫃碼頭需要廣大的空地來容納起重機和成堆的貨櫃，因此像曼哈頓和倫敦那些擁擠的老碼頭逐漸沒落，新的碼頭則在有空間的地方興起。與此同時，臺灣的高雄也成為亞洲主要的貨櫃樞紐港之一。\n\n並不是每個人都從中受益。貨櫃化讓許多碼頭工人失去了工作：過去裝一艘船需要一大群工人，如今一小組操作起重機的人員就能更快完成，而舊碼頭周圍關係緊密的社區也逐漸凋零。工會曾為這項改變奮力抗爭，在一些港口，他們爭取到補償金或收入保障，作為接受新作業方式的交換條件。貨櫃的故事提醒我們，有些影響深遠的科技，外表看起來卻平淡無奇，而它們帶來的好處與代價，很少被平均分攤。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["A plain metal box and the system built around it changed trade, ports and work.", "Malcom McLean grew rich by designing a faster and larger kind of oil tanker.", "Dockworkers were mainly to blame for the slow growth of world trade before 1956.", "The world's busiest ports are still found in the centers of old port cities."], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文主旨：第一段指出貨櫃的重要性 lies less in the box itself than in the system that grew up around it；第二至四段說明貨櫃出現前後裝卸方式的改變與尺寸標準化；第五段談全球貿易與港口版圖的改變；第六段談碼頭工人失去工作。可知本文在說明一個平凡的金屬箱及其周邊系統，如何改變了貿易、港口和工作。(B) 麥克林是貨運業者，理想 X 號是由舊油輪改裝而成，並非他設計的新型油輪；(C) 文中說的是裝卸方式本身緩慢昂貴，並未把責任歸咎於碼頭工人；(D) 與第五段相反，老港市中心的碼頭已經沒落。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what was one problem with shipping before containers?", "options": ["Ships were too small to carry heavy machinery across the ocean.", "Ports had too few workers to load and unload their ships.", "Coffee and machinery could not be lifted aboard in nets or slings.", "Ships stayed in port for a long time, and cargo was often stolen or damaged."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：A ship could spend days or even weeks in port, and theft and damage were common. 也就是船隻要在港口停留很久，貨物也常遭竊或損壞。(A) 文中未提到船隻太小；(B) 文中描述一組組碼頭工人搬運貨物，並未說人手不足；(C) 與 Gangs of dockworkers lifted goods aboard in nets or slings 不符，貨物正是用網子或吊索吊上船的。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why were standard container sizes so important?", "options": ["They let each shipping company design its own special cranes.", "They allowed goods to move between ships, trains and trucks without unpacking.", "They made containers much lighter and cheaper to build.", "They made it easier for officials to open and inspect cargo."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：The resulting 20-foot and 40-foot boxes could move from ship to train to truck without ever being opened，並說 This seamless movement between different kinds of transport... is what made the container revolutionary。標準尺寸讓貨物能在船、火車和卡車之間轉運而不必拆開。(A) 正好相反，起初各公司使用自己的尺寸才造成不便，標準化是為了讓大家通用；(C) 文中未提到貨櫃的重量或造價；(D) 文中說 their contents untouched from factory to warehouse，並未提到方便官員開箱檢查。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"seamless\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["smooth and uninterrupted", "slow and carefully checked", "costly and complicated", "secret and hidden"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段前文說貨櫃 could move from ship to train to truck without ever being opened，而且 contents untouched from factory to warehouse，可知 seamless movement 指在不同運輸方式之間「順暢、不中斷」的移動（字面意思是「沒有接縫的」）。(B) 緩慢且仔細檢查、(C) 昂貴且複雜，都與貨櫃省時省錢的特點相反；(D) 祕密隱藏與文意無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the writer's view of containerization?", "options": ["It benefited dockworkers more than it benefited anyone else.", "It was a mistake that the unions should have stopped.", "It brought great gains but also real losses for some people.", "It is too dull a subject to deserve serious attention."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段說明貨櫃降低運輸成本、促進全球貿易，第六段卻以 Not everyone gained 開頭，指出碼頭工人失去工作、社區凋零，最後說 their benefits and costs are rarely shared equally。可推知作者認為貨櫃化帶來巨大好處，但也讓部分人蒙受實際損失。(A) 與第六段相反，碼頭工人正是受害者；(B) 作者只陳述工會的抗爭，並未主張工會應該阻止這項改變；(D) 作者雖說有些科技 look remarkably dull（外表平淡無奇），卻是要強調它們影響深遠、值得重視。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a287", "title": "Hot Water, Cold Questions: The Puzzle of the Mpemba Effect", "title_zh": "熱水先結冰？姆潘巴效應之謎", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Science", "Education"], "passage": "Ask almost anyone whether hot or cold water will freeze first, and the answer seems obvious. Cold water starts closer to zero degrees, so it should get there sooner, while hot water must first lose all its extra heat. Yet for over half a century, scientists have debated whether hot water can sometimes freeze faster. The idea is known as the Mpemba effect, and its story is as much about curiosity as about physics.\n\nIn 1963, Erasto Mpemba, a schoolboy in what is now Tanzania, was making ice cream with his classmates. Students were supposed to let their boiled milk-and-sugar mixtures cool before putting them in the freezer, but Mpemba, afraid of losing the last space, put his in while it was still hot. To his surprise, it froze before a classmate's cooler mixture. When he later raised the matter, a teacher waved it away as \"Mpemba's physics,\" and for a time his classmates made fun of him.\n\nHis chance came when Denis Osborne, a British physicist teaching at a university in Dar es Salaam, gave a talk at Mpemba's school. At the end, Mpemba asked why hot water might freeze first. Osborne did not dismiss the question. Instead, he had it tested in his laboratory and found that, in some cases, the warmer water did freeze sooner. In 1969, the two published a short paper describing their results. As it turned out, similar observations had been recorded as far back as ancient Greece, but the question had never been settled.\n\nSeveral explanations have been proposed. Hot water evaporates faster, so a hot sample loses some of its mass and has less water left to freeze. Hot water also has stronger convection currents, which may carry heat to the surface faster. Water that has been heated holds less dissolved gas, which could change how it cools and freezes. Finally, there is supercooling: water often drops below zero before ice actually forms, and some scientists suggest that water which was heated first supercools less, so it starts freezing sooner. Each idea is plausible, but none has been shown to explain every result.\n\nCareful experiments, however, have produced mixed results. The outcome can depend on many details: the shape of the container, the amount of water, the freezer and even where the thermometer sits. There is also the question of what \"freezing first\" means: forming the first ice crystals, turning solid all the way through, or simply reaching zero degrees. In 2016, two researchers in Britain who carefully timed how long samples took to cool to zero found no evidence that the initially hotter water got there first. Some physicists now argue that, once measurements are properly controlled, the effect may disappear altogether, though others disagree.\n\nWhether or not the effect is real for ordinary water, the story carries a lesson. A question that any student can ask, using nothing more than a freezer and a clock, has resisted a clear answer for decades, not because scientists are careless but because simple-looking systems can hide surprising complexity. Mpemba's experience also shows the value of taking a strange observation seriously instead of laughing it off. Good science often begins not with an answer, but with a question that refuses to go away.", "passage_zh": "隨便問一個人，熱水和冷水哪一個會先結冰，答案似乎再明顯不過。冷水的起始溫度比較接近零度，應該比較快到達冰點；熱水則必須先散掉所有多出來的熱量。然而半個多世紀以來，科學家一直在爭論熱水是否有時反而結冰得更快。這個概念被稱為「姆潘巴效應」，而它的故事既關乎物理，也同樣關乎好奇心。\n\n1963 年，在今日坦尚尼亞就讀中學的埃拉斯托‧姆潘巴（Erasto Mpemba）正和同學們一起做冰淇淋。學生們原本應該先讓煮過的牛奶加糖混合物冷卻，再放進冷凍庫，但姆潘巴怕失去最後一個空位，就趁混合物還熱的時候放了進去。令他驚訝的是，他的混合物竟然比一位同學較涼的混合物先結冰。後來他提起這件事時，一位老師不屑一顧，說那只是「姆潘巴的物理學」；有一段時間，同學們都拿這件事取笑他。\n\n他的機會出現在英國物理學家丹尼斯‧奧斯朋（Denis Osborne）到姆潘巴的學校演講時；奧斯朋當時在三蘭港的一所大學任教。演講結束時，姆潘巴問他，為什麼熱水可能會先結冰。奧斯朋並沒有對這個問題置之不理，而是請人在自己的實驗室裡進行測試，結果發現在某些情況下，較溫暖的水確實比較早結冰。1969 年，兩人共同發表了一篇簡短的論文，說明他們的結果。後來人們發現，類似的觀察早在古希臘時代就有記載，但這個問題始終沒有定論。\n\n人們提出了幾種解釋。熱水蒸發得比較快，因此熱的樣本會失去一部分質量，需要結冰的水也就比較少。熱水的對流也比較強，可能更快把熱量帶到表面。加熱過的水所含的溶解氣體較少，這可能會改變它冷卻和結冰的方式。最後還有「過冷」現象：水常常要降到零度以下之後才真正開始結冰，而有些科學家認為，先加熱過的水過冷的程度較小，因此比較早開始結冰。每一種說法都言之成理，但沒有一種被證明能解釋所有的結果。\n\n然而，嚴謹的實驗得出的結果並不一致。結果可能取決於許多細節：容器的形狀、水量、冷凍庫，甚至溫度計放在什麼位置。此外還有一個問題：「先結冰」究竟是什麼意思？是形成第一批冰晶、整個完全凍硬，還是只是降到零度？2016 年，英國的兩位研究者仔細測量了樣本冷卻到零度所需的時間，結果找不到任何證據顯示原本較熱的水會先到達零度。有些物理學家如今認為，只要把測量條件控制好，這個效應可能就會完全消失，不過也有人不同意。\n\n無論這個效應對一般的水來說是否真的存在，這個故事都帶來了一個教訓。一個任何學生只要用冷凍庫和時鐘就能提出的問題，竟然幾十年來都找不到明確的答案；這並不是因為科學家粗心，而是因為看似簡單的系統，可能隱藏著令人意想不到的複雜性。姆潘巴的經歷也說明了認真看待奇怪的觀察、而不是一笑置之的價值。好的科學往往不是從答案開始，而是從一個揮之不去的問題開始。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How schools in Tanzania taught students to make ice cream", "Why hot water always freezes faster than cold water", "How Denis Osborne proved that the Mpemba effect was false", "How a schoolboy's odd observation became a puzzle scientists still debate"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文主旨：第一段提出科學家爭論了半個多世紀的問題——熱水是否有時反而結冰得更快；第二、三段敘述姆潘巴做冰淇淋時的意外發現，以及奧斯朋如何認真看待並與他發表論文；第四、五段說明各種可能的解釋與分歧的實驗結果；第六段指出 A question that any student can ask... has resisted a clear answer for decades。可知本文在說明一個學生的奇特觀察，如何成為科學家至今仍在爭論的謎題。(A) 做冰淇淋只是故事的開端；(B) 說法太絕對，第五段指出實驗結果並不一致，這個效應甚至可能並不存在；(C) 第三段說奧斯朋發現 in some cases, the warmer water did freeze sooner，他並未證明此效應是錯的。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did Mpemba put his ice-cream mixture into the freezer while it was still hot?", "options": ["He worried that there would be no space left for it.", "His teacher had told him that hot mixtures freeze faster.", "He wanted to test an idea he had read about in a book.", "He had forgotten that it was supposed to cool first."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Mpemba, afraid of losing the last space, put his in while it was still hot. 他怕失去冷凍庫最後一個空位，所以趁熱放進去。(B) 文中說老師後來 waved it away（不屑一顧），並未告訴他熱的混合物結冰較快；(C) 文中未提到他讀過相關書籍；(D) 他是因為怕沒有空位而刻意提早放入，並非忘記要先冷卻。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, how might evaporation help hot water freeze first?", "options": ["It adds extra heat to the air inside the freezer.", "It increases the amount of gas dissolved in the water.", "It leaves a smaller amount of water that needs to freeze.", "It makes the water drop below zero before ice forms."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：Hot water evaporates faster, so a hot sample loses some of its mass and has less water left to freeze. 蒸發使熱水樣本失去部分質量，需要結冰的水就變少了。(A) 文中未提到蒸發會讓冷凍庫裡的空氣變熱；(B) 正好相反，同段說加熱過的水 holds less dissolved gas；(D) 降到零度以下才結冰是「過冷」現象，與蒸發無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"dismiss\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["answer in great detail", "write down carefully", "repeat to other people", "refuse to consider seriously"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Osborne did not dismiss the question. Instead, he had it tested in his laboratory... 奧斯朋沒有 dismiss 這個問題，「反而」在實驗室裡加以測試，這也和第二段老師 waved it away（不屑一顧）的態度形成對比，可知 dismiss 意為「不予理會、不當一回事」。(A) 詳細回答、(B) 仔細記下、(C) 轉述給別人，都無法與 Instead, he had it tested 形成對比，皆不符文意。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following can be inferred from paragraph 5?", "options": ["The 2016 study showed that Mpemba had made up his story.", "Whether hot water seems to freeze first may depend on how a test is set up and measured.", "Scientists now agree that the effect occurs in all kinds of liquids.", "Modern freezers are too different from older ones to be compared."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：The outcome can depend on many details: the shape of the container, the amount of water, the freezer and even where the thermometer sits，接著指出「先結冰」的定義也不明確，並說 once measurements are properly controlled, the effect may disappear altogether。可推知熱水是否「看起來」先結冰，可能取決於實驗如何設計與測量。(A) 2016 年的研究只是找不到原本較熱的水先降到零度的證據，並未說姆潘巴捏造故事；(C) 文中說 others disagree，科學家並未達成共識，也未提到所有液體；(D) 文中未比較不同年代的冷凍庫。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a288", "title": "Mandarin, Hokkien and a Dash of English: Why Mixing Languages Takes Skill", "title_zh": "華語、台語，再加一點英語：混用語言為什麼需要真功夫", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Culture", "Education"], "passage": "You are chatting with classmates in Mandarin when an English word like \"deadline\" slips out, followed by a Taiwanese Hokkien phrase from your grandmother. An adult nearby frowns and tells you to \"speak properly,\" and suddenly you feel embarrassed, as if mixing languages proved that you had mastered none of them. Linguists, however, see the scene very differently. What you were doing is called code-switching: moving between two or more languages within a single conversation or even a single sentence. It is one of the most common things bilingual people do.\n\nFor much of the twentieth century, code-switching had a poor reputation. Teachers, parents and even some scholars regarded it as a sign of deficiency: speakers who mixed languages were assumed to be confused, careless or simply unable to finish a thought in either one. Mixed varieties were given mocking labels, and children in many countries were told to keep their languages strictly separate.\n\nThat view changed once researchers began recording and analyzing real bilingual conversations. What they found was not chaos but order. Switches, many studies have shown, tend to fall at points where the grammar of the two languages fits together, so that each part of the sentence sounds natural in its own language. Bilinguals can usually tell at once when a mixed sentence sounds wrong, even if they cannot explain why. An influential study of Spanish–English speakers in New York even found that the most intricate switches, those made mid-sentence, came mainly from the people most at home in both languages. Mixing well, in other words, requires knowing both systems well.\n\nGrammar explains how people switch, but not why. Here the answers are largely social. A switch can signal identity, reminding listeners which communities a speaker belongs to. It can create closeness, since a language shared only by friends or family draws a circle around those who understand it. It can also add humor or emphasis, or let speakers quote someone in that person's own words. Sometimes a word in one language simply captures an idea better than anything in the other.\n\nTaiwan offers a rich example. Many people grow up hearing Mandarin at school, Hokkien at home or in markets, and English in class and online. In one conversation, a speaker might discuss work in Mandarin while dropping in English terms such as \"case\" or \"meeting,\" then switch to Hokkien to tease a friend or to tell a story the way an older relative would. Politicians campaigning on the island often turn to Hokkien to sound closer to local voters. Each choice tells listeners something about the speaker and the relationship.\n\nThis does not mean every situation welcomes mixed speech. In an exam essay, a formal speech or a conversation with someone who understands only one of your languages, sticking to a single language is usually wiser. Yet knowing when to switch and when not to is itself part of the skill. So the next time you move from Mandarin to Hokkien to English in one breath, there is no need to feel ashamed. Your sentence is evidence not of a weak mind but of one that holds several languages and moves between them with care.", "passage_zh": "你正用華語和同學聊天，一個像 deadline 這樣的英文字脫口而出，接著又冒出一句從阿嬤那裡學來的台語。旁邊一位大人皺起眉頭，要你「好好講話」，你突然覺得很難為情，彷彿混用語言就證明你哪一種語言都沒學好。然而，語言學家對這個場景的看法截然不同。你剛才做的事叫做「語碼轉換」（code-switching），也就是在同一段對話、甚至同一個句子裡，在兩種以上的語言之間切換。這是雙語者最常做的事情之一。\n\n在二十世紀的大半時間裡，語碼轉換的名聲並不好。老師、家長，甚至一些學者，都把它視為能力不足的表徵：混用語言的人被認為思緒混亂、說話隨便，或根本無法用任何一種語言把一個想法說完。混合而成的語言變體被冠上嘲諷的稱號，許多國家的孩子也被告知要把自己會的語言嚴格分開。\n\n等到研究者開始錄下並分析真實的雙語對話，這種看法才有所改變。他們發現的不是一團混亂，而是秩序。許多研究顯示，語言切換往往發生在兩種語言的文法能夠接合的地方，因此句子的每一部分，在各自的語言裡聽起來都很自然。一個混合句聽起來不對勁時，雙語者通常馬上就聽得出來，即使說不出原因。一項針對紐約西班牙語與英語使用者、深具影響力的研究甚至發現，最複雜的切換——也就是在句子中間進行的切換——主要出自那些對兩種語言最為熟悉的人。換句話說，要混得好，就必須對兩套系統都很了解。\n\n文法說明了人們「如何」切換，卻無法說明「為什麼」切換。這方面的答案大多和社會因素有關。語言切換可以表明身分，提醒聽者說話者屬於哪些群體。它可以拉近距離，因為只有朋友或家人才懂的語言，就像在聽得懂的人周圍畫了一個圈。它也可以增添幽默或加強語氣，或讓說話者用某人自己的原話來引述他。有時候，一種語言裡的某個字，就是比另一種語言裡的任何說法更能傳達某個概念。\n\n臺灣就是一個豐富的例子。許多人從小在學校聽華語，在家裡或市場聽台語，在課堂上和網路上聽英語。在一段對話中，說話者可能用華語談工作，同時夾雜 case 或 meeting 之類的英文詞彙，接著又換成台語來調侃朋友，或用長輩的口吻講故事。在臺灣各地競選的政治人物，也常常改說台語，好讓自己聽起來和在地選民更親近。每一個選擇，都在告訴聽者一些關於說話者以及彼此關係的訊息。\n\n這並不代表每一種場合都歡迎混合的說話方式。在考試作文、正式演講中，或是和只懂你其中一種語言的人交談時，只用一種語言通常比較明智。不過，知道何時該切換、何時不該切換，本身就是這項技巧的一部分。所以，下次你一口氣從華語換到台語再換到英語時，不必覺得丟臉。你的句子證明的不是頭腦不靈光，而是一個能容納好幾種語言、並能用心在其間穿梭的頭腦。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?", "options": ["To warn students that mixing languages can lower their exam scores", "To explain how the grammar of Hokkien differs from that of Mandarin", "To reassure students that mixing languages reflects skill rather than weakness", "To argue that schools should teach Hokkien in place of English"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文是寫給為混用語言感到難為情的學生。第一段指出 Linguists, however, see the scene very differently；第三段說明語碼轉換有規則可循，並強調 Mixing well, in other words, requires knowing both systems well；最後一段更明言 there is no need to feel ashamed。可知作者的目的是讓學生明白，混用語言代表的是能力而非弱點。(A) 第六段只說考試作文等場合宜用單一語言，並非警告混用會拉低成績，也不是全文重點；(B) 文中並未比較台語和華語的文法差異；(D) 文中沒有主張學校應以台語取代英語。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what did researchers discover when they studied real bilingual conversations?", "options": ["Most switches happened when speakers forgot a word in their first language.", "Switches usually occurred where the grammar of both languages could fit together.", "Speakers who switched often tended to be less fluent in both of their languages.", "Bilinguals could seldom tell whether a mixed sentence sounded natural or not."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Switches, many studies have shown, tend to fall at points where the grammar of the two languages fits together. 語言切換往往發生在兩種語言文法能夠接合之處，與 (B) 相符。(A) 第三段並未說切換是因為忘了字，第二段提到「無法把想法說完」只是過去的偏見；(C) 與該段相反，研究發現最複雜的切換出自 the people most at home in both languages，也就是對兩種語言最熟悉的人；(D) 與 Bilinguals can usually tell at once when a mixed sentence sounds wrong 相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why might a speaker in Taiwan switch into Hokkien?", "options": ["To joke with a friend or retell a story as an older family member might", "To keep a conversation secret from classmates who understand English", "To show respect to a teacher during a formal lesson at school", "To replace English terms that are too difficult to pronounce"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：then switch to Hokkien to tease a friend or to tell a story the way an older relative would。說話者可能換成台語來調侃朋友，或用長輩的口吻說故事，與 (A) 相符。(B) 文中沒有提到用台語瞞著懂英語的同學；(C) 文中未說換成台語是為了在正式課堂上對老師表示尊敬，第六段反而指出正式場合通常宜用單一語言；(D) 文中說 case、meeting 等英文詞是夾雜在華語中使用的，並未說要用台語取代難發音的英文詞。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"deficiency\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["an unusual talent", "a deliberate choice", "a lack of something needed", "a strong preference"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段說老師、家長把語碼轉換視為 a sign of deficiency，接著說明混用語言的人被認為 confused, careless or simply unable to finish a thought in either one，也就是「能力不足」，可知 deficiency 意為「缺乏（必要的東西）、不足」。(A)「不尋常的天分」意思相反；(B)「刻意的選擇」與 (D)「強烈的偏好」都不符合「被視為能力不足」的上下文。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How would the writer most likely respond to a student who mixes English words into a Mandarin chat with friends?", "options": ["The student should stop at once, because mixing shows poor language skills.", "The student should speak only English with friends in order to improve faster.", "The student is probably confused about the grammar of both languages.", "The student is using language skillfully, though not every situation suits it."], "answer": 3, "explain": "作者在第三段說混得好需要 knowing both systems well，最後一段又說 there is no need to feel ashamed，但也提醒 In an exam essay, a formal speech... sticking to a single language is usually wiser。可推知作者會認為，和朋友聊天時夾雜英文是熟練運用語言的表現，只是並非每種場合都適合。(A) 正是第二段所描述、作者不認同的舊觀念；(B) 文中並未建議和朋友只說英語；(C) 第三段指出擅長切換的人往往最熟悉兩種語言，與「搞不清楚文法」相反。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a289", "title": "Come Back, All Is Forgiven: Lindenvale Library Ends Overdue Fines", "title_zh": "歡迎回來，既往不咎：林登維爾圖書館取消逾期罰款", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Notice", "Education"], "passage": "Lindenvale Public Library. Notice to All Members: The End of Overdue Fines. Issued by the Office of the Library Director on 15 November 2026. From 1 January 2027, the Library will no longer charge fines on books, magazines, audiobooks or DVDs returned after their due date. On the same date, all outstanding overdue fines on existing accounts will be cancelled in full. This change was approved by the Library Board after a review of borrowing records, financial reports and members' comments, and it follows a growing trend among public libraries in many countries.\n\n1. Why fines are ending. The Library exists to give everyone free and equal access to books and information, and the review found that fines were working against that purpose. A few dollars means little to some households, but for families on tight budgets, a growing fine can become a reason to stop visiting altogether. Children and teenagers, who often have no money of their own, were especially affected: many of the accounts blocked for unpaid fines belonged to young readers. In short, those who most needed the Library were the most likely to be kept away from it.\n\n2. What fines achieved. Fines also proved far less useful than is often assumed. They provided only a very small share of the Library's annual income, and much of that was offset by the staff time spent recording and collecting them. Meanwhile, many public libraries that have stopped charging fines have reported that borrowers who had stayed away for years began to return, in some cases bringing long-overdue items with them. The Board expects Lindenvale to see similar results.\n\n3. What will not change. Ending fines does not mean ending responsibility. Due dates remain, and members will still receive a reminder by email or text message three days before an item is due. If an item is more than 30 days overdue, the member's borrowing privileges will be paused until it is returned. If it is more than 60 days overdue, it will be treated as lost and the member will be charged the cost of replacing it; this charge will be removed once the item is returned in good condition. Damaged items must still be paid for, and printing and photocopying charges are unaffected.\n\n4. An invitation to return. If you have avoided the Library because of old fines, we would like to welcome you back. From 1 January, simply bring any overdue items to any branch. No fines will be charged, no explanations will be required, and your account will be restored on the spot. Members whose cards have expired may renew them free of charge during the same visit. If you have lost an item and are worried about the cost of replacing it, please speak to a member of staff, who can discuss a payment plan with you in confidence.\n\n5. Further information. Answers to frequently asked questions are available on the Library's website and at every branch's information desk. Questions about individual accounts may be directed to Member Services by telephone or email. The Library thanks its members for their comments and looks forward to welcoming both familiar and new faces in the year ahead.", "passage_zh": "林登維爾公共圖書館。致全體會員公告：取消逾期罰款。本公告由圖書館館長室於 2026 年 11 月 15 日發布。自 2027 年 1 月 1 日起，凡於到期日之後歸還的圖書、雜誌、有聲書或 DVD，本館將不再收取罰款。同日起，現有帳戶中所有尚未繳納的逾期罰款也將全數註銷。這項改變是圖書館理事會在檢視借閱紀錄、財務報告及會員意見之後核准的，也順應了許多國家公共圖書館日益普遍的趨勢。\n\n一、取消罰款的原因。圖書館存在的目的，是讓每個人都能免費、平等地取得書籍與資訊，而這次檢討發現，罰款正與此目的背道而馳。幾塊錢對某些家庭來說微不足道，但對於手頭拮据的家庭而言，不斷累積的罰款可能成為他們從此不再上門的理由。兒童和青少年往往沒有自己的錢，受到的影響尤其嚴重：許多因未繳罰款而遭停權的帳戶，都屬於年輕讀者。簡言之，最需要圖書館的人，恰恰最可能被擋在門外。\n\n二、罰款的實際成效。罰款的用處也遠不如一般人所想像。罰款只占本館年度收入的極小部分，而且其中很大一部分，又被登錄與收取罰款所耗費的人力時間抵銷。與此同時，許多已停止收取罰款的公共圖書館都表示，多年未曾上門的借閱者開始回來了，有些人甚至帶回了逾期很久的館藏。理事會預期林登維爾也會看到類似的成果。\n\n三、維持不變的規定。取消罰款並不代表取消責任。到期日依然存在，會員在館藏到期前三天，仍會收到電子郵件或簡訊提醒。館藏逾期超過 30 天者，該會員的借閱權將暫停，直到歸還為止。逾期超過 60 天者，該館藏將視為遺失，會員須支付重置費用；一旦館藏完好歸還，此項費用即予撤銷。損壞的館藏仍須賠償，列印及影印的收費也不受影響。\n\n四、歡迎回來。如果您曾因舊罰款而不敢踏進圖書館，我們誠摯歡迎您回來。自 1 月 1 日起，只要將任何逾期館藏帶到任一分館即可。我們不會收取罰款，也不需要您做任何解釋，您的帳戶將當場恢復。借書證已過期的會員，可在同一次到館時免費換發。如果您遺失了館藏，並擔心重置費用，請洽詢館員，館員可在保密的情況下與您討論分期付款方案。\n\n五、其他資訊。常見問題的解答可在本館網站及各分館服務台取得。個別帳戶的相關問題，可以電話或電子郵件洽詢會員服務組。本館感謝全體會員提供的意見，並期待在新的一年迎接熟悉的老朋友與新面孔。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this notice?", "options": ["To remind members to return their overdue items before the end of the year", "To ask members for their opinions on a proposed change to library rules", "To announce that overdue fines will end and explain the reasons for the change", "To explain why the Library must raise its charges for lost and damaged items"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段（開頭段落）：From 1 January 2027, the Library will no longer charge fines... 宣布自 2027 年 1 月 1 日起取消逾期罰款，第二、三段（Section 1 Why fines are ending、Section 2 What fines achieved）再說明原因，可知公告的目的是宣布取消逾期罰款並解釋理由。(A) 公告並非提醒會員在年底前還書，第五段（Section 4）反而歡迎大家從 1 月 1 日起把逾期館藏帶回來；(B) 第一段指出這項改變 was approved by the Library Board，已經定案，並非徵求意見；(D) 第四段（Section 3）只說遺失與損壞的館藏仍須賠償，並沒有提高收費。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to Section 1, why did the Library consider fines unfair?", "options": ["They were higher than the fines charged by other public libraries.", "They were charged to adults but never to children or teenagers.", "They were collected by staff members who lacked proper training.", "They discouraged poorer families and young readers from using the Library."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段（Section 1）：for families on tight budgets, a growing fine can become a reason to stop visiting altogether... many of the accounts blocked for unpaid fines belonged to young readers. 罰款讓經濟拮据的家庭和年輕讀者不再上門，與 (D) 相符。(A) 文中沒有和其他圖書館的罰款金額比較；(B) 與原文相反，許多遭停權的帳戶正屬於兒童和青少年；(C) 文中未提及館員訓練不足。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following will still apply after 1 January 2027?", "options": ["Members will pay a small amount for each day that an item is late.", "A member whose item is over two months late will be charged for a replacement.", "Members whose cards have expired will have to pay to renew them.", "Borrowers will no longer receive reminders before their items are due."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段（Section 3）：If it is more than 60 days overdue, it will be treated as lost and the member will be charged the cost of replacing it. 逾期超過 60 天（約兩個月）的館藏將被視為遺失，會員須支付重置費用，與 (B) 相符。(A) 第一段說 the Library will no longer charge fines，新制下不再按逾期天數收取罰款；(C) 第五段（Section 4）說 Members whose cards have expired may renew them free of charge，可免費換發；(D) 第四段（Section 3）說會員 will still receive a reminder，到期前三天仍會收到提醒。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"outstanding\" in paragraph 1 (the opening paragraph) is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["still unpaid", "remarkably good", "newly charged", "unusually large"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段（開頭段落，不是 Section 1）：all outstanding overdue fines on existing accounts will be cancelled in full. 這裡指現有帳戶中「尚未繳納」的逾期罰款將全數註銷，outstanding 意為「未付清的」。(B)「非常出色的」是 outstanding 常見的另一個意思，但用來形容罰款不合文意；(C)「新近加收的」與 (D)「異常龐大的」都不符合上下文，公告註銷的是所有尚未繳納的罰款，與新舊或金額大小無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Ms. Chen has kept a library book at home for three years and has never paid the fines on it. What can be inferred from the notice?", "options": ["She must pay all of her old fines before she is allowed to return the book.", "She can return the book and borrow again without paying her old fines.", "She will be banned from the Library because the book is so badly overdue.", "She will have to explain to staff why she kept the book for so long."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說所有未繳的逾期罰款將 cancelled in full；第五段（Section 4）又說 simply bring any overdue items to any branch. No fines will be charged, no explanations will be required, and your account will be restored on the spot；第四段（Section 3）也說逾期館藏的重置費用 will be removed once the item is returned in good condition。可推知陳女士只要把書還回去，就能在不付舊罰款的情況下再次借書。(A) 舊罰款已全數註銷，不必先繳清；(C) 公告中沒有因逾期太久而禁止使用圖書館的規定，借閱權只會暫停到歸還為止；(D) 與 no explanations will be required 矛盾。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a290", "title": "On Fire or Just Lucky? The Hot Hand Revisited", "title_zh": "手感發燙，還是只是運氣好？重探「熱手效應」", "level": "Reading · C1", "tags": ["Sports", "Science"], "passage": "Every basketball fan has seen it happen. A player sinks one shot, then another, then a third, and the crowd is on its feet. Commentators say he is \"on fire,\" and teammates keep passing him the ball. Behind all this lies a simple belief: a player who has made several shots in a row has a \"hot hand\" and is more likely than usual to make the next one. To most fans and players, this seems too obvious to need proof.\n\nIn 1985, the psychologists Thomas Gilovich, Robert Vallone and Amos Tversky tested it anyway. They examined shooting records from the Philadelphia 76ers, free throws by the Boston Celtics, and a controlled experiment in which college players at Cornell University took shot after shot. Most fans they surveyed believed in the hot hand, but the data disagreed: a player was no more likely to score after a run of hits than after a run of misses. The hot hand, they concluded, was a cognitive illusion: because people expect random sequences to alternate more than they really do, they mistake chance streaks for skill.\n\nThe study became one of the best-known findings in the psychology of judgment. The \"hot hand fallacy\" entered textbooks as a classic example of the mind inventing patterns in randomness. Coaches and players protested that they knew better, but their protests were often treated as further proof of how deeply rooted the illusion was. For decades, the scientific verdict seemed settled.\n\nIn a paper published in 2018, however, the economists Joshua Miller and Adam Sanjurjo showed that the original analysis contained a subtle statistical bias. Their argument is easiest to see with a coin. Flip a fair coin four times and look only at the flips that come right after a head. You might expect about half of them to be heads. Yet if you repeat the exercise many times and average the share of heads across all the sets, the figure is only about 40 percent. In short sequences, simply picking out the attempts that follow a success quietly tilts the count against further successes. So a player whose chances never changed would appear to shoot worse after a streak, and one who showed \"no difference\" may in fact have been shooting better.\n\nAfter correcting for this bias in the original Cornell data, Miller and Sanjurjo reached the opposite conclusion: players were somewhat more likely to make a shot after a streak of hits. The effect appears to be modest rather than magical, and researchers still debate how large it is in real games, where defenders adjust and confident shooters may attempt harder shots. But the hot hand, it seems, is not purely an illusion.\n\nThe lesson reaches well beyond basketball. The 1985 study was the work of respected psychologists, including Tversky, a pioneer in research on errors of human judgment, and it had been widely cited for years. Yet a flaw hidden in its arithmetic went unnoticed for about three decades. Being skeptical of our intuitions is wise, but the tools we use to correct them deserve the same scrutiny. The fans in the stands, it turns out, were seeing something real, even if they exaggerated how much of it there was.", "passage_zh": "每個籃球迷都看過這種場面。一名球員投進一球，接著又進一球，然後是第三球，觀眾全都站了起來。球評說他「手感發燙」，隊友也不斷把球傳給他。這一切背後是一個簡單的信念：連續投進好幾球的球員擁有「熱手」（hot hand），下一球投進的機率會比平常更高。對大多數球迷和球員來說，這似乎是再明顯不過的事，根本不需要證明。\n\n1985 年，心理學家湯瑪斯‧季洛維奇、羅伯特‧瓦隆和阿莫斯‧特沃斯基還是決定加以檢驗。他們分析了費城七六人隊的投籃紀錄、波士頓塞爾提克隊的罰球紀錄，以及一項對照實驗，讓康乃爾大學的校隊球員一球接一球地投籃。他們調查的球迷大多相信熱手的存在，但數據卻不支持：球員在連續投進之後得分的機率，並不比連續投失之後更高。他們因此斷定，熱手是一種認知錯覺：由於人們以為隨機序列會比實際上更常來回交替，於是把純屬巧合的連續命中誤當成實力。\n\n這項研究成為判斷心理學中最廣為人知的發現之一。「熱手謬誤」被寫進教科書，成為大腦在隨機事件中憑空捏造規律的經典例子。教練和球員抗議說他們比誰都清楚，但他們的抗議往往被當成進一步的證據，證明這種錯覺有多根深柢固。數十年來，科學界的定論似乎已無可動搖。\n\n然而，經濟學家約書亞‧米勒和亞當‧桑胡爾霍在 2018 年發表的論文中指出，原本的分析中藏有一個不易察覺的統計偏誤。他們的論點用硬幣來說明最容易理解。把一枚公正的硬幣擲四次，只看緊接在正面之後的那幾次投擲。你可能以為其中大約一半會是正面。然而，如果你把這個練習重複很多次，再將每一組中正面所占的比例加以平均，得到的數字只有大約 40%。在很短的序列中，光是挑出緊接在成功之後的嘗試，就會悄悄讓計算結果不利於再次成功。因此，一名命中機率從未改變的球員，在連續命中後看起來會投得比較差；而一名看似「沒有差別」的球員，實際上可能投得更好。\n\n米勒和桑胡爾霍針對當年康乃爾實驗的數據修正了這個偏誤之後，得出了相反的結論：球員在連續投進之後，投進下一球的機率確實稍微高一些。這個效應看來只是有限的提升，並非什麼神奇的魔力；研究者至今仍在爭論它在真正的比賽中究竟有多大，因為在比賽中，防守者會調整策略，而充滿自信的射手也可能嘗試難度更高的出手。不過，熱手看來並不完全是一種錯覺。\n\n這個啟示遠遠超出籃球的範圍。1985 年的那項研究出自備受敬重的心理學家之手，其中包括研究人類判斷錯誤的先驅特沃斯基，而且這項研究多年來一直被廣泛引用。然而，藏在其計算中的一個缺陷，卻在大約三十年間都沒有人發現。對自己的直覺抱持懷疑是明智的，但我們用來修正直覺的工具，也值得同樣嚴格的檢視。事實證明，看台上的球迷確實看到了某種真實存在的東西，即使他們誇大了它的程度。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following would be the best title for the passage?", "options": ["Trust Your Eyes: Why Fans Always Understand the Game Best", "Heads or Tails: How Coin Flips Decide Basketball Games", "Better on Campus: Why College Players Outshoot Professionals", "The Hot Hand: An Illusion That Turned Out to Be Partly Real"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文先介紹球迷對「熱手」的信念（第一段），再說明 1985 年的研究認為那是 a cognitive illusion（第二段），接著指出 2018 年米勒與桑胡爾霍發現原分析有偏誤，修正後 the hot hand, it seems, is not purely an illusion（第五段）。可知最佳標題是「熱手：一個原來部分屬實的錯覺」。(A) 太極端，第六段指出球迷 exaggerated how much of it there was，並非球迷永遠最懂；(B) 擲硬幣只是第四段用來說明統計偏誤的例子，與比賽勝負無關；(C) 文中並未比較大學球員與職業球員的命中率。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what did the 1985 study find?", "options": ["Players grew more accurate the longer a streak of hits went on.", "A run of successful shots did not make the next shot more likely to go in.", "Most fans doubted that a player could ever develop a hot hand.", "Professional players shot better than college players in a controlled test."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：a player was no more likely to score after a run of hits than after a run of misses. 球員連續投進之後，下一球投進的機率並沒有比較高，與 (B) 相符。(A) 與研究結論相反；(C) 同段指出 Most fans they surveyed believed in the hot hand，受訪的球迷大多相信熱手；(D) 文中並未比較職業球員與大學球員的命中率。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What did Miller and Sanjurjo show about the original analysis?", "options": ["Its method made streaks seem less likely to continue than they really were.", "It had relied on records from too few professional basketball teams.", "It had mistakenly counted free throws as if they were ordinary shots.", "Its data had been changed to support what the researchers believed."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：simply picking out the attempts that follow a success quietly tilts the count against further successes. So a player whose chances never changed would appear to shoot worse after a streak。原本的分析方法會讓連續命中「看起來」比實際上更不容易延續，與 (A) 相符。(B) 文中未說原研究採用的職業球隊太少；(C) 文中未提到把罰球誤算成一般投籃；(D) 文中說的是不易察覺的統計偏誤（a subtle statistical bias），並非資料遭人竄改。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"modest\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["humble about one's own success", "carefully hidden from view", "limited in size or amount", "surprisingly large and strong"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：The effect appears to be modest rather than magical，並接著說研究者仍在爭論它在真實比賽中 how large it is，可知 modest 在此形容效應「規模不大、有限的」。(A)「對自己的成就謙虛」是 modest 形容人時的意思，不適用於形容「效應」；(B)「小心藏起來」與文意無關；(D)「出奇地大而強」意思相反，也與 rather than magical 矛盾。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["The methods used to correct our intuitions need to be checked carefully too.", "The conclusions that experts draw from data are rarely worth trusting.", "Fans' beliefs about shooting streaks have been proved entirely correct.", "Statistical studies of sport are too complicated to be of any real use."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第六段：Being skeptical of our intuitions is wise, but the tools we use to correct them deserve the same scrutiny. 作者認為用來修正直覺的方法，也應該和直覺一樣受到嚴格檢驗，與 (A) 相符。(B) 太極端，作者只是指出專家也可能出錯，並未說專家的結論不值得信任；(C) 與最後一句 even if they exaggerated how much of it there was 矛盾，球迷的信念只是部分正確；(D) 文中並未說運動統計研究沒有用處，修正後的分析反而得出了新的發現。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a291", "title": "Plank by Plank: The Ship of Theseus and the Puzzle of Staying the Same", "title_zh": "一塊接一塊地換：忒修斯之船與「同一性」之謎", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Culture", "Society"], "passage": "My grandfather bought his motorcycle the summer before he married and rode it until his knees gave out. By then little of the original machine survived. The engine had been rebuilt twice and the fuel tank replaced after a minor accident; the seat, wheels, chain and mirrors had been swapped out one by one as they wore. When a neighbor once asked whether it was really the same bike, my grandfather looked at him as though the question were faintly indecent. Of course it was. He had ridden it to his own wedding.\n\nWithout realizing it, the neighbor had raised one of the oldest puzzles in philosophy. An ancient Greek writer records that the Athenians preserved the ship on which the legendary hero Theseus had sailed home, removing each plank as it decayed and fitting a sound one in its place, until the vessel became a standing example for philosophers: once every timber had been replaced, was it still the same ship? Many centuries later, an English philosopher added a twist that turns the puzzle into a trap. Suppose someone had gathered the discarded planks and reassembled them, in their original order, into a second ship. Now there are two candidates, one with an unbroken history, the other made of the very wood on which Theseus stood.\n\nThe obvious answers each fail in instructive ways. If identity lies in material, the reassembled vessel is the genuine one, and the ship the Athenians tended so devotedly became an impostor somewhere along the way—though no one could say on which day, or with which plank. If identity lies in continuous history, the repaired ship wins, but the principle behaves oddly elsewhere: a temple replaced overnight by a perfect replica would cease to be itself, while one renewed piece by piece over a century would not. Some philosophers conclude that there is no hidden fact to discover, only a decision about how to use the word \"same,\" and that different purposes justify different decisions.\n\nJapan offers an example of a culture that seems to have made such a decision deliberately. At the Ise Grand Shrine, the principal sanctuaries have been rebuilt with fresh timber roughly every twenty years since the seventh century (apart from a long break during an era of civil war), each time on a neighboring plot, after which the old buildings are taken down. By the standard of material, nothing there is ancient. Yet few visitors would call the shrine a copy. What is handed down is not wood but knowledge: the techniques of carpenters who learn from those who built the previous shrine. Continuity, in this view, is something people do rather than something objects possess.\n\nThe puzzle grows more personal when we turn it on ourselves. It is often said that the body replaces all its cells every seven years, a claim that is tidy, memorable and false. The truth is more uneven: the lining of the gut renews itself within days and red blood cells within months, while most neurons in the brain's outer layer stay with us for life. Our opinions are even less faithful; the boy I was at fifteen would quarrel with most of what I believe now. If neither our material nor our minds stay fixed, what makes the boy in an old photograph and the man studying it one person rather than two? Perhaps the answer that serves the shrine serves us too: a chain of memories and habits, each link overlapping the next, so that no single change is ever total.\n\nCities pose the question on a grander scale, keeping their names and, more mysteriously, something of their character long after nearly every building and resident has changed. I think I now understand why my grandfather found the neighbor's question impolite. The motorcycle mattered to him not as a collection of parts but as the companion of his whole life, and every repair was an act of keeping faith with it. When he died, my father could not bring himself to sell it, and it waits in his garage now. If I ride it one day, I doubt I will ask whether it is still the same machine. The harder question will be whether I can look after it as he did—for that, I suspect, is what kept it the same all along.", "passage_zh": "我祖父在結婚前的那個夏天買下他的機車，一直騎到膝蓋撐不住為止。到了那時，原本那台車留下來的部分已經所剩無幾。引擎重新整修過兩次，油箱也在一次小車禍後換掉了；坐墊、輪子、鏈條和後照鏡則隨著磨損一一換新。有一次，一位鄰居問道：這真的還是同一台車嗎？祖父看著他的神情，彷彿這個問題有點失禮。當然是同一台。他當年就是騎著它去結婚的。\n\n那位鄰居不知不覺間，提出了哲學中最古老的難題之一。一位古希臘作家記載，雅典人一直保存著傳說中的英雄忒修斯航行返鄉時所乘的船：每當有木板腐朽，就把它拆下，換上一塊完好的木板，久而久之，這艘船成了哲學家們經常引用的例子——當每一根木料都被換過之後，它還是同一艘船嗎？許多世紀之後，一位英國哲學家為這個問題加上了一個轉折，讓難題變成了陷阱。假設有人把拆下來的舊木板收集起來，按照原本的順序重新組裝成第二艘船。現在就有了兩艘候選的船：一艘擁有未曾中斷的歷史，另一艘則是用忒修斯當年真正站過的木頭造成的。\n\n那些顯而易見的答案，各自以發人深省的方式碰了壁。如果同一性取決於材料，那麼重新組裝的那艘才是真品，而雅典人如此悉心照料的那艘船，則在某個時候變成了冒牌貨——儘管沒有人說得出是在哪一天、因為哪一塊木板。如果同一性取決於連續的歷史，那麼修補過的那艘船勝出，但這個原則套用到別處時卻顯得古怪：一座廟宇若在一夜之間被一模一樣的複製品取代，就不再是它自己；而一座在一百年間逐一翻新的廟宇卻仍然是。有些哲學家因此得出結論：這裡並沒有什麼隱藏的事實等待發現，只有一個關於該如何使用「同一」這個詞的決定，而不同的目的可以合理地導向不同的決定。\n\n日本提供了一個例子：一個文化似乎刻意做出了這樣的決定。在伊勢神宮，主要的正殿自七世紀以來，大約每二十年就用新的木料重建一次（只在一段漫長的戰亂時期中斷過），每次都建在相鄰的基地上，之後再拆除舊的建築。以材料的標準來看，那裡沒有任何東西是古老的。然而，很少有參訪者會說這座神宮是複製品。代代相傳的不是木頭，而是知識：木匠們向建造上一座神宮的前輩學來的技藝。從這個角度看，延續是人們「做」出來的，而不是物品本身「擁有」的。\n\n當我們把這個難題轉向自己，它就變得更切身了。人們常說，身體每七年就會把所有細胞換過一遍——這種說法簡潔、好記，卻是錯的。實際情況要參差得多：腸道內壁幾天之內就會更新，紅血球幾個月內更新，但大腦外層的大多數神經元卻會陪伴我們一輩子。我們的觀點就更不忠誠了；十五歲時的我，若聽到我現在的大多數想法，一定會跟我吵起來。如果我們的材料和心智都不是固定不變的，那麼是什麼讓舊照片中的男孩和正在端詳照片的這個男人是同一個人，而不是兩個人？也許適用於神宮的答案也同樣適用於我們：一條由記憶與習慣串成的鎖鏈，每一環都與下一環相互重疊，因此沒有任何一次改變是徹底的。\n\n城市則以更宏大的規模提出這個問題：即使幾乎每一棟建築、每一位居民都已更替，城市依然保有它的名字，而且更不可思議的是，還保有某種性格。我想我現在明白了，祖父為什麼會覺得鄰居的問題失禮。那台機車對他而言，重要的不是一堆零件的組合，而是陪伴他一生的夥伴；每一次修理，都是在對它信守一份承諾。祖父過世後，父親捨不得把它賣掉，如今它就停在父親的車庫裡等待著。如果有一天我騎上它，我想我不會問它是否還是同一台機器。更難的問題會是：我能不能像祖父那樣照顧它——因為我猜想，一直以來讓它保持為「同一台」的，正是這份照顧。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?", "options": ["To reflect, through personal and cultural examples, on how things and people remain themselves while changing", "To prove that the reassembled ship, rather than the repaired one, is the real ship of Theseus", "To describe how the writer's grandfather repaired his motorcycle over many years", "To argue that old buildings should always be preserved instead of being rebuilt"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以祖父的機車開頭，引出忒修斯之船的難題（第二段），再討論以材料或以歷史為準的答案（第三段）、伊勢神宮（第四段）、人的細胞與觀點（第五段）以及城市（第六段），最後回到機車：what kept it the same all along。可見作者是藉由個人與文化的例子，思考事物和人如何在改變中仍保持為自己。(B) 第三段說這些顯而易見的答案 each fail in instructive ways，作者並未主張重組的船才是真品；(C) 機車的修理只是開頭與結尾的例子，不是全文重點；(D) 第四段反而以定期重建的伊勢神宮為例，文中並未主張建築只能保存、不可重建。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what did the English philosopher add to the original puzzle?", "options": ["The claim that the Athenians had never really replaced any of the planks", "The suggestion that the old ship should be kept safely in a museum", "A second ship built from the old planks, which creates a rival claim to be the original", "The proof that the identity of a ship depends only on the wood it is made of"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Suppose someone had gathered the discarded planks and reassembled them, in their original order, into a second ship. Now there are two candidates。這位英國哲學家設想用拆下的舊木板重組出第二艘船，於是兩艘船都可以宣稱自己是原來那艘。(A) 與同段雅典人 removing each plank as it decayed 的記載相反；(B) 文中完全沒有提到博物館；(D) 他只是讓難題更棘手，並未證明任何答案，第三段更指出以材料為準的答案同樣有問題。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer say about the idea that the body replaces all of its cells every seven years?", "options": ["It is true for most parts of the body except the skin.", "It was first suggested by the English philosopher in paragraph 2.", "It underestimates how quickly the brain replaces its neurons.", "It is easy to remember but wrong, since cells are renewed at very different rates."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：a claim that is tidy, memorable and false. The truth is more uneven: the lining of the gut renews itself within days and red blood cells within months, while most neurons in the brain's outer layer stay with us for life。作者說這個說法好記卻是錯的，因為不同細胞更新的速度差異很大。(A) 作者說這個說法是錯的，而且文中並未提到皮膚；(B) 文中沒有說這個說法出自那位英國哲學家；(C) 正好相反，大腦外層的大多數神經元會陪伴我們一生，並不會很快被替換。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"impostor\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a vessel that has been badly damaged", "something that falsely claims to be what it is not", "a ship that has been sold to a stranger", "an object that is kept only for display"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：If identity lies in material, the reassembled vessel is the genuine one, and the ship the Athenians tended so devotedly became an impostor somewhere along the way。如果以材料為準，重組的船才是「真品」(genuine)，那麼雅典人細心照料的那艘船就成了「冒充原船的冒牌貨」，impostor 正好與 genuine 形成對比。(A) 文中並未說那艘船受損；(C) 文中沒有提到把船賣掉；(D)「只供展示的物品」與文意無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about how the writer now thinks of the motorcycle?", "options": ["It stopped being the same machine once its engine had been rebuilt.", "It ought to be sold, since almost none of its original parts remain.", "The neighbor was right to doubt that it was still the same bike.", "What keeps it the same is the care given to it more than its original parts."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第六段：The motorcycle mattered to him not as a collection of parts... every repair was an act of keeping faith with it，最後又說 The harder question will be whether I can look after it as he did—for that, I suspect, is what kept it the same all along。可推知作者認為讓機車保持「同一台」的，是人對它的照顧與用心，而不是原本的零件。(A) 作者從未這樣主張；(B) 文中說父親捨不得賣掉，作者也設想將來會騎它；(C) 作者說他現在明白祖父為何覺得這個問題失禮，並不認同鄰居的懷疑。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a292", "title": "Hitting the Target, Missing the Point: The Trouble with Goodhart's Law", "title_zh": "命中目標，卻錯失重點：古德哈特定律的難題", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Society", "Education"], "passage": "An old joke about Soviet central planning, almost certainly invented, tells of a nail factory whose output was judged by weight. The managers obligingly produced a few enormous nails, far too heavy to be of any use. When the planners switched to counting nails instead, the factory turned out millions of tiny ones, too flimsy to hold anything together. The joke has survived because it captures something that real institutions keep rediscovering at great expense: a number that faithfully reflects what we care about can stop doing so the moment people are rewarded for moving it.\n\nThe idea now bears the name of Charles Goodhart, an economist who advised the Bank of England. In 1975, writing about British monetary policy, he observed that statistical relationships which had seemed dependable—between measures of the money supply and the wider economy, for instance—tended to break down once the authorities tried to use them as instruments of control. His point was technical and his audience narrow. At almost the same time, the American social scientist Donald Campbell reached a similar conclusion from another direction, warning that the more a quantitative indicator is used for social decisions, the more it invites corruption and distorts the very activity it was meant to monitor. The version of Goodhart's idea that most people quote, however, came more than two decades later from the anthropologist Marilyn Strathern, writing about the auditing of British universities: \"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.\"\n\nSchools offer the most familiar illustration. A test score is a reasonable, if partial, sign of what students have learned, as long as nobody's career depends on it. Once teachers and principals are judged by those scores, the incentive shifts from teaching a subject to teaching the test: drilling the question types likely to appear, neglecting whatever the exam cannot easily check, and concentrating effort on students just below the passing mark, whose improvement moves the statistics most. None of this requires dishonesty. Each choice may be sensible on its own; it is their accumulation that hollows the measure out, until rising scores no longer mean that students know more.\n\nHospitals and laboratories tell versions of the same story. When emergency departments in England were judged for years on whether patients were dealt with within four hours, the target helped push many hospitals to work faster. But observers also noticed a suspicious crowding of patients admitted or discharged in the final minutes before the deadline, and worried that some were being moved wherever the clock would stop rather than wherever they would be best treated. In science, citation counts were meant to reveal which research had influenced others. As they came to shape hiring and funding, some researchers began citing their own work generously, and some editors were found pressing authors to add citations to their own journals. The numbers rose; what they were supposed to show became harder to see.\n\nIt would be tempting to conclude that measurement itself is the enemy, but that conclusion is both wrong and dangerous. Without numbers, institutions fall back on reputation, intuition and the confidence of whoever speaks loudest—hardly a recipe for fairness. Waiting times genuinely matter to patients, and reading ability matters to children. The lesson of Goodhart's law is not to stop counting but to stop pretending that any single figure can stand for everything we value. A school judged on attendance, the quality of students' work and their wellbeing, as well as on test results, is far harder to game, because improving one number at the expense of the others quickly becomes visible.\n\nEqually important is judgment, which no system of indicators can replace. Numbers are best treated as prompts for questions rather than as verdicts: a sudden improvement should invite curiosity about how it was achieved, not merely applause. Measures can be changed from time to time and combined with direct observation by people who understand the work. None of these remedies is perfect, and each has costs. What they share is a recognition that Goodhart's insight is ultimately about people, not statistics. Whenever a number carries rewards and punishments, those being measured will learn its weaknesses faster than those doing the measuring. A wise institution assumes as much from the start.", "passage_zh": "有一則關於蘇聯計畫經濟的老笑話，幾乎可以確定是虛構的：一家鐵釘工廠的產量以重量來評定，於是經理們乖乖地生產出幾根巨大的鐵釘，重得完全派不上用場。等計畫官員改成以數量計算，工廠又生產出數以百萬計的小釘子，細得什麼東西都釘不牢。這則笑話之所以流傳至今，是因為它點出了一件現實中的機構一再付出高昂代價才重新學到的事：一個原本忠實反映我們所重視之事的數字，一旦人們會因為推高它而獲得獎賞，就可能不再如此。\n\n這個概念如今以查爾斯‧古德哈特命名，他是一位曾擔任英格蘭銀行顧問的經濟學家。1975 年，他在討論英國的貨幣政策時觀察到，一些原本看似可靠的統計關係——例如貨幣供給的衡量指標與整體經濟之間的關係——一旦當局試圖把它們當作控制的工具，往往就會失靈。他的論點相當專業，讀者群也很狹窄。幾乎在同一時間，美國社會科學家唐納‧坎貝爾從另一個方向得出了類似的結論，他警告：一項量化指標越常被用於社會決策，就越容易招致腐化，也越會扭曲它原本要監測的那項活動。不過，這個概念如今最常被引用的說法，出自二十多年後的人類學家瑪麗蓮‧史翠珊（Marilyn Strathern），她在探討英國大學的評鑑制度時寫道：「當一項指標變成目標，它就不再是一項好的指標。」\n\n學校提供了最為人熟知的例子。只要沒有人的前途取決於考試成績，它就是學生學習成果一個合理（雖然不完整）的指標。一旦老師和校長要以這些分數來評定，誘因就會從教好一門學科轉變成「為考試而教」：反覆操練可能出現的題型、忽略考試不容易檢測的部分，並把心力集中在剛好低於及格線的學生身上，因為他們的進步最能推動統計數字。這些都不需要任何不誠實的行為。每一個選擇單獨看來或許都很合理；是這些選擇的累積掏空了這項指標，直到分數上升不再代表學生懂得更多。\n\n醫院和實驗室也上演著同一個故事的不同版本。英格蘭的急診部門多年來以病人是否在四小時內得到處置來評定，這個目標促使許多醫院加快了速度。但觀察者也注意到，在期限前的最後幾分鐘，被收治住院或離院的病人異常密集，並擔心有些病人被移到能讓計時停止的地方，而不是能讓他們得到最好治療的地方。在科學界，論文被引用的次數原本是用來顯示哪些研究影響了其他人。隨著引用次數開始左右聘任與經費的決定，有些研究者開始大方地引用自己的著作，也有些期刊編輯被發現施壓作者，要他們多引用編輯自家期刊的論文。數字上升了；它們原本應該呈現的東西卻變得更難看清。\n\n我們很容易就此斷定衡量本身就是敵人，但這個結論既錯誤又危險。沒有了數字，機構就只能仰賴名聲、直覺，以及說話最大聲者的自信——這可算不上公平之道。等候時間對病人確實重要，閱讀能力對孩子也很重要。古德哈特定律的教訓不是停止計算，而是別再假裝任何單一數字能代表我們所重視的一切。一所學校若除了考試成績之外，也以出席率、學生作業的品質和他們的身心健康來評定，就難以被鑽漏洞得多，因為犧牲其他數字來提升某一個數字，很快就會被看出來。\n\n同樣重要的是判斷力，這是任何指標系統都無法取代的。數字最好被當作提出問題的提示，而不是最終的判決：一項突然的進步應該引發人們好奇它是怎麼達成的，而不只是換來掌聲。指標可以不時更換，也可以搭配由了解這項工作的人所做的直接觀察。這些補救辦法沒有一個是完美的，而且各有代價。它們的共同點在於體認到：古德哈特的洞見歸根究柢是關於人，而不是關於統計。只要一個數字帶有獎懲，被衡量的人就會比負責衡量的人更快摸清它的弱點。明智的機構從一開始就會這樣預設。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Numbers should be removed from schools, hospitals and laboratories altogether.", "Measures lose their value when treated as targets, so they should be used in combination and with judgment.", "Goodhart's law applies mainly to the way central banks control the money supply.", "Teachers who prepare their students for tests are usually acting dishonestly."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一、二段說明古德哈特定律：When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure；第三、四段以學校、醫院和科學界為例；第五、六段則主張 not to stop counting but to stop pretending that any single figure can stand for everything we value，並強調 judgment 的重要。可見主旨是：指標一旦成為目標就會失真，因此應併用多項指標並保留人的判斷。(A) 與第五段 that conclusion is both wrong and dangerous 相反；(C) 第二段說古德哈特最初談的是貨幣政策，但全文顯示這個定律的適用範圍廣得多；(D) 第三段明言 None of this requires dishonesty。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what was Goodhart's original point about?", "options": ["Statistical relationships in monetary policy that broke down once they were used for control", "The way universities in Britain were audited and judged by the government", "A nail factory in the Soviet Union that cheated the central planners", "Social indicators that led to corrupt decisions in American schools"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：In 1975, writing about British monetary policy, he observed that statistical relationships which had seemed dependable... tended to break down once the authorities tried to use them as instruments of control。(B) 英國大學的評鑑是史翠珊寫作的背景，不是古德哈特；(C) 鐵釘工廠是第一段的笑話；(D) 指標招致腐化是坎貝爾的論點，而且文中並未提到美國學校。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why might teachers concentrate on students just below the passing mark?", "options": ["Those students are usually the ones who enjoy the subject most.", "Principals require teachers to ignore their strongest students.", "Raising those students' results improves the statistics the most.", "The exams are designed mainly to test the weaker students."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：concentrating effort on students just below the passing mark, whose improvement moves the statistics most。老師把心力放在剛好低於及格線的學生身上，是因為他們的進步最能推高統計數字。(A) 文中沒有提到學生是否喜歡這個科目；(B) 文中沒有說校長要求老師忽略程度好的學生；(D) 文中並未提到考試是為誰設計的。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"hollows\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["fills completely", "measures exactly", "hides from view", "empties of real meaning"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：it is their accumulation that hollows the measure out, until rising scores no longer mean that students know more。這些選擇累積起來，讓分數上升不再代表學生懂得更多，也就是使指標「被掏空、失去實質意義」；hollow out 意為「掏空」。(A)「完全填滿」意思相反；(B)「精確地衡量」也與文意相反；(C) 指標並不是被藏起來，而是失去了意義。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["A hospital that meets every target must be providing excellent care.", "Waiting times are too unimportant to be worth measuring at all.", "A sudden rise in a figure deserves investigation as well as praise.", "Institutions should rely on reputation and intuition instead of numbers."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第六段：Numbers are best treated as prompts for questions rather than as verdicts: a sudden improvement should invite curiosity about how it was achieved, not merely applause。作者認為數字突然變好時，除了給予掌聲，更應追問它是如何達成的。(A) 第四段指出四小時目標可能讓醫院為了停止計時而移動病人，達標不等於照護良好；(B) 第五段說 Waiting times genuinely matter to patients；(D) 第五段說仰賴名聲與直覺 hardly a recipe for fairness。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a293", "title": "Cash for Tails: The Great Hanoi Rat Hunt and the Art of Backfiring Incentives", "title_zh": "用鼠尾換現金：河內大獵鼠與適得其反的誘因", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["History", "Society"], "passage": "At the turn of the twentieth century, Hanoi was meant to be a showcase. The French authorities, who were making the city the capital of their Indochinese colonies, were rebuilding its European quarter in their own image, with broad boulevards, elegant villas and the pride of modern urban planning: a network of underground sewers. The sewers, unfortunately, proved to be ideal homes for rats. Sheltered from predators and supplied with a steady stream of waste, the animals multiplied, and by the early 1900s they were turning up in the most refined households, emerging, according to some accounts, from the plumbing itself.\n\nThe rats were more than a nuisance. Plague had broken out in Hong Kong in 1894 and was spreading along the trade routes of Asia, and doctors had come to suspect that rats, and the fleas they carried, played a central role in its transmission. In 1902 the colonial government responded with a campaign of extermination. At first it hired Vietnamese workers to descend into the sewers—grim, dangerous work—and paid them according to the number of rats they killed. The daily tallies soon climbed into the thousands, yet the rats showed little sign of disappearing.\n\nSo the authorities widened the scheme and invited the public to take part. Anyone who brought in a rat would receive a small bounty, and to spare officials the unpleasant task of handling thousands of corpses, the only proof required was the tail. For a while the policy appeared to work, as tails poured in. Then reports began to circulate of tailless rats scurrying about the city. The explanation was not hard to guess: a rat that was caught, docked and released could go on breeding, and each of its offspring would be worth money too. Before long, officials also discovered people on the outskirts of the city raising rats for the reward. The bounty was eventually abandoned. Much of what we know about the episode comes from the historian Michael Vann, who reconstructed it from French colonial archives.\n\nVann's account has since become a favorite of economists and policy writers, partly because it is far better documented than the story usually told to make the same point. That story is set in Delhi under British rule, where officials alarmed by venomous snakes are said to have offered a reward for every dead cobra. Enterprising residents supposedly began breeding cobras, and when the government discovered the trick and cancelled the reward, the breeders released their now-worthless snakes, leaving the city with more cobras than before. The tale is so neat that a whole category of policy failure, the \"cobra effect,\" is named after it. Yet those who have searched for contemporary records of the scheme have found little to confirm it. It may be true; it may equally be a parable that improved with each retelling.\n\nWhatever happened in Delhi, the pattern itself is real and still common in modern policy. When Mexico City tried to reduce air pollution in 1989 by banning each car from the roads on one weekday according to its license plate, some households responded by buying a second car, often an older and dirtier one. Under an international climate scheme, factories that destroyed a powerful greenhouse gas created as a byproduct of making a refrigerant could earn valuable credits—an arrangement that, critics argued, gave some of them a reason to produce more of the refrigerant than they otherwise would have. In each case, the rule fixed on something easy to observe—tails, license plates, tons of gas destroyed—rather than the outcome its designers actually wanted.\n\nIt would be a mistake to read these stories as proof that ordinary people are cheats, or that governments should never offer rewards. Those who bred rats in Hanoi were responding rationally to a scheme designed with little thought for their circumstances, and in a colony governed chiefly in the interests of its rulers, they had few reasons to share those rulers' goals. The deeper lesson concerns the gap between a measure and a purpose. Any incentive invites people to find the cheapest way of satisfying it, and the cheapest way is rarely the one the designers imagined. Policies work best when they anticipate this—rewarding results that are hard to fake, and testing their assumptions on a small scale before staking a whole city on them.", "passage_zh": "二十世紀初，河內原本應該是一座展示櫥窗。正著手把這座城市定為印度支那殖民地首都的法國當局，仿照法國本土的樣貌改建城中的歐洲區：寬闊的林蔭大道、典雅的別墅，以及現代都市規劃的驕傲——一套地下汙水道系統。不幸的是，這些汙水道竟成了老鼠的理想住所。牠們躲開了天敵，又有源源不絕的廢棄物可吃，於是大量繁殖；到了 1900 年代初期，老鼠已開始出現在最講究的人家裡，根據某些記載，甚至直接從水管裡鑽出來。\n\n這些老鼠不只是令人困擾而已。鼠疫在 1894 年於香港爆發，並沿著亞洲的貿易路線蔓延，而醫生們也已開始懷疑，老鼠及其身上的跳蚤在疾病的傳播中扮演了關鍵角色。1902 年，殖民政府展開了一場撲滅老鼠的行動。起初，政府僱用越南工人深入汙水道——那是既艱苦又危險的工作——並依照他們殺死的老鼠數量支付報酬。每日的捕殺數量很快就攀升到數以千計，但老鼠卻絲毫沒有減少的跡象。\n\n於是當局擴大計畫，邀請民眾一起參與。任何人只要交出一隻老鼠，就能領到一小筆賞金；而為了讓官員免於處理成千上萬具老鼠屍體的苦差事，唯一需要的證明就是鼠尾。有一段時間，這項政策似乎奏效了，鼠尾如潮水般湧入。接著，開始有人回報看見沒有尾巴的老鼠在城裡四處亂竄。原因並不難猜：一隻被抓到、剪掉尾巴後再放走的老鼠可以繼續繁殖，而牠的每一隻後代也都能換錢。不久之後，官員還發現城市郊區有人為了領賞金而養老鼠。這項賞金最後被取消了。我們對這段往事的了解，大多來自歷史學家麥可‧范恩，他從法國殖民時期的檔案中重建了事情的經過。\n\n范恩的記述後來成為經濟學家和政策評論者的最愛，部分原因在於，比起通常被拿來說明同一個道理的那個故事，它的文獻紀錄要充分得多。那個故事發生在英國統治下的德里：據說官員擔心毒蛇為患，於是懸賞每一條死掉的眼鏡蛇。據說腦筋動得快的居民開始養殖眼鏡蛇，等到政府發現這個伎倆而取消賞金時，養蛇人便把已經不值錢的蛇放走，結果城裡的眼鏡蛇比以前更多。這個故事太過工整，一整類的政策失敗甚至都以它命名，稱為「眼鏡蛇效應」。然而，那些尋找這項計畫當時紀錄的人，卻幾乎找不到任何證據。它或許是真的；但也同樣可能只是一則寓言，每被轉述一次就變得更精彩一些。\n\n無論德里當年發生了什麼事，這種模式本身確實存在，而且在現代政策中依然常見。1989 年，墨西哥城為了減少空氣汙染，規定每輛車依車牌號碼每週有一個平日不得上路，結果有些家庭的對策是再買一輛車，而且往往是一輛更老舊、汙染更嚴重的車。在一項國際氣候機制下，工廠若銷毀某種強效溫室氣體——那是製造某種冷媒時產生的副產品——就能賺取有價值的額度；批評者認為，這樣的安排讓其中一些工廠有理由生產比原本更多的冷媒。在每一個例子中，規則鎖定的都是容易觀察到的東西——鼠尾、車牌號碼、被銷毀的氣體噸數——而不是設計者真正想要的結果。\n\n如果把這些故事解讀為一般人都是騙子的證據，或是政府永遠不該提供獎勵，那就錯了。在河內養老鼠的人，是在對一項幾乎沒有考慮到他們處境的計畫做出理性的反應；而在一個主要為統治者利益而治理的殖民地裡，他們也沒什麼理由去認同統治者的目標。更深層的教訓在於指標與目的之間的落差。任何誘因都會促使人們尋找滿足它的最便宜方法，而最便宜的方法很少是設計者原本所設想的。能預先考慮到這一點的政策效果最好——獎勵難以造假的成果，並在把整座城市押上去之前，先在小規模範圍內檢驗自己的假設。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?", "options": ["To prove that the story about the cobras of Delhi is completely false", "To describe how the French designed and built the sewers of Hanoi", "To argue that offering rewards always makes a problem worse", "To use a well-documented colonial episode to show how incentives can backfire"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文以 1902 年河內的鼠尾賞金為核心（第一至三段），說明賞金反而促使人們養老鼠；第五段再舉現代政策的例子；第六段點出 The deeper lesson concerns the gap between a measure and a purpose。可見作者是藉由這段有文獻佐證的殖民往事，說明誘因可能適得其反。(A) 第四段說德里的故事 It may be true，作者並未斷言它完全是假的；(B) 汙水道只是第一段的背景；(C) 第六段明言不該把這些故事解讀成政府 should never offer rewards，「總是」太過絕對。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did the authorities ask for rat tails as proof?", "options": ["Tails could be sold to doctors who were studying the plague.", "Officials wanted to avoid handling large numbers of dead rats.", "Tails showed which of the rats had been carrying the disease.", "Rats without tails were unable to survive in the sewers."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：to spare officials the unpleasant task of handling thousands of corpses, the only proof required was the tail。要求以鼠尾為證，是為了讓官員不必處理大量的老鼠屍體。(A)(C) 文中都沒有提到；(D) 第三段反而指出沒有尾巴的老鼠在城裡四處亂竄，還能繼續繁殖。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why do economists and policy writers now often prefer the Hanoi story?", "options": ["There is much stronger historical evidence that it really happened.", "It took place more recently than the events said to have happened in Delhi.", "It led to a far greater increase in the number of animals.", "It was the first case ever to be described as the \"cobra effect.\""], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：partly because it is far better documented than the story usually told to make the same point，並說德里的故事 those who have searched for contemporary records of the scheme have found little to confirm it。可知河內的故事有較充分的史料佐證。(B) 文中沒有交代德里的故事發生在哪一年，無從比較；(C) 文中並未比較兩地動物增加的數量；(D)「眼鏡蛇效應」是以德里的故事命名，而不是河內。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"docked\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["brought into a harbor", "having had its tail cut off", "counted by an official", "kept inside a cage"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：a rat that was caught, docked and released could go on breeding，前文又提到 tailless rats scurrying about the city，可知 docked 指老鼠被「剪掉尾巴」：人們拿尾巴去領賞，再把老鼠放走。(A)「停靠港口」是 dock 常見的另一個意思，但不符文意；(C) 被官員點算、(D) 被關在籠子裡，都無法解釋為何城裡會出現沒有尾巴的老鼠。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's attitude toward the people who bred rats for the bounty?", "options": ["Angry, because they deliberately helped the plague to spread", "Admiring, because their actions brought colonial rule to an end", "Understanding, because they reacted sensibly to a poorly designed scheme", "Indifferent, because their actions made no difference to the campaign"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第六段：Those who bred rats in Hanoi were responding rationally to a scheme designed with little thought for their circumstances，並說在殖民地中他們 had few reasons to share those rulers' goals。作者認為他們是對一項設計不良的計畫做出合理的反應，態度是理解的。(A) 文中並未指責他們故意散播鼠疫，第六段更說不該把這些故事當成一般人都是騙子的證據；(B) 文中沒有說他們的行為終結了殖民統治；(D) 第三段顯示他們的做法讓賞金計畫適得其反，最後只好取消，並非毫無影響。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a294", "title": "A Gentle Push and Its Limits: The Long Argument over Nudges", "title_zh": "輕輕一推的極限：關於「推力」的長期爭論", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Society", "Money"], "passage": "In 2008 the economist Richard Thaler and the legal scholar Cass Sunstein published a book with a deceptively modest title. Nudge argued that the way choices are presented—the order of dishes in a cafeteria, the wording of a form, the option that applies if a person does nothing at all—shapes what people choose, often more powerfully than information or argument. Since someone has to design these features anyway, the authors reasoned, they might as well be designed to help people make the decisions they would make for themselves if they had unlimited time, attention and willpower. They called the approach \"libertarian paternalism\": steering people toward better outcomes while leaving them free to choose otherwise.\n\nThe most celebrated example concerns retirement savings. In many workplace pension plans, employees traditionally had to sign up actively, and large numbers never got around to it—not because they had decided against saving, but because the paperwork could always wait until next month. When employers changed the default, enrolling workers automatically while allowing them to leave whenever they wished, participation rose sharply, and most people simply stayed. Britain began phasing in automatic enrollment in workplace pensions in 2012, and by then it had already set up a Behavioural Insights Team, quickly nicknamed the \"Nudge Unit,\" to apply such ideas across government.\n\nFrom the beginning, however, the approach drew criticism from more than one direction. Some objected to the paternalism: who are officials to decide which choices are \"better,\" and why should they be trusted to judge our interests more reliably than we do? Others worried about manipulation. A nudge often works precisely because it operates below the level of conscious deliberation; a default succeeds because people do not notice it or cannot be bothered to change it. If a policy works only when its targets fail to think about it, critics asked, can it really be said to respect their freedom? Defenders reply that there is no neutral way to design a form, and that a nudge which is openly disclosed and easy to refuse is far less coercive than a tax or a ban.\n\nA different challenge concerned not whether nudges are acceptable but whether they work. In 2022 a large meta-analysis, pooling results from more than two hundred studies, reported that such interventions changed behavior with an average effect its authors described as small to medium, and that altering the structure of a decision, for example through defaults, tended to work better than merely providing information. To enthusiasts this looked like a vindication. Later the same year, however, other researchers reanalyzed the same data while correcting for publication bias—the tendency for striking positive results to be published while disappointing ones stay in file drawers. After that correction, they argued, the evidence for an average effect largely disappeared.\n\nWhat follows from this? Probably not that nudges are worthless. An average across hundreds of very different interventions can conceal a few that work well among many that achieve almost nothing. Automatic enrollment, the most robust case, changes what happens when people do nothing, and its effects show up in participation figures from real workplaces rather than in brief laboratory experiments. Many other nudges—a reminder letter here, a reworded sign there—may produce effects that are modest, fragile or confined to the setting in which they were first tested. The sensible response is to treat each proposed nudge as a hypothesis, test it on a realistic scale, and publish the results whether or not they flatter the idea.\n\nThere is also a larger danger, one that several prominent behavioral scientists have come to emphasize: that nudges become a substitute for policy rather than a complement to it. A government that encourages saving through cleverly designed forms may feel less pressure to deal with low wages; one that nudges citizens toward healthier lunches may avoid confronting the price of fresh food or the marketing of junk food. Even automatic enrollment in Britain did not rely on the default alone; it was backed by legislation requiring employers to contribute. The book promised a gentle push, and at their best nudges deliver exactly that. Larger problems usually require something firmer, and the gentlest tools are most valuable when they accompany such measures rather than replace them.", "passage_zh": "2008 年，經濟學家理查‧塞勒與法學學者凱斯‧桑思坦出版了一本書名看似謙遜的書。《推力》（Nudge）主張，選項呈現的方式——自助餐廳裡菜餚擺放的順序、表格的措辭、一個人什麼都不做時自動適用的選項——會左右人們的選擇，而且往往比資訊或說理更有力量。兩位作者推論，既然這些設計總得有人來做，倒不如把它們設計成能幫助人們做出「假如擁有無限的時間、注意力和意志力，自己也會做的決定」。他們把這種方法稱為「自由家長主義」：引導人們走向更好的結果，同時仍讓他們可以自由做出別的選擇。\n\n最著名的例子與退休儲蓄有關。在許多職場退休金計畫中，員工傳統上必須主動申請加入，而許多人始終沒有去辦——不是因為他們決定不存錢，而是因為那些文書手續總是可以拖到下個月再說。當雇主改變了預設選項，自動為員工加入計畫，同時允許他們隨時退出，參加率便大幅上升，而且大多數人就這樣留了下來。英國從 2012 年起逐步實施職場退休金的自動加入制度；在那之前，英國政府已成立了「行為洞察小組」，很快就被暱稱為「推力小組」，負責把這類概念應用到政府各部門。\n\n然而，這種方法從一開始就招致來自多方的批評。有些人反對其中的家長主義：官員憑什麼決定哪些選擇「比較好」？又為什麼要相信他們比我們自己更能可靠地判斷我們的利益？另一些人則擔心操弄。推力之所以有效，往往正是因為它在意識思考的層次之下運作；預設選項之所以成功，是因為人們沒有注意到它，或是懶得去改。批評者問道：如果一項政策只有在對象沒有思考它時才有效，它真的稱得上尊重了他們的自由嗎？支持者則回應，設計表格本來就不可能完全中立，而一個公開揭露、又容易拒絕的推力，其強制性遠低於稅收或禁令。\n\n另一個挑戰關心的不是推力是否可以接受，而是它是否有效。2022 年，一項綜合了兩百多篇研究結果的大型統合分析指出，這類介入確實能改變行為，其平均效果被作者形容為「小到中等」；而且改變決策的結構——例如透過預設選項——往往比單純提供資訊更有效。對支持者來說，這看起來就像是證明了他們是對的。然而，同一年稍晚，其他研究者重新分析了同一批資料，並校正了發表偏誤——也就是亮眼的正面結果容易被發表，令人失望的結果卻被擱在抽屜裡的傾向。他們認為，經過校正之後，支持平均效果存在的證據大致上消失了。\n\n這意味著什麼呢？大概不是說推力毫無價值。數百種差異極大的介入措施中，可能有少數效果很好，卻夾雜在許多幾乎毫無成效的措施之間，一取平均值就被掩蓋了。最站得住腳的例子——自動加入制度——改變的是人們什麼都不做時會發生的事，而它的效果呈現在真實職場的參加人數上，而不是短暫的實驗室實驗裡。許多其他的推力——這裡一封提醒信、那裡一個改寫過的標示——產生的效果可能很有限、很脆弱，或只局限於最初被測試的情境。合理的做法是把每一個被提出的推力都當成一個假設，在符合現實的規模上加以檢驗，並且不論結果是否對這個構想有利，都把它公開發表。\n\n此外還有一個更大的危險，好幾位知名的行為科學家近來越來越強調這一點：推力可能變成政策的替代品，而不是它的補充。一個透過巧妙設計的表格來鼓勵儲蓄的政府，可能會覺得沒那麼迫切需要處理低薪問題；一個用推力引導民眾選擇更健康午餐的政府，可能會迴避新鮮食物的價格或垃圾食物的行銷等問題。即使是英國的自動加入制度，也不是單靠預設選項；它背後有要求雇主提撥退休金的法律作為支撐。這本書承諾的是輕輕一推，而推力在最好的情況下，提供的正是這個。更大的問題通常需要更強硬的手段，而最溫和的工具在搭配這些措施、而非取而代之時，才最有價值。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following would be the best title for the passage?", "options": ["How Britain Finally Solved Its Retirement Problem", "Small Pushes, Big Debates: What Nudges Can and Cannot Do", "Why Every Nudge Is Really a Form of Manipulation", "Publication Bias: A Short Guide for Beginners"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先介紹《推力》一書與自動加入退休金的成功例子（第一、二段），再談家長主義與操弄的批評（第三段）、統合分析對效果的爭議（第四、五段），最後主張推力應補充而非取代政策（第六段）。(B)「小小一推，大大爭論：推力能做到與做不到的事」最能涵蓋全文。(A) 英國的退休金制度只是第二段的例子，文中也沒有說英國解決了退休問題；(C) 第三段只是呈現批評者的看法，同段也列出支持者的回應；(D) 發表偏誤只是第四段的一部分。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did many employees fail to join pension plans when they had to sign up themselves?", "options": ["They kept putting off the paperwork rather than deciding not to save.", "They had worked out that saving for retirement was not worthwhile.", "Their employers actively discouraged them from joining the plans.", "The plans were far too expensive for most ordinary workers."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：large numbers never got around to it—not because they had decided against saving, but because the paperwork could always wait until next month。許多員工沒有加入，是因為一再拖延文書手續，而不是決定不存錢。(B) 與 not because they had decided against saving 相反；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What did the researchers who reanalyzed the 2022 data conclude?", "options": ["Nudges work better than taxes or bans in most situations.", "The original meta-analysis had included too few studies.", "Once publication bias was corrected for, little evidence of an average effect remained.", "Defaults were the only kind of nudge that had been properly tested."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：other researchers reanalyzed the same data while correcting for publication bias... After that correction, they argued, the evidence for an average effect largely disappeared。(A) 第三段拿推力與稅收、禁令相比的是強制性而非效果，而且那是支持者的說法；(B) 原分析綜合了 more than two hundred studies，文中並未批評研究數量太少；(D) 文中沒有這樣的說法。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"vindication\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["proof of being right", "a serious warning", "a public apology", "an unexpected defeat"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段先說 2022 年的統合分析顯示推力有 small to medium 的平均效果，接著說 To enthusiasts this looked like a vindication。對支持者而言，這項結果看起來就像「證明他們是對的」，vindication 意為「證明正確、平反」。(B) 嚴重的警告、(C) 公開道歉、(D) 意外的失敗，都不符合支持者看到正面結果時的感受。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's attitude toward nudges?", "options": ["Enthusiastic: they can take the place of most traditional regulation", "Hostile: they are a kind of manipulation that ought to be banned", "Indifferent: the evidence is too unclear to support any opinion", "Cautious: they can be useful but should support firmer policies, not replace them"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段說 Probably not that nudges are worthless，第六段又說 the gentlest tools are most valuable when they accompany such measures rather than replace them。作者認為推力有其用處，但應作為更強硬政策的輔助，而非替代品，態度審慎。(A) 與第六段的警告相反；(B) 作者只是轉述批評者對操弄的擔憂，並未主張禁止推力；(C) 第五段作者明確主張把推力當作假設來檢驗，並非漠不關心。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a295", "title": "Nothing to Delete: Learning to Type on My Mother's Machine", "title_zh": "無從刪除：在母親的打字機上學打字", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Arts", "Life"], "passage": "The typewriter came to me in a gray case that smelled faintly of machine oil and of twenty years in a cupboard. My mother had bought it secondhand at nineteen, to practice at home while she worked as a clerk at a trading company in Kaohsiung, typing invoices and letters in a language she had learned mostly from textbooks. After she died, my brother took the photographs, and no one objected when I asked for a machine that none of us had ever seen her use. I told myself I wanted it as an object, something to set on a shelf. Within a week I had bought a ribbon and was at the kitchen table, pressing keys that resisted my fingers like a stiff door.\n\nThe first lesson the typewriter taught me was about force. A computer keyboard asks almost nothing of the hand. My mother's machine required a deliberate strike, each key driving a thin metal arm up toward the paper, where it hit the ribbon with a sound like a small, decisive knock. Type too softly and the letter came out pale, a ghost of itself; too hard and the full stops punched tiny holes in the page. Within a few weeks my hands had found a rhythm that was not entirely my own. They paused near the end of each line when the little bell rang, then reached for the lever and swept the carriage back, repeating a movement my mother's hands must have made thousands of times.\n\nThe second lesson was harder. On the typewriter, nothing can be deleted. A wrong letter stays where it fell; one can type over it with a row of x's, or paint it out with correction fluid, but the error remains visible, a small scar on the page. At first this made me timid. I composed each sentence in my head before typing it, and a letter that would have taken twenty minutes on a laptop took an evening. Because I could not revise endlessly, I began to think before writing rather than through writing, and the sentences that emerged were plainer and, to my surprise, more honest. I had not realized how much of my writing on a screen consisted of trying out phrases until one of them sounded clever.\n\nIt would be easy to turn this into a sermon about slowness, and I distrust the sermon. The machine is not wiser than my laptop. It is louder, heavier and less forgiving, and my mother, who typed for a living, would have laughed at anyone who romanticized it. She switched to a computer the moment her office offered one and never, as far as I know, looked back. What the typewriter did for me was not to reveal some lost virtue but to make visible a habit I had stopped noticing: the assumption that every mistake can be undone without leaving a trace.\n\nGrief, I have come to think, works more like the typewriter than like the screen. There is no key that returns a life to the moment before it was altered. The things I failed to say to my mother in her final months remain on the page, as it were, struck over but legible. For weeks after her death I kept trying to revise them, rehearsing conversations in which I was more patient and asked what I had meant to ask. The typewriter, with its stubborn refusal to erase, offered a different model. You do not remove the error. You keep typing, and the line goes on beyond it.\n\nWe like to believe that our tools serve our thoughts, but they also shape them, quietly, by deciding what is easy and what is costly. A word processor makes revision almost free, and so we revise; a typewriter makes it expensive, and so we choose. Neither is the correct way to think. Still, I now draft anything that matters to me on my mother's machine, in a room that fills with the sound of keys. She must have known that sound by heart long before I existed. I hear it now as a kind of conversation, conducted slowly, in which nothing can be taken back and nothing, therefore, is said carelessly.", "passage_zh": "那臺打字機來到我手上時，裝在一個灰色的提箱裡，隱約散發著機油的氣味，還有在櫥櫃裡擱了二十年的氣息。母親十九歲時買下了這臺二手打字機，好在家裡練習；當時她在高雄一家貿易公司當職員，用一種主要從課本上學來的語言打發票、打信件。她過世後，哥哥拿走了照片；我開口要這臺我們誰都沒見過她使用的機器時，沒有人反對。我告訴自己，我只是想把它當成一件擺設，放在架上。不到一個星期，我已經買了色帶，坐在廚房的餐桌前，按著那些像一扇卡住的門一樣抗拒我手指的按鍵。\n\n打字機教我的第一課，是關於力道。電腦鍵盤對手幾乎毫無要求。母親的打字機卻需要刻意的敲擊：每按一個鍵，就會推動一根細細的金屬桿向上撲向紙面，打在色帶上，發出一聲像是小而果斷的叩門聲。打得太輕，字母就印得淡淡的，彷彿只剩一抹幽影；打得太重，句點就會在紙上戳出小洞。幾個星期之內，我的手就找到了一種並不完全屬於我自己的節奏。每當小鈴聲響起，它們就在快到行尾時停下，接著伸手去拉那根桿子，把滑架推回行首——重複著一個母親的手想必做過成千上萬次的動作。\n\n第二課比較難。在打字機上，什麼都無法刪除。打錯的字母就留在它落下的地方；你可以用一排 x 把它蓋掉，或者用修正液把它塗白，但錯誤依然看得見，像紙上一道小小的疤。起初，這讓我變得畏首畏尾。每個句子我都先在腦子裡想好，才打出來；一封在筆電上只要二十分鐘的信，得花上一整個晚上。因為我無法無止盡地修改，我開始在動筆之前思考，而不是一邊寫一邊思考；寫出來的句子更樸素，也出乎我意料地更誠實。直到那時我才明白，我在螢幕上寫作時，有多少時候其實只是在試用各種說法，直到其中一個聽起來夠聰明為止。\n\n要把這件事講成一篇讚頌慢活的說教並不難，而我對這種說教心存懷疑。這臺機器並不比我的筆電更有智慧。它更吵、更重，也更不寬容；而以打字為生的母親，大概會嘲笑任何把它浪漫化的人。她的辦公室一提供電腦，她就立刻改用，而且據我所知，從未回頭。打字機為我做的，不是揭示某種失落的美德，而是讓一個我早已不再察覺的習慣現形：以為每一個錯誤都能被復原，而且不留痕跡。\n\n我漸漸覺得，悲傷的運作方式比較像打字機，而不像螢幕。沒有哪個按鍵能把一段人生帶回它被改變之前的那一刻。母親生命的最後幾個月裡，那些我沒能對她說出口的話，彷彿仍留在紙頁上，被打叉蓋過，卻依然清晰可讀。她過世後的好幾個星期，我一直試圖修改它們，一遍遍在心裡排練另一種對話：在那些對話裡，我更有耐心，也問出了我原本想問的事。打字機固執地拒絕抹除，正好提供了另一種模式。你不會把錯誤移除。你繼續打下去，那一行字就越過它，繼續延伸。\n\n我們喜歡相信工具是為思想服務的，但工具也在悄悄地塑造思想——透過決定什麼容易、什麼代價高昂。文書處理軟體讓修改幾乎不花成本，所以我們修改；打字機讓修改變得昂貴，所以我們選擇。沒有哪一種才是正確的思考方式。儘管如此，如今凡是對我重要的文字，我都在母親的打字機上起草，讓房間裡充滿按鍵的聲音。早在我出生之前，她想必就已熟悉這個聲音。如今我把它聽成一種緩慢進行的對話；在這場對話裡，說出的話都收不回來，也因此，沒有一句話是隨口說的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the writer's mother built a career as a typist in Kaohsiung", "Why typewriters produce better writing than modern computers", "How learning to use an inherited typewriter led the writer to reflect on writing and loss", "The mechanical differences between typewriters and computer keyboards"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文從第一段繼承母親的打字機寫起，第二、三段描述學打字的兩課——「力道」與「無法刪除」；第五段 Grief, I have come to think, works more like the typewriter than like the screen 將打字經驗連結到喪母之痛；第六段再談 our tools... also shape them（工具也會塑造思想）。可見本文主要寫作者學用繼承來的打字機，並由此反思寫作、錯誤與失去。(A) 母親在高雄貿易公司工作只是第一段的背景；(B) 第四段明言 The machine is not wiser than my laptop，第六段也說 Neither is the correct way to think；(D) 第二段雖比較了兩者的手感，但只是引出反思的細節，並非全文重點。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, how did the fact that nothing could be deleted change the writer's writing?", "options": ["The writer gave up writing letters and returned to the laptop.", "The writer's sentences became longer and more decorative.", "The writer learned to hide every mistake completely with correction fluid.", "The writer planned sentences in advance, and they became simpler and more sincere."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Because I could not revise endlessly, I began to think before writing rather than through writing, and the sentences that emerged were plainer and, to my surprise, more honest. 作者開始先想好句子再打，寫出的句子更樸素、更誠實，與 (D) 相符。(A) 作者並未放棄而改回筆電，第六段反而說凡是重要的文字都在打字機上起草；(B) 與 plainer 相反；(C) 同段指出即使用修正液塗白，the error remains visible，錯誤無法完全隱藏。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What does the passage tell us about the writer's mother?", "options": ["She kept using the typewriter at home long after computers became common.", "She gave up typewriters as soon as her office provided a computer.", "She taught the writer to type on the machine as a child.", "She treated the typewriter as a precious object for display."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：She switched to a computer the moment her office offered one and never, as far as I know, looked back. 可知辦公室一有電腦，母親就立刻改用，不再回頭。(A) 與此相反；(C) 第一段說這是 a machine that none of us had ever seen her use，她不可能用它教作者打字；(D) 第一段想把它放在架上當擺設的是作者自己（I told myself I wanted it as an object），並非母親。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"legible\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["quickly forgotten", "fully forgiven", "deeply painful", "able to be read"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：The things I failed to say to my mother... remain on the page, as it were, struck over but legible. 作者把沒說出口的話比喻成紙上被打叉蓋過、卻仍「看得清楚」的字，呼應第三段錯字 the error remains visible 的描述，故 legible 意為「可辨讀的」。(A) quickly forgotten 與「仍留在紙頁上」相反；(B) 文中並未說這些遺憾已被原諒；(C) deeply painful 雖符合悲傷的氣氛，但 struck over but legible 對比的是「被蓋掉」與「仍看得見」，並非痛苦的程度。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["The tools we use shape how we think, though no single tool is the right one.", "People who write on computers are less honest than those who use typewriters.", "Everyone should give up word processors and return to older machines.", "Tools have little effect on thought because they simply follow our intentions."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第六段：We like to believe that our tools serve our thoughts, but they also shape them... by deciding what is easy and what is costly，接著又說 Neither is the correct way to think。可知作者認為工具會影響我們的思考方式，但沒有哪一種工具才是正確的。(B) 太極端，第三段的 more honest 只是作者個人的經驗，並未評斷使用電腦的人；(C) 與 Neither is the correct way to think 以及第四段 I distrust the sermon 矛盾；(D) 與 they also shape them 相反。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a296", "title": "The Planes That Never Came Back: Abraham Wald and the Evidence We Cannot See", "title_zh": "沒有回來的飛機：亞伯拉罕‧瓦爾德與看不見的證據", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["History", "Science"], "passage": "During the Second World War, a small team of mathematicians and statisticians attached to Columbia University in New York worked on questions that the war, rather than the blackboard, had set. The Statistical Research Group, as it was called, advised the American military on problems involving weapons, testing and supplies. Among its members was Abraham Wald, a mathematician who had come to the United States shortly before the war. One question put to the group has since become perhaps the most famous parable in statistics. It concerns aircraft, bullet holes and a kind of evidence that is easy to overlook because it is, quite literally, not there.\n\nThe problem, in the form in which it is usually told, went like this. Planes returning from missions over enemy territory were examined, and the bullet holes in them were recorded. The damage was not spread evenly: some parts of the aircraft were riddled with holes, while others, such as the engines, showed comparatively few. Armor was the obvious remedy, but armor is heavy, and a plane that carries too much of it flies slower, burns more fuel and becomes harder to maneuver. The military therefore wanted to know where a limited amount of protection would do the most good. The intuitive answer was to reinforce the areas with the most holes, since those were evidently the places where planes were being hit.\n\nWald's reply turned the intuition upside down. The planes being examined, he pointed out, were not a fair sample of all the planes that had flown. They were the ones that had come back. If bullets struck aircraft more or less at random, then the parts that were rarely damaged among the survivors were probably being hit just as often on the planes that did not return, and those hits had brought them down. The holes on the returning planes did not show where planes were vulnerable; they showed where a plane could be hit and still make it home. The armor, therefore, belonged where the holes were not. The missing damage was the most important data of all, recorded only by the absence of the aircraft that had suffered it.\n\nThe story deserves a note of caution, which careful retellings supply. Wald's actual contribution was a series of technical memoranda that developed mathematical methods for estimating how vulnerable different parts of an aircraft were, using only the damage found on survivors; the tidy exchange between a clever mathematician and puzzled officers is a later simplification. The famous image that now accompanies the story, a plane speckled with red dots and with some areas conspicuously untouched, is a modern illustration, not a reproduction of wartime records. None of this weakens the insight. If anything, it shows the parable doing what parables do: compressing a subtle argument into a picture that anyone can remember.\n\nThe error Wald identified is now called survivorship bias: drawing conclusions from the cases that passed through some filter while forgetting the ones the filter removed. Once noticed, it appears everywhere. We hear about entrepreneurs who left university and built famous companies, and conclude that a degree is unnecessary for success, without hearing from the far larger number who left and did not succeed, since nobody interviews them. We admire old buildings and sigh that they don't build them like they used to, forgetting that the old buildings still standing are the sturdiest and best-loved of their era; the poorly made ones were torn down or fell down long ago.\n\nWhat makes survivorship bias so persistent is that it requires us to reason about evidence we cannot see, and the mind does not do this naturally. The visible cases are vivid, countable and close at hand; the missing ones leave no trace unless we deliberately ask where they went. Wald's lesson is therefore less a rule of statistics than a habit of thought. Before trusting a pattern, we should ask how the sample was gathered and what might have been excluded along the way. The question is simple to state and surprisingly hard to remember, which is perhaps why, more than eighty years later, we still need a picture of a plane to remind us.", "passage_zh": "第二次世界大戰期間，一支隸屬於紐約哥倫比亞大學、由數學家與統計學家組成的小團隊，研究的不是黑板上出的題目，而是戰爭丟給他們的難題。這個名為「統計研究小組」的單位，為美國軍方提供關於武器、測試與補給等問題的建議。成員之中有一位名叫亞伯拉罕‧瓦爾德的數學家，他在戰爭爆發前不久才來到美國。小組被問到的一個問題，後來或許成了統計學中最有名的寓言。它關乎飛機、彈孔，以及一種很容易被忽略的證據——因為它真的就「不在那裡」。\n\n這個問題通常被講成這樣：在敵方領空執行完任務後返航的飛機會接受檢查，機身上的彈孔也會被記錄下來。損傷的分布並不平均：飛機的某些部位布滿彈孔，其他部位，例如引擎，彈孔卻相對較少。加裝裝甲是顯而易見的對策，但裝甲很重；一架載了太多裝甲的飛機會飛得更慢、更耗油，也更難操控。因此，軍方想知道有限的防護該加在哪裡，才能發揮最大的效用。直覺上的答案是強化彈孔最多的部位，因為那些地方顯然就是飛機被擊中的地方。\n\n瓦爾德的回答把這個直覺整個顛倒過來。他指出，接受檢查的飛機並不是所有出勤飛機的公平樣本——它們是飛回來的那些。如果子彈大致是隨機打在飛機上，那麼在倖存的飛機上很少受損的部位，在那些沒有回來的飛機上，被擊中的次數很可能一樣多，而正是那些命中讓它們墜毀。返航飛機上的彈孔並沒有顯示飛機哪裡脆弱；它們顯示的是飛機哪裡中彈後仍能飛回家。因此，裝甲應該加在沒有彈孔的地方。那些看不見的損傷才是最重要的資料，而它們只以一種方式被記錄下來：承受了這些損傷的飛機，再也沒有回來。\n\n這個故事值得加上一點提醒，而謹慎的轉述也確實會這麼做。瓦爾德實際的貢獻，是一系列技術備忘錄，發展出只利用倖存飛機上的損傷，來估計飛機各部位有多脆弱的數學方法；聰明的數學家與困惑的軍官之間那段乾淨俐落的對話，是後來簡化的版本。如今常伴隨這個故事出現的那張著名圖片——一架布滿紅點、某些部位卻明顯毫無痕跡的飛機——是現代的示意圖，而不是戰時紀錄的翻印。這些都無損於其中的洞見。甚至可以說，這正顯示了這則寓言在做寓言該做的事：把一個細膩的論證，壓縮成一幅人人都記得住的圖像。\n\n瓦爾德指出的這種錯誤，如今稱為「倖存者偏差」：只根據通過某種篩選的案例下結論，卻忘了被篩選淘汰的那些。一旦注意到它，就會發現它無所不在。我們聽說有些創業家從大學休學後創立了知名公司，便斷定成功不需要學位，卻沒聽到人數多得多的另一群人——他們也休了學，卻沒有成功，因為沒有人會去訪問他們。我們欣賞老建築，感嘆現在的房子蓋得不如從前，卻忘了至今仍屹立的老建築，是那個時代最堅固、最受喜愛的一批；蓋得粗糙的那些，早就被拆除或自己倒塌了。\n\n倖存者偏差之所以如此頑強，是因為它要求我們針對看不見的證據進行推理，而人的心智天生並不擅長這件事。看得見的案例生動、可以計數，而且近在眼前；缺席的案例則不留痕跡，除非我們刻意追問它們去了哪裡。因此，瓦爾德的教訓與其說是一條統計規則，不如說是一種思考習慣。在相信一個規律之前，我們應該先問：樣本是怎麼蒐集的？一路上可能漏掉了什麼？這個問題說來簡單，卻出奇地難以記住；或許正因如此，八十多年後的今天，我們仍需要一張飛機的圖片來提醒自己。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following would be the best title for the passage?", "options": ["Reading the Holes That Weren't There: Wald and Survivorship Bias", "Heavier and Safer: How Armor Won the Air War", "Numbers at War: A History of Columbia University", "Built to Last: Why Old Buildings Are Stronger"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以瓦爾德分析返航飛機彈孔的故事為核心：第三段指出 The armor, therefore, belonged where the holes were not；第五段將這種錯誤命名為 survivorship bias，並舉休學創業家、老建築為例；第六段總結我們應追問樣本中漏掉了什麼。(A)「解讀不存在的彈孔：瓦爾德與倖存者偏差」最能涵蓋全文。(B) 文中並未談裝甲如何贏得空戰；(C) 哥倫比亞大學只是第一段的背景；(D) 與第五段相反，作者指出老建築看似堅固，是因為蓋得粗糙的早已被拆除或倒塌。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why could the military not simply put armor on every part of the plane?", "options": ["Commanders doubted that armor offered any real protection.", "There was not enough metal available during the war.", "Too much weight would make planes slower, less efficient and harder to handle.", "Wald's calculations showed that armor was useless on most parts."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：armor is heavy, and a plane that carries too much of it flies slower, burns more fuel and becomes harder to maneuver. 裝甲太重會讓飛機變慢、更耗油、更難操控，所以只能把有限的防護加在最需要的地方。(A) 文中說裝甲是 the obvious remedy，軍方並未懷疑其效用；(B) 文中未提到金屬短缺；(D) 瓦爾德並未說裝甲在多數部位無用，而是指出應加在沒有彈孔的地方。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer say about the image of a plane covered in red dots?", "options": ["Wald drew it himself to explain his idea to military officers.", "It was copied directly from damage records kept during the war.", "It proves that the engines were the part most often hit by bullets.", "It is a modern illustration rather than a record of real wartime damage."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The famous image that now accompanies the story... is a modern illustration, not a reproduction of wartime records. 可知紅點圖是現代的示意圖，並非戰時真實損傷的紀錄。(A) 文中並未說此圖出自瓦爾德之手；(B) 與 not a reproduction of wartime records 相反；(C) 這張圖只是示意，並不能證明什麼；而且依第三段瓦爾德的推理，倖存飛機的引擎彈孔少，是因為引擎中彈的飛機沒能回來，而非引擎最常被擊中。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"riddled\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["puzzled", "repaired", "filled", "protected"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：some parts of the aircraft were riddled with holes, while others, such as the engines, showed comparatively few. 句中以 while 形成對比：相對於彈孔「較少」的部位，riddled with holes 指「布滿」彈孔，故 riddled 意為 filled（充滿）。(A) riddle 作名詞是「謎語」，容易聯想到 puzzled，但此處描述的是彈孔多寡，與困惑無關；(B) 文中描述的是返航後檢查到的損傷，並非修補；(D) 被保護與布滿彈孔的文意相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention entrepreneurs who left university in paragraph 5?", "options": ["To argue that a university degree is no longer necessary for success", "To show how looking only at visible successes can lead to a false conclusion", "To prove that most people who leave university go on to start companies", "To suggest that successful entrepreneurs often give misleading interviews"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：We hear about entrepreneurs who left university and built famous companies, and conclude that a degree is unnecessary for success, without hearing from the far larger number who left and did not succeed. 作者舉此例，是要說明只看見成功的「倖存者」，就會得出錯誤的結論，這正是倖存者偏差。(A) 這正是作者要指出的錯誤推論，並非作者本身的主張；(C) 與 the far larger number who left and did not succeed 相反；(D) 文中說的是沒有人去訪問失敗者（nobody interviews them），並非成功者在訪問中說了誤導的話。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a297", "title": "Still on the Shelf: The Case for Reading Old Books", "title_zh": "仍在書架上：為什麼還要讀老書", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Arts", "Culture"], "passage": "In my second year of senior high school in Taiwan, our Chinese teacher handed out photocopied passages from the Analects and asked us to memorize them for a Friday quiz. Most of us complained. The sentences were more than two thousand years old, written in a classical language no one around us spoke, and they seemed to have nothing to say about entrance exams or the phones buzzing in our bags. Now, years later, a handful of those lines have outlasted almost everything else I learned that year, and they still seem to have something to say. Why do certain old books keep their hold on readers when so many newer ones vanish within a season?\n\nOne answer is known as the Lindy effect, after a New York restaurant where, as the story goes, comedians gathered to speculate about whose careers would last. For things that do not wear out the way bodies do, such as books and ideas, the longer something has already survived, the longer it can be expected to go on surviving. A novel that has stayed in print for a decade may well last another decade; a text read for two thousand years has a reasonable chance of being read for centuries more. Every year a book remains in circulation, it passes another test: readers in a new era, with new concerns, have found it worth keeping. Thousands of such tests are hard to pass by luck alone.\n\nThe Lindy argument is persuasive, but it hides an assumption: that the tests were fair. Critics of the literary canon point out that, for most of history, the people able to write books were a narrow group, usually wealthy, usually male, and often attached to courts or temples. Survival, moreover, depended on copying, and copying depended on someone judging a text worth the labor. Works that offended rulers could be banned or burned, and many texts simply perished in fires, floods and wars; of the plays performed in ancient Athens, only a small fraction has come down to us. What we call the classics, then, are not simply the best books of the past. They are the books that the powerful chose to preserve and that accident happened to spare.\n\nThis criticism deserves to be taken seriously, yet it does not quite overturn the case for reading the classics. Granting that the selection was biased does not show that the works selected are worthless; it shows that they are incomplete as a record of human experience. A book can be both a product of privilege and a genuine achievement. Moreover, the critics' own arguments depend on old books: we know about the voices that were excluded partly because surviving texts mention them or argue with them. To understand how power shaped what was remembered, one must read what was remembered, carefully and against the grain.\n\nThe stronger case for old books, I think, is not that they are better than new ones but that they are different. Every era has its blind spots, assumptions so widely shared that no one thinks to question them. Books written in our own time tend to share ours; they may disagree about everything except what we all take for granted. An old book, written by someone who took different things for granted, can make our assumptions visible simply by not sharing them. Its errors are easy for us to see, a reminder that our own, invisible to us now, will be just as obvious to readers centuries from now.\n\nNone of this means that students should read only the classics, or that a book becomes wise merely by growing old. The sensible course is to read old books alongside new ones, letting each correct the other. New books speak to our circumstances and bring in voices the old canon left out; old books test whether our circumstances are as unprecedented as we like to believe. In Taiwan, debate flares up now and then over how much classical Chinese students should read, and both sides often argue as though the choice were between the past and the present. It is not. My teacher's photocopies did not take me out of my own time. They gave me a second place to stand while looking at it.", "passage_zh": "我在臺灣讀高二那年，國文老師發下《論語》選段的影印講義，要我們背起來，準備星期五的小考。我們大多數人都在抱怨。那些句子已有兩千多年的歷史，用的是我們身邊沒有人會說的古典語言，而且似乎跟升學考試、書包裡嗡嗡震動的手機毫無關係。多年以後，其中少數幾句比我那一年學到的幾乎所有東西都留存得更久，而且似乎仍有話要對我說。為什麼有些老書能一直吸引讀者，而那麼多較新的書卻在一季之內就消失無蹤？\n\n一種解答被稱為「林迪效應」，名稱來自紐約的一家餐廳；據說，喜劇演員常聚在那裡，推測誰的演藝生涯能夠長久。對於書籍與思想這類不像身體那樣會耗損的事物來說，一樣東西已經存活得越久，就越可以預期它還會繼續存活得更久。一本已經持續印行十年的小說，很可能再流傳十年；一部被閱讀了兩千年的典籍，則相當有機會再被閱讀好幾個世紀。一本書每多流傳一年，就又通過了一次考驗：身處新時代、懷抱新關切的讀者，認為它值得保留。要通過數千次這樣的考驗，很難只靠運氣。\n\n林迪效應的論證很有說服力，但它隱含了一個假設：那些考驗是公平的。對文學經典提出批評的人指出，在歷史上的大部分時期，有能力寫書的只是一小群人：通常富有，通常是男性，而且往往依附於宮廷或廟宇。此外，文本能否存留取決於抄寫，而抄寫又取決於是否有人認為這份文本值得花力氣去抄。冒犯統治者的作品可能遭到查禁或焚毀，還有許多文本就在火災、水災和戰爭中散佚；古代雅典上演過的劇作，只有一小部分流傳至今。因此，我們所謂的經典，並不單純是過去最好的書；它們是有權勢的人選擇保存、而意外又恰好放過的書。\n\n這項批評值得認真看待，但它並沒有完全推翻閱讀經典的理由。承認篩選過程有所偏頗，並不能證明被選中的作品毫無價值；它證明的是，這些作品作為人類經驗的紀錄並不完整。一本書可以既是特權的產物，也是真正的成就。再者，批評者自己的論證也仰賴老書：我們之所以知道有哪些聲音被排除在外，部分正是因為存留下來的文本提到了他們，或與他們爭辯。要理解權力如何形塑了哪些東西被記住，就必須仔細地、逆著文本的紋理，去閱讀那些被記住的東西。\n\n我認為，支持閱讀老書更有力的理由，不在於它們比新書好，而在於它們與新書不同。每個時代都有自己的盲點，也就是那些普遍到沒有人想到要質疑的假設。我們這個時代寫成的書，往往和我們有相同的盲點；它們或許在每件事上都意見分歧，唯獨對我們都視為理所當然的事沒有異議。一本老書的作者視為理所當然的是別的事，於是它光是不認同我們的假設，就能讓這些假設現形。它的錯誤在我們眼中顯而易見，這也提醒我們：我們自己現在看不見的錯誤，在幾個世紀後的讀者眼中，也會同樣明顯。\n\n這一切並不表示學生應該只讀經典，也不表示一本書只要變老就會變得睿智。明智的做法是把老書和新書放在一起讀，讓它們互相修正。新書回應我們的處境，也帶進舊經典所遺漏的聲音；老書則檢驗我們的處境是否真如我們所願意相信的那樣前所未有。在臺灣，每隔一段時間就會掀起一場辯論，爭論學生該讀多少文言文，而雙方往往都說得好像這是過去與現在之間的二選一。其實不是。老師的那些影印講義並沒有把我帶離我自己的時代；它們給了我第二個立足點，讓我能從那裡觀看這個時代。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?", "options": ["To argue that classical texts should replace modern books in Taiwan's schools", "To defend reading old books while admitting that the classics were not chosen fairly", "To explain how the Lindy effect got its name from a New York restaurant", "To prove that the classics are simply the best books ever written"], "answer": 1, "explain": "作者在第二段以林迪效應說明老書為何能流傳；第三段承認批評者的看法，指出經典是 the books that the powerful chose to preserve and that accident happened to spare；第四段說這項批評 does not quite overturn the case for reading the classics；第五、六段再主張老書的價值在於與新書不同，應與新書並讀。可見作者的目的是在承認經典的篩選並不公平的同時，為閱讀老書辯護。(A) 第六段明言 None of this means that students should read only the classics；(C) 餐廳只是第二段解釋名稱由來的細節；(D) 與第三段 not simply the best books of the past 相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the idea described in paragraph 2, which of the following is most likely to still be read far into the future?", "options": ["A text that has already been read for many centuries", "A best-selling novel that came out only last year", "A book that is currently popular among young readers", "A book written by a famous comedian"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：the longer something has already survived, the longer it can be expected to go on surviving，並舉例說被閱讀了兩千年的典籍 has a reasonable chance of being read for centuries more。依此，已被閱讀許多世紀的文本最可能繼續流傳。(B) 才出版一年的暢銷書存活時間最短，依林迪效應，預期能再流傳的時間也最短；(C) 林迪效應看的是已經存活的時間長短，而非目前是否受歡迎，眼下流行的書尚未通過長期的考驗；(D) 喜劇演員只出現在名稱由來的故事中，與作品能否流傳無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why might the classics not be a fair selection from the past?", "options": ["Modern readers can no longer understand the languages they were written in.", "Whether a text survived often depended on powerful people judging it worth copying.", "Ancient authors deliberately wrote so that ordinary readers could not follow them.", "Most ancient books were destroyed by scholars who criticized the canon."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Survival, moreover, depended on copying, and copying depended on someone judging a text worth the labor，段末又說經典是 the books that the powerful chose to preserve。可知文本能否存留，往往取決於有權勢的人是否認為值得抄寫，這正是批評者認為經典篩選不公平的理由之一。(A) 文中未提到現代讀者看不懂古代語言；(C) 文中未說古代作者刻意寫得讓一般人看不懂；(D) 文中說文本是毀於查禁、火災、水災與戰爭，並非被批評經典的學者毀掉。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"spare\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["give away", "use carefully", "leave unharmed", "set aside for later"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：They are the books that the powerful chose to preserve and that accident happened to spare. 前一句提到許多文本 perished in fires, floods and wars，可知 spare 指火災、戰爭等意外「放過、未加毀壞」這些書，即 leave unharmed（使免於受損）。(A) give away（送出）與 (D) set aside for later（留著以後用）來自 spare 作「撥出、騰出」的用法；(B) use carefully 來自 spare 作「節省」的意思；三者皆不符此處「從災禍中倖存」的文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by saying that the photocopies gave the writer \"a second place to stand\"?", "options": ["Reading the classics allowed the writer to escape the pressures of modern life.", "Memorizing the passages helped the writer do well in the entrance exams.", "Classical Chinese should be taught together with a second foreign language.", "Old books offered a different viewpoint from which to examine the present."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第六段：My teacher's photocopies did not take me out of my own time. They gave me a second place to stand while looking at it. 配合第五段 An old book... can make our assumptions visible simply by not sharing them，可知作者的意思是：老書提供了另一個立足點，讓他能從不同的角度審視自己所處的時代。(A) 與 did not take me out of my own time 相反；(B) 文中並未提到背誦是否幫助考試；(C) 文中未提到第二外語，second 指的是「另一個」立足點。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a298", "title": "Better Safe Than Sorry? The Double Edge of the Precautionary Principle", "title_zh": "小心駛得萬年船？預防原則的雙面刃", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Environment", "Society"], "passage": "Every generation inherits a question that science alone cannot settle: what should we do about a danger we cannot yet prove? When the first signs of harm appear, the evidence is almost always incomplete, and waiting for certainty, though it seems prudent, can mean acting only after the damage is done. In 1992, at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro, the world's governments offered an answer. Principle 15 of the Rio Declaration on Environment and Development states that where there are threats of serious or irreversible damage, lack of full scientific certainty shall not be used as a reason for postponing cost-effective measures to prevent environmental degradation. The idea, now usually called the precautionary principle, has since found its way into many treaties and national laws.\n\nIt is worth reading the sentence slowly, because it is more modest than its reputation. It does not say that a new technology must be proven harmless before it may be used, nor that any conceivable risk justifies a ban. It is hedged with conditions: the threat must be serious or irreversible, the measures must be cost-effective, and states are to apply it according to their capabilities. What the sentence does is shift the burden of uncertainty. Ignorance, it insists, is not an alibi. The absence of conclusive proof is not permission to do nothing.\n\nSupporters regard this as common sense written into law, and they can call history as a witness. The hazards of asbestos were suspected long before its use was widely restricted, and in the meantime many workers paid with their lungs. Industries with much to lose have sometimes found that demanding more research is an effective way to delay regulation. Above all, supporters point to an asymmetry between kinds of mistake. If we act against a danger that proves imaginary, we have wasted money, and money can be earned again. If we fail to act against one that proves real (a collapsing fishery, a destabilized climate, a vanished species), there may be no second chance.\n\nCritics rarely object to so modest a rule. Their quarrel is with the stronger versions that have grown up around it, which treat any new activity as guilty until proven innocent. Risk, they argue, lies on every side of a decision, including the side of doing nothing. A regulator who delays a new medicine until every doubt is settled may spare patients an unknown side effect, but the patients the drug might have helped in the meantime pay a price too, unnoticed because harms caused by inaction rarely make headlines. A ban on a new pesticide may send farmers back to an older and more toxic one. Precaution applied in only one direction is thus not caution at all but a preference for familiar dangers over unfamiliar ones, and familiar dangers are not necessarily the smaller ones.\n\nIf neither side is simply wrong, the real task is to weigh risks that point in opposite directions. The first step is to compare: to ask not only what an action might cost, but what the alternatives, inaction included, would cost as well. The second is to give special weight to harms that are irreversible or catastrophic, because these leave no room to learn from experience. The third is to keep measures proportionate and provisional, so that a restriction adopted under uncertainty is revisited as evidence accumulates rather than hardening into a permanent ban whose reasons no one remembers. Understood in this way, precaution is less a verdict than a procedure: a commitment to act sensibly while continuing to learn.\n\nBetter safe than sorry, says the proverb, but the proverb assumes that we know which course is the safe one. Often we do not. The lasting value of the Rio formulation lies not in any answer it supplies but in the question it refuses to let us dodge. It forbids us to hide behind uncertainty, and the most thoughtful critics of precaution add that we must not hide behind caution either. To take precaution seriously is to take seriously both the harm we might cause by acting and the harm we might permit by standing still. The question is never simply whether to be careful. It is what, exactly, we ought to be careful about.", "passage_zh": "每一個世代都會承繼一個單憑科學無法解決的問題：面對一個我們還無法證實的危險，我們該怎麼辦？當傷害的最初跡象出現時，證據幾乎總是不完整的；而等待確定性雖然看似審慎，卻可能意味著要等到損害已經造成，才採取行動。1992 年，在里約熱內盧舉行的地球高峰會上，世界各國政府提出了一個答案。《里約環境與發展宣言》第 15 條原則指出：遇有嚴重或不可逆轉損害的威脅時，不得以缺乏充分的科學確定性為理由，延後採取符合成本效益的措施來防止環境惡化。這個如今通常被稱為「預防原則」的理念，此後已被納入許多條約和各國法律之中。\n\n這句話值得慢慢讀，因為它比它的名聲要溫和得多。它並沒有說新技術必須先被證明無害才能使用，也沒有說任何可以想像得到的風險都足以構成禁止的理由。它附帶了種種條件：威脅必須是嚴重或不可逆轉的，措施必須符合成本效益，而且各國應依其能力加以應用。這句話真正做的，是轉移不確定性的負擔。它堅持，無知不能拿來當推託的藉口；缺乏確鑿的證據，並不等於獲准袖手旁觀。\n\n支持者認為，這不過是把常識寫進了法律，而且他們可以請歷史出庭作證。早在石綿的使用受到廣泛限制之前許久，它的危害就已受到懷疑，而在這段期間，許多工人賠上了自己的肺部健康。可能蒙受重大損失的產業有時會發現，要求進行更多研究，是拖延管制的有效手段。最重要的是，支持者指出兩種錯誤之間的不對稱。如果我們針對一個後來證明只是想像出來的危險採取行動，我們浪費的是金錢，而錢還可以再賺。但如果我們沒有針對一個後來證明是真實的危險採取行動（漁業資源崩潰、氣候失去穩定、一個物種就此消失），可能就再也沒有第二次機會了。\n\n批評者很少反對如此溫和的規則。他們爭論的對象，是在它周圍發展出來的那些較強硬的版本——這些版本把任何新活動都視為「在證明無罪之前一律有罪」。他們主張，風險存在於一個決定的每一面，包括什麼都不做的那一面。一位監管人員若把一種新藥的核准延後到所有疑慮都消除為止，也許能讓病人免於某種未知的副作用；但在這段期間原本可能因這種藥而受益的病人，同樣付出了代價，只是沒有人注意到，因為不作為所造成的傷害很少登上新聞頭條。禁用一種新農藥，可能會讓農民回頭使用一種更舊、毒性更強的農藥。因此，只朝單一方向施行的預防，根本稱不上謹慎，而是一種偏好熟悉的危險、排斥陌生危險的傾向——而熟悉的危險，未必就是比較小的危險。\n\n如果雙方都不能說是全然錯誤，那麼真正的任務，就是權衡指向相反方向的種種風險。第一步是比較：不只要問一項行動可能付出什麼代價，也要問其他選項——包括不採取行動——又會付出什麼代價。第二步是對不可逆轉或災難性的傷害給予特別的重視，因為這類傷害不會留給我們從經驗中學習的餘地。第三步是讓措施合乎比例，並且只是暫時性的，如此一來，在不確定的情況下所採取的限制，就會隨著證據的累積而被重新檢視，而不會僵化成一項沒有人記得當初理由的永久禁令。這樣理解的話，預防與其說是一項判決，不如說是一套程序：一種一邊持續學習、一邊明智行動的承諾。\n\n俗話說「小心駛得萬年船」，但這句俗話預設了我們知道哪一條路才是安全的。我們往往並不知道。里約宣言這段表述的持久價值，不在於它提供了什麼答案，而在於它不讓我們迴避的那個問題。它禁止我們躲在不確定性的背後，而最有見地的預防原則批評者則補充說，我們也不能躲在謹慎的背後。認真看待預防，就是要同時認真看待我們可能因採取行動而造成的傷害，以及我們可能因原地不動而放任發生的傷害。問題從來不只是要不要小心，而是我們究竟應該對什麼小心。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To examine a rule for acting under uncertainty and argue that the risks of inaction must be weighed as well", "To show that the Rio Declaration has prevented most of the environmental damage it addressed", "To argue that new technologies should be banned until they have been proven completely safe", "To describe how the 1992 Earth Summit was organized and which countries took part in it"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段提出「面對尚無法證實的危險該怎麼辦」的問題，並介紹《里約宣言》第 15 條原則；第三、四段分別說明支持者與批評者的看法；第五段指出 the real task is to weigh risks that point in opposite directions，第六段更總結 To take precaution seriously is to take seriously both the harm we might cause by acting and the harm we might permit by standing still。可見作者旨在探討這項在不確定中行動的原則，並主張必須同時權衡行動與不行動的風險。(B) 文中並未說宣言已防止了大部分的環境損害；(C) 這正是第四段批評者所反對的「強硬版本」，並非作者的立場；(D) 地球高峰會只是第一段的背景，文中並未描述其籌辦過程或與會國家。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, which of the following is true of Principle 15?", "options": ["It requires new technologies to be proven harmless before they may be used.", "It expects every country to take the same measures regardless of its resources.", "It applies only where the threatened damage is serious or irreversible.", "It allows governments to wait until scientists agree before taking action."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：It is hedged with conditions: the threat must be serious or irreversible, the measures must be cost-effective, and states are to apply it according to their capabilities，可知這項原則只適用於損害嚴重或不可逆轉的情況。(A) 同段明說 It does not say that a new technology must be proven harmless before it may be used；(B) 原則要求各國 according to their capabilities（依其能力）加以應用，並非不論資源多寡都採取相同措施；(D) 與原則相反，它正是說 lack of full scientific certainty 不得作為延後行動的理由。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the critics described in paragraph 4, how can precaution itself cause harm?", "options": ["Delaying or banning something new can leave people exposed to risks it might have reduced.", "Regulators tend to approve new medicines too quickly to discover their side effects.", "Farmers usually refuse to use any pesticide that has been officially tested.", "Newspapers report the harms of inaction more often than the harms of action."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Risk, they argue, lies on every side of a decision, including the side of doing nothing，並舉例說延後核准新藥時，the patients the drug might have helped in the meantime pay a price too；禁用新農藥則 may send farmers back to an older and more toxic one。可見延後或禁止新事物，可能讓人們繼續暴露在它原本能減少的風險中。(B) 文中說的是監管人員把核准延後，與「核准太快」相反；(C) 文中並未提到農民拒用經過測試的農藥；(D) 與文意相反，文中說不作為所造成的傷害 rarely make headlines。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"hedged\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["strongly opposed", "widely praised", "quickly replaced", "carefully limited"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：It is hedged with conditions: the threat must be serious or irreversible, the measures must be cost-effective...。後面列出一連串條件，呼應前句 it is more modest than its reputation，說明這項原則「受到條件的限制」，故 hedged 在此意為「加以限制、附帶條件」。(A) 強烈反對、(B) 廣受讚揚、(C) 迅速被取代，都與「附帶條件」的文意不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's attitude toward the precautionary principle?", "options": ["Hostile: it is a fashionable slogan that does more harm than good", "Sympathetic but critical: its core idea is sound, but it can do harm when applied in only one direction", "Wholeheartedly enthusiastic: it should be applied to every new technology without exception", "Indifferent: the writer reports both sides of the debate without taking any position"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段作者指出這項原則 more modest than its reputation，並肯定 Ignorance, it insists, is not an alibi；第六段又說 The lasting value of the Rio formulation lies... in the question it refuses to let us dodge，可見作者認同其核心精神。但作者也接受第四段批評者的觀點，即 Precaution applied in only one direction is thus not caution at all，並在第六段強調 we must not hide behind caution either。(A) 作者並未認為它弊大於利；(C) 作者並不贊成把任何新活動都視為有罪的強硬版本；(D) 作者在第五、六段明確提出自己的主張，並非不表態。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a299", "title": "In Praise of Being Wrong: A Speech to the Graduating Class", "title_zh": "犯錯禮讚：給畢業生的一席話", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Education", "Life"], "passage": "Graduates, families, and teachers who have read more of these young people's essays than anyone should have to: good morning. A commencement speech, I am told, should send you into the world with a few words of wisdom. I would rather send you out with a few words in praise of being wrong, a subject on which, after forty years as a geologist, I can claim genuine expertise. Let me begin with a show of hands. How many of you have ever been completely, confidently mistaken about something? Look around. Nearly every hand is up, and the few that are not belong, I suspect, to people who are mistaken about that, too.\n\nEarly in my career, I spent a whole field season building a theory about the rocks along a stretch of coast, and presented it confidently at a conference. Then a quiet graduate student asked me one question about a thin layer of sand I had dismissed as unimportant. I had no answer. I went back to the cliffs, and within a week I knew that the layer was the key to everything and that my theory was wrong. I would love to tell you that I felt grateful. I felt sick. I spent two days drafting a letter explaining why I had been almost right. Then I tore it up and wrote one sentence instead: \"You were right, and I was wrong.\" It was the most useful thing I have ever written.\n\nWhy was that sentence so hard to write? Part of the answer is that a mistake, while we are making it, does not feel like a mistake; it feels like knowledge. By the time we discover the error, we have usually built something on top of it: an argument, a reputation, sometimes a sense of who we are. Admitting it seems to mean tearing all that down, so we defend it more fiercely than it ever deserved. This is not a weakness peculiar to stubborn people. It is the ordinary equipment of the human mind, and you have it, and so do I.\n\nYet the most successful method humans have devised for understanding the world is built on exactly this painful experience. Science is not a collection of truths; it is a procedure for discovering which of our beliefs are false. In my own field, the idea that continents drift was widely dismissed for decades, until evidence from the ocean floor made a revised form of it, plate tectonics, the foundation of the discipline. Not long ago, most doctors were sure that stomach ulcers were caused by stress and lifestyle, until researchers showed that a bacterium is behind a great many of them, a discovery later honored with a Nobel Prize. In each case, progress came not from people who were never wrong but from a community that had agreed to treat being wrong as information rather than disgrace.\n\nHere, then, is my advice, some of which may prove mistaken. First, practice saying the words. \"I was wrong\" is a sentence like any other, and the more you say it about small things, the less it will choke you when the stakes are high. Second, before you reject a view, make sure you can state it so fairly that its supporters would nod along. Until you can, you have not earned your disagreement. Third, hold your opinions as you would a cup of hot tea: firmly enough to use them, loosely enough to put them down. You are not your opinions; you are the person who chose them, and you may choose again. Fourth, find friends who will tell you when you are wrong, and thank them when they do, even if, like me, you need two days to mean it.\n\nGraduates, you have spent years being rewarded for right answers, and right answers matter. But the world you are entering will not print the answers at the back. It will ask you things no one has answered yet, and you will get many of them wrong. When that happens, remember that changing your mind is not a sign that your mind has failed. It is a sign that it is working. Congratulations. Go out and be wrong, boldly and often, and then, please, be a little less wrong tomorrow.", "passage_zh": "各位畢業生、各位家長，還有讀了這些年輕人那麼多篇作文、多到超出任何人該承受程度的各位老師：早安。有人告訴我，畢業典禮的致詞應該用幾句智慧之言，送各位踏入這個世界。我倒寧願用幾句讚美「犯錯」的話送各位出發——在這個主題上，當了四十年地質學家的我，可以說是貨真價實的專家。讓我先請大家舉個手。你們之中，有多少人曾經完完全全、而且信心滿滿地弄錯過某件事？看看四周。幾乎每一隻手都舉起來了；至於少數沒舉手的人，我猜，他們在這件事上也弄錯了。\n\n在我職業生涯的早期，我花了一整個野外調查季，針對某段海岸的岩石建立了一套理論，還在一場學術研討會上自信滿滿地發表。後來，一位文靜的研究生只問了我一個問題，是關於一層被我認定無關緊要的薄沙層。我答不出來。我回到那片懸崖，不到一個星期就明白了：那一層沙正是一切的關鍵，而我的理論是錯的。我很想告訴各位，我當時心懷感激。事實上，我只覺得一陣反胃。我花了兩天起草一封信，解釋為什麼我其實幾乎是對的。然後我把信撕了，改寫一句話：「你是對的，我錯了。」那是我這輩子寫過最有用的東西。\n\n為什麼那句話那麼難寫？部分原因在於，錯誤在我們犯下的當下，感覺起來並不像錯誤，而像是知識。等到我們發現錯誤時，通常已經在它上面蓋起了一些東西：一套論證、一份名聲，有時甚至是對自我的認同。承認錯誤，似乎就意味著要把這一切全部拆掉，於是我們捍衛它，捍衛得比它應得的還要激烈。這並不是固執的人才有的弱點，而是人類心智的標準配備——你們有，我也有。\n\n然而，人類為了理解世界所發明過最成功的方法，正是建立在這種令人痛苦的經驗之上。科學不是一堆真理的集合，而是一套找出我們哪些信念是錯誤的程序。在我自己的領域裡，「大陸會漂移」的想法曾經被廣泛否定了數十年，直到來自海底的證據，使它的修訂版——板塊構造學說——成為這門學科的基礎。不久以前，大多數醫師還深信胃潰瘍是壓力和生活方式造成的，直到研究人員證明，其中有許多是由一種細菌引起的；這項發現後來還獲得了諾貝爾獎。在每一個例子中，進步都不是來自從不犯錯的人，而是來自一個早已同意把犯錯視為資訊、而非恥辱的群體。\n\n那麼，以下是我的建議，其中有些也可能會被證明是錯的。第一，練習說出這句話。「我錯了」跟其他句子沒什麼兩樣；你越常在小事上說它，到了事關重大的時候，它就越不會哽在你的喉嚨裡。第二，在你否定一個觀點之前，先確定你能把它陳述得十分公允，公允到連支持它的人都會點頭稱是。在你做到之前，你還沒有資格表示反對。第三，拿你的意見，就像拿一杯熱茶那樣：握得夠緊，才能使用它；握得夠鬆，才能把它放下。你不等於你的意見；你是選擇了這些意見的人，而你可以重新選擇。第四，找一些會在你錯的時候告訴你的朋友，而當他們這麼做時，向他們道謝——即使你像我一樣，需要兩天才能真心誠意地說出口。\n\n畢業生們，你們花了好幾年因為答對而得到獎勵，而正確的答案確實很重要。但你們即將踏入的世界，不會把答案印在最後一頁。它會問你們一些從來沒有人回答過的問題，而你們會答錯其中的許多題。當那種時候來臨，請記得：改變心意不代表你的心智失靈了，而代表它正在運作。恭喜各位。勇敢地、經常地去犯錯吧，然後，拜託，明天要錯得少一點。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the speaker's main message to the graduates?", "options": ["Science is the only reliable way of finding out what is true.", "They should keep their opinions to themselves until they are certain.", "Right answers matter far less in real life than they did at school.", "Admitting and learning from mistakes is a strength worth developing."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段講者表明要送給畢業生 a few words in praise of being wrong；第二段以自己承認錯誤的經驗為例，第四段說明科學靠修正錯誤而進步，第五段提供練習承認錯誤的具體建議，第六段結語說 changing your mind is not a sign that your mind has failed. It is a sign that it is working。可見主旨在鼓勵畢業生把承認錯誤、從錯誤中學習視為值得培養的能力。(A) 科學只是第四段的例證，講者並未說它是唯一可靠的方法；(B) 講者鼓勵大家表達並修正想法，並未要畢業生在確定之前保持沉默；(C) 與第六段 right answers matter 相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what did the speaker do after the graduate student asked about the layer of sand?", "options": ["Published a paper explaining why the theory was almost correct", "Went back to the site and realized that the theory was mistaken", "Convinced the student that the layer of sand was unimportant", "Gave a second talk at the conference to thank the student"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：I went back to the cliffs, and within a week I knew that the layer was the key to everything and that my theory was wrong，也就是講者回到現場，發現自己的理論是錯的。(A) 講者只是起草了一封解釋自己 almost right 的信，而且後來撕掉了，並未發表論文；(C) 與文意相反，講者發現那層沙正是關鍵；(D) 文中只說講者先前在研討會上發表理論，並未提到再去演講道謝。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the speaker advise graduates to do before rejecting a view?", "options": ["Ask their friends whether the view is popular", "Wait two days before saying what they think of it", "Check whether scientists have already proven it false", "Make sure they can describe it fairly enough to satisfy its supporters"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：before you reject a view, make sure you can state it so fairly that its supporters would nod along，也就是要能公允地陳述該觀點，連支持者都認同這樣的描述。(A) 講者建議找會指出自己錯誤的朋友，並非詢問觀點是否受歡迎；(B)「兩天」是講者說自己需要兩天才能真心道謝，並非對否定觀點的建議；(C) 文中並未如此建議。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"peculiar\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["limited to", "strange to", "hidden from", "useful for"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：This is not a weakness peculiar to stubborn people. It is the ordinary equipment of the human mind, and you have it, and so do I。後句說這是人類心智的標準配備、人人都有，可知前句意為「這不是固執的人才『特有』的弱點」，peculiar to 意為「某人或某物所特有的、只限於」。(B) strange（奇怪的）雖是 peculiar 常見的意思，但放在 peculiar to 這個用法中不合文意；(C) 隱藏、(D) 有用都與上下文無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the speaker ask the audience for a show of hands in paragraph 1?", "options": ["To find out how many graduates plan to study geology", "To test whether the audience has been listening carefully", "To show with a touch of humor that everyone has been mistaken", "To embarrass the few graduates who never admit their mistakes"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段講者問 How many of you have ever been completely, confidently mistaken about something?，接著說 Nearly every hand is up，並打趣說沒舉手的人 are mistaken about that, too。這是以幽默的方式說明人人都曾犯錯，為整篇讚美犯錯的演講鋪路。(A) 舉手與是否打算讀地質學無關；(B) 演講才剛開始，並非測試聽眾是否專心；(D) 那句話是輕鬆的玩笑，並非要讓人難堪。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a300", "title": "The Gift of Forgetting: Socrates' Quarrel with Writing", "title_zh": "遺忘的禮物：蘇格拉底與文字之爭", "level": "Reading · C2", "tags": ["Technology", "Culture"], "passage": "Near the end of Plato's dialogue the Phaedrus, Socrates tells a story set in ancient Egypt. The god Theuth, inventor of numbers, geometry and astronomy, presents his creations to King Thamus. When they come to writing, Theuth promises that it will make the Egyptians wiser and improve their memories. The king is unimpressed. The inventor of a thing, he observes, is not the best judge of whether it will help or harm its users. Writing will breed forgetfulness, because people who trust in marks outside themselves will stop exercising the memory within. It is a remedy not for remembering but for reminding. And it will give learners not wisdom but its appearance: having heard much without being taught, they will seem to know a great deal while knowing little.\n\nSocrates then adds a complaint of his own. Written words, he says, are like paintings: they seem alive, but ask them a question and they keep a solemn silence. They say the same thing forever, cannot explain themselves to a reader who misunderstands, and cannot choose whom to address. Real knowledge, for Socrates, can answer back, defend itself and adapt to the listener, and such knowledge lives only in a mind that has made it its own. A text can hold information; it cannot, on this view, hold understanding.\n\nThe irony is impossible to miss. Socrates wrote nothing, and we know of his objection to writing only because his student Plato wrote it down, in a dialogue that has survived for some twenty-four centuries. Had Socrates had his way, the warning would have died with the last person who heard it. Yet the irony may be less damning than it first appears. Plato chose to write not treatises but dialogues, which imitate the questioning and answering of living conversation, as though trying to build into writing some of what Socrates feared it lacked. Nor did Thamus claim that writing does nothing; his fear was that it does its job so well that we stop doing that job ourselves.\n\nThat complaint has accompanied almost every tool we have invented to think with. When printing spread across Europe, scholars complained of a bewildering flood of books. When pocket calculators reached classrooms, many feared that children would never learn mental arithmetic. When search engines put the world's information a few keystrokes away, critics asked whether we now remembered where facts could be found rather than the facts themselves, and some studies, not all of which have held up, seemed to agree. Now artificial intelligence that can draft an essay revives the worry in its sharpest form, since what we hand over is no longer merely memory but something closer to thought itself. Thamus would recognize the fear at once: the appearance of wisdom without the substance.\n\nHonesty requires admitting what the pessimists missed. Writing probably did weaken certain feats of memory; performers in oral cultures, by most accounts, carried in their heads poems few of us could now learn by heart. But writing also let knowledge accumulate beyond the capacity of any single mind. Without it there would be no science that builds on the work of the dead, no laws that bind rulers as well as the ruled, no history beyond living memory. Besides, people have always shared the work of remembering; in families and communities, no one carries everything. A mind that relies on outside help is not necessarily diminished; it may simply have freed its attention for other work.\n\nWhat, then, should we take from Thamus? Not his conclusion, surely; few of us would trade our libraries for his court. But his distinction remains a useful test. A tool that reminds us of what we already understand extends the mind; a tool that spares us the need to understand may quietly empty it. The difference lies less in the tool than in its use. A student who looks up a forgotten date loses little. A student who asks a machine for an argument she could not explain if questioned possesses the words without the knowledge, precisely the condition Socrates described. Every age must decide which kinds of thinking to hand over and which to keep, and the decision is rarely made all at once. It is made, as Thamus might have predicted, one convenience at a time.", "passage_zh": "在柏拉圖的對話錄《斐德羅篇》接近結尾之處，蘇格拉底講了一個以古埃及為背景的故事。發明了數字、幾何與天文學的神祇圖特（Theuth），向國王塔穆斯（Thamus）展示他的種種創造。說到文字時，圖特保證，它會讓埃及人變得更有智慧，並增進他們的記憶力。國王卻不以為然。他指出，一樣東西的發明者，並不是判斷它對使用者有益或有害的最佳人選。文字會滋生遺忘，因為人們一旦信賴自身以外的符號，就不會再鍛鍊內在的記憶。它是一帖幫助「提醒」的藥方，而不是幫助「記憶」的藥方。而且，它帶給學習者的不是智慧，而是智慧的表象：他們聽說了許多事，卻沒有真正受教，於是看起來懂得很多，其實所知甚少。\n\n蘇格拉底接著補充了他自己的不滿。他說，寫下來的文字就像繪畫：看起來栩栩如生，但你若向它們提問，它們就保持莊嚴的沉默。它們永遠說著同樣的話，無法向誤解它們的讀者解釋自己，也無法選擇要對誰說話。對蘇格拉底而言，真正的知識能夠回應、能夠為自己辯護，也能因應聽者而調整；而這樣的知識，只存在於一個已將它化為己有的心靈之中。依照這種觀點，文本可以承載資訊，卻無法承載理解。\n\n這其中的諷刺顯而易見。蘇格拉底本人從未寫下任何著作；我們之所以知道他反對書寫，完全是因為他的學生柏拉圖把它寫了下來，寫在一部流傳了大約二十四個世紀的對話錄裡。如果照蘇格拉底的意思，這個警告早就隨著最後一個聽過它的人一起消逝了。然而，這個諷刺或許不像乍看之下那麼具殺傷力。柏拉圖選擇寫的不是論文，而是對話錄——一種模仿活生生的對話中一問一答的文體，彷彿他試圖在書寫之中注入一些蘇格拉底擔心它所欠缺的特質。塔穆斯也從未說過文字毫無用處；他擔心的是，文字把它的工作做得太好，以至於我們不再親自去做那份工作。\n\n這種抱怨，伴隨著我們為了思考而發明的幾乎每一種工具。當印刷術傳遍歐洲時，學者們抱怨書籍氾濫，多得令人眼花撩亂。當袖珍計算機進入教室時，許多人擔心孩子們永遠學不會心算。當搜尋引擎讓世上的資訊只需敲幾下鍵盤就能取得時，批評者質疑，我們如今記住的，是不是只有到哪裡可以找到事實，而不是事實本身；而一些研究——雖然並非全都經得起後來的檢驗——似乎也支持這種說法。如今，能夠撰寫文章的人工智慧，把這種憂慮推向了最尖銳的形式，因為我們交出去的不再只是記憶，而是某種更接近思考本身的東西。塔穆斯一眼就能認出這種恐懼：有智慧的表象，卻沒有智慧的實質。\n\n平心而論，我們必須承認那些悲觀者所忽略的地方。文字或許確實削弱了某些驚人的記憶本領；據多數說法，口傳文化中的表演者能把長篇詩歌記在腦中，而那樣的詩歌，如今我們很少有人背得下來。但文字也讓知識得以累積，超越任何單一心靈的容量。沒有文字，就不會有建立在已故前人成果之上的科學，不會有既約束被統治者、也約束統治者的法律，也不會有超越在世者記憶範圍的歷史。何況，人們向來就是分工合作來記憶的；在家庭和群體之中，沒有人需要記住所有的事。依賴外在協助的心靈，未必就是退化的心靈；它或許只是把注意力騰了出來，去做其他的工作。\n\n那麼，我們該從塔穆斯身上學到什麼？想必不是他的結論；我們很少有人願意拿自己的圖書館去換他的宮廷。但他所做的區分，至今仍是一個有用的檢驗標準。一種提醒我們想起已經理解之事的工具，能延伸我們的心智；一種讓我們不必再去理解的工具，則可能悄悄地把心智掏空。差別與其說在於工具本身，不如說在於如何使用它。一個查詢自己忘記的日期的學生，損失不大；但一個請機器替她提出一套論證、被人問起時卻無法解釋的學生，擁有的是字句，而不是知識——這正是蘇格拉底所描述的情況。每個時代都必須決定，要把哪些種類的思考交出去，又要把哪些留給自己，而這個決定很少是一次就做成的。正如塔穆斯或許早已料到的，它是隨著一次又一次的便利，一點一點做成的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Socrates was right that writing has made human beings less intelligent.", "Artificial intelligence is far more dangerous than any earlier invention.", "An ancient warning about writing still helps us weigh what we gain and lose by relying on tools.", "Plato disagreed with Socrates and wrote his dialogues to prove him wrong."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一、二段介紹《斐德羅篇》中塔穆斯與蘇格拉底對文字的批評；第四段說 That complaint has accompanied almost every tool we have invented to think with，並延伸到印刷術、計算機、搜尋引擎與人工智慧；第五段承認文字帶來的好處；第六段則說 his distinction remains a useful test。可見主旨是：這則古老的警告至今仍能幫助我們判斷依賴工具的得與失。(A) 作者在第五段指出悲觀者忽略了文字的好處，第六段也說不會接受塔穆斯的結論；(B) 作者只說人工智慧使這種憂慮 in its sharpest form，並未說它比以往任何發明都危險；(D) 第三段只說柏拉圖的對話體或許彌補了文字的缺點，並未說他想證明蘇格拉底是錯的。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to Thamus, why would writing harm those who use it?", "options": ["It would be too difficult for ordinary Egyptians to learn.", "It would make people forget the gods who had invented it.", "It could not record numbers and astronomy accurately.", "It would lead people to rely on outside marks instead of their memory."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：Writing will breed forgetfulness, because people who trust in marks outside themselves will stop exercising the memory within，也就是說，人們會依賴自身以外的符號，而不再鍛鍊自己的記憶。(A) 文中並未提到文字難學；(B) 文中並未說人們會忘記發明文字的神；(C) 數字與天文學是圖特的其他發明，與塔穆斯對文字的批評無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why might the irony be less damning than it first appears?", "options": ["Plato wrote the dialogue in secret so that Socrates would never read it.", "Plato destroyed his own notes after Socrates had died.", "Plato wrote in a form that imitates the exchange of a living conversation.", "Plato asked his students to memorize the dialogue instead of reading it."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：Yet the irony may be less damning than it first appears. Plato chose to write not treatises but dialogues, which imitate the questioning and answering of living conversation，彷彿試圖在書寫中加入蘇格拉底擔心它欠缺的特質。(A) 文中並未說柏拉圖祕密寫作；(B) 文中並未提到柏拉圖銷毀筆記；(D) 文中並未提到要學生背誦對話錄。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"breed\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["prevent", "produce", "measure", "conceal"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：Writing will breed forgetfulness, because people who trust in marks outside themselves will stop exercising the memory within。塔穆斯認為，人們不再鍛鍊記憶，因此文字會「導致、產生」遺忘；breed 在此意為「引起、滋生」，而非常見的「飼養、繁殖」。(A) prevent（防止）與文意相反；(C) measure（測量）、(D) conceal（隱藏）都與上下文不合。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["Using a tool to recall a forgotten fact is less worrying than using it to replace one's own reasoning.", "Students should not be allowed to use search engines or AI tools at school.", "Oral cultures were wiser than literate ones because they relied on memory.", "The fears once raised about printing and calculators have all proved correct."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第六段：A student who looks up a forgotten date loses little. A student who asks a machine for an argument she could not explain if questioned possesses the words without the knowledge，並說 The difference lies less in the tool than in its use。可見作者認為用工具查回遺忘的事實不太令人擔心，用工具取代自己的思考才值得警惕。(B) 作者強調差別在於使用方式，並未主張全面禁止；(C) 第五段雖提到口傳文化的記憶本領，但也強調文字讓知識得以累積，並未說口傳文化比較有智慧；(D) 第五段 Honesty requires admitting what the pessimists missed，可見作者並不認為過去的擔憂都已應驗。故選 (A)。"}]}];
